\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for June 5, 2026, MS NOW\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, John Brennan, Lisa Rubin \par \uc2 8,425 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 June 2026\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MS NOW HOST: Do not go anywhere. "ALL IN" with Chris Hayes starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MS NOW HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM PLATNER (D-ME), SENATE CANDIDATE: There are some allegations in this piece that I just want to be kind of unequivocal about are simply not true.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voiceover): The primary allegation shaking up the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 PLATNER: There are things in this that I absolutely will take responsibility for.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voiceover): Tonight, as Graham Platner carries on the unbelievable stakes of this Senate race and the reaction on the ground in Maine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The biggest difference between the Graham Platner situation and the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  situation, one person says, yes, that`s wrong, I need to do better. The other person is like, what are you talking about?\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voiceover): Then the president says the quiet part out loud about Bill Pulte`s promotion.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He`s a very smart guy and he may find out some things about the rigged elections.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voiceover): And why the threat of Trump family corruption is igniting protests across Albania.\par \par \uc2 IVANKA TRUMP, DAUGHTER OF \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`m working on an incredible project with my husband in the Mediterranean. It`s massive in scale.\par \par \uc2 HAYES (voiceover): When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. We begin tonight with some election news out of California where the AP now projects that former \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary and Attorney General \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  has advanced to the general election for governor. It`s a big deal.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the country, you are looking live at a Graham Platner rally in Bar Harbor, Maine. Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  of California is stumping with the Democratic Senate candidate you see there tonight in front of a large crowd. We should say Platner received a standing ovation. He thanked Maine voters for having his back.\par \par \uc2 And this, of course, his first big public rally with the primary on Tuesday since the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reported that three of Platner`s ex-girlfriends found his behavior unsettling. It included allegations by a woman named that could not be corroborated by The Times, a woman named Lyndsey Fifield who has spent much of her life as a professional conservative operative living in the D.C. area.\par \par \uc2 Fifield said Platner was rough with her on two occasions, specifically an allegation he yanked her out of a cab by her wrist. An allegation he twisted her arm, shoved her in a bedroom, and closed the door from the other side. She says it hurt. She said she was not injured. They never punched or hit her.\par \par \uc2 During tonight`s remarks, Platner decried what he said are politically motivated and false allegations. Of course, last night, we had Platner on this very program for an exclusive interview where he unequivocally denied those claims.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PLATNER: There are some allegations in this piece that I just want to be kind of unequivocal about are simply not true. Anything alleging physicality, anything alleging that I knew what my tattoo was, these are the statements of someone who`s politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 In this piece, there`s a lot about my struggling not being a good boyfriend, certainly self-medicating with alcohol, and I`ve been very upfront since the beginning of this campaign that that was a pretty dark period of my life after I come came back from my combat service. And that`s what that combat -- that`s what that kind of life looks like. And so, there are things in this that I absolutely will take responsibility for and have been speaking about openly for months now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As I said, I just want to be clear. Fifield says that Platner never hit or punched her. There were three other ex-girlfriends who also spoke to the Times who described Platner as supportive boyfriend, a gentle giant. Platner has been very open about his past struggles with both alcoholism and PTSD after multiple combat tours. And in fact, the story of his redemption arc, as he presents it, his transformation is kind of at the core of his campaign message to Maine voters, which is what popped up when MS NOW Correspondent Kevin Frey went actually to Ellsworth in Maine to talk to voters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What I genuinely appreciate about Gram Platner is this story of reclamation of who he was 15 years ago sent into a war that we asked him to do horrific things and he saw horrific things and he became a really broken hard person at that time and that`s not who he is now. So, people are bringing up stuff from 15 years ago. We did that to him. And he has healed from that in large part from what I see with his wife Amy helping him through. So, if the stories that are horrific that people are bringing up are from 15 years ago, they`re not relevant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That to me is the biggest difference between the Graham Platner situation and the Donald Trump situation. One person says, yes, that`s wrong, I need to do better. The other person is like, what are you talking about? That`s not that bad.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Does he have a problematic past? Yes. But I would rather have a redemption story than somebody telling you how wonderful they are, how much research they do, and yet they still make the wrong decision for the people of Maine. I desperately don`t want to have Susan Collins as my senator. I feel that she is -- she says that she cares about Maine, yet she`s shutting down our rural hospitals. She voted for the war in Iran. People here have to drive incredible distances because it`s Maine and they`re paying a fortune for gas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:06:08]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That last moment, I think, really gets to the heart of the choice for Maine voters. A lot of them have reservations about Platner`s past, his character. They think he`s probably done and said some pretty bad things. A lot of them also really do not want to send Susan Collins back to the Senate. And I think voters are increasingly aware of the fact that Collins is really a party-line Republican. She has been a rubber stamp for the Trump agenda during both terms.\par \par \uc2 I also think Senate Republicans realize she`s in trouble, right? I mean, this is a state that Donald Trump has lost three times. She managed to win in 2020, but she`s got a real tough road ahead of her. So, yesterday -- this is important to understand how this goes. So, Senate controls the majority, right? They`re doing this reconciliation bill where they`ve got a bunch of votes. And yesterday, the Senate majority basically staged a sham vote intentionally, like a show, like a little play where she and a couple of other Republicans, also up for reelection, got to cast meaningless votes against Donald Trump`s $1.8 billion slush fund for insurrectionists.\par \par \uc2 Now, everyone knew that it was doomed to fail from the beginning. They weren`t actually going to vote it down because Republicans don`t actually want to cross the lead of their party. And which is weird because on this particular thing, the slush fund, the Trump administration says it`s not going forward. So, it should be easy to put in legislation that says it can`t happen, right? We`re all in agreement. But they don`t actually want to borrow your money from being stolen from the government to pay off cop beaters and seditionists. And so, what they do is Collins gets to pretend to be independent when the states don`t actually matter.\par \par \uc2 But this is the other part of this. When everything is on the line, well then Collins is a reliable vote. She`s demonstrated she was always stand with Trump. Of course, voting for the big ugly bill that that woman mentioned that has led to hospital closures. And also, after all, she was the key vote, the key vote to get Brett Kavanaaugh on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. SUSAN COLLINS (R-ME): There is also been considerable focus on the future of abortion rights based on the concern that Judge Kavanaugh would seek to overturn Roe v. Wade. Protecting this right is important to me. To my knowledge, Judge Kavanaugh is the first Supreme Court nominee to express the view that precedent is not merely a practice and tradition, but rooted in article three of our constitution itself.\par \par \uc2 As the judge asserted to me, a long-established president is not something to be trimmed, narrowed, discarded, or overlooked. Mr. President, I will vote to confirm Judge Kavanaugh.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Man, is that wild to watch in the time capsule. See the staging there. Of course, not a lot of ton of women in the Republican Senate caucus, but framed up in that way. I think he`s going to listen to precedent, just volcanic levels of B.S. because of course everyone there just like in the sham vote that they did yesterday, everyone there knew exactly what they were doing. They all knew.\par \par \uc2 Less than four years later, whoopsies, Kavanaugh voted to overturn Roe to overturn the precedent that had been in place for decades and launch the ongoing erosion of abortion rights and women`s access to healthcare throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, Susan Collins has not faced voters since that monumental decision since Roe was overturned, nor has she apologized for her role or her evident credulousness in the end of the constitutional right to an abortion. She also has not attended a Senate Health Committee meeting focused on reproductive rights since that decision.\par \par \uc2 It cannot go unsaid either since we`re talking about him that Brett Kavanaugh`s confirmation was not guaranteed before Collins agreed to support him. In fact, it was really up in the air because for one, he faced very serious allegations of sexual misconduct. You probably remember this. A woman named Christine Blasey Ford testified before the Senate that Kavanaugh and a friend had sexually assaulted her when she was in high school. They were at a party. Everyone was drunk. He denies it. When she stepped forward, there was a, you know, an intense partisan effort to discredit her. I mean, again, there`s a Supreme Court seat on the line.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:32]\par \par \uc2 That effort was joined by a group called Ladies for Kavanaugh, which was co-founded by none other than Lyndsey Fifield. That is the same woman who dated Platner and who alleges he treated her roughly on two occasions. She has since in posts on Twitter called him abusive. Speaking of the New York Post at the time though, Fifield said of the Kavanaugh allegations, "In the wake of the baseless 11th hour accusations orchestrated to stop Kavanaugh`s confirmation, we couldn`t stay silent anymore." And for her part, Susan Collins also cast out on Ford`s allegation against Kavanaugh, citing what she said was a lack of corroboration.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, all of this stuff gets refracted through our priors and our biases and, you know, our politics. I mean, there`s just no way it can`t be, right? So, whether out of genuine skepticism or motivated reasoning or the sheer calculation of their sheer political interest, which I think was probably the dominant factor here, conservatives clearly understood how important getting Kavanaugh on the court was to the right`s political project.\par \par \uc2 And they rallied around him and they pushed him through, culminating with Susan Collins big triumphant speech. Which is why we are here where we are. Which is why the eyes of the country are on a state of about 1.4 four million people because if Susan Collins is reelected, remember this, she will continue to rubber stamp the Trump agenda. And all that means, and mark my words, there will be more chances for her to install right-wing justices on the court to help cement conservative control of that institution for another generation.\par \par \uc2 Sam Alito is 76 years old. He is all but confirmed. He`s retiring soon. Clarence Thomas is 77. He`s already threatened to quit a