ame a big thing on the right, that became a big thing. Maria Bartiromo on Fox News, Fox Business was saying, oh, look at this, this proves the fraud. John Solomon, who is a reporter who now is going to work at the White House, talking all about how this shows the fraud in California that predated the election, so now they`re building on that. They`ve already germinated this idea that California is not trustworthy.\par \par \uc2 It is impossible to believe, given all the pieces that you just outlined, that there is not intentionality here, particularly given the fact that California just redrew all of its house districts.\par \par \uc2 If they`re able to on election night, say, hey, we got to shut this whole thing down and deal with the election day totals. Maybe they can scrape some of those house seats back, despite what the voters actually wanted.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that is I mean, you articulated what I think is the worst-case scenario, right? And the one that seems like the most obvious. And again, remember, Trump did this in 2020 the whole rigged election was like these dumps came in late.\par \par \uc2 There`s another big legal development today on the slush fund, which I think is very interesting. Now, quite notably, the Senate Republicans declined to put anything in writing to bar the slush fund. In fact, went through great pains to engineer a set of sham votes that would avoid it going into the law, going into their law to ban it, because they could, they could easily have done that, and you know, Trump would have to sign it, or he wouldn`t need to veto it.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Department of Justice was before the judge on the slush fund and they said what?\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: They submitted actually two briefs today in two different cases regarding the slush fund and they essentially say both of the cases are moot, that`s their principal argument these cases are dead because Todd Blanche stood in front of a House Appropriations Subcommittee earlier this week and when asked by Congresswoman Grace Meng is the slush fund, in fact, something that you`re dropping. He confirmed we are dropping the slush fund. Will you put that in writing, Mr. Blanche? Was her follow-up question, and he refused to commit to doing anything.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He literally said, I`m not -- he literally said, `I`m not putting anything in writing, if I`m recalling correctly.\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: Yes, I don`t remember what the exact words he used are, and I don`t want to misrepresent them, but at the very least, it was declining to commit to put anything in writing, and now they`re saying it`s moot.\par \par \uc2 That`s basically the legal equivalent of saying this case is over because you should just trust my verbal representations. I`ve never seen a court declare mootness on that basis before, nor do I think it`s going to do so now.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:06]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It seems to me, Philip, there`s a real tell in the machinations of the Senate that we reviewed earlier in the hour to not actually kill this dead, and I just, again, there`s a world in which, like, post midterms, it`s like, whoops, it`s back.\par \par \uc2 BUMP: Yes. No, absolutely. You know, but part of this is that very famous post-2020 election quote from the Republican strategist. Look, nothing`s going to happen. Let`s just -- though, let`s just humor him for a little bit, right? That has been sort of the standard operating procedure for what, a decade, 11 years now. That is, it seems to play here.\par \par \uc2 Senate Republicans, Republicans broadly understand this is poison, this is the last thing they need before the midterm elections, but they also realize we don`t want to make Donald Trump and his base mad before the midterm elections. The standing mode of the GOP right now is, how can I not make everyone mad at me? Right?\par \par \uc2 And so, what they`re doing here on a lot of these issues is doing just enough to keep the voters happy and just