h on January 6th, the ones that were like trying to crush him in the door until he died and then he was -- he was rescued. Or maybe they`ll give it to the dude with the Auschwitz T- shirt or the guy with the Confederate flag. Or maybe he`ll dole out millions to the men convicted of seditious conspiracy for an attempt armed insurrection against our democracy. You believe the guy who reportedly wants that deal has your best interest at heart?\par \par \uc2 Congressman Jamie Raskin is a Democrat of Maryland. He serves as ranking member of the Judiciary Committee and he joins me now. OK, I don`t know where to start with this. Again, you`re the constitutional law professor and you`re the lawyer and I`m not. I`m just -- I`m married to a constitutional law professor and a very good lawyer. Can this possibly be legal? I want to say that it`s not legal, but can this possibly be legal?\par \par \uc2 [20:10:56]\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Well, it`s an outrageous abuse of the judgment fund that the Department of Justice has had since the 1960s to settle actually valid legal causes of action claims brought against the government. And for many decades now, Congress has trusted the Department of Justice to administer this fund fairly. But of course, we`re living in Trump land. And so, it`s just one more political slush fund for them.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s utterly bogus, spurious $10 billion lawsuit against the IRS is not being settled. That`s just an excuse for them to go ahead and create this fund. If he went to court with that cause of action against the IRS, his lawyers would be sanctioned like so many of them. It`s just utterly ridiculous. It is ludicrous. But they want to use it as the pretext to set up this $1.7 billion slush fund.\par \par \uc2 Congress has got to reign in the administration and we`ve got to restore control over the appropriations of the government because the Department of Justice, you know, if you do the math on this, Chris, the first thing I