hip with Jeffrey Epstein in your public statements. Your lack of candor demonstrates you are unfit to perform the duties required of you as U.S. Secretary of Commerce.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Yassamin Ansari of Arizona is one of the Oversight Democrats who attended the hearing and signed that letter and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 I mean, obviously, I think Democrats are going to be skeptical of any Trump cabinet member and he did seem to be not the most forthcoming. Why call for his resignation?\par \par \uc2 [20:35:36]\par \par \uc2 REP. YASSAMIN ANSARI (D-AZ): I think of all of the people that we have conducted interviews with so far throughout the Epstein investigation, Howard Lutnick, the Commerce Secretary of the United States of America, was the most blatantly deceptive, the most obviously covering up for, you know, the lies that he has -- he has already told to the American people. And I think we`re all just so sick and tired of how disgustingly corrupt the Trump administration is.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that people in my district, the vast majority of them, and you know, the majority of Americans across this country are just so sick of this corruption as they take away people`s health care. And then Howard Lutnick just sits in front of us behind closed doors without being on video and just lies over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 I read back parts of the transcript and I was just disgusted all over again myself.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I have not read the entire transcript, but I was going through a bunch of it today and he is very I`ll say like squirrely and evasive. Is if that`s a fair or charitable read? Was that how it felt in the room?\par \par \uc2 ANSARI: Yes. And that`s why, you know, I actually wasn`t that concerned for this one that it wasn`t on video compared to some of the others because it`s so clear even when you read the back and forth that he`s lying and that he`s uncomfortable. And it it`s just clear from the responses. I mean, whether you look at our dialogue where I asked him about whether or not in the year 2012 when he went to Epstein`s Island whether he had heard about the 2008 Alex Acosta sweetheart plea deal and the many accusations and the charges against Epstein. He said no even though this was his next-door neighbor and they ran in the same circles. Whether you look at other members exchanges with him and you read them and, you know, how he takes back the fact that he had claimed in October of 2025 that he believed that Jeffrey Epstein blackmailed people.\par \par \uc2 And he`s taken that back and I think that`s one of the pieces that we need to look at most closely and I think the interview with Lutnick as well as our trip earlier this week to West Palm Beach and the hearing that oversight Democrats had with survivors of these heinous crimes really has demonstrated to me that we will have to open much of this back up that we have to bring these individuals back when we take the majority in Congress. And there for these lies because these are crimes.\par \par \uc2 I mean whether you`re under oath or not under oath, lying to Congress is a lie and someone like Howard Lutnick has clearly done so over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to just stay on that one point that you made because that I was looking at the transcript today. That point where you ask OK in 2012 you know were you aware and he says no. Now, at -- you know, at this time this is -- this is a guy who`s like sort of notorious and has a huge reputation and has moved next door and is a billionaire and like people know like they travel in similar circles. It`s on the internet. You`re like, that one is pretty tough and implausible, particularly when paired with the story that he told at length about his wife and him going over there and Epstein saying an incredibly creepy thing about the massage room he keeps in his house. That like all combined his testimony before your committee is that like no, never put the name into Google, like knew nothing about it, right? Like, that`s what he`s saying.\par \par \uc2 ANSARI: Yes, that part was really heated in the moment because I was so shocked that he said that. I mean, I think my expectation for some of these things. It`s like when you`ve been caught so red-handed or you`ve done -- you`ve made a mistake in your life, everybody does make mistakes and maybe you made a bad judgment call. Just say that. I think it`s really bizarre and raises so many more questions that he tries to claim that he didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 And again, I`ve said this many times, but -- and I said it to him. I was like -- I was a child then and I heard about it. It was a kind of a tabloid thing. It was all over the news. I mean everyone knew about this. So, to say that you didn`t know, you are so clearly lying. And again, this is now -- I just -- I just feel like he`s a -- he`s a pathological liar. He`s digging himself deeper into a hole. I don`t think he has any morals whatsoever. And I think he should be fired immediately for that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: On the -- on the sort of original sin here which is that Acosta deal, right? I mean, the idea of someone getting a sweetheart deal or connected. I have to ask about this news and I`m not sure you saw it today. You`re on the Oversight Committee. But about a billionaire indicted in the final days of the Biden administration, Indian billionaire Adani, who hired Trump`s personal lawyer, made a presentation where they said why they think they have a case. It`s Robert Giuffra Jr. And then said we`re going to, you know, invest money in the U.S. and hire a bunch of people, $10 billion investment, and then has the case dismissed, which is a different shade than the sort of original sin that happened with Alex Costa. But there`s a sort of thematic connection here about like who is -- who gets justice and who doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:43]\par \par \uc2 ANSARI: Yes. And let`s talk about who`s gotten 