d.\par \par \uc2 Well, last fall, that state challenged the FDA`s decision to make abortion medication available by mail. Today the Supreme Court issued a stay on a ruling by the lower Fifth Circuit that would have basically blocked telehealth access to mifepristone. It`s one of the two pills used in most medication abortions. The court`s move today means mifepristone still available by mail for now.\par \par \uc2 But again, there is a widespread effort across multiple fronts in Congress, at the regulatory level, in the courts to end medical abortion, to go after states where doctors are mailing the pills to other states where abortion`s banned. And in this midterm election year, some conservatives on the Supreme Court may want to deflect attention from this, sort of nothing to see here. But one of the things I saw today and you see now is the clock is ticking on their ability to keep doing that.\par \par \uc2 Philip Bump is a senior data editor, columnist for CT Insider. Michele Goodwin is professor of constitutional law and global health policy at Georgetown Law School, author of the book Policing the Womb. And they join me now.\par \par \uc2 Michele, let me just start with you. So, basically, you have -- there`s been multiple attempts, right, through the courts to stop the FDA from allowing doctors to prescribe this through telemedicine to mail these this medication. The Fifth Circuit, very conservative court, sided with the Louisiana AG who`s trying to stop that. And then the Supreme Court today stayed that pending appeal, meaning people get to still have the medication.\par \par \uc2 But there were some angry dissents by Alito. What do you make of what the court did today?\par \par \uc2 MICHELE GOODWIN IS PROFESSOR OF CONSTITUTIONAL LAW AND GLOBAL HEALTH POLICY, GEORGETOWN LAW SCHOOL: Well, it`s important what the court did today, but you`re absolutely right that the court has become deeply politicized. It`s a kind of framing that even 10 years ago people would