 Supreme Court is doing now and what Justice Alito has claimed is that the Supreme Court is acting recklessly and disregarding what happened in Dobbs. But that`s exactly what he did with regard to Row.\par \par \uc2 It is highly unusual. In fact, it had never happened that the Supreme Court overturned a right and certainly not one in which there had been a reliance interest which means that there were people actually relying on what the Supreme Court had actually decided in Ro v. Wade. So, it`s a bit ironic. It`s like the pot calling the kettle black or any of those ways in which you see a bit of a hypocrisy, which is exactly what you see from Justice Alito.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also worth noting where Justice Thomas has landed on this. He`s been concerned and mentioned it in Dobbs that all of what we`ve seen basically of the last 50 years of substantive due process rights. He says put them on the table, put them on the chopping block. All of those with the exception of interracial marriage which affects his life, but anything else that we`ve seen with regard to gay marriage, contraceptive access, all of that. So we should be concerned not just about what`s happening here with abortion, but we should also be concerned about contraception and any of the other areas in which Americans have said we want to live in that kind of democracy. All of that is a perfect storm coming from the Supreme Court potentially.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And Thomas in his dissent today talks about the possible criminal prosecution of these doctors. You`ve got a sort of -- you know, we talked about Iran. You know, here`s the polling on abortion just, you know, legal 60 percent, illegal 38 percent. You know Iran is a sort of similar dynamic, right? It`s like not very popular at all. People hate it. It sort of goes back on one of his core promises. There`s a small group of super motivated and powerful people in the Republican coalition who want to go with the war in Iran.\par \par \uc2 Exact same dynamics here. Like you -- we showed the polling it screwed the Republicans in the year of Dobbs in 2022 in the midterms, and yet there`s a small group of people who pretty close to him who really want their version of an Iran war which is mass, mass criminalization of abortion.\par \par \uc2 BUMP: Yes, that`s exactly -- I mean, I was going to mention 2022. People forget that that was a better-than-expected year for Democrats primarily because of the Dobbs decision. I mean, I do think that it`s important to remember the distinction here which is that there is a -- there is a core moral belief that some people have that leads him to this decision. Again, though, that`s not how Donald Trump has ever viewed this. It`s not how he`s ever talked about it. And in 2016, he never betrayed any understanding of the nuance of the issue. And so, now he`s sort of here with his hands tied.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I mean the question is who does he listen to though as we come down the stretch? Because I think you can go either way, right? Like, keep this far away or my base is getting restless. I got to give him something. Philip Bump, Michele Goodwin, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Stay tuned. In just a few minutes. Jen Psaki will be speaking with former FBI Director James Comey. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:55:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As Donald Trump meets with Xi Jinping in China, his Treasury Secretary is discussing what should be the top issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BESSENT, U.S. TREASURY SECRETARY: The U.S. is the undisputed leader in the world here. We have the greatest A.I. companies. We`re actually going to be discussing the A.I. guardrails with the Chinese. It will -- because the Chinese are substantially behind us but they have a very advanced AI industry here. So, the two A.I. superpowers are going to start talking. We`re going to set up a protocol.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Setting up an international protocol of A.I. is a good idea. I don`t know if the Trump administration negotiating is though, but it`s one of the topics and themes we discuss in my new podcast series, Why Is This Happening: The A.I. Endgame. New episodes drop every Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 That`s ALL IN on this Thursday night. "THE BRIEFING" with Jen Psaki starts right now. Good evening, Jen.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2026 Versant Inc.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials  herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be  reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020260515em5e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The Last Word With Lawrence O`Donnell for May 5, 2026, MS NOW\b0\par\par\uc2 Jonathan Capehart \par \uc2 7,454 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2026\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:17]\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , MS NOW HOST: Hey, Jen. So, you talked to the Democratic nominee for governor of Ohio. We`ve got the former Democratic senator from Ohio, \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , who is on the ballot. And I haven`t seen the results yet. I don`t know if his race has been called yet, but he could be the next nominee.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MS NOW HOST, "THE BRIEFING WITH JEN PSAKI": He`s good.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: He`s good?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: He`ll be good in the -- he`ll be the nominee. I think we`re safe to not go out on a limb. But yeah.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Yeah. So, all about Ohio tonight.\par \par \uc2 And also, I should say that our buddy Melissa Murray is going to be on the show later on to talk to talk about her new book about the Constitution. So --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Amazing. What -- what a great show. And Ohio is an exciting one to watch. I -- I`m telling people, it`s like we always say that, but it really is one. So, you got a great conversation ahead.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Well, thanks very much, Jen. Thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Thanks so much. Have a great show.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: All right.\par \par \uc2 If today`s conflicting messages from the Trump administration on its war of choice in Iran tells us anything, it`s that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has no plan. He had no plan at the start of his war, and now he has no plan to get out of his war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`re in a little skirmish. Military -- I call it a skirmish because Iran has no chance. They never did. They know it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: The fact that day to day \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can refer to his war in Iran as a war, then a detour, then a skirmish shows that there are wide gaps between reality and the words Trump is using about his war. And it`s the global economy that is suffering as the chaos in the Strait of Hormuz escalates, instead of winding down. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports today that the United States and Iran made competing claims about having control of the strait, which remains effectively closed, and an American ally, the United Arab Emirates, said. It said that it had come under attack by Iranian missiles and drones for the second day in a row. Commercial traffic is at a total standstill, despite American promises to protect ships.\par \par \uc2 Two commercial vessels crossed the strait under U.S. military protection on Monday and only one on Tuesday, a trickle compared to before the war, when around 130 vessels a day made the passage.\par \par \uc2 So, Donald Trump`s so-called Project Freedom is not actually helping increase the movement of ships through the Strait of Hormuz. Despite the exchange of fire in the strait of Hormuz and Iran`s attack on the United Arab Emirates, Defense Secretary \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, DEFENSE SECRETARY: Ceasefire is not over. Ultimately, this is a separate and distinct project, and we expected there would be some -- some churn at the beginning, which happened. And we said we would defend and defend aggressively. And we absolutely have. Iran knows that.\par \par \uc2 And ultimately, the president is going to make a decision whether anything were to escalate into a violation of a ceasefire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: When asked about whether the ceasefire has been violated, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: They`ve fired at U.S. ships several times in the last 24 hours. What do they need to do to violate the ceasefire?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, you`ll find out, because I`ll let you know. They know what to do, and they know what to do. They know what not to do, more importantly, actually.\par \par \uc2 But they respect us. They didn`t used to respect us, but they respect us more than we`ve ever been respected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Secretary of State \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  today said that the operation is over, while claiming that what`s happening in Iran right now is not war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO, SECRETARY OF STATE: This is a defensive operation. And what that means is very simple. There`s no shooting unless we`re shot at first. Okay? We`re not attacking them. We`re not. But if they`re attacking us, they`re attacking a ship. You need to respond to that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: That`s rich considering that it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who decided to attack Iran in the first place, which led to Iran closing the Strait of Hormuz.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" reports this about how Donald Trump`s attempt to get ships through the Strait of Hormuz, quote, "shows how the U.S. can still influence the battlefield, but faces limits in its ability to change the status quo in the strait. At the same time, the operation comes with risks to U.S. personnel and to commercial ships, most of which are still refusing to transit the strait without clear guarantees that Iran won`t attack them."\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Two sources, including a U.S. official, tell "\uc2 Axios\uc2 " that a high-level Trump administration official informed Iran on Sunday of the impending U.S. operation to guide ships through the Strait of Hormuz, and warned Tehran not to interfere. The private message suggests the White House wanted to mitigate the risk of potential escalation. Nevertheless, some U.S. and Israeli officials believe President Trump could give an order to resume the war later this week if the diplomatic stalemate continues.\par \par \uc2 Today, Donald Trump retreated from his day-old operation when he announced that the U.S. will pause Project Freedom for an unidentified amount of time. Quote, "Based on the request of Pakistan and other countries, the tremendous military success that we have had during the campaign against the country of Iran. And additionally, the fact that great progress has been made toward a complete and final agreement with representatives of Iran, we have mutually agreed that while the blockade will remain in full force, in effect, Project Freedom, the movement of ships through the Strait of Hormuz will be paused for a short period of time to see whether or not the agreement can be finalized and signed."\par \par \uc2 Like I said, no plan. The new line from the administration these days to justify the high cost of the war, both from an economic and a humanitarian standpoint, is it`s better than Iran having a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I also thought oil would go up to 200, 250, maybe 300, and I knew it would be short term, but I thought it would go. I -- look, today, it`s like at 102. And that`s a very small price to pay for getting rid of a nuclear weapon from people that are really mentally deranged.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Donald Trump made those comments about Iran being mentally deranged and lunatics in front of children in the Oval Office, by the way.\par \par \uc2 Talk about bad optics. And Donald Trump says that`s a very small price to pay. Tell that to the average American paying 50 percent more at the gas pump today than before his war started. It also doesn`t seem that the Trump administration is any closer to preventing Iran from getting a nuclear weapon than they were before Trump`s war started. Three sources familiar with the matter told "Reuters" that U.S. intelligence assessments indicate that the time Iran would need to build a nuclear weapon has not changed since last summer, when analysts estimated that a U.S.-Israeli attack had pushed back the timeline to up to a year.\par \par \uc2 Former Fox host Megyn Kelly didn`t mince words yesterday on the disaster that is Donald Trump`s war, that he has no idea how to end. And normally we would not play this much tape of someone like Megyn Kelly, but it`s worth hearing exactly just how angry people like her are -- people who were once Trump`s most ardent supporters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, HOST: I don`t see any upside in this being strung out through the summer. Do you?\par \par \uc2 MEGYN KELLY, FORMER FOX NEWS HOST: No, this has been a disaster for the Trump administration and for our country and for the world. We know they were nowhere near getting a nuclear bomb from our own intelligence community who told that to President Trump just before we unleashed this war on them.\par \par \uc2 So, we don`t need much from the Iranians on that front. They haven`t gotten better able to produce a nuke as a result of this war. Just get out. Let them give us some stupid fig leaf that we can declare as somehow a victory. Oh, it`s better than Obama`s joint, you know, agreement and reopen the strait, which is the number one thing.\par \par \uc2 You did decimate pieces of their military. Great. Call it a win. This is a nightmare. We don`t have to get into specifics, but get out.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Just get out. And what you can see, Piers, is he knows that. He knows that. But the Iranians know it too. And they won`t just bend the knee.\par \par \uc2 And Trump is not used to being in this position. He`s used to bullying his way out of everything, and these guys cannot be bullied. He doesn`t know what to do.\par \par \uc2 He`s great at (EXPLETIVE DELETED) talking and he`s really not great at winding up wars that he starts by choice. That`s what we`re learning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Salty language.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight, Democratic Congressman Jason Crow of Colorado is a member of the House Intelligence and Armed Services Committees and is a former U.S. Army Ranger.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you for coming to THE LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of one of Trump`s Trump voters in this case, Megyn Kelly, her assessment of where we are now in Trump`s war?\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON CROW (D-CO): Well, I wish I could say I was surprised, but this was entirely foreseeable. You know, we saw the last 14 months Donald Trump conducts seven different bombing campaigns in seven different countries. And he treated all of them like a reality TV show, right?\par \par \uc2 [22:10:04]\par \par \uc2 Put the video on there. Come up with great names for the operation, Operation Epic Fury, Operation Midnight Hammer, you know, Project Freedom, you know who could be against Project Freedom?\par \par \uc2 Problem is, is when you`re dealing with great power competition in a global national security strategy, it takes more than a great buzz line, a great name of an operation. In short, video clips to actually deliver a result. In which case, here we are in a quagmire with no real way out of it.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Trump has backed down from his day old operation in the strait in search of some kind of deal. Previous talks yielded nothing, in part because, as Trump himself has said, he`s not even sure who`s in charge in Iran.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Yeah. That`s right. I mean, let`s look at some of the most recent events, right? We initiated a blockade of their blockade. Then we had Project Freedom, which was going to be navigating ships through the Straits of Hormuz, which lasted less than 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 Then we said, you know, earlier in the war that we had obliterated their nuclear weapons. Now they came back and said, no, we haven`t obliterated their nuclear weapons. In fact, most of it remains their nuclear stockpile, rather their capability. Most of it remains in place.\par \par \uc2 So, this is just bumbling from one day to the next. Meanwhile, we have become the laughingstock around the world, right? Our allies have been pushed away, have been demeaned, and we have marginalized ourselves. We have really no coalition here.\par \par \uc2 We have spent tens of billions of dollars of taxpayer money in this and depleted our munitions stockpile. And the worst is there are 13 service members who have died and given their lives in over 400 who have been wounded. Right?\par \par \uc2 This is -- this is so absurd. I`m just angry talking about it right now. And it`s exactly why congress, which actually has the power to stop this right now. Tomorrow, we could go to congress and take a vote and stop this. That`s why it`s Congress`s job to rein in this out-of-control presidency.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Well, on that point, Congressman, according to Marco Rubio, the war is over. And what`s happening in the Strait of Hormuz is defensive. Is this just a way to avoid getting congressional approval for Trump`s war?\par \par \uc2 CROW: Well, why don`t they tell that to the 55,000 U.S. service members stationed throughout the Middle East and the folks on the ships who he been attacked in the last 48 hours, why didn`t they tell that to the service members who have been wounded? Why don`t they tell that to the American taxpayers who are still paying billions of dollars for our troop presence, the navy blockade, for the munitions that we`re using? They just like to claim things are over. Yet the reality is starkly different.\par \par \uc2 You know, Americans, working class Americans like I fought with and who served and died during the 20 years in Iraq and Afghanistan that served those three combat tours with me. You know, they know better. They`re the ones that actually have to still be on the line.\par \par \uc2 So, no, it`s not over. The adversary always has a vote when something is over. And we have unfortunately started something without a plan for finishing it. The first step here, and I don`t want to just be the person criticizing, there is a first step that we can take that can get us back on a path to winding this down and keeping us safe.\par \par \uc2 And that is we have to reengage with our allies. Donald Trump has to stop demeaning and attacking NATO, and we have to build an international coalition of countries that are willing to join with us to secure the region, to stop the shooting, to actually secure passage through the straits and get us back on track to a permanent and verifiable deal to prevent nuclear weapons from ever happening in Iran. But that so far has not happened.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Well, Congressman, is -- your first step is a logical one. I`m just wondering, is that even realistic now, given who the president of the United States is, the commander in chief? Is he spent his entire -- the entirety of his presidency now degrading and humiliating our allies.\par \par \uc2 Would it just be self-preservation on their part if the president were to take the logical step for a step that you`re talking about and reengage with the United States?\par \par \uc2 CROW: Well, first of all, I`m not saying Donald Trump is going to do this right. I mean, it`s one thing to say what actually needs to happen and what the right thing to do is. It`s another to look at Donald Trump, who I don`t think is mentally fit to be in office right now, who operates by vengeance primarily in emotion. Right?\par \par \uc2 Let`s not forget that he just announced the drawdown of thousands of troops in Europe just because he`s upset at the chancellor of Germany, right?\par \par \uc2 [22:15:06]\par \par \uc2 This man is in charge of our national security and the security of our country and our military. And he makes decisions primarily based on emotion, not facts, not the advice of the generals, the admirals and our intelligence community.\par \par \uc2 So that is the danger that we all now face. So, no, I`m not holding my breath to think that he`s going to do that.\par \par \uc2 And then the second part of the question is, well, our allies join with us? Maybe. I mean, there is common cause here. They have a stake at the outcome of this as well. But we just have to have the right leadership.\par \par \uc2 And if he doesn`t do the right thing, then again, this comes back to Congress. Congress can force them to do the right thing because constitutionally, we do have the authority over matters of war and peace, and we can assert that authority.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Congressman Jason Crow of Colorado, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up today, thanks to Donald Trump`s war, gas prices are soaring, and voters know exactly who is to blame. Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 And in battleground Ohio, gas is almost $5 a gallon. Sherrod Brown, who as of a few hours ago is officially the Democratic Senate nominee in Ohio, will join us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:25]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: With Trump currently seeing some of his worst poll numbers on the economy, cost of living and overall job approval, Trump actually had the nerve to say this last night at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Our economy is roaring and factory construction is way up. Consumer confidence is way up. Business investment is more than triple compared to that of just a short time ago and before I took office. Nobody can believe it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Well, he is right about one thing. Nobody can believe it.\par \par \uc2 "The New York Times" reports, Mr. Trump`s comments on Monday created a sharp contrast with the economic reality outside Washington, as rising energy prices hammer families and businesses alike. With the midterm elections just months away, Mr. Trump has struggled to hone an economic message despite pleas from Republicans to focus on how his policies are improving the lives of everyday Americans, and pledges from his aides that he would travel the country doing just that.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump has faced accusations from some in his America first base that he is too focused on foreign policy and global conflicts, and not enough on the kitchen table issues that he campaigned on addressing, such as the cost of living and grocery and gas prices.\par \par \uc2 Anyone who drives by a gas station right now can see how much Donald Trump is costing them in real time. According to AAA, the national average for a gallon of gas went up another three cents today to $4.48 per gallon. That`s about a buck 50 higher than it was at the start of Donald Trump`s war with Iran.\par \par \uc2 And in battleground Ohio, today`s average hit $4.83 per gallon.\par \par \uc2 MS NOW`s Will McDuffie talked to voters in the Buckeye State who seem fed up with the rising prices.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE MCLAUGHLIN, OHO VOTER: This world is not what I grew up in, and I would like to see it go back to what I grew up in, where we care about our neighbors, care about one another, and we`re not out for the billionaire who wants to stick everything in his pocket. And he doesn`t care at all about us.\par \par \uc2 STEVE PADGETT, OHIO VOTER: I voted for Trump, too. I`m not saying I disagree with him, but I think it`s getting a little bit carried away. He`s more concerned about the war over there and killing everybody over here. But increased prices and he just doesn`t seem to care. And his big, beautiful build didn`t help me.\par \par \uc2 TOM OSWALD, OHIO VOTER: What a surprise. The oilmen are all making a bunch of money, and the arms manufacturers are all making a bunch of money. It`s the usual villains are running the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: It was election day today in Ohio, along with neighboring Michigan and Indiana. "The Hill" reports a slate of primary elections for the state legislature in Indiana on Tuesday will offer a key test of President Trump`s influence as he seeks to oust the Republican state senators who resisted his redistricting push. Trump has endorsed primary challengers against seven state Senate Republicans who voted against a new GOP favored congressional map in Indiana last year.\par \par \uc2 At least five of those seven Indiana Republicans have lost their primaries tonight. In Ohio, former Senator Sherrod Brown officially won the Democratic nomination in what is sure to be one of the most closely watched senate races in November. Look at that spread. Huge.\par \par \uc2 He will take on Republican Senator Jon Husted, who ran unopposed tonight after being appointed to Vice President J.D. Vance`s Senate seat last year.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is former Democratic Senator Sherrod Brown. He is running once again for the United States senate in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Congrats on your blowout victory tonight, Senator Brown.\par \par \uc2 SHERROD BROWN (D), OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Donald Trump says -- Donald Trump says the economy is roaring. Is that what you`re hearing from Ohio voters? They can`t get enough of this economy?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, Donald Trump is saying that Jon Husted, who`s -- who is appointed by the governor of this state, says the work ethic is broken. People should just go out and earn more. He said, people are struggling, are just don`t know how to navigate the real world.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he`s not -- he either doesn`t care or he`s not coming back to the state. He`s not hearing veterans in Jackson like I`ve heard talk about this war.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:00]\par \par \uc2 He`s not hearing farmers in Crestline who have seen not -- you talk about gas prices, as the media often do. You don`t talk as much about diesel prices and fertilizer, and it`s planting season and farmers are getting killed.\par \par \uc2 Diesel prices have gone up even more sharply. There are more than a dollar and a half, most places higher than gas prices, and they don`t know what to do. I mean, there`s planting season and they don`t have much choice but they`re -- they`re getting hit really, really hard. And Jon Husted doesn`t seem to care.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Well, Senator Husted has voted to allow Trump to continue his war with no oversight from Congress. Do you think Ohio voters agree with that?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, what I hear is they don`t. He`s had six times -- six times he`s voted to continue this war. Three of the 14 servicemen and women who were killed are from Ohio.\par \par \uc2 We are spending -- Ohio taxpayers are literally spending -- spent $1 billion in this war, that money that could go to schools in Cincinnati or could go to health care in Gallipolis, or that could go to road construction in Toledo. Farmers talk to me about fertilizer costs, and they will -- their productivity will be less this year as their markets have been shrunk or eliminated in some cases by Trump`s tariffs.\par \par \uc2 So, they`re saying that Jon Husted is not even talking, saying the war is actually gone better than he expected or than people expected, which is ludicrous. But he`s not addressing this. He`s shown no interest in standing up on inflation, on any of this.\par \par \uc2 But it`s an expensive race. They`re coming hard. And I -- it`s a hard state. As you know, I asked people to come to sherrodbrown.com and chip in 15 or 20 bucks if they could.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Well, you`re going to need that money because your race will be one of the most expensive of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Your campaign recently released this TV ad about where at least some of Senator Husted`s campaign cash is coming from.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AD NARRATOR: Of all 535 members of congress who`s taken the most money from associates of Jeffrey Epstein, Jon Husted, that`s who. In fact, Husted has taken over $116,000 from one of Epstein`s closest friends and coconspirators, and last year took a maximum donation from him just weeks before voting to block the release of the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Senator Brown, what kind of response have you gotten from that so far?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: People see Jon Husted as a special interest guy when he was in Columbus, standing up for wall street and the drug companies and utility companies. Now they see him as a special interest guy in Washington.\par \par \uc2 They`re also learning more about him. They`re learning about his connection to Epstein coconspirator after taking, well, more than $100,000, they`re hearing about his connection with a bribe where he`s testified in court for a CEO for the defense in a case of $61 million bribe to the speaker of the house who`s now in prison, one thing after another.\par \par \uc2 And the voters are learning a lot of things about Jon Husted that they don`t like. And they also realize that higher gas prices, higher -- higher costs, and the drug -- in the drug store and the grocery store are laid at the feet and part of Jon Husted.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Ohio Democratic Senate nominee officially tonight, Sherrod Brown, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Coming up, a secretive private slush fund with billions of U.S. taxpayer and foreign government dollars. That doesn`t sound good. But that is what top Democrat Congressman Jamie Raskin says Trump`s so-called board of peace is. And 182 days from today, he could very well be the next chair of the Judiciary Committee and bring the Trump government grift train to a halt. That`s next, with Congressman Jamie Raskin.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:33:42]\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART, MSNBC HOST: If Democrats win back control of the House, our next guest, Congressman Jamie Raskin, is expected to become the chair of the powerful judiciary committee. And now The Washington Post is reporting that the White House Counsel`s office has been quietly conducting private briefings for Trump administration staff, preparing them for the possibility that Democrats reclaim oversight authority in Congress, quote, the roughly 30-minute briefings have included a PowerPoint presentation about how congressional oversight works and best practices for handling it, according to the two people who spoke on the condition of anonymity because they were not authorized to discuss the matter publicly.\par \par \uc2 Staff from the counsel`s office have encouraged political appointees to be careful about what they put in writing and provided guidance for how to respond to congressional inquiries in a timely manner. At least some staff members have considered the briefings preparatory, given the growing sense across the Trump administration that the Republican Party is in trouble and that the time has come to prepare for worst case scenarios.\par \par \uc2 Winning the house would give Democrats subpoena power and control of key investigations into Trump`s corruption, including his so-called Board of Peace allegedly set up to help with the reconstruction of Gaza. [22:35:02] According to its charter, member states are limited to three-year terms unless they contribute $1 billion to secure permanent membership.\par \par \uc2 Today, Congressman Jamie Raskin wrote to the director of the United States Patent and Trademark Office asking about the agency`s involvement in concealing information about Trump`s Board of Peace. Congressman Raskin writes, quote, are you a willing accomplice to President Trump`s efforts to cover up that his Board of Peace is an attempt to create a secretive private slush fund with billions of U.S. taxpayer and foreign government dollars? Or are you an unwitting enabler to this scheme, being forced to do the president`s bidding outside your lawful duties and powers?\par \par \uc2 The State Department has transferred $1.25 billion in taxpayer dollars to President Trump`s Board of Peace. That is extraordinary and likely unlawful. Yet, we still do not know what the Board of Peace is, and you could not tell us, confessing ignorance of the most basic facts of its existence and of the essential character of this entity that you wish to protect under the trademark laws. Is it a private entity or a government entity? Is it based in the United States or is it based abroad? Does its Chairman for Life, Donald Trump, have unfettered personal control over the board`s finances in perpetuity?\par \par \uc2 That`s my question. But in order to take the majority, Democrats have to deal with the Supreme Court`s recent gutting of the Voting Rights Act, which could potentially allow Republicans to redistrict out several Black Democratic House members in southern states. Last night, the Supreme Court issued an emergency order expediting Louisiana`s redistricting process, which is expected to create an additional Republican-leaning seat in November.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Congressman Jamie Raskin of Maryland. He is the top Democrat on the House Judiciary Committee. Congressman, always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with Louisiana. That state`s governor, Jeff Landry, has suspended the state -- that state`s May 16th primaries until mid-July to allow the legislature time to redraw maps. More than 42,000 voters have already cast absentee ballots in that election. What legal recourse exists when an election is effectively paused after voting has already begun?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Well, not much, Jonathan, and that`s the whole point. You know, we know that Donald Trump`s mid-decade Texas gerrymander massacre plan hasn`t worked out so well for them because the Democrats have been fighting back strong and because their political support is melting away. So now the Roberts court comes rushing to the rescue with the Callais decision, a decision of this gravity and importance usually would be released in June. But clearly they rushed it to come out now, precisely so they could set into motion a chain of events, including these governors literally stopping an election while it was going.\par \par \uc2 There were already thousands and thousands of people in Louisiana who had voted by mail. And the governor essentially just declared that election null and void, and then continued with the election for U.S. Senate and other state offices, but canceled out the election for the U.S. House so the legislature could redraw and gerrymander, you know, perhaps a complete Republican delegation in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, that is exactly where they`re headed. It`s like the end of reconstruction in the 1870s, where, you know, a dozen African-American members of the House and two Black senators were basically purged from Congress through different disenfranchisement mechanisms. And they`re trying to do the same thing in the south.\par \par \uc2 Now, in the north, there are lots of African-American representatives who have been elected in majority White or non-majority African-American districts, but it`s in the south where this is the only chance both for African-Americans to get elected and for Democrats to get elected because of the racially polarized voting. They clearly understand that. And now they`ve basically said, that`s perfectly fine if they want to create all Republican delegations in Louisiana, Arkansas, Tennessee, Florida, what have you.\par \par \uc2 And the only counter amend to that from the people will be a massive landslide turnout for Democrats and for democracy.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Let`s talk about that, the -- the Board of Peace in that letter that we read, your letter to the director of the U.S. Patent and Trademark Office. You asked, and I`m quoting you to you. Do you know what entity will have authority over the $1.25 billion in taxpayer funds transferred from the State Department to the Board of Peace? [22:40:00] How concerning is it that it`s not clear who`s in charge of taxpayer money once it`s transferred out? And I am so glad you asked that question, because the moment I read the terms of that Board of Peace that jumped out at me.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, our colloquy during Mr. Squire`s first appearance before the House Judiciary Committee was absolutely extraordinary. He could not tell me whether the Board of Peace was a public entity or a private entity. He could not tell me if it was located in America or abroad. We don`t know whether it`s for profit or not for profit. He told me that he was not doing it at the instruction of the president or the direction of the president, even though he claimed that his authority to register a trademark, which, as far as I can tell, no director of the USPTO has ever done for a private individual or entity before in its history, because the law requires you to register your own trademark. He could not tell us who he was doing it for.\par \par \uc2 So the whole thing is a complete mystery. And now we know that there`s this billion dollar ante up fee for Saudi Arabia, United Arab Emirates, Qatar, the other oil monarchies and governments that the president has pulled into it. And he transferred $1.25 billion from American taxpayers, including $1 billion that was supposed to be for disaster relief to this mysterious, inscrutable entity, which Congress never set up and Congress has never appropriated a single dollar to. So this thing just gets curiouser and curiouser. And we need some real answers from John Squires.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And also, Trump is the Chairman for Life. But I have to ask you about that a little later. But let`s talk about Secretary of State Marco Rubio. Listen to what he had to say today about the War Powers Act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO, UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: The War Powers Act is unconstitutional, 100%. Now, this is not the position of me, not the position of the president of the United States now, this is the position of every single president that has occupied this position since the day that law passed. It`s completely unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Congressman, your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Okay. Well, let`s take the bait on that. Let`s say it is unconstitutional. It`s unconstitutional because it allows the president to engage in war without a congressional declaration of war. So let`s take the War Powers Act off the table if they don`t want to deal with it, and they`re clearly in violation of the War Powers Act. When did Congress declare war on Iran? It never happened.\par \par \uc2 And yet James Madison was emphatic that it would only be the representatives of the people who could take the country to war, because the kings had always been plunging their countries into wars of pique and impetuous vanity and conceit and imperial plunder. And they said this was a critical assignment to the Article I branch to congress to decide, it should never be up to the president alone. And yet they`re totally violating the constitution. So if he doesn`t like the War Powers Act, fine. How about the constitution of the United States?\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Great place to end. Congressman Jamie Raskin, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: You bet.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Coming up, MS NOW colleague Melissa Murray opens her new book on the U.S. Constitution. With this, some current events make clear that the safeguards that the constitution put in place to ensure democratic government may not be adequate in our volatile, politically polarized environment. That is like TLDR for every news story of this era, and I cannot wait to talk to her about what she discovered in her research and what we might be able to do about it. Melissa Murray joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:48:16]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: From limiting women`s reproductive rights to targeting LGBTQ youth, Donald Trump`s former personal attorney turned acting attorney general, Todd Blanche, is making his vision for the Department of Justice unmistakably clear as he continues to audition for a permanent seat in Trump`s cabinet. At the center of it, tonight, the legal battle over mifepristone, one of the most widely used abortion medications, is once again putting reproductive rights back in the national spotlight.\par \par \uc2 The Washington Post reports, on Friday, a federal appeals court severely curbed abortion access when it required women to get an abortion pill in person rather than by mail. Activists called the decision the biggest attack on reproductive rights since Roe`s fall. A Supreme Court justice on Monday then restored mail access to the drug, mifepristone, for one week. Advocates view the legal scuffle over the pill as a fresh opening for Democrats to take abortion off the back burner and capitalize on the issue as they look to win back majorities in Congress. Democrats with presidential aspirations are raising alarms, while congressional candidates in competitive districts have stayed relatively quiet, a sign that the party is still figuring out how to react.\par \par \uc2 And as the battle for abortion access, especially access by mail, snaps back into focus in the middle of a midterm election year, Todd Blanche`s justice department is opening up another front, this time in Illinois. Last week, the DOJ announced that its so-called Civil Rights Division is investigating more than a dozen schools and multiple districts to, quote, determine whether they have included sexual orientation and gender ideology content in any class for grades pre-K through 12.\par \par \uc2 [22:50:06]]\par \par \uc2 And then there was this moment today inside the Oval Office where Donald Trump took that same message directly to children.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: What about you? Look at this guy. He`s a big guy.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Me, I`m doing football right now, and next year I`m going to be trying to do power lifting.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Oh, whoa, whoa, whoa.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: And you`ll never compete against women in powerlifting.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: That is the culture war that Donald Trump wants. But more than that, it`s about control. Control over women`s reproductive choices, control over how young people understand themselves, control over what is taught and what is erased. But the stakes are even higher when an ultra- conservative agenda like this coincides with a Republican supermajority on the United States Supreme Court that has shown little appetite for standing in Donald Trump`s way.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Melissa Murray, professor of law at New York University and an MS NOW legal analyst. Her new book, The U.S. Constitution, A Comprehensive and Annotated Guide for the Modern Reader, is out today. Happy publish day.\par \par \uc2 Melissa, let`s start with the fight, this fight over mifepristone, one of the drugs used in the standard protocol for medication abortion. What does this moment tell us about where the Supreme Court stands on abortion right now?\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, PROFESSOR OF LAW AT NEW YORK UNIVERSITY: Well, Jonathan, thanks for having me. This was predicted. I predicted this would happen. And it was predictable. The court in Dobbs said that they were settling the abortion question by returning this to the state. But the fact of the matter is that this patchwork solution was not tenable in the long term. Medication abortion and mifepristone obviously emerged in the aftermath of Dobbs as a means for women to seek reproductive care, because it can be dispensed relatively easily.\par \par \uc2 And in 2021, the FDA made the decision in the middle of the pandemic to change the dispensation regulations and to allow mifepristone to be dispensed via telehealth and through the mail. And that obviously made it easier for women and pregnant people in abortion deserts to access that material. The question, of course, going forward is what the Supreme Court is going to do. As you mentioned, Justice Alito issued an administrative stay allowing mifepristone to be restored to its distribution, it`s regular nationwide distribution for a week, and the court will, in a week, determine how it will proceed on this case. But the manufacturers of the drug are seeking review of the fifth circuit`s underlying decision.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Melissa, perpetually auditioning acting Attorney General Todd Blanche is launching another investigation in a democratic led state, taking aim at Illinois schools over LGBTQ inclusive curricula. This is coming from the Civil Rights Division, which is hard to believe. How does that square with that division`s traditional mission?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I love that you notice that Todd Blanche is auditioning to be America`s next top attorney general. He`s still in the running, apparently, which is why he`s working so hard at this. But the Civil Rights Division has really strayed from its storied past. You know, we jokingly call it on the Strict Scrutiny Podcast, the Civil Wrongs Division, because this is a division of the Department of Justice that is no longer interested in promoting traditional civil rights, which is to say it isn`t interested in promoting the interests of those who have been underrepresented in society. Instead, they are pushing the view that certain groups have been discriminated against, reverse discrimination, and that certain groups, simply by being represented or seen in society, have gone too far. And this, I think, helps explain what you`re seeing in that Illinois school with regard to a more inclusive curriculum.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: All right. Your new book gives us a guide to the U.S. Constitution for modern readers. What`s one part of the constitution where that added context completely changes how we should understand that document today?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: So I think there are lots of pieces of the constitution that we over fetishize. So we spend a lot of time talking about the framers and the framing in 1787, and James Madison. We spend a lot less time thinking about reconstruction, the 13th, 14th, and 15th amendments, which are a fundamental renegotiation of the constitution and the balance of power between the federal government, the states and the federal government, the states and the people. And we`ve totally ignored, to our detriment the gilded age amendment.\par \par \uc2 So these were the amendments that were passed at the end of the 19th century, the beginning of the 20th century, in a fervor of populist agitation. The people were angry about the consolidation of wealth in this country. They were angry that the working classes had no say. They were angry that state legislatures got to choose senators, and they took matters into their own hands, and they amended the constitution. They got the 16th amendment. They were so angry at the fact that the federal government used tariffs to raise revenue, which they thought were regressive and unfairly burdened the working class. Sounds very familiar.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:14]\par \par \uc2 And so they wanted a progressive income tax. They amended the constitution to eliminate a provision of Article I that prevented the imposition of an income tax, and they got the rich to pay their fair share. We`ve forgotten that. We`ve forgotten that the people can make constitutional claims as much as the Supreme Court, as much as the president, as much as Congress. We too, have this authority. We`ve forgotten that the first three words in this document are we the people, and we need to reclaim that.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Today is the day your book is out, The U.S. Constitution, A Comprehensive Guide and -- A Comprehensive and Annotated Guide for the Modern Reader. Melissa Murray, congratulations. Thank you for joining us tonight. There it is.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Thank you for having me, Jonathan. There it is.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Congrats again Melissa.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: That is tonight`s last word. The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2026 Versant Inc. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein  are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020260506em5500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The Last Word With Lawrence O`Donnell for MAY 1, 2026, MS NOW\b0\par\par\uc2 Jen Psaki, Ali Velshi, Vin Gupta \par \uc2 8,291 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2026\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2026 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MS NOW HOST: That`s going to do it for me tonight. It`s been a hell of a week. THE LAST WORD with Ali Velshi in for Lawrence starts right now. Hey, Ali.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MS NOW HOST: It has been a hell of a week. I hope you get rest for a couple of days because --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: -- this one`s been a hard one to keep up with.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Certainly. We`ll keep talking about all of it next week.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes, all right. Have a great weekend, my friend.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Thanks, Ali.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, the War Powers Act establishes a 60-day deadline during which Congress must either authorize or end military action in Iran. That deadline, that 60 days, is today. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  now says his war of choice with Iran has been terminated because of a ceasefire between the Iran -- between Iran and the United States.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to Republican leaders, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wrote, quote, there`s been no exchange of fire between the United States forces and Iran since April 7th of 2026. The hostilities that began on February 28th, 2026 have terminated, end quote.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s just consider how ridiculous that statement is, that because the ceasefire is holding for now means that the war is over. A ceasefire simply means a pause in fighting. Termination implies the war is over. So, that`s the president`s take for the moment.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, House Speaker Mike Johnson just blatantly denied that we`re even in a war. What war? There`s still a U.S. blockade in the Strait of Hormuz. Blockades are, under international law, acts of war.\par \par \uc2 Trump even contradicts himself in the next line of the letter that he sent to Congress when he says, quote, the threat posed by Iran to the United States and our armed forces remain significant. Accordingly, the Department of War continues to update its force posture, end quote.\par \par \uc2 Okay. So, the war is terminated. The threat remains significant. The U.S. continues to update its force posture, but the war is over. Got it?\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Axios\uc2  reports that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was briefed yesterday on other possible military options in Iran after he continues to refuse every proposal to actually end the war that Tehran sends his way. Two sources with knowledge told \uc2 Axios\uc2 , quote, CENTCOM prepared a plan for a short and powerful wave of strikes on Iran, likely including infrastructure targets, in the hopes of breaking the negotiating deadlock. Another plan is focused on taking over part of the Strait of Hormuz to reopen it to commercial shipping. Such an operation could include ground forces. Another option is a Special Forces operation to secure Iran`s stockpile of highly enriched uranium.\par \par \uc2 The long that this war drags on, the more support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  loses, both from within the Republican Party and with the American people at large. In fact, a new \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  poll found that Trump`s war in Iran is as unpopular among Americans as the Iraq war was during the year of peak violence in 2006, and the Vietnam War was in the early 1970s. 61 percent saying it was a mistake for Trump to use our military against Iran, 61 percent disapproval in two months.\par \par \uc2 This is important. The two-month thing is going to come up again in a minute, so it`s important to understand this. It took years for the Iraq and Vietnam wars to become this unpopular.\par \par \uc2 But 60 days in, key Republicans are getting impatient with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . This week, Republican Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  voted with Democrats for the first time on a failed vote to end the war. Republican Senator \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  threatened to force a vote to authorize the war in the coming weeks. Even Republican Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  told \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , if Trump doesn`t pull U.S. troops out or come up with a legal reason to keep them there, Congress would need to proactively pass legislation authorizing the war, quote, and I don`t really want to do that because I don`t want to open up further conflict, said Mr. Hawley. I want to wind it down, end quote.\par \par \uc2 So, Iran has reportedly sent a new proposal to the United States to negotiate an end to the war. Two senior Iranian officials say to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , quote, the new proposal removes a previous condition Iran had said that Mr. Trump must first lift the sea blockade against Iran for negotiators to meet face-to-face. The proposal also offers more flexibility on the sequencing of steps, with Iran willing to first open the Strait of Hormuz followed by the U.S. announcing the blockade was ending. The proposal still calls for nuclear negotiations to be postponed for a second phase of talks after a permanent ceasefire was reached, end quote.\par \par \uc2 Okay. So, that`s what the Iranians said. Here`s how Trump responded.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: They want to make a deal, but I don`t -- I`m not satisfied with it. So, we`ll see what happens. Iran wants to make a deal because they have no military left, essentially, and they want to make a deal, but I`m not satisfied.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Of course, he didn`t specify exactly what he wasn`t satisfied with.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:02]\par \par \uc2 People familiar with the details tell The Wall Street Journal that Iran has indicated to mediators that it would be ready to sit down for talks in Pakistan by early next week if Washington is open to its new proposal. So, that`s Iran giving Donald Trump an off-ramp in this war that Donald Trump is stuck in that he won`t call a war.\par \par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal reports, quote, Iran is betting that America will crack first and end its blockade of Iranian ports in order to calm global markets and bring down American gasoline prices. U.S. officials are betting that Iran will instead relent because of the deepening economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 So, yesterday, the defense secretary, Pete Hegseth, admitted to Congress that it could take months or even years to replenish the munition stockpiles that are being depleted by Trump`s war on Iran. Our first guest tonight, Democratic Congressman Eugene Vindman, questioned Pete Hegseth when he testified to the House Armed Services Committee on Wednesday about American lives lost in Trump`s war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. EUGENE VINDMAN (D-VA): On March 1st, six Army soldiers, including a constituent of mine were killed by an Iranian drone strike. Do you take any responsibility for those deaths?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, DEFENSE SECRETARY: I was there at Dover when they came back. Of course, I take responsibility for every aspect of this issue.\par \par \uc2 VINDMAN: Well, I appreciate that. Because you also complained about the fact that tragic things happen on, the news makes it front page news. The press only wants to make the president look bad. Is it about the soldiers or is it about the president looking bad?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: It`s not just the president who wants -- the media that wants to make him look bad. It`s you.\par \par \uc2 VINDMAN: It`s actually about the soldiers, and it`s, frankly, reprehensible that you would make that statement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Joining us now is the Democratic congressman, Eugene Vindman, of Virginia. He`s a member of the Armed Services Committee. He`s a retired Army colonel.\par \par \uc2 I remember when Pete Hegseth was in the Pentagon briefing room and he said that. He said, you know, why do you guys always go for these headlines to make the president look bad? This was after soldiers were killed in a war that they won`t call a war, that they won`t ask Congress permission to fight and they won`t justify why it`s happening or how it`s going to end.\par \par \uc2 I think the point you brought up was key. People have died. Innocent people have died, whether they are U.S. -- members of the U.S. military or civilians on the ground all across the region who had nothing to do with this war.\par \par \uc2 VINDMAN: Absolutely. Look, the statement that the secretary made during that press conference was shameful. It was dishonorable. It was disgusting. And I gave him an opportunity to take it back and he doubled down. I mean, for him, he`s got an audience of one. He`s got to please the president. And the cavalier attitude that he and the president have displayed to American casualties and the deaths in this war and, frankly, the 400 or so that have been wounded is unacceptable. That is -- those lives are permanently injured.\par \par \uc2 The families, I`ve met the families, I met the children that had lost parents, the parents that had lost children, and just unacceptable behavior.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, one of your colleagues suggested that the U.S. might have got himself into a bit of a quagmire here, which happens, he thought that was insulting. Hegseth said it`s two months in to call this a quagmire as an insult to the troops who are fighting this war. But it`s an insult to the troops to send them into wars without any valid justification.\par \par \uc2 VINDMAN: Absolutely. I mean, we have a job to do. We represent constituencies. In my case, I represent three military installations, 72,000 veterans, not only 20,000 active duty service members, and it is my job to ask the tough questions about how we ended up in a war without a plan, and what is our plan on getting out of this war. And the secretary, frankly, did not have a plan.\par \par \uc2 And right now, this preposterous statement, saying that the ceasefire is a termination of the conflict, is just absolute nonsense. A ceasefire is just that, cease firing. It`s a temporary pause to bombing, shooting, and fighting. The president and the secretary owe it to the American people and owe it to Congress to come with a plan on how we actually exit a conflict where the status quo before would be, frankly, a dream, you know? The Iranians control the Strait of Hormuz. Oil prices have skyrocketed. The American people are paying the price for this war.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, there`s a lot of trickery going on because they really haven`t called it a war. They`ve not gone to Congress to ask permission for this. Now, Donald Trump says, he used the term the war has been terminated because of the ceasefire. The idea here is not simply that Congress may not give Donald Trump permission to conduct this war.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:03]\par \par \uc2 It might. It might not. It`s going to be close one way or the other. It`s that the president seems to be making the point that I don`t really have to get your approval to go to war.\par \par \uc2 VINDMAN: Well, he`s done that repeatedly. I mean, this is sort of the eighth conflict twice now in Iran that we`ve been engaged in conflict, and the president has not sought Congressional approval for any of these. And he`s gotten basically a blank check, a rubber stamp from the Republican-led House of Representatives and the Senate. And he doesn`t feel like he has a need, has an obligation to take this to the American people or Congress.\par \par \uc2 And that is why this war is so unpopular. Two months in, obviously, the American people are paying higher gas prices and, you know, there`s significant inflation. But I`ll tell you, one of the reasons is the president never laid out the case of why we need to fight now in Iran. And that`s showing in the approval.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And there were -- there are a lot of experts who are involved in this who say there was more space on the diplomatic front, there was more space on the sanctions front. There was a lot more to do. The idea of choosing war when you have other options is a difficult and complicated one, and it doesn`t seem to make sense. And Pete Hegseth couldn`t seem to make it make sense in his performance before Congress this week.\par \par \uc2 VINDMAN: Yes, that`s right. I mean, he struggled to make the case. He claimed repeatedly that, you know, imagine Iran with a nuclear weapon. Well, guess what? Iran not have a nuclear weapon, Iran has not demonstrated a desire to get a nuclear weapon. In fact, there was a fatwa issued by the previous ayatollah prohibiting a nuclear weapon. The assessments were that there was no nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, that would be an unacceptable outcome, but it is an absolute fabrication to claim that they were on the verge of a nuclear weapon, especially eight or nine months now, or I guess a little bit more, since we obliterated --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We obliterated it, yes.\par \par \uc2 VINDMAN: -- their capabilities last year. It`s outrageous. It`s nonsensical.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And they both make the point that a nuclear weapon on a missile that can hit America also that they don`t have. So, at some point, this -- whatever this is, we call it a war or not, it`s built on a house of cards.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, good to see you, as always, rhank you, Congressman Eugene Vindman of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, how much more are you paying for attacking gas these days? With the cost of Donald Trump`s war hitting Americans harder and harder, his plan seems to be to tell you not to believe your lying eyes. The former Labor Secretary, Robert Reisch, joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:15:00]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Today kind of feels weird to tell people things that they obviously know themselves, but this is what we have to do. Gas prices are at the highest level they`ve been in four years. But if you use gas, you know these things. Donald Trump continues to make promises that oil experts say he can`t keep.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: You`re going to see prices dropping on gasoline like you`ve never seen, and all of that, electricity, gasoline, anything having to do with energy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That`s not going to age well. Gasoline, energy, electricity, everything`s going down in ways you`ve never seen, in ways you`ve never seen. That usually means that you`re in a very serious depression or a recession.\par \par \uc2 Today on a first quarter earnings call, ExxonMobil`s CEO told investors, quote, it`s obvious to most that if you look at the unprecedented disruption in the world`s supply of oil and natural gas, the market hasn`t seen the full impact of that yet. There`s more to come if the strait remains closed, end quote. The Strait of Hormuz remains closed. This is going to get worse.\par \par \uc2 Since Donald Trump`s war started, the average price of gas has jumped more than 47 percent, which was entirely predictable because that`s how much oil went up by, and gas always goes up by the same amount. According to AAA, gas prices shot up 9 cents overnight. That`s the largest single day increase since the Iran ceasefire was announced, not the war, the ceasefire. A new poll found 50 percent of Americans think gas prices will get worse over the next year. 21 percent think they`ll get better. 60 percent say Donald Trump`s war has increased the risk of an economy going into a recession, which will get them the lower gas prices, I suppose.\par \par \uc2 As tensions between the United States and Europe escalate, Donald Trump has threatened to raise tariffs on European cars and trucks to 25 percent starting next week. Donald Trump posted today that the increase was, quote, based on the fact that the European Union is not complying with our fully agreed to trade deal.\par \par \uc2 Reuters reports that the European Commission rejected Trump`s claim and said, quote, it would keep its options open to protect E.U. interests if Washington breached the terms of the agreement, end quote. Trump`s new tariff threat could be in retaliation of comments made by the German chancellor, Friedrich Merz, who said this week that the U.S. is, quote, being humiliated by the Iranian leadership, end quote.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t take much more than that, a comment that sets Donald Trump off and next thing you`ve got tariffs and you`re paying more for things that you`re buying.\par \par \uc2 This week, the defense secretary, Pete Hegseth, childishly dismissed American economic concerns.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): Do you know how much it will cost Americans in terms of their increased cost in gas and food over the next year because of the Iran war?\par \par \uc2 [22:20:02]\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: I would simply ask you what the cost is of an Iranian nuclear bomb.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I`m going to give you that --\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: I would simply ask you what the -- you`re playing gotcha questions about domestic things. I`m not --\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: You`re asking -- you`re saying it`s a gotcha question to ask what it`s going to be in terms of the increased cost of gas?\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Why won`t you answer what it costs to prevent Iran from getting a nuclear bomb?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I give you that, sir, but let me --\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: What would it cost? What would you pay to ensure Iran doesn`t get a nuclear bomb? What would you pay?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: You don`t even know what the average American is paying. You don`t know what we paid in terms of the missiles that hit the Iranian school. You don`t know what we`re paying in terms of gas. You don`t know what we`re paying in terms of food. Your $25 billion number is totally off. It`s the incompetence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It is the incompetence.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Robert Reisch who served as the secretary of labor under President Clinton. He`s a professor of public policy at U.C. Berkeley and the co-founder of Inequality Media.\par \par \uc2 Professor, there`s a lot to get to here. It`s easy to write this off as amateurish and Pete Hegseth has been over his skis. But he`s echoing a Trump thing that says that they have saved America from being hit by a missile with a nuclear bomb on it by Iran. Iran doesn`t have a missile that could hit America. Iran doesn`t have the capability of having nuclear bomb. And we were once in a deal with Iran to make sure that they weren`t enriching enough uranium to make a nuclear bomb. I don`t know how else to say it. What Hegseth said was just B.S.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REISCH, FORMER LABOR SECRETARY UNDER CLINTON: Oh, B.S., yes, that`s a good summary of exactly what the Trump regime is trying to pull off. And they substitute belligerence for facts. They substitute kind of anger and sort of -- they avoid hard questions, but the fact of the matter is that this entire war and this economy that is suffering because of the war is all because of one person, Just one person not Congress, not our European allies, not the corporations in the United States. It`s one person named Donald Trump, who decided to go to war, decided to raise tariffs.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is -- you cannot run an economy in a dictatorship like this. You cannot possibly expect an economy to work for all of the people when you have one person making all these decisions.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, Gallup says this is the highest number recorded in this century, in the last 25 years, that the economy is getting worse for people.\par \par \uc2 This is -- There have been times that the economy I think might even have been measurably worse in your and my lifetime, but there was a sense, if you go back to 2008 and the financial crisis of various recessions, there was a sense that there was an all of government effort to get us out of whatever that problem was that we were in.\par \par \uc2 But to your point, the two things that are causing inflation right now are tariffs and increasing oil prices and fertilizer and things like that. In other words, this is not an exogenous thing. This is actually a thing caused by the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 REISCH: And that`s the most amazing aspect of all of this, Ali, because before the Trump administration, before this regime started, I mean, if we just go back to, you know, a year-and-a-half ago, we didn`t have these problems. We didn`t have this degree of inflation. We didn`t have the tariffs. We didn`t have a war. We didn`t have gas prices going up. We didn`t have any of this.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that all Democrats need to ask the old political question in 2028 and even in the midterms is, are you better off now than you were before? And people, if they`re just honest with themselves, are going to have to say, no, we are worse off, because we are in fact worse off.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The senator from South Carolina, Tim Scott, disagrees with you. And this is an interesting matter because, in my opinion, this is a matter of fact, not a matter of opinion, but let`s listen to what he said with response to gas prices.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): You can even feel in our environment how good things are getting. Gas prices continue to come down, which means that your groceries will come down a little bit as well. We`ve got a lot of good signs in the economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Now, what you have is a stock market that is -- it`s a little bit irrational at the moment. It`s pretending like this is the best world that we`ve ever been in. But what Tim Scott said is just wrong. Gas prices are not coming down. Oil prices are not coming down. Food prices are not coming down. This is what you call gaslighting.\par \par \uc2 REISCH: Not only is it gaslighting, but most people see very graphically on almost every corner of the United States, there is a gas station that has prices. And you can see exactly what gas prices are. So, when a politician comes along and says, no, gas prices are going down, most Americans say, that is total and unadulterated B.S. because I can see on my quarter (ph), I know what I paid.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:04]\par \par \uc2 You know, this morning, Ali, it cost me $80 to fill up a tank of gas. I`ve never paid that much. And I know.\par \par \uc2 you know, put politics aside, put Republican, Democrat, whoever, we all want an economy and a country that`s working for everybody, and lying like that to the American people is not helping anyone. I mean, it`s --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It doesn`t make it feel like the government --\par \par \uc2 REISCH: But it`s also not helping any of us.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Right. How are they helping you out of this if they can`t actually admit that you paid $80 and you don`t drive more for that? You don`t get more gas for your $80. You just filled up the same amount of tank. If there`s -- you don`t have to be an economist to understand inflation when you pay more money to get used the same amount of gas.\par \par \uc2 Robert, good to see you, as always, the former secretary of labor, Robert Reisch.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, frustration is boiling over nationwide this May Day, today`s May Day, but Donald Trump says Republican`s biggest problem this year is that he`s not on the ballot. Is the GOP ready for a new wave of activism heading into the midterms? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:30:34]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We were just talking about workers across the country are grappling with rising costs from groceries to rent to gas. At the center of that frustration is Donald Trump. He pledged economic relief.\par \par \uc2 But for many Americans, the day-to-day reality still feels more expensive and more uncertain than ever. Which is why today, on May Day, we saw thousands take to the streets in opposition to Donald Trump. These demonstrations are driven by a growing sense among working class voters that Donald Trump`s promises to rein in those costs have fallen short.\par \par \uc2 A new "Reuters" poll shows Donald Trump`s approval rating at 34 percent. We`re seeing these every week, these numbers. You might not like a particular poll or worry about the margin of error, but this is the kind of stuff we`re seeing week after week after week.\par \par \uc2 But despite those numbers, here`s what Donald Trump thinks that republican`s biggest problem ahead of the midterms are.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP, UNITED STATES PRESIDENT: It is a problem I`m not on the ballot. And I have to convince, everyone says if I was on the ballot we`d win in a landslide. I have the best -- I have some of the best poll numbers I`ve ever had.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I have some of the best poll numbers I`ve ever had. You`re going to see gas prices go down like you`ve never seen them going to be before. You`re going to see energy prices go. Everything`s a superlative with him.\par \par \uc2 Best poll numbers he`s ever had. Thirty-four percent approval. And the problem is he`s not on the ballot. Donald Trump was elected because people thought life in America cost too much. But the polls prove Americans know that Donald Trump has actually done nothing to fix that.\par \par \uc2 May Day protests are the fourth nationwide rally against Trump`s policies that we`ve seen since he took office last year. And if today is any indication, these demonstrations aren`t fading. With midterm elections 186 days away, these protests may just be getting started.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Leah Greenberg, Co-Founder and Co-Executive Director of the Indivisible Project. Leah, thank you for being with us. You and I talked a lot about this. On one hand, elections in most cases, the majority of elections in recent history in America and everywhere have been about the economy.\par \par \uc2 It`s about people feeling that they are not managing, or someone promises them that they -- they can do better for them. How do you distinguish in this world about the protest that`s about the economy, about the part of the protest that`s about wars, or about immigration, or about democracy being eroded? Does it -- does it even matter at this point?\par \par \uc2 LEAH GREENBERG, THE INDIVISIBLE PROJECT CO-FOUNDER: Well, I think what we`re seeing when we -- when we make this transition from No Kings to May Day, when we talk to people who are out in the streets, when we talk to people who are active in their communities, is that the binaries about democracy versus the economy, or it`s about this issue versus that issue.\par \par \uc2 They collapse pretty quickly, right? Voters are totally capable of making the connections. They`re capable of understanding that money that is going to bombs, that are hurting -- or that are bombing Iranian school girls abroad is coming from the same Treasury that is supposed to be providing money for healthcare and education and roads.\par \par \uc2 That the tax cuts that are going to billionaires are coming out of their pockets. You know that the ways in which the rules of the economy are rigged are tightly connected to the ways in which the rules of law and morality have been rigged to benefit the Epstein class. All these things connect in people`s minds if we`re willing to make those connections with them.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And once you make the connections, we are all just much more sophisticated this than we were a year ago, right? The protests. I -- I -- I routinely and you must do the same thing. I run into people who tell me they`ve attended a protest or two protests in the last year, and they had never attended a protest in their life prior to that.\par \par \uc2 And these are people in their 60s, 70s, 80s, 90s, who are going out there. What does it look like? On one hand, it`s great to see people out and show aerial photographs like we just did, but what else does this protest look like?\par \par \uc2 I`m assuming it`s the voting, it`s the primaries, it`s -- we haven`t quite gotten too far with economic boycotts yet. But tell me how this develops.\par \par \uc2 GREENBERG: I think what we`re seeing is people using all of the above to make their voices heard, to build their power, to build their connections. When you actually show up to one of these protests, what you see is people forming community.\par \par \uc2 What you see is people building and strengthening and feeling their own power. What you see is somebody going around with a clipboard, signing folks up for the action next week. The people who are organizing these things, they`re also organizing ICE watch trainings in their neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 They`re also making sure that people are getting on the WhatsApp chain to keep an eye out for ICE as they`re coming through. They`re also making sure -- they`re also registering voters and turning people out and getting ready for a big electoral turnout.\par \par \uc2 [22:35:09]\par \par \uc2 I think what we`re seeing pretty consistently across the board is that once we move people into activism, once we move people into getting involved in their communities, they find that there are a thousand different ways that they can make their voices heard. And we`re seeing that today in every day.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Leah, thanks again for your hard work. We appreciate you being with us. Leah Greenberg is 