ar \par \uc2 ELIAS: That he does not like the direction in which it`s going. Now. I wonder, Chris, when we get to next November, when we get to the general election, whether or not we will have, for example, a president of the United States who will think he can do this.\par \par \uc2 And if -- if we do not sort of put a stake in the ground now and say that you do not get to declare an emergency and suspend voting and sort of restart things --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: -- because you don`t like how things are going. If we don`t do that now, it`s not going to make it easier for us to do it in November.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yesterday, at a Senate Armed Services Committee hearing, the Defense Secretary, Pete Hegseth, refused to say if he would carry out orders if Donald Trump asked him to use the military to seize voting machines this November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELISA SLOTKIN (D-MI), ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE: It`s not hypothetical. Tell the American people, will you deploy the uniformed military to our polls to collect voter rolls or machines?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, U.S. DEFENSE SECRETARY: Are -- are you accusing me of performing? Because you`re performing for cable news right now.\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: Dude, just answer the question.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: But -- but Mr. Secretary, we have --\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: It`s a hypothetical.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The news is not all bad. This week, Donald Trump`s Justice Department was handed a major blow in its effort to seize voter rolls. The Arizona republic reports that, "A federal judge has dismissed the Trump Administration`s lawsuit seeking the state`s voter registration files, adding to a string of legal losses in other states."\par \par \uc2 "The April 28th ruling from the U.S. District Court Susan Brnovich, a District Judge Susan Brnovich offered a swift end to an effort that has played out across the country. The federal government has sued 30 states and already lost in at least five of them before the Arizona ruling."\par \par \uc2 This week, House democrats on the Homeland Security Committee exposed the Trump Administration`s cuts to the cybersecurity and infrastructure security agency and how that could further undermine the midterms.\par \par \uc2 My next guest, the Democratic Congressman, James Walkinshaw of Virginia, was part of that shadow hearing that brought experts, election experts together on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES WALKINSHAW (D-VA), OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE: How have these cuts to CISA`s election security programs, from your perspective, impacted your ability to -- to prepare and -- prepare for and secure the upcoming elections in Michigan?\par \par \uc2 AGHOGHO EDEVBIE (D-MI), DEPUTY SECRETARY OF STATE: Yes, it`s been very damaging. We had CISA employees and officials work alongside us in tabletop exercises, actually go to a physical places where voting was taking place, where votes were being counted, and assess the physical security. All of those relationships have been destroyed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Joining us now is the Democratic Congressman James Walkinshaw of Virginia, he`s a member of the House Homeland Security Committee and the Oversight Committee. Congressman, good to see you. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 What`s the point in -- in dismantling or weakening CISA? I -- I don`t understand. We -- we`ve understood this -- in several elections now that there are reasons to need to keep our voting secure, they`ve got nothing to do with the stuff that Donald Trump talks about generally, but they`re actually real reasons to keep them secure, particularly on a cyber level. Why do this?\par \par \uc2 WALKINSHAW: You know, ironically, CISA was created, signed into law by President Trump in his first term, in part to protect our elections from foreign interference, from -- from foreign adversaries that might try to hack our election systems.\par \par \uc2 And Donald Trump turned on CISA in 2020 when the leader at the time of the agency, a real patriot, refused to go along with Trump`s big lie and saying that the election in 2020 was rigged or that Trump won that election.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:14]\par \par \uc2 And since then, he`s gone about trying to systematically dismantle the agency, especially the pieces of the agency that were put together to help secure our elections from cyber attacks, especially from foreign adversaries.\par \par \uc2 So it all comes down, as it usually does, to Donald Trump`s ego and his desire to hold on to power at all costs. But it hurts our state and local election officials. By the way, democrats and republicans who are responsible for running these local elections are hurt by these decisions coming out of the White House.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Right. Because it`s important to understand that these are -- there are these federal institutions that can be helpful in elections. But most elections are locally run by local people who do not necessarily possess either the funding or the level of sophistication required to figure out whether a foreign entity is actually interfering in elections.\par \par \uc2 But this plays into Donald Trump`s narrative, right? Weaken the people who actually guarantee that that`s not going to happen, and then float the idea that all sorts of crazy things are happening in the election, so you can`t trust the outcomes.\par \par \uc2 WALKINSHAW: That`s right. And take away any source of accurate, impartial information about our elections. He doesn`t want anyone out there who`s trusted to be able to tell the truth about our elections and the security of our elections.\par \par \uc2 So he can shape the narrative and contest any results that he doesn`t like. That`s what we have to be prepared for going into the elections this year.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It`s important because that`s happening across government, right? We can`t measure agricultural output anymore. We can`t trust labor numbers anymore. We don`t know what the fed -- why the fed is acting the way it might.\par \par \uc2 We heard Pete Hegseth at -- at Congress not answer very basic questions about the costs of this war that no one can explain. So it`s just -- it`s across the -- the federal government, this effort to say there are not numbers that you can access on yourself or data that you can access on your own to determine the truth. The truth comes from us.\par \par \uc2 WALKINSHAW: That`s right. I mean, that is part of the agenda. And these aren`t one offs, right? The Voting Rights Act decision, the attempts to seize voter rolls, shutting down cybersecurity protections for our state and local election officials, all part of a coordinated campaign.\par \par \uc2 The good news is we are coordinating our response to that as well. Democrats on the Homeland Security Committee, civil rights leaders, state and local election officials, we will not be unprepared for this assault on the elections, that we have to be prepared for it coming in November.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: There`s also this other thing which we saw in the Virginia referendum. There -- there is a continuing effort. We`re going to see it on the republican side to continue to gerrymander more and more districts.\par \par \uc2 Was Virginia an example that other states can -- can look to right now to sort of figure out ways to fight back.\par \par \uc2 WALKINSHAW: Yes. Look, I think for those southern states that are trying to capitalize on this egregious Supreme Court decision right now, it might look good or feel good to do it right now, but blue states will be prepared in advance of 2028 to do the same thing.\par \par \uc2 And look, if you look at the math across the country, there`s more democratic states districts added in California, in New York, in Illinois, all kinds of states. At the end of the day, democrats will come out on top of this redistricting war.\par \par \uc2 Best thing republicans could do is join hands with us and support a national ban on partisan gerrymandering. Someday, maybe they`ll come to their senses and do that. Until then, we`re going to fight them tooth and nail.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That`s smart thinking. Thank you so much, Congressman. Good to see you. Congressman James Walkinshaw of Virginia. All right coming up, third time`s a charm. When -- what you need to know about Donald Trump`s third nominee to be surgeon general. That`s next with Dr. Vin Gupta.\par \par \uc2 [22:48:59]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:53:29]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: On breaking medical news now, MS NOW has confirmed "The conservative Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals in Louisiana on Friday temporarily blocked telehealth provision of abortion pills nationwide.\par \par \uc2 All three of the court`s judges, two appointed by president Donald Trump and one appointed by former President George W. Bush, unanimously ruled in favor of Louisiana`s request to temporarily halt telehealth access to mifepristone, while a legal battle over its broader availability continues to play out."\par \par \uc2 "Mifepristone is one of two pills used in medication abortions." The majority of abortions in America are medication abortions. This comes at the same week that "NPR" reports this about the federal government`s actions overseas.\par \par \uc2 "The second Trump Administration has moved to eliminate programs for contraception and other family planning work abroad. Congress actually appropriated funds for this work, but the administration has not spent it."\par \par \uc2 "And it shut down programs aimed at helping people choose when to have children, such as efforts to improve access to birth control and provide resources for treating sexually transmitted diseases. The issue here is not abortion."\par \par \uc2 "For more than 50 years, it`s been illegal for foreign aid to fund abortions." This particular fight has been going on for a while. Here`s Donald Trump, Secretary of State Marco Rubio in a House Appropriations Subcommittee hearing a year ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. GRACE MENG (D-NY), APPROPRIATIONS COMMITTEE: All of these programs. These programs are lifesaving. In 2024 alone, they were estimated to save the lives of 34,000 women and girls, prevent over five million unsafe abortions, and serve over 47 million women and couples around the world with modern contraceptive care. What is the plan?\par \par \uc2 [22:55:13]\par \par \uc2 MARCO RUBIO, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: No, there is no plan. As I said on those programs with regard to that, there`s no plan to spend that money. We`re not going to be in that business globally. I mean, we -- we don`t -- we`re not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 We will work with Congress to find a way to channel those funds in a way that comply with the statute, but at the same time are not funding activities that the administration feels is outside the purview of the statute.\par \par \uc2 MENG: I just want to say that that is statute, that is law. And contrary to your testimony, you are touching and changing it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And then just yesterday, Donald Trump pulled his second surgeon general nominee, making a third, marking -- making a third. Dr. Nicole Saphier, a radiologist with a wellness podcast. "The New York Times" notes, "She rejects vaccine mandates and wants the childhood vaccine schedule to be flexible enough to allow some parents to delay giving infants their shots."\par \par \uc2 All right joining us now is Dr. Vin Gupta, a practicing Pulmonologist and Affiliate Professor at the Institute for Health Metrics and Evaluation. He`s an MS NOW Medical Contributor. Vin, great to see you again. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Let -- let`s start with the mifepristone. Maybe people don`t know this, but it`s the most common way people get abortions in the United States now with these medications. Whatever your views are on abortion, there`s actually no validity to the -- to the complaint that mifepristone as part of a cocktail of drugs, is unsafe.\par \par \uc2 These have been on the market for a long time. They`ve been tested and -- and approved by medical authorities for more than a quarter of a century.\par \par \uc2 DR. VIN GUPTA, MS NOW MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, good evening Ali. That`s right. So what they`re doing with mifepristone is they`re getting at mail order. And so the -- whether it`s mifepristone, you teed up family planning globally, there`s this issue of thimerosal in -- in vaccines globally.\par \par \uc2 They`re really getting at these access points for these critical medications. One-in-four medication abortions now occur through telehealth delivered then ultimately that medication by mail order. And they`re now saying, you know, inventing a reason why somehow that`s unsafe.\par \par \uc2 To your point, decades of research, incredibly safe. And this is great for women`s health. This allows people that are in -- in healthcare deserts, Ali, to get needed medication. it gives people privacy. It has ushered in a different way for people to have needed access.\par \par \uc2 And if you -- and -- and if you interfere with this, suddenly you`re creating a whole new load of emergencies, potentially, because you`re creating or removing this important lifeline.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes. And by the way, I mean, when you and I started -- when we met and started talking several years ago around COVID, part of that telehealth had not been a fully well-developed thing. It existed in some remote areas, but people didn`t generally use it for -- for regular things.\par \par \uc2 Now they do. And that is a massive development. I don`t know what the numbers are, but I imagine the number of emergency room visits, the number of critical situations that are avoided because people can use their phone to -- to get a medical consultation and prescribe -- be prescribed medicines. It`s just a major advance in -- in healthcare and medication in general.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: Absolutely. And they`re cherry picking what they will and will not allow in a telehealth environment. One-in-two people, Ali, do not have access, and it`s been made worse under the Trump Administration the same day or next day care if they have an emergency.\par \par \uc2 And so imagine this, now we have greater access to telehealth. And they`re saying for a medication that`s incredibly safe, there`s no issues from a safety standpoint, they`re going to cherry pick what they will and will not allow while they`re trying to expand telehealth more broadly.\par \par \uc2 This has been a bipartisan move. They`re just cherry picking what they don`t want.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: What`s the issue with the surgeon general? Why -- why are we having such difficulty putting a surgeon general? This is not historically a very controversial position.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: Your guess is as good as mine. What I will say is with the third now nominee, it`s -- it`s -- it is ultimately not necessarily all that easy to find somebody that has relevant clinical expertise. We now have a radiologist.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s safe to say radiologists serve a vital function for the American healthcare system. But do they have, Ali, relevant clinical expertise when it comes to population health, basic primary care access, cardiovascular disease burden, mental health, you name it.\par \par \uc2 No, of course not. I think anybody would be -- would be would be willing to say that. Do they have relevant public health expertise? Ideally, this is a public health communications --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: -- job, medical communications job.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUPTA: You would think you would have some service to -- to government, military service, some public health training. There`s none of that in Dr. Saphier`s background. And then you brought this up too, she`s made questionable statements when -- when it comes to the childhood vaccine schedule.\par \par \uc2 It seems like she wants to accommodate her new boss. I also point out, she continues to do what others in this administration do, like Dr. Mehmet Oz, Casey Means, Calley Means, they -- they now have these -- you know, supposedly these supplements and their own brand line of supplements that they`re likely profiting off of given their position, that`s worrisome.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Vin, good to see you as always. Thank you for joining us. Dr. Vin Gupta. All right coming up, tomorrow on "The Velshi Banned Book Club," we`ll open the pages of a national book award finalist that`s got a new distinction.\par \par \uc2 America`s most banned book, "Sold" by Patricia McCormick, tackles the difficult subject of trafficking with sensitivity.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:15]\par \par \uc2 It`s a story about resilience, good versus evil, and the power of the human spirit. The author, Patricia McCormick, joins me tomorrow at 10 A.M. Eastern on "Velshi," right here on MS NOW. That`s tonight`s "Last Word." "The 11th Hour" with Stephanie Ruhle begins right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2026 Versant Inc. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2026 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All materials herein  are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020260502em5100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for December 17, 2025, MS NOW\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Lauren Miller, Simon Rosenberg \par \uc2 7,245 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2025\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2025 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:07]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MS NOW HOST: Hey, Jen. We have Democratic leader of the House, Representative \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  here for our vision of the Democratic response tonight.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MS NOW HOST, "THE BRIEFING WITH \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 ": Perfect. I can`t wait to hear what he has to say about that fast-talking, live-filled speech. It was --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, that`s a -- that`s a pretty good description of it. We`re going to get right to it. Thanks, Jen.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Sounds good.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, at the end of the day that began with David Remnick asking the question in "The New Yorker", have you ever in your life encountered a character as wretched as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  delivered that character to millions of Americans tonight in a 15-minute shouted speech from the White House. Donald Trump`s shouting seems to indicate he has not yet learned how microphones actually work.\par \par \uc2 America`s worst public speaker struggled mightily reading a teleprompter full of lies tonight. His speech, if we can call it that. In it, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said that when he took office, quote, "Inflation was the worst in the history of our country." That is a lie. A typical Trump lie.\par \par \uc2 Inflation was at 3 percent when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took office. Inflation is at 3 percent right now. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said that when he took office, we had, quote, transgender for everybody.\par \par \uc2 That would mean that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is transgender. That would mean that everybody is transgender. It is, of course, Trumpian madness. We did not have transgender for everybody.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said, "I was elected in a landslide." That is a lie. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won by 1.5 percent of the vote.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said they released violent felons, meaning the Biden administration. No president in history has released more violent felons in one day than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did on his inauguration day when he pardoned every violent felon who attacked the Capitol on January 6th for him to try to overturn a presidential election for him.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said that tariffs is, quote, my favorite word. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lied when he said we made a lot more money because of tariffs. We didn`t make any money because of tariffs. America made no money because of that. The Trump tariffs paid to the United States treasury have all been paid in the United States by Americans. The federal government has simply taken money away from Americans through the Trump tariffs.\par \par \uc2 Americans have had to take money out of their pockets to pay for the Trump tariffs in this country. This is the first time that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made a public reference to tariffs without telling the lie that foreign countries pay his tariffs. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lied about the Democrats saying, "Democrats are demanding increases in health insurance costs."\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  delivered his speech in a monotone anger. He seemed to be angry at the truth.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now for the Democratic response is the Democratic leader of the House of Representatives, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight. Please just -- I want to offer you an open opportunity to respond to that speech in any way you choose.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY), MINORITY LEADER: Well, it`s great to be with you. It was an unhinged speech that was, of course, untethered from reality and truth. You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has made things worse for the American people. He refuses to acknowledge that the tariffs that he is solely responsible for have actually increase costs on everyday Americans by thousands of dollars per year. At the same time, when it comes to this healthcare crisis, it`s one that Republicans have created. It was Donald Trump`s one big, ugly bill that enacted the largest cut to Medicaid in American history.\par \par \uc2 In that same bill, they ripped food away from hungry children, seniors and veterans. And all of this was done so that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Republicans could reward their billionaire donors with massive tax breaks, which they`ve now made permanent. At the same period of time, it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who promised to lower the high cost of living on day one, to lower costs on day one. Those aren`t my words. Those are his words.\par \par \uc2 But he broke that promise. And life isn`t getting more affordable in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s getting more expensive. And that is the problem that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  refuses to confront.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: He said, and these were his exact words, "Democrats are demanding increases in health insurance costs."\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: It`s the most ridiculous thing in the world and everybody knows it. The overwhelming majority of the American people support the Democratic position, which is to make sure that we are driving down health care costs in part by ensuring that we extend the Affordable Care Act tax credits so that tens of millions of Americans don`t experience dramatically increased health insurance premiums, co-pays, and deductibles.\par \par \uc2 The problem that we`ve confronted is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republicans are blocking our efforts to try to protect the health care of the American people. But earlier today, of course, we were successful in reaching the 218 signatures necessary to hit the discharge petition threshold where we will now force an up or down vote on extending the Affordable Care Act tax credits for three years.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Now, you know, I saw some coverage about the discharge petition today, which I have to say is an extraordinary achievement by the Democrats in the minority to be able to do that ever. And now, you`re on a series of discharge petitions after the successful one uh to get the Epstein files.\par \par \uc2 When the discharge petition for the Epstein files was being considered in the House and when it was getting close to the required number of signatures, all the coverage of that story indicated that it would -- there was -- it didn`t really matter because the Senate would kill that bill. The Senate would kill the Epstein bill.\par \par \uc2 As it happens, the Senate passed it unanimously. Tonight, people are saying the same thing again. They`re saying, well, if the House gets this discharge petition, it doesn`t have a chance in the Senate. We have seen that prediction be very, very wrong already this year.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it`s possible that the way this story develops day by day, week by week, and this discharge petition vote in the House is now weeks away in January, that by the time we get to that vote, we could be in a different political landscape and the Senate will definitely be in a different political landscape by then?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, Republicans are on the run right now. They`re losing elections all across the country. The American people are rejecting their extremism and embracing the Democratic agenda to drive down the high cost of living, fix our broken health care system, and of course, extend the Affordable Care Act tax credits.\par \par \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 , as you know, Lawrence, once made the observation that public sentiment is everything. With it, nothing can fail. Without it, nothing can succeed. And we know that public sentiment is on our side in terms of extending the Affordable Care Act tax credits and decisively confronting the Republican health care crisis that is devastating working class Americans, middle class Americans, and everyday Americans.\par \par \uc2 And so, we know both that the votes exist to extend the Affordable Care Act tax credits in a straightforward way consistent with our legislation, a three-year extension in the House, and that there`s already a bipartisan majority in the Senate that had previously voted in support of a three-year extension. And as public pressure continues to mount, particularly after the House acts, we believe that there`s real opportunity to get this done.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, the current speaker, Mike Johnson, is, on his way to setting a record, uh, for the most discharged petitions, succeeding against him. Let`s listen what he said today about his control of the House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Have you lost control of the House, sir?\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE JOHNSON (R-LA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: I have not lost control of the House.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: And you know what was interesting, Lawrence, is that he went on to say uh that this is the narrowest majority that any party has ever had in modern political history. It`s probably the only truthful thing that he said in that back and forth.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is that`s been the case throughout the entirety of the year. But Mike Johnson to start this year started to, you know, brag about the fact that they`ve got some historic mandate and that the American people were ready to embrace their extremism. Nothing can be further from the truth. And things have been falling apart over the last several months clearly and that was accelerated by the decisive defeat by Democrats of Republicans all across the country up and down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Now, to your point about the discharge petition, what`s been interesting is that we`ve basically had three successive weeks of Democratic-led discharge petitions in partnership with a handful of Republicans behaving in a principal way that have been successful. First, it was a discharge petition that enabled the Epstein files to be released as mandated by the legislation compelling the Department of Justice to do exactly what the survivors have been asking for so there can be transparency and accountability.\par \par \uc2 Then last week, 20 Republicans joined Democrats in successfully moving legislation that wiped out Donald Trump`s executive order, which took away collective bargaining rights from more than a million hardworking federal employees. That legislation passed the House and is now sitting in the Senate. This week, of course, we`ve achieved the signatures necessary to force an up or down vote on extending the Affordable Care Act tax credits.\par \par \uc2 So, you`ve had three successful discharge petitions over the last three weeks. That`s more, Lawrence, as you know, than in the last years combined. So, Mike Johnson has already broken a record.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, that`s what you call losing control of the House, to put it mildly.\par \par \uc2 So you picked up -- you picked up four Republicans on this discharge petition for the Affordable Care Act subsidies. Three of them from Pennsylvania, one of them from your state of New York. Those members -- they voted for the bill that took those subsidies away. Is this the kind of thing that can rectify that vote for them with their constituents when they`re facing reelection?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, I definitely think -- and each of them will be able to speak for themselves, but uh the pressure has mounted on people throughout the Congress, particularly my colleagues on the other side of the aisle who are being held responsible for this devastating Republican healthcare crisis. And there`s significant pressure that has been brought to bear by the American people who overwhelmingly -- Democrats, Republicans, and independents -- support an extension of the Affordable Care Act tax credits because they know it`s unacceptable to have millions of people who may face dramatic increases in their health insurance premiums in January.\par \par \uc2 In some cases, premiums may increase by $1,000 or $2,000 per month. These are working class Americans. Ninety percent of the people who benefit from the tax credits make around $63,000 per year. And so, I think that the public pressure will continue to mount on my Republican colleagues in the House and in the Senate to do the right thing. We believe that Mike Johnson needs to bring the bill to a vote this week, tomorrow. We should not leave town until we vote on extending the Affordable Care Act tax credits.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s Mike Johnson, desperately trying to say, "I have not lost control of the house." \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , one of his most prominent Republican members of the House, has said, "the dam has broken." She joined -- she was one of the first Republicans to join a Democratic discharge petition uh, on the Epstein files. She believes the dam has broken. We`re going to see more of this.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Yeah, I think that`s correct. Here`s the problem, and I`ve said this repeatedly to Mike Johnson, that this my way or the highway approach where you`re trying to jam right-wing extremism down the throats of the American people is not working. And you can either continue to lose complete control of the House of Representatives or try to course correct and find a bipartisan path forward to actually getting things done for the American people.\par \par \uc2 We`re trying to help them help the American people, help their own constituents because a significant number of the people who will actually be hurt by a failure to extend the Affordable Care Act tax credits, just like a significant number of people who are hurt by the largest cut to Medicaid in American history, which was part of their one big ugly bill, are the folks they represent in Republican-run states.\par \par \uc2 So, this is not a partisan fight for us. It`s a patriotic fight and we`re going to continue to stand up for the healthcare of the American people and continue to stand up to drive down the high cost of living in this country which is out of control and getting worse because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and failed Republican policies.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said tonight that what he wants to do is just give people money to buy health insurance. Just give them money to -- he doesn`t -- he did -- actually didn`t say what for. He said he wants to give them money and not give money to health insurance companies. Of course, the money he wants to give them, they then have to use to buy health insurance. So, yes, that money, if you did that, would end up at the health insurance company.\par \par \uc2 He doesn`t seem to understand that the tax credits for the -- for the Affordable Care Act, health care costs. That is exactly what that is. A tax credit is giving people money to help them pay their health insurance premiums. That is exactly what you`re proposing.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t seem to even understand what these tax credits are.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Yeah. He seems clueless about that. The only thing they understand are tax breaks that are massive for the wealthy, the well-off, and the well-connected in order to subsidize the lifestyles of the rich and shameless.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to tax credits for everyday Americans, he claims to be clueless about the thing. And he actually may be because perhaps there`s no interest.\par \par \uc2 The reality is the affordable care act tax credits can be taken either on a monthly basis to lower your health insurance costs, which most Americans elect to do, or you can receive the tax credits when you file your taxes in April. And so that already exists as an option under law. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t seem to understand that.\par \par \uc2 Now, we believe as Democrats, yes, we have a broken health care system. We need to fix it. And we need to fix it anchored in the principle that in this great country, the wealthiest country in the history of the world, access to high quality and affordable health care cannot simply be a privilege for the wealthiest amongst us. It has to be a right available for every single American.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we`re going to continue to work on anchored in that principle and that value.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Donald Trump`s White House chief of staff said in her interviews that have now been published in "Vanity Fair" that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows nothing, absolutely nothing about policy and about what the agencies do. That`s her quote. That \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows nothing and never will know anything.\par \par \uc2 Did you hear anything tonight about any policy that indicated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually knew what he was talking about?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: No. Not at all. And you know, again, he`s running away from the fact that he promised to lower costs on day one. Now he wants to act like the affordability crisis is a Democratic hoax. But last year, all he did was talk about how he was going to drive down the high cost of living, how America`s too expensive and out of control.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, Lawrence, people are tired of him trying to throw President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  under the bus. What`s been clear in public opinion survey after public opinion survey, the American people know this is Donald Trump`s economy, this is the Republicans` economy, and it`s been a complete and total disaster.\par \par \uc2 That dog doesn`t hunt anymore, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is struggling to find someone who can actually blame his failed policies on, but unfortunately for him, the American people aren`t buying it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: House Democratic Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , thank you very much for starting off our coverage tonight.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, former special prosecutor Jack Smith wanted to testify publicly about the two criminal prosecutions he brought against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But House Republicans refused to allow Jack Smith`s testimony to be public. And now we know why.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Dan Goldman, who was in the room for Jack Smith`s closed door testimony today, will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:22:43]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Proof beyond a reasonable doubt that President Trump engaged in a criminal scheme. That is what former special prosecutor Jack Smith told members of the \uc2 House Judiciary Committee\uc2  today during a more than eight- hour closed door testimony about the criminal charges that he brought against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in two separate federal cases. Jack Smith defended his decision to prosecute \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for his conspiracy to overturn the results of the 2020 presidential election and Donald Trump`s possession of classified documents in violation of the Espionage Act.\par \par \uc2 According to portions of his opening statement obtained by MS NOW, Jack Smith said, "Our investigation developed proof beyond a reasonable doubt that President Trump engaged in a criminal scheme to overturn the results of the presidential election and to prevent the lawful transfer of power. Our investigation also developed powerful evidence that showed President Trump willfully retained, highly classified documents after he left office in January 2021, storing them at his social club, including in a bathroom and a ballroom where events and gatherings took place.\par \par \uc2 He then repeatedly tried to obstruct justice to conceal his continued retention of those documents. The decision to bring charges against President Trump was mine, but the basis for those charges rests entirely with President Trump and his actions as alleged in the indictments returned by grand juries in two different districts. If asked whether to prosecute a former president based on the same facts today, I would do so regardless of whether the president was a Republican or Democrat."\par \par \uc2 Jack smith volunteered to testify about his findings in a public hearing, but House Republicans refused to allow his testimony to be public.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-MD): Chairman Jordan made an excellent decision in not allowing Jack Smith to testify publicly because had he done so, it would have been absolutely devastating to the president and all the president`s men involved in the insurrectionary activities of January the 20th.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:01]\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN GOLDMAN (D-NY): The only reason that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is did not go to trial and was not convicted is because of the Supreme Court and because of the timing of it and him becoming president. He is guilty of those two indictments and no bias or anything else ever was involved.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Congressman Daniel Goldman of New York. He`s a member of the \uc2 House Judiciary Committee\uc2  and he was in the room today for Jack Smith`s closed-door testimony.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Goldman, was this a version of a trial? Was this a courtroom atmosphere where the evidence was being weighed?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: He did go through a little bit of the evidence, but to be honest, Lawrence, it was less about the specific evidence and more just a demonstration of a purely straight shooting prosecutor who operates by the book, who operated by the book, and even wouldn`t answer a lot of questions that we had for him because he was, in my view, overly concerned about potentially violating court orders, which just reinforced his dutiful focus on the rule of law. And that`s exactly how he ran these investigations. That is exactly what the indictments revealed.\par \par \uc2 And it is also clearly why Chairman Jim Jordan did not want him to testify publicly. But what we need right away, right now, is that transcript to be released. There is nothing in it that`s classified. There is no reason why the American public should not see that transcript. And then the American public should be able to judge for itself whether or not Jack Smith harbored any bias or there was any political influence on anything that he did in charging \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And the only way to do that is to have a public hearing.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, that transcript is the congressional record. Those are pages now of the congressional record. There is no conceivable reason why that would not be released.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Well, except for Chairman Jim Jordan has control over it. And as we saw during uh the bungled and sham Biden impeachment investigation, the Republicans will pick and choose and be selective about what the parts of transcripts they will release, whether they will release them all together or whether they won`t even release them. They are in control and so they have ultimate responsibility for releasing those transcripts.\par \par \uc2 So, I don`t know that it`ll be released but let`s just remember he is the first special counsel who was not allowed to publicly testify. This is the volume two of the special counsel`s report is the first special counsel`s report that has not been turned over to Congress.\par \par \uc2 There is a reason why those two things are firsts and it is because the allegations by the Republicans and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that Jack Smith harbored or used any bias, any political influence at all are complete and utter bogus. And that is why this was behind closed doors. That is why we do not have volume two, because it is going to be incredibly incriminating of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and others, including Kash Patel, who Jack Smith did acknowledge today testified in front of the grand jury.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What -- and we also learned today while Jack Smith was testifying that Dan Bongino is leaving the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in that number two position in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Yeah, it wasn`t clear to me he was ever really there. As early as September, a few months after he took over, there was the unprecedented move to put in a co-deputy director. There`s no question Dan Bongino was completely unqualified for the job. And I think what ultimately happened to him is what happens when a conspiracy theorist meets the truth. And he had made his reputation on conspiracy theories, especially about the Epstein files. And then he goes into the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and realizes, oh, crap, I actually now am responsible for releasing all of these things. I can`t say there`s a conspiracy because I`m on the inside and there`s no truth to anything that I`ve been saying.\par \par \uc2 So, his -- he was doomed from the beginning. He was unqualified from the beginning. And it appears that he realizes that his better pathway is on the outside, making bogus allegations about conspiracy theories rather than actually doing the hard and necessary work of the deputy director of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman Dan Goldman, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, Donald Trump`s \uc2 FCC\uc2  Chairman, \uc2 Brendan Carr\uc2 , had no answers in a Senate hearing today after he tried to get Jimmy Kimmel fired. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:37]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:35:08]\par \par \uc2 The worst \uc2 FCC\uc2  Chair in history, Donald Trump`s \uc2 FCC\uc2  Chair, \uc2 Brendan Carr\uc2 , tried to threaten "ABC" into canceling Jimmy Kimmel`s show.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN CARR\uc2 , FCC CHAIRMAN: We can do this the easy way or the hard way. These companies can find ways to change conduct, to take action, frankly, on "Kimmel" or, you know, there`s going to be additional work for the \uc2 FCC\uc2  ahead.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: To by -- despite his thug language, the \uc2 FCC\uc2  Chair has no legal power to penalize the "ABC" network in any way for anything that Jimmy Kimmel says. And so Jimmy Kimmel continues to say whatever he wants to say about Brendan Carr`s deeply perverse idol, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIMMY KIMMEL, "JIMMY KIMMEL SHOW" HOST: The Trumps are hosting multiple Christmas events at the White House this week. yesterday, after briefly mentioning the shootings in Australia and at Brown, he got distracted by a familiar looking woman in the crowd.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , UNITED STATES PRESIDENT: Then we have a man who, to me, this is the most interesting story. Boy, do you look like Ivanka. Has anyone ever told you that? I`m looking -- I`m saying, is that Ivan -- could you just turn around for the camera?\par \par \uc2 Does she look -- does she look like Ivanka? It`s the most unbelievable thing. So I wouldn`t -- I didn`t want to take a chance. I say, is that Ivanka? You look just like Ivanka, which is a great compliment, actually.\par \par \uc2 KIMMEL: What stage of dementia is not recognizing the daughter you are physically attracted to?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGH)\par \par \uc2 Eleven?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And, again, this was a Christmas -- this was a Christmas party for supporters and staff. Trump bing-bong from random topic to random topic until finally settling into a six-minute rant about snakes in Peru.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today, the disgraced \uc2 Brendan Carr\uc2  stuck to boilerplate language in refusing to actually answer questions in a Senate hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN), COMMERCE COMMITTEE: After Jimmy Kimmel`s monologue, you went on a podcast and suggested that "ABC" should take Kimmel off the air, saying, we can do this the easy way or the hard way. Those were your words. Do you think it is appropriate to use your position to threaten companies that broadcast political satire?\par \par \uc2 CARR: I think any licensee that operates on the public airwaves has a responsibility to comply with the public interest standard, and that`s been the case for decades.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: That`s -- I asked if you should -- if you think it`s appropriate for you to use your position to threaten companies. And this incident with Kimmel wasn`t an isolated event. You`ve launched investigations into every major broadcast network except "Fox," is that correct?\par \par \uc2 CARR: I don`t know if that`s true or not. We do have investigations going on \uc2 NPR\uc2  and \uc2 PBS\uc2 . We have a number of investigations that are ongoing.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: OK. You brought up social media. So do you think it`s appropriate for after a horrific murder in the stabbing -- the stabbing of Rob Reiner and his wife? Do you think it`s appropriate for someone to say that it happened when they know better?\par \par \uc2 Reportedly due to the anger he caused others through his massive, unyielding, and incurable affliction with a mind-crippling disease known as Trump Derangement Syndrome. He was known to have driven people crazy by his raging obsession of President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  with his obvious paranoia reaching new heights.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that`s appropriate for the president of the United States to do that? And if Jimmy Kimmel would have said that, would have you have threatened to take him off the air?\par \par \uc2 CARR: Senator, look, democrats on this dais are accusing me of engaging in censorship, and now you`re trying to encourage me to police speech on the internet. I`m simply not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: I`m just --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Senator Amy Klobuchar of Minnesota. She serves on the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  and the Senate Commerce Committee. Senator, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 He -- he tried to shift that to policing social media when your question was, if Jimmy Kimmel said that? Meaning, if Jimmy Kimmel said that on his show on "ABC," what would your reaction be? And, of course, he seemed afraid of even coming close to trying to answer any of your questions.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: That`s right, Lawrence. And first of all, I want to say how sorry I am. I know that you were close to Rob Reiner and knew him and Michelle. And this was just a horrific tragedy. And -- but one of the things that I wanted to point out is, you know, how dare the president of the United States talk like that about anyone?\par \par \uc2 And yet you`ve got in front of you \uc2 Brendan Carr\uc2 , which I did, who literally has threatened many, and it`s not just Jimmy Kimmel, Seth Meyers, he has threatened -- he`s done investigations of every network except "Fox."\par \par \uc2 Clearly showing that he`s not independent, they`re not independent, they`re not trying to enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 [22:40:12]\par \par \uc2 They`re just becoming a tool, he is of the president of the United States. And he wouldn`t answer or whether or not which he well knows, they were investigating every major network except "Fox," which we believe to be true.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: As we go forward, what are you expecting from \uc2 Brendan Carr\uc2  in this job?\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: I expect him to do his job. What we should have been talking about today is broadband, getting that funding out, and getting it to every corner of the country. It stalled out. What we should be talking about is spectrum and various things that can be kind of on the spectrum, but are really important for this country right now.\par \par \uc2 We should be talking about the Universal Service Fund. But instead, he`s using this as a political post and not doing his job. One of the most interesting exchanges was when my colleague, \uc2 Ben Ray Lujan\uc2 , asked him if he felt that the \uc2 FCC\uc2  was independent.\par \par \uc2 And he said, no, it`s not independent. Then Ben Ray read the website and said it says it`s independent. And shortly thereafter the word came off the website. So this is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing. He is politicizing the tools of government, including, as you very well know, the Justice Department, and using this as either revenge or to help his buddies or to help his billionaire friends.\par \par \uc2 And who is getting hurt in all this is the American people.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And Donald Trump`s speech tonight, he said that tariffs is his favorite word.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: You mean in his rant? In his --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: -- rant that ended with Merry Christmas? OK, just to clarify. All right, yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, no. Go ahead, Senator. Your reaction to the speech.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Well, I just thought -- I mean, it was a rant. And then suddenly at the end he said, Merry Christmas. And I did feel like he was channeling something other than the Christmas spirit. I think that he was defensive. And so many of the things that he said weren`t true.\par \par \uc2 The fact is he has not brought inflation down, that`s what he promised he would do on day one. The fact is, these tariffs, as you just pointed out, that he was so proud of in this speech, have caused countless harm to small businesses, there are tariff tax on the American people.\par \par \uc2 And ever since he put tariffs out there, manufacturing jobs have come down every single month. Not up. Manufacturing jobs are down, inflation is not down. But unemployment is at its highest level since the pandemic. Those are the facts.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: He told the American people tonight, I brought prices down.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: It`s just not true. And the problem is, Lawrence, that no matter how much he yells and looks at the camera, and tries to blame everyone in sight, the American people are the ones going to the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 They are the ones that know how much it costs now to buy beef for Christmas or they are trying to get some coffee, or they`re looking at toys for their kids, or they`re trying to figure out how they`re going to afford their healthcare premiums while still trying to afford the prescription drug copays.\par \par \uc2 They`re well aware of what`s going on right now. And so no matter how much he tries to distract them or he yells at the camera, in those quiet moments when they`re looking at their bills, and they`re looking at the notices they get on their healthcare premiums, and the doubling and tripling that`s occurred in these states all over the country, particularly red states, they know the truth.\par \par \uc2 And the truth hurts. And nothing he yells about is going to change that. And that`s why we today, the democrats in the Senate, with Senator Schumer and several others, Senator Warren, we put out a very clear cost agenda.\par \par \uc2 What we`re focusing on. We`re focusing on groceries and household goods, we`re focusing on electricity, we`re going to be talking about housing, and what`s happening for the American people when it comes to housing. And then, of course, healthcare.\par \par \uc2 And it is in their power to do something today, it is in their power to do something next week or the week after that, January to bring down healthcare costs. And they are accountable. They run both houses of Congress and the presidency, and the American people know it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator Amy Klobuchar, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 KLOBUCHAR: Great to be on. Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, it`s the only video of the United States military killing people in very small boats near the coast of Venezuela, thousands of miles away from the United States, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Pete Hegseth\uc2  don`t want you to see.\par \par \uc2 A former U.S. Army Ranger who saw that unedited video for the first time today of the deadly second strike, killing two people who were clinging to the wreckage of a small boat, said there was, "No justification for it." That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:10]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:49:41]\par \par \uc2 Today, the House and Senate Armed Services Committees were allowed to view the video of a September 2nd military operation that ended with the United States launching a second missile to kill two people who were clinging to the wreckage of a small boat in the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2 , a former U.S. Army Ranger, said this after seeing that video today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JASON CROW\uc2  (D-CO), ARNED SERVICES COMMITTEE: I can tell you my own conclusions, and my own conclusions are -- are twofold.\par \par \uc2 [22:50:13]\par \par \uc2 Number one, there`s no reason why they can`t release this video. No rationale provided as to why they can`t release this video like they have many other videos. I can tell you that I vehemently disagree with the ultimate conclusion that take that second strike, and I saw no justification for it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The republican Chair of the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2 , \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2  of Alabama said, "I`m satisfied everybody answered all the questions. And I`m also satisfied that this was a lawful process that was followed. My committee is done."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2  of Connecticut. He is the Top Democrat on the House Intelligence Committee. Congressman Himes, you were among the very first to see this video as a leader of the Intelligence Committee. What is your reaction to Chairman Rogers saying, my committee is done?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM HIMES (D-CT), INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE RANKING MEMBER: Well, there`s a very good chance that the United States military, in the case of these two individuals who, again, were - let`s -- let`s stipulate that they were drug runners. But, you know, they had just had a huge piece of munitions go off.\par \par \uc2 Their boat had been destroyed, they were clinging to a piece of wreckage in the middle of the ocean. Our military watched them for a lengthy period of time. And though they were not just incapable of continuing hostilities, which is the standard here, you know, had we walked away, or had we not done anything, they would have slipped under the waves and -- and -- and never to be heard of again.\par \par \uc2 The decision was taken to kill them. So you ask about the chairman, the United States military, depending on whether you believe that this war is authorized or not, either murdered two individuals or committed a war crime.\par \par \uc2 That`s, by the way, by the Pentagon`s own standards for what a war crime is. Where the standards say that if you attack somebody who has been shipwrecked, that is a war crime. So the fact that the republican chairman of this important committee has decided that there`s nothing to see here, folks, all I can say is he`s dead wrong.\par \par \uc2 And, number two, the other thing I want to say is, the American public needs to see this video. \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2 , who you just interviewed or -- or -- or featured there, you know, is a -- is a combat veteran. I`ve seen a lot of these videos over the years and this was very, very different.\par \par \uc2 This wasn`t taking out a bunch of guys with AK-47s on their way to do something terrible. This was two guys who were completely distressed, without any ability to consider -- to continue hostilities against anybody, and we killed them.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, Congressman, the -- they watched this for a number of hours after the -- you tell me how much time transpired between the first missile strike and the second one?\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Yes. I -- I -- I don`t want to get into super specific details because there are reasons why some of that stuff is classified. But I will tell you that it was a lengthy period of time. It was not the fog of war, as the Secretary of Defense tried to say.\par \par \uc2 The -- the -- there was a lot of time, more than five minutes, more than ten minutes, more than fifteen minutes in which our surveillance watched these two individuals. They didn`t -- and -- and -- and we know absolutely clearly that they did not have weapons, they did not have a radio.\par \par \uc2 Admiral Bradley suggested that, yes, well, maybe they might have dived under the wreckage and found a radio to call a boat, and we`re not quite certain that they were there. So -- I mean, to answer your question, we watched these two.\par \par \uc2 This is -- this is part of the reason that the video is so grim. It wasn`t quick. We watched them for a long time. Apparently there were discussions in the Pentagon with lawyers and others, and after a lengthy period of time, they decided to kill these two individuals.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So one way of putting it is, they lost patience. They were watching two people in the -- in the -- in the ocean who, as you pointed out, were on their way to drowning. That`s what was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 They -- they weren`t going to make it to shore, sharks would have get them, or they were going to drown. Anyone who has ever seen any kind of vessel in distress at sea knows what was going to happen to them.\par \par \uc2 They could have just watched that. They could have watched that for hours on end. Or they could have waited for this so-called rescue boat of their coconspirators to show up, and then maybe taking a shot at that one, if that`s the way they wanted to approach it.\par \par \uc2 It seems like, ah, let`s just get out of here. we`re done. We don`t have any more patience to watch this any more, let`s kill them.\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Well, Lawrence, under the law, there is a legal requirement in a situation like that to render aid, OK? And, in fact, am I crazy to say that? No, I`m not. You know why I know I`m not crazy to say that? Because several weeks later, in exactly the same situation, you had two drug runners in the water.\par \par \uc2 And as you will recall in that later, and I suspect later means after lots of consideration over what they had done in the boat strike that we`re talking about, they rescued the two individuals, right?\par \par \uc2 [22:55:16]\par \par \uc2 And here we`re told that these are the worst of the worst Narco terrorists who are dedicated to the destruction of the American people. So did we try them in a court of law? Did we invite -- indict them? did we put them in Guantanamo? No, we did not.\par \par \uc2 We sent them back to their home countries. If I recall correctly, that was Colombia and Ecuador. Why did that happen? They rescued them in that instance, I think, because they thought twice about what they had done in the September 2nd strike.\par \par \uc2 And, oh, by the way, sent them to their home countries because the last thing the administration wants is to have these guys testifying in court and their lawyers saying, what is the authority to do what you`re doing here?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:56:06]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:00:07]\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2  gets tonight`s "Last Word." "The 11th Hour" with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2025 Versant Inc.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Copyright 2025 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All mat