ving us about the Justice Department`s overall process. Like, if you are shocked at this point that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is a cautious man, good morning, how was your arrest?\par \par \uc2 But in terms of -- I think -- I think the part that`s nuanced and is interesting is the different slices of it, right? Like the Mar-a-Lago classified documents thing proceeded at one pace. The fake electors thing proceeded at a different pace. I think they should explain why when the \uc2 National Archives and Records Administration\uc2  inspector general referred that to them to prosecute. They were like, man, nothing to worry about, and sat on it for a year before they did anything, you know?\par \par \uc2 Then there are other pieces of it in terms of Trump`s involvement with trying to overturn the election in the various states and potentially trying to incite the attack on Congress on January 6. I mean, it seems like different slices of it took on different paces depending on which actors within the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Justice Department were winning which fights.\par \par \uc2 Ad I`m happy to know it, but the fact that things were deliberate and slow and there was a lot of yada, yada inside the department trying to figure it out is not shocking.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah and I`m sure if I were inside the department, I have a feeling I know which group I`d be in. I would have been in the come on let`s go the agitating group and I love the reporting. I mean, Carol Lennox work on this is great. I love all of the detail in it.\par \par \uc2 But the case seems to be tonight where it should be -- it could have been there months ago, it probably could have. But you know, here it is. And so, I -- you know --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know what? What matters is what is -- what is Andrew Weissmann and \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  think about it, they`re going to tell us when they join us tonight.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Very good. Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thanks very much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the breaking news of the night is that federal criminal defendant \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  testified in his own defense tonight on Fox. We will show you that testimony in a moment. It includes statements that can and will be held against him in court.\par \par \uc2 The defense \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  offered tonight which once again may be more of a confession than a defense comes one day after his last secretary of defense said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cannot be trusted with government secrets ever again, and it comes one day after Donald Trump`s last Senate confirmed attorney general said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL: This was a case that entirely of his own making. He had no right to those documents. The government tried for over a year quietly, and with respect to get them back, which was essential that they do and he jerked them around. And he had no legal basis for keeping him.\par \par \uc2 But beyond that, when he faces subpoena, he didn`t raise any legal arguments.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:05]\par \par \uc2 He engaged in a course of deceitful conduct according to the -- the indictment. There was a clear crime if those allegations are true, and where -- it was outrageous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: A clear crime says Donald Trump`s last attorney general.\par \par \uc2 One of our first guests tonight, Bradley Moss, tweeted this question for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  today. If you gave the order to declassify all the records and your orders have the force of law, even if only done in your own mind, why would your lawyers need clearances to see the records in the indictment? Why aren`t you contesting that order?\par \par \uc2 In his interview tonight \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  repeatedly said that all of the documents that he took with him when he left the presidency were declassified, said that repeatedly. We are now going to show some of defended Trump`s testimony tonight, but don`t worry. You won`t have to watch it alone.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to watch and analyze, Donald Trump`s testimony are, Bradley Moss, Andrew Weissmann and \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  , and as you watch this testimony at home, you can look for what our panel of legal experts will be looking for, first, anything that can actually be presented in court as a legal defense and anything that sounds incriminating, anything that sounds like a confession, okay?\par \par \uc2 Here`s the first simple question \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was asked tonight about the evidence in the case against him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS HOST: Why did you have this very sensitive national security defense documents, like the war plans for a strike on Iran?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: So like every other president, I take things out. And in my case, I took it out pretty much in a hurry but people packed it up and we left, and I had clothing in there.\par \par \uc2 I will go through those boxes. I have to go through those boxes. I take out personal things as far as the levels and all, everything was declassified because I had the right to declassify.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They went to DOJ to subpoena you to get them --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Which they`ve never done before.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right.\par \par \uc2 And you know why not just hand them over them?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Because I have boxes. I want to go through the boxes and get all my personal things out. I don`t want to hand that over to NARA yet, and I was very busy as you`ve sort of seen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yeah, but according to the indictment, you then tell this aide to move to other locations after telling your lawyers to say you`d fully complied with the subpoena when you hadn`t.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : But before I send boxes over, I have to take all of my things out. These boxes were interspersed with all sorts of things, golf shirts, clothing, pants, shoes. There were many things. I would say much more, not that I know of, but not that I know of. But everything was declassified.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann, what did you hear from defendant Trump in that testimony that could be a defense in court and what did you hear in that testimony that could be incriminating?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So I`m going to jump right to the second part because there is no nothing in the first part. So I`ll be interested in what everyone else thinks just in terms of demeanor. It`s a little striking to me that he sounds very unsure of himself and frankly a little scared and he rightly should be this.\par \par \uc2 He`s facing 71 felony counts in federal and state court and may be very well be facing additional charges. That was sort of the thing that struck me the most was his tone.\par \par \uc2 What he had to say is preposterous, so let`s just start with this idea of, you know, what he is doing with the boxes and he is saying that he is taking out and he didn`t have enough time to take out personal things. So, first, he`s already said that everything`s his, everything`s personal. There`s no triage that needs to be done because according to his prior defense, everything`s personal because they`re, quote, mine.\par \par \uc2 So it`s the inconsistency is something that Jack Smith can easily point out. Second, people should understand, when you get a subpoena, if you don`t have time to comply if the deadline`s not there, it`s not like you don`t have the ability to have your lawyer call up and say, you know what, I need more time or I can partially comply, but I need more time. This is what -- if that was true, what you don`t do is send something to the Department of Justice that says I fully complied.\par \par \uc2 Now, he is saying, in spite of the fact that he said I fully complied, he is now saying, no, no. I did -- needed to take out my golf shirts. I mean, this is the kind of thing that Jack Smith has to be salivating over because as we`ve talked about, his talking is the best possible thing as E. Jean Carroll knows full well.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:06]\par \par \uc2 So, this is, you know, a colossal blunder on his part to continue down this road.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: All right. Let`s move on to another piece of testimony here. Defendant Trump was asked tonight about an audio recording in which he says he possesses a classified document, that he`s not supposed, to show to the people he`s talking to about that document.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: According to the indictment, you were here at Bedminster on July 21st, 2001, after you`re no longer president and you were recorded saying that you had a document detailing a planned attack on another country that was prepared by the U.S. military for you when you were president. The Iran attack plan, you remember that.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Ready?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You were recorded --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It wasn`t a document.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I had lots of paper, I had copies of newspaper articles, I had copies of magazines.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know, this is specifically a quote. You`re quoted on the recording saying the document was secret, adding that you could have declassified it while you were president, but, quote, now I can`t, you know, this is still secret, highly confidential.\par \par \uc2 And the indictment cites the recording and the testimony from people in the room saying you showed it to people there that day. So you say on this, on tape --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: It`s just the opposite.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- that you can`t declassify it. So why have it, is my question.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: What I said -- when I said that I couldn`t declassify it now, that`s because I wasn`t president. I never made any bones about that. When I`m not president, I can`t declassify it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That`s what you said, you didn`t declassify it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I said -- no, no, I said, I couldn`t --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I could have declassified it.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Bret, there was no document. That was a massive amount of papers and everything else, talking about Iran and other things. And it may have been held up or may not, but that was not a document. I didn`t have a document per se. There was nothing to declassify.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , what did you hear from defendant Trump in that testimony that could be a defense in court and what did you hear that could be incriminating?\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, Lawrence, the breaking news tonight is that the special prosecutor Jack Smith has a new addition to his legal team tonight, in unpaid new deputy special counsel. His name is \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 . And in this particular clip, Trump is claiming there were no Iran documents. That is slightly contradicted by the tape, flatly contradicted by the witnesses in the room.\par \par \uc2 So, Mr. Trump, good luck with that defense. If it were just newspaper articles, why in the world are you saying on the tape that it`s classified. It`s totally incoherent, and the other parts of the tape like the part you just showed played for Andrew really is an admission of guilt like if you just think about like the Espionage Act, what does the prosecution need to show.\par \par \uc2 They need to show the defendant had unauthorized possession of a defense intelligence -- National Defense document that he willfully retained the document and failed to give the document to an officer of the U.S. Those are the elements.\par \par \uc2 So the Trump admission goes to all of them. He`s saying that he had the documents and the boxes, that he held on to them because he`s too busy. Too busy as Andrew rightly gets absolutely right, there`s no defense that I`m too busy. You don`t get to lie and say I turned everything over because I was too busy.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, it`s the -- I was too busy to file my tax return defense. Now, let -- we`ve got one more piece of this. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was also asked again why he was holding on to those documents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are documents listed nuclear capabilities of foreign countries, related to military capabilities of foreign countries, intelligence briefings on foreign countries. Why do you want to hold on to those documents after you`re a president?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don`t say I do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You just didn`t know where it was in the box.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We were discussing with NARA, giving them back. All of a sudden, we`ve got raided.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Okay. Bradley Moss, what did you hear from defendant Trump in that one, that could be part of the defense or could be incriminating?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY MOSS, NATIONAL SECURITY ATTORNEY: Oh, I`ve got some more breaking news I figured out Trump`s defense. It`s going to be insanity. He`s going to say he`s gotten old -- he`s like all these retirees down in Florida.\par \par \uc2 He just can`t think keep things straight. He doesn`t know what he has. He forgets things, he gets facts jumbled up. Look at that clip. He`s talking about oh we were negotiating with NARA and then I got raided.\par \par \uc2 Well, no, you left something out. There was a subpoena that came after that, after you started giving stuff back, and you lied to the DOJ. You had Nauta moving documents. That`s why you got raided.\par \par \uc2 I understand it happens to lots of individuals when they move down to Florida. They start to get confused. It`ll be me one day I`m sure.\par \par \uc2 But no, this is not a defense. He has clearly walked himself into a bunch of damaging admissions.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:02]\par \par \uc2 Jack Smith has got -- a team has got to be recording all of that, and they`re just laughing and popping popcorn, because he did nothing to help his case tonight.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Okay. Well, he did repeatedly tonight claim that the Presidential Records Act made it legal for him to take any document that he wanted, anything, when he left the White House.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday, Donald Trump`s last attorney general demolished that defense.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR, FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL: Absolutely. The legal theory by which he gets to take battle plans and sensitive national security information as his personal papers is absurd. It`s just as wacky as the legal doctrine they came up with for, you know, having the vice president unilaterally determine who won the election.\par \par \uc2 The whole purpose of the statute the Presidential Records Act is -- was to stop presidents from taking official documents out of the White House. It was passed after Watergate, that`s the whole purpose of it. And therefore, it restricted what a president can take. It says it`s purely private that had nothing to do with the deliberations of government policy.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, these documents are not purely private. It`s obvious.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann, what -- what can the Trump criminal defense lawyers be thinking tonight or when they see their client go on television say these things try to cling to the federal records act as if it has anything to do with this.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Well, look, they knew what they were getting into, in terms of a client who is not capable of being controlled. So, you know, he`s his own worst enemy as the point of, you know, what you`re doing right now and showing these clips and having us comment on it. So they knew what they were getting into.\par \par \uc2 On the President Records Act, it obviously is not what the law says. I mean, in black and white, the law is absolutely clear -- the only possible way for Trump to use this is to say, okay, well, maybe it`s clear on its face. But that`s not what I thought at the time -- meaning I thought that the law allowed me to keep it.\par \par \uc2 Well, first of all, that`s inconsistent with what he just said on Fox News which is he was separating out the personal from government documents. But if he`s saying that all is personal, then why is he doing any separation. All the documents are like his golf shirt.\par \par \uc2 Second, in order to make that defense, somebody has to take the stand, presumably him. That`s going to be the best possible thing for Jack Smith is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  taking the stand. But that isn`t not winnable case for the defense.\par \par \uc2 There`s the reason he is, you know, he didn`t testify in the E. Jean Carroll case. It`s the reason he never came in for an interview in the Mueller investigation, not possible and finally, what lawyer is going to say he advised him about. I mean, Mr. Corcoran isn`t going to say that.\par \par \uc2 No, no credible lawyer will possibly say that because the Presidential Record Act is absolutely clear on its face. When Bill Barr is saying this in that clip, this is not like, oh, isn`t it great that Bill Barr is saying that. He might as well have just read it, the statute. It literally says that.\par \par \uc2 So, no, this is just not anything anyone should worry about and, you know, for him to keep repeating this, the president to say this is just not helping his case.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Bradley Moss, the Magistrate Judge Bruce Reinhardt in the case issued an order on the non-classified discovery material that the defense will be obtaining from the prosecution, the order saying that they absolutely cannot use it publicly in any way. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cannot possess it. The lawyers have to possess it. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can look at it only in the presence of the lawyers, clearly trying to prevent \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from taking any of this evidence out onto the campaign trail with him.\par \par \uc2 MOSS: Yeah. And also, you know, rather expected protective order. I believe they did something similar in the Manhattan case to prevent this type of tainting of the larger process and exposure this information that`s supposed to be used at trial not as part of a public relations spectacle. That, of course, this only dealt with the unclassified aspect.\par \par \uc2 We`re now going to have a fight despite the fact that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  declassified everything we`re going to have a fight over classified discovery. There`s going to be the use of the classified information procedures act to determine what information is going to be provided, how they`re going to present, you know, documents at trial, what kind of discovery materials will be released to the defense, particularly his lawyers who slow to get clearances, but even to Mr. Trump himself.\par \par \uc2 That will be the next step. This current protective order was completely expected. I don`t think he even opposed it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Bradley Moss, thank you very much for starting off our discussion tonight. Andrew Weissmann and \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , please remain in place to answer the question that I was talking to Rachel about.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:01]\par \par \uc2 When we come back, "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporting that the Justice Department avoided investigating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for his role in the January 6th attack on the Capitol for over a year. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is now reporting what we all publicly suspected for over a year under the headline, \uc2 FBI\uc2  resisted opening probe into Trump`s role in January 6 for more than a year.\par \par \uc2 Carol Leonnig and Aaron Davis have done deep important reporting on what was happening inside the Justice Department as it slowly, carefully approached what now seems inevitable.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:12]\par \par \uc2 A special prosecutor investigating Donald Trump`s role in the attack on the Capitol on January 6th and Donald Trump`s attempts to overturn the presidential election in various states leading up to January 6th. The article says a "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " investigation found that more than a year would pass before prosecutors and \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents jointly embarked on a formal probe of actions directed from the White House to try to steal the election. Even then, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  stopped short of identifying the former president as a focus of that investigation.\par \par \uc2 A wariness about appearing partisan institutional caution and clashes over how much evidence was sufficient to investigate the actions of Trump and those around him, all contributed to the slow pace. Attorney General Garland and the Deputy Attorney General \uc2 Lisa Monaco\uc2  charted a cautious course aimed at restoring public trust in the department while some prosecutors below them chafed, feeling top officials were shying away from looking at evidence of potential crimes by Trump and those close to him, "The Post" found.\par \par \uc2 The reporting has interesting, colorful dramatic behind-the-scenes moments, but it does not show any willful, active attempt to protect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  against the evidence, mounting against him. Prosecutorial caution is not unusual in a Justice Department that took five years, yes, five years to investigate the killing of Eric Garner by a New York City police officer using a chokehold, a killing that was recorded on video took the Justice Department five years to investigate that killing before concluding they would not prosecute the officer.\par \par \uc2 Two and a half of those years was during the Obama administration and two and a half during the Trump administration. And for more perspective, it is worth remembering that the last time a present was brought down by an investigation President Richard Nixon`s Republican Attorney General \uc2 John Mitchell\uc2  was actively covering up for the president and was eventually convicted of crimes himself, the first attorney general in history to be convicted of crimes in office.\par \par \uc2 And in the end, despite all of that, to borrow a phrase from the title of Jimmy Breslin`s brilliant book about Watergate, the good guys finally won.\par \par \uc2 Back with us, Andrew Weissmann and \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Andrew, go ahead I want to hear from both of you from inside the Justice Department. Your experience. Your reading of this article.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So, it really it`s not something I`m like looking forward to saying. I worked in the Justice Department for over 20 years, and it`s never fun to criticize a place where you have deep ties and real love for the institution. But I actually thought this was amazing reporting by "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ", and I don`t think that the answer is to say, well, we are where we would have been because we -- that`s -- I don`t think that`s necessarily true.\par \par \uc2 I think if the Justice Department had done its job and looked at the evidence clearly when it was presented before them as outlined in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ", we could have been in a very different situation. So, we are very much in a situation where we`re racing against a clock. You could I`m sure if you asked Jack Smith that he would say that`s certainly true.\par \par \uc2 For Democrats and people who believe in the rule of law, the delay is something that is undermine their faith in accountability and having seeing somebody held to account after many, many years of avoiding that kind of responsibility. For Republicans who want to see and should see a trial and have a resolution of what happened before, there`s a nomination process. That is something that is very much in jeopardy because of delay, and for the country having this process and having the continued delay, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continuing to inflict harm on the country, all of that is something that means that the Justice Department when there is sufficient predication, which I think there was, to go forward with an investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The same way they went into an investigation of the foot soldiers should have acted sooner.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:49]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , it took \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  six months just to name the members of the January 6th committee that ended up doing a fantastic job. When you look at - when I look at the Justice Department from the outside, this kind of pace doesn`t surprise me at all.\par \par \uc2 Andrew`s points are really important here. This is one of those cases where speed matters.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Yes, so Lawrence, the American system is really based on the idea that no person is above the law. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has gotten special treatment and he has gotten it for years. He got it when he was president to the point where Bill Barr said you could not indict him, even in Andrew`s Mueller investigation, and you know, all sorts of coddling and lying about what the Mueller report said from the attorney general of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And now we are learning that special treatment continued even in 2021 and 2022. And the first place I think that needs to be looked at is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and \uc2 Chris Wray\uc2 , who was in charge of this investigation appointed by a Republican president, had all the credentials and capability to carry the investigation out. And it looks like he didn`t.\par \par \uc2 Now if the story is right today, it also looks like Garland sat on his hands too long, that he was too worried about appearing as being political so he`d been too far in the other direction.\par \par \uc2 And as Andrew said, that means precious time was lost. I will say on the other hand Garland did ultimately make the right decision of appointing special counsel Jack Smith who moved this thing much, much more quickly. And my hope is that all`s well that will end well. But this delay is a bit worrisome.\par \par \uc2 I think the other thing that shows though is that the idea that the Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is somehow in the Democratic tank or in Biden`s tank, or is anti Trump, or any of that, is so palpably absurd.\par \par \uc2 If anything, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has been acting much more like someone to protect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than anything else. There is zero favoritism here.\par \par \uc2 I mean like for example at Mar-a-Lago any person that did what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did would have been in jail by now. Trump has gotten every excuse from \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  in the book. I`m glad that Garland made the right choice to appoint Smith and move those prosecutions along.\par \par \uc2 But you know, not even have people looking in to January six for over a year? Once the new administration took office, you know, that`s a serious issue if these reports are right.\par \par \uc2 There are some questions about whether the Justice Department was truly sitting on its hands. People have been attacking the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " story for that on those grounds. So you know, it`s a big if. If it`s true, there is a issue.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , Andrew Weissmann -- thank you both very much for joining us tonight. As always, really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, this week marks one year since the Supreme Court revoked the constitutional right to reproductive freedom, and a majority of Americans think that decision was mistake.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , who will be interviewing Vice President Harris about this tomorrow will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:33:09]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: This week marks one year since the Supreme Court`s decision to revoke a constitutional right for the first time in history, the right to reproductive freedom. In the one year since that right was revoked, abortion bans have become law in 14 states.\par \par \uc2 A new Gallup poll shows that 61 percent of Americans think overturning Roe versus Wade was a bad thing. Tomorrow, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will be interviewed MSNBC`s \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 . Here`s what Vice President Harris said on June 24th of last year, the day the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Millions of women in America will go to bed tonight without access to the health care and reproductive care that they had this morning, without access to the same health care, or reproductive health care that their mothers and grandmothers had for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 You have the power to elect leaders who will defend and protect your rights. And as the president said I earlier today, with your vote you can act, and you have the final word. So this is not over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , her interview with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will be on tomorrow`s special edition of "THE REID OUT" at 7:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Joy, thank you very much for joining us tonight. What are you expecting in tomorrow`s discussion with the vice president?\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Well thank you, Lawrence for having me on. You know, we are here in Texas we are in Dallas. And you know Texas really is ground zero for the abortion fight.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:47]\par \par \uc2 REID: This is the state that pioneered the idea of a bounty law that allows people to collect cash bounties by suing people who assist anyone in getting an abortion. Abortion is completely illegal here other than to save the life of the pregnant woman. It is one of the -- it is the state with one of the most harsh abortion laws in the country.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I am really interested to hear what Vice President Harris has to say, because not only is she the first woman vice president, and really the voice of the administration on this issue.\par \par \uc2 She is a former prosecutor who dealt with issues of exploited women who have dealt with rape, et cetera, you know, when she was a prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 And I am just curious to hear her perspective on how we live in a world where the criminals in states like Texas are people who try to assist women who are in some of the worst crises of their lives. They are now the criminals with incredibly harsh penalties facing them if they tried to help women to get medical care.\par \par \uc2 It`s quite a shift for the country, and I`m sure that she has lots of thoughts on it. She and I both have children who have fewer rights than we did and who`s on status quo ante to the time before either she or I were born\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And she is obviously an expert on the novel kind of legal issues that have developed in the abortion ban movement around these Republican states where they`re actually beyond just trying to ban abortion in their states, they are trying to control interstate travel. They are trying to reach across state borders to things that residents of their states might do when they are not in their states. We`ve never seen anything like that.\par \par \uc2 REID: Well, I mean the thing is the big lie. There is a big lie in the antiabortion world as well and that is that they just wanted to return to the state and let the 50 laboratories of democracy decide on abortion law.\par \par \uc2 No, what they want is a national prohibition o abortion and if you get antiabortion activists, the really, you know, the hardest core advocates, give them a little sodium pentothal or even not any sodium pentothal, some of them will just admit it. What they want is a total ban.\par \par \uc2 And these bans on interstate travel are a part of that. They do not want women in blue states to be able to get abortions anymore than they want them in states like Texas.\par \par \uc2 Right now it is legal for women in Texas to travel across state lines, and I suspect a lot o them will be going to Mexico if they can get there, because if you try to go north you have to go pretty far if you live in the American South, because they have locked the former slave states in a sort of cord around the women of the former confederate states.\par \par \uc2 And happy Juneteenth, so it is kind of -- sort of poetic that they have locked the former slave states which oh by the way - very few of them expanded Obamacare.\par \par \uc2 So what they are essentially saying is that women in the states that have the worst health care outcomes, the highest female mortality in terms of childbirth, particularly women of color, the states with some of the worst performing preschools, who don`t even fund preschool, and essentially provide very little care for women who have children, and for children, are saying no, everybody has to give birth no matter what.\par \par \uc2 Raped, incest victim, child, we do not care. You are going to give birth. And so we were going to have some stakeholder sitting alongside the vice president, some of whom have suffered real. injury, material injury. And sometimes permanent medical injury because of these laws\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Oh, so this will be a conversation beyond just the two of you?\par \par \uc2 REID: Absolutely. So we are going to have -- and one of the guests that I am the most excited to talk with, you know, when people talk about the end of Roe, they talk about Dobbs. And that`s sort5 of the shorthand that we use.\par \par \uc2 But there was another part to that case,