ew tonight the front runner for the Republican presidential nomination repeatedly got away with simply refusing to answer foreign policy questions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF POLITICAL ANCHOR: Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  met with Chinese president Xi Jinping in Beijing.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You`ve been very critical towards the Biden administration policy toward China. Today Blinken said this. We do not support Taiwan independence. Should Taiwan be independent?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : So if I tell you an answer it is going to hurt me in negotiations on the assumption that I win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And of course no one in the history of presidential campaigns has ever answered a presidential foreign policy like that. And in a diplomatic breakthrough today, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  met with China`s President Xi and this is what he actually said about Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: I reiterated the long-standing U.S. One-China policy, that policy has no changed. It is guided by the Taiwan`s Relations Act free joint communiques, the six assurances.\par \par \uc2 We do not support Taiwan independence. We remain opposed to any unilateral changes to the status quo by either side. We continue to expect peaceful resolution across great differences. We remain committed to meeting our responsibilities under the Taiwan Relations Act including making sure that Taiwan has the ability to defend itself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not know it but of course that was his administration`s position on Taiwan too. In his Fox interview tonight \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  refused to answer another very, very simple foreign policy question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Was it wrong to invade Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: He wouldn`t have done it if it were me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And in China today, the secretary of state said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: With regard to lethal aid to Russia for the use in Ukraine, we and other countries have received assurances from China that it is not and will not provide lethal assistance to Russia for use in Ukraine. We appreciate that and we have not seen any evidence that contradicts that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now David Rothkopf, foreign affairs analyst and host of "The Deep State" radio podcast. David, thank you very much for joining us tonight it is quite startling to see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  refusing and unable anyway to answer foreign policy questions on the same day when the secretary of state is getting, you know, not a major diplomatic breakthrough but a significant improvement in the administration`s ability to deal with China.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ROTHKOPF, FOREIGN AFFAIRS ANALYST: Well it`s the difference between competence and incompetence. \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2  is a gifted diplomat. The U.S. was in very tough position with China just a few months ago because of the surveillance balloon. And as a result of systematic, methodical outreach to the Chinese that included the CIA director \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2 , the national security adviser Jake Sullivan and finally \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2  on this trip. The U.S. has gotten back to communicating with the other most important country in the world.\par \par \uc2 Trump, on the other hand you know, this is the guy who embraced Kim Jong- un. This is the guy who embraced MBS. This is the guy who kowtowed to Putin. This is the guy who wanted to blackmail Zelenskyy. This is the guy who wanted to get out of \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He was a foreign policy disaster from beginning to end because he does not care about the U.S. or U.S. national interest and nothing of course shows that quite as much as his abuse of classified document and his, you know, taking of national secrets and pretending that they are his own.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let`s listen to what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said tonight about Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We have lost Saudi Arabia. Saudi Arabia, they`re friends of mine. They`re great people. The crown prince and the king -- I get along with them incredibly, they would do anything. They are great, great people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So the murderer is a great, great person according to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ROTHKOPF: Well, he did give his son-in-law $2 billion, you know that. That probably produces a little warm feeling from Trump. But I think Trump needs to just keep quiet on this one as well.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:47]\par \par \uc2 ROTHKOPF: Not only were his policies towards MBS and the Khashoggi murder repulsive but this administration is actually not (INAUDIBLE) Saudi Arabia and I think it`s not out of the question that we soon have an improvement in relations between the Saudis and the Israelis. It`s brokered by the Biden team.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: David Rothkopf, thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ROTHKOPF: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Time for tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL BARR, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: He will always put his own interests and gratifying his own ego ahead of everything else including the country`s interests. There`s no question about it.\par \par \uc2 This is a perfect example of that. He is like -- you know, he`s like a nine-year-old, defiant nine-year-old kid who is always pushing the glass towards the edge of the table defying his parents to stop him from doing it.\par \par \uc2 It`s a means of self assertion and exerting his dominance over other people. And he is a very petty individua who will always put his interests ahead of the country`s. His personal gratification of, you know, of his ego.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:58]\par \par \uc2 BARR: But our country cannot be a therapy session for, you know, a troubled man like this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Donald Trump`s last attorney general gets tonight`s LAST WORD about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230620ej6j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for June 15, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Barbara McQuade, Harry Litman \par \uc2 7,083 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 June 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:36]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like it`s time to check the latest rules on write-in ballots for the New Hampshire primary.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": Yes, exactly. Bring a pen if you`re a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah. You know, back in `68, when the New Hampshire primary became a giant event because Gene McCarthy went up there and challenged the sitting president, Lyndon Johnson, it -- the sensation was, that McCarthy won because he got more than 40 percent of the vote. But Lyndon Johnson won, and his name was not on the ballot. It had to be written in.\par \par \uc2 And so, who knows? Who knows, Alex?\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Who knows? Listen, \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  will tell you write in ballot is just fine by her.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 So, Alex, before you go, I`m a little -- I`m a little concerned. I have Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  booked for the show tonight, but that was --\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Excellent.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- that was done before I read Jennifer Palmieri`s "Vanity Fair" profile of her. Have you read this?\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I saw it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: OK.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I got an alert about it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: OK.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I`m a big fan of Jennifer Palmieri.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah. OK, great. But when you get, like, I don`t know, 50 words in, it says -- this is Jennifer Palmieri`s reporting, so it`s real, it`s true. She says, Whitmer does not go looking for trouble, but if you come for her, she will punch you in the mouth. Alex --\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Is it an on-set interview, Lawrence?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Should I -- should I be afraid? Should I -- do you want to hang around and interview the governor and I`ll --\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I`m just saying, if she on set? That`s the biggest concern right now.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know how much command of the detail of the logistics I have, Alex. I -- I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I`m just saying, you`re safe for now if she`s not on set.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: OK, that`s a good point. That`s a good point. I`ll -- I`ll find out if she`s on set.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Good luck, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Alex. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, federal criminal defendant, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  , has a question for President Biden., the Department of Justice, and special prosecutor, \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 . On social media tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  asked, when are they going to drop all charges against me, apologize, and return everything that was illegally taken from my home?\par \par \uc2 The short answer is, of course, never. First of all, President Biden had nothing to do with the federal indictment \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  faces right now, which includes violations of the Espionage Act. The attorney general, specifically, removed the normal Department of Justice decision-making structure from the case, and left it to an independent special counsel, \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 , who is never going to drop these charges, or apologize to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , or return everything that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  seized from Donald Trump`s Florida residence with a search warrant on August 8th of last year. None of which was seized illegally.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was subjected to a carefully crafted legal search and seizure of government documents that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was hiding from the federal government`s pursuit of those documents, a year and a half pursuit of those documents. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is making it very clear what he will demand of his next attorney general, if he returns to the presidency, the next Trump attorney general, would be forced to drop all charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , and have the DOJ formally apologized to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , and he -- Trump will require his attorney general to return to him everything that was taken with that search warrant.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s next attorney general would no doubt demand a pardon for himself, or herself for every crime that the attorney general commits for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . That`s the kind of madness that would run rampant in another Trump administration. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tells the lie that President Biden has weaponized the Justice Department, by ordering the Justice Department to indict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , which is, of course, untrue. President Biden has never done and would never do that.\par \par \uc2 But, after condemning that conduct, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says that is exactly what he plans to do.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT: I will appoint a real special prosecutor to go after the most corrupt president in the history of the United States of America, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:05:05]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So there is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promising to weaponize the Justice Department against his political opponents if he is restored to the presidency.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has never appointed a special prosecutor. \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  was appointed by Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  without ever consulting the president. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promises to appoint special prosecutors himself if he is president. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be choosing the special prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 The United States and the world have much to fear in a second Trump presidency and I don`t mean to ever stoke that fear on this program because I strongly believe that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the Republican candidate for president in the next election, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will beat him by an even greater margin than he beat him before.\par \par \uc2 I mentioned Donald Trump`s demented dream of appointing a special prosecutor to go after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  merely to illustrate how much \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has to say about the federal criminal case against him that is not actually about the federal criminal case against him. It can be fairly taken as an indicator of just how hopelessly guilty \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  thinks he is that he says so little about the actual evidence in the case and instead turns his comments about being the first federally prosecuted former president for the United States into a fantasy for his weakest minded followers that will generate campaign contributions from them to restore \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the presidency to carry out their fantasy.\par \par \uc2 Because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his lawyers cannot come up with one word of legitimate sounding legal defense to the charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , you can expect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to continue to make utterly empty statements and accusations that have absolutely nothing to do with whether he is guilty or not guilty of the espionage charges that he now faces.\par \par \uc2 As of tonight, the case of the United States of America versus \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  and Waltine Nauta is moving along briskly with an order issued by Judge Aileen Mercedes Cannon who shows no signs of possibly recusing herself.\par \par \uc2 That order today says: On or before June 16, 2023, which is tomorrow, all attorneys of record and forthcoming attorneys of records shall contact the litigation security group of the \uc2 U.S. Department of Justice\uc2  , if they have not done so already, to expedite the necessary clearance process for all team members anticipated to participate in this matter and thereafter file a notice of compliance no later than June 20th, 2023.\par \par \uc2 Delay is Donald Trump`s friend in this case and Judge Cannon has shown herself to be Donald Trump`s legal very best friend in her previous judicial interference in the investigation that led to this criminal case. And so, it is good news tonight that Judge Cannon has given the lawyers days instead of weeks to rush the security clearance process for the criminal defense lawyers in the case.\par \par \uc2 And Donald Trump`s question tonight about when are they going to return everything that was taken from my home, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  comes once again very close to admitting to the crimes that he is charged with. That question is a public declaration by inference that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew that he had classified documents in his home and that he wants to continue to illegally possess them.\par \par \uc2 So it is now a consistent pattern with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that whenever he makes the slightest reference to the evidence in the case, he in some way, by implication at least and sometimes very directly, confesses to the crimes that he has been charged with.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s social media post by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is an echo of what former federal prosecutor Andrew Weissmann called a confession on this program when he heard \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  say this:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT: I had every right to have these documents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That was Tuesday night. That was after he was arraigned for illegally possessing those documents.\par \par \uc2 Some of the things like that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says publicly like that the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says probably can and will be used against him in this trial. And so, as we proceed through this year of defendant Trump and next year of defendant Trump and possibly through the appeals process to come, many possible years of criminal defendant Trump, it is best to remain mindful of the fact that every time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says the slightest thing, no matter how untrue about the evidence in any of the criminal cases against him, that is a very good day for the prosecutors of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who just want him to continue to hand them statements that can and will be used against him.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:37]\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  " is reporting tonight how the beautiful mind boxes were born that was the term the White House staff used to describe Donald Trump`s personal assembly of cardboard boxes that he moved back and forth from the West Wing of the White House to the residents of the White House and then finally to Florida. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  " tonight explains why the staff called those boxes the beautiful mind boxes. It was a reference to the title of a book and movie depicting the life of John F. Nash Jr., the mathematician with schizophrenia played in the film by Russell Crowe who covered his office with newspaper clippings believing they held a Russian code he needed to crack.\par \par \uc2 And so, in this context, beautiful mind is the Trump staff way of saying that Donald Trump`s handling of those boxes was inexplicably crazy. "The Times" reports he could point to specific boxes that he wanted to take with him on Air Force One when he was traveling and declined to take others appearing aware of the contents inside the boxes he chose. Mr. Trump repeatedly told advisors but boxes of documents were mine, according to several people familiar with his remarks.\par \par \uc2 With the Trump staff calling them the beautiful mind boxes, the question of Donald Trump`s peculiar and highly risky attachment to the boxes seemed worthy of a psychiatric opinion. And so, on this program last night, I asked former \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2  assistant professor of psychiatry, Dr. Lance Dodes, for a psychiatric explanation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Is there a psychiatric explanation for why he kept these documents after the federal government demanded them, sent a subpoena for him after he was told by lawyers he had to give them back?\par \par \uc2 DR. LANCE DODES, RET. ASST. CLINICAL PROFESSOR OF PSYCHIATRY, \uc2 HARVARD MEDICAL\uc2  SCHOOL: My guess would be that it makes him greater in his own mind. Now he has secret documents which he had when he was president but if he loses the presidency, at least he has the secret documents. It`s like having a badge, you know, when you`re four years old that says you`re a secret policeman. I think it`s something like that. He needs it for himself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading off our discussion tonight, Harry Litman, former U.S. attorney and former deputy assistant attorney general. He is a senior legal affairs columnist for "The \uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2  ". Also with us, Barbara McQuade , former U.S. attorney and a law professor at the University of Michigan Law School. She`s an MSNBC analyst.\par \par \uc2 Barbara McQuade , grasping at straws here, we have seen now one order issued by Judge Cannon. And it is the smallest of orders. It`s procedural. It`s in the bookkeeping category of make sure you get your security clearances, but she`s pushing the lawyers to do it really fast, to get at it in a matter of days, to respond to the order within a day. So, so far, on order number one, Judge Cannon seems to be doing the right thing.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE , MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, I actually am impressed at how quickly she did this. Walt Nauta hasn`t even been arraigned yet, but she wants to get going on these security clearances. It can take some time. These lawyers are going to have to complete a form that is incredibly extensive.\par \par \uc2 It asks about every place you`ve ever lived, every job you`ve ever held, any foreign contacts that you may have, and then the \uc2 FBI\uc2  actually investigates to see if all of that stuff is true and goes out and talks to those people. So it can take some time, even if they push it to try to get it done as quickly as possible. It just takes quite a bit of time to get it done.\par \par \uc2 So I`m actually gratified to see that she`s doing it this quickly, that you know, she`s saying let`s get this paperwork going and let`s get it submitted no later than June 20th. That`s pretty quick. So I`m impressed with the pace that she has set so far.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Harry Litman, what does it tell us if anything about the possible recusal of Judge Cannon by Judge Cannon, her own decision to recuse that she could make herself? Is that something you make in a day one, day two scenario, or is it -- is it -- is this the kind of thing where she`s just issuing a simple procedural order, maybe she could decide herself next week to recuse herself? Or does this order tell you she`s just not going to recuse herself?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: It comes pretty close to that, Lawrence, I think. I mean, it`s -- she certainly could do that next week.\par \par \uc2 We already thought from what she what we learned on the last episode when she, you know, doubled down after being pretty sharply abraded by the 11th Circuit, she was unlikely to recuse, and there`s net the time as you suggest is up front, so that she`s trying to you know take the reins makes it less likely that she`ll just let them go down the line.\par \par \uc2 So it seems to me it points in the direction of she`s going to -- she thinks she`s keeping the case.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Barbara McQuade , on the -- the statements that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  makes, some of those will be usable in court whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  takes the witness stand or not. Of course, if he does take the witness stand, you can always use those statements to just -- when they contradict something he says on the witness stand. But as you see these statements come out, they all have some to varying degrees some element of admission in them.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: They do, Lawrence. I mean, one of the reasons that lawyers advise their clients not to speak is because they might say something inadvertently that is damaging to them or it does not allow them to preserve their options for trial. So, right now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seems to be saying, yes, I did have these documents, which is part of, you know, one of the elements the government would have to prove at trial. So thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 But his defense seems to be -- I had them because I was entitled to keep them. I think a savvy lawyer would say, please keep your mouth shut, so that we can figure out what`s the best defense that we`re going to raise here. Now that he`s played this card, it is out there in the public domain and whether he testifies or not, the prosecution can use this.\par \par \uc2 I`ve had cases where people were public officials and we got video recordings of these kinds of things and played them for the jury at trial, so they could see and hear the words coming out of the defendant`s mouth. So, you can bet that they will be gathering this material and use it if they need to.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Harry, the -- as we go forward we don`t know -- to say the obvious, we don`t know everything that the special prosecutor knows, we don`t know everything that was said in the grand jury. But when we get this kind of reporting tonight from "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2  " about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  during the presidency, in the birthing of these boxes in bringing them to life in the first place, moving them around on Air Force One when he goes on trips um and knowing what`s in each one, saying I want to take that one and not that one because he knows the contents. It seems highly likely that with all of the Trump staff people who have testified to the grand jury, that \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  knows that, that he knows that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew when he was creating these boxes what was in those boxes.\par \par \uc2 And so, that defense that has been thrown around by Trump`s lawyers but not necessarily by him, that he didn`t know it was in the boxes, that this evidence indicates that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew exactly what he was doing with the boxes.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Highly likely. These are his little toys in the sandbox that he gets -- pardon me, that he gets the actual, you know, little badge that your psychiatrist was taught -- was talking about, you know? And, look, it`s so true generally, we find out every week how much more \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  knows than we know.\par \par \uc2 A couple weeks ago, there was kind of a thunderbolt that went unnoticed relatively because so much else is happening. \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  has testified in both the January 6th grand jury and the Mar-a-Lago grand jury. Yesterday, we found out about other testimony about January 6.\par \par \uc2 What we have is the little snippets that enterprising reporters have been able to ferret out, but all -- every time we find out something new it just brings home the mountain of evidence that Smith is sitting on that that we really don`t know about. And you can -- with all the witnesses he`s interviewed, you can bet that nearly everything that`s out there he knows.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`re going to he`s in a quick commercial break right here. If you can stay with us, when we come back, we want to consider the predicament of Donald Trump`s co-defendant Walt Nauta. Is there a chance Walt Nauta could turn against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in this case? That question is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:24:03]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In an article titled "Trump`s Bag Man", "The Atlantic" today focused on Donald Trump`s valet and box-moving assistant Walt Nauta who is now Donald Trump`s co-defendant.\par \par \uc2 Nauta seems screwed no matter what he does. If he sticks with Trump, he faces the prospect of years in jail. If he Cooperates against Trump, he might avoid prison but he stands to lose his legal representation, his livelihood and his chance at a pardon if Trump is reelected.\par \par \uc2 But what if Walt Nauta is not facing the prospect of years in jail? There is no mandatory minimum sentence for what Walt Nauta is charged with, and he could not possibly have a more sympathetic judge. Walt Nauta and his lawyer have strong reason to bet that even if found guilty in Judge Cannon`s courtroom, there is a strong likelihood that his sentence if any would not include any prison time.\par \par \uc2 Back with us, Barbara McQuade and Harry Litman.\par \par \uc2 Barbara, there`s a -- there seems to be a stress point in the in this particular notion of putting pressure on Walt Nauta because the pressure you`re always putting is the prospect of a prison term you won`t want to serve versus no time if you cooperate with the prosecutor, but he could end up with no time even if he`s found guilty.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yeah, it`s -- it`s a bit of a wild card with Judge Cannon here. You know, the charges he is facing are very serious. The obstruction of justice alone carries statutory maximum sentence of 20 years in prison, although most often, the sentencing guidelines end up with a sentence lower than that, and I think a lawyer could make a pretty compelling case here that he was a minor participant in a more serious crime and maybe ask for an even lower sentence.\par \par \uc2 But nonetheless, he is facing some jail time. As you point out though, Judge Cannon has an awful lot of power as the sentencing judge. Typically, appellate courts are very deferential to trial judges to impose sentence. Only if she did something that was so unreasonable would the Justice Department be able to get a sentence that she imposes reversed.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Harry, what about that? Do you -- do you think there`s any real pressure on Walt Nauta to flip as long as Judge Cannon is the judge in this case?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Yeah, I do. I mean, look, here`s the thing -- the DOJ gave him almost certainly an offer and it`s probably still on the table that he wouldn`t have to do any jail time, might not even face a conviction.\par \par \uc2 Conspiracy to obstruct justice, as opposed to just the false statement, which they haven`t did the rights on, three, four years at a minimum -- man, that`s quite a plunge if she were to do it. I think it`s in his interest to cooperate, but I think what`s going on on the other side, I`ve really -- he`s a -- he`s a bit of a cipher, but I think one thing he could be thinking is, even if I wind up doing a little jail time, the Trump family, I`m 40 years old, they`ll take care of me for the rest of my life, and if I don`t -- now, I`m, you know, I -- back sort of in the wilderness.\par \par \uc2 But I don`t think he should be sanguine about walking completely if he doesn`t cooperate.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Barbara, there`s another downward pressure to put it mildly on Nauta`s possible sentencing and that is the sentencing of his co- conspirator. It is extremely unlikely that Judge Cannon would sentence \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to any form of confinement beyond home confinement.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Well, I think the way that might work, Lawrence, would be that he would get a sentence, and it might be a substantial sentence of many years. But I do agree with you that it`s unlikely he would serve his sentence in a prison, and I think that although it`s likely that the number that Walt Nauta gets will be less than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , just because his role in the offense was less egregious, I think that because Walt Nauta does not have the same Secret Service concerns as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , it`s possible that Walt Nauta could serve his sentence in prison while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could serve his in-home confinement because of the extraordinary reasons that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would serve home confinement which is his lifetime of Secret Service protection.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Barbara McQuade and Harry Litman, thank you both very much for joining us tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, coming up, a new "Vanity Fair" profile of Michigan`s governor says Whitmer doesn`t go looking for trouble but if you come for her, she will punch you in the mouth. Governor Whitmer joins us next and luckily, the governor I`m told will be joining us from East Lansing, Michigan, 680 miles away from where I`m sitting in Midtown Manhattan. So, a punch in the mouth tonight is out of the question.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:33:26]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Our next guest says, "If you want to get something done, give it to a busy woman."\par \par \uc2 She won her first election 23 years ago and has never lost an election since. Her life has been threatened repeatedly, the safety of her family has been threatened repeatedly, a right-wing criminal plot was hatched to kidnap and murder her.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Palmieri has 30 years experience in politics, and campaigning, including as the communications director in the presidential campaign of the first woman candidate nominated by a major party who won the most votes for president in 2016, but came in second in the electoral college.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Palmieri went to Michigan to deliver her experienced assessment of that state`s governor, which she has done for "Vanity Fair", under the headline, "Why \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  has what it takes for a White House run".\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Palmieri writes, "Whitmer doesn`t go looking for trouble but if you come for her, she will punch you in the mouth."\par \par \uc2 Here is Governor Whitmer two months ago, signing a bill passed by the state legislature, after the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-WI): Today, we`re going to take action to make sure that our statutes, our laws, reflect our values, and our constitution. I`m excited today to be signing a bill that will repeal our extreme 1931 law that bans abortion and criminalizes nurses and doctors for just doing their jobs.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:54]\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: We know that this fight continues, but I also know this. After the last year that I have lived through, I can tell you, we are going to win this fight. We just stay organized. We stay vigilant. We stay active. And we work together.\par \par \uc2 So who would like to watch me slay a zombie?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  . She is a co-chair of the Biden-Harris 2024 reelection campaign. Governor, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: Thanks Lawrence. It`s great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So there`s really been a political revolution of sorts in Michigan that the Democratic Party is watching. You had, for the first time in 40 years, Democratic control of the state legislature there. How did all of this happen?\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: A lot of hard work, Lawrence. I have to tell you, we knew that with all of the threats to our democracy, to our fundamental freedoms, it was important that we fought like hell. And that`s why we coalesced, we built a campaign, we stayed focused on the things that matter to people and we won. And we didn`t just win, but we won overwhelmingly, and flipped both chambers of our legislature in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 So you see some states rolling rights back, taking away freedoms, Michigan is moving in the opposite direction and I`m really proud of that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: On gun safety legislation, you did something that in most states, is impossible at this point politically. You had a horrible shooting in the state, and there was a reaction to it. There was a governing reaction to it. What were you able to accomplish?\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: So we finally got Michigan on the right side of some common sense gun policies. And it`s simple things like background checks, and secure storage law, as well as red flag laws. These are the things that the average person expects and wants to see.\par \par \uc2 We finally -- we`re able to get it done because I have this new legislature. And we stayed focused, we listen to people and I think if that is your north star, you`re going to be successful.\par \par \uc2 But the people are telling us loud and clear, they expect their fundamental rights, they expect democracy, and justice and I think that`s what -- why this moment is still so very real and scary and why we have to stay vigilant, and continue to work to protect these things.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: How stable do you think the Democratic position is the party`s position is in Michigan? I mean it`s not like you have massive, vote winning margins when you win these elections. It seems like these things could go the other way.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: Well Michigan is still a very blue state -- sorry, a very purple state. We turned it blue in this last election, but no one should make any assumption that the fight is over. The fight continues. And that`s why we`ve got to fight like hell. It`s why the work that we are doing to create a PAC to support candidates and causes that are on the front line of a democracy, FightLikeHellPAC.org is a way that we are working to ensure we hold on to these fundamental freedoms in Michigan but also expand and support candidates who or on the frontlines to protect this democracy. Because we are in a very precarious moment in this country.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So you are a reelected governor in Michigan, which means you are term limited to two terms there. You don`t have a campaign to embark on on your own. Is this why you are dedicating your political energies to the Biden-Harris campaign?\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: Absolutely. you know, I`m excited about supporting the Biden- Harris campaign. We created this PAC to ensure that we can replicate what we did in Michigan in 2024, but also beyond Michigan.\par \par \uc2 The fight for these fundamental freedoms does not end at state lines. That is precisely why supporting causes and candidates, who are fighting to protect our elections and our democracy, who are fighting to hold on to fundamental freedoms is so important not just to Michiganders, but to all Americans.\par \par \uc2 And that is why our work to create this FightLikeHellPAC.org is so important because we made some great strides here in Michigan, but make no mistake, this is an unprecedented moment in American history. And we`ve got to continue to fight like hell to protect these rights.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, I know that no one has ever run for governor of any state in this country thinking, I am running for a job that is so dangerous, people might be plotting to kidnap and murder me. And yet, that happened to you as governor.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:49]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What do you say to people who, when they are considering possibly getting into elective (ph) office, that this is the new dynamic of what might be involved? And they don`t want to do it because of what they`ve seen happen to you.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: Well, I understand the reticence to engage when you can see such horrible, not just rhetoric, but actions being taken against elected officials or their spouses or family members, as we saw with \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 And yet, it`s that very reason that we can`t turn away, we can`t pull out, we can`t look backwards. We`ve got to face forward, fight forward, and fight like hell.\par \par \uc2 And we need good people to run for office. I`m always working to make sure that we are creating space, and have a real, empowered representation at all tables at which I`m sitting.\par \par \uc2 But we`ve got to encourage people to lean in here and we have to hold people accountable. You know, it has been tough, and the rhetoric has gotten so ugly and scary in this country, but this isn`t how it should be, and we can fix this. But we don`t fix it by turning away. We fix it by leaning in and fighting.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, in reading Jennifer Palmieri `s profile of you, which she seems to be reporting, her sense of it is, that in Michigan, yes, they are voting for your policies, but they are very much voting for the person.\par \par \uc2 So it seems like for the formula to work for Democrats elsewhere, the \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  -like candidate is a very important part of this campaign.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: I don`t know about that Lawrence. I could just tell you, you know, I`m just an ordinary person. I have an extraordinary job. I`m serving in extraordinary times. I take very seriously the oath of office I took to the constitution, to fighting for the people that I serve in the state of Michigan.\par \par \uc2 But you know, Michiganders are like Americans everywhere, right. They work hard, they try to do right by their family and loved ones and they expect their leaders to do the same. And that is why, as I think about the high stakes in this moment, the high stakes in this young democracy of ours, the high stakes of these fundamental rights, whether it`s LGBTQ or women`s rights, or people of color, when we speak together, we are the majority.\par \par \uc2 We make a seat at the table for every person, and that is why -- that`s why this work is so important. I may not be on the ballot next year, but that doesn`t keep me from wanting to make sure that we are on the front lines, and holding the line on these fundamental freedoms.\par \par \uc2 You know, I probably sound like a commercial, but it is why FightLikeHellPAC.org is the tool that I`m going to use to support candidates and causes. And I invite anyone who wants to stand with us, to help, to $5 or whatever they can afford because that is how we win. That is how we ensure that this democracy continues, and is strengthened by the upcoming election.\par \par \uc2 And there`s just so much at stake, and I can`t not think of my kids in all things that I do. From the way I treat people, to the words that I use, to the issues that I champion and that is what it`s all about for me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Will your PAC be supporting the down ballot candidates like For example, state legislature, which is how you started. And the officers, other than just the presidential campaign?\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: Yes, absolutely. so it is the first time I have had a federal PAC. It gives us the ability to help candidates whether they are congressional or state legislature, or the Biden-Harris campaign. And causes as well that are on the front lines.\par \par \uc2 You know, there are a host of partners that were integral in terms of turnout and registration and persuasion. So this work is not done by one person or organization. It`s got to be about all of us. And so anyone who fits that mold of a Fight Like Hell Candidate will benefit from the FightLikeHellPAC.org.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  with a very easy to remember name of her PAC. Thank you very so much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: Thank you Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, he first met Joanna McClinton on this program when she was on the verge of becoming the first Democratic speaker of the Pennsylvania House of Representatives in 12 yards and the first black woman speaker in the Pennsylvania House of Representatives has ever had.\par \par \uc2 Speaker McClinton joined Vice President Harris, and other state legislatures at the White House today to mark the one year anniversary of the Supreme Court overturning Roe versus Wade and more importantly, to strategize how to preserve access to abortion care for all women in this country. Speaker McClinton joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:48]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The White House convened more than 80 state legislators from 41 states to discuss access to abortion today. Pennsylvania`s first black woman speaker of the Pennsylvania House of Representatives, Joanna McClinton, who led the Pennsylvania delegation to the White House spoke about how Democrats are defending reproductive rights for Pennsylvania`s women.\par \par \uc2 Joanna McClinton joined us on this program after she was sworn in as speaker.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:55]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOANNA MCCLINTON (D-PA), SPEAKER OF THE STATE HOUSE: Neighbors do not always know how extreme Republicans behave and how extreme their agenda is. So certainly them moving very quickly on a Friday night in July, our last night of session, to pass a constitutional abortion ban, the first step, it definitely shines a bright light on their agenda. And it exposed what they would try to do, if they were to remain in control here in Harrisburg.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is House Speaker of the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, Joanna McClinton. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 So at your meeting at the White House with the vice president, was this informational for the vice president? Meaning, you`re reporting to her from out there in the field in the states, or was the vice president also making suggestions about how the administration might be able to help?\par \par \uc2 MCCLINTON: So it was definitely a two-way street today at the White House. Vice President Harris was not on the official agenda, so all of us were pleasantly surprised to see her come into our room where we were having the convening and addressed us, giving us updates on what is happening at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, we all know Vice President Harris has been leading in this space, traveling across our nation, meeting with state lawmakers and organizations to make sure that women in this country are not ignored, are not dismissed, and that we have rights in 2023.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And was this as much a campaign discussion as a governing discussion? You know, Obviously, there was discussion about what you can do in the legislatures to try to prevent some of these bills from being passed, that Republicans would try and pass. But there is also a campaign issue involved here.\par \par \uc2 MCCLINTON: Indefinitely. Voters go to the polls every six months. Here in Pennsylvania, we`re choosing judges on our highest state courts this year, and we, know next year, we`re going to reelect President Biden and Vice President Harris.\par \par \uc2 But we were not there for a campaign event today. We were there on official business, talking about how those of us who are in Democratic majorities are governing, and protecting women in our various states.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: There have been 563 provisions to restrict access to abortion introduced in state legislatures this year, just in the last year. And some of that is, obviously, happening in states where Republicans are the majority, but there are Democratic members of those legislatures in minorities that are usually powerless.\par \par \uc2 Was there any strategy developed for them in the minorities that they are in the legislatures to try to work against this?\par \par \uc2 MCCLINTON: So you know I know all about being a minority. Our caucus was in the minority in Harrisburg for more than a decade, so one of the things that we discussed today was inter compact -- our inter state compacts. Where state surrounding areas where women don`t have access to this critical health care are able to be able to safely go to other states.\par \par \uc2 And we know that the federal government has done a lot for that. But we can look at states like where I live in the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania. We have neighbors from Ohio, from West Virginia. They`ll frequently cross those state lines, that come to get this health care to Pennsylvania to get it.\par \par \uc2 And in addition to that, we have states surrounding us like New York, New Jersey, Maryland and Delaware, and that have shield law, that are protecting providers, that have taken a lot of steps, not only to protect providers and patients, but to codify this crucial right.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: How much can states do to help women with this -- what has now become a travel issue? How do they get out of their state, where it`s illegal, to a state where what they need is legal?\par \par \uc2 MCCLINTON: Well, there are some interesting legislation pending in New York state that make sure providers in New York state will not ever be criminally prosecuted by states where, otherwise, they could not practice. And they are practicing through digital communication. They`re having telehealth visit right on a cell phone, you know, prescribing mifepristone, and whatever else could possibly be necessary for these women patients who are in states where it is, unfortunately, no longer available.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Pennsylvania House Speaker Joanna McClinton, thank you very much for joining us tonight, Madam Speaker. We always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MCCLINTON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s LAST WORD is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:21]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Here is Republican Senator Lisa Murkowski`s reaction to the 37- count federal indictment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for violations of the Espionage Act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LISA MURKOWSKI (R-AK): A pretty comprehensive -- I would say condemnation of the president`s actions here.\par \par \uc2 Keeping (ph) classified documents and from what we understand from the indictment out there, the types of documents that were -- that were retained.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:52]\par \par \uc2 MURKOWSKI: And then further to the point that when this came to his attention, he chose to not return it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230616ej6f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for June 5, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Jen Psaki, Ari Melber \par \uc2 8,847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 June 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:02]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Because they can`t stand them and don`t relate to them, and they see that they`re hateful and cruel. Kelly, well, that was me, not you. I won`t put that mean stuff on myself because I believe it. Kelly Robinson, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN" with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have the right to take stuff. Do you know that they ended up paying \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  I think $18 million for what he had.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI (voiceover): Imminent indictment watch. The Trump attorneys make their case directly to Jack Smith in Washington.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: The exact same thing happened in Manhattan which is as you see, defense lawyer is making an argument, and then you see charges.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI (voiceover): Tonight, Ari Melber on tonight`s showdown and what it could mean for charges.\par \par \uc2 JAMES COMEY\uc2 , FORMER DIRECTOR, \uc2 FBI\uc2 : He could be wearing an ankle bracelet while accepting the nomination.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI (voiceover): Plus, former CIA Director John Brennan on the national security concerns. Then, a round table of White House insiders, \uc2 Kate Bedingfield\uc2 , Robert Gibbs, and me.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There`s an awful lot of really good Republicans. But the MAGA Republicans are a different breed of cat.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI (voiceover): And the Republican field shrinks by one. And that`s the strategy.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (R-NH): Every candidate needs to understand the responsibility of getting out and getting up quickly.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI (voiceover): New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  joins me live when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  in for Chris Hayes tonight. As many of you may know, I have some experience working with presidents, both President Obama and President Biden. And in those jobs, I`ve spent a fair amount of time with classified information, both in the White House and also at the State Department.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what I can tell you from my experience. Despite what Donald Trump`s lawyers are telling all of you, there is neither a magic wand a president has to retroactively declassify documents, nor is there some sort of special declassification process available only to the White House.\par \par \uc2 And the argument from Trump allies that knowingly taking classified documents about issues as sensitive as nuclear secrets or a hypothetical war with Iran, say, and sharing that with others, which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has reportedly done, is the exact same thing as unknowingly, that`s the key word there, retaining classified documents and then returning them, which President Biden and Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  both did, is not exactly on the level as my mother might say.\par \par \uc2 And having worked on three presidential campaigns and in politics for 20 years, the argument -- the political argument from Trump allies that more indictments will help Trump sounds like a bit of wishful thinking to me. It`s a little spin there especially when the next indictments could be about pretty popular issues like the protection of our democracy and our national security.\par \par \uc2 Traditionally getting indicted isn`t a good thing. It`s not a good thing. But we`re in unprecedented territory here. And even I have to admit, spending a lot of time in the political world, of all the time I have spent on campaign buses and in the Oval Office, I have never worked for a candidate under indictment with additional charges likely on the way. So, this situation is new to me too. We`re all in it together.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what we know right now. In yet another sign that one of at least four ongoing criminal investigations into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is reaching its end game, lawyers for the ex-president were spotted leaving the Department of Justice today. Three of Trump`s attorneys made the trip to meet with Special Counsel Jack Smith himself apparently as part of a latch last-ditch effort to convince him not to indict their client.\par \par \uc2 NBC News has learned that Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  who will make the final decision on whether to indict Trump on the recommendation of Jack Smith, of course, was not present for today`s meeting. Not necessarily a surprise but important to note. Trump`s lawyers foreshadowed all of this last month when they sent a very Trumpian blustery letter to the Attorney General requesting such a meeting to make their case.\par \par \uc2 And these kinds of meetings are pretty common especially when the parties involved believe criminal charges may be imminent. That timeline is also backed up by a recent report from NBC News which says the grand jury investigating Trump will reconvene this week. That grand jury had previously been on something of a hiatus, a little bit of a break for several weeks. And this latest development is leading many legal eagles we all know to speculate that an indictment could be handed down any day now. Not if but when.\par \par \uc2 Look, you don`t need me to tell you that none of this is normal at all. We`re less than a year out from the start of the 2024 Republican primary. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should be focused frankly on fried pork chops in Iowa and glad-handing in New Hampshire. Instead, the already-indicted ex-president sure seems like he`s panicking about the potential for more criminal charges. Either that or his caps lock key is pretty broken.\par \par \uc2 This afternoon he went on social media posting in part, "How can DOJ possibly charge me who did nothing wrong? The greatest Witch Hunt of all time." Totally innocent there. I mean, that`s the vibe. Nothing to see here. With potential charges in the documents case coming as soon as this week, plus, a third possible indictment from Georgia prosecutor \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  later this summer, and the looming special counsel investigation into January 6 and the big lie -- I almost don`t have enough breath for this -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to have a lot to post in all caps about. So, stay tuned for that.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:21]\par \par \uc2 Devlin Barrett is a reporter for the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  where he has closely followed the classified documents investigation. His latest piece is titled, Trump Lawyers Ask Justice Department Not To Charge Trump in Classified Docs Case. And he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So, Devlin, you`ve been following this case really closely. What more do we know about today`s meeting?\par \par \uc2 DEVLIN BARRETT, REPORTER, WASHINGTON POST: So, we know there were two people on the Justice Department`s side at the meeting, one being Jack Smith, the special counsel, which makes sense, the other being a career senior official. And I think it`s important to understand some of the particular mechanics of a special counsel to know why that matters.\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Garland can only alter Jack Smith`s decisions if he finds that Jack Smith has done things that are not in keeping with Justice Department practice. That`s a high bar. It`s not simply that Attorney General Garland has to say I approve or disapprove. If he`s going to disapprove of whatever decision Jack Smith makes, he has to do so because he has found Jack Smith has done something far outside the Norms of the Justice Department. So, that`s a high bar.\par \par \uc2 But you can see how Trump`s lawyers are trying to get there by that letter you mentioned because they`re trying to make the argument this is not how this is supposed to work.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, Devlin, I mean, I just want to jump in there because how -- do you have any knowledge or anything from your sources on how their pitch was received. We saw them outline it in that letter a couple of weeks ago. Do you know anything about how they received it today?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: So, a couple of things. One, these pitches happen as you said pretty often in high-profile cases, they often don`t work though. Like, when you`re at this stage of the process, a prosecutor who knows all the nuts and bolts of a case is working toward a decision. And what you`re trying to do is get the boss to come in and say no, no, no, wait, everybody stop. That does not often work at the Justice Department. Not -- I wouldn`t say never but it`s rare.\par \par \uc2 And my understanding is from sources at this point that this meeting also did not go particularly well for the Trump lawyers.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So., what do you -- what does it tell you this meeting today, obviously the reports of the grand jury meeting again, about the potential timeline for an indictment?\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: So, I still and -- I don`t like making predictions about the future. I think for one thing, prosecutors are --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Come on, Devlin.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Prosecutors are so notorious for playing with their food. I don`t -- I mean, you can list off any number of cases in which they took months and months and months to finally decide what to do. I don`t think we`ll ultimately see months and months but I don`t -- I don`t know that we have much reason to say -- to you to use words like imminent. I just think we know that this type of meeting happens at the end of an investigation.\par \par \uc2 And so, because we saw this meeting today, because we know that, you know, they have made an argument for, you know, please don`t charge our guy, and that argument we are told fell flat --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: You know, we have some work to do.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, Devlin, lots to watch, lots to read in your reporting. Thank you so much for joining me this evening.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by my colleague Ari Melber, MSNBC Chief Legal Correspondent and the host of "THE BEAT." So, Ari, you have definitely been spending a lot of time focused on these cases and watching them. What`s your take on this meeting that happened today between Trump`s lawyers and the Special Counsel?\par \par \uc2 ARI MELBER, MSNBC CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Jen, number one, Trump lost his request, an effort to get a meeting with Attorney General Garland, as you alluded to.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Number two, the Trump --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, what`s the significance of that, that Trump didn`t get that meeting?\par \par \uc2 MELBER: I just think it shows that he`s out of power and running out of options. You know as well as anyone that in politics you do project power and he looks at this as a public fight. So, asking for something in public and not getting it isn`t good. Number two, I think we are seeing that the Trump team on their side is very concerned that the DOJ is approaching a possible indictment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes, I mean, in their letter to Mayor Garland a couple of weeks ago, his -- Trump`s lawyers accused the special counsel of prosecutorial misconduct. So, for us non-lawyers, what does that even mean and what kind of legal case are they going to try to make along those lines?\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Defense lawyers often try to raise objections to even good evidence. So, you could say this evidence might be true but it was obtained illegally, right? We hear debates about searches. Prosecutorial misconduct could be doing things with the prosecutors themselves are breaking or bending the rules or it could be selective prosecution, an argument we`ve heard other Trump lawyers make in the separate New York case. They argue that Trump`s fame, his political standing is why he`s being targeted.\par \par \uc2 The problem in both cases would be you can`t just say that. You need actual evidence of bias. If you have a smoking gun email where some prosecutor says I`m only doing this to affect the election or some such imagined smoking gun, sure. But just saying it doesn`t get you anywhere.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:20]\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, one of the other arguments that`s really stood out to me that they`ve made, the Trump lawyers, is that the White House does not handle classified secrets in the same way than as the rest of government, which is something they`ve claimed. That`s not my experience having worked in government, but what is the legal argument they`re trying to make there?\par \par \uc2 MELBER: Well, I think they can try to make a legal argument and we don`t know exactly what they said today, but you could imagine them saying this before and after a potential indictment that basically the president is fundamentally different. There is precedent of even very senior people in the military, in National Security getting in trouble for mishandling documents out of power, but that the president is somehow a different operator, he does have ultimate classification authority, and so that even an ex-president should be dealt with differently.\par \par \uc2 It`s not an argument supported by a lot of precedent, Jen, but it`s not one that we can dismiss completely out of hand for two reasons. One, because there`s no precedent, it might be viewed favorably in court. We don`t know. And two, if this case goes to a jury -- if you say for example to a jury, well, he was the president so of course he can stage a coup. I don`t think that goes very far with most jurors --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It probably doesn`t fly like most juries.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: -- voters, no. But you could imagine if getting one juror where you say well, he was the president. If he had this authority on the 19th, and let`s say for the sake of argument he forgot or did not use it. But if he had it on the 19th, is it really a crime that could keep you out of office or in jail on the21st? I wouldn`t call that a strictly legal argument, but I wouldn`t call it the -- I wouldn`t call it out of bounds for lawyers to try to make that to jurors. And I would say in some military cases, we`ve seen that. It`s not technically a legal defense but they basically say, hey, give this war hero a break. And I say that not implying that any of the potential defendants we`re discussing are war heroes.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: There`s a lot in the timeline that would have to work out there. But CNN is also reporting today that prosecutors are investigating a flood -- this sounds very shady to me -- at Mar-A-Lago last year that impacted a room where computer servers containing surveillance video logs were kept. We don`t know much more than that but what are your thoughts on that reporting?\par \par \uc2 MELBER: I think it`s super shady. I think it is -- they use the word suspicious. It`s the kind of thing that prosecutors are going to look very closely at. If you did stage any activity, moving stuff around, lying, or an actual destruction of evidence or flood, you would be on the hook for a crime. So, unless you think that you either think it happened totally coincidentally, possible, or if you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did it all by himself with the hoses, possible. Or more likely, if it was nefarious, that some other individuals were involved.\par \par \uc2 This is why conspiracies unravel often when you have tough Federal prosecutors because people get questioned about it. There`s other evidence and if anyone was involved in it, they may ultimately -- and this may be why we`re learning about it, not want to own it. So, there are people, Jen, who believe that that floods can be an act of nature or of God, but if this was something where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  thought he could just clear the deck on a great flood, I`m not sure that that will also work out.\par \par \uc2 And again, Jack Smith -- you were just discussing with Devlin, Jack Smith and Garland will come together on any final decision.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: The more there`s lying and obstruction, the more pressure that puts to indict rather than to let it go.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Before I let you go, Ari, just the reconvening of the grand jury this week. Do you think that means that an indictment is imminent? And does that mean days, weeks? What`s your assessment?\par \par \uc2 MELBER: I don`t think we know. Everyone has cautiously said it looks more like an ending than the beginning. It`s big news that we start the week with the first-ever meeting we know of between Special Counsel Smith and the lawyers. But weather that to any other new leads, process, double checking, that`s a thing that could add time, this meeting or whatever the grand jury does this week or not.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, we are more on the eighth or the ninth in the first inning, but whether we`re near the end or whether it goes into extra innings, we don`t know.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Ari Melber, this is why I watch you every day in my office at 6:00 p.m. Thank you so much for joining me tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, all signs point to another Trump indictment. But how concerned should we be about what`s in those documents and who he might have showed them to? A former CIA director weighs in on that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: As all evidence points to Special Counsel Jack Smith wrapping up his investigation into Donald Trump`s handling of classified documents, there still remained the pressing concern about the contents of those classified documents and Trump`s motive for keeping them. But we learned last week that Federal prosecutors have obtained an audio recording from July of 2021 of Trump discussing a sensitive Pentagon document, discussing military options for attacking Iran.\par \par \uc2 What is also interesting to me and should be alarming to everyone is why on Earth Trump held on to that document in particular about Iran and what he wanted to do with it? There are a number of possibilities, and we don`t know the answer at this point. It could be that Trump wanted to get back at the chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  who Trump was reportedly infuriated with after reports leaked that Milley tried to desperately stop Trump from attacking Iran. It could have been that Trump wanted to show off to wealthy donors. Certainly possible. That sounds very, very Trumpian.\par \par \uc2 There are also far more sinister explanations that I think it`s vital that we explore. One possibility and one that is quite concerning is that Trump could have wanted to share this with foreign adversaries or foreign officials. Many who would love to know what the range of options are that the United States has for military action against Iran.\par \par \uc2 One country that comes to mind -- not the only one, but one that comes to mind, is Saudi Arabia whose leaders have great interest in any Pentagon plans to attack Iran. And yes, Saudi Arabia is the same country where Trump has been strikingly -- striking strange and extremely lucrative golf tournament deals. Maybe acquaintance coincidence, maybe not. And those all benefit his businesses. These are extremely sensitive issues with immense national security implications.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:52]\par \par \uc2 John Brennan serves as the director of the CIA under President Obama, and he joins me now. So, let me start with -- we don`t actually know what his motive was and it`s not a factor in the legal component of this, but it`s important for people to understand what`s at stake here. So, having worked in the National Security world for many decades, what are the range of options of what a document like this could actually be?\par \par \uc2 JOHN BRENNAN, FORMER DIRECTOR, CIA: Well, Jen, it can include a lot of sensitive -- very sensitive information obviously about U.S. military capabilities, the sequence of actions that we might undertake if there was going to be some type of action against Iran.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Like the military actions, the range of military reactions?\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Military actions --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: -- as well as the different types of engagements that we might want to have with partners and allies in support of any type of activity against Iran. And so, there are a range of things that I would reveal again some very sensitive information about our ability to carry out a military operation with the success that we certainly want. And so, therefore, sharing it with others it just exposes and reveals again these particular dependencies that we have for a military operation like that.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Now, I`m certainly not going to ask you to get into Trump`s mind, but why would he, knowing the geopolitics, want to keep this document or documents around Iran in particular and potentially share it?\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Well, that`s the big question. You know, unless it`s some type of strange fetish that he just likes to keep these documents close to him --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Possible.\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: -- what type of -- what type of leverage might he try to use with them. You point out before, there are some foreign countries that would be very interested in what our military plans are for some type of military action against Iran. You mentioned Saudi Arabia. Certainly, Gulf Arab states would be interested. But if you note, the very increasing close relationship between Russia and Iran including on the military cooperation front, and so, might in fact Russia be very interested in something like that. The same thing with China. China and Iran have very close relations as well. Defense contractors, others who in fact would find some very valuable nuggets in there about how they should be thinking about those war plans and how they could capitalize and exploit knowledge of them.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, you mentioned Russia which I have not heard speculated about before, so I want to ask you more about that. What would President Putin be looking for in these documents. You mentioned cooperation. Tell me a little bit more about that.\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Well, we`ve seen that Russia and Iran have cooperated on drones and also in military production and other types of things. And I`m sure that Iran would love to be able to have some insight about what U.S. plans are for any type of military action. And the problem is if Trump did share this information with somebody, foreign officials or others, you don`t control the information. Who knows how it might in fact find its way to other quarters and find its way into places like Russia or China or other places.\par \par \uc2 And so, therefore, again, it`s very valuable, very extensive information which is why the United States keeps such close hold over it. And as you showed the photo before of those documents, they -- those cover sheets show the most sensitive code words and the highest classifications that the \uc2 US government\uc2  has. And top-secret documents by definition would cause exceptionally grave damage to U.S. national security if that information is exposed, which is why I think the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is taking this case so very seriously and pursuing it to make sure that we understand exactly not only who to hold accountable for this unlawful retention and handling of information, but also what the possible damage is if indeed this information was shared beyond Mar-A-Lago.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, we don`t know what we don`t know, which is always true, but certainly true in this case. We know there`s been reporting about him -- Former President Trump showing a map. We know there`s been reporting about nuclear documents and obviously this one about Iran and Military potential steps. What of what you`ve heard so far is most concerning in terms of the type of damage it could do to our national security?\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Well, again, I think it`s the cavalier, you know, sharing of this information. He clearly knows this is highly classified information, but the potential exposure a very sensitive technical collection capabilities, the human source networks, lives on the line that could in fact be compromised as a result of this exposure. So, in addition to the military plans that might have in fact been shared, it`s those sensitive intelligence capabilities, human, technical, and so on, that we really rely on in order to keep our country secure. And so, sharing it with individuals -- again, who knows where it might have gone beyond any single individual that he might have shared it with.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:38]\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I understand that the extremely sensitive documents like this can`t like just be seen in the public if an indictment is brought. What role and help us understand what role the Intel Community plays in any potential decisions by the Justice Department? Meaning, could you -- could the Intel Community hold back documents that they don`t want a court to see. And how do you think that could factor in this case?\par \par \uc2 BRENNAN: Yes, it`s an important point because the documents with the testimony would need to presented -- be presented as evidence in a court of law. And there have been procedures in place for many years under what`s called the classified information procedures act that allow the Intelligence Community to share with the court, with a judge what`s called in camera in chambers to protect it and prevent it from being publicly exposed. Because what you don`t want to do is declassify the information. That would undercut the government`s argument that this in fact is very sensitive information.\par \par \uc2 So again, these modalities and mechanisms are in place that will allow the government, the Intelligence Community, to share documents that will not be publicly released. Maybe they`ll be redacted, maybe summaries of some of these documents, but they will petition the court to allow for maybe some subsets of this documents in whole and in part to be presented as evidence so that there can in fact be a fair trial if in fact we are going to see the indictment.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, John Brennan, I was already alarmed. You`ve added to my alarm in a very helpful way. Thank you so much for joining me tonight.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, there`s a spin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LAUREN BOEBERT\uc2  (R-CO): Hey, everyone, I am back in Colorado, but let`s talk about D.C. No excuses, I was ticked off. They wouldn`t let me do my job. So, I didn`t take the vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: And then there`s the tape. Congresswoman \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  is caught in a big old fib coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, last week, the bipartisan deal to avoid defaulting on our national debt passed with overwhelming majorities in Congress, over the objections of mainly a group of far-right Republicans. One of those outspoken critics was Congressman \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  of Colorado, no surprise, who repeatedly promised to vote against the bill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOEBERT: The bill Biden negotiated fuels inflation, adds trillions to our debt, and leaves a blank check for more of the same nonsense I came here to stop. I am not for an unlimited debt ceiling increase. As presented today, I`m voting no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: You heard her. She`s pretty intense there. I`m voting no. So, it seemed a bit strange when \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  just didn`t show up at all to vote on Wednesday. Over the weekend, the Congresswoman posted a video claiming she skipped the vote in protest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOEBERT: Hey, everyone. I am back in Colorado, but let`s talk about D.C. No excuses. I was ticked off. They wouldn`t let me do my job. So, I didn`t take the vote. Once again, Washington`s power machine shoved a multi- trillion-dollar bill down our throat, refused to allow debate or amendments, disregarded everything we fought for in January to actually allow representatives to do their jobs. and instead, they served us up a crap sandwich.\par \par \uc2 Call it a no-show protest, but I certainly let every one of my colleagues and the country know I was against this garbage of a bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: A no-show protest to a crap sandwich. It`s quite a phrase, according to the Congresswoman. But as it turns out, that doesn`t seem to be actually what happened. A Capitol Hill producer caught \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  on tape frantically running into the building Wednesday evening too late to vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN RIMMER, CNN PRODUCER: They closed it.\par \par \uc2 BOEBERT: They closed it?\par \par \uc2 RIMMER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I can`t stop watching that. And Boebert actually admitted that she was late and missed the vote on the congressional record, saying that she was unavoidably detained. And if she, "had been present, she would have voted nay." So, it`s sort of like saying, if I hadn`t slept through my alarm, I swear I would have aced that test. It`s almost as if she didn`t think anyone would check, no one was tracking. I guess it`s not a huge surprise, given \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  has a pretty loose relationship with facts.\par \par \uc2 And I can tell you, she wouldn`t be the first on President Biden`s call sheet of Republicans. But there are lots of Republicans that this president can find common cause with, showing his willingness to extend olive branches across the aisle. It`s basically part of what got him elected in 2020. It was part of his strategy and a big part of the strategy going into 2024. Much more of that coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:35:00]\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I left the Biden administration just over a year ago. And in my time working for the president as his White House Press Secretary, I saw firsthand just how he thinks about his role and how he thinks about Republicans. And as he looks ahead to 2024, he is reaching back to the success of his 2020 bid and the messaging around that, campaigning against Trumpism without totally alienating all Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The idea in part is for the President to convince people who may not be Democrats to come over and support him too, if they can`t stomach Trump. And it`s not just politics. That`s also who he is. When I worked for him, he would regularly pick up the phone and call Republicans without much fanfare, without announcing it. He`s kind of a bridge builder by nature. The open question is if that strategy will pay off in 2024.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:09]\par \par \uc2 My next guest are people who I`ve spent a lot of time with in their offices, on the phone, texting them about strategy, so I`m so grateful for them for being here tonight. \uc2 Kate Bedingfield\uc2  is the Former White House Communications Director under President Biden. We worked very closely together. Robert Gibbs served as the White House Press Secretary for President Obama. Also, I`ve worked very closely with him.\par \par \uc2 I want to start just with this overarching question, Kate, for you, because you worked for the president for so long. You know him very well. I think his overarching view is that there`s a real appetite out there in the country for someone who can find common ground, even with members of the modern-day Republican Party. That promise was, of course, a big pitch in 2020. How central, Kate, do you think that is going to be to the reelection campaign and the messaging of that as we look to 2024?\par \par \uc2 KATE BEDINGFIELD\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Well, first of all, thanks for having me, Jen. This is a treat. But, yes, I think that`s going to be a critical piece of the messaging in 2024, because for two reasons. One, because, as you said, it is fundamentally what President Biden believes. It is how he has long-navigated Washington, frankly, successfully. And he fundamentally believes that leaders should be able to find common cause when they can.\par \par \uc2 And I think, secondly, it will be a huge piece of the messaging because we should do away with this notion that it`s an either-or dichotomy, that you either have to fire up the base or you can reach out across the aisle. Remember, Democratic voters are people who also want to see progress. They want to see things get done. And so, what President Biden`s been able to do in the first few years of his presidency is notch wins where Republicans wouldn`t come along. Things that, you know, they couldn`t get them to do, he went and did them himself, the Inflation Reduction Act, the biggest investment in tackling climate change in a generation. He did that without Republican votes.\par \par \uc2 But he was able to get Republican votes for a gun safety bill, able to get meaningful things done with Republicans. And for him, that`s fundamental. And I think that is what voters want to see. And you can bet that that will be a central piece of his pitch for 2024.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, you know, obviously it`s going to be a part of his pitch. Kate just confirmed that for us. Gibbs, you`ve done a lot of campaigns. You didn`t work on the Biden campaign, but in this age of Trump, do you think that message in this environment is going to work if it`s central to the President`s reelection campaign?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I think it won`t only work, I think it`s what the American people yearn for. And I don`t think it`s a glowy, romanticized version of it, they just know that in an argument where two people are having that argument, that the likely answer lies somewhere in the middle. And I totally agree with Kate. I think people come to Washington to get things done, whether you`re the most junior member of Congress or the most senior member of the Senate, or the President of the United States, you want to see progress on things.\par \par \uc2 And the American people are sophisticated enough to know that the only way they`re going to see progress is if both sides work together. And they spent four years watching somebody foment division sometimes and almost always unnecessarily. Biden pitched himself as the competent grown up in the room that would wipe away the chaos and could bring Washington together. I love the line he`s been using. And I`m sure he`s been using it for a while that he`s, you know, I was told this bipartisanship was dead.\par \par \uc2 I think that is a great way to pitch it. I think his accomplishments show it. And I have no doubt we`re going to hear a lot about it over the course of the next two years.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, one of the big elephants in the room here -- see what I did there -- is all of these legal woes and troubles for Trump. I mean, he`s already been indicted. He could be indicted in a federal court soon. The President has not spoken about it. He is an observer of the tradition that you should not comment on an ongoing investigation. Do you think, Kate, that continues to be the strategy moving forward as these cases become not about hush money to an adult film star, but about democracy and about national security and rule of law?\par \par \uc2 BEDINGFIELD: Well, I think President Biden has never hesitated to call \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out for the threat that he believes that he poses to democracy. So, you know, I would say that there`s no question that President Biden is going to always -- he`s never going to shy away from calling out what he sees as a really, frankly, an existential threat to our country. However, he`s also going to continue to focus on what he`s gotten done on the case that he has made for bringing back manufacturing jobs, for, you know, bringing semiconductor chips back to this country, making sure we`re having our supply chains here in the United States were not dependent on countries overseas.\par \par \uc2 He`s doing things that are making a difference for the economy. He`s doing things that are tackling, as I said earlier, our climate crisis, the gun crisis. He`s fighting to protect our rights. He`s fighting to protect the right of women to have access to abortion, to reproductive health. So, I think what you`re going to see, I would imagine, what you will see from the President is you will continue to see him make the case for the things he`s done, the promises he`s kept, frankly, from the 2020 campaign. And -- but you will also see him never shy away from calling out the threat that he believes that Trump and MAGA-ism posed to this country.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:15]\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I ask this because I think it`s a really tricky balance, right? And most Democratic candidates, most candidates don`t have to run against Trump. So, Gibbs, what do you think about whether the President should weigh in more on the broad messaging of these cases or these cases in general?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: You know, I think the tact he`s taken so far, I think, is the right one. Look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  guarantees every day that the news about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to get hefty placement in our news diet. There`s not really anything that the President needs to do to push that case forward. I think it`s also going to be interesting to watch when or if the Republicans running against Trump begin to try to shout out a difference between him and an indictment, whether it`s in New York or whether it could be coming in diamonds.\par \par \uc2 I think the president is best suited, as Kate described, to make the case that really works so well in 2022, which is, are you in the mainstream or are you in the extreme? And I think what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , what Republicans are, whether it`s -- whether it`s on guns, whether it`s on abortion, whether it`s on health care costs, whether it`s on the health care costs, I think they`re a party that seems to be a step outside of the mainstream, particularly when it comes to swing voters.\par \par \uc2 That`s the contrast I think he draws the most blood from. I think it`s the one he`s best suited given his political leanings for the course of his career. I think those are the contrast that you`ll see him make the most.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, Kate, I`m a believer that the President is often underestimated. I know you are too from talking to you about it. There is one thing a lot of people are freaking out about in the Democratic Party, and that is enthusiasm among young people, among progressives. What is your take, or what would you tell them so that they don`t freak out about where this is headed and how the President is going to work to win those votes?\par \par \uc2 BEDINGFIELD: Well, first of all, it feels like sometimes there`s a cottage industry in professional Democratic circles of one, both underestimating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but also wringing your hands and panicking and, you know, frantic. We spent as much energy tackling the problems as we did, you know, telling reporters we were worried the narratives might be very different.\par \par \uc2 But look, I think ultimately what the president is going to do is going to continue to focus on the case he`s made. And I think it`s important to remember elections don`t happen in a vacuum. Elections are about choice and about contrast. And, you know, the President frequently says, you know, don`t compare me to the Almighty. Compare me to the alternative.\par \par \uc2 And so, as the campaign goes on, and you continue to see this contrast between the extremism of Trump and the MAGA Republicans, and this progress that Joe Biden`s offering, I think people will, at the end of the day, they`re going to pull that lever for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: \uc2 Kate Bedingfield\uc2  and Robert Gibbs, two of the smartest people I know in Democratic politics and communications, thank you so much for joining me tonight.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, if you are a Republican governor who wants to stop \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from becoming president again, is the best option not to run against him? New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  seems to think so, and he joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: The Republican primary field for 2024 is getting more crowded by the day. You may have noticed that with \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , and North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  all expected to enter the race this week. But today, one other Republican who was considered a possible candidate announced that he`s not running.\par \par \uc2 New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , a moderate, self-described pro-choice Republican who handedly won reelection to a fourth term last year writes in a \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  op-ed that "our party is in a collision course toward electoral irrelevance without significant corrective action." And that "the stakes are too high for a crowded field to hand the nomination to a candidate who earns just 35 percent of the vote. And I will help ensure this does not happen."\par \par \uc2 Strong words there. We all know which Republican primary candidate he means. I think we know. And Governor Sununu joins me now.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s start with an announcement that we`re -- you`re the big news today, but let`s start with an announcement that we`re going to see in New Hampshire tomorrow. Former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  is going to make his run official in your home state. Neither of you hold back in criticizing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You have that in common. Do you think he has a path to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the primary?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (R-NH): Oh, sure. Look, I think almost all the candidates have a very viable path to beating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And you know, you just said it right there. Chris isn`t shy about, you know, casting criticism where it is well deserved with the former president. But this is the problem.\par \par \uc2 All 12 of the other candidates need to cast the same type of criticism because it`s not that we`re there out there to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t represent the Republican Party, right? He`s the outsider. And we`ve kind of let him get away with kind of co-opting I think what are better traditional ideals of the Republican Party of limited government and local control. He wants to re-litigate the past. And if we`re going to be successful in the Republican Party, we`re going to move forward in the future.\par \par \uc2 So, I think Christie is a very good candidate. I think they`re all really good candidates. They`re all my friends. I think there`s a lot to play out. We haven`t even had a debate yet. So, we`re going to see who can take a punch, who can give a punch, who can really stand on that stage and make the case of America`s future not just worrying about litigating the past.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:10]\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, we know Governor Christie can give a punch. We don`t know what his path is yet. We`ll see. But I do want to give you an opportunity to make a little news. So, are you going to endorse Governor Christie tomorrow when he`s in your home state?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No. Look, I love Chris. He`s one of my better friends, really. But no, I may endorse somebody at some point, but that`ll be down the road. They got to earn it, right? They got to be in the living rooms of New Hampshire. They got to go to the 99 counties in Iowa. They got to go to the fairs. They got to do the retail politics because that proves a sense of good retail management. And that`s exactly what this country is looking for, good retail managers that don`t just talk about their issues, but that can listen and talk to the issues that are actually resonating with the -- with the constituents.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the real difference. It`s not about money or headlines or any of that. It`s about really connecting with people.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, as you`re watching this, you said very clearly that if a candidate isn`t breaking through, they need to drop out by Christmas. I`ve been in politics a while. Candidates don`t usually like to be told when to drop out. But are you going to be calling out specific candidates in public when you think it`s time for them to go? If not publicly, are you going to be having these conversations privately?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: What can we expect?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No, look, it`s not just me. I think there`ll be a lot of voices calling out candidates. They got plenty of time, though. We have all fall to prove themselves. And anyone who wants to get in can get in. But it`s not just the Chris Sununus of the world. I think there`ll be a lot of voices. They`re going to be telling folks, hey, you gave it a shot. It`s not there. The donor base, the donor base that has given money to these candidates have to be the first ones to say, I know I gave you a lot of money. It`s not happening. Pull out. Let`s narrow this field down.So, it really has to be a resonating message so we don`t have a repeat of `16.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It looks like, according to a lot of legal eagles, we could see another indictment for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  soon. Now, we know the hush money indictment didn`t seem to change the dynamic. If anything, it seemed to help him in the polls. Do you think any indictment, any indictments on issues around --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: -- other indictments could help other candidates or would hurt Trump?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: No. You don`t think the indictments matter?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No. No.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I don`t want to put words in your mouth. Tell me what you think.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Obviously they don`t.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: They don`t?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No. They absolutely don`t matter. No, these indictments, and if I make a combination of the media, you`re creating the boy who cried wolf syndrome, right? What happened in New York was wrong. I mean, it really was. They are beating up on Trump for political reasons. Everybody sees that. What happened with the Russian collusion that never existed effectively exonerated the guy. So, he can play a victim card.\par \par \uc2 You`ve all created a situation where God -- what kind of planet are we on where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has become the victim? That`s really what it is.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, first of all, I think -- I think we`ve --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: And so, nobody believes it. Nobody believes it.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: No, we know -- I have to stop you on there, because we know very clearly that Putin and the Kremlin did intervene in our election in order to try to help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  back in 2016. So, we know that is true. But you may think that they shouldn`t engage in this as a messaging tool, because it`s not helpful to them. Is that what you`re saying?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes. I`m not going to re-relitigate the Russian collusion nonsense that went on for two years where everyone said that it was --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: You don`t need to re-relitigate. It`s been litigated by lawyers and investigations.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes, yes, it`s nonsense. But now, nobody buys -- nobody --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: That`s arguably not true.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes, but nobody buys that any attack on Trump isn`t anything but political. You`ve created -- everyone has kind of created this scenario. And he is playing it to the fullest. He is playing the violin strings better than anyone imagine, which is why his poll numbers miraculously are actually going up. So, these candidates are going to have to get on the stage, talk about what they`re going to do going forward. Every one of them has to call them out.\par \par \uc2 If you`re not calling out \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for the non-Republican that he really is, for the mistakes he made in the past, then you don`t deserve to be on the stage. We`re not here to run for vice president. We`re not here to sell books. If you want to be president, then you better stand up and give it everything you got. And those candidates that don`t do that aren`t going to go very far. They`re going to be driven out of the race. And we`re going to get down to one or two candidates because one-on-one, Trump and somebody else, he loses. He loses the nomination. And then the Republicans actually have a shot to win in `24. I think almost anybody would be \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  right now in `24 except \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, I want to ask you about abortion. We`ve talked about this a little bit before. The range of candidates in the field have a lot of different positions. You`re a pro-choice governor. What do you think the position of the party should be and how should these candidates be talking about it on the trail?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Look, I think it`s really simple. Dobbs now says that the states can make their decisions and they will. And everyone has to understand that California and Mississippi are going to have very different paths on abortion. And that`s now up to the voters to decide whether they want their legislatures and those governors to make those decisions. And so there`s more voter connection there.\par \par \uc2 That`s the new reality. So, anything that the Republicans are talking about in terms of national abortion bans and all that, it becomes a very scary message that can`t be explained, and you drive independence in young voters away. And that`s exactly the opposite of what we should be doing.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to bring them back on to the team. We`ve got to make them feel empowered, get them excited about something, not confuse them with national abortion ban message. It just -- it isn`t what carries the day. We need to talk about inflation and energy independence and healthcare reform, border security. These are the things that really matter at a national level and can be fixed. And if we`re talking about abortion, we`re getting distracted and probably losing voters in the process.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , thank you so much for joining me this evening. You got a big day.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Monday night. Chris Hayes will be back here tomorrow night. And I`ll be back on MSNBC this Sunday at noon Eastern for my show "INSIDE WITH \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 ." Plus, catch new original episodes every Thursday exclusively on Peacock. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230606ej6500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD for May 25, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell; Ryan Reilly \par \uc2 7,126 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:21]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Hi, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 I was just listening to every word that you and Senator McCaskill had to say because I`m sitting here kind of -- kind of delighted so far on what is being reported in "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " because on Tuesday night here, I -- I got to the point where no one had any idea what was happening behind closed doors in the debt ceiling negotiations. So I offered a wild guess, right, because that`s all anyone was doing.\par \par \uc2 And it`s turning out to be pretty much based on pure guesswork I came up with Tuesday night and so I`m hoping this thing kind of stays in this direction. So --\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: You -- you say it`s pure guesswork, but that`s also someone who is a veteran of Capitol Hill and has seen negotiations and knows what they look like, and also understands the stakes, Lawrence, right? Because that`s the most important thing here, not just the political stakes, but what it would mean for the U.S. economy if we were to default.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNEL: Uh-huh. And so, we have Congressman \uc2 Jim McGovern\uc2 , the former chairman of the \uc2 House Rules Committee\uc2 , the all-important \uc2 House Rules Committee\uc2 , now the top Democrat on the committee, joining us tonight. And we`ll get the update from him as best he can reveal it at this point of exactly what the state of play is on the debt ceiling, above which I am even more excited than I was Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I`m so glad that your predictions are coming true.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, very bad news for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Washington today, with "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporting that special prosecutor Jack Smith has obtained previously unrevealed evidence, publicly anyway, of possible obstruction of justice in Donald Trump`s hoarding of government documents at his Florida home, including what prosecutors have called a dress rehearsal for the possible crime of obstruction of justice.\par \par \uc2 Also in Washington today, in federal court, the longest sentence for any convicted defendant in the January 6th attack on the capital was given to the man who believed he was acting as Donald Trump`s fields general in the attack on the Capitol. Elmer Stewart Rhodes, who will be 78 years old the next time he is free to attack the Capitol during a January 6th certification of the Electoral College vote. He was hit with an 18-year sentence for seditious conspiracy which means that the next presidential election when he will be a free man is the 2044 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 So on January 6, 2045, when Elmer Rhodes is 78 years old, look for him with a bull horn on the Capitol grounds, if one of Donald Trump`s children is not elected president that year.\par \par \uc2 If special prosecutor Jack Smith brings charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for seditious conspiracy, what does today`s sentence mean for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he is convicted on the same charge that Elmer Rhodes just got 18 years for?\par \par \uc2 Elmer Rhodes was convicted for essentially being the Trump field general on January 6, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the commander-in-chief of the attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 In court today before he was sentenced, Elmer Rhodes who hates his first name so much, he has tried to obliterate it from public use, squeezed in an endorsement in the current presidential campaign saying: I hope Trump wins in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Elmer Stewart Rhodes would be the first in line for one of the pardons that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has publicly promised to convicted January 6 defendants. And if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is sworn in as president on January 20th, 2025, by that time, the first order of business might be pardoning himself in multiple federal cases that could be brought by special prosecutor Jack Smith.\par \par \uc2 Of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will never be able to pardon himself for any crimes that he might be convicted of in the state of Georgia where Fulton County District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  has indicated that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to be indicted there, it will likely occur in the first two weeks of August.\par \par \uc2 We are at the "according to people familiar with the matter" stage of special prosecutor Jack Smith`s investigation.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:05]\par \par \uc2 That is the phrase used to describe the sources of today`s "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporting about new evidence of obstruction of justice and the identical phrase is used by "\uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 " in their report today saying, quote, special counsel Jack Smith is wrapping up his investigation into former President Donald Trump`s refusal to return classified documents after his election defeat and is poised to announce possible criminal charges in the days or weeks after Memorial Day.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is reporting that boxes were moved at Mar-a-Lago the day before Justice Department officials made their first visit to Donald Trump`s home to obtain documents. That visit was agreeably arranged with Donald Trump`s lawyers ahead of time. No search warrant was involved, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew well in advance exactly when the Justice Department officials would arrive.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is reporting, two of Donald Trump`s employees moved boxes of papers the day before \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents and a prosecutor visited the former president`s Florida home to retrieve classified documents in response to a subpoena, timing that investigators have come to view as suspicious, and an indication of possible obstruction, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Trump and his aides also allegedly carried out a dress rehearsal for moving sensitive papers even before his office received the May 2022 subpoena, according to people familiar with the matter who spoke on the condition of anonymity to describe a sensitive going investigation. Prosecutors in addition have gathered evidence indicating that Trump at times kept classified documents in his office in a place where they were visible and sometimes showed them to others these people said.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is famously way too impatient to rehearse things like speeches, but the evidence seems to indicate that he was so determined to commit the crime of obstruction of justice that he personally directed a dress rehearsal of moving government documents illegally in his possession before the \uc2 FBI\uc2  might show up looking for them. "The Post" reports there is, quote, evidence showing that boxes of documents were moved to a storage area on June 2nd just before senior Justice Department lawyer Jay Bratt arrived at Mar-a-Lago with agents. The June 3rd visit by law enforcement officials was to collect material in response to the May 2022 grand jury subpoena, demanding the return of all documents with classified markings.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors also have gathered evidence that even before Trump`s office received the subpoena in May, he had what some officials have dubbed a dress rehearsal for moving government documents that he did not want to relinquish, people familiar with the investigation said. The term "dress rehearsal" was used in a sealed judicial opinion issued earlier this year in one of several legal battles over the government`s access to particular witnesses and evidence, some of the people said.\par \par \uc2 The dress rehearsal could be extremely damning evidence of obstruction of justice which carries a maximum sentence of 20 years in prison. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " also reports prosecutors separately have been told by more than one witness that Trump at times kept classified documents out in the open in his Florida office where others could see them people familiar with the matter said and sometimes showed them to people, including aides and visitors.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAITLAN COLLINS, CNN MODERATOR: Did you ever show those classified documents to anyone?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Not really, I would have the right to. By the way, they were declassified after --\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: What do you mean not really?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Not that I can think of. Let me just tell you, I have the absolute right to do whatever I want with them. I have the right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Leading off our discussions tonight is Harry Litman, former U.S. attorney and former deputy assistant attorney general. He is a senior legal affairs analyst for "The L.A. Times".\par \par \uc2 And Bradley Moss is with us, a national security attorney and former clerk for the National Security Archive.\par \par \uc2 And, Harry Litman, there is so much here. We have to begin with the dress rehearsal. As a former federal prosecutor, when you were looking for obstruction of justice, how good would you feel if you found a dress rehearsal?\par \par \uc2 [22:10:03]\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Really good, but I`m already feeling very good and I think Jack Smith is feeling very good about obstruction. Now, what this does is make it even more severe and bring it earlier, so we had been considering the receipt of the subpoena as the moment that sobers him up and makes him know he has to comply. Now, in fact, he`s already plotting out his criminal non-compliance before he even receives it. That`s good stuff and it adds to what I think is already mountainous evidence.\par \par \uc2 For us, though, Lawrence, it`s new news. For Smith, it`s old news. And the other -- two other things really caught my attention in "The Post" article. First, the grand jury hasn`t met since May 5th, the longest hiatus since Smith was appointed. I think that`s behind these reports that maybe there, you know, Smith is readying charges.\par \par \uc2 Second, that point that you just raised a couple minutes ago that he may have showed them to others, that`s a whole new crime. That is Espionage Act, 18 USC 793, and if they have that, that would -- that would really add to the time and everything.\par \par \uc2 But I think there`s a real judgment call here, how much he wants to load up his case and complicate it if he already has the other two charges solid.\par \par \uc2 Finally, I just want to say on timing in Bloomberg, I think what they`re saying is, we`ve got on good authority the grand jury is about done. Why do we say days or weeks?\par \par \uc2 Remember, there`s a big process still going to happen with \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , it`ll be very deferential, and he will really -- I think -- I think there`s no chance that he won`t defer to Smith at the end of the day. On the other hand, I think he will take his time methodically go through everything.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why we don`t know exactly when even if the grand jury is about done, the actual indictment will come down.\par \par \uc2 And, Bradley Moss, to that point tonight about him showing these documents to other people, I can never forget the night we discovered that one of the class -- at least one of the classified documents that was found with the search warrant was found in Donald Trump`s office in his desk. And so, now, we have reporting tonight saying and apparently at times, they were just kind of laying on top of his desk, just out there, as exhibits that you could see walking in the room with them.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY MOSS, NATIONAL SECURITY ATTORNEY: Yeah, what a shock. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  likes to show off, he loves these mementos, and he had the ultimate mementos. He had classified records, the people had never seen him and say look what I have got, look what I learned while I was pre -- while I was president. He could show it off.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, to follow up on Harry`s point, it goes to this issue. There was always the idea of an Espionage Act charge in terms of willful retention of national defense information and not returning it. This would be a separate one of disseminating information. If he showed people and let them read from it, if he gave them a copy of it, anything like that, that being entirely separate charge.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know if it`s gone that far. We don`t know what Jack Smith has on that front, but it sounds at a minimum like he`s got him rock solid in terms of intent, in terms of knowledge, not just on obstruction but on these Espionage Act violations that if he brings indictments.\par \par \uc2 I still think it`s going to be the whole enchilada, not just obstruction.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Harry Litman, we saw Donald Trump`s lawyers do a classically \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lawyer kind of thing which is outrageously unprofessional. No one else would do it, publicly send a letter to \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  filled with crazy rhetoric about things like \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the bottom -- literally, the bottom line of the letter was, would you please have a meeting with us, so we can try to talk you out of prosecuting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We are apparently unlikely to have any public response from \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  in response to that, but assuming they`re -- let`s just assume for a moment there is something going on there, how -- how long would that kind of meeting be? How much time would that take up in this process if \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is thinking we should meet with the Trump lawyers?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Two hours, that is -- you know, they will hear them out politely, but they can`t take the whole day and typically when this happens.\par \par \uc2 First, it can happen with a U.S. attorney and Smith is functioning as a U.S. attorney, and they might come in with a PowerPoint, three hours, but it`s a very rare thing to get an audience with an attorney general, as you say, because they`ll want to really leave no avenue for charging. They didn`t give them full process. He might well do it or one of his aides, but that`s not the delay.\par \par \uc2 The delay is as Garland really. You know, as a judge would, as he did as a judge even if he was applying a deferential standard review, he will look through that record, talk to Smith about it, his deputy, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:07]\par \par \uc2 But the meeting with Trump is not a time consumer.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Bradley Moss, what should the Trump defense lawyers -- I shouldn`t -- let me put it another way. What would defense lawyers be doing in a case like this if their client was not just publicly insane, like the one they have?\par \par \uc2 MOSS: Well, one -- one is they would never have their client talking publicly about it like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who likes to incriminate himself. But no, kind of they would definitely have been asking for this kind of meeting that they asked for with \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . They would give that kind of presentation.\par \par \uc2 But they would have also a long time ago would have had a full drawn-out chronology for their own purposes and have us long sit down with their client, where did these documents show up? What did you tell people? Make sure you lock down what these witnesses would be saying so you understand it and so you can prepare a legitimate defense, not just if there`s an indictment, but realistically to try to persuade the Justice Department not to bring an indictment in the first place, to outline why the documents the markings were invalid or to outline why the obstruction was inadvertent. It doesn`t warrant charges.\par \par \uc2 There`s no indication this legal team, which was thrown together, you know, a bunch of hyenas nipping at each other`s heels, and now at least one`s already left and another one had to get pulled off the case. There`s no indication they`ve been able to do that. This is a political collaboration masquerading as a legal team, and if this goes to indictment, they`ve got real problems.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Harry Litman, plenty of detail on "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " about Jack Smith and his investigators questioning people working at Mar-a-Lago who just helped carry boxes. Apparently, at least one of them, just the worker there, walking by lending a hand.\par \par \uc2 That worker is represented by a counsel who was insisting to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " that all -- all he did was just see some people who needed help carrying boxes and lent to hand. But he -- but that person was repeatedly questioned and so -- according to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ", and being repeatedly questioned seems to -- especially something as simple as that, seems to indicate that they -- it`s it -- that they were discovering more about that movement of boxes that needed to create repeated questioning situations.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Yeah, they`re seeing it as critical and it`s understandable. Now, remember, there also was reports they`ve talked to every maintenance person et cetera. This is different. This guy whose name has not been identified is represented -- he was just passing by when Walt, his buddy, was putting boxes in there.\par \par \uc2 Both of them are now represented by counsel because that`s the very -- that`s part and parcel of the dress rehearsal you refer to where Trump is impresario, getting people to move things, putting boxes with documents in the storage room and the next day telling the visitors from DOJ, oh, by the way, you can`t search the boxes in the storage room. So that is really pretty incendiary evidence that he knew they were coming. He hid it from them, and he had some helpers, who I think now may testify just to the facts.\par \par \uc2 But that`s all you need, what a very -- an honest, unbiased witness to say yep, this is -- this is what Trump told me to do and this is where I put it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Harry Litman and Bradley Moss, thank you both very much for starting off our conversations tonight.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, there is breaking news tonight on the debt ceiling. We will be joined by the top Democrat on the committee that will send a debt ceiling bill to the floor of the House of Representatives. Congressman \uc2 Jim McGovern\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 And later, more tonight about the hardest decision the chief justice ever had to make.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JUSTICE JOHN ROBERTS, U.S. SUPREME COURT: The hardest decision I had to make was whether to erect fences and barricades around the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:23:37]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We have breaking debt ceiling news at this hour. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is reporting that a possible deal to raise the debt ceiling is taking shape pretty much along the lines that I presented to you here on Tuesday night as pure guesswork based on my own experience working on the Senate committee with jurisdiction over the debt ceiling back in the more enlightened era when raising the debt ceiling was not a controversial issue in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is reporting tonight that President Biden has negotiated a two-year extension of the debt ceiling which means the issue won`t come up again until after the next presidential election, and the president has also negotiated the basics of a budget agreement that will avoid the possibility of a Republican-driven government shutdown in September. Not everything in the deal has been agreed to yet.\par \par \uc2 One item reported by "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " is a reduction in the $80 billion President Biden has obtained an additional funding for the \uc2 IRS\uc2  that could be cut by billion dollars in the in the concessions to Republicans who are opposed to all of that funding, and that $10 billion taken out of \uc2 IRS\uc2  funding could then be spread to other non-military discretionary spending in the budget that the president wants to support.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:06]\par \par \uc2 Military spending would be unaffected by this potential deal. Kevin McCarthy told Republican members of the House today: I don`t think everybody is going to be happy at the end of the day.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jim McGovern\uc2  of Massachusetts. He`s the top-ranking Democrat on the all-important \uc2 House Rules Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressman McGovern, really thrilled to have you here, especially in this breaking news situation with the debt ceiling, which you are -- have been intimately involved with in the most elaborate parliamentary maneuver called the discharge petition, which looks like you`re not going to need it, depending on how these negotiations going.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us? What do you know about the state of the negotiations at this hour?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM MCGOVERN (D-MA): Well, I know about as much as you know right now and I hope we can avoid defaulting on our debt, which means we would not pay our bills, which we which would be ruinous for our economy. But I`m interested to see the details. You know, you know, what is the price that we are expected to pay to get this deal?\par \par \uc2 And as I`ve been saying for a while now -- I mean, what concerns me is that the Republicans, their priorities -- they`re not my priorities. Their values are not my values. I mean, they want to find savings by cutting programs to help poor people, to help people with childcare, to help people with food benefits, and they don`t want to touch defense spending, they don`t want to touch tax cuts for millionaires and billionaires, and they don`t want to touch subsidies for big oil and fossil fuels or big farmer.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we have to find out what the details are and again I trust, President Biden and I -- and I`m a big supporter. But for me, I`m going to have to see the details because, again, their priorities, the Republican priorities are not my priorities and I`m not going to vote for a bill that screws poor people and that`s what they`ve been pushing for from the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: There`s -- there are no details here on nutrition funding that I know is a major concern of yours, and that`s an area where Republicans have been talking about work requirements. There are some work requirements that already exist. What is your -- what are you hoping the president can achieve on that?\par \par \uc2 MCGOVERN: Yeah, I`ll hope president can hold harmless our nutrition programs and programs that help the most vulnerable in our country. What the Republicans keep on talking about is work requirements, work requirements. We have work requirements in place for programs like SNAP, and -- and the majority of people on the program actually work.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, the benefit is on average of about six dollars per day per person. That`s it, $2 on average per meal, for every person. That`s the benefit and the people who are not working right now who need the benefit are people who are homeless. Some of them are veterans, some of them are people with undiagnosed mental illnesses, kids graduate out of foster care.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we -- they really want to go after those people and throw them off of a of a food benefit? I mean, give me a goddamn break. I mean, I can`t quite understand what they`re thinking when they believe it is appropriate to try to balance the budget on the backs of those people, the vulnerable in our country, but we can`t touch tax cuts for billionaires, we can`t touch military spending.\par \par \uc2 I mean, last week on "60 Minutes", they had a former high-level Pentagon official say that the cost overruns are astounding. He talked about a -- the Pentagon paying $10,000 for a $300 oil switch.\par \par \uc2 You mean to tell me you can`t find one penny of savings in the Pentagon budget, that that`s somehow sacrosanct, off limits? We can`t go -- we can`t look there. But instead, we have to look at programs that help poor people?\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, I think it tells you all you need to know about who`s running the House of Representatives right now.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You have now all 213 Democrats signatures on the discharge petition that you are now in charge of. The discharge petition could be a way for Democrats to force a vote on the increase in the debt ceiling going around the Republican roadblock that the speaker represents. You would need five signatures from Republicans in order to get there, to 218.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe that that discharge petition is working as a real pressure on Kevin McCarthy, forcing him closer to an agreement with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 [22:29:48]\par \par \uc2 MCGOVERN: Well, I do think it`s an important pressure. Because, you know, at some point, if we can`t get to a deal, then some of the so-called moderate Republicans, although I haven`t seen them behave in a moderate way up to this point, but I think they will feel more pressure to try to avoid a catastrophe for our economy.\par \par \uc2 And maybe we could get them to join with us, so we could bring something to the floor that is reasonable, something that is balanced. Something that, quite frankly, the American people would think is a good deal.\par \par \uc2 But look, you know, Kevin McCarthy I think is wholly owned by the MAGA extremists in the Republican conference. I don`t think he has much control over, you know, what direction the House of Representatives is going to move in. He`s totally beholden to the extremists.\par \par \uc2 And up to this point, they have been unreasonable, they have been unrealistic, and they have been unhinged.\par \par \uc2 And again, the stuff that we are seeing, the details of what they are trying to do, I think the vast majority of people in this country, including a number of high percentage of Republicans, I think would be horrified by what they are trying to do.\par \par \uc2 Again, you know, going after the most vulnerable and protecting those who are well off and well connected. I think again, it does not reflect my values, I don`t think it reflects the values of the majority of people in this country.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman \uc2 Jim McGovern\uc2  of Massachusetts, thank you very much for joining us once again.\par \par \uc2 MCGOVERN: It`s great to be with you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, after we learned the hardest decision Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has faced in his professional life was putting up a fence, a temporary fence, around the Supreme Court, a ten-year-old girl who had to make much harder decisions about what to do when she discovered she was pregnant after being raped was helped by Dr. Caitlin Bernard, who appeared on this program last year and is now being attacked by Republicans who want to revoke her license to practice medicine for helping that ten-year-old girl. That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:31:55]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The chief justice of the United States thought long and hard about it. And he wrote a passage of a speech that he gave earlier this week in which he described the hardest decision he ever had to make.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , CHIEF JUSTICE, SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: In 18 years, I was asked what was the hardest thing, what was the hardest decision I had to make in 18 years. Was it the First Amendment case? Was it that death penalty case? Was it some major separation of powers case? None of those.\par \par \uc2 The hardest decision I had to make was whether to erect fences and barricades around the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It would be hard to imagine a more shallow-minded chief justice serving over the last 18 years than \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . From his seat on the Supreme Court, he has ordered people to their deaths in capital punishment cases. And apparently, those were not hard decisions for him. Even though his Catholic religion and the Pope opposed every one of those executions, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  had no problem supporting them.\par \par \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has made important decisions on countless cases that have affected the health and safety of millions and millions of people, and that apparently was not hard for him.\par \par \uc2 He decided to repeal portions of Obamacare and take health insurance away from people. That was not hard.\par \par \uc2 And of course, he decided to revoke a constitutional right that allowed women the control their own bodies and that was not hard for \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But he did not want the protesters against his easy decisions to revoke the constitutional rights from women to get close to the Supreme Court so he ordered a temporary fence erected around the Supreme Court which he just might have to keep there or decide to keep there for the rest of his life so that the voices of those protesters can be pushed farther away from his window.\par \par \uc2 It was not hard for \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  to make a decision that forced a ten-year- old girl, who was raped in Ohio, to give birth. That is what \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 ` decision did. And that was not hard for him.\par \par \uc2 But that ten-year-old girl found the help she needed to leave the state of Ohio, to get the legal abortion she needed in Indiana.\par \par \uc2 And, thanks to \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , Dr. Caitlin Bernard, who helped that ten-year- old girl is now the target of a disciplinary hearing by the medical board in Indiana on charges of failing to report child abuse to authorities and violating patient privacy. Dr. Caitlin Bernard appeared on this program last year and said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. CAITLIN BERNARD, OB GYN: Something that we feel very strongly, that we need to be able to provide this care for everyone, for every single person that we get a call about from any state, all over the country.\par \par \uc2 There is no way that you can expect anyone, much less a young person, to be able to identify and get to care by six weeks pregnant.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:52]\par \par \uc2 BERNARD: And we know, particularly, people who are young, do face additional risks, or consequences are more likely to present later both because of difficulty identifying a pregnancy, and fear, as you said, of the repercussions of that care.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is the world that Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  created, and the real world experience of that ten-year-old girl is why the chief justice put a fence around the Supreme Court, so that he would not have to hear about that ten-year-old girl who has had to make, at age ten, harder decisions than \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  apparently has ever had to make in his pampered, isolated professional life.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Dr. Tracey Wilkinson, a pediatrician and associate professor at Indiana University School of Medicine. She has been attending today`s hearing in support of Dr. Bernard.\par \par \uc2 And Dr. Wilkinson, as I understand it, the hearing is still going on at this late hour?\par \par \uc2 DR. TRACEY WILKINSON, ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR, INDIANA UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF MEDICINE: It is. We have been here for 13 hours, and we are still not done.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The issue of violation of the patient`s privacy is something that Dr. Bernard has denied, and also the issue of, the other issues involved are things that she has made clear she did indeed notify and made sure that authorities were notified about this ten-year-old girl being raped.\par \par \uc2 WILKINSON: Yes, I mean we`ve been watching the attacks on Dr. Bernard for the last year, but today has been especially hard to watch 13 hours of just persistent attacks and attempts to politically harass her for doing her job.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let`s listen to some of what Dr. Bernard has said today at the hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BERNARD: I think that it is incredibly important for people to understand the real world impacts of the laws of this country about abortion or otherwise.\par \par \uc2 I think it is important for people to know what patients will have to go through, because of legislation that is being passed. And a hypothetical does not make that impact, it does not help people understand what is happening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Why is the hearing going on for so long?\par \par \uc2 WILKINSON: I mean, I think multiple people today have said that this is a hearing that is never -- it`s unlike any other hearing that has ever happened at the Indiana Medical Licensing Board.\par \par \uc2 I think that this case is unique. I think it is extreme. And I think, for that reason, it is taking us over 12 hours to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: There are, on that board, very heavy contributors, financial contributors, to the state attorney general`s political campaigns, the Republican attorney general, and it is that attorney general who has brought this complaint against Dr. Bernard.\par \par \uc2 WILKINSON: I read that same article this morning about those political contributions, but ultimately this comes down to harassment and trying to intimidate physicians from being able to do their job, which is to take care of patients and offer them comprehensive health care, which includes abortion care in many cases and even in these horrific cases, such as this particular patient that we`ve been talking about today.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What are the possible penalties that Dr. Bernard is facing?\par \par \uc2 WILKINSON: From my understanding it can be anything from a letter, you know, disciplinary action, to a fine, to a suspension of her medical license either temporarily or permanently.\par \par \uc2 But what is important, is that whatever happens today, is going to have repercussions in the medical field as there are many people watching what is happening today, thinking about the care that they are providing their patients, and wondering if they could be the next person in front of their medical licensing board.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: How are physicians dealing with this now, knowing that these kinds of threats are out there?\par \par \uc2 I mean I think it is traumatic for all of us that is watching this. Either on the ground in Indiana, or virtually all over the country and all over the world. Ultimately, this type of political harassment has been happening to abortion providers for decades, but this has become much more the norm and on the national scale and can be perpetrated to many other fields outside of abortion providers.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Dr. Tracey Wilkinson, thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WILKINSON: Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Elmer Stewart Rhodes, who was sentenced to 18 years in federal prison for seditious conspiracy today has more in common with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than their attempt to illegally overturn the last presidential election.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:51]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Elmer Rhodes` stupidity, cruelty and vulgarity make him seem like a member of Donald Trump`s family. That is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Elmer Stewart Rhodes and \uc2 Donald John Trump\uc2  have a few things in common. They both tried to illegally overturn the last presidential election and they both believe they shouldn`t have to earn their way in this world.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:50]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was born rich and so never spent a day in his life doing anything that actually look like work.\par \par \uc2 Elmer Rhodes sounds like who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be if he weren`t born rich. Elmer Rhodes wanted his wife to support him. 30 years ago he was a student supported by his wife who told the "\uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2 " every day he was like, you should be a stripper and make more money.\par \par \uc2 She followed those orders from her husband. Elmer Rhodes and his wife had six children who could never rely on stability and support from their father who today was sentenced to spend the next 18 years of his life in federal prison for seditious conspiracy on January 6th as the field general of the absurdly named group, Oath Keepers. They of course do not keep any oaths to anyone.\par \par \uc2 In court today, at Elmer Rhodes sentencing, \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  officer Harry Dunn was allowed to speak as one of the victims of the attack on the Capitol and he said that the officers who were attacked and injured by Elmer Rhodes` were quote, "the real oath keepers that day".\par \par \uc2 Before sentencing Elmer Rhodes told the judge that he was a political prisoner to which Judge \uc2 Amit Mehta\uc2  said, you are not a political prisoner, Mr. Rhodes, you are here because of your actions.\par \par \uc2 In sentencing the 58-year-old Rhodes to federal prison until he is 76 years old Judge Mehta said "You sir, present an ongoing threat and a peril to this country and to the republic and to the very fabric of this democracy.\par \par \uc2 Elmer Rhodes also in his way, has always presented a threat to himself because of his relentless stupidity, which is now sending him for possibly the rest of his life, to federal prison.\par \par \uc2 But it is also the reason he wears an eye patch. His wife told the "\uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2 " that Rhodes accidentally dropped a 22-caliber handgun and shot himself in the face blinding himself in the left eye. She says she felt obligated to assist him. "I was suddenly taking care of a man with a hole in its head," she said.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is NBC News justice reporter Ryan Reilly who was in the courtroom today. Ryan, take us through what it was like. This is the strangest sentencing hearing I have ever read about.\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, NBC NEWS JUSTICE REPORTER: Yes, it was very bizarre because, you know, usually you have defendants come up and try to sort of make an elocution, try to reduce their sentence. Say something that would give the judge something to grab onto to maybe, you know -- or come down a little bit.\par \par \uc2 And you know, sometimes you can get a little bit of wiggle room from a judge in a lot of these situations even for people convicted of horrendous crimes. If you go through some of the more difficult parts of your life, what led you up to this moment and particularly with apologies.\par \par \uc2 I think apologies play a tremendous role in a lot of these January 6 cases. And you know, there have been a lot of very valid apologies, some ones that maybe weren`t so valid, but they definitely play a role with judges.\par \par \uc2 If people think you are sorry and feel you`re sincere about that.\par \par \uc2 And that just wasn`t what Stewart Rhodes was going to do today. And that was clear from, you know, yesterday when he sort of got the signals from his lawyers and clear throughout this trial and clear leading up to his sentencing, where he`s been doing a lot of these media appearances. Or even just calling some of the January six supporters who gather almost every night outside of the D.C. jailhouse as he did earlier this week and make a comment about the need for a regime change.\par \par \uc2 And what he said in court today after the prosecutors brought up that comment that he was just talking about the fact that he would like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be elected in 2024 and come back to office because that`s what Stewart Rhodes and a lot of January 6 defendants want is for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to come back into office and do what he`s indicated that he could do if he comes back. which is pardon a lot of, if not all of, the January 6th defendants.\par \par \uc2 That`s sort of their get out of jail free card because otherwise Stewart Rhodes is going to be spending, as you mentioned, he`s going to be deep into his 70s by the time he gets out of prison on this offense.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I wanted to read more of what the judge had to say in the sentencing. Judge \uc2 Amit Mehta\uc2  said he was the one giving the orders. He was the one organizing the teams that day. He was the reason they were in fact in Washington, D.C. Oath Keepers wouldn`t have been there but for Stewart Rhodes. I don`t think anyone contends otherwise. He was the one who gave the order to go, and they went.\par \par \uc2 It is fascinating that Stewart Rhodes entire bet in the courtroom today. His whole bet is on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  winning the next presidential election. That is his way out of prison. He hopes, possibly, two years from now.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:58]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Otherwise, he may be there for what turns out to be the rest of his life?\par \par \uc2 REILLY: Yes, that is really it for him. And I think, you know, the other option for him is that he just goes out as what he sees as sort of America`s Nelson Mandela.\par \par \uc2 He really believes that he is this political prisoner, and he has made that explicit reference before. Saying that he is this historic figure and comparing himself to that sort of figure in history and saying that this is -- really, he is a political prisoner, and this is -- this is essentially what he wants to be remembered for even if he does end up spending all these days in prison.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Ryan Reilly, thank you for always being in the courtroom for us on these days. Really appreciate it. Thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 REILLY: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:43]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230526ej5p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May 25, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Ryan Reilly \par \uc2 8,226 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Normative books that have sexual situations in them, and the girl in the boy kissing in the books, they`ve never been a child or a teenager because kids are already reading that stuff and they read it online. Grow up. Your kids are more mature than you.\par \par \uc2 Suzanne Nossle and Ashley Hope Perez, thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN" with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voice-over): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELMER STEWART RHODES, LEADER, OATH KEEPERS: We have been already stationed outside D.C. as a nuclear option. In case they attempt to remove the president illegally, we will step in and stop it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The top Oath Keeper gets 18 years for seditious conspiracy. The guy with his feet on the Speaker`s desk gets four years. But the guy who sent them to the Capitol is still scot-free.\par \par \uc2 Then, the special counsel is closing in as we get new reporting on team Trump moving classified documents.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the disastrous rollout of the DeSantis campaign.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Rob DeSanctimonious and his poll numbers are dropping like a rock. Is he a fool who has no idea what the hell he`s doing?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And a showdown in Texas as the House Republicans call for their attorney general to be impeached after he says the House Speaker was drunk on the job.\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. DADE PHELAN (R-TX): The amendment is (INAUDIBLE). Is their objections on the amendment? (INAUDIBLE). The amendment is adopted.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York, I`m Chris Hayes. It was a dramatic scene inside a Washington courthouse today as a federal judge handed down the longest sentence yet for a January 6th insurrectionist. Elmer Stewart Rhodes III, the founder of the far-right gang the Oath Keepers, was convicted of the very rare crime of seditious conspiracy late last year. Today, he was sentenced to 18 years in federal prison.\par \par \uc2 Another old keeper, Kelly Meggs, who was also convicted on seditious conspiracy, was sentenced to 12 years in prison. But by all accounts, Rhodes was the guy in charge. If you`re a regular viewer of this program, you probably recognize Rhodes by now. He`s the guy who wears an eye patch because he accidentally shot himself in the face with his own gun. And the 57-year-old will likely spend most of the remainder of his life in prison.\par \par \uc2 Today, Judge Amit Mehta justified the harsh sentence due to the severity of Rhodes` crimes telling him, "You, sir, present an ongoing threat and a peril to this country and to the Republic and to the very fabric of this democracy." And "for decades, Mr. Rhodes, it is clear you`ve wanted the democracy of the country to devolve into violence."\par \par \uc2 In fact, even after January 6th, Rhodes was still calling on militias to "defend yourselves against an illegitimate Biden administration."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RHODES: We have to then now prepare to walk the path of the Founding Fathers or your independence from that illegitimate regime. We still defend the Constitution but they`re the ones that are usurpers and the violators and declare that. And then get stronger your communities. You need be raising local militias in your towns and counties like the Founders did.\par \par \uc2 You need to then nullify, refuse the comply. And when they come for you, you defend yourselves. But you got to do it together. Don`t be isolated and alone. Do it together as the united community -- if you can get the people in your community to unite. So many Americans now are running and hiding. It`s not getting any good. So, you need to get together and prepare to defend yourselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Rhodes carry that same sense of defiance through to his hearing today. Wearing an orange prison jumpsuit, Rhodes address the court before his sentencing. He referred to all January 6 defendants including himself as political prisoners. He compared himself to the unjustly prosecuted protagonists of Franz Kafka`s The Trial. He claimed his case was dividing the country and he vowed to "expose the criminality of this regime from his prison cell."\par \par \uc2 Bluster aside, Rhodes` actual defense mostly relied on the fact that he never actually set foot inside the Capitol. But that didn`t really matter for the prosecution`s case because prosecutors described him as a battlefield general who ordered his foot soldiers to do his dirty work for him. And that speaks to the severity of what Rhodes did do. He may not have personally stormed the Capitol but prosecutors were still able to prove he was part of a criminal conspiracy to overthrow the government, to end free and fair elections in this country by force, and to install \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as president of the United States against the will of the American people.\par \par \uc2 Now, seditious conspiracy convictions are incredibly rare and the cases are notoriously difficult to prosecute. But even after a lengthy trial and significantly more due process than the average incarcerated person gets in this country, Rhodes is found guilty. And what Rhodes did, ordering around his lackeys from outside the Capitol, commanding them as a general would his soldiers, sounds pretty applicable to someone else, doesn`t it? I mean, isn`t that effectively what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did on January 6th?\par \par \uc2 Trump didn`t actually go to the Capitol himself although we know he wanted to, and reportedly got violent when he was not able to go. But the January 6th insurrection was at his sole direction. The conspiracy to overthrow the American government did not start with Stewart Rhodes. It of course started with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . In fact, it`s a comparison Rhodes himself invited today. He argued that like the ex-president, he was simply trying to oppose anti- American forces within our country.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:32]\par \par \uc2 Now, unlike \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Rhodes will be going to prison for his crimes for a long time. But again, his conviction raises a question that we`ve asked many times on this show over the past several years. When are we going to see accountability for the man behind the big lie, the attempted coup, the January 6th insurrection, the individual but for whom there would be none of this? When is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself going to face legal consequences for his actions?\par \par \uc2 NBC News Justice Reporter Ryan Reilly was at the court for the sentencing. And he joins me now. Ryan, give us just a sense of what it was like. It seemed like the words the judge were quite impassioned, that Rhodes was incredibly defiant. Give us a sense of what -- how it played out.\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, NBC NEWS JUSTICE REPORTER: Yes, I mean, it was just a remarkable moment because we -- this is -- we`ve sort of been building this event. The lawyers want to alert attending reporters that you know, there is some consternation about what Rhodes was going to say, and they really haven`t been able to restrain him. In fact, you know, they had three lawyers. They are -- one of them was this guy who was added last minute at Rhodes`s request. He tried to push other people on the team out. So, there`s been this ongoing tension probably within his legal team about what the best defense strategy was here because, you know, this is a guy who went to Yale and thinks he`s very smart, so wants to, you know, take up essentially his own defense here.\par \par \uc2 But I don`t think he was looking for any sort of mitigation necessarily in that -- in that hearing. He was willing to you know take the hit on whatever marginal impact, any words at the last minute that the judge might end up having whether that shaves off a few months or a couple of years perhaps all of the sentence, because he just wanted to put that political message out there. He really wanted to get that through and get that out to the American people and you know put himself in this the same bin with all these other January 6th defendants because he hopes that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gets reelected and he hopes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  comes in and hopes he gets pardoned, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, that`s what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been talking about for a very long time on the trail now in addition to playing theme songs from the January 6th choir at his events and hugging members -- people who were prosecuted for January -- in relation to January 6th campaign events. He`s also been talking about potential pardons for these individuals. I mean, we saw that again from \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  today talking about whether or not he would part of these individuals.\par \par \uc2 So, this is really going to be this ongoing theme within the Republican primary with people getting pressed about whether -- what they`re going to do with these January 6th defendants because there`s just a bunch of misinformation out there about what these cases are all about and how these cases have actually impacted individuals, you know, sort of -- and you know, so Rhodes, putting all this rhetoric out there and talking about how he wants to overthrow this regime, he wants to be in the same boat as, you know, your grandma who walked into the Capitol and got probation, right? He wants them all to be lumped in together so that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or someone else comes in, they all sort of get pardoned off of this.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it`s a great point that part of the defiance here is partly some calculated defiance with the hope that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or someone else, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  today, as you noted, said he was open to January 6 pardons would come in later and grant that pardon power.\par \par \uc2 There was one moment where the judge said at one point, I think, I`ve never said this to a defendant, but you`re -- that line I read, you`re an ongoing peril to the democracy. It really stuck with me.\par \par \uc2 REILLY: It did. And that`s -- you know, that really set him apart. And I think that`s why you saw the differentiated sentence for Kelly Meggs who was sentenced this afternoon into 12 years as a big split and a big difference despite -- maybe Kelly Meggs is actually on the ground conduct being worse than then Rhodes is, but they were just in a different category in terms of what they -- you know, in terms of the ongoing threat is what Judge Mehta was saying that, you know, this is someone who is espousing these beliefs and thinks and you know, (INAUDIBLE) frankly saying he`s put -- someone who`s pretty actually, you know, charismatic and could get an audience to perhaps do what he wants to do.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Went to Yale and thinks he`s smart. I`ve heard that before and it`s true a number of people who end up being nowhere near as smart as I think they are. Ryan Reilly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining me I was author Jeffrey Toobin whose latest book is incredibly relevant. It`s called Homegrown: Timothy McVeigh And The Rise Of Right-Wing Extremism. It`s good to have you here, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY TOOBIN, AUTHOR: Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The book is excellent, but it`s about -- I thought about the book when I was listening to that court reporting about -- we played that tape there when Rhodes talks about literally a call from militias. I mean, the Oath Keepers is founded in that. And he says to people out there, you should be going back to your home and organizing militias.\par \par \uc2 TOOBIN: And he says -- I just loved that sound that you just played because it was we are like the American revolutionaries, we are like 1776. On January 6th, \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 , the Congresswoman, tweeted, today is 1776. Alex Jones said the same thing. Timothy McVeigh, that was his justification in 1995. He said he had the Declaration of Independence memorized. He told his lawyer that we are like the Founding Fathers and we are rebelling against a corrupt regime.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:46]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s interesting because I think -- obviously in the 1990s, when Timothy McVeigh orchestrated this mass murder -- and I want to just be very clear that obviously what he did is just unfathomably worse than what Rhodes did in terms of just the sheer human toll. But this idea of like, the Founders and might make right in a kind of today`s revolution, it feels very much closer in some ways to the core ethos of the Republican Party in 2023 than it did to the Republican Party in 1993.\par \par \uc2 TOOBIN: Oh, absolutely. But the motivations are even more similar today than they were in 1995. Talk about guns, the obsession with the Second Amendment and the idea -- incorrect -- but the idea that Democrats are always going to take your guns away --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And this is of Rhodes` --\par \par \uc2 TOOBIN: Central to the Oath Keeper -- central to the Oath Keepers. McVeigh, I think a lot of people remember that he was motivated by anger about the Waco raid, the bombing was on the second anniversary of the Waco raid. He was just as outraged by \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  signing the assault weapons ban on September 13, 1994. That was the event that -- where he and Terry Nichols said, that does it, we`re bombing a building.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, and this is where you get to this sort of existential point. You know, we talked today about DeSantis talking about, he`s entertaining the notion of maybe pardoning Jan sixers. Majority of the House caucus voted for the actual insurrection. And the rhetoric around, again, in mainstream politics around the use of guns and the Second Amendment, routinely sort of flirts with a seditious conception of why you have them. I mean, basically you have them to make war against the government.\par \par \uc2 TOOBIN: Well -- and this is how the Republican Party has evolved. You know, when the Second Amendment -- when they first started arguing against all history that the Second Amendment implies an individual right, the idea was, well, you have the right to defend yourself in your home from criminals. Today, like Tim McVeigh, they`re saying, it`s not just to defend yourself against criminals, it`s to defend yourself against the government itself. That is an extreme version but one that, again, comes out of what the militias and what McVeigh was thinking in `95.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s what -- that`s what the militia movement was. It`s what it carries through, I think, to the Oath Keepers, you know, in terms of Rhodes, and is now captured a huge part of sort of mainstream Republican thought. I wonder if you -- does it matter the sentence today that it is as long as it is, that it is -- that there is accountability, that there was due process, that these men were convicted of the majority of their peers in Washington.\par \par \uc2 TOOBIN: I think it does matter. I mean, 18 years is a long time.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: An enormous time.\par \par \uc2 TOOBIN: And remember, in federal prison, you have to do 85 percent of your sentence. So, he is going to be in jail for a very, very long time. But the other thing that`s very important about the sentence is that it was not just about the violence in the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TOOBIN: It was about the violence to the democratic system. That`s what made the sentence a lot longer than those other knuckleheads who were just -- who were inside. This was someone who was trying to overthrow the government. And that`s what the judge`s language was so important.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I totally agree with you. And I think it elevates it also to the obvious comparison of the person who ordered the whole thing, the person that ordered the job.\par \par \uc2 TOOBIN: You know, it`s increasingly difficult to imagine how 1,000 people - - I mean, that`s how many people are being prosecuted here. 1,000 people are being prosecuted. Their lives disrupted or ruined because they followed their leaders` instructions.\par \par \uc2 Now, you know, it`s still very hard to prove that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  intended violence. You know, he said fight. Well, politicians, they fight all the time.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 TOOBIN: But, you know, there will be conversations that Jack Smith has access to that we don`t know yet, but it`s getting closer.\par \par \uc2 HAYES; Jeffrey Toobin, whose latest book is called Homegrown. It`s about Timothy McVeigh.There`s a lot of just revelatory new details in there. It`s a fantastic book. Check it out. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:10]\par \par \uc2 TOOBIN: Thanks, pal.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s indictment watch once again, as Jeff just alluded to. The special counsel nearing the end of one of his Trump investigations. And we`ve got new reporting almost sort of comically incriminating about the game of musical boxes that are apparently being played down in Mar-a-Lago as the Justice Department worked to collect classified documents from Trump`s retirement home. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, so we are all watching very carefully. Special counsel Jack Smith and his criminal investigation into Trump squirreling away hundreds of classified documents in Mar-a-Lago. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  today reporting, according to people familiar with the matter, that not only is a special counsel close to wrapping up his investigation, but that Smith is "poised to announce possible criminal charges in the days or weeks after Memorial Day."\par \par \uc2 This comes as The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  has new reporting that points to just how much the investigation has uncovered. According to people familiar with the matter, two Trump workers at Mar-a-Lago moved boxes of paper, get this, just one day before the Justice Department prosecutors came to Mar-a-Lago in June 2022 to retrieve documents in response to a subpoena for classified documents.\par \par \uc2 The Post reports that federal investigators have come to view the timing of that movement of boxes as "suspicious and an indication of possible obstruction." That`s a very gentle way to say it. NBC News has not confirmed this reporting, though the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  is reporting along similar lines this evening.\par \par \uc2 The Post report is startling. That piece also says prosecutors have gathered evidence that "even before Trump`s office received the subpoena in May 2022, he had what some officials have dubbed a dress rehearsal for moving government documents he did not want to relinquish. The timing of t