pened the next day.\par \par \uc2 But we`ve also got, according to reporting, 50 pages of his former attorney`s notes that seem to corroborate that Trump had been instructed on what to do and what not to do. And this sounds like was what not to do. And, you know, there`s obviously surveillance. There are witnesses that have been testifying, including some who apparently have said that Trump was showing off these documents to people.\par \par \uc2 So, the notion that, you know, it`s kind of a shifting defense, but that he wasn`t aware that he had them. Obviously, that appears to have gone out the door, but that he didn`t know that he wasn`t supposed to have them and that they weren`t supposed to remain sounds like it`s getting a little harder for him to maintain that defense.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, the key -- one of the key things here -- I mean, there`s two things. One, this idea of address rehearsal, an episode when Trump allegedly reviewed the contents of some, but not all the boxes containing classified material. Like, the idea that -- I mean, when you find out, right, that you go through this sort of series of like, it was an oversight or who knows what was where, he was sloppy, they attested. But there`s a bunch of pieces that come into view that do make it seem like -- and not just in the reporting, after the fact he keeps saying they were mine, they were mine.\par \par \uc2 Like, Hannity said to him, well, you wouldn`t just like hold on to classified documents. He says, I would. So, it just seems more like he was like, these are my documents and I`m not giving him back essentially.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: I feel like we`ve had this conversation before where you turn to me and you say, I`m not a lawyer, but that seems like a bad idea. And it does seem like a bad idea. And he -- you know, from day one, he probably should have buttoned down what his defense is, but instead it`s been the scattershot thing of I didn`t know, I didn`t have them. And then it`s, I`m allowed to do whatever I wanted with them.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:10]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. This quote I thought was the former president -- this is according to people familiar with the situation. The Former President told aides he wanted to make sure he could keep papers that he considered his property. I mean, that really I think -- and that -- if you listen to him after the fact, that`s what he`s been saying.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that seems key to me is that the apparent either cooperation or compelled testimony of Evan Corcoran, who is one of the lawyers who ends up, I think, signing the document saying nothing to see here. And according to the Mar-a-Lago that this \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reporting, it seems like maybe they were hiding the stuff from Corcoran who was attempting to in good faith comply with the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: That`s what the reporting does, does seem to point to. It also sounds like Trump lawyers have now propounded some kind of defense and, you know, a white paper to the Department of Justice or to special counsel in which they maybe seem to be trying to shift some blame onto Corcoran saying, well, he just didn`t do a very good job, he didn`t take enough time to review the documents. So, again, it`s another one of these, you know, try and choose a defense, you know, as you go and see what sticks.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, yes.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: But generally you have to commit to one.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, and one of the things that was notable, of course, is that obstruction was what was identified in the -- in the search warrant, right? That there was probable cause of obstruction of justice. And there`s always been this question about, like, what are they looking for the crime here? Is it retention of classified documents? Is it violations of the Espionage Act? This would follow the obstruction category, right? Like, where this the pattern the facts were to obtain?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Yes. That -- it sounds like from everything we`ve been reading recently that that is definitely -- that is the focus. And in a lot of ways, that`s usually a neater case. It`s usually easier to prove. Here there`s questions about classification and, you know, were they classified, were they not? It doesn`t matter. If they said they were classified, the subpoena said, give me classified documents, you didn`t, that`s an open shot case.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s really interesting. Danya Perry, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 A disastrous rollout for the DeSantis campaign as the front runner faces more criminal charges. David Plouffe joins me on the state of the Republican primary. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Tonight, I`m pleased to introduce two individuals who have done more to loosen the --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Let`s see, so --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE 2: There you go.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE 1: Yes, I think so. Just to simplify this --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE 2: The thing keeps crashing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, that`s like very close to nightmares I have. But that happened, and it has now been more than 24 hours since \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  announced his candidacy for president in the way that can only be seen as a really unmitigated disaster. His big \uc2 Twitter\uc2  spaces launch kept crashing, having technical difficulties, which the DeSantis team, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , and some rich Silicon Valley dude named David Sachs are desperately trying to frame as proof of success.\par \par \uc2 The thing about it, though, with the day to sort of reflect on it and sort of listen to certain parts of it, is that even if it had gone flawlessly, like once it got up and running, still a deeply weird way for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to introduce himself as a candidate for president of the United States. On \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , with only audio, this odd stilted reading from a script to start off, and then is harping on extremely online hot-button issues, most notable the dreaded woke.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RON DESANTIS, PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: From the woke mob, replacing the woke mind virus, Biden`s allowed woke ideology to drive his agenda. We will never surrender to the woke mob, and we will leave woke ideology in the dustbin of history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Who talks like that? I mean, I guess people running for Republican president do, but this is DeSantis`s stick using sort of these buzzwords that appeal to online right with cultural wariors. But to me, again, I`m not the target audience, they don`t say anything to like ordinary voters about how he plans to make sure they can earn a decent living, enjoy a middle-class life. A message that is the core of any successful political campaign, something that even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  understands.\par \par \uc2 David Plouffe understands this as well. He was a campaign manager for Barack Obama in 2008. He served as a senior advisor to President Obama. And he joins me now. Well, what do you think of the launch?\par \par \uc2 DAVID PLOUFFE, FORMER CAMPAIGN MANAGER FOR OBAMA 2008: I`ll put it in perspective, back when I used to manage campaigns. I mean, your announcement is your one free day. It`s going to be one of your best days of the campaign. You`re given the benefit of the doubt, people cover what you say. You`re basically shooting free throws. And so, it`s a really hard thing to mess up.\par \par \uc2 And he didn`t -- I agree, the tech fail was a disaster, but it was weird, man. We live in a visual world. This was audio. He`s reading off a script. His voice was sort of monotone. There was no energy to it. And so, I think it was a disaster. Now -- so, he`s in the penalty box. He can get back on the ice. Plenty of time left, and there`s a market out there for a non- Trump candidate. I think there`s grave concerns about Trump`s electability amongst enough Republicans. But he`s not got to worry about someone like Tim Scott. I mean, he`s got to guard his lane, and he`s off to an inauspicious start. So, he`s got to get some real stuff going on the early states, which is where this gets decided.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, I was thinking about announcement. I was there in Springfield in 2007 on that -- I mean, that was one of the coldest days I`ve ever -- do you remember that?\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: It`s about 12.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It was so cold.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Windchill maybe zero.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:08]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Which was when Barak Obama announced. But again, it was like he came out. He had a family. He had a big speech. It was like a big crowd. It was just, you know, all that sort of stuff. Again, you can make an argument that like we live in different times and this is -- you know, you play around with methods. The biggest thing to me though is like, what are you saying about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and what is your case against him?\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Do you have a sense of what that is?\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: I actually think it`s simple. To me, it`s like third-grade politics. He can`t win. He lost in `18, lost in `20, lost in `22. He can`t win in `24. I can win. Look, I won Florida by 18 points. And then he`s got a Florida story. By the way, a story I think in the general election will cause him problems, but in a Republican primary, he`s got a great story.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: This is not that hard. Look, I don`t understand why he`s struggling so much to basically present something that I think is just right in front of you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Here`s -- OK, but here`s part of the reason, I think. And I think this is a key part to understand. We saw a polling the other day that 60 percent of Republicans don`t think the 2020 election was legitimately won by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . The key to any argument that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is that he`s a loser --\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- who`s lost and will lose again. That`s it. That`s -- again, that`s --\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: That`s where it starts.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- the one sentence argument against this guy if you`re a Republican. Again, these are not people -- no, I`m not those voters. This is not my worldview. Part of what the big lie is about is inoculating him against that. So that if you say he lost, the plain truth, you are on the side of \uc2 George Soros\uc2  and the deep state.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Right. But you can say, we lost in 18 badly as a party. That was him.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: We lost in `22. That was him. He barely beat Hillary.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: And I think he could also make the general race argument, which is, you know, Trump`s also going to struggle because of his age. We don`t even have that contrast against Biden. So, I get a lot of Republicans aren`t going to buy it, but you got to sell your strongest product. And right now, I think electability would be DeSantis is number one. And then I think number two, he`s got a story to tell in Florida that will appeal to a lot. Appealing to 60 percent of those folks who don`t think Trump lost. But he`s got a Florida story that will be appealing to a bunch of them.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, Trump is beating him over the head, you know, and that`s not going to stop. And that`s the other big thing to me is just like, can you take the punch? Like, no one figured out how to go at Trump really in 2016. And one of the things I`ve seen from DeSantis a little bit is starting this like -- you know, Trump attacked him for some, you know, votes he took and him trying to come back like, the omnibus and amnesty, right. Basically, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a crypto liberal.\par \par \uc2 We saw them try this in 2016. Ted Cruz tried it. He`s from a pro -- he`s a pro-choice pro, abortion back from New York. I just think it`s utterly the wrong way.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Am I wrong?\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: You`re not going to --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I`m watching this and I`m like, guys, they don`t -- you are not going to get to Donald Trump`s right on immigration. You`re -- that`s not the -- you`re just not going to, just purely tactically.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Right. No, you`re not going to -- this is not issues. This has got to be about electability.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Which is I think his fatal weakness in the Republican primary. You can attack his character if you want. Like, you`re not going to be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if you`re not willing to get in the ring and go haymaker to haymaker. You`re just not.\par \par \uc2 By the way, if you`re able to do that and then you get an early win, then I think a lot of Republicans will say maybe this is the person.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Again, I think -- I sometimes think that these people are too freaked out and scared of their own base and realize that they`ll ultimately dish him. I do think that`s possible.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I don`t know. It`s untested but I do think that`s possible. The last thing is just on the -- on the indictment. I mean, I also just think with the electability, it`s like he`s created this alternate universe where like every time -- you know, if he gets indicted, it`s like good for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right? He raised a lot of money. But like someone`s got to be able to say -- I mean, they were talking about tens of millions of primary voters, right? Not all of them are like culturally devoted \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Like, is it a great idea for us to have a guy who`s facing like multiple indictments for a nominee?\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: No. It adds a lot more weight to the back of a rickety wagon, right? So, well more than 50 percent of Republican primary voters are open to someone other than Trump.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Largely because they`re concerned about his electability. So, so a DeSantis, a Tim Scott has to say if he gets another indictment, particularly if it comes from the DOJ, which is he`s already probably going to lose. This is just going to make it harder. Like, you have to say that the minute the indictment comes down.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Not to give them too much advice, but they`re --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No, no. You`re right. You`re right. You`re right. David Plouffe, good to have you here in the studio.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, high noon in Texas. The Republican House wants to impeach the Republican Attorney General. He in turn accuses the Republican Speaker of being drunk on the job.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PHELAN The chair recognizes Mr. Johnson of Harris -- Mr. Johnson of Harris is speaking opposition to the bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In the second most populous state in the country, Texas, there`s this weird mismatch between the 30 million citizens and their political leadership. The government is just solid deep red. Republicans hold majorities in both houses of the legislature. They control every statewide office and the state Supreme Court. And it`s been this way for nearly 30 years.\par \par \uc2 But the citizens of Texas are nowhere near as homogenous. In fact, the state is trending towards the Democrats to the left, shifting nearly eight points towards Democrats in just the past decade. And we have seen some close races famously that Senate race in 2018 when \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  came within three points of unseating Ted Cruz.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:15]\par \par \uc2 But as of yet, that Democratic shift, and we`ve seen it at the presidential level, has not been big enough to change much of anything in Texas state politics. So, elected Republicans in that state have long faced essentially zero political accountability. And that generally, as a general matter, leads to widespread corruption and misconduct.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take Republican State Representative Bryan Slaton, remember that guy? So-called family values conservative, led the charge on anti-LGBTQ legislation, describing drag performers as "perverted adults." Well, an internal investigation just determined that 45-year-old Slaton had sex with his 19-year-old aide after getting her drunk. Now, to their credit, after a solemn, sometimes angry and tearful recounting of Slaton`s graphic and offensive behavior, the Texas House voted to expel him 147 to zero, making him the first member of the Texas legislature to be removed from office for nearly a century.\par \par \uc2 It just gives you some of the idea of some of the characters who make up the Republican caucus in the Texas state legislation. It`s quite a crew.\par \par \uc2 Then you have the attorney general of Texas, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , who was just reelected to a third term after attempting to overturn the 2020 election, suing the Biden administration over and over, joining the effort to ban abortion medication. In his eight years in office, Paxton has faced multiple criminal investigations, including a 2015 indictment on felony of securities fraud. In 2020, senior members of this guy`s own office came forward alleging he committed bribery, abusive office, and other potential crimes. The Department of Justice is investigating those claims. Then, some of those aides who came forward to blow the whistle were fired and subsequently filed a lawsuit alleging improper retaliation.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, Paxton settled with the whistleblowers for $3.3 million, money he asked the legislature to give him out of taxpayer funds. And now, these two Republican-led branches of Texas government have a beef that is exploding out into the open.\par \par \uc2 The House, particularly the speaker, Dave Phelan, does not want to give Paxton that money, so they launched an investigation into the attorney general accusing him of committing multiple crimes, including felonies of hearing yesterday. Paxton immediately fired back accusing Speaker Phelan of being drunk on the job and calling on him to resign. And again, to be fair to Paxton here, something did sound off the other day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PHELAN: Mr. Campbell, the Senate amendment is (INAUDIBLE). Is there objections to the amendment? The chair is (INAUDIBLE). The amendment is adopted.\par \par \uc2 The chair recognizes Mr. Johnson of Harris -- Mr. Johnson of Harris to speak in opposition to the bill.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I don`t know about that. Now, to top it all off, just hours after that hearing where investigators ordered Paxton to preserve documents and evidence related to the accusations against him, a literal dumpster fire broke out of his office. Paxton initially released this video referring to it as a case of arson. Today, he announced the suspect has been arrested and charged with criminal mischief. And the preliminary investigation concluded the fire was accidental.\par \par \uc2 But it looks like the House has the upper hand. The investigating committee just formally recommended that \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  be impeached for his potential crimes. If the impeachment is successful, and one Democratic lawmaker says they do have enough votes, Paxton would be required to step down from his post temporarily while facing trial in the State Senate, which would mean maybe finally some accountability is coming for \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  and Texas Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, South Carolina became the 21st state to have an abortion ban on the books after Republican Governor \uc2 Henry McMaster\uc2  signed a six-week abortion ban to law like the one in Florida. Not only are conservatives trying to attack abortion access through state legislatures, they`re going through the courts as well.\par \par \uc2 An anonymous plaintiff backed by Texas Republican Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , the one that we just talked about, just sued \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  for $1.8 billion with a B dollars. You will never guess in a million years where this was filed. Which American city do you think they file this anti- abortion lawsuit in? You guessed it, Amarillo, Texas. That`s right. Amarillo`s only federal judge, Matthew Kacsmaryk, the right-wing, Trump- appointed judge, who single-handedly struck down the FDA`s approval of Mifepristone which have been safely on the market for over 20 years. That is the judge that will hear this new case.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2  is CEO and president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2  and \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 , and she joins me now. It`s good to have you in the program. I have to say I saw the news of this lawsuit, and when I read -- when I read some of the details -- I`ll give an example from a Vox explainer here. Basically, they`re suing \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  for complying with a federal court order. That`s the short version.\par \par \uc2 The longer version, the Doe lawsuit alleges \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , should have to repay all the money it received for providing care to Medicaid patients in Texas and Louisiana during the period those two states were legally bound to keep working with them. They claim that \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is liable for three times the amount of money it received, plus a penalty of up to $11,000 for each of the thousands of claims for payment filed with those two states.\par \par \uc2 How serious is this lawsuit, and does it pose a threat to your organization?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:12]\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 , CEO AND PRESIDENT, PLANNED PARENTHOOD ACTION FUND: Chris, yes, it`s serious, right? Because yes, it is happening, you know, again in a state and in a district that, you know, we have seen has been hostile to reproductive freedom. So, are we concerned? Absolutely. Is there any merit to the case? Absolutely not. Exactly what you said, right? These are baseless claims. They`re essentially suing for \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  getting reimbursed for providing healthcare services in Texas to patients, just like every other healthcare provider does.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, we follow the law, you know. We ensure that we are compliant. We are a nonprofit. And millions of people depend on \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  for their healthcare services. But what is not, you know, clearly sufficient just to provide healthcare for some of these folks is that they are hell-bent on trying to make sure that not only will they ban access to abortion, not only will they ban access to gender affirming care, but they are hell-bent on also trying to shutter our doors.\par \par \uc2 And so, we are fighting it every step of the way because it is completely baseless, it is not true. And honestly, you know, is a clear example of why we need court reform.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There -- the case that also came out of the same district in Matthew Kacsmaryk in Amarillo, Mifepristone, this was a, you know, credible, audacious injunction nationwide that he`d ordered for that to be taken off the -- taken off the shelves. The Supreme Court ultimately stepping in and stopping that. But that case has now gone up to the appellate level in the Fifth Circuit in Texas, which is notoriously probably the most conservative. And it seems like the conservative judges on that are pretty open to some of the arguments on Mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Like, how is \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  gaming out the fact that this is likely going to go to Supreme Court and the ability and viability of it on store shells is not completely clear?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, look, we believe that Mifepristone will be available for the foreseeable future, but you were absolutely right. Again, that`s why I ended my point on the need for court reform. When you can form shop in district in northern Texas in order to secure one judge who has, you know, indicated, you know -- and clearly in his own opinion indicated that he did not believe that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  had done proper due diligence on Mifepristone, which we know is safe and effective after 23 years and five million patients and, you know, being available in 60 countries, that it now goes to a conservative Trump-appointed largely judges at the Fifth Circuit and then all the way up to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 So, our reproductive freedom is not just in states like Texas and Nebraska and South Carolina are under attack, but it is one case that could have implications for the entire country. This court capture is the thing that is most threatening to women in this country, and that`s where -- you know, that`s the message that we need to get out so people understand it`s not just in the ban states, the 21 ban states nearly have the country, this is an attack that is coming to you, you know, right now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, it seems to me very clear -- I think it was clear after Dobbs that they`re going to keep doing everything they can until it`s banned everywhere all over the nation completely. I mean, is that your expectation of what the ultimate vision here is that you`re working against?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes, I believe that they were never real about returning the power to the states, right? They came out during the leak time, right? I think the day that the leak happened, they announced -- the opposition announced that they wanted a nationwide ban. Every Republican candidate is now being queried as a litmus test on where they stand on a nationwide ban.\par \par \uc2 And we know that the fear of the nationwide ban is what is mobilizing people. That is why you saw what you saw in the midterm elections. That`s why you saw what you saw in Kansas right before that, and in Wisconsin right after. And so, you know, we need to ensure that people both have access to care, but also understand that every vote they take up and down the ballot is going to be voted related to this care because you have folks who clearly need ethics reform holding all of these offices from attorney general, to the state`s Supreme Court, to lawmakers. And that`s obviously incredibly concerning.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. One of those -- one of those fights happening in Ohio we spotlighted yesterday in which they`ve now inserted a ballot amendment in August to raise the threshold to 60 percent to stop a majority of Ohioans who they suspect would vote for abortion rights from being able to do that. So, there`s going to be a pre-vote in August. Just one of the many fights on this front.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McGill-Johnson\uc2 . Thank you very much for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now with Alicia Menendez in for Alex. Good evening, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230526ej5p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for May 24, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell \par \uc2 6,588 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:27]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 And we have \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  joining us tonight on the one year mark since the mass murder in Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 And Andrew Weissmann has pulled off a miracle. He`s going to -- he`s found a way to join us from somewhere across the sea. I don`t even know where.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": You don`t need to know where. All you need to know is that he`s going to be there.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah. Somewhere, a lot of time zones from here, and you need a passport to be there. But, you know, this letter that Donald Trump`s lawyers sent to the attorney general, Andrew can`t contain himself.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: He`s got to -- he`s going to get a word in here about it.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: And as you should, I`m eager to hear what Andrew Weissmann thinks of this "letter".\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Have a good show.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Well, when your job is making decisions, and the decision to have to make are the most important decisions made in the United States of America, and you have been making those decisions for 18 years, it seems like the most difficult question you could ever be asked is, what is the hardest decision you`ve ever had to make as chief justice of the United States?\par \par \uc2 Where would you begin to answer that question? There must have been so many very, very, very, very, very hard decisions. Human lives at stake, human futures at stake, human possibilities at stake in these decisions.\par \par \uc2 It turns out that is a very easy question for the chief justice who revealed just how coddled and insulated his life and work experience has been from his days at boarding school, then Harvard College, then Harvard law school, on his very determined way to the Supreme Court, when he offered last night this shockingly shallow answer to that question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JUSTICE JOHN ROBERTS, U.S. SUPREME COURT: The hardest decision I had to make was whether to erect fences and barricades around the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Now, just in case you were wondering if the Catholic chief justice of the United States faces a difficult struggle against the teachings of his own church and the pope when the chief justice orders the execution of prisoners facing the death penalty in this country, you now have the answer to that question. That stuff is easy for this chief justice, doesn`t matter to him how often the pope preaches against the death penalty because the chief justice has no problem separating his church from the state when the state is on his orders putting a prisoner to death.\par \par \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  made the decision as a Supreme Court justice to make it illegal for a 10-year-old girl who was raped to obtain an abortion in her state and because of John Robert`s decision that 10-year-old girl had to travel to another state, a hardship that obviously no one related to \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  would ever have to endure. There is not a member of that Supreme Court who would force ten-year-old girl in their family to give birth to a child after being raped.\par \par \uc2 That 10-year-old girl has already had to make more difficult decisions than \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has ever had to make in his entire professional life at 10 years old.\par \par \uc2 In that speech last night, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  said Supreme Court standing open and unfenced last year when the Supreme Court began its Republican campaign to remove constitutional rights from the people of this country took its place as a building on Capitol Hill as the symbol of this country`s faith.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:24]\par \par \uc2 He used the word faith that the Supreme Court building opened and unfenced was the symbol of this country`s faith. The chief justice revealed himself yesterday more than he has ever revealed himself publicly, we should all encourage more of this kind of public speaking by the chief justice and the other justices so that the mentality of the born rich American aristocrats like \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  on the court can be exposed just as the Supreme Court has now been exposed under John Robert`s watch as wandering through the single most publicly corrupt period of the Supreme Court`s history. \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  offered the weakest possible defense of the Supreme Court`s low ethical standards, which has held the lowest ethical standards of any governing body in the federal government.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I want to assure people that I am committed to making certain that we as a court adhere to the highest standards of conduct. We are continuing to look at things we can do to give practical effect to that commitment, and I am confident there are ways to do that that are consistent with our status as an independent branch of government under the Constitution`s separation of powers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You can see the obliviousness there.\par \par \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  is completely oblivious to how wildly out of control the ethnic standards that the Supreme Court have become because of Clarence Thomas`s flagrant use of private jets and yachts in violation of the disclosure requirements of the Supreme Court justices and also because of John Robert`s wife`s $10 million income from selling her services to many of the major law firms in the country who have interests in the outcome of Supreme Court cases.\par \par \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  has not made a single sacrifice to be chief justice of the United States. His personal income at the court is limited by law, defined by law. But his wife is not. And so, on the salary of a Supreme Court justice, he lives the life of a fabulously wealthy man married to a very high earning wife. \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  will never do anything about the corruption of Republican-appointed Supreme Court justices, and he will never do anything to correct the possible conflicts of interest and appearance of conflict of interest that his wife`s massive income derived from law firms presents for the chief justice.\par \par \uc2 The chief justice and his wife will never live by the ethical standard established by the vice president and her husband. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` husband Doug Emhoff was a very high earning partner in a very powerful Los Angeles law firm, with offices around the world. When Doug Emhoff`s wife was elected vice president of the United States, even though his personal legal practice mostly involved entertainment law and to present no possible conflict with the vice president`s work, Doug Emhoff decided to quit his job. He drastically cut his income to become a law professor at a local Washington law school, an ethical choice available to the chief justice`s wife.\par \par \uc2 But because the chief justice and his wife have assigned to themselves a much lower ethical standard than the vice president and her husband, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  is just going to force the country to live with the constant potential for conflict of interest with the way his wife makes him an even richer man than the wealth he was born into.\par \par \uc2 And John Robert`s glancing reference to maintaining the low ethical standards of the Supreme Court, you just heard the chief justice stress, quote: Our status as an independent branch of government under the Constitution`s separation of powers.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:16]\par \par \uc2 And there he`s trying to imply that the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2  has absolutely no right to legislate the ethics of the Supreme Court, but as the chief justice knows, the Congress has every right to do that and has already done that in the most minimal ways. For example, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  could not be paid a lecture fee for his speech yesterday because Congress made that illegal for Supreme Court justices. Congress did that, not the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And now we know, now we know, it`s all worse than we thought, much worse. That`s what \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  proved last night. Until last night, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  had enjoyed the public image of being possibly the most thoughtful Republican appointed member of the Supreme Court, and the terrible truth might be that he is the most thoughtful Republican appointed member of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 We now know that he`s not just a Republican politician. He is a small- minded thinker who has no idea of the pain and anguish, the cruelty of his decisions of has inflicted on people, including 10 year old girls, and the only pain and anguish he seems to be capable of feeling in his job is when he gazes out his office window and seize the fence that he feels he was forced to erect to keep the sounds of the pain and anguish he has inflicted on other people, as far from his tender ears as possible.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is Cecile Richards, co-chair of American Bridge 21st Century and former president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also with us, Mini Timmaraju, president of NARAL Pro-choice America.\par \par \uc2 And, Cecile, when I heard \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  say that the hardest thing he ever had to do and ever had to decide was putting up that fence, I just thought of how -- how better could he prove to us what he does not feel when he reaches in to his bag of decisions and revokes constitutional rights from women.\par \par \uc2 CECILE RICHARDS, FORMER PRESIDENT, PLANNED PARENTHOOD: Absolutely. I mean, Lawrence, it`s disgusting and I think of you think about hard decisions I think about Kirsten Hogan, a woman in Texas who`s water broke during her pregnancy at 19 weeks, so she had to go to the emergency room, but she couldn`t get care and then was threatened that if she left, she could be criminally charged with having killed her son, stayed in the hospital under duress because of Texas law and delivered her stillborn baby five days later.\par \par \uc2 That`s hard -- those are hard decisions, and these are the hard decisions that doctors, that women, that pregnant people all across the country are having to make now as a direct result of \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  and the Supreme Court taking away their rights. So I do wish he would listen to the stories not only in Texas, they`re all across the country of the harm that is happening as a result of this Supreme Court taking away a constitutional right we`ve had for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 And I would point out the reason he built that fence is because he didn`t want to hear from people about what it means to take away their rights.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Mini Timmaraju, I want to hear more from \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . I want to hear more from all of the Supreme Court justices about their thinking on things like this because within their opinions, their written opinions, it`s such a controlled, you know, verbal and rhetorical environment that we don`t really get to find out as much about them as people as \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  inadvertently revealed to us about himself in that speech.\par \par \uc2 MINI TIMMARJAU, PRESIDENT, NARAL PRO-CHOICE AMERICA: It`s really dangerous and tricky when these Supreme Court justices are caught talking out loud about their innermost thoughts, right?\par \par \uc2 [22:15:01]\par \par \uc2 We`re learning so much in the last month -- few months of how low they can go. I mean, from Justice Thomas and Justice Alito, now to Justice Roberts. I think what`s challenging for them and why we might not hear as much after this is that their approval ratings have never been lower. The American public has no confidence in this court and we have an upcoming set of decisions in June that could plummet this support even lower.\par \par \uc2 So, look, reproductive freedom voters are disgusted. They`re angry. They think this court betrayed them, and it did, and I think we`re going to see a lot worse coming soon.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Cecile, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  clearly has no idea what he was saying. It was a written speech. You know, if this was a kind of a give- and-take sometimes, they submit to questions and answers at law school. If he had said this in response to a question, it would be one thing. But he sat down and premeditated and contemplated and wrote what is the hardest decision I`ve ever had to make.\par \par \uc2 He was reading that. He had days, maybe weeks to write that, and this is what he came up with as the hardest decision he ever made.\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: I mean, you`re completely right, Lawrence. He is totally shielded from what`s happening in America, and I think what`s -- you know, this story of what`s happening as a result of the overturning of roe versus way is one that is not aging well, and as we are seeing now, more and more Republican states jamming through abortion bans now in North Carolina, now in South Carolina, now in Florida, this could be anyone`s daughter, granddaughter, sister, mother.\par \par \uc2 These are stories and these are hard decisions that people are having to make, that doctors and women are having to make every single day, and it all lies at the at the feet of the Supreme Court. It`s -- I do think as Mini said, I think this is absolutely related to the undercutting of public trust in the height what we used to think of as the highest court in the land.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Cecile Richards and Mini Timmaraju, thank you both very much for starting off our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And up next, we`ll get Andrew Weissmann`s opinion of the letter Donald Trump`s lawyers sent to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  requesting a meeting with the attorney general.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:22:01]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: I want to acknowledge the weight this day for the community of Uvalde, Texas. I know that every day since May 24, 2022, has been a difficult one in Uvalde, but today is particularly painful. Today marks one year since 19 children and two of their teachers were killed in a mass shooting at an elementary school. Today marks one year that the families of those victims have spent mourning and unimaginable loss.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  delivered that speech this afternoon at the justice department and that is that is the only thing we have heard from the attorney general the day after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his lawyers publicly released a letter sent to the attorney general last night. The Trump lawyer`s letter says: Dear Attorney General Garland, we represent Donald J. Trump, the 45th president of the United States and the investigation currently being conducted by the special counsel`s office.\par \par \uc2 No president of the United States has ever in our -- in the history of our country been baselessly investigated in such an outrageous and unlawful fashion. We request a meeting at your earliest convenience to discuss the ongoing injustice that is being perpetrated by your special counsel and his prosecutors. Thank you for your attention to this matter.\par \par \uc2 And today, we got not a single public word from the attorney general in reply to that letter.\par \par \uc2 Former federal prosecutor Andrew Weissmann, who has been tracking the criminal investigation of Donald Trump`s possession of government documents from the start with us on this program can`t be with us tonight because he is overseas, but he sent us a video this evening with his reaction to the Trump letter to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOI CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Good evening, Lawrence. I wanted to talk to you about the letter that Donald Trump`s attorneys sent to \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . First, everyone knows that it is commonplace for prosecutors to give defense counsel an opportunity to be heard. It is less common however for the attorney general to hear each and every appeal that a defense lawyer may have. Although in big cases, it`s not unheard of or uncommon. It is somewhat unusual of course in a special counsel case because the whole point of appointing a special counsel is that the attorney general has decided that it is not appropriate for the attorney general to handle the day-to-day running and oversight of a case, and there`ll be enormous deference to the special counsel.\par \par \uc2 My take on this is that it very well could be that Donald Trump`s attorneys have already asked for and been given an audience with Jack Smith and his attorneys and been rebuffed, and that this is more of an appeal.\par \par \uc2 Of course, it could be that they`ve skipped that step and they`re trying to go directly to the attorney general. That would be a loser, because your first step really needs to be with a special counsel.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line here is that this is definitely signaling that we are at the very end of the investigation by Jack Smith, whether it`s on Mar-a-Lago or the January investigation, we don`t know. I would suspect it`s the Mar-a- Lago case because that is the simpler and easiest case. So I would think that this is a real sign that we`re about to see charges brought against, the former president by the federal government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Here is what Attorney General Merrick Maryland said when he announced the appointment of Jack Smith as special counsel on November 18th of last year.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: As special counsel, he will exercise independent prosecutorial judgment to decide whether charges should be brought.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And CNN is reporting that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his lawyers are very worried about the possibility of Donald Trump`s last White House chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  cooperating with special prosecutor Jack Smith. Quote: A source close to Trump`s legal team said Trump`s lawyers have had no contact with Meadows and his team and are in the dark on what Meadows is doing in the investigation, fueling speculation about whether Meadows is cooperating with the special counsel`s probe, or if Meadows himself is a target of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 The silence from Meadows has irked lawyers representing other defendants aligned with Trump who have been more open, according to several sources familiar with the Trump-aligned legal teams. In particular, they point to a $900,000 payment Trump`s Save America political action committee paid to the firm representing Meadows, McGuire Woods at the end of last year.\par \par \uc2 We we`ve all heard the same rumors, one Trump adviser told CNN. No one really knows what he`s doing though.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is Dan Horwitz. He`s a former Manhattan assistant district attorney where he prosecuted white-collar crimes like tax fraud, securities fraud.\par \par \uc2 Dan, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to begin with that final point about \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , which is how do you know if you`re -- if you`re as -- in a subject of a criminal investigation and one of your compatriots has become a cooperating witness, when do you usually find that out and how do you find that out?\par \par \uc2 DNA HORWITZ, FORMER ASSISTANT MANHATTAN DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Well, usually, you`re talking to the lawyers. The lawyers are talking to each other. And one telltale sign is when you don`t get a return phone call from lawyers that you`re working with, or if you`re close with those lawyers, they`ll say: Dan, it was good working with you on this matter, I`m not able to talk to you anymore. So that`s one telltale sign.\par \par \uc2 And usually, and certainly in a high profile case like this, where there`s a lot that gets into the media, there are signs that you can see whether somebody`s talking and then they stop talking so there are all sorts of markers that you can look at to read the tea leaves to see if somebody`s cooperating.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, you know, on that letter to the attorney general, it`s such a Trumpian, unserious letter because it has all this irrelevant stuff. It throws in a line about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . What do you do when you get a letter like that that is so kind of legally unserious?\par \par \uc2 The fundamental concept of it is okay. We would like to talk to you, try to head off this prosecution. But when you see these kind of crazy Trump rantings inside the letter, what`s the reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 HORWITZ: It`s got to be it`s all for show, because as you just said, there is a grain of seriousness to the latter which is either as Andrew said, I want my appeal for my meeting with Jack Smith or I`m trying to jump over Jack Smith. But all of this is political theater.\par \par \uc2 Like yesterday in the court hearing in 