so it`s the banning of art, it`s the beginning of literature, and really, what it gets down to, it`s exactly what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has been looking to do anyway. It`s really the -- it`s really behind the legislation. He`s looking to get rid of black history and black excellence in schools.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why he has no problem with this piece of art being taken away from elementary school kids. And so, my message to the kids and my message to people in the state, is to continue to fight for a world where we`re not banning books or banning poetry.\par \par \uc2 And if Amanda Gorman wants to come to Florida and read that poem that was - - that piece of poetry that was banned from that school, we`re more than happy to host her and make sure students actually hear it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. I re-read the poem today, as I`m sure a lot of people have, searching, searching, searching for the words that could possibly have sent this one parent in that direction, which she claims, this parent claims, there`s a hate message in there. And I just couldn`t find a single word.\par \par \uc2 Of course, there is that reference that she makes to being a descendant of slaves. That`s the only clue that there`s something in there that drives people like that crazy, that kind of truth.\par \par \uc2 I just want to read what Amanda Gorman said tonight about the DeSantis defense that, oh, we just moved it to a middle school. You can still get it once you make it up to middle school. She said, "A book ban is any action taken against a book that leaves access to a book restricted or diminished. This decision of moving my book from its original place taken after one parent complained, diminishes the access elementary schoolers would have previously had to my poem."\par \par \uc2 This is the kind of thing that makes absolutely no impression on \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  at all.\par \par \uc2 FROST: Yes, 100 percent. And what we see here, Lawrence is this is a microcosm, it`s a model, of what the far-right extremist want to do. The way that book ban has been set in place, is that one parent, and not even a parent -- any person in the community can essentially submit a form and have a book taken off the shelves of any public school.\par \par \uc2 That is a minority rule situation, that`s what`s going on with the Republican Party right now. They know that they have lost a complete generation of voters, Gen Z that voted over 70 percent for Democrats in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 And so what are they doing? They`re trying to morph government in a way where it can be set up for minority rule situation. And that book banning is really a microcosm of that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And I just want to stress this point that, of course, it does not apply in any way to the rich private schools that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and his rich Republican friends all aspire to see their children attend.\par \par \uc2 FROST: Exactly, exactly. We see this with Republican policies all the time. They want to legislate away just to give themselves wedge issues for elections. But all this legislation really doesn`t impact them or their children, and I`m talking about the leaders of the Republican Party. But it`s for everyone else.\par \par \uc2 It makes me think about this whole thing in Florida, where they`re always talking about socialism, and then they go and legislate and give socialism to corporations, and then harsh competitive for working families everywhere else. It`s the same thing with the book banning. And it`s complete hypocrisy and people see through that.\par \par \uc2 And I think with DeSantis now being on even more of a national stage, people are going to reject this far-right fascism, is what it is, just defining what it is, book banning, telling people they`re not who they are, criminalizing immigrants and doing everything that he can do to set himself up to run for president, rather than run the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman Maxwell Frost, thank you very much for joining our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 FROST: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: A mass murderer at Robb Elementary School in Uvalde, Texas occurred one year ago today. \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  went to Uvalde after that massacre, where he mourned with the families of the 19 children and two teachers who were murdered.\par \par \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:40]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: One year ago today in Uvalde, Texas 19 children and two teachers at Robb Elementary School were murdered by a graduate of that school, using an assault rifle that left the children in some cases unrecognizable. And according to an emergency room doctor, actually decapitated two children.\par \par \uc2 Today, three grieving mothers spoke with NBC News.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:45]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GLORIA CAZARES, MOTHER OF UVALDE VICTIM JACKIE CAZARES: The pain, the anguish, it`s just like it was yesterday. But not being able to hold your daughter, give her a kiss, say I love you, it feels like an eternity.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA MATA, MOTHER OF UVALDE VICTIM, TESS MATA: It is hard walking into our house, there`s nobody in our house anymore. And we just want it to stop.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY RUBIO, MOTHER OF UVALDE VICTIM LEXI RUBIO: It`s not fair. it`s not fair that she only got ten years. It`s not fair that I only got ten years with her. None of it is fair.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Flanked by 21 candles to commemorate each of the victims of the Uvalde mass murder President Biden sent this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Too many schools, too many everyday places have become killing field in communities all across every part of America. And at each place you hear the same message. Do something. For God`s sake, please do something.\par \par \uc2 I know for a long time it has been hard to make progress. But there will come a point where our voices are so loud, our determination is so clear that they can no longer be stopped. We will act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2 , former Democratic congressman who represented El Paso, Texas. Thank you very much for joining us on this difficult night.\par \par \uc2 It was a year ago when you got the word about what happened in Uvalde. Tell us what it was like when you went there?\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE, FORMER CONGRESSMAN, EL PASO TEXAS: Well I came the following day and the timing of my arrival happened to coincide with a press conference that Governor Greg Abbott was holding. And like everyone in that community who was interested in finding the truth and understanding what the response would be I showed up.\par \par \uc2 And to hear the governor say, first thing almost out of his mouth is it could have been worse to this community that had just lost 19 children and those two extraordinary teachers, was almost too much to take. But we listened to his full comments. And nowhere in them did he ever identify the problem, this access to AR-15s and almost limitless rounds of ammunition purchased by an 18-year-old who had set off so many red flags, that his friends called him or referred to him as the school shooter.\par \par \uc2 And instead, described it as a mental health care issue though we are dead last in the nation in mental health care spending. And he had just cut more than $200 million from the Texas mental health care budget.\par \par \uc2 From him we did not receive truth. But when we went out into the community and met with the parents, meeting with Jess and Ryan who had just lost their daughter Olivia not even 24 hours before.\par \par \uc2 Then we got the truth. And we began to understand what had happened to this community. The devastation, the suffering, and the loss. But Lawrence, I`ll tell you in the year since then, I have stayed in touch with those parents and those family members. And they are the most extraordinary people I have ever met. They are leading this state and they`re leading this country in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 Though they know they will never get their kids back, they are fighting for all of our kids. They are holding vigil at the state legislator here in Texas to get their age raised to buy an AR-15 to 21. They are forcing the school district in Uvalde to hold those police officers accountable for their failure for 77 minutes to intervene and save the lives of those kids.\par \par \uc2 Those parents some of whom you just featured in that opening clip are my heroes, and I will follow them to the ends of the earth, until we get the change that they are working and fighting for and that their children deserved. But that they are going to accomplish for all of our children.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, I cannot imagine what they have been through. And I mean that literally and fully. I cannot imagine what they have been through. What have you learned from them?\par \par \uc2 O`ROURKE: I have learned what courage and service and leadership looks like. I will give you one example. Brett Cross lost his son Uziyah, they call him Uzi on May 24th, 2022. Within weeks from that moment, he was posted up outside of the Uvalde school district headquarters, literally sleeping on a cot for ten days to force the accountability that he and every member of that community deserved. And he ultimately got every member of that law enforcement body suspended so that they could proceed with an investigation.\par \par \uc2 He has since been joined by people like Kim and Felix Rubio, who you just featured Kim, a second ago. The Cazares family, Faith Mata, the older sister of Tess Mata who has testified before the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 . They have testified before the Texas State House. And in a small but very important victory, they were able to pass a bill out of committee in Texas to raise the age to 21.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:00]\par \par \uc2 O`ROURKE: Now, in the dead of night that bill was killed by Republican leadership. But in order to get it passed out of committee, they had to convince two Republicans to vote with the majority, with the rest of the Democrats on that panel. And one of those Republicans said that the only reason he voted for that was the testimony, the persistence, the perseverance of those parents. They are making a difference.\par \par \uc2 And though we are deeply dissatisfied, we haven`t seen the amount of progress that they and all of us deserve in Texas so far. I am betting on them. And I`m going to follow them, and I`m going to work with them and as are millions of my fellow Texans to make sure that we finally get there.\par \par \uc2 And when we do, it will be because of these families and these parents who lost everything in the world but are still staying in this fight for all of us.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us on this important night.\par \par \uc2 O`ROURKE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:58]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Beto O`Rourke\uc2  gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230525ej5o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May 23, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,437 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 SOLEIL HO, OPINION COLUMNIST, SAN FRANCISCO CHRONICLE: I mean, trans individuals just around the world have the highest rates of suicide in the LGBT community. And --\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 HO: You just feel so lost otherwise.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes. Well, thank you so much for going undercover and getting us that amazing story. Soleil Ho, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN" with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have a Trump-hating judge with a Trump-hating wife and family.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , back in court, as a judge warns him to watch his mouth. Tonight, the trial date for the people of the state of New York against \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then, the latest on the man with a Nazi flag arrested for threatening the President. Plus --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m really tired of the Republicans acting like they own this state.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The outrageous move by Ohio Republicans undermining democracy to ban abortion. And this poem read at the inauguration of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is now banned from a classroom in DeSantis-run Florida.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Because being American is more than a pride we inherit. It`s the past we step into and how we repair it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appeared in Manhattan Court virtually for the first time since his arraignment on 34 criminal counts of falsifying business records. And Judge Juan Merchan announced that the case related to the ex-president`s hush money payments to adult film actress Stormy Daniels will go to trial in March of next year.\par \par \uc2 That means that if all this holds, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be on trial smack dab in the middle of the presidential primary. This afternoon, he presented himself as if he were still in the White House, sitting with his attorney in front of a customary wall of American flags. The ex-president`s live feed from Florida was projected on several screens throughout the courtroom, packed with reporters, and a team of no less than nine prosecutors from district attorney Alvin Bragg`s office.\par \par \uc2 Now, the main purpose of that hearing today was for the judge to officially advise Trump about the protective order in the case. The order, typically a room team part of the discovery process, prohibits the ex-president from speaking or posting publicly about the evidence he sees as the proceedings move forward. He will only be allowed to view sensitive materials that prosecutors are required to turn over, including emails, photographs, and witness testimony, in the presence of his lawyers.\par \par \uc2 And the ex-president will not be allowed to, "copy, photograph, transcribe, or otherwise independently possess the materials." It`s key for this guy, as we know. The judge`s order specifically mentions Trump`s fake \uc2 Twitter\uc2  website, Truth Social, as a platform where he cannot share this evidence.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, Trump shares lots of things he shouldn`t on Truth Social, and into nearly every microphone that he sees and uses. Trump even attacked district attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  and the judge, Merchan, on the evening after his arraignment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The criminal is the district attorney because he illegally leaked massive amounts of grand jury information, for which he should be prosecuted or at a minimum he should resign.\par \par \uc2 I have a Trump-hating judge with a Trump-hating wife and family whose daughter worked for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and now receives money from the Biden- Harris campaign and a lot of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Leading up to the judge`s decision and today`s hearing, prosecutors actually argued that, "the order was necessary in part because of Trump`s repeated public trashing of two of the witnesses of their case, former Trump attorney \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  and Stormy Daniels. And in those previous filings, Trump`s lawyers countered, the order was, "extremely restrictive, that it would be an unprecedented and extraordinarily broad muzzle on a leading contender for the presidency of the United States."\par \par \uc2 Today, Trump lawyer Todd Blanche told the judge his client is, "concerned his First Amendment rights are being violated. But Judge Merchan disagreed, reiterating that it is not a gag order, that the ex-president is, "free to campaign and do anything that does not violate the order." For his part, Trump spoke up only once to answer the judge`s question about whether he`d received a copy of the protective order. During the rest of the brief hearing, Trump appeared dower according to MSNBC Legal Analyst Lisa Rubin in the courtroom. At one point, he vociferously shook his head and waved his hands as if to signal no way when told his March 25, 2024 trial date is firm.\par \par \uc2 Rubin also notes Trump`s next appearance in the Manhattan court will come no later than January 4, 2024, meaning he will be back in front of the judge just days before the third anniversary of the January 6 attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:05]\par \par \uc2 So, what can we take away from all these developments? Well, we know, the judge knows, Trump`s lawyers know, everybody knows he`s going to use whatever platform he has available to bully and outright intimidate anyone or anything standing in his way up to and including the justice system and judges. He`s done it before. And now, this judge, knowing that, is putting up very specific guardrails to try to prevent the ex-president from being able to do that in this case.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the judge even warned Trump that if he does not obey the protective order, he could face a, "wide-range of possible sanctions, including a finding of contempt." Now, of course, this comes just two weeks after we got just an object lesson in all this, right? Two weeks after Trump was found liable for sexually abusing and defaming a woman who accuses him of rape, E. Jean Carroll, and ordered to pay her $5 million -- and the thing is, the ex-president can`t seem to stop defaming her over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Carroll asked a judge to update her other pending defamation suit against Trump, seeking, "very substantial new damages in light of Trump`s recent comments about her at a nationally televised town hall. Carroll is now asking for at least $10 million in that suit. But even that is not deterred Trump. Just this morning in a post on Truth Social, he called her claims, "fake made-up story." Again, a jury found that was not true. Adding, "it never happened, is a total scam." Again, lengthy trial, jury of his peers, unanimously concluding he`s wrong and she`s right.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, the only way to constrain \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is with punishment and accountability. It increasingly looks like some may be heading his way in one of the several ongoing investigations in the ex-president`s behavior because the Wall Street Journal reports today, Special Counsel Jack Smith is "wrapping up his investigation into Trump`s handling of classified documents at Mar-a-Lago." People familiar with the matter tell the Journal that the Special Counsel has all but finished obtaining testimony and other evidence.\par \par \uc2 They say in recent weeks, prosecutors working for Smith have completed interviews with nearly every employee at Trump`s Florida home from top political aides to maids and maintenance staff. And prosecutors have pressed those witnesses on questions that appear to be honed in on specific elements of Smith`s team when need to show to prove a crime. Meanwhile, people in the ex-president`s circle said that some of his close associates are bracing for his indictment and get this, anticipate being able to fundraise off a prosecution.\par \par \uc2 This is the lesson of all of this of the many years that we`ve spent covering this individual, the investigations and legal challenges against him, is that you cannot send some ground rules, you cannot anticipate he will just not do the worst thing, it won`t work, he will barrel right over them until he faces some kind of real accountability. And we are finally seeing the first real versions of that. I mean, potential incoming charges in from the Special Counsel, a $5 million verdict against him that could very well go up in another related suit, and an actual real-life criminal trial that`s going to begin right now in March, next year.\par \par \uc2 Harry Litman is a former U.S. attorney and legal affairs columnist for the \uc2 Los Angeles Times\uc2 . Jose Pagliery is the political investigations reporter at the Daily Beast. He was in the courtroom in Manhattan for Donald Trump`s hearing today. And both joined me now.\par \par \uc2 Jose, let me just start with you about the -- your read on the room and what the proceeding was like.\par \par \uc2 JOSE PAGLIERY, POLITICAL INVESTIGATIONS REPORTER, THE DAILY BEAST: Well, I`ve got to tell you, I was really struck by how this is essentially a preview of a showdown we`re going to increasingly see in the coming months. And that is between this caged tiger of a former president and the American justice system.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve got to tell you, I was pretty disappointed by what I saw in the courtroom because what I was expecting going into this hearing was a judge who was going to really lay down the law and tell the former president, you cannot do this. Don`t talk about me, don`t talk about my daughter, don`t target prosecutors. And instead, we`ve got Justice Merchan being very cautious, using gentle wording to remind the president`s counsel that there could be consequences if he doesn`t abide by this.\par \par \uc2 But let`s be clear, what Trump has done and what he appears to be doing is not violating this protective order. It doesn`t seem like he`s going to be sharing the secrets he gets from his lawyers, but what he is continuing to do is attack the judge and the process.\par \par \uc2 I mean, just an hour after the hearing was over, he took the truth social and called the entire thing election interference, basically blaming the judge for the timing of this trial to mess with his aspirations to return to the White House. But I`ve got to tell you, there was a notable power dynamic there where with Trump, with two American flags behind him, he was not portraying himself as the defendant in this case.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAGLIERY: I mean, I would argue that he was -- he was sort of showing, having a show of power, saying like, I am the former president, you are a mere judge. You`ve got one flag behind you, I`ve got two. Defendants don`t do this, right? For him to do this, that was a show. I mean, he was putting -- it was sort of like, muted theatrics, which is extremely awkward when we have this much on the line.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:13]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, the showdown you mentioned there, Jose, is an interesting one. It`s going to be something that a bunch of -- I suspect, a number of different judges will have to navigate. Right now, Judge Merchan has to navigate it.\par \par \uc2 And Harry, there are these first amendment issues, right? I mean, like, I get the sort of caution here and also the fairly unprecedented territory and line he is walking here. How do you interpret this order and the sort of balancing test he might be using about a gag order that would be more broad, like, go after my daughter, for instance?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, LEGAL AFFAIRS COLUMNIST, LOS ANGELES TIMES: Yes, or don`t try to pollute the jury pool or a foment danger. I think it`s pretty straightforward, actually. And I take your point but, you know, I think he came nose to nose with the judicial system more than he has at any time so far. And it was pretty clear who was boss, and it wasn`t him. And by all accounts, at least this and others, he was seething and glowering.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the report, he`s gotten millions of pages now. There`s only one justified use for it that`s to prepare his defense and not to wave around, you know, for read me talking points in political rallies. So, if those -- if that information appears in the public realm, that`s when Merchan will really, you know, bring the hammer down, I think.\par \par \uc2 And I`m not surprised that he didn`t go incredibly starchy against him. This could be, you know, something that he`s going to have to justify on appeal. But I think he made it pretty clear who`s the boss and it isn`t \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And going forward is just a matter of whether all these pages in discovery stay with Trump.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, in terms of keeping him on a short lease and almost infantilizing him, you can only look at it with your lawyers. You know, he really, I think, came close to slapping him around with a robe on politely.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, in terms of that, Jose, this is striking that part of the protective order. And also, we`ve seen this with other judges, right? We saw this a little bit in the -- in the E. Jean Carroll case. There`s a little bit of this sense in which the judge is trying to sort of work with the lawyer to get some kind of compliance out of the client, right?\par \par \uc2 And we`ve seen Tacopina say this to the judge. Like, you know, I`m doing my best. He says, I`m not -- this idea that like, you`re only going to be able to view these documents with your lawyer is an attempt essentially to create some kind of ground rules there, particularly give him what he did with the classified documents that were not -- you know, developing the other case.\par \par \uc2 PAGLIERY: Now, we did see that in the E. Jean Carroll case. I mean, I covered that wall to wall. And I can tell you that the federal judge in that instance, Lou Kaplan, was really harsh. He made very clear that Trump was walking the line and there could be some serious consequences to it. And you could tell that the judge was frustrated at Trump`s counsel`s inability to really communicate with their client and get that message through.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to see that a whole lot more. I mean, if the indictment comes down in Atlanta with the Fulton County DA, if the special counsel, Jack Smith, comes down with one or two different indictments with the two different investigations he`s got going, we`ll see that in federal court again.\par \par \uc2 And so, what I`m getting at here is judges are increasingly showing the trouble they`re having in dealing with Trump just flaunt through it. He`s taunting the judge constantly and we`re gonna keep seeing this in months to come. What I`m concerned about is whether or not the American justice system is capable of actually following through and making sure there are consequences. Because the showdown I`m worried about is one that pits politics with the justice system. And we really are going to see this, not a theoretical fight. We`re going to see this play out in the next 18 months.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No, in fact, when Trump is going no, like this about the trial, like they`re going to try to get that child move, right? And the argument is going to be -- and of course this is -- Harry, this is part of the sort of entire logic of the presidential campaign, which was done prophylactically in part if reporting is to believed and just Occam`s razor to essentially provide some kind of immunity or insurance or protection from these criminal trials.\par \par \uc2 When we get to the Jack Smith situation, I`m curious, Harry, as someone who worked both as a U.S. attorney and then inside main justice, what your read on that reporting is about the sort of coming to an end which felt to me like, dot, dot, dot, some decision is going to happen soon and it`s going to go up to the main guy, the attorney general, and then we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Look, we`ve been -- we`ve been thinking we`re at an end game for a long time. And Smith has really shown himself to want to be methodical. And when the grand jury is around, he can use it to investigate in a way he can`t when it`s done. But if the Journal reporting is to be believed, and they source it to people who know, yes, he really is at an end. And I can`t think of other rocks he hasn`t turned over for God`s sakes, all the maintenance staff and the like.\par \par \uc2 What`s it going to look like? He`s going to do a prosecution memo. It`s going to go to the attorney general. Almost certainly it will recommend -- and I`m talking about the documents case.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:06]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Prosecution, I can`t imagine his going this far and then bailing. And almost certainly, Garland will approve it. And this -- if the -- if the Journal is right, that will be in short order, but if not, it will be an intermediate order. That is almost certainly coming. And talk about a, you know, come up and it 