ner, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , has not only trafficked in conspiracy theories about what happened in Allen, Texas, but created a platform where users can pay for amplification via the verification system. So, if you tried to find information on this attack, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s trending topics were flooded with paid verified accounts claiming that this attack was not legitimate, that it was a set up by law enforcement organizations.\par \par \uc2 And that is a very dangerous thing when you have these lone wolf attacks that are intended to create fear, intended to scare people into living within the fear that at any moment in time they too might be the victim of one of these attacks.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it`s a great point. And I saw some of that as well on the right, very similar to the Allen, Texas, where, oh, this -- it`s all too neat. It`s all too obvious that this -- well, it`s like, well, yes, Nazis tend to be pretty clear. Like, you had a swastika --\par \par \uc2 RAMIREZ: Pretty exclusive.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- on your body or you carry around a Nazi flag. It`s not -- you know, it`s not a lot of subterfuge there. I`m glad that everyone seems OK and uninjured here. I suspect we will find out more. Nikki McCann Ramirez, thank you very much for your great reporting. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 RAMIREZ: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, the brazen move by Ohio Republicans. This one`s a really good one, all right. Well, it`s terrible but the story, they want to try to change the Constitution of Ohio to stop a majority of their constituents from protecting abortion rights. I`m going to tell you all about it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, the argument from the Federal Society and the anti-abortion or at lest some of them for overturning Roe v. Wade was and has been that Roe wrongly took the issue of abortion rights away from the people and that the Dobbs decision that overturned Roe then returned the democratic process to its rightful place and to the people in the states.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  in his concurring opinion stated that simply that argument quite simply. He wrote the Dobbs decision "properly leaves the question of abortion for the people in the democratic process. Through that democratic process, the people and their representatives may decide to allow or limit abortion."\par \par \uc2 [20:30:09]\par \par \uc2 Now, as poll after poll have shown, the people aren`t that interested in bans on abortion, the kinds that have come in the wake of the Dobbs decision. Abortion bans are really incredibly unpopular. And so, Republicans and conservatives have been using all sorts of increasingly anti-democratic subterfuge to have their way on banning abortion over the objections of the majority of their own voters.\par \par \uc2 Take North Carolina, for example, right? Republican in the state legislature recently gained a super-majority when this woman, Tricia Cotham, a Democrat who ran on abortion rights and co-sponsored a bill to codify Roe in the state`s constitution, inexplicably switched parties. And she gave them a super majority, and then conservatives and Republicans then used their new veto-proof super majority to pass an abortion ban in North Carolina over the objections of the electorate, it polled pretty poorly, and over the veto of the governor who`s a Democrat and was democratically elected statewide.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s North Carolina. Now, here`s what`s happening in Ohio right now. That state`s six-week abortion ban is currently tied up in court. It`s 22- week ban is still in effect. There`s a coalition of organizations that are currently collecting hundreds of thousands of signatures to put the question of a constitutional amendment to enshrine reproductive rights in the state`s constitution on the ballot in November.\par \par \uc2 The people, right, voting or in Justice Kavanaugh`s words, to leave the question of abortion for the people in the democratic process. Similar efforts to support abortion rights have been very successful, even in states that are pretty conservative like Kentucky. And Ohio Republicans and anti-abortion activists know this. So, what they`re trying to do is take the decision out of the hands of majority of Ohio voters. They introduced their own constitutional amendment that would raise the ballot initiative threshold that has been in place for, get this, over 100 years from a simple majority of 50 percent plus one to 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans are now trying to do this and get it done before that pro- abortion rights constitutional amendment can get on November`s ballot. Meaning they want to put their own anti-democratic proposal up for a vote during a special election in August even though Republicans in Ohio themselves recently passed a law outlawing the majority of August special elections, not to mention a bipartisan group of Ohio election officials formally voted to oppose the Republican bill because they don`t want to run a statewide election in August.\par \par \uc2 The Republican`s play here as being challenged in court, but it`s clear what`s going on here, right? A maneuver designed explicitly to motivate hardcore anti-abortion proponents to come out in the dead of summer in a low-turnout election so then they can move the goalposts and make it nearly impossible for a simple majority of Ohioans to decide to secure abortion rights in their state.\par \par \uc2 No one has been chronicling this movement of anti-democratic, anti- majoritarian politics and states more than David Pepper. He`s the author of Laboratories of Autocracy: A Wake-Up Call From Behind The Lines, and the new book Saving Democracy: A User`s Manual for Every American. He`s also the former chair of the Ohio Democratic Party, and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID PEPPER, FORMER CHAIR, OHIO DEMOCRATIC PARTY: Great to be with you. Although it makes my blood boil hearing that summary.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, it is really -- it`s almost -- I mean, it`s not comical because the stakes are high, but it`s so -- the sort of manifest pretext behind the whole thing always just strikes me.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: It`s outrageous, I mean, the whole sequence. And keep in mind, this legislature itself is a result of multiple violations of Supreme Court orders that this map is itself gerrymandered. So, their legitimacy is already in question. This is an unconstitutional map that led to these guys in the first place. We`re piling lawlessness on top of lawlessness over and over again, and this is what you get when that happens.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, because you`ve got -- it`s one of these states -- it`s not quite as bad as Wisconsin or North Carolina, but it`s in the ballpark where you`ve got a state that`s a pretty Republican state at this point, it`s a plus-seven state, plus-eight in presidential elections. But you`ve got enormous majorities, right, in both houses because they set those gerrymandered.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: They would not have had the votes to approve this measure a couple weeks ago without the illegal gerrymandered they`re living within. So, that`s just one other level of lawlessness.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, they`ve got the gerrymander, and they use that supermajority to put this on the ballot. They`re now going to put -- they`re going to put this on the ballot. Are they honest about why they`re doing this? Like, when they -- when give the argument for why suddenly it has to be a 60 percent threshold?\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: No, it`s a joke. I mean, they`re saying they need to protect the Constitution. For the first time in 100 years, they have to protect the Constitution months before what you just described, a citizen-led ballot initiative to actually have the people vote on abortion access.\par \par \uc2 By the way, when the Supreme Court came out with that opinion, it was gas- sliding from the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Sure. Yes.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: They know that most of these states are gerrymandered. They do not reflect the people. Kansas showed that with its referendum last August. And these legislators know it. That`s why every poll in Ohio shows that about close to 60 percent -- and that`s why that`s such an important number, close to 60 percent of Ohioans support Roe v. Wade in general abortion access. Almost none support abortion bans, no exceptions that said that 10- year-old girl to Indiana who was raped.\par \par \uc2 These guys know that. And as you`ve described, it`s so blatant. They`re moving the goalpost. But let`s be clear. Now, I hope this comes out this way. I`m not sure it will. They themselves made a special election August illegal.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:30]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: And they did not revoke that law.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK. So, this is another great layer on top of this. So, basically they`re on this race, right? They see -- the abortion rights advocates are going to get this ballot in the show in November.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, they`re like, well, we want to raise the threshold, change the rules of the game before they can get there in November. So, we have to have our election here in August.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: Correct.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In the dead of August.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, the state -- the county election officials who are a lot of them were Republican are like, what the hell are you doing? We don`t want this, right? But they have already passed a law saying you can`t have special elections in August.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: Yes. And they tried to get the votes to repeal that law, but they didn`t have the vote, so they just stopped trying to repeal it and they`re just saying we should have it anyway. By the way, the Secretary of State announced to those elections officials months ago the great victory of getting rid of these August elections. It was a bipartisan victory.\par \par \uc2 The author of the August election idea himself said this was a career-long goal of mine to get rid of August elections. Months later, they can`t repeal it, but they still demand an election on a date where it`s currently illegal to have a statewide special election in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, this is where we get back to these sort of layers of anti- democratic --\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, that challenge to the legality of this August state election is going to go to a state --\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: The Ohio Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And what`s -- how are we doing over in the Ohio of the State Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: Unfortunately, we had our Wisconsin moment a couple of years ago. We actually got ourselves an independent court. Then they changed those rules and added party label to the ballot, moved those races up to the top and now they have themselves --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, I forgot about that. Right.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: There`s another one.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The court changed hands.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: It did. We had a moderate Chief Justice who was a longtime Republican who with three Democrats voted against every gerrymander map. But in the meantime, they switched the rules of how we elect justices, like they did North Carolina, and then violated for a year all the court orders. As I always say, if we saw this in another country, we would be saying, my God, you have no rule of law. Well, that`s Ohio right now in 2023.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And it`s not just Ohio. I mean, we have seen similar stuff happen in Wisconsin. We`ve seen it happen in North Carolina. There`s proposals in Missouri right now. I mean, your first book, which I read and is excellent, really does a really good job of talking about this. I mean, the flip side of it, which I think has been somewhat successful in Ohio is there really has been mobilization by voters. And Ohioans be like, we don`t -- this is not OK.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: It reminds me of 2011, the anti-collective bargaining bill that we rescinded. Right now, you`re seeing it just so over the top, it`s so blatantly dishonest. And you even have Former Governor Taft, who was a former Secretary of State, John Kasich, former Attorney General, all saying that this is outrageous. So, I hope that we`re seeing a bill -- the Libertarian Party came out against this. It`s very conservative groups who themselves want to bring things to the ballot are saying, this is too much.\par \par \uc2 So, my hope is -- and by the way, one other thing, backdrop. This is the most corrupt state house in the country according to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . The Speaker of the House, who had to resign --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I remember.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: -- will be sentenced in June for the largest bribery scale in the history of Ohio. So, this is against the backdrop of corruption. That trial is still playing out. And so, a state house normally would respond with reform, but they`re responding, grab more power from the voters, more power from themselves.\par \par \uc2 So, my hope is, there`s enough of buildup that we crushed this thing in August. And one of the things that`s really important here is, if this passes in Ohio, they do operate as laboratories of autocracy. Let`s be clear.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They`ll do it anywhere.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: Other states will do it. So, we can all be the country --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Why stop at 60? Make it 80.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, right. I mean, this is --\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: So, crush in Ohio so that the billionaire -- there`s this big Illinois billionaire backing the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: I don`t remember. His name is Uihlein or something.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, yes, Uihlein.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: Uihlein. I want him to feel like this was such a monumental waste of money. It`s not worth doing anywhere else. It`s too much. It`s too much of a power grab. And that`s -- again, if this passes, it`s coming everywhere that they can do this. If it fails, then I think we make progress beyond Ohio. It`s that important.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, David Pepper, whose new book is called Saving Democracy: A User`s Manual For Every American, out now. Thanks so much for coming in.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: Thanks. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, how one single solitary complaint from one single parent got a poem from the first ever National Youth Poet Laureate banded at a school in Florida. It`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: An 8-year-old child named Anadith Tanay Reyes Alvarez who was detained with her family this month after crossing to South Texas died after a prolonged stay in Customs and Border Patrol custody. Anadith`s family had been at an immigration processing facility for four days when she started having abdominal pain, nasal congestion, and the cough.\par \par \uc2 Her mother took her to the medical facility where Anadith tested positive for flu with a temperature of 101.8. She was given over-the-counter medications. But as the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  writes, days later, on May 17th, the girls condition appeared to worsen. She and her mother went three times in the medical unit at the border station complaining of vomiting. Later a stomach ache.\par \par \uc2 CBP medical contractors listed her condition as stable according to the agency`s timeline, but at 1:55, the child`s mother returned carrying her daughter who appeared to be having a seizure. Paramedics arrived, 12 minutes later rushed Anadith to the hospital in Harlingen. She was pronounced dead less than an hour later according to the timeline.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:20]\par \par \uc2 Now, according to Customs and Border Patrol, Anadith was not initially sent to a hospital despite the agency knowing about a medical history of heart disease and sickle cell anemia. CBP`s acting commissioner has now ordered a review of medical practices at its facilities. At the time of her death, and this is crucial, Anadith and her family had been held for eight days in CBP custody. The agency policy is that migrants should not be detained more than 72 hours. The processing can get backed up, just lead to longer detentions, overcrowding and unsafe conditions.\par \par \uc2 In the week before Anadith`s death, a 17-year-old boy named Angel Eduardo Maradiaga Espinoza died in detention in Florida. In total, three migrant children have died in U.S. custody this year. That is three too many. It goes without saying.\par \par \uc2 The core problem here is there are more people coming across the border seeking asylum than there is space to detain them for processing. That is irrespective of the swarms of migrants Republicans and Fox News warned about this month which we should note, never really materialized.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration runs CBP. It is responsible morally for these deaths and responsible for a full public explanation of just what went so horribly wrong. But the thing is it is not just the Biden administration that is running our immigration policy. You see, earlier this month, the White House planned to direct CBP to start releasing asylum seekers without a court date and tell them to report to an immigration customs enforcement office. It is known as humanitarian parole and it can prevent overcrowding in processing centers.\par \par \uc2 That has been blocked however by a Trump-appointed federal judge in Florida. A move in line with conservative judges and right-wing activists and Republicans who wanted to detain people for as long as possible as a kind of deterrent.\par \par \uc2 People talk about our broken immigration system all the time, well known that there is a humanitarian crisis at our southern border particularly on the Mexican side where thousands of people are now awaiting asylum. But when you have children as young as eight years old dying in our custody, U.S. custody, it is an obvious sign, a red flag and alarm the current system is profoundly broken, and if nothing changes it is going to happen again.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just two weeks after January 6, an incredibly poignant and powerful moment, 22-year-old Amanda Gorman stood up at President Biden`s inauguration and read one of her poems.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: We will not be turned around or interrupted by intimidation because we know our inaction and inertia will be the inheritance of the next generation. Our blenders become their burdens. But one thing is certain if emerged mercy with might and might with right, then love becomes our legacy and change our children`s birthright.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That poem, The Hill We Climb, is now banned for elementary classes at a school of Miami-Dade after one parent, just a single parent complained the poem was not educational and had, I quote directly, indirectly hate -- is not educational -- indirectly hate messages. That its purpose was to cause confusion and indoctrinate students.\par \par \uc2 That one complaint was enough for the school to ban elementary school kids from reading that poem by the first-ever National Youth Port Laureate and the youngest-ever inaugural poet. That was made possible by one man and his, you know, facilitators in the legislature, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Amanda Gorman has responded to the ban saying, "I am gutted. I wrote The Hill We Climb so that all young people could see themselves in a historical moment. Robbing children of the chance to find their voices in literature is a violation of their right to free thought and free speech."\par \par \uc2 She also encourage people to speak out pointing to a lawsuit. Her publisher has joined along with Pen America and others against another Florida school district in Escambia County, that has banned books.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Suzanne Nossel, who`s the CEO of Pen America and Lindsay Durtschi, who has two children in that district, and is also a plaintiff in the suit. It`s good to have you both here.\par \par \uc2 Let me start with you, Suzanne, just in terms of level setting on these book bands and why they`re happening in Florida. Like, there`s a little bit of ambiguity here, right? They pass a law that essentially routinizes parental complaints as a means of getting books reviewed -- removed, but then the state doesn`t have to sort of take credit for it happening. Like, how does it actually happen and what`s the contestation of your lawsuit?\par \par \uc2 SUZANNE NOSSEL, CEO PEN AMERICA: Sure. Look, there are two laws in Florida, what we call the, Don`t Say Gay Law, the law that prohibits discussion of LGBTQ topics in schools, and then a law that does similarly when it comes to discussions about race that are contentious, that could make somebody feel perhaps guilty about their race.\par \par \uc2 And what those two laws have done is create an enabling environment for book banning. And in many districts, as you say, it takes only a single individual -- it doesn`t necessarily even have to be a parent to lodge an objection. I mean, that objection to Amanda Gorman`s book is illustrative. It`s a few lines just scribbled out. The person clearly hasn`t read the poem. They don`t give any detail. And that is enough to get a book pulled from the shelves for a review process that can go on for months and months, denying kids the book.\par \par \uc2 In Escambia, it`s particularly egregious, because there is a review panel of educators and librarians who look at these books, but time and again, the school board has overridden them. They`ve decided these books have merit, they belong in the classroom, there`s value here that the kids can draw and the school board says, no, you know, these are contentious, we think we can score political or ideological points by whisking these books away, and that`s what they`ve done.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:19]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Lindsey, you`re a parent in Escambia County. Why did you choose to join this lawsuit?\par \par \uc2 LINDSAY DURTSCHI, PLAINTIFF SUING FLORIDA SCHOOL DISTRICT: Well, Chris, restricted access to information and viewpoint censorship violates our students` First Amendment rights. And I think there are so many teachers and parents that just don`t feel safe speaking up against it, so I thought it was time that I say something.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Have you had experience firsthand, like, in terms of your kid`s schools or in terms of other parents lodging complaints for books. Has this energized, what seems to me, a relatively small group of obsessives, a lot of file complaints about books, but have you seen this firsthand in Escambia county where your kids go to school?\par \par \uc2 DURTSCHI: 100 percent, yes. Primarily at, you know, school board meetings and moments where we`re talking about the books that these committees have reviewed, like Suzanne said.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Suzanne, what is the contention? I mean, Lindsey just said this about sort of First Amendment rights. What`s the substance of the lawsuit?\par \par \uc2 NOSSEL: It`s two-fold, 1st Amendment and 14th Amendment. The 1st Amendment aspect is that what these districts are doing, what Escambia is doing is picking and choosing on ideological grounds, which books are going to be taken off shelves. That`s viewpoint-based discrimination. It strikes right at the heart of what the First Amendment protects against and bars government from doing. So, that`s piece one.\par \par \uc2 The second piece is 14th Amendment. Overwhelmingly, the targeted books are books buying about authors of color, LGBTQ narratives, and so it`s quite clear there`s a discriminatory purpose. They`re trying to eradicate certain stories, certain individuals from the curriculum, and that`s discriminatory, and that violates kids` right to an equal education. And so we`re bringing both claims and trying to vindicate both sets of constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Lindsey, there`s this amazing \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reporting. This is looking at from 30,000 feet from -- nationally. An analysis of book challenges from across the nation shows the majority were filed by, and this is not a typo, just 11 people. So, there`s people that are really, really into this. But I do wonder, I guess the perception we end up having is that like, DeSantis did this, but he won a resounding reelection. He`s popular in Florida. This is what parents want. I`m just curious, like to the extent there`s conversation around the soccer field or at PTA, what your sense of the popularity or unpopularity of this is?\par \par \uc2 DURTSCHI: So, I`ve gotten nothing but positive feedback from our community from -- surprisingly, I`ve not gotten a lot of negative feedback at all, but I think that our district`s policy manual clearly says that it`s our school`s responsibility, our public school`s responsibility to provide a comprehensive collection of books that foster respect for diversity and cultural appreciation.\par \par \uc2 And so, I understand that it`s not going to resonate with everyone, but it does resonate with most people.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, Lindsay, I`m a parent of three school-aged children myself, and I see people make this argument that parents should be the ones that decide what`s in a kids` school library, and I`m like, well, you want me to go through a list of 200 book? I don`t -- that`s -- I can`t do that. Wait, every parent in the school? Like, do you think it`s a good idea to have one parent to exercise veto power over books in schools?\par \par \uc2 DURTSCHI: Well, of course not. And, you know, to quote Jody Pico, there`s absolutely nothing wrong with the parent deciding a certain book`s not right for their child, but there`s a colossal problem with the parent deciding, therefore, that no child should be able to read that book.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This has been filed, I believe, in federal court. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 NOSSEL: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There is, it seems, there`s, like, the legal aspect and the cultural aspect, which Pen, I think, is uniquely sort of situated. Sometimes I wonder, like, are we in the grips of a panic? Are we in the grips of a panic about the panic? Like, are there a lot of books really being banned? It does seem like the numbers bear this out. What is your sense of where we are, sort of, culturally in terms of this kind of censoriousness that we`re seeing?\par \par \uc2 NOSSEL: Look, I think Lindsay is right that the overwhelming majority of Americans don`t like book bans. It runs counter to our basic notions of our freedom to speak, freedom to read. And so, it`s a question of mobilizing people. I think there`s an effort here to play to a very particular constituency --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, the 11 people.\par \par \uc2 NOSSEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The 11 righteous book-banners.\par \par \uc2 NOSSEL: Right. And, you know, there`s an election on the horizon. And I think there`s a notion that certain people can get energized and motivated by this, and they`re going to play to that segment and roll them up, and it`s up to us to mobilize the rest, the majority. This is about parents` rights indeed. It`s not the rights of those 11 parents, it`s the right -- the rights of the millions of parents who`s access -- kids` access at stake.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Suzanne Nossel and Lindsay Durtschi, thank you both. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Tuesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230524ej5n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May 22, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,037 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:38]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN. Republican hostage-taking 101.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Biden would take the blame and that`s the one way to make sure Biden is not reelected.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, another round of negotiations as the clock ticks towards default. Then --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think this obstruction case is a type case. And yes, I do think he`ll go to jail.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The classified document investigation expands into Trump`s business dealings. As E. Jean Carroll says, she plans to seek more damages from the ex-president.\par \par \uc2 E. JEAN CARROLL, TRUMP ACCUSER: I love to have Robbie Kaplan and just shut him up.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And the latest chilling example of America in reverse as Nebraska`s governor signs a combination of anti-trans and anti-abortion law.\par \par \uc2 MACHAELA CAVANAUGH (D-NE), STATE SENATOR: Women will die. Children are dying. It is your fault. It is your fault. And you are allowing it to happen.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Well, we appear to be just days away from the potential collapse of the global economy. It`s a strange way to start the show, but that`s where we`re headed at least as of now. Again, I don`t think we`re yet there, but that`s the sort of the way that we`re going. And it`s amidst that, and the threat explicitly Republicans are threatening that President Biden held another round of negotiations with Republican Speaker of the House Kevin McCarthy over a deal to raise the so-called debt ceiling. That is the amount of money the U.S. is allowed to borrow.\par \par \uc2 There is still no deal ahead of a deadline that could come as soon as June 1st, although McCarthy called today`s meeting productive.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): They`re going to come back together, work through the night, and see if the President and I -- look, we know the deadline. I think the President and I are going to talk every day to try to find until we get this done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: McCarthy also decried "governing by a chaos, which is a bit rich, because he and his fellow Republicans are, of course, holding the economy hostage, threatening to default on the national debt unless Biden and Democrats agreed to federal spending cuts. And to be clear, these cuts would make life materially worse from millions of Americans. Cutting the budget by a fifth, 22 percent, which Republicans have proposed would result in -- here`s just some estimates, 80,000 students losing access to Pell Grants, 200,000 kids losing access to Head Start programs, 430,000 low- income families getting evicted, 640,000 American families losing access to rental assistance, one million poor Americans with young kids losing access to food assistance, two million individuals and families losing access to healthcare via community health centers, and many other terrible preventable outcomes.\par \par \uc2 And while a lot of folks are talking about how bad these spending cuts would be, or how catastrophic, truly, defaulting on the debt would be for the first time in American history, tonight I want to talk about something a little different because not enough people are talking about why this is all happening in the first place. And I mean, like, really, let`s just go down to fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 We`re watching the story, maybe you`ve seen it. What is this about? Why are we having a debt ceiling showdown? Why are Republicans threatening to tank the global economy and default for the first time in this country`s history unless they get their way to impose austerity and cut federal spending on those programs severely?\par \par \uc2 Now, the state of justification for the hostage-taking, you`re there everywhere, doesn`t make any sense. And this is really the key to understanding this whole thing. The sort of top-line answer, which, again, has been taken and for granted as the answer in most mainstream coverage, is that Republicans want to cut spending. And they want to cut spending because they have an ideological commitment to cutting spending.\par \par \uc2 You see that everywhere. Kevin McCarthy says it, the reporters by and large that are reporting on him tend to sort of go along with that. That`s the fight. It is simply not true. It is maddening to see it repeated because it cannot possibly be the actual answer. And this isn`t speculation or mind- reading on my part. These are just the facts, the history. This is an empirical situation.\par \par \uc2 We know that cannot be the answer because we have seen what the Republican Party does when it has control of the government and is free to pursue its agenda, when Republicans control both houses of Congress and the presidency. You were alive for this. I was too. We were all here. We saw it all happen, right?\par \par \uc2 What did they do? Well, they cut taxes for corporations and rich people. And they do that every time without fail, right? And that`s because the Republican Party really does believe in that. They really do believe in cutting taxes for corporations and rich people. They really do use their power to make it happen. It`s the one piece of legislation they`ll manage to get passed, even when they`re screwing up left and right. They did with \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , right? They did with Trump.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:34]\par \par \uc2 They push deregulation. They make life harder for undocumented immigrants. They restricted legal immigration. Sometimes they pick cultural fights. They tend to boost spending for defense. But one thing they do not do when they have unified control of the federal government is reduce spending. Again, this is just a historical fact. I`m not doing any mind reading. I`m just telling you the simple fact of the matter. When they have control of the government, they do the opposite of reducing spending. They increase spending when their party is in power, when they control the White House.\par \par \uc2 Why? Well, I think because in the main, government spending is popular and tends to boost the political fortunes of the White House and the president, in this case, a Republican president. It helps the Republican president get reelected.\par \par \uc2 Axios put together this handy chart of government spending. The purple line is defense, the brown line is everything else. You can see it there. Spending goes up during the administration of \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . And then it slows down a little bit -- see that little flat line -- during the Clinton years, OK. Again, that`s when \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was the president and then after the House got taken over by Newt Gingrich, Republicans had a big budget standoff and they imposed spending cuts, right?\par \par \uc2 Then what happens? Well, then spending skyrocketed under \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . OK, see? See how that line goes up? Right? Again, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  takes office. He`s got a House and Senate, right? Republicans, they forced the country into two very pointless, very expensive wars. They also ran up a lot of domestic spending. And then what happens after that? Oh, look, it drops again pretty dramatically under the Democratic president.\par \par \uc2 And see the dip around 2011 there? That`s the last time that Republicans were in opposition and held the House and held the debt ceiling hostage, the exact same playbook as right now. They demanded Democrats agree to painful, unpopular spending cuts or else Republicans would crater the economy. So, again, Republicans in power spent, spent, spent for decades while their president -- while they had the presidency, turned around and pretended to care about curbing spending once Democrats are in power, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , certainly brought Obama.\par \par \uc2 And again, why? What`s going on? Why are they not consistent, right? The answer is very simple and straightforward. They wanted to make it harder for Barack Obama to get reelected. Here`s \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  just days after Republicans retook Congress in 2010.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Over the past weeks, some have said it was indelicate of me to suggest that our top political priority over the next two years should be to deny President Obama a second term. But the fact is, if our primary legislative goals are to repeal and replace the health spending bill, to end the bailouts, cut spending, and shrink the size and scope of government, the only way to do all of those things is to put someone in the White House who won`t veto any of these things.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But they don`t want to shrink the size and scope of government. They just had control of the government under Georgia W. Bush and they did not shrink its scope for its size. Again, you can`t take what they`re saying at face value because it makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 So, Obama was forced to strike a deal to get the debt ceiling raised, which pushed austerity measures that appreciably slowed the recovery from the financial crisis. It made the economy worse. It created unnecessary misery for millions. It also made it worse than it should have been when he was running for a second term in 2012. You can see it right here in this chart. The top line is how much the government was projected to spend during the Obama years, the bottom line is how much the government actually spent after the Tea Party forced Democrats to cut spending.\par \par \uc2 See how the bottom line, the amount the country has spent is way lower than what originally was projected before Republicans demanded cuts? But then you`ll see something else. See how it kind of bows up there, it starts to go up? It looks like the bottom line starts to tick up around. Drum roll, please. When could it be? Ah, yes, 27 -- 2017, right after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was elected and Republicans controlled Congress. What did they do? They started spending, of course.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go back to the first chart for a second and look at government spending under Trump. Oh, yes, it skyrocketed. Look at the line shoot up. Non-military spending, in particular, went way up under Trump and the Republicans. In fact, Republicans spent so much, partnered with massive tax cuts to the rich and corporations, the deficit increased nearly 40 percent under Trump.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:10]\par \par \uc2 Now, again, I`m belaboring this a little bit, but I just, I can`t bear it, OK? I think at this point, everyone sort of agrees Republicans don`t actually care about the federal deficit. That was the old excuse, and no one buys it anymore. Clearly, they don`t. You can`t care about the deficit while you`re also pushing massive tax cuts to the rich. Untenable. But people still haven`t really come around to the even more basic idea that Republicans, they don`t care about spending either the size and scope of government. But that`s what the numbers say. Again, this is just -- I`m just reading you the history.\par \par \uc2 So, then the question becomes, this is a little bit of a mystery -- well, not really a big one, but the question is, if Republicans don`t care about spending, which they don`t, then why are they forcing a crisis over the debt ceiling? What`s it really all about? And again, the answer is very simple. It is about asserting dominance over the Democratic president and hurting his political fortunes.\par \par \uc2 I know that sounds cynical, but there is literally no other way to interpret the remarkably consistent position Republicans take about these matters of spending. President Biden himself acknowledged as much over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think there are some MAGA Republicans in the House who know the damage that it would do to the economy. And because I am president, and president is responsible for everything, Biden would take the blame and that`s the one way to make sure Biden is not reelected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Look at it this way. Republicans recognize high degrees of spending under a president tend to be politically popular. In fact, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  admitted as much back in 2019. Everyone else has forgotten this quote, but I have not. This is tattooed in my brain because back in 2019, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reported, "\uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  told President Trump privately that no politician has ever lost an election for spending more money."\par \par \uc2 Inverse is also true. Lowering the amount of government spending tends to hurt the economy, hurt American voters, tends to be politically unpopular. So, again, the Republican calculus is simple. You spend like there`s no tomorrow when you`re in power because it helps you politically. When you`re out of power, you do everything possible to stop Democrats from spending in order to hurt them. And it just so happens, Republicans have some pretty great leverage in these negotiations by again, threatening to blow up the world economy.\par \par \uc2 The current MAGA Republican Party is completely untethered from any unifying vision of the social contract, particularly on matters of political economy where they`re all over the place. They don`t really know even what they want. So, they`re totally fine taking the economy hostage to get their way. They have the leverage because so many of them are political nihilists who don`t really believe in anything except their own power.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a reason why you have this cliche, you`ve heard it all the time, right, negotiating with terrorists. And the reason people invoke that cliche is you`re at an inherent disadvantage negotiating with someone who is going to do something malevolent and destructive completely unbound by any rules and norms. But also, if you give into that person and their demands, it then incentivizes them to act in the same way in the future.\par \par \uc2 And this is why we`re here at Barack Obama gave in largely to their demands in 2011. And here we are more than a decade later in the exact same position. Only now, I would argue, Republicans are even possibly more sociopathic about this stuff than they were during the Tea Party years.\par \par \uc2 Like do you really think the Republican conference that voted for Donald Trump`s coup is going to think twice before blowing up the economy and hurt \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? I frankly don`t have a solution here. President Biden says he won`t sidestep the issue by invoking the constitutional requirement that the government pay its debts. It`s written right there in the 14th Amendment. It says the debt shall not be questioned. That seems pretty definitive to me. And many of them have called to do that.\par \par \uc2 And the Democrats get some blame here because they did not raise the debt ceiling during the lame-duck session after last year`s midterms, like many folks, including myself and Senator Bernie Sanders said they should do. So here we are. But we`re in this position chiefly because the Republican Party wants more than anything else in the world for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to lose and a Republican most likely the guy who tried to end American democracy to win in 2024. But the least we in the media can do is accurately describe what`s actually happening.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Jeff Stein\uc2 , the Washington Post White House economics reporter. Jeff, I find it hard to follow all this partly because it`s opaque, partly because I think a lot of the stated rationale for a lot of it doesn`t make a lot of sense. But if I`m following it properly, about 85 percent of the total federal budget has been taken off the table by Republicans as have increases in revenue, increases in taxes, closing tax loopholes. So, it`s like there`s a tiny sliver of the budget left and that`s going to have to get fairly severe cuts according to the math provided by the Republican House caucus, right?\par \par \uc2 [20:15:11]\par \par \uc2 JEFF STEIN\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE ECONOMICS REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: Well, what the Republicans would say is that the numbers that they`re proposing would still be below, even adjusting for inflation, what President Obama called for at the end of his administration. So, by some standards, this is historically elevated. It`s not, you know, by European standards or by standards of even really the most Western democracies. Elevated levels of spending. 