tan were told by the hotels that their temporary housing was getting pulled out from under them." According to the Post, the hotels are not believed to have migrants yet or reportedly on the city`s shortlist to take some.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 Now, the CEO of that non-profit did admit that the hotels didn`t explicitly say the vets had to move because of the migrants, but it was clear to her that was the case given the timing. But this was far more than enough for Fox News. They really ran with this story featuring it on no less than 15 different shows over the past 10 days. The network even claimed to have, crucially, substantiated the reporting themselves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A group of veterans, some suffering from PTSD, may now be victims of New York`s migrant busing battle. Fox News confirms 20 homeless vets just got kicked out of several hotels in the suburbs north of Manhattan to make room for those migrants getting bused in from the city.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Remember that phrase, Fox News confirms. Fox News confirms. Republican lawmakers piled on with one New York state assemblyman Brian Maher introducing a bill in response to the story that would ban displacing homeless veterans from shelters. Presidential contender Nikki Haley retweeted the New York Post article, commenting, "Liberal insanity at work."\par \par \uc2 And Speaker of the House Kevin McCarthy, not to be left behind, blamed Joe Biden`s immigration policy is calling the entire situation shameful. \uc2 Todd Young\uc2  of Indiana even brought up the story on the Senate floor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 TODD YOUNG\uc2  (R-IN): In New York City where illegal migrants have displaced homeless veterans in hotels, Mayor Eric Adams said, "The President in the White House have failed this city."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK. Do you know where I`m going with this? What do you think? Well, the whole thing unraveled. A local paper, God bless them, the Mid Hudson News, reported the story was a lie according to the hotel that was accused of forcing the veterans out. The paper and the hotel debunked a purported receipt for the veterans` rooms provided by Assemblyman Maher as a poorly edited fake. The hotel manager said there were no veterans at the hotel. None were kicked out and no other guests were told to vacate.\par \par \uc2 So, you might think, OK, maybe they just invented the whole thing, but no, it gets better. Because the following day the paper reported that several homeless men, not veterans, had come forward to say they had been recruited for the scam. They were told they were going to meet with an elected official for discussion of homelessness and were each promised $200 along with food and alcohol.\par \par \uc2 After eating and drinking at a diner, the CEO of the nonprofit told them they were going to a meeting where she would be explaining how they had been kicked out of a hotel to make room for migrants and coach them to, and I quote here, to act like we were the veterans that had been displaced. The CEO admitted to the Mid Hudson News that she provided the men from the shelter to appear as if they were displaced vets, but denies offering to pay them.\par \par \uc2 According to three of the men who spoke to Times Union, they never received that money they were promised. "Everything was a scam. She didn`t have the intention of paying us anything." Assemblyman Maher says he was duped by the nonprofit as well. And now, well, Fox News, having gone pretty all in on this story, has been forced to issue a rare correction. That job fallen to unlucky host Laura Ingraham on Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, HOST, FOX NEWS: Before we go, a little update on a story we brought you this week about homeless vets being displaced from hotels so that illegals could move in. It turns out the group behind the claim made it up. We have no clue as to why anyone would do such a thing, but we`ll bring you any updates should they come.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, at the end of the show on a Friday night. You just got her in there, why would anyone want to make up a story that`s too good to check but plays directly into the most deranged bias of your conservative audience? I can`t imagine Fox News airing those kinds of lies, but I guess there`s a first time for everything.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: You do literally have blood on your hands, and if you vote for this you will have buckets and buckets of blood on your hands.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got maybe a handful of weeks to get the medical care that you need if you are pregnant right now. Nothing I say matters in this chamber, so I`m just going to say the unvarnished truth, that this place is morally bankrupt. That you are playing political games with the lives of children and parents in this state to get something. It`s gross. It`s vile.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That was Nebraska Democratic State Senator Machaela Cavanaugh fighting against a bill that would deny transgender minors gender affirming care and ban abortion at 12 weeks. Ultimately, the majority Republican legislature passed the bill, and today Republican Governor \uc2 Jim Pillen\uc2  signed it. Nebraska is now one of 20 states with abortion bans in the books as well as the 20th state to ban gender-affirming care for trans youth.\par \par \uc2 And Nebraska Democratic State Senator Machaela Cavanaugh who represents people of central and west central Omaha joins me now. Senator, thank you for making time for us tonight. I guess I first start with just given how hard you fought against this, you were on standing filibuster for a long time to try to sort of slow down the wheels of progress on this legislation, the meaning of this this being signed into law, what it means for the people that you represent.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:02]\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: Well, I would say first of all today starts week 13. I`m not done filibustering. So, there`s more bad things that they can do and I`m not going to stop just because they accomplished one terrible goal or I guess they got a twofer on this one. But what the passage of this bill means is that women and birthing people in Nebraska are going to be denied access to essential health care starting today. The governor signed it into law today and it had an emergency clause that impacts reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 And so, from this moment forward, no one who has a gestational pregnancy after nine to 10 weeks can get the health care that they need. So, that`s a huge devastation for the people of Nebraska, and then the added devastation of the underlying bill which is to prohibit gender-firming care for transgender youth. And I think that the signing of this bill today just marks the beginning of a much, much bigger, broader fight that we are going to have in this state and across this country to ensure that health care for transgendered individuals is protected.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let me ask you this. You`re up close and personal with this. And I think if you covered local politics as a reporter and particularly looked at sort of what the right was sort of really fixated on, it wouldn`t be that surprising that these two issues, abortion bans and bans for care -- trans care, not just for minors in some states, would be top of the line. What is your understanding of this obsession, this focus, this prioritization of these two issues among your Republican colleagues in your state? Why?\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: I think they have the numbers right now and so they have to capitalize on that. And the only way to get this done is to do it immediately, to do it messy and to rush it through because they can`t jeopardize that they`ll have the same number of votes when we have our next legislative session. After the 2024 elections, this entire body could look very different. And they got this by one vote.\par \par \uc2 And the original bill, the LB626, that was a total ban on abortion, failed by one vote. So, they got this bill by one vote. So, this is not white sweeping in Nebraska. This is very, very tight margins. And they are trying to get their wins in for their base. And that`s what it`s about, is getting the wins for the base. It`s not about health care for Nebraskans. It`s politics. What do you think the political ramifications -- obviously, the substantive ramifications are dire, but the political ramifications will be.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that idea of rushing, right? This idea of sort of get it through while you have the vote suggests they don`t feel particularly confident about the degree to which this reflects public opinion in the state. Do you think there will be political backlash for this bill that they just passed and signed?\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: Absolutely. We saw an outpouring of the business community, the religious community, the medical community, the parental community, every community that you could possibly think about came out in force and opposition to these bans, and they still did it. So, there`s absolutely going to be political ramifications for them. And we`re seeing that with -- my colleagues and I, we started a group called Don`tLegislativeHate.org because we knew that this was coming and because we knew that we had to get prepared, get organized for the future of the political outbreak on all of this.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to be a mess. And I hope that the phoenix that rises out of this mess is that we come to have transgendered individuals in our state and in our country living in the light that they deserve to live in.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In terms of prioritization -- so there`s political backlash both sort of on the issue, right? People that want there -- to want trans folks to live in the light, as you say, who want women to have control of their bodily autonomy, but there`s also prioritization. I`m just -- it just doesn`t seem to me like this is the biggest issue, like trans youth healthcare in Nebraska. And I`m just curious about how that prioritization lands or how they justify it.\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: Yes. They haven`t justified it. We haven`t passed any of our tax packages here in Nebraska and we are on day 82 of a 90-day session and we haven`t signed -- passed a single tax cut when we have massive wealth at this moment in time in the state and we`re going to pass tax cuts, I think, in the next eight days. But I don`t know. And they prioritize this over what is generally our job taxes, budget.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, just to be clear, they`ve been -- they`ve been so insistent on this, they have pushed off all the budget stuff for 82 days of the 90 day session so that they can get this dual ban of abortions and trans healthcare for minors?\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: We passed the budget on day 80, which is the last day possible in our state constitution --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: -- to pass the budget. So, they pushed it to the end, and they still rushed this through. And yes, they`re prioritizing this above everything else. And I don`t think that that`s what Nebraska really wants.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Nebraska State --\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: I don`t think that`s what anybody wants.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I don`t disagree. Nebraska State Senator Machaela Cavanaugh, thank you so much for joining us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW" show starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230523ej5m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD for May 19, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alex Wagner, Ali Velshi \par \uc2 8,211 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: Exactly, empathy, knowledge, truth. \uc2 Maya Wiley\uc2 , thank you for being here tonight. It is always such an honor to hear from you and to see you in person.\par \par \uc2 That is our show for tonight. Now, it is time for THE LAST WORD with Ali Velshi who is in for Lawrence. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: You implied last week that it`s going to be, but you didn`t tell us. \uc2 Maya Wiley\uc2  could not be a better person to graduate to. And that whole point about how whether it`s a mental illness or not having enough food, these are just not intractable problems in the least. They are uniquely exacerbated in the United States as it relates to developed countries but absolutely solvable problems.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Especially here in New York City, a city awash in wealth.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: And among a very few number of households.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I appreciate that you are staying on top of. It thanks, Alex, have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Thanks, Ali, have a great show.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The validity of the public debt of the United States shall not be questioned. That is America`s constitutional mandate. And who in the current negotiations between President Biden and Kevin McCarthy appears to be violating that? Who really knows what`s going on with the White House negotiations with Kevin McCarthy ten days before a potential default date?\par \par \uc2 Here`s the update. Talks briefly resume tonight with no progress after Republican negotiators abruptly left a meeting with the Biden administration officials this morning, saying that they needed to, quote, press pause, end quote, calling the talks not productive. The Republicans` exit was widely seen not as an order from the speaker, Kevin McCarthy, but rather as Kevin McCarthy taking marching orders from the chaos caucus, otherwise known as the House Freedom Caucus and it`s spiritual leader, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who said today, quote, Republicans should not make a deal on the debt ceiling unless they get everything they want, including the kitchen sink. That`s the way Democrats have always dealt with us. Do not fold, exclamation, exclamation, exclamation, end quote.\par \par \uc2 Now, if the chaos of Kevin McCarthy`s speakership fight taught us anything, it`s that Kevin McCarthy does not control his caucus. And now, those hard- liners who one unknown concessions from McCarthy to make and the speaker seemed to be calling the shots. A source familiar with the negotiations tells NBC News that hard line conservatives were pleased by the press pause. One GOP aide said, conservatives, quote, are privately seething at how the talks have been going and said that Republicans are giving their leverage away.\par \par \uc2 Speaker McCarthy yesterday sounded optimistic that he could reach an agreement with the White House as soon as this weekend. Here he is trying to explain the reversal to NBC`s Garrett Haake.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Well, we`ve got to get movement by the White House, and we don`t have any movement. So, yes, we`ve got a pause.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: The tone seemed really optimistic yesterday. Is it --\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes. Yesterday, I really felt we were at the location where I could see the path. The White House is just -- look, we can`t be spending more money next year. We have to spend less. We have spent the year before. It`s pretty easy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: There`s actually a venue for the discussions that McCarthy was talking about, addressing spending and cutting down the deficit. It`s the budgeting and appropriations process. It`s actually the one and only thing Congress has mandated to do in the Constitution. This is not that process. This is making the case for not paying the bill, because you think the bill is too high.\par \par \uc2 Now, for the rest of us, you pay the bill, and you budget for the next time. Before you spend the money, if you think that you spent too much money. That part is not debatable, it`s not negotiable and it`s not partisan. There is something else worth noting in all of this. Playing chicken with America`s full faith and credit is bad. It`s indescribably a bad.\par \par \uc2 Being cavalier about the catastrophic impact that default would have on our economy, the jobs that would be lost, the cost of businesses, the cost to consumers, the cost to you and to me is bad. The cuts to government spending that Republicans are demanding, that would hurt a lot of vulnerable Americans, and those are bad.\par \par \uc2 But what is also very bad, before any of that, is the time that is being wasted in a hostage negotiation that could be focused on addressing literally any of the many other urgent issues facing this country, poverty, immigration, education, gun control, voting rights, abortion rights, China, the climate, democracy, the war in Ukraine, artificial intelligence, pick any of them. That precious attention is being spent on helping Kevin McCarthy save face with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and her ilk, and Democrats have had enough.\par \par \uc2 They are offering an escape hatch to any reasonable Republican who wants off of this reckless ride. House Democrats are just eight signatures away from the 218 signatures needed to use something called a discharge petition to force a vote on the debt ceiling without the spending cuts that Republicans are demanding, which is the way this is supposed to go, which is the way it`s always gone, which is the way that Republicans have always done it, including when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president. But so far, not a single Republican has joined the effort.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 So, it`s unclear if pressure is going to build on some vulnerable Republicans to sign as we get closer to that June 1st deadline with no deal.\par \par \uc2 And some Democrats are pushing President Biden to reconsider his constitutional duty, the one I mentioned at the top of the show. Not to spend money, because that is the power of Congress, Congress controls the purse, but to make sure the debts of the United States are paid.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, 11 Senate Democrats called on President Biden to invoke the 14th Amendment of the United States Constitution to raise the debt ceiling, and bypass Congress entirely. And today, House Democrats are echoing that call, over 60 members of the Congressional progressive caucus, including our first guest tonight, Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , sent a letter to President Biden urging him to use his constitutional duty to uphold the full faith and credit of the United States.\par \par \uc2 The letter says, quote, if the options are either agreeing to major cuts to domestic priorities under the Republican threat of destroying the economy and moving forward to honor American debts, we join prominent legal scholars, economists, former budget officials, and a former president in advocating for invoking the 14th Amendment of the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Lots to talk about. Leading off our discussion tonight is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  of California. He`s a member of the Oversight Committee and of the Congressional Progressive Caucus, and \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 , former secretary of labor under President Clinton, he is a professor of public policy at \uc2 U.C. Berkeley\uc2  and he`s a cofounder of Inequality Media. Gentlemen, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, let me start with you. Talk to me about the state of things right now. We have on again off again negotiations. We have about ten days. We have a call for Republicans to come over and sign on an effort to just get this vote passed, a clean vote passed and we have discussions about the 14th Amendment. All of these are remarkably serious things. What is the state of play?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): Ali, it sounds complex but it`s really pretty simple. We don`t negotiate about whether we pay our bills in America. We pay our bills. Democrats did this when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president and now President Biden needs to pay the debt. This is not Congress against the president. This is Congress trying to abrogate what past Congresses have done. Past Congresses have said, pay this money. President Biden needs to pay. We have repetition saying, basically, President Biden, pay the debt. 210 Democrats sided on day one. Two other Democrats are expected to sign it on Monday. That gets us to 212 with one person we feel have to convince. We just six Republicans to sign that. And I think there`s going to be growing pressure for them to do that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Bob, I want to ask you about the constitutionality of this, because this is actually in the Constitution. It`s not an abstraction. The 14th Amendment says America needs to pay its debts. Even if it weren`t in the Constitution, one should assume that`s what you do. When a country does not play its debts, it`s in default, that`s the worst kind of stuff. You have high interest rates, you have inflation, people leave your economy.\par \par \uc2 But even if this went to court, your thought on this is that a lot of originalists, textualists on the Supreme Court, the Constitution says what it says, you have to pay your debts.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT REICH\uc2 , FORMER LABOR SECRETARY, CLINTON ADMINISTRATION: The Constitution says what it has. I mean, I have my Constitution right here. I can read it to you. The validity of the public debt of the United States shall not be questioned. If you have an originalist on the Supreme Court, who says we have got to go exactly by the words of the Constitution, and we`ve got to understand where those words came from, then the president is clear.\par \par \uc2 The president has a constitutional duty. He has made and taken an oath of office to follow the Constitution. And anybody who says otherwise is not paying very much attention. The economic calamity that would ensue failing to pay our bills, not honoring the full faith and credit of the United States has never happened before because we never had a default before.\par \par \uc2 People may be confused about this, Ali. I mean, some people say, well, we`ve had government shutdowns before. Yes, we have. In fact, I`ve been through government shutdowns in my capacity as government official. They are not pleasant. They`re not nice. But this is different. A default on the debt of the United States means that the full faith and credit of the United States, around the world, to all of our creditors, all the bond holders, all the people who are expecting to get Social Security checks, none of them will be safe.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Congressman, I have trouble calling this a negotiation, because it`s not supposed to be a debatable matter. It`s akin to saying I won`t shoot you in the head if you give me a peanut butter sandwich. It`s like, I would like if you don`t shoot me in the head. Maybe we can discuss a peanut butter sandwich.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say they are negotiating lowering spending, right? Tell me what that looks like. Because there are things that they`re asking for right now in order to agree to raising the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:00]\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, first of all, as you pointed out in your opening, there is a separate process for that, the budget process. And Democrats have said, we`ll talk about it, but the issue is a revenue problem. The reason if you want to lower the deficit, what we ought to be doing is repealing the Trump and Bush tax cuts to the very wealthy. They won`t do that. They`re instead trying to hurt the poorest Americans, the most vulnerable Americans at a time that people are hurting in the economy. And they`re not willing to talk about the almost trillion-dollar defense budget, which is most of the discretionary budget. We`ve said, let`s have some cuts there.\par \par \uc2 It`s really important to understand that the vast majority, I think 70 percent of the deficit has been accumulated under Republican presidents, not Democratic presidents.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Bob Reich, let`s talk about what happens. You said it`s a very different thing, it`s not a government shutdown. We know government shutdowns are bad but a lot of Americans who say, I`ve seen a couple of them, it doesn`t have that much impact on my life. Talked to me about -- a default has a macro impact. It does things broadly to the economy, which will trickle down and hit people, and it does specific things to certain people as it relates to their income.\par \par \uc2 REICH: Ali, the worst thing about a default is that we don`t know exactly what it would do. We know it would create chaos. We know that it would reduce and eliminate the full faith and credit of the United States, that all of the global bond holders of the United States would no longer trust the United States, that America would no longer we have the capacity to continue doing what we want to do. In fact, it could explode the deficit because there are so many ways in which the economy would contract, and collapse and tax revenue would therefore decline and the deficit would go up.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is not just a nuclear option. This is sort of the end of time option, Armageddon option, and you don`t want to do that. There`s no reason to do it. And the fact that you don`t have a single Republican in the House willing to join the Democrats and avoiding this Armageddon itself is frightening.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , you had mentioned there are two ways to solve money that you think is a budget that is off. That is you either cut spending or you increase revenue, or you do both. The Republicans are proposing a bunch of things, including some work requirements on things that people receive, whether it`s Medicaid or food stamps or temporary financial assistance. Many of those cases already have work requirements.\par \par \uc2 But the interesting thing here is that we have 3.5 percent unemployment in this country. We don`t have a work ethic problem in this country. We have people who don`t use their vacations, don`t take time off. But we are leaning into this thing that we leaned into during COVID, and we always seem to lean into, is that let`s take stuff away from the least among us right now, that`s a way to cut the budget.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Well, first of all, you pointed out, there already are work requirements. President Clinton, to some controversy, put those requirements in the 1990s. They exist.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what the Republicans want to do. They want to now take away the benefits, take away food stamps, take away health care from the most vulnerable, and there is going to be zero increase in actual employment. Because there have been studies that have been done that show all of this is going to do is take away the benefits.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats have said it`s a nonstarter, absolutely not. We`re not going to allow it. And it has nothing to do to lower the deficit. I mean, that`s a fraction of the actual budget.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Bob, you studied this very closely, this whole idea of inequality, that \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  is exactly right, it will not increase employment. It will not give what \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  called couch potatoes off of couches because most Americans are working. All we have is a mismatch between open jobs and people looking for work. But it will actually cause fewer people who are at the lowest end of the economic poll to get money.\par \par \uc2 REICH: Yes. And there have been two studies just done showing that the effect of a default would, at the minimum, be about 3 million jobs. It could be as high as 8.2 million jobs. It`s not just the people at the bottom are going to be affected. I mean, working class people, middle class people are going to be affected. A default would, in a way, I think it`s not too farfetched to say, explode the economy.\par \par \uc2 Now, if the president were to go along with what the Republicans want and hurt the most vulnerable members of this society, we would be turning our back on the biggest problem we have in America today, apart from democracy and preserving democracy, which is widening inequality.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that we have a small group of people at the top who are raking in most of the economic gains of the economy, have been doing so for years, and we have a larger and larger number of vulnerable Americans, including a lot of working class people who have voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the past, because they are so frustrated and so angry and so mystified in how or why they`re not getting ahead.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:13]\par \par \uc2 Well, you can imagine what that is going to do in and of itself.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We have amongst the highest poverty rates in the developed world, amongst the highest maternal mortality rates, amongst the highest child poverty rates, amongst the highest food insecurity rates. But what are we looking at? Cutting benefits to people on Medicaid and SNAP.\par \par \uc2 All right, gentlemen, thank you for joining us tonight, Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  and \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Coming up, breaking news, and this is a big one in the effort to beat \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in Ukraine. Lieutenant Colonel Alexander Vindman and the former ambassador to Russia, Michael McFaul, join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: NBC News is reporting that President Biden is set to announce a $375 million military aid package for Ukraine this weekend at the G7 Summit in Japan. That package is going to a include artillery shells, ammunition and armed vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, President Biden told \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders that the U.S. will support allies in a joint effort to deliver F-16 fire jets to Ukraine, according to a senior administration official. The U.S. will also provide trading for the American-made fighter jets. In a statement, the official said, quote, as the training takes place over the coming months, our coalition of countries participating in this effort will decide when to actually provide jets, how many we will provide and who will provide them, end quote.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  is scheduled to appear at the \uc2 G7\uc2  summit in Japan this weekend. He tweeted, quote, I welcome the historic decision of the United States and President Biden to support an international fighter jet coalition. This will greatly enhance our army in the sky. I count on discussing the practical implementation of this decision at the G7 Summit in Hiroshima.\par \par \uc2 The major investment in offensive military power to Ukraine follows a week during which the Americans supported defensive systems made a critical impact on the defense of Kyiv. U.S.-made Patriot missiles, which arrived in Ukraine just last month, successfully beat back a massive Russian attack on Kyiv, on Monday. 18 Russian missiles flying faster than the speed of sound locked in on the capital city. 18 U.S.-made patriot missiles intercepted them before they hit their targets. Yesterday, Ukraine`s air defenses intercepted 29 of 30 missiles that Russia launched at Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Michael McFaul, who served as the United States of ambassador to Russia from 2012 to 2014, he`s an MSNBC International Affairs Analyst. Also with us, the retired \uc2 United States Army\uc2  lieutenant colonel, Alexander Vindman, he served as the director for European Affairs at the National Security Council during the Trump administration. Gentlemen, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 You have both, for a long time, called for the type of armaments and support that we have now seen in the last few months the world giving to Ukraine. Colonel Vindman, let me just ask you about the stuff that we saw this week watching those -- we couldn`t watch a hypersonic missile come in, but knowing that hypersonic missiles were coming in, watching those Patriots launch and take out every single one of them was vindicating.\par \par \uc2 LT. COL. ALEXANDER VINDMAN, FORMER NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL EUROPEAN AFFAIRS DIRECTOR: It`s a powerful demonstration of U.S. military might. The Patriots system has been long heralded as the premier air defense system unraveled by any, maybe closest rival would be Russia. But it hasn`t been tested against hypersonics. And now it`s proven it`s worth. And, frankly, the most important thing about what we`ve seen unfold over the past few weeks with the defeat of hypersonics, the Russians are going to keep trying, is that it could start to introduce a level of normalcy back into Ukrainian society.\par \par \uc2 Russia has had a -- the threat of fear has had an impact on just their normal day to day lives, has prohibited the ability for air travel into Ukraine. You can conceive a situation in which Ukraine is well defended with integrated air defense systems, where much like Israel operates under the threat of rocket attacks, you could see the same thing happen in Ukraine, and western cities start to have some normalcy, particularly with regards to our power.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I`m sorry, Colonel. Ambassador McFaul, I`ve so many thoughts that I want to get to, but I do want to say something interesting and that is that we`ve got the \uc2 G7\uc2  this weekend. \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  is going to be at the \uc2 G7\uc2  with these world leaders. The \uc2 G7\uc2 , not that long ago, used to be the G8. It`s only not the G8 because Russia invaded Ukraine. Russia took over Crimea and was kicked out of the G8. Now, \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  is going to be with those world leaders this weekend.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCFAUL, : Fantastic symbolism, isn`t it, just truly tremendous. And, by the way, coming on a tour throughout Europe, where at each stop, President Zelenskyy got new gifts. He got new packages, especially when he was in Germany. And now, on the eve of his visit, we are learning that we are going to start trending on F-16s.\par \par \uc2 Now, you`re right, both Colonel Vindman and I have been asking, demanding, thinking we should do this for a long, long time, better late than never, same with the Patriots that you were just talking about, fantastic achievement, by the way. This weapon that Putin used, this missile, I remember the first day I was briefed about it.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And then I remember the day he announced it to the world in 2018. He said, everybody, it can`t be taken down by any missile defense system. That was proven wrong by the Ukrainians, by that Patriots system. And what you see, the west -- slower than I would like, but the west is getting Ukraine the weapons they need. I think, to push Russia, the Russian army, out of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, Colonel, this is now another -- yet another turning point. We`ve had many of them in the last several -- since this war began. The turning point here is that Ukraine is being asked in this offensive that is likely to come to gain ground, to not fight battles that they are not going to win, to actually look like there is progress, to continue to reinforce the idea that they can receive weaponry. Those Patriots and these air defenses are as sophisticated as it gets from a defensive perspective.\par \par \uc2 The F-16s are what Zelenskyy and the Ukrainians have been asking for since the beginning. And now, as you have often said, what seems impossible will seem very possible in the future. Now, it seems likely they are going to get trained on them and they will use them. It will take a long time to train them on F-16s, months, but what happens that? What happens when Ukraine gets these F-16s?\par \par \uc2 VINDMAN: So, I think the first thing we should recognize is that really what we see is the lifting of their red lines, the kind of limitations on systems that we were prepared to grant to Ukraine in order to defend itself, liberate its territory, and, frankly, defend democracy world over. So, that`s the most important signaling that we see as a result of the provision of Patriots, tanks, and now F-16s and fourth generation fighters.\par \par \uc2 Once the Ukrainians introduce and incorporate them into their war fighting -- and it`s going to be surprisingly fast, frankly. We`ve been doubters of Ukraine`s capabilities to absorb advanced systems. They`ve proven us wrong time and time again. What we will see is an increasing dominance of the sky.\par \par \uc2 I would argue that, frankly, in a lot of ways, Ukraine already has dominance of the skies. They have it through these integrated air defense systems, like the Patriots, like the NASAMs, like the French and Italian systems that are there, like the U.K. systems. And they have an amazing capacity to conduct drone attacks for closer support.\par \par \uc2 They`re just going to get another enabling capability in this, in the form of these advanced planes that will pushback Russia`s ability to use their planes to support their troops to launch missiles. And it`s going to make Russia`s task to hang on, nearly impossible. Ukraine stands every possibility of making major gains over the course of the summer and the fall. And this is just going to make it that much tougher for Russia to hang on.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, I`ve been talking to the two of you since before the beginning of this invasion because there`re no better people to talk to. And, Colonel, you know so much about the military and strategic aspects of this. And, Ambassador, you`re, of the three of us, I think the only person who`s been maybe -- I don`t know if, Colonel, you`ve been in a room with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  but I need to ask you, Ambassador, what Vladimir Putin`s thinking right now. He had a victory day parade that was a bust. Things are not happening. The massive attack on Kyiv this week literally didn`t succeed with the best weaponry they`ve got. What happens now? Can this war end?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, I think it`s important to realize the non-events too in history, Ali. Remember six months ago? Everyone was talking about the Russian offense. If you go back and you look at press reports, we`re all waiting for the Russian offensive. And then the Ukrainians were going to do their counteroffensive. Guess what? There was no offensive. They`re still stuck in Bakhmut. They`ve barely made any progress. Their soldiers, their generals are fighting with Prigozhin, this private guy. There`s a lot of -- if you watch Russian television, there`s a lot of anxiety about how poorly things are going. So, yes, he`s saying, stay the course. But I think you sense nervousness.\par \par \uc2 And I think the symbolism of what happened today is also important. It`s going to take a while for F-16s to get there, just like it`s going to take a while for Abrams tanks to get there. But what we are seeing is, gradually, Ukraine is adopting our systems, and over the courts of five to ten years, we will be completely on our systems. That signals a real big problem for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Guys, thanks very much, as always, we appreciate this. We`ve counted on you so much since the beginning of this war, Alexander Vindman and Michael McFaul.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, a Republican governor just banned TikTok. Is that a good thing or a bad thing? And how does the state ban work on social media? And for those of you, like me, who don`t really know how to TikTok, if that`s a verb, perhaps you`re more concerned about all this talk about artificial intelligence. Whatever it is, these are urgent topics for the economy, for national security and for democracy, and we`re going to talk about them next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:34:35]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: There are 332 million people in the United States and 150 million of them have TikTok. That`s more people than watch the Super Bowl. That`s more people than voted in the last presidential election. And I say that so that we have the proper context for this news.\par \par \uc2 This week Montana`s Republican governor banned TikTok. Now, the move comes over supposed concerns for user privacy because of the Chinese company that owns the app with Montana Governor \uc2 Greg Gianforte\uc2  describing the new law in the statement as, quote, "the most decisive action of any state to protect Montanans` private data and sensitive personal information from being harvested by the Chinese Communist Party", end quote.\par \par \uc2 Now government concerns over user privacy are nothing new for TikTok. At a congressional hearing in March, the company`s CEO told legislators, quote, "TikTok has never shared or received a request to share U.S. user data with the Chinese government. Nor would TikTok honor such a request if one were ever made," end quote.\par \par \uc2 Now, while it`s true that TikTok does collect a lot of data from its users, that`s also true of every social media and tech platform not just the one with the Chinese ownership. So how exactly does one ban a social media platform in a specific state in 2023?\par \par \uc2 The law, which is set to take effect in January of next year, would impose daily fines on TikTok for operating in the state, and it would prohibit the \uc2 Apple\uc2  App Store and the \uc2 Google\uc2  Play Store from making TikTok available to download to users inside Montana.\par \par \uc2 Here`s how the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " describes the conundrum, quote, "\uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  have both said blocking Montanans from downloading TikTok would require a complete rewrite of how their users are tracked. The app stores technical experts say, are divided by country or global region. They don`t change our discriminate based on which state in the United States a user is in. Changing that system would require not just carving the stores into state-specific chunks, but closer monitoring of people`s locations a by- the-minute system to divide what happens, when for instance a user drives over state lines.\par \par \uc2 TikTok says it does not collect precise location information on users, so complying with the Montana law would require the company to start recording users` GPS data or demanding that they supply routine updates on the state that they are currently in.\par \par \uc2 So it seems that in order to enforce a new law supposedly aimed at protecting user privacy from tech giants, the same tech giants are now going to need to know the precise GPS location of its users at all times in order to comply with the law. Seems clunky.\par \par \uc2 A lawsuit was filed this week in response to the new law with five TikTok content creators claiming that the ban is an attempt to suppress their freedom of speech. So I`m yet unclear about whether TikTok is good or bad and whether banning it is good or bad.\par \par \uc2 But this whole thing does demonstrate a very real problem. That governments are way behind on technology and what to do about it. TikTok is a several years and 150 million people ago problem and we`re just starting to have a conversation about it now as the artificial intelligence tsunami is upon us.\par \par \uc2 So who do I turn to? Roger McNamee, longtime Silicon Valley investor, author of the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestseller "Zucked: Waking up to the Facebook Catastrophe". And Roger, you and I have had many conversations like this, in which I am a brand newbie to this. I do not have TikTok on my phone. I don`t know how one TikToks and I don`t understand much of this stuff. And now, all we talk about it is A.I. and as you know, I`m also a newbie at that.\par \par \uc2 So help me out here. Talk to me about this ban in TikTok.\par \par \uc2 ROGER MCNAMEE, LONGTIME SILICON VALLEY INVESTOR: So Ali, the issue here is that the federal government has done absolutely nothing to protect Americans from the harm being done by Internet platforms. You and I have been talking about this since 2017.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  had a profound effect on the 2016 election. And all Internet platforms have done massive harm to public health during the pandemic and to our politics and to just about every aspect of our lives.\par \par \uc2 And TikTok is the latest big, big deal here and because it`s owned by the Chinese, it`s politically attractive to attack them. They are, however, quite a lot smaller than \uc2 Facebook\uc2  in the United States, a lot smaller than \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , a lot smaller than \uc2 YouTube\uc2  -- all of which do exactly the same kind of harm and collect a lot more data.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese are able to buy data on every single American from \uc2 Oracle\uc2 . So, the issue here is Gianforte is trying to borrow slam TikTok in order to get some kind of political gain.\par \par \uc2 But he`s not wrong that this is harmful. He`s also not wrong that states do need to act because the feds have not done their job.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Is there a job to do? Because you and I have sat and generally speaking watched a lot of these congressional hearings on technology and phones and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and all the stuff. And sometimes the people who are there to testify are amazing and brilliant, and have lots of good information. But they get stupid questions because we are not really up to speed on how we`re supposed to regulate technology.\par \par \uc2 MCNAMEE: Well, let`s be clear, Internet platforms are the source of most of the fundraising dollars that come into candidates these days. It is the way the candidates communicate with the electorate.\par \par \uc2 So as a consequence, there is no incentive right now for any politician to get on the wrong side of an Internet platform. They fear that the Internet platform can put its thumb on the scale, and push the politician out of office. That fear is preventing any kind of action in Washington.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:53]\par \par \uc2 MCNAMEE: You`ve seen in Illinois, a law to protect people from facial recognition. You`ve seen in California a law to protect the citizens on data privacy.\par \par \uc2 The thing that is going on with Gianforte is a joke because it is not possible for the state of Montana to implement this. It is, however, absolutely the right thing to call attention to parents That their children are using a platform. It is causing great harm. And they shouldn`t be doing that.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got to somehow change the culture. So we stop accepting what technology companies tell us about their products and stop using them just because they are fun.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Now, let`s say we stop using them. There`s a woman who`s suing \uc2 Google\uc2  right now, not that she was using a platform. She claims that she was filling out a form having to do with Planned Parenthood or scheduling application, that is actually, I don`t know, powered by or run by the back offices of \uc2 Google\uc2  and that \uc2 Google\uc2  has access to that information.\par \par \uc2 You have given me some version of this story for years, that it may not be the email provider you use or the social media site you`re on. But these companies including \uc2 Google\uc2 , have lots of data on you that you may not even know about yourself. But they certainly have that data, and there`s no laws as to how, there are many laws, as to how they`re supposed to handle that.\par \par \uc2 MCNAMEE: Well, in the United States, we`ve taken the position that corporations are allowed to collect data anyway they can get it. And they`re allowed to claim ownership of any data that they touch. And as a result, companies like \uc2 Google\uc2  have built their entire business model on capturing data, and then either exploiting it themselves, or in other companies` cases, selling it to third parties.\par \par \uc2 And that behavior has put all of us at great risk. You sit there and think to yourself, well hey, I don`t use \uc2 Google\uc2 , therefore I`m safe. But that`s not how it works.\par \par \uc2 This woman did not have a relationship with \uc2 Google\uc2 . She had a relationship with Planned Parenthood. And because Planned Parenthood had a relationship with \uc2 Google\uc2 , the data went to \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  is claiming that that amounts to consent by the woman.\par \par \uc2 This is a really important case because there`s no reason why \uc2 Google\uc2  should have the rights to that data. They certainly shouldn`t be able to monetize it. And the courts have done a terrible job for citizens, essentially, you know,, we treat corporations as people too. It`s time to treat people as corporations to give people the same kind of power we give corporations to protect themselves in court and in the law.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes, when \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  said people and corporations are people. I think what would`ve been better to say is that if people only had the protections that corporations do in some cases, we`d be better.\par \par \uc2 Roger, we`re running out of time to talk about A.I. But you and I have an standing invitation. You`re going to come back.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCNAMEE: It`s just the whole thing is spin, it is all phony.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right. We`re going to continue this conversation. Thank you my friend. I appreciate that you have made it very accessible for someone like me. Roger McNamee.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up, Republicans in North Carolina overrode the Democratic governor`s veto on a 12-week abortion ban bill but young activists and voters are working to prevent these Republican attacks, including, our next guest who is the youngest state party leader in the United States.\par \par \uc2 25-year-old Anderson Clayton, leads North Carolina`s Democratic Party. She joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:43:02]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER (D-NC): If just one Republican in either the House or the Senate, keeps a campaign promise to protect women`s reproductive health, we can stop this ban.\par \par \uc2 Tell them, tell them, tell them that we need to stop going back to the last century and move forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It would`ve taken just one Republican to stop the override of the Democratic Governor Roy Cooper`s veto of a 12-week abortion ban in North Carolina. The law will go into effect on July 1st now. That`s a major victory for North Carolina Republicans, who only recently obtained a veto- proof majority in the legislature.\par \par \uc2 But hope is not lost. Republican attacks on reproductive rights and social freedoms are being challenged particularly by young voters and activists. A new report shows that Democrats won 65 percent of voters under the age of 30 in the 2022 midterms. These Gen Z voters helped lead Democrats to victory in a majority of the heavily-contested races. And earlier this year, our next guest made history when she was elected North Carolina`s Democratic Party chair.\par \par \uc2 25-year-old, Anderson Clayton, is the youngest leader of any political party in the country. Anderson Clayton became involved in activism while in college as a student body president. After graduation she worked on the presidential campaigns of Senator \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and Elizabeth Warren and on the Senate campaign of \uc2 Amy McGrath\uc2  in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 In 2021, Clayton was elected chair of the Person County North Carolina Democratic Party. Now, Clayton is fighting to protect the rights being challenged by the Republican Party ahead of the 2024 midterms. She was in the gallery to voice her concerns with her fellow voters after Governor Cooper`s abortion ban veto was overridden.\par \par \uc2 Clayton`s a champion for bringing rural voters, like her into the Democratic Party. She estimates that she`s driven about 7,000 miles, visiting her state`s farthest counties to connect with voters of all ages and backgrounds.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON CLAYTON, NORHT CAROLINA DEMOCRATIC PARTY CHAIRPEROSN: I think that we have to be giving the -- we`ve got to be passing the baton. Giving the reins over the young people that want to take on this work. Whatever Republicans have been doing for rural North Carolina in my opinion?\par \par \uc2 [22:49:53]\par \par \uc2 CLAYTON: And that is my fight. That is my message right now is that Democrats are actually trying to get things done in the state on behalf of rural North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Joining us now is Anderson Clayton, chair of the North Carolina Democratic Party. Anderson, welcome to the show. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 CLAYTON: Thank you for having me. I`m so excited to be here.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: This is quite a week in North Carolina. We actually had the governor on last -- you know, last Saturday morning. This happened last Sunday. I spoke to the governor about it, he was really hopeful that he would be able to -- that not he would, but that North Carolina voters and activists would be able to turn somebody over. It didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 And generally speaking, that feels like a sad thing for North Carolinians and their right. You see it differently.\par \par \uc2 CLAYTON: Yes, I think that they`re -- obviously it was a disappointment, right? When representative (INAUDIBLE) them and no other Republican honestly voted to sustain Governor Cooper`s veto last week. And we saw the, or this week I mean, and we saw the turmoil in that right? When we`re seeing rights stripped away from folks across our state for reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 But that doesn`t mean that we stop fighting. Our party is a party of resistance and we always have been from Republican oppression in our state. When we think about how far our party and our state actually has come since, and overcome, Republican gerrymandering and racial gerrymandering from the Republican at the state legislative level.\par \par \uc2 I really do believe that we have an opportunity here to galvanize Democrats and folks across our state who believe in bodily autonomy and reproductive freedom. In order to change their minds, honestly, and, say like, you know, my opportunity is this year and getting involved is more important now than ever. Because we have the opportunity to change the future of our state and take it back and build it up to be what we know it can be.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: There`s a couple things going on. First of all, in North Carolina you have a lot of statewide officials who are Democratic who win, in a statewide election. And yet your legislature and your House are completely -- they have veto proof Republican majorities now.\par \par \uc2 What does taking it back look like? What does doing the right thing look like? Is this people you are getting to vote? Is it people you`re getting to run? Is it people who you are convincing who might have voted Republican to say, this abortion stuff is a bridge too far?\par \par \uc2 CLAYTON: Well, if I`m doing it right, it`s all of the above, right? In some capacities, I think it`s got to be a little bit of everything. And in order for us to have the right mix of what we need in North Carolina this year, it has to be strong candidates at every State and House and Senate seat, right.\par \par \uc2 In 2022, we left 44 seats uncontested last cycle and that`s not something the North Carolina Democratic Party plans to do again. And so we`re recruiting folks from across North Carolina. If you`d like to run, please head to NCDP.org to make sure that you`re signed up with us and ready to go for 2023 because municipal elections are happening this year and battleground 2024 for us starts with battleground 2023.\par \par \uc2 We want to contest municipal races and give folks an opportunity to vote for somebody else on a ballot because we know that democracy is not democracy without choices. And voters across North Carolina haven`t had a choice in a lot of their elections in the last few years.\par \par \uc2 And so the Democratic party as prioritizing that. but we also know that the two jobs of the Democratic Party is to get out our base and to make sure we`re protecting our incumbents. We didn`t do that in 2020 or in 2022, for that matter. You know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  lost our state by 100,000 votes. \uc2 Cheri Beasley\uc2  lost our state by 134,000 votes. and that to me are Democrats that didn`t have their doors knocked, didn`t have their phones called an didn`t understand that we are active and in our communities year-round.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the organization that I want to build across North Carolina this year because I know it`s what wins elections. When Democrats across our state get organized and energized and put together, Republicans lose. And they know that and they`re scared of that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I have just a little bit of time left, but I want to ask you, what about the rest of it? The rural white south is a very comfortable well-worn slipper for Republicans. You also want to go there and convince people to vote for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 CLAYTON: Yes, I think that we have a really interesting picture when we think about what rural really means. And you know, I understand that rural to a lot of folks means white. I know it means white Republicans.\par \par \uc2 But to me, it honestly means black and brown populations across rural North Carolina. Person County where I`m from is both -- the city of Roxboro within it is 51 percent black.\par \par \uc2 And when I became the Person County Democratic Party chair, I made the first headline in North Carolina by the fact that we flipped the Roxboro city council from red to blue with three amazing black Democrats who are representative of the communities that they were from. And that they were vocal about the issues that that community was really driving towards and pushing for.\par \par \uc2 And so, we need to do that across North Carolina and I know it`s possible and a lot of our communities that we have just have not been active in because we`ve been writing off rural North Carolina as something that is, you know, Trump country or full of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And to me, everybody`s worth talking to. We`ve got to make sure that`s the message this year.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: A great message, Anderson. Good to see you. Thank you for being with us tonight. Anderson Clayton is the head of the North Carolina Democratic Party. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:39]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That`s tonight`s LAST WORD. I will see you tomorrow morning for this week`s meeting at "THE VELSHI BANNED BOOK CLUB". We`re going to check in on a Florida school district that`s become a national symbol for banning and the coalition of free speech advocates and authors and parents who`ve come together to fight that.\par \par \uc2 Books by two of our previously featured "VELSHI BANNED BOOK CLUB" authors, George M. Johnson, and Ashley Hope Perez are among at least 160 titles about race and LGBTQ identity that have been challenged or removed from library shelves.\par \par \uc2 But this week Johnson and Perez, along with other authors and parents, the free speech organization Pan America and the publisher \uc2 Penguin Random House\uc2  filed a lawsuit in federal court challenging the book bans on the grounds of violating the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:57]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I`ll talk to both authors about being banned, fighting back, and reading as resistance. That`s 10:00 a.m. tomorrow morning. I hope you`ll join us.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR" with my friend Stephanie Ruhle begins right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230520ej5j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May 19, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,007 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: And if I can be shallow, my dear, you look real beautiful. That dress was everything, that hair was everything, that look was everything, you are everything. LaTosha Brown, you won the week. Thank you so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s REIDOUT. ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voice-over): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. MACHAELA CAVANAUGH (D-NE): Women will die. Children are dying. It is your fault.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Republican states using their culture war to take your freedom.\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: If you vote for this, you will have buckets and buckets of blood on your hands.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, why Florida residents are saying their governor is inflicting a kind of torture on them.\par \par \uc2 Then, big movement in Fulton County.\par \par \uc2 FANI WILLIS\uc2 , DISTRICT ATTORNEY, FULTON COUNTY, GEORGIA: I think that people thought that we came into this as some kind of game. This is not a game.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Plus, the field of candidates waiting for a Trump indictment keeps growing. And does the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  have any hope of regulating A.I. like this?\par \par \uc2 A.I. GENERATED VOICE: What if I told you, you could own this car for less than the lifetime cost of a \uc2 Toyota\uc2  Corolla?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` state of Florida is forcing a kind of torture on its residents. That`s not my conclusion. It`s not my word, in fact. The word comes from Florida resident Peter Rogell. He`s a Catholic father of seven, a grandfather of 17, who had to watch his daughter endure unimaginable horror at the hands of the state, which has banned most abortions.\par \par \uc2 As Rogell told the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , "To me, it`s just pure torture. The law has created torture." This is what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has forced upon this family. Deborah and Lee Dorbert, Peter`s daughter and son of law, learned 23 weeks into her pregnancy that their child had a condition called Potter syndrome, that is incompatible with life. The baby did not develop kidneys and was suffering from a series of other abnormalities of the heart and stomach due to a lack of amniotic fluid.\par \par \uc2 And Potter syndrome also causes underdeveloped lungs. And babies who survived to delivery are born unable to breathe. Typically, they suffocate within minutes to hours after birth. After receiving that diagnosis, Deborah and Lee decided they wanted to terminate the pregnancy. "But as long as there`s babies heart kept beating, doctor would not honor their request."\par \par \uc2 Now, current law in Florida that bans abortions of 15 weeks, does have an exception for fetal fatal abnormalities. But the doctors wouldn`t say how they reached their decision. The key thing though here, and we`ve seen this in state after state that have so-called exceptions, is the law carries severe penalties including prison time for medical practitioners who run afoul of it. So, what do you think is going to happen?\par \par \uc2 So, the Dorberts weren`t allowed to terminate the pregnancy. They did not have control of that decision. That decision was in the hands of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and they had to wait. They do wait three and a half months until Deborah was close to full term to have labor induced.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEBORAH DORBERT, FLORIDA RESIDENT: We pretty much made a plan for everything at this point now and I have to carry to full term. So, we kind of have come to accept that. I mean, we don`t have any choice but to accept those terms. It`s exhausting. Just the anxiety and the depression and -- you know, carrying a baby and knowing what`s about to happen, it`s mentally -- it`s challenging.\par \par \uc2 And then just my 4-year-old, he doesn`t see the same mommy all the time because I break down crying. He`s wiping my eyes and tell me it`s OK. So, I know it`s probably hard on him seeing me sad.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Deborah recalled when she finally made it to 37 weeks that they sat down with their son who you saw their, Caden, to explain the baby`s body had stopped working and that he would not come home. Instead, someday they told Caden they would all meet his angels. The 4-year-old burst into tears telling them he did not want to be an angel.\par \par \uc2 On March 3rd, after 12 hours of labor, Deborah gave birth to her second son Milo. He was four pounds, 12 ounces, and he did not cry or open his eyes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER ROGELL, FLORIDA RESIDENT: But when they pulled the baby on out, I thought my miracle happened. I thought, because the baby was -- he looked perfect for me. And then I realized once Debbie hand it to me, that the baby was doing hiccups. I call it hiccups. And finally, I realized after about five, 10 minutes that it wasn`t hiccups, it was gasping for air.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:05:08]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Milo lived for just 99 minutes. If you got to take a second of this story, I understand because it`s so devastating, too much to handle. Just one story. It`s just one story. Big state, Florida. From this family, that was so brave to tell it. But we know there are many others, innumerable others, like it that we don`t know, that we haven`t heard about it. And there will be many more in Florida and in all of the states that have adopted the Republican vision of control of people`s bodies and their families.\par \par \uc2 They are taking away the very values they claim to uphold like freedom and choice, as Deborah Dobert explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DORBERT: You say we live in that land of freedom and making choices for ourselves. But yet, I can`t. Right now, I can`t make a choice for what`s best for me, this baby, and my family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, we know that story thanks to some phenomenal reporting from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 . All those interviews, that footage, all from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 . They told this one story of what it looks like when the Republican vision of control happens to your family in a moment of crisis. Control is now a pillar of the Republican vision. It does not stop at pregnancy in women`s bodies. No. In Florida, public expressions and LGBTQ pride are now under threat.\par \par \uc2 Today, organizers canceled a Tampa Pride event in the wake of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  expansion of the so-called, Don`t-Say-Gay Law. The legislation now allows the state to find, suspend, or revoke the license of any establishment if the establishment admits a child to an adult life performance.\par \par \uc2 Now, while the text has not specifically mentioned drag performances, a handout from the Governor`s News Conference Wednesday stated that drag shows are considered live performances, "without serious literary, artistic, political, or scientific value for the age of the child present as the law describes it." Because drag queens perform in the open at the Tampa Pride Festival, well, organizers worried, understandably, they could be violating the new law. So, no Pride parade now in the free state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 And this comes on the heels of the report we brought you last night about the impact of another law \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed this week, banning gender- affirming health care for children. You don`t get to choose what kind of health care your kid gets, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  chooses, just like he chooses to keep you pregnant for 12 weeks to deliver a baby you know will die.\par \par \uc2 And these laws as Gothic as they are in their effects are what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is going to run on in his soon to be officially declared presidential campaign. And that is no surprise because control over your body, your self-expression, gender identity, your children, your family, that`s what the Republican Party has been obsessed with in the last few years, particularly all over the country, in every state where they have political power. This is the priority controlling those things.\par \par \uc2 Just look where Republicans have passed bans on abortion and on gender- affirming care for minors. Do those look similar to you? Yes. They are nearly the same map and that is because they are all part of the same political project of control. The goal is to control how and when you give birth, what your family looks like, how it can love, how you and your children can express themselves.\par \par \uc2 In fact, to make my point as clear as possible, just hours ago in Nebraska, lawmakers in the Republican dominated legislature voted on a bill where they just cut to the chase and merged together two of their main goals into one bill, restricting access to abortion, a statewide abortion ban, and care for transgender minors. They put it all in one deadly package as Democratic State Senator Michaela Kavanaugh told her conservative colleagues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: Women will die, children are dying. It is your fault. It is your fault. And you are allowing it to happen. You do literally have blood on your hands. And if you vote for this, you will have buckets and buckets of blood on your hands.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That bill pass 33 to 15, the Republican governor has said that he will sign it. Jamelle Bouie, columnist from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , summed up the Republican agenda in an opinion piece for the time today. He references Franklin Delano Roosevelt`s famous Four Freedoms, and names the new freedoms we can glean from the Republican program. The freedom to control, the freedom to exploit, the freedom to censor, and the freedom to menace. Concluding "These Republican freedoms are building blocks not of a humane society but of a rigid and hierarchical one in which you can either dominate or be dominated."\par \par \uc2 And Jamelle Bouie, opinion columnist for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , joins me now. I was so struck by your column about freedom because it just seems to me freedom is always one of the most contested words in America back to, of course, the founding and Frederick Douglas. Right now, it really does seem like even if they`ve kept some conception of the rhetoric, they really have abandoned it as a kind of ethos to the extent they ever had it as you look at this like real insistence on state control in state after state, particularly in Florida and other Republican states.\par \par \uc2 JAMELLE BOUIE, OPINION COLUMNIST, "THE NEW YORK TIMES": Right. They`ve abandoned any idea of freedom as something that gives you the right of self-expression, that gives you the right to direct your own path in life. It`s -- they still do believe in freedom, but it`s freedom for particular people, for particular classes, and for particular groups. And if you fall outside of that, if you happen to be transgender, if you happen to be a woman who believes she should be able to get an abortion, if you happen to think that your children should learn about the history of slavery and racism in this country, then no longer, your freedom doesn`t apply in the same way. You are now subject to these strict controls about what you can learn, what you can do, and what you can express in the public.\par \par \uc2 And so, they`re still going to say that this is freedom, but we should understand that it is -- it`s really a restricted freedom and a circumscribed freedom that doesn`t apply to everyone, and it only applies to people who agree with the cultural and ideological and political vision that the Republican Party is putting forth.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, folks that are opposed to abortion and call them themselves pro-life will say that it`s not about enforcing gender hierarchies or gender norms, patriarchy, it`s about actually the life of the unborn child in their terminology. And that it`s wrong to conceive of it otherwise. I have to say, you know, I can`t declare what`s in people`s hearts, but there`s something about that map that says a lot about where this is all coming from where the same states, the priority of they set themselves are banning gender affirming care because explicitly they do not want trans kids to exist and do not want those gender norms bent, and also banning abortion. It does seem more than an accident to me.\par \par \uc2 BOUIE: Right. I mean, people -- well, likewise, I`m not going to say what`s in anyone`s heart, right? And people can have their beliefs and they have their rationalizations for their beliefs. And those are what they are. But I don`t know how you can look at, as you see these maps, think about these policies and not see it in terms of an attempt to impose patriarchal control over people, right?\par \par \uc2 What -- if you are someone who believes in traditional hierarchies of gender and sexuality, transgender people, period, are a profound threat to that because they suggest strongly that these hierarchies are completely artificial and do not need to be honored. Abortion, bodily autonomy in general, right, suggests that you do not have control over the people in your household. You do not have the right. You`re not entitled to how they use their bodies if you are this patriarchal man.\par \par \uc2 And so, taken together, right, like taken together a right to abortion, a right to bodily autonomy, which includes a right to live your life as a transgender person openly does constitute this profound threat to traditional notions of patriarchal morality and patriarchal hierarchy. And so, in my mind, this is what this is about.\par \par \uc2 And the extent to which the concern also extends to schools, right, and attempt to prevent the next generation of children from absorbing the idea that they have any particular autonomy over their bodies just puts a period on the point.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I think that the only silver lining I find this, and I keep saying it over and over again because I think it`s true, is that I don`t -- I think Americans don`t like this in the main. I mean, we`ve seen it in the polling on Dobbs, right? We`re like -- but you read that story in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , it`s like, who can possibly support this?\par \par \uc2 I mean, there`s 20 or 25 percent of the population, I think, that believes that, like, that`s too bad, but actually that`s what -- but I mean, I really do think that, like, the best friend of people opposed to this vision of control is just the plain truth about what it looks like and what it means for people that are freedom loving people as complicated and tortured as that sometimes is.\par \par \uc2 BOUIE: No, I think, I think -- I think that`s exactly right. If you look at, sort of, in the last decade or so, there have been several countries around the world that have liberalized their abortion laws. And if you look at the impetus for why that happened, it is precisely because of stories like this one, precisely because of the clear implications, the clear results of these kinds of restrictions on people`s bodily autonomy, that when most people see them, they are repulsed by them. And I think it`s clearly happening now and I think we can expect to see more of that in this country.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:22]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Jamelle Bouie, always a pleasure. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the Georgia prosecutor investigating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  asks a judge to clear the court`s schedule. When is that happening? What it all means? Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, it appears it is time to mark your calendars. Maybe if you work in media like I do, post-pone the summer vacation you`d plan for early August because we now have new clues on when Fulton County District Attorney Fannie Willis will announce her decision on charges into the multi-pronged efforts to overturn Georgia`s 2020 election rolls, including Donald Trump`s infamous phone call to the secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: So, look, all I want to do is this. I just want to find 11,780 votes which is one more that we have, because we won the state, and flipping the state is a great testament to our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The criminal investigation is also focusing on the 16 fake Trump electors that met in the Georgia state Capitol, some of whom are now cooperating with \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 . Trump`s former lawyer, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , is also a target of the same \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  investigation. And the who`s who of Trump`s inner circle has been subpoenaed provided testimony.\par \par \uc2 Remember we had South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  who failed in his fight to quash that subpoena until ultimately reached the Supreme Court. Special grand jury that heard that testimony finished its report in January and D.A. Willis hinted she would make a decision on charges this summer.\par \par \uc2 So, today, we have new clues into exactly when that will happen. In a letter sent to 21 Fulton County officials, Willis is instructing 70 % of her staff to work remotely for a handful of days spanning July 31st to August 18th. She also asked a judge not to schedule any trials for a two- week period in the beginning of August. A reason for that request was not given, but it now looks like it will be early August when the world will find out if a grand jury indicted the ex-president.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Roiphe\uc2  is a former Manhattan assistant district attorney. She`s currently a professor at \uc2 New York Law School\uc2 . She joins me now. It`s good to have you here. We`ve been doing a lot of guessing on timelines in that back and forth about whether the grand jury report could be released. She said before a judge that charging decisions were imminent. That was many months ago. So, there`s a question about imminence. What`s your first reaction to this letter that she sent to officials?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA ROIPHE, FORMER MANHATTAN ASSISTANT DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Well, you know, first of all, I think it`s important to pause and think about why she made this announcement, which is probably about security concerns at the court. And the fact that there are security concerns because of the rhetoric coming from a former president is something quite extraordinary. And those were the same security concerns that were addressed by the New York court in Manhattan when he was arraigned there.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that`s worth noting. I think also in terms of timeline, there`s a big question mark about whether if there are charges then and the charges do include an indictment of the former president, whether there will be able to be a trial prior to the campaign and the election.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: To go on that first point, she says in the letter something about thank you for all of your help in keeping our court complex safe. And clearly, as we saw with \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , like -- and we just saw this in the civil trial with E. Jean Carroll in which the judge basically tells the jury, if I were you, I wouldn`t ditch your anonymity because this idea of violence, threats of violence, menace hangs over all this because we know what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has done and will do, particularly worried to be indicted by Willis.\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: Exactly. And so, you know, again, this is so unusual. Unusual not coming from, you know, a defendant in a trial.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Any defendant. It is unusual for any defendant, right?\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: Well, especially a defendant who has that much power to use his words to create an actual palpable risk. And I think that that`s -- you know, that is something worth noting because it is such a departure from the old Republican Party that had such respect for law and order and for, you know, the judicial process. This is a different Republican Party and a different leader of the Republican party going into that election.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Punitive respect for law.\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: Right, right, right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think it`s how I would say it. Trump has a lawyer, a 2020 lawyer who has indicated he may be a target of the Fulton County probe, according to court docs. And this is Smith`s attorney, characterized Smith as something between a target and a witness in the nearly completed investigation. We know there are a lot of people involved. There`s a lot of testimony given. We know there`s a whole grand jury.\par \par \uc2 When you talk about the timeline here, like if charges, if he were indicted in Fulton County -- and obviously process there is different than it might be in Manhattan -- what would your expectation be of what would happen next?\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: After an indictment, well, those --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:03]\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: I mean, there would certainly be a lot of motions, attempts there - - you know, I think that people have speculated about an attempt to remove this to federal court, which could also create delay, although it doesn`t necessarily stay the court proceedings. So, there are going to be a number of efforts on the part of all of these defendants, I would think, to push this back because if you manage to push it back, pass that election, and if, again, the putative candidate became the president, again, huge ifs, then what would happen to this trial. And would the trial itself be paused?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, there`s a real running-for-his-life kind of scenario here.\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, which is -- which is, to the extent, should he be indicted for this, then you`re running to be -- you`re basically got this like time ticking -- you got your lawyers trying to kick it back, right? And you`re trying to delay it as long as you can run for president with your possible freedom and not going to prison being the crown jewel as the court of your campaign.\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: And don`t forget, this is just one front. I mean, this is a war that is being -- a legal war that is being fought on multiple different fronts or perhaps there are at least threats coming from multiple different areas. And so, it`s not only is he running from this one, but there are other ones that he`s also running from or maybe running from in the future.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, just to finish on that. Do you think that the same timeline challenges would face Jack Smith in a federal case?\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: Yes, I think so. At this point, it`s clear that they would.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: \uc2 Rebecca Roiphe\uc2 , thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 ROIPHE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, you`re not the only one waiting for \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  to charge out Trump. There`s a whole gang of Republican presidential hopefuls too afraid to take them on themselves, just treading water, crossing their fingers. I`ll tell you how that field is growing. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:31:27]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yet another presidential candidate is making it official today. Republican South Carolina Senator Tim Scott announced his bid for presidency in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Scott is now the sixth official candidate running against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the Republican primary, joining other declared candidates like former Trump \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley, former Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson.\par \par \uc2 There`s a plethora of other potential candidates. Two more hopefuls reportedly joined that list today, New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  and former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 David Jolly, a former Republican congressman who from Florida who left the party in 2018, Tara Setmayer served as Republican communications director on Capitol Hill, and is now a senior advisor for The Lincoln Project and both join me now.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with what I think is the default strategy for most of the people in this race and get your thoughts on it.\par \par \uc2 I`ll go to you first, David, which is basically get in the race, try to raise money, go do the campaigning, never attack \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and basically hope for some deus ex machina. He gets indicted. And the indictment really does crater his control of the party or he, you know, has a health issue. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 Something happens from above, and then you`re around to like fight it out. I really think that`s -- I mean, is that -- what do you think? Do you think that`s the actual strategy for a lot of them?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, FORMER FLORIDA CONGRESSMAN: I think that`s the conventional wisdom of most candidates, including \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , that only \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can wound \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Chris, we don`t know what that looks like. It`s not credibly accused of rape. It`s not January 6, it`s not indictments. So, it may be that Trump camp wound Trump.\par \par \uc2 I do have a theory though, why we`re seeing these other candidates say, hey, I might get in the race. And it speaks to the fundamental weakness of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and follow me on this, Chris, but we`ve seen DeSantis pop it around 20 to 25 percent.\par \par \uc2 But when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  really started his game, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  started going up and up and up. And I think what we`re seeing is other candidates making a bet that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has yet to actually take a Trump voter for himself.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  picked up the no more Trump constituency within the Republican Party based on his performance in Florida. And enough people say, hey, I like Trump, but I just want to get past Trump, but \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has proven he can`t actually take Trump voters.\par \par \uc2 So, what you see in Sununu and Christie are candidates who say, you know what, I`m going to go after the Trump voters and if I get the Trump voters from Trump, I have -- I have more on the board than \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and I just might win this thing.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I had the same instinct when I saw particularly Sununu saying, because he`s been sort of, you know, handling it about whether he`s going to do it or not. And we`ve also seen, I want to play Tara this Glenn Youngkin video, which again, like he`s been sort of futzing with it, maybe he`s going to do it. That was a very funny, usual expression, I basically share that. Here`s the Glenn Youngkin on the second coming of \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  video.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): We can usher in a new era of American values. President \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  changed lives. And now it`s our turn. A time to choose life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness over oppression and dictatorial rule. The stakes are high. And the consequences couldn`t be greater.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Does seem like someone toying with running for president, am I crazy?\par \par \uc2 TARA SETMAYER, SENIOR ADVISOR,