d he`s going to lose again.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the -- here`s the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , this is the, you know, decide DeSantis who has not officially announced yet. He quoted a voter, he had talked with at an event in Iowa saying, you know, Trump was somebody, we liked his policies, but we didn`t like his values. With you, we like your policies, but also know that your share our values.\par \par \uc2 I mean, OK, fine, but I don`t know about any of that. But what I do know is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost in 2020. And the way to beat him in the primary is to convince a critical mass of Republican primary voters that he will lose again, that`s it, that`s the way to do it.\par \par \uc2 SETMAYER: That`s the only way to do it. But voters don`t want that. They don`t want to hear that yet. They still aren`t prepared to hear that.\par \par \uc2 And historically, only less than five percent of single-digit candidates have ever actually won the nomination, including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but that`s very rare historically.\par \par \uc2 So, the others that these vanity candidacies like Tim Scott and Nikki Haley and all the rest of them, they do not stand a snowball`s chance in hell of overtaking Trump`s lead at this point. And it`s just going to further accelerate is win the primary, it`s winner take all. David knows how it works. It`s not -- you don`t have the proportional, it`s winner takes all in the primary.\par \par \uc2 So, the more that are in, the more likely Trump wins. Not tonight.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What do you think, David?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes, look, this is Trump`s party unless he wounds himself. I think the intriguing thing about \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  and Sununu is they`re willing to go right at Trump.\par \par \uc2 Like you said, Chris. I mean, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  talks about the culture of losing without identifying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he looks like the weak kid on the playground. Ron DeSantis`s biggest problem is timing is everything in politics. DeSantis has to go now, he doesn`t have a lane. And this might be a very short race for the Florida Governor.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: David Jolly.\par \par \uc2 SETMAYER: He missed the moment. It`s too late. He waited too late. And you know, and that`s because he just doesn`t have it. And we`ve been predicting that. You`re at the Lincoln project, so buckle up.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: David Jolly and Tara Setmayer, thank you both, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, you may have seen this viral photo of the pope in a puffy coat. Of course, it didn`t exist, he didn`t really wear that coat. It was made by a computer.\par \par \uc2 The risks and rewards that come with artificial intelligence, ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:43:19]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Dozens of wildfires in Western Canada are generating immense amounts of smoke that are drifting across the country into the U.S. sparking air quality alerts in parts of five U.S. states today, even there in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Young children, the elderly, people with health conditions in those areas are being advised to stay inside.\par \par \uc2 Now, obviously, wildfires are a seasonal hazard. But it`s May and it`s in Canada, and fire season there usually doesn`t peak until July.\par \par \uc2 But of course, hotter temperatures and much longer fire seasons are part of the new climate reality.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the average global temperature is one degree Celsius warmer than in the late 1800s. We warmed at one degree.\par \par \uc2 When I started covering climate 15 years ago, 20 years ago, a global temperature increase of 1.5 degrees Celsius degrees was viewed as the real danger threshold, because anything over that was going to get real, real ugly.\par \par \uc2 This week, we got word from scientists that it is very likely we will hit that threshold 1.5 degrees by 2027.\par \par \uc2 So, the old window of what used to be considered the outer bounds of what we can deal with, we`re likely going to hit that within the next five years.\par \par \uc2 And of course, with one degree, we are already seeing and we are going to see more of an unfathomable amount of human misery across the globe.\par \par \uc2 As the destruction and economic damage from climate change forces people to flee their homes and to become refugees. It is the number one challenge in the world as it has been, and it is only becoming more so.\par \par \uc2 A civilizational challenge, unprecedented scale and scope. And it is really striking, as we begin the official 2024 campaign with people like Tim Scott and Nikki Haley and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  jumping in to the challenge with the ex- president for the Republican nomination.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:11]\par \par \uc2 This issue, the most important one, the central civilizational challenge of our time is utterly and completely absent and ignored from all Republican political campaign discourse.\par \par \uc2 I was thinking this the other day when I saw that the Glenn Youngkin, the flashy new video suggesting maybe he might be thinking about running in 2024. It almost seemed like a joke like a comedic stick to wonder, well, what`s his climate plan?\par \par \uc2 Because of course it`s preposterous. I don`t think he has one or will have one because Republicans don`t care, they truly do not care if we melt the earth, they are either in denial or they`re actively on the side of the polluters.\par \par \uc2 But it is actually the single most important question for anyone running for president. What are you going to do to meet the targets we have to meet to decarbonize as rapidly as we have to to have a chance of not heating the planet two to three, four degrees Celsius?\par \par \uc2 Now, in the case of the incoming President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he got the Inflation Reduction Act passed, the first ever climate legislation that we`ve had, really, which by itself is insufficient. But so far has been successful beyond all expectations. Demands for tax credits for things like wind and solar projects, and electric cars are exceeding projections. We`re seeing real movement in the energy transition for the first time.\par \par \uc2 Sales of energy-efficient heat pumps are now outpacing traditional fossil fuel furnaces in people`s homes. We may be about to get a huge boom in the use of hydrogen for industrial purposes, which is a game changer.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s the first part of the change, right? We need way, way more investment from where that came from, more things like the IRA. But the Republican Party doesn`t care or talk about that, it just doesn`t exist in their universe.\par \par \uc2 Instead, what they do is go to bat on behalf of the fossil fuel companies to try to destroy the energy transition. That is what they`re doing right now in this fight over the debt ceiling, their demand to repeal most of the new tax credits that make energy cheaper and cleaner, and to allow more drilling for fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 Those are part of the demands of the House negotiators who briefly paused debt ceiling talks today to extract more leverage over the White House. That`s what they want.\par \par \uc2 Drill, baby drill, here we are 15 years later, essentially, the guiding ethos, the whole party and all the candidates. And so, we`re just stuck in this bizarre situation.\par \par \uc2 The most pressing challenge in the world, the most important thing to have a position on and a plan for. Republicans have succeeded in rendering completely invisible in the number one political contest in the country.\par \par \uc2 And I guess what I would say was, I would urge fellow journalists, particularly political and campaign journalists, not to collude in that ratio. Because of course, the climate doesn`t care whether we talk about it, it`s going to make itself known either way, just asked the folks choking on all that smoke.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:52:48]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s been about 5-1/2 months since the artificial intelligence tool ChatGPT was released to the public. And man, I`ve never seen a technology explode in this way in the consciousness of folks. I mean, it has been moving in astonishingly fast clip ever since.\par \par \uc2 This week, here`s an example, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  user created a \uc2 Tesla\uc2  ad, he used featured actor Ryan Reynolds. It was all made with A.I. so called deep fake. Reynolds had nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 Then, there`s all sorts of stuff people are making this, downright weird. You might even say cursed. A.I. generated video, right? The script, all the shots, everything for a fake pizza chain.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you ready for the best pizza of life? Bring friends down to Pepperoni Hug Spot. Our chefs make pizza with heart and special touch. Cheese, pepperoni, vegetable and more secret things. Need delivery? Pizzas come fast, knock knock, who`s there? Pizza magic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That entire thing. The images, the music, the voice they were all created by different A.I. programs.\par \par \uc2 So, on Tuesday, a Senate subcommittee held a hearing on oversight for the proliferation of artificial intelligence. This is how it started.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): Too often, we`ve seen what happens when technology outpaces regulation, the unbridled exploitation of personal data, the proliferation of disinformation and the deepening of societal inequalities.\par \par \uc2 We have seen how algorithmic biases can perpetuate discrimination and prejudice, and how the lack of transparency can undermine public trust. This is not the future we want.\par \par \uc2 If you are listening from home, you might have thought that voice was mine and the words from me, but in fact, that voice was not mine. The words were not mine. And the audio was an A.I. voice cloning software trained on my floor speeches. The remarks were written by ChatGPT.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:55:04]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Joining me now Sophie Bushwick, associate editor who covers technology at Scientific American, explores artificial intelligence on the podcast Science, Quickly, great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 SOPHIE BUSHWICK, TECHNOLOGY EDITOR, SCIENTIFIC AMERICAN: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, this was the first -- I mean, first of all, let`s just talk about how quickly this has gone from something that obviously tons of tech firms were working on to just like, this explosion in consciousness and people using it and talking about it and worrying about it.\par \par \uc2 Have you ever seen a technology do that so quickly?\par \par \uc2 BUSHWICK: No, I haven`t. People started trying out ChatGPT, became aware of just how good this technology was. And all of a sudden, because awareness was so high, all the companies that had been working on large language models, maybe behind the scenes, but had not, you know, created chat bots to interface with them were like, well, we got to roll our stuff out too, we need to make our own chat bots to show people what our language models can do. And it`s just been going from there.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, one of the things that -- so, there`s a bunch of legal questions and regulatory questions. One to me is an intellectual property question. And when you`re looking at mid-journey, for instance, these graphic, you know, the way that these large language models work is they just train on like millions of other people`s illustrations?\par \par \uc2 BUSHWICK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And then they use that data to sort of be like, this is what a poster would look like. And we already have examples of -- there`s publications, also people that are replacing human illustrators with that A.I. software.\par \par \uc2 BUSHWICK: It`s already happening. An A.I. photo won this prestigious photo competition. And then the creator said, I don`t want to have this award. I just did this to prove a point that these are fooling people.\par \par \uc2 But these are trained on the work of real humans of any A.I. that produces music is trained on the work of musicians, an A.I. that produces images has taken the work of artists and it`s not paying them for using that work. It`s not -- they`re not benefiting from it at all.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, there`s this -- there`s this lawsuit right now, in which you know, stability A.I. DeviantArt Midjourney are involved in a lawsuit that alleges the company`s use of A.I. violate the rights of millions of artists, these images were all taken with consent, without compensation. There`s no attribution or credit given to the artists said Matthew Butterick, a lawyer and computer programmer involved in lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 This is -- so, this is just like, the frontier right now. Like, you`re already seeing people like outsource. People were talking about it, you know, people that work in the music business, right? Lots of people write jingles for a lot of different things where they like write ambient music, that`s starting to move to A.I., you`re seeing publication.\par \par \uc2 So, right up front, you`ve got this like big technology, you got this big legal question about like whether that`s kosher.\par \par \uc2 BUSHWICK: There`s another lawsuit about code as well. So, \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  is being sued over the tool they built that generates code, but it was trained on some open-source software that people had published. And it doesn`t give credit to the people who made that software. So they made it open source. They`re not expecting to get paid, but they do want credit for it. Because it`s being used by other people to make their own work.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I have to say, there`s something really pernicious to me. I`m just editorializing here about the notion of you scrape all this human toil and labor and creative work, people`s illustrations, the music, they make, the code they write.\par \par \uc2 BUSHWICK: The words they write.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The words they write, and then it`s like, you turn around, words, same thing with words, and then you turn around you say, like, cool, we`ve now got this bot that`s going to replace you illustrator and you musician and you writer.\par \par \uc2 BUSHWICK: You`ve trained your own replacement, the work that you did is now going to be used to take work away from you. Yes, it`s really messed up.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, there`s -- so there`s -- that`s like the to me, like a top, sort of pressing issue right away, right? There`s a regulatory question about that. What are the other kind of -- so that`s the thing I`ve been focused on. But what are -- what are the other regulatory questions even before you even get to answers?\par \par \uc2 BUSHWICK: Well, about what about labeling it, right? Is there going to be a requirement that if you do use A.I., or you have to label it clearly. And if you do make that requirement, how are you going to enforce it? How are you going to stop someone from, you know, spinning up their own \uc2 Tesla\uc2  commercial with Brian Reynolds image in it? How are you going to prevent people from using a tool that you created and then lying and saying, no, it`s not A.I. at all?\par \par \uc2 So, one idea has been to create a watermark, where anything produced by this A.I. has a mark on it. But that`s first of all, that`s difficult.\par \par \uc2 Second of all, they`ve already started making these tools available without a built-in watermark. So, it`s kind of like it`s out there already. You can`t stop it now.\par \par \uc2 And also, even if you have a watermark, and you have a tool that can identify that watermark, they can have false positives.\par \par \uc2 So, if you`re a professor, and you`re using a tool to identify if the students using ChatGPT to write a paper. What if that tool gets it wrong? What if you accuse someone of plagiarism? What if they fail? What if they can`t get their degree? You know, there could be really serious consequences and maybe it was a false positive?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, this is already actually coming up, a bunch of it. This is just -- this is literally just the first cut of this, like, what are we doing about credit? What are we doing about you know, folks work that`s been used to train these models. There`s a bunch of questions about like, you know, how do we make sure they don`t like take over the nuclear plants, but I want to deal with these things first.\par \par \uc2 BUSHWICK: I completely agree, right? Like the killer A.I. scenario is down the road dealing with things like the transparency of the data on which the A.I. was trained. That`s happening right now. Things like preparing for the jobs that are already being lost to A.I. That`s an immediate concern.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:13]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s an issue in the (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 Sophie Bushwick, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BUSHWICK: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN for this week. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good, evening Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230520ej5j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May 17, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,321 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris 