Brown\uc2 , a potential Democratic workaround for debt-limit hostage-taking.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The leaders all agree we will not default.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York, I`m Chris Hayes. We`ve got some new polling just out. And this one shows \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  maintaining a lead, a significant one over his potential Republican challenger for the presidency next year. Registered voters give Biden a six-point advantage over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , 44 to 38 percent. And the reason I`m beginning tonight`s show with this polling, which, again, we`re a long way from the election, is simply because it comes after -- two weeks after a shock result in another poll that showed those numbers flipped, 44 percent for Trump, 38 percent for Biden, and there was a lot written about those polls.\par \par \uc2 Now, what I would like to tell you is you can watch those polls bounce up and down and worry about what they mean. The reality is the election is 18 months away. I mean, think about all we`ve been through in the last like three or four years. Think about how long 18 months is and what could happen. The point is polling for the next presidential election is not a very useful barometer at this point.\par \par \uc2 The most we can say right now, I think for sure, is that one, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the front-runner for the Republican nomination. Number two, he`s not popular, but number three, if he wins a nomination, he`ll still be like a little more than a coin flip away from the White House. If there`s a recession, which is not impossible, he might even be the favorite to win.\par \par \uc2 So, again, we`re working with limited amount of information here. It`s a year and a half out from Election Day, but that doesn`t mean we`re totally flying blind, right? It doesn`t mean that we don`t have any way of knowing like where the political mood of the country is. There is another source of data, real data. And it tends to be way more reliable as a window into the political mood of the country. And that is the way people are actually voting in current elections, not polls, right? Ballots cast in races around the country tend to tell us which way the winds are blowing much better than the polls.\par \par \uc2 We saw this in the run up to the midterm elections last year. There were a few races that came after the Supreme Court struck down Roe v. Wade in June, and ahead of November election that gave us the first indications that wait a second, Democrats were not getting crushed the way that everybody expected.\par \par \uc2 Now, the first one came in a special election that took place in Nebraska`s first congressional district. And that was to fill the vacancy left by \uc2 Jeff Fortenberry\uc2 , who resigned after he was convicted of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Now, the Republican in that race, a guy by the name Mike Flood won that special election, as you can see there, a healthy margin, more than five points. But that five points was a huge swing from Jeff Fortenberry`s 20-point margin of victory just two years prior.\par \par \uc2 And that result sent a signal to people that observed this kind of thing. Something was going on here. Remember, this is summer of the midterms, right? Republicans are expected to win big in the midterms. There are low approval ratings for the Democratic president, high inflation, huge percentages of the country saying the country`s on the wrong track, but a pattern emerged. It wasn`t just that Nebraska race. Democrats overperformed in several races in Minnesota and New York.\par \par \uc2 And those over performances in that period of special elections between the Dobbs decision and the actual election proved to be some of the most reliable and predictive data we had about the electorate leading up to Democrats dramatic upset last November where they did way better than many forecasters believe they would, or at least those who were looking solely at the polling.\par \par \uc2 And now we just got a perfect test case for this upcoming election cycle, OK. Again, real ballots cast this time coming out of Florida. Now, Florida, as you may know, I suspect you do, is a state where -- how should we say this? Democratic Party, not doing great, pretty weak, reeling from a slew of losses last year and in the elections before that. And it comes from the city Jacksonville. It`s pretty conservative. In fact, here`s the thing. Jacksonville`s government is combined with Duvall County. It encompasses a large politically diverse metro area that actually went as a whole for Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  by more than 10 points just last year. That`s the benchmark, plus 10 for DeSantis, OK.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:24]\par \par \uc2 It actually happens to be the largest city in America that`s currently governed by a Republican mayor. But that`s about to change because last night, Democrat \uc2 Donna Deegan\uc2  won the race for mayor of Jacksonville. Now, Deegan is a former television news anchor who had not held elected office previously, pulled off a surprise upset after Republicans threw their entire typical Fox News cultural world playbook at her.\par \par \uc2 Attack ads tie Deegan to \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  and the movement to defund the police.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Protest went on two days here in the River City, at one point, even took a violent turn.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  organization is calling for a national defunding of the police.\par \par \uc2 DEEGAN: I went to every one of those Black Lives Matter protests.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You see the way they do that? \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  organizations calling for defund the police. I went to \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 . Two plus two equals four. Do you really want this woman to be your mayor? The Republican Party in Duvall County targeted \uc2 Donna Deegan\uc2  as a quote liberal radical warning of the dangers of Democrat-run cities like the \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  closing in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Not only that, the popular Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  endorsed Deegan`s opponent, and yet despite all that, Deegan flipped this Republican-held mayorality winning by about four points according to unofficial results. First of all, it`s some good news for Florida Democrats. It`s the first good news for Florida Democrats in a while. And as far as we can tell, her win looks a lot like the Democratic wins that we saw in those 2020 midterms. She won by winning over the center on the issues.\par \par \uc2 As New York Times points out, 39 percent of Duvall County voters are registered Democrats, 35 percent registered Republicans, 24 percent registered without party affiliation. And yesterday, Republican voters outnumber Democratic ones by about 3.5 percentage points, meaning Ms. Deegan won independence and crossover Republican votes.\par \par \uc2 In Jacksonville, the Democrat captured a majority coalition from the center to the left the same way so many Democrats and contested races and in Senate races did last November. Democratic issues like abortion rights, protecting democracy in the case of the midterms defeated the right`s cultural wars and their narrative particularly about crime in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Something happened last night in Jacksonville. Now, local races are local races, but this is an important signal, I think, because we head into the next big election season. It`s made even stronger by another interesting result last night, the other major upset that happened, and that one is in Colorado Springs. There the independent candidate for mayor flipped a seat that had been held by Republicans for, get this, 45 years. If you started covering politics like I did in like the late 90s, Colorado Springs was like the epicenter of conservatism in America.\par \par \uc2 It`s the seventh largest city in the country run by a Republican. You would think the message might be getting to the third Republicans at this point that their messaging strategy is broken. I really -- I`m saying this dispassionately. You know, I got a dog in the fight as they say, but it really doesn`t seem to be working. But no one seems to be picking up on this in Republican circles. They are pushing the same unpopular messages over and over, right? Going hard on out-of-control crime and culture wars and outlawing abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I want to ask also, do you think that six weeks is going too far? Is that going to doom Republicans with the moderates in this country when it comes to 2024?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Rob, first of all, I`m the one that get rid of Roe v. Wade, and everybody said that was an impossible thing to do. I put on three Supreme Court justices. Very few people have been -- had that privilege or honor. And they are terrific people and they happen to believe that Roe v. Wade should not be there. It`s been now brought back to the states. And what I`ve done is I`ve given the pro-life people who are wonderful people and loving people. I`ve given them the power of negotiation because now they`re able to negotiate something that`s going to be very important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I have done it. You have me to thank, says the front runner for the Republican nomination. A rare Trump boast that`s basically true. And of course, overturning Roe v. Wade already helped Republicans lose the last election. And it hasn`t gotten any more popular since then. And if you needed any more proof that Republicans are flailing right now, just listen to their propaganda media outlet brow beating the head of the national committee.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: The Democrats are killing the Republicans with early voting -- early voting and ballot harvesting, right? They are killing them. And that`s why --\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Sean, that`s not true.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That`s why on election day --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: California, we just won in Duarte without harvesting.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Hang on a second.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: We won statewide races in Nevada. But --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Then why do Republicans -- why are they starting out these races behind by 100,000, 250,000, 500,000 votes before election day even starts? Why are they so far behind?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Not in North Carolina, not in Florida. Sean --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: They`re running behind the eight ball. That`s my point.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: No, we are not behind the eight ball. I`m saying we need more money. We need resources. Of course, we do. And I will say the Democrats are out raising us 100 percent because they invest in process. And I talk to voters and donors all the time. They say, I want to say -- see who the candidate is. I`m going to wait to see who the nominee is. You have to invest in both, the process and the nominee to get it done.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, and you can`t make a candidate that doesn`t make exceptions for rape, incest, or the mother`s life in a state like Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, there`s a lot of -- a lot going on there. First, I just want to note before we continue, the reason that they`re starting behind, right, he`s talking about that, is because of this guy named \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You remember him? Remember how he went on like that multi-year crusade about how early voting and mail-in voting was like fraudulent and rigged, and the only true vote was election day? That`s why. It`s not Ronna Romney McDaniel`s fault.\par \par \uc2 But the exchange is also so revealing because it`s a story that \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  is telling about a lack of mobilization, right? We`re not getting our voters out. It`s a story that people love to tell themselves when they`re losing. And this goes to both sides. I`ve seen it in the times that I`ve covered progressives. The idea is that you`re losing because you`re not mobilizing your people. And sometimes that is true. Mobilization turnout really matters.\par \par \uc2 But it also happens to be a very comforting and tempting way to think because if you`re not mobilizing your people, you never have to think about whether you are failing to persuade people. You`re just not mobilizing enough. We`re not banking enough early votes, right?\par \par \uc2 That`s not what happened in Jacksonville. It wasn`t a mobilization story in Jacksonville. Republicans didn`t persuade people in the middle. We have seen how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican Party have tried to persuade voters with the presidential campaigns wrapping up this year, Republicans controlling the House. They`re doing what they do. Hunter Biden`s laptop, trans swimmers, you know, crime hellhole, urban hellhole, they`ve gone all in on the culture war. There were on crime, on women`s bodily autonomy. And last night, we got the first major real concrete real-life data signals that we`re getting that tell us they are still heading towards the same result as their last big midterm flop.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is \uc2 Donna Deegan\uc2  who was elected mayor of Jacksonville, Florida last night. It`s -- Mayor-Elect \uc2 Donna Deegan\uc2 , congratulations on your victory last night. I`d love to hear from your perspective -- I think this is the first race you ever ran -- How you understood what your message was and why it worked.\par \par \uc2 DEEGAN: Well, Chris, people have known me in this city for a very long time. So, first of all, let me apologize for the voice. I`ve been talking all day long. And -- but let me just say that that I anchored the news here for almost 30 years. Then as a three-time breast cancer survivor, I started a foundation for breast cancer patients that has a big race here every year. So, people know me very well. We`ve lived a very shared life in this community. So, I think it was very hard for people to come in and try to frame me as something that most people in this community simply knew I was not. And that`s the bottom line.\par \par \uc2 I`ve spent my entire life here. I`m a fifth-generation Jacksonville native. And I spent a year and a half just out in the streets knocking on doors, talking to people, making sure that we were listening to people in the communities. And I think the grassroots effort along with the relationship made all the difference.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, one of the things that figured in race is the question of crime. And this has been something that has been intensely contested and political races all over the country in all kinds of different places, partly because we have seen particularly the most serious violent crime gone up in many cities across the country, including, I believe, statistically in Jacksonville.\par \par \uc2 But in that case, the incumbent mayor is a Republican. I just wonder how you felt that discussion or that debate played out in your race where you`ve got the incumbent who`s overseen whatever`s happened trying to run against you on that issue.\par \par \uc2 DEEGAN: Yes. Well, we made sure we pointed that out. We have a murder rate here in Jacksonville that is four times per capita that of New York City. And that`s been the case for a long time. We`ve been the murder capital for a while. And so, we sort of flipped that argument and said, look, you know, it`s hard to use that argument when we are here in this place. So, let`s try something different. You know, you can`t do the same thing over and over again and expect a different result. And so, I think people recognize that.\par \par \uc2 But I think more than anything, Chris, I think it was that people recognize the argument was just a stale wrong argument against me.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:11]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You are going to be the mayor of a city that is a really interesting place. It`s a thriving metro area, but it`s also -- it`s co-terminus with Duval County. So, you`ve got -- it`s a very big and politically diverse place. It`s diverse in lots in lots of ways. I mean, it`s in some ways kind of a bellwether place. Tell us about the constituents that you are going to be now representing.\par \par \uc2 DEEGAN: Well, I was very intentional in this race to talk to everybody. I - - you know, as a news anchor, you know, you bring everybody in. You bring everybody`s voices in. You try to very accurately bring everybody`s voices into the conversation. As a nonprofit leader, there`s no red or blue in cancer. So, I very much went about my campaign that way and said, look, let`s just talk to each other as Jacksonville citizens. We have Jacksonville values. Let`s talk about our failing infrastructure. Let`s talk about our failing health care numbers. Let`s talk about our small businesses that aren`t having the opportunity that they need. And let`s make sure that we`re creating a plan for that.\par \par \uc2 So, we very much stuck to local issues and talked across party lines. And I think at the end of the day, that`s what it was. People recognize that I have a love for Jacksonville and that I wasn`t trying to play the culture war game. I didn`t bring in national issues. I didn`t try to fight with the governor. I simply said, let`s talk about Jacksonville. Let`s make sure we are bringing everybody in and let`s choose love and unity over fear and division. And I think given what people have heard from me in this community my whole life, that resonated.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Mayor-elect \uc2 Donna Deegan\uc2 , who is well known to the voters in Duval County in Jacksonville that just elected her to be the next mayor. Thank you so much. I know you`ve been very busy, and I really appreciate you pushing yourself to do our show tonight. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 DEEGAN: Forgive my voice. I appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All good.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, House Republicans unanimously vote to keep their indicted colleague in Congress. How serial fabulous George Santo survived today`s expulsion vote as his staff begins jumping ship. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, House Democrats forced their Republican colleagues to take action on Congressman George Santos after the scandal-plagued Republican was indicted on more than a dozen federal charges last week. Democrats used a fairly obscure provision in the House rolls to make the Republican- controlled chamber take up the question of whether or not to expel Santos. It`s called a privileged resolution.\par \par \uc2 Now, House Republicans really don`t want to do that. They know, of course, Santos is a serial liar who has been accused of a litany of crimes, grifts, and schemes. They need his vote anyway. So, this afternoon, Kevin McCarthy`s Republicans once again tried to just bury the whole thing, sweep it under the rug. Instead of an up or down vote on whether or not to expel Santos, Republicans voted instead along party lines to kick the issue over to the House Ethics Committee, which get this, is already investigating Santos.\par \par \uc2 After the vote, an ever defiant Santos spoke to reporters outside Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: I have not not done my job since I`ve gotten here. I can chew and walk gum at the same time. I can chew gum and walk at the same time. I have been -- not been convicted of any crime. You insist in saying that. I will have my day to defend myself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congresswoman Becca Balint is a Democrat of Vermont. She`s one of the co-sponsors of the resolution to expel George Santos, and she joins me now. Congresswoman, you succeeded in getting a privileged resolution taken up and dealt with on the floor. Your reaction to the vote today.\par \par \uc2 REP. BECCA BALINT (D-VT): It`s incredibly disappointing. It is a dark day for America when you can`t even get a serial liar, a fraudster, someone who pulled the wool over the eyes of all the voters in his district. You can`t even get that kind of person expelled from Congress. This is a very sad day.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let me -- I`ve talked to a lot of members of Congress about George Santos. Robert Garcia is the president of the freshman class. I talked to a lot of New York Congressman who have a kind of, what the heck is this dude doing in our state caucus, feeling. What`s your motivation here? I imagine it doesn`t matter one way or the other that your constituents in Vermont and, you know, your voters. There`s no huge political upside or downside here. But you seem pretty motivated. Why?\par \par \uc2 BALINT: I really appreciate that question. I ran for this seat because of the actions of the Trump supporters on January 6th. I believe this is an inflection moment for our democracy. Democracies don`t fail overnight. They fail little by little as norms are upended, as integrity is eroded. And I feel very strongly that I have to be able to look my own kids in the eye when I come home to Vermont on the weekends when they ask me, how is it that a serial liar can continue to serve in Congress and be paid by the American people?\par \par \uc2 And I think about all of the students that I taught. I was a history and civics teacher for a long time. And so, my motivation is about protecting the democracy, absolutely. And actually, Vermonters care a whole lot about this.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I should know just some news today when you talk about, you know, him being a serial liar. This is -- the vote today was partisan for the reasons we explained the intro, but the sentiment is not. I mean, all the Republicans in his home county have called on him to resign.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:02]\par \par \uc2 BALINT: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The five or six members of the Republican Party in New York have called him to resign, though crucially didn`t vote for it today.\par \par \uc2 BALINT: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Which is notable.\par \par \uc2 BALINT: It is notable.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He also -- he also lost a staff for a day. Naysa Woomer, who is the communications director, has resigned. She tells Scripps News, with respect for my colleagues, the people in New York and most importantly myself, I am honored to tender my resignation -- Woomer said in her resignation email. She said that a departure appearing to direct the comment towards Santos. Unfortunately, he never took one point of professional advice given. I suppose that`s not surprising. Do you think we`ll see more of that over time?\par \par \uc2 BALINT: Here`s the thing that you have to understand is that there have been a series of votes that had been taken on the floor in the last few months, and George Santos is at times applauded by his colleagues, especially if he`s a deciding vote. They are certainly interested in protecting him. And that speaks to where this party is and where this conference is at this moment when you have members of his conference standing up and applauding him when he takes a vote. Meanwhile, voters back in New York do not feel like they are represented.\par \par \uc2 This is really an American issue. Every voter should feel like they know who they are voting for. And the person that they voted for was not, in fact, George Santos. It was somebody entirely different from that. And I just think that this is -- this is an indication of a deep rot. And we, as members of Congress, need to do everything that we can to return some semblance of integrity to the body.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You`re also calling for a censure on another one of your colleagues. And I think your description about your motivation to run shed some light on your motivation here. You issued a letter calling on a representative Gosar, who we`ve covered on this program last few evenings to be censured, saying his history of associating with a now hiring white nationalists and neo-Nazis is deeply disturbing. The presence of proud neo-Nazis is a threat to people of color. Jewish Americans who work here at the Capitol as well as people we serve. Speaker McCarthy must hold Rep. Gosar accountable for this kind of overt neo-Nazi sympathizing.\par \par \uc2 That`s pursuing, of course, to Hunter Walker`s reporting about this we featured on the program. Has there been any official response to this from anyone in Republican leadership or from Gosar`s office? We haven`t gotten any.\par \par \uc2 BALINT: Not at this time. And I just want to be really clear, too. I am the grandchild of somebody who was killed in the Holocaust. My grandfather was killed in the last few weeks of the war on a forced march from the concentration camp that he was a prisoner in Austria. This is very personal to me that I should not have to worry about walking these halls of Congress with Nazis, avowed neo-Nazis serving in the offices of people who serve on committee with me. What has happened to this nation?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think it`s a good question. Congresswoman Becca Balint, thank you very much for your time tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BALINT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, the Republican hostage-sakers say they will blow up the global economy if they cannot inflict draconian spending cuts on working people. Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  joins me on the Democrat`s plan to fight back next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I made clear that, and I`ll say it again, America is not a deadbeat nation. We pay our bills. The nation has never default on its debt, and it never will.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: President has now had to cancel part of his upcoming G7 trip because House Republicans are monkeying around with the debt ceiling. The Treasury Secretary says the U.S. could default for the first time ever as early as June 1st, which is coming quickly. So, President Biden will now hurry back to Washington rather than visiting Australia and Papua New Guinea, where he planned to affirm U.S. commitment to the region as China`s influence rises.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the thing, as negotiations continue, it`s still not clear to me what even a yes to the ransom demands from McCarthy`s caucus would look like or mean.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , Democrat of Ohio, is the chairman of the Senate Banking Committee, and he joins me now. Senator, I asked Elizabeth Warren just last night and I`ll ask you tonight, what`s the official channel to you folks in the Senate about these negotiations? Like, what is your understanding of what yes would look like or what you think about how they`re going?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 SHERROD BROWN\uc2  (D-OH): Well, the ultimate position is Schumer and McConnell and the Democratic leader in the House all took default off the table. McCarthy won`t. He`s controlled by -- look at it this way, Chris, that McCarthy -- Speaker McCarthy went to New York, went to Wall Street maybe three weeks ago. He got his marching orders, his corporate wish list, and that`s what he`s operating under.\par \par \uc2 He has no control of his right-wing extremist caucus, and we`ve got to -- we`ve got to keep the pressure on. And you do that in part, and we`ve all talked about this in the Democratic side, you do that in part by showing what these cuts will mean, what his budget cuts would mean, or what default mean. The budget cuts means cuts to pensions, cuts to veterans benefits, cuts to meals on wheels. A default would obviously be something even worse.\par \par \uc2 As Biden said, we`re not a deadbeat nation. We pair bills. McCarthy`s got to do that, got to move on this, and then let`s figure out all the budget discussions. I mean, we know Republicans want to privatize Social Security and the V.A. and other things. Let`s talk about those in the budget. We`ll reject them, of course, on those positions, but let`s talk about the budget. But do what we did three times with President Trump, and that is don`t default, pay our bills, do what we should do as a nation.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:21]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s a letter circulating among Senate Democrats urging Biden to prepare to invoke the 15th Amendment to tackle the debt limit. Of course, the 15th Amendment has specific words about how the debt to the U.S. shall not be questioned. The letter has been signed by a number of Democratic colleagues. Are you going to sign that letter? What`s your feeling about the 14th Amendment being invoked?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: I`m not. I don`t reject -- I don`t reject doing that. I think we keep the pressure, though, now on McCarthy. I don`t want to give him that out at this point. I think, for instance, there`s a discharge petition in the House if Republican businesspeople who know -- I mean, business people across the country who know how devastating it would be for our economy if we default, if we refuse to pay our debts.\par \par \uc2 Some Republican members will ultimately, I hope soon, go along, sign that discharge petition. Then the House would automatically passed the bill if enough people sign it. The Democrats will sign it. I`m hopeful that pressure will get Republicans to sign it, because McCarthy doesn`t know how to lead. He`s a captive, as I said, of his right-wing extremist members of Congress. And there are -- there are dozens and dozens and dozens of them now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You have a particularly important position in the Senate overseeing financial regulation and banks. And we`ve had a lot go down. I mean, we had one of the largest bank failures recently with \uc2 SVB\uc2 , who`s followed by First Republic, which is bought by \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2 . There`s a lot of concern about whether there will be further contagion in the system. You`ve got banks blaming the Fed for hiking rates, which, you know, isn`t it your job to kind of game this out? How stable is the system right now, in your view? And what is your number one concern or priority in terms of the financial system?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, the good news is that we`re going to move away from the deregulation in the last -- the last president. If you remember, Congress passed a terrible bill that we can loosen the banks soon after Trump took office. And then the Trump nominee, the Trump Vice Chair for Supervision at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , a guy named Quarles, weakened them further. Then Quarles made speeches to the -- to the auditors and the examiners go light on these banks. These banks took more risks.\par \par \uc2 They`re all the way at fault. They`re the ones that did it, but it was also conservatives, deregulators in the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and in Congress and in the White House that pushed them towards that. So, we were holding these bank -- these bank executives accountable. We want them to pay back their ill-gotten gains. One bank -- one bank president I think sold stock about a week and a half out for $3 million, then his bank collapse. He had no real answer for that in our committee this week.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got -- we`ve got to hold them accountable. They can`t serve as bank directors or executives in the future. We claw back millions of those dollars that they essentially have absconded with. We`ve got to make them pay, make them accountable, and then tougher rules. And that`s the way to make this work in the years ahead.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One more question about accountability. Of course, several months ago there was that train derailment in East Palestine, Ohio. Of course, a town in the state that you represent. Subsequently, I think in the last week there`s been two more derailments in Ohio if I`m not mistaken. My understanding is that you and Senator Vance of Ohio, the Republican and Democrat have a rail regulation bill. There was a lot of folks very upset and concerned about what happened in East Palestine. It went viral on social media. Now comes the rubber hitting the road. Are we going to do something about it? What is the status of that regulatory legislation?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: My job is two things. I go to East Palestine more often than once a month. I`ve have been five times now since the derailment to help restore people`s lives to some normalcy. We can talk about that later what we`re doing there. Vance and I teamed up for the railroad safety bill. The big railroads don`t like it. They`ve had their way too often with Congress and too often with the regulators. Our bill passed bipartisan way out of the committee.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Senator Schumer has told me that we will schedule that for a vote. The real challenge -- we`re going to pass in the Senate, the real challenge is the Speaker of the House. McCarthy, is in the pocket of the railroads. And we`re going to have to put intense pressure on him to schedule it for a vote. Because we will pass it if McCarthy lets us vote on it. Biden has already made the promise to sign it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, that`s a good thing for us to keep our eyes on because there was so much coverage of that derailment, rightly so. But this is one of those things where attention tends to fade. So, I`m glad that we can stay on that.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: That`s one of the fears, Chris. Thank you, Chris. That`s one of the fears of people in East Palestine is that we`ll forget about them. We`re not going to let that happen. And thank you for being in that position too. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:06]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, she was in Democratic politics for more than 15 years. So, how did she almost single-handedly get abortion banned in North Carolina? Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM MOORE, HOUSE SPEAKER, NORTH CAROLINA: The house has overridden the governor`s veto, and the bill becomes law, notwithstanding the governor`s objections. So be notified.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Shame! Shame! Shame!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:09]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That was a moment last night when North Carolina`s Republican supermajority overrode the Democratic governor`s veto and instituted a 12- week abortion ban, all abortions after 12 weeks after that first trimester. Starting in July, people in North Carolina will not have access to that abortion care. And despite the fact this is a devastating blow to abortion access both in the state and across the South, Republicans are trying to brand this as a compromise.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOORE: There were certainly a lot on my side of the aisle who felt like a six-week bill, like it`s being debated in South Carolina and other states would be more appropriate. And then of course there`s some that wanted more weeks than 12. And so, the compromise came down to 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 JENNY BLACK, PRESIDENT AND CEO, PLANNED PARENTHOOD SOUTH ATLANTIC: There are so many extra restrictions packed onto this bill that make it really hard to get care before 12 weeks. So, I would really push back strongly against that narrative that this is any sort of compromise approach.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, let`s talk about compromise. They rolled back a right women had had in this country for 50 years. That`s the starting point, OK? And banning abortion at 12 weeks isn`t even popular in the state. A recent poll showed that 57 percent of North Carolina voters, 57 percent either support the state`s current 20-week ban or would expand it, meaning further add than 20 weeks. Only 35 percent, just over a third of voters, wanted to restrict access to 15 weeks or less. And they went lower than that.\par \par \uc2 And here`s the thing. North Carolina is not a guide in the wool red Republican state. It`s basically a 50-50 state. They have a Democratic governor, \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 . They have a Democratic Secretary of State and Attorney General. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won the state in 2020 narrowly. Look at that, less than 50 percent of the vote. It`s a pretty evenly state -- split state. And yet, and yet, and yet through aggressive gerrymandering, Republicans have been able to secure a majority in both houses of the legislature, even as they lost the governor`s mansion twice in a row.\par \par \uc2 But even that gerrymandering couldn`t get them the supermajority that they would need to do things like institute of veto-proof abortion ban. What allowed them to do that is one person, State Representative Tricia Cotham.\par \par \uc2 Last year, Cotham ran to represent the 112th district as a Democrat. She pledged during her campaign to oppose further restrictions on abortion. She was backed by Emily`s List, which works to support women candidates who are pro-abortion rights. And she won that seat with 59 percent of the vote. And then when she got to the legislature, she fulfilled her campaign promise by signing on as a co-sponsor to a bill that would codify Roe v. Wade`s abortion protections in the state of North Carolina. In fact, on the state house`s website to this day, she is still listed on the bill as a co- sponsor.\par \par \uc2 But in April, just five months after she was elected as a Democrat who wanted to secure abortion rights, out of the blue, Tricia Cotham switched parties. And that switched parties, it was that decision and her vote that then turned the Republican majority of North Carolina into a supermajority. Here`s her incredibly round about non-explanation for why she took such a catastrophically consequential step to become a Republican.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRICIA COTHAM (R-NC) STATE REPRESENTATIVE: I have decided to change my party affiliation joining the Republican party, and have been welcomed with open arms by my colleagues. This now where we are, modern-day Democratic Party has become unrecognizable to me and to so many others throughout this state and this country. The party wants to villainize anyone who has free thought, free judgment, has solutions, who wants to get to work to better our state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Did someone roast you really bad on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and then you changed your party? Is that what happened? I mean, state representative Cotham never addressed why she changed -- forget what you think about the Democratic Party. Let`s say she`s right about that. Why did she change her position on abortion? Fine, you`re a Republican now. Why did you change her abortion position so radically? Like what changed?\par \par \uc2 In five months, from a Democrat who was spoken movingly about her own abortion and reproductive rights, who supports codifying Roe v. Wade, who`s a co-sponsor on legislation listed on the state website to this day to a Republican who voted to override a congressional -- gubernatorial veto for a 12-week abortion ban that only a third of voters want. What happened? We got nothing.\par \par \uc2 [20:50:03]\par \par \uc2 She owes that explanation to the people of her state, to the voters in the 112th district, who sent a Democrat who wanted to protect Roe and abortion to the state house, a woman who just put a 12-week abortion ban on the books.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Most of the Republicans trying to take it on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the Republican primary have shied away from actually criticizing the former president. That has certainly been part of the strategy for Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , seen here on his recent visit to Iowa, where he was just a regular guy looking totally normal and not attacking Trump in any way whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 It`s going great for him, which is why it was so unusual when Never Back Down, a \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 -affiliated super back sent out a tweet after the Trump town hall, slamming Trump`s actions on January 6th and his offer to pardon riders, went after his obsession with 2020 election and his sex abuse liability. It took on his hoarding classified documents, his attempts to interfere with 2020 election. It was punctuated with a quote, "How does this make America great again?"\par \par \uc2 And yes, that is what a political campaign that`s affiliate should do. This happens all the -- I cannot tell you how many, you know, post-debate town hall tweets and it press releases I read like that. That`s what they do. Tell voters why not to vote for the opponent. But don`t expect that kind of thing again.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis allies told news sites Semafor the tweet was a quote massive mistake. One DeSantis ally familiar with their thinking told Semafor the group`s leadership 100 percent recognize it as an error. And another ally said, "It sounded like it came from CNN. And I think people inside realize that was a massive mistake. And I hope it won`t be repeated again."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Jennifer Horn, former chair of the New Hampshire Republican Party. Jennifer, I saw that tweet and I thought finally. Again, this is like done at like a staffer level. This is not the principle and the candidate saying it. But this is the kind of thing just everyone does this about everything. It`s the normal parry and thrust of campaigns. What is your reaction to hearing that the DeSantis people are like, oh God, no.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HORN, FORMER CHAIR, NEW HAMPSHIRE REPUBLICAN PARTY: Well, it`s just an example of how the degree to which they all lack courage. No one becomes the nominee for the party for -- whether running for town council or president of the United States if they don`t have the courage and the strength to show the difference between themselves and their opponent.\par \par \uc2 And in this case, in the Republican primary for president, we`ve seen over and over and over again that Donald Trump`s opponents are afraid to take him on. DeSantis` greatest strength is his authoritarian attacks on -- or his greatest strength among Republican primary voters are his authoritarian attacks on \uc2 Disney\uc2  and the Department of Education and banning books and anti-transgender rhetoric. That`s what they love to hear from him. But if he`s afraid to take on his own opponent, he`s never going to be able to convince the voters that he`s the fighter that he wants them to believe he is.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s a really good point in terms of form and content. He`s very tough with like 14-year-old trans teens, less so when it`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HORN: Right, exactly.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, here`s what stuck with me about that quote. I`ve really been turning this over. That line from whatever anonymous person said it sounded like CNN was so interesting to me because here`s the thing, no one on the right criticizes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , ergo criticisms like of him sound like coming from the other side. The way you change that is you start to criticize \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  so that all criticism doesn`t just come from people you consider outside your sphere.\par \par \uc2 HORN: That`s exactly right. Every candidate in that race should be criticizing the front runner. That`s how you get ahead. But beyond that, when they say, oh criticizing Trump makes you sound like a liberal or sound like you`re coming from CNN, that tells you very clearly how the Republican Party defines themselves, how they see themselves as political messengers. They are \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There was an amazing and classic primary controversy about endorsees. I don`t know if you follow this, but there was a list of endorsees. I love these stories. Basically, a list of endorses comes out. DeSantis has a list of endorses. Some I think in New Hampshire and some in Iowa, if I`m not mistaken, then the Trump people say -- then people check the list and there were people who were on both the Trump list and the DeSantis list, then some of the people climb down from it.\par \par \uc2 This is sort of the kind of thing that happens in these primary fights. My question is like, how much on the ground in New Hampshire is it already -- does it already feel like a real race there?\par \par \uc2 HORN: Well, I`ll be honest with you. I don`t think it really feels like a real race, honestly, anywhere. You know, people ending up on two lists, that happens all the time.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 HORN: Especially this early on, right, this early in the race. But you know, where is -- the question is where is the fight? Like if this is a real race, if it`s really feeling like there`s going -- there`s going to be a competition here, you know, where is the fight? There isn`t one. The numbers -- you know, for DeSantis, his numbers are going down instead of up and he hasn`t even gotten into the race yet. You know, there`s nobody else in the Republican primary who`s fighting.\par \par \uc2 Trump isn`t afraid to criticize all of the people running against him. I don`t know why they`re afraid to fight back.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and I think that point about a fighter, it really does -- that`s the thing that I can`t get over. You cannot fight and then tell people what a tough guy you are.\par \par \uc2 HORN: Of course.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, they got to understand that, right? Don`t they understand that? They`re smart enough to know that.\par \par \uc2 HORN: Well, they should understand it because they`re the ones that are looking for a real fighter. They want somebody who would --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that is their obsession.\par \par \uc2 HORN: Exactly. That`s -- and so it just -- I can`t -- I mean, I don`t know what else to say about it. I can`t agree with you more. It doesn`t make any sense at all. Look back throughout the history of primaries. You know, \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , Barack Obama, George Bush, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They have to go at each other.\par \par \uc2 HORN: Nobody becomes the nominee without fighting for it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right. Jennifer Horn, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Wednesday evening. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230518ej5h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for May 16 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Anand Giridharadas, Glenn Kirschner \par \uc2 6,741 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:12]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 Do you have a dress code on your show?\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": Yes, it is always be well- dressed.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, the guy who was just on with you, I had no idea who that was, because it was Steve Kornacki in a jacket.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I know.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And I immediately went to my locker to see if he had borrowed one of mine.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I feel like it`s like if Kor -- if you don`t see Kornacki`s khakis on air, was he ever really there? It`s like, if it a tree falls in the forest, nobody`s around to hear it?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: No, but seriously, did you -- did you tell him, go back and get a jacket if you`re coming on this show?\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Listen, he was like, I`m not on the big board. I`m a guest. I`m going to -- I`m going to dress for the occasion. I`m going to wear my finest threads, and that`s what America got tonight.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Okay. All right.\par \par \uc2 Well, actually, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  got a little bit more, because I took a picture of you through the window.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I love when you -- I love when you do those lurking photos, it makes me feel like -- it`s like we all live in this strange glass house together.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I am the paparazzi on the third floor at 9:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: You`re the best pop in America.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Alex, you know, it is my policy to never talk about \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  until your kids have gone to bed.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: You`re safe.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And that`s more important than ever. But I now -- I have to worry about the kids on the West Coast, you know, where it`s still 7:00 p.m., because we`re going to be talking about him a little bit at the beginning of the show and more at the end of the show with Glenn Kirschner, because there is plenty to talk about with a former federal prosecutor about this new lawsuit against \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: There sure is. It`s not safe for families, so take your kids out of the room, but it is riveting discussion, Lawrence. I look forward to hearing it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Alex. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, yes, please get the young children out of the room right now, especially on the West Coast, where young children might still be awake, because we are, just for the next couple of minutes at the beginning of the program, and then again at the end of the program -- going to, once again tonight, discuss the depravity of \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2 , depravity which \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  has denied through a written statement from a spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 Last night, at this hour, we broke the news on this network that \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  was hit with a lawsuit in Manhattan, suing him for a minimum of $10 million for violations of labor law, including refusing to pay the salary of the plaintiff of that lawsuit, Noelle Dunphy, who says that \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  sexually harassed her throughout her employment with Giuliani and that he sexually attacked her while at work and at other times.\par \par \uc2 According to the complaint in the lawsuit, many though not all of her conversations with \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  were recorded because he encouraged her to use recordings to take accurate notes during their work discussions and he hoped that some of the recordings could be used for a book about \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2 . Many of the damning conversations quoted in the lawsuit are followed by the statement in writing repeatedly, this conversation was recorded.\par \par \uc2 Many other conversations in the lawsuit do not specify whether they were recorded including a conversation in which \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  tells Ms. Dunphy that he was selling pardons for $2 million, Trump pardons in 2019, and that he was splitting the two million dollars in of extortion money for the pardons with $1 million going to real Giuliani and million going to then President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NBC News has asked the Trump campaign about that today, about that allegation. They`ve not received a response which is strange.\par \par \uc2 Now, the recorded conversations in the lawsuit that are specified as recorded are the only conversations that were recorded according to the plaintiff`s lawyer who was here in this studio last night explaining that distinction in the lawsuit. So for all the statements in the lawsuit that don`t specify they were recorded, we can presume that they were not recorded.\par \par \uc2 And I told you that there were many statements last night, that there are many quoted statements in that lawsuit that says they were not -- that they were recorded, they were indeed recorded but they`re so bad I cannot say them on television, and I can`t -- cannot even hint at what these lines actually say. They are lines of dialogue spoken by \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  during his sexual attacks on this woman.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:08]\par \par \uc2 And some of them are lines, some of those lines, you could hear some versions of them on HBO or Showtime and sex scenes. But some of the other lines that appear in the lawsuit in quotation marks and were recorded, according to the document are lines that HBO would not allow a fictional character to say in a fictional sex scene. Even HBO has limits, but \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  does not.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit says that \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2 , quote: regularly drank all day and night, and that he was constantly consuming Viagra and that he was constantly demanding sex from Ms. Dunphy who he was also representing as a lawyer in a lawsuit she was involved in before she went to work for Giuliani, and Giuliani volunteering to represent her as a lawyer for free was part of the reason she went to work for him.\par \par \uc2 What I`m about to read to you from the lawsuit against \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  is much tamer than the most graphic lines in the lawsuit, but it is profoundly perverse and it reveals, if true, that what passes through the mind of the man we all saw ranting with hair dye dripping down the sides of his face is much, much uglier than dripping hair dye.\par \par \uc2 And remember as I read this, that this scene alleged in the lawsuit is not happening in the office of the attorney general of the United States tonight because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the presidential election and killed Rudolph Giuliani`s dream of becoming attorney general or secretary of state, another job that he craved.\par \par \uc2 Page 20, paragraph 109: Throughout the employment an attorney-client relationship, Giuliani forced Ms. Dunphy to perform oral sex on him. He often demanded oral sex while he took phone calls on speakerphone from high-profile friends and clients including then President Trump. Giuliani told Ms. Dunphy that he enjoyed engaging in this conduct while on the telephone because it made him feel like \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That was Donald Trump`s chief co-conspirator in trying to overturn the last presidential election. That is who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brought into the White House and it is the kind of person he brought into government, the worst people. This is who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brought into the White House to advise him and it is the kind of person \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brought into government to serve in government, the very worst people, the kind of people who think government is a joke, to be exploited for their own purposes, like -- according to this lawsuit -- selling pardons for $2 million and splitting the money between \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  saved us from all of that madness described in the lawsuit against \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  by winning the presidential election an election that he would not have won without the forceful help of our first guest tonight. Republicans in Congress have been thwarted by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at every turn because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the presidential election. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  passed, over the objection of Republicans, the biggest infrastructure bill in history.\par \par \uc2 He increased investment in environmental policies and in progressive social policies and cut the deficit at the same time. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has achieved record cuts in the deficits, something \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  never did. The deficit and the debt ceiling -- the deficit and the debt went straight up every day under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , every day, and you got absolutely nothing for it. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  built nothing in this country except a couple of miles of ineffectual wall on the southern border.\par \par \uc2 And after doing nothing except cutting taxes and dramatically increasing the national debt, Republicans in Congress happily increased the debt ceiling with no strings attached three times for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:10:05]\par \par \uc2 And now, those same Republicans are refusing to do it even once for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  because for Trump, Republicans governing is a joke.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAITLAN COLLINS, CNN MODERATOR: You once said that using the -- that using the debt ceiling is a negotiating wedge just could not happen. You said that when you were on the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: That`s when I was president.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: So why is it different now that you`re out of office?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Because now I`m not president.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That`s the kind of hypocritical statement that used to destroy a political career.\par \par \uc2 When he was president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said, I can`t imagine anybody ever even thinking of using the debt ceiling as a negotiating wager. That`s a sacred element of our country. They can`t use the debt ceiling to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 And now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  treats that statement that he made as president as a joke. It`s exactly the same way \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  treats the law. We saw \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  treating the law as a joke publicly when he was lying his way through television interviews about overturning the presidential election. And in the new lawsuit filed against \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2 , if the allegations are true, we see \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  treating laws against sexual harassment as a joke, treating laws against sexual assault as a joke, treating the crime of selling pardons as a joke.\par \par \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have conditioned Trump supporting voters to believe it`s all a joke, everything about government is a joke. The law is a joke, the debt ceiling is a joke. And now, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has to deal with the Republican members of Congress who believe that the debt ceiling is a joke.\par \par \uc2 Eight years ago, Kevin McCarthy was running for speaker for the first time and he had to drop out of the race when he said this on Fox.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Everybody thought \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was unbeatable, right? But we put together a Benghazi special committee, a select committee. What are her numbers today? Her numbers are dropping. Why? Because she`s untrustable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Republicans thought Kevin McCarthy was the stupidest person in Congress because Kevin McCarthy right there said the secret part out loud. He said that the Benghazi hearings were simply to attack \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and drive down her poll numbers as a presidential candidate and Kevin McCarthy`s fellow Republicans thought that was so stupid he lost all support immediately for becoming speaker.\par \par \uc2 And there`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last week saying what is supposed to be the secret part out loud, that the debt ceiling is sacred when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is president. But, of course, Republicans can default on the debt when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is president. So \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is trying to force an increase in the debt ceiling from the most reckless congressional Republicans in history in control of the House of Representatives from people who are supported by voters who think the debt ceiling is a joke.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has once again been given an impossible tax task. President Biden announced today that he will cut short a trip to Asia which begins tomorrow and return to Washington by Sunday in order to pursue an agreement on raising the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 Today, after a meeting in the Oval Office with the president, the vice president, the majority leader of the Senate, the minority leader of the Senate, along with the speaker of the House and the minority leader of the House, Speaker McCarthy said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: The structure of how we negotiate has improved. So it now gives you a better opportunity, even though we only have a few days to get it done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: After that meeting, tonight, in an interview with \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , House Democratic Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  who was in the Oval Office today discussed the possibility of Democrats forcing a vote on an increase in the debt ceiling in the House of Representatives through a process called a discharge petition.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): One of the options that could be available to us is a discharge petition.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST, "REIDOUT": Right.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Now, that doesn`t -- that doesn`t go live until tomorrow and we`ll have to have a conversation about the best way to proceed, but it will require not just the signatures of 213 Democrats, but as you pointed out in your opening, question to me, we`d also have to find at least five Republicans --\par \par \uc2 [22:15:05]\par \par \uc2 REID: Right.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: -- to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 And my view is, look, we should just be able to find common ground with the fierce urgency of now because a dangerous default will be bad for everyone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading off our discussion tonight is Democratic Congressman James Clyburn of South Carolina. He is assistant Democratic leader in the House of Representatives and it was his endorsement of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the South Carolina primary after \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  lost the Iowa caucuses and lost the New Hampshire primary that sent \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on his way to the Democratic nomination.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Clyburn, thank you very much for joining us tonight on this very important night. We have been discussing discharge petitions on this program a few times. It`s complicated and I have to review it a little bit for the audience each time. It`s a petition that requires the signatures of a majority of members of the House and those signatures then force a vote on the bill in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 And so, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , Leader Jeffries was in the meeting today in the Oval Office. The possibility of moving -- beginning to try to move a discharge position begins tomorrow, according to the parliamentary rules. Has there been a decision by Leader Jeffries and you and others about how to proceed on a discharge petition.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): Well, thank you very much for having me, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think so. I had dinner in a meeting with Leader Jeffries last night. He is very much in touch with our caucus. A few minutes ago I interacted with our whip and their discussion best to go forward. As you know, he will make that decision, he will be relying upon his team like his flipping operation, to make sure that we got the 213 Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And then we will be hopeful, as the (INAUDIBLE), pick up five Republicans to come along. So I don`t know that they`ve made a final decision on that, but I hope that he will let us know tomorrow, and we can start the process, just in case it is something that is needed.\par \par \uc2 I`m sure, like everybody else, he would like to see us do this, using what we call a regular order and not go to a discharge petition. But that should be on the table just in case we need to.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah. I mean, he has presented it as an option and as you say, you could start collecting those signatures tomorrow and it would just be a way theoretically of adding some pressure to getting to a final agreement that people can vote through. One of the things that I think might be confusing people a little bit is President Biden saying he wasn`t going to negotiate on the debt ceiling and what I seem to be hearing the White House say is we`re not negotiating on the debt ceiling, we still want to have a clean debt ceiling bill, but we are negotiating on the budget of the United States of America going forward, and we`re trying to reach some agreements that we`re going to have to reach for the Republican House on the budget.\par \par \uc2 And because those two discussions are happening at the same time, it looks like it`s negotiating on the debt ceiling with the White House is insisting so far anyway that these are actually separate procedures.\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: I think they are, and that`s one of the reasons I`ve advocated for some time now I think without us on your show the last time that we looked at because this is coming a little quicker than we thought. We started out the year thinking it would be sometimes in August or September before we reach uh that uh debt ceiling, but it looks like it`s going to be June 1st.\par \par \uc2 So I`ve advocated that we should do a clean short-term CR, I mean, sorry, debt limit bill to expire maybe September 30th, which is the same date that the fiscal year will expire and let those two negotiations go simultaneously on two different parallel courses. And so, you have the debt limit being discussed and negotiated at the same time that we`re negotiating spending for the next fiscal year. There will be separate bills but -- and on different tracks, but running parallel.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:04]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, as you know, in the history of debt ceiling increases in the Congress, most of the time, it was done without even provoking a headline. It was done often unanimously in the Senate, just without any controversy whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 But now, you have Republican members of the House of Representatives who are saying -- well, you know, let`s just crash right through the debt ceiling, what difference does it make?\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Well, I`m not sure that they mention the difference. I don`t know how you go about doing that. It would be -- wind up in the coast I`m sure, but how many other countries got this kind of a debt ceiling. We -- I think we`re the only one left this is not the way to do things in this day and time.\par \par \uc2 I think we have a ceiling on expenditures every time you adopt a budget and you have a debt when you go over that budget and your deficit when you are spending more than you`re taking in.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t know that we need this debt limit stuff. It doesn`t do anything for any other country. Why do we have it? I`ve advocated ever since I`ve been in the Congress for getting rid of that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman James Clyburn, and I have to say, that September 30th plan sounds like a sensible one. Thank you very much for joining us again tonight, really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Well, thank you very much for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back, for the first time in history, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is calling for reform of the Supreme Court, including expanding the Supreme Court and adding term limits for Supreme Court justices and ethics rules. The president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:26:30]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It was actually a year ago this month when we were all together at this dinner, and the Dobbs decision had just been leaked. And there were three words on my mind that night: How dare they?\par \par \uc2 How dare they attack our health care system? How dare they attack our fundamental rights? How dare they attack the freedom of the women of America to make decisions about their own bodies?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That was the vice president speaking in Washington tonight.\par \par \uc2 This week, \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  issued a statement saying: As we continue to face unrelenting attacks on our basic freedoms, our courts must be one backstop to protecting our rights. Instead, the courts have been used as a vehicle to advance the dangerous agenda against abortion rights, voting rights, LGBTQ+ rights, and so much more.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  now supports expanding the number of Supreme Court justices, implementing term limits for Supreme Court justices, and imposing ethics rules on members of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 It`s support for those reforms come as more states passed legislation banning abortion in the aftermath of the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 After North Carolina`s Democratic Governor \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2  vetoed legislation on Saturday that would`ve banned all abortions in his state after 12 weeks of pregnancy, today, North Carolina`s Republican controlled legislature voted to override the governor`s veto, making that abortion ban law.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , president and CEO of \uc2 Planned Parenthood Federation of America\uc2 , and the \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 First of all, I want to get your reaction to one day of seeming success in North Carolina with that veto, and now the setback of the veto being overridden.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCGILL JOHNSON\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, PLANNED PARENTHOOD: Well, thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 Look, I was in North Carolina on Saturday with the governor and with hundreds of people out on the Mother`s Day weekend, screaming and yelling in support of his veto because they were screaming and yelling in support of their own freedom.\par \par \uc2 And to see that vote -- that veto be overridden today, I think it`s just emblematic of how much of a crisis our democracy is in. The great majority of people in North Carolina support reproductive freedom, and there`s no accountability for Democratic legislator -- legislators in a -- in a state house because they`ve been gerrymandered into their positions, and now, in fact, can represent their own ideas as opposed to -- as opposed to what their constituents believe.\par \par \uc2 I think that is exactly why we are, you know, leaning into the need for not just democracy reform writ large, but court reform as well is a critical component of that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, the Constitution does not establish a number of Supreme Court justices. Congress has done that. It has raised the number. It has lowered the number, raised it again.\par \par \uc2 It`s set at nine since I believe 1869. The country has more than tripled in size since then in terms of population.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:02]\par \par \uc2 It has more than tripled in terms of complexity, and American law has more than tripled in complexity. And yet, it`s still just the nine, deciding all of those issues that come to the court. How did you come to the conclusion that the court should be expanded?\par \par \uc2 MCGILL: Well, we also know that justices are living a lot longer than their original lifetime span during their lifetime appointments. And so, you know, we came to this position, because we fundamentally understand that the courts should protect our rights, right? The courts should be the ones that are adjudicating whether or not our fundamental rights exist. And we have watched lower courts all the way up through the Supreme Court be weaponized to erode and literally outright revoke our rights in unbelievably unprecedented ways.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, look, we have lots of partners in this movement who`ve been calling for court reform. We have been very clear that this overturn of Roe v. Wade and the Dobbs decision was just the canary in the coalmine in terms of the kinds of rights that that will be taken away in future courts. And we just think it`s incredibly critical in this moment for us and for Americans to understand the full complexity of American democracy and how it is in crisis in this moment when fundamental freedoms are being taken away.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to get into much more detail about expanding the court and the ethics rules you would like to see imposed there on another night when we have more time in the program to do that, because this is such a worthy and important subject.\par \par \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Joe Biden`s reelection campaign was launched on the strength of one word, it is the same word that launched Republican president Ronald Reagan`s career in national politics. It`s the one word that all Americans seem to value, but it`s a word that now means something different to Republicans than it means to Democrats. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:35:08]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Freedom. Personal freedom is fundamental to who we are as Americans. There`s nothing more important, nothing more sacred.\par \par \uc2 It has been the work of my first term. The question we`re facing is whether in the years ahead, we have more freedom or less freedom, more rights or fewer. I know what I want this to be and I think you do too. This is not a time to be complacent. That`s why I`m running for re-election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The very first words spoken by President Biden in his reelection campaign was freedom. You saw it right there. In a recent article for his newsletter titled Biden`s freedom pitch and the coming political realignment, our next guest, Anand Giridharadas, writes, "The Republican retreat from the frame of freedom is a tectonic underappreciated shift in American politics. And it may be the start of a profound political realignment for the first time in a generation. The idea of freedom is not an especially important animating principle for the right. They use the word still, but they are fundamentally about something else now, fundamentally about protecting people from supposedly menacing forces, like demographic change, and changes to M&Ms and children`s books and woke corporations. This is the pitch not of freedom but of the strongman. The right`s desertion of freedom creates an historic opportunity for the left."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Anand Giridharadas, author and MSNBC political analyst, and publisher of The Ink newsletter.\par \par \uc2 Anand, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Since I read this, I`ve been very eager to talk to you about it, because I hadn`t noticed this. I hadn`t noticed what has happened to the word freedom and its meaning and how it changes whether it`s used by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , as we saw it there. And Republican officials now.\par \par \uc2 Describe what you see here as the opportunity for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 ANAND GIRIDHARADAS, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, you know, Lawrence, you and I are both language people and, you know, and politics is about policies, and it`s about ideas, and it`s about stupid fights. But at some deep level, it`s about language. It`s about leaders, offering different options to people in the form of language and people choosing which of those languages resonates with where they are.\par \par \uc2 And I as a language person paying a lot of attention to what the shifting language is. And one thing I noticed -- two things I noticed I would say, first of all, in my entire lifetime, the Republican Party has gone very long on the language of freedom and \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in particular, I think started that phenomenon of freedom, freedom, freedom. If when in doubt, we don`t know what to say, say freedom.\par \par \uc2 [22:40:16]\par \par \uc2 Everything is kind of laundered through a frame of freedom, you know, fomenting tyranny. In Latin America, it`s called freedom every -- you know, busting unions is called freedom. But the Republicans did a very smart, tactical job of rebranding every single striving of theirs, including things that made people`s lives harder, more precarious, and less free as freedom.\par \par \uc2 And I think in many ways, the political left Democrats in particular, over the last generation, kind of passively accepted this as, OK, this is the reality. You`re the freedom people. So we`ll do justice. We`ll do solidarity. We`ll do equality. We`ll do the future. We`ll do hope. We`ll do stronger together. We have all these other frames. But we`ll kind of leave you this freedom territory.\par \par \uc2 I think this is a catastrophic mistake, as I write about in The Ink. And what is so interesting is that before \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came along and made that -- made that ad, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the bridge, where freedom kind of flew off into the water, because in his American carnage inaugural speech, interestingly, he mentioned it one time and it kind of boilerplate thing. But more importantly, under the Trump kind of view of the world, freedom is not a particularly important animating value.\par \par \uc2 And what I write about in The Ink is that the Reaganite idea of freedom was, you are powerful as individuals. You have bubbling, fizzing potential as Americans. And if we could just let you alone, you would be free and thrive.\par \par \uc2 By the time we move from the libertarian Republican Party to the authoritarian one today, the pitch has changed, the view of human nature has changed. The view now is you are weak and vulnerable and can`t do anything on your own because these powerful forces are out to get you. And only I, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , only I, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , can protect you.\par \par \uc2 And so the right is not really about strong, powerful individuals needing to be set free anymore. And this creates space for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , for the Democrats, for the left, in general, to enter that terrain of freedom that they wrongfully deserted and make, as your last guest was talking about, make abortion as it is a referendum on the freedom over one`s body.\par \par \uc2 Make, you know, the fights over spending right now -- would you have the freedom to thrive with a modern safety net. You have the freedom to get the education you need. You have the freedom to be able to have support when life knocks you down. This question of freedom is now front and center back in American politics. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done a very smart thing by ending this silly concession of freedom to the right and stepping in to claim it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, I think in the audience`s minds are racing as mine are with applying this concept to our politics now, for example, the freedom to read when you`re going to high school in Florida, that all of these things, it`s just everywhere in these political dynamics.\par \par \uc2 GIRIDHARADAS: It`s everywhere. And it`s not made up. I mean, I got to say, you and I are both having this conversation, because we had the freedom to read growing up. I don`t think I would be capable of having this conversation with you if I had not had the freedom to read.\par \par \uc2 I have traveled to many countries, as I`m sure you have, where people did not have the same freedom to read that I did. And I`m very aware that I`m able to have this conversation with you because I did have that freedom.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, that`s a pretty important freedom. You know, they`re talking about the freedom to, you know, have reproductive rights and reproductive justice. I mean, that`s -- it`s about control of your body. And it`s also about the freedom to, frankly, like go out on a Friday night and not be afraid you`re making a lifelong decision. If you meet someone at the bar, you know.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Marcotte wrote this great piece a few months ago about the Democratic Party and strategy saying, look, if we as Democrats cannot make the case to people that we are the party trying to, you know, have you curl up to have the freedom, to curl up with a good book and get laid without fear, we don`t deserve to win.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, to be very serious about it, all of these fights are fights for freedom. And often, they are reframed in these like wonky policy terms by folks on the left. And we have to go guttural and we have to remember people don`t understand what Medicare for all means, except people who really focus on this issue, but they know what it means to be free. They know what it means to be free from their boss deciding what kind of health care they have. And so speaking to people in the -- in that kind of physiological language of freedom is incredibly important.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:11]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, I want to rename the infrastructure bill or the Build Back Better thing. I want to rename that the Freedom to Drive bill right away with this -- with this inspiration.\par \par \uc2 Anand Giridharadas, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GIRIDHARADAS: Thank you so much, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  has had a full 24 hours, actually more than that, to deny the allegations in a civil lawsuit against him that accused him of committing sex crimes and confessing to tax crimes and sharing bribe money with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  has not said one word in his defense about any of those charges. Former federal prosecutor, Glenn Kirschner, joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:50:33]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  has had over 24 hours to respond to this 69- page civil complaint filed in a lawsuit against him yesterday seeking a minimum of $10 million for sexual harassment, sexual assault, and labor law violations, including denial of wages to the tune of $1 million of income denied to Noelle Dunphy, who went to work for Giuliani at the promised salary of $1 million a year which was never paid to her.\par \par \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2 , through a spokesperson, issued a written statement yesterday saying that he denies the allegations in the lawsuit. But those allegations include criminal, federal, and state tax evasion that could get \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  five years in federal prison which might, at this stage of Giuliani`s life, be in effect a life sentence.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit also quotes \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2 , telling Ms. Dunphy that he was selling Trump pardons for $2 million and splitting the extortion money between himself and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That is another possible federal crime alleged in the lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Rudolph Giuliani\uc2  is quoted in the lawsuit as saying that he and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would insist that the presidential election was stolen from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if Trump lost the election. And he said that a year before the election.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Glenn Kirschner, former federal prosecutor and an MSNBC legal analyst. He is the host of the Justice Matters podcast. And, Glenn. I first talked about this lawsuit last night. And when I had this in my hands for just a few hours, there`s so much in it that it was impossible to get everything in last night. We won`t get everything in tonight. And some of it we simply cannot say on television.\par \par \uc2 But I want to go to the last point, which is Giuliani discussing, claiming that the election was stolen a year before the election happened. That seems to me to be information of interest to \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  in her investigation of possible election fraud in Georgia to Jack Smith in his investigation of the events leading up to January 6 than Donald Trump`s apparent attempts to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: There`s a word in the law for that kind of evidence and information, premeditation. And, you know, Lawrence, it dovetails nicely with what we all heard \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  say well before the election was ever held, which was, if I lose, it was rigged.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they made no secret of their criminal scheme to sort of use a claim of a rigged election to try to overturn the will, the express will of the American voters. So, you know, this lawsuit, which, as you say, has so much really blockbuster information and horrific information with respect to what Rudy allegedly did to Ms. Dunphy.\par \par \uc2 And I will add that, you know, Ms. Dunphy certainly makes clear that she has some receipts, some hard corroboration. I mean, just for a two-week period in February, she details the date and the number of phone calls \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  made to her. Sometimes he was calling her 50 times a day. That shows a fairly close relationship. I think it totaled 177 calls in a two- week period in February of 2019. I can`t imagine even Rudy will claim those were 177 misdials.\par \par \uc2 And then you have this really blockbuster information about the alleged sale of presidential pardons. But, you know, we`ve heard this before, as far back as 2021 when a CIA officer who was convicted of illegally leaking some material, I think went public with it was the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  who first reported it.\par \par \uc2 I believe that a \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  associate said presidential pardons were going for $2 million. And it conjures up all of these other data points. \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , we saw sort of infamously in the January 6 House Select Committee public hearings said in the waning hours of the Trump presidency he didn`t have time for White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  to be whining about ethical lapses in the Oval Office because he, \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 , was too busy pushing out pardon after pardon after pardon, perhaps including the pardon that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gave to Jared Kushner`s father.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:23]\par \par \uc2 And one other data point, Lawrence, we saw in a book that was published by Kellyanne Conway, her allegation that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  walked up to her and said, hey, you want a pardon? Everybody needs one. So it becomes pretty clear. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was willing to give out pardons to friends to associates and perhaps even sell them together with \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. And as I`ve said, not every conversation was recorded, the ones that were recorded are specified in this 69-page complaint. And the lawyer who wrote this complaint was here last night. Lawyers cannot just type into a complaint filed in court. This conversation was recorded.\par \par \uc2 If it wasn`t recorded, the judge is going to find out eventually exactly what that recording says. And if it doesn`t exist, these lawyers would be in very serious trouble.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: And just because the conversation wasn`t recorded, doesn`t mean there wasn`t corroboration of it, for example, from other witnesses.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Glenn Kirschner, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is tonight`s LAST WORD. "The 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230517ej5g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 Interview with Gov. \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  (R-NH)\b0\par\par\uc2 Jen Psaki \par \uc2 2,808 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: The former president was, of course, in your home state for a town hall this week. He repeated the big lie, called January 6th a, quote, beautiful day, mocked a person who he sexually assaulted, according to a jury.\par \par \uc2 I know you said you`d vote for him if he`s the nominee. But watching that, hearing that, I mean, do you still -- that`s still where you stand?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  (R), NEW HAMPSHIRE: Well, he`s not going to be the nominee. I used to say that`s a hypothetical, but I think everyone`s kind of reassessing, you know, even some of his supporters are saying, well, okay, this can`t be the message. This is a terrible message with a terrible messenger.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not define the Republican Party. I don`t think any one individual ever defines the party, right? It`s about those pillars about what we`re about at our -- at our core.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: You`ve said this many times, that he is not going to be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: But, right now, he`s ahead by double digits, by a lot.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Against who?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Against \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , against Nikki Haley, against sort of his former vice president.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, what exactly is going to stop him? Is that you?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No, I guess my point is, there are so many other candidates to get in the race. There are so many more parts of the discussion we haven`t had a single debate yet. In fact, we won`t have a debate for the next two or three months.\par \par \uc2 And then, you`ll have maybe a dozen debates. You go to Iowa and New Hampshire, and all of this politics is yet to play out. They`ll be more indictments on his side and more distractions and everything.\par \par \uc2 And I think the opportunity is for candidates to talk about -- not just about the Trump thing, but to actually show themselves, show their personalities, show their opportunity, their -- their record of success. I think it`s just a huge opportunity, and a lot more political game to be played for candidates, whoever they may be, to get on the race, get on the debate stage and see where it falls. Maybe I do it, maybe I don`t.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: You`re still deciding.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah, I love to be on a debate stage.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: You`re welcome to tell me now if you`re going to run for president, of course. But what would you -- what would you be your first critique of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, you know, I think a lot of folks say -- well, you know, I see -- I`m going to go back to `16. I saw a lot of candidates trying to debate him on policy.\par \par \uc2 Look, you don`t debate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on policy. The guy has massive insecurities. He`s a loser and he lost many times.\par \par \uc2 He lost races in New Hampshire, not just for himself but for my fellow candidates. He really got behind really bad candidates that were extreme, that obviously couldn`t win in November.\par \par \uc2 If you can`t win in November, then you shouldn`t even be -- you shouldn`t even be in the discussion, and he can`t win.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, your -- you think the best argument is he`s a loser?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes, because he can`t win.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I did want to ask you about a scene from the town hall this week, which one of the things that struck me was the laughter in the audience, particularly when Trump was talking about E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 Some in the jury says he sexually abused, defamed and now has to pay her $5 million.\par \par \uc2 Now, you were not involved in making up the audience.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: But these are -- these are people in the audience from New Hampshire, Republicans in New Hampshire --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: -- undeclared voters in that room.\par \par \uc2 I was curious just what your reaction was when you saw them laughing?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, it was embarrassing, right? Now, I can understand as the camera pans to that audience, I knew pretty much everybody in that audience -- they`re all Trump supporters.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: So, the audience was absolutely photos from supporters. I wasn`t surprised to hear the support.\par \par \uc2 But when you`re talking about a serious issue like that and laughter and mocking and all, that`s -- it`s completely inappropriate, without a doubt and it doesn`t shine a positive light on New Hampshire. But, again, understand what the audience make up was.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Large percentage of Trump supporters.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Oh, I would say almost all of them, yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, you are contemplating running for president. So, I want to dive into a bunch of the issues that you talked about and you have positions on.\par \par \uc2 Trump was asked repeatedly if he`d sign a national abortion ban, if it came to his desk.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: He wouldn`t say. I mean, he was asked repeatedly.\par \par \uc2 Would you -- can you categorically say you would or wouldn`t sign an abortion ban?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Not only would I not sign a national abortion ban, but nobody should be talking about signing a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 Look, Dobbs said that states are going to make their -- basically can make their own decisions. So they will. And this is not a national argument where the pro-life is ever going to win or the pro-choice movement is never going to win. It`s never going to be one.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s have those battles at the state level. What Mississippi wants is probably different than California or New Hampshire or New York or Missouri. So, let the states decide because the accountability those decisions by the governors and the legislature are held directly with those voters. If they don`t like it, they can make the change.\par \par \uc2 That`s actually good government. That`s -- that`s what the -- again, I don`t think the Founding Fathers had battles on abortion rights envisioned when they created this country.\par \par \uc2 But the process by which we hammer out tough issues should always be handled at a more localized level, because the voters have more power. They have more say. They can design the path for their communities in a much better way, as probably oppose to a one-size-fits-all out of that building over there. That`s really the answer.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, moving on to other things. You have an "A" rating from the \uc2 NRA\uc2 . Not something you`ve run away from. You went to the \uc2 NRA\uc2  convention just a few days after three kids were killed in a school shooting in Nashville. There have been, of course, a number of mass shootings --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: -- over the last few years.\par \par \uc2 Why did you think it was important to go and be there?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, I don`t -- look, I wouldn`t say it was important to be there, but it`s a large constituency. New Hampshire has, I would say, a very strong record on supporting individual`s rights around the Second Amendment and gun rights.\par \par \uc2 And there`s, you know, 60,000 people there or whatever, a lot of our manufacturers were there. You know, it was good to go and kind of talk about our record of success -- not just around the laws but around the individual freedoms that drive that.\par \par \uc2 If you just -- I mean, those mass shootings -- I`m a governor, right? So I`m 24/7. God forbid there`s a mass shooting, it could happen to anyone at anytime anywhere in this country.\par \par \uc2 There`s no law you`re going to pass that`s going to stop mass shootings. There`s no law you`re going to pass that`s just going to magically stop gun violence. That`s clear.\par \par \uc2 So --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Do you think there are federal restrictions on guns that should be the law of the land?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: I think where the laws are right now are pretty good. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, let me ask you some of the New Hampshire laws because you`re the expert on it and you know how guns are thought of in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 So, in 2019, you vetoed three gun safety bills passed by the state legislature. One of those bills would`ve prevented guns on school property. They would`ve required parents to leave their guns in the car when picking up their kids from school.\par \par \uc2 So, to me as a parent, that sounds like it makes a lot of sense. What doesn`t make sense about that? Or --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: It`s public property. It`s public ground.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So parents should be allowed to bring guns into schools?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Technically, they can, yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: But why would that be something that would make kids safer or teachers --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: I`m not saying it`s making people safer but it`s -- but it would be pulling away an individual`s right when they -- when they have the right to do that on public property and public ground.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So it`s freedom? Or it`s right to be able to --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Okay. Do you think teachers -- because one of the things that President Trump called for was hardening of schools.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Do you thing more teachers should be armed in schools?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: If you have training programs and a training program and a system where teacher can be armed and, again, there`s -- yeah, I mean, that`s a real possibility if you don`t have other school resource officers. Otherwise, you can bring -- bring in outside school resource officers, or if teacher wants to be trained, you have a system in place to get appropriate training. Not just a guy -- you know, Mr. Smith, a cactus (ph) teacher, carries a gun. You got to have the right training and protocols and all of that.\par \par \uc2 And we made tens of millions of dollars in investment in New Hampshire around the hardening of our schools, around investing and safety. It isn`t just about whether someone has a gun and a training or not -- we have that, of course -- but it`s really about, you know, lockdown systems, communication, safe -- safe doors, all that sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 Those things can sound small but in the moment of that crisis, they can be, you know, very big deals. So, we take that very seriously.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I mean, teachers across the country don`t necessarily want to be armed. They`ve been pretty outspoken --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: If they don`t want to be, then they shouldn`t be. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: -- about it. And police groups, they are not calling for more teachers to be armed. Nor are first responders calling for more teachers to be armed.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m just calling that out as like a solution that`s --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, I`m not calling for more teachers to be armed other than to say if schools -- schools should have the ability to provide some type of resource officer, some type of safety protocols and officer, whether it`s being armed or not, whatever they think is the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 And if it`s a teacher, then that has the right training and protocols, and they want to allow that, then that`s fine. I don`t think we should be handing out guns to teachers per se. But if there`s a teacher that has that training and experience and can provide that resource, and the school is okay with it, I have no problem with that.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, another issue close to your heart -- you call yourself a fiscal hawk.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: That sounds like you might need a t-shirt or something like that.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah. There you go.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: In the town hall this week, former President Trump also urged the House Republicans to force a debt default if the Democrats don`t agree to spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 Given how catastrophic that would be, is rooting for that the right thing right now in terms of a message?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Oh, God, no. No, no, no. No one should be rooting for a default.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a little step back. I can tell you, my -- one of my biggest frustrations with this town is I think 98 percent of people in that building right there don`t have an economic sense. I think there`s a massive economic illiteracy -- I can`t even get the word out -- massive economic illiteracy in Washington, D.C. And that has real repercussions to everyday citizens, to families, to local banks, to local small businesses.\par \par \uc2 Defaulting on the debt would be catastrophic. It would send a chain of reaction of events across the country that would really freeze everything up. It would be terrible.\par \par \uc2 Republicans typically don`t like to increase, you know, our debt limit, right? We want to be more fiscally responsible, I support that 100 percent. So, the fact that Republicans --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Unless there`s a Republican president and they voted for it three times under Trump. Yeah, yeah.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah, and I agree. And again, you know, they should have -- I don`t increasing the debt limit as long as there`s some -- there`s some cuts, like -- Republicans are giving in. They`re saying, well, we don`t really want to increase it but we`ll capitulate, we`ll negotiate, we`ll give you something, we`ll increase that.\par \par \uc2 But you`ve got to show that there`s some fiscal discipline instill back in the country and then the federal government and that comes with control and spending.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, the biggies, as you know, because you`re a fistful hawk, our defense spending.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Would you be open to defense spending? Cutting defense spending, I should say?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Look, I wouldn`t -- I don`t -- I don`t -- if you start saying, well, education spending versus defense (ph) -- look, I think you can find massive efficiency in spending in every department in government.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: But there are very big buckets as you know that have the most size.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So, defense spending or cuts to Social Security, Medicare, Medicaid?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Those would be the biggest buckets.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah. Well, so, I`m going to jump around a little bit. You put up Social Security. Social Security, 23 percent cut in Social Security benefits are coming eight years from now. That`s in the law. That is fact. That`s happening.\par \par \uc2 Every senior in America should be very concerned that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says I`m not doing anything about it. Even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says, me neither. They`re joining at the hip saying, massive cuts are coming to Social Security benefits and we`re not doing anything about it.\par \par \uc2 How about a candidate that absorb (ph) and say we`re going to in a fix that, we`re going to make sure that doesn`t happen, we`re going to create opportunity, efficiency in the system? Not just --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, you talked about -- I mean, you`ve talked about supporting privatizing Social Security. Is that something you still --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: It`s not about privatizing. I mean, that`s kind of an easy answer. It`s really about creating up other opportunities, options for the individual in terms of how they invest their dollars and ensuring that we can get kind of the fiscal house back in order before these 23 -- this 22 percent cut isn`t coming two decades from now. It`s literally coming eight years from now. Biden and Trump just don`t care.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It requires tough political conversations, which you -- we have not --\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: I don`t think it`s that tough.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, but you`ve not -- you`ve not shied away from.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Would you be open to raising the retirement age?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: I think if someone wanted to move the retirement age -- yeah, look, did -- they do it in France, they do it in Chile, they did it all in these other countries without a problem.\par \par \uc2 Now, do you have to move to 70? No, I don`t think you have to go that -- that far. You can look at the actual numbers.\par \par \uc2 But the retirement age hasn`t moved in decades, or maybe ever. I don`t know. You`d probably know better than I.\par \par \uc2 So, I think there`s just a lot of other doors that have to be open and address -- address them (ph) and talk about.\par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020230514ej5e0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for May 11, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Mehdi Hasan, Charles Blow, Andrew Weissmann \par \uc2 7,320 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:15]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 And last night when I was watching the Trump show, I was thinking about the people who could have handled that better the interviewers who could have handled that better. One of them is going to join us tonight, actually more than one, but one professional interviewer is going to join us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Weissmann`s going to join us too. He would have been great on that stage. I mean, it`s not -- it`s not his normal forum, the courtroom is his normal forum, but it needed kind of a courtroom approach, a more precise targeted question than many of the questions we got. And so, we`re going to try to see what we -- we might have some suggestions.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": I would like to see Andrew Weissmann v. Trump, but I`d actually like to see it in a courtroom.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: But that`s another matter for another day. I will be watching, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Listen, I think CNN did the best they could, using conventional tools and we kind of knew ahead of time the conventional tools don`t work. I only hope that they are honestly, privately, I`m not asking them to admit any mistakes, but publicly. But just privately, watch the game film, try to figure out what you did right because they did do some things right, what you did wrong and try to do better the next time, because you know there`s going to be a next time.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Oh, yeah, there is.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Trump is coming back to CNN. He has found a new home. He will be back and let`s just hope CNN is watching the game film and figuring out how to do it better.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Here`s the fingers crossed. Have good show, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah. Thanks, Alex. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  only submits to interviews with people he thinks he can beat, and he has been right most of the time, with Jonathan Swann being the most memorable exception. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has never done an interview or a town hall with anyone he fears like say Rachel Maddow or Chris Hayes, both of whom have had prime time shows since long before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  became a politician.\par \par \uc2 Now`s the moment where you just want to close your eyes just for a second, and imagine what would have been different with Rachel Maddow on that stage last night with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I was the first person in the news business to call \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a liar. I did that on television, and I labeled what he was saying about President Obama`s birth certificate a lie. That was on this program in 2011.\par \par \uc2 Many, many years before anyone at the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  or CNN or any other major news organization dared to use the word "lie" in the same sentence with the words \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . On \uc2 Twitter\uc2  then, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  threatened to sue me for calling him a liar and on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , I begged him to sue me, so I could get him in an under oath deposition like E. Jean Carroll did.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s not surprising that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has never done an interview with me, on a program that has a larger audience than any show on CNN. Usually, this program has triple the audience that CNN has.\par \par \uc2 When I was calling \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a liar in 2015, when he was running for president, last night`s CNN moderator was worried about someone else.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAITLAN COLLINS, CNN MODERATOR: \uc2 George Soros\uc2  is this foreign-born left- wing guy who essentially wants to change the nature of our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Alabama-born Kaitlan Collins was not yet working for CNN. She was working for \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  then at a right-wing website where she fell for the lie that she was never fact checked on that \uc2 George Soros\uc2  was funding \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: I think it`s an issue also that the Democratic Party needs to address because so many Democrats have been openly endorsed \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 , and now that we see that this guy who is funding Hillary Clinton is also funding \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 , that`s a problem and that`s something that Americans should be aware of.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That was 2015. That was just two presidential elections ago.\par \par \uc2 There are people working at CNN right now who knew that that was crazy talk then, and those people all have more experience interviewing presidents and presidential candidates but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  chose Kaitlan Collins.\par \par \uc2 That is common in these situations with news networks. The candidate in effect chooses who the moderator will be. CNN may have said the name Kaitlan Collins first before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did, but Trump then accepted that name when he could have rejected that name and gotten anyone else he wanted at CNN.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:04]\par \par \uc2 And this is the way all the networks do these things. This is not an accident that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  found himself on the stage with Kaitlan Collins. I have never seen anyone at CNN do a better job of interviewing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I`ve never seen anyone at any other network do a better job of interviewing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I don`t think I could have done a better job of interviewing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But there are many people who could and many of them do not work in TV news where the conventional style of TV questioning absolutely does not work with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , never has, never will. The one thing I would not have done is wasted questions, asked some stupid questions. And it took a village and took a village to ask the questions from the stage last night, a CNN village.\par \par \uc2 "Politico" reported: We`re told Collins has been steadily prepping since the town hall, which has been in the works for months, was finally confirmed two weeks ago, spending hours meeting with producers, network CEO Chris Licht and top execs Mark Preston and David Chalian at a New Hampshire hotel. A senior CNN staffer played Trump in the prep sessions we`re told and last night, Collins and team hit the stage at Saint Anselm, going through staging and lighting -- staging and lighting was beautiful, just perfect, nice work.\par \par \uc2 David Chalian took a high-minded angle with "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " saying: I obviously can`t control what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says, but we can control -- what we can control is our journalism. And the one thing they absolutely knew that they could control was the first question.\par \par \uc2 They had weeks and weeks and weeks of meetings to decide what the first question would be and the first question was classic Washington press group think. It was a childish question, but didn`t ask for an answer to anything. It asked for a speech and the question worked. The question that they worked on four weeks and delivered to Trump by the team that CNN was, why should Americans put you back in the White House?\par \par \uc2 That was the first question and, of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  gave a speech and he got laughs right away and he ignored as the question invited him to the preamble to the question that CNN worked on for weeks, preambles to questions are meaningless. It`s the question that counts.\par \par \uc2 And here`s what the CNN team came up with as the hard hitting, totally ignorable preamble to the question. Your polls show that you are dominating the Republican race right now, but you are also under active federal investigation for trying to overturn the 2020 election results. Your first term ended with a deadly riot at the Capitol and you still have not publicly acknowledged the election results. Why should Americans put you back in the White House?\par \par \uc2 To CNN`s surprise, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ignored all of those points in the preamble, leaving Kaitlan Collins hopelessly trying to wrestle him into answering the question, can you publicly acknowledge that you did lose the 2020 election? It was a very conventional line of questioning for Washington reporters, most of them would have chosen such hopeless open- ended questions, and most Washington reporters could not have done better than the question, do you have any regrets about your actions on January 6th?\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s answer to that question quickly got laughter from the audience at the mention of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  in the most insulting terms he could find for \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . We know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has no regrets about his actions on January 6th. What could possibly give anyone the idea that that was a good question to ask? That was a question that wasted several minutes of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lying about what happened on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Kaitlan Collins lost that struggle with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about January 6th. She tried, she challenged him but never in the right way. Her toughest question about January 6 was, so why didn`t you tell him to go home sooner?\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was never asked why he did not make a single phone call to anyone in the government, in the Defense Department, Justice Department, in law enforcement, to stop the ongoing attack on the Capitol, while it was going on.\par \par \uc2 [22:1 0:12]\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not asked why he never ordered any action during the attack on the Capitol to stop the attack on the Capitol, and no one -- no one at CNN dared to consider playing the video for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and as importantly his audience, his cheering audience the tape of Liz Cheney`s description of Donald Trump`s dereliction of duty during the infamous 187 minutes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THEN-REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): He did not call his secretary of defense on January 6th. He did not talk to his attorney general. He did not talk to the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 . President Trump gave no order to deploy the national guard that day and he made no effort to work with the Department of Justice to coordinate and display and deploy law enforcement assets.\par \par \uc2 But Vice President Pence did each of those things.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And now allow yourself a second to imagine if \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  had been on that stage with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last night.\par \par \uc2 Kaitlan Collins pointed out that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was at the Capitol that day and felt that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  endangered his life to which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said, I don`t think he was in any danger. Then came the question which was probably worked on for weeks by the CNN team, Mr. President, do you feel that you owe him an apology? Big surprise, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t think he owes \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  an apology. That was a wasted question.\par \par \uc2 What should have been asked was, why didn`t you call the vice president of the United States when he was in a building that was being attacked? Why didn`t you take action to protect the life of the vice president of the United States when he was in a building that you were watching being attacked on television by people who were threatening to kill \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 CNN came up with, do you feel that you owe him an apology? When CNN was rehearsing these questions with their staffer standing in for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , did the Trump impersonator apologize to \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 ? Did the Trump impersonator just buckle under the weight of that question and prove to the CNN staff that that`s a great question, that`ll work.\par \par \uc2 Kaitlan Collins did a very good job of exposing Donald Trump`s ignorance of what the debt ceiling is and his recklessness and urging Republicans to default on the debt, and she did an excellent job in exposing Donald Trump`s full recklessness on the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: You once said that using the -- that using the debt ceiling as a negotiating wedge, it just could not happen. You ou said that when you were on the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: That`s when I was president.\par \par \uc2 TCOLLINS: So why is it different now that you`re out of office?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Because now I`m not president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The hypocrisy is exposed. The hypocrite gets lost that gets laughter. The laughter and applause by the CNN assembled audience tells you everything you need to know about the Trump voters in that room. There is nothing in government that they take seriously. They think it`s all a joke, because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has proved to them that it`s all a joke. He himself is a joke and he was president because they voted for the joke.\par \par \uc2 And that now widespread Republican belief that the debt ceiling is a joke has the country on the precipice of a possible economic disaster. Kaitlan Collins repeatedly used an old Washington standard, that unthinking reporters always rely on, do you stand by those comments? When has \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ever said, no, I don`t -- I didn`t mean what I said in the past? The "do you stand by those comments" question which was repeated over and over is the weakest possible way to approach \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , because you know you`re going to get a long rambling speech after a question like that.\par \par \uc2 Kaitlan Collins and/or the CNN team came up with a very good, clear, simple, pointed question about abortion. Would you sign a federal abortion ban into law? Excellent question.\par \par \uc2 It became very clear very quickly that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was never going to answer that question but Kaitlan Collins continued to chase him on that particular question and ignored stunning lies \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told about Democrats, saying that Roe versus Wade -- under Roe versus Wade, quote, they could kill the baby at any time they wanted to. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  reminded the audience that he accused \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  of wanting to, quote, rip the baby out of the womb at the end of the ninth month. They will kill the baby in the ninth month.\par \par \uc2 Kara Swisher of "New York Magazine" proved last night on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and has approved many times in interviews that she`s conducted that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could not possibly have survived with Kara Swisher on that stage. She wrote: You cannot get caught up in getting him to answer your question when it becomes clear he has exactly no intention of answering it. A good example was the obvious lie of the nine month abortion he kept repeating unchallenged.\par \par \uc2 Rather than trying to get him to answer the original question about a federal ban on abortion, you need to quickly shift to calling out the lie very clearly and then asking him why he chose to lie about it, because arguing about the lie itself is a dead end. Call it a "weird" lie, too, since it is so provably wrong and definitely ask him about his personal experience when it came to abortion. Oh, if Kara Swisher had been on that stage last night.\par \par \uc2 I have no doubt that everyone involved at CNN approached the questioning of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  honorably and with the best of professional intentions. I know the head of CNN and I am fully confident that he was trying to deliver a useful hour of television to American voters. But using the conventional tools TV so-called town halls, including selecting an audience of only Republican voters and potential Republican voters and inviting the most unconventional candidate in history inevitably created the problem of what do you do about the constant flow of lies during the hour.\par \par \uc2 Using the conventional tools, CNN did the best that they could do. But using the conventional tools mean that CNN has not figured out a way to improve its coverage of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the eight years since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  became a politician and Kaitlan Collins was still concerned about the foreign-born \uc2 George Soros\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Mehdi Hasan, host of "THE MEHDI HASAN SHOW" on MSNBC and Peacock. He is the author of "Win Every Argument: The Art of Debating, Persuading and Public Speaking", and that`s why he`s here tonight.\par \par \uc2 Also with us is Charles Blow, columnist for "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " and an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Mehdi, I was thinking about you every minute of last night because you`re one of the people -- you`re one of the people that could have been sent on to that stage and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could not have survived.\par \par \uc2 You know, listen, I am here to try to help CNN with their post-game analysis of how to do this better because we know they`re going to do it again and we can hope they will do it better, but the -- they`ve got to work a lot harder on the specificity of their questions. But you`re the expert on this, you`re way better than I am on this. So tell us what you saw and what you think they need to improve.\par \par \uc2 MEHDI HASAN, MSNBC HOST, "THE MEHDI HASAN SHOW": Well, before I do that, you made a very good point, Lawrence, about the host and Trump selection of the host. I would say there`s only been three television interviews in the last decade where Trump`s feet have been held to the fire, where he`s looked nervous, defensive, held to account. Jake Tapper in 2016 and Jonathan Swann and Chris Wallace in 2020 for HBO and Fox.\par \par \uc2 Interestingly, CNN employed two of those three people, Wallace and Tapper. Funny enough, they didn`t do the interview on Wednesday night, surprising that, not so much.\par \par \uc2 What would I have done differently? Well, first of all, I wouldn`t have done the interview but we`ll part that for a moment. If you are going to do the interview, number one, you don`t do it live, Lawrence. Number two, you don`t do it in front of an audience of his groupies and super fans.\par \par \uc2 And number three, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  turns up with a piece of paper in his pocket, when he has a prop that he pulls out to laughter and starts reading his own quotes back to you, what do you have? What does the host have? What weaponry, conventional, unconventional, can you bring to this event?\par \par \uc2 And I think that the town hall format was a was a disaster for CNN and for Kaitlan Collins when she said to him, you asked for extra vote from \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , and he says, no, I didn`t, and he lies, that`s the moment you played a phone call. We`ve never seen \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  react to the phone call, played a phone call to your three million viewers that you`ve got for the first time at nine o`clock on Wednesday night on CNN.\par \par \uc2 When he says, for example, oh, Chris Miller, the acting defense secretary, oh, no, he didn`t -- he didn`t say what you think I said about the National Guard, play the 1/6 testimony.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:07]\par \par \uc2 When he says, oh, Ashli Babbitt, poor Ashli Babbitt, was killed, forget the police officers, play the violence against the police officers at length, put the camera on Trump`s face as you do it and get him to react. You`ve got to create those moments where he`s put on the defensive.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Swann did it wonderfully when Trump threw graphs about South Korean COVID. Jonathan didn`t move on. He famously said, okay, let`s look at these graphs and then you can see Trump panicking because Trump expects the interviewer to move on.\par \par \uc2 You don`t move on. As you say, you asked the pointed questions and then you stick with it. Too often on Wednesday night, Kaitlan Collins moved on or handed the mic over to an audience member who invariably was a Trump supporter.\par \par \uc2 Nine people spoke from the audience, Lawrence. Seven of those people were supporters of Trump`s 2020 re-election bid. That is a joke. That is not an audience of undecided.\par \par \uc2 So the audience was a disaster and a joke, and I`m sorry, one last point, you mentioned the CEO of CNN. Chris Licht says this morning apparently on a -- on an editorial call, it was a success because we made news.\par \par \uc2 Number one, they didn`t make that much news. Most of the Trump lies were old lies. Number two, that is not our job, Lawrence, to make news. In a situation like that our job is to report the news, our job is to educate and inform and our job is to hold the people in power to account.\par \par \uc2 Trump is celebrating, his supporters are celebrating he was not held to account.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, I want to be fair to CNN on this point about the audience because it is very common at the presidential primary stage for the networks to assemble an audience of voters who are going to vote in that primary. So mostly Democratic voters and some undecided voters or mostly Republican voters and some undecided voters.\par \par \uc2 HASSAN: Two, they had two undecideds.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, it didn`t seem that many undecideds there.\par \par \uc2 But so -- so -- but what I would say about that, Charles Blow, is it`s one of the examples of the things that I think all of these networks have to reconsider. Would it be so bad for the Democratic candidate for president in a town hall to have to hear a question from a Republican in the audience? And let`s remember, every one of the those questions was screened by CNN. They were forced to read their questions.\par \par \uc2 So CNN knew what every question was before they put that question from the audience on the air. You don`t have to put crazy questions on the air and they didn`t, but I`m just thinking the point about the audience is that the networks have always done it that way. It`s time to rethink that.\par \par \uc2 Among, Charles, I think many other things about the conventional approach to this.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BLOW, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Right, I think your use of the words conventional tools and conventional approach are very appropriate here because I -- you know, Mehdi kind of skipped over the this idea, you know, having the town hall in the first place, he said we shouldn`t have had it, I want to stay on that a little while though.\par \par \uc2 You know, you can make a really strong argument, journalistic argument on both sides and people who have, that this is the front runner among Republicans so you have to be able to accommodate some sort of town hall, some sort of interview to have that person be heard, and so that people can get to understand who they are.\par \par \uc2 There`s a counterargument though which is that none of our conventional tools were ever built for a person like this. We keep trying to apply conventional tools in all parts of society, whether they are government, media, or otherwise to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they don`t fit. There is no way to deal with this torrent of lies coming at you.\par \par \uc2 You can say, let`s stop this, contest it, or show video, I think that probably helps, but the problem is what is the benefit at the end of the day? In journalism, we are taught -- you know, for those of us who went to journalism school, you`re taught about like what are the benefits of journalism writ large to society? It is to uncover and to expose.\par \par \uc2 What journalistic -- what is societal benefit comes from allowing someone that much time to unleash a torrent of lies which you cannot fact check all of them in real time. That is not a situation that we`ve ever found ourselves in. You know, we have fact checks and have that for probably decades, but they were fact checking a couple of things, the person exaggerated here, maybe there`s a -- maybe there`s an all-out lie somewhere.\par \par \uc2 This idea that the person that you`re -- that`s on the screen or in an interview is lying all the time, there is -- there`s no way to deal with that. And you end up, although your motives are good journalistically, you end up in the end doing a disservice because the lies have gotten out of the bag and now you have to do spend the next couple of days trying to shove the lies back in, but you will never have the same audience.\par \par \uc2 So then we have to stop and consider whether or not we have to change conventional wisdom, conventional tactics for this one person, because he is such a threat to not only journalism but to the country itself.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:03]\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah. I`m officially undecided on whether CNN should or should not do this. I am not undecided on whether they should try harder. They absolutely should try harder.\par \par \uc2 They said they had no rules with Trump, no agreements with Trump. Well, that means there was no reason they couldn`t have used the kind of video help, that many is talking about, that I`m referring to.\par \par \uc2 But they`ve -- they`ve really absolutely, Charles and Mehdi, they have to figure out new ways of doing this because we know they`re going to keep doing it, and -- my -- I`m going to take the role of encouraging to try to do it better. That`s about all I can offer them.\par \par \uc2 Mehdi Hasan, Charles Blow, thank you very much, for joining our discussion tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, last night, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made statements that can and will be used against him by special prosecutor Jack Smith. Andrew Weissmann joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Do you wish that you had testified?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, it wouldn`t have made a difference. This is a rigged deal. This was a -- my lawyer said, sir, you don`t have to do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Do wish that you had testified was the weakest possible way of asking \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about his fear of testifying in E. Jean Carroll`s lawsuit, where the jury found in favor of E. Jean Carroll and awarded her $5 million for Donald Trump`s sexual of assault E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 The right question would have been, why did you lie to reporters when you were standing on a golf course in Ireland last week and tell them that you were leaving early to rush back to New York to, in your words, confront that woman in court, a lie that provoked the judge in the case to extend the deadline until Sunday night, for you, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , to decide whether you would testify in that case. Why did you lie that you were rushing back to New York?\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , of course, would have lied his way around that question, but you have to load his previous lies into the question in order to make the question worth asking. Do you wish that you had testified was a terrible question on that point. Kaitlan Collins did effectively obtain testimony from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last night that can and will be used against him by Special Prosecutor Jack Smith.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Why did you take those documents with you when you left the White House?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I had every right tool under the Presidential Records Act. You have the Presidential Records Act. I was there and I took what I took and it gets declassified.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That shows Jack Smith that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  willfully and knowingly took the classified documents that are the subject of a criminal investigation. And then there was this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Did you ever show those classified documents to anyone?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Not really. I would have the right to. By the way, they were declassified after --\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: What do you mean not, really?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Not that I can think of.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: One of Donald Trump`s answers also indicates that he knew that he willfully did not comply with the subpoena requiring him to return the documents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Why did you not turn them over when you got a subpoena asking for you to turn them over?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Because we were negotiating with them. We were negotiating with them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, there`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in that answer to Kaitlan Collins saying that he knew that he willfully did not comply with the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is Andrew Weissmann, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  general counsel and former chief of the Criminal Division of Eastern District of New York. He`s a professor of practice at NYU Law School and an MSNBC Legal Analyst.\par \par \uc2 And, Andrew, I think tonight there is one person out there who is cheering CNN on, please, more Trump town halls, and that would be Jack Smith.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Absolutely. And, to relate that to the conversation you are having in a previous block, the reason is that all of these are admissions. They are all admissible. And one of the things that Jack Smith, when this is indicted, is going to do is have a chart of all of the admissions of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said, particularly his inconsistent stories, saying that this is inconceivable that he is telling the truth because his stories about what happened to the documents keeps changing.\par \par \uc2 You remember when we first heard of this, it was basically a defense de jure. We are going to see a chart which lists that all out. And when you are questioning \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in an interview, as was happened last night, you would to go through all of those inconsistent stories. And you would do what Mehdi Hasan said, which is slow down and take your time and make him have to eat each one of those to point out all of these inconsistent stories.\par \par \uc2 The second thing you could do is you can also sort of sum up on him. It`s a classic sort of prosecutorial technique. You saw it in the E. Jean Carroll case, which is to say, you say X, but let`s talk about all the other people who say Y. Mike pence told you that the election wasn`t stolen. Bill Barr, your own attorney general, said that. The leaders of the Department of Justice who you charge (ph) said that. And go through the litany of all the evidence of people saying it did not happen and then say, but we have your word with no evidence, just to a word that was stolen. So, that kind of technique is one that you are going to see in court and it`s something that could be used by CNN and other reporters if you slow down and sort of really force the person to have to confront all of that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I wish you could give a class at NYU Law School for questioning \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on television, which is a separate discipline, since there is no judge there to gavel in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a witness.\par \par \uc2 [22:35:06]\par \par \uc2 Quickly, before we go, it seems that the case for obstruction of justice was strengthened last night when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  consciously saying I knew that I didn`t return all the documents for the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Yes. Look, the obstruction case got stronger. An interesting part of the case, which was, we`ve all been thinking about this case as an illegal retention case, that he illegally retained the documents at Mar-a- Lago, but it was unclear if he illegally took the documents from the White House. And his lawyers have been saying it was inadvertent. Well, according to his own testimony yesterday, that`s not what it was. It was intentional.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann, thank you very much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  offered a solution to school shootings last night, teachers with guns. Fred Guttenberg, who lost his daughter, Jaime, in the 2018 Parkland school shooting, will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:40:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I love teachers was one of the minor lies \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told last night. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  offered his solution to end school shootings in America when he said, I love teachers. Many of these teachers are soldiers, ex-soldiers, ex-policemen. They are people that really understand weapons. Teachers with guns is the Trump solution.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest, Fred Guttenberg, whose daughter, Jaime, was murdered in the Parkland Florida massacre at \uc2 Marjory Stoneman Douglas High School\uc2 , that was Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  here, before Senate Democrats met to discuss gun safety legislation. The Senate Democrats` meeting was scheduled after eight people were murdered by a Nazi with an AR-15 at a Texas mall last Saturday. The shooting left six-year-old boy named William without his parents and three-year-old brother.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Fred Guttenberg, who lost his daughter, Jaime, in the 2018 Parkland school shooting. He is the co-author of American Carnage, Shattering the Myths that Fuel Gun Violence. Fred, I`m very sorry that you have to be here on this subject and that we have met the way we have met. And I am sorry for what you feel every time you have to discuss this. What is your reaction to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying teachers with guns, especially all those former cops who were teachers, which many of us who have been public school teachers have never met a former cop who`s a teacher?\par \par \uc2 FRED GUTTENBERG, CO-AUTHOR, AMERICAN CARNAGE: So, I didn`t watch last night. I actually chose to ignore him. And I`m going to be honest with you, I`m going to continue to choose to ignore him. However, the entire first 30 minutes of your show is my reaction. I have spent the past week here in Washington telling every single legislator, stop listening to the liars. And last night, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the liar, and I just won`t listen to the liars anymore. They put us in the position that we are in.\par \par \uc2 So, let me just kind of frame the schools issue because we`re not going to listen to him. Here is what you need to know. There are about 115,000 schools in America. Since Columbine, there have been about 400 school shootings. That`s a lot of school shootings. However, it`s 0.3 percent of the total number of schools. And so the idea of arming teachers to solve that is simply -- it is fulfilling the goal of a gun lobby that still wants to sell more guns. It will not make kids or faculty safer. In fact, it will guarantee more gun violence in schools, because you will have more guns in schools with people who may react emotionally, who may leave them out. It is a terrible idea.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What are you hoping that the Democrats might be able to achieve?\par \par \uc2 GUTTENBERG: You know, listen, there`s a lot we need to do. 2024 election is around the corner. It is not far away. I need the Democrats to put a plan out there that inspires Americans, whether it is around bans, around repeal of PLCAA, around the age, around expanding background checks. Lawrence, I don`t need to tell you this, this is the election of our lifetime, and this is the issue that is going to drive people to vote in record numbers in `24.\par \par \uc2 I can assure you of one thing, the other side, the reason why they go so hard on that base is that base will vote. But 80 percent of America is with me on this one. 80 percent of America wants this problem solved. And we need to put out a plan that shows the 80 percent of America, they will get it delivered, if we can put more senators in the Senate and return the House and, yes, return \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to the presidency.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Fred Guttenberg, I am very sorry for what you have been through. I thank you very much for what you do and thank you for joining us again tonight.\par \par \uc2 GUTTENBERG: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. Fred`s new book is American Carnage, Shattering the Myths that Fuel Gun Violence.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:00]\par \par \uc2 Coming up, last night, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  once again secured \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  support for his presidential campaign. Yale Professor Timothy Snyder joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Last night, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would not answer a very good question about who he wants to win the war in Ukraine that Russia started, which obviously makes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the candidate \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  once again wants to win the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 [22:50:05]\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his propaganda supporters and Russia continue to threaten to the use of nuclear weapons. In The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Yale Professor Timothy Snyder writes, in nearly 15 months of war, despite Russian nuclear propaganda and western anxiety, there has been no use of nuclear weapons. This is an absence worthy of an explanation. Those who predicted escalation if Ukrainians resisted, if the west supplied weapons or if Russia suffer defeat, have thus far been wrong.\par \par \uc2 Strategic thinkers point to deterrence and note that nuclear use would not, in fact, bring a Russian victory. It would ensure a dramatic western response and make Russian leaders pariahs. But there is a deeper explanation, Russia`s nuclear talk is itself the weapon.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Timothy Snyder, professor of history at \uc2 Yale University\uc2 . He is the author of the Road to Unfreedom, Russia, Europe and America.\par \par \uc2 Professor Snyder, I have to say, that was one of those sentences just completely blew the subject wide open for me with stunning clarity, Russia`s nuclear talk is itself the weapon. Now, everything I have been hearing for the last year makes sense.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHY SNYDER, HISTORY PROFESSOR, YALE UNIVERSITY: Well, thank you for the kind word. It is I think something very important to understand. Either you have the nuclear propaganda or you have the nuclear use, but you don`t have both. What Russia has been doing has been using the nuclear talk for a very specific purpose, which is to try to prevent or at least slow down the delivery of western weapons, which would allow Ukraine to win the war.\par \par \uc2 That does not mean -- in fact, it means that Russia won`t use a nuclear weapon, because using the weapon would take the possibility of bullying with nuclear weapons. That`s what I mean when I say the talk itself is the weapon. We need to get through this as quickly as we can because this propaganda, thus far, this propaganda victory is Russia`s only victory and it`s a thing which is preventing a Ukrainian victory.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. I was very concerned about the nuclear possibility at the beginning, in the first month, two months, when Putin wasn`t really saber- rattling with nuclear at that point because he didn`t have to. He thought the imminent victory was any minute now. But then, as time wore on, two things happened. Putin and his acolytes started to talk about it. And I got increasingly less concerned, as they talked about it more and more, because of what I was seeing them do on the battlefield, do in action, it looked like every time we supplied the new thing that they said was the step too far, they did nothing.\par \par \uc2 SNYDER: Or, if anything, they de-escalated. We`ve supplied new weapons, they lost battles, they retreated. That`s not an escalation. That`s a de- escalation. The escalatory work has been done by the Russian propagandists themselves who have worked very hard to explain how withdrawal is not withdrawal and defeat is not a defeat, and how the purposes of the war are constantly changing. So, I think are observations there are very astute.\par \par \uc2 What we are in is a conventional war where some battles are won by one side and other battles are won by the other. And it`s very important for a thousand reasons that the right side when this war, which is why, of course, Mr. Trump`s remarks are so concerning. War is not -- it`s not one of these everybody is a winner-type situations. One side is going to win, one side is going to lose, and we want to make sure that the right side winds.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You make such a great point in the New York times, which has also been in the back of my mind but not as focused as this, and nuclear weapons did not bring the French victory in Algeria, nor did they preserve the British Empire. The Soviet Union lost its war on Afghanistan. America lost in Vietnam and in Iraq and in Afghanistan. Israel failed to win in Lebanon. Nuclear powers lose wars with some regularity.\par \par \uc2 Professor Snyder, the Vietnam example was the one that was so vivid in my mind. I wasn`t even counting the rest of them but also I guess Afghanistan with the Russians, that nuclear weapons, since World War II, have never helped a country win a war.\par \par \uc2 SNYDER: The only time they were used was by country that had already won a war, and that`s us, and it was a very special situation. Most of the time, since the Second World War, the smaller country beats the bigger country. It`s totally normal for big empires, for nuclear powers, to lose wars.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:00]\par \par \uc2 And this is why history is so wonderful and why it`s so important to understand history, because it can help us to see that an individual moment which seems very threatening is part of a larger pattern. War is terrifying. War contains risks. But we also know that nuclear powers regularly fight and lose conventional wars.\par \par \uc2 If we remember that, it can make us less anxious and it could help us understand what the Russians are anxious about, which is losing their own superpower status. If they did use a nuclear weapon, that would not confirm that it would remove forever, it would obliterate their superpower status. They would be pariahs and everybody around them would build up nuclear powers, which is one more reason why they`re not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Professor Timothy Snyder once again enlightening us as no one else can, we really appreciate you joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 SNYDER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230512ej5b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for May 10, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 7,437 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:28]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 And we have \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  leading off with us tonight, as -- he is, of course, the Democratic leader of the House of Representatives, highest ranking Democrat in the House. And it was a scheduled engagement for him to be here tonight.\par \par \uc2 What we did not know was that it would be his first opportunity to offer the Democratic response to over an hour of Trump lies delivered by CNN, and over the course of the previous hour. So -- and so, this will be his first opportunity to react to things like a former president of the United States advocating a default on the debt, you know, stuff that we`ve never heard before.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": You know it`s been a crazy hour when we didn`t even have time to get to that part of global financial catastrophe --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: -- because there was so much other crazy we had to plow through first. I am eager to see Leader Jeffries` response to all of that madness.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Have a great show.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, as I said, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  is our first guest tonight. And our first guest tonight was in the room yesterday. In Washington, nothing matters more than who was in the room and yesterday, the Democratic leader of the House Representatives, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , was in the room with three other congressional leaders and the president of the United States in their first bipartisan discussion of the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was president, Democrats never threatened to block an increase in the debt ceiling, but that is exactly what House Republicans and Senate Republicans are now threatening to do and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is urging them to do. Tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who increased the debt more than any president in history before him and signed three easily passed increases in the debt ceiling, cheered on House Republicans and urged them to force for the first time in history a default on the debt of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 So the day after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  became the first former president of the United States to be hit with a $5 million verdict for his sexual assault of E. Jean Carroll, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  became the first former president in history to advocate a default on the debt.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAITLAN COLLINS, CNN MODERATOR: You think the U.S. should default if the White House does not agree to the spending cuts Republicans are demanding?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Well, you might as well do it now because you`ll do it later.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is House Democratic Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you very, very much for joining us tonight, Mr. Leader. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Good evening, Lawrence. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, we didn`t plan this as a Democratic response to Trump madness, but I`m afraid that`s where we are at 10 p.m. tonight. You just heard a former president of the United States front runner for the Republican nomination saying that it`s time for the Republicans to force a default on the debt. He actually wants them to do it. What was your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, the Republican Party under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Trumpism has three basic philosophical pillars. One, facts don`t matter. Two, hypocrisy is not a constraint to their behavior. And three, they actually believe that shamelessness is a superpower.\par \par \uc2 This is a circumstance where Democrats, as you pointed out, Lawrence, for three times during the previous administration, raised debt ceiling without partisanship, without gamesmanship, without showmanship, notwithstanding the fact that in our 247-year history, 25 percent of the nation`s debt was accumulated during the four years of the Trump presidency. Yet we understood that a default would be catastrophic, that it would hurt everyday Americans, hurt small businesses, hurt the economy, and was not something that could ever be allowed to happen.\par \par \uc2 And here we have a former president making this extraordinary declaration. In part, perhaps, it`s because they believe that if they blow up the economy, it may ignore to their political benefit. I think they`re wrong about that because the American people understand how extreme and how reckless and how dangerous this group of extreme MAGA Republicans have become.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:03]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, when he advocated tonight defaulting on the debt, he gave us multiple choices. He says he doesn`t actually know what will happen. He said, you really don`t know what will happen until you do it. He said it might be very bad. It might not, it might not be very bad. He gave -- he gave the country that whole range of possible outcomes from this very reckless idea that he`s advocating.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: It`s very clear that if a default were to occur, it would be extremely bad for the country, for the economy, for everyday Americans. One, it would likely result in triggering a job killing recession. Millions upon millions of good paying jobs will be wiped out almost in an instant. It will crash the stock market, which will result in millions of retirees being put into a very difficult situation, creating a wide range of retirement insecurity for people who are heading into or are in their golden years.\par \par \uc2 And it`s also going to dramatically increase costs for people all across the country. It`s a nonstarter. It cannot happen. And it`s unfortunate that there are some in the House of Representatives who nonetheless, perhaps because they`re taking orders from the former president, are taking us down this path.\par \par \uc2 I`m thankful for President Biden`s leadership. He convened us yesterday. It was a forward-looking conversation. Staff met for several hours today. That will continue tomorrow. President Biden is going to bring us back together on Friday, and hopefully we`ll be able to find a constructive path forward. Certainly, House and Senate Democrats, under the leadership of President Biden, are committed to making sure we do everything possible to avoid a dangerous default.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Some of Donald Trump`s lies were challenged on CNN tonight, but not nearly close to all of Donald Trump`s lies were challenged. None, none of Donald Trump`s lies about abortion were challenged on CNN tonight, including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying that Democrats support abortion at nine months of pregnancy and that Democrats support abortion, which is to say killing infants after they are naturally born.\par \par \uc2 He said that they are in favor of infanticide. He called it abortion after the birth of the child. But he got away with all of that on CNN without any resistance whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has never met a lie, that he doesn`t like or that he`s unafraid to tell. And I think the challenge here is that the Democratic position on reproductive freedom is very clear. We support Roe v. Wade. We support a woman`s freedom to make her own reproductive health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 And it`s unfortunate that he was allowed to articulate those lies repeatedly without it being questioned, including by anyone in the audience, which was very disturbing, as I`ve seen reports of how the audience consistently reacted to many of the inhumane or undignified or reckless things that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was allowed to spout over the air for more than an hour. It appears.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: We support that freedom. We support that woman`s freedom. And, Lawrence, what the Republicans want to do is criminalize abortion care and impose a nationwide ban. That`s what`s at stake. One of the things at stake in the upcoming election.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Just to be clear, that was not in no sense a town hall and real town hall in New Hampshire. That audience was chosen person by person by CNN. That`s how they got in that room. Here is a lie \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told to that audience, to the country and the world, without any resistance at all from anyone in that room. He said. We have run out of ammunition because we gave it all to Ukraine. Does the United States military have any ammunition left tonight?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Certainly in the United States military remains the strongest military in the world. And by the way, our involvement in Ukraine is incredibly important. The people of Ukraine have fought valiantly, and it`s a struggle not just between Ukraine and Russia, but between the and autocracy, between truth and propaganda, between freedom and tyranny. And it appears that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may be on the side of autocracy, of propaganda, of tyranny, but that should surprise no one.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to go to another point where there was some resistance to what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was saying about the way he took classified documents into his possession after the presidency, but he got away with an awful lot of lies about it, even within that resistance. And what CNN could never penetrate was the reason there was what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  called a raid on his home.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:01]\par \par \uc2 And, of course, the reason the \uc2 FBI\uc2  went to his home with a search warrant is because they had credible information, probable cause to believe that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had committed obstruction of justice. Because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had submitted through his lawyers a written certification swearing that he had already turned over all classified documents, all relevant documents, to the Justice Department and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , that he`d already complied fully with the subpoena because the \uc2 FBI\uc2  knew that that wasn`t true. That is why they actually went there with a search warrant. And no one in that audience ever heard that information tonight.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: You know, the wonderful thing about America, and George Washington said this in his farewell address to the nation at the end of his presidency, is that the Constitution is sacredly, obligatory upon all. That includes former presidents here in this country. No one is above the law, and ultimately, people who are wrongdoers, like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , will face accountability.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll see that multiple investigations, including the one that you referenced, Lawrence, that are underway, I expect that those prosecutors will follow the facts, apply the law, be guided by the Constitution, and make sure that people are held accountable for their actions. And I expect that this may be a summer of accountability that we are about to enter into for the former, twice impeached president of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, I`ve seen your press conferences, and I know historically that the Capitol Hill press corps can be much more challenging of Speakers of the House or Minority Leaders of the House than ever happens with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And one of the things that I think we can expect is that anyone who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  agrees to be interviewed by will not be capable of challenging \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on every single lie that he tells in any kind of interview.\par \par \uc2 And so this is the kind of thing where we may be seeing more of these over time. Will the Democrats have a strategy of response to these events, or are you going to let this play out within the Republican primaries?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, first of all, you`re exactly right that the Capitol Hill press corps is not a bashful group, and we look forward to that continued back and forth. And engagement is good for democracy, the fourth estate. In terms of holding \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or other extreme MAGA Republicans responsible for their lies, their falsehoods, their half-truths at best in certain circumstances, I expect that the Democratic Party will be prepared to do that on an ongoing basis, legislatively as well as politically.\par \par \uc2 But what perhaps is going to be most important is that President Biden, Democrats in the House and the Senate will continue to articulate our vision for making life better for everyday Americans, for putting people over politics, for focusing on core issues. Lower costs, better paying jobs, safer communities, certainly fighting for reproductive freedom, defending our democracy and articulating a vision to build an economy that really does work for everyday Americans that elevates the American dream in every single zip code.\par \par \uc2 I think the combination of that affirmative vision and also making it clear to the American people that this is an extreme group of people on the Republican side of the aisle, extreme on Social Security, extreme on Medicare, extreme on gun safety, extreme on reproductive freedom, extreme on democracy. If we`re able to articulate that vision and compare and contrast who we are versus who these extreme MAGA Republicans are, I think the American people will positively respond to President Biden and to our vision.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not asked a single question about the spending cuts that House Republicans are trying to force onto a debt ceiling bill. What are those spending cuts that you think that audience should have heard about tonight?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Yeah, this is the challenge with the House Republican Default on America Act is that what they are trying to do is jam extreme, reckless, dangerous cuts down the throats of the American people, 22 percent across the board cut that will include dramatically cutting Medicaid for children with disabilities and older Americans, including those in nursing homes.\par \par \uc2 Dramatically cut education assistance, resulting in the loss of jobs for teachers all across America, in all likelihood, dramatically cut support for public safety. Dramatically cut nutrition and food assistance for more than 30 million food insecure Americans, dramatically cut health care, dramatically cut veterans benefits.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:08]\par \par \uc2 This is what has been proposed by House Republicans.\par \par \uc2 They haven`t submitted a budget, but they did give us a ransom note, essentially saying to the American people, either we`re going to jam these extreme cuts down the throats of the American people, or alternatively force a catastrophic default. That`s not responsible government. And so hopefully, we`ll be able to come together in a reasoned, inspired, enlightened way as we move forward coming out of the meeting on Friday.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: One of the Republicans in the House who voted for that package of cuts, which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  presumably supports, since he supports forcing a default if they don`t get those cuts. One of the people who voted for those cuts is George Santos, who now is the indicted George Santos, Republican member of the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 Kevin McCarthy said, an indictment like that is a perfectly OK thing for a Republican member of the House to have. And there will be no consideration of any changes in George Santos` membership in the House until a jury convicts him.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: You know, at this point, George Santos remains a critical member of the House Republican Conference. And to your point, Lawrence, in terms of that extreme bill that was passed, The Default on America Act by House Republicans, I believe it was on April 26, George Santos was the deciding vote. That`s how central he is to policy making for House Republicans at this particular point in time. It`s unfortunate that this is someone who was unqualified, unfit to serve. Everybody understood this, and yet he was allowed to be seated. Initially given committee assignments and remains welcome with open arms in the House Republican Conference.\par \par \uc2 Hopefully, we`ll start to see some actual accountability undertaken related to George Santos. But I`m not going to hold my breath, because in many ways, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , George Santos, \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 , this group of people are central players for how House Republicans at this particular point in time.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, it`s on nights like this with the Santos indictments, and in fact, long before the Santos indictments, there were enough -- there`s enough information about Santos to take this kind of action. But it reminds me of what \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  did when \uc2 Anthony Weiner\uc2  was revealed to have been putting out pictures of himself that he shouldn`t have out.\par \par \uc2 There no violation of law by \uc2 Anthony Weiner\uc2  at the time. None, absolutely none. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  personally kicked him out of the House of Representatives. No ethics hearing, nothing. Gave him 48 hours to get out. And she took it upon herself to bring him into that room, as you know what it`s like with her and order him out. Kevin McCarthy clearly does not have that kind of strength.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, Speaker Pelosi is as strong and as serious and as intellectually powerful as it gets, with a core sense of right and wrong. And that certainly was the case in that episode and continues to be the case throughout her historic, legendary career. In this particular instance, George Santos has been allowed to run amok and make a mockery of serving in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 I`m confident that the voters of the Third Congressional District in New York will ultimately end this long national nightmare for us in November of 2024, if not sooner. But unfortunately, it may take until that point in time since the votes don`t seem to exist on the Republican side to do anything about George Santos. Notwithstanding the fact that he`s acknowledged the lies and the falsehoods.\par \par \uc2 He perpetrated a clear fraud on the people of the state of New York in order to be elected, regardless of what the outcome may be of this particular indictment, which, by the way, is incredibly serious, including the fact that he appears to have engaged in fraud related to unemployment insurance. And the Republicans this week are about to try to defund the ability of the federal government to root out unemployment fraud. You can`t make this stuff up.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Quickly before you go, you have the weakest Speaker of the House that I`ve ever seen in my lifetime. And the leader of his party, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , tonight is urging him, urging Kevin McCarthy to default on the debt of the United States. What do you think that does to Kevin McCarthy`s position?\par \par \uc2 [22:20:02]\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, you know, it`s certainly my hope that Speaker McCarthy, in the meeting on Friday, will continue to work with the rest of us, including Leader McConnell, who has said, we shall not default to find a responsible pathway forward, avoid a default in a manner that has been done consistently under Democratic presidents and Republican president for decades.\par \par \uc2 And we can have a separate conversation about future spending, future investments, future revenues, what makes sense to protect the health, the safety, and the economic well-being of the American people, but it cannot be done in a hostage taking situation.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: House Democratic Leader Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us on this important night. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, a federal jury found that everything \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said about E. Jean Carroll was a lie and tonight, CNN gave \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a forum to continue lying about E. Jean Carroll. Attorney Faith Gay and Michelle Goldberg will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:25:45]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  complained about the federal jury that unanimously returned a verdict of $5 million dollars against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for sexually assaulting E. Jean Carroll and then lying about her publicly. Because the trial was in federal court the jury pool is selected from a large geographical area that includes some of New York City and the northern suburbs of New York City. Only two of the jurors were from New York City, according to E. Jean Carroll`s lawyer Robbie Kaplan speaking this morning on "MORNING JOE", and one of those jurors from New York City was revealed to be a follower of a right-wing podcast, Donald Trump`s lawyers successfully fought to keep that juror on the jury and that juror joined in that jury`s unanimous verdict.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said this on CNN.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Can I ask you because --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: I swear on my children which I`d never do, I have no idea who this woman. This is a fake story.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Joining us now are attorney Faith Gay, a former federal prosecutor for the Eastern District of New York. Also with us, Michelle Goldberg, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " columnist and an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Michelle, let me begin with you in the aftermath of that Trump hour on CNN and I -- I want to hear from you on what you heard him say about E. Jean Carroll and what you heard him get away with saying about abortion and anything else that you`d like to comment on.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I guess, I mean, the first thing I`d say is I think E. Jean Carroll should sue him again. I mean, I understand why she`s going to want to put herself through that process again, but he was just found liable not just for sexual abuse but also for defamation. He went on TV and defamed her again.\par \par \uc2 It was out -- and CNN abetted it, right? It was obvious that that`s exactly what he was going to do and CNN is now a party to her continuing defamation. I`m not sure if that`s true in a legal sense, but certainly true in a moral and journalistic sense.\par \par \uc2 Look, this whole -- I think that on the one hand, this was a travesty. On the other hand, it`s a -- not welcome but useful reminder of exactly who this man is, the depth of his misogyny. I mean, certainly, I think people you know the fact that he won`t commit to not signing a federal abortion ban, that he celebrated the end of Roe versus Wade is going to go into a lot of Democratic ads, if he`s the nominee, and the fact that he doubled down on the "Access Hollywood" tape, right?\par \par \uc2 He moved away from it was locker room talk and now, he`s just saying it`s true, you know? And so, I also want to say that, you know, what we see with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a small sense, you know, that he`s found guilty, he`s found responsible for defamation one day, the next day, he`s back to doing it again is just a microcosm of his general promise of renewed lawlessness if he becomes president again.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Faith Gay, one of the failures of CNN tonight in that interview with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not pointing out that the "Access Hollywood" video is an actual confession by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of his own practices.\par \par \uc2 He wasn`t saying other famous people do it. He wasn`t talking about other famous people. He was saying this is what I get to do, have done, will continue to do as a famous person.\par \par \uc2 FAITH GAY, FORMER PROSECUTOR, EASTERN DISTRICT OF NEW YORK: So, Lawrence, not only was that a failure tonight, but there was also a failure to point out that in this courtroom, there was every opportunity over and over and over again for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to get his story out. Not only did he have an opportunity to testify and put on evidence which he declined day after day, the judge whom he now says is unfair actually reopened the case, gave \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  another couple of days to decide whether to testify and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still declined.\par \par \uc2 So, now when it`s absolutely safe, when there`s no cross-examiner standing there ready to cross-examine \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  under oath, he`s free to say all these things. But I hope the American people who still care about equality and fairness and due process will see that it was an absolute act of cowardice what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did when he had a chance to look the jury in the eye, to put his hand on the Bible, to testify in an ethical and responsible way, he declined.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:20]\par \par \uc2 But what did he do once the case was over? He whined about an unfair trial, and now he says, in the absolute protection, where no one is pushing back against him, including CNN. He says what he has to say. It`s absolutely despicable.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: And Michelle, CNN did ask him, why didn`t you testify at the trial? And he said, well, you know, my lawyers told me it would be a waste of time. It`s ridiculous. But CNN did not say, why did you lie last week when you were standing on a golf course in Ireland and telling those reporters you were going to rush back to New York, leave early, fly back to New York to go confront that woman? That`s what he said. That`s what he said. And his plane never went to New York, and he never went near that courthouse, and he was never asked tonight why he lied about issuing that threat. Which, by the way, is what provoked the judge to extend Donald Trump`s decision making all the way to Sunday night at 05:00 p.m. about whether he wanted to come into that courthouse.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, THE NEW YORK TIMES COLUMNIST: I mean, look, I think it`s a fallacy. One of the ways that CNN and defenders of CNN justified this whole exercise, besides the newsworthiness of it, was that they could fact check \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in real time. And it`s just a mug game when everything someone says is a lie, when they don`t answer questions, when they play to this kind of racist crowd, and when they lie so unceasingly and steamroll over, you know, Kaitlan Collins` firm but polite questions. There`s just no way that you can kind of control him in that situation.\par \par \uc2 I think if they had, you know, a kind of closed studio, if they had had even an audience of undecided voters. But he was sucking up on -- he was sucking the kind of vicious, you know, gladiatorial energy of the crowd and was able to turn it into more of a campaign rally. And her efforts to kind of get questions in edgewise were just almost overwhelmingly futile.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And Faith Gay, we have an indication based on some information that became public about one of the jurors because of arguments that the lawyers had about one of those jurors, that that juror was listener. His principal source of news was a right-wing podcast. And so that -- there was at least one juror that we know of now who was probably in many ways inclined to be supportive of the kind of thinking that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has. But that juror, too, joined in that unanimous verdict.\par \par \uc2 GAY: Indeed, that`s right. Unsealed after the jury verdict was the motion made by E. Jean Carroll`s lawyers to remove that jury from -- juror for potential bias because of the right-wing shows that were followed by the juror. But that juror stayed on the jury. Obviously was influential in the deliberations, as all jurors are, probably helped reach compromises as to the claims that were sustained as to the amount of damages, but fully participated in this unanimous verdict, finding that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not only sexually assaulted Ms. Carroll but defamed her.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, that juror was there all the way through despite exposure to right-wing TV. Now, on top of that, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  today complained on CNN or this evening on CNN that all kinds of evidence came in or was excluded from Ms. Carroll, the nicknames for her husband. The nicknames for her dog. Other prejudicial evidence. But if that kind of name calling was let in on Trump`s side, the number of names he has called people, the number of prejudicial statements he has made, the jurors would still be in the box now.\par \par \uc2 I mean, our courts are committed to keeping out irrelevant evidence. There is no testimony in this case about the fact that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is under indictment. There is no testimony in this case that he still under investigation, that he`s known to have cheated on his taxes. All those things didn`t come in because they were irrelevant and unfair in this trial, which was not about those things. The same thing for these little snippets of name calling that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  now claims should have been admitted against Ms. Carroll.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And Michelle, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  complained tonight on CNN that the Access Hollywood video was allowed to be an exhibit in the case, and never was it pointed out on CNN that it was allowed to be an exhibit because it was a confession in Donald Trump`s own voice to precisely the kind of crime he committed against E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 [22:35:10]\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. And that`s imminent. I think that was very clear in this trial, not just E. Jean Carroll, but the two other women who testified that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had assaulted them, Jessica Leeds and Natasha Stoynoff. You know, I mean, that`s what -- that`s why I think the Access Hollywood tape was so powerful. A lot of us have a vague memory of it, but the extent to which he outlined an MO that way then had three women testifying to him using on them in real life was so clear and so overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 And yes, I mean, he`s been -- I`m still a little bit gobsmacked, I have to say, by the fact that rather that he`s now kind of doubling down on the Access Hollywood tape instead of taking his former task, which was treated as, you know, kind of boyish hyperbole.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Michelle Goldberg and Attorney Faith Gay, thank you both very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAY: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you. Coming up, we`ll have more on Donald Trump`s urging Republicans to default on paying the debt of the United States. That is the most important thing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said about the current governance of the United States tonight.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll also have what President Biden said about the debt ceiling yesterday and the advice that he is considering from Harvard Law Professor Laurence Tribe, who delivered that advice on this program on Monday. And who will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:41:12]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAITLAN COLLINS, CNN WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: You think the U.S. should default if the White House does not agree to the spending cuts, Republicans are demanding?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Well, you might as well do it now because you`ll do it later.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Monday night on this program, Harvard Institutional Law Professor Laurence Tribe said this about the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAURENCE TRIBE, HARVARD LAW SCHOOL CONSTITUTIONAL LAW PROFESSOR: But the real question isn`t what powers does the president have? It is what duties does the president have? Does the president have a duty to execute of the United States? The ones that Congress passed telling him to spend money? He does have that duty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And the day after Professor Tribe said that after an Oval Office meeting with congressional leaders on the debt ceiling, which was yesterday, President said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: I have been considering the 14th Amendment. And a man I have enormous respect for, Larry Tribe, who advised me for a long time, thinks that it would be legitimate. But the problem is it would have to be litigated. And in the meantime, without an extension, it would still end up in the same place.\par \par \uc2 I`ll be very blunt with you: When we get by this, I`m thinking about taking a look at -- months down the road -- to see whether -- what the court would say about whether or not the -- it does work. There have been discussions about whether or not the 14th Amendment is -- can be invoked.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Is that the most likely unilateral executive path --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, yeah, but I --\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: -- worst-case scenario?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I -- I don`t -- I don`t think that solves our problem now. I think that only solves your problem if -- once the court has ruled that it does apply for future endeavors.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Professor Laurence Tribe, who has taught constitutional law at Harvard Law School for five decades. As the New York times noted in his latest opinion essay about the debt ceiling, he has advised three democratic presidents on constitutional issues involving the separation of powers and the 14th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Professor Tribe, I was hanging on every word that the President said about the 14th Amendment, and you, what is your response to what you just heard the President say?\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: Well, I`m glad the President is considering the constitutional reason that I think he has really a duty to spend the money that Congress has directed him to spend, even if that means going beyond the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 I think the President is worried about litigation, but he doesn`t need to be, because this is not a situation in which either Kevin McCarthy or the House of representatives or anybody else is in a position to file a lawsuit against the president or against the federal government or doing what Congress has told them to do.\par \par \uc2 What kind of lawsuit would it be? Just imagine, suppose you could find someone who is injured when the President spends the money that Congress has told him to spend. What would that person ask the court to do? You have to ask the court to say to the president of the United States, violate the spending laws, Mr. President. Violate the spending laws, and by the way, we can`t tell you which programs to cut. I guess you`ve got to stop spending altogether, but that would put the country in default anyway, because we would end up with IOUs to all the people who were being stiffed by the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 So it wouldn`t achieve the purpose of any such lawsuit. It would be asking a court to order the president to violate Section Four of the 14th Amendment, which says that we must pay our debts. So I think the President really needn`t worry about any lawsuit that would stop him from obeying the spending laws.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:07]\par \par \uc2 And I`m not suggesting, as some people have, that he go to court. He doesn`t need the court`s permission to follow these laws. And this is not at all like the lawsuit that was brought on Monday by 75,000 federal workers who said that they want the debt ceiling to be held unconstitutional because it threatens their jobs. That`s a different kind of lawsuit. It`s a lawsuit by someone who says, I think that unless you follow your constitutional obligations and if you allow the Kevin McCarthy`s of the world to hold you hostage, we`re going to lose our jobs.\par \par \uc2 So in that lawsuit, they want the court to give them some understandable relief. But the President doesn`t have to go to court, and there`s nobody who has a gripe against him who could take him to court, except maybe Kevin McCarthy, who would say, you know, this takes away from me the ability I have to hold you hostage. He doesn`t have standing to go to court for that purpose. It`s not at all like the case where, in 2015, the House of Representatives was allowed by the District Court, in the case of \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2  against Burwell to take the Secretary of Health and Human Services to court for spending money that was never appropriated by Congress.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s different. When you cut the House of Representatives out of the process and spend money they haven`t appropriated, they have the ability to sue. But in this case, the money has been appropriated. That`s the whole point. The debt limit doesn`t limit the debt. It just gets in the way of the President carrying out the law.\par \par \uc2 So, Mr. President, if you`re listening or if your White House counsel is listening, don`t worry about being sued in this case. You`ve got an airtight reason, and it`s written right there in the Constitution of the United States, which I know you believe in and which you have studied. I don`t think there`s any reason for you to be held hostage, and there`s every reason for you do your duty under the 14th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, when I kind of fast forward to the notion of some kind of lawsuit, some way in which this issue presents itself to a federal court and let`s assume ultimately the Supreme Court, it is inconceivable to me that the Supreme Court could find a way to look at the budget of the United States and do what? Order a stay of spending by the administration? What would they stay? Would they stay all payments by the federal government? Or would the Supreme Court sit there and decide which pieces of these obligations should we, the Supreme Court, decide the government will pay while we, the Supreme Court, deliberate over the larger question? It just seems procedurally impossible.\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: Impossible. They would be wearing a green eye shade over their black robes and they would be sort of performing the role of the Office of Management and Budget.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: Line-item veto by the judicial branch. It`s insane. It`s just not going to happen. It can`t happen.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Professor Laurence Tribe, thank you very much for joining us once again in your ongoing now public dialogue with the President of the United States about the debt ceiling. I am very glad he`s listening.\par \par \uc2 TRIBE: Well, I hope he is. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you very much, once again.\par \par \uc2 And coming up today, George Anthony Devolder Santos, pleaded not guilty to 13 federal charges, including counts of wire fraud, money laundering, theft of public funds, and lying to the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2 . That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:54:20]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: George Santos` absurdly obvious pathological lying caught up with him today when he was arrested and arraigned on a federal indictment in the Eastern District of New York, which includes Long Island, where George Santos committed most of these crimes.\par \par \uc2 The indictment charges George Santos with 13 felonies, including seven counts of wire fraud, three counts of money laundering, two counts of making materially false statements to the House Representatives, and one count of theft of public funds.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Dan Horwitz. He is a former Manhattan Assistant District Attorney where he prosecuted white collar crimes like tax fraud, securities fraud, and fraud against the government.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:02]\par \par \uc2 And let me to make an amendment to what I just said that Long Island is where these crimes were allegedly committed. I forgot the word allegedly in George Santos` case. I won`t -- I won`t again.\par \par \uc2 Dan Horwitz, the defrauding of the government is -- I mean, so much is fascinating about this, but he defrauded the government, according to this indictment, by an illegitimately, falsely claiming unemployment benefits to the tune of $24,000 while he was being paid a salary of $120,000. And this same person is a sponsor of the Republican bill that they are calling the Victims of Unemployment Fraud Act to really crack down on people like George Santos.\par \par \uc2 DAN HORWITZ, FORMER ASSISTANT MANHATTAN DISTRICT ATTORNEY: This guy, Lawrence, is a defense lawyer`s nightmare. Every time he opens his mouth, it`s like, please convict me. I guess maybe he`s thinking, you know, the best defense is a good offense. I couldn`t begin to rationalize this guy`s conduct.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the 13 counts, of course, there`s all of the lies and misstatements he`s made outside the context of the indictment, and prosecutors will certainly seize on all of those to introduce them at a trial, if this ever gets there, of course, as what`s called bad act evidence to show that there is a modus operandi for this guy, which is that he`s a pathological liar.\par \par \uc2 And so, as I said a minute ago, he truly, as a defense lawyer`s nightmare, I -- you know, I couldn`t imagine how to represent somebody like this. But you`re absolutely right. It`s absurd.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, I`ve been thinking today about just how hard it is, how hard it is to commit the crime of making materially false statements to the House of Representatives. I mean, the thousands of lying members of the House of Representatives who have filed financial disclosure forms and never been caught up in a crime as a result of filing those forms. And yet here comes George Santos, and as soon as you give him a form to file in the House Representatives, he`s charged with a crime?\par \par \uc2 HORWITZ: Well, wait for it. Will there be a Defense of Speech and Debate clause. Again, if I was his defense lawyer, I don`t know what I would do. But I will say that there`s no way in the world that the speech and debate clause covers the chutzpah of committing all of these crimes.\par \par \uc2 And then while you`re running for Congress, covering it up by lying about it. I just am astounded, it`s really, as a defense lawyer, I`d be thinking this is just rearranging deck chairs on the Titanic.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah. It seems -- it feels like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  let loose a contagion that pathological liars were going to get caught by. And so there`s, you know, George Santos sitting out there in Long Island. He`s watching \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lie his way through a political life. And he thinks, I can do that. Not thinking about just how much -- how many legal fees` \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  willing to pay for how much litigation, which is basically unlimited in his case. And here is George Santos stepping right in it as soon as he can.\par \par \uc2 HORWITZ: Yeah, the knock up. Are we going to have knock off \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  white collar political criminals? I don`t know. But I think you`re absolutely right. I mean, that`s got to be what he`s thinking. I am just going to continue to spew it and if I say it, it will be so, which is, as you know, Lawrence, the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  mantra.\par \par \uc2 And for him, obviously, that seems to be catching up with him. And I know we`re talking about George Santos, but you`ve got the verdict in the E. Jean Carroll case, you`ve got the DA`s case in Manhattan going forward and you`ve got Georgia and the Department of Justice looming on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Dan Horwitz, thank you for joining us once again. Always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HORWITZ: Thanks, Lawrence, too.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Dan Horwitz gets tonight`s Last Word. The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. Okie-doke, folks. It is not news what happened tonight at 08:00 p.m. on another network and there is no sense in fact checking or replaying the highlights.\par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230511ej5a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News;\par \b \uc2 MSNBC interview with Asa Hutchinson (R), Presidential Candidate\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 1,725 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC ANCHOR: Joining me now, Republican presidential candidate and former Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson.\par \par \uc2 Governor, thank you for being here. When you heard that verdict today, what was your reaction?\par \par \uc2 ASA HUTCHINSON (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Well, I trained as a lawyer. I practiced law for 25 years and anytime a jury comes back with a verdict, I respect the verdict to the jury.\par \par \uc2 Jury verdicts reflect the community, they reflect America, and they also have weighed, as no one else has, the credibility of the witnesses and the truth of the allegations. And they found unanimously that the allegations of the sexual assault were true.\par \par \uc2 And so, I believe we all, as a society, and as Republicans, ought to take that jury verdict very seriously. To do otherwise undermines our system of justice, which is second to none in the world. And also, it`s just simply a reflection of continued indefensible conduct by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That`s what it means to me.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: There are other Republicans that would potentially like the Republican nomination, potentially Nikki Haley, potentially Tim Scott. Why don`t you think any of them are taking the similar position that you are? A former president found liable of sexual abuse.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, they`ll have to address that.\par \par \uc2 But to me, it`s important to speak the truth. It`s important to lead. It`s important to guide. And it`s important to respect, again, this system of justice that we have. And we`ve seen too much from leaders that undermine both our criminal justice system, but also our civil justice system.\par \par \uc2 And here`s an example of where the jury system worked. You had defense lawyers there. You had the cross-examination of witnesses. And the jury determines truth. We ought to have some respect for that.\par \par \uc2 And so, to me, it`s serious, again. And, you know, the public is going to decide this issue. And it`s all going to be decided at the ballot box. But I`m running for president of the United States because I believe that I`m the leader that this country needs and that the Republican Party needs to have a different nominee than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It`s as simple as that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Respect, decency, a party that says they`re the party of family values. One would think they would find this reprehensible.\par \par \uc2 I want to share what Republican Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  said when he was (ph) asked how this would impact Trump`s campaign.\par \par \uc2 Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JOHN CORYNN (R-TX): I`m not sure it really has any effect on his candidacy per se.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What about Republicans` chances of taking the Senate if he was the nominee? Would that hurt?\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: Well, I mean, I think we have such a great, great map. I think there`s a good opportunity to pick up at least three sets, no matter if he runs or not. But the fact is, I do not think he could win the presidency. And so, I think, regardless of what you think about him as an individual, to me, electability is the - is the sole criterion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You don`t think that Trump could be elected president again?\par \par \uc2 CORNYN: I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So, he`s saying he could not win in a general election. But like so many others, he said this won`t bother the base. Who is the Republican base today, that they would be A-OK with this?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, I think it will bother many in the base. And - but it also depends on what people say about it and how they approach it. And for example -\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What does that mean, what they say about it -\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well -\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: - and how they approach it?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to say this is old, these are false. It`s a - it`s a crooked system or it`s a fixed system. Well, some will buy into that. But I think whenever you have leaders like myself that speak out, Senator Cornyn, it does make a difference. And whenever we uphold the rule of law, when we talk about how important it is in our society, it makes sense to people.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is a time for leadership. It`s a time for setting the differences and the right tone in America. And that`s what I hope that I`m doing in setting out a contrast that`s there. I believe it makes a difference for the Republican base. And I think that they`re going to evaluate both the electability side of it but also the rightness side of it.\par \par \uc2 And is - do the Americans believe that the allegations are true? Probably most of them do, just like the jury did. Some will excuse it. I don`t believe it`s excusable and defensible.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s talk about that contrast. Because on Friday I sat down with President Biden, and I asked him -- right now people are assuming it could be a matchup, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  versus \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- and I asked him, what is the difference between the two of them.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What`s the difference between the two of you?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Everything. Everything. Everything.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He clearly says everything. You`re making a case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . What is the case for you? Who`s your voter? Who`s your base?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, it`s - it`s Republicans. I`ve been a consistent conservative. Whenever you look at my record in Arkansas of lowering taxes, growing the economy, improving education, these are Republican principles. I left a $2 billion surplus. This is what Republicans do, we lead, and we solve problems.\par \par \uc2 And so, I`m second to none in terms of a consistent conservative record. But also a vision for the future and one that is filled with optimism and the strength of America in the global stage. That to me is a Republican Party, it`s my record, and it`s the future of America. And we need that kind of leadership.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned President Biden, and I`m running against him for president. And I believe that we need a change in policy, the direction that President Biden has taken us. And so, let`s, hopefully, get away from all of the distractions in this campaign and talk about border security, energy policy, talk about the debt and the - the spending in Washington, D.C., a balanced budget.\par \par \uc2 These are the things that we need to be talking about. I hope that we can. We need to eliminate some of the distractions.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Right now, two main things that seem to be a focus for Republicans is this near or total abortion ban and permitless carry-around guns. They seem to be deeply unpopular with a national voter in a general audience.\par \par \uc2 Where do you stand on those two issues?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, I`m pro-life. And whenever you look at restrictions on abortion, I support those if there`s reasonable exceptions. And I believe that`s where a vast majority of Americans are. That they don`t want abortion up to time of birth. They want restrictions on it, but they also want reasonable exceptions.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it`s at the state level. I expect it actually to stay there. So, each state will make their own determination because I don`t believe we will have the depth of Democrat majorities in the House and the Senate and the presidency. I think it will probably be divided.\par \par \uc2 And so, it leaves it with the states. And so, each state will make the determination. I`m pro-life. And I think we`ve got to message it very carefully so that we do support those moms with risky pregnancies and abortion service - I mean, excuse me, adoption services.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: This sort of sounds like that Republican Party that people were accustomed to for years and years, even during the Trump presidency we would hear within Republican circles people were longing for that George Bush-style president.\par \par \uc2 When you announced though, there were no Republican lawmakers from your state, whether it`s the current governor, your two senators, there aren`t Republican lawmakers who have endorsed you at this point. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, it`s early. And I have a great deal of -\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has gotten endorsements already.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, yes, he has. And some of them are changing as well. And so, it`s very fluid. You make your case. You make your arguments.\par \par \uc2 And - and in Arkansas is a good example where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , in 2020, carried it 60 plus percent. It`s one of the most red states in America. And so, I`m going against the grain. I believe it`s important.\par \par \uc2 And I think that I`ll ultimately win. But we`ve got to make our case. We`ve got a long ways to go.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: If you don`t, will you endorse him as the Republican nominee?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, I expect to be on the debate stage. I think there`s other ways to do that. I think it`s a little bit early. I don`t like party loyalty oaths. I don`t like them for Democrats, don`t like them for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s see how that develops. I expect someone other than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be our nominee. And I certainly want to support the Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Governor \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Thank you, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020230510ej5a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May 4, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Ryan Reilly, Barbara McQuade \par \uc2 7,730 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: And that is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris H. starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (on camera): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 DOMINIC PEZZOLA, MEMBER, PROUD BOYS: So, we`re going to storm the (BLEEP) Capitol. January 6 will be a day in infamy.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The proud boys will stand back and stand by for sentencing.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: We have secured the convictions of leaders of both the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers for seditious conspiracy. Our work will continue.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, the guilty verdict for Donald Trump`s foot soldiers and Congressman Jamie Raskin on the connective tissue from the leaders of the mob to the man who sent them.\par \par \uc2 Then, another Supreme Court ethics blockbuster. The same mega donor treating \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  to lavish vacations and paying his mother`s rent is caught paying the private school tuition the child Thomas raised as a son.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the prosecution rests in the Trump civil rape trial as he accused is offered a second chance to testify. And the mysterious case of a formerly pro-abortion North Carolina Democrat now suddenly voting with Republicans to restrict abortion access. When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Man, we have a packed one for you tonight. There are now 14 supporters of Donald Trump`s attempted coup who are guilty of one of the rarest and most serious crimes in the entire U.S. criminal code, Seditious Conspiracy. After a lengthy trial today, a jury found \uc2 Enrique Tarrio\uc2 , the leader of the right- wing extremist group known as the Proud Boys, and three of his peers guilty of plotting a criminal conspiracy to overthrow the government by force in furtherance of Donald Trump`s attempted coup.\par \par \uc2 A fifth defended a man named Dominic Pezzola, who you may recognize from some of that January 6 footage was acquitted of the conspiracy charge, although he was convicted of multiple other federal offenses. This is now the third seditious conspiracy trial to secure the convictions of right- wing gang members related to the January 6th insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Nine members of the fringe extremist group known as the Oath Keepers were either convicted or pled guilty to seditious conspiracy in two previous cases. It is difficult to overstate how rare these kinds of convictions are. It`s really unusual for prosecutors to even bring charges of seditious conspiracy. It is even more unusual for them to successfully prosecute the cases.\par \par \uc2 The government has not successfully prosecuted a case like this before January 6th, since the early 1990s. And the last time the government even tried prior to January 6th was back in the early 2010s when they tried a group of anti-government militia members who called themselves the Hutaree.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , along with local police and other federal agents, swooped down in force over the weekend arresting eight people, most of them in southern Michigan, and a few in Ohio and Indiana. All were members of a militia group that called itself the Hutaree, saying on their website that they believed in preparing for an antichrist and in getting ready to defend themselves. Among the forces of evil the group believed was anyone in the government beginning with local police.\par \par \uc2 Court documents say the group talked about ways to lure police for an attack. In one plan, they`d kill a policeman and then bomb the officer`s funeral procession.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, here`s the thing about that case. It failed. The seditious conspiracy charges ended up getting dropped against the Hutaree in large part because a case of seditious conspiracy is extraordinarily difficult to prove, even against people whose sentiments or beliefs are clearly anti- government extremists.\par \par \uc2 But the Department of Justice has met that very, very high bar with 14 of these pro-Trump militia members. Federal prosecutors able to prove beyond a reasonable doubt that more than a dozen people conspired to violently overthrow the government under what they believe to be, according to testimony in multiple trials, orders from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And let`s be very clear about what these trials were. I know I bang on this a lot, but it`s true, OK. These were not sham show trials. This latest one that resulted in today`s verdict lasted nearly 15 weeks. It involved lengthy witness testimony. And to be clear, this is far, far, far more due process than the vast and overwhelming majority of incarcerated people in this country get, frankly.\par \par \uc2 So, despite what \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and the fired \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  say, January 6 was not an innocent tourist visit. Of course, obviously it wasn`t. You can see it right there in front of your eyes. It was not a first amendment protected peaceful protest. There were multiple violent militia groups acting as the tip of Donald Trump`s spear after he told them on national television to be prepared for further instruction.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:22]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, JOURNALIST: Are you willing tonight to condemn white supremacist and militia groups and to say that need to stand down and not add the vuilence.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Give me a name. Give me a name.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: White supremacist --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Go ahead. Who do I need to condemn?\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Proud Boys, stand back and stand by.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The Proud Boys listened. They stood by. They acted. And today, four of them were convicted of seditious conspiracy as a result. At a press conference this afternoon, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said in no uncertain terms, the Department of Justice is not done seeking justice for Trump`s attempted coup.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: We have secured the convictions of leaders of both the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers for seditious conspiracy, specifically conspiring to oppose by force the lawful transfer of presidential power. Our work will continue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And you may be asking yourself, as I am after watching that clip, the obvious question. What about the man at whose behest this conspiracy was launched, the man who would stand most to benefit? We have seen the government in what is the largest criminal case they`ve ever undertaken in toto prosecute the hundreds of foot soldiers of January 6, the people that entered the Capitol. They`ve now moved or rung up the ladder to the organized self-proclaimed militia groups and prosecuted them successfully for seditious conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 What about the man who incited insurrection in the first place? The man these militias believe they were acting on behalf of when they tried to overturn the results of a free and fair election by force? Because make no mistake, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is running for president a third time in order to finish the job he started on January 6 when he almost ended free and fair elections in this country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And another thing we`ll do, and so many people have been asking me about it, if I run and if I win, we will treat those people from January 6 fairly. We will treat them fairly. And if it requires pardons, we will give them pardons because they are being treated so unfairly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is not a side issue. This is not an ancillary. This is not a gimmick. Embracing the January 6 insurrectionists is a central component of Trump`s campaign. He released a song featuring a choir of January 6 defendants who are currently incarcerated. His official position is that the January 6 rioters who broke into the Capitol, many of whom assaulted police officers, are political prisoners who should be pardoned.\par \par \uc2 Just watch this remarkable encounter the experts in have last week while campaigning in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICKI LARSON-OLSON, JANUARY 6 RIOTER: President Trump, can you sign my Trump backpack that I carried on January 6th? I went to jail for 161 days for January 6th.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : And that`s terrible. What they`re saying is so sad what they`ve done to January 6. It`s so bad. Partiots. And I hear the woman, a terrific woman. And you just take care of yourself, OK.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There he is literally embracing a convicted January 6er after learning that she`d stormed the Capitol. That`s the leading candidate for the Republican nomination for president. And throughout the primary, and if he gets a nomination, which right now looks likely, though, who knows, he is going to single-mindedly advocate for the pursue and pursue the successful completion of the same overall project the man in court today were convicted or furthering. Which is quite literally the destruction of American democracy. Force over votes. The end of the experiment. So far, 14 seditious conspirators have faced some measure of justice for that crime, but not the guy at the top.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Ryan Reilly, justice reporter for NBC News, who`s been covering the Proud Boys trial from the start. And \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2  who served as the U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District in Michigan, where she was the one who prosecuted that seditious conspiracy case against members of the Hutaree militia.\par \par \uc2 Ryan, let me start with you as someone who covered this trial. And it was a complicated trial. It was a long trial, much longer than 90 percent of trials that happened in America. I`m pulling it out of my head, but I think that`s right. Were you surprised by this conclusion?\par \par \uc2 RYAN REILLY, NBC NEWS JUSTICE REPORTER: I don`t think I was terribly surprised. I was kind of surprised about who the jury actually struggled over. I mean, honestly, in my mind, it was the \uc2 Enrique Tarrio\uc2  one which was going to be the toughest struggle for them to reach on the seditious conspiracy charge. But now that it comes out, you can very much follow the logic of what the jurors did.\par \par \uc2 And that`s always nice to see when you can say, OK, I see what they did with the evidence here, right? And in this case, I think what they saw is that the individual Dominic Pezzola who smashed that window open with that stolen police shield, he wasn`t in communication with so many of the other Proud Boys as much as the other members of this conspiracy were. And in fact, he only joined the Proud Boys pretty late in the game, only a few weeks before January 6 itself.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s definitely a logic to it here, but you know, definitely the takeaway for me in the Dominic -- in rather Enrique Tarrio`s case is that you now have someone who was at a Baltimore hotel who was off scene, who wasn`t at the United States Capitol who`s been convicted of seditious conspiracy in connection with the attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 And you know, that Baltimore hotel is a lot further away from the Capitol than 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue. So, I think that that`s definitely something that we`re going to see some broader implications from, because now it`s been proven that you can in fact get a guilty verdict from a D.C. jury on a charge involving somebody who was not actually physically at the United States Capitol on January 6 itself.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is a really excellent point, Ryan, that you`re making, right? Tarrio had been busted for weapons charge. He was found with a gun. He had already been in D.C. because he had been charged with defacing a church actually in a previous visit to D.C. in December, right? So, he definitively is not at the Capitol. He`s in a Baltimore hotel room, and yet a D.C. jury convicts him of this very serious crime despite his lack of physical presence.\par \par \uc2 Barbara, as someone who has experience in this, knows how hard it is, what are your conclusions about the success the Department of Justice has had now in three different trials of obtaining this many convictions on this very difficult charge?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: It`s very gratifying to see that they were able to get these convictions, because as you say, Chris, this is a difficult charge to prove, and there`s not a lot of case law out there that can help prosecutors hang their hat on various aspects of it. And so, you know, as you mentioned, the Hutaree case is one that my former office brought in 2010 and was unsuccessful.\par \par \uc2 And I think one of the challenges that made it such an uphill battle is that when you argue that this group tried to oppose by force the authority of the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , I think the natural reaction for people at least then was, oh, come on, who would try that because you could never succeed? That`s absurd. And in fact, the defense is often, these are just a bunch of goofy guys, you know, it was all talk. And so, you imagine, you know, the same kind of defense here.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the things that makes this case more compelling, however, is that it was at the United States Capitol, and they were fighting hand-to- hand combat. I think one of the things is also they actually got to a point closer than other cases that they were actually physically at the Capitol. But as Ryan said, the conviction of \uc2 Enrique Tarrio\uc2 , I think, is a really interesting point and makes this case, unlike the Oath Keepers, a much more significant conviction because it extends it away from the Capitol to planners.\par \par \uc2 And as he said, and I believe, it really ought to give \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and Jack Smith the encouragement to think about people at the Willard Hotel War Room or inside the Oval Office who might likewise have been planning things, even though they weren`t there at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, let me follow up with you on that since you just said that. Ryan Goodman, a law professor who writes about this stuff at Just Security quite a bit said, the verdict empowers a special counsel to bring indictments for the efforts to overturn the election. \uc2 New York University\uc2  law professor Ryan Goodman said, it underscores enormous stakes in mobilizing Americans to believe the big lie and directing an arm crowd to interfere with congressional proceedings.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like you agree that this does actually matter for however Department of Justice prosecutors in Jack Smith`s office might be thinking about conceptualizing whatever might happen to the man that called for this to happen.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: I do, but of course you do need evidence. So, some of the key evidence they had against \uc2 Enrique Tarrio\uc2  were text messages, where he was talking with these other plotters, and so you could directly tie him to this activity. You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is somebody who, you know, like a mob boss does not use text messaging, does not use email, and so it`s going to be difficult to find that digital paper trail.\par \par \uc2 But if there is a connection where they were directing or planning or agreeing to this attack, then it would be easy enough to consider -- you know, think about a similar charge.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Ryan, final question for you. As someone to cover this trial, I was struck today that this was a long and complicated trial, and the jury came back with fairly complicated and specific findings, right? There were some -- there were deadlocked on, there were some not guilty, there were some guilty. What does that say to you about just the sort of thoroughness with which those jurors did their job?\par \par \uc2 REILLY: I mean, you know, the jury -- the jury system is not perfect, but I think it`s definitely the best system we have, because I think in all of these cases, you`ve been able to follow that logic. And you know, in a lot of these cases, despite all of the stuff that`s thrown out there about DC jurors, especially from a lot of these defense attorneys, you know, DC is a democratic town, right? It`s mostly Democrats who live here, and that`s where the jurors are coming from. But they`ve been able to look at this evidence, you know, very carefully and follow it where it goes and not, you know, go above and beyond what where the evidence actually leads them. So, I think, you know, it`s a good support system for our criminal justice system here.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:24]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Ryan Reilly and \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2 , thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, the man who laid out the case against this gang, the Proud Boys, and against the president who called them to the Capitol, former January 6th Committee member Jamie Raskin joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The conviction of four leaders of the Proud Boys on seditious conspiracy comes nearly a year after the January 6 Committee laid out their role in the attack on the Capitol. It was the very first public hearing, and in it the committee showed a video of violence from that day. And in the very first frame of footage filmed at 10:00 a.m. you see the Proud Boys marching to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m proud of your boys. Proud of your boys.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Proud of your boys.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Be advised, there`s probably about 300 Proud Boys marching eastbound in this 400 block of kind of Independence -- actually on the Mall towards the United States Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The Proud Boys also feature prominently in a later hearing where Congressman Jamie Raskin laid out how Donald Trump`s tweet in December 2020, telling his followers explicitly to come to D.C. on the 6th, be there, will be wild, spurred the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers to conspire together.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Trump`s December 19 tweet motivated these two extremist groups which have historically not worked together to coordinate their activities. December 19th, at 10:22 a.m., just hours after President Trump`s tweet, Kelly Meggs, the head of the Florida Oath Keepers, declared an alliance among the Oath Keepers, the Proud Boys, and the Florida Three Percenters, another militia group. He wrote, we have decided to work together and shut this (BLEEP) down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And Congressman Jamie Raskin joins me now. Congressman, it`s great to have you on. First, you`re just reaction to now the third successful Department of Justice prosecution for seditious conspiracy of these groups, including the one that you spent so much time on the committee.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, it certainly refutes all of the claims that there was no sedition, there was no real attempt to overthrow the government, there was no insurrection. So, it puts to rest all of those false claims. I think you`ll remember, Chris, I think I appeared on your show right near the beginning of the January 6 hearings. And I described three rings of insurrectionary activity that day. And one was the outer ring of just lots of Trump protesters who were brought in, and many of them, not all of them were drawn into the violence.\par \par \uc2 The middle ring was made up of these organized extremist groups, specifically the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers, who were, as they describe it, the tip of the spear who led the broader mob into violence. And then, of course, the innermost group was the group of the coup that is Trump and his henchmen, those who surrounded him, who were attempting to overthrow the peaceful transfer of power, nullify the results of the 2020 election, and get him installed as president.\par \par \uc2 And these convictions support what the January 6 Committee was saying, support what the House of Representatives voted with the 232-vote margin in peaching \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for inciting an insurrection, and support what 57 of 100 senators voted to convict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on, which was incitement of insurrection against the Union. There was a real insurrection, and this violence was right at the heart of it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That point about the inner rings, I mean, this is more than just conceptual in the sense that as you laid out in some of those hearings, it`s only one note of connection to get from these members to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , because you had \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , right, who was a key Trump associate. We don`t know the degree to which he was in contact with the president during this time. That`s sort of an unanswered question in the end. But, you know, Stone had this -- you know, had constellation of far-right characters. There`s \uc2 Enrique Tarrio\uc2  with Stone. They came together in late 2020 as part of the Stop the Steal cause. Among them was \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One way in which Stone maintained these contacts was through a signal check group named FOS, Friends of Stone. And you have Tarrio and Oath Keepers who were part of that group. So, it`s not like a hypothetical or conceptual thing. Like, they are quite directly connected to a person who is connected to the president.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: There were definite intermediaries and \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  was clearly one of them. And there were other people who were operating either in back channels or openly, like \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , to connect the effort at a political coup overthrowing the election with all of the violence and the turbulence that was brought outside of the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 The scary thing to me is that the insurrectionist philosophy behind this extraordinary event has only spread within the Republican Party and is now a defining attribute of their understanding of the Second Amendment. I mean, you can hear my colleagues, \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 , \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , Chip Roy talk about how the purpose of the Second Amendment is to allow the people to overthrow the government, which means by definition, to attack the police and to overthrow anybody who`s defending our government. This is despite the fact that there are half a dozen different places in the Constitution that specifically condemn insurrection.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:40]\par \par \uc2 Article One, Section 8, Clause 15, for example, says Congress has the power to call forth the state militias in order to suppress insurrections. The Republican Guarantee Clause says, we in Congress have got to guarantee to the people of the United States a Republican form of government and put down domestic violence against the states, and on and on. And it`s even in, you know, the 14th Amendment, of course, which says that if you`ve sworn an oath to the Constitution and you`ve violate it by participating in insurrection, you can never hold federal or state office again.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congressman, I just want to say, you came on this program, I think, shortly after you were diagnosed with cancer and have been in chemotherapy now for the last, I believe, it was six weeks or two months, something like that, and recently announced that you`ve been found cancer free. I saw you ringing the bell in the treatment wing that you were in. So, I just want to say thank -- it`s just wonderful news and we`re also happy about this.\par \par \uc2 And I also want to ask you a question because I`m a journalist too. Ben Cardin, the senator from your state is retiring. There`s a lot of talk about possibilities of who might run from that seat. Are you considering running for Senate?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, first let me say thank you for your kind words and yes, I am done with the nausea and the neuropathy and hoping to grow my eyebrows and my eyelashes and my hair back soon. And yes, we have a sea change in our politics here in the free state in Maryland. And I`m going to take several weeks to examine the whole political situation.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the 2024 election for the reason that we`ve been discussing still puts us in the fight of our lives in terms of defending democratic institutions and values and practices against Trumpism and the authoritarianism and insurrectionism that`s been unleashed there. And I want to look to see where I can best be deployed to defend democracy and the freedoms of our people. And I`m open to all of the suggestions I`m getting from friends and it`ll just take me several weeks given that I`m just coming off of the chemo experience.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, congratulations again. We`re so, so happy with the news. Congressman Jamie Raskin, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Thanks so much for having me, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: First, luxury travel on yachts and private jets, then letting mom stay rent-free, now thousands in tuition for relative. The latest large jest from the billionaire benefactor of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, tonight, we have yet another story about the perks Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  accepted and did not disclose from his billionaire buddy Harlan Crow. \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  uncovered the evidence showing that Crow paid for Thomas` grandnephew to attend private boarding schools to the tune of potentially $150,000.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and wife Ginni had custody of their grandnephew Mark Martin since he was six years old. According to Thomas, they raised him as a son. At Harlan Crow`s recommendation, the Thomas` sent Martin to Crow`s alma mater for high school, Randolph-Macon Academy in Georgia, a military boarding school where Crow`s name is on a classroom building. He also donated this bronze sculpture of the Air Force Honor Guard, bringing \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  along for his 2007 dedication.\par \par \uc2 And of course, Crow footed the bill for Martin`s tuition, running between $25,000 and $30,000 a year at the time. When Martin transferred to a completely different boarding school in his junior year, Harlan Crow still paid $6,200 a month as you can see on this bank statement.\par \par \uc2 Now, that is certainly a nice thing to do for a young man from an underprivileged background and the family supporting him. But of course, the Thomases are not just any family. \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , as a justice on the highest court of the land, is required by law to disclose most gifts. And in fact, Thomas did disclose an earlier gift for his grandnephew`s education.\par \par \uc2 In 2002, Thomas reported receiving $5,000 from another friend, Earl Dixon. But he somehow never disclosed any of the tuition payments made by the Crows just a few years later. For his part, Harlan Crow is defending his actions. His office released a statement saying in part, "Harlan Crow has long been passionate about the importance of quality education, giving back to those less fortunate, especially at-risk youth."\par \par \uc2 A friend of Justice Thomas also released a long statement on the matter, arguing it as a, "malicious story." It shows nothing except from the fact that Thomases and Crows are kind, generous, and loving people who tried to help this young man. The statement also confirms that Harlan Crow did pay for Mark Martin`s first year at Randolph-Macon Academy and a year at his other boarding school in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 I should note, the friend who wrote that long statement is the man depicted in the center of this painting. Harlan Crow, far right, commission this artwork to commemorate the time spent at his Adirondack Resort every summer with Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , second from the right.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s just take a step back for a moment and consider the nature of this relationship between Harlan Crow and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . They call it a deer and deep friendship. And I am not a party to it, so I cannot say one more to the other. But I can say it sure seems like a strange kind of friendship. According to Crow, it is a friendship that began when the billionaire offered the justice, he was already a justice, a ride on his private jet.\par \par \uc2 It is a friendship in which Crow regularly takes Thomas and his wife and the grandnephew that they raised as a son on six-figure vacations around the world on his yacht. \uc2 Crow\uc2  also purchased the Justice`s mother`s house, spent tens of thousands of dollars to fix it up, and allows her to live there to this day rent-free. And now we know that Crow also paid for the tuition of the adopted son of \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There is a word for this kind of relationship in English. It is called a benefactor. Obviously, for someone who is supposed to be an independent arbiter of justice on the highest court in the land, having a benefactor raises some questions about your impartiality and your independence. Even Crow himself does not seem to deny the true nature of this relationship.\par \par \uc2 In an oddly honest seeming interview, Crow was asked, if he would still be friends with Thomas if Thomas was not a Supreme Court justice. Crow answered, "It`s an interesting good question. I don`t know how to answer that. Maybe not. Maybe yes. I don`t know." And when asked if he can ever consider their friendship to be a quid pro quo, Crow did not say no. Instead, he replied, "every single relationship, a baby`s relationship to his mom, has some kind of reciprocity. I have to say, it`s a little unclear just who is nursing at who`s breast in this metaphor.\par \par \uc2 Senator Peter Welch is a Democrat of Vermont. He serves on the Judiciary Committee that called for a code of conduct for Supreme Court justices in the committee`s hearing on Tuesday. And he joins me now. Senator, do you think these payments should have been disclosed? And do you think they`re appropriate?\par \par \uc2 SEN. PETER WELCH (D-VT): I think they should have been disclosed and they`re totally inappropriate. I mean, that was a really damning introduction that you gave. You know, the Supreme Court used to be our most respected institution. 20 years ago, two thirds of the American people looked up to it. Today, two-thirds of the people looked down on it. And the loss of respect for the Supreme Court is really a threat to our democracy. We need a court that has the esteem and respect of the American people.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s really two issues here. One is, you know, these extraordinary gifts that were given to Justice Thomas. They`re really shocking. But the other shocking thing is the lack of a code of ethics in transparency in the Supreme Court. You know, Chris, we have about 850 judges at the district court in the appellate court level in the federal judiciary. All of them would have had to report every single one of those gifts, except for nine. And those nine happened to be on the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, I am mystified that the chief justice, who has the custodial responsibility to do everything he can to enhance the reputation and respect for the court, won`t step forward and have a code of conduct that applies to those 850 other circuit and appellate judges.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to bring another story, your attention, because it just was published just before we came on air. It`s a story about \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 . Now, if we -- I don`t know if we have the photo back from up in the -- up in the script, the amazing painting, if there`s any way to put that on screen right now, but Leonard Leo`s also in that painting. He is notoriously the sort of mastermind behind the entire \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  right-wing judicial network that vets judges and puts them on the court.\par \par \uc2 We have a story about him directing Kellyanne Conway about 10 years ago. There he is on the left-right next to Short Sky. Him directing Kellyanne Conway to give Ginni Thomas $25,000 of payment through a nonprofit he was not on the board of. And he emphasized the paperwork should have no mention of Ginni, of course. This nonprofit will go on to pay her nearly $100,000. None of it mentioning that it`s to Ginni Thomas. We don`t know if the other payments were hurt as well, though they did pay her firm. This does not seem great. What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 WELCH: Well, I use the word deceit. I`d use the word cover up. I`d use the word evasion. It`s clear that \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  knows that if this saw the light of day, it would cause controversy. And the bottom line here is that the court is getting himself into this amount of trouble and that`s bad for a democracy. You know, whatever the relationship is with Thomas and his benefactor, it`s a pretty shocking thing to be getting vacations on yachts in Greece, in New Zealand, to be flying on private jets and have that not be known.\par \par \uc2 And obviously, the whole \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  relationship is something that`s extraordinarily important. It`s been very discouraging. I`m on the Judiciary Committee now, as you know. And I`ve watched these Judiciary Committee hearings before, and when these nominees are put forward. The Judiciary Committee is an afterthought. \uc2 The Federalist Society\uc2  is the interview that really matters for those folks to get the imprimatur for approval on the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 And that has been a long-term concerted and, unfortunately, effective effort by \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 . And we have to have a Supreme Court in this country that people respect. It`s really important. And anything that any one of those justices does that erodes the confidence that our people are all entitled to, the decisions around the level is wrong.\par \par \uc2 [20:41:10]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The chair of your committee, Senator Dick Durbin of Illinois, has written several letters, I believe, to the Chief Justice. He`s called on the Chief Justice to launch an investigation, to police the court more thoroughly. The Chief Justice rebuffed an invitation to come testify. Is that sufficient to put this on the Chief Justice?\par \par \uc2 WELCH: It absolutely -- it is, actually. I mean, first of all, if the Chief Justice doesn`t act, we can. And what I want to make clear is that when it comes to the judicial decisions, Congress has to stay out. The court has the authority to make those decisions. But Congress has other authority, and that includes, with respect to the Judiciary and the Executive, setting salaries, setting ethical codes, setting what jurisdiction is appropriate for the court.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s a role that the legislative body plays in our system of checks and balances. We can`t get involved in judicial decisions, but we can`t look the other way when there is conduct that is clearly eroding confidence in the judicial branch of our government.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I don`t know anything in particular, but just based on what we`ve seen in the last few weeks, my suspicion is that they`ll likely not be the end of this. We`ll see. Senator Peter Welch of Vermont, come back anytime. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 WELCH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, Team Trump rests without a defense in his civil rape case, while the ex-president blusters from an Irish golf course. Now, the judge is calling his bluff. I`ll tell you about it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, on the final day of testimony in the civil rape trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the jury heard from TV news legend Carol Martin, a friend of E. Jean Carroll`s. Martin is the second friend to testify that Carroll told her contemporaneously that she had been assaulted by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . She recounted that she told Carroll not to go to the police. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reporting she said, "Because it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he had a lot of attorneys." Martin told the jury, "I thought he would bury her."\par \par \uc2 Now, this is a very rare circumstance in which a woman has been assaulted and confides in friends who know exactly who that person is at the time. Then the jury also heard from the person himself, but only through a deposition he taped back in October. In it he said, E. Jean Carroll was "not my type, and he misidentified a picture of Carroll from the 90s as his ex-wife."\par \par \uc2 Carroll`s team rested their case today as did team Trump without a defense. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at some level knows how bad that looks, which is why he was all full of bravado on the golf course in Ireland today, declaring he was going home early to confront the charges.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I`ll be going back early because a woman made a claim that`s totally false. It was fake. She`s a fake. It`s all this big scam. It`s all a big political scam. So, because of that, I have to leave Ireland and I have to leave Scotland. We`re at great properties. I have to leave early. I don`t have to, but I choose to.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Will you attend the trial, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I`ll probably attend. I think it`s a disgrace. I`m going to go back and I`m going to confront this. This woman is a disgrace and it shouldn`t be allowed to happen in our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: While he`s calling that one a liar, he was of course lying. The Trump campaign has always said he would be out of the country through Thursday. The judge then double-checked with Trump`s lawyer that he was still waving his right to testify despite the bravado.\par \par \uc2 Right now, closing arguments are expected to start on Monday. The judge gave Trump until Sunday at 5:00 p.m. to change his mind, fly back, defend himself, testify in his own civil rape trial.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:55:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today in North Carolina, the Republican-controlled legislature approved a 12-week abortion ban that looks like it`s going to become law. Up until last month, Republicans held majorities in both the Statehouse and the Senate, but anything they passed could be vetoed by the Democratic Governor \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 . That is until a state representative named Tricia Cotham who ran and won as a pro-choice Democrat in 2022 -- that`s what she was elected as -- in a sudden, startling, and at least partially unexplained move, flipped her party affiliation seemingly out of nowhere to become a Republican. She has said she felt too controlled by the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 This is a woman who spoke movingly about her own abortion on the floor of the North Carolina Statehouse in 2015 when she was arguing against abortion restrictions. She even co-sponsored a bill in January four months ago to codify abortion protections in her state. Yesterday, that same woman you see there cast the deciding vote, in line with all of the Republicans to approve a 12-week abortion ban, and ensured that even if Governor Cooper vetoes the bill, which he has said he will, Republicans can override the veto and make the ban law.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:10]\par \par \uc2 Jessica Valenti`s work has been essential in tracking the story of abortion rights in the post-Roe era. She`s the author of the newsletter, Abortion Every Day, where today she published a story titled, Texas is Fabricating Abortion Data. We`ll talk about it in a second. And she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Jess, I saw you also write about the North Carolina law saying it`s even worse than you think. What`s your rundown, the basic takeaway of this law?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA VALENTI, AUTHOR, ABORTION EVERY DAY: Sure. They are trying very, very hard in North Carolina to make this bill come across as if it`s a moderate bill. They`re calling it things like common sense, reasonable. One of the sponsors even said it`s not an abortion ban, it`s a pro-life plan because they know just how much Americans and voters in North Carolina don`t want abortion to be restricted.\par \par \uc2 And so, they`re trying to make it seem like it`s super moderate, super common sense when, in fact, it`s pretty old school. It`s a very old-school punitive abortion ban. It has mandates that make women look at ultrasounds and listen to fetal heartbeats while the doctor explains the ultrasound, like really sort of old-school Republican stuff that is not moderate, not reasonable in the slightest.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s also this -- this jumped out at me, and I don`t know if you were the person, I think, who pulled this out or someone else did, but the definition of a woman, a female human, whether or not she is an adult, which of course just lets you know that some of the people who will be forced to carry pregnancies to term will be children under this law.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: Yes. They are -- I mean, there`s a lot of language in the bill that makes it clear that they understand the kind of suffering that this bill is going to cause, right? They`re talking about children, though they`re saying in a roundabout way they`re talking about fetal abnormalities, and talking about providing women with palliative consultations for the newborns that they`re going to be forced to carry. Really, really dark stuff, honestly.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They have to also explain in writing orally or provide to the women all the following information. These are women that are before the 12-week band who do want to get an abortion, while there exists a risk of stillbirth with life-limiting anomalies, life-limiting anomalies have resulted in live bursts of infants with unpredictable and variable lengths of life. This is part of the sort of like state-sanctioned propaganda that has to be directed to every woman actually obtaining an abortion before the cutoff.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: Yes. And they have different rules for medication abortion, which they`re trying to sneak by too. They are saying it`s a 12-week bad-for- medication abortion. It`s a 10-week bad. And they have things in terms of like the terrible things that doctors are being forced to tell patients. Doctors are forced to tell patients if you have a medication abortion, you may see the remains of your unborn child. That`s their language. Again, really dark, really, really punitive. It`s very, very cruel.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You had some reporting in your excellent \uc2 Substack\uc2  today about Texas abortion data where essentially you sort of uncovered just almost preposterously shoddy, double counting and fab -- and sort of outright fabrication of data around complications due to abortion. Explain the story.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: So, essentially, conservatives are super desperate right now to prove that abortion is unsafe, but we know that abortion is incredibly safe. And so, because they don`t have the science on their side, they`ve sort of decided to make up statistics using this reporting law. So, they are forcing doctors in Texas, and the doctors that I`ve spoken to, by the way, are describing sobbing as they`re filling out these forms. They`re forcing doctors to fill out forms on a state website with their patients` private data, connecting their medical conditions to abortion complications, even when there is no connection whatsoever, right?\par \par \uc2 They are doing anything that they can. And any doctor that this patient speaks to while they`re at the hospital or while they`re at their doctor`s office has to report as well. And so, one patient who may not even have a complication at all is all of a sudden responsible for three or four complication reports that they will then use in an annual abortion complication report to prove that abortion is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I`m just reading from your reporting, Sue, a pseudonym for emergency medicine physician and other Texas doctors have been required to submit patients` private medical information to state-run website without their knowledge or consent, adhering to a mandate that forces them to report women as suffering from abortion complications even when. This rarely reported on section of Texas law list 28 medical issues as abortion complications. Doctors are required to tell the state about any woman who developed one of these issues if she happens to have had an abortion at any point in her life, meaning they don`t actually have to be connected to the abortion. They`re used to pad the stats.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Valenti, who`s \uc2 Substack\uc2  is a vital during these times, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230505ej5400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May3, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Lisa Rubin, Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 8,665 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: We are actually out of time. I`m going to hold -- I`m going to ask you to come back and we will talk again about whether we think this trend can be reversed. Janai Nelson, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I got right to the finish there. That is tonight`s "REIDOUT", everyone. ALL IN with Chris H. starts right now. It`s time for us to go.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 The core question is, why the hell does \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  still have a job here in the first place? This is someone who said, white supremacy is a hoax.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s a brand-new answer to that core question.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FORMER HOST, FOX NEWS: Now that we`re waging war on white supremacists, can somebody tell us in very clear language what a white supremacist is?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, new reporting on the text messages at the center of the Fox News decision to fire Tucker.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SCHMIDT, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: The idea that this could become public was very frightening for the company.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Then, another twist in the Trump trial.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Trump, why are you here instead of in New York for your civil case, please?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Why Trump`s attorneys say they will present no defense. Plus, how should American news media appropriately cover a twice impeached, criminally indicted front-runner who`s credibly accused of rape.\par \par \uc2 And former attorney general on the anti-democratic outbreak in state houses from Ohio to North Carolina and beyond. When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. As we dive into the third consecutive presidential election cycle with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a candidate, I think there`s been an undeniable change in our political discourse over these eight years. It has to do with the acceptability and ubiquity of just open explicit racism.\par \par \uc2 Now, I don`t want to say our politics themselves in substance have become more racist. I think it`s hard to say, but I do think and I think a lot of people feel there`s been a noticeable shift in just the kinds of things that people, politicians, public officials will say openly in public. And of course, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the pioneer of all this, starting with his very first day as a presidential candidate when he came down that dopey escalator at Trump Tower and called Mexican immigrants rapists. A man who tried to ban Muslims from traveling to the United States, a billion of them, referred to African nations as S-hole countries.\par \par \uc2 This is a guy that said that a group of non-white progressive American congresswoman should go back to the countries they came from and led a crowd in a chant of that, more or less. He attacked the American federal judge presiding over his fraud case against his bogus university, saying he could not be impartial because he`s Mexican. All of it just bog-standard, appalling, disgusting racism, right?\par \par \uc2 And he got away with it with very few consequences. Though I have to say, I do think it helped turn him into such a reviled figure with a favorability rating of just 25 percent according to one recent poll. So, it`s not like he`s winning enormous popularity context with this shtick.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a partner in this same project of bringing racism out in the open. That man, of course, is the now-disgraced ex-cable news host, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 , who once he took over for the disgraced ex-cable news host before him, I know it`s hard to keep track, used his hour to regularly spew pretty blatant hateful racist views.\par \par \uc2 He attacks immigrants, for example, because they make America "dirtier." He referred to the majority Black city of Baltimore as one of the worst places in the Western Hemisphere, calling it a little bit of Haiti in the Mid- Atlantic. He also, while he was doing that, claimed that white supremacy is a hoax. What`s white supremacy? What are you talking about? Define it for me.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  concluded, after analyzing over a thousand episodes of his program, that Tucker "constructed what may be the most racist show in the history of cable news." And I think that judgment was likely correct. And he was obviously not hiding any of this. This wasn`t done in some secret back room. It was the program, readily accessible night in and night out to anyone with a cable box, including the Fox News executives.\par \par \uc2 People have been writing and talking about it for years, which is why it`s a little hard to believe that the thing that ultimately did Tucker in a box was one private racist text message. But that is the latest reporting. Remember, last week we brought you this story about a mystery message that helped lead to the breaking point between Fox and \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , private messages discovered on the eve of the \uc2 Dominion Voting\uc2  System`s defamation trial, which would subsequently be settled, showed him making highly offensive and crude remarks that went beyond the inflammatory, often racist comments of his prime time show and anything disclosed in the lead up to the trial.\par \par \uc2 Well, today, the Times published that secret message. I`m not going to read you the entire text, which is a long message that Tucker sent to one of his producers, and life is short. It was the day after the January 6 attack on the Capitol. He talks about watching a video and rooting for Trump supporters to beat up and even kill an alleged member of Antifa before realizing there was something horrible about that impulse, whatever.\par \par \uc2 The key line is this. "It was three against one at least. Jumping a guy like that is dishonorable, obviously. It`s not how white men fight." It`s not how white men fight. I have a sense of the kind of videos and the kind of websites you`re reading if that phrase just jumps into your fingers on the phone, not how white men fight. So, for the question about what`s white supremacy, it`s this. White supremacy 101, right? What Tucker means is that non-white people fight a certain way, which is dishonorably, which is to say, like savages.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:32]\par \par \uc2 Well, white people have an innate ethical nobility and superiority. That`s it. That`s the oldest of old school, bog standard, like I said, uncomplicated dyed-in-the-wool, white supremacy racism. And to be clear, this is not even his first secret racist communique scandal. Back in 2020, it was revealed that his chief writer had been sharing racist, sexist, and homophobic posts on an online message board. At the time, Tucker amusingly tried to distance himself from his own writer`s views.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: What Blake wrote anonymously was wrong. We don`t endorse those words. They have no connection to the show. It is wrong to attack people for qualities they cannot control. In this country