ing to quote illegal immigrants. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, HOST, FOX NEWS: Now, I saw on the way into work a legal immigrant family digging through the trash looking for recyclables.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: How did you know they were illegal?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You can tell.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Jesse?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I can tell. I`m a city guy. And you don`t want me to get into it, but I can tell .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:10:10]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Again, that`s not an -- I don`t think that`s an act. I think that, again -- I think people have recoil at the thought that these are the actual views. But I think the evidence, and I`m curious what you think. I mean, you`ve written about this, and the cruelty and the point in some ways talks about this that like, these are the actual views. This is not just a thing that`s being sold for consumption.\par \par \uc2 SERWER: Yes, I mean, with the example of Tucker in particular, when you go back, you could see him making remarks like this in like 2004, 2005. He`s been like this for years. The distinction is that in the Republican Party, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  created this sort of -- or took advantage of this sort of groundswell of this filling that they really wanted to be able to say these things and get away with it without consequences. And Donald Trump`s ability to do that encouraged other people to engage in similar behavior, to take license and similar behavior, and you know, live through the thrill of being able to disrespect people in this way seemingly without consequences.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, the question of whether Tucker really believes that it`s kind of irrelevant, but what`s very clear is that he has a record of saying -- of saying things like this over a period of decades. So, you know, the question of whether it`s sincere or not, you know, it doesn`t really matter. He`s been pushing this stuff for a really 