rsuing this path to power, as long as they`re not trying to broaden their coalition but take advantage of this ideally geographically distributed coalition in order to win power through minoritarian means, they`re going to continue sort of trying to convince white people that the apocalypse is at hand, you know, whether it`s through gay rights, whether it`s through, you know, Black people`s probably having more rights than everybody else, or like Latinos having more rights than everybody else, that are going to continue to pursue that race-baiting strategy because so far they are still able to win. To win using that strategy, even if they don`t win a majority of votes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I think, you know, you and I have talked about this a lot through the years, right? Whether this is a majority-making sort of message, right? Do you gain more than you lose in this -- in this sort of trading in this really gross and base racism? And I wonder what you think - - because I think you`re right. Like, it`s ultimately not an effective majority strategy in this country, even though it allows you to retain a lot of power.\par \par \uc2 SERWER: I mean, so far it has not been an effective majority strategy. But I don`t think it`s -- you know, there are a lot of people of color who have conservative beliefs, conservative ideas. It`s not necessarily the case that the Republican Party cannot win those votes. It is a strange, in fact, that it continue to engage in sort of explicit racism as a political strategy precisely because these are groups of people who would probably be more open to voting for the Republican Party without it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. When the racism is so sincerely held, sometimes you can`t see the tactical advantage of shelving it. Adam Serwer, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 You may have been following the news out of Atlanta today where a suspect killed one person, wounded four others earlier today. \uc2 Atlanta police\uc2  has confirmed the suspect is now in police custody. We`ll bring you further developments as we get them.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:06]\par \par \uc2 Coming up, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has spent the week in Europe effectively hiding from his civil rape trial. So, how are his lawyers going to defend him? Well, it turns out today, Trump`s lawyers announced there will not be a defense. What it all means to the indicted ex-president next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Trump, why are you here instead of in New York for your civil case, please?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Because we have a longstanding agreement to come here. We`ve had a tremendous reception, a beautiful reception. The people of Ireland have been great. And we have a tremendous success. And I hear we`re doing very well in New York.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But that does not show --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:18]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Instead of testifying at a civil rape trial, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in Ireland breaking ground on yet another golf course. And today we learned not only is Trump not going to show up to his trial in New York, he`s not going to put on any kind of defense either. His lawyer telling the court, "We are just going to move forward."\par \par \uc2 But while the jury won`t hear from Trump, they have been hearing plenty from E. Jean Carroll and her lawyers. Today, the jury heard testimony from another woman who said Trump assaulted her at Mar-a-Lago in 2005 when she was interviewing him for People Magazine. And the jury heard clips from the infamous Access Hollywood tape where Trump boasted about grabbing women. The court resumes tomorrow morning with closing arguments expected on Monday.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC Legal Analyst Lisa Rubin has been closely following this trial from inside the courtroom, and joins me now. And it`s wonderful to have you here. Thank you for sticking around after a long day at the court. First, let`s just start with the decision not to mount a defense, which again, independent of the legal ramifications, right, the guy is running for president. He`s accused in the court of rape. You would think, reputationally, one would want to mount a defense.\par \par \uc2 LISA RUBIN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: But if you know that your defense is particularly weak, maybe the best strategy politically is to minimize the entire trial by suggesting that the whole thing is beneath you and less important than going to Scotland to invest in yet another golf course. And that`s what I think he`s trying to signal to this jury. Do I think it will work? I don`t.\par \par \uc2 And that`s because, as our good friend Joyce Vance said earlier today, in a civil case, the burden of proof is preponderance of the evidence, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: More likely than not.\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: More likely than not. It`s not beyond a reasonable doubt. In the defamation claim, there`s one part of it that they have to show by clear and convincing evidence. That`s a higher standard than more likely than not and a lower standard than beyond a reasonable doubt. But the point still stands, which is to say, there isn`t that high of a bar that they have to clear here. And therefore, his decision not to come is particularly, I think, a misstep.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s talk about the testimony today from Natasha Stoynoff, who talked about being assaulted by Trump and Mar-a-Lago. What was her testimony today?\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: Natasha Stoynoff was such a compelling witness. In part because, like E. Jean Carroll, she`s very professionally accomplished. She talked about the fact that she`d been a staff writer at People magazine for 10- plus years. She`s written 16 books. It seems like she makes her living right now as a celebrity memoir ghostwriter and still remains a freelance journalist, writing about entertainers, actors, musicians.\par \par \uc2 When she was at People, she 