ou were raped, you have to go to the police.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Go to the police, right.\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: Carol Martin said, this is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He has hundreds of lawyers. He will destroy you. And that was the advice that E. Jean Carroll followed.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We`re going to see her making that case and presenting that on the stand. That`s going to be very powerful.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Rubin, it has been such a value to this network to have you in the courtroom every day and have you here at night. I really do appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a pathological liar who incited a deadly insurrection and threatened the D.A. who indicted him. So, why is he still being treated like just a normal presidential candidate? And how should the media at large cover him? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just hours after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was arraigned in a Manhattan courtroom last month on 34 felony criminal charges of falsifying business records, he jetted back to Florida to give what he billed as a primetime address from his retirement home. Now, this network decided not to air that speech live, and my colleague Rachel Maddow explained why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: As far as we can tell, and what we`re prepared for here is that this is basically a campaign speech in which he is repeating his same lies and allegations against his perceived enemies. It is just getting started. So far, he`s just giving his normal list of grievances. We don`t consider that necessarily newsworthy, and there`s a cost to us as a news organization of knowingly broadcasting untrue things.\par \par \uc2 So, our deal with you is that we will monitor these remarks. If he does say anything newsworthy, we will turn them around and report on that right away. But for now, just know that it`s happening and we`re not taking it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, fraught decisions about how to cover Trump have been ubiquitous since he first ran for president back in 2015, but this decision was made in the context of a man who used his public platform to incite a violent insurrection against the \uc2 United States government\uc2  who repeatedly lies in the most pernicious way on TV, a guy who ranted against the Manhattan District Attorney on the social media platforms he has access to, he was kicked off the other ones, if he has given live airtime. There is no way to know if he will do something like, I don`t know, give out the personal information about the D.A., something that once said on the live broadcast cannot be unsaid and undone.\par \par \uc2 So, I find it very hard to defend the choice to give him a live platform, no matter how it is dressed up. But that`s one of the challenges he presents as networks begin the work of campaign coverage during a presidential election, which centers on the guy who tried to kill American democracy.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Michelle Goldberg, opinion columnist for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . Michelle, good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s always been this gap between his aberrant behavior and the kind of like normal mechanics of campaign coverage, and they were clear from the beginning, but they feel wider than ever now.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Well, yes, because I think that, you know, in 2016, you know, people like me and you were maybe saying this man poses a threat to American democracy. Now that`s pretty well substantiated, and you kind of can`t argue otherwise in good faith. And also, he`s running with the specific promise to sort of finish the insurrection that he started, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: He`s saying he`s running for revenge. He has this choir of January 6 prisoners. I mean, he`s kind of running on the platform of --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Finish the coup.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Yes, exactly. And kind of, you know, one man, one vote one time. And so -- but it`s a really difficult challenge because you can`t kind of not cover the likely Republican candidate for president and the fact that he is doing this. The fact that he is running this frontal challenge to our way of government and our way of life is a major news story.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s the major news.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: And so, I think that the important thing is to kind of cover it through that lens as opposed to, oh, these wacky things that he keeps saying.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I just think also just in a technical way, like not even like, forget \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and like sort of who he is and even his politics, right? One of the subjects of the Dominion lawsuit was that they kept putting the pillow guy on TV knowing full well the pillow guy was going to go on and defame Dominion. At a certain point, you just can`t do that responsibly. And it`s like, well, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is worse than the pillow guy.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right, yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Like, you cannot -- like, I wouldn`t put someone on our program who I know is going to come on and, you know, try to incite violence or like, defame the voting machine company. He`s clearly going to do that.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. But again -- but I think the tricky thing is that the fact that he keeps doing that is new.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, you can`t write that out and ignore it.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Yes, I think that -- and the media made such a mistake the way they covered him in 2016 when they just sort of covered his antics --\par \par \uc2 [20:35:09]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, yes.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: -- as opposed to the stakes of the election, as opposed to, you know, sort of his record on things like Trump University and the like, right? He was just this side so clown that drove a lot of ratings. And I think that a lot of people have learned a lesson, you know, maybe they haven`t learned the right lesson, but there has to be -- because there has to be fine line, because on the one hand, the fact that he poses such a grave threat to American democracy has to be sort of hammered home.\par \par \uc2 And it`s also true that when he`s sort of out of the public eye, his ratings go up, because there`s this bizarre amnesia of what kind of -- what he`s done.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: And yet at the same time, I think what he can`t be given is just unfettered access to airtime.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And you`re, what you`re pointing towards, and I think something that you and I, I think, agree on, right, which is like, what is the role of attention to him and his -- and popularity, right? Because there`s a sense that like, all he wants is attention. If you give it to him, you give him power. But we showed the favor of real rating. It`s 26 percent for a reason. Like, it`s like he -- like, this shtick is not popular.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The sort of -- you know, the kind of like lost cause, stabbed in the back, I`m going to finish the coup, I mean, even Republicans are like, please move on to something else. So, like, it`s -- in some ways, you have to show the American people that that`s -- like, the guy is running on finishing the coup and ending American democracy for real this time, and you can`t hide that from them.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. But I think you also have to -- you just also can`t just kind of get -- you know, let him on your air to say whatever he will in the hopes that none of it is, you know, actionable.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, we just did the E. Jean Carroll story. Like, you`re going to have to ask him on a live -- if you`re doing something live with him, about E. Jean Carroll, and he`s going to just defame her again. I mean, he`s literally