, from Ohio to North Carolina, beyond Republican lawmakers, states across America are engaged in a really just shameless and persistent attempt to subvert democracy. And it`s hard to keep track of them. We`ve got former Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  who`s on the front line of all these fights and he`s going to join me to talk about it ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right now, Montana is about to become the first state in the U.S. to just flat-out ban the social media app TikTok. The bill has been approved by the state legislature and the Senate, and the governor has said he`s going to sign it. And we`re in Arden charted territory now. This is a first-of-its-kind legislation, but Montana likely is just the beginning. There are already proposals in Congress to ban the app nationwide with bipartisan support.\par \par \uc2 Now, the folks that are arguing for a ban argue that there are concerns, severe ones, about data security, particularly because TikTok is owned by a Chinese company called ByteDance, and critics say ByteDance is subject to the whims of the ruling Chinese Communist Party. TikTok denies this.\par \par \uc2 There have been spirited Congressional hearings. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has threatened to ban it outright. And I have to say, this is one of those stories in the news where I have some instincts about where I fall on this issue, but I don`t really know. And I found some persuasive arguments, both for and against a ban.\par \par \uc2 And so, on my podcast Why Is This Happening, I got to sit down with Jacob Ward, brilliant tech correspondent for us at NBC News, and tried to wrestle through the arguments on either side. How should we think about this fight over TikTok?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD, NBC TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT: The Montana bill is a side effect of, again, this lack of national political consensus on what we should be doing about social media broadly.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 WARD: It is a side effect of the fact that we just have not been able to get out of our own way on this stuff. There should be, by now, all experts say to me, some federal regulation around data privacy, data transparency, and we don`t have any of that.\par \par \uc2 What happens for people using or creating, right? Are you -- can you literally drive across the border?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WARD: You know, could you drive into Wyoming, you know, to post? I don`t know. You know, do you go into one of the seven, you know, autonomous tribal territories?\par \par \uc2 [20:45:13]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Right? And do they become havens of TikTok, you know, entrepreneurs? You know, is that`s a new business model? Like, I don`t know what the heck we`re going to do out of that. All I know is this is not a model that American social media companies want.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I like that idea of driving into Wyoming to upload your TikTok dance. You can scan the QR code of your -- on your screen to listen to that full episode and find Why Is This Happening wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The number one priority of Republican state officials, particularly state legislatures across the country right now, aside from targeting trans folks and banning abortion, is to subvert democratic accountability. And in some states, they are attacking democracy precisely so they can ban abortion.\par \par \uc2 For instance, Ohio. That is where Republican lawmakers are trying to make an end run around abortion rights supporters who are working to place a constitutional amendment on the ballot in November to guarantee the right to abortion. The opponents of that are pushing their own ballot initiative that would make it far harder to get such amendments passed, increasing the required threshold from a civil majority votes to 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 And not only that, get this, the plan is to rush that measure, the one that raises the threshold, out of the ballot in August, right, in the dead of summer, low turnout, so they can head off the abortion rights referendum in November. That proposed change is so divisive, it drew hundreds of protesters to Ohio Statehouse today, even has some Republican lawmakers concerned.\par \par \uc2 Aversion of Ohio`s attack on democracy is opposite of happening in Texas. The Republican-led legislature there is advancing a bill that would allow the Secretary of State appointed by the Republican governor to, get this, just throw out election results in polling locations that run out of ballot paper. And here`s the -- here`s the rub. The measure applies only to counties with at least 2.7 million people. There is only one county meeting this criteria, the overwhelmingly Democratic Harris County, home to Houston, which means a Republican Secretary of State would have unilateral power to throw out an election in the state`s largest county and one of its most Democratic.\par \par \uc2 There`s also North Carolina. You`ve got a Democratic governor there, but the newly elected Republican majority on the state Supreme Court just reversed that court`s own decision from just last year to allow aggressive partisan gerrymandering, a move that one of the two dissenting state justices derided as lawless and a "shameful manipulation of fundamental principles of our democracy and rule of law."\par \par \uc2 But while state Republicans work to rig democracy in their favor or to subvert the popular will of the people, one of the people who`s been most active in fighting against these moves around the country is former U.S. Attorney General under President Obama, \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  now chairs the National Democratic Redistricting Committee, and he joins me here tonight. It`s good to have you on, Mr. Holder. I know that you have been very active in North Carolina. And North Carolina really seems like a wild and special case where you have a state that`s pretty close. It`s a 52-48 state. It`s got a Democratic governor elected statewide twice, super majorities in the state legislature of Republicans. How did this happen? And what does it mean now that they`ve been given the green light to gerrymandering even more aggressively?\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER, CHAIRMAN, NATIONAL DEMOCRATIC REDISTRICTING COMMITTEE: Well, I think it means that democracy is at risk in North Carolina, as it is really around this country in the ways that you have just described. I mean, what happened in North Carolina was that there was a judicial election and the composition of the court changed. You now have more Republicans than Democrats on the Supreme Court. And this new Supreme Court overruled -- overturned a Supreme Court decision from just a few months ago that will now allow partisan gerrymandering to occur in North Carolina, a place with a supermajority of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And you know what`s going to happen. I mean, this supermajority of Republican legislature is going to come up with districts that will favor in an inappropriate way. They will gerrymander on a partisan and racial basis to guarantee that they can continue to control the North Carolina legislature.\par \par \uc2 What the North Carolina Supreme Court did was not based on principle. It was not based on precedent, as courts are supposed to do. It was based on personnel, the change in personnel, and it puts democracy at risk.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We`ve seen these kind of doom loops, right? And Wisconsin`s a sort of interesting comparison place, right? Wisconsin is sort of the other state where if you look at statewide elections, it`s a competitive state, it`s not like a deeply conservative Republican state. You get 50-50 elections. You got a Democratic governor, and yet they gerrymandered the state Republican legislature came up with sort of barricades them in against Democratic accountability.\par \par \uc2 There, the election of a Democrat to that Supreme Court seems to sort of augur in the opposite direction in terms of unwinding that doom loop. How do you see the way out of this kind of suffocation from these sort of super majorities that are gerrymandered into place in states like this?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: It`s a really difficult thing. You know, voter suppression is one thing, and that is something that I`ve worked against or something that I stand against. But if people have to wait in line three, you know, four hours, if they have to get photo ID, that`s something that people can do. It is extremely difficult to overcome a well-constructed partisan or racial gerrymander.\par \par \uc2 Now, you would always look to the courts, you know, whether it is the Supreme Court in the state, lower courts in the states to say that, you know, the gerrymander that has been put in place is unconstitutional when one looks at the state`s constitution. In North Carolina, what they essentially done is to say, all right, we`re going to allow this gerrymandered legislature to keep itself in power, and we`re not going to have any control over that legislature because we`re going to allow this newly-conformed Supreme Court to essentially rubber stamp that which they are going to do.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:41]\par \par \uc2 So, we have to win elections to the extent that we possibly can. People have to be engaged in this, and progressives and Democrats have to play the long game. You know, this is not something that can be episodic. If we want to have democracy, we have to do democracy. It means we have to vote. We have to organize. We have to get people focused on these issues like partisan and racial gerrymandering, you know, which we`ve not really focused on to the degree that I think we -- that should have.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, I think -- you know, one of the things you saw in 