ings that are inconsistent with the desires of the people who they supposedly want to represent.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are afraid of the people. They are afraid to go to the people with their policies. And I think you have to ask yourself, what does the Republican Party stand for now on a national level or on a state level? Well, you know, they cater to the special interests. They cater to the extremists. They don`t do -- they don`t do the kinds of things or come up with the kinds of policies that will help the average American do better in life, either economically, socially, and they certainly don`t want to help the average American when it comes to being better politically.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, what`s striking to me too is just that is the kind of sense of sort of paranoia here, right? I mean, in the case of North Carolina, Wisconsin, right, these are closely divided states. They have close statewide elections, whether the statewide elections or presidential elections, right? They`re contested. You know, Abbott won by 11 points. His approval rating is pretty high. Texas remains a fairly Republican state. It`s not like -- they hold every statewide office. The Democrats (INAUDIBLE). They don`t actually have to do this, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s a, it`s a bizarre confession of insecurity that you`re going to do this to Harris County when it`s like -- it`s not like you can`t just on the merits win elections in Texas. In fact, that`s all they do, win state-wide elections in Texas.\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Sure, but they don`t even want to run the possibility, run the risk that they might lose an election. And so, they gerrymander a system or suppress the vote so that those margins that, you know, that Governor Abbott ran up, probably not as high as they as they actually are, given the fact that Texas is the hardest state in the country, to cast a vote.\par \par \uc2 And so, if you didn`t have those restrictions in place, those margins would probably be, you know, be a little lower. But that shows the bankruptcy of that party. They don`t -- they are so concerned with just losing an election. They`re willing to put at risk their reputation, their place in history, simply so that they win. And again, this is all about the acquisition and the retention of power at all costs.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Former Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2 , thank you so much making some time with us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Wednesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now with my good friend Alex Wagner. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230504ej5300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May 1, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ayman Mohyeldin \par \uc2 7,889 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 May 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Brilliant.\par \par \uc2 ROY WOOD JR., COMEDIAN: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 REID: Well done, my friend.\par \par \uc2 WOOD JR.: You got to look her good in the eyes and I`m so happy when I get that compliment. I looked over and she gave it a smirk. She`s --\par \par \uc2 REID: She gave you that smile because, you know, she gave it a (INAUDIBLE) instead. And you feel like --\par \par \uc2 WOOD JR.: She gave me that Mr. Miyagi night. I`ll get you.\par \par \uc2 REID: Roy Wood Jr., thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AYMAN MOHYELDIN, MSNBC HOST (on camera): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 GREG CAPERS, SHERIFF, SAN JACINTO COUNTY, TEXAS: My heart is with this 8- year-old little boy. I don`t -- I don`t care if he was here legally. I don`t care if he was here illegally.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Shock and outrage after another mass murder in Texas and the unbelievable attack on the victims by the state`s governor.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This cried out for a little bit of compassion for some leadership. Of course, we got neither of those.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Then, the woman accusing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of rape leaves the witness stand. What we learned from the cross-examination of E. Jean Carroll. Plus --\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): The highest court in the land should not have the lowest standard of ethics in the federal government.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: They said it moves to check the Supreme Court amid new reporting on massive earnings for the wife of the chief justice. And \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  joins me to talk about how Republicans can`t stay out of their own way when it comes to restrictive bans on abortion.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think we`ll get it done in some level. It could be on different levels, but we`re going to get it done.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Good evening, everyone. I`m Ayman Mohyeldin in for Chris Hayes from New York tonight. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says it has zero leads in the manhunt for Francisco Oropeza, the suspect in this weekend`s tragic shooting in Cleveland, Texas. Late Friday night, according to police, Oropeza`s neighbor, a man named Wilson Garcia, approached Oropeza to ask him to stop firing his gun so close to Garcia`s home. Oropeza refused, but it didn`t end there. He then retrieved an AR-15 rifle, went to Garcia`s home, and murdered five people, including Garcia`s wife and 9-year-old son.\par \par \uc2 It is a senseless tragedy and part of a larger pattern of interpersonal disputes turning deadly when guns are involved in this country. It is hard to imagine that horrible situation being any worse, but Texas Republican Governor Greg Abbott`s response somehow did, in fact, make things even worse. After reports of the shooting had already circulated Saturday, Abbott tweeted this out, a picture of his dog with the caption, all smiles for the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, the governor might argue that that tweet was pre-scheduled, or he didn`t know about the shooting when it was posted, but that in of itself raises the question, was the governor unaware of a horrible mass shooting in his own state? The alternative is somehow even worse that Greg Abbott did know about the shooting and even after knowing about it, posted that tweet anyway. And when Governor Abbott finally did address the shooting, his response was somehow even worse than the dog picture.\par \par \uc2 Even in the wake of a horrible tragedy, Abbott couldn`t help but take the opportunity to demonize Texas` immigrant community, writing in part, "I`ve announced a $50,000 reward for info on the criminal who killed five illegal immigrants Friday."First of all, let`s be clear, that is not true. We have learned at least one of the victims was a legal resident of the United States. But honestly, that is irrelevant as the sheriff representing Cleveland, Texas made it plain. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPERS: My heart is with this 8-year-old little boy. I don`t -- I don`t care if he was here legally. I don`t care if he was here illegally. He was in my county. Five people died in my county. And that is where my heart is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: It didn`t matter to him, but it certainly mattered to Governor Abbott. His worldview has been poisoned by right-wing xenophobia. And even in the wake of a tragedy like this, he is unable to see the victims for who they are, human beings, and reduce them to simply their immigration status. And this kind of rhetoric is part of a large pattern from Governor Abbott who has arguably displayed a sense of indifference towards gun violence in his state especially when the victims are people of color.\par \par \uc2 Don`t forget, the night of the shooting in Uvalde, Texas, when a gunman armed with an AR-15 slaughtered 19 children and two teachers, what did Greg Abbott do? He flew. He flew to a fundraiser. Abbott then praised law enforcement for their response to the shooting, a response which we now know was entirely negligent because police said they actually didn`t go into the school because they were no match for the AR-15 that they would face.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:12]\par \par \uc2 Again, it raises this fundamental question. Was Greg Abbott ignorant of the truth or was he callously lying to the public about what he knew? It`s not just Uvalde. In 2019, after an anti-immigrant domestic terrorist massacre, 23 people and injured 23 more, in a shooting specifically targeting the Latinx community in El Paso, Texas, folks were quick to point out that Abbott`s rhetoric about immigrants only added feel to the fire, including a mailer that went out the day before the shooting, telling voters, "If we are going to defend Texas, we`ll need to take matters into our own hands."\par \par \uc2 And less than three weeks after the deadly anti-immigrant hate crime, he was back at it again, lambasting undocumented immigrants on social media. In the years since the El Paso massacre, Governor Greg Abbott and Texas Republicans have only made it easier to carry guns in Texas, not harder, and they did so with a controversial 2021 law allowing most residents to open carry a handgun without a permit.\par \par \uc2 One Texas sheriff told the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , "It seems like now there`s been a tipping point where just everybody is armed." But even amid an epidemic of gun violence in his state, we have no choice but to assume that everybody being armed is exactly what Governor Abbott wants.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Veronica Escobar\uc2  is a Democrat representing El Paso, Texas, who had suffered a mass shooting in 2019. She is also the national co-chair for President Joe Biden`s reelection campaign. State Senator Roland Gutierrez is a Democrat representing State Senate District 19, which of course includes Uvalde, the site of that tragic Robb Elementary School shooting last year. They both joined me now.\par \par \uc2 It`s great to have both of you with us tonight. I hate that it is about this subject and the response of the Texas governor to it all. But let me start with you, Congresswoman Escobar here, and I want to start by posing this question regarding Governor Greg Abbott`s excuses for seeming, you know, just any basic humanity in the way he responds to mass shootings. Do you believe he is incompetent or callous?\par \par \uc2 REP. VERONICA ESCOBAR (D-TX): Well, first of all, thank you for having this conversation. It is so important that we confront hate speech and whether it be incompetence or callousness. What we have seen from Governor Greg Abbott is the governor leaning more and more into extremism. He believes probably that is the only way he can win Texas, and what we get instead of compassion is inhumanity.\par \par \uc2 And it is inhumanity towards some of the most vulnerable populations that we have in our country and in our state, starting with immigrants. The governor has worked very hard to lean in on dehumanizing them. He has leaned in on rhetoric calling, you know, their presence in our country or asylum seekers seeking asylum. He has likened it to an invasion, which is the exact same rhetoric that the monster who came to El Paso drove 10 hours to slaughter immigrants and Hispanics, the same rhetoric that he used.\par \par \uc2 And so, it`s really important that we confront this kind of hate speech because that`s what it is. Our state, bottom line has become far more dangerous because of the policies of Governor Greg Abbott and other extremist Republicans like him.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Speaking of those Republican Senator Gutierrez, you have introduced a host of gun control bills this session. Among them raising the gun buying age, safe storage requirements, ammunition, sale regulations, requiring liability insurance for gun owners. Have you come across any Republicans willing to engage with you on the substance to move any of these measures forward?\par \par \uc2 ROLAND GUTIERREZ (D-TX), STATE SENATOR: Sadly, Ayman, no. I mean, we can`t even get the most benign bills. I mean, these families in Uvalde are asking for basic common sense gun safety solutions, raising the age limit to buy an AR-15, extreme risk protective orders to keep guns away from mentally ill people, and closing the gun show loophole for universal background checks.\par \par \uc2 Greg Abbott wants to talk about immigrants to create some kind of culture of chaos, some kind of scene that this dystopian nightmare that things are happening. The fact is, the dystopian nightmare is happening because of their loose gun laws or lack thereof. You could walk down the street with an AR-15 in Austin, Texas, and a police officer can`t even stop and ask you a question.\par \par \uc2 This is their narrative, and they now must own it. It`s -- you know, I`m used to Greg Abbott not being compassionate. I don`t think that he has a compassionate bone in his body. But we`re going to keep working -- we`re going to keep working for these families in Uvalde and for all Texans.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:21]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Congressman Escobar, there was a state representative from your district who co-authored a recent op-ed titled, We Have Gun laws, We Aren`t Using Them, and writes, "Take someone convicted of domestic violence assault. In most cases, nothing is done about it besides a brief warning, even if that. Prohibited possessors are on the honor system here.\par \par \uc2 He is now sponsoring legislation at least requiring those prohibited from possessing firearms to sign affidavits the weapons are out of their possession. But that bill is currently pending in committee. Do you think the state legislator will move forward even on this small step?\par \par \uc2 ESCOBAR: Well, I mean, I hope so. You know, one of the things that we need to acknowledge is there`s not one single solution to addressing what has become a national nightmare. A national nightmare that only occurs in our country because we are a country awash in guns. And I applaud Representative Moody for trying to close this gap and this loophole, but we will need innumerable other gun violence prevention bills in order to address what has happened over the last several years.\par \par \uc2 And what has happened is that instead of passing common sense gun laws, many states, including Texas, have loosened gun laws. So, we are actually moving very rapidly in the other direction, making it so that almost anyone can have a gun, including some of the deadliest guns that exist. I mean, one of the common denominators in many of these mass shootings, including in El Paso, in Uvalde, the current mass shooting that involved that precious little boy and his family, the common denominator is assault-style weapons.\par \par \uc2 There`s absolutely no reason to have so many -- to have any assault-style weapons in any community. But because we have moved into such extreme territory of allowing the most dangerous weapons available on the market and allowing them to be legal and allowing almost anybody to buy them, the carnage that exists is horrific.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you saw the reaction from law enforcement after Uvalde, after El Paso, after this current slaughter and massacre. This kind of massacre should not happen. We should not have these kinds of weapons on the streets.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Senator, you spend a lot of time in Austin. You talk to Republicans there. You know, look, we`ve seen these tipping points happen in like in Tennessee after Nashville, in Florida, after Marjorie Stoneman Douglas -- the high school there. I forgot the name of it. I apologize. But Marjory Stoneman Douglas -- I apologize. But those were tipping moments in creating a movement that`s built over. Is Texas at that tipping point after the strings of mass shootings that happen there?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Ayman, we`ve been past that tipping point. I mean, you`re talking about the Santa Fe shooting that killed 10 kids here in Texas, the \uc2 Walmart\uc2  shooting, Midland, Odessa, a church in Sutherland Springs, Uvalde, now this unfortunate tragedy that occurred on Friday night. The chaos that has ensued, not just in this state, but across this nation is at the hands of Republicans that have created it.\par \par \uc2 Texas, like many other states that are controlled by the United States don`t have any laws to keep us in check. They have an expert that says more guns equals less crime. That is simply not the truth. And the Republicans need to own the increase in crime and they need to own the increase in violent crime across this nation.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: State Senator Roland Gutierrez, Congresswoman \uc2 Veronica Escobar\uc2 , thanks to both of you for starting us off this evening. I greatly appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, 30 years ago, the Waco standoff was an anomaly, but with the country awash with millions of assault weapons like we were just discussing, how long until we see it repeated? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Thirty years ago, the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms, and Explosives determined that a religious cult was stockpiling weapons, including automatic guns at their Waco, Texas compound. They called themselves the Branch Davidians, and they were led by this man named \uc2 David Koresh\uc2 , who told them that he was the son of God. The ATF received evidence from a \uc2 UPS\uc2  driver who delivered big shipments of gun parts, ammo, grenade halls, and chemicals to the Branch Davidians. They also had reports of explosions and automatic gunfire on their property.\par \par \uc2 So, on February 28, 1993, the ATF launched a raid on that Waco compound and sadly turned into a bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JIM CAVANAUGH\uc2 , RETIRED SPECIAL AGENT IN CHARGE, BUREAU OF ALCOHOL, TOBACCO, FIREARMS: It was a massive, massive amount of gunfire. I remember one of the agents saying, well, we know the word is good, because the word was for machine guns.\par \par \uc2 Kenny King, who was a friend of mine, was calling -- telling us he was bleeding to death. And one of the towers got him. That was tough. He was a Marine veteran. And when he`s telling you that, you know it`s bad.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:23]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Four ATF agents and five Branch Davidians were killed that day. And by the end of the week`s long siege on their compound carried out by the ATF and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , 76 more members of the cult were dead, including 25 children. Now, three decades later, some on the far right actually still use the Waco siege as a rallying cry for their extreme pro-gun views. Not the opposite.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  even held his first re-election rally in Waco earlier this year, coinciding with the anniversary of the siege. And the right wing that Trump still leads has succeeded in creating the world we live in now where it is perfectly legal to own a weapon of war in the vast majority of this country. And that continues to lead to tragic situations like the one that we just saw unfold in Cleveland, Texas, over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Late on Friday night, a 38-year-old man was reportedly drinking and firing an AR-15 rifle in front of his home. And when a neighbor asked him to stop because their baby was trying to sleep, the suspect then entered their home, shooting and killing five people, including a 9-year-old boy. Today, authorities are still searching for the suspect who they consider armed and dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of the key special agents involved in the Waco standoff you heard and saw in that documentary there. \uc2 Jim Cavanaugh\uc2  spent over 30 years working for the Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms, and Explosives, including as the special agent in charge and as a hostage negotiator.\par \par \uc2 Jim, it is great to see you. It is always a pleasure to talk to you. I hate that it is about tragedies like this, but it has happened again in our country. And given what we were just showing our viewers there, your long experience on the front lines of this fight, why -- you know, what do you see as the lasting effects of Waco? I mean, on guns and the continued gun violence we see in this country and why it has become such a rallying cry for the far right.\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: Well, of course, the far right has always used, you know, these absurdities, like they try to twist Waco. As Voltaire said, you know, if you can make a person believe absurdities, you can make them commit atrocities. I mean, they want to support \uc2 David Koresh\uc2 . He was a pedophile. He was raping children. He was stockpiling machine guns, hand grenades, and silencers. And he ultimately murdered four federal agents and wounded 16 more. And he took over that cult in a gun battle. And yet people will support him while they talk about made up pedophiles. So, this is an absurdity when people talk like this.\par \par \uc2 This is a person who, you know, the sheriff and the state troopers down there really just implored ATF to help them on him and do something about him and try to get him. I remember the call from the Houston division. Ayman, I was the assistant of Special Agent George in the Dallas division at the time, and they asked me about the place and the plan warrant. And I said, well, we can`t -- we can`t raid. The driveway is too long. We`ll have to call in and negotiate it.\par \par \uc2 And the problem was our intelligence agents that interviewed many of the cult members who had left the compound, they told the agents that Koresh had made them practice putting pistols in their mouths and pulling the trigger. So, the commanders in Houston were faced with the dilemma of, you know, if we call on the phone and negotiate like Jim suggested, maybe they`ll commit mass suicide like Jonestown.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: And of course, then everybody will say, well, why didn`t you just do a raid and stop them? So, you -- when you`re -- you know, again, I go back to the wise men of history, like Churchill says, anybody can plan a war if they don`t have the responsibility. When you have the responsibility, you have to weigh --\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Of course.\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: -- the different ways. So, you know, they knew we were law enforcement and he had named the place Ranch Apocalypse, and they wanted to shoot out with law enforcement. And a lot of people do. And we`ve had militia barricades across the country, the -- you know, Oregon at their wildlife refuge, and also in San Las Vegas at the BLM property. Of course, we`ve had the Free Men of Montana. We have many of them, and any of those could have been a bloodbath as well.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And you can almost see a straight-through line between what happened in Waco and what we`re seeing happen with the proliferation of guns in our country today. What is your reaction to the facts as we know them in this Cleveland, Texas shooting, that this man was firing his AR-15 in his front yard on a Friday night, and what -- you know, what could probably happen thousands of times in this country, an interpersonal dispute between neighbors turns into an assault, a mass murder, and now a manhunt for a killer?\par \par \uc2 [20:25:10]\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: Yes, right, Ayman. Good point. You know, I think people discount the power of firearm makes a human feel. When you take a powerful weapon like an AR-15 and you shoot one, you actually shoot one -- and we shot those all the time in ATF. I mean, this is a very, very powerful gun. It has a large cartridge. Its velocity is 3,000 feet per second, a devastating wound. It`s a military weapon designed for the military.\par \par \uc2 But it was designed for a soldier who would be trained and disciplined and have sergeants and lieutenants and a plan and, you know, it was part of the military world. Now, of course, we`re awash in these guns and civilians have them. They have the civilian version. There`s is not fully automatic, but nevertheless, you can empty the magazine in just, you know, 15 seconds. So, a 30 round magazine, you could shoot in 15 seconds or so.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s devastating. It`s powerful. And it changes a person. Not all people. Most people can handle it, but some can`t. And they get to drinking, they get angry, they get disrespected, like this killer in Texas. And then he runs in his slaughter`s women and children and a family over what, over, please stop shooting my little babies asleep, it`s 11:00 at night. It`s not even an unreasonable request.\par \par \uc2 But he can`t control himself, and he has this weapon. And he feels the power, and then he goes in and he uses it for slaughter. And we just see it -- we just see it too much.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: We see it too much and too often for a country that is developed because this does not happen in any other country. We are not seeing this type of frequency. This is the, I believe, 174th mass shooting this year in America. Absolutely tragic and unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 Jim Cavanaugh\uc2 , it`s always a pleasure. Thank you so much for your time, sir.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, the head of the \uc2 RNC\uc2  says they know exactly what went wrong in the midterms, but guess what? What is the reality show? Well, we`ll see what the party`s actually doing.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , CHAIRWOMAN, \uc2 RNC\uc2 : The biggest takeaway we`re taking is independence did not break our way, which has to happen if we`re going to win in 2024, which usually that`s what causes that red wave. And abortion was a big issue in key states like Michigan and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So, the chairwoman of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , acknowledges that her party needs a new strategy for winning elections with 2024 quickly approaching right around the corner. And according to the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , that was the finding of a Republican autopsy of the 2022 election when that red wave -- you remember that red wave that was supposed to happen, never really showed up.\par \par \uc2 According to the Post, they draft version of the report says, "abortion hurt the Republicans in the midterm elections with candidates trying to avoid the issue and alienating some voters, while Democrats saw their bases energy grow, according to the people familiar with that document." But Republicans are once again trapped between their own unpopular policies, and guess who, their frontrunner, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who was asked whether he would sign Republican Senator Lindsey Graham`s bill that amounts to a national abortion ban if he were president. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : We`ll get something done where everyone is going to be very satisfied. I believe that --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: On the national level -- national level?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think we`ll get it done in one some level. It could be on different levels, but we`re going to get it done. I know the issue very well. I think I know the issue better than most, and we will get that taken care of.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, for more on Republicans getting hamstrung by their own policies, I`m joined by the one and only, my good friend, \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , host of the REIDOUT right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 First of all, how does \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  still have a job? If you had a track record of losing that many times, you get to still stay in your job?\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes. Romney privilege. It`s Romney. Don`t ever let her go.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, it`s got to be something. Wait, tell me this, though. Like, it doesn`t seem like Republicans have learned anything from their autopsy, right? Because they still feel after that red wave did not materialize, let`s double down on the abortion rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 REID: I think this is the proof of why the bubble is dangerous, right? Like, Republicans, they all just listen to Fox News, they read the Daily Caller or whatever, and they come to believe that the vast majority of Americans want to ban abortion, want to ban drag shows, want to ban trans kids. Like, they cook themselves into a pot that they can`t climb out of now.\par \par \uc2 And on abortion, it should have been perfectly obvious to them. All the polling has been very clear. The majority of Americans, even a pretty big plurality of Republicans, do not want abortion to be illegal. But rather than learn from the whipping they got after their tailor-made Supreme Court got rid of Roe v. Wade and the drumming that everyone should have been able to see they were going to take when you take half of the country`s rights away from them and turn them into essentially womb slaves of any red state, yes, you`re going to lose. And yes, they lost.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And she`s explaining this by saying, oh, it was just that they didn`t engage in the debate on abortion. And she -- I guess she`s not reading the polling, let alone the results of the election, but she`s saying what candidates need to do in 2024 is engage with the subject as opposed to run away from it like they did in 2022 with their heads buried in the sand.\par \par \uc2 REID: You know, I`m used to Republicans from the old school. I used to interview Republicans back in the early aughts, right? In the early 2000`s, it was very clear that George W. Bush Republicans wanted the issue of abortion because it motivated evangelical, particularly white evangelical voters, got them to the polls, promising to give them a court that would overturn Roe was great politics.\par \par \uc2 Actually, overturning Roe they knew was the terrible politics because then you would have 50 wars with women. They don`t want to fight women in 50 states, but they knew at least in the Bush Republican era when they were rational about politics, they understood that if they actually ever did overturn Roe, they`d be in trouble.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:36]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: They just wanted to dangle it. They wanted to dangle it to get the evangelicals all hopped up, right? But then \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  comes along and he actually gets it. And then when he gets it, they didn`t know what to do with the car that they had caught. But so, the Republicans in the states, because again, all they do is watch Fox News and Tucker and stuff, they thought, well, the thing to do is lean in, make it illegal to travel, make it so 10-year-olds have to have, you know, the baby of the rapist. Make it so that rape victims and incest victims, make them all have kids. Push harder.\par \par \uc2 No, you need to pull back because everything you do on abortion, it`s not just independents you`re chasing away, it`s women in your own party.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And you were talking about the examples. There was this one very powerful story, this one account from NPR, and they report on the Oklahoma woman. I want to read a part of it. It says, she was denied an abortion for a non-viable fetus because she wasn`t sick enough. At Oklahoma`s Children Hospital, she says the medical staff told her that her condition was serious, but you at the most will last maybe two weeks, she remembers them telling her. They said, the best we can tell you to do is sit in the parking lot, and if anything else happens, we`ll be ready to help you.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: That is what they are telling women in this country.\par \par \uc2 REID: Absolutely. They`re telling -- doctors are telling women when you fill a diaper with enough blood, you can come back and maybe get a D&C. I mean, you`re telling women that you have to carry to term a dead fetus. You have to carry dead tissue inside of you because the state tells you to. They`re supposed to be the party of personal freedom, right? But suddenly, it`s not personal freedom of what to do with your body. It`s not personal freedom to go to a drag show if you want to. Their entire sort of animated -- animating feature now is that the state will tell you how to live. They will make you live by their version of a very particular type of Christianity that most people don`t practice.\par \par \uc2 They are at most, that kind of evangelical, is maybe 18 percent of the country. But what they want to do with government, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Nikki Haley, all the rest of them, is force Americans to live by that 18 percent Christian sect. That is not liberty. It`s the opposite of that. On guns, they`re on the opposite side of everybody. On abortion, they`re on the opposite side, and on culture. They`re on the wrong side of all these issues and they don`t have to get out of it.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And what`s troubling is I`m old enough to remember when they said, hey, this should be -- this should be a state`s right, so let`s kick that back to the states. And then as soon as it went to the states and it passed and we started seeing this, what do we get now in this election? We get \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  saying he wants a national abortion ban and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying yes.\par \par \uc2 REID: That`s right. And Donald -- and by the way, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is smarter than every single one of them. And I would never normally say this about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But at least he`s smart enough to be kind of vague.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REID: The rest of them are like, no, I`ll sign the most far-right abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: For 15 weeks to nine weeks, to here we are --\par \par \uc2 REID: You`re going to lose voters under 30 by a landslide. And only 23 percent of voters, young voters, went to the polls in the midterms and look what they did to them. You`re not going to get young voters. You`re not going to get particularly young women. Voters of color are off the table for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , it`s always a pleasure. Thank you, my friend. It`s great to see you.\par \par \uc2 REID: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Take care.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, more courtroom drama as Donald Trump`s lawyer fails to get his civil rape case tossed on a technicality. What the motion could mean? Plus, we`re going to have the very latest details from today`s testimony, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: While \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left the country today landing in Scotland to open a new golf course, back in New York, his lawyers once again lost a bid to derail his civil rape trial after the judge denied Joe Tacopina`s request for a mistrial. Writer E. Jean Carroll took the stand for the third day. She was once again cross-examined by Tacopina. And she also defended her decision not to call the police after the attack she said took place.\par \par \uc2 Testifying, "Mr. Tacopina, I was born in 1943. I`m a member of the silent generation. Women like me were taught and trained to keep our chins up and to not complain." E. Jean Carroll finished her testimony this afternoon. The trial picks up again tomorrow morning at 10:00 a.m. eastern.\par \par \uc2 Jane Manning served as a sex crimes prosecutor right here in New York. She is currently the director of the Women`s Equal Justice Project where she helps survivors of sexual assault navigate the criminal justice system. She joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Jane, it`s great to have you with us. You know, what did you make of E. Jean Carroll`s testimony? I mean, did you find it compelling as somebody who has been on the front lines of this fight and then has heard the testimonies of women who have been abused and raped?\par \par \uc2 JANE MANNING, FORMER SEX CRIMES PROSECUTOR: One of the things that was remarkable about E. Jean Carroll`s testimony is how much her personality and character were able to emerge through her direct and cross-examination testimony. And she presented herself as a person who has always used humor and optimism to cope and to make life worth living. And it`s a through line through her testimony.\par \par \uc2 What got her into this mess was that she saw a situation that she thought would be funny and a lark and adventurous. After the assault was over, she called a friend and when a friend urged her to report, she said this was 15 minutes of my life and I just want to put it behind me. Even in her testimony, there were times when she diffused a situation using humor.\par \par \uc2 And so, the jury has seen that personality emerge. And so, not only are there all of the reasons that E. Jean Carroll explained why she didn`t report and come forward -- I was ashamed, I thought it was my fault, I thought no one would believe me, I thought it would be a career-ending move to accuse a powerful man, but also she describes herself, as you just said, as a member of that chin up generation. And that personality emerged many times throughout her testimony.\par \par \uc2 And I think if the jury has gotten a sense that they know sort of who Jean Carroll is and how she copes with life, it`s going to connect a lot of the dots in her testimony.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:59]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Let me get your thoughts on Trump`s defense attorney Joe Tacopina. Our legal analyst, Lisa Rubin, who was in the courtroom today tweeted out during the cross-examination. Tacopina implied that Carroll`s tears during her testimony were fake because she never cried during her deposition or during multiple media interviews. What is your reaction to that? How do you -- how do you view that defense tactic?\par \par \uc2 MANNING: You know, his tactics today were a little bit of this and a little bit of that. You know, go back to the issue of why she didn`t call the police. You know, talk to her about why she has sometimes described herself as I`m doing fine if this is really what happened. And I think he`s hoping that something will stick. He asked her a lot of questions about how she behaved afterwards. Why did you go to a watch party with friends? You know, why did you watch The Apprentice? He`s hoping that the jury will hear these questions and think that`s not how a real rape victim would behave. Again, if they`ve bought into that idea of E. Jean Carroll as someone who goes on with her life, I don`t think any of those questions are going to be dispositive.\par \par \uc2 There was one moment in the cross-examination where Joe Tacopina raised a question about a Law and Order episode that aired in 2012 where someone was raped in a dressing room at \uc2 Bergdorf Goodman\uc2 . His clear implication is that, oh, you got this idea from watching Law and Order SVU. E. Jean Carroll testified that she doesn`t watch the show and she didn`t know there was a storyline like that. If the jury has otherwise found E. Jean Carroll`s testimony to be credible and compelling, I don`t see that question to change anything.\par \par \uc2 If they still had questions in their mind, and if this Law and Order issue raised any questions in their mind, what that`s going to do is it`s going to elevate the importance of her outcry witnesses, the two women that she spoke to immediately after the assault, or in the -- in the day is after the assault, because they say that she confided in them in the mid-1990s, and if the jury believes them, then there`s no way E. Jean`s story could have come out of a 2012 plot if she was telling people about it in the mid- 1990s.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Jane Manning, thank you so much. I greatly appreciate your legal expertise in helping us make sense of this. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MANNING: Pleasure. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Coming up, the very latest Supreme Court drama. Just how much was the Chief Justice`s wife getting paid and why her salary could be yet another conflict of interest for the Supreme Court? Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WOOD JR.: This man bought a Supreme Court Justice. Do you understand how rich you have to be to buy a Supreme Court, a Black one, on top of that? There`s only two in stock. And Harlan Crow owns half the inventory. We can all see \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , but he belongs to billionaire Harlan Crow. And that`s what an NFT is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: One of the best punchlines there coming at this weekend`s White House Correspondents Dinner from the cozy financial relationship between Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and conservative activist Harlan Crow. And Thomas isn`t the only justice with ethics concerns at the moment. There`s also \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  and the property he sold to a law firm CEO just nine days after his Senate confirmation.\par \par \uc2 And now, there`s Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . Business insider reporting that his wife made over $10 million recruiting for elite law firms, at least one of whom argued a case before the Supreme Court. The \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  is holding a hearing on all of these ethics concerns tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Melissa Murray, a professor at the New York University School of Law who clerked for Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  when Sotomayor was a judge on the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit.\par \par \uc2 Professor Murray, it`s always great to see you. Thank you so much for making time for us. Your reaction to this news about Chief Justice Roberts` wife. Is it in the same category as the issues surrounding Justice Thomas and Justice Gorsuch?\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, PROFESSOR, \uc2 NEW YORK UNIVERSITY\uc2  SCHOOL OF LAW: Well, let me just say, I think we have to be really careful here. I don`t like the idea of suggesting that Supreme Court spouse can`t have a career. Mrs. Roberts had been a lawyer before her husband became the Chief Justice. She took this job as a legal recruiter ostensibly to avoid any conflicts or the appearance of any conflicts by being a legal recruiter instead of a practicing lawyer.\par \par \uc2 But it seems from the testimony provided by her former colleague that it may be the case that her association with the Chief Justice and her closeness to the Chief Justice was actually very attractive to law firms trying to recruit new talent and to clients who wanted to find a legal recruiter to place them in particular firms. And so, again, it just seems here that there is no actual corruption. No one has said that anything corrupt has gone on or that people have bought favors here, but the appearance seems pretty bad.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:42]\par \par \uc2 And again, this is in large part a bad appearance because it comes on the heels of these two other allegations of alleged improprieties that haven`t been proven to be an actual corruption but look pretty bad nonetheless.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: I think you bring up a very important point. And certainly, we don`t want to create that perception. But also there -- I think in basic public life, there is a desire for disclosure to reduce the perception of a conflict of interest. And certainly, no one is saying that she did anything wrong, but also, there is this lack of transparency about what the court is doing that when it`s revealed it becomes disturbing.\par \par \uc2 And you have Chief Justice Roberts who has already declined an invitation to appear before the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 . Yes, he sent a letter today responding to some of their questions, but nonetheless, he`s not being as transparent as the public wants and deserves about the court.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I mean, this is an institution that is perhaps less transparent than any other branch of government. I think we`re really seeing that. Again, there have been no allegations of corruption. No one has proven that there is corruption here. It`s really just the appearance of impropriety that these associations are too close and the optics are poor for an institution that`s supposed to remain outside of the political fray.\par \par \uc2 And again, what we`re seeing here is a failure to disclose some of these things, a failure to disclose or distinguish between actual salary and commissions or to disclose the nature of a particular property transaction. And I don`t think anyone saying justices can`t have friends, they can`t have rich friends, they can`t sell property, they can`t have jobs, their wives can`t have jobs. But I think what the public is saying for an institution like this one that really depends on the public trust, that can only happen if there is real transparency and disclosure.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: With the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  holding a hearing on this issue tomorrow morning, its chairman, Senator Dick Durbin said this today about his hope for a bipartisan bill of an ethics reform watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I think we can come up with a bipartisan measure and move forward and say to the Supreme Court the highest court in the land should not have the lowest standard of ethics in the federal government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Realistically, even if the Senate passes such a measure, it seems doubtful that it could get through this Republican-led House. But his congressional action the answer here? Is it the only action here or are there other ways to get the Supreme Court to follow ethics rules similar to the executive branch in Congress or perhaps even lower courts?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, again, I think it`s unlikely that anything, even a bipartisan measure is going to get through the House and then on to the Senate to become an actual law here. But there are other things that Congress can do. Congress set salaries for the Supreme Court. It also sets the court`s budget. I mean, if Congress really wanted to put it to the court, and they were serious about ethics reform, they could withhold the budget for the Supreme Court. Make them do their work by themselves without the help of clerks or support staff or anything like that. And I think we probably see some movement from the court on that front.\par \par \uc2 But we might also see movement simply because there has been so much public attention to this. And that`s something the court, especially the Chief Justice cannot like. We have seen a story about the Supreme Court almost every day for the last two weeks. And that`s an addition to the reporting about efforts to get close to certain justices by conservative interests that we`ve heard about over the last couple of months. So, it`s been a pretty bad news cycle for the court.\par \par \uc2 There was a recent poll that finds 62 percent of Americans, nearly two thirds, they have very little or no confidence in the Supreme Court. Lay out the significance of this for the court`s legitimacy in the eyes of the public.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: The court absolutely depends on public opinion for its legitimacy. It`s not like Congress or the president. It doesn`t have the power of the purse. It doesn`t have the power of the sword. The only thing that makes us obey the court and heed its decisions is our sense that the court is doing the right thing. The court is a legitimate institution. Once the public stops thinking of the court in that way, then there`s really no reason to abide by its decision. So, the court`s legitimacy is absolutely everything.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Melissa Murray, it`s always a pleasure to talk to you. Thank you so much. I greatly appreciate your insights.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Monday night. I`ll be back on Saturdays 8:00 p.m. Eastern, Sundays 9:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. "THE RACHEL MADDOX SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Ayman, I felt like we were in your very steady hands on Saturday night watching you anchor the White House Correspondents Dinner.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230502ej5100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for April 28, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alex Wagner, Jonathan Capehart, Glenn Kirschner \par \uc2 7,383 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: That`s our show for tonight. Now, it`s time for THE LAST WORD with \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  in for Lawrence. Good evening, my friend.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Hey, good evening, Alex. Great to see you, have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: You too.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: America, Republicans are gaslighting you. It`s only April, but America has already had 17 mass shootings. Republicans want you to believe that most people aren`t bothered by mass slaughter with military grade weapons. Republicans want to believe that most people feel the solution is to arm more people in more places. Republicans want you to believe that most people would think it`s cute, not crazy, to give guns to a toddler.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM (R-SD): Little Miss Adi, who was almost two, they have brought us so much joy.\par \par \uc2 Now, Adi, who you know, soon will need them, I want to reassure you, she already has a shotgun and she already has a rifle. And she`s got a little pony named Sparkles too, so the girl is set up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Only 1 percent of Americans are members of the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , but the \uc2 NRA\uc2  controls 100 percent of the Republican agenda on guns.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Tennessee Congressman Andy Ogles` 2021 Christmas card. He represents the district where three and nine-year-old children and three adults were killed at the Covenant School in Nashville last month. Congressman Ogles deleted the photo off his social media but not before digging the photo after the mass shooting at a Nashville elementary school.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what Americans actually think about anti-gun violence measures according to a New Fox poll. 87 percent of voters support background checks for guns. 81 percent of voters support improving the enforcement of existing gun laws. 81 percent of voters support raising the legal age to buy a gun from 18 to 21 years old. 80 percent of voters support requiring mental health checks on gun buyers. 80 percent of voters support allowing police to take guns from those considered a danger to themselves or others. 77 percent of voters support requiring a 30-day waiting period for all gun purchases. 61 percent of voters support banning assault rifles.\par \par \uc2 This poll, coupled with the Republican response, or lack of a response, after every mass shooting, shows just how out of step Republicans are with the American people. Those are super majorities in support of common sense reforms. And, today, Democrats are taking action in states across the country.\par \par \uc2 Colorado`s Democratic Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  signed four anti-gun violence bills into law. The laws established a three-day waiting period to obtain a firearm, make it easier to sue gun manufacturers, expand the state`s red flag law and raise the age minimum to purchase firearms from 18 to 21.\par \par \uc2 Raising the age minimum to 21 to purchase firearms is something Uvalde families have been funding for. Next month marks one year since an 18-year- old murdered 19 children and two teachers in a mass shooting at Robb Elementary. For the last 11 months, the families of those fifth graders have been learning to live without their children while the nightmare for the families of the three nine-year-olds murdered in Nashville is just starting.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. JUSTIN PEARSON (D-TN): What about the thousands of people who were here that Thursday who you never recognized? What about the thousands of people here who your colleagues never made eye contact with or smirk that and gave a thumbs up or laughed at? What about the thousands of people who came here saying, we need to end gun violence and we need to do something, and they were recalled insurrectionists by the speaker of this House? What about the thousands of people who marched this streets of Nashville and came to the Capitol asking we do something, but instead of getting just legislation that might protect our schools, we`re saying put more guns in schools?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Joining us now, State Representative Justin J. Pearson of Tennessee and Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez, who represents Uvalde. Also with us, Shannon Watts, she founded Moms Demand Action after the massacre at Sandy Hook. Thank you all -- we`ll get that figured out, Senator Gutierrez, but thank you all very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:02]\par \par \uc2 Representative Pearson and Senator Gutierrez, we all now know \uc2 Covenant\uc2  and Robb Elementary, but, Shannon Watts, I want to ask you first. You`ve been organizing voters since 2012. Do those numbers in the Fox News poll that I just read extensively surprise you?\par \par \uc2 SHANNON WATTS, FOUNDER, MOMS DEMAND ACTION: They don`t. You know, I think we talk about this, so it`s a polarizing issue in America, but, in fact, the vast majority of Americans have and continue to believe that stronger gun laws make us safer, that the Second Amendment can go hand in hand with responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 I think this polling is very bad news for the gun lobby but it`s very good news for public safety. We have known for a long time that this is not a red or blue issue, this is a life or death issue. And that more and more Americans are going to the polls and they`re voting on this issue because they don`t believe their families or their communities are safe.\par \par \uc2 And let`s make no mistake, what we saw in places like Tennessee and other legislatures are run by MAGA Republicans is the fact that there is this toxic stew of gerrymandering, of racism, of gun extremism, and it is ensuring that there are more guns in more places resulting in more violence and more gun death.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And, Representative Pearson, that poll taken after covenant, not even a year after Uvalde, have you seen minds changed on the ground?\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: I don`t know if it`s many minds have changed from the Republican Party after the tragic Nashville Covenant shooting, as the perspective has changed. But there are thousands and thousands and hundreds of thousands of children and teenagers and young people and parents who are advocating for sensible gun reform, and they`re not going away. Whether they`d be Republicans or Democrats, the moral problem that we have about whether this is right or wrong is being answered by the people who are supposed to be getting represented.\par \par \uc2 And we are saying that is right for kids to go to school and be able to come home. It is right for kids to be able to go to a bank and come home and for people to go and live their lives normally without the fear of gun violence. It is wrong for the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and the Tennessee Firearms Association to have a hold on politicians. It is wrong for people`s money to be determining the public policy of a state like Tennessee. And so I am hopeful that some people are really realizing the power of this movement in this moment.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Senator Gutierrez, you introduced a bill to raise the age limit to purchase certain weapons, making Texas the tenth state to do so. The question is do you have the Republican votes needed to pass the bill?\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. ROLAND GUTIERREZ (D-TX): Well, actually, Jonathan, we have an age limit on handguns. Unfortunately, we have no such age limit on long rifles, like AR-15s, which makes absolutely no sense. Our bill in the Senate hasn`t really moved much. The House bill has finally got a committee hearing. It seems to have found some obstacles, and we`ve got to keep pushing forward.\par \par \uc2 The families from Uvalde aren`t going to stop. They`re not going to stop fighting for justice. I`m not going to stop fighting for justice. I`m so proud of everything that Justin and his colleagues have done in Tennessee, even getting \uc2 Bill Lee\uc2  to acknowledge that you need extreme risk protective orders. Texas seems to be the last day to the dance. These Republicans don`t want to do anything in this state to protect our children.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Well, speaking of Uvalde families, Senator, your bill and others like it have the support of Uvalde families, and Kimberly Mata-Rubio, who lost her daughter in Uvalde, told legislators in the Texas House, quote, had this bill been the law in the state of Texas one year ago, the gunman would not have been able to purchase the semiautomatic weapon he used to murder our daughter. Our hearts may be broken but our result has never been stronger.\par \par \uc2 Senator, are there pleas to back stronger gun reform bills having any impact on Republicans in your state?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Well, because of their hard work, they managed to get a hearing in the House of Representatives. It`s a shame that the lieutenant governor will not do so in the Senate. But the families are resolute and they`re going to keep moving forward to find justice here.\par \par \uc2 It`s important to know that what happened in Uvalde, we have to look at these things and they don`t happen in a vacuum. All of that failure that the entire nation saw, we have to understand that when we`re also talking about gun violence and the additional loss of life that happened when you had all these goods guys with guns, children still die.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Representative Pearson, Axios is reporting Tennessee lawmakers probably won`t return to the legislature to consider gun reforms until after July 4th. The Republican supermajority has historically been against gun restrictions. Do you think this time it will be different?\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: No. This time, it must be different. The reality is, this movement of people is not going away, and it is not being silenced.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:03]\par \par \uc2 It is yet to see the government make some steps but it`s not nearly enough. We need to have risk protection orders. We need to have an age limit on rifles. We need to ban assault weapons in our state. There`s so much that we can do to make sure not only schools are safer but our communities are safer so that we don`t have to go to funerals, as I had to go with my own classmate, Larry Thorn, earlier this year, because of gun violence.\par \par \uc2 We are living in a gun violence epidemic and we have to take much more serious action as legislatures, particularly at the state level, to do something about this to stop this violence from happening. And I`m hopeful that in Tennessee, we`re going to see not just one law but several laws pass that actually make our communities safer rather than these false solutions of arming teachers and arming security guards, things that we know will not have a serious impact in our communities.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Well, more on that, Representative Pearson, since your reinstatement, have you spoken to any of your Republican colleagues about gun reforms?\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: My Republican colleagues have not closed (ph) the subject of real gun reform. The only things that they have talked about is more arming teachers, making it so that people between the ages of 18 and 20 could be up to have a gun. However, I have seen some statements that folks are willing to go into a special session with the governor`s bill to amend it in some ways.\par \par \uc2 And, again, I believe people are saying that this moment, this fusion movement about black folks, white folks, rich folks, poor folks who are paying attention to the epidemic that we`re experiencing because of proliferation of guns and the bad policies, is going to create positive change in our country.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And, Shannon Watts, what do you say to people who don`t understand the disconnect between the policies that are passed and what the public actually wants?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: When you have conversations about this with every day Americans, with your family and friends, you realize that you have more in common than you don`t on this issue. It is these gun extremists that we`ve elected to state legislatures or even in Congress who are standing in the way of forward movement.\par \par \uc2 But I want to be clear, we are making huge progress on this issue. We have passed over 500 new gun laws in the last decade. We have stopped the NRA`s agenda 90 percent of the year in statehouses for the last eight years in a row. We are educating people about secure gun storage, changing this issue legislatively, electorally and culturally. In fact, 140 of our own volunteers ran for office and won this last election cycle. So, huge progress is being made, but, obviously, we need more people to get off to the sidelines and use their voices and votes on this issue. I would encourage people who want to get involved in our Mother`s Day Action to text the word Fed Up to 64433. We have to show our lawmakers and that there will be consequences for inaction.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Representative Pearson, let me get one more question in to you before I go to Senator Gutierrez. Let me read you something my colleague, \uc2 David Brooks\uc2 , over on \uc2 PBS\uc2  wrote in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  today. He wrote, Biden talks a lot about the struggle between democracy and authoritarianism. At its deepest level, that struggle is between systems that put the dignity of individual souls at the center and systems that operate by the logic of dominance and submission. You met with President Biden this week. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: I believe \uc2 David Brooks\uc2  is right when talking about President Biden and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . I believe that they too recognize that what we`re seeing at the states legislatures is the erosion of our democracy by expelling lawmakers who you disagree with, now in Montana silencing lawmakers who are seeking to advocate for those who have been pushed to the (INAUDIBLE) periphery and bringing their voices to the center.\par \par \uc2 We are seeing a real scary time in our American history with the erosion of our democracy and the assault on our democracy instead of real attention on issues that really can help people focus on health care, eradicate poverty, things that we know government can do if we focus on caring for the lives, the lost and those made least (ph). But instead, we`re seeing real authoritarian and really anti-democratic behavior from leaders in her Houses, like Speaker Cameron Sexton in Tennessee, who is silencing members and who protects -- literally protecting someone who had committed sexual assault against a 19-year-old intern at the same time that he was expelling us for advocating for the ban of assault weapons. We must preserve our democracy by advocating on behalf of the people who have been oppressed, and I believe Mr. Brooks is right.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Senator Gutierrez, we all now know the police were afraid to go into the classroom at Robb Elementary. Are any law enforcement officers saying privately that they don`t want to face those kinds of weapons on the street? Would that make a difference in a red state?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Well, Jonathan, I`ve seen hundreds of hours of body cam footage, and I`ve seen all of the exit interviews. The police were absolutely afraid. In their exit interviews and they were absolutely afraid of the AR-15.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:01]\par \par \uc2 The speed of that bullet goes three times as much as any other regular pistol. The kind of bullets that this young man used turned back on themselves and tore through these little babies, just obliterating their bodies.\par \par \uc2 America needs to understand that we are in a real crisis here, that we are really broken here and that the Republican Party has truly made our community more dangerous. Let`s be very clear here. The access and the proliferation of weapons in our country by the Republican Party have made our communities more dangerous.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And, Senator Gutierrez, as we approach the one year since the mass shooting at Robb Elementary, tell us about the families and how they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: It`s been very hard, Jonathan. I am so proud to be their friend, I am so glad that they brought me into their lives. I love these people. They are the strongest people I`ve ever met in my life. I don`t know that I could do it. I just -- the whole thing is just the most horrific thing you could ever imagine. If you have children, it just breaks your heart. And I am just glad to be part of their world. We`ve just got to give them a lot of love and a lot of support and understand that this could happen to any one of us. And we`ve got to change things. We are broken in this country. We have to change this now.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Texas State Senator Roland Gutierrez, State Representative Justin J. Pearson and Shannon Watts, thank you all for coming to THE LAST WORD tonight.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 PEARSON\uc2 : Thank you so much. We will keep fighting.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And coming up, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has made it official, he will be seeking re-election with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as his running mate. And for the president, that was never in doubt. More on that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: Now, I stand here proud to run for re- election with President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as vice president of the United States of America so we can finish the job.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: That was Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  earlier this week speaking at her alma mater, \uc2 Howard University\uc2 , where she headlined a rally on reproductive freedom. It marked her first remarks since President Biden announced on Tuesday the two would be in re-election for 2024.\par \par \uc2 Her appearance at that event at \uc2 Howard University\uc2 , a historically black university, highlights the unique role she occupies as vice president. As the first black, and the first South Asian vice president in U.S. history, Harris represents an idealized picture of American progress, serving in a role that nearly decades ago would seem impossible for anyone who looks like her.\par \par \uc2 And as the first woman to occupy the role, Harris is uniquely equipped to lead the Biden administration in its fight against Republican attacks on reproductive rights, an issue that, will no doubt, to be key to turning out voters in 2024.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: What`s happening in our country right now is that these extremist, the so-called leaders, would dare to tell us what is in our own best interest. Well, I say, I trust the women of America. I trust the people of America to make decisions about themselves. I trust them. And so don`t get in our way, because if we do, we are going to stand up and we`re going to organize and we`re going to speak up and we are going to say we`re not having that, we`re not playing that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: There was some speculation about whether or not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would select \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  as his 2024 running mate given some, frankly, undue negative coverage the vice president has had while in office. But for President Biden, it was never a question. He said it himself. She makes him look good. And that`s both here and abroad.\par \par \uc2 NBC news reports since the beginning of the year Vice President Harris has made 29 trips to at least 17 states, speaking about issues that are central to Biden`s re-election pitch, voting rights, police violence, environmental justice, economic barriers, gun violence and abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 The vice president has also been playing a crucial role in U.S. foreign affairs. Just this week, she welcomed the president of South Korea where the duo toured NASA`s facilities and discussed their contributions to strengthen a bilateral space partnership. Last month, she did an eight-day tour of Africa, announcing $1 billion of new investments to support African women and business, as the U.S. looks to counter growing Chinese influence in the region.\par \par \uc2 During that same trip, she spoke in Ghana at the Cape Coast Castle, one of the departure points from the African continent for thousands of people who were forced into slavery.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:02]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Being here was -- it was immensely powerful and moving. When we think about how human beings were treated by the hundreds of thousands in this very place that we now stand, the crimes that happened here, the blood that was shed here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And since the beginning of Vladimir Putin`s illegal invasion of Ukraine, Vice President Harris has often been the face of a Biden administration in Europe, working to sustain \uc2 NATO\uc2  support for Ukraine as it defends itself from Russian aggression.\par \par \uc2 Eugene Robinson writes in The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , as has been the case with virtually every vice president since the nation`s founding, Harris gets a bad rap. The first They duty of the job is to avoid upstaging the president, which means surrendering any political autonomy and never being out in front of the west wing on any issue.\par \par \uc2 As the first woman and first black and South Asian-American to serve as vice president, Harris was destined to be held to in impossible standard. Now, as the re-election campaign begins, she gets to show the talent and drive that got her there.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Keesha Middlemass, a political science professor at \uc2 Howard University\uc2 , also with us, Christina Greer, a political science professor at \uc2 Fordham University\uc2 . Professors, welcome to THE LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 Professor Middlemass, let`s start with the made in that \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  piece. Describe the talent and drive that makes Vice President Harris a key part of this re-election campaign.\par \par \uc2 KEESHA MIDDLEMASS, POLITICAL SCIENCE PROFESSOR, HOWARD UNIVERSITY: Well, she is, first of all, talented and she has been elected at local, state and national level. She is not new to politics. She, of course, ran her own presidential election campaign before withdrawing to then be nominated as Biden`s vice president.\par \par \uc2 She is an important component is because she is reflective of America. She energizes the voting base of the Democratic Party, black women, but more importantly is she energizes young voters. And then I think any distraction from her past as a prosecutor is now in the rearview mirror because she now has a record of 30 months or so of being vice president, representing the nation, as you mentioned, in your opening, across 17 nations. She has been across the country giving us speeches. She energizes crowds and I think that will translate into a positive outcome in 2024 for the Biden/Harris ticket.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And, Professor Greer, Republican Presidential Candidate Nikki Haley, who was previously called for a competency test for any politician older than 75 said this on Fox News. I`m going to quote, if you vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , you are really counting on a President Harris, because the idea that he would make it until 86 years old is not something that I think is likely. What in the entire -- Professor Greer, what should the Biden/Harris counter to that messaging be?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA GREER, POLITICAL SCIENCE PROFESSOR, FORDHAM UNIVERSITY: Well, yes, Jonathan. We know that the Republican Party is going to come out with obviously a lot of ageist tactics, no surprise from Nikki Haley because she needs to say that to detract from her lack of record. And I think the Biden/Harris administration needs to stick to the facts, look at all the wins that they have had these last few months, the policies, the money that they`ve put towards helping Americans, the fact that they are not trying to move this country towards fascism, the fact that they are thinking about the environment or a woman`s right to choose and framing it as such, not sort of base level pro-life arguments, which is not what it is. And they`re really thinking about education and gun control and all these issues that actually matter to usually two-thirds of Americans. And the Republican Party right now is out of step with the vast majority of Americans and what they want, as far as Republican opinion in policy.\par \par \uc2 And so when we think about ages argument, we do know that the Republican Party will try and scare their voters essentially to say, if you deviate from us, he will essentially be putting a black woman in charge of this nation. And so the Biden/Harris administration really does need to just stay the course and really think more about policy and the pocketbook issues that they`ve provided for Americans that will jump into the polls.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:43]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And Professor Middlemass, NBC News is reporting that the White House wants to elevate Harris`s work heading into 2024.\par \par \uc2 A person familiar with the conversations inside the White House said, and I want to quote, "They know she is a target, and the attacks have always been intense. And the ante is going to be upped. They want to make sure that she is on the best possible footing.\par \par \uc2 I was going to ask, how do they do that? But it seems to me that sort of the times are doing that between the attacks on reproductive rights, the anti small d democratic things that are happening in Tennessee and now in Montana, mass shootings, the vice president and the times are meeting. Is that how they do that?\par \par \uc2 MIDDLEMASS: Quite frankly, the Biden-Harris administration and Vice President Harris in particular, just to stay on track. They are doing a fabulous job, they have to just stay on message, but also have be prepared for the racist and misogynistic attacks that are bound to increase in frequency, but also in the speed of them.\par \par \uc2 There will be undercurrents on social media, the national rags, for a lack of a better description, will attack her. So, if the Biden-Harris team literally have to be ready for those attacks, but they also as you mentioned, they just have to say, and oh by the way, we are for reproductive rights. We are for LGBTQ+ rights. And, we do believe in protecting the environment. And oh, by the way, we also believe in gun control.\par \par \uc2 I mean it`s really as simple as talking about policies that Americans care about, that really matter in the day to day lives. And then have the outside of the White House doing those attacks against the GOP, bring forth up and coming, but also, the statesmen of the Democratic Party, to make sure that any attacks against Harris, in particular, are then countered in an effective way.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And Professor Greer, aren`t attacks against Vice President Harris, particularly by Republicans, actually a sign of her strength because they know, if they don`t hobble her now -- and by now, I mean since day one of the administration that they know how far she can go?\par \par \uc2 GREER: Yes. I mean we do also note we need to be realistic Jonathan that, you know, we`ve never had a black female governor in this country. You know, we`ve only had two black female senators, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was one.\par \par \uc2 You know, there is something in the intersectionality of race and gender, when it comes to black women and leadership. It makes certain people apoplectic.\par \par \uc2 I think what the Republican Party most likely will try and do is chip away at black male voters and try and sort of double down on her time as D.A. or attorney general. And really exacerbate certain parts of her resume and really not think about the evolution that most politicians have. Either moving from the middle to the left, or the middle to the right.\par \par \uc2 And I do think you know, if we`re going to have an honest conversation about \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , we have seen an evolution of her as a politician into the role of vice president, which has never defined.\par \par \uc2 Every vice president has a different role as we`ve seen from \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  to \uc2 Al Gore\uc2 , and you know, all the others. You know, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who served, you know Barack Obama. He`s been loyal for eight years.\par \par \uc2 So I do think that, you know, part of the attack that (INAUDIBLE) lay out, you know, will also be to sort of chip away at some moderate to -- moderate Democrats, especially black men specifically, and try and distort her record from her time when she was attorney general and district attorney in California.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Well, I`m just going to -- here is a preview on Sunday. My end of show commentary is going to be on this very topic. So how about that for a tease?\par \par \uc2 Keesha Middlemass, Christina Greer, thank you both for coming to the LAST WORD tonight.\par \par \uc2 MIDDLEMASS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Up next, Republicans in Texas are back at it again trying to suppress the vote. And Texas Democrats yet again are not having it. Two of them will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:03]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TREY MARTINEZ FISCHER\uc2  (D-TX), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: They are not going to force a voting suppression bill down our throats and expect us to take it. We will fight. We will fight. We will fight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: That was our next guest. Texas Democratic State Representative \uc2 Trey Martinez Fischer\uc2  two years ago, fighting against Republican efforts to pass a voter suppression bill in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Texas Republicans are at it again. Two years ago, Texas Democrats made national news when they walked out of a legislative session, that killed the Republican legislation, fled the state, and brought their fight against voter suppression to Washington and the National Press Corps.\par \par \uc2 In response, Texas Republicans cowed, walking back some of the worst provisions and stating, "horrendous policy and it would never be healthy for the democracy".\par \par \uc2 But now, two years later, the right to vote is being challenged again in Texas. On Thursday, Texas House Republicans voted for a bill that would make it a crime to vote illegally, even if there was zero intention to commit voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:53]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Democrats noted that a study by the ACLU of Texas found that 86 percent of prosecutions for alleged offenses, not convictions, which were rare, were prosecuted in communities that were majority nonwhite.\par \par \uc2 But according to a Texas Republican cosponsor of the bill, this voter suppression legislation does not see color.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COLE HEFNER (R-TX), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: This bill does not see color, it doesn`t see race, it doesn`t see gender. We just want to make sure that our elections are fair, and secure, and that we can rely on them to be accurate. And that`s simply what it does.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Joining us now Democratic Congresswoman Jasmine Crockett of Texas. She`s a member of the House Oversight Committee. And Democratic Minority Leader of the Texas House of Representatives, \uc2 Trey Martinez Fischer\uc2 . Thank you both very much for coming to the LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Crockett, you`ve just heard Texas Republicans say that their voter suppression legislation, quote, "doesn`t see color". Your reaction?\par \par \uc2 REP. JASMINE CROCKETT: Jonathan, listen. Are we at Sunday already? Because like we need to go to church. Like somebody needs to pray.\par \par \uc2 I`m so tired of them playing games. We know that they don`t see anything but color, and they recognize that the state of Texas is actually more full of color than not, right. We are a majority minority state. We know that the census revealed that. We added 4 million new people in the last decade, 95 percent of which were people of color, so all of a sudden, now our elections are not secure even though they have already gerrymandered their way into their seats in the first place, and have been so excited that the federal courts are not stopping them, like they used to, when we had voting rights legislation that protected us.\par \par \uc2 It is not enough. Because they know that their policies are failed policies and these radical Republicans only give rise to people like me, rising new Democratic stars. This is why I`m in D.C. now. This is why we know Justin Jones, its` why we know Justin Pearson. So, this is on them.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And State Representative \uc2 Trey Martinez Fischer\uc2 , you have been fighting Republican voter suppression for years. How are these new Republican tactics impacting your community?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Well, I`ll tell you what. You know, when they say they don`t see color, I mean I think they are right because they don`t see all of our black and brown faces on the House floor saying, what are you doing? This is not our problem. I mean just two years ago they lowered this to a misdemeanor, at the request of the Republican Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 . And all of a sudden, without any, you know, massive voter fraud, or any prosecutions, or anything, for that matter, they want to turn this back into a felony.\par \par \uc2 And look, I mean Congresswoman Crockett is right. These Republicans were afraid of something. I think they can see into the future, and I think that they`re worried about the change that is happening in states like Texas. And so maybe, the only way to hang on to power is if you can`t persuade them at the ballot box, maybe we need to change the rules, and keep people from voting. I think that is how you hang on to power when you are desperate.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And Congresswoman Crockett, change the rules to the point where there is a threat of prosecution. How does that threat of prosecution impact voter participation?\par \par \uc2 CROCKETT: Yes. No. I mean we know, first of all, that people that are definitely going to say, you know what, it`s not even worth it, because they have historically been disenfranchised anyways. They are already skeptical about the voting process.\par \par \uc2 Now, you want to add to that yet another thing that somebody can go to jail for. Like black and brown people have been over prosecuted in this country for decades, right. And so now, it is a matter of, oh gosh, this is yet another threat, another thing that can take me away from my family. It just is not worth it because those people that get into office, they`re not going to do anything for me, they`re not going to hear me, they don`t feel me. And these people are desperate, like Trey has said.\par \par \uc2 And it is really unfortunate because you know, I really was green enough to believe that democracy was a real thing that we all believed in, we trusted in, and we would fight for. \\\par \par \uc2 But right now it is very clear that there is only one group of folk that`s fighting for democracy, and another group of folk that actually want to just rewrite the script.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Congresswoman Jasmine Crockett and Democratic Minority Leader of the Texas House of Representatives \uc2 Trey Martinez Fischer\uc2  -- thank you both for coming to the LAST WORD tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, it`s been a bad week for Defendant Trump and there`s no indication next week is going to be any better. The latest on the multiple legal problems, the twice-impeached, and criminally indicted former president faces is up next.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:37]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: The legal vice tightened around the criminally-indicted, twice- impeached \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this week in multiple court houses in multiple jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Fulton County district attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 , told local law enforcement to prepare for indictments in her case, probing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and his allies` efforts to overturn the 2020 election results in Georgia between July 11th and September 1st.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, just blocks from where he was arraigned earlier this month on 34 felonies in the hush money case, the civil rape trial against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  began.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:51]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Writer E. Jean Carroll accuses \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of raping her in the mid 1990s and then defaming her when she went public with the allegations.\par \par \uc2 Over two days of powerful and emotional testimony, E. Jean Carroll described in vivid detail how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  raped her and the impacts it has had on her life to a jury who will decide whether to award monetary damages if they find \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  liable.\par \par \uc2 And last night, we learned that former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  testified behind closed doors for more than five hours to Special Counsel Jack Smith`s federal grand jury, investigating Donald Trump`s role in the January 6th attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Faith Gay, former prosecutor for the eastern district of New York and Glenn Kirschner, former federal prosecutor and an MSNBC legal analyst. He is the host of the "JUSTICE MATTERS" podcast.\par \par \uc2 Faith, E. Jean Carroll takes the stand again Monday for Trump`s lawyer to finish cross-examination. How do you expect that to go given the dynamic we saw yesterday?\par \par \uc2 FAITH GAY, FORMER PROSECUTOR, EASTERN DISTRICT OF NEW YORK: Jonathan, in a jury trial, every day is a brave new world. Last week, Carroll had the jury mesmerized. They could feel what Trump did to her and it`s sickened them.\par \par \uc2 This week the whole game could change. I doubt it, but the whole game could change. Trump`s lawyer will be trying -- for probably a whole day on Monday, maybe part of Tuesday, to show that this powerful story by Jean Carroll, is nothing but just that, a story, a fever dream. That there is no other evidence, no witnesses, no police report, that she didn`t even bother to write it in her diary. Diaries that showed every time she walked her dog, every time -- whatever she ate for breakfast.\par \par \uc2 So the whole entire idea, right now, will be to show that what Jean said last week is just nothing. It`s going to disappear into thin air. I doubt that will happen, but that is what the attempt is going to be on Monday.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And let me follow up with you. So far, do you think the defense has made its case against E. Jean Carroll?\par \par \uc2 GAY: At this point, not at all. It seems to me that if the jury is voting today Carroll wins, hands down. And what we have ahead of us, of course, are two other women who were sexually assaulted by Trump. Their testimonies are coming in. The judge has already said it`s coming in.\par \par \uc2 Trump may or may not testify, I doubt that he will, his deposition was a disaster. So I would say, right now, Carroll is definitely in the catbird`s (ph) seat. Not only is she winning, but she`s winning with big damages because she made very credible statements that this man, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , had changed her life forever.\par \par \uc2 She has had no personal relationship, no intimate life, for the past 30 years, because of this incident with the former president.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And Glenn, let`s switch gears to Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , here in Washington. How helpful do you think his testimony was? And does it signal that Jack Smith`s investigation into January 6th might be is nearing an end? What about the --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Not just helpful evidence, but I suspect, it is sharply incriminating evidence. And I actually say that because of something \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  put in his book.\par \par \uc2 But first, you know, what is important is \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  has evidence, directly from the mouth of the target of the investigation, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Recall, during the January 6th Select Committee hearings, we heard lots of hearsay. We heard things from Cassidy Hutchinson, for example that well \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  told me that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said XY and Z. or \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  told me, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said XY and Z.\par \par \uc2 In \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , you have a witness who has testified, before the grand jury, who can say, this is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  told me, when he was pressuring me to try to, corruptly refuse to certify Joe Biden`s win.\par \par \uc2 And, in his book, which I think is ironically titled, "So Help Me God", \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  said the following. He said that during this pressure campaign, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , at one point said to me, you know what Mike? You know what your problem is? You are too honest.\par \par \uc2 That is some important evidence to prosecutors because what that proves is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knew what he was urging \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to do called for dishonesty. That is evidence of his corrupt intent.\par \par \uc2 I say the title is ironic, Jonathan, because the last words that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  would have uttered, "So Help Me God", would have been part of the oath that was administered to him, right before he began testifying before the grand jury, and incriminating his old boss.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And unfortunately, we are out of time in this segment. Glenn Kirschner, you and I, are going to be talking in a few hours on "THE SATURDAY SHOW" so we can continue this conversation tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 Faith Gay thank you. Glenn Kirschner thank you both for coming to the LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s LAST WORD is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:48]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Before we go tonight, we have breaking news from Montana. It has now become the latest state to ban gender affirming health care for trans minors. Republican Governor \uc2 Greg Gianforte\uc2  has now signed that bill. This is the same bill that Montana State Representative Zooey Zephyr told State Republicans would leave, quote, blood on their hands.\par \par \uc2 Zooey Zephyr, who is banned from the State House by Republicans earlier this week, now, has the support of the senior U.S. Senator from Montana.\par \par \uc2 Here is Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2 , speaking on a call with Montana radio reporters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER\uc2  (D-MT): What I would rather have IS make national news for is keeping 11 restaurants open that are closing, making steps to make childcare affordable or homeownership affordable.\par \par \uc2 Barring a duly elected representative who, quite frankly, represents thousands of Montanans is extreme. I think it is undemocratic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:48]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: State Representative Zephyr shared this photo today on social media. The caption reads, "Republicans used a series of procedures to remove every bill from my committees Silencing my constituents beyond what they already voted to do. My constituents elected me to speak on their behalf, in committee and on the floor. And stopping me from doing so is anti-democratic."\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow, at 9:00 a.m. Eastern, I will be joined by Montana State Representative Zooey Zephyr on "THE SATURDAY SHOW". Also joining us, Congressman James Clyburn, who is a national co-chair for President Biden`s reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230429ej4s00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for April 27, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Laura Jarrett, Mary McCord, Lisa Rubin, Michael  Schmidt \par \uc2 8,741 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: 400 plus million firearms at home stashed wherever and the kind of lethal capabilities that our civilians have, and they`re hopped up on this kind of rage. It is kind of frightening. It is more than kind of frightening. Kathleen Belew, always appreciate you. Thank you so much for being here.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I hope Mike is going to do the right thing. I hope so. I hope so. Because if \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  does the right thing, we win the election.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  gets another chance to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 NICOLLE WALLACE, MSNBC HOST: This is big. We have just learned and are able to confirm \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  testified to be the grand jury today.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, what we know about the Former Vice President`s participation in the criminal investigation of his former boss. Then, the explosive scene inside a New York courtroom where E. Jean Carroll`s rape allegation faced cross-examination from Donald Trump`s lawyer.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Eventually, she just sort of lost it and said, you get to beat me up for not screaming. Some women scream, some women don`t. He raped me whether or not I screamed.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Plus, Michael Schmidt of the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  on what we actually know about the firing of \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): They literally fired him because they cave to the woke mob.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And Senator Bernie Sanders on why he`s endorsing a second term for President Biden when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Well, it sure looks like \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  has flipped on his old boss. Today, the Former Vice President testified before a federal grand jury, as part of special counsel Jack Smith`s investigation into Donald Trump`s attempted coup. This latest development has moved with remarkable speed.\par \par \uc2 It was just last night that a federal appeals court ruled that Pence had to testify. Less than 24 hours later, he was speaking to Smith`s grand jury. We don`t know how long Pence actually testified today, but an SUV transported him arrived in federal courthouse this morning just before 9:00 a.m., it departed nearly eight hours later.\par \par \uc2 And I got to say, those of us who follow for a living or, you know, you watching at home to various legal entanglements surrounding \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  which are prodigious, you can run the risk of growing numb to all of it. There`s just so much. But just, you know, just take a second here. This one is a really big deal. Just step back for a moment. This is the Former Vice President of the United States testifying under oath to a federal grand jury as part of a federal criminal investigation into the man he worked for, the Former President of the United States not for hush money payments, not for something else, for attempting to overthrow the incoming elected government.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Donald Trump`s legal team has spent months trying to make sure today`s testimony never happened. Jack Smith actually first subpoenaed Pence back in February. Both Pence and Trump spent months on multiple appeals. At one point, Pence even vowed to take his appeal all the way to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I`m going to fight the Biden DOJ`s subpoena for me to appear before the grand jury because I believe it`s unconstitutional and it`s unprecedented. Never before in American history has a vice president been summoned to appear in court to testify against the president with whom they serve. We`ll take that case as far as it needs to go, if needs to be to the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I got to say that tape is a little funny today given the steely resolve that he is trying so hard to project in that moment. So, that was the Pence position. You`re going to have to take it from me. Then, last month, once a D.C. judge ruled Pence must testify but could limit the scope of his testimony, the Former Vice President softened his stance, even signals his future cooperation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All right, so it`s not just whether to appeal or not, at least at this point, it just happened today that we`re learning about it. Is that a fair way to describe it?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, we`re evaluating the court`s decision how they sorted that out and what other testimony might be required. We`re currently reviewing. But look, let me be clear, I have nothing to hide. We`re currently speaking to our attorneys about the proper way forward. And as I said, we`ll have a decision in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, so quite a different tone there, right? He`s not doing the whole like, weird knockoff Clint Eastwood bit there. He`s like, yes, we`re probably going to talk. Now, you can imagine \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn`t like that idea very much, so he appealed again to a different court trying to block Pence`s cooperation. And then last night, that court, the federal appeals court again rejected Trump`s