ght?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Well, here`s the thing. They sort of come up with what I think they think is an elegant solution which is we`re going to fight this on speech or debate. We`re going to say we`re standing up for the Constitution, we get an order from the court that says oh yes, OK, I recognize you were serving as president of the Senate on January 6th. However, Smith has alleged some pretty significant illegality. And as to that, you are not immune from testifying about it. That`s what Smith wants. And so, he gets to say, I tried to fight this, but then he doesn`t have to pay like, massive legal bills to take this any further, right? He gets to sort of have that put to bed and have his story --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. They resolved this and say yes, there`s no -- we`re not going to ask you about anything that`s your role as the -- right, because he had concocted to me a facially preposterous but whatever points for trying, you know, the \uc2 Taco Bell\uc2 , \uc2 Pizza Hut\uc2  theory of Constitutional immunity in which he`s both in the executive and also in the legislative, you know, depending --\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Yes, never been done before.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Depending on where you`re trying to get him. The court basically says whatever that`s not really relevant here. We`ll limit the testimony to just what you talked about with the president.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Well, not just what you talked about with the president, it`s the idea is that on January 6th -- OK, so maybe like his notes about his speech that day, maybe that`s privileged.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, right, yes.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: But all of -- again, all of the material conversations that Smith wants to know about, that`s fare game.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And that was presumably what happened today in that courthouse.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, it`s sort of game over for that part of the story. Like, he talked to him.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s no one doing that. That bell cannot be un-rung.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Yes. And that`s why it`s, again, interesting that the former president is not fighting this.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Laura Jarrett, that was great. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Zoe Lofgren is a Democrat of California who served on the January 6 Committee. And Mary McCord serves the acting Assistant Attorney General for National Security at the Department of Justice. She also is the co-host of the MSNBC podcast Prosecuting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And both joining me now.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, let me start with you. You know, I thought the -- one of the things the committee did well, I thought, was to highlight Pence`s unique role and also the fact that he was in conversations that no one else knew. What do you make of today`s development?\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN (D-CA): Well, I think it`s probably a significant one for the Department of Justice. First, he was there a long time. We know of personal conversations that he had with the ex-president and including the morning of January 6 where we don`t have the full story on what was said by both of them, also the night before where he talked to the Ex-President.\par \par \uc2 We had -- you know, the committee did get a testimony from Pence`s top aides and that was helpful. But they weren`t in on some of these conversations between Pence and Trump. So, I`m sure that this was enlightening. Of course, as you know Trump -- Pence and Trump, for that matter, declined to come to the committee and testify. So, this will be new ground I think for the effort to nail down what Trump knew, what he said. We know what he did.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Mary, I want to talk a little bit about timeline here. I mean, I will -- I`m going to stipulate up front here that I am an inpatient person by nature and also a daily cable news host, right? So, like, we just do this every day. It`s like, what are 