tion because they don`t want the perception of trying to influence that. Now, granted it`s a while before we have an election, but it`s a presidential election. So, he`s moving a pace. And you know, I`m not going to give you a projected date but, I mean, I do think he`s probably trying to now pull all the loose ends together.\par \par \uc2 And I`ll also just say with respect to Pence, regardless of what he put in his book or his Wall Street Journal article, that wasn`t under oath, right? So, it was important to get him into the grand jury --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, totally.\par \par \uc2 MCCORD: -- and say those things under oath and then he can`t change up later on.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congresswoman, you`re -- the committee, one of its final acts was to recommend specific criminal charges against the president. You know, are you confident that`s going to happen?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Well, I don`t know. I mean, obviously, we thought there was evidence sufficient to charge and convict the ex-president on a number of charges. The Department of Justice, you know, they have to make their own conclusion. They can`t take, you know, our word for it. So, I think, you know, it`s very clear that they are doing an extensive investigation. And as to whether this is the end, I would say a person who knows a considerable amount is \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  who refused to come in to the committee to testify. And we`ve now cleared up that executive privilege is not going to apply. He`s not an attorney so there`s no attorney-client privilege. He -- I think he has some --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  was the big -- we loss of Congresswoman`s shot there, but \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  was the big sort of missing piece there, Mary McCord. And I`m not sure where that is in the chain of endless litigation about who`s testifying to whom.\par \par \uc2 MCCORD: Yes, that`s right. And you know, that -- he`s an interesting one, right, because if -- because as Representative 