t week or a couple weeks ago. We have five women in that state suing because their lives were placed at risk. And they`re also concerned about their doctors. Just as you mentioned in the state of Texas, and what we`ll end up seeing in Florida, is that the risks will be really high.\par \par \uc2 In Texas we have doctors that risk losing medical licenses to practice up to 99 years incarceration, and also $100,000 in fines and fees. In Florida which had been a bit of a mecca for people who were in abortion deserts, meaning in states where abortion has been totally banned, at least they could come to Florida. Now, that`s off the table.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, this is the map we should show. I mean, this really is seismic for the entire South and all the neighboring border states where Florida had the 15-week ban but a more permissive regime than what was in other places. That`s now gone throughout the entire South.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about something that I`m finding particularly despicable actually in the discourse here, which is very clear that Republican politicos realize that they`re getting killed on the exceptions thing. People hate it. You got to throw some exceptions in there. And so, in Florida, there are "exceptions" for incest and rape. But in order to make that substantively operable in any way, it`s essentially impossible. You have to show proof to get an exception to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What does this mean these exceptions on the actual ground as someone who`s studied this?\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: Well, you know, for the people that are pushing for them, they`re using -- they`re looking to use it as a means to gain votes because otherwise everyone understands the cruelty behind these kinds of legislation. But it is really difficult to actually on the ground utilize these exceptions.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s think about it this way. A 9-year-old girl who`s been serially raped or a 10-year-old