ering a pardon for Daniel Perry. But for some reason, Governor Greg Abbott`s office told us he just can`t make it and that we should talk to the Attorney General of Texas, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , instead. So, that is Greg Abbott`s position.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Less than 24 hours after that aired on Fox, Governor Abbott announced his intentions to pursue a pardon for Daniel Perry. This is just the latest example of a really really chilling trend among the political right to excuse or embrace deadly right-wing vigilantism.\par \par \uc2 There`s a real argument among some conservatives, they should, first of all, be able to hoard guns, and then crucially use those guns to shoot protesters? Shoot liberals and that should be legal to do so? The theory Greg Abbott is appears to be effectively endorsing now.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:08]\par \par \uc2 Sarah Jones is a senior writer at New York Magazine. Her latest piece on this frightening trend is titled The United States of vigilantes and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Sarah, I`m always a big fan of your writing. I thought what you have to say here was really good. How did this case become this sort of cause celeb?\par \par \uc2 SARAH JONES, SENIOR WRITER, NEW YORK MAGAZINE: I think because there were so many parallels to the case of Kyle Rittenhouse from several years ago who also became something of a hero to the right after he shot three protesters and killed two in Kenosha, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 So, the president was already there. It was already established and I think everything kind of fell into place after Perry`s conviction.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and I should show you that they made this parallel quite clear. Here is \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  interviewing Kyle Rittenhouse precisely to draw this connection. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I wanted to speak to one of the few people who knows tonight with Daniel Perry is going through, sitting behind bars for defending himself. Kyle Rittenhouse with that experience.\par \par \uc2 How do you think Daniel Perry feels tonight sitting in jail?\par \par \uc2 KYLE RITTENHOUSE: I can only imagine he`s probably sitting there wondering what happened. He defended himself, he knows what he did was right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Of course, that argument was presented to the jury, which convicted him none the less. What is the implication? And what does it mean to see this now become something of a trend that these figures are held up not just as they shouldn`t be convicted, but as actual heroes, right? People to look up to and to emulate?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Right, the implication of what Carlson and Rittenhouse are saying is that Perry was morally justified in what he did. And that`s what I find most disturbing about all of this.\par \par \uc2 It casts any sort of dissent or criticism of the right and the conservative project as being illegitimate.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Say more about that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I see in these attempts to pardon Perry, a bigger attempt to eradicate dissent, to crack down on it to intimidate people and scare them away from protesting in the streets.\par \par \uc2 I think when that happens, we have to ask ourselves, you know, about the health of our democracy at large, and whether the right really has the best interest of our democracy at heart.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, this sort of -- there`s two things happening here, right? There`s a sort of fetishization of the gun and the vigilante figure, but they`re also this really, really obsessive, almost neurotic obsession with the protesters and particularly protests in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things we saw, you know, after 2020, where all these laws being passed in states that would make it more -- you would have a defense if you ran protesters over, that, like, the protests themselves represent this kind of existential threat and disorder that has to be crushed, and that view seems like a mainstream view on the right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It absolutely does. And I think it`s become more and more apparent over time, we saw it particularly pronounced in 2020, where I note in my article, there were over 70 incidents of cars ramming into crowds of Black Lives Matter protests within a very short period of time in 2020 alone.\par \par \uc2 So, not only is this view becoming mainstream, but people are acting on it, and people will be injured more and people will be killed.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s also in the details of the Perry case to me, you know, the Rittenhouse case, I think I was skeptical of whether the jury reached the right conclusion there. I didn`t think they did.\par \par \uc2 But in the Perry case, the record itself and the facts, I mean, this is someone who literally was like, I`m going to go shoot some protesters (INAUDIBLE) protesters, like the factual record here and the record that`s now been made released public, Abbott going along with a party in that context is pretty appalling.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It is, and I think it shows the degree to which the contemporary GOP has been captured by extremist elements within the party, where somebody like \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  can have such an outsized role and influence the decisions of the sitting politician like Greg Abbott.\par \par \uc2 It really seems like its figures like \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  that are steering the party where it`s going to be going for the next few years. And that`s really concerning.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it does seem to me there`s also political space here for a battle over this. This is -- Texas is a place that has a somewhat attenuated pardon process on like other states, it`s not the unilateral power of the governor, he`s going to depend on a recommendation from a pardon board.\par \par \uc2 So, it does seem like there`s political space for people in Texas, particularly to make their voices heard about whether they think this is an appropriate use of that power.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I think that space exist, and it`s incredibly important to defend that space for as long as it exists.\par \par \uc2 I think those protesters are now contending with a governor who`s tried to chill whatever they might do in the future to influence him or to influence other members of his party. And that`s one of the things I found about his decision to preemptively announce his decision to pardon Perry.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:10]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Sarah Jones, thank you so much for your time. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN for this week. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230415ej4e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for April 13, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,214 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOHN BRENNAN, FORMER DIRECTOR, CIA: Absolutely. That`s why there`s such risk in terms of what he did. And he intentionally violated his security obligations. Irrespective of his age, he should have known better. He didn`t know better, but he decided to push this out. And he`ll be held accountable for it.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Oh, he`s going to find out the real-world jail and the whole \uc2 Twitter\uc2  world that you`re doing are two different things. John Brennan and Ben Collins, thank you.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have all members voted? Have all members voted?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The abortion bans keep passing and national Republicans cannot defend it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): When you look at the issues on abortion, I start with the very important conversation I had at a banking hearing.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, Florida`s vote to reverse reproductive rights as the lawless ruling on abortion pills likely heads to the Supreme Court. Then, another deposition for disgraced ex-president. Will his supporters get away with threatening his prosecutor? Plus, what we know about the 21-year-old Air National Guardsman arrested for leaking Pentagon docs.\par \par \uc2 And first, it was secret lavish gifts from billionaire donor to Supreme Court justice. So, why was the same donor secretly buying real estate from \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ? When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Today, lawmakers in the Republican-controlled Florida State House passed a bill banning abortion after just six weeks of pregnancy, effectively a complete and total ban. A new ban now goes to Gov \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who is expected to sign it into law which will make Florida one of the most restrictive states in the Union on abortion. This highlights just how seriously the Republican Party, Conservatives, the American right-wing is pursuing its anti-abortion aims and how key the issue is going to be in the next presidential election.\par \par \uc2 If you want to run for president as a Republican in 2024, and folks are lining up, you need to have an answer to this simple direct question. Do you support a national ban on abortion? Now, of course until last summer, a national ban on abortion was impossible, in fact, unconstitutional, because the Supreme Court ruled in Roe v. Wade and affirmed in Casey that the right to an abortion is protected by the Constitution. But the anti-abortion movement, the Republican Party, the American right-wing were successful in taking that away. They got the three justices they needed on the court who gutted Roe, overturned it.\par \par \uc2 And ever since the court overturned roe last June, a national abortion ban has once again become a real live question. We`re already seeing Republicans introduced bans in state after state where they have control. Now, including Florida. Some outright, some after a certain length of time, like six weeks. On top of that, a potential Republican candidate for president has to do with the fact that the right-wing position on abortion is politically toxic. And the reason for that, is that the fundamental ideological underpinning of the anti-abortion movement is an idea about when life begins that really is quite radical, and really isn`t shared by most people.\par \par \uc2 This has been the case for decades, all the way back to as long as I can remember my childhood, when my deeply religious, devout Catholic grandparents participated in the march for life every year and went to Washington every single year for it. And they believed deeply, genuinely, as people do now the fundamental ideological commitment of people in the anti-abortion movement that a fertilized egg is a person, that a fetus is a baby, and abortion is murder.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, if you believe that as millions do, you believe there shouldn`t be a national board on abortion -- ban on abortion. You can`t just let babies get murdered in states that you don`t have Republican control of. And this is what many Republican primary voters believe, the people whose votes that you need as a Republican candidate for president. But here`s the rub. The overwhelming majority of Americans do not agree at all. 62 percent say abortion should be legal in all or most cases. That`s a 62-38 issue.\par \par \uc2 So, a presidential candidate who will need to win over persuadable and swing voters has to figure out what do you do about this. How do you thread the needle? We`re watching a potential candidate attempt that right now. Republican Senator Tim Scott of South Carolina just announced that he is launching an exploratory committee for a presidential run. And surprise, he was immediately asked the all-important screamingly obvious question, do you support a national ban on abortion?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Your colleague from South Carolina \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has a bill to limit abortions at 15 weeks, Federal restriction. Do you support that measure?\par \par \uc2 [20:05:04]\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes, so, I will say this. I am certainly 100 percent pro-life without any question. I`ve been very, very clear that, very consistent about that. I do think we spend not enough time understanding how far the far left has gone on the issue of abortion. Senate Democrats have voted for late-term abortions well into the third trimester, some even suggesting that we should have abortion up until the day of birth.\par \par \uc2 The fact that we are one of a handful of countries that allows for late- term abortions is a challenging predicament for us to be in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But in order to solve that, do you think the federal government should be involved with something like what \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is proposing?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes, there`s no question that we`re going to have lots of folks talk about legislation from a federal perspective. But what I`ve heard so far, and what I`ve seen in the Senate aren`t proposals but those from the left trying to figure out how to continue their campaign towards late term abortions, even allowing abortions based on the gender of the child or the race of the child or the disabilities at the child.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But as a president, if you were president, would you advocate for federal limits?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes. So, once again, I -- once again, I`m 100 percent pro-life. And I do believe --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: No, that`s not what I said. I do believe that we should have a robust conversation about what`s happening in the -- on a very important topic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, so you think we should have important conversations on a very important topic. I mean, that was tough to watch. Now, Senator Tim Scott`s answers there were kind of the rhetorical equivalent, and you see this with a lot of Republicans, of watching someone who`s not very good at swimming like a kid who`s just learned desperately flail about in the pool looking for something to hold on to so they don`t drown.\par \par \uc2 And Republican politicians have been trained, right, to look for these kinds of different floaties they will cling to in any situation having to do with abortion, right? You just heard Tim Scott grabbing a bunch of them. I don`t -- I`m pro-life. I make no apologies for being pro-life. I`m pro- life. You hear that all the time. I mean, sure, who`s anti-life. The Democrats -- the Democrats are so extreme.\par \par \uc2 To be clear, no one is arguing for abortion up to the date of birth. But the point is, you`re not going anywhere. You`re not getting any -- you`re just thrashing around long enough in the pool grabbing hold from one thing to the next these life preservers with the thought that like if you just tread water long enough that you find a way out of the question. That`s not -- that`s not going to work. It`s not working for Tim Scott. And I can imagine if you`re working for Senator Tim Scott, particularly his exploratory committee, you might have seen that answer and thought, all right, team, we need a huddle on that one. We need an actual answer on the very straightforward, very relevant question of whether he supports, yes or no, a national abortion ban, particularly considering the fact that the senior senator from our home state, South Carolina, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has already proposed one.\par \par \uc2 So, I assumed team -- Scott`s team got together. They worked on the game plan and tried again today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Would you support a federal ban on abortions?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I will simply say that the fact of the matter is, when you look at the issue of abortion, one of the challenges that we have, we continue to go to the most restrictive conversations without broadening the scope and taking a look at the fact that I`m 100 percent pro-life. I never walk away from that. But the truth of the matter is that when you look at the issues on abortion, I start with the various important conversation I had in a banking hearing. When I was sitting in my office and listening to \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , the Secretary of the Treasury, talk about increasing the labor force participation rate for African-American women who are in poverty by having abortions. I think we`re just having the wrong conversation.\par \par \uc2 I ran down to the banking hearing to see if I heard her right. Are you actually saying that a mom like mine should have an abortion so that we increase the labor force participation rate? That just seems ridiculous to me. And so, I want to continue to have a serious conversation about the issues that affect the American people. I want to start by pointing out the absolute hypocrisy of the left on the most one of the more important issues.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just total cartoonish flop sweat. Just -- I mean, I would say this, that I am certainly 100 percent pro-life without question. I`ve been very clear about that. Very consistent of that. I do not think we -- not enough time understanding how far the left has gone, yada, yada, OK. Honestly, not a lot of improvement there. It may even be a few steps back.\par \par \uc2 And here`s the thing. It`s not really Tim Scott`s fault. It`s not his team`s fault. It isn`t anyone`s fault in particular from a communications perspective, it`s the rotten fruit of a poison tree. The wildly extreme ideology that has driven the anti-abortion movement for decades. The folks who have won now, who believe a fertilized egg, those cells inside a woman`s body, in a pregnant person`s body at three, or four weeks, or five weeks, or six weeks, or eight weeks, or 12 weeks, that that is as much a person as a living human being. That`s the belief.\par \par \uc2 And as a Republican presidential candidate, the people that believe that, those people are your base. Which brings us back to Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , another likely Republican candidate for president. He is too trying to do what Tim Scott did. He`s trying to thread the needle. But he`s just sort of going all in at least action-wise, right. He`s signing an extreme, one of the most extreme bands in the country that just passed today. He hasn`t signed it yet, but he`s going to sign it.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:25]\par \par \uc2 If he succeeds in winning the Republican nomination, this bill that he says he will sign that bans effectively abortion in Florida, this will rightly become a massive political Albatross, an obstacles to his desire to become present United States. It`s not even popular in Florida. In Florida, a majority of adults think abortion should be legal in all or most cases. We saw protests like this erupt today in the capitol building. There`ll be more, I imagine. I`m sure -- well, I suspect that if \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has anyone with two brain cells to rub together around him, they understand that signing this bill is, in the grand sense, a political mistake.\par \par \uc2 But in the immediate sense, he`s got a primary to win before he can win the general. The only hope for Republicans who want to dodge, who want to yada yada, of course, I`m pro-life, but really have you heard \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  -- the only hope for getting their -- having their cake and eating it too, right, is that they know they`re on the wrong side of this issue. Their hope is that the judiciary can do it for them.\par \par \uc2 If a national abortion were to come through the courts rather than democratic means, they would be saved from having to go on the record. And so, they have stacked the federal judiciary with a whole bunch of extremists who share the views of the right-wing base. We saw the consequences of that beginning to unfold last week, when a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 - appointed judge in Texas ordered a key abortion drug taken off the shelves in a week`s time nationwide, something that millions of pregnant folks use.\par \par \uc2 Just last night, two more Trump-appointed judges, appellate judges issued a complicated decision that allows part of that lawless ruling to stand. And now, access to the medication will be limited. Again, because a few federal judges and their clerks working on a crash schedule in an emergency filing over the course of like 48 hours decided the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  got it wrong in the 20 years they`ve been studying the drug.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Department of Justice is asking the Supreme Court to take emergency action, the same court that overturned Roe vs. Wade less than a year ago. Who knows what will happen. This case as Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said today is a step towards a national abortion ban. Of course, it is. Of course, that is exactly what the base of the anti-abortion movement wants. That is what they will demand of a Republican president of any Republican politician as soon as they take power as much as the candidates don`t want to tell you that.\par \par \uc2 Leah Litman is a professor of law at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 , co-host of the Strict Scrutiny podcast along with Melissa Murray and my wife, Kate Shaw. Dr. \uc2 Joshua Sharfstein\uc2  is a vice dean at Johns Hopkins School of Public Health, previously served as the principal deputy commissioner of the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 . He recently wrote an opinion piece the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  entitled I worked at the \uc2 FDA\uc2 . The abortion pill decision is dangerous. Both join me now.\par \par \uc2 Leah, I want to just start with you on a somewhat technical but really important thing here, and then I`ll come to you Dr. Sharfstein on the two appellate judges in the Fifth Circuit, who again, issue this complicated ruling. But one of the most important things here is, do the plaintiffs, the people suing, these anti-abortion groups, medical providers who want this bill taken off the shelves, do they have standing? Can they show the court there`s an injury? Do they get to come into court and say we were hurt by this decision? And those two judges upheld that they do have standing. Why is that such a radical interpretation of this basic legal concept?\par \par \uc2 LEAH LITMAN, PROFESSOR OF LAW, UNIVERSITY OF MICHIGAN: So, the ruling might be complicated, but it`s actually pretty simple. These plaintiffs just don`t have standing at all. The Court of Appeal said these plaintiffs and organization of various doctors have standing because the court declared a statistical certainty that some person somewhere would experience complications from medication abortion, and therefore one of these doctors would be called upon to treat them.\par \par \uc2 But that argument just rests on three problems that are inconsistent with the law, the facts, and common sense. So, on the law, the Supreme Court in 2009 in an opinion by Justice Scalia said organizations don`t have standing merely because they can show a statistical probability that one of their members is going to be injured, rather they have to show which member is actually going to be injured.\par \par \uc2 Second is the Court of Appeals said that the FDA`s patient agreement form established that two out of seven women would experience complications that would send them to emergency rooms. But all of the, you know, patient agreement says that two to seven out of 100 women won`t successfully end their pregnancies via medication abortion, but those people are not necessarily going to end up in emergency rooms.\par \par \uc2 And then third, the idea that even if they did these doctors were injured, I mean, your emergency room doctors, if you don`t want to treat people experiencing emergencies, don`t be an ER doctor.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:21]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, the harm here that they these plaintiffs had to have it they have to treat people which is their job. And Dr. Sharfstein, I mean, your op-ed, you make the point this -- what this opens up, the standing analysis here which is any sort of ideological motivated group of care providers can sue over any drug that anyone takes anywhere just seems like, obviously disastrous and unworkable unless I`m missing something.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 JOSHUA SHARFSTEIN\uc2 , FORMER PRINCIPAL COMMISSIONER, \uc2 FDA\uc2 : I don`t think you`re missing anything. I think that`s why we`ve heard from the \uc2 Cystic Fibrosis Foundation\uc2 , the ALS Foundation, groups that represent patients with Multiple sclerosis and other diseases. Why we`ve heard from many companies, that this is a decision that threatens the whole basis of drug approval and scientific oversight of the medicines that we use.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is the CEOs -- drug CEOs issued a letter, 400 leaders of biotech companies saying if courts can overturn drug approvals without regard for science or evidence, or for the complexity required to fully vet the safety and efficacy of new drugs, any medicine is at risk for the same outcome as mifepristone. And Doctor, I got to say, the thing that`s particularly striking and galling -- and I`m curious as someone who actually worked at this agency right, at the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , this is expedited review, and an emergency appeal on a stay which means there`s like, a few clerks pounding Red Bulls, staying up till 3:00 a.m. reading through the record and writing this opinion that`s like the \uc2 FDA\uc2  got 20 years of science wrong. Like, the sheer hubris here is really something to marvel at.\par \par \uc2 SHARFSTEIN: Well, it`s important to ask the question, who do you want making decisions about the medications that could mean the difference for you and the treatment that you need? And whether you want it to be people who that`s their job, they`ve been trained for it, they think about it, they`re transparent and explaining their reasoning for what they did or do you want it to be just a, you know, random court on a random day waking up and saying, you know, we don`t like this.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, it may be an abortion medication today. But you know, there are people who object to vaccines. There are people who object to antidepressants. There are all kinds of issues that people are constantly bringing, you know, cases about. And if it`s open season, then there is a lot to lose. I have never seen the level of engagement from the pharmaceutical industry saying, this threatens everything we`re trying to do and putting, you know, research together for new cures and the level of engagement from patient groups saying this threatens us. You know, not -- we`re not just advocating here on the question of abortion. We`re advocating for muscular sclerosis, for cystic fibrosis, because we`re worried about, you know, attacks at the foundation of science-based drug approval in the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Leah, we`ve got another federal judge who has issued us to two rulings that essentially facially contradict the ruling coming out of Texas, which is the \uc2 FDA\uc2  cannot do anything to mess with mifepristone. It can`t restrict access in some of the ways that the Fifth Circuit indicated. It has to keep them there. The Department of Justice says, OK, well, we can`t obey both rulings, we`re going to the Supreme Court. What happens next?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: I mean, the Supreme Court should step in. You know, what the Fifth Circuit did is they slapped on some additional restrictions that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  concluded was not warranted based on the science or the evidence, in part for that reason. The district court in Washington forbid the \uc2 FDA\uc2  from actually changing the status quo and putting back those restrictions into place. And so, now you have these conflicting rulings that are telling you \uc2 FDA\uc2  to do one thing in some states and, you know, the Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit to say, no, you can`t actually do those things, even in states outside of the Fifth Circuit.\par \par \uc2 And so, the Biden administration had said, we`re going to go to the supreme court and ask them, you know, to put aside the Fifth Circuit ruling, and in any case to resolve any of the conflict between these two rulings, and hopefully, the Supreme Court will act before this Court of Appeals ruling is supposed to go into effect at the end of Friday.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Leah Litman, Dr. \uc2 Joshua Sharfstein\uc2 , thank you both. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 As the Manhattan district attorney receives another threat, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  only adds fuel to the fire. More than the latest risk to and created by the disgraced ex-President next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: For the second time since Trump said he was going to be arrested and started attacking the Manhattan district attorney, \uc2 NYPD\uc2  had to rush to D.A. Bragg`s office to investigate a threatening letter in an envelope containing white powder. Now, the powder was determined to be nonhazardous, thankfully. The envelope contained a vulgar note along with a picture of Bragg and Trump. The previous envelope which was mailed from Orlando, Florida, according to the postal markings, contain an explicit death threat against Bragg.\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement is investigating the envelope, so they are just a small sample of the hate mail that inundates Bragg`s office. New York Daily News reports that beyond physical mail, the office is getting streams of emails with racial slurs and threats like, "remember, we are everywhere and we have guns." It`s not just the Manhattan D.A. receiving threats after the ex-President attacks him on social media.\par \par \uc2 Today, Trump was back in New York for a deposition in a business fraud case brought by the New York State Attorney General. As he got ready to go to that deposition, he took the time to write a few racist posts attacking that prosecutor as well.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:19]\par \par \uc2 Danya Perry is a former Deputy New York Attorney General, serves as a deputy chief of the Criminal Division in the U.S. Attorney`s Office for the Southern District of New York. She`s also representing \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  in the $500 million civil lawsuit Trump filed against him yesterday. We`ll talk about that. And she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Danya, first, let me just talk about these threats and the ability of the Manhattan District Attorney`s office to do its job and what it means for the courts and the court system. Obviously, I`ve talked to a bunch of prosecutors say, yes, you know, prosecutors` offices get a lot of grief. I mean, there`s a lot of people who were angry about getting prosecuted, so they have security, there`s a certain level to which they drown this out. This does seem to be to me another level. And at what point is there some recourse here in terms of the judge stepping in?\par \par \uc2 DANYA PERRY, FORMER DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL, NEW YORK: Yes, this is a whole other level. You know, I was a federal prosecutor for well over a decade, and we prosecuted all manner of violent crime and organized crime and never saw anything quite like this. So, the judge has already, I think, issued a shot across the bow, and has warned Trump and his supporters that they better, you know, chill out a little bit be cool and not threaten -- I mean, of course, the judge has been threatened, his family has been called out, the district attorney, his family, the attorney general, as you mentioned, witnesses. We just saw this lawsuit that was filed.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is kind of stock and trade witness intimidation and obstruction but there is recourse. If the judge feels that Trump has crossed the line and there is an actual threat, and that it will be impossible for the district attorney`s office to conduct its investigation and this prosecution, he will issue some kind of gag order. He`s already said that. That`s highly unusual. But I think in a case where there`s a level of threat, we`ve already seen that and we`re many, many months away from trial. I think it`s a realistic possibility.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, and this is obviously the sort of go-to tactic here. And he did it with Tish James. He`s done it with \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , who is your client in this lawsuit where he has filed this -- what seems obviously retaliatory civil suit against \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  as, you know, the punishment for his disloyalty. What is the status of that suit?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Well, it`s punishment and also probably as some form of workaround for a potential gag order. So, he might cloak himself in the mantle of litigation ledge and, you know, say all the things that he`s wanted to say and issue the same kinds of threats as he otherwise would have. The posture is, as far as I`m aware, Mr. Cohen has not yet been served, so the clock has not started to tick. I think there are all kinds of problems with the charges here. I don`t think they stand up. I think there are some gating issues, including jurisdiction, perhaps statute limitations issues, and failure to state a claim I think on pretty much all of these claims.\par \par \uc2 So, I think there will be some very, very strong defenses. If Mr. Cohen is ever served, it may well be that, the purpose of the lawsuit has been served, and -- no pun intended -- and that there really is no other purpose because I don`t think Mr. Trump actually wants to litigate this and get -- you know, get the case handed to him again as he has so many times, including by Mr. Cohen in a case that I brought against the Trump administration and Attorney General Barr.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s a really good point. And again, it`s a sort of rule of reporting, right, that you try not to report on threatened lawsuits until they happen for precisely this reason so they can`t have the effect without actually filing. So, it`s a sort of put up or shut up situation. Danya Perry, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, remember the story about the right-wing billionaire who was flying Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  around the world on a private jet. It turns out the two of them may also have done some business together. In fact, looks like they did. Sen. \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  on the latest egregious ethics brief, ahead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Oh, the Chrises of influence. There`s Chris Pine, Chris Pratt, Chris Hemsworth, I`m going to set a shine for Chris Farley. But when you really get down to it, there`s only one Chris who wins our hearts every night of the week. There`s only one Chris Hayes. And we have gone all in for the last 10 years. And here`s the 10 more ahead of us. Chris says, congratulations. We love you. See you tomorrow night at 8:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Four days from now, Jim Jordan will be in New York City to conduct what he`s calling a field hearing to expose the Manhattan D.A. for ignoring real crime. What does this have to do with Congress or his committee of jurisdiction, I`m not really sure. It`s stonework, essentially. But unfortunately for Chairman Jim, it likely won`t make air.\par \par \uc2 Apparently \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2  till the Judiciary Committee they don`t have the resources take a road trip to cover made-for-TV hearing at the, I don`t know, LaGuardia Airport Holiday Inn or wherever they happen to stage it. The committee didn`t take this well, tweeting at \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2  today, it must be tough to find a camera crew in New York City, the media capital of the world on a weekday when there are no other house hearings going on at \uc2 C- SPAN\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing, Jim Jordan, if \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2  skips your hearing, I believe they`re doing you a big favor. Hear me out because outside of the mega bubble, I`m not sure media attention on your party`s views is helping your cause have a moment. Take for example, Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  who gave this performance for the national audience watching the State of the Union. Last week, she got herself on 60 minutes. And here Republicans is the new National face of your party.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:56]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You want to bring the Republican Party closer to your views. You want to bring the country closer to your views. You`ve said that.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARJORIE TAYLOR-GREENE (R-GA): Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK. Here are some of the things you`ve said, that America should have a Christian government, that abortion should be banned nationally, that you want to defund the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR-GREENE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You want immigration to stop for four years. You`ve said those things, correct?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR-GREENE: Yes, these are -- these are some of my views.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Constitution, the Very First Amendment prohibits having a religion in the government.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR-GREENE: Yet the Founding Fathers quoted the Bible constantly and were driven by their faith.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Touche. Well, \uc2 CBS\uc2  took some heat for platforming Greene, which I kind of get. She champions such extremist views. But again, she`s not exactly winning people over to her cause, with her appearances. It`s just the opposite. A new survey from Navigator Research appears to bear that out. Only 35 percent of Americans approve of the job Republicans are doing in Congress, while 59 percent disapprove.\par \par \uc2 Look at the public`s view of how Republicans have handled inflation, an issue they staked their midterm campaigns on. 37 percent of Americans approve of their track record on inflation, while 52 percent disapprove. Jobs and the economy, another issue Republican centered in 2022, you`d expect Republicans to do better here, their approval ratings are just dead even at 44 percent.\par \par \uc2 It`s not shocking that House Republicans would have low poll numbers. Public opinion tends to dip for the party in power. But Republicans -- Americans disapprove of how Republicans handle almost every major issue from abortion, to gun violence, to health care and immigration. The more America sees these Republicans and their policies, the less they liked them. Taking their act out on the road isn`t going to help whether \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2  covers it or not.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, we`ve got another eyebrow-raising revelations tonight about Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and his financial dealings with a billionaire buddy. Last week, \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  revealed that virtually every year for decades Thomas has been treated to luxury vacations by billionaire conservative donor \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2 , including trips on his private jet and super yacht worth hundreds of thousands of dollars which crucially Thomas never disclosed in his financial disclosures.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve learned there are other financial dealings between the two men that Thomas also didn`t disclose. In 2014, \uc2 Crow\uc2  bought several properties in this quiet residential neighborhood in Savannah, Georgia that had belonged to \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and his relatives including a house where Thomas`s mother was still living and two vacant house plots.\par \par \uc2 The purchase marks the first known instance of money flowing from Crow to Thomas. And although a Watergate era law requires justices and other officials to disclose details of most real estate sales over $1,000, and this was $133,000, Thomas never disclosed his sell of the properties. In fact, Thomas has gone silent. He has not responded to the report today. \uc2 Crow\uc2  has issued a statement. He says he purchased Thomas`s mother`s house to preserve it for posterity.\par \par \uc2 But as Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 , Democrat of Connecticut, tweeted last week, it`s not like \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  is some apolitical pal of Thomas, he constantly has cases before the Supreme Court. He funds groups that argue for outcomes that benefit him. One group filed eight briefs before the court. Thomas sided with Crow in all eight cases.\par \par \uc2 And Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  joins me now. What`s your reaction to this latest report on this house sale?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): I just think we are coming to understand that there is an incredibly intimate financial relationship between \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and his wife. It may be that they have been financing a certain lifestyle for the Thomases, and this has been true in secret for the better part of the last 20 years.\par \par \uc2 Now, Thomas says, well, I sought guidance on this, and I was told that I didn`t have to report anything from a personal friend who doesn`t have cases before the court. Well, that`s just not true. I mean, there may not be cases before the court in which Harlan Crow`s name is in the title, but he funds a massive infrastructure that files briefs before the court.\par \par \uc2 So, there are constantly cases before the court in which Harlan Crow registers a public opinion through the groups that he funds. And as I mentioned in that tweet, it just doesn`t seem coincidental that every time \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  declares an opinion on a case before \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  sides with a guy who seems to be funding a lot of his lifestyle, his vacations, and the purchase of his properties. That`s a huge problem. It is not something that we allow in American democracy.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:19]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, we do kind of allow it is the thing. Like, I mean -- like -- here`s the question is, and so then what? I mean, it does seem like the Article Three judiciary kind of makes its own rules. There`s no -- there`s no ethical guidelines to the Supreme Court justices. They can kind of do whatever they want. And it`s sort of like, well, what are you going to -- what are you going to make me do?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Yes --no, so you are right that there are nine individuals at the highest levels of the federal government who are effectively exempt from any ethical rules. And those are those Supreme Court justices. The rest of the federal judiciary actually has a binding code of conduct. And there are consequences for those judges if they violate the code of conduct.\par \par \uc2 We have a code of conduct. Members of Congress have an ethical code, and there are consequences from the body in which we sit if we violate that code. The Supreme Court basically has told us for decades, don`t worry about it, we don`t need a binding code, we don`t need to adopt one. Congress, you don`t need to apply one to us because we can just handle ourselves. There`s no problems. Well, now we see that they obviously can`t handle it themselves. And so, we need to pass legislation that creates a code of conduct disclosure rules just like all the other federal judges are subject to.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, obviously, a real estate sale is like a black-and-white issue that you put in a financial disclosure. But I also wanted to ask you, I`ve been watching and I though you have been watching what`s been unfolding in Tennessee, both with the shooting there, the brutal shooting in the school, the aftermath of the gun protests there, the kicking out of legislators, then just a week later, another mass shooting, this one in Louisville.\par \par \uc2 And one thing women it`s been really been striking me and I -- you and I have been talking about this issue for more than a decade, unfortunately, is the reach of this. It is so striking to hear the governor of Tennessee say his wife had a friend who was killed in Nashville. \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , your colleague in Florida said my banker was killed in Louisville. The governor of Kentucky, saying a friend was killed. Like, do you think this shocking proximity of this violence uniquely American as you`ve documented in your book is going to have an impact on lawmakers?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, unmistakeably it is. And as we see the spike in gun violence, we`re now seeing 110, 120 people die every day of gun deaths, many of them suicides, it does increasingly personally affect more and more Americans. And you are seeing that more and more Republicans are deciding that they just don`t want to have to face the wrath of all of these young people, all of these parents that are demanding action. That`s why for the first time in 30 years, we passed federal gun safety legislation last year. And that`s why you see somebody like Bill Lee who had been pretty unsympathetic to, you know, this movement in the past finally saying, hey, Tennessee needs a Red Flag Law, something that the \uc2 NRA\uc2  opposes.\par \par \uc2 So, I think increasingly it`s going to be impossible for Republicans to stand up against an anti-gun violence movement that is now, you know, pretty mainstreamed in America. I think that`s what the sort of next 10- year holds is just a question of when Republicans start to advocate and vote for some of this stuff, not if they`re going to do it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, it strikes me -- we started the show talking about abortion that in both issues, there`s a kind of paradox at the heart of it, which is that the worst things get substantively and I think things are really bad right now, the gun violence. I mean, just as an empirical matter, it`s shot up, right? Things are really bad and reproductive rights. we saw a right overturned after 60 years. The only silver lining is that the politics get better because the -- you know, the problem and the and the results of the policies that are being advocated for become so crystal clear. That`s the only way out at a certain level.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, listen, that`s the essence of democracy, right, is that you know, eventually you get to have a say on all these things at the polls. And, you know, for decades, the most vocal voices on the issue of guns were on the side of the gun lobby. I mean, if you held a town hall and somebody stood up and said, I want to talk to you about guns, you knew that they were going to be advocates for the Second Amendment. That`s totally flipped.\par \par \uc2 Today, in red and blue states, if somebody says I want to talk to you about guns, they want you to pass the assault weapons ban or universal background checks. That`s pretty exceptional that in only 15 years the sort of the activation, the urgency, the power on this issue is flipped from one side to the other.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [20:50:01]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, an arrest in that classified document leaks scandal. Who was he? What does this say about the state of American counterintelligence next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, federal law enforcement arrested a 21-year-old airman in connection with a high-profile leak of U.S. military secrets. Jack Teixeira is a member the Massachusetts Air National Guard and was taken into custody this afternoon. He`s expected to be charged violations of the Espionage Act stemming from a torrent of highly classified documents he shared with friends in a private group on the social media app \uc2 Discord\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The leaked documents included sensitive information about the war in Ukraine, as well as records the United States spying on key allies, including Israel and South Korea. Well, the documents first gained public attention last month, it`s actually unclear how long they`ve been on the internet or how many more might still be out there.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , an anonymous member of the social media group where Teixeira posted of the document said he did not think the leak was ideologically motivated.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:06]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would not call O.G. a whistleblower in the slightest. I don`t think that there was a goal nor some sort of accomplishment that he was looking for in sharing these documents. Of course, there`s some anti- government sentiment, but that`s not unlike most right-wingers. O.G. was not hostile to the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , however, he had disagreed with several occasions such as Waco and Ruby Ridge and thought that the government is overreaching in several aspects. There was no heavy Snowden-like conspiracy here like some people may believe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Jamil Jaffer is the Founder and Executive Director of the National Security Institute, previously served as Associate White House Counsel to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , as well as chief counsel, senior adviser to the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Jamil, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  and the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  did remarkable reporting on this. And one of the things I wanted to first ask is, it certainly looks like the out -- from the outside, like journalists track this down faster than the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or counterintelligence did.\par \par \uc2 JAMIL JAFFER, FOUNDER AND EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, NATIONAL SECURITY INSTITUTE: It is kind of astounding. You know, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reported that the young man that they talked to that on tape there that you just showed hadn`t even been contacted by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  yet. Now, obviously, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was able to quickly pick it up and find this 21-year-old airman there in Massachusetts, but obviously troubling that the Post in the Times had deeper reporting on it than apparently the \uc2 FBI\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and the Times, we should say, the Times were the ones and the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  did remarkable reporting the next day. The Times today identifies -- you know, identifies the individual. And then he`s arrested later that day. The second big question is, OK, this is a 21-year-old airman. How exactly does he have access to the documents he has? And what does that say about the entire classification system that we have?\par \par \uc2 JAFFER: Well, you know, Chris, I mean, he had access to Top Secret Sensitive Compartmented Information in part because he had a single scope background investigation that he needed to have for his job as a cyber support journeyman, right? He was working on phone systems and telecommunications systems for computer data. So, it`s not surprising he had that level of clearance. And once you have that access, you can access quite a bit of information, perhaps he shouldn`t have been able to access everything that he had, but a lot of that information is available to people who have that level of clearance.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and I guess it`s not that. I mean, \uc2 Chelsea Manning\uc2 , obviously, was just a private when Chelsea leaked that information. Snowden was a contractor or those sort of further up. So, there always is, of course, this balance, right, in any sort of system. And it`s an enormous one inside the government national security apparatus between sharing information and compartmentalizing it. And it does really seem like we`re overdue for some kind of like, holistic review of what we`re doing here.\par \par \uc2 JAFFER: There`s no doubt that`s probably right, Chris. You know, one of the things is after 9/11, we really started to share a lot more information. We`ve always had a need-to-know system, but at some level, we want to move to a need-to-share to ensure that people didn`t lose information on the process and weren`t able to identify things like the 9/11 hijackers.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, in some ways, that`s -- that was the story of 9/11 and one of the big things that comes out of the report. And you might say the cost of that, right, is a tradeoff, right? If you`re going to make things more accessible for that particular reason, you`re going to have this kind of thing happen as -- or at least be a risk of what happens on the other end.\par \par \uc2 JAFFER: I think that`s exactly right, Chris. One of the challenges here, of course, is what we see is that he`s sharing it not because he`s ideological affiliated with some other nation-state or because he wants money. It`s primarily about ego and about getting his own ego stroked by the folks of this group. Now, that happens a lot. You know, people disclose to the media for the same reasons but this was different because he was doing with the small select group, people he knew didn`t have clearances. But what`s even worse, Chris, is where he knew there were foreigners involved. That`s the real shame here and the real problem with what Jack Teixeira did.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Wait, say more about -- just so -- just so -- if folks haven`t read about this, it`s sort of remarkable where the motive here according the folks who were in the small Discord channel with him, were a gamer, some much younger than him, like teenagers, it basically was for cloud. It was like, hey, check out this cool stuff I have access to. And when people didn`t pay enough attention, he leaked more. And it wasn`t ideologically motivated. He wasn`t sending it to newspapers to cover it. He wanted to kind of show off to his -- to his buddies.\par \par \uc2 What is -- when does that -- obviously, it`s wrong in terms of the oath he took, but what do you -- what did you say about the foreign aspect of it?\par \par \uc2 JAFFER: Well, you know, in that group that he was in, the group of 25 or so folks, he knew that there were Russian nationals and Ukrainian nationals in that group. It wasn`t just Americans in that 25-person Discord group that he was helping leading and using these classified documents to gain leadership and gain authority.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it really is -- it strikes me as a very 2023 story of someone leaking -- a 21-year-old leaking highly sensitive documents of the war in Ukraine for clout among, you know, like his acolytes in a Discord gaming channel. Jamil Jaffer, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JAFFER: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230414ej4d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for April 12, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell \par \uc2 7,043 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:14]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 And I was just listening to Senator Warnock on your show talking about the 27-year-olds in Tennessee, in the Tennessee House of Representatives who have jumped into our present day and we believe into our history in a way that we haven`t seen as he pointed out since \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  was 27 years old, when he came to prominence as a civil rights leader.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: It is a terrib -- what feels like a terrible moment for the country, but out of it, there are these absolute like lightning sparks of hope in the form of those three lawmakers who have been so unbelievably strong and principled and just brilliantly voiced the struggle, if you will, and it`s a reason for hope in the darkness I guess.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, one of those sparks of hope, Representative Justin Jones, is going to join us tonight. So, very glad to have Reverend Warnock kind of leading into that.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Yes, what a -- what a ticket, what a billing tonight, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. Thank you, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: See you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 On this day, this very day, 60 years ago, the Reverend Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. was arrested, along with 55 others, and was jailed in Birmingham, Alabama. The notorious segregationist Bull Connor ordered the arrest of Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  on April 12, 1963 for, quote, parading without a permit. That was during an protest against segregation in Birmingham.\par \par \uc2 And Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  was sentenced to a Birmingham jail. After the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  paid his $160,000 bail, Dr. King was released from that jail. Eight days later, on April 20th, while in that jail, Dr. King was in a dark cell alone with no mattress and was denied any phone calls, he was smuggled a Birmingham newspaper that had published a letter by eight White local clergy men in response to civil rights protests in Alabama.\par \par \uc2 In that letter, the White clergy men wrote that they, quote, strongly urge our own Negro community to withdraw support from these demonstrations and to unite locally and working peacefully for a better Birmingham. When rights are consistently denied, a cause should be pressed in the courts and in negotiations among local leaders and not in the streets.\par \par \uc2 Dr. King responded with a 21-page handwritten letter written in the darkness of that cell which was soon read around the world and has been read ever since, having become known simply as "The Letter from Birmingham City Jail". It is the signal most widely read letter in the history of letter writing.\par \par \uc2 Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  wrote: We know through painful experience that freedom is never voluntarily given by the oppressor. It must be demanded by the oppressed. Frankly, I have yet to engage in a direct action movement that was "well timed," according to the timetable of those who have not suffered unduly from the disease of segregation.\par \par \uc2 For years now, I have heard the word "Wait". It rings in the ear of every Negro with a piercing familiarity. This weight has almost always meant never.\par \par \uc2 We must come to see with the distinguished jurists of yesterday that justice too long delayed is justice denied.\par \par \uc2 There comes a time when the cup of endurance runs over, and men are no longer willing to be plunged into an abyss of injustice where they experience the bleakness of corroding despair.\par \par \uc2 I submit that an individual who breaks the law that conscience tells him is unjust and willingly accepts the penalty by staying in jail to arouse the conscience of the community over its injustice is in reality expressing the very highest respect for law.\par \par \uc2 Today, in Tennessee, the Tennessee Three invoked the legacy of Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. when they spoke in Memphis, outside the Lorraine Motel, where Dr. King was assassinated in 1968. It is now a museum.\par \par \uc2 They began last week as three members of the Tennessee House of Representatives subjected to an expulsion vote by the Republican majority in the House. The week ended with Representative Gloria Johnson surviving the vote to expel her. Between the votes that did actually expel our first guest tonight, Representative Justin Jones, and then Representative Justin J. Pearson, whose Memphis district includes the Lorraine Motel.\par \par \uc2 Representative Justin Jones has already been reinstated as a member of the House of Representatives after local officials in his district voted for Justin Jones to fill the vacancy in the House of Representatives created by Justin Jones` expulsion. The same thing happened today in Memphis with Representative Pearson, but he will not be sworn in as a member of the House until tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. JUSTIN PEARSON (D), TENNESSEE: The movement lives or dies in Memphis and here at the hallowed place, and this sacred place, we show in the United States of America and the Republicans in Tennessee, that the movement is still alive!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. GLORIA JOHNSON (D), TENNESSEE: We need to lift up these amazing voices of these young people --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 A multiracial, multigenerational organization in the Tennessee legislature.\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. JUSTIN JONES (D), TENNESSEE: Something`s changing in Tennessee, something changing in our state that is going to restore democracy and get rid of these forces that are trying to take us backwards.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Something is changing.\par \par \uc2 After the local vote by officials in Memphis to reinstate Representative Justin Pearson, he said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PEARSON\uc2 : We do not speak alone. We speak together. We fight together.\par \par \uc2 But, still, the message to all of the people in Nashville who decided to expel us -- you can`t expel hope.\par \par \uc2 Now that the Shelby County Commission has done their job, I`m so glad we get to get back to doing our job, and what is our job is to elevate the voices of the six kids -- the six people in Nashville, the three children who were just nine years old.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In an op-ed piece in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " today titled: Defiant and determined, I`m ready to keep fighting for Tennessee," Representative Pearson wrote: It`s not just our individual voices that were sanctioned and silenced last Thursday. It was the voices of the 135,000 nearly Tennesseans, we were -- we represented, many desperate for protection from the absence of many common sense gun safety laws in our state.\par \par \uc2 Since the Covenant School shooting, the Republican supermajority in the state house has done little but advance a bill that would allow teachers to carry guns in school and propose a $140 million budget increase to pay for the presence of armed guards in public schools, further militarizing them without adequate evidence that this makes schools safer.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Tennessee State Representative Justin Jones.\par \par \uc2 Representative Jones, thank you very much for joining us on what is once again an historic night with the vote to restore Justin Pearson to the House, which presumably will happen tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Tell us about what it felt like to be in front of the Lorraine Motel today, in pressing your case.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, thank you so much, Lawrence, for having me on and thank you for reading that letter from a Birmingham jail. I actually read that letter to prepare for my expulsion trial on last Thursday.\par \par \uc2 But being outside of Lorraine Motel I think was a very sacred and important place to be. It was a place where a great prophet of America was slain. It was a place of crucifixion, and we went there today as a testament to resurrection. The resurrection of a reconstruction here in the South, the resurrection of multi-racial democracy, the resurrection of people power over the power of authoritarianism.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:03]\par \par \uc2 And so, it was very important to be in Memphis because this movement that we`re a part of, calling for common sense gun laws, calling for democracy is from Memphis to Nashville to Knoxville, that`s the areas of Tennessee Three that we represent. And it was very powerful to stand together united, to say that we`re going to welcome our brother back into the people`s house on tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Talk to us about reading Dr. King`s letter from jail as you were approaching that expulsion vote in the House. What did you find in the letter as you were approaching that moment?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Dr. King wrote, you deploy the demonstrations that are happening in Birmingham but you have that expressed similar concern for the conditions that brought the protest into being in the first place. And as I thought about being expelled, I thought about that they were trying to expel us for a protest, but we weren`t talking about the conditions that brought us to the well to protest in the first place. That was the conditions of weapons of war slaying our children and terrorizing our schools and terrorizing our communities. The conditions that would make it so that the \uc2 NRA\uc2  has ultimate voice and not the people of Tennessee. The conditions in which we worship guns at the expense of our children.\par \par \uc2 In that -- in that letter, Dr. King talks about creative tension. They say, you know, you create attention, you weren`t acting peacefully. And while we were non-violent, we were creating tension because we were trying to force a conversation, because we had heard "wait", like Dr. King says but there comes a time where weight becomes never, or wait becomes, you know, delaying justice, delaying conversation. And that`s what we`ve seen time and time again.\par \par \uc2 You know, the truth be told the shooting at \uc2 Covenant\uc2  was not the first mass shooting in Nashville. We had a mass shooting in a part of the area where I represented, Antioch, at the \uc2 Waffle House\uc2  a few years ago, and if we don`t act now, there will be another mass shooting. And so, that`s why we said "no action, no peace", but reading that letter, I think it`s sacred text. I think every American should read it.\par \par \uc2 I`m in Tennessee, they`re trying to ban that type of reading from happening. But it`s very important for us to know the lineage of liberation of which we are a part.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, it`s been years since I read it, I reread it today. And it really bears rereading every year on this day or as often as possible.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Bill Lee\uc2  of your state has now issued an executive order, clearly in response to your actions. I`m not sure this would have happened without you, and this executive order as I read it and I want you to clarify it for me -- requires within hours for certain information to be entered into the Tennessee instant background check system. That is presumably legal developments, possibly arrests or information like that.\par \par \uc2 What -- what is it? How -- what does this executive order do?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, this executive order and I actually met with the governor yesterday, along with other members of the national delegation and sitting right next to him, and when he explained this. And the only issue with the executive order is that -- is that it expires when the governor leaves.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, we want long lasting legislation. And so, this is, you know, he`s talking about some background checks. He`s starting to ask us, in the legislature, to push red flag law, something that Democrats have been fighting for for years.\par \par \uc2 But we know it`s not enough, and that`s what I told the governor yesterday when we met with him. I was sitting across from him, I said, Governor, this is going to take political courage and political sacrifice. You know, we know he`s endorsed by the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , the Tennessee Firearms Association, these very fringe extreme groups, but it`s going to take him putting the lives of children in our state, the lives of our community members over the campaign donations of the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and the Tennessee Firearms Association.\par \par \uc2 I really hope and I believe that the governor wants to be a good faith partner, but I know there are extreme forces even within this body, like Speaker -- House Speaker Cameron Sexton -- who are trying to obstruct any type of meaningful legislation. Their solution is to militarize our schools, to have more police, to have more locks. One member said, if we have to put a tank outside the schools in Tennessee, we`ll do that.\par \par \uc2 But who wants to live in a society like that? Dr. King says, I want a society that is at peace with itself. That`s what we`re fighting for, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 And so, the governor`s executive order is a step forward but it`s not enough. We need universal background checks. We need -- we need a ban on assault weapons like AR-15s and we need safe storage laws. There`s so much more that we can do and I hope that the governor will step into this moment of courage and conscience and act for the people of our state.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The speaker of the house who we saw conducting your expulsion votes has gotten a lot more attention than I think he wanted to by doing this, including attention to the legal question of, does he actually live in the district that he claims to represent in the legislature? That`s usually not a question for a speaker of the house, but that`s being studied in a way that it hasn`t been studied before.\par \par \uc2 Do the Republicans understand what a huge mistake it was to follow the speaker of that house down this road they went down in which a week later, you`re both back in office as members of the House?\par \par \uc2 [22:15:02]\par \par \uc2 JONES: I mean, I see a lot of conversations and hear a lot of conversations happening here. I`m here at the capitol right now, where I think a lot of Republicans are even starting to see if the speaker needs to resign. Speaker Cameron Sexton tried to flex this extremist authoritarian abuse of power and it`s backfired. You know, Newton`s third law says that for every action, there`s an equal and opposite reaction.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we`re seeing here is that what he planned as -- for our demise, if I go to Scripture, what you meant for evil is being used for good, to shine a light here in this capitol. And so I think it`s very important that the speaker do some self-reflection.\par \par \uc2 I`ve called for his resignation. I think that he is not fit to serve in our state. He`s an enemy of democracy. He does not live in his district. I heard that when I went to Crossville to campaign and knock doors or talk to people in his district.\par \par \uc2 And Cameron Sexton represents a darker day in Tennessee history, a history which was not about multi-racial democracy, where people like me were not even able to walk into the legislature and serve as equals in this branch of government, as equals at the dais in the House chamber.\par \par \uc2 And so, I hope that Cameron Sexton will do the honorable thing and that is to resign and to bring some order and decency and integrity back to the House. I don`t think that his own members are even comfortable with him serving, at least a few I`ve talked to. And so, I hope that we see better days ahead.\par \par \uc2 But no matter what, I know that power concedes nothing without a demand, and we must continue to push, and that`s going to be these young people who restore the heart and soul of our democracy here in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let`s listen to what Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  said about you and Representative Pearson in the last hour with Alex Wagner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): These are young people still in their 20s, and, you know, I remember that there was another young preacher named \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 . who stood up in Montgomery. He was 27 years old, the age of these young legislators and the rest is history.\par \par \uc2 So you never know the difference that your voice will make, but I`m glad that they decided to stand up and buy some stroke of grace and human resilience. They have captured the imagination of the whole country, and I hope pushed us closer to finally getting some common sense gun safety laws passed into Congress, and the kind of movement we need in order to safeguard our democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What is your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Wow. Senator Warnock, I talked to him earlier today. I mean, that is just -- it means a lot, for someone who stands -- he stands in the tradition of Dr. King and he knows, you know, what it means to be in this work.\par \par \uc2 And so to hear that, it`s just a reminder that we are, you know, on the right side of history. That this happened by accident. You know, no one wants to be expelled and wanted to be put, you know, in this platform. But we know that there`s something beyond us working in this moment, that when they attempted to crucify democracy, it`s really resurrected something that we haven`t seen in Tennessee or in the South I think for a while, and it stands in the tradition of people like here in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 John Lewis, he`s from Nashville. That`s where the student movement, Diane Nash, Bernard Lafayette and when Dr. King came to Nashville. He said, I came to Nashville not to bring inspiration, but to gain inspiration from the movement happening here.\par \par \uc2 And that movement was led by young people -- those who had the courage to sit in, that`s a way to stand up. Those who are willing to get on the freedom rides after they were bombed in Anniston.\par \par \uc2 And so, I come -- you know, I`m 27, and the youngest Black lawmaker here, and I`m just so hopeful for the days ahead. And I hope more young people will run. I hope more young people step into our power, because if we were not powerful as a generation, they would not be trying to expel us and silence us.\par \par \uc2 And so, to hear Senator Warnock say that means a lot and I hope that other young people hear that as well because it`s not just about us, it`s about the movement as a whole and I honored to be a member of that movement.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And member of the Tennessee House of Representatives, the Honorable Justin Jones -- cannot thank you enough for joining us tonight on this important night. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you so much, Lawrence. It means a lot.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, in another brilliant column for "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", Jamelle Bouie, our next guest, says that the power and isolation of the Supreme Court, quote, has made it a magnet for corruption and influence peddling. "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " columnist Jamelle Bouie joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The Supreme Court is not going to police itself. That is a statement that critics and supporters of the current Supreme Court should be able to agree on because it is a simple fact. That might be the only sentence in Jamelle Bouie`s brilliant column for "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " about \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  that supporters and critics of \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  should, in honesty, be able to agree on.\par \par \uc2 The supporters of \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  appear to believe not only that the Supreme Court is not going to police itself, but that it should not police itself, and no one should police the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Leaving the Supreme Court unpoliced for 234 years as its power grew exponentially led it to become, according to Jamelle Bouie, a magnet for corruption and influence peddling, and it has allowed \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , quote, to treat his seat on the court ostensibly a public trust, like a winning lottery ticket to redeem what the nearest friendly billionaire who happens to have a collection of Nazi paraphernalia and Hitler-related souvenirs.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:06]\par \par \uc2 Jamelle Bouie goes on to say in his "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " column: corruption is much more than a cartoonish quid pro quo where cash changes hands and the state is used for private gain. Corruption, more often than not, looks like an ordinary relationship, even a friendship. It is perks and benefits freely given to a powerful friend. It is expensive gifts and tokens of appreciation between those friends, except that one holds office and the other wants to influence its ideological course.\par \par \uc2 Whether it`s through structural change or a simple ethics code, it is up to elected officials to remind the court that it serves the republic and not the other way around.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Jamelle Bouie, columnist for "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " and co- host of the podcast "Unclear and Present Danger".\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us once again.\par \par \uc2 I learn more in every column of yours than I do in any other collection of 800 words. And today, you make a point. You make a point in this piece, where you refer to the Supreme Court, you acknowledge, you give it the tip of the hat, that it has had occasional bouts of decency.\par \par \uc2 And I find that quite striking that in -- you say in its 234 history, it`s had occasional bouts of decency, because what striking to me about it is when I was growing up, what I was learning in school and what I was learning out there in the world around me was only about the bouts of decency, beginning in 1954, with the desegregation decision in the Brown case. That`s really kind of where it began as we were being guided through the Supreme Court, and then living through an era where the Supreme Court was the leading edge of progressive and modern thought about the way we should live with Roe versus Wade and other decisions.\par \par \uc2 That all seem to be in what -- as we look back on it now -- was a kind of extended multi-decade bout of decency within a couple of hundred years that have been about something else.\par \par \uc2 JAMELLE BOUIE, COLUMNIST, THE NEW YORK TIMES: That`s right. When you look at the court`s entire history, and like you, Lawrence, when I learned about the court in grade school, right, in high school, so on and so forth, what you learned is you learned Brown v. Board. You learn the civil rights cases.\par \par \uc2 You also learn, you know, what`s called, you know, the cases that are everyone in the line. Dred Scott, Plessy v. Ferguson, you learned that stuff, too. And together, kind of a narrative you get is that, with the exceptions of these really horrible decisions, a long time ago, the Supreme Court has been a generally positive force for American life.\par \par \uc2 But when you actually kind of like zoom out and look at its entire history, the picture becomes very different. The Supreme Court, for example, throughout the 1860s, `70s and `80s, systematically dismantles the federal government`s ability to enforce the laws that passed, to enforce the constitutional amendments that were written to preserve the rights of the formerly enslaved. Completely destroys the 14th amendment until the 20th century.\par \par \uc2 There`s a way you can think of Brown v. Board as less this bout the decency, excuse me and more the court correcting its own mistake, correcting the things that had already done previously, to undermine the federal government`s ability to protect the rights of Americans. The economic rulings of the court throughout its history, we -- many people know Lochner, Lochner v. New York, which sort of sets up this regime of laissez faire in the United States.\par \par \uc2 But generally, the court has been very friendly to corporate interests, corporate interests, very friendly to moneyed interest, and very much willing to defer to those who already have power and who already have influence, who already have wealth.\par \par \uc2 And so I think it`s important when we`re thinking of the Supreme Court to as much as possible think of those years, the Warren court in the `50s and the `60s in particular as being an exception, an exception to the rule, and that the rule of the Supreme Court is to entrench the influence of already powerful minorities -- whether they are economic minorities, whether they are political minorities who happen to hold a great deal of power, but that is what the court has done traditionally.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It`s such an enlightening way to look at it because, for example, we all know that the Franklin Roosevelt presidency was an exceptional presidency in what it was able to achieve, creating Social Security, minimum wage, 40-hour workweek, ending child labor, you go on and on and on and on.\par \par \uc2 Transformational in terms of the government`s relationship to workers and to its people. And we know that the presidencies that followed that didn`t achieve anything like that.\par \par \uc2 But we were always taught the Supreme Court is different, that the Supreme Court isn`t about phases, except that ancient history, where they had some bad decisions. There is also -- there`s this line in here which you were just getting to, in effect, in your piece. I wanted to read it for the audience.\par \par \uc2 With his close ties to a powerful property owning billionaire, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  embodies the historic role of the Supreme Court in American politics, not as liberator or defender of the rights of political and social minorities, but as a partner to and ally of moneyed interests.\par \par \uc2 And so he fits in perfectly in the longer historical flow of the court.\par \par \uc2 BOUIE: That`s exactly right. Regardless of whether or not we think of \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` behavior as criminal, and some of his defenders were trying to frame it in those terms, he did nothing illegal.\par \par \uc2 Sure. It`s certainly the case that he`s doing something improper and perhaps unethical. It means hitting this extremely close relationship with this powerful billionaire and accepting gifts and trips and such from him. And entangling himself and enmeshing himself in this relationship.\par \par \uc2 That is maybe not the behavior is typical but I kind of larger relationship between social forces in the country, right. The Supreme Court as it relate to American big business, the Supreme Court as it relates to the largest property owners, the Supreme Court as it relate to the most powerful political interests in the country usually connected with great sums of wealth and power, wealth and money.\par \par \uc2 That`s a very typical relationship. The court has traditionally been enmeshed with those interests, with those groups. So Thomas is really embodying this. And I think it`s important again to not treat it as exceptional.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I hope this is going to get the audience to go straight to NewYorkTimes.com and read this article. It is full of more of what we`ve been able to squeeze into these few minutes here.\par \par \uc2 Jamelle Bouie, thank you very much for joining us once again and enlightening us once again.\par \par \uc2 BOUIE: Thank you so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Senate Democrats got the news they have been hoping for today when another Democratic senator in a very important swing state, difficult state for Democrats to win, announced that she will run for reelection. Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  of the importance swing state of Wisconsin will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:32:53]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: At any moment now the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals, federal court of appeals, could decide whether to block a Trump-appointed federal judge`s ruling revoking the FDA`s 23 year approval of an abortion pill. In a filing overnight the groups at the center of the lawsuit claimed that no harm would result in upholding the judges ruling. They say the order merely removes the drug from the malls and the market. Abortion will still be available in states that permit it.\par \par \uc2 Hours later, the Justice Department responded to that filing, saying it is a safe and effective drug and, quote, "If allowed to take effect, the court`s order will cause irreparable harm across the country. Preserving abortion rights in Wisconsin played a very large role in last week`s election of Janet Protasiewicz to the Wisconsin state Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats have been anxiously awaiting Wisconsin Democratic Senator Tammy Baldwin`s decision about running for reelection in that swing state.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is Democratic Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  of Wisconsin. Senator Baldwin, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Have you thought about you future?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TAMMY BALDWIN\uc2  (D-WI): In fact, I have. And today I have announced that I am running for reelection in order to fight for working families across Wisconsin. We launched our campaign, as you mentioned, just a week after an extraordinary election in the state of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 And as I traveled the state listening to voters talking about kitchen table issues, I can tell you that I wanted to continue the fight for good paying jobs, for affordable health care, for lower prescription drug costs.\par \par \uc2 And I want to fight so that Wisconsinites can regain the rights and freedoms that their mothers and grandmothers and fathers and grandfathers had and that includes the right to control your health care, your reproductive rights, and abortion care.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:54]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I`d like to listen to what Vice President Harris said today about this appeal that`s pending on the abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 Let`s listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And I would ask every person who is aware of this to understand the implications of this ruling by just opening your medicine cabinet. Because it is very likely that you rely on some type of medication prescribed by a doctor, approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , to alleviate your health concerns and to improve your condition in life. So the ramifications of this decision five days ago are wide- sweeping.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator, it seems now that according to this Republican judge in Texas that we cannot rely on being able to obtain \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved drugs, that a judge like him can just take them off the shelves with the stroke of a pen.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: You know, certainly that is a concern but I just want to say about that case decided last week, there was no science involved in that case. This was another example of a judge in this case and politicians, the extreme Republicans who really want to make sure that there is no access to reproductive care and abortion care in the United States.\par \par \uc2 They are looking at a nationwide ban and this is part of that overall effort. But in Wisconsin our voters overwhelmingly said last week that we want our rights and freedoms back. We do not want judges and politicians deciding how we access health care and whether or when we start a family.\par \par \uc2 And this is of such significance and the battle is not over in Wisconsin. Let me remind your viewers that in Wisconsin right now we are subject to a law that was passed in 1849 -- a criminal abortion ban with no exception for rape, incest, or the health of the mother.\par \par \uc2 And we hope that we will see that statute at some point overturned but I can tell you that your access to reproductive health care should not depend on what zip code you live in or what state you reside in.\par \par \uc2 That is why we have to enact the measure that I championed, the Women`s Health Protection Act in Congress to restore Roe as the law of the land and to stop states like Wisconsin and Texas and so many others from interfering with that right.\par \par \uc2 But it`s going to be a tough race for me to win and so hopefully we`ll talk about what we can do to make sure this fight carries on .\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , welcome to the campaign trail, and please return as often as you can during the campaign. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Thank you. Well, as I said, I have a tough race so go to TammyBaldwin.com and give me a good start.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you very much Senator. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, what is the difference between a Protestant and a Catholic? That question came up today when President Biden visited Northern Ireland, where the answer to that question is not so much a matter of religion as it is a matter of history.\par \par \uc2 And for that and more about the president`s trip to Ireland we will turn to this program`s highest authority on such matters. Fintan O`Toole, columnist for the "Irish Times" joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:43:36]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In a sense, I know why my ancestors and many of your relatives left during the famine and -- but you know, when you`re here, you wonder why anyone would ever want to leave.\par \par \uc2 And I wish to wish our mom Catherine Eugenia Finnegan Biden were here today, she`d be so damn proud. You know, she (INAUDIBLE) -- held such a special place in her heart, and it really did. And today, we carry her home in our heart, for real. We think about her all the time and coming here feels like coming home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That was President Biden today, on his first day in Ireland. He began the day with a speech at \uc2 Ulster University\uc2  in Belfast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`s good to see Belfast, a city that`s alive with commerce, art, and I`d argue inspiration. The dividends of peace are all around us. This very campus is situated in the intersection of where conflict and bloodshed once held terrible sway.\par \par \uc2 The idea, as I said to have a glass building here when I was here in 1991 was highly unlikely. Where barbed wire once sliced up the city, today we find a cathedral of learning, a globe (ph) of glass, and let the shine light in and out.\par \par \uc2 It just has a profound impact. And for someone who has come back to see it, you know, it`s incredible testament to the power and the possibilities of peace.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The president was in Belfast, Northern Ireland, to celebrate the 25th anniversary of a peace agreement known as the Good Friday Agreement, named for the day it was signed 25 years ago after diplomatic intervention by the United States helped bring the two sides together.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:57]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The two sides in those days were described in news accounts of that violent period by the difference in their religions.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden remarked about how much has changed in Northern Ireland in the 25 years of peace.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Young people, like Amy Clint, born in 2000, whose parents like to tell a story about how she came home from her first day of secondary school and asked what`s the difference between a Protestant and a Catholic? What`s the difference between Protestant and a Catholic? She didn`t grow up thinking in sectarian divide.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now, Fintan O`Toole, columnist and writer for the "Irish Times". He is the author of the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestselling book "We Don`t Know Ourselves: A Personal History of Modern Ireland".\par \par \uc2 And Fintan O`Toole, for us it`s pretty surprising that a kid could now grow up in Belfast and get all the way to high school before wondering what`s the difference between a Protestant and a Catholic.\par \par \uc2 And when I heard that question today I thought, oh, there is a question, there is a question for O`Toole tonight. Let`s see if he can explained that one to America.\par \par \uc2 FINTAN O`TOOLE, COLUMNIST, "IRISH TIMES": Well, you know, Lawrence, it is wonderful to think that that might be a question. Because of course, for so long these identities were just so tribally opposed, that each side thought of itself as being not the other you know.\par \par \uc2 They did not really think about who they were themselves, they thought about, you know we`re not those guys. And that`s a terrible way to think about yourself.\par \par \uc2 The difference, of course, and you hinted at this earlier, really is this fusion of religion and politics, right. It`s never really about religion as such but you know very interesting talking, watching you talking to the senator just before the break there, you know.\par \par \uc2 And Americans also experiencing in many ways the attempts to fuse a religious identity with a political identity. And you know, we know in Ireland just how toxic that becomes and I think in a way, in his own pretty gentle way today, that`s part of what President Biden was reflecting on.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You make the point about the peace that was finally achieve in Belfast which, I have to say, for me in the 1970s and the 1980s, it was unimaginable that it could get to that negotiated spot that it got to.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it took, you know, 30 years or more to do it. But most of those years it seemed unimaginable. And you say that final achievement showed that politics can work, that we are not doomed to tribalism, that history is not faith, and that self determination can mean not getting everything you want, but consciously choosing generosity, compromise, and reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 And I would have thought, and did think at the time, that if that`s possible in Northern Ireland, it`s possible anywhere.\par \par \uc2 O`TOOLE: I think that`s a really good point. And that`s maybe why sometimes these anniversaries are actually very important, you know. Because they do remind us, the take us back to, well, wasn`t it a great achievement, and an achievement, which for all its problems, it`s not perfect. But it has lasted. And has made it possible for a whole generation of young people in Northern Ireland not to have experienced violence.\par \par \uc2 And we`re in a period when so many people feel very cynical about politics, very disillusioned about politics and politicians. You know, this was done by politicians. You know, it was done by really skillful, courageous working of the political system and critically, as well by American politics.\par \par \uc2 You know, people may have very mixed feelings about \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  now but you know, his contribution to this was huge, And of course, Senator George Mitchell from Maine, selflessly, and you know, I think the man should be remembered as a saint. He was the one who went through that incredibly tedious process of getting everybody to an agreement.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re Irish, you know, you still feel enormous gratitude to politicians from another country, friendly politicians from another country, who really helped us to be able to live in a much better place and look forward to a much better future.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It was striking that the United States, and I`m not sure the United States still has the status in many places, but in those days, was fully respected by both sides of that conflict. And so Senator Mitchell could actually leave the senate and then go in there as a special envoy. He described it, I believe once, as 700 days of failure followed by one day of success.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:51]\par \par \uc2 O`TOOLE: Yes, you know, and this is where, as you say, America`s standing at that time as an honest broker, was accepted by both sides but also something that maybe, you know, we`re losing which is a sense of American optimism you know.\par \par \uc2 There was still that epic, you know, that fundamental belief in politics in America which transferred itself, you know, into other parts of the world, to say that this can be done, you know, as you are quoting there.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`re not doomed to just continually repeating over and over again the same mistakes. And I think if it wasn`t -- if we didn`t have that sort of sense of American optimism that can-do attitude, it`s possible this conflict could still be going on today.\par \par \uc2 And maybe if Ireland has one thing to give back to America now it might be to say, you know, perhaps you guys need to recover that sense of political sensibility for yourself.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Fintan O`Toole, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 O`TOOLE: Pleasure. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We`ll be right back\par \par \uc2 [22:55:55]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Fintan O`Toole gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230413ej4c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O'DONNELL for April 11, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O'Donnell, Andrew Weissmann \par \uc2 6,847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:21]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O'DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Alex, don't make me try to reimagine my childhood without our local branch of the Boston Public Library on Adams Street in Dorchester. The Adams Street branch I spent so many great hours.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": Me, too. My grandmother was a librarian books are important. Reading is a skill that you want young children and all children to have. I mean, literally, the dictionary definition of cutting off your nose to spite your face.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: But like so many public rights now in America, depends on what state you live in. So --\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: -- there's no threatening of libraries in Massachusetts, you know, where I grew up. And there's also no threatening of abortion rights in Massachusetts. We're going to have Massachusetts new attorney general join us tonight to talk about this, the Massachusetts plan to continue -- continue to deliver abortion medication no matter what a federal judge in Texas says.\par \par \uc2 This is a fascinating plan they have in Massachusetts. We're going to see how that's going to work.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Fascinating and important, especially at this hour. I will be watching, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Thanks. Thank you, Alex. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the breaking legal news of the day is Manhattan District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  taking legal action against the Republican chairman of the House Judiciary Committee Jim Jordan by suing Jim Jordan in federal court for a, quote, unprecedentedly brazen and unconstitutional attack. That's what he called it, on District Attorney Bragg and his investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Also today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did his first television interview since becoming a criminal defendant. We will show you a portion of that interview with \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  in a moment, a small portion. But, first, this reminder -- \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  hates \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  passionately and he put it in writing and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows that. On January 4th, 2021, two days before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sent a violent mob to attack the Capitol, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  texted a co-worker saying we are very, very close to being able to avoid Trump most nights. I hate him passionately.\par \par \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  lives in fear of his television audience and his audience loves \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  has been doing everything he possibly can since that text became public a few weeks ago and the Dominion voting systems lawsuit against Fox to convince his viewers that he really, really does love \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows that \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  hates him passionately because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  read that text, but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants access to \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 's audience. And so, two deeply hateful men filled with hatred faced each other today in an interview in which they were both desperately trying to stay in favor with \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 's audience.\par \par \uc2 Here then is 30 seconds and only 30 seconds of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lying about what happened when he was arrested and arraigned last week in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Last week here in New York, for this arraignment, the world watched it, you've not given an interview since. You were there I think 57 minutes. Tell us from your perspective what that was like?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: They were incredible. When I went to the courthouse, which is also a prison in a sense, they signed me in. And I'll tell you -- people were crying, people that worked there, professionally worked there, that have no problems putting in murderers and they see everybody. It's tough, tough place, and they were crying. They were actually crying. They said, I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: The only person in the courthouse who looked close to tears that day was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and even the emotionally unstable \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  managed to only cry on the inside. Courthouse workers should get used to this. If he's arraigned next month in Atlanta, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will surely tell the lie that courthouse workers were crying, and he'll say the same thing if special federal prosecutor Jack Smith brings \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in for arraignment on the documents case and/or the January 6th case, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is arraigned four times -- four times in courthouses around America, people working on those courthouses will be accused of crying by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:05:20]\par \par \uc2 And no one will see them cry, except \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  who hates \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  passionately will beg \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to give his first television interview after his latest arraignment to \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  every time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is arraigned. And Tucker and Donald will sit across from each other each time, each of them filled with hatred, each of them in his own way desperately trying to get the approval of Tucker Carlson's audience.\par \par \uc2 District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's lawsuit against Jim Jordan is asking the federal court in Manhattan to block a subpoena from Jim Jordan to Mark Pomerantz, a former special assistant district attorney who participated in \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Trump businesses.\par \par \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's lawsuit tells the federal court: Chairman Jordan's demands, including his subpoena to Mr. Pomerantz, seek highly sensitive and confidential local prosecutorial information that belongs to the office of the district attorney and the people of New York. Basic principles of federalism and common sense as well as binding Supreme Court precedent forbid Congress from demanding it. Congress has no power to supervise state criminal prosecutions, nor does Congress have the power to serve subpoenas for the personal aggrandizement of the investigators or to punish those investigated.\par \par \uc2 Subpoenaing a former line prosecutor to talk about an ongoing criminal prosecution an investigation is an affront to state sovereignty, then -- as much as subpoenaing the district attorney himself. Chairman Jordan claims he is seeking to conduct oversight but he has no power under the Constitution to oversee state and local criminal matters. By definition then, he has no legitimate legislative purpose for issuing the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 The subpoena threatens the sovereign powers of the states, confidence in the secrecy of grand jury proceedings and the integrity of an ongoing criminal prosecution. The court should enjoin its enforcement.\par \par \uc2 The court set a deadline of next Wednesday for Jim Jordan to reply to \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's civil complaint against him.\par \par \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 's 50-page legal filing against Jim Jordan says that Jim Jordan is in effect colluding with criminal defendant Trump to interfere with the prosecution of criminal defendant Trump. Quote: Mr. Trump in particular has threatened New York officials with violent and racist vitriol on social media. He threatened death and destruction and to wage war if he was indicted. Mr. Trump even shared a social media post that appeared to be a picture of himself threateningly wielding a baseball bat to District Attorney Bragg's head.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Jim Jordan announced that his house committee would hold a hearing, a field hearing in a federal building in Manhattan on Monday to evaluate the job \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  is doing as to attorney of Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Dan Goldman represents the congressional district where the hearing will be held in the Javits Federal Building, close to the criminal courthouse where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was arraigned in Lower Manhattan, Congressman Goldman issued a statement saying: Instead of focusing on improving the lives of the American people, Jim Jordan has decided to come to my district at the behest of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to continue to weaponize Congress to obstruct an ongoing, non-federal criminal prosecution. If Jordan truly cared about public safety, he'd be having a field hearing in Nashville, Tennessee, or Louisville, Kentucky, where the most recent of the daily mass shootings have killed more innocent Americans. Chairman Jordan is not welcome in my district for this political stunt that is simply a further waste of taxpayer money to support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's legal defense.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is Congressman Daniel Goldman of New York. He served as a House majority counsel during the first impeachment trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and represents the district where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's hush money case is currently being heard.\par \par \uc2 Also with us is Andrew Weissmann, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  general counsel and former chief of the criminal division in the Eastern District of New York.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:00]\par \par \uc2 He's professor of practice at NYU law school and MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Goldman, first, to this so-called field hearing that's going to be scheduled for the Javits Federal Building on Monday, is that open to the public, that hearing?\par \par \uc2 REP. DANIEL GOLDMAN (D-NY): I don't know the answer to that. I don't know whether it will be televised or open to the public. I assume both. Certainly, the chairman is doing this purely as a political stunt and I would imagine he would like as much attention on it for that reason as possible.\par \par \uc2 But it is purely just that, Lawrence, and I think that the District Attorney Bragg's lawsuit today put a real cherry on the top of some of these arguments that I've also been making which is that Jim Jordan is acting at the behest of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and this lawsuit will be a mechanism through which the District Attorney Bragg can get information and documents and material about how much collusion and coordination there has been between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , his legal team and Jim Jordan and other House Republicans, in a way that they would not have to turn that over otherwise.\par \par \uc2 And if it is the case that they did as it is reported initiate this investigation the day after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's attorney asked them to intervene in this ongoing criminal matter, then their argument that this is a legitimate oversight investigation falls on its head because it is not legitimate if they are doing it at the direction of a criminal defendant who's a private citizen.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: So I want to double underline this point because this is something I hadn't yet thought about today which is always the case in civil litigation. One of the reasons you want to stay out of it is that each side has equal discovery powers legally. And so, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  in this lawsuit is empowered to subpoena material from Jim Jordan and to see his email records uh in developing the rationale for this hearing.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: That's right. He's -- he has a discovery right to get information and documents from the other party that would be pertinent to the claim, and if Jim Jordan and the judiciary committee is arguing that this is legitimate oversight, that their intervention in a state-level prosecution is somehow legit -- within the jurisdiction of Congress, well, then, the district attorney has the right to figure out whether that's the case. And something that's seems pretty dispositive is whether this investigation was initiated at the direction and behest of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or not, because if it is, then it is not for any legitimate oversight purpose and we've already seen shifting explanations from Jim Jordan as to what the basis is for this investigation and that to me would be dispositive that it has no legitimate legislative purpose, which is the legal standard established in the Mazars case, which is actually a case that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brought all the way up to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann, I know you spent a lot of time around courtrooms and I'm not sure how often you've seen courthouse workers crying for defendants in white collar crime cases. The only thing we have on the video are the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers letting the door slammed on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as they pass through the door in front of him. They won't even hold the door for him. We don't see any tears in that video.\par \par \uc2 But I guess we're going to have to get used to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  describing courthouse workers all over the country if he's indicted all over the country as he might be in that kind of crying for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because he's so unfairly indicted.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So I think Dan who spent years as a federal prosecutor also, I think he and I have the same experience. That's not common. The people who are crying in court are, you know, at times the defendant, certainly the defendant's family, for understandable reasons.\par \par \uc2 Court officers and police officers are trained -- they just keep that all inside whatever they're thinking. I can't say that I think that they have a ton of sympathy for people who are in charge. They know that that the people who are charged are the vast, vast, vast majority are guilty, and they are just doing the job to make sure that due process is upheld.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:05]\par \par \uc2 But, you know, we are as you know going to have to listen to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  say this over and over again and it's going to be in circumstances where it can't be checked, no surprise there.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: The other -- the other --\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: It's also pathetic.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: The other legal news \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made in that interview which I think we all saw coming was he said that if he's convicted of a crime, he still will not drop out of the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Yeah. I mean, I -- it'll -- it'll be interesting because as, you know, he could end up being charged in four separate cases to federal and to state. I do think that there is an issue about timing, whether any of those cases will actually come to fruition uh before the election.\par \par \uc2 The D.A. Bragg is trying very hard to have the trial in January, so he is seeking that. And I have to say, D.A. Bragg is showing a real fearlessness in terms of how he's handling this and I think strategically as Dan pointed out is doing exactly the right thing, which is getting into court and saying, here's the proof we have.\par \par \uc2 I agree with Dan that he'd be entitled to more, and it puts Jordan in a position where he actually has to put up or shut up. I mean, he has to come up with some actual -- basically valid reason that he would be seeking oversight, and in a court of law. And, you know, this is with a federal judge who's not going to put up with a lot of nonsense.\par \par \uc2 You know, in -- during the special counsel Mueller investigation, the federal judges there used to say, you know, what's great about the federal court is this is where facts and law matter. So what Jim Jordan does publicly and that and what Dan has been calling about to do is not going to play in a court of law.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: You know, Dan Goldman, I'm thinking back to my first interaction with Congress was as a witness to this very committee, to the House Judiciary Committee in the 1980s, I had just written my first book about police use of deadly force. There was very little information about it in the country.\par \par \uc2 And so, I was one of the very few people who -- it was a field hearing by the House Judiciary Committee in Manhattan, in northern Manhattan, in Charlie Rangel's district and it was filled -- filled with people from the community wide open access to that hearing.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Yeah, I didn't even realize that a field hearing had had been held in Manhattan. But I do hope there will be access and I think what you will see from uh the Democrats, Lawrence, is an emphasis that you've seen in some of the reactions that we have had, which is let's -- let's focus on things that matter, not this sort of trivial political game that the Republicans are playing to try to curry favor with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or to try to gain favor in term -- electoral leave in advance of the next election.\par \par \uc2 Let's focus on you -- crime. You want to focus on crime, let's focus on crime. Let's focus on gun crime and the scourge of mass shootings that is going on around the country, yet another one this week, with assault weapons that have no place in this country, in any non-military person's hands. So we're going to be emphasizing that the real issue in public safety around this country is this epidemic of mass shootings, gun violence assault weapons, the failure to enact meaningful background checks.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about crim -- let's talk about public safety. That's what we're going to be talking about.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Congressman Daniel Goldman and Andrew Weissmann, thank you both very much for starting off our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: And to listen to the newest episode of Andrew Weissmann's podcast "Prosecuting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 " just scanned this QR code.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, once again, just as it was during the segregation period in this country, your rights depend on which state you live in. And the governor and attorney general of Massachusetts have a plan for that state to be able to provide abortion medication no matter what a Trump appointed federal judge says. Massachusetts Attorney General \uc2 Andrea Campbell\uc2  will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:24:16]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA CAMPBELL (D), MASSACHUSETTS ATTORNEY GENERAL: This judge ignored the decades of research showing that medication abortion, including mifepristone, is safe and effective, and decided that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  got it wrong because that's what he wanted to do. His decision was based on ideology not science and certainly not the law.\par \par \uc2 And as attorney general, I will use the full legal authority of the office to protect our residents, to ensure they have access to the full spectrum of reproductive health care right here in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: That is our next guest, Massachusetts Attorney General \uc2 Andrea Campbell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And last night, Rachel Maddow asked Massachusetts Governor \uc2 Maura Healey\uc2  about the state's plan to stockpile abortion pills now that a Trump appointed federal judge in Amarillo, Texas, who is not now and has never been a scientist, but who is a graduate of a Christian college that teaches that all abortion is sinful, revoked the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval for abortion medication, an approval that the scientists at the \uc2 FDA\uc2  issued 23 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. MAURA HEALEY (D), MASSACHUSETTS: Well, look Rachel, as you know, the battle to protect abortion is really being waged in the states, and Democratic governors, myself included, are stepping up to protect women's freedom. My goal here, and the goal of the last few weeks, was number one, to make sure we have enough doses available to cover as many women as we needed to cover either in the state or coming into the state.\par \par \uc2 And number two, to take action to be really clear to providers, prescribers -- we're going to protect your licenses, we're going to protect your malpractice insurance, and bottom line, we're going to make sure that business as usual continues here in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 So that's why we requested our state university -- very proud of the \uc2 University of Massachusetts\uc2  who stepped forward for this bulk purchase. We have that in hand. It will be available. We've also worked with our terrific network of hospitals. Massachusetts is home to so many great health care institutions who are also buying mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 And we will make sure that anyone who needs mifepristone has that available to them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Joining us now is Massachusetts Attorney General \uc2 Andrea Campbell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 I want to get into the details of what we heard the governor say last night about the legal protection that medical practitioners will have in Massachusetts for their licenses, as for medical malpractice insurance we just heard about, because if the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of this drug is in effect removed, medical practitioners will still have it on hand but they'd be very worried about using it without \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval. What will you be able to say to those medical practitioners in Massachusetts?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: First of all, thank you for having me on, and there's a lot that we can do to protect our providers and our patients, and immediately when taking office, set up a hotline in Massachusetts, a free confidential hotline for providers and patients to call -- to have any questions or concerns answered. I want folks in the face of what's happening in Texas not to lose hope.\par \par \uc2 As our governor said, we have stockpiled, of course, this medication that is critical to many in Massachusetts, and we have one of the strongest shield laws in the country because it protects providers regardless of the patient's location. And the executive order that the governor signed and that we all were there to support builds upon that shield law. And so, there are a lot of protections here that exist in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 We also, of course, want to work in partnership with our attorney general - - attorneys general across the country to support those who are in fear. And I'm proud of Massachusetts and the work we're doing to make sure that people have access to this critical medication, and that there are many other steps that folks have to take in order to take this off the market.\par \par \uc2 So I want folks not to be in fear, but to frankly stress to them that nothing has changed. This is still available. This medication which has been on the market for years is still safe and still effective.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: So will it remain available -- are you confident it will remain available, freely available in Massachusetts, as available as it is now if this ruling stays in effect?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: I am confident. You know, right now, the Department of Justice, of course, has filed an appeal. I am in my office, along with many other states have filed an amicus brief to support that appeal. There are several -- there are other cases as well, of course, that will possibly bring this to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And so, there are a lot of steps and a lot of things that have to happen before folks get to a place where this is off the market completely. And I want folks to stay in a place of hope and not fear.\par \par \uc2 And so, in Massachusetts, yesterday, we demonstrated, in collective sort of a power with our federal delegation, our local, and county and state delegation as well, that not only do we have the medication available here, but that nothing has changed. It is still available. It is still legal to use, to prescribe.\par \par \uc2 That providers should not have fear, that patients should not have fear, and as attorney general here in Massachusetts, I will do everything in my power, using the full authority of the office, to protect a woman's right not only to choose, but a woman's right to the safe and effective medicated abortion in the face of these anti-science, anti-Democratic right-wing attacks.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: So Attorney General Campbell, this country has now reverted to a condition that we haven't seen since before you were born. To the condition of the era of segregation in this country, where your rights depended on which state you lived in. And Massachusetts was always a good state to live in in these situations as it is now.\par \par \uc2 But did you anticipate that it was ever going to come to this that rights would be dependent on which state you lived in?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: Well, obviously we have been following the Supreme Court and these decisions that go in the face, frankly, of significant precedent and have been politicized sadly and so as the Supreme Court is going backwards, Massachusetts has stepped up, as it always has, to protect our residents, ensure access to basic civil protections.\par \par \uc2 And I've been stressing to folks that regardless of how they feel about this right to an abortion, they should care about this Dobbs case, which eliminated this constitutional protection in the face of significant precedent.\par \par \uc2 That means every civil right that we are discussing or that we care about is under threat. And so if that is the case, that means not only do we have to mobilize state by state but collectively, but Massachusetts has to continue to take the lead.\par \par \uc2 And I'm really proud to be A.G. and attorney general of course of Massachusetts because that's exactly what we'll continue to do, working with a whole host of stakeholders here to protect, of course, access to reproductive health care, but to protect every other civil rights that you mentioned.\par \par \uc2 And I have been stressing everyone should care about this. This is not just a woman's issue. This is a human rights issue. This is a civil rights issue. And A.G.'s across the country are going to have to step up and lead the way to protect our residents.\par \par \uc2 I'm really proud of what we are doing here in Massachusetts, not only for Massachusetts residents but for anyone who wants to come here for these critical services.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Before we go, let's just underline that point for people outside of Massachusetts. Women and girls outside of Massachusetts who will need this medication possibly a month from now. What is your message to them?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: Come to Massachusetts. Come to those states that are at the forefront, of course, legally and have the right here to protect folks. And we know that Massachusetts given our infrastructure, our ecosystem our hospital system, and our providers and the training that we have provided to folks all across the country to provide this critical reproductive care, and of course access to abortion that we're going to have to have to step up.\par \par \uc2 And Massachusetts is ready to do that to protect our residents and anyone else.\par \par \uc2 Massachusetts attorney general \uc2 Andrea Campbell\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us on this important issue. And we're going to please ask you to come back as this case progresses. Thank you very much, General.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, with control of the Senate hanging in the balance of the next election, Democrats got some very welcome news from Pennsylvania's senior Senator Bob Casey who has finally made the big decision about running for reelection. Senator Bob Casey will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:33:30]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Because the Democratic majority in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  is so slim, every Senate election for the foreseeable future will be about control of the Senate. The party that controls the Senate controls confirmation for federal judgeships and we have seen how crucial that is during the Trump presidency and the Biden presidency, in which \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has set a record for confirmation of federal judges. Those judges will remain in service long after the Biden presidency is over, issuing rulings possibly about things like access to abortion medication or even birth control.\par \par \uc2 So this is one of those year where majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has been very anxiously awaiting decisions by Democratic senators in battleground states like Montana, Nevada and Ohio. And so far, the Democratic senators in all of those states have announced they are running for reelection. And some of them, like \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  seem to be the only Democrat who could possibly win in that state.\par \par \uc2 That also seems to be the case for Democratic Senator Bob Casey of Pennsylvania, who thrilled \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and every other Democrat in the Senate yesterday with his decision about running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR BOB CASEY (D-PA): Hi. I'm Bob Casey, running for reelection in 2024. I need your help, because I'm already under attack, already being targeted by corporate special interests. They're already attacking me and my family and others. We need your help to fight back against that and to win in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for all your help.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Joining us now, Democratic Senator Bob Casey of Pennsylvania. He's a member of the Senate Finance Committee and chair of the Special Committee on Aging. He is running for reelection to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in 2024.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:56]\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Senator Casey, you've got that Pennsylvania map behind you which I have to say was invaluable when you were doing Pennsylvania analysis in the last election for everyone who was paying attention to that map of yours. What are the issues of importance to the voters in that map?\par \par \uc2 CASEY: Well Lawrence, great to be with you. I don't think there's any question that folks in our state are up against a lot every day. Just the cost that families have and I think my record as a senator and I think the record of our caucus in the Senate in lowering those costs is going to be a big issue.\par \par \uc2 But there is still a lot more work to do and I want to continue to try to deliver for the people of our state and to take on those corporate special interests that are opposing me and every other Democrat. So we have a tough fight ahead but we are ready.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: I want to know the, literally the issue of the day that we were just discussing with the Massachusetts Attorney General \uc2 Andrea Campbell\uc2 . And that is a Trump appointed judge showing once again the importance of control of the Senate, a Trump appointed judge in Texas, in a small federal courthouse in Texas, ruling for the entire country to shut off access to an abortion pill that was approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  23 years ago.\par \par \uc2 And this is what some Republican said they weren't trying to do, they were just trying to move abortion back to a decision made by the states. What can Democrats do about a situation like this?\par \par \uc2 CASEY: One thing I said last may when we were just introducing the Women's Health Protection Act was that the Dobbs decision would lead to abortion bans across the country, and maybe even a nationwide abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 So far, we have seen that play out, 13 states with bans in place, categorical bans. And now this federal district court decision, as you said, by one district court overturning in a different way, overturning the determinations made by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  23 years ago, it is really a back door nationwide abortion ban, is what it is.\par \par \uc2 And so that seems to be their goal not just to overturn Roe through their Supreme Court appointments but to in fact put in place nationwide abortion ban. And we have to pass federal legislation, as you know Lawrence, we don't have the votes for that yet but there has been a determined effort to pass federal legislation to codify Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Senator, you wrote an op-ed piece about the AR-15 and how it's a different weapons from what hunters in Pennsylvania need and why it should not be on the market. And one of the things that's important about tracking your thinking on this is that you're thinking changed over time.\par \par \uc2 And ten years ago, what happened at Sandy Hook, you watched that happen and you decided you had to change your view on what hunters in Pennsylvania needed and how to support them.\par \par \uc2 And so you have moved on this issue into a kind of position where most of the Democrats in the Senate are on this. Is there something in the way you adjusted on this issue that enables you to try to convince other people and Pennsylvania voters who haven't come as far as you have on this issue?\par \par \uc2 CASEY: Lawrence, I think at that time in 2012, in the aftermath of the horror, the absolute horror of Sandy Hook, one of the questions I had to ask myself and my wife and my four daughters were asking me is what are you going to do about this? Are you going to say that there's nothing you can do about this at all? You are a United States senator. You are a member of a legislative body, you're going to say there's nothing you can do about it.\par \par \uc2 And that is what -- I think that's what we have to challenge Republicans on. You're saying that the most powerful country in the world must surrender to this terribly complex problem that no other country in the world has. I just don't -- I think that's against the American spirit to surrender to a problem. We know what we can do. We can take a series of common sense measures and put them in place, like comprehensive background checks and banning military style assault weapons.\par \par \uc2 So many things we can do to reduce the likelihood of more -- the more these horrific mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Senator Bob Casey of Pennsylvania, welcome to the campaign trail. And you know, I am kind of lost about how to cover your campaign, because you are by far the best election analyst and campaign analyst of Pennsylvania, using that map right behind you. So maybe you can tell us who should take over that map to cover it for us. But please join us as a candidate as often as you can.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:59]\par \par \uc2 CASEY: Thanks Lawrence. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Thank you Senator.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the white Republicans in control of the Tennessee legislature expelled two black members of the House of Representatives and reminded the country and the world about Tennessee's troubled history.\par \par \uc2 The granddaughter of the first black member of the Tennessee Senate in the 20th century, poet marker (ph) Caroline Randall Williams, will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:27]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES (D), REINSTATED TENNESSEE STATE REPRESENTATIVE: I want to thank you all, not for what you did but for awakening the people of the state, particularly the young people. Thank you for reminding us that the struggle for justice is fought in one in every generation.\par \par \uc2 And to the people of Tennessee, I stand with you. We will continue to be your voice here and no expulsion, no attempt to silence us will stop us. It will only galvanize and strengthen our movement. And we'll continue to show up in the people's house. Power to the people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: That was Tennessee State Representative Justin Jones after he was reinstated to the house of representatives yesterday. He was expelled from the Tennessee house of representative along with Representative Justin J. Pearson on Thursday. Justin Pearson is expected to be reinstated when local officials in his district will vote to fill the vacancy created by the expulsion with the person who was expelled, which is how Representative Jones returned to the house of representatives.\par \par \uc2 The white Republicans in power in the Tennessee state house which was built by slaves have shown how little has changed in that building inn the 55 years since Avon Williams Jr. became the first black person elected to the Tennessee Senate in the 20th century.\par \par \uc2 Senator Williams served in the state Senate in the Nashville district close to the district of Representative Justin Jones.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, the granddaughter of Tennessee state senator Avon Williams, the award winning poet and author Caroline Randall Williams. She is the writer in residence at \uc2 Vanderbilt University\uc2  in Nashville, Tennessee and president of the Southern Equity Collective.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight. I have been wondering what you have been thinking as we have watched this drama unfold, this drama that is both tragic and at times hopeful as we see this reinstatement process racing through for both of these representatives.\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE RANDALL WILLIAMS, WRITER IN RESIDENT VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY: Lawrence, thank you so much for having me this evening. I hope it is hopeful. That's what I'll say. I hope it is hopeful.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: As you saw what was happening last week and Representative Pearson, after Representative Jones, coming to the microphone in the floor of the house where they were not just engaged in speech making, they were engaged in something I actually have never seen before which was a debate, an actual debate over these issues that were in front of this body.\par \par \uc2 We certainly saw, you know, Reverend \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  give speeches but to debate -- to debate back and forth the way we saw Representative Pearson doing for quite awhile with these white Republicans literally pointing the finger at him, kind of ordering him out of that room, was really an extraordinary thing to see.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It really was an extraordinary thing. And Justin Pearson -- I mean both Justin Jones and Justin Pearson are resurrections for the ages. I'm honored to live in a moment of their presence. I will say I have the honor of teaching Justin Jones. He was a senior and a class (INAUDIBLE) the first year I moved home to Nashville and he was a light and a luminary even then as a college student.\par \par \uc2 I think in this moment we are watching history being made. We are watching great speakers who are voices for a significant chapter of history begin to cut their teeth and I think, you know, Lawrence, he said earlier in the segment that like many freedoms in America it depends on the state you live in.\par \par \uc2 But I think, you know, let me tell you something. As a black person in the United States, when people start hearing new language, not retired language but new language about how your freedom depends on which state you live in, that is grounds for profound concern and more importantly, you know, when you're right to representation is unilaterally and unprecedentedly stripped away by a repressive, bigoted, myopic legislative body, you are righteously alarmed. you know, I know that on a personal level my grandfather, you very graciously introduced in the segment, you know, he stood on the floor of that legislature from 1968 to 1991, and you know, the \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2  burned a cross in their front yard on my father's birthday because my grandfather was trying to advocate for the desegregation of Nashville public schools in the early 1970s.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:56]\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Because this is a limited town. Nashville is a limited city. Tennessee is a limited state. We were the -- we are harbingers for things to come. And I think that the country should be looking very scrupulously and very intentionally at what is happening in Tennessee, because we were the last state to leave the union when the civil war started.\par \par \uc2 And we were the first state to join because we have real good manners after we lost the war. We were a state that started, you know, John Lewis and Diane Nash, you know, were luminaries of the civil rights movement. And the sit-ins that happened at (INAUDIBLE) in downtown Nashville were a model for sit ins that happened around the southeast afterwards. A\par \par \uc2 And you know what happened after that? Nothing. Because Nashville is a place that's really efficient at quashing democracy. We have to be very conscious of that in this moment in history that this is a light for things to come and we should be hopeful but cautious.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Caroline Randall Williams, we've been waiting to hear your voice on this. So glad you can join us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:56:06]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O'DONNELL: That is tonight's LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230412ej4b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for April 11, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi \par \uc2 8,151 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JOHNSON, MAYOR-ELECT, CHICAGO: And we talked a lot about, you know what it means to be a Black executive and, of course, what it means to raise a family. And so, I`m looking forward to the Obamas hanging out with us at the City of Chicago. And hopefully, you know, he shares some of that spotlight with the brother on the fifth floor.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: And listen, you made history. You kept this Chicago - - the seat, the greatest mayoralty, a lot of people would argue other than NYC, in the hands of an African American candidate. So, congratulations again. And we`ll see you at the convention. Chicago Mayor-Elect Brandon --\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Thank you so much. And of course, you got to meet my wife, OK. Like, because this doesn`t matter if you don`t get a chance to meet my wife, so let`s make sure we do that.\par \par \uc2 REID: Oh, I`ll come to dinner.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: OK, I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 REID: I`ll be at your house. Yes, I`m going to come to dinner. I invited myself. I just invited myself. Thank you, Chicago Mayor-Elect Brandon Johnson and his wife. We`ll see y`all there. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 That`s tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES (D-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: We continue to show up in the people`s house. Power to the people.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: With one expelled lawmaker returned to his position and another on his way, tonight, how the anti-democratic movement thrives in state houses across the country. Then --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Trump, will you come and speak to us? President Trump?\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: As the Manhattan D.A. sues Jim Jordan and House Republicans for trying to obstruct the prosecution of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Rebecca Traister on what the focus on Trump`s arrest may be costing us. Plus --\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GREENBERG, MAYOR OF LOUISVILLE, KENTUCKY: Under current Kentucky law, the assault rifle that was used to murder five of our neighbors and shoot at rescuing police officers will one day be auctioned off. Think about that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: As Kentucky searches for answers in the wake of a mass murderer, the governor of one state that`s about to sign an assault weapons ban joins me live when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Ali Velshi in for Chris Hayes. We`re watching the continuing fallout from the aggressive anti- democratic power played by Republicans in the Tennessee State House. Now, tomorrow, a local board of commissioners in Memphis which is in Shelby County is expected to vote on whether to reinstate Justin Pearson who`s one of two Black Democratic lawmakers expelled by Republicans over decorum violations, stemming from a gun reform protest that they held at the state capitol after a mass school shooting in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 As we covered on the show, the other expelled lawmaker, Justin Jones, made his triumphant return to the State House yesterday after a local council in Nashville, the county he`s from voted unanimously to return him to fill the vacancy left by his own expulsion. Now, this particular ploy by the legislature was so egregious, a Republican supermajority wielding its power to remove two democratically elected lawmakers on trivial charges, that it got national attention and backfired.\par \par \uc2 A new poll out today shows that a majority of Tennessee residents think the expulsions were wrong and Tennessee is a very Republican state. It doesn`t have a single statewide elected Democrat. But because this scheme in Tennessee was so brazen, and because voters who care about democracy mobilized so quickly, it was able to become a national story practically overnight, even amid a very busy news cycle. And because that mobilization was so quick, Representative Justin Jones didn`t even miss a day of work. He was expelled and reinstated over the course of one weekend.\par \par \uc2 And while what happened in Tennessee is particularly notable, it`s far from the only example of conservative legislatures working quietly to roll democracy back. Just ask Justin Jones.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: This is a historic day for Tennessee, but it is -- it may mark a very dark day for Tennessee, because it was signaled to the nation that there is no democracy in this state. It will signal to the nation that if it can happen here in Tennessee, it`s coming to your state next. And that is why the nation is watching us what we do here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: There`s no other way to put it. Authoritarianism is ascendant in the Republican Party, and state legislatures are leading that charge. In Montana, State Republicans are trying to change how the elections are run, but only for the 2024 Senate race. They`re trying to switch that race in particular to a two -- to a top two primary system which would effectively block any third party candidates from the ballot. It`s all part of a scheme to defeat the popular incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Republicans think he benefits from the current system which allows third- party candidates to siphon votes from their nominees. And Montana has taken quite a few steps recently to rollback democratic norms. In 2021, the Republican state legislature tried to end the statewide elections of Supreme Court justices and instead have those justices elected by district, which of course means the legislature could gerrymander the map and supportive conservatives the same year they also successfully changed the state`s voter ID law to ban the use of student IDs from Montana colleges, making it that much more difficult for young people to participate in local democracy. According to one Republican legislator, her party changed the rules because, "college students tend to be liberal." I love when they say the quiet parts out loud.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:28]\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, Republicans are already threatening to impeach a liberal state supreme court justice who won last week by 11 points because they are worried that she will help overturn the state`s abortion ban as well as their draconian worst-in-the-nation gerrymander. Those partisan voting maps are of course in and of themselves inherently anti-democratic, while Wisconsin is essentially evenly split 50-50 between the two parties in terms of voters. Republicans have been able to rig themselves a near permanent supermajority in the state legislature. In North Carolina, Republicans managed to convince a Democrat in a blue district to switch parties so they can achieve a supermajority and override the two-term Democratic governor`s veto power.\par \par \uc2 And these are just a handful of examples. There are so, so many more. Maybe you know about some of these, maybe you didn`t. If you did, it may be because you live in one of those states that I`ve just talked about, or you heard or read about it in the national media. But some of these stories go underreported, because local news has been on the decline for years, slowly bled to death by a lack of funding, or rated for parts by venture capitalists.\par \par \uc2 A lot of this stuff just flies under the radar. We all know when \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  or \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  say something ridiculous and offensive because Capitol Hill is full of reporters. But what about when a Florida House Republican compares trans folks to mutants and demons like a representative named Webster Barnaby did just yesterday?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WEBSTER BARNABY (R-FL), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: To all the folks that are in the audience that consider themselves gender dysphoria, says -- I don`t know what all that means. I really don`t know what all that means. I`m looking at society today and it`s like I`m watching an X-Men movie. It`s like we have mutants living among us on planet Earth.\par \par \uc2 The Lord rebuke you, Satan, and all of your demons and all of your imps are coming parade before us. That`s right. I called you demons and imps.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That`s some crazy stuff right there. But that kind of rhetoric is all over the place within the state Republican Parties. Nearly every troubling trend and the creeping threat of right-wing authoritarianism in this country is spearheaded in Republican-led state houses. They get the least amount of attention both from the media and from the voters, but the fight to save democracy will have to start at the state and local level.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Jasmine Crockett is a Democrat of Texas who previously served as a state lawmaker in the Texas House. Ben Wikler is the chair of the Wisconsin Democratic Party, played a major role in securing a victory for Democrats in that state Supreme Court election last week. Both join me now. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Crockett, good to see you. You`re OG on this -- on this topic, you and your fellow Texas state representatives when you were in the State House. Had this kind of thing going on, you had to stage protests and activities within the confines of the legislature and the legislative system in order to stop what ended up being a first in the nation abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 REP. JASMINE CROCKETT (D-TX): Yes, no, you`re absolutely right. And Ali, the only reason that you and I came to even know each other is because of that protest. You`re right, I`m listening to us speak and your monologue, and I`m snapping and I`m saying amen, brother, Amen. Keep preaching, right? Because that`s exactly what it`s been. We could not get the coverage, not that we weren`t necessarily looking for coverage, but we want to make sure that our constituents are informed.\par \par \uc2 Most people didn`t even have an idea of who their state representatives were. They could tell you who their senators were, who their congresspeople were, but they couldn`t tell you about the state reps. And they had no idea that when they were complaining about criminal justice issues, they really needed to be talking to us. They had no idea that an abortion ban was going to be coming down. They had no idea that you know, what started off as a bathroom bill would then continue on and turn into anti-trans legislation. And they had no idea that the Republicans would be so desperate to bring about voter suppression in the State of Texas that we would flee the state and end up having warrants out for our rights, and really bring national attention to the issue.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And I guess that`s the point of the exercise. I was talking to Reverend Al the other day, and he was saying, you know, people always say you`re just doing it for the attention. He says, yes, that`s kind of the point. We need to actually get attention on these issues because when voters, Ben, start to learn about what`s going on in your state, in Wisconsin, what you found is they actually did respond.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:01]\par \par \uc2 BEN WIKLER, CHAIR, WISCONSIN DEMOCRATIC PARTY: Absolutely. They`re furious when they find out. But Republicans don`t want them to find out. One of the stunning things about politics today is that when you just accurately describe what Republicans are trying to do, in state houses in Wisconsin, across the country, voters initially think that it must be a lie, because they can`t imagine that anyone would try to get away with a power grab so brazen and extreme.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, we had this Supreme Court race. There were millions of dollars of ads. Eventually, it sank in for folks that Republicans were trying to, on the one hand, rip away their reproductive freedom, and on the other hand, smashed democracy so badly, that voters would have no ability to fight back against it. Their only path for voters to take back control over their own bodies over their democracy was to win the Supreme Court race. And that`s exactly what they did, by a landslide.\par \par \uc2 But frankly, if it hadn`t been for this opportunity, we would have been stuck in a doom loop that could have lasted for decades.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: A doom loop. That`s interesting, because one could look at these electoral and gerrymandered and weirdly rigged systems, Congresswoman Crockett, and say we`re in a doom loop. You chose not to do that in Texas, but much like Tennessee, Texas is a -- at the moment, a very red state. Options are limited. They were limited for you to counter these majorities.\par \par \uc2 So, what`s the lesson you learn from all of the things that you and your colleagues did in the Texas State Legislature that you`re now seeing happen in certain places like, you know, what the two Justins did in Tennessee? What is -- what are the lessons for both democracy, for legislators, and for voters to look at these things and say, we`re in a doom loop, we can`t get out of it?\par \par \uc2 CROCKETT: No, I think that, you know, we, as the Texas Democrats, we rang the alarms, and we basically said, listen, democracy is under attack, most people weren`t paying attention to what the Supreme Court was doing as it relates to the Voting Rights Act. Most people haven`t connected the dots around gerrymandering. Most people don`t quite understand what gerrymandering looks like, right? But when you talk about Wisconsin, Wisconsin really is a very good example.\par \par \uc2 How is it that when you have a \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race, it`s almost 50-50? How is it that you have a Democrat that wins for the Supreme Court and wins by over, you know, 10 points, but for some reason, when you look at how the state is up, it doesn`t look anywhere near like 50-50 when it comes to representation. But one of the things that the Justins have brought out is that representation matters, even when you are in the extremely minority, it is important that you speak up for your constituency and that they have a voice. And that`s exactly what they did.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, the Republicans aren`t really the smartest folks sometimes, right? And so, they didn`t think this through. They thought that they would just go ahead and do this, and no one would pay attention, or no one would care, because it was two Black guys, right? They were like, well, maybe we shouldn`t do it for the White woman. But no one is going to care about these Black men. But guess what? The entire community came together and showed them that they did care because this isn`t about or it shouldn`t be about race, it should be about fairness. And it should be about letting people be the voice that they have been elected to be even if you disagree with that, because that`s what democracy looks like.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And ironically, the protest was actually about guns just to start with right after a mass shooting in Texas, something that majority -- even Tennesseans, even in a -- in a very red state like that believe in common sense gun laws. But Ben, what was fascinating about this, I know that you have been in contact with the -- with the Democratic Party in in Tennessee, we saw a remarkably rapid mobilization over the weekend partly because of what Congresswoman Crockett has said that I don`t know what Republicans in that statehouse were thinking this was going to end up as. But they ended up creating democratic folk heroes out of the two Justins.\par \par \uc2 Justin Jones was expelled Thursday, he was back on Monday. Tell me about this coalition that comes into play that we don`t even know exists, people who on Thursday and Friday did not know that they were warriors for democracy who suddenly went into action.\par \par \uc2 WIKLER: You know, they said democracy dies in darkness. Well, it thrives in the sunlight. When people see what they do, when these people see these authoritarian attacks across the country, they will rally to your side. And there have been hundreds of thousands of dollars flowing into both Justins` campaign accounts, to the Tennessee Democratic Party`s account. People are ready to make phone calls to go to bat to support them.\par \par \uc2 Not only are they coming back to the state legislature, but also Democrats are in court to fight back against the gerrymandered maps that gave Republicans supermajorities in the first place. It`s a 60-40 state for Trump in Tennessee, but it`s not a Republicans supermajority state. Democrats are going to be able to win back state legislative seats and make a massive difference. The Republicans scheme backfired.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I could talk to you both about this for a really long time. I appreciate you both taking time to be with us. You are both going down in the history books for the things that you have done to uphold democracy in this country. The Congresswoman Jasmine Crockett and Ben Wikler, thank you very much for being with us this evening and for kicking the show off.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:04]\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, the Manhattan district attorney fights back against Republican interference into his prosecution of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but is the case distracting America from the larger threat that Trump poses? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Today, the Manhattan district attorney who`s prosecuting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took the extraordinary step of suing a sitting member of Congress. D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  is accusing Jim Jordan of Ohio, the Republican chairman of the House Judiciary Committee of "An unprecedentedly brazen and unconstitutional attack by members of Congress on an ongoing New York State criminal prosecution and investigation."\par \par \uc2 [20:20:13]\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit alleges that on March 20th, 10 days before Trump was actually indicted, Jordan "began a transparent campaign to intimidate and attack District Attorney Bragg, making demands for confidential documents and testimony from the district attorney himself as well as his current and former employees and officials." Bragg says the campaign originated with a Trump lawyer, and he`s asking the court to block a subpoena from Jordan to a former assistant D.A. who investigated Trump as well as any future subpoenas against Bragg or his staff.\par \par \uc2 Now, this unusual lawsuit is just the latest twist in the unprecedented indictment of the former U.S. president, a saga that has dominated the news for weeks. But is it possible as Rebecca Traister writes in New York Magazine that the focus on the hope that the ex-president may finally get his legal (INAUDIBLE) distracts us from a far greater danger than the man himself, which is the dire anti-democratic breakdown that Trump unleashed, and that the conservative movement in this country has embraced.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Traister, writer-at-large for New York Magazine joins me now. Rebecca, good to see you. I was so taken by this fascinating piece in which you seem to be having a debate with yourself about how you should -- how one should feel about the fact that the accountability for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may have begun. You talk about something called the dangerous fantasy of Trump`s indictment. Tell me what you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA TRAISTER, WRITER-AT-LARGE, NEW YORK MAGAZINE: Well, I think that stems from my suspicion which I`ve harbored for a long time, that so much focus on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as an individual works actually to cast him as an aberration rather than as an emblem of his party and where his party was moving before he became president and where it continues to move.\par \par \uc2 You know, you noted the anti-democratic wave that Trump unleashed. I would argue that that wave was being unleashed long before. Listening to the conversation you just had with Jasmine and with Ben talking about the Republican Party`s takeover of state legislatures while the Democratic Party and I would argue a lot of the political media looked in other directions for years preceding Donald Trump`s candidacy and presidency.\par \par \uc2 You know, we know that he won thanks to the Electoral College, an institution that was set up to permit minorities of voters to have power. \uc2 The Federalist Society\uc2  was already had created a pipeline for the Supreme Court. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  had already stolen a seat from the sitting President Barack Obama. All that preceded \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And all of that tells us a lot about where we are now.\par \par \uc2 Trump was an incredibly powerful, dangerous, awful tool of that party. But -- and he actually permitted us to see some of these things which I think both Democrats and a political media were willfully blind to for a long time. That`s part of the conversation you just had. But right now, in this moment where he`s being, you know, tried for things indicted for things that actually aren`t about the harm he did to the nation, right? They may include legal accountability for crimes he`s committed. The amount of attention being directed to him worries me because it makes -- it makes me think that people are going to think we`ve somehow solved the problem by getting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And that couldn`t be further from the truth.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The conservative jurist, Michael Luttig made a similar point the other day. He thinks that there is a serious and pernicious threat to democracy afoot, and that Republicans are behind it. And that, as you say, account -- this Manhattan case is not accountability for any of what Trump or his party really did and continue to do with this country. That`s not to say that there is justification in prosecuting him for these things, but you are worried that it`s not the thing that is at the root of the erosion of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 TRAISTER: Well, it worries me for a couple of reasons. I mean, for one thing, I think that for me, the kind of savage system of the criminal justice system, the fantasy that it can solve. problems created by other savage systems and structures politically I think is a fantasy. And again, I think it`s a fallacy that Trump himself is an outlier rather than, you know, an avatar of what his party has become.\par \par \uc2 And in focusing on him and the singularity of Trump as this kind of tacky criminal and you know, being able to finally get him, I think it makes other members of his party who are arguably more dangerous, certainly as dangerous, arguably more dangerous, whether you`re talking about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , whether you`re talking about Brian Kemp, Greg Abbott, whether you`re talking about the Republican legislatures, and you were just describing some of what we`re seeing them do, full-on attacks on human beings, on health care, on voting rights, gerrymandering districts, trying to seize all the mechanisms of political power in this country. And none of those -- none of those things necessarily have anything to do with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And if we permit ourselves to think that there`s some end goal in you know, in putting him in prison, it takes so much of our attention away from these crucial fights all around the country right now. Democracy is in dire danger. And -- but it`s not going to be -- we`re not going to be saved from that danger in lower Manhattan, you know, at 100 Center Street at Donald Trump`s indictment.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:28]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, before your \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed explodes, I just think it`s fair without giving away the story that I really think everybody should read, that you do conclude with the idea that you can hold both thoughts at the same time, right? You can -- you can enjoy the fact that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may be facing justice and realize the fact that it may have nothing to do with solving our larger problem. But you heard that conversation. You didn`t write this as a media criticism necessarily. But this whole issue of everything that is going on in these laboratories of autocracy that our state legislatures that are run by Republicans, in some cases, as we were pointing out last night in Tennessee, where they don`t run against anybody. A lot of people are claimed. There aren`t even people running against some of these people.\par \par \uc2 TRAISTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: What do we do to make everybody realize because it`s not as handy and convenient as thinking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and focusing your energies on him? How do you focus your energy on something much broader and much more dangerous?\par \par \uc2 TRAISTER: Well, here`s where I`m going to say something actually about contradicting myself about what`s been good about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is that his election actually prompted a flood of first-time candidates, people who hadn`t been paying attention, who hadn`t been directed by either the political media or the Democratic Party to pay attention to the local politics.\par \par \uc2 One of the things you saw coming out of his victory in 2016 and Hillary Clinton`s loss in 2016 was a flood of people suddenly interested in politics and organizing in ways they hadn`t been before. And so, you can already -- it`s not fixed and we need more of that flood and we need more people getting involved and running for spots on school boards and city councils and state legislatures. But that has been a real shift, in part prompted by some of the focus on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when he was the president and figuring out how to fight him.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Rebecca, thank you for a very thoughtful article. I always appreciate talking to you, Rebecca Traister.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, new research details the rapidly increasing threat that climate change poses to American cities. Is the water level creeping up near your home? We`ll talk about that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: This is Isle de Jean Charles, Louisiana. People here have lived on the water and off the land for centuries. But now that land is disappearing, and the encroaching water is forcing them out, making them among what some have called the first climate refugees in America. Because it isn`t just Isle de Jean Charles that is under threat, the entire Louisiana coastline is disappearing.\par \par \uc2 ALEX KOLKER, PROFESSOR OF EARTH SCIENCES, TULANE UNIVERSITY: Louisiana has lost a very large amount of land over the last century, something close to 1900 square miles worth of land. That is equivalent today to about a football field and our football field an hour.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Football field an hour. That piece, by the way, ran on this show nearly seven years ago. This is what Isle de Jean Charles looks like now, seven years, more rising seas, less than one square miles of land remains above water. Even in 2016, it was clear that the rising sea levels situation was bad, but today it`s even worse than Chris and scientists thought when they did that story.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports on two new academic papers showing the dramatic and abnormal sea level rise along the southern coast of the United States since 2010. One paper calculates, "the rise of sea level -- the rate of sea level rise since 2010 at more than 10 millimeters or one centimeter per year in the region, more than double the global average over the same period.\par \par \uc2 The second study finds the same trend since 2010 across the U.S. Gulf Coast and southeastern coastlines, calling the rise unprecedented in at least 120 years. A researcher involved ominously told the post, "it`s a window into the future."\par \par \uc2 Pulitzer Prize-winning reporter Chris Mooney covered this for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . He joins me now. Chris, thank you for being with us. You know, there`s been -- in the last six weeks, there`s been a number of articles warning about how dire this is and past the point of no return. But when you talk about keeping climate change to 1.5 degrees Celsius above pre-industrial levels or measuring increase in sea level in 10-millimeter increments, one-centimeter increments, it becomes hard for people to understand that when you measure this across the entire Earth, this is really, really very serious.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS MOONEY, REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: It adds up quite fast. You`re talking about five inches over a period of 10 years, some places may be even a little bit faster than that. So, we did the numbers on Pensacola, Florida, which is one of the places that has these fast rates. And it`s -- basically, it`s happened in a year since the year 2010. So, it adds up really -- it makes a big difference especially in a hurricane but also for more regular flooding.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes, just think about the -- you know, if you have a house, think about what that would feel like if you`re -- the water level rises at that rate. Let`s talk a little bit about what can be done. What`s the practical effect on people in the Gulf in the southeast. I know whenever there`s a hurricane, we end up in places for instance, in the southeast, even if the hurricanes not going to Charleston, would go to Charleston because Charleston is going to flood anyway. Miami still has sunny day storm sewer backups. You know, practically speaking, what does this mean?\par \par \uc2 [20:35:08]\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: Well, so firstly, if there`s a hurricane, it`s going to be able send more water on the lake. But even outside of a hurricane, you have a great, dramatic uptick in regular what they call sunny day flooding, which this whole region has experienced much more frequently. And it seems pretty clear that the rapid increase in the level of the sea is contributing a great deal to that. And that can be a big inconvenience.\par \par \uc2 Also, when it rains, it may not rain as quickly because the sea level is high. So, it really starts to become a problem for infrastructure. You have roads you can`t travel across a lot of the time. In terms of what can you do, there`s different levels of adaptation. So, you run from New Orleans which has really expensive walls around it to a lot of places that really don`t have much at all. It varies greatly.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Chris, talk to me about your takeaway. I was quoting from the IPCC study the other day where they`re saying, we`re at the point of no return. We may be past the point of no return. And we`re not doing things as aggressively as we should. Even as a country that has committed to meeting the Paris Climate Accord, we`re just -- we`re just necessarily not going to. And in fact, some of the things we`re undertaking today continue to move us in the wrong direction. Particularly -- I mean, this is -- this is one thing, sea level rises, but fossil fuels and the contribution of CO2 to the environment. It doesn`t seem like as a world, people are taking it seriously, but governments are taking it as seriously as they should.\par \par \uc2 MOONEY: What`s happening is that change is too slow unlike the rate of sea level rises too fast, right? The adoption of renewable energy and declining use of fossil fuels, adoption of electric vehicles, they`re all happening but if you look at the math of 1.5 degrees Celsius and how fast emissions have to fall in order to keep that in sight, which is something we actually did really extensively in a big data journalism piece last year, then you realize that the change is far too slow. And actually, you`re almost certainly going to go over 1.5 degrees Celsius, and that means you`re going to have full carbon back out of the air smell. It`s not at all clear how that`s going to happen. And certainly, the technology and infrastructure not built out to do that at this point.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Chris, thank you. We appreciate your reporting on this. Chris Mooney is a reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right, still to come, the widespread impact of gun violence across this country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. JASON SMITH, CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER, UNIVERSITY OF LOUISVILLE HEALTH: I`m a doctor I don`t know what the answers are. But to everyone who helps make policy both at state, city, federal, I would simply ask you to do something because doing nothing which is what we`ve been doing is not working.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Kentucky officials plead for action with another state -- while another state takes action to crack down on gun violence. That`s up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The city of Louisville is reeling from yesterday`s shooting, the 146th mass shooting in America so far this year. And we now know that the gunman used a depressingly familiar murder weapon in that attack, an AR-15 rifle purchased legally last week. Now, according to data compiled by Mother Jones, this makes the Louisville shooting the seventh mass shooting in the past 11 months alone to be carried out with an AR-15.\par \par \uc2 Today, Louisville`s Mayor, Democrat Craig Greenberg, highlighted a shocking Kentucky law that governs what will happen to that lethal weapon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREENBERG: Under current Kentucky law, the assault rifle that was used to murder five of our neighbors and shoot at rescuing police officers will one day be auctioned off. Think about that. That murder weapon will be back on the streets one day under Kentucky`s current law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Thanks to those kinds of dangerously twisted gun laws around the country, a majority of adult Americans "have either personally or had a family member who has been impacted by a gun-related incident such as witnessing a shooting, being threatened by a gun, or being injured or killed by a gun. And about one in five have a family member who has been killed by a gun including death by suicide."\par \par \uc2 Mayor Greenberg, a survivor of an attempted shooting himself spoke about how this epidemic of gun violence now touches all of us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GREENBERG: This isn`t about partisan politics. This is about life and death. This is about preventing tragedies. You may think this will never happen to you, never happened to any of your friends, or loved ones. I used to think that. The sad truth is that now no one in our city, no one in our state, no one in our country has that luxury anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: At the same press conference, we heard from the chief medical officer at the Louisville hospital that cared for several of yesterday`s victims. Dr. Jason Smith begged the policymakers to do something about the gun violence that plagues his emergency room, his city, and this entire country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I`ll be honest, caring for three shooting victims, plus the other that came in is not a frequent day for us. And the events surrounding this made this obviously much more difficult, but to be honest with you, we barely had to adjust our operating room schedule to be able to do this. That`s how frequent we are having to deal with gun violence in our community. I`m weary. There`s only so many times you can walk into a room and tell someone they`re not coming home tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 I`m a doctor. I don`t know what the answers are. But to everyone who helps make policy both at state, city, federal, I would simply ask you to do something because doing nothing, which is what we`ve been doing is not working.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Now, for now, a solution is not evident in deep-red Kentucky or on the federal level with a divided Congress, but there is one state that is about to implement a big step in the right direction, an assault weapons ban. I`ll talk to the governor signing that bill into law next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Back in 1994, the Democrat \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2  was a freshman U.S. Congressman representing a Central Eastern District of Washington State when he voted for a Federal Assault Weapons Ban. He later said the vote cost him his seat. And indeed, Inslee lost his bid for reelection as the 1994 Republican revolution swept a wave of conservatives into office.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 30 years later, Inslee is now the Democratic governor of Washington. And while the national Assault Weapons Ban has expired, his state is forging its own gun regulations. The State Senate just approved a bill that would ban assault weapons in Washington and now returns to the State House for final approval. And if it passes, it heads to Governor Inslee`s desk for his signature.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the governor, \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2 , the Democratic governor of Washington State. Governor Inslee, good to see you. Thank you for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 JAY INSLEE\uc2  (D-WA): Good evening! Good evening!\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Your state -- this ban has been introduced in Washington state for a few years now. It`s never passed until now. What`s changed?\par \par \uc2 INSLEE: Well, people have had a bellyful of violence, gun violence. They understand there is no reason on this green earth why we need weapons of war in our streets, and they`ve seen their children being exposed to violence in their schools. And now, this is a vast, vast majority of Washingtonians and Americans who want to take these weapons of war off our streets. So, I will be signing up bill in the next couple of weeks to ban assault weapons. And since 1994, it`s been a long road, but thank goodness, we`re getting there.\par \par \uc2 But this is not the only thing we need to do. We`re also -- I`m confident we`ll be passing a bill this year to require a safety training course for people before they buy a firearm and have a 10-day waiting period, which will join our other laws we passed in last year to require the gun owners to safely store their firearms. We need to do several things, not just one thing.\par \par \uc2 But the time has come for action. Inaction is not an excuse. We may not solve all the problems, but we can take major steps. And I`m glad I`ll be taking a significant step in the next couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, look, what you say makes sense that people are fed up. They are -- they`re prepared to see common sense regulations on guns that do not mean the elimination of the Second Amendment. And you`re moving in lockstep with where the population is. There are a whole lot of states moving in the other direction. And I don`t know whether it`s a protection racket that is the \uc2 NRA\uc2  that warns people that no matter whether there was a mass shooting in your town or a school shooting, do not suggest anything, otherwise, you`ll lose your protection.\par \par \uc2 Why are there states, particularly in the southeast going in entirely the opposite direction than your country is -- your state is?\par \par \uc2 INSLEE: Well, it has been my experience frequently that politicians have been listening to the \uc2 NRA\uc2  rather than their constituents. In 1994, even when I was removed from office, in the state of Washington, there was a majority in favor of this. But the \uc2 NRA\uc2  has some political clout, and you have some folks who vote just on one issue, and therefore democracy doesn`t work always and the public will is not followed.\par \par \uc2 But in this case, I am confident that the public will is they don`t want AR-15 intruding on their children`s schools. And we need to continue to press this. And maybe we won`t succeed in every single state in every single month. But every state that follows my state and following democracy and addressing common sense gun measures, we will keep this ball rolling, just like we are doing in protecting a woman`s right of choice, just like we`re doing and moving against climate change.\par \par \uc2 These are the majority opinions of vast majorities of Americans. And sometimes you have to make progress state by state, not necessarily in the congressional field. And the more states to do this, the more it moves the needle, both politically and culturally. And I`m convinced that the momentum is now to give our children some measure of safety. And that means passing these commonsense measures.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You talked about protecting a woman`s rights to choose. You`re in the midst of up a few major stories in the last few days. You have stockpiled medication of abortion, the pill mifepristone, which might be banned nationwide in a matter of days. Tell me -- tell me what your thought processes behind that and what that actually means. If the -- if the medication is banned for distribution, will people be able to get mifepristone that you have stockpiled?\par \par \uc2 INSLEE: Yes, in our state, because our state had the foresight to stockpile three or four years in supply of mifepristone, we took possession of that, it was safely in our hands before the judge in Texas, the Trump appointee issued their ruling. And because of that, we now will be free to continue to dispense this to women in Washington state, even if the Texas judge`s decision was affirmed by the Supreme Court. So, I can pretty much say with confidence, we will be able to continue to dispense this product.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:25]\par \par \uc2 We also, as you know, had one federal judge in Washington state actually ruled the opposite because of the great work of our Attorney General Bob Ferguson, so we don`t know what the Supreme Court is going to do. But we do know that Washington women are going to have access to the mifepristone. I`m glad other states are now joining us in this regard. And I think this is kind of one of the measures as an eye opener, we`re going to have to be creative and aggressive on a state-by-state basis, because those people who would want to suppress this right and women are going to come after us state by state.\par \par \uc2 In Idaho, my adjoining state, they`re trying to ban the right to cross state lines to exercise the right of choice. And I think this just an opening salvo and we need to be aggressive and creative. I`m glad we have it. It`s going to work. And I hope other states join us.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Let me ask you about Idaho. You actually wrote a letter to the governor of Idaho condemning that state`s bill that makes it a crime to help a minor get an abortion out of state. In the letter you wrote, we welcome Idaho`s patients and healthcare providers with open arms in Washington. Abortions banned in Idaho with very rare exceptions. In your state, minors can get an abortion without parental consent. Tell me how this all plays out for you. Do you actually imagine that you`re going to see Idahoan fugitives coming into Washington because we`ve seen what happens in in Ohio when somebody -- when a minor can`t get an abortion, you know, it`s draconian.\par \par \uc2 INSLEE: Look, there`s a really simple principle here. Idaho can make the laws for their state, but they`re not going to make the laws for Washington State. Women has a right of choice and the laws are determined by our legislature and our people. And those laws apply to activities within the state of Washington. And we are not going to allow Idaho politicians dictate to the citizens of Washington what happens in the state of Washington.\par \par \uc2 This bill attempts to regulate what happens in Washington State. The Idaho legislature does not have jurisdiction in my state, in our state, and therefore they will not be allowed to dictate who can travel to and from Idaho. By the way, this -- they attempt to apply this to Washington citizens too. They try to say Washington citizens who happen to travel and Idaho essentially can`t travel back in some sense.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to resist this. I assume there`s going to be litigation in some sense. We`re going to have to figure out whether we can be a party or we just Planned Parenthood. But one way or another, we cannot allow one state to cut off the right of travel in the United States. This is a constitutional right to travel across state lines. And what is legal in Washington will remain legal in Washington state. That`s a very important constitutional principle.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I got a couple of minutes left. I don`t want to start a fight between fantastic Canadian border states. But last night, I had Governor Whitmer on the show and she said that if people want to live in a state that respects the rights that they`ve come to expect, they should move to Michigan. You seem to be doing something like that in Washington. You`re creating a safe haven for people who care about gun safety, people who care about abortion, people who care about living their best free lives, something that libertarians and conservatives used to pride red states for being.\par \par \uc2 INSLEE: Well, we -- I mean, we don`t -- that`s -- I don`t get up in the morning trying to create a sanctuary. But we do care about other people. Look, when Idaho and their politicians did -- frankly, did not act responsibly during the COVID epidemic, they did not take measures that could have saved people`s lives, as a consequence, they had scores of people get sick. And they came over to Washington and we gave them health care. We treated them. They`re Americans. We care about them in their lives. We care about everybody.\par \par \uc2 And we care about the right choice for all women. So, women are welcome in our state, and we will have the means to provide them the right of choice in our state. And that will -- that will continue. And these are basically fundamental freedom issues. We ought to have freedom for a woman`s right of choice. We ought to have freedom for kids to go to school and not be assaulted by a weapon of war. There ought to be a freedom to breathe and not be consumed by forest fire smoke because of climate change.\par \par \uc2 So, I would say that we are a state that believes in freedom, and others are welcome to come and enjoy it. I think \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  is a tremendous governor. She`s got a beautiful state and she can come visit us too. So, I`m glad to be a party that stands up for freedom --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, played.\par \par \uc2 INSLEE: -- right now in this clash.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, played, Governor. And all of you can go to Canada really easily, so that`s good too. Nice to see you, Governor as always. Governor \uc2 Jay Inslee\uc2  of Washington.\par \par \uc2 INSLEE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That is ALL IN for Tuesday night. Chris is going to be back tomorrow. You can catch me on Saturday and Sunday at 10:00 a.m. right here on MSNBC. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230412ej4b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for April 10, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Michael Steele, Brandy Zadrozny \par \uc2 8,453 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JEFF SHARLET, JOURNALIST: licenses what Trump has called. Remember, in Waco, he said it`s time for the final battle. And Easter, the day Christ `s post arise, he says World War III wearing on his face this strange American flag paint like a supervillain.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Yes. It is wild. These folks have replaced Jesus, the God of peace and of loving the poor, with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the AR-15. If they aren`t apostasy, I don`t know what is. Jeff Sharlet, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, on ALL IN. One, of the Tennessee three now back at his job.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES (D-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: Power to the people.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: That unanimous vote to reappoint Justin Jones to his seat. As hundreds of protesters accompany him back to the state capitol. Tonight, the fight to save democracy in Tennessee. Then --\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): I have a very close friend that didn`t make it tonight. And I have another close friend who didn`t either and one who`s at the hospital.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: At least four people killed, nine more injured in the latest mass shooting in America. Plus, Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  on the threat to abortion access, Trump`s latest attempt to stop his Vice President from testifying, and an NBC News exclusive as the victim of a years` long conspiracy finally breaks her silence.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY DOVER, NURSE: That message is simple it is that I am alive. I`m well.\par \par \uc2 BRANDY ZADROZNY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That`s it?\par \par \uc2 DOVER: I hope they believe it.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Good evening from New York. I`m Ali Velshi in for Chris Hayes. Today, members of Nashville`s Metropolitan Council took steps to undo the Tennessee State House`s undemocratic expulsion of two Black lawmakers. This was the scene just a little over an hour ago when the Tennessee State Representative Justin Jones triumphantly returned to work as a state legislature. Accompanied by thunderous applause from the gallery, Jones strode right past some of the 72 Republicans who voted to expel him on trumped up charges, ready to get right back to work.\par \par \uc2 And all day he was joined by a massive crowd protesting in support of the democratically elected lawmakers who were unjustly removed from office under contrived circumstances. Last week, the Republican House voted to remove Jones and another man named Justin Pearson -- he`s the man on the right -- for minor rules violations, so-called decorum violations stemming from a protest that they led on gun reform. Notably, the legislature did not remove a third lawmaker. The woman in the middle of the screen who helped lead the protests, a woman named Gloria Johnson, who unlike the two Justin`s on her side, is white.\par \par \uc2 And this was just the latest example of Republican anti-democratic overreach especially in the Tennessee statehouse. Despite Republican attempts to paint the gun protest, which was a vocal, nonviolent protest as an insurrection, a framing that is completely disconnected from the facts, it is clear that their actual goal was to silence their critics and shut down all dissenting voices in what is an ostensible democracy, a state legislature.\par \par \uc2 But there is a way for Tennessee Democrats to fight back. You see, it`s up to local commissions and councils to fill vacant positions in the Tennessee House. And since expelling Jones left a vacant seat, today, the Nashville City Council was able to unanimously vote to return the Democrat Justin Jones to the House to fill his own vacancy on an interim basis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you are for the election of Justin Jones to be the interim successor for the vacancy of Tennessee House District 52, you will vote aye. If not, you will vote no. Votes are in. Mr. Clark, close the machines. Take the vote.\par \par \uc2 All right, so we can keep moving. Justin Jones has been elected as the interim successor for the vacancy of Tennessee House District 52.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Justin Jones appointed as the interim successor for the vacancy -- the vacancy, of course, is his -- unanimously. Zero nay votes. Supporters then marched to the state capitol where they once again demanded a vote on the commonsense gun reforms that the so-called Tennessee three were initially advocating for.\par \par \uc2 While only Justin Jones was reinstated today, another local board, the Shelby County Board of Commissioners, will vote on the status of this man, the other lawmaker Justin Pearson, on Wednesday. And while today`s vote marks a very, very small step for democracy, maybe no step at all because it`s not even back to the status quo what it was a week ago. We`re still a long way to go.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:04]\par \par \uc2 Right now, Tennessee Republicans are threatening to withhold funding from Justin Pearson`s district if he is reinstated in a transparent attempt to overturn the will of its elected representatives. The rule say that the county or the municipality from which the representative comes from decides who the interim representative will be. And now, they`re saying they`ll hold the funding back. The Shelby County Commissioner telling a local news outlet that threats over funding were "about bullying people into submission and enough is enough. We`ve got to stand for something or fall for everything. And we`ve been bullied by the state for too long now."\par \par \uc2 It remains to be seen if Tennessee Republicans will make good on those defunding threats, but their Democratic colleagues certainly appear defiant.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: I`m hopeful -- I`m hopeful for the days ahead for Tennessee, not because of the actions of this body, but because of the actions of the people out there and the thousands gathered outside this chamber right now who are calling for something better, who`s -- who responded to your attacks on democracy with an attack of a mass movement for social justice and racial justice and economic justice to restore the heart of our state.\par \par \uc2 And so, I want to thank you all not for what you did but for awakening the people of this state, particularly the young people. Thank you for reminding us that the struggle for justice is fought in one in every generation. And so, the people of Tennessee, I stand with you. We will continue to be your voice here. And no expulsion, no attempt to silence us will stop us but will only galvanize and strengthen our movement. And we continue to show up in the people`s house. Power to the people.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON SEXTON (R-TN), HOUSE SPEAKER: All right, that`s out of order.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Michael Steele is a former chairman of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  . David Pepper is the former chairman of the Ohio Democratic Party and the author of Laboratories of Autocracy: A Wake-Up Call From Behind The Lines. Both join me now.\par \par \uc2 I got to ask you, Michael, what`s your -- what`s your looking at, what you`re smiling about because a week ago, nobody in this country knew who Justin Jones was.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: They didn`t. And thanks to these ignorant Republicans in this legislature, we all do and we stand with them, and we`ll look forward to continue with this conversation. What struck me in the clip, and forgive me for sort of chuckling on the way into our conversation, was the sheer contempt on the speaker`s face when he said power to the people. The sheer contempt when he put the gavel down, you`re out of order. You know, that dripping condescension and in the sense that I at least picked up from him that all of this means nothing, all of this means nothing.\par \par \uc2 The fact that they`re even threatening to withhold funding to Pearson`s district if they reappoint him tells you everything you want to know about these anti-democratic illiberal Republicans. And it is -- it is -- I`ve never seen anything like it inside the party. And I`ve been in this party since 1976 and I`ve never seen anything so hateful and so disgusting in terms of how they`re looking at these young African American men in the first instance, and then on the back end, after the people who sent them to the legislature say, these are our representatives. In the case of Mr. Pearson, they`re like, well, we`ll decide that. And with Mr. Jones, they`re like, well, you come back, but you better behave.\par \par \uc2 So, folks, there`s -- like you said, Ali, coming into this conversation, there`s a lot more work ahead not just in Mississippi, folks. Don`t get it twisted. This just isn`t Mississippi, OK. Look for this across the country.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, let`s talk about that, David, because what we`ve seen in the last year, including in the Midterm Elections, was some outlandish stuff going on in Arizona, in Pennsylvania, to some degree in Georgia, in Michigan. We just saw some responses to that in Wisconsin. Your book is about how state legislatures -- you know, we have this thing in America that everything is to the states that is not ascribed to the federal government in the Constitution. It makes sense. But the state legislatures have been doing some wildly undemocratic things, starting from gerrymandering, to passing laws that don`t make sense that are entirely out of sync with what their own populations want. Your view is that this is actually more normal than many of us may be familiar with.\par \par \uc2 DAVID PEPPER, FORMER CHAIRMAN, OHIO DEMOCRATIC PARTY: Totally, it`s been going on for frankly, more than a decade. And if we saw all the things happening in another country that are happening in our state houses, we would literally be ringing alarm bells saying you are losing your democracy, gerrymandering, attacks on voters, the nonsense we`re seeing in Tennessee, total lawlessness. In Ohio, they violate the constitution again and again and again to gerrymander districts, ignored their own Supreme Court. If we saw this elsewhere, we would literally see it for what it is.\par \par \uc2 But I`m afraid that we`re so focused on Congress here, and we love focusing on \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  or Santos, there are hundreds of people just like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  in state houses in this country. They`re not just talking or tweeting, they`re passing laws attacking democracy every week if we don`t see it. And we have to start doing exactly what they`re doing in Tennessee, stand up to the bullies, call them out, make them infamous because too often we`re letting -- we`re not seeing it and we`re letting them get away with it.\par \par \uc2 And so, what we`re seeing is this has become the far rights frontline in their attack on democracy far more than in Congress. It`s happening in state after state. And too often those of us who care about democracy don`t see it, we don`t fight back. And so, even when we win the elections for the federal level we get excited about, they`re still winning the battle for democracy because we`re not even contesting their battlefield. We have to change that. We did in `22.We got to do a lot more `24.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:59]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We`ve got to -- we`ve got a lot of reporters on Capitol Hill. They`re some of the state legislatures and state houses where they`re -- you know, there are fewer reporters, there are fewer people covering. And Michael, you were in -- you were part of the Republican Party at a national level and at the state level. I`m talking to \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  shortly. And in, you know, there are places like Wisconsin, and Michigan, and Pennsylvania, and Arizona where Democrats were running in the last election, in some cases on abortion rights, in some cases, on the fact that they were running against people who were election deniers and extremists.\par \par \uc2 You and I have talked about this. Back -- was it 2012, after the 2012 election, the Republican Party commissioned a post-mortem and came up with a conclusion that if everybody were just a little less, if you pulled back from the crazy a little bit, this could be a successful endeavor. And the modern Republican Party has told the Republican Party of 2012, hold my beer.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: They -- in fact, they did. And what is even more stunning is that we were well on that course. You know, not to pat myself on the back but, you know, we tried, you know. In 2010, we elected a lot of good people around the country, the vast majority of whom are no longer serving in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  . And in a number of state legislatures around the country, they got ousted by what came after 2012. You want to get a sense of that, talk to someone like Joe Walsh, who came in the class of 2010, but by 2012, 2014, he was gone.\par \par \uc2 And you see the remnants of that in a way they went after, you know, quintessential conservative Republican in \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  . So, this sense within the party that they not only hold my beer, I got a whole bar behind me I need you to pay attention to in terms of what they`re going to do.\par \par \uc2 But one real quick point I want to stress with, David was exactly right in terms of what`s happening at the state legislative level, that`s the laboratory that Republicans have been working for a number of years now. You may win federally, but trust me, in a couple of years, they`re going to gerrymander that seat.\par \par \uc2 One last point, Democrats do it too. I`m in blue Maryland and we don`t -- we don`t have the numbers that we used to based on the population and the vote support at the grassroots level.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: David, let me ask you one thing, I was talking to Timothy Snyder, the sort of expert on misinformation and the big lie the other day, and I said, what do we do about this stuff? And he said, support local journalism, go to school board meetings, run for office, local office. The bad news is we don`t cover state and local politics where this stuff is happening. The good news is the average voter who is incensed actually has much more power at the state level than they do at the -- at the federal level. And that`s what we saw play out in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: Yes, absolutely. And it`s a real problem the anonymity these places. So, that old spider man quote, with great power comes great responsibility. Here, it`s great power total anonymity, which makes the more dangerous. The local media is struggling makes it worst. And here`s another big issue by the way. More than half of the Republicans who voted out those representatives the other day last November did not even face a contested election.\par \par \uc2 He walks back into office like an appointment, so no one has any idea these people are. We have to bring more attention and we got to run against all these people. Even if you don`t win, running against them makes them accountable. You`re knocking on thousands of doors saying did you know your neighbor, the state rep is an extremist.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, one other big point. Look at any poll. Most Tennesseans support common sense gun reform. The fact that these have ceased being democracies is what allows these people to stand against laws that most people support and literally kick out the representatives like the two -- the two representatives the other day who actually support what the majority of Tennessee have support. These places have broken away from being functioning democracies because no one`s paying attention to them, they`re so gerrymandered, and because at this point, so many of these people -- the reason they`re so arrogant, as Michael Steele pointed out, most of them don`t face an election anyway, so they get worse and worse and worse.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:03]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It is amazing.\par \par \uc2 PEPPER: We`ve got to hold them accountable by running against all these state reps just like we do in Congress.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You`re exactly right. There are a couple of people in Congress who get elected without running against anyone too which shouldn`t be happening in America either. There should be no acclamations in America. Guys, thanks very much for kicking us off tonight. Michael Steele and David Pepper,