er say to you, good to see you because it`s never good to see you, unfortunately. My last guest, David Pepper, said something really interesting about Tennessee. This whole thing in Tennessee was actually about a gun violence protest, a vocal, non- violent gun violence protests in the legislature which is actually a really good place to protest laws.\par \par \uc2 Against the will of what most Tennesseans believe, most Tennesseans, like most Americans, by an overwhelming majority support common sense gun laws. But in places like Kentucky and Texas, and others, they`re going completely the other direction, no restrictions or virtually no restrictions at all on gun ownership.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON WATTS, FOUNDER, MOMS DEMAND ACTION: You raise a really important point which is that the vast majority of constituents, including law enforcement, oppose these dangerous gun extremist laws that are of course supported by gun makers and gun lobbyists. And even when police testify against these dangerous laws, lawmakers pass them anyway. And they`re playing politics. They don`t seem to care. The data shows that these lax laws will kill their constituents.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s pretty intuitive that when we look at data, red states that have weaken their gun laws have more gun violence and more gun deaths, and blue states that have strengthened their gun laws have less gun violence and less gun deaths. We need to follow the lead of these blue states like California and other states where they have strong gun laws. Right now, we`re all only as safe as the closest state with the weakest gun laws. And so, that is why we put pressure on Congress to act. But in the meantime, the state lawmakers have an obligation to look at the data.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I want to just put some of the data up on the screen. We`ve got a few -- a couple of graphs that I want to show you that will just be shocking in terms of mass shootings. It may look when you`re looking -- it may seem as you