nd over the weekend, Washington passed one through both chambers to the General Assembly, and Illinois passed one too. So, I would just ask everyone watching to text Fed Up to 64433. Get off the sidelines and do this work where you live.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:23]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Washington and sounded like an interesting place to be, an assault weapons ban and a three-year supply of Mifepristone. So, you know, who knows? Maybe there`s some good ideas out there. Shannon, good to see you as always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, After a Trump judge tries to ban a vital abortion drug across the country, the one I was just talking about, Michigan`s Democratic governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  , joins me on how states can continue to protect women`s rights. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Today, the Department of Justice asked a federal court to block Friday`s ruling that threatens access to a key drug that`s used in medication abortion. That ruling came on Friday evening from a district judge in Texas who was appointed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . It would suspend the Food and Drug Administration`s 20-year-old approval of Mifepristone which is one of two medications that`s used together in the majority of medication abortions in this country. In fact, they make up the majority of all abortions in this country now.\par \par \uc2 And that further complicates things. Another federal judge, this time in Washington state, issued a dueling ruling on mifepristone on Friday night. His decision in an entirely separate case bars the \uc2 FDA\uc2  from "altering the status quo and rights as it relates to the availability of mifepristone. And now that we have these two contradictory rulings, the issue could soon land before the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, there is bipartisan agreement among the likes of New York Democrat Alexandria Ocasio Cortes and South Carolina Republican Nancy Mace who 