l care for women for decades. The people of this country are telling us over and over again, they expect these fundamental rights to continue to be there for future generations.\par \par \uc2 You look at Kansas, Wisconsin, Michigan. In 2016, they were completely Republican-controlled states. Now, there are three Democratic governors. The people have spoken. They expect these rights. Overwhelmingly they turn out. So, this is not a Republican or Democratic issue. This is an issue that the majority of people in this country expect to have been settled and want to have these tools in the event that a woman is in a position that she needs to make a decision about her future. And guess what, that happens every day in this country.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Now, ahead of the election in November, polling in Michigan showed that 43 percent of your state`s voters said that abortion was in fact the single biggest issue motivating them to vote. On election day, voters approve this amendment to the state constitution guaranteeing a right to an abortion. How much was the threat to abortion rights a factor in you and your Democratic colleagues sweeping the state last year? How big a deal was that particular topic?\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: I think it was a big part of it. You know, we had gerrymandered districts for the first time that helped too. Voting rights was definitely on the ballot as well. But I think abortion was central, right? I did roundtables all across the state, Ali. And it was when I was sitting there, a Republican Woman who said, I didn`t vote for you when he first ran in 2018. I`ve never voted for Democrat, but I`m knocking doors for you and I`m going to make sure that we passed this constitutional amendment, because I`m furious that people are trying to take rights away from my daughters.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: If this mifepristone ruling stands -- and we don`t know how this is going to go, it`s all going to play out this week -- if Republicans around the country get 