ectively a total ban on abortions.\par \par \uc2 To some people, the fall of Roe was couched in terms of letting states decide. And now what we`re seeing is that this is like Margaret Atwood`s Handmaid`s Tale. The stuff that`s happening to women particularly in the southeast of this country is draconian.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: Well, what do these guys stand for? You know, other than to take away rights from people, what do they really stand for? I think that`s where my frustration is. Here in Michigan, we`ve expanded rights for the LGBTQ community. We have expanded workers` rights. We have expanded reproductive rights. We`ve made Michigan a more competitive state that is going to lure talent here. And these other governors and legislatures are trying to roll back those rights to target people.\par \par \uc2 We have big problems in this country to solve. We`ve got to worry about national security, cyberattacks. We`ve got climate change. I mean, there`s a million things we could be working on that all of humanity depends on us getting right, and yet they`re waging war against their own people. It`s crazy. And if, you know, if this continues, I think that we are going to be very much a nation at Risk as well as the democracy that we`ve been fighting so hard to make sure continues and is safe and voting rights.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there`s just so much at risk here, Ali. And I just cannot get my head around where these guys are going. We deserve better. We`re leading here in Michigan. It`s been a productive 100 days. And if people are looking for a state where you can be who you are and have all the rights you`ve come to expect, maybe you should move to Michigan.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Your secretary of state -- I`m from Ontario, so I think the argument about moving to Michigan is actually a really good idea.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: OK.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: But your secretary of state was sort of at the tip of the spear on voting rights, which was also a very, very big 