 a host of problems.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You and I have been talking about conspiracy theories for a long time. Most of them are medical, or at least historically have been. I don`t know how the last few years of politics have changed that. And many of them are rooted in anti-vax stuff. You spoke to her. Did she have some sense about the fact that she had fallen into this, as you say, one of the biggest and craziest conspiracy theories you`ve covered?\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Yes. I mean, things were really hard for her. You know, she was harassed. She was doxxed. Her family was followed. She was harassed at work. People showed up with GoPros to her house. But you know, the hardest thing for her, I think, when we talked was she said that she felt really guilty about other people possibly not getting the shot.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:03]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Oh, wow. Let`s listen to that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Online, anti-vaxxers in the movement are sort of using you as their poster girl.\par \par \uc2 DOVER: It`s hard to think that you are being used to deter somebody from getting the vaccine. And that was really hard for me to cope with.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Are you saying you blamed yourself?\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Yes, absolutely. I would have people say like, how many people did not receive the vaccine because of you. It`s crazy when you go on to save lives and then something like that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right, you have actually talked to a lot of anti-vax conspiracy theorists in the course of the podcast who just did not believe that she was alive. Do you think this changes people`s minds? Because this is my big fear with conspiracy theories, and I`ve learned this all from you, that evidence doesn`t actually change some people`s minds.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Yes. So, part of the podcast is we reached back out to the people who are conspiracy theorists and said, I have her, I have what you wanted. You said you want a video, I got a video. You want a podcast, I`ve got a podcast. I have pictures. I have everything you said you wanted. And it will shock you to learn that those conspiracy theorists moved the goalposts. Now, they wanted her medical information, then they wanted to drive out to Higdon, Alabama to interview herself.\par \par \uc2 And so, at the end of it, we had to just say, you know, we`re never going to convince these people. But I can say today that we`ve convinced a lot of people because in this instance, there was an information vacuum. She wasn`t posting. She really did fall off the map. And so, most of the people who wonder where is Tiffany Dover were normal folks that were just curious and reasonably concerned. And for those people, they now know that the vaccine did not kill her, and that she`s advocating for the vaccine today.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right, so what`s the takeaway here? Because I sort of get it that the hospital told her, don`t get involved in this thing. And a regular person, a nurse, don`t want to get involved in this kind of stuff because the people who post this nonsense have way more time and energy than the rest of us do. What are you supposed to do about stuff like this? How do you fight back against this misinformation if one doesn`t have a personal Brandy Zadrozny around to chase this around for two years?\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Well, we don`t know what to do yet, right. And we see this all the time. Conspiracy theories used to revolve around presidents and popes, and you know, the elite. And now, it`s election workers in Georgia. Now, it`s school shooting survivors and parents of dead children. Now, it`s people like Tiffany Dover. So, we don`t know necessarily what to do to fix it. But this example really shows us what not to do. And it`s you can`t hide.\par \par \uc2 And these -- in this sort of brave new world, truth needs an advocate. And, you know, we have to do that for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: But you bring up a good example, school shootings. This is -- I don`t know what it is about school shootings. For the rest of us, it breaks our heart, but it sort of activates conspiracy theorists. Anything new with vaccine activates conspiracy theorists. Lots of things to do with medical stuff and cures activate them. It doesn`t seem logical to fight back against it because some of these conspiracy theories are so illogical, right? If we saw her get up, then why would you think she died?\par \par \uc2 Have you gotten into that because you and I have talked about this for so long? Is there something to be fought at that level where people are getting activated by the nonsense that they see on the internet?\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: I mean, we could stop school shootings. That would be a start. But since that doesn`t seem like it`s going to happen right now, you know, I think what`s more important is the fact that you just said, we know when these things are going to happen. We can anticipate that. So, if you`re a hospital worker, if you are a pediatrician, if you are in the news media, if you are a teacher, you need to be prepared for this. And the organizations that we work for and who are supposed to protect us to take care of us need to have a plan in place, need to know what to do, and need to know what not to do.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Where do -- where did this misinformation about her mostly spread? Because you and I have spent a lot of time talking about content moderation on the major social media platforms, although some of this stuff skips the social media -- major social media platforms, so where is our attention best spent?\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: It was everywhere. This one was really a special case. And all of the platforms did do something. A couple of months after she fainted, all the platforms were really strangling talking about Tiffany Dover. But this is the sort of example again of the time that we live in that they only grew on alternate platforms. So, the great migration that we`ve seen of conspiracy theorists from places like \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  to more niche platforms like Telegram or \uc2 Rumble\uc2  or whatever it is, these are where you`re going to see all that content right now. And people have found those places, so it`s really not a -- it`s not really not a conversation about content moderation. We`ve just moved so far past that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, you can`t convince the hardcore conspiracy theorists, but everybody knows somebody who believes some of this stuff, right? What`s -- what do you do for them? How do you tell people hey, what`s the -- what`s your version of don`t believe everything you see on the internet?\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: I mean, I think it`s more creating a connection with the people who these conspiracy theories are about. You know, regular people, they might even -- you know, school shooting victims parents have said, you know, I once watched Alex Jones, I once believed in conspiracy theories. But once you can make a personal connection with a person who`s at the center of this, once you can see the harm that these things do, then maybe you`ll be hesitant to share something like that next time.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: One only hopes. Thanks for the great work that you do always. Brandy, we appreciate you so much. Brandy Zadrozny, senior reporter. You can listen to that exclusive interview with Tiffany Dover by scanning the Q.R. code on your screen or by searching Tiffany Dover Is Dead wherever you get your podcast.\par \par \uc2 That`s it for this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now with my good friend, Rachel Maddow. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230411ej4a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for April 10, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Andrew Weissmann \par \uc2 6,922 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:47]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 It is Justin night here on MSNBC. You just -- you just had reinstated Representative Justin Jones from Tennessee. I am going to have the not yet reinstated Representative Justin Pearson join us --\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST, "TRMS": Oh, good.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: As our first guest tonight. And so, it is remarkable to see how fast this story has moved and to see that reinstatement tonight was just a really, really exciting video. And I`m glad you relayed it the way you did during your hour, that mini documentary you gave us.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Well, I have to say -- seeing people on the lawn or the front of the Tennessee capitol today saying, "no, Justins, no peace, no Justins, no peace" as opposed to just `no justice, no peace," I thought that was pretty irresistible. But yeah, I`m looking forward to seeing that with Representative Pearson tonight.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Rachel. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Thank, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, the resurrection that was promised last week came tonight at the state capitol in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. JUSTIN J. PEARSON (D-TN): They tried to kill hope and love, but resurrection is always guaranteed after persecution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That was Tennessee Representative Justin J. Pearson who will be our first guest tonight.\par \par \uc2 Today, Mr. Pearson`s partner in expulsion from the Tennessee House, Representative Justin Jones, was resurrected as a member of the legislature, when local officials in his district following the procedure for filling a vacancy in the legislature voted to fill the vacancy created by Justin Jones` expulsion by Republicans from the legislature, with Justin Jones.\par \par \uc2 Justin Jones was then sworn in as a member of the legislature which was followed by Representative Justin Jones triumphant, truly triumphant return to the House of Representatives while the Republican members of that House watched in humiliation and possibly rage.\par \par \uc2 Local officials and Justin Pearson`s district are expected to meet Wednesday to vote to send him back to the House of Representatives and thus complete the triumphant return of the only two members of the Tennessee house representatives to be expelled because of what the Republicans of the House called a violation of decorum.\par \par \uc2 After not being allowed to speak on the House floor about the latest mass murder in a Tennessee school in which three educators and three and three nine-year-old children were murdered, Representatives Pearson, Jones and Gloria Johnson went to the floor of the House with a megaphone during a recess to respond to and encourage student protesters in the galleries who were protesting the Republican legislature`s refusal to do anything about mass murder in Tennessee, including access to the AR-15 which was used in that school shooting in Tennessee and was used again today in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Today`s mass murder in Kentucky was someone who would pass any background check for purchasing firearms that exists anywhere in America. Background checks do not prevent first time murderers from buying their first weapons of murder, buying their first AR-15s. A 25-year-old man working at a bank in Louisville, Kentucky, showed up at work today with an AR-15, and shot and murdered five people working at the bank, including his bosses.\par \par \uc2 He was a high school basketball star. A graduate of the University of Alabama and a banker. So you can add that to the profile of people who should not be allowed to purchase AR-15s, which is to say everyone.\par \par \uc2 The only thing that can prevent a person like that legally purchasing an AR-15 is to make the sale of the AR-15 illegal, the way it was for years after Senator \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  passed a law banning the sale of assault weapons in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:07]\par \par \uc2 That law stayed in effect for 10 years until Republican President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and the Republican-controlled Congress refused, deliberately refused to extend the life of that law. And in the process, those Republicans have ended lives because \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and the Republican Congress and Republican legislators in state legislatures in Tennessee and Kentucky have done everything they possibly can to make sure that America`s mass murderers are the very best equipped mass murderers in the world. There`s nothing more they could possibly do.\par \par \uc2 If mass murderers had a lobby, they would have nothing to ask for, because Republicans have given them every law they could possibly want, in order to easily purchase AR-15s and kill as many people as they possibly can before, as happened today, the police kill the mass murderer.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know if any Democrats in the Kentucky state legislature across the border from Tennessee will be expelled this week or next week because they protest inaction by Republican legislators. But we can be absolutely sure that the Republicans and the Kentucky legislature will do absolutely nothing to stop the next mass murderer from legally purchasing an AR-15 in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 Am I next? That`s what children protesting mass murders of their classmates around the country are asking. Am I next?\par \par \uc2 And tonight, local bank managers and bank tellers can ask themselves, am I next? Anyone in an American movie theater can ask, am I next? Anyone in the shopping mall, anyone in a church. It doesn`t matter where you are or where you go. You could be next and Republicans insist, that`s the way it should be.\par \par \uc2 I am a man that was the rallying cry in Tennessee, in 1968, that brought the Reverend \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 . to Tennessee to support the sanitation workers strike in Memphis after two sanitation workers, Robert Walker, and Echol Cole, were killed on the job. The workers who collected the trash from the street and threw it into the trucks we`re not allowed to ride inside the trucks, even when it was raining.\par \par \uc2 In the rain, those workers took shelter, by sitting inside that huge scoop at the back of the trash trucks. And when the compressor on their truck suddenly malfunctioned, Robert Walker and Echol Cole sheltering from the rain in the scoop were dragged into the innards of the truck with the garbage. Robert Walker and Echol Cole were swallowed broken up and crushed to death in the trash inside that truck.\par \par \uc2 All of their co-workers walked off the job and demanded the right to join a union. Their protest slogan was simply: I am a man. That was what brought Dr. King to Memphis for what turned out to be the final speech of his life. The mayor of Memphis then got a court ordered injunction to forbid Dr. King from leading a protest march in Memphis.\par \par \uc2 The right-wing conservatives of Tennessee used a legal process to try to silence \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , exactly what right-wing conservatives are still trying to do in Tennessee as we saw last week. Dr. King said that the injunction against the protest march was something that he would expect from the totalitarian governments in China and Russia at the time. To a packed audience inside the Mason Temple in Memphis, the night before he was assassinated.\par \par \uc2 Dr. King said: Like anybody, I would like to live a long life. Longevity has its place but I`m not concerned about that now, I just want to do God`s will and he`s allowed me to go up to the mountain and I`ve looked over and I`ve seen the promised land. I may not get there with you but I want you to know tonight that we as a people will get to the Promised Land and I am happy. I am so happy tonight.\par \par \uc2 Those were the last words publicly spoken by \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  last week this country heard the very first words spoken by our next guest that went national, that had the reach of Martin Luther King`s speeches, reached beyond Tennessee, words that were heard in every corner of this nation. People who heard Dr. King speak have told me that what they heard last week from Representative Jones and Representative Pearson where voices like Dr. King`s, voices that can lead this country through the darkest days with hope.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: This week especially, holy week, we remember also Dr. King and the forbearers of the civil rights movement who taught us about civil disobedience as a way to get action. Dr. King taught us that sometimes there`s a consciousness above rule, above what you might say is law, and that the true forms of protest is non-violent disobedience for less than a few minutes, we and you are seeking to expel District 86`s representation from this House, in a country that was built on a protest, in a country that was built on a protest.\par \par \uc2 You who celebrate July 4th, 1776, pop fireworks and eat hot dogs, you say to protest is wrong, because you spoke out of turn, because you spoke up for people who are marginalized, you spoke up for children who won`t ever be able to speak again, you spoke out for parents who don`t want to live in fear, you spoke up for Larry Thorne who was murdered by gun violence, you spoke out for people that we don`t want to care about, in a country built on people who speak out of turn, who spoke out of turn, who fought out a turn to build a nation.\par \par \uc2 I come from a long line of people who have resisted. I come from a long line of people who have fought injustice. I come from a long line of people who know what it means to face adversity in difficult days, we all have a responsibility, we all have an obligation to do what is just, to do what is right, and to do what is fair.\par \par \uc2 And what I can promise to those of you who have cried today, those of you who mourn the seeming end and assault on our democracy, or I can say to folks who are worried about whether or not as one sign said, "am I next?" but I can say to you is that the movement for justice can never die, because the heart for justice can never be killed, because it lives and it beats in each and every one of us. It cannot die because we will not let it die. I`ll tell you that folks have tried to kill it, they tried to put it on a tree called Calvary, they tried to kill hope and love. But resurrection is always guaranteed after persecution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading off our discussion tonight is expelled -- currently expelled Democratic State Representative Justin J. Pearson of Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very, very much for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it and let me begin just by saying thank you for what you taught us all last week about how to follow the events of what was happening in Tennessee and what was important about them.\par \par \uc2 As you look at the last few days through to tonight, what would you say about winning and losing, who is winning what and who is losing what in Tennessee?\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: At first, my heart goes out to the folks in Louisville who are suffering from another of the countries often too frequent mass shootings because of the inaction of people in state legislatures across this country who are much more willing to listen to the voices of the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2  than the nation of people who are asking for common sense gun laws to prevent weapons of war from being in the hands of normal people. And it is very sad that day after day, week after week, we are having to know that loved ones are preparing for funerals of people who should not be gone this soon.\par \par \uc2 And so, to those families, we grieve and we still mourn with you just as we do our loved ones here in Nashville and in our community too often we experience these murders on a consistent basis. Democracy is losing in Tennessee. We who believe in justice and freedom are losing in a state where you have leaders in positions of power who are wielding that power abusively, and are operating much more like this is a mobiocracy than a democracy. They are operating much more like they are dictators rather than people who are representing the will of those who want to see justice done, folks like Cameron Sexton who refuses to hear the voices and the cries and the dissent of people and would rather have us expelled out of the House than to have District 86`s representation there.\par \par \uc2 And still even a mispersecution, what we are witnessing across the state and across this nation is a resurgence of hope that we can end gun violence, and we can center the voices of those who may not be able to speak anymore but ensure that their lives are not lost in vain. That there is a resurgence of optimism that even in states like Tennessee, we can pass just gun laws, we can make sure we protect our communities and that can be done, if we were made and as we remain persistent to this moment and to this cause, despite all that the Republican Party is seeking to do and despite all that those who want to keep the status quo the same are doing, and that is reason for folks to have hope and to have optimism.\par \par \uc2 But when democracy is at stake in the way that it is, as elected leaders are being expelled from a state house after we were duly elected by the people we represented to do just what we did which was speak up on behalf of them and alongside them in those galleries, those thousands of children and parents who are speaker called insurrectionists, we were sent there to speak up on behalf of them and answer the question that they are telling us to do something, and am I next to respond that we don`t want that to happen.\par \par \uc2 But instead of that, we`re focused on expelling members of the House, rather than answering the calls of our people to do something.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It seems the only vote of any kind in reaction to a mass murder in Tennessee that this speaker has allowed is a vote to expel you.\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: Yeah, I mean this is this is the problem in our state. The response is never to the issue at hand. It is to silence the voice of the minority. It is to silence the conversation of dissent that says the \uc2 NRA\uc2  doesn`t deserve to dictate our legislative policies. It is always to silence the people who are trying to bring folks from the periphery to the center of the conversation, the mothers and the fathers and the grandparents and the siblings who are sad and who are mourning and grieving the loss of loved ones from gun violence that we`re experiencing every day.\par \par \uc2 What we are -- are seeing it from Cameron Sexton, from the Republican Party in Tennessee, that they do not want to have any compassion or any empathy or any care. They would rather legislate inequity and injustice on behalf of gun lobbyists, than actually listen to the voices of people in our community who are asking for us to do something.\par \par \uc2 We oftentimes hear the complaint and I`ve had it myself about the politicians sending their thoughts and prayers. And now that we are in elected positions, we don`t just have to have thoughts and prayers. We can have action and legislation passed that prevents gun violence in the first place. We have budgets that can put resources behind supporting people and communities to ensure that gun violence is not something that is perpetuated.\par \par \uc2 And we cannot look and isolate this issue just to our schools as we see in Louisville, it`s in our banks, as we saw in Charleston. It`s our churches. There`s a proliferation of gun violence in our communities and instead of addressing the issue, Cameron Sexton and the Republican Party is doing things that are anti-democratic in order to silence the voice of people who are exercising the First Amendment in a peaceful way, to ensure that the voices of those who may not be able to speak anymore still ring loud in our present day.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Your colleague Justin Jones told Rachel Maddow in the previous hour that he was on an elevator just today with one of the Republicans who initiated this resolution to expel you. And he said, I asked him, did he learn anything from this experience and he was silent.\par \par \uc2 Can you imagine a moment like that -- that will be in your future as you expect to be reinstated on Wednesday, in one of those elevators in the capitol this week or next week with someone who was who part of the initiative to get you expelled? Can you imagine what you might ask them in a moment like that?\par \par \uc2 [22:20:05]\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: Yes, what I`ll ask them is, what just legislation are you going to pass to ensure that we don`t have more children and families suffering under the brutality of gun violence? What actions are you going to deliberately take to ensure that our communities are actually protected and not offer false solutions about putting more guns in schools or more guns into our community to protect us?\par \par \uc2 I`ll ask, how many hundreds of millions of dollars can we provide to people in our communities who are doing the work of preventing gun violence in order that we can ensure that this is no longer a problem? How much money are we going to put towards poverty reduction? How much money can we put towards Medicaid expansion to ensure those who need access to health care and medical care are able to receive it?\par \par \uc2 That`s the type of question that I`m going to be sure to ask because their silence is something that I do expect.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Former and future Democratic State Representative Justin J. Pearson of Tennessee, thank you very, very much for joining us tonight. Please come back as soon as you can find the time to do so. We need to hear more of what you have to tell us about all of this -- these issues and more. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: Thank you, Lawrence. We`ll keep fighting and we`ll do.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  is calling for an investigation of Supreme Court Justice Clarence Thomas`s acceptance of unreported gifts for over two decades. Senator Sheldon Whitehouse, member of the Senate judiciary committee, will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:26:05]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): I know that there are calls for chief justice to -- for the Chief Justice Roberts to initiate an investigation. I do not think that this court any longer has the legitimacy, and it is the House`s responsibility to pursue that investigation in the form of impeachment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is one reaction to the latest reporting on just how far \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is willing to go in creating an atmosphere of corruption on the Supreme Court and just how much the Supreme Court refuses to in any way police its members.\par \par \uc2 The latest revelations about Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  come from an investigative report in "\uc2 ProPublica\uc2 " revealing \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , quote, has vacationed on Harlan Crow`s super yacht around the globe. He flies on Harlan Crow`s Bombardier Global 5000 jet. He has gone with Crow to the Bohemian Grove, the exclusive California all-male retreat and to Crow`s sprawling ranch in East Texas. And Thomas typically spends about a week every summer at Crow`s private resort in the Adirondacks.\par \par \uc2 We also learned this weekend that \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  is a collector of Nazi and White supremacist memorabilia. \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  is a right-wing Republican with a strong interest in cases before the Supreme Court. He denies talking to \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  about the cases he is interested in at the Supreme Court. The latest reports come nearly two decades after "The Los Angeles Times" first reported that from 1998 to 2003, Justice Thomas accepted more than thousand dollars in gifts, the most of any justice at that time.\par \par \uc2 Justice Thomas`s apparent reaction to that report in "The Los Angeles Times" was to simply refuse to report any more gifts. \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  says that he met \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  roughly seven years into his tenure on the Supreme Court, the fair inference, by any reasonable human being and any reasonable Supreme Court justice would be that \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  wanted to be friends with \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  simply because \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  was a Supreme Court justice. That fact should have controlled and limited Justice Thomas`s acquaintance with Republican billionaire \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  who`s a major funder of Republican campaigns.\par \par \uc2 You know that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  knows he`s in trouble because he finally has been forced to issue a written statement about this latest report, saying early in my tenure at the court, I sought guidance from my colleagues and others in the judiciary and was advised that this sort of personal hospitality from close personal friends who did not have business before the court was not reportable. I have endeavored to follow that council throughout my tenure and have always sought to comply with disclosure guidelines.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  is not satisfied with Clarence Thomas`s written statement. Committee chairman, Senator \uc2 Richard Durbin\uc2 , called on the chief justice of the Supreme Court to, quote, investigate Justice Thomas`s undisclosed gifts and take action to prevent further misconduct.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Senator Sheldon Whitehouse, a member of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  and the chairman of the \uc2 Senate Budget Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 If you could get \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  back before the committee confirmation style hearing, what would you have to ask him at this point?\par \par \uc2 [22:29:49]\par \par \uc2 SEN. SHELDON WHITEHOUSE (D-RI): Well, I would want to do my homework first and do the kind of preliminary investigation that is usually done before there is a hearing on confirmation and that we ordinarily do before really any hearing.\par \par \uc2 And that is why we have asked Chief Justice Roberts to do what we know he can do, because he did it in the Dobbs leak case and that is call an investigation and let`s get the facts out on the table as to what has taken place over these years.\par \par \uc2 And I think what all of this will lead towards is that we are going to have to put an end to this business of having these nine justices be the only people in the entire \uc2 United States government\uc2  who are subject to no review of their compliance with the ethics code.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator, I know if the United States senator is new on the job and wants to know exactly are the ethics rules on gifts and what do I have to disclose, you can just open up that rulebook very clearly written Senate rules about exactly what you can accept, what you can`t accept, what you must declare on a document disclosing your gifts through the course of the year.\par \par \uc2 And yet you hear a Supreme Court justices say, well, you know, I just asked around the building. I didn`t read anything. I didn`t look at any document to guide me on what I can do.\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: And there actually are committees within the judiciary, within the administrative conference that look into these questions. And I`m informed that Supreme Court justices have asked opinions before when they were getting into situations when they thought that there was a question and they wanted to determine it.\par \par \uc2 But just as important as being able to go someplace and ask a question yourself is when somebody else has the concern that you have violated the ethics code, that then somebody neutral is deployed to investigate that allegation and find out what actually took place and come up with recommendation and findings about what actually took place, rather than have that request just come to you and say, oh, I feel pretty ethical, I don`t think I did anything wrong without anybody neutral or anybody knowledgeable looking into the compliance with the ethics code.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So is it your sense that the chief justice has the authority to investigate the situation like this simply because there is nothing written down about what the chief justice can or cannot do, and there is nothing written down that says he cannot investigate?\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: When the Alito leak too place of the Dobbs opinion, he didn`t hesitate to call in an investigation, when I think at the time he probably thought that it was some staffer and they really let loose the dogs, they seized telephones, they went through emails, they took sworn statements, they had affidavits, they brought in investigators.\par \par \uc2 They did all of that and they never mentioned their fellow justices. And then when everybody noticed, wait a minute, didn`t you leave some people out here who also had access to that opinion? They went back and said oh yes, we had this iterative process with the justices, but no statements, nothing that resembles real investigative behavior.\par \par \uc2 So yes he can do it, they made a hash out of it last time they will have to do it right sooner or later. And if they won`t do this themselves then one of the (INAUDIBLE) prescribed judiciary hearings gets to start setting what a proper investigative method should look like for the court to apply.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator Sheldon Whitehouse, thank you very much for joining us once again tonight.\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the Justice Department appealed a ruling today. Made the appeal today of a Trump ruling last week by a Trump appointed judge in Texas that suspended an abortion pill that was approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  23 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:07]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today the Justice Department appealed a ruling by a Trump appointed federal judge in Amarillo, Texas overturning the FDA`s approval of an abortion pill that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved 23 years ago -- saying that -- the appeal says that the judge quote, "provided no basis for second guessing FDA`s scientific judgment.\par \par \uc2 Nevada Senator \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  said, "I`m appalled by this disastrous court decision restricting access to the abortion medication mifepristone. This is just the latest attack on Nevada women`s ability to access abortion care and sets a reckless precedent for a court to overrule an \uc2 FDA\uc2  decision based on science. I`ll always fight back against these anti-choice attacks on reproductive freedom and we won`t stop until we finally protect reproductive rights in federal law."\par \par \uc2 Senator Rosen has just announced that defending abortion rights is one of the reasons she has decided to run for reelection.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:50]\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JACKY ROSEN (D-NV): But we still have big problems to solve. Lowering the cost for the middle class, defending abortion rights, tackling the climate crisis, protecting Social Security and Medicare. With so many important fights ahead, it`s why I`m officially launching my reelection campaign today.\par \par \uc2 What happens in Nevada in 2024 could once again decide control of the Senate. Nevada is always a battleground and this Senate race will be one of the toughest in the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Senator \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  of Nevada. She is running for reelection for the United Senate in 2024. Senator, thank you very much for joining us tonight. This has become something of a new Senate tradition for Democratic senators announcing their reelections to come on to this program and discuss it.\par \par \uc2 But in the -- you have also been responding immediately to this judge`s ruling in Amarillo, Texas, which is unlike anything we have ever seen a federal judge do on any matter involving the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of any drug.\par \par \uc2 ROSEN: Well, I will tell you, I`m glad to be part of the tradition of announcing my Senate race and campaign here on your program.\par \par \uc2 But what I do want to tell you is that it is appalling. It is outrageous that one judge can overrule our \uc2 FDA\uc2 , a drug, like you said, 23 years. We`ve had the data. It`s safe. It`s effective, and what a precedent it would set, that if some judge anywhere decides that they don`t care for a particular drug or they don`t think their judgment on a disease or treatment for a disease.\par \par \uc2 This is unprecedented, it is a slippery slope and we have to stop it now. We`re going to see it go through legislation in the court. I recently signed on to an amicus brief with my colleagues, contesting protesting against this judge`s ruling, and we will see it play out through the court system.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: This is what the Supreme Court seemed to say they didn`t want to happen when they overturned Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 ROSEN: Well, they didn`t think about the unintended consequences. And I want to talk more than just abortion care. This is about women controlling their bodies, women being able to go to their provider and make the choices that are right for them at any stage in their life in the privacy of their provider`s office, without judgment from a judge or a politician.\par \par \uc2 It is between them and their doctor, their nurse practitioner, whoever their provider is. And so we need to do everything we can, that`s one of the reasons I`m running to protect the Senate majority and expand it. Because this matters and if we don`t do this in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , if we don`t have the votes to codify this, who knows what is next, and women are under attack.\par \par \uc2 So if you want to find out what we I`m doing, I`m going to say you can go to Rosen4Nevada.com (ph), find out all the things I`m doing in this regard and others. But it is important, Nevada, like you said, our state motto is battle born, we`re battle born -- and battleground. Races are always tough here. \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  -- as you said, won by less than 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 We have got a fight in our hands. I`m fighting for women`s rights. I`m fighting for our seniors. I`m fighting for every single family that works hard in Nevada and that is why I am in it to win it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Control of the Senate, think people have finally gotten to realize, it`s finally gotten through that control of the Senate is what decides who ends up in these federal judgeships, and obviously control of the presidency. So \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was able to get this judge on the bench in Amarillo, Texas, and the plaintiffs bringing this lawsuit were able to look at every single federal judge, every Trump appointed judge in the country and pick the one they wanted to go to, to file this lawsuit with.\par \par \uc2 ROSEN: Yes. And that`s not how our system should be working. This is -- our judicial system should have people there who look at the rule of law, who look at the precedent and then they make good, sound decisions based on that.\par \par \uc2 This ruling by this judge is frankly unforgivable. And so we`re going to, like I said, do everything we can, go through the courts, go through the Amicus Brief, I can tell you that our local, state, federal -- everywhere we have the fight. Look at the laws that they`re passing in other state that hurt women. They are just against women.\par \par \uc2 This is so wrong and, of course, we don`t even have to look too far against voting rights, equal rights, look what happened in Tennessee this week. Our democracy is under attack.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2 , now running for reelection in Nevada. Thank you very much for joining our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 ROSEN: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back, the continuing disgrace of criminal lawyers who worked for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Andrew Weissmann will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:55]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Because Donald Trump`s criminal defense lawyers are among the lowest forms of life currently licensed to practice law in America, they are defending Donald Trump`s attacks on a judge, on the judge`s wife, and on the judge`s daughter.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:49]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES TRUSTY, TRUMP LAWYER: I think the criticisms of the family were not something personal. It was pointing out that they have a bias, that they have a political interest that is contrary to President Trump`s.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: A lawyer with the most minimal level of professional decency would never defend a client attacking a judge, the wife and the judge`s daughter. And a lawyer with a well developed sense of decency would immediately quit the representation of such a client.\par \par \uc2 That was James Trusty, who is the latest Trump legal buffoon to be sent on television to defend the indefensible.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS HOST: Do you know for sure whether the former president got involved with the unpacking and moving of the boxes or not?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yes. I mean can you imagine -- I mean does anybody in their right mind really think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came down to Mar-a-Lago while still president, I guess, in January, and said hey, these are the boxes I packed, let`s be careful with those. That goes in the dining room that goes in the closet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now, Andrew Weissmann, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  general counsel and former chief of the criminal division in the Eastern District of New York. Heis professor of practice at NYU Law School, and an MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 And Andrew, of course, that was an idiotic, irrelevant reply. Chuck Todd was not asking about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did with documents while he was still president, and that is how Trusty chose to answer it.\par \par \uc2 The question, of course, is what was he doing with them in Florida, and how many of them was he personally handling after he was president, and oh by the way, and this question wasn`t asked by anyone who interviewed any Trump lawyer how did that classified document get in his desk, in his desk drawer? Whose hand put that there?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: So, maybe the way to start is for everyone to understand that every criminal defendant including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , has a Sixth Amendment right to counsel. That is, it`s in the constitution, every criminal defendant has a right to zealous advocacy.\par \par \uc2 And frankly, if you couldn`t afford one, the Supreme Court has said that this state will pay for that. So that is the baseline. And it is important to know that people have a job to do.\par \par \uc2 But Lawrence, you are totally right that there is a difference between what you se Mr. Tacopina or Mr. Trusty doing and essentially spinning out things that are clearly false, so why even bother?\par \par \uc2 I mean, you can go ahead and mislead the public, when you know that is what you are doing in dodging questions. That is on the one hand.\par \par \uc2 And the other, you have Susan Necheles who is one of Trump`s counsel, and y know what, when she was asked about this right after the arraignment, she gave I thought, the best answer. She didn`t try and defend it. She said look he was angry. That is about the best you can do, you know.\par \par \uc2 And I really feel like when you are a criminal defense lawyer you have to maintain you integrity and you have to know when you are willing to quit because you are not going to cross the line.\par \par \uc2 One of the concerns I have about the newest member is that he left a large law firm to now be a solo practitioner, meaning he has one and only one client. It is going to be really hard for that person to say no to his one client.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t expect that we are going to see more of this but I`ll call the Susan Necheles path and we are going to see more of the kind of things that you`ve been showing on your show.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And Andrew, NBC News is reporting today that one of the Trump lawyers, the one who is, at this moment, closest to entering \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  status as former Trump lawyer who I also a criminal defendant, Evan Corcoran, was at the federal courthouse in Washington, D.C. today, where that special prosecutor Jack Smith`s grand jury meets. When asked by NBC news what brought him to the courthouse today, Evan Corcoran said he was quote, just getting coffee.\par \par \uc2 So I`m going to give him a pass on that particular lie. I get the joke.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So, one of the issues here is Evan Corcoran is going to be a witness in the Mar-a-Lago documents case, if that is brought. There is no two ways about that he is a key witness, because he drafted the June certification that we know is false and he has been in the grand jury presumably testifying about who gave him the information that was false in the certification.\par \par \uc2 You cannot be a defense lawyer and a witness I mean, this is not like that Woody Allen movie where you jump back and forth between asking questions and answering them on the witness stand. So at some point he is really going to have to bow out of this case.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:44]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew, you were, you had a pretty accurate gauge about when it was likely we were going to see indictments in Manhattan. I think in the end you were off by one day, if that.\par \par \uc2 There is every indication that the first week of May is when we would likely expect the first possibility of indictments in Georgia because of the cycle of when grand juries meet there, along with the speedy trial laws.\par \par \uc2 But what about the documents case? With your experience, staring at that one, and I know how hard it is from the outside, but knowing what you know about federal investigative procedure, where would you gauge they might be at this stage? What distance from a possible decision, the moment where you make a decision about pursuing an indictment?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So the thing that I`m going to look for is the thing I looked for in Manhattan, which is news reports that the defense has been given an opportunity to come in and explain why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should not be charged. And that includes factually, legally, in terms of discretionary call -- that is usually the very las step we haven`t heard reports o that but I have to say, without giving, I`m not going to give the specific date, but I just don`t see what else there is to do on that case. All of the sort of key witnesses seem to have been interviewed.\par \par \uc2 We`ve heard about the Secret Service going in. We`ve heard about Mr. Corcoran going in. So it is hard to see how much there is that is left. Could there be some cleanup witnesses? Yes. But I think we are very much close to the end.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann, thank you very much for joining us again tonight.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Welcome\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You can hear more of legal analysis from Andrew Weissmann on MSNBC`s new podcast. Prosecute -- why would that be a mini series? Why would that be a very long series. It is called prosecuting former president Trump.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:56:48]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230411ej4a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 MSNBC Interview with Michigan Attorney General Dana Nessel\b0\par\par\uc2 Ana Cabrera \par \uc2 793 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 ANA CABRERA, MSNBC ANCHOR: For more, now, I want to bring in Michigan Attorney General Dana Nessel. Thanks so much for joining us, Attorney General.\par \par \uc2 Michigan has had a rush of abortion rights victories in recent months. Govern \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  just repealed the state`s abortion ban last week, months after voters had enshrined abortion rights into the state`s constitution. And you wasted no time emphasizing that medication abortion is still legal in your state despite this recent ruling. So, how are you prepared to defend that position if necessary?\par \par \uc2 DANA NESSEL, MICHIGAN ATTORNEY GENERAL: Well, the simple answer to that is in every way possible. You know, the voters in our state have spoken, following the decision by the Supreme Court in Dobbs. I mean, the Supreme Court is the one who told us that this was an issue to be decided by the states.\par \par \uc2 And in Michigan, we did exactly that. So, this valid proposal that enshrined reproductive rights into the Michigan constitution passed in overwhelming numbers. And more so than that, we flipped the legislature for the first time in 40 years, pro-choice Democrats now control every branch of government in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we`re not going to take this lying down. We don`t believe -\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: Do you -\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: -- that - sorry, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: Sorry. Please continue.\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: No, we`re not - we`re not going to let one judge in the state of Texas tell us in Michigan what we can and cannot do as it pertains to reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: Do you worry about how the Supreme Court may rule, should it end up there?\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Yes. So, of course, I`m worried about it. And it will, obviously, be in direct conflict with what we heard last year in the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 But that being the case, you know, we`re making preparations in the state of Michigan. We want to ensure that Mifepristone remains available. It`s going to absolutely remain legal in our state. The question, of course, is one of availability. And we think there`s a number of different avenues in which we can ensure that that occurs.\par \par \uc2 But in the meantime, you know, we have this contrary decision by Judge Rice out in Washington, who has told the \uc2 FDA\uc2  that they are not to remove \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval from Mifepristone. And so, what we are hoping is this. We`re hoping that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  will keep the status quo, which it`s been for well over 20 years now. And that this particular product will remain on the market and remain available to people in states like mine, where it`s perfectly legal and there are no laws to the contrary.\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: But that does remain in limbo with this Texas ruling. We mentioned there have been calls for the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to just ignore this ruling. Is that a stance you`re willing to take?\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Well, I think that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  should listen to Judge Rice, who`s indicated that the status quo should remain in effect. Remember, in terms of this ruling from Judge Kacsmaryk, it really undermines every notion of legal jurisprudence that we`ve had historically in this country.\par \par \uc2 You know, this is not an emergency set of circumstances. There`s been no evidence that Mifepristone is unsafe or is dangerous. And in fact, just to the contrary, it`s one of the safest drugs on the market. And so, in cases like this, generally speaking, you know, you don`t have an immediate order that goes into effect prior to the case even being fully heard by the district court, let alone a circuit court of appeals, let alone the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, we`re hopeful that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  will, you know, comport itself to the decision and the order by, you know, Judge Rice. And not make any changes in regard to this particular product so it remains available to people in all the states in which it`s legal.\par \par \uc2 CABRERA: Well, thank you very much for offering your perspective on this. Michigan Attorney General Dana Nessel, great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020230410ej4a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 MSNBC Interview with Sen. \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  (D-NV)\b0\par\par\uc2 Jen Psaki \par \uc2 1,309 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: So, if you thought the Dobbs decision was the last word on the rollback of women`s rights in this country by our court system, Judge Kacsmaryk had other plans.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  of Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for joining me here this morning.\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs decision was, I think, unquestionably a five-alarm fire for abortion access for millions of women across the country. You, Senator, have been sounding the alarm over access to mifepristone for months. How does the impact of this ruling compare to Dobbs, in your view, in terms of the impact on women?\par \par \uc2 SEN. CATHERINE CORTEZ MASTO (D-NV): Well, it`s a continuation, Jen.\par \par \uc2 It`s just what you said. It`s -- we are alarmist, and we should be. But it should -- it started way back when, when they started putting these far right federalist judges on the bench for this very reason.\par \par \uc2 And this is what -- and, listen, I come from Nevada. I`m a state that`s a proud pro-choice state. Two-thirds of the voters here, two-thirds, voted to allow and codify Roe vs. Wade. And we really respect a woman`s right to choose. And that`s Democrats. That`s Republicans. That`s nonpartisans. That`s men. That`s women.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I can tell you, just in the last two years, as I was going around this state talking about my reelection, this was an issue for so many people, because they saw what was happening, this erosion of women`s rights in this country when it comes to the right to choose and reproductive freedom rights, right?\par \par \uc2 So, that`s -- to me, this is a path that far right extremist Republicans started when they started stacking the Supreme Court, when they started stacking these benches with these federalist judges. And now we`re seeing the repercussions.\par \par \uc2 And what`s so crazy about all of this, Jen -- and you know this -- is the repeal of Roe vs. Wade, which is what -- which was their ultimate goal, the ruling in there said, well, let`s leave it to the states.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ MASTO: Let`s give this back to the states and the elected officials. It`s a states` rights issue.\par \par \uc2 Well, my state is a proud pro-choice state, but this decision by this right-wing judge is going to have an impact on whether we can provide the essential care, the safe and effective care that women need when it comes to Medicaid abortion. It`s unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: And -- it is unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 And, again, it`s worth repeating, a judge has never overruled the \uc2 FDA\uc2  on these grounds. There are some members of Congress who have suggested that President Biden and other leaders should ignore the ruling. I certainly understand the sentiment.\par \par \uc2 But do you think that is a viable path forward?\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ MASTO: No, we can`t ignore the rule of -- I believe in the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 And, listen, we -- I -- I -- again, I`m in Nevada. I`m one of those 17 states that you just talked about that has part of that ruling in Washington that is protecting the right to the access to this Medicaid abortion, mifepristone. And it is important that we let it play out in the courts, but we continue to fight in the courts.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is a call to action for everyone, whether it`s in the court system, whether it`s when we go to vote, whether it`s making sure that our elected representatives, particularly those far right elected Republicans who are making these decisions on our behalf, they are listening to the American public, because, in Nevada, two-thirds of the voters support a woman`s -- to choose.\par \par \uc2 And, across the country, a majority of Americans support a woman`s right to choose. Here`s what I know. And this is the -- concerning, I think, for all of us to pay attention, and this is why it is important we see the next step.\par \par \uc2 This is a concerted effort they have had to really erode and take back women`s rights in this country. And this is about our freedom. This -- and, you know, when I talk to people in Nevada, this is about women`s right to choose and the freedom to access the science and the medication they need when they need it.\par \par \uc2 I would never impose my rights -- my beliefs on a woman. I couldn`t -- I don`t know what it`s like to walk in their shoes, for a woman to make these decisions. She should have all of the available information and medication and access and science and doctors and make those decisions that are important for her.\par \par \uc2 But what these right-wing judges and others who are making these decisions for us think it`s the representatives who should make these decisions and take away this right for women. It`s starting with the repeal of Roe vs. Wade. Now they`re taking away a key, key -- a drug that women need with their doctors to make these decisions.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just about abortion. It`s about when there is a miscarriage. The next step is contraception...\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ MASTO: ... going after women`s rights when it comes to our reproductive freedom and the need for contraception. So, this is a concerted effort that we see playing across the country. And we have to be vigilant.\par \par \uc2 I will say this, Jen. The good news is what we saw in Wisconsin, what we saw by the voters in Wisconsin, the voters in Kansas, the voters in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I don`t understand why these elected Republicans don`t get that this is such an emotional, personal issue for individuals. And that`s why you see voters coming out and saying, no, yo