rporate the plaintiffs in Amarillo because they got cosmetics there. They say they have standing because they`re healthcare providers who -- let me -- let me talk about the standing finding here, why can you sue over this, right. The Medical Associates members of standing because they allege adverse events from chemical abortion drugs can overwhelm the medical system and please enormous pressure and stress on doctors during emergencies and complications. Like this entire thing is fabricated, transparently so and it worked, you know, up to this point.\par \par \uc2 IRIN CARMON, SENIOR CORRESPONDENT, NEW YORK MAGAZINE: Well, he did what he came there to do. As you say, this is a closed loop of anti-choice propaganda. I also flag the same phrases the judge did. Post abort of women and girls starves the unborn human until death. But let me just add here that for 23 years in the U.S. and longer in other jurisdictions, people have taken this pill safely. It is a safe -- a better safety profile than Tylenol. There is no epidemic of "post-abortive women" showing up in emergency rooms such that this meetup group alleges.\par \par \uc2 But maybe some of your viewers might be wondering, why are they doing this when abortion is illegal in Texas, illegal in all of these other states. They want obviously, to limit abortion in blue states. But they also realize the power of medication abortion to flout many of these restrictions to use the mail to empower individuals to end pregnancies on their own.\par \par \uc2 They realize that it`s going to be really hard to ransack the medicine cabinets of every woman, every family in America. And if they can crack down on the supply of these pills, they can further tighten device that is already incredibly tight of reproductive health access.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, one of the -- I means, to that -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GERTNER: To put the line -- to put the line to the notion that we want to leave it to the states, we don`t want to leave it to the states. We want to leave it to the red states and that`s all.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s transparently that. And to the -- to the point Irin about this -- the sort of acquisition, right, of this medication which we should say the majority of abortions in the United States are through these two -- through these two drugs. Am I right, Melissa -- and again, I read this very quickly, I skimmed it before I came on air -- there`s a 19th century law called the Comstock Act which bans mailing smut. It was used licentious material, I think, is the statutory language. It was used to stop birth control until the Supreme Court case in Griswold finds right to privacy under substantive due process for people to be able to access that. They`re reviving the Comstock deck. Is that right here?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, first let me just say, I did not mean to suggest that I do not think that this is all pretextual. This is filed in Amarillo where Judge Kacsmaryk is the only federal judge sitting because they knew he would respond in this way. So, it`s very clear what the interest was and why this was done. So, this is not a rational decision in any way and the text of it makes that clear.\par \par \uc2 With regard to the Comstock Act, the Comstock Act was passed in 1879 to prohibit the distribution of "articles used for immoral purposes through the federal mail service or in interstate commerce." And that could be a lot of things, information about birth control, actual birth control, abortifacients, even erotica and sex toys and things of that nature, but it was meant to cut down on vice. The law that you mentioned that was invalidated in 1965 Griswold versus Connecticut was what`s known as a mini- Comstock law, which is a state level Comstock law that aims to do the same thing within the jurisdiction of the state. That was invalidated in Griswold.\par \par \uc2 The Comstock Act, although it`s basically been in a state of destitute in the United States, that was not. 