ared -- is the Supreme Court prepared to layer on top of the outrageous effects of Dobbs or do they feel sufficiently insulated from public opinion? We`re about to find out.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and I just note as a political journalist myself that all political journalists should be in the business of getting quotes from the members of the Republican Party that brought this about, that brought this judge about, that confirmed -- voted for his confirmation but also have produced this moment about whether they think that all of the millions of pregnant folks and women in this country who have access to the drugs should have it taken away from them. That`s a vital matter of public import to get everyone on the record on.\par \par \uc2 Melissa Murray, Irin Carmon, and Nancy Gertner, that was fantastic, at the last second. Thank you all three. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, after Tennessee Republicans set the country back 60 years, one of the Black legislators they expelled from state government joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS, FORMER GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: Hi, this is \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . Congratulations, Chris, on 10 extraordinary years. Thank you to you and the ALL IN crew for helping us understand the world, see a little deeper, and do a little better. Congratulations!\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, the Vice President of the United States \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has just departed the city of Nashville, Tennessee concluding a last-minute trip to meet with the Tennessee three, the Democratic lawmakers who faced expulsion from their seats yesterday. Their visit followed a call with President Biden who thanked the members "for their leadership and courage in the face of a blatant disregard of our nation`s democratic values."\par \par \uc2 The President was of course talking about the extraordinary scene that played on the Tennessee House floor last evening as Republicans attempted to kick out three Democratic members simply for protesting gunfire violence. The incident in question occurred last week when those Democrats joined in with hundreds of Tennesseans who descended on the state capitol. They were calling for new gun control legislation in the wake of a shooting that killed six people, including three 9-year-olds at a Nashville elementary school.\par \par \uc2 Now, the protesters made it into the gallery of the State House. And Democrats Justin Jones, Justin Pearson, and Gloria Johnson went to the front of the chamber to join in their chance for gun control. The Republican Speaker of the House compared their actions to the insurrectionists on January 6, and immediately scheduled yesterday`s vote on whether to expel them for essentially violating house rules.\par \par \uc2 In the end, the Republican supermajority voted overwhelmingly to oust Mr. Jones and Mr. Pearson. The motion to expel Gloria Johnson, however, a 60- year-old white woman failed by a single vote. Now one of the most shocking elements of a whole scene yesterday was the clearly evident rage and vitriol we saw coming from Tennessee House Republicans.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:20]\par \par \uc2 Remember, they hold an overwhelming majority in this chamber, OK. 75 seats to just 22 Democrats. Face basically zero checks on their power, Republicans control both state houses and the governorship. But they did not react well to just a little bit of a challenge for just a little while when the three Democratic members participated in that protest last week.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LAMBERTH (R-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: When you step into the well with a bullhorn and are yelling and screaming, and are sharing with the people of this state that you believe that none of us care, and you shut down the ability of even a response, surely you can at least admit that that is an egregious thing to shut out other voices.\par \par \uc2 It`s not about the rules. It`s about that you elevated yourself above the very dead bodies that had not been put in the ground yet. How can you not get that?\par \par \uc2 GINO BULSO (R-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: The gentleman shows no remorse. He does not even recognize that what he did was wrong. So, not to expel him would simply invite him and his colleagues to continue to engage in mutiny on the House floor.\par \par \uc2 FARMER: Just because you don`t get your way, you can`t come to the well, bring your friends, and throw a temper tantrum with the adolescent bullhorn. That`s why you`re standing there because of that temper tantrum that day, for that yearning to have attention. That`s what you wanted. Well, you`re getting them now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Racial dynamic at play here pretty obvious. You just heard that condescending, demeaning tone used by the white Republican lawmakers speaking about their young Black colleagues. Keep in mind this protest was like a few minutes that they were in the well. You heard their clear overreaction to even the smallest threat to their power.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, again, Tennessee is a reliably Republican state. Increasingly though, its metro areas are becoming more and more Democratic. Just look at Nashville, a thriving boom town, one of the fastest-growing metro areas in the entire country. Davidson County, which encompasses Nashville, went for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  with more than 64 percent of the vote. It was the largest Democratic share of the vote in that county since FDR`s reelection in 1944.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen the state Republican Party react to that threat using its strength in rural areas and gerrymandering to split up overpower the cities. Right now, in fact, Republican lawmakers are in the midst of an attempt to strip power from Nashville`s Metro Council. It`s like their city council. A newly enacted law passed in retaliation because, ready for this, the council refused to host the 2024 Republican National Convention. The new state law will cut the council in half. Just you didn`t -- you wouldn`t take the \uc2 RNC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And this is not just happening in Tennessee. In state after state, Republicans are combining their perfectly democratic legitimate strength in rural areas where they are very good at getting elected, with the illegitimate practice of aggressive partisan gerrymandering and extremely aggressive tactics to lock in their hold on power. And they`re doing it even in the face of statewide opposition.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look at Wisconsin. The Republicans rigged the worst gerrymander in the country last year. This week, the liberal candidate overwhelmingly won a seat -- a seat on the state Supreme Court, the very same court that approved those new gerrymandered maps which now might be reversed. In North Carolina, Republicans now hold a veto-proof supermajority in both houses legislator, despite the fact that the state is not an overwhelmingly Republican state at all. Cities are reliably liberal, a Democrat holds the governorship. He`s been elected twice.\par \par \uc2 Once again, North Carolina Republicans use the power they have to draw maps to execute another extremely aggressive gerrymander that is still being argued over in the state Supreme Court. And the overall reliably red Texas, Republicans state government are constantly battling the blue cities. As the Houston Chronicle reports, Texas Republicans are flooding a slew of new ideas to rein in the state`s largest cities and counties, most of which are run by Democrats. Among the ideas are enhancing state oversight of county- run elections, taking aim at Progressive judges and prosecutors, slashing local officials` emergency powers, broadly restricting the types of regulations cities and counties can enact.\par \par \uc2 This dynamic plays out in state after state. Republicans in majority use anti-democratic hardball, even constitutionally questionable means to maintain their grasp on the reins of power and impose their will. And it`s all part of this panic reaction to the threat posed by younger voters, voters of color, liberal voters particularly in metro areas who the Republican Party continues to alienate.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:11]\par \par \uc2 We saw one of the most extreme examples of this in the Tennessee House last night with the essentially unprecedented expulsion of two rightfully elected Democratic members. And one of those lawmakers is Justin J. Pearson, Democrat of Memphis, Tennessee and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to call you Representative Pearson if it`s OK with you, I suspect you may be soon again.\par \par \uc2 24 hours later, having had a chance to talk to both colleagues and your constituents, how are you feeling about what happened yesterday?\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN J. PEARSON, FORMER REPRESENTATIVE OF MEMPHIS, TENNESSEE: You know, what happened yesterday was an egregious and unprecedented abuse of power by the Tennessee Republican majority. It disenfranchise all of the people of District 86 from the representative that they send here in order to elevate the issues that they care about.\par \par \uc2 We have to remember that the catalyst for this was the killing of six people slain by an assault weapon by advocating that we do something that we end gun violence that we listen to the children and the parents and the adults and grandparents who have come to our capitol consistently by the thousands to say that we act against gun violence.\par \par \uc2 The State House Republicans decided that their best response was not to pass sensible legislation. It was not to say we need to put in effect red flag laws or we need to ban assault weapons, it was to expel members who exercise their First Amendment rights to speak up and to listen to those who want to see more justice done.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Were you at all surprised or taken aback by the visceral ferocity of the reaction from the Republican lawmakers in that body in which you`ve served?\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: No, I am not surprised at all by the way that the Republicans acted. I`m not surprised at all by our expulsion and the overreaction by the Tennessee Republican House Speaker Cameron Sexton or the leaders of the Republican Party, William Lamberth, or Jeremy Faison, and I`m not surprised at all by their actions and their overreaction.\par \par \uc2 I have seen in my time there even serving for the last couple of months how the Republican Party operates in the state of Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 Cameron, the speaker even called himself at one point, overseer, they are not thinking about how we can prevent gun violence. They`re not thinking about how we protect the lives of those children and those adults at schools like covenant and Nashville or my own classmate, Larry Thorn, who was killed in our community that are thinking about that.\par \par \uc2 They`re only concerned about how they can perpetuate a status quo that is dangerous. And that perpetuates evil in the way that it operates and functions.\par \par \uc2 And so, I`m not surprised by what the Republican Party has done. What has to continue to happen is we raise our voices, we fight back against the status quo, we do everything that we can to protect the lives of our communities.\par \par \uc2 Because in Tennessee, Chris, we`ve got a lot of issues as it relates to poverty, as it relates to health care, as it relates to educational opportunity.\par \par \uc2 But this overreach of power, this abuse of power against our community is a signal to other Republican states what they can do to expel voices of dissension, and that is the erosion of our democracy that`s occurring.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I just wanted to follow up if it`s OK with you, you just mentioned that a classmate of yours when you were in school died from gun violence, what effect did that have on you?\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: So, here is an important part of this argument. Larry died actually this year in January, and it wasn`t in a school. This is an important fact, because too many folks are just keeping the conversation to about school safety.\par \par \uc2 But we graduate, we get older, he and I graduated together from high school 10 years ago. But while I was being elected by the people in District 86 in Memphis and Millington, Tennessee, I was also going to the funeral of a classmate who was the exact same age as I am.\par \par \uc2 Because there`s a proliferation of guns that is happening in our communities, because the Tennessee State Legislatures passing laws like permitless carry. Passing laws that say, we need to reduce the age for people to carry guns from 21 to 18. We need to make it so that you don`t need to have a lot of training for you to carry long armed rifles and shotguns around in public.\par \par \uc2 We have communities that are under siege by gun violence every single day. And no, it`s not always a mass shooting, but one in two and three people too often in Memphis and Shelby County are being killed by gun violence.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is legislation has ramifications. And action does as well. And this led Republican led legislature is only leaning into more inaction as it relates to assault weapons. And as it relates to guns instead of doing what the people are asking us to do, which is to do something that can meaningfully help protect our communities and save our lives. They pass bills that they know will not have more of a positive impact for us.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You are -- you are expelled yesterday, and there`s some question about what happens next. I think that you could be reappointed by the local Metro Council in Memphis. And I wanted to read you this reporting of Fox 13 local news outlet there.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:02]\par \par \uc2 A Shelby County commissioner told Fox 13 on Friday that national leaders are threatening to take away funding from Memphis and other Shelby County projects if the County Commission decides to reinstate expelled State Representative Justin J. Pearson, what`s your reaction?\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: Again, I`m not surprised that this Republican-led supermajority legislature is wielding their power, their authority, the ability to pass a budget against a majority black county in the state of Tennessee, and also one of the counties that pays the highest amount of taxes for the state.\par \par \uc2 I`m not surprised that this same legislature that refuses to do something about the ending of gun violence, the same legislature that refuses to do anything about the reduction of poverty, the expansion of Medicaid, the same legislature that won`t educate kids, but will punish them with their grade retention laws is the same one that would go to elected leaders and threaten them as though they were some mob.\par \par \uc2 Because they want to ensure that representation that was elected by the people of District 86 remains as a representative for District 86. And the question for our Shelby County commissioners that I believe the leaders here in Nashville will learn is that you cannot buy us, we cannot be purchased with the price and our democracy is not for sale.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What is your plan?\par \par \uc2 PEARSON\uc2 : To keep fighting with people in District 86, the 80 and 90-year- olds who came to the polls for us to be here to advocate, the kid who cast his ballot for the first time saying I`m voting for justice, because I want to see something different.\par \par \uc2 They continue to inspire and to motivate this movement. And that`s what this is. This is not a moment only in American history. This is a movement to end gun violence. This is a movement to have the voices of people who for too long have been pushed to the periphery, for too long have been silenced, for too long had been told that they don`t belong here that their voices don`t matter that the issues that they care about don`t deserve their day in the people`s house.\par \par \uc2 This is a moment in a movement that says we are going to continue to push forward and push on because justice is still possible even in places like the Republican supermajority, Tennessee state legislature, justice is still possible.\par \par \uc2 And so, for me and for the people who are with me and beside me, my family, my community, this state and everybody who believes that a better day is possible in Tennessee, I can promise and commit that we`re going to continue to fight to get just legislation in this place and turn Tennessee into the place that it is supposed to be and not the place that it currently is.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Justin J. Pearson, who is one of the two representatives expelled by that supermajority of Republicans in the Tennessee State House yesterday, a great pleasure to talk to you tonight, sir, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: Thank you so much, Chris and Representative Johnson and I will be there Monday to advocate gun violence and just laws in our state.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Sounds like (INAUDIBLE) there on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, what was a Supreme Court justice doing on a billionaire`s private jet. One of the reporters who broke this scandalous story joins me right here, ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:42:42]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARI MELBER, MSNBC HOST: I want to send a big shout-out to Chris Hayes and the whole hard-working team at ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES. 10 years is an eternity in this business. You guys have the Emmys and the audience and the love to show for it.\par \par \uc2 So again, a big congratulations, it`s a big deal. I hope you can enjoy it. I hope you savor it. And as Chris Hayes might say, this isn`t your twilight. This is just the early morning sunrise of a long run to come. Congrats you guys.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All week as you see, we`ve been celebrating the 10-year anniversary of this show ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES, but it`s also the five-year anniversary of my podcast Why Is This Happening? And our latest episode is about the intensifying legal assault on transgender people across the country.\par \par \uc2 In just the days between when I recorded this episode and today, four different states have instituted restrictions or bans affecting trans people.\par \par \uc2 In Kentucky, Republican lawmakers overrode the democratic governor`s veto of a bill restricting gender-affirming health care for trans youths as well as which bathrooms they can use.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Idaho Republican Governor \uc2 Brad Little\uc2  signed a bill banning transgender minors from accessing gender affirming care.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Indiana`s Republican Governor \uc2 Eric Holcomb\uc2  followed suit, signing another ban on transgender care for minors into law.\par \par \uc2 And that same day in Kansas, another Republican legislature voted to override another Democratic governor in this case Laura Kelly`s veto to impose a ban on transgender youth in sports.\par \par \uc2 The speed with which these bands are moving across the country is both dizzying and nauseating.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to Chase Strangio, the Deputy Director for transgender justice with the ACLU`s LGBT and HIV Project who is himself a trans man and an expert on trans rights.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHASE STRANGIO, DEPUTY DIRECTOR FOR TRANSGENDER JUSTICE, ACLU`S LGBT AND HIV PROJECT: Throughout history, attacking trans people has been a gateway to some of the most far right and extreme fascist governments that we`ve seen, in part, because if you erode this level of bodily autonomy to the point where you can posit someone, as so deviant and destructive that the state has the authority to prevent them from existing, and you start with a group of people that is widely misunderstood, that becomes a gateway to other things. And I think there`s that.\par \par \uc2 I also happen to think that in a time of great just anxiety in the world, that trans people represent a type of freedom that is deeply disconcerting to people. Like, the one thing people think they know about the world is like you`re born a boy or a girl and that is like comforting with that restriction. And we`re just like blowing it up. And people are troubled by that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:10]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s a really, really important conversation, particularly right at this moment, you can scan the QR code on your screen to listen that full episode now and find Why Is This Happening? Wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has been forced to issue a very rare public statement defending his actions in the wake of a blockbuster piece of investigative reporting by \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  that published yesterday.\par \par \uc2 [20:50:08]\par \par \uc2 A story examines the relationship between Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and Dallas billionaire \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  depicted here to write the justice in this incredible painting with \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2 , \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  guru, showing them smoking cigars alongside conservative lawyers at Crow`s resort.\par \par \uc2 Now, over the last 20 years, the real estate magnate and Republican super donor has been lavishing Thomas with gifts. Here`s an example, in 2019 just after the courtroom ended, Justice Thomas and his wife Ginny hopped on a private plane and toured Indonesia for nine days on Crow`s 162 foot yacht complete with private chef. These images you`re seeing are from what appears to have been a pretty dope trip.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the thing, according to \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 , "If Thomas had chartered the plane and the yacht himself, the total cost of the trip could have exceeded $500,000." That would be pretty steep for a government official making a little more than half of that each year but, "fortunately for him that wasn`t necessary. He was on vacation with real estate magnate and Republican mega donor \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2 , who owned the jet and the yacht too.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s the Adirondacks Resort owned by \uc2 Crow\uc2 . Publica reports -- \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  reports, "Thomas has been vacationing at Topridge virtually every summer for more than two decades. For comparison rooms at a nearby hotel built by the Rockefellers start at $2,250 a night. Crow`s invitation- only resort is even more exclusive. Guests stay for free."\par \par \uc2 Crucially, Thomas did not report any of this. It is unclear if accepting and not disclosing these gifts violated federal law, some ethics experts suggest the practice runs afoul of the post-Watergate Ethics in Government Act.\par \par \uc2 So today, Justice Thomas issued this statement saying in part that early in his tenure, he sought guidance and "was advised this sort of personal hospitality from close personal friends who did not have business before the court was not reportable. Guidelines are now being changed. And it is of course, my intent to follow the guidance in the future."\par \par \uc2 Now that excuse is a little suspect I got to say because as the LA Times reported, in 2004, Justice Thomas listed several guests from Crow on previous financial disclosure.\par \par \uc2 For example, in 1997, Crow flew Thomas on his personal plane to San Francisco area. Crow and his wife Kathy and she also gave Thomas the Bible that once belonged to Frederick Douglass. And disclosing the gift in his report for that year, Thomas valued the Bible at $19,000 and listed the Crows as personal friends.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing, after the LA Times ran that story on the financial disclosure, as Thomas himself had reported, Thomas simply stopped reporting gifts.\par \par \uc2 It all begins to look a lot like he`s been trying to hide something for the last 20 years and now he just got caught.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Josh Kaplan, one of the reporters of \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  who broke the story yesterday. Great reporting, Josh, great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KAPLAN, REPORTER, \uc2 PROPUBLICA\uc2 : Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just give us a sense of the scope of what you uncovered in terms of these two man`s relationship and the gifts that Thomas and his wife Ginny have received.\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: So yes, so I mean, the trips stand out both for their lavishness and for their frequency. So, we`ve found that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has been accepting these luxury trips virtually every year for more than 20 years without disclosing that. And --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s a lot.\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: That`s a lot. It`s a lot. So, multiple international cruises on a super yacht, private jet flights around the world, virtually annual, if not annual vacations at this private resort. The scale is unprecedented in the modern history of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What are the financial disclosure rules here?\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: Yes, so Justices are required to disclose gifts over $415 to the public.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I feel like -- and I`m not great at math, and I don`t know the prices of everything. But I feel like a private jet ride is bigger than is more than $415.\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: Yes, but it is important to emphasize there are exceptions in the rule and the kind of exception that as Thomas seems to be alluding to in his defense. And also that experts have latched on to is like, seemingly, what might justify the disclosure, his failure to disclose is something called the personal hospitality exemption. And it is extremely technical. It`s actually not that technical.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. It`s like if someone has you over your house.\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: Right. So, if someone -- if you go over to someone`s house for dinner and they serve you food, you don`t have to report it.\par \par \uc2 And what it actually says is food, lodging and entertainment don`t have to be disclosed, if they`re at someone`s house or a property they own.\par \par \uc2 We actually -- after Thomas released a statement today, we went to 77 ethics law experts, including former Congressional Ethics lawyers, former White House Ethics lawyers, from both parties. They all said that you know, things like this particularly private jet trips clearly need to be disclosed and have been Thomas is saying that that`s not the case, they say he`s wrong.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:16]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A private jet trip isn`t a hospital -- I mean, the Adirondack thing maybe, you put in that.\par \par \uc2 But can I just say this, and I`m going to editorialize, you`re just a reporter. So I don`t want to put this on you. But like, the man`s job is to judge. He`s one of the most powerful judges in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Judging is when the text of some guidelines statute constitution is unclear or ambiguous. And then you use your judgment to think about what you should do. In this case, he used his judgment in the face of what he contends is ambiguous wording to say I can take hundreds of thousands if not millions of dollars worth of gifts from a billionaire and not tell anyone about it.\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: And I think it`s worth noting that Thomas did not respond to, you know, I think what has been for many people the largest concern about this, which is a broader ethical concern, not the specifics of the disclosure law.\par \par \uc2 You know, we talk to current and former federal judges who said that this broke long-standing norms for judges conduct and one said, it`s incomprehensible to me that someone that would do this.\par \par \uc2 You know, judges sit in a unique position of public trust, and they`re supposed to hold themselves to the highest standards of property.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, I just want to say, as a news person, as a journalist, you could never do this. Like it`d be wildly, wildly, wildly inappropriate for like, a reporter at \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 . Like, for me, I would never do anything like this.\par \par \uc2 Also, I want to dig into Crow here. I mean, they keep saying he has no business for the court. And it`s true, that like if he gets into a lawsuit with his neighbor, it`s not going to \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But this guy is an early patient of the Club for Growth. He`s been on the board of the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  for 25 years. He sits on the board of the \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2  and other conservative think tank. Like, these conservative groups have been in for court all the time. They`re filing amicus briefs all the time. Of course, they`re before the court, right?\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: I mean, so obviously, these think tanks do a lot of things. But I mean, what is important to emphasize here is that he has spent millions on groups that are committed to moving the judiciary to the right and the law to the right.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, and it`s also worth emphasizing here that, you know, he`s given a lot of dark money and we don`t have the full picture of where that dark money went.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Some of the money you guys were able to track down is money that appears to have gone and donations to Ginny Thomas, right?\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: We didn`t track that down. That`s a political finding from 2011.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, great. OK. So there`s a there`s something he`s also donated money to groups that Ginny Thomas runs, on a salary from there`s like a very -- that`s even more direct into the household of the Thomas (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 You`ve also got the sort of broader ethical question of like, what is the code of conduct for the justices and who`s regulating?\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: So, all judges other than the Supreme Court have a code of conduct that you know, requires them to avoid even the appearance of impropriety. Technically, the Supreme Court doesn`t have one.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, because they run the federal judiciary.\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: They run the federal judiciary and then --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They get to regulate themselves.\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: They get to police themselves. And I think -- and I think that`s a, you know, a route, you know, in my opinion, probably the root issue here is that the Supreme Court has very little transparency and very little oversight compared to you know, virtually every other member of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Is there any evidence and again, I can`t like it`s hard to report negative, but like that this is not just a one off, that there`s lots of justices across the ideological spectrum and spending lots of time with lots of billionaire`s super yachts?\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: So, in terms of what`s known, there`s nothing comparable. But you know, it`s worth underscoring that nothing we found was disclosed and it all happened in secret.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, frankly, it took a lot of work. We are looking at all the justices, and we`re going to keep looking.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, tell me about the work. I mean, you know, Thomas says, well, you know, there`s a sort of nothing to see here. Everyone knows these guys are friends. Everyone knows he`s rich. What`s the big deal? But you guys are like, you have like, plane spotters, and you had like an image of a tip that he gave, he gave someone on one of the yachts in New Zealand, a signed copy of his book as a tip. Thank you so much for all your hard work on the New Zealand adventure. I wish you all the best \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . You guys obtained an image of this, like you had to track all this down?\par \par \uc2 KAPLAN: Yes. We talked to dozens of people around the world from, you know, workers on the billionaire Harlem cruise super yacht to members of the Bohemian Grove, which is a secret of all men`s club and to Indonesian scuba diving instructor.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:05]\par \par \uc2 And then we pieced that together with records we got from sources and from the Freedom of Information Act and eventually put this all together.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right. Josh Kaplan, great reporting. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN for the week. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now with Ali Velshi in for Alex. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230408ej4700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for April 7, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Jonathan Capehart \par \uc2 6,739 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Julia Alvarez will join me to talk about her modern classic, How the Garcia Girls Lost Their Accents.\par \par \uc2 [22:00:07]\par \par \uc2 The book follows the four sisters and their parents from the Dominican Republic to the streets of New York City. It`s written reversed chronologically. The reader discovers the four girls` distinct worlds and identities piece by piece.\par \par \uc2 Decades after its original publication, it`s still deeply resonant. It talks about family dynamics, adolescence and the immigrant experience.\par \par \uc2 Time now for THE LAST WORD with \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  who is in for Lawrence tonight. Good evening, my friend.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Hey, Ali, great to see you, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Have a great show\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: All right. This historic week rolls on. Monday, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  arrested and charged with nearly three dozen felonies. Tuesday, voters in Wisconsin ended the right-wing lock on the state Supreme Court. And yesterday, the Tennessee three faced expulsion votes for daring to demand action after six more children and school staff were shot to death with an assault rifle at Nashville elementary school last week. A political debacle that turned into a firestorm last night after the nearly all white Republican supermajority voted to expel the two black lawmakers, but not the white lawmaker.\par \par \uc2 Both President Biden and former President Obama spoke out after the dramatic speeches by State Representatives Justin Jones and Justin Pearson. Today, Vice President Harris flew into Nashville to respond to the ongoing protests against gun violence and the expulsion of the black representatives. That is where we had planned to start tonight. And we will have much more on Tennessee coming up.\par \par \uc2 But just after 5:00 P.M. Texas time, a Trump-appointed federal judge ruled to suspend the FDA`s approval of the abortion pill mifepristone, siding with anti-abortion groups in a case that could upend access to medication abortion in all 50 states. That`s right, in all of 50 states, even in states where abortion is legal. The ruling will not go into effect for seven days. And just in the last hour, the Justice Department filed notice that it will appeal the judge`s ruling to the conservative-leaning Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals.\par \par \uc2 But it`s hard to understate the importance of this ruling for crippling abortion access nationwide. Mifepristone, one of two medications used together to induce an abortion has been on the market for more than 20 years and has become one of the most common and private methods for abortions in the country. And as \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  points out, quote, it would be the first time a court order the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to remove a medication from the market despite opposition from the agency and the drug`s manufacturer.\par \par \uc2 This ruling is exposing the lie we heard from Republican lawmakers and the conservative majority on the Supreme Court when they overturned Roe v. Wade. This is not about sending abortion rights back to the states. It`s about abolishing women`s rights in every state in the country, including states where voters have spoken loud and clear in support of reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 Here is what Vice President Harris said as she received the news on her way home from Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: There is no question that the president and I are going to stand with the women of America and do everything we can to ensure that women have the ability to make decisions about their health care, their reproductive health care in a manner that is what they need and they decide that, not their government.\par \par \uc2 This is a drug that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved as safe 20 years ago, and has been proven to be safe for 20 years. So, this is a dangerous precedent and I will have more to say after I review the decision in its entirety. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And leading off our discussion tonight, is Michele Goodwin, Chancellors Professor of Law at the \uc2 University of California, Irvine\uc2 , and a visiting professor at Harvard Law. She is the author of Policing the Womb, Invisible Women and the Criminalization of Motherhood, and Dr. Jamila Perritt, an OB-GYN and abortion provider, she`s also -- she`s president and CEO of Physicians for Reproductive Health. Thank you both very much for coming to THE LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 Professor Goodwin, there`s even more news on the abortion ruling tonight less than an hour after Judge Kacsmaryk`s ruling. A judge in Washington State issued a ruling that directly contradicted the Texas decision, ordering the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to make no changes to availability of mifepristone. The conflicting orders by two federal judges appeared to create a legal standoff likely to escalate to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:03]\par \par \uc2 How do you think this will likely play out in the next seven days and in the Supreme Court if it gets there?\par \par \uc2 MICHELE GOODWIN, LAW PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA, IRVINE: Well, let me say, it`s a pleasure to be back with you and also with Dr. Perritt. This may in fact been the intention behind the petitioners who sought out the judge in Amarillo, Texas, Judge Kacsmaryk, who, in the hearing involving this matter, asked the petitioners exactly how they would, in fact, like for him to rule. And in reading his order today, it seems that he leaned in quite heavily on exactly what they were expressing, including the chief thing what they wanted for him to do, which was to order the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to remove this drug from the marketplace.\par \par \uc2 Now, if it goes up before the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , petitioners may, in fact, find a group of a sufficient number of justices on the Supreme Court who may also lean into exactly what it is that they are looking for. And, in fact, it may have been why teasing up this conflict, such that it could get to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And, Dr. Perritt, tell us about this pill, what benefits it has, and what will be lost if women in America lose access to it?\par \par \uc2 DR. JAMILA PERRITT, OB-GYN AND ABORTION PROVIDER: Thank you so much. I also want to express gratitude for the opportunity to be here. So often, we have conversation about medical care and particularly in reproductive health care space and we never talk to doctors. We never talk to people that are seeking this care. So, it really is, I think, critical.\par \par \uc2 I want to say that -- you know, I want to be clear, I`m not a doctor -- I`m sorry, I`m not a lawyer. I`m not a politician. I am a doctor, and I have trained for more than 20 years to do this work. And our ask is that we are allowed to take care of people in the way that they need.\par \par \uc2 When we look at this medication specifically, and Professor Goodwin mentioned it in her response, this has been approved for more than 20 years in this country and has been in use for decades around the world, not just in the United States. We know that it is safe. We know that it is effective. So, this assault against our care, our opportunity to make decisions about ourselves has never been about the science, it has never been about the medicine, it is only about an attempt to eliminate access to abortion care specifically and reproductive autonomy more broadly.\par \par \uc2 And it has implications not just for abortion access. We use this medication for labor and delivery. We use it for a miscarriage management. We use it throughout obstetrics and gynecological care. And so banning this medication has significant implications for the health and well-being for all of our communities, even for folks who think they will never need an abortion.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And, you know, Professor Michele Goodwin, The Texas Tribune notes, quote, Kacsmaryk has deep ties to the anti-abortion movement and the language reflects those ties, calling abortion providers, quote, abortionists and describing the procedure a killing or, quote, starving the unborn human until death. Professor Michele Goodwin, I this an ideological lawsuit masquerading as a medical or regulatory one?\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: That`s a very good question. Let`s keep in mind that the American public now has the lowest confidence in the \uc2 American Supreme Court\uc2 , and I think that could hold true too for the full federal bench, that is to say that American perceive it as being vulnerable to political tides. And in this case, sadly, it`s quite transparent.\par \par \uc2 And that what one sees in this is that a group of petitioners have come forward asking for a federal judge to do something that has never been done before, which is to remove a drug that has been approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  from the marketplace.\par \par \uc2 Furthermore, in asking them to do that, it is a drug that they said was rushed to the market. But the reality is there was 54 months of review. The other drugs that were approved in that same period had 15 months of review. So, there was no rush to the market.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also worth noting that the allegation is that this drug is unsafe. That, too, is untrue, in that this drug is actually even safer than Tylenol and Penicillin in terms of the health effects. And so what we see then is not only than this question involving a kind of forum shopping for a judge, not only do we see the sort of political influence an decisions like this, we also see the distortion of science and medicine and the inflammatory use of language such as establishing a personhood in fetuses and embryos.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:05]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, Dr. Perritt, I saw you nodding in agreement with Professor Goodwin, talking about the safety in efficacy with of mifepristone. What I would love to know is the medical response to the language used by the judge in his ruling, calling doctors like you, quote, abortionists and the reproductive care that you give as, quote, starving the unborn human until death. What is the medical response to language like that?\par \par \uc2 PERRITT: It`s inflammatory. It`s violent. It incites violence for folk like me that have been working under really arduous circumstances often to take care of our communities and to support people in making the decisions that are best for themselves, for their families, for their communities.\par \par \uc2 It`s challenging to me to see folks with no medical training or background attempt to legislate medicine, and that is not what we are asking for. You didn`t go to med school, you didn`t go to residency training, you are not a nurse, you are not a nurse practitioner or a midwife.\par \par \uc2 And so the idea that you could possibly know more than folks who have studied this for our entire careers or even folks that are making these really critical conditions decisions for themselves and their families is not just arrogant, it`s dangerous. And it is actually the foundation of reproductive health care in this country in the attempts to control and restrict the fertility of people with the capacity for pregnancy. It has caused confusion. It has caused fear, not just for folks seeking abortions but for folks that are seeking care throughout the reproductive life course.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s terrifying and it is just the beginning I think that that`s the important thing for people to understand. This is the beginning of the assault. It is not the end. So, the calls for compromise, the calls to cede ground, the calls to relinquish our human rights is what we are being demanded to do, right? And so we have to stand strong and firm and we can stand on the science and the medicine and say, this is not care. This is not medicine. This is not science. This is not what we have been training to do. This is not what our communities deserve.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Dr. Jamila Perritt, and Professor Michele Goodwin, thank you both for joining u tonight.\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PERRITT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Coming up, the reason Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was in Nashville today and her message to the protesters there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 K. HARRIS: They understood the importance, these three, of standing to say that people will not be silenced, to say that a democracy hears the cries, hears the pleas, who hears the demands of its people, who say that children should be able to live and be safe and go to school and not be in fear.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: That was Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in Nashville today after meeting with the Tennessee three, Justin Jones, Gloria Johnson and Justin Pearson. Last night, former State Representatives Justin Jones and Justin Pearson were expelled from the Tennessee House of Representatives. Here is Justin Jones before Republicans in the Tennessee legislature voted to remove him from office.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. JUSTIN JONES (D-TN): This is not about me. This is about those young people who are asking us to use our position and to uphold our oath, to protest and dissent from any action or legislation that is injurious to the people. I pray that we uphold our oath on this floor, because, colleagues, the world is watching.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And the world did watch, as racist Republicans in the Tennessee legislature voted to expel two young black legislators for protesting gun violence that left three nine-year-old children dead at a Nashville elementary school. The world watched those same Republicans a vote to spare Gloria Johnson, a white woman, from expulsion, who is accused of the same so-called crime of protesting for gun reform in a state with some of the weakest gun laws in the nation. State Representative Gloria Johnson had this to say after Republicans failed to find enough votes to remove her.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. GLORIA JOHNSON (D-TN): Keep watching. To the nation, keep watching. We are losing our democracy. We need to make sure that we stomp out this march to fascism.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Gloria Johnson speaking directly to the student protesters at the Tennessee State Capitol, who have been demonstrating in the aftermath of the latest mass murder for the simple reason, they don`t want to be murdered in their classrooms.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , more than 349,000 students have experienced gun violence at school since Columbine. There were more school shooting in 2022, 46, than in any year since at least 1999.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:07]\par \par \uc2 More than 349,000 American have been traumatized by Republican inaction.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden spoke with Justin Jones, Gloria Johnson and Justin Pearson to, quote, thank them for their leadership and courage in the face of a blatant disregard of our nation`s democratic values. Our country needs to take action on gun violence. And to do that, we need more voices like theirs speaking out. Today, Vice President Harris called out the Republicans who voted to expel Representatives Jones and Pearson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 K. HARRIS: Let`s expel them. Can you imagine? Let`s get rid of them entirely. Let`s remove them not only for that moment but remove these people who have been elected to represent the people and let us decide who should represent the people. What is that? That is not a democracy. That is not a democracy.\par \par \uc2 You can`t walk around with your lapel pin and you are not representing the values that we hold dear as Americans. You can`t walk around and talk about protocol. Protocol and procedures were devised to require and allow and encourage debate and discussion and, yes, dissension. But these so-called leaders tried to shut it down instead. But we are not having that. We are not having that.\par \par \uc2 And so the thousands of young leaders who descended on the Capitol and continued to organize, continued to require that the voices be heard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: That was the vice president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Democratic State Representative Torrey Harris of Memphis. Representative Harris, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 You were there for the three expulsion votes. How do you make sense of what happened yesterday?\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. TORREY HARRIS (D-TN): Thank you for having me. Jonathan. You know, what we saw on yesterday was Tennessee racism confirmation day. That`s what we saw. And our skin color offends them so much that they had to take out two young black men, Representative Justin Pearson, Representative Justin Jones, and those are my brothers and I`m going to stand by them.\par \par \uc2 They are tired of us challenging them, simply put it. We are losing what we see as our democracy right before our eyes and our Tennessee House Republicans literally just showed their hand. You had legislators who were unsure attorneys serving is not only the witness, the prosecutor, the jury and the judge of two black men on yesterday. And our voices are going to get heard, regardless, out of all of this.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Well, a couple of people who have heard you are President Biden and former President Obama. They showed their support. But what did it mean to have the vice president of the United States actually come and be with you today?\par \par \uc2 T. HARRIS: What I saw with Madam Vice President Harris in Nashville with us on today was the ability for us to say that we are unified. That`s what we are. We are one unit. Whether it`s the state, whether it`s the federal level, we are one unified body of people. To be able to see another black woman come and stand with black people, black men in our community to know that there is someone else fighting just as hard for us.\par \par \uc2 The world is watching Tennessee right now. It is something that everyone keeps on saying and it`s really, really true. You can be assured that we are going to continue to fight with our partners in the federal level for the justice that we need here at the state of Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, Phil Williams, a local Nashville reporter, tweeted, quote, privately, some Republicans expressed concern to me that these events are not only alienating independents but also GOP women who don`t want their kids getting killed at school, GOP women who don`t want their kids getting killed at school. That`s not optics. That`s policy. Is this a sea change moment?\par \par \uc2 [22:25:00]\par \par \uc2 T. HARRIS: It really is. And so what we are going to see is next year`s election time is going to show real change, because this is not going to stop just today. This is going to be another year-and-a-half of us really working really hard to make sure that people who are in the suburban areas, suburban women, are seeing that our parties are not working together for a reason, because race plays a part, because gun violence is playing a part.\par \par \uc2 I want to mention that there was a turning point on yesterday, I think, for a lot of people, when they were watching us live on television from their homes when Representative Pearson was being minimized to a boy, who you would have thought was a slave, by the sponsor of his expulsion. And all of a sudden he pauses and for a moment he had to ask the body, is that a way you would like to be talked to?\par \par \uc2 And people are watching that, no matter what color you are, what race, what backgrounds you are, but the way you they were spoken to, the way they were spoken to is being seen by everybody. And so representation really matters and everybody is going to see a change in that.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: I want to bring in two students who helped organize these protests, Brynn Jones and Ezri Tyler, our March for Our Lives organizers from \uc2 Vanderbilt University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You have both been organizing and protesting at the statehouse since Monday. Ezri, who are these protesters and what drives you to be there?\par \par \uc2 EZRI TYLER, STUDENT ORGANIZER, MARCH FOR OUR LIVES: Yes, absolutely. These protesters is a broad coalition of students across every single level of education. We have students as young as six years old coming out, we have middle schoolers, high schoolers and we also have university students from across not only the city of Nashville but also across the state. We have busloads of students coming from Memphis, from Knoxville, to support their representatives.\par \par \uc2 And besides the students, we have a coalition of parents and elected officials at every level, from school boards all the way to our state reps that we are standing behind now\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And, Brynn, you wrote in The Tennessean, quote, I have message for the members of the Tennessee legislature. Shame on you. Shame on you for spending time and taxpayer money on banning books and drag, trying to rename streets in Nashville and reducing the size of city councils. Are these really the issue that are affecting Tennesseans the most while kids are dying in our schools?\par \par \uc2 Brynn, has any Republican member sat down with you t discuss this issue?\par \par \uc2 BRYNN JONES, STUDENT ORGANIZER, MARCH FOR OUR LIVES: No, they haven`t. I`m from Nashville, actually. I`ve lived here for almost all of my life. And I have six generations of Tennesseans in my family and not a single one has reached out to any of us to talk about what it is that we really want behind these issues, which it`s not anything radical, it`s common sense gun control. We want to stop people from dying in our cities. We want safer communities.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, Representative Harris, what effect has it had to have had people raising their voices in the statehouse?\par \par \uc2 T. HARRIS: I mean, it has been a very powerful moment. I don`t know if you are seeing all of the movement ever since the first protests here at the Capitol. It was a magical experience. To be a part of all of that, to start with them on the outside and come here and be on the inside now, it`s a life-changing experience to watch.\par \par \uc2 There were thousands and thousands of people here and it was done in such unification to the point where people were standing out in the rain. People were packed on the inside of the building. It was so organized people. Were actually getting seen and their voices were being heard. Their representatives had to see them, walk past them and actually had to speak and hear them calling out for gun control, calling out for the things that really matter to people, not this pettiness that`s going on right now, where we are acting like little kids.\par \par \uc2 I was, a few months ago, was the youngest elected official here, and we brought into more young elected officials who are now expelled. And, unfortunately, it`s sad to have to watch. I think this is going to be turning point. People are going to take their eyes off of what is going on here in Tennessee and I expect people to be out here protesting again here on Monday, Thursday and every single week moving forward until some change happens here.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Ezri Tyler and Brynn Jones, thank you for coming to the show and thank you, personally, for what you are doing. Representative Harris, please stay with us, because up next, we will be joined by a professor from a historically black university in Nashville to talk about the dark roots Justin Jones alluded to in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:34:28]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES (D-TN), FORMER STATE REPRESENTATIVE: What you are really putting on trial is the state of Tennessee. What you are really showing for the world is holding up a mirror to a state that is going back to some dark, dark roots; a state in which the \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2  was founded, is now attempting another power grab by silencing the two youngest black representatives and one of the only women -- Democratic women - in this body. That is what this is about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:35:00]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: The Republican expulsion of two black legislators in Tennessee is shining a light on the state`s long history of racism. While Tennessee was the first confederate state to be re-admitted to the union after the civil war ended in 1865, the white majority in the state had no interest in giving up any of its power.\par \par \uc2 In 1875, three weeks after Congress passed the Civil Rights Act of 1875, the Tennessee legislature passed a bill allowing business owners to continue to discriminate based on the color of a person`s skin.\par \par \uc2 In the years to come, lawmakers in the Tennessee state house would work to, quote, "redeem the south by restoring white supremacy and the exploitation of black labor".\par \par \uc2 From 1866 onward, the white majority in Tennessee would end up passing at least 20 different Jim Crow laws outlawing interracial marriage, and keeping schools and transportations segregated.\par \par \uc2 As we saw this week, this is not just ancient history either. Many of those Jim Crow laws were still on the books well into the 20th century. And the bust of \uc2 Nathan Bedford Forrest\uc2 , a confederate general and the first grand wizard o the \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2  was only removed from the Tennessee state capitol in 2021.\par \par \uc2 And just last month, Republican state representative Paul Sherrell, you all, he was forced to apologize after he suggested that Tennessee should bring back hanging by a tree, also known as lynching, as a form of capital punishment in the state.\par \par \uc2 So, with all that context in mind, it`s hard not to agree with this op-ed by activist Aftyn Behn that was first printed in the Tennessean newspaper back in 2019 yet remains painfully accurate today.\par \par \uc2 "Let me be clear. Tennessee is a racist state. Racism is in the air we breathe, permeating the state capitol codified in the legislation being passed at the detriment of women, communities of color and the working poor.\par \par \uc2 Our problem with racism in the state`s wild and untamed and Justin Jones has turned a mirror to the legislature and the gubernatorial administration, inviting them to look inward and prompt introspection towards their ideologically destructive agendas.\par \par \uc2 Look into the mirror Tennessee. If you don`t like what you see, shift."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now Learotha Williams Jr., professor of African American history at \uc2 Tennessee State University\uc2 , a historically black university. And back with us is Democratic Tennessee State Representative Torrey Harris.\par \par \uc2 Professor Williams, thank you for being here. I`d love to get your perspective on everything we saw yesterday. How does it fit into Tennessee`s long troubling history of racism?\par \par \uc2 LEAROTHA WILLIAMS, JR., PROFESSOR, \uc2 TENNESSEE STATE UNIVERSITY\uc2 : I was -- thank you for having me on this evening. I was deeply troubled by what I saw because it was something that I had read about, that I had studied in history, but I had never anticipated witnessing this in real time.\par \par \uc2 As I listened to the young man speak yesterday, I thought about how, you know, just a couple of summers ago Justin Jones was out in the Legislative Plaza protesting. They were involved in the \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  struggle that gained a lot of notoriety and he was arrested and placed in jail and faced a lot of heat for just taking a stand, by saying that \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 People in his district responded by electing him and sending him inside the house. And now the response to that has been to kick him and Justin Pearson out. And it did not escape me the language that was used yesterday -- didn`t escape me the talking down to these young men, as though they were - - they were little boys. These two men were elected in the same process that everybody else was elected in the chambers but they did not receive the same respect.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:44]\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But as I thought about it in the larger historical context, it`s -- and we look at African Americans relationship with the state, it`s always been one where we either have been partially included or totally excluded from most of the good things that the state has to offer.\par \par \uc2 You referenced the KKK -- the KKK was a violent response to the idea of black equality, to the idea of black freedom. And we talk about this in class a lot about how Tennessee was very quick to rejoin the union to be reconstructed, to consider itself reconstructed.\par \par \uc2 But less than a year after the war ended, you had a group of guys that meet in a judge`s office in Pulaski in Tennessee and they formed the KKK which was, by any definition, a terrorist organization.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right, right, right.\par \par \uc2 Representative Harris, let me get you in here. Take a look at this moment from the house floor yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: What we did was the only logical thing to do after a session defined by extreme silencing of representatives of this body, a session defined by multiple moments in which the attempt to even bring up gun violence was immediately out of order.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That was your 20 minutes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Knowing the Republican supermajority as you do, Representative Harris, how do you see that moment?\par \par \uc2 TORREY HARRIS (D-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: The silencing of our voices is no longer what it used to be, where it was with whips and chains and from hanging trees. What it is now is a rope around our voices, around our throats as we speak.\par \par \uc2 We have been limited to how long we can speak. Vice President Harris said something earlier today, that if what you are saying is so right, then why not allow me to debate with you on it?\par \par \uc2 The ability to speak out and speak on whatever the issues are gets limited because they only want us to see -- they only want us, the public, to see only so much of what goes on.\par \par \uc2 I actually was pleased by the performance that took place on yesterday in a way that was inspiring to so many people because most people don`t get an opportunity to really, truly see how we are treated.\par \par \uc2 So, when we were treated the way we were in public, you can only imagine how we are treated in private.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: The silence is there and it`s forced to be there. It`s in the rules for us to only speak for so long -- being black and being loud and being vocal is a problem for them.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, Professor Williams, we have a little less than a minute left but as a professor, what conversations are you having with your students about this?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: On the one hand, I want them to realize that protests have been part and parcel of our experience here in Nashville. And yesterday was very much reminiscent of the Civil Rights Movement in the 1960s which was just a couple of blocks away from the capitol where John Lewis, Bernard Lafayette and Diane Nash were removed from spaces where they were unwelcome.\par \par \uc2 Their voices that challenged Tennessee to be committed to equality were stumped out, were erased. So we saw something similar to that occur yesterday.\par \par \uc2 We like to celebrate this city but the city needs to do better by its citizens.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Amen to that. Professor Learotha Williams, Tennessee state representative Torrey Harris, thank you both for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, more on our breaking news tonight. A Trump appointed judge ruled to end women`s access to the abortion pill days after voters in Wisconsin made their voices heard loud and clear in support of reproductive freedom. That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:20]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: President Biden has just issued a statement about the ruling from a Trump-appointed federal judge in Texas against the abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 "This does not just affect women in Texas. If it stands, it would prevent women in every state from accessing the medication regardless of whether abortion is legal in a state.\par \par \uc2 It is the next big step toward the national ban on abortion that Republican elected officials have vowed to make law in America. My administration will fight this ruling.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice has already filed an appeal and will seek an immediate stay of the decision. But let`s be clear, the only way to stop those who are committed to taking away women`s rights and freedoms in every state is to elect a Congress who will pass a law restoring Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris and I will continue to lead the fight to protect a woman`s right to an abortion and to make her own decisions about her own health. That is our commitment."\par \par \uc2 [22:49:57]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: It`s worth remembering that support for abortion access in America stands at 64 percent. Wisconsin Republicans were re-educated about that on Tuesday when Wisconsin voters chose to upend the political direction of their state by electing a liberal candidate to the state Supreme Court, flipping majority control from conservatives.\par \par \uc2 The result means that in the next year, the court is likely to reverse the state`s abortion ban and end the use of gerrymandered legislative maps drawn by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And remember, Kansas, a ruby red state, overwhelmingly voted to uphold abortion rights when given the chance in last year`s election cycle. The architect of the Republicans` grip on power in Wisconsin, former Governor Scott Walker, said this about Wisconsin voters giving liberals control of the state`s Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT WALKER, FORMER WISCONSIN GOVERNOR: I think the larger issue here -- and we`ve seen it particularly in Wisconsin but across the country, is younger voters. All they have heard are radical ideas of climate change and defunding the police and abortion and all these sorts of other issues. And so they`ve never heard the opposing viewpoint.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Access to abortion isn`t a radical idea, as Scott Walker called it, to most Americans. Voters keep sending Republicans a message but Republicans seem incapable of listening.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now John Nichols, national affairs correspondent for "The Nation". His latest work is titled "Wisconsin chooses a progressive justice in the most important election of 2023".\par \par \uc2 John, great to see you again. You are a Wisconsin native. Your family has been there for generations. Why was this election so critical?\par \par \uc2 JOHN NICHOLS, NATIONAL AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT FOR "THE NATION": Well, it was an election for control of a branch of government. And if you go all the way back to 2011, when Scott Walker came in, Republicans had control of the executive branch, the legislative branch, and the judicial branch.\par \par \uc2 They used that control of the judicial branch to approve all of the abuses that they committed in the other two branches. So the judiciary in Wisconsin has become a really big deal. It was, as you note, a 4-3 conservative majority. On Tuesday, it flipped to 4-3 liberal majority.\par \par \uc2 And as you are suggesting here central to that election was the issue of abortion. And abortion rights was a huge issue in this race because the liberal candidate -- Janet Protasiewicz said from the start of her campaign that she was going to talk about her values and one of her values was a woman`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right. I interviewed her on "THE SUNDAY SHOW" and she made that exact point. But you know, John, after President Obama was reelected in 2012, there was a Republican autopsy that concluded to quote "Politico", the party would re-brand as a kinder, more inclusive GOP.\par \par \uc2 John, how did the Republican Party go from that to Donald?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Well, I would argue that Scott Walker and Wisconsin started to clear the way for that change. Walker and the Republicans in Wisconsin assaulted voting rights, they assaulted labor unions. They gerrymandered the maps. They used their power in an extreme and abusive way.\par \par \uc2 And I think that opened up kind of a new stream within the Republican Party that Trump picked up on. What we really need to understand is that at core the problem in Wisconsin for now a dozen years, has been those legislative maps which gave so much power to the Republicans, that even when Democrats won the most votes statewide, they did not advance in the legislature.\par \par \uc2 So now, this court - this new court - has the potential to address those maps along with a host of other issues like abortion rights and labor rights.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: But after what we saw in Tennessee this week, are Democrats in Wisconsin preparing for this new judge - Judge Protasiewicz - to be impeached?\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS\uc2 : There`s been talk of that but I think it`s highly unlikely. This is an important thing to understand. The Republicans do now have -- because of a special election -- a supermajority in the state senate. Therefore if the state house were to impeach, the state senate could convict. So you have that outside possibility.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the important thing to understand. The Republican leaders in the state senate have said they don`t want to go there and they are smart to say that. If they try this, it would blow up on them in all sorts of ways politically, just as things blew up on them on April 4th.\par \par \uc2 And there`s one other subtlety to remember, Jonathan. If the Republicans removed Janet Protasiewicz, the liberal Democratic governor of Wisconsin, \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  would replace that removed justice.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:50]\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS\uc2 : So the bottom line, is that the Republicans -- I don`t think they will go to impeachment. But as Tennessee teaches us, they are constantly looking for some way to disempower anybody that gets in the way of their power grabs.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: But that is a good data point. A good thing to know there, John. Thank you, as always, for coming to THE LAST WORD, John.\par \par \uc2 And tonight`s LAST WORD is next\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Before we go tonight, I want to share just a little bit more of the remarks made in the Tennessee state capitol yesterday by Justin Jones, on a day when he and another black lawmaker were expelled from the general assembly by Republican lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: What we see today is a lynch mob assembled to not lynch me but our democratic process.\par \par \uc2 This is a historic day for Tennessee. But it may mark a very dark day for Tennessee. Because it will signal to the nation that there is no democracy in this state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:52]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: We end with a programming note. I hope you will be watching when ousted Tennessee state representative Justin Jones will be my guest tomorrow on "THE SATURDAY SHOW", along with California Democratic Congresswoman Barbara Lee, for whom he interned. That`s at 9:00 a.m. Eastern here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 I will also see you again on Sunday at 9:00 a.m. at Eastern for "THE SUNDAY SHOW".\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230408ej4700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for April 5, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Andrew Weissmann, Neal Katyal, Michelle  Goldberg \par \uc2 6,792 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:31]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 And, I hope my friend Carlos Clemente -- Clemente in Los Angeles is watching to know that his cousin, the book about his cousin, Roberto Clemente, is back on the shelf where it belongs.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": Thank goodness. Where it belongs, exactly.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, exactly.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Have a good show.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  will never be president, but he will be witness against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  has decided not to appeal a judge`s order forcing him to testify to special prosecutor Jack Smith`s grand jury investigating possible crimes by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , leading up to and on January 6th. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  has already told much of that story for money in a lifeless book he wrote titled "So Help Me God".\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  appears to have told his story to other authors like, possibly, Bob Woodward and Robert Costa as an unnamed source for books -- for their books about the final days of the Trump White House.\par \par \uc2 And now, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  will testify under oath to Jack Smith`s grand jury about every single word \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said to him when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was trying to get Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to break the law and overturn the presidential election on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 If those conversations with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  are evidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed a crime, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  could be the star witness, the \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  in the case of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  -- the United States of America versus \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  , if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is charged by Jack Smith`s grand jury with crimes leading up to and on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Mike Pence`s refusal to appeal the ruling that he must testify is an indication of just how much \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  himself might not have believed the absurdist grounds on which he was claiming he did not have to testify. It is more likely that future losing presidential candidate \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was trying to somehow preserve what he thinks is his perceived loyalty to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , by fighting or pretending to fight Jack Smith`s effort to put \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  under oath in front of the grand jury. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was only willing to go one round in that fight, instead of taking it to an appeals court.\par \par \uc2 Today, the leader of the Republican Party once again called for defunding the police. No one near the leadership of the Democratic Party has ever called for defunding the police. Defund the police was a protest slogan used in protest marches against the police murder of George Floyd. It has never been a political platform of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 But today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said Republicans in Congress should defund the Department of Justice and \uc2 FBI\uc2  . No Democrat in history has ever proposed anything like that. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has also proposed defunding the Manhattan district attorney which means defunding the \uc2 New York Police Department\uc2  and the police officers who work in support of the district attorney`s office.\par \par \uc2 Last night in a speech to an assembly of rich Republicans at his Florida club, who have heard it all before from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  once again attacked the Manhattan attorney and his family. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  once again attacked the presiding judge in the criminal case in Manhattan, in which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is now charged with felonies. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  also attacked the judge`s family.\par \par \uc2 This is unprecedented in the history of American jurisprudence. The district attorney and the judge are showing special lenience to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a situation where certainly in other states, lawyers assure me \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would already have been ordered into court based on what he said last night and forced to show why he should not be held in contempt of court.\par \par \uc2 It is impossible to express more contempt of court than to attack the court, which is to say the judge and the judge`s family and to lie about them. But the Manhattan district attorney and the judge presiding over the case, no doubt, to shield themselves from any sense of political bias, they are going far beyond their normal level of tolerance for contempt of court, so that they can appear not to be prejudiced against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 [22:05:23]\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  calls the Manhattan district attorney and the judge in this case Trump haters. He calls their wives Trump haters. There is nothing improper about a prosecutor or a judge hating a defendant. It has happened many, many times.\par \par \uc2 But they do take an oath to treat that defendant fairly. Prosecutors and judges at the Nuremberg trials hated the Nazis who were put on trial for the extermination of six million Jews. Many prosecutors have hated murderers who they have prosecuted. Many judges have hated rapists of children and murderers of children who have appeared before them.\par \par \uc2 But their oath required them to treat those defendants fairly. The prosecutors have organized crimes bosses like John Gotti in Manhattan who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tries to model himself after had every right to hate John Gotti but they had no right to treat him unfairly as a defendant. I know prosecutors who hated defendants they prosecuted and it made no difference to how they do -- how they did their jobs. Some of those defendants were found guilty, some of those defendants were found not guilty.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s ramblings into a microphone last night delivered yet another confession that special prosecutor \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  can evaluate in his investigation of Donald Trump`s possession of government documents. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said: As president, I have the right to declassify documents and the process is automatic. If I take them with me, it`s automatic.\par \par \uc2 Good luck trying that under oath, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  continues to speed ahead in forcing former Trump staff to testify under oath to his grand juries. CNN is reporting, quote, former top national security officials have testified to a federal grand jury that they repeatedly told former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies that the government didn`t have the authority to seize voting machines after the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 CNN has learned \uc2 Chad Wolf\uc2  , the former acting homeland security secretary and his former deputy, Ken Cuccinelli , were asked about discussions inside the administration around DHS seizing voting machines when they appeared before the grand jury earlier this year. According to three people familiar with the proceedings, Cuccinelli testified that he made clear at all times that DHS did not have the authority to take such a step.\par \par \uc2 One of the sources said, Trump`s former national security adviser \uc2 Robert O`Brien\uc2  in a closed door interview with federal prosecutors earlier this year also recounted conversations about seizing voting machines after the 2020 election, including during a heated Oval Office meeting that Trump participated in, according to a source familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is Andrew Weissmann, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  general counsel and former chief of the criminal division in the Eastern District of New York. He`s a professor of practice at NYU Law School. Also with us, \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  , former acting U.S. solicitor general. They are both MSNBC legal analysts.\par \par \uc2 And, Andrew, I want to begin with the last point I mentioned and tie it back to the first point.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a report of grand jury testimony about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  trying -- asking about seizing voting machines. There`s also the fact that we now know \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is going to testify to the grand jury. It seems the Pence testimony could be a lot more important than this voting machine testimony depending on what that voting machine testimony is. If it`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  asking, can we seize voting machines, and he`s told that we`re that you can`t, no matter how much yelling is involved, there may not be any crime in asking those kinds of questions compared to what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was asking the vice president to do.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes. so I agree with you. Obviously, the devil`s in the detail (ph). But I do think that voting machines could be more (AUDIO GAP) think at first flush, is that the reports are that these are (AUDIO GAP) of people (AUDIO GAP).\par \par \uc2 And so this could just be one of many, many in his own administration, his people saying that what you are trying to convince us of did not happen, and we are not going to take steps based on this erroneous case.\par \par \uc2 But I agree with you that it is much more likely that the vice president has -- if he is truthful -- key information. It could go as far as Mar-a- Lago to talk about what he knew about documents being taken and training, and that was given to him and to the president about what was his and what we`re not his, to obviously much more central things to the January 6 investigation, private conversations, including just -- did he think that they won the election? Because, of course, he was the vice president, and did he have any conversations with the president about whether they won or not?\par \par \uc2 But I think a key thing that I`ve always thought was going to be critical for Vice President Pence is the January 5th email where you have the president saying that the vice president agrees that he has the power to not count the votes. The reason that is so critical is that the vice president in the grand jury says that is not true and I did not say it and that was not my position and you know that because the very next day, I did not do that.\par \par \uc2 That means that you know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the day before January 6 was lying, and this case is about his intent. And if you can show that he deliberately was fomenting his people with a lie about the power of the vice president, I think that is a huge home run for \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  , how many ways does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have to publicly confess in the document`s case for \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  to be ready to go? This latest one, as president, I have the right to declassify documents and the process is automatic. If I take them with me, it`s automatic.\par \par \uc2 Now, the government has not even referred to the fact that the documents are classified when they were going for their search warrant. That was not what it was about. It was about government documents, classified or not, along with other elements that this strange statement that sounds like a confession plays into.\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yeah. So, first of all, Lawrence, before we get to the stolen documents case, I think the big news tonight, I think America many Americans people around the world are asking, why is this night different than any other night? And for the first time, for the second day in American history, a former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is under arrest, and under arrest for what`s going on in New York. I don`t think we should lose sight of that as we talk about these other investigations, including the stolen documents.\par \par \uc2 With respect to stolen documents, it`s astounding to me this guy can`t keep his mouth shut. And, yes, absolutely, this is all -- every time he opens his mouth, there`s further evidence for \uc2 Jack Smith\uc2  and the prosecution. And, you know, yesterday`s speech is a really good example of this. Trump flies to Mar-a-Lago to rebut the charges against him in New York.\par \par \uc2 Mara-a-Lago is, of course, the scene -- it`s a crime scene. It`s a scene of this other investigation into the stolen documents and Trump cannot help himself from saying exactly what you pointed out, from attacking the district attorney in New York. You know, it`s par for the course for him.\par \par \uc2 I mean he was told he lost the election, so he claims he won it. He was told he needed to give back this sense initial security material, so he says, I`m going to keep it. He was told he shouldn`t make remarks about in New York that would incite violence, and that`s what the judge warned him yesterday. So he goes ahead and does it anyway, you know?\par \par \uc2 So it`s a gift for prosecutors and, you know, I -- if I were his attorney, I`d be really worried.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann, can you explain to lawyers around the country outside of New York what the reluctance is in a Manhattan criminal court on the issue of contempt of court when a defendant specifically attacks a judge, specifically attacks the judge`s wife, attacks the judge`s daughter, there are lawyers in other jurisdictions who are telling me that the judge without any motion by the prosecution at all would immediately the next day summon the defense lawyers into court to show cause as to why the defendant should not be held in contempt. There -- they say that what they`ve seen in the Trump statements in other states is contempt on its face.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: So I think it could be a combination of things. As you suggest, some of this is in fact bending over backwards and trying to give him extra leeway so that there is all appearances of fairness. And I do think that there is a legal issue here, which is New York has one of the most pro- defense issues with respect to when you can be remanded to jail and when you can`t.\par \par \uc2 Now obviously if you commit a crime in front of the judge, there can be summary contempt. But you do need to have for contempt, usually an order from the judge that you are then violating.\par \par \uc2 Here the judge took the first step of giving notice and saying what he expected. The next step would be some kind of order and I do think we will get there because as Neal correctly points out, he can`t help himself. I mean if there is some somebody trying to exert control over him, he will violate that rule no matter what is being said.\par \par \uc2 But there is a sort of very pro-defense, pro-defense rights regime now in New York that does cabin the discretion of a court as to when they can actually hold someone in contempt and send them to jail. But we could get there knowing that this is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . As I think I said last night, if he is charged in federal court the rules are very different and there is an easier path for a judge to do what Amy Berman Jackson almost did with \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2  , but she took the step of saying, all social media, you`re out, because of what he did when he was out on bail.\par \par \uc2 But in federal court, he could find himself in jail awaiting trial.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  , I for one don`t think there`s any practical way a former president can be sent to jail. I think the home confinement is the best you can do given that they have a dozen Secret Service agents with them, and the complexity of that. However, contempt of court in a lot of jurisdictions involves fines. The judge can just hit him with a big Trump fine, payable immediately that day. I mean, it`s just -- it`s a marvel.\par \par \uc2 I get what Andrew said and I`m learning a lot about the New York position on this, and it is different from other states and I think that`s very helpful and enlightening about where we are tonight. But it`s hard to imagine another year of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  attacking this judge and his family.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: There are certainly fines, Lawrence, that are available and before though, if we go far, I do want to push back against the premise of your question, because it does seem to me that jail should be available for a former president that commits crimes. I don`t think you get some special treatment just because you have Secret Service protection, you know? And even this crime in New York, there`s a lot of criticism of the district attorney for saying that this is, you know, aggravating this to a felony, which I think is wrong. I think there`s a lot of reasons why it will be considered a felony, and that that charge can stand up in court.\par \par \uc2 But even if it`s a misdemeanor, in New York, a misdemeanor has up to a year in prison as part of its criminal activity. And remember, it was a guy named \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who in 2016 campaigned on "lock her up, lock her up" about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  . What was -- what was he accusing \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  of? Not a felony, a misdemeanor. So, this is serious stuff.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  , Andrew Weissmann, thank you again very much for starting off our discussion tonight. Always appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And you can listen to more of Andrew Weissmann`s analysis of the Trump indictment on the new episode of MSNBC`s podcast series "Prosecuting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ".\par \par \uc2 And when we come back, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  called for death and destruction in New York City yesterday, but what he got was our next guest and me on a beautiful day, wandering around his tiny group of protesters who never got a chance to see him and mostly looked as dispirited as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  looked at the defense table. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:22:59]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: He landed at LaGuardia Airport, which is named for Fiorello LaGuardia, the first mayor of New York to serve 12 years in office. The next day, he rode to his arrest and arraignment in Lower Manhattan on FDR Drive, the most famous urban highway in America named for Franklin Delano Roosevelt, a New Yorker who was the only president elected four times to the presidency before term limits were imposed by constitutional amendment.\par \par \uc2 On his way to plead not guilty to a 24-count indictment approved by a Manhattan grand jury, he passed two federal courthouses both named for New Yorkers. The Thurgood Marshall United States Courthouse named for Supreme Court Justice Thurgood Marshall who did his most important work as a civil rights lawyer while living in Harlem and running the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  legal defense fund.\par \par \uc2 The other was the Daniel Patrick Moynihan United States Courthouse named for a New Yorker who served 24 years in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  . The glorious new addition to Penn Station on the West Side of Manhattan is also named for Senator Moynihan. It is a short walk away from the Javits Center a massive convention center named for Jacob Javits who served 24 years in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  as a liberal Republican back when there was such a thing as a liberal Republican.\par \par \uc2 The Javits center is next door to the Lincoln Tunnel, one of the miracles of American infrastructure when it was completed in 1937 under the Hudson River. The Lincoln Tunnel, of course, named for President \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  . And it took some of the load off the spectacular George Washington Bridge, which crosses the Hudson River, near Grant`s Tomb in Manhattan, the monument to President Ulysses S. Grant.\par \par \uc2 As he rode back up FDR Drive, after pleading not guilty, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could gaze out the window at a magnificent monument to Franklin Delano Roosevelt that was built just a few years ago on Roosevelt Island in the East River.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:02]\par \par \uc2 New York City is willing to change the names of important things in order to honor people who deserve such an honor. The name of New York City`s largest international airport was changed to honor President \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2  after he was assassinated. And the name of the bridge \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  drove across to and from LaGuardia Airport was changed recently to honor JFK`s younger brother Robert F. Kennedy, a New York senator who was assassinated in 1968, while running for president.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows that his hometown will never change the name of anything for him. The name Trump will never be honored in New York City. The name Trump in New York City to borrow a phrase from FDR will live in infamy. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows it.\par \par \uc2 And New York City proved it again yesterday when the protesters \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  summoned to the courthouse to protest his arrest did not show up. It was a tiny turnout. By my count walking among the crowd of Trump supporters, there were maybe 100 to 120 of them, not always easy to distinguish them from the news media near them.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won only 12 percent of the vote in Manhattan in the last presidential election but that`s 85,185 votes in Manhattan. That means there were thousands of Trump voters who live within walking distance of the courthouse.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  couldn`t get one percent of the people who voted for him in Manhattan to walk down to the courthouse or take the subway. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  understandably wants to move his trial to Staten Island, to cross the harbor on a ferry ride to Staten Island because he won Staten Island in the last election. Staten Island is the only Republican section of New York City and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won 123,320 votes in Staten Island, and not one percent of the people who voted for him in Staten Island were willing to take a ferry ride over to the courthouse for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s biggest vote count in New York City was in his old neighborhood of Queens where he got 212,665 votes and it`s possible that no one from Queens bothered to go to the courthouse because that`s how tiny the crowd for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was. In New York City, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got 691,682 votes and at the courthouse yesterday, he got dozens of people.\par \par \uc2 It was as chance would have it, the single most beautiful day of the year 2023 in New York City so far, the warmest sunniest day of the year, and the anti-Trump protesters at the courthouse thought that wasn`t a coincidence. That`s one of the pictures I took among the anti-Trump crowd, which was at least as big as the Trump supporting crowd. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  called for death and destruction yesterday and he didn`t get it.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, another witness to history yesterday, Michelle Goldberg. Her latest column in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  " is titled, "You Could Have Walked A Block Away and Had No Clue Trump Just Got Arrested".\par \par \uc2 Michelle, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I was thrilled to see your column today because I thought people won`t believe me that when you -- if you went down there, there was nothing and, you know, Donald Trump`s lawyer told the following lie in court. He said: All of Lower Manhattan was shut down today. He was telling the judge about, you know, please don`t make \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  come back here very often because look at how difficult it is. All of Lower Manhattan was shut down today.\par \par \uc2 Exactly one section of one street was shut down yesterday. It was just remarkable down there.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Right. Okay, if you don`t mind, I want to correct you on one thing because there actually has been a building in New York city I believe changed because of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . I`m pretty sure that the tenants or the owners in one of his apartment buildings or condo buildings demanded that his name be taken off of it --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: -- because of what it was doing to real estate prices.\par \par \uc2 But yes, I mean, not only was it a small crowd, it was also the usual suspects. You know, if you`ve been sort of around Trump world since he descended that golden elevator, this was really the kind of the diehards, the dregs, you know, the member of the -- you know, the former member of a violent cult who then later on started a group called Blacks for Trump. You know, just sort of various people who have made a career out of hawking Trump merchandise or being sort of Trump`s sideshow performer, or you know kind of D-list hangers on\par \par \uc2 It tells you something when one of the most famous established politicians who actually made their way down to the park was George Santos.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes. Yes. And \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  , who barked into a bullhorn for about five minutes and that was it. That was it, that was the whole thing. And no one else.\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  didn`t come up there and cry. He saves that for television for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . I mean it was a wonderful test, I think, of America`s resistance to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  now calling for death and destruction.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t tell us what`s going to happen in Atlanta. I mean you would think that in a southern state like that, there might be a better turnout for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . But there were 600,000 Trump voters in New York City yesterday who resisted the impulse to go anywhere near there.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: And also, you know, the people who came out for January 6th, right. Washington D.C. like New York City is not a hotbed of Trump support. But in the past, people have been willing to travel. I don`t think we should discount the possibility of death and destruction, especially on an individual basis.\par \par \uc2 I doubt we`re going to see a lot of mob violence in reaction to this or any future indictments but certainly, you can have one or two people who`ve been radicalized as people have been radicalized in the past in support of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 And it`s been really reprehensible the way he and his family have been singling out the daughter of the judge overseeing this case, and sort of all but, you know, begging people to harass her and make her life untenable.\par \par \uc2 But yes -- I mean -- and I also think that this is just a sign that the January 6th prosecution has had an effect. A lot of the people who would need kind of -- paramilitary violence, or you know, who would kind organize a real violent response have either been imprisoned or indicted, and are kind of out of commission.\par \par \uc2 And so what you have left are, you know, a lot of QAnon people, people who want to talk to you about you know, there was a clutch of supporters of the Chinese billionaire who`s close to \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  , who was recently indicted for massive fraud but you know, just kind of assorted eccentrics -- let me put it that way.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I`m so glad I was able to just walk down there yesterday, see it myself, and so glad to see it again today through your eyes in your brilliant column.\par \par \uc2 Michelle Goldberg, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, a huge win for democracy last night in Wisconsin, a win that could affect the outcome of the next presidential election. One of the masterminds of that big win in Wisconsin will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:33:19]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It was a huge win for democracy last night in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET PROTASIEWICZ, WISCONSIN SUPREME COURT JUSTICE-ELECT: Wisconsin voters have made their voices heard. They have chosen to reject partisan extremism in this state.\par \par \uc2 And second, it means our democracy will always prevail. Our state is taking a step forward to a better and brighter future where our rights and freedoms will be protected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The Wisconsin Supreme Court was the only court in the country that actually considered overturning the last presidential election. Last night in a decisive 11-point victory, Milwaukee County Judge Janet Protasiewicz won a seat on the Wisconsin state Supreme Court beating extremist Dan Kelly. This flips the court from an extremist right-wing Trump supporting court to a sane court. Some might say a liberal court for the first time in 15 years. Sanity now has a four to three majority on that court which could be making crucial decisions for the state on abortion rights, gerrymandered legislative districts, and possibly the outcome of the 2024 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 Ben Wikler\uc2  , chair of the state Democratic Party tweeted, "This election was a release valve for 12 years of Democratic rage in Wisconsin about Republicans rigging our state and smashing our democracy and then using that power to rip away our rights."\par \par \uc2 In the winners circle tonight, \uc2 Ben Wikler\uc2  , chair of the Democratic Party of Wisconsin. Thank you very much for joining us. It must feel like an amazing place to be.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:50]\par \par \uc2 11 points -- I know I had a feeling based on some of all the things you said before the election that you were confident but I didn`t sense 11 points of confidence.\par \par \uc2 BEN WIKLER\uc2  , CHAIRMAN, WISCONSIN DEMOCRATIC PARTY: No. This was a voter uprising. This was a state that has suffered through blow after blow after blow. The smashing of unions, the ripping apart of voting rights, the gerrymandering of districts and then re-gerrymandering unlike anywhere else in the country by the Supreme Court even after we elected a governor who ran on the promise of fair maps, the Supreme Court just went around voters and chose the most partisan gerrymander in the UNITED STATES.\par \par \uc2 But last night voters cast their final ballots and said absolutely no more. And today we are waking up in a state that has a path forward to actually become a legitimate functioning democracy.\par \par \uc2 My eight-year-old daughter will cast a ballot for president in 2032 while Janet Protasiewicz sits on the Wisconsin state Supreme Court and safeguards the promise that when voters choose their leaders, those leaders stay chosen.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Were abortion rights the principal issue here?\par \par \uc2 WIKLER: Abortion rights were the most powerful issue in this race and we`re linked inextricably to these questions around democracy. Wisconsin has an abortion ban that was passed 174 years ago before women had the right to vote, before the germ theory of disease was widely accepted.\par \par \uc2 It starts at zero weeks, has no exceptions for rape or incest or the health of the mother. Dan Kelly absolutely would have affirmed that abortion ban if he were on the state Supreme Court. Janet Protasiewicz has been very clear, she won`t prejudge a case. But her view is that abortion is a decision that should be left up to the person whose body is affected.\par \par \uc2 So this was a central issue for people of all generations. We had a huge surge in youth turnout. This was an issue that motivated a huge gender gap. But also enraged men who don`t want to see people that they love and people in their communities endangered and threatened and restricted and unfree because of this ban.\par \par \uc2 But even though two thirds of Wisconsinites oppose the abortion ban, the state legislature dominated through this Republican gerrymander refused to do anything about it. The governor called for a special session to repeal it they gaveled in and gaveled out within a few seconds. It was a slap in the face to the Wisconsin public and the Wisconsin public fought back.\par \par \uc2 Ben Wikler\uc2  , amazing job. It is so hard just to get attention to judicial elections like this one. You have got a lot more than attention. \uc2 Ben Wikler\uc2  , thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 WIKLER: Thanks so much, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you\par \par \uc2 And coming up, when \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  goes on Fox after dinner and after whatever he has to drink at dinner he frequently cries. He cries for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has never once cried for a child murdered in an American classroom by an AR-15. The tears of \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  , next.\par \par \uc2 [22:42:52]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: When \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  goes on TV after dinner and presumably whatever he drinks and has with dinner, there is a very good chance he is going to cry.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): You need to help this man, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  , they are trying to drain him dry. He`s spent more money on lawyers than most people spend on campaigns. They are trying to bleed him dry.\par \par \uc2 DonaldJTrump.com, go tonight, give the president some money to fight this (EXPLETIVE DELETED).\par \par \uc2 I am sorry I am so upset, but please help President Trump. If you can afford 5 or 10 bucks. If you can`t afford $1, fine, just pray.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is crying and begging for money for a man who said this when he began running for president against \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and other Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don`t need anybody`s money. I am using my own money. I`m not using the lobbyists. I`m not using donors, I don`t care.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That was a foundational lie of the Trump political career. Trump claimed to be so rich that he would never need any contributions at all and therefore he would be incorruptible.\par \par \uc2 And now \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the most corrupt president in history and he has taken more money out of the pockets of his supporters than any other president in history after first telling his supporters he didn`t need their money.\par \par \uc2 That`s the person \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is crying for now. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has never cried for a single child who has been murdered in a classroom in America. Not one.\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  did not go on TV to cry for nine year old Evelyn Dieckhaus, nine year old William Kinney, nine year old Hallie Scruggs who were murdered in a Nashville elementary school last week by a mass murderer who also killed \uc2 Katherine Koonce\uc2  the head of the school, Cynthia Peak a substitute teacher, and Mike Hill, the school`s custodian.\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  did not shed a single tear for any one of those victims of America`s latest mass murderer who used an AR-15 to fire 152 bullets in that school.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:49]\par \par \uc2 After that mass murder, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  proudly told reporters in Washington that he owns an AR-15. He didn`t say why. He didn`t cry while casually talking to Washington reporters in the Senate Hallway about America`s latest mass murderer. And he won`t cry the next time if your child`s body is ripped apart by an AR-15 in your child`s classroom. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  will not cry for your child and he will not cry for you.\par \par \uc2 He cried for one of Donald Trump`s nominees to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  when that nominee had an uncomfortable day in the Senate confirmation hearing. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  cried for him. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  got hysterical for him.\par \par \uc2 And when that Supreme Court justice for the first time in the history of the Supreme Court voted to revoke a constitutional right from the women of this country and forced a ten-year-old pregnant girl to leave her state of Ohio to obtain urgently needed abortion services, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  didn`t cry.\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  didn`t cry about the rape of that ten year old girl and he did not cry about what was forced upon her after the rape by the Supreme Court that \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  voted into place.\par \par \uc2 Professional Republicans absolutely do not cry for dead American children when they are murdered by AR-15s.\par \par \uc2 Here is Tennessee Republican state representative William Lamberth speaking to student protesters in Nashville who have spontaneously been demonstrating at the state capitol in the aftermath of the latest mass murder there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LAMBERTH (R), TENNESSEE STATE REPRESENTATIVE: So you are not going to like my answer. Look, I`m going to say that straight up. It is not about this one gun. If there is a firearm out there that you are comfortable being shot with, please show me which one it is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There`s none.\par \par \uc2 LAMBERTH: There`s none. There`s none. Right there. every single gun in the hands of a crazy person, a deranged person, a convicted felon, every single one -\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Right. But when are you going to be (INAUDIBLE) when somebody is walking on the street with a giant ass gun for no reason. Nobody`s going to do good with that gun.\par \par \uc2 LAMBERTH: I understand that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The goal is not to -\par \par \uc2 LAMBERTH: But it is not, you could ban that specific gun and you are going to do almost nothing to improve your safety. I`m sorry that is fact.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I`m sorry, that is not a fact. The "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2  " has been reporting extensively on the unique damage to the human body done by AR- 15s, which produce exit wounds much larger than the entrance wound of the bullet because the bullet is designed to pass through the body in way that expands the size of the wound and the damage as the bullet passes through the body.\par \par \uc2 An emergency room doctor in Uvalde, Texas described how unrecognizable some of the dead children were there because of what an AR-15 did to them. Some of the children had to be identified only by their clothing.\par \par \uc2 Children in classrooms in America have literally had their heads blown off by AR-15s. So yes, the AR-15 does make a difference. Banning the sale of AR-15s would make a difference. Some children might have survived some of the gunshot wounds the suffered in schools around the country, if the shooter had a less powerful gun. 152 bullets were fired from that AR-15 in that Nashville school last week, 152.\par \par \uc2 A weapon not designed for war and for mass murder as the AR-15 was might only have allowed that shooter to get off a few dozen shots. Maybe half as many shots -- we don`t know.\par \par \uc2 We do know that 400 Texas police officers were afraid to enter a classroom where children were dying for 77 minutes because they were afraid of the AR-15 inside that classroom.\par \par \uc2 What Republican state representative William Lamberth and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  have in common with every other elected Republican in Washington is the absolute determination to make sure that every mass murderer who walks into an American school will be able to legally purchase an AR-15 to bring into that school with them.\par \par \uc2 Elected Republicans are dedicated to making sure that America`s mass murderers are the very best equipped masa murderers in the world.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:30]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Time for tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 Today, Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  signed into the law the repeal of a 1931 Michigan law that banned abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): No one is going to restrict our reproductive rights and freedoms in the state, no one. Abortion rights are now enshrined into our state constitution.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:52]\par \par \uc2 We know that the decision of when and whether or not to bear a child is the most consequential decision economically that a family or a woman will make in her lifetime.\par \par \uc2 The government has no business getting in the way of conversations between women and their doctors.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230406ej4500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for April 5, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 7,986 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 April 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Disconnected from reality Republicans plan to vote tomorrow to expel three Democratic lawmakers for supporting Tennessee students in a gun control protest at the State Capitol. We will talk to two of those state representatives, Gloria Johnson and Justin Jones tomorrow. You don`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have nothing to hide. I have a constitution to uphold.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The Former Vice President agrees to talk after his boss surrendered for arrest in New York.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Trump, will you come speak to us. President Trump?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, 24 hours of legal disaster for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , 24 hours of political disaster for the party he leads, and why there`s no sign of any of it letting up.\par \par \uc2 Who would like to watch me slay a zombie?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And it`s the latest legal setback for Fox News.\par \par \uc2 MIKE LINDELL, CEO, MY PILLOW: I dare Dominion to sue me because then it would get all faster.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Explaining your pandering to pillow salesman from the stand when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. I got to say, OK, even by the standards of our time, of this day and age, try the avalanche of news and spectacle, this sort of dizzying calling for your attention, the last 24 hours have really been something else. So, I want to begin tonight by just taking a second here to try to collectively just wrap our heads around the meaning and significance of what we`ve seen in the last 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 So, of course, we have to begin with the biggest story. The Former President of the United States pleadeing not guilty to 34 felony charges stemming from hush money payments he made to an adult film star. But in addition to the material facts we saw yesterday, there was, of course, as I`m sure you noted, also the sheer spectacle at all, the Trumpian circus if you will. The live 24-hour coverage of his drive to the airport, his plane landing, his drive from the airport to Trump Tower, all of which I think triggers a deep and profound reaction in lots of folks because of what happened in 2016.\par \par \uc2 There are lots of people who will watch politics closely. We`ve got a lot of anxiety stemming from that 2016 election. And probably will for as long as they live. I know I will. Trump`s surprise victory that year really warped the way I think a lot of people think about American politics and his supposed strengths as a candidate specifically.\par \par \uc2 You see this theory reflected everywhere. Lots of people across the political spectrum, people on the right and left, in the middle, they believe it. Republicans, Democrats, they all believe that Trump is a kind of monster who feeds on attention. And the more attention you give the monster, the more powerful the monster becomes. And this is not a crazy theory, OK. There is some truth to it. In fact, it`s definitely what happened in the 2015 and 2016 Republican primary. There is no question the coverage of Trump obsessive and constant, his name recognition going in really did help elevate him in that race.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not 2016 anymore. Right now, Trump is the subject of attention because he`s the first-ever ex-president to be indicted because he`s currently facing down the possibility of two other indictments. That`s not a great reason to get attention. That`s negative attention whether Trump likes it or not. And since 2016, there is pretty good evidence that has accrued, maybe counter-intuitively but robust that the more attention voters pay to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the less they like him. In fact, even in 2016, voters didn`t really like him very much. He lost a popular vote to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  by about three million votes. He narrowly won a fluke Electoral College victory with the help of two different criminal conspiracies, one from Russia which illegally interfered in the election on behalf of Trump, one from \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  and David Pecker to pay hush money to Stormy Daniels which is now the subject of Trump`s indictment.\par \par \uc2 And people forget this but I was there and I`m telling you this is how it went down. The thing that really hurt \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in that election was the fact that she was the object of attention certainly down the stretch. It was the Clinton campaign everyone was obsessively focused on, her emails, the Comey letter, Huma Abedin`s laptop.\par \par \uc2 In the ensuing years since 2016, since that rupture in the space-time continuum, it has become clear when the general voting public, median persuadable normie voters pay attention to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they don`t like him. They don`t like what they see. This has been demonstrated over and over again in election after election. It was true in 2018 when the election became a midterm referendum on his presidency and Republicans lost 40 seats in the House. It was true in 2020 when Trump became the first incumbent president to lose re-election since George H.W. Bush in 1992 losing the popular vote by seven million and underperforming other Republicans up and down the ballot who add up actually a pretty good night.\par \par \uc2 It was certainly true in the most recent 2022 Midterms when against all odds the Democrats had a surprisingly good night. That is in large part because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had his fingerprints all over that election, endorsing a bunch of unlikable MAGA weirdos or as \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  euphemistically call it, "candidate quality."\par \par \uc2 [20:05:38]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Our ability to control primary outcome was quite limited in `22 because of the support of the former president proved to be very decisive in these primaries. So, my view was do the best you can with the cards you`re dealt. Hopefully in the next cycle, we`ll have quality candidates everywhere and a better outcome.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I know the point that I`m making here is kind of banal and maybe to some it`s obvious but it`s the core truth and it`s this: Everywhere you look, the signs point to the fact that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is incredibly unpopular with persuadable voters outside the Republican base. But everyone is so freaked out, so permanently scarred by 2016 that obvious truth is often obscured. And the truth is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- listen to me now -- does not possess a magic power that turns political gravity upside down. He is an unlikeable figure who lots of persuadable voters find repulsive.\par \par \uc2 Now, he is very, very good. I mean, incredible really, a generational talent at demanding and receiving lots of attention. But here`s the thing, right now, that is the worst possible thing for the Republican Party because he cannot be ignored. He refuses to be ignored. He will do anything not to be ignored. There is a reason why the smart people in the Republican party like \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  who you can`t deny has very strong political instincts, doesn`t want to talk about Trump. he doesn`t give quotes on Trump directly. There`s a reason they deflect or go off the record when asked about his latest scandal du jour because they understand. This the more you talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the worse it is for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing. They`re stuck with the guy, aren`t they? The guy is still the clear and decisive front-runner of the Republican primary for 2024 even though he got arraigned in federal court yesterday. Then he went back to Florida to deliver this just unhinged nasty threatening, utterly boring listless, low-energy speech that did nothing to earn him a single new vote from anyone outside his die-hard base of supporters. The Republican party has still yoked themselves to him hopelessly to the point where even his challengers for the 2024 nomination are one-upping each other to associate themselves with that spectacle.\par \par \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  who Trump reportedly thought deserved chants of hang my Pence from an armed mob still defends him to this day. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who Trump smeared as a groomer implying he acted inappropriately with young women or girls still issued a ridiculous statement against Trump`s indictment vowing to fight his extradition. At some point, Republicans are going to have to face this reality. The guy is utterly toxic. As long as you keep supporting him, you`re going to lose a lot of winnable elections. And again, we keep seeing the proof.\par \par \uc2 Last night, while most of the country was focused on Trump`s arraignment, there were two very telling elections. Now the first was the mayoral race in Chicago, which yes, is of course, a dyed-in-the-wool Democratic town. But it`s a Democratic town that also had Richie Daley as mayor for 20 years, elected \uc2 Rahm Emanuel\uc2  twice. It is more than capable of electing what you might call Conservative Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And the Conservative Democrat in this race, a guy named \uc2 Paul Vallas\uc2 , was running on a tough-on-crime platform. And in certain ways, he had the wind at his back frankly. I mean, first of all, he had a lot of money. He outspend his opponent two to one. And also, violence, gun violence particularly is a very real problem in Chicago. People are pretty freaked out by it. It`s upsetting. The city faces lots of challenges. Yet Vallas lost the race to Progressive challenger Brandon Johnson for a bunch of reasons, not the least of which is because Johnson successfully painted Vallas as a secret Republican, tied him to Trump at every turn, pointing to comments like this that Vallas made about Trump`s second impeachment after January 6th when he filled in as a co-host in a right-wing talk show.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He should have been censured. He should not have been impeached. And we knew that this was going to happen yet they went along with the trial anyway.\par \par \uc2 PAUL VALLAS\uc2 , FORMER MAYORAL CANDIDATE, CHICAGO: Right. Also, for those wanting to put Republicans on the spot, if he would have been censured, it would have put much more pressure on them. And you know, I always felt that it was a witch hunt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A witch hunt. What are you doing there, Paul? It`s not a good look, buddy. Yes, Vallas is a Conservative Democrat. He`s not a Republican. The election result once again highlights the conundrum for conservatives broadly in the Republican Party. I mean, look, if you`re the conservative in the race, right, you`re running in the right lane in a race, running on law-and-order campaign, right, crackdown on crime against sky-high inflation, those should be slam dunk issues for right-wingers. This political terrain should be the best terrain for them. They`re the political conditions that generally lead to pretty significant electoral victories in the past for conservatives, except their brand has been so thoroughly poisoned by Trump and Trumpism.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:37]\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing. Here`s why it`s even worse than that. It`s deeper than just the taint of Trump. They`ve also got an agenda that is not popular. And they keep doing it over and over. Their pushed to ban abortions for instance, along with Trump, abortion was the top issue that drove Democrats to over-perform the 2022 midterms. It is still absolutely driving voters to the polls today.\par \par \uc2 You see, the other big election last night was in Wisconsin where a crucial race for a state supreme court was effectively a referendum on abortion. Abortion is currently illegal in Wisconsin. Conservatives controlled that Supreme Court. If liberals flipped control with this one seat, they could lift the state`s abortion ban. Those were the stakes and voters showed up.\par \par \uc2 There`s a massive turnout. And the liberal candidate, a woman named Janet Protasiewicz won decisively. Get this. It is looking like she won by about 10 points. That is a pretty dramatic victory for what is effectively a 50- 50 state. And even in the ancestral Republican enclave of the Milwaukee suburbs, the party of Trump keeps losing ground.\par \par \uc2 Take Ozaukee County for example. One of the three conservative counties crucial Republicans would be competitive in Wisconsin. Last night, Republicans barely eked out a victory. In that county, about five points. Get this. In 2012, Republican Presidential nominee \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  won Ozaukee by 30 points. In 2014, incumbent Republican governor Scott Walker won it by 40. It now seems completely plausible it could flip blue in a matter of years.\par \par \uc2 None other than Ann Coulter who of course is recklessly offensive and cruel hit the nail on the head. Seriously, listen this, when she tweeted, "The demand for anti-abortion legislation just cost Republicans another crucial race. Pro-lifers, we won. Abortion is not a constitutional right anymore. Please stop pushing strict limits on abortion or there will be no Republicans left."\par \par \uc2 She`s right. That`s the problem. The base believes it. They didn`t want to just get rid of Roe v. Wade. They want to outlaw abortion everywhere. They demand less -- nothing less than full abortion criminalization everywhere. Look at Florida. Again, all eyes on Trump at the agreement, right? We got Wisconsin, we got Chicago. Meanwhile, look at Florida. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is about to do one of the most politically disastrous things he could possibly do. He is going to sign a law that effectively bans abortion of Florida. He has no choice. The primary voters in the Republican Party demand it. It`s going to be a massive Anvil around his neck if and when he runs for president if he gets the nomination. But he`s going to do it because that`s what the MAGA base wants, even though what they want is increasingly out of touch with the median voter.\par \par \uc2 That is once again the fundamental conundrum. Republicans have locked themselves in this MAGA universe that voters, general election voters, persuadable voters keep recoiling from. Not just on abortion. I mean, the House Republican Caucus took over the House and they`re holding multiple hearings about \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , for the love of God. Republican state legislators, state after state, governors are waging an all-out assault on trans teenagers, including a Kansas bill Republicans there passed today that critics say could expose children to genital inspections. This stuff, listen to me, is not winning politics. They keep doing it. They keep going further and they keep having a harder time persuading anyone who doesn`t already agree with them. Which leads them to their final recourse which is to just withdraw from or subvert to democratic processes altogether.\par \par \uc2 Just listen to the losing candidate effectively refusing to concede that Wisconsin judicial race last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANIEL KELLY, FORMER WISCONSIN SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: I wish that in a circumstance like this I would be able to concede to a worthy opponent. But I do not have a worthy opponent to which I can concede. This was the most deeply deceitful, dishonorable, despicable campaign I have ever seen run for the courts. It was truly beneath contempt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Beneath contempt all right. Wisconsin is one of if not the most gerrymandered states in the union. The maps are drawn to effectively give Republicans universal control regardless of election results. They`re already talking about just straight up impeaching the liberal candidate who won yesterday so they could yank back power despite the will of the voters, despite getting drubbed by 10 points.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:14]\par \par \uc2 So, that`s Wisconsin. Then there`s this move from Tennessee. Republicans already control everything but they`re going to move to expel three democratically elected members in the legislature for non-violently protesting gun violence. They`re just saying no, we don`t care what the voter said, we`re going to kick you out anyway.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina where the gerrymandering is almost as bad as Wisconsin, they`ve managed to persuade a formerly pro-choice Democrat elected in a blue district to switch to the Republican Party giving them two-thirds of majority, so now they can override all the vetoes issued by the Democratic governor who won Statewide twice.\par \par \uc2 The more Republicans invest in Trump and in their extreme agenda and this massively unpopular ideology, the less able they are to persuade people to win elections, the more committed they become to anti-democratic principles. That is the story of the Trump era all on display just in the last 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 And listen, it`s a divided country. Conservative rhetoric is always going to have an appeal. It`s a polarized dynamic electorate. Republicans will always effectively be a little more than a coin flip away from winning the White House. So, nothing is guaranteed. No one should be lackadaisical. But I`m telling you here tonight 24 hours after watching this whole spectacle play out, progressives, liberals, the center left in this country, the broad anti-Trump majority are winning the argument. They are.\par \par \uc2 The other side has absolutely sabotaged itself somehow because of what happened in 2016, it is hard for everyone to see that obvious reality staring us in the face. I am telling you, I think there are many voters who are not happy about where the Republican Party stands right now. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a very unpopular figure. The Republican party has tied its fate to him. It has been and continues to be a political disaster for them. Please don`t overthink it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REID: Chris Hayes! Happy 10-year anniversary to ALL IN with Chris Hayes! I love the show. It`s always super smart. I always learned so much listening to you. I love having you on the big panel too when we`re all there together with Rachel Maddow, and Nicolle, and all of us gang all together. But really, I`m just so proud of you. I`m so happy for you. 10 years is a major anniversary in this business. So, congratulations, my friend. Big news. Congrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Thank you, Joy. Well, it is well known that Democratic donors and politicians and political operatives like to freak out or wring their hands all the time. It`s part of their DNA. But if I were a professional Republican watching everything that happened yesterday, I would honestly be losing my mind with panic. Because the things that make \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican Party less appealing to general election voters are the very things that are consolidating the base behind Trump. Like this from Republican Congressman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  during her short trip to Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MARJORIE TAYLOR-GREENE (R-GA): President Trump is joining some of the most incredible people in history being arrested today. \uc2 Nelson Mandela\uc2  was arrested, served time in prison. Jesus, Jesus was arrested and murdered by the Roman government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s true. Jesus was arrested, not arraigned. I don`t think they did arraignment, but he`s arrested. Notice that \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  had to do interviews yesterday from inside a car because the heckling from New Yorkers outside the courthouse was too much to bear. Her act is not the kind of thing that`s going to help pick up middle-of-the-road voters and so on. No one seems to know how to interrupt the Republican Party`s extremely self-destructive cycle.\par \par \uc2 Cornell Belcher is a Democratic pollster and strategist and he joins me. Cornell, let me first start on this just basic question of the data. The more attention that`s paid to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the worse it is for the Republican Party generally. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, DEMOCRATIC POLLSTER: I think that`s -- I think that`s absolutely right. Look, I think if you look at what`s been happening over the last 24 hours, 48 hours, and you`re -- and you`re \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , and you`re sitting there thinking, I may have a pretty good map again this cycle where I can perhaps take -- you know, take back the Senate and then watch \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  suck all the oxygen out of the room, dominate all the media everywhere all at once.\par \par \uc2 And you know, Chris, it`s not going away. I mean, he will be the story going into the summer, if not going into the summer and going into the fall, and it drowns out every ounce of Republican messaging. So, instead of Republicans talking about -- well, it`d be interesting because they don`t actually have --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They don`t have one. I was just about to say -- I was just about to say, drown out what messaging? I don`t like the old \uc2 Twitter\uc2  ownerships policies vis-a-vis which tweets they posted. That`s the message. We need your 60 -- we`re going to look -- check your 16-year-old`s genitals before we let them compete in sports.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: That messaging that`s going to appeal to the middle of the -- middle of the road, and the majority of Americans which also is interesting, Chris, because it ties into your -- to your last segment. We`re talking about how frankly, they`re trying to make elections not matter or count, because they actually don`t have a popular agenda. You know, if you look at what Republicans have been trying to do over the last couple of elections, nothing that they`re really trying to do has strong majority support among Americans.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, and it`s -- it is -- you`re right I think about Trump and the drowning out and how -- you know, again, if you`re a political strategist to the Republican Party, it`s bad. There`s also though, I mean, abortion is just it`s not going away. It`s not -- it`s a live issue. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  -- I mean, again, if you`re paid, Cornell Belcher, to advise \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , you know, he`s going to sign it because he`s -- you know, Republican primary voters will demand nothing less. And maybe he believes in it, too. I can`t say whether he does or not.\par \par \uc2 But it`s a board -- ban on abortion in Florida, effectively a complete ban on abortion in Florida. Like, if he`s the nominee, that`s like one of the top three things you run against.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:57]\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: No, it absolutely is. I mean, if you look at what happened in Wisconsin, and it was 11 points, I mean, that is -- that is a tale weapon in a battleground -- in a battleground, toss up state. You know, I was the one who argued going into the last midterm election that the reproductive rights was a dynamic, game-changing event. And it was one that was going to wake up a sleeping giant. Like, for years, you know, the idea of women losing their reproductive rights was just theory, it wasn`t actually practiced, and women weren`t mobilizing around it.\par \par \uc2 Well, we saw women voters and younger voters and even in Wisconsin, you look at the sort of younger voters and men -- the younger men as well. It is a mobilizing issue. I know we always want to talk about, yes, economies, economies, economy. No, this last midterm, thank God it was not the economy stupid, because if it wasn`t for reproductive rights, our ability to mobilize voters around reproductive rights and, Chris, it`s the sleeping issue here which you also have talked about, and also mobilizing voters around threats to our democracy, we would have had a red wave.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. This is what Ben Wikler said about Wisconsin. It`s not just the Democrats have a powerful message for the post-Trump era, Ben Wikler, who`s the chair of the Wisconsin Democratic Party said on Wednesday morning interview, it`s that Republicans don`t have a message. It`s just crime, crime, crime, crime.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what I find fascinating about this cycle. Party should be relatively self-correcting, you know, general sort of political science theory, sort of vote as median voter. Like, Democrats a great example, right? George Floyd protests, huge discussion of police brutality and accountability, a huge post-2020 freakout by many of the Democratic Party that the messaging on defund the police, for instance, had been massively costly. Never say that phrase again. There`s all this internal debate, right, about did we lose voters on that, how do we get back. Wherever you land up substantively on that, an internal debate about that. I just don`t see them learning any lessons over the other side.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Well, no. It was because -- and look, because, Chris, it looks like a cult more than a political party. And even right now, you have a president who`s been indicted -- the former President who`s been indicted, and Republicans are coming out and supporting him. I mean, even Republicans who you would think are not his friends are coming out to support him and fight for him. And as long as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  stays at the head of the Republican Party, there`s not -- there`s not going to be any change. There`s not going to be -- there`s not going to be changes.\par \par \uc2 Look, you remember the -- after Obama won, Republicans came out with their autopsy and started talking about, quite frankly, what we have to do to be a majority party again, particularly looking at the changes that are happening across our country demographically. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came in, threw that out, and there`s never been any questioning of any of that ever again well, since he`s been at the head of the party.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s exactly right. Cornell Belcher, always a pleasure to have you. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, the Manhattan charges are just the tip of the legal iceberg heading towards the twice-impeach, now-indicted ex-President. There`s a lot more to come, it appears. What lies ahead is coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Trump`s seeming pretty much unraveled. And in fact, if you haven`t checked in with him in his public appearances over the last two years, you may have noticed he seemed a lot looser really than he did when last we saw him in January of 2021. In case you haven`t been following.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  in full scramble mode last night, coming out of Donald Trump`s lethargic, vindictive speech, desperately pretending Trump was looser than the last time we saw him in January 2021. Now, of course, we know from internal emails that Tucker is terrified his audience will abandon him for \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  if he says anything less than fawning about the criminal defendant ex-president.\par \par \uc2 This is why the Fox News Channel is in so much trouble right now. It`s why a Delaware judge not only allowed Dominions $1.6 billion lawsuit to go to trial later this month, but explicitly ruled that statements \uc2 Fox\uc2  hosts and guests said about Dominion were false. Quoting from the judge`s opinion, "The evidence developed in a civil proceeding demonstrates that it is crystal clear that none of the statements relating to Dominion about the 2020 election are true." The judge even emphasized that word crystal in his ruling.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:02]\par \par \uc2 Today that same judge said he would compel \uc2 Fox Corp\uc2  executives, \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2 , \uc2 Lachlan Murdoch\uc2  to testify live. Plus, Fox`s attorneys agreed to let host Tucker, \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2 , Lou Dobbs, Jeanine Pirro, and \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  testify live as well. There`s a lot they have to answer for. One of the things I`m most fascinated by is their commitment over to Fox to booking one specific guest, Mike Lindell, the pillow guy is a major advertiser on Fox News, especially Tucker`s show, which has hemorrhaged more respectable advertisers due to the host`s habit of promoting white nationalist talking points.\par \par \uc2 We counted one night alone last week. Lindell ran three ads during Tucker`s one-hour program. It was always kind of clear Lindell was maybe keeping Tucker show afloat, but now we have proof. You see, in addition to being the pillow guy, Lindell was also a MAGA conspiracy enthusiast who regularly promotes the most outlandish and possibly defamatory version of Trump`s big lie of a stolen election. Now, that creates kind of a problematic incentive structure for a show that needs his advertising bucks.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to Dominion and their lawsuit which is currently suing Fox for defamation in part due to claims that Lindell himself made, we know exactly how Tucker`s producers handle that thorny question behind the scenes. On the afternoon of January 26, 2021, well after the insurrection, after Biden`s inauguration, Tucker`s show decided to have Lindell on. They were hoping to frame the segment around his recent \uc2 Twitter\uc2  ban. A booking producer message the team. "Good morning all! Mike Lindell was pitched last night for stores having pulled his product and Tucker approved it. I see the news this morning that his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has been suspended. I just wanted to flag all this because is he someone we`d want to have on?"\par \par \uc2 About 30 seconds later, she sent a parenthetical follow-up. "Lindell seems to have really gone off the deep end." About a minute later, another producer responded, "He`s definitely crazy but this will be a big story today. They`re trying to completely shut the guy down." Yes, why would they want to shut the guy down? The next hour, an unknown producer then responds, "I`m going to have a conversation with Raj in the next few minutes. Presumably that`s Raj Shah, Senior Vice President at \uc2 Fox\uc2 . I`ll take the temperature there. But for the time being, we should put the brakes on it.\par \par \uc2 That to your viewer is the end of the story. They put the brakes on booking the pillow man and no, no, of course, they put them on TV anyway. Because later that afternoon, Tucker`s senior producer responded, "Copy. I know there`s concern about Lindell being a conspiracy theorist now but he has bailed us out loads of times when no one else would. I think there`s a way we can concentrate on canceled culture rather than his wild claims." Before adding "And he`s also been moving to \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  lately. Might be a good business move to signal we won`t desert him."\par \par \uc2 That booker who initiated the conversation then chimes back into add, "Yes, I do see the point that more exposure of him also means more people dropping him potentially. And if no one is buying him, he loses money to be able to advertise with us. And then we lose our biggest advertiser." The booking producer desperately trying to save Mike Lindell`s pillow business from Mike Lindell. About an hour later, one more producer chimes in with some frankly reasonable concerns, "Mike Lindell is crazy and about to get sued by Dominion. WTF is he going to say on our air?"\par \par \uc2 You know what, producer, great question. What is Lindell going to say? Well, you see, we`ve got the answer to that question because Tucker did book Lindell, did claim the interview will be focused on Cancel Culture, but Mike Pillow had other ideas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Of course, you will likely recognize our next guest. His name is Mike Lindell. He runs My Pillow. We advertise this every night on this show and across Fox News. He`s one of our biggest sponsors and we are grateful for that. He is sponsoring free speech when he does it. But of course, the enforcers of orthodoxy are not impressed. They`re enraged. For the crime of having different opinions, Mike Lindell has just been banned from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Several retailers have also stopped selling his products. That happened today.\par \par \uc2 LINDELL: All these outlets that have been calling me from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , every outlet in the country, they go Mike Lindell, there`s no evidence and he`s making fraudulent statements. No, I have the evidence. I dare people to put it on. I dare Dominion to show me because then it would get out faster.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I dare diming you to sue me or maybe sue you because it will get out faster. Honestly, this is just scratching the surface of everything we learned about the inner workings of \uc2 Fox\uc2  this week. But thanks to that Delaware judge, we will soon be learning a lot more.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Good news and bad news today for the residents of Mayflower, Arkansas. The good news is that \uc2 Exxon\uc2  announced they`re developing an official plan to evacuate an oil pipeline that ruptured underneath a Mayflower subdivision on Friday. The bad news is this. This is how residents of that subdivision spent their Easter weekend.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, that is a pipeline that is busted and is flooded the neighborhood and is going all the way to the drain at the end of the street. Luckily, our house is here which is seemingly unaffected but the smell is unbelievable. I mean, look. Incredible, and that is oil.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:17]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That his oil. And that is what the very first minute of ALL IN look like 10 years ago. That pipeline owned by \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  ended up spilling around 134,000 gallons of oil in that Arkansas community. The company ended up paying a $5 million settlement for violating the Clean Water Act, as well as state environmental laws, although it did not admit liability.\par \par \uc2 I was lucky to be joined by an outstanding panel, some familiar faces that included Vermont Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . He also covered the Keystone pipeline, the \uc2 NCAA\uc2 , and the cheating scandal in the Atlanta Public School System. Nearly three dozen educators were indicted and at the time, I was particularly interested in how the lead prosecutor was approaching the charges.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The statute that the indictment stems from is Georgia`s version of the RICO which is a conspiracy statute, right, which is something prosecutors use to -- which often allows them to sidestep showing some kind of direct moment of implicated guilt in the guilty party, right? If you`re part of a conspiracy, you should have known or didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Eventually, 11 of the 12 educators who went on trial were convicted of racketeering charges. It was a career-making case from lead prosecutor, a woman named \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 . Of course, \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  is now the district attorney for Fulton County, and leading one of the most significant criminal investigations into Donald Trump`s attempt to steal the 2020 election. The status of that and all the other legal issues facing the first former president to be criminally prosecuted next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , FORMER CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISOR TO THE PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: Hi, Chris! \uc2 Tony Fauci\uc2  here. I want to take this opportunity to offer you my hearty congratulations for 10 wonderful years at MSNBC in your show ALL IN with Chris Hayes. It`s been an absolute pleasure to have been on your show so many times and have the opportunity to interact with you. You are really one of the best and a real pro. And besides, I`m still waiting to have the one-on-one pickup basketball game with you. We better do it soon, Chris, because you`re getting kind of old now. And I don`t want to have to completely outrun you in front of everybody. Again, congratulations, my friend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Regis High School`s own starting point guard \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 . And he`s right, we got to get that one-on-one game off before I have to retire from the sport. Now, it is not just what happened in New York yesterday. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is under investigation up and down the eastern seaboard. See this helpful map. In Washington D.C. where special counsel Jack Smith is investigating both the classified documents at Mar-a-Lago and Trump`s efforts to overturn the 2020 election. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  just agreed to testify to the grand jury investigating January 6. Now, Pence is a hugely crucial witness, so that`s a big development.\par \par \uc2 Then, we`ve got Fulton County, Georgia. We are waiting on a decision from that D.A., \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  on possible charges related to Trump`s attempted election interference. In Manhattan, Trump not only faces the criminal charges he was arraigned on yesterday, but here`s something that`s not under the radar a bit. He is also being sued for sexual assault by writer E. Jean Carroll. And that civil trial goes to trial in just three weeks.\par \par \uc2 Diana Florence served as an assistant district attorney in Manhattan for 25 years. Nick Akerman is a former Assistant Special Watergate prosecutor, also served as a federal prosecutor in the Southern District of New York. And both join me now. It`s good to have you guys here.\par \par \uc2 DIANA FLORENCE, FORMER MANHATTAN ASSISTANT DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NICK AKERMAN, FORMER ASSISTANT SPECIAL WATERGATE PROSECUTOR: Glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Diana, as someone who worked in this office for 25 years and did I think construction fraud and white collar cases as we call them, sort of a funny name.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What`s your sort of top line on the -- on the indictment yesterday and on the case?\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: The case, if you move aside the salacious details, the porn star and the hush money, at the end of the day, it`s a fairly mundane, not very sexy case. This is a very routine. I think D.A. Bragg called it the bread- and-butter type of charges and that`s what it looks like to me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Is this the kind of thing that you know that office has done before?\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: I did it myself scores of times. And yes, this is a very run-of- the-mill case.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You worked in the Southern District for federal prosecutors. Of course, there`s a little bit of like, come at the king best not miss kind of situation here. I`m sure \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  knows that.\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: Of course.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is the first criminal case ever brought against an ex- president. What do you think of its strength?\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: I think it`s an excellent case. In my day as a prosecutor, if I had two cooperating witnesses like you have here, Alan Cohen and David Pecker, and you have the --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  and David --\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: Yes, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  and David Pecker. If you put that together with the corroboration that`s out there, the tape recording between Cohen and Trump, all of the documentation basically lining up with everything both individuals are saying, it`s a pretty overwhelming case. But then, if you put into account other witnesses who are likely to testify, like \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2 , and others in the White House who they`ve called in to testify, I mean, this could wind up being a slam dunk if you`ve got so many people pointing at Donald Trump`s guilt.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:07]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, here`s my understanding. Again, I find myself in reading commentary about this case -- because it`s been somewhat polarized. People have very different views, even people that I trust and agree with, kind of convinced by the last thing I read, right? So, like, Jed Shugerman, for instance, who we`ve had on the show, \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , he calls it a legal embarrassment. He says the case appears so weak on its legal and jurisdictional basis. A state judge might dismiss the case and mitigate the damage.\par \par \uc2 That seems not to be the case in your mind. I don`t think so either. But let me point to the criticisms here. The faxing beyond dispute. Like, he paid the hush money. It was a catch-and-kill operation. It`s all there. We all know it. The checks are there, right? Boom. Him committing misdemeanor offenses under state law, kind of beyond dispute, right? The legal theory that kicks it up to a felony in pursuit of another crime, what do you think about how it`s described in the indictment and whether that will stand up to legal challenge?\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: Well, it`s actually not described in the indictment. And that`s by design. New York law does not require you to name the underlying crime. And there was speculation, lots of speculation prior to the unsealing that the crime -- the underlying crimes were election frauds and there was some talk of that. But you know, and my esteemed colleague here is a former federal prosecutor. They`re good at federal prosecutions. State prosecutors, not so much. We don`t -- we don`t do those.\par \par \uc2 To me, you know, what it`s always struck me is this stronger underlying crime or tax fraud. And it`s just about, you know, how they booked the payments the -- characterizing them, as you know, legal services when in fact, they were hush money.\par \par \uc2 So, that -- just to follow up on that. Bragg says that it`s -- there`s a crime in the New York statutes, right, in the criminal code which is like, conspiring to use unlawful means to elect a candidate, right?\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, that`s one, right? He also mentioned taxes multiple times. Did they have to pick? Like, at some point, it just seems like it`s sort of hanging out there. There`s a bunch of crimes that might have done it. At some point, you got to say what your legal theory is.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: You absolutely have to pick. And that sort of obscure state election crime is very circuitous. It`s sort of like, you know, if you asked me, how do I get to L.A., I`d say, you will fly to London, and then get another plane. I mean, it`s not direct.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: But a tax bribe is so much easier. You know, it`s -- they wrote something. It`s clearly false. There`s multiple to Mr. Akerman`s point. It`s multiply corroborated to be false.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, no question about right.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: Right. So, to me, the only reason you put that in your books is because you`re going to book it as an expense, not as revenue, not as income.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And that`s tax evasion.\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: Well, it`s not tax evasion. It`s actually filing a false tax return. Tax evasion requires a substantial amount of money that is you`re cheating the government.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I see. I see.\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: But what you`re going to get at this trial is an accountant up there who`s going to be extremely boring. He`s going to go straight from the false statements, right through the books and records of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , right to the tax return, and show that they filed a false return, end the story.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, the tax one seems more straightforward. Let me just ask one more question just because is this -- is this -- presenting a legal theory like this to move misdemeanors up to a felony, is that something you`ve done in your time?\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: Oh, many times, of course. This doesn`t strike you as like, whoa, this is some crazy thing to pull out of a hat for Trump.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE: No, this is, as I said, when I use the word mundane, it is mundane. This is a run-of-the-mill white-collar case except for who the defendant is.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s another case. I had -- it was literally my wife, Kate, pulling this to my attention, pointing this out to me yesterday. I didn`t realize the E. Jean Carroll lawsuit -- again, this is not on definition. This is a New York law that allows survivors of sexual assault passed the statute of limitations to sue for civil damages, right? She has a lawsuit. She`s got a defamation suit because he said you`re a liar. That`s hung up in a weird procedural thing in which a D.C. --\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: Court of Appeals.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- Court of Appeals has a rule whether the President defaming her was in his professional capacity.\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This case, though, is going to go to trial.\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: Yes, that`s in three weeks.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In three weeks about did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sexually assault E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: Did he rape her? That is the issue.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Did he rape her.\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And this is what you write. Carroll can introduce the testimony of two women who were allegedly sexually assaulted by Trump. The judge ruled the Carroll can introduce into evidence his statements on the Access Hollywood tape. What are we -- what are we going to make of this case?\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: We make this case as an absolute disaster for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I mean, the only way he can defend himself, in this case, is to take the witness stand. He is the only witness that he can call that can give him any possibility --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s partly he-said-she-said in that sense.\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: Well, it`s not only a he-said-she-said, it`s a he-said-she-said, she said, she said.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, yes, she has corroborating --\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: And she is also corroborated by two other witnesses that confirmed that she told them about this incident immediately after it happened.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And said it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 AKERMAN: Right. And so, his own attorney general has said that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ever takes a stand, it`s a complete disaster. We all know that. So, he is likely in by May of this year to be found by a New York jury as a rapist.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Diana Florence and Nick Akerman, great to have you here at the table.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Wednesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230406ej4500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for March 29, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, David Jolly, Ali Velshi \par \uc2 7,781 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Harris` photo and skirts with a picture of Tanzania`s first woman president. She closes up her trip in Zambia, a place that she has a personal connection to because her maternal grandfather worked in Zambia years ago. And she visited him there -- visited him there as a young girl. VP Harris` trip comes on the hills of visits by First Lady Jill Biden, Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  , and Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  . President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is expected to travel to Africa later this year.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, on ALL IN. America in reverse on reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The legislation would create a whole new crime called abortion trafficking.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, Rebecca Traister on the shock and awe of state-by-state legislation criminalizing reproductive rights. Then, just when you thought it couldn`t get worse for Fox News.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This has to stop now. The audience is furious. Bad for business.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Devastating new filings in the Dominion lawsuit against Fox News. And Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  unloads on \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has waged the most aggressive and illegal union-busting campaign in the modern history of our country.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  joins me live when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Republicans in Idaho are set to ban interstate travel for abortion for some of their citizens. If that sounds shocking to you, if that sounds like an attack on the very foundation or a union, you`re correct. But that is how far anti-abortion extremists who control the state government of Idaho are willing to go in their pursuit of forcing women and girls to carry pregnancies against their will. For all the talk on the right about loving the Constitution, this law and many others show what they actually think of it in practice.\par \par \uc2 Now, Idaho has been at the forefront of the national movement to criminalize abortion in the wake of last June`s Supreme Court decision from right-wing justices overturning Roe v Wade. There was a so-called trigger law in Idaho. It went to effect two months after that ruling. That outlawed all abortions in the state with very limited exceptions. The law also prescribes prison sentences for doctors who perform abortions and making it one of the most extreme bans in the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, Idaho Republicans not satisfied with that, of course, right? They`re advancing a new bill that would create a brand new crime their billing as abortion trafficking.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The bill in Idaho would restrict minor`s ability to travel for abortion care without parental consent. The legislation would create a whole new crime called Abortion Trafficking which is defined as obtaining an abortion drug or transporting a pregnant minor with the intent to hide the abortion from the minor`s legal guardians.\par \par \uc2 According to the bill, violators would face a felony and to a two-to-five- year prison sentence. It just passed through the state house and it`s going to likely very -- move very quickly through the senate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Republican Governor \uc2 Brad Little\uc2  is expected to sign that bill into law as soon as it makes it out of legislature. The way this bill is written is a somewhat clever attempt to get around the fact that a state law can`t prohibit interstate travel. As HuffPo explains, the legislation doesn`t actually say anything about crossing state lines, but Republican lawmakers are creative. Most pregnant people in Idaho are not traveling to obtain an abortion elsewhere in the state since nearly all abortions are illegal in Idaho, they`re traveling to the border with the intent of crossing state lines likely into Washington, Oregon, or Montana to get an abortion there.\par \par \uc2 Now, the right to interstate travel and interstate commerce is a core tenant guaranteed by our constitution. We have probably not seen these kinds of challenges to those rights since a long time ago, since the controversy over the Fugitive Slave Act in 1850 which is part of a broad compromise between northern and southern states. A bill Congress passed requiring law enforcement to arrest people who are suspected of having escaped slavery in the northern states, in the states where slavery did not obtain. Anyone who helped an enslaved person escape could also be fined or imprisoned. And this is applied across the country. It was an exporting of the slave tyranny from the south to the free states.\par \par \uc2 It was odious and the backlash led to the expansion of the Abolitionist Movement and the Underground Railroad as well as contributing to tensions leading up to the Civil War. And after the war, the 14th Amendment added protection to the right to interstate travel and freedom of movement which courts have upheld over and over. And so, this bill in Idaho in its core conceit -- and I`m not exaggerating here -- in intent and in fact is trying to take that right away from the population that it targets, teenagers.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:02]\par \par \uc2 If you help a minor in Idaho get an abortion, even by dropping them off the airport or obtaining medication from another state without their parents knowledge, you would violate this law and could be thrown in prison. Now, you might think to yourself well, you know, parents should know. Do you think a legal guarding has ever impregnated a minor in the state of Idaho? Do you think that happens anywhere in this country? Do you think a legal a minor might be pregnant by someone that has sexually assaulted them that has power over them and might need to go across state lines to obtain abortion? You think that ever happens?\par \par \uc2 The minor part here is key because minors` rights are circumscribed constitutionally. Targeting them brings this law right up to the line of an obvious and flavored assault on the union. But remember, tactics that go just up to the line are the approach of the anti-abortion rights movement right before they step over it. So, where do you think this is going? And of course it isn`t just Idaho pushing these kinds of extreme restrictions. I mean, we`ve been seeing this everywhere. The tip of the iceberg has been coming since the day the Supreme Court overturned Roe, the ultimate goal for the anti-abortion right. And it logically follows from what they say they believe is obviously a full and complete national ban on all abortion for everyone everywhere and ultimately the finding of the fetus as a constitutionally protected person. That is the end goal.\par \par \uc2 Just today, North Carolina Republicans filed a bill in the house that would totally band abortion from the point of conception except to save the mother`s life. That follows a proposed bill in South Carolina under which women who get an abortion could face the death penalty. And the Republican member who sponsored the bill, Rob Harris, admirably honest in his logic. "We have to ask ourselves as a culture whether we believe life begins at conception or not. The ramifications of that are the same for anybody else who would take another life." In other words, abortion is murder and people who obtain abortions should be punished as such.\par \par \uc2 Now, in Wisconsin, Republicans are proposing a bill that would "bar public employees from being able to speak about -- speak about or perform abortion services while on the job. And in Florida, this is largely flown over the radar remarkably. Republicans are now pushing a six-week ban to replace the current 15-week ban that Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  says he will sign if it reaches his desk. Yes, that DeSantis who`s going to run for president. Florida Democrats including the State House Minority Leader are warning of the far-reaching ramifications.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FENTRICE DRISKELL (D-FL), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: This is such an unpopular law or unpopular proposal with Floridians and most Americans. Most Americans broadly want to protect abortion access and that`s true here with Floridians. So, I think that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is out of touch. If this should pass, it`s going to take a long road back I think to recover these rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And of course, hanging over all this is the pending case before a right-wing Trump-appointed judge in Amarillo, Texas in his little jurisdiction where they run and go file their appeals to go file their cases. Anti-abortion groups teed up this suit, brought it to him, incorporated in Amarillo just to do this, right, before U.S. District Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk asking him to block the use of one of the two most commonly used medications in medical abortions.\par \par \uc2 This is the method used by more than half of all women in this country across the country who have an abortion in red states, in blue states, in states that have abortion rights and don`t have abortion rights. Judge Kacsmaryk could issue a ruling at any moment that would block access to this medication, again, across the country immediately in every state across the country exporting the anti-abortion politics of a place like Texas to the entire Union.\par \par \uc2 And there is only one single solitary silver lining in all this. By and large, all of it is incredibly unpopular. 61 of Americans say abortion should be legal in all or most cases according to Pew polling. Restrictive abortion bans are so unpopular that in the last midterm election, ballot measures that would protect abortion rights passed in every state they were up all over the country even quite conservative ones like the state of Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 And get this. In Ohio, Republicans there are now so concerned about a similar measure passing in their state, they`re now trying to change the rules to make it harder to amend the state constitution. This is an a state \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won by eight points. And as Rebecca Traister writes in the cover story for New York Magazine this week, Democrats have an opportunity here to use the fight for Reproductive Rights to their advantage. "What Democrats have is incentive. One of their most urgent policy issues has just shown itself to be their most politically effective. And they are undergoing a generational turnover that has already started to reshape the party and it`s approach to the battle -- a dawning in the midst of cataclysm with a new era of political possibility." Democrats have the absolute political Advantage here if they will press it.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:15]\par \par \uc2 I`m joined Now by Rebecca Traister, writer-at-large for New York Magazine. Rebecca, it was -- it was a great piece, I mean, obviously, substantively. They`re -- you`re committed to abortion rights. You spent your career, you know, reporting on that. But you know, things can be substantively correct and politically tenuous. Like, this there`s lots of things -- I mean, you know, the death penalty for a while has been pretty popular even though I think it`s awful. There is an alignment now between the substance of the politics that I think is unlike anything I`ve seen. What do you -- what do you think about that alignment?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA TRAISTER, WRITER-AT-LARGE, NEW YORK MAGAZINE: Well, the alignment has actually been true for some time, but it`s taken a long time for us to catch up with how popular keeping abortion legal is in this country. You know, I go back in this story to the late 60s and early 70s which was the last time that this was a legislative question, right? There were states that were beginning to legalize abortion on a state level, in New York, Hawaii, Alaska, Washington. There -- and there was pressure being put on the federal government to legalize it, you know, via a Senate bill.\par \par \uc2 And at that time \uc2 Shirley Chisholm\uc2  who is the representative from New York gave remarks in which she said 64 percent of Americans according to a recent poll are in support of abortion rights or want it to be legal, don`t want the government to interfere. And interestingly, then the onus on the legislative work was lifted by Roe in January of 1973. Suddenly, neither political party had to do the work of figuring out how to legislate these rights or this access because the Supreme Court had decided in Roe.\par \par \uc2 And during the Roe years, polling turned up over and over again, 50 divided. This country irrevocably divided. And the Democrats who became the party supporting what was called Choice euphemistically, abortion rights, treated it very gingerly as if it was electric. Like, if they got too close to it, it was -- it was a sharply dividing issue. And it was really only in the past 10 years that a new generation of pollsters began to ask questions differently to ask first are you personally, you know, opposed to abortion, and then second do you think that the government should make decisions about its availability or its legality.\par \par \uc2 When you ask the questions that way, for the past 10 years, we`ve known that somewhere between 60 and 75 percent of Americans do not believe the government should be making the decisions about whether or not you can get abortion care in this country. But it`s taken a party, the Democratic party which is now in charge -- of once again doing this legislatively, a long time to catch up to that reality and realize how popular this position is and to begin to run on it and embrace it as a core value which is the part of the generational shift I think we`re seeing now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Michigan is one example where we`ve seen this a bit, I mean that -- you know. you talk about this a bit that the statewide sweep by democrats There happened when they had a ballot initiative explicitly about abortion that a ton of money was poured into by the other side, right, to scaremonger about that`s going to force sterilization of your daughters. And it was on inarguably a huge part of the success of the Democrats in that state.\par \par \uc2 TRAISTER: It was. And it was -- it was the ballot initiative, it was also you had a set of Democratic lawmakers in the state legislature and the governor and the attorney general there who were fighting on this in advance of the Dobbs decision. So, a lot of Democrats somehow seem to be caught by surprise by Dobbs, even though many people have been able to see this coming for a long time. A lot of the Democratic Party was kind of caught flat-footed.\par \par \uc2 But in Michigan Governor Whitmer had already begun legal maneuverings in advance of Dobbs by several months along with Dana Nessel, her attorney general. And the state legislature had already begun to work toward repealing its 1931 law that could have gone into effect if Roe fell. And so, the elected officials were also tied up with this.\par \par \uc2 And even in the weeks and months directly prior to the Midterm Elections, when a lot of people in the Democratic Party and in the political press were beginning to say, no, no don`t run so much on abortion, making false distinctions between abortion and economic issues, Whitmer and that whole slate of Democrats did not back down and continue to run on it and to talk about it very freely connecting it to so many other issues including the economy and other civil rights and education, all kinds of issues that abortion is connected to. And they won. The proposition three won by 13 points and Whitmer won by 11 points.\par \par \uc2 And it was a remarkable instance of what is possible if you are willing and fluent and able to take this issue up as a -- as a moral good that you understand is connected to so much in American life and American values.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:06]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The flip side of this if you look at DeSantis, I mean, it would be - - it`s political malpractice for him to sign a six-week ban, just independent of what you think about the issue, if you`re solely paid as a political consultant. But he -- they have to do this stuff. Like, the true believers of the Republic Party really want to see abortion ban everywhere for every woman everywhere and will continue to push that.\par \par \uc2 TRAISTER: Yes. Well, and there`s a real -- I think that my story is about the Democratic Party but I think the Republican party is facing its own crucible here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TRAISTER: You know, there is a reason that House Republicans have not yet passed a national ban. They sure could. I mean, it would die in the Senate and Biden would veto it, you know, but there`s no reason for them not to have passed that national ban that \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  was talking about before the election, you know, but they haven`t. There`s a reason why. People -- there`s a reason why they`re fighting. As you point out, these ballot initiatives, they know they`re on the losing side. But Democrats have to learn that they`re on the winning side and actually talk and act like it and think about how and why to present that winning case.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s exactly right. If you`re talking about abortion, you`re winning. You should pick the fight and you should talk about it and center it. And that`s especially true with someone like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  coming off signing a six-week ban. Rebecca Traister, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, what was Fox News hiding and all those redacted documents -- remember that from the Dominion lawsuit? They came out. They were redacted. We found out today in a new court filing. And you`ve thought their craving dishonesty was bad before, just wait. All the details next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Every time we get a new batch of evidence for the Dominion voting machines lawsuit, things look worse and worse for \uc2 Fox\uc2  . Today, NBC News obtained a slide deck presented by Dominion`s lawyers during a hearing last week. It paints a damning picture of a network that was absolutely terrified of reporting the truth if it meant telling its viewers something they did not want to hear. The most revelatory piece of evidence is an email from Fox News CEO Suzanne Scott -- that`s her -- that was previously redacted when \uc2 Fox\uc2  first disclosed these internal communications. Remember there`s a bunch of redactions? This is one of them.\par \par \uc2 Now, in this email, Scott is reacting to this fact check from a Fox reporter in December 2020 who was correctly throwing cold water on Donald Trump`s incendiary claims of a rigged election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It was a massive dump of votes, mostly Biden, almost all Biden. And to this day, everyone is trying to figure out where did it come from.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, ANCHOR, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: We just heard the president say it. He brands it a massive dump of votes. But election officials say it is not what he implies. They say there`s no nefarious big batch for Biden, just votes they insist that are being counted properly as the law requires.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: By the way, that`s not even a rebuttal, that`s just a he said he said situation. Like, Trump says this, election officials say -- after that fact check aired on Fox, CEO Suzanne Scott wrote an email. This is amazing, OK. She wrote an email with \uc2 Fox\uc2  executive who oversees Prime Time programming saying, "This has to stop now. This is bad business. The audience is furious. Bad for business."\par \par \uc2 You can see it right there in these slides directly from Dominion. I have to say, that email from Suzanne Scott, the CEO of \uc2 Fox\uc2  just distills the entire Fox Dominion saga down to its very essence. You truly could not script a better tagline for this whole debacle than this is bad business, the audience is furious. Now, again, we`re just learning of that email now because \uc2 Fox\uc2  initially redacted that email -- I wonder why -- in the big batch of documents that were made public a few weeks ago. I guess they didn`t want public to realize quite how cynical their entire operation is. Believe me, there is much more where that came from.\par \par \uc2 Here`s another one. A few weeks after 2020 election, Suzanne Scott again writes, "I can`t keep defending these reporters who don`t understand our viewers and how to handle stories. The audience feels like we crapped on and we have damaged their trust and belief in us. We lost 25 000 subs from Fox Nation. We can`t fix this but we cannot -- we can fix this but we cannot smirk at our viewers any longer." Smirk at our viewers telling them the truth, telling the election was not rigged.\par \par \uc2 There was obviously real panic at the top level of the Fox -- of the network because \uc2 Fox\uc2  viewers didn`t want to hear their favorite anchors and pundits on their favorite network fact-checking their favorite President. Another exchange is on January 5, 2021. Fox`s owner \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  floats the possibility of having the network`s prime-time host explicitly tell viewers that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the election. This is January 5, OK. Only for Suzanne Scott to appear to pass the message along to another executive with the warning, "we need to be careful about using the shows and pissing off the viewers."\par \par \uc2 In December, Scott also sent along a suggestion to book Mike Pillow, that`s the MAGA conspiracy theorist. I think that`s his name. Who`s one of the most vocal proponents of the big lie because he would "get ratings." Speaking of the Pillow Guy, in another exchange, staff on Tucker Carson`s Show said he "seems to have gone off the deep end" and is "definitely crazy but they still wanted to book him on the show." And this is amazing, all right. Listen to this. Because he was a major advertiser who stuck around. I quote here. "I know there`s concern about him being a conspiracy theorist now, but he has bailed us out loads of times when no one else would." Meaning, he`s bought ads on their program when no one else would. He`s also been moving to \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  lately. It might be good business move to signal we wouldn`t desert him.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:24]\par \par \uc2 At which point, another producer chimes in to add, "he is crazy and about to get sued by Dominion. Prescient. WTF is he going to say on our air.? So, on one hand you`ve got a conspiracy theorist who you admit is a lawsuit waiting to happen because he`s making probably defamatory claims on your air. On the other hand, he`s making it rain all over your show when no one else will. So, maybe you just give him a platform anyway. After all, you can`t have him go spend his money at rival \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  instead.\par \par \uc2 Honestly, there`s so much damning information there. We can easily spend half the show unpacking it. The biggest takeaway is this. Fox`s profit line, and it`s a high profit, was and is so dependent on its audience believing lies that it had to stamp out the truth on its own air in order to keep the money flowing. It is more cynical and craven than just about anything Fox`s worst critics could have imagined.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, a pretty incredible and emotional scene in the United States Capitol. Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  who spent more than a decade as an educator in New York Public Schools said what many of us are feeling thinking today when he took his Republican colleagues to task for their refusal to