ar \uc2 HAYES: Joining me now David Jolly, former Republican Congressman of Florida. \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  was on the program yesterday and she said the terrain is shifting right now. I can feel it. I`m in a swing state. I`m running statewide. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I don`t think it`s going to shift among Republican politicians but it could be shifting with voters who get to hold Republican politicians accountable. And that will be the ultimate test. I mean, we have a generation of Republican politicians who basically have been indoctrinated from the time they enter politics alongside the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and gun lobby talking points. And I think you`re seeing that from \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The reality is even if there is truth to the fact there has not been a school shooting in schools that armed teachers, I would suggest causation - - or correlation does not imply causation.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: And secondly, inevitably, Chris, there will be. And \uc2 Thomas Massie\uc2  will be proven wrong. The data point that should inform all of our judgment on gun deaths is that the United States per capita for 100 people has a higher density of gun ownership by a rate of three to ten times than other developed countries. And we have three to ten times the guns deaths in the United States compared to other developed countries. It is the guns. And I think that`s what Bowman was making clear.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I also think -- I mean, Massie there used to -- not defend him but to I think make clear here, you know, the gun lobby matters a lot. But you`re not you`re not putting your family on a Christmas card holding guns for the gun lobby. Like, again, this is a -- like, the fetishization, the celebration of the gun is an intense cultural totem for folks. It`s not -- like, it`s obviously I think been created by a gun industry that needs to sell weapons, but you know, I think these people really do think that like every single American carrying a weapon would be better for the country. I think that`s their vision.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: No, that`s right. Yes, Chris, I think it`s important difference here between what has to happen with the law and what happens -- has to happen with culture. But the law it`s clear we need to ban weapons of war. We need comprehensive and Universal background checks. And we need zero tolerance. We need to see parents like in the case of the Michigan Shooting face actually legal culpability for their actions of their son should a court find them negligent.\par \par \uc2 But that is -- those are the law fixes if you will. The question around culture is something that ultimately we just have to crush the culture of gun ownership here in the United States because we do have within our -- within the United States this culture that somehow gun ownership creates virulence and creates strength. But what it actually leads to is the death of children.\par \par \uc2 I think it was Claire McCaskill yesterday or a day before who said maybe Democrats and those who sympathize with the Democratic position on this need to launch our own culture war. In the face of all the Republican culture wars around transgenderism and Black and Brown communities and wokism, maybe the culture war in 2024 or needs to be to crush the culture of gun violence in the United States and humiliate Republicans who continue to platform laws that allow the shootings we saw this week in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:57]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s -- when you say the word gun violence, one of the things that always strikes me here and you see it here is if you say the word crime, Republicans -- there`s no -- there`s nothing that`s too much to go after a crime in the big cities. Crime. Crime is the enemy. Throw it. If you say gun violence which is probably the most deadly subset of crime, all of a sudden, it`s like well, what are you going to do. It`s just -- whatever the word is, that just changes everything.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes, Chris, this is going to be hard but for Republicans crime is something that`s committed by Black Americans.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Gun ownership and gun violence is something that is part of the culture of young White men and so Republicans protect it. And that`s just the reality.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, David Jolly, thank you very much. Still ahead, Georgia Republicans try to rip power away from the district attorney investigating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . The latest Republican-led threat to democracy next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As we await a decision from The Fulton County District Attorney on whether to charge anyone in Donald Trump`s attempts to interfere with the 2020 election in Georgia, there`s a bill that`s on its way to the Republican governor of Georgia that would give the state new power over local district attorneys. The Atlanta Journal-Constitution reports that Republicans pushing the bill see it as a way to "sanction rogue prosecutors they say is ineffective or inept."\par \par \uc2 One factor in their push, "Republican leaders clashed last year with Progressive DAs when several announced they had no intention of making arrests related to the state`s newly enacted six-week abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 Sherry Boston is the District Attorney of DeKalb County Georgia, one of those DAs refusing to prosecute abortion-related cases. And she joins me now. I wonder first you could just explain from your standpoint what your understanding of what this law would actually do if and when it signed into law.\par \par \uc2 SHERRY BOSTON, DISTRICT ATTORNEY, DEKALB COUNTY GEORGIA: Well, what this law would do would be that a complaint could then be filed with commit -- this commission by any citizen whether they had personal knowledge or not to try to get a local district attorney removed from office. And it should concern everyone because it absolutely is a way to usurp the local voters who have made a decision about the type of district attorney they want protecting and serving their community.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, this would give this commission appointed by the governor essentially kind of like the power to just impeach essentially or just remove local district attorneys based on a citizen complaint?\par \par \uc2 BOSTON: Absolutely. Removing someone from office and if removed, they would be unable to run for re-election again for 10 years. And what is even more strikingly upsetting about this is while in the state of Georgia, we have the ability for lawyers and judges to be reviewed for their actions, all of that goes through the Supreme Court of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 BOSTON: But this commission singularly would be able to make these decisions about individual DAs.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Do you think the motivation -- what do you think -- what is your understanding of why this laws is being passed?\par \par \uc2 BOSTON: Well, when they use headlines and talk about rogue or woke prosecutors. I think their intentions are pretty clear, that they want to make sure that locally elected DAs who ran on campaign promises of criminal justice reform and protecting their communities in the way that they see fit, they`re not happy with that. They want justice done in just one way.\par \par \uc2 And we know that communities are all different and that every community has an idea of how they can best be served. And as we see a history-making number of elected district attorneys of color, of elected Democratic district attorneys in Georgia and across the country, it`s not a coincidence that now you`re seeing these legislative and gubernatorial actions to try to stamp out what the voters have asked to be placed in.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The situation in Fulton County, obviously, I don`t think that was the motivation for this. I think there was a pre-existing motivation of this sort of desire to take away self-determination from cities -- voting citizens in places like your county right that voted in a way State Republicans don`t like. But it obviously clearly amounts to a kind of, you know, a sort of implicit threat, right? You know, who`s going to be on this commission and who`s -- is anyone going to file a complaint? Certainly, if \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  were to indict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or people around him, this seems to me that kind of poison the well should it come to pass vis-a-vis that case in particular.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:24]\par \par \uc2 BOSTON: Well, I think you`re absolutely going to see circumstances where anyone that is not happy with a prosecution or that`s happening or not happening, you`re absolutely going to see abuse of this type of commission against district attorneys who are just trying to do their jobs and that are trying to make the difficult prosecution decisions that we make every day by using our best discretion. And that is what these types of bills all across the country and in Georgia are trying to do which is to take away our discretion and dictate how a D.A. should properly do their job based on what folks under -- in the legislature think it`s the right way to do it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Maybe I`m being overly literal here, but aren`t they going to get like, tens of thousands of complaints? I mean, is there anyone who`s prosecuted successfully for a crime going to -- I mean, you know, I don`t know, maybe that`d be good. Maybe that`s -- maybe we should have this everywhere. But like, just the sheer torrent of complaints, right? I mean, if all you have to do is be a citizen and you can file a complaint, it just seems like a totally unworkable idea.\par \par \uc2 BOSTON: It absolutely is. And I can tell you, I`ve been district attorney since 2017 in DeKalb County, and I can tell you when you`re doing your job right, it means that you`re always not making one side of a party particularly not happy. So, the idea that we`re setting up a commission that can feel these types of complaints that many of which are going to be without merit but are going to force prosecutors to have to explain their actions to persons that were put on that commission from a political body with no Supreme Court oversight is absolutely a recipe for disaster and certainly is aimed at certain prosecutors and not all of them.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I think that`s pretty clear. Sherry Boston, thank you for making some time with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 BOSTON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, after he squared off with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  Chief \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  earlier today, Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  joins me on the company`s union-busting tactics, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s a moment back in 2019 when it appeared that former \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  CEO \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  was going to run for president as an independent in 2020. He suggests his campaign would be funded by his own personal fortune. He hired high-profile consultants like \uc2 Steve Schmidt\uc2  and Bill Burton. But his nascent presidential bid flamed out incredibly fast. I mean, record time really. And after that, two things happened. Schultz returned to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  as an interim CEO in 2022 and the labor movement at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  broke through after decades of not having a single unionized shop in the entire country.\par \par \uc2 After the first win for Starbucks Workers United in Buffalo in 2021, more than 270 other stores followed suit and unionized. But \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  under Schultz has been fighting unionization tooth and nail. Judges have found the company has broken federal labor law 130 times. All that teed up today`s anticipated showdown between one of the most pro-labor members of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  , Vermont Independent Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  who chairs the Health Education Labor and Pensions Committee, and Schultz who was interim CEO until earlier this month and who came to testify about \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  labor practices.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): Have you ever threatened, coerced, or intimidated a worker for supporting a union?\par \par \uc2 HOWARD SCHULTZ\uc2  , INTERIM CEO, \uc2 STARBUCKS\uc2  : I`ve had conversations that could have been interpreted in a different way than I intended.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Have you ever asked a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  worker, "If you hate \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  so much, why don`t you go work somewhere else.?"\par \par \uc2 SCHULTZ: I`m glad you asked that question because I`ve read in the press that quote, and that`s not exactly what I said.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Joining me now is Vermont independent Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  , the chair of that committee. Senator, why was it important for you to have this hearing today and to have \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  there?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, a couple of reasons, Chris. First of all, it`s important for the people of this country to understand that everybody has got to obey the law even if you are a billionaire and the head of a large multinational corporation like \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  . And what \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has done on the Schultz`s leadership is basically break the law and they have absolute confidence they can get away with it. Who cares? They`re rich, they`re powerful, what is anyone going to do to them? So, today was a reminder that billionaires and corporate leaders are going to have to obey the law.\par \par \uc2 Second point is at a time when the middle class and working class of this country are struggling, workers in large numbers are now saying, you know what, we need better wages, better health care, better working conditions, better scheduling. We are going to come together and form unions, not just at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  , but all over this country. We`re seeing a huge uptick in labor organizing.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is able to crush that union movement because they break the law and refuse to negotiate a first contract, it will have a very chilling effect on workers all over this country who are standing up and fighting back. That`s why the hearing was important.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:20]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, I want follow up on that to because, you know, there`s two things always happening in labor law. One is violations of labor law which don`t -- often don`t get punished, right? People are able to break it. And then the labor law itself not being sufficiently strong. You just mentioned something that`s been a real problem across the country, unionizing efforts in which the union wins recognition whether through voluntary recognition or through union election, and then finds themselves stonewalled on that first contract.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What does the law say about that?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, the law is very weak. So, if you are a smart executive like Mr. Schultz who wants to break a union, what you do is you stall and stall and you refuse to negotiate. And it -- what`s even worse is there are instances where people, you know, just takes them forever to negotiate a first contract. And you have workers who give up, turnover is very high, and that`s how they were able to defeat unions.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of that -- your point is well taken. And that is why we need strong labor law reform which is what the Pro Act is about which says that if you violate labor law, you know what, you`re going to get fined, you`re going to get punished. If you don`t sign a first contract within a reasonable period of time, you`re going to get fined, you`re going to get punished. No, you cannot take workers into a back room and threaten them. That`s illegal. You will be punished for that.\par \par \uc2 So, we need that strong labor law to give strength to workers and support to workers who are fighting for their dignity on the jobs.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, just to be clear, they`re like punishment recrimination, intimidation about someone`s union support is flatly illegal under the Wagner Act, the National Labor Relations Act. But you know, extending contract time isn`t, right? That`s -- right now, that`s under law to your point about the Pro Act.\par \par \uc2 I want to play something that your colleague \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  said about a conflict of interest that he alleged that you and other Democrats on the hearing had and give you a chance to respond to. That take a listen to what Senator Romney said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITT ROMNEY (R-UT): It`s somewhat rich that you`re being grilled by people who have never had the opportunity to create a single job, and yet they believe that they know better how to do so. I also think it`s rich to not recognize the extraordinary conflict of interest we have which is our Democratic colleagues overwhelmingly get their campaign funds from unions, and therefore would like to find every possible way to extend unions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What do you say to that allegation of conflict?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, I find it amazing that people who refused to overturn \uc2 Citizens United\uc2  , who get elected by super PACs which in an undisclosed way raise money from a handful of billionaires is talking about, you know. candidates who got their money from labor unions. Second of all, I think you know, what Romney was trying to do which is what other Republicans were trying to do because they have nothing real to say about the issue is to deflect attention from one simple fact. Should a large company be able to get away with breaking the law and making it impossible for workers to exercise their constitutional rights to form union.\par \par \uc2 That was the issue of this hearing and unfortunately, Romney and some of the other Republicans had nothing relevant to say about that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One of the huge consequences of presidential elections has to do with the staffing of places like the FTC, the \uc2 FCC\uc2  , the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  , the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  . This hugely consequential often flies under the radar. Corporate America and lobbyists tend to be very keyed into this because it matters a lot.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What is your assessment of the performance of this \uc2 NLRB\uc2  under President Biden?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  and Chief Counsel Abruzzo were being attacked today by the Republicans. They are actually shocked that we have an agency of government doing what it is supposed to do. And that`s what the mission of the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  is to protect not to be pro-worker or pro-corporation but protect workers` rights and make sure the corporations obey the law. And that`s what this \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has done. And they deserve credit for doing that.\par \par \uc2 But this is really quite shocking to some of my Republican colleagues who cannot believe there is actually an agency of government which is demanding fair play for workers.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  who is the chair of that HELP Committee today that had \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  in before them, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, that is all in on this Wednesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now with my friend Ali Velshi who is in for Alex. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Which I`m not ready to do because I spent the last few minutes just listening to that conversation.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230330ej3t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for March 29, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Kathy Park, Harry Smith \par \uc2 8,050 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Randall Williams gets tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:08]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight never before seen evidence in Dominion`s lawsuit against Fox News. The network`s so called Brain Room found no evidence of fraud. But hosts continued to push the conspiracy theories on air as top leadership called fact checking bad for business.\par \par \uc2 Then, Democrats holding Republicans feet to the fire over gun safety legislation demanding a vote be brought to the floor. Congressman Dave Cicilline joins me live.\par \par \uc2 And a new Republican led bill in Idaho would make abortion access a whole lot harder in a first of its kind trafficking law as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle, live from Washington, DC. Newly revealed evidence is ratcheting up the legal pressure on Fox News. The network is facing a 1.6 billion with a V dollar defamation suit from \uc2 Dominion Voting Systems\uc2 . MSNBC has obtained several slides of internal Fox communications used by Dominion lawyers in pretrial hearings.\par \par \uc2 Fox had tried to keep these secret, but a judge released them. They show in new detail how the network repeatedly aired conspiracy theories about Dominion in the 2020 election, even after Fox`s fact checkers in what it calls the Brain Room said they were not true.\par \par \uc2 One fact check from November 13, 2020 says this quote, claims about Dominion switching or deleting votes are 100 percent false. Another reads the facts. There`s no evidence of widespread fraud in 2020 election. By the way, if you`re looking at your screen, that right there, that was highlighted in yellow. That was done in the court document, not by NBC News.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who promoted and spread those lies about the election and continues to, has appealed a judge`s order that his former chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and other top aides must appear before the grand jury investigating January 6. Of course, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  has also been ordered to testify in that investigation.\par \par \uc2 We told you all about it last night and here`s what he said when asked if he planned to appeal that ruling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: I`ll be in Washington DC later this week and I`ll be meeting with my counsel at that time, and we`ll review the judge`s decision and we`ll determine the best way forward. But again, I have nothing to hide. At the end of the day, we`ll obey the law, but right now we`re evaluating what the proper course is going forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: As he said again, he has nothing to hide. I will say again, he`s welcome to join me any night. Also tonight, First Lady Jill Biden joined Nashville residents at a candle at Vigil in honor of the three nine year old children and three adults who were killed in a shooting at Covenant School just two days ago.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Kathy Park talked to one man who helped some of the children escape that gunfire.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): New images from Monday`s mass shooting in Nashville show young children running across a busy road escaping gunfire from the Covenant School.\par \par \uc2 JASON HOFFMAN, NASHIVILLE SHOOTING WITNESS: I saw these kids were screaming. They were crying, and I had no idea what was even happening. But I knew that it wasn`t good.\par \par \uc2 PARK: Hearing the gunshots and seeing the panic in front of him, Jason Hoffman knew he had to stop driving and help them get away by stopping traffic.\par \par \uc2 PARK (on camera): There a moment that`s kind of seared in your mind forever?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: The teacher hugging that little girl? Because all I could think about was my child in that same spot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: With that, let`s get smarter. With the help of our lead off panel tonight, my dear friend Carol Leonnig is here in the studio in person. Pulitzer prize winning investigative reporter with the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , former federal prosecutor Glenn Kirschner and Geoff Bennett, the co-anchor of PBS NewsHour, he is also PBS`s chief Washington correspondent and an MSNBC political contributor.\par \par \uc2 I feel like I`m living in a pre-COVID world, surrounded by friends and colleagues with some serious news to cover. Geoff, I turn to you. The CEO of Fox News, Suzanne Scott, sent this to an executive about in December of 2020, about a reporter who was fact checking Donald Trump`s fraud claims, which we know were false. Suzanne Scott, president of the news organization, writes this, quote, this has to stop now. This is bad business. The audience is furious. Bad for business. End quote.\par \par \uc2 Bad for business. So let`s keep lying to them.\par \par \uc2 GEOFF BENNETT, CO-ANCHOR PBS NEWSHOUR: What it shows is that Fox News is primarily a business enterprise. Here you have the head of the entire, quote, unquote news organization castigating a reporter for doing their job. Fact checking I think it was a Trump tweet.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:00]\par \par \uc2 If I were counsel for Fox, I would try to settle this case on the courthouse steps, in part because in this defamation suit, the standard is not intent. The standard is reckless disregard. And what you have are emails from Suzanne Scott, from all of the primetime personalities, saying that they know that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the rightful winner of the 2020 election. They know that everything that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was saying, all the conspiracy theories he floated, they knew all of that were vicious lies. And yet telling the truth to their viewers that was bad for business. That needed to stop to use the phrase that Suzanne Scott used.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But if they settle, Glenn, can`t that lead to a cascade of other lawsuits about other vicious lies that they might be spreading day in and day out, possibly tonight?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: And it probably should lead to other suits. I agree with Geoff. You know, liability here seems to be a given. Now it`s all about the green. It`s all about the damages that Dominion can prove Fox did to their business. But I don`t think it stops here because when they are caught in these kind of lies, I agree they blow right past recklessness and they are intentionally selling lies to the American people because they want the revenue to continue flowing in to Fox`s coffers.\par \par \uc2 So, we likely will see suit after suit. The question is, does this spell the end of Fox because they can`t weather the financial storm? That`s a question I can`t answer.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But why would we think this would end Fox News? If you watch it any night or day of the week, they haven`t changed what they`re doing. Even though those specific hosts that are the most prominent here, they`re still on every day. If Fox was feeling the heat or the pressure, don`t you think they would change course?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You know, nobody can accuse Fox of learning from its mistakes, there`s no doubt about that. But how many billion dollar plus settlements can an organization like that weather? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, THE WASHINGTON POST INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER: Those emails are blockbuster. I`m sorry, I just have to intervene and say, you know, if anybody in our fact checking room and this is true for NBC News, but at the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  if anybody in our fact checking room had said in writing orally and someone else was a witness, this is factually wrong, and we continued to do it, we`d all be out of jobs. I think the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  would have a new name, and it would be whomever had sued us.\par \par \uc2 It is stunning to me that the actual standards unit, the ethics, the factual basis team, the brain room, told them stop and they kept doing it. That, to me, is going to tip this in the wrong direction for Fox.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: They don`t actually have a standards department, though. So in n any scenario, could we see regulations change? Right? Think about how big a standards department is at a news organization. They don`t even have one. And it`s not like they`re trying to create one. Are we kidding ourselves to say, My gosh, they`re going to really have to change? They`re not. And guess what?\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Here`s the ugly truth. There`s a whole lot more people watching Fox News at this very moment than are watching the four of us. And that`s the truth.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Here`s the thing that gives me hope to your point. Fox News pulls in on a good night, 3 million viewers. 150 million people voted in the last election. So what I`m hoping is that good reporting wins the day and that even if Fox isn`t reporting on itself, and people who watch Fox have no idea that \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  are saying one thing about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in private and saying something very different on the air every night, I`m hoping that good reporting rises to the top.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Are you surprised Fox hasn`t settled? I mean, jury selection could start in a couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: It is strange that they`re letting this information blow up in the public square. These emails, this internal, you know, you have the Fox brain room, which appears to be completely disconnected from the Fox body, right? The anchors and the guests whose lies they sponsor.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t quite understand why Fox is playing it this way, letting all the evidence spill out into the public square, and they`re still going to have to pay out at the end of the day. I don`t know why they didn`t rush to settle this.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: New topic. Carol, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is appealing this ruling that \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and other aides are going to have to testify. What does that tell you about how important or damaging to Trump Meadows testimony could be?\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a team of lawyers that were giving him good legal advice, he would know that the only thing going for him in this filing is delay. The only thing going because executive privilege doesn`t Trump, no pun intended, a criminal grand jury, and especially when you`re no longer the president.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:05]\par \par \uc2 President Biden holds the executive privilege at this point. President -- former President Trump should know at this point, the only thing he`s buying is time.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And that works for him.\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: It works for him until it doesn`t. I mean, remember, a lot of people had to tell \uc2 Bob Mueller\uc2  what they actually saw and heard inside the White House. And I know that everyone with a partisan view has some disappointment about Mueller`s report, but ultimately, he got to some really important kernels of truth.\par \par \uc2 And in this case, it`s impossible to believe that the chief judge is not going to rule in favor again, and then the appellate court is going to rule in favor again of this testimony being the property of Jack Smith.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes, but will the testimony actually ever happen? Right? We`re talking about it. We`re talking about \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is being compelled to testify. He`s most likely going to appeal. Do we actually think in any timely manner right? I don`t even use the word imminent anymore because it`s meaningless that we`ll actually see \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  or see \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  sitting before a grand jury?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: We won`t see it because it will be secret --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: -- but it will happen. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  will absolutely be told to raise his right hand, swear to tell the truth, and be questioned about the pressure campaign waged by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to try to get \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to overturn the results of a presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Why do I say that, Steph? First of all, the courts seem to be their patience is wearing thin. Look at how quickly they forced Evan Corcoran, Donald Trump`s own criminal defense attorney into that grand jury, and he testified for three and a half hours. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , he has the same fate awaiting him.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I asked this question last night, and I have to ask it again. What political lane is \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  trying to operate in? Right? If he doesn`t really want to testify, if he`s not really turning on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because he doesn`t want to alienate his base said base has already been alienated. They`re not coming back.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: OK, I`ll explain it this way. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , his base of support is about 30 percent to 40 percent, not a majority. It`s enough to win a primary if there are enough other Republicans running and they split the vote. OK, so set that aside.\par \par \uc2 What \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is banking on is that he can, state by state in Iowa, in New Hampshire, pick off enough support so that he can emerge as the acceptable alternative to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He`s trying to win the so called normal Republican lane. We will see if what he`s betting on actually works. But that`s the dice that he`s rolling.\par \par \uc2 And so the idea that he would say, well, I`m not going to, I think he used the phrase my testimony is not something that belongs to Congress, or that`s why he said he wasn`t going to testify to January 6 Committee. And I`ve been told that one of the things he was waiting for was potential cover to testify and that the subpoena might actually do that.\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: I have to agree with you, Geoff. I think that Pence`s choice of legal strategy here, choosing the very novel speech or debate clause as his privilege he knew executive privilege wasn`t going to shield his testimony. And the speech or debate clause refers to this tiny portion, essentially, of his day on January 6, his role as the leader of the Senate. So he -- he is trying to tell that Republican base, I`m not giving it up willy nilly, but he is ultimately going to comply with this. He is going to provide his testimony.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: If I were \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , though, I wouldn`t be so worried about the \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  testimony. I`d be worried about the \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  testimony because there is no other White House staffer who was in as close contact with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the day of the siege. Meadows had intimate knowledge of everything that led up to January 6, and so he can speak to Donald Trump`s state of mind. Remember Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Sure do.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: Where she said that the former president didn`t want to help \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , even as people were saying, Hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , because he, quote unquote, deserved it. \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  knows all about that. And by the way, he was also dispatched to Georgia in December of 2020 to set up that now infamous call with Brad Vassensberger. So there`s a lot and texts.\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: He gave 2,300 texts to the House Committee, and there`s like a thousand more he never turned over.\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: Broken record. I agree with you again, Geoff. There`s another important moment. \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  insisted to aides in the White House in mid- December to late December. I want you to tell me if any members of the Freedom Caucus, Republicans come in and try to meet with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, who --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He was trying to run interference.\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: Well, he was trying to bird dog what was happening, and he is in the room with Freedom Caucus members when, as you all may famously remember, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  comes out I think it`s December 19 or December 20, posts a video saying we`re so proud.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:10]\par \par \uc2 We`ve just come up with -- we`ve just met with the President. We`ve got a plan for January 6 and how we`re going to, you know, stop the steal.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Here`s how this old prosecutor sees the difference between Mike Pence`s situation and Mark Meadow`s situation. They both have sharply incriminating evidence about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , though, probably doesn`t have a Fifth Amendment right against self-incrimination to invoke \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . Absolutely does.\par \par \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  lawyer will say, if push comes to shove and the executive privilege claim fails, you need to walk into the grand jury and plead the Fifth over and over and over again. Then Jack Smith has a decision to make. Does he immunize \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and compel his testimony against Trump? Or does he make \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  a co-defendant and put him at council table with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , trying to force him to flip and become a cooperating witness? That`s the way I see this playing out.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, we`re out of time. But as Carol said a moment ago, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had good lawyers giving him good advice, anyone who we just mentioned in the last 16 minutes, I sure hope they were tuning in for the last 16 minutes, or a friend should send them that video. You all just got some legal advice.\par \par \uc2 Geoff Bennett, Carol Leonnig and Glenn Kirschner. He gave it to you for free. Thank you all for being here in person. And before we go, I want to share a quick note about how we are covering former President Trump. We are going to cover facts and court filings and indictments surrounding the former president when they happen. We are not going to breathlessly chase every rumor when the grand jury may be meeting, or maybe not. There`s plenty of actual news today, like the shootings in Tennessee and what we can do about it, and abortion rights being under attack in places like the state of Idaho.\par \par \uc2 That is what you will hear before you turn in every night on THE 11TH HOUR.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of when we come back, Rhode Island Congressman Dave Cicilline is here on his renewed push for an assault weapons ban. We`ll bring you the passionate arguments from Democrats today after the Nashville devastating school shooting.\par \par \uc2 Later, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ramping up attacks on \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , as some big money Republican donors are looking for someone else. Our political panel is here to break it all down. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a busy Wednesday night in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:22:08]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JARED MOSKOWITZ (R-FL): AR-15s. You have seen what those bullets do to children? You know why you don`t hunt with an AR-15 with a deer? Because there`s nothing left. There`s nothing left as these kids when people go into school and murder them while they`re trying to read. You guys are worried about banning books. Dead kids can`t read.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Dead kids can`t read. There you have it. Democratic lawmakers are demanding action after another tragic school shooting. They`re renewing their calls for a vote on an assault weapons ban. But despite no urgency from Republicans to tackle this, Democrats are not backing down. Watch what we saw today between Democrat \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2  and Republican Tom Massie.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JAMAAL BOWMAN\uc2  (D-NY): They`re all cowards. They won`t do anything to save the lives of our children at all. Cowards. Pressure them. Force them to respond to the question, why the hell won`t you do anything to save America`s children?\par \par \uc2 And let them explain that all the way up until next day on 2024. Let them explain it all the way up to election day on 2024. They`re freaking cowards. They`re gullets they`re not here. I`m talking about gun violence.\par \par \uc2 REP. THOMAS MASSIE (R-KY): You know, there`s never been a school shooting in a school that allows teachers to carry.\par \par \uc2 BOWMAN: Carry guns? More guns lead to more death. More guns lead to more death. Look at the data. You`re not looking at any data for the gun lobby. Look at the data. More guns lead to more death. States that have open carry laws have more death. States that have open carry laws have more death. States that have open carry laws have more death\par \par \uc2 Listen to what I`m saying. What (INAUDIBLE) children are dying.\par \par \uc2 MASSIE: I know I got --\par \par \uc2 BOWMAN: Nine-year old children. The solution is not arming teachers. Have you ever worked in the school? Have you ever worked in the school? Have you ever worked in the school? Have you ever worked in the school?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do you why \uc2 Jamaal Bowman\uc2 , congressman from New York, is asking Tom Massie, have you ever worked in a school? Because Congressman Bowman has. He has been a teacher and a principal. He has had to stand before dozens of students and he`s just picturing imagine of a shooting happened in his classroom.\par \par \uc2 With us for more, Democratic Congressman Dave Cicilline of Rhode Island. He is one of the House Democrats calling on House Speaker Kevin McCarthy to schedule a vote on an assault weapons ban. Congressman, I appreciate you being here. We`ve had an assault weapons ban in this country before. What do you want? Want our viewers to know about your plan?\par \par \uc2 [23:25:00]\par \par \uc2 REP. DAVID CICILLINE (D-RI): Well, first of all, the assault weapons ban worked. When the assault weapons ban was in place, there was a 25 percent reduction in mass shootings, a 40 percent reduction in fatalities. These are weapons that were designed for the battlefield. They are designed with one purpose to kill as many people as quickly as humanly possible.\par \par \uc2 They have characteristics that are designed for warfare. They don`t belong in our communities. They don`t belong in our schools or our theaters or in shopping malls. And we had a ban. It worked. And we have tried ever since to put it back into effect. We passed it in the last Congress out of the House in a bipartisan vote. We reinstated the assault weapons ban. It wasn`t taken up by the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But this is something we can do to take these weapons, which have such lethality, they can kill at such a rate that they were getting used on the battlefield. That`s what they were designed for. They don`t belong in communities where people live.\par \par \uc2 So we`re asking the Speaker to bring the bill to the floor for a vote. We passed it in the last Congress. You know, since Columbine, there have been 348,000 children in this country who have been exposed to gun violence. It is now the leading cause of death among children in America gun violence.\par \par \uc2 We have a school shooting almost every day. We have 43,000 Americans who die by gun violence every year. This is an epidemic. And, by the way, it`s in a peculiarly American problem. This doesn`t exist in any other country.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, most important question. You asked McCarthy to bring it to a vote. Did he respond?\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: It has not responded yet. We know from what we`re hearing from the Republican conference that their response to this gun violence has been two things. One, a hearing we just had to abolish the ATF. And secondly, proposals that will be introduced, apparently, this week to eliminate gun free zones at schools.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, but that`s from the likes of a Tom Massie, who`s saying, more guns, more safety. There are other Republicans. You said this has passed in the House before. Since you proposed this morning, have you heard from any of your Republican colleagues who have said, I could get on board with that?\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: No. We only had two Republicans in the last Congress. One of them is no longer there, and one of them remains. So we likely have at least one Republican vote. But the point is, this bill ought to be brought to the floor so the American people can see where their members of Congress stand on this issue. And hopefully, they will reelect members who are committed to protecting young people in this country from gun violence, and they will throw out the people who are not willing to do that.\par \par \uc2 But avoiding a vote is cowardly. It allows them to avoid responsibility for their decision. We have an absolute responsibility to show the American people who is fighting for common sense gun safety legislation that will help keep our kids and our community safe and who`s standing in the way.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: New topic. Last year, you proposed different legislation, antitrust legislation. You`ve had a huge amount of focus on social media companies, the impact they`re having on our kids, on our culture, on society. What is your take on TikTok? Do you support this idea to ban the app entirely in the United States?\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: Look, I think a lot of the legislation we propose is because these large technology platforms are monopolies. They are using their monopoly power to relentlessly surveil and collect data and information about American citizens. There are no privacy protections in place. And because they have no real competition, there`s no incentive for them to be careful about what they use or how they apply algorithms to amplify what people post.\par \par \uc2 This is a big problem. It`s a big problem for American technology companies. It`s a big problem for any of these large platforms, including TikTok.\par \par \uc2 What we ought to be doing is passing legislation to protect the privacy of all Americans, to protect the use of data so that people will have to consent to the use of their data before it can be monetized. And that won`t apply to anyone who does business in the United States, not just TikTok.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I think there`s a lot of interest in TikTok, but the very same issues that people are critical of in terms of the misinformation, the collection of data, the misuse of that. It`s happening every single day on all these other large platforms twitter, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 . I mean, these are important.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I mean, we giggle when we think about a product, when we barely even say it out loud, and then 4 hours later, we`re seeing an ad for it.\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But that`s scary.\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: Yes. And that`s because these companies are surveillance machines. They`re just collecting and collecting and then monetizing that data. Most people don`t realize that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Lobbying machines, too. That why none of this is getting passed?\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: We spent hundreds of millions of dollars to stop our proposals. We got three proposals passed, three we didn`t. They`ll be back. But they have spent, you know, look, they have generated profits never seen in the history of the world. They want to protect this ecosystem that allows them not to have competition, to behave like monopolies, to abuse workers, to take advantage of consumers. They`re going to spend every penny they can to protect that. They spend hundreds of millions of dollars to defeat these bills and prevent reforms.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:03[\par \par \uc2 They`re going to keep at it. But the good news is the American people are demanding that we rein in big tech they understand the danger.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Congressman Cicilline, you are so accustomed to doing television, there you are just staring right at the camera where I`m looking at you, right here, like, come on, Congressman, face to face. Here`s what I really want you to answer for me.\par \par \uc2 I watched the TikTok hearing last week. I was fired up over it. I watched \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in his hearing today. They get grilled on the Hill. A lot of important questions, a lot of big issues. But what happens next? Because based on my experience, they get humiliated on the Hill, they hop on their private planes, they go home, they`re having a good night`s sleep and nothing comes of it. It`s a big show for Congress that makes a great sound bite for you guys and video that goes viral and does nothing for the American people.\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: No, look, you`re absolutely right. That`s why when we had the hearings, when the Democrats were in charge, we did the hearings. We had the four CEOs there. We generated a 450-page report and then we introduced five pieces of legislation. They were all bipartisan. We fought hard to get them across the finish line. We got three of them done, two remain.\par \par \uc2 But look, there`s a reason that monopolies are so dangerous because with huge economic power comes tremendous political power. And that`s why democracies are monopolies are inconsistent with democracy. This is not going to be an easy fight, but the American people expect us to reign in big tech and to protect them. And Congress will get that done.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: With huge economic power comes huge political power, which is why we have got to follow the money every damn night. Congressman Dave Cicilline. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 CICILLINE: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we come back, former President Trump is not waiting for a formal announcement from his former ally \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . He is already hitting his potential 2024 rival and it may be just the tip of the iceberg when it comes to a brutal primary fight. When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:36:33]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Ron DeSanctimonious is being crushed in the polls. All of them. Many globalists are having second thoughts. Rhinos like Karl Rove, Pauline Ryan and Jeb low energy Bush are beside themselves. They don`t know what`s going on. They have rarely witnessed a cratering of this magnitude.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I mean, the words Pauline Ryan appeared to be scripted in a prompter. This is 2023. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  unleashed a new string of direct attacks on \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . In several videos he shot today, he repeated one of his favorites that Florida was great long before the current governor got there. Trump also said DeSantis was not loyal to him. Back with us tonight to discuss my colleague, MSNBC. Host Symone Sanders-Townsend, formerly Vice President Harris`s chief spokesperson and former Connecticut Republican Congressman Chris Shays.\par \par \uc2 Symone, there`s a new report out that says a Trump-DeSantis matchup could be unpredictable and a long and bitter dog fight.\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, FORMERLY VICE PRESIDENT HARRIS`S CHIEF SPOKESPERSON: Yes, I agree. Look, but I think it`s I again, I could be wrong, but I do believe that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is quite possibly the Scott Walker of 2024. It is a long remember Scott Walker? Very popular Republican governor, took on the Democrats in Wisconsin, gave it to him, got Republican gains, and then do the kids even know Scott Walker nowadays? They don`t.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: They don`t remember Scott Walker or the big \uc2 Foxconn\uc2  plant that was supposed to be breaking ground there. He didn`t break through, and neither did that plant.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Exactly. So I do think it is a long road before the first Republican debate. I know folks are like, will there be debates? I think there will be in August. So, I really think people need to pump their brakes. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  hasn`t even declared his candidacy yet, but I really think people putting all their eggs in Ron DeSantis`s basket. They may be surprised. We don`t know how he`s going to perform on a debate stage.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Chris, The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is reporting that there are some big GOP donors that are feeling skittish about Ron DeSantis`s ability to step to Trump, and they want to find a backup candidate. If not DeSantis, who would that be? Right. Who`s in the roster? Right.\par \par \uc2 There are some traditional Republicans of a different time who you may know well, who are thinking, what about the Mitt Romney`s of the world? Let`s be honest about who the true Republican base is today and who other contenders could be realistically.\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. CHRISTOPHER SHAYS, (R-CT): It gives me a headache. That`s what we do here, because what you have is you have Trump and then you have a Florida governor running as the moderate Trump. And it`s not a great choice, but.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Is he the moderate Trump or is he just not potentially going to be indicted Trump?\par \par \uc2 SHAYS: He`s a Trump that`s not going to say nasty things, but he`s going to focus on some of the same issues that Trump is focused on.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, I want to talk to you about a different topic, something you had been focused on in the past, a different topic. Gun legislation. As a Republican in Congress in 1994, you bucked your party and you led the GOP support for the 1994 assault weapons ban. And I actually want to share what you said on the House floor back then. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAYS: Americans have the constitutional right to bear arms. They have that protection. It`s there, and it must be maintained. Their government has the constitutional right and responsibility to regulate that right.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [23:40:11]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All these years later, after so much has happened, what are you thinking when it comes to gun safety and the things your party is saying today?\par \par \uc2 SHAYS: Well, when I watched your program and the ones that preceded you yesterday, I felt sick. Because you heard Congressman say, well, there`s nothing we can do. And that`s just bullshit. The bottom line, the Democrats could petition out the bill. Petition out the bill.\par \par \uc2 We did that with campaign finance reform. It was a Republican House. The Republicans didn`t want the campaign finance reform. It was shades (ph) me in the House, McCain fine gold in the Senate, and the Democrats and enough Republicans petitioned the bill out. So petition it out. There`s only a margin of four Democrats, excuse me, Republicans in the House.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But where do you think this is coming from? Truly, there`s nothing we can do. Does the \uc2 NRA\uc2  have that much money and power?\par \par \uc2 SHAYS: I`m not sure it`s the \uc2 NRA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Who is it?\par \par \uc2 SHAYS: I was trying to encourage Republicans to sign on to the bill, `94. And some out west, they got nervous. They knew they should do it, but they couldn`t do it because there are some parts of the country where people don`t abuse guns and they represent those people. I mean, there are some people that respect guns. So that was part of the fact.\par \par \uc2 And remember, Republicans took control in `95 because 19 Democrats voted for the assault weapon ban. Powerful Democrats who lost. I had two good friends I encouraged to vote for the bill. Dick Sweat, a Democrat from New Hampshire, Peter from -- Smith from Vermont. Bernie Sanders, in fact, opposed the assault weapon ban and be Peter Smith. So, Republicans remember, Democrats remember that it was not a positive thing to vote for the bill. They lost power.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: I think it`s really important what the Congressman said about that he does not think it`s just the \uc2 NRA\uc2 . I think we put a lot of stock into the \uc2 NRA\uc2 . They used to once be a very powerful lobby. Yes, they are still giving dollars.\par \par \uc2 But it is not the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , it is voters. What Republican members of Congress are and the Congressman just told you from 1994 to all the way up to 2023, what people are hamstrung by right now are the folks within their districts. And I think that we have to stop putting this in partisan language. We just need to ask the question, do we think children should go to school for fear of losing their lives at the hand of an active shooter?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Or do we need to get some of those former colleagues of yours in those Western states? Right? I don`t come from a state that has gun culture. Right. Guns have never been part of my life. But for those who represent those states, should they be the ones that should start to try to appeal to their voters on common sense gun legislation. Somebody from Montana might not want to hear from a New York City person, but maybe if their local lawmaker started to think about this.\par \par \uc2 SHAYS: Whoever runs against Trump has to show that he`s willing to take the heat. Wouldn`t it be interesting if DeSantis said we need some gun control legislation, something that would stir things up. Wouldn`t it be interesting if Pence said, you know what? I know what we all think. I know the debate, but this is crazy. We got to do something about it. And I`m going to support sensible gun legislation.\par \par \uc2 Gun legislation just, you know, having people have background checks, making sure they lock up their guns. The reason why the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and other gun advocates oppose they think it`s the camel`s head under the tent. They think if you do one thing, you`ll do another. But let`s just do sensible stuff.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Except, of course, those are two unemployed guys and the governor of Florida that have no control of any federal lawmaking. Congress could do something tomorrow. Symone Sanders-Towsend, Christopher Shays. Thank you both for being here tonight.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, seven months after a near total abortion ban went into effect in Idaho, lawmakers there say even more drastic action is needed to cut off any access to abortion. We`ll explain on the other side of the break, at the side of the hour.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:49:09]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Another very serious story to discuss tonight. Idaho, one of 13 states with a near total abortion banned, and their Republican led state legislature is now doubling down. The Idaho state house advanced a bill banning so called abortion trafficking. The bill could make it a felony to help a minor leave the state to access abortion care without parental consent. The bill is expected to pass the state senate and will likely be signed into law by Republican governor \uc2 Brad Little\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Minority leader of the Idaho legislature. Democrat Ilana Rubel joins me now. Help us understand. Thank you for joining us. What is at stake with this bill?\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. ILANA RUBEL (D) IDAHO: It is really disturbing. Idaho has such an extreme abortion ban in effect act right now.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:00]\par \par \uc2 There is barely a life of the woman exception. It`s extremely difficult to even fall under any there`s no exceptions, actually. They`re treated as affirmative defenses. You`re treated as a doctor, treated as guilty unless proven innocent, and faces five years in prison for performing an abortion, even where a woman`s health is at stake in all of these circumstances.\par \par \uc2 And now we`ve got a real problem in that. One of our big hospitals just shut down all maternity services. We`ve lost a third of the state`s fetal maternal medicine specialists, and about half of our OB/GYNs are currently looking at either early retirement or leaving the state.\par \par \uc2 And rather than fix it in the face of this, they want to make it a felony to help minors leave the state for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So you said there`s virtually no exceptions. Help me understand the debate process. Like there are no provisions for, let`s say, minors who are victims of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 RUBEL: Well, currently it`s very difficult. And again, the way our law is crafted, there actually are no exceptions currently whatsoever. Everybody who performs an abortion is considered guilty until proven innocent. So it`s framed more of as an affirmative defense where you get charged with the crime, and then it`s on you to try to prove there was rape or incest or something or, you know, the woman was going to die.\par \par \uc2 And there has to be basically certainty of death under our law, you know, hemorrhaging, permanent infertility, paralysis, loss of body organs, none of that suffices. Even a 50-50 chance of death doesn`t suffice you. You have to basically show certainty of death. But, you know, those are pretty much the affirmative defenses that you`re currently permitted.\par \par \uc2 But this law actually also would have no exceptions to, again, it would be an affirmative defense. If you transport a minor out of state, you would be charged with a felony, and then you`d be presumed guilty until proven innocent. And there is no rape exception, no incest exception, no medical emergency exception.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s go back to the other thing you mentioned doctors retiring. The only hospital in rural Sandpoint, Idaho, recently said they`re done delivering babies, in part because of the political climate. Do your Republican colleagues in the House have any plans to address that? I`m pretty sure people in Iowa are going to keep getting pregnant.\par \par \uc2 RUBEL: It`s a huge problem, and I think they`re feeling some heat. Yes, they are feeling some heat because it has been all over our headlines out here that doctors are fleeing the state. They are unable to recruit doctors to come here. And again, a major hospital just shut down maternity services.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, their response is they just brought it through today is this purported fix of a bill that fixes just about nothing. It doesn`t provide health of the mother exception. It doesn`t expand on the life exception. The only thing it does is it flips the presumption of guilt. It presumes doctors, then innocent until proven guilty instead of proven -- presuming guilty instead of having to prove innocence.\par \par \uc2 But it still exposes them to five years of prison, permanent loss of their license, and doctors are saying, you know, we`re used to practicing under a threat of, you know, malpractice litigation and things like that, and civil exposure, but they are not used to practicing under a cloud of five years in prison for doing their job. It`s just what doctor would want to practice in a state like that when they could practice in a reasonable state that doesn`t expose them to extensive prison time.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Thank you for joining us. Very, very important story happening in Idaho right now. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 When we come back --\par \par \uc2 RUBEL: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: -- we are closing out on a very important story. 50 years since the Vietnam War. We honor those who serve tonight with an incredible story with an ending decades in the making when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:54]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, an important one, Vietnam War Veterans Day. Today marks 50 years since the last U. S. troops left South Vietnam. The war was costly, divisive, and one of America`s longest foreign conflicts. Over 58,000 Americans died, and many of the soldiers who returned were never the same. Our dear friend Harry Smith has the story of something one veteran lost on the battlefield and its long journey home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRY SMITH, NBC NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): There were few parades little fanfare just welcome home from family and friends for those who served in Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 JIM WEBB, FORMER U.S. SENATOR: I was a rifle platoon commander and then a company commander in the First Battalion, Fifth Marine Regiment in the Anwa Basin.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Last fall, former Senator Jim Webb led a group of Notre Dame students to that battlefield, that province where --\par \par \uc2 WEBB: More than 7,000 Marines had lost their lives. And they needed to understand how and what were trying to do.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: While there, a local man appeared with a dog tag. Professor Mike Desh bought it for $20. Name and rank, Larry Hughes. Corporal. Webb wondered.\par \par \uc2 WEBB: Whether were able to find him or his family so that we could pay our respects to them.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: They did.\par \par \uc2 CARL HUGHES, CORPORAL LARRY HUGHES SON: I was like, oh, this has got to be some kind of scam or something. I would say, it doesn`t sound real.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Carl Hughes was wary. His aunt Patricia broke down in tears.\par \par \uc2 PATRICIA HUGHES, CORPORAL LARRY HUGHES SISTER: I just couldn`t believe it. I said, are you serious?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Corporal Larry Hughes was Carl`s dad. Patricia Hughes, his sister.\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: It took me way back in time as a teenage girl, and my brother`s gone. We don`t know if he`s going to come home.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Corporal Hughes did come home to Northern Florida, but passed away a couple of years ago. Carl said his dad never spoke to him about Vietnam, yet --\par \par \uc2 C. HUGHES: I joined the Marines as well, just like him. Wanted to follow in his footsteps.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: As did Patricia`s son.\par \par \uc2 P. HUGHES: He served in Iraq. I was in with a Marine Corps parents because we said, we will never let our sons be treated like they treated the Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 [00:00:07]\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Patricia told us her brother would change out of his uniform when he came home to avoid the stares and hateful words of some civilians. Jim Webb had the dog tag framed along with Corporal Hughes military ribbons, and a ceremony was held to honor his service.\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: The recognition for the Vietnam vet, you couldn`t have asked it for anything any better.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Fulfilling the Marine motto, semper fidelis, semper phi. Always faithful. Harry Smith, NBC News, Landa Lakes, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: My dad, Frank Ruhle, who served in that era, called me this morning and asked me to acknowledge this day. I`m grateful that we are, because we are grateful today and every day for the service and sacrifice of our military and their families.\par \par \uc2 On that note, I wish you all a very good night from Washington, DC. And from all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC, thanks for staying up. I`ll see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020230330ej3t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for March 22, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Garrett Haake, Paul Butler, Brendan Buck, Robert  Pisani, Marissa Parra, Megan Fitzgerald, Josh Lederman, Susan Page,  Danielle Campoamor, Sam Brock \par \uc2 7,848 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 History on hold. Just in the last hour, we`ve learned the Manhattan DA has canceled today`s scheduled grand jury meeting with no word on why. Now, as the country waits for those jurors to decide Donald Trump`s fate, the former president is reportedly invigorated by the possibility of an indictment. Does he have reason to be or is it all just wishful thinking?\par \par \uc2 Plus, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  with a big decision to make and is expected to hike interest rates just an hour from now, but that may be the easiest part of the Fed Chairman`s Day. \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  trying to balance multiple challenges, from bank failures to stubborn inflation, and will face a grilling from reporters later this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The mother of Irvo Otieno praises a grand jury for indicting the people she calls monsters now charged with murdering her son. And her impassioned plea to do better in dealing with mental health issues, saying mental illness should not be a ticket to death.\par \par \uc2 But we begin today, the day we thought the grand jury would reconvene and possibly vote on what would be a first-in-history indictment against a former president. But that meeting has been pushed off until tomorrow at the earliest. Meanwhile, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , according to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , is preparing for a possible indictment by fixating on a possible perp walk, privately describing it as a fun experience and considering whether to smile for the cameras. But overshadowing those optics is a sobering reality that Trump`s fate along with that of the party that`s tied its own future to his are at stake.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Garrett Haake joining me from outside the DA`s office in Manhattan. Brendan Buck served as an aide to Republican House Speakers \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 . He is an MSNBC political analyst. Paul Butler is a former federal prosecutor and law professor at Georgetown. He`s also an MSNBC legal analyst. So, Garrett, what, if anything, do we know about this decision by the DA?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Chris, unfortunately, quite a little. The DA has told the grand jurors to stay home today, according to two sources familiar, and to be on standby for tomorrow. I should say at this point that grand jury proceedings are done in secret. The DA`s office doesn`t comment on them. The grand jurors don`t comment on them.\par \par \uc2 Even what we know about the schedule is what we`ve just been able to glean largely from movements back and forth. And so, you know, the relative schedule when things are canceled when events come and go can mean a lot or it can mean not that much. It could mean a witness wasn`t available today, or somebody has COVID. I mean, you could name the possibilities here.\par \par \uc2 But what this does, I think is obviously draw out the timeline, which as recently as late last week, we believe would be you know, that this kind of case could be drawn to a conclusion at some point this week. Obviously, Donald Trump`s post over the weekend elevated that possibility. And now as we all stand around and wait, I can tell you that Trump allies are pretty pleased with this result. They think it makes it look like the Manhattan DA doesn`t really have his act together, that perhaps this case isn`t as strong as the DA believes.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know any of that to be true. But this is how stories like this tend to work or in the absence of information, we`re left to wait and wait and wait until this grand jury reconvenes and makes their decision.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK, Paul, what`s your take on this delay? I mean, obviously, as we know, because Garrett just said it, this is all done in secret. There`s a number of possibilities. Is there in your mind a probability?\par \par \uc2 PAUL BUTLER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, there is. It`s true that grand juries are secret, but they follow standard procedures. These prosecutors have been signaling that they`re at the end of their investigation and that they expect the grand jury to return an indictment. They signaled that by inviting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to testify and also by presenting that rebuttal witness, that attorney Robert Costello, who basically appears to have testified on Trump`s behalf.\par \par \uc2 So, Chris, the next step is that prosecutors would deliver something like a closing statement to the grand jury, summarizing all of the witnesses that they`ve heard from, all of the documents that they`ve seen, and then the grand jury votes. The cliche is if you aim for the king, you best not miss. But the bottom line is this would be a risky prosecution for the Manhattan DA with several opportunities to miss, including by getting the case thrown out by a judge or going to trial and losing.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: And so, prosecutors want to make sure that they have as compelling a case as possible. And that`s probably what they`re working on right now. For them, it doesn`t make a big difference, whether the grand jury votes today, tomorrow, or next week.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Brendan, I -- we -- you know, we`ve been waiting for this and we`ve been talking about the importance of this. Is it hyperbole to say that the stakes are high? They`re high for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who`s running for president in 2024. They`re high for his party, as we mentioned, they`re the folks who have largely tied their fortunes to his and frankly, for history.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, it`s remarkable. On one hand, it feels like this historic event, obviously, something we have never seen. But it also in some ways feels a little familiar, right? I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , inviting chaos, having all of us think that maybe this is the thing that finally gets people to turn on him or move in a different direction. And it just doesn`t feel like that`s going to happen this time.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have polling out in recent days that show that he`s as strong as ever, or at least in the last -- his standing in the last couple of years is as strong as ever. So, it shows you that Republican voters like the show. They still like the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  show. They like the chaos that exists. And as long as Republican voters are there for it, you`re probably not going to see elected officials walk away from him anytime. So, it ultimately has the same feeling.\par \par \uc2 Now, I don`t know that an indictment is ultimately good for anybody. But it certainly does feel like it plays into what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  loves. He loves the attention. He loves being a victim. He loves controlling narratives. And I think that`s what he`s been doing for the last several days is trying to get out ahead of this, muddy the waters, put in context that helps him the most, and here we are, again, talking about him and his poll numbers are on the rise. It feels very, very familiar.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. So, in that context of the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  show, the reality show star, Brendan, when we hear that \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporting that he`s you know, talking about the perp walk, should he smile or not? We know that, at least according to their reporting, he thinks this could be a fun experience. He wants to show signals of strength not of shame. It all fits into what we know about the show part of him, the optics side of it. Because what I posited at the top is that there`s the legal side of this and there`s the optics side of this, Brendan.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes. And look, the legal side is serious and I`ll let Paul talk about how much trouble he is -- he`s actually in. But we know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t like to allow events to take over. He likes to control events. And that`s clearly what`s going on here.\par \par \uc2 Now, how much he actually thinks this is exciting? I don`t know how much that is spin or reality, and probably it`s a little bit of both. But what we know is that he`s not going to allow this to control his own narrative. He`s going to get out there. He`s going to fight. He`s going to rally people behind him.\par \par \uc2 We`ve already seen it. Whether it`s your average voter or the rest of the Republican establishment, all rushing to his defense, rehabbing him, and he is always in control. And that`s just the way he likes it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Paul, Trump is also described as "significantly disconnected from the legal trouble he`s in." Let me read to you part of the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporting. "He has been spotted zipping around his Palm Beach Resort in his golf cart and at one recent evening, acted as DJ at a party with his personally curated \uc2 Spotify\uc2  playlists, which often include music from the Rolling Stones to the Phantom of the Opera." So, if he`s disconnected from reality, Paul, give us a reality check. What really is he facing here potentially?\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: So, he`s facing being arrested by the -- a local law enforcement officials in New York. He`s facing being -- having his mugshot taken, being fingerprinted, and being read Miranda warnings. And, Chris, when that happens to you, your provato tends to go away really quickly. He`s facing misdemeanor charge or possibly a felony charge in New York.\par \par \uc2 And here`s the thing, the Manhattan prosecution would be the least of his worries. If he`s charged by DA Fanni Willis in Georgia, he`s looking at up to 20 years in prison. If she goes with the racketeering charge, he`s also got a serious federal investigation involving both Mar-a-Lago and whether he illegally hoarded documents there and obstructed justice and, of course, January 6. He`s in huge trouble. This is probably the worst trouble he`s ever been in in his life.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me get back to one of the things you just mentioned, Mar-a- Lago, because there`s new reporting on that from ABC news about the investigation into those classified documents. It says prosecutors in the Special Counsel`s Office have presented "compelling preliminary evidence that Trump knowingly and deliberately misled his own attorneys about classified materials after leaving office." And committed "criminal violations."\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s according to the judge who was overseeing the case. And NBC News has not confirmed that. But assuming it`s true, what does it tell us? What`s your reaction?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: It`s hard to imagine anything more incriminating than someone`s own lawyer implicating you in a crime. And a lawyer is only able to do that if the judge allows this crime-fraud exception. Lawyers are supposed to keep their communications with their clients confidential but not if the client tells you to do something illegal or asked you to help him do something illegal.\par \par \uc2 So, Chris, this will be impossible evidence for Jack Smith to ignore. Sometimes when we look at the information that`s come out about documents and how, as vice president, both Biden and Pence also kept documents that they shouldn`t have, that might have made the case against Trump seem weaker, but neither Biden nor Pence is accused of obstructing justice. And that`s just the kind of testimony that Evan Corcoran can now offer the grand jury.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Garrett Haake, thank you. Brendan and Paul, both of you are sticking around.\par \par \uc2 And we`re expecting a major decision from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  in the next hour as the central bank weighs whether to raise interest rates again and by how much. The Fed`s year-long war on inflation now complicated by new uncertainty in the banking system. Those problems are blamed in part on those high-interest rates. The markets are expecting a quarter-point bump, less aggressive than what we`ve seen over the past year, but that would still leave rates at a 16-year high with massive implications for your money.\par \par \uc2 CNBC`s senior markets correspondent Bob Pisani joins us now from the New York Stock Exchange. And let`s start there where you are, Bob. What do investors want from Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  and what are they likely to get?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PISANI, MSNBC SENIOR MARKETS CORRESPONDENT: Well, what they want is they want the Fed to hike 25 basis points -- a quarter point and then stop. Signal that they`re pausing. They want them to hike a little bit, that 25 basis points, because they want the Fed to imply they`re still concerned about inflation. But the pause is the key here. They want the Fed to acknowledge that this banking crisis might slow the economy down a little bit.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, that`s the Fed -- that`s what the Fed wants to do to fight inflation, slow the economy. But they also want the Fed to say, you know, we`ve hiked interest rates, you know, 4 percent -- 400 basis points more than that, in the last year or so. Let`s just stop and see what the effects are.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the problem. The problem will come if they don`t imply that they`re going to pause. If the Fed comes out and says, you know, we`re not really done fighting inflation, it hasn`t gone away, we`re still worried we can`t preclude the idea that maybe we`ll do additional rate hikes at the next meeting. If they don`t give a clear signal of a pause, I think the market is going to have some problems with that. Because that`s why we`ve been rallying in the last few days.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let`s show folks a graphic because I looked at and I had forgotten just how many three-quarters of a point rate hikes there have been. For the average American you know, I think a quarter of a point is just the latest in this long line, some might argue brutal series of increases. And I guess to your point, the question then becomes, at what point does the Fed say this isn`t working and so we`re going to stop or it has worked and we`re going to stop, or is stopping period, not really in the cards. A pause is the most likely scenario.\par \par \uc2 PISANI: Yes. I think the pause is the best that anybody can hope for. So, it`s -- there`s an argument for a pause. There -- many people have argued that this banking crisis is going to slow economic growth down. And that`s a -- that`s a good thing --that`s least what the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  wants. And that`s almost the effect of additional rate hikes. There`s an argument to be made about this.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the problem. The fed`s reputation for fighting inflation has been badly tarnished. They were very late hiking interest rates. They were behind the curve. There`s not a lot of plus for them to say, oh, OK, we`re going to go along with all the people who don`t want us to do anything because it`s good for the stock market. And we`ll keep mortgage rates lower, but it may not finish the inflation fight.\par \par \uc2 And I think they`re very worried about their reputation at this point. That`s why I think it`s a little problematic that they`re going to give some kind of all-clear signal, don`t worry, we`re done right now. We`re just going to sit around and watch what happens to the economy. I think that they`re going to say we want to be very vigilant about this. And it`s going to be difficult for the market to sit around and say, ah, they`ve clearly told us this is it. I think they`re not going to go that far.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: CNBC`s Bob Pisani, thank you. As we wait for that information to come out about 45 minutes from now. The new and gut-wrenching reaction of Irvo Otieno`s family, their first since the public saw a video that his mother says shows a homicide and his brother says, has left the family broken.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Emmanuel Macron`s first comments about his decision to raise the retirement age in France as Paris continues to burn. Is his defense helping him or hurting his case? And the tale from the high seas that even Herman Melville might not believe until you hear the firsthand story. That`s ahead. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It is raw, and at times heartbreaking. The family of Irvo Otieno opening up for the first time since the public release of a video that appears to show officers and medical staff piling on top of the 28-year-old for 11 minutes. Ten people have now been indicted by the Grand Jury. Prosecutors arguing that Otieno was smothered to death three weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE, OUKO, MOTHER OF IRVO OTIENO: And monsters. Those ten criminals. I was happy to hear that they were indicted.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 LEON OCHIENG, BROTHER OF IRVO OTIENO: For 11 minutes, focus on your breathing. Imagine what Irvo`s body was doing, how hard his heart was working. Don`t fail us this time. We don`t need another Irvo after this. Families deserve to wake up and love their loved ones even though they sometimes can`t love themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Marisa Parra is live on the ground in Virginia. I`m also joined by Paul Butler, MSNBC legal analyst, and former federal prosecutor. Marissa, what is next in this case against those seven sheriff`s deputies and three medical professionals?\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, we know that there will be a jury trial at some point. But between now and then, there are bond court hearings that are actually happening today. Several of them have happened this morning. We know that there`s at least one happening right now as we speak.\par \par \uc2 And we have learned at least some new details from the defense for several of those deputies as well as hospital workers, you see on your screen right there. Some of the questions that they have brought up, they had a question on the injection, there was an injection that was given to Otieno and so one of the questions that one of the defense attorneys had was, did that play a role in Otieno`s death? And so they`ve pointed to the autopsy results from the medical examiner`s office, which haven`t been released yet. We do know that that is expected to not come out for at least another 12 weeks, which isn`t entirely uncommon with a situation like this. But again, that`s a lot longer than people are hoping.\par \par \uc2 And then we have the question of the intake process. And that was something that`s been brought up over the last week but we`ve heard more details in court today. The defense alleging that it took 20 minutes to even bring Otieno inside that apparently there weren`t hospital staff to help them with that until much later. And then there was questions on why were deputies happened to be part of that process. Should that have been something that they had to be involved in? Those are questions that the agency that oversees psychiatric hospitals in the Commonwealth of Virginia are facing right now.\par \par \uc2 So, this morning, just a few hours ago, the Department of Behavioral Health and Developmental Services did come out with a statement not in response to those questions but they did say in part that the three hospital staff members who have been charged will be placed on admin leave pending the results of the legal proceedings. That`s, you know, an entirely routine thing. We know that the Henrico Sheriff`s Department deputies involved in this, also on administrative leave as part of this process here. And they said that they`re cooperating with Virginia State Police on this investigation.\par \par \uc2 And, Chris, another thing to point out here when we talk about the next steps when we talk about questions, that remain unanswered. And where the system failed? One of the things that defense attorneys were pointing to is that the Virginia State Police Report hasn`t been completed and won`t be completed for some time. So, a lot that still needs answering as we look forward and ahead towards that trial that at some point while I`ll be watching unfold. Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Paul, my NBC News colleague Catie Beck spoke directly to Otieno`s mother yesterday. And I want to play a little bit more of that conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: They saw the evidence. They took about three hours and they came back with 10 indictments. What does that say to you?\par \par \uc2 OUKO: It says to me that these nine men and one woman murdered my son. Those animals that treated him so wrong and squeezed the life out of him, I want them brought to justice. And with every breath that I`ve got, as long as I have life in me, I will work towards that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Paul, that emotion from his mother -- the outrage, how much of this case going forward, we`ll focus on what she saw and what presumably the jurors will be able to see on that video for themselves.\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: The video is going to be key. According to what the video reportedly shows, Chris, this might be worse than George Floyd. Seven police officers, three hospital workers hands on Mr. Otieno`s mouth, his head, on his braids all the way down to his toes, they`re using the restraint procedure of putting pressure on someone lying in a prone position, handcuffed, leg cuffed the whole world learned during the Floyd trial, how life-threatening that is.\par \par \uc2 And prosecutors also suggested a cover-up. Medical workers gave Mr. Otieno a sedative but the prosecutor says this was after he was already dead. The State Police are supposed to be called whenever there`s a death under unusual circumstances at this health facility. But according to the people who`ve seen the video, Mr. Otieno`s body appears to go (INAUDIBLE) around 4:40 p.m. The state police weren`t called until 7:30. Almost three hours later.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, Paul, after all the outrage over videos that show us the use of force, including as you mentioned, George Floyd`s death in 2020, do you have any answer for why it doesn`t seem to be a deterrent? Surely, the folks knew that they were on camera.\par \par \uc2 BUTLER: It`s such a great question and a tragic question that how could a human being let another human being do this? Asphyxiation was identified as the cause of death by the medical examiner but as the prosecutor has said, they smothered him to death. That takes a long time, Chris, 11 or 12 minutes in this case.\par \par \uc2 And if there`s -- you can`t talk about encouraging or good news in this case, but the fact that ten people have been charged with second-degree murder should certainly serve as a deterrent to others. Sometimes in group situations, there`s a sense that we`ll have everybody gets down and dirty nobody`s going to get in trouble. That`s not true here. It`s not true in Memphis, Tennessee, where we have five officers charged with murder. And so, the criminal legal system seems to be catching up and finally holding officers accountable.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Marissa Parra and Paul Butler, thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 In the fight of his political life, what French President Macron had to say about that controversial pension bill as nationwide protests continue in France? We`ll head to Paris for the latest.\par \par \uc2 And joint war games, the U.S. and South Korea launched their largest military exercises in five years. NBC News has been on the ground in the middle of the action. We`ve got a live report from Seoul, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:54]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We have breaking news out of Colorado. You`re looking at the live pictures after a shooting at a school and a manhunt is underway for the gunman.\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you what we know from a press conference that was just held. Two staff members were hurt in that shooting at Denver`s East High School. One is said to be in serious condition. One in critical condition, one other student suffered an allergic reaction and was also taken to the hospital. Police say the suspect is a student and what happened was he agreed to be patted down and then opened fire on the two male staffers.\par \par \uc2 That school is in lockdown. You say the number of people who are now outside. The local paper "The Denver Post" is reporting a large police presence in the area east of downtown. The ATF is already on scene and the mayor has come to the school, according to "The Post," saying, "We know who he is," regarding the suspect. "We will find him and we will hold him accountable,"\par \par \uc2 But again that is an active manhunt in the east side of Denver, Colorado.\par \par \uc2 Meantime abroad, President Macron for the first time today defending the decision that has ignited a massive nationwide protest. Macron sat down with two top French journalists after his government survived two no confidence votes over the pension reform bill that would effectively raise the retirement age for most folks from 62 to 64.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in NBC`s Megan Fitzgerald who is on the ground in Paris where she has been talking directly with protesters.\par \par \uc2 What exactly did President Macron have to say today, Megan, and what reaction are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 MEGAN FITZGERALD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, what we saw today was a defiant \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  who really stood his ground, doubling down on his decision to push through that legislation, bypassing a vote in parliament, to increase the retirement age, as you said, from 62 to 64. He said he didn`t want to do it but that he had to do it because the current pension system is in jeopardy of running a deficit, in jeopardy of going bankrupt.\par \par \uc2 He also said that he hopes in future that the French people will see that he`s right and that his only regret is that he didn`t convince the people. Now look, he`s got a lot of opposition here. A lot of members of parliament that are angry at him. They say that he didn`t have to do it this way. He could have found the money elsewhere. You know, you`ve got a country that has erupted in anger. Some 70 percent of French people are opposed to what he did and the way in which he did it.\par \par \uc2 He`s at a 28 percent favorability rating right now. There is speculation on how effective he will be moving forward as he goes forward for four more years in his presidency. The people are upset. They have been protesting for the last six days since he pushed through that legislation on Thursday. People angry because they feel as though he`s not listening to them and that he didn`t go about this in a democratic.\par \par \uc2 Where we`re standing right now we`re anticipating another protest. Protesters tell us they`re not going to stop. This is just the start of many more demonstrations to come and then of course tomorrow, Chris, we`re looking at a nationwide strike. That certainly has the potential to bring France to a standstill.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Megan Fitzgerald, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Also today the U.S. and South Korea wrapping up their largest military exercises in six years and we`ve got the video of these live fire exercises which involved everything from artillery to \uc2 Blackhawks\uc2 , to offensive operation drills. They come as North Korea is ramping up missile tests, firing multiple cruise missiles off its east coast today. That is according to South Korea`s military.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Josh Lederman following it all from Seoul, South Korea.\par \par \uc2 Tell us a little more, Josh, about these exercises and why they`re so important.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, it`s in part because the U.S. has really gotten out of the business of holding these massive joint exercises in South Korea ever since former President Trump scaled them back when he was pursuing diplomacy with Kim Jong-un. And there is growing concern and skepticism here in South Korea about the U.S. really stepping up to deter a North Korean nuclear attack on South Korea. And in fact there is growing support here for South Korea to obtain its own nuclear weapons as a deterrent, something the U.S. clearly does not want to see happening.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:08]\par \par \uc2 I spoke today in the middle of these war games to Colonel Brandon Anderson from the 2nd Infantry Division about what it is like to be training literally in the shadows of North Korea. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COL. BRANDON ANDERSON, 2ND INFANTRY DIVISION DEPUTY COMMANDING OFFICER: Nothing provides more motivation to be all you can be for the U.S. service member than to sweep 10 miles from the adversary. And when we see these provocations we know that we need to have a focused training on it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: And it`s not just provocations, Chris. In the midst of all of these launches, the North is advancing its weapons system, testing out its new Hwasong-17 intercontinental ballistic missile and also, according to photographs, developing underground silos that they can use to launch ICBMs with very little notice, that are very difficult for the U.S. and South Korea to detect.\par \par \uc2 And so those are causes for increasing concern for the U.S. Military that is really driving the sense of urgency behind these war games and behind U.S. effort to show that it is going to stand up to this aggression and to make sure that South Korea is protected against any potential threat from North Korea.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Josh Lederman, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 A new normal. How the Trump team is reportedly preparing for a possible indictment heading into 2024 as Governor DeSantis gives us a preview of candidate DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 That`s all next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:37]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A new interview and new insights, the clearest maybe yet into \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` opening argument if he runs for president. In that interview with Piers Morgan, the Florida governor slammed Trump`s chaotic leadership style. He went there on the character issue and did a test drive responding to the use of derogatory nicknames.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, HOST, "PIERS MORGAN UNCENSORED": What`s your favorite nickname that Trump has given you so far? Is it Ron DeSanctimonious or Meatball Ron?\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: Well, I can`t --\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Even he went off Meatball Ron.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I can`t -- I don`t know how to spell DeSanctimonious. I don`t even know what it means but, you know, I kind of like it. It`s long, it`s got a lot of vowels. So we`ll with that. That`s fine. You know, you can call me whatever you want. I mean, just as long as you, you know, also call me a winner.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Meantime for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  whose combative campaign style has been, well, consistent, there`s the potential of a new normal if he ends up running while under indictment.\par \par \uc2 Let`s discuss this with Susan Page, "USA Today`s" Washington bureau chief and Brendan Buck is back with us.\par \par \uc2 So, Susan, I`m curious what your take is on this interview. We`ve only heard a bit of it, you know, we`ve read more about it. But obviously with a friendly Piers Morgan, do you see it as maybe a preview of a 2024 opening argument?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2  WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF: Yes. I think so. I think you see Governor DeSantis attacking former President Trump on competence, on character, not on policy. So what he`s really offering voters is if you like Donald Trump`s policies, if you like what he did in office, I can offer you that with a little less drama. And you know there are some signs we`ve seen in polling that while Donald Trump`s core support remains pretty strong, there are supporters who are a little tired of the chaos it generally surrounds them.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, you know, Brendan, we`ve talked a lot about the chaos, the exhaustion from the chaos that surrounds \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But character is a different issue and I wonder if you think, well, what you think of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  bringing it up, is it already baked in?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER AIDE TO SPEAKERS RYAN AND BOEHNER: It might be, but I guess I would argue that no one has really pressed him on it in a Republican primary sense. Republicans, as you know, has sort of given him a pass on a lot of these questions. Obviously the question fundamental to his indictment. Allegedly cheating on his wife and making payments to keep her quiet.\par \par \uc2 Look, you don`t beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by just presenting yourself as a sunny alternative. I think we know enough about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that he`s going to come swinging at you. And that`s what he`s been doing to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  for a very long time, and it is clearly having an effect. \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  had a poll out this week that has \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  up 14 points on \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Three months ago \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was up 13 points.\par \par \uc2 That`s how quickly things can change. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a master at driving up people`s negatives. That`s what he`s doing here. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is going to have to find a way to attack \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he wants to have a chance to do so when it doesn`t alienate people. And that`s a really tough road to hoe, but, you know, you`ve got to go after him at some point, and clearly this is the start of that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Those are the things that known, right, Susan? The unknown is whether he`s going to have to run, as the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " put it, under a new normal, running under, right, a possible indictment. A campaign spokesman said that the former president has been battle-tested. This operation has been fine-tuned since 2016, dealing with these types of new cycles. You learn to get good at it. But I don`t know, is this one of these types of news cycles or is this something different and how much of that is just positive spin?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You know, I do think this is different from the attacks that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has faced and survived in the past. We have never in our history had a situation where a president or former president has been indicted on criminal charges. We`ve never had a situation where we`ve been debating or discussing whether he`ll have to wear a handcuff and be fingerprinted if he is indicted by this grand jury in New York.\par \par \uc2 This is new territory. I know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters have stuck with him through a lot but this is more -- this is something more additional historic than we`ve ever seen before, and I`m not sure we -- I think we should be modest about projecting or predicting what exactly even his own voters are going to think about this.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. I don`t know that we know.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:00]\par \par \uc2 We do now there`s a core, how big that core will be, right, Brendan, has yet to be seen, and we don`t even know if an indictment is going to happen. So let`s be clear about that. But I wonder what you think the conversations are like in the DeSantis campaign. Is it about just continuing to do what he`s previewed which is I`m going to offer you an alternative to this, and simply present that as part of the, quote-unquote, "chaos"?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Well, I think the \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  camp is realizing that what was working for them for a very long time may not be working anything. DeSantis has indicated that he`s not actually going to jump into the race until this summer, that he`s going to do somewhat of a Rose Garden strategy where he`s just going to run the state of Florida, he`s going to pass a bunch of bills in legislature, and kind of show you what a positive alternative would look like. He said there he wants you to call him a winner because he`s had such success.\par \par \uc2 But obviously things are starting to look a little different for him. And, you know, I don`t want to put too much weight on one poll. But I think he is finding out what so many people who have challenged \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  before have found out that he`s going to pull you in the mud, and there`s really no other way about that. You have to figure out how to play his game because he blocks up the sun. He controls the narrative.\par \par \uc2 There`s no other way about that, and I don`t think that you can dance around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I just haven`t tried a lot to dance around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I know that he can`t do it very well. So he`s going to have to take him on at some point. Now they may be patient, they may want to wait until June to start doing that, but this shows me that I think they realized they need to get in now before he does any more damage to him.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Brendan Buck, Susan Page, thank you both very much.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has been making phone calls to fellow GOP senators as he continues to recover from a concussion and broken rib, telling him he`s eager to return to the Capitol. McConnell is still being treated at an inpatient rehabilitation facility after suffering a fall earlier this month. But several Senate colleagues who spoke with him by phone say he sounds good and is anxious to get back. Still no word on exactly when McConnell will return but the Senate is about to begin a two-week recess.\par \par \uc2 An Idaho maternity ward shutting its doors. The big reason why, and what it could mean for pregnant women living close by. And a major interest rate decision to be released just minutes from now. What it could mean for your bottom line. All of that ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:14]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Imagine this. You`re nine months pregnant, about to deliver, but the nearest hospital is now an hour way. That scenario could soon be reality in northern Idaho. Banner General Health in Sandpoint Idaho, has announced it`s shattering its labor and delivery unit citing staffing issues and the state`s, quote, "legal and political climate."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is reporter for "Today Parents," Danielle Campoamor.\par \par \uc2 Danielle, explain this. I know there are multiple reasons but part of it is concerned about new abortion laws, right?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE CAMPOAMOR, NBC NEWS TODAY CORRESPONDENT: Absolutely. As you said they cited staffing issues, the decline in the number of babies being born in that hospital but really went into the anti-abortion laws in the state saying that OB-GYNs are leaving the state. They can`t have them come to a state where they could potentially be criminalized, could potentially be sued if they`re giving care that could qualify as an abortion under the anti-abortion laws in the state. And so they`re saying that this is just not a safe environment for OB-GYNs anymore.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You talk to a lot of women, a lot of moms. What did they tell you?\par \par \uc2 CAMPOAMOR: And so I talked to one mom in particular who eight years ago she`s from Sandpoint, she wanted to have a homebirth. Something went wrong. She experienced complications. She was rushed to that hospital and was able to have an emergency C-section. She says now considering what her community is going to have to go through she shudders to think of what would have happened to her under the circumstance.\par \par \uc2 And that will be a reality for some women there. They will need to travel over an hour to get the care they need, and in emergency situations who knows what that could mean, and certainly potentially horrible health outcomes for women and their children.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That really is the key question. What could it mean? Could the baby survive? Could the mom survive? Are hospital officials offering any advice or what -- I mean you never know, some people obviously have a high risk pregnancy, but you never know if you`re going to be in the situation that mother was in, which suddenly she needs a C-section. What do they do?\par \par \uc2 CAMPOAMOR: They have to travel further. An emergency room is not equipped to deal with what an OB-GYN have to deal with in that kind of situation. That`s really what it comes down to is travel further. And that`s what`s going to plague this community and it`s plaguing OB-GYNs both in the state who feel like they have to prioritize their families over the duty that they have as doctors, but even future OB-GYNs.\par \par \uc2 I`ve talked to one future OB-GYN who wanted to go back after her four-year residency and is now saying I have to choose between, is this a community that I could give birth and safely, or am I going to put myself in a position where if I do have a family, I could be imprisoned or fined or sued because I was actually just doing my job.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, there is that thought. I mean, here`s the only OB-GYN and if you get pregnant where do you go? What do you do?\par \par \uc2 Danielle, thank you so much for this reporting. We appreciate you coming on the program.\par \par \uc2 It`s a story that could be straight out of an epic novel or a movie. The stunning rescue of a sailboat crew left stranded in the Pacific after a whale -- you heard me, a whale sunk their boat. You got to see this story next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:57:54]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And now to a whale of a tale. A sailboat crew very lucky to be alive today after abandoning their sunken ship in the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Sam Brock has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): From aboard the boat that came to his rescue.\par \par \uc2 RICK RODRIGUEZ, SAILOR: My initial thought was (EXPLETIVE DELETED) that.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Rick Rodriguez describes the sensation that was unlike anything he`d ever experienced as a sailor.\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: Sounded like something broke and we immediately look to the side and saw a really big whale bleeding.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Four friends were sharing a pizza about a week ago on their long- awaited sailing excursion to Polynesia on board the Rain Dancer when that initial jolt quickly forced them to snap into survival mode.\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: There was just an incredible amount of water coming in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I felt like it was just a scene out of a movie, like everything was floating.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: At once Rick sent out a mayday call to authorities and sent text messages to his brother Roger in Miami and friend Tommy Joyce who was sailing a buddy boat in the area, specifically there for safety.\par \par \uc2 Tommy, this is no joke, we hit a whale and the ship went down. We`re in the life raft. We need help ASAP.\par \par \uc2 What followed was a digital version of telephone involving text, WhatsApp messages, the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  and the Peruvian government. But as the friends anxiously waited on an inflatable life raft and dingy, a sign of hope.\par \par \uc2 TOMMY JOYCE, SAILOR: And then I posted on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 `s boat watch and it was the boat watch group that ended up having somebody on there that knew a sailing vessel Rolling Stone.\par \par \uc2 RODRIGUEZ: Think about 60 miles, 65 miles away when we realized that we`re close (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Nine hours later that boat named Rolling Stones pinpointed their exact location and scooped up the four sailors.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE: We saw them so far out and then we realized that we`re still like an hour and a half away from them. But an hour and a half we were really, really excited.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: For this confident group, there was never any doubt they`d survive even in rough waters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This experience made me realize how, you know, how capable we are and how scared we are to manage in focused situations like this.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Sam Brock, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230323ej3m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for March 19, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Jessica Layton \par \uc2 6,555 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:21]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Uncharted Territory.\par \par \uc2 Right now, America is bracing for what could be a historically unprecedented event: The possible criminal indictment of a former President.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  put out a series of posts on his Truth Social media platform, referencing reports he could soon face criminal indictment in New York as early as next week. Trump claims that leaks indicate he will be arrested on Tuesday, but he did not say whether he has actually been informed of any charges or what those charges would be. A spokesman for the Manhattan DA`s Office has declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Trump also called for protest. NBC News reported, Friday, law enforcement agencies including the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 , \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and the Secret Service, are already discussing security plans should charges come down.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to be talking about all of it ahead with our guests including former Trump Attorney \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , who testified before the grand jury this very week.\par \par \uc2 And later, the Attorney General of Black America joins us with an update on the case of Tyre Nichols, who died at the hands of police in Memphis, Tennessee, earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 But first, we`re joined by Democratic Senator Richard Blumenthal of Connecticut. Senator, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICHARD BLUMENTHAL (D-CT): Thank you for having me, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I want to get your take on the possible charges against Trump. But first, I must ask you to address his calls "to protest and take your country back." Now, the former President didn`t bother to specify whether the protests should be violent or nonviolent, which seems stunningly irresponsible after being impeached just two years ago in part for riling up the crowd ahead of the January 6th insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts, Senator?\par \par \uc2 BLUMENTHAL: Your point is exactly right, Reverend Al. It is very reminiscent of his call on people to come to Washington, be there, will be wild. Now, he is saying protest, take our nation back. It is apparently an excitement of his base, but also an incitement of violence potentially.\par \par \uc2 And I call on my Republican colleagues to condemn it. So far, they have in fact done the opposite. They`ve condoned and even encouraged him in this apparent incitement of violence.\par \par \uc2 All of us should be saying, resoundingly, loudly, swiftly, violence has no place, incitement of violence should be condemned. And unfortunately, that is not what we`re seeing from most Republicans right now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And to tell them to take America back, I mean, isn`t America supposed to be, if you break the law, you are held accountable? I mean, what America is he talking about?\par \par \uc2 But let`s get to the possible charges against the former President. We don`t know for sure what they will be or even if they will be filed. What do you know? What are you doing -- let me put it this way, what do you do and what do -- is Trump being investigated? He is being investigated for hush money payments he made to Stormy Daniels. Trump has called the probe politically motivated, something Manhattan DA \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  denied on this very program last Saturday night, one week ago.\par \par \uc2 For Americans who aren`t following every detail, can you explain why the alleged offenses here are worthy of a criminal investigation?\par \par \uc2 BLUMENTHAL: What he is alleged to have done is not only paid this hush money to Stormy Daniels after an affair with her, channeling it through Cohen, his then lawyer and disguising it as legal fees and then misrepresenting it on the books of his company. That is formally the kind of charge that could be brought.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:00]\par \par \uc2 BLUMENTHAL: Of course, Reverend Al, as you pointed out in the opening there are many questions, if not more as there are answers. We don`t know what the charges or the timing will be, and the question should be asked, why is Trump now predicting it will be on Tuesday and there is no factual basis for it? Well, I think he is just trying to excite his base, and potentially incite violence, which will distract from the charges and undermine the credibility of the criminal justice system.\par \par \uc2 I don`t believe that \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  is a politically motivated prosecutor, but that is what Kevin McCarthy is saying. And he is, in effect, aiding and abetting the Trump incitement, if that proves to be what it is, by these kinds of comments, as are some of his Republican colleagues.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I want to shift to the economy, but I want to be sure that I`m clear.\par \par \uc2 You`re saying that if this proves not to be the case, on Tuesday, that you feel that the President may -- the former President may be just inciting people and possibly violence in an irresponsible way to say something that has no factual basis.\par \par \uc2 BLUMENTHAL: Only Trump knows for sure, and perhaps he doesn`t know for sure himself given the unpredictability of his mind. But the important point is, no one is above the law and the facts and the evidence are the basis for any criminal charges, just as they would be in any other criminal case.\par \par \uc2 I am a former United States Attorney in Connecticut, as well as the State Attorney General for 20 years. The way the criminal justice process works is follow the facts and the law. And unfortunately, a lot of Republicans are really trying to undermine the people who enforce our laws, whether it`s the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or the DEA, they seem more interested in undermining the credibility of the criminal justice system and they are playing into Trump`s hands predicting without any factual basis, that the charges are going to be on Tuesday. And yes, you have it exactly right. I think the purpose can only be to try to incite violence at this point.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator, I want to shift focus to some turmoil we are seeing in the economy. After the collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  earlier this month, the Biden administration has been working with some of Wall Street`s larger banks to potentially prop up other troubled institutions.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Republicans have gone from claiming the crisis was caused by bankers distracted by diversity to claiming \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  was "too woke to fail." Is this kind of rhetoric making a difficult situation even harder to manage?\par \par \uc2 BLUMENTHAL: This kind of rhetoric about too woke to fail, or whatever the exact terminology is absolutely absurd. No serious economist, no serious expert on the financial system would take it seriously, but we do have a serious situation with our economy and I proposed legislation that in effect would return to taxpayers the bonuses and the other proceeds of any kind of top executive as happened with \uc2 SVB\uc2  bank and the Signature Bank, where in effect, they rewarded themselves for mismanagement.\par \par \uc2 We need to restore trust in the system, but also more broadly, strengthen our regional and community banks because of the flight of capital from them to the bigger banks, the consolidation and mergers that have taken place have really concentrated power in fewer hands in the banking system.\par \par \uc2 There are serious issues here that need to be confronted, and I believe our economy is basically strong. I think the administration was right to step in forcefully and swiftly as it did, but issues remain and we need to continue to address them.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Before I let you go, Senator, the \uc2 International Criminal Court\uc2  yesterday issued an arrest warrant for Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  days before the Russian leader is due to host Chinese President Xi in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 The ICC is accusing Putin of committing war crimes during his invasion of Ukraine. What are your thoughts on the charges and on the upcoming meeting between the Russians and Chinese? Briefly, please.\par \par \uc2 BLUMENTHAL: Reverend Al, I visited Ukraine three times over the past year. I`ve been to the mass graves in Bucha where women and children had their hands tied behind their back, hundreds of them.\par \par \uc2 The moral conscience of the world should be aroused most especially by these charges, which are the abduction and kidnapping of children -- Ukrainian children -- some 16,000 of them. It makes \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  exactly what he is in the world`s eyes, a pariah.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:04]\par \par \uc2 BLUMENTHAL: And let me say this very emphatically, the administration now has an obligation to designate Russia a state sponsor of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 I introduced a resolution. It is bipartisan with my colleague, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , passed unanimously in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  unanimously calling on the administration to designate Russia, a state sponsor of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 If he has committed crimes against humanity, as this indictment or arrest warrant clearly indicate, we should designate Russia a state sponsor of terror and the Wagner Group should be designated a foreign terrorist organization.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator Richard Blumenthal, thank you for joining me.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Donald Trump`s former personal attorney, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 . He is the author of "Revenge: How \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Weaponized the US Department of Justice Against His Critics."\par \par \uc2 Michael, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER DONALD TRUMP ATTORNEY: Always, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I want to start with the former President`s post this morning. He has claimed that he will be arrested on Tuesday and has called for protest.\par \par \uc2 You worked closely with Trump for many years as close as anyone and I knew you then and I know how close you were. What do you think is going on with him right now?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: So I think Senator Blumenthal was exactly correct, only \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knows, but the problem is that even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t know.\par \par \uc2 You know, around the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , there was this statement that we used to all say that the last person in Donald`s ear controls his brain. So chances are that one of the wackos that are around him in -- you know, the -- who call him the Clown Prince of Gotham, right? They`re sitting there, and they`re telling him, Donald, you`re going to go in on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Now, it doesn`t make any sense because this grand jury, I believe, is Monday, Wednesday and Thursday. But putting all that aside, unless, and I really truly thought that Donald knew the answer to this that the obviously spoke because Donald is not proactive, he is a reactionary individual.\par \par \uc2 So who knows?\par \par \uc2 The right thing to do is to leave it for \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 . When the indictment comes down, we will all know. Look at the chaos that one tweet from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has caused?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: He likes chaos, if nothing else.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Captain Chaos.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But now, you say that the grand jury meets you saying Monday, Wednesday, and Thursday, which means that he was going to be arrested next Tuesday, they would have had the vote in indictment Thursday, because you were there testifying Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Correct.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, you testified before the grand jury in this case this week and you spent over a year in prison and another year-and-a-half at home confinement for your role in the hush money payment to Stormy Daniels, that is at the center of the probe.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know you can`t talk about your testimony or speculate on the charges, but can you share your impressions of the seriousness of the investigation?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Well, it was serious enough to send me away. There is only one set of laws in this country, what affects Rev, you and I needs to affect somebody like Donald as well. No one is above the law.\par \par \uc2 So if it was good enough for me to go, it should be good enough for him. And a lot of people get upset -- I was with a friend of mine for lunch. Super, super bright guy, well-educated and his comment to me was very similar to what the question that you`re asking, how is it that this Tiki top type of a case is going to be the first impression that we have as Americans for a President to be indicted, first time in US history?\par \par \uc2 Again, the answer is, the law is the law. The same way under the Capone -- the Al Capone theory. They couldn`t get him for murder, racketeering, extortion. They couldn`t get him for bootlegging or any of the other crimes. They got him for this, because rest assured \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  is not making a mistake.\par \par \uc2 And the team that is behind him, the team that is putting this case together, they`re very competent, they know all the facts.\par \par \uc2 Now they could attack me and talk about credibility, which is something you seeing on a lot of the other stations. That`s okay. Very much like when I went and I testified before the House Oversight Committee, I come with the facts. I come with the documentary evidence. It`s irrefutable.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But what confuses me is you went to jail for what you`re saying \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did. You did a year in jail, the year-and-a-half home confinement. I know because I know you. You called me to have prayer with you before you went to jail. I talked to you also while you were in jail as a matter of fact.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: I know.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: A lot of anguish. So if it didn`t happen, then why did you plead and go to jail?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Right. Well, first of all, my case is also somewhat different in the fact that the Southern District of New York on a Friday at 5:30 PM basically said to me -- not basically -- they said to me empathically, you have until Monday, 48 hours over a weekend when you can`t accomplish anything, either you plead guilty to whatever we tell you to plead guilty to, or we are filing an 80-page indictment that would include my wife.\par \par \uc2 And so the strong arm of the law, but truth be told, that is the one case that I take responsibility for is the Stormy Daniels` payment, as well as the 1001 violation, which was lying to Congress, despite the fact nobody ever finishes that line and says that I also did at the direction of -- in connection with and for the benefit of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So he is connected all you`ve pled to.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: It is connected to everything.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, President Trump`s attorney, Joe Tacopina came on this network earlier this week, and had this to say about this investigation and you. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE TACOPINA, \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  ATTORNEY: It`s funny that we have now reduced ourselves to the point of relying solely on \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , a guy who is a convicted liar, to bring a case against President Trump when they have nothing else. They scoured for three years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: What`s your reaction?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: First of all, he doesn`t know the case. It may be any lawyer that`s out there. He happens to be particularly bad, right? I mean, I guarantee like the \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , what was it, the Four Seasons landscaping debacle with the shoe polish down his face. He got sidelined.\par \par \uc2 And I`m sure Joe Tacopina is going to be sidelined as well, because he went out there and he made a fool of himself, and worse than that, he actually implicated his own client and said, yes, it`s okay. Yes, so he lied. It doesn`t make a difference. No, no, it actually does make a difference.\par \par \uc2 He doesn`t know the facts, and the fact that he continues to promote the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  line, which is a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  moniker that he gave to me "convicted liar." He still doesn`t even know.\par \par \uc2 Tacopina is just dumb. He doesn`t know what the lie was. You and I have talked about it over breakfast when I asked you to pray, right, for me. The number of times that I spoke to Donald about the failed real estate project in Moscow, the Trump Tower Moscow project, at Donald`s direction in connection with him, Ivanka, Jared, Abbe Lowell, \uc2 Ty Cobb\uc2 , and a whole slew of other people. I was told to say three. But the real truth is that the answer was 10.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to Donald about it 10 times. That`s the big lie.\par \par \uc2 And if Joe Tacopina, the genius that he is thinks that is going to keep me from being the witness that \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  believes me to be with all the documentary evidence, he needs a refresher course in law.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Finally, Michael, you worked with President Trump for many years. You know him personally, and you`ve seen him with -- you`ve seen him when he feels pressured or threatened.\par \par \uc2 How do you expect him to respond if indeed charges are filed whenever? And if an arrest is made and a criminal proceeding take place. How do you think he`ll respond?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Obviously not well. That is why he`s calling for protest. What he`s doing is he`s trying to threaten \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 . He is trying to threaten the American judicial system that not just \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , but \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  in Georgia, Washington, any Court that thinks that they`re going to bring \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in, I`m going to call for protest.\par \par \uc2 This is who he is. This is an autocratic wannabe. This is a guy who doesn`t want to be President of the United States. He wants to be our monarch, our ruler, the Fuhrer. That`s who he is.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So you think whether or not there`s really going to be an arrest Tuesday or not, that his idea is to, in some way, try to intimidate the prosecutors because he is not even saying many of us have protests, I do. But we are careful to say non-violent protests.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: He has left that wide open, and in light of January 6, you would think he would be more careful.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: But he is not careful, and not only does he want there to be another violent protest, he is also looking to do the great grift, continue the great grift and obtain campaign contributions off of this. Oh, they`re attacking your favorite President. Send me $5.00. Send me $10.00. It is all part of the great grift.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 While Trump sits in his glass house preparing for a possible arrest, his allies are throwing stones at a growing political rival.\par \par \uc2 I`ll explain in this week`s Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 And later, the mother of Tyre Nicole joins POLITICSNATION along with civil rights attorney, \uc2 Benjamin Crump\uc2 . We will talk about what`s next in their quest for justice.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague Jessica Layton, with today`s top news stories -- Jessica.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LAYTON, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Reverend Al. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stories, we are watching it this hour: Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is facing a warrant for arrest for alleged war crimes. He traveled to Crimea today on the ninth anniversary of its annexation by Russia.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:06]\par \par \uc2 LAYTON: Putin will be meeting with Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  in Moscow next week.\par \par \uc2 In France, thousands of protesters continue gathering in Paris to speak out against the President`s plan to raise the French retirement age from 62 to 64. Riot police clashed with protesters on Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, garbage is still piling up in the streets of Paris as sanitation workers are among those striking against the plan.\par \par \uc2 And the remains of a South Carolina teen with connections to the Alex Murdaugh case will be exhumed for an independent autopsy. The mother of 19- year-old Stephen Smith made that announcement after interest in her son`s mysterious deaths spiked in the wake of Murdaugh`s recent double murder conviction.\par \par \uc2 Smith died of an apparent hit and run on a railroad back in 2015.\par \par \uc2 I`m Jessica Layton. More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:03]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we all await a possible historic criminal indictment of former President Trump, I wanted to mention another story out of Trump world this week.\par \par \uc2 A super PAC supporting the former President has filed an ethics complaint against Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Normally, when you hear Trump and ethics in the same sentence, it is the former President who has been accused of a violation.\par \par \uc2 But in a 15-page letter to FTC, Trump`s allies claimed DeSantis was running a shadow campaign to build his national profile and enrich himself.\par \par \uc2 Of course, this is exactly what many of us thought Trump was doing in 2016 and 2011, as well, when he hinted in 2011 that he may run against President Obama before returning to his cushy gig as a reality show host.\par \par \uc2 Even in 2016, numerous insider accounts say Trump did not believe he would win, but saw the campaign as an opportunity to build his brand.\par \par \uc2 When Trump did end up in the White House, his administration demonstrated an unprecedented contempt for the guidelines honored by past Presidents of both parties.\par \par \uc2 Trump clashed with his own ethics watchdog repeatedly on issues of financial disclosures, record keeping and contact with foreign lobbyists, until eventually the watchdog resigned, and his office was rendered irrelevant.\par \par \uc2 With all that history, it seems pretty clear Trump and his supporters having suddenly developed a passion for clean politics. More likely, they are becoming increasingly desperate in their efforts to stop a new rival.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, Trump has struggled to find a consistent line of attack against DeSantis, or even a good nickname. In public and private, Trump has tested out a handful of labels like Ron DeSanctimonious and 