t not understanding because he was actually one of the gentlemen that came to my home that informed me that my son was arrested. So, for him to be able to retire before all of this, that doesn`t make sense to me.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And for him to be there without a body camera, left before the ambulance. I mean, a series of things that was part of what happened to your son. I mean, has to affect you and Rodney personally. I know from talking to you all since the funeral, how you are still in a lot of anguish, as anyone would understand you to be.\par \par \uc2 WELLS: Yes, at first I was -- it is getting to the point now where I`m starting to get angry because a lot of new developments are happening, and things are not getting done in a timely manner that I feel that it should be done.\par \par \uc2 Just say for instance, that Hemphill fellow, they know he was involved. They know that he tased my son, but yet and still, he hasn`t been arrested for anything. No charges have been filed against him.\par \par \uc2 They charged the five officers, but what about the rest of the people? What are they doing with those people? They are allowing them to retire. This the second person that they`ve allowed to retire if I`m not mistaken.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:02]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Attorney Crump, last week officials announced, the City of Memphis completed its investigation. What`s next as you hear the mother talk about others that should be prosecuted. What is next in the quest to get justice for Tyre?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN CRUMP\uc2 , ATTORNEY: Well, it is making sure that each and every one of them are held accountable, every one of them. And when it comes to Lieutenant Smith, Reverend Al, the Vice Mayor said it best, I`m very troubled that citizens like RowVaughn and Rodney, the taxpayers has to pay his pension and all of his benefits, even though he offered Tyre Nichols no humanity, when he saw him bleeding out there on the scene, when he tried to tell them that, oh, what kind of drugs are your son on? That is not right.\par \par \uc2 That is not what you reward for allowing them to be retiring, and so that`s something we`re focused on. The other thing we have to answer your question directly, we are going to be bringing a civil action because the truth of why they really stopped Tyre Nichols is going to come out.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Mrs. Wells, you stood with another family, that of Gershun Freeman yesterday in Memphis, to join them in calling for justice for their loved ones after their son, Gershun Freeman was killed by law enforcement in the Shelby County jail last October.\par \par \uc2 Why was it important for you and Rodney to be there to help that family?\par \par \uc2 WELLS: Well, we wanted to show our support. We wanted to show other people that this is still happening, where we`re not the only families that this is happening to. And this woman, she lost her only child. This was her only child, and for a mother to lose a child, period, is devastating, but to lose your only child?\par \par \uc2 So I felt like my husband and I needed to be there to support her. Because as I`ve said, I have three other children that I can follow and be there for, but she has no other children. So I felt it was good for us to be there to support her.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Ben, I want to ask you about two other cases you`re involved with. First, after facing backlash, a Mississippi Sheriff now says he wasn`t ruling out foul play in Rasheem Carter`s death months after initially saying there was no reason to suspect foul play.\par \par \uc2 This comes after the 25-year-old was found dead last fall after he warned his mother that he was being chased by White men hurling racial slurs. What`s your reaction to how this investigation in Mississippi is being handled?\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: It is outrageous, Reverend Al. We`re going to need you and National Action Network`s help definitely in Mississippi, because when this young man first went missing, and he was found a month later with his head decapitated. They had the audacity to tell his mother that he may have gotten dehydrated and animals tore his body apart.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there was no reason to suspect foul play. If that is not foul play, Reverend Al, what is?\par \par \uc2 This is a Mississippi lynching and we can`t let his death get swept under the rug.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now lastly, I want to ask you about the incident that led to the death of 28-year-old Irvo Otieno in Virginia. Ten people so far are now facing second-degree murder charges in Irvo`s death, after video showed the man who was handcuffed and shackled being pinned to the ground at a state mental hospital.\par \par \uc2 You compared this incident to George Floyd`s death, and I talked to the mother and brother the other day with you.\par \par \uc2 You compared it to George Floyd. How do you see this case playing out?\par \par \uc2 CRUMP: You know, it`s sad, Reverend Al because I think it`s going to play out a lot like George Florida and Tyre Nichols. The family saw the video with their legal team, just like in Tyre Nichols we saw it, and then America is going to see this video and it`s going to be very troubling, because like the prosecutor said, they literally smothered him to death.\par \par \uc2 He was handcuffed. He had leg irons on. He wasn`t a threat to anybody. But they all put their weight on him. And three years after George Floyd, Reverend Al, we`ve still got law enforcement officers putting knees on necks of restrained Black men.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, well, we`re going to have to leave it there and thank you for coming in and again, RowVaughn, we are with the pursuit of justice with you and Rodney all the way. Thank you for being here, Attorney \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After the break, a look on Trump`s possible arrest and what reaction from the GOP could say about the party and his place within it.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:43:51]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s bring in my political panel. Joining me now is former Democratic Congressman Max Rose and former Republican Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with the breaking news: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  claiming he will be arrested on Tuesday, although he does not indicate that the Manhattan DA has informed him of any imminent criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 The former President has also posted repeated calls for his supporters to protest. Republican Speaker of the House, Kevin McCarthy ran to Trump`s defense saying on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Any indictment is "outrageous abuse of power by a radical DA."\par \par \uc2 Carlos, Republicans largely rallied around Trump when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched his Mar-a-Lago resort for classified documents last year. Is that what we are seeing play out again now? And should they be defending him?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:00]\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO (R), FORMER US REPRESENTATIVE: Well, Reverend, what we`re seeing is that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is testing the loyalty of his supporters, of his base. This is what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does. He demands absolute loyalty every day at every hour. No matter what he does, he expects people to come out and defend him and support him.\par \par \uc2 And now, as he is facing a lot of legal scrutiny, by the way for doing things that, regardless of whether he is convicted or not, are terrible, right? I mean, this Stormy Daniels case is an embarrassment. It`s a horrible situation for him, but he is demanding that people step out and defend him and stand by him, and clearly, some people are doing that, and a lot of Republicans continue to be intimidated by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They continue to live in fear of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and that is why you`re seeing some step up and defend him.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Max, President Trump just posted on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  this week for the first time since January 6, 2021 and now he is using his Truth Social platform to make very similar calls to protest as he did on that day.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  tweeted today that Trump`s announcement this morning is, "reckless, doing so to keep himself in the news and to foment unrest among his supporters." Should the major tech companies be rethinking that decision to allow the former President to return?\par \par \uc2 MAX ROSE (D), FORMER US REPRESENTATIVE: One hundred percent. They never should have let him back on in the first place. It is eerily reminiscent of January 6th, everything that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is saying here and to all of those Republicans who are right now standing beside him, and behind him, God forbid, if there is violence next week, that will be on them as well.\par \par \uc2 Each and every Republican in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  had an opportunity to stop this. They had an opportunity to disqualify \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from ever running for President Again during his second impeachment trial and they chose once again, cowardice. This is awfully, awfully scary because what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is doing is he is looking to use the power of the mob as a means of defending himself in the Court of law to intimidate whether it is jurors or anyone and the like, and he has got to be stopped any which way is possible and certainly that includes these social media companies.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Carlos, Wyoming became the first State to outlaw the use of common abortion pills as Republican Governor Mark Gordon signed the ban into law on Friday. The move comes as a Texas Judge considers restricting access to the pill nationwide. What would be the impact of such a decision?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Well, Reverend, we know that the basis of each respective party are very passionate about abortion, but the people who decide elections, the swing voters, the people in suburban America, a lot of minority voters who are increasingly open to supporting both parties, they don`t support any radical views on abortion on either side.\par \par \uc2 And this is where Republicans, after having won, after having fought for decades to overturn Roe v. Wade and having achieved that goal now are continuing to press on. They`re kind of spiking the ball in the endzone. And there`s a lot of political risk there for Republicans, especially with suburban women who share they are for reasonable abortion restrictions, but once you start outlawing medicines, that`s really when you start losing a lot of these voters.\par \par \uc2 And it really seems like Republicans don`t learn the lesson from the 2022 election. They were supposed to win big. They did not. Why? Because they went too far. They were too radical. They were way too far outside of the mainstream and they were punished for it and if they keep doing this on the abortion issue and on other issues like election denial, then they`re going to continue paying the consequences.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Max, embattled New York Congressman George Santos this week filed paperwork for a possible re-election bid. The filings don`t guarantee he will run, but it keeps the option open as Representative Santos is being investigated by House Ethics Committee for allegedly lying about his background and mishandling campaign money.\par \par \uc2 You know New York politics. You know it well.\par \par \uc2 If Santos does run again, are Democrats ready to challenge him?\par \par \uc2 ROSE: Well, Rev, I don`t think I know it nearly as well as you do, but I`ll give you my two cents. There is zero chance that George Santos wins in 2024 and I would offer that it is incredibly unlikely that he even wins the Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:10]\par \par \uc2 ROSE: But this is not about either of those two things. This is about one, George Santos` personal business model. This is about him being now part of the foundation of the MAGA movement, but what is shocking amidst all of this is George Santos should not be in Congress right now.\par \par \uc2 The Republican leadership should have weeks ago, months ago called for him to step down. That is Kevin McCarthy, as well as his peers in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So you know, what is scary about this, in the end, is that they need George Santos. They need him to stay in office through this next election because their margins are so slim. That is not really a Republican seat, at least, if the Democratic Party in New York gets ahead and she doesn`t run all the way to the left and disqualify itself.\par \par \uc2 So, there is a lot of different dynamics at play here, but George Santos is thinking about 2025-2026and he wants to be \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 . He wants to be Bannon. He wants to have a podcast with a lot of followers. So this is a personal business model for him.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, I`m out of time, and I do know New York politics well, if he does run again, I can hear some people saying Max Rose ought to move over into that district and think about running himself. I don`t want to start no rumors.\par \par \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2  and Max Rose, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:55:26]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We are faced with an unprecedented pending possibility. No one really knows for sure, but the Manhattan DA, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  and his ADAs, but the possibility there and in Georgia and the Justice Department of the possible indictment of a former President.\par \par \uc2 Whatever the case may be or not be, it should be met with civility and non- violence.\par \par \uc2 We say before all of our marches that they must be peaceful or don`t come. Whatever the former President wants to do, he has the right to do, but he should, with no equivocation call for it to be peaceful and nonviolent to show what he claims he never had anything to do with January 6th. We`ll see how he behaves in the next few days.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:00]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020230319ej3i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for March 16, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Jonathan Capehart, Cynthia McFadden \par \uc2 8,135 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [23:00:10]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: British Member of Parliament \uc2 David Lammy\uc2  gets tonight`s Last Word. The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the rush to stabilize first republic bank after last week`s \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  collapse, a $30 billion rescue plan for another bank. What happens now?\par \par \uc2 Then, the latest developments in the many investigations surrounding the former President. The New York AG attempts to combat Trump`s delay tactics. And an NBC News investigation why are kids still going to school next to a plant emitting toxic chemicals as the 11th Hour gets underway on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. The nation`s biggest banks made a dramatic move today to rescue a smaller bank and head off a potential crisis. 11 of the top financial institutions including \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  and Citibank will pump $30 billion into \uc2 First Republic Bank\uc2  to keep it from collapsing. This huge cash injection comes as \uc2 First Republic\uc2  struggles with a falling stock price and fleeing depositors. And it comes just days after the sudden collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  and of course Signature Bank.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reporting the plan to save \uc2 First Republic\uc2 , are you ready for this? It was hatched by quite the trio Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , Fed Chair \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2 , and \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  CEO \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 . This morning, Yellen went before the Senate Finance Committee, and she insisted that the U.S. banking system is sound and defended the emergency action to contain the fallout after the \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  collapse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET L. YELLEN\uc2 , TREASURY SECRETARY: We felt that there was a serious risk of contagion that could have brought down and triggered runs on many banks. We wanted to make sure that the problems at \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  and Signature Bank didn`t undermine confidence in the soundness of banks around the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And tonight, we are keeping an eye on the former President`s legal troubles that are mounting and his strategy to fight possible charges from the Manhattan District Attorney. According to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , the Trump campaign no surprise plans to portray any charges from DA \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  as part of a so-called coordinated attack of the Democratic Party. The paper says the campaign is adding staff to help get that message out.\par \par \uc2 With that, we got a lot to cover. So, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel tonight. Kyle Cheney`s here. Politico`s Senior Legal Affairs reporter. Guy Adami, an Original Member of CNBC Fast Money Team. And \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , Department of Justice veteran and former Acting Solicitor General during the Obama administration. Guy, we`ve got to start with this bank news.\par \par \uc2 GUY ADAMI, CNBC ORIGINAL MEMBER, FAST MONEY: It`s crazy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It has been a brutal week for banks. But here we are 11 of the country`s biggest banks stepped in to rescue a smaller one. Now, we love to hate on banks for many, many good reasons. But will a move like this change their reputation in any way, right? They saved \uc2 First Republic\uc2 , and it didn`t cost taxpayers a dime.\par \par \uc2 ADAMI: No. Well, we can have that conversation another time. But will it save the reputation? Absolutely not. I mean, there are people watching this right now. They`re saying, wait a second, another bailout of these big banks. What`s going on? Why don`t I get bailed out. And I totally get it. In terms of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , let`s just be clear, you`ve talked about this. They were bailing out the depositors. It`s much different, right? It`s much different. And people say wait a second, the deposit base for these rich people, rich people that had to make payroll. So, they saved the working guy and gal, so that was a good thing they did what they needed to do. This time is a little bit different. Let`s see what happens with \uc2 First Republic\uc2 . But I guarantee it`s not that these bankers are all that magnanimous. That`s $30 billion, by the way, for 120 days only. So, we`ll see what happens at the end.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So why did they do it?\par \par \uc2 ADAMI: Well, they did it because they were told to do it, Steph. Let`s just not mince words here. I mean, they said listen, for the good of us as a group, as an entity and for our system, you need to pony up and we don`t want to make the same mistakes we made 15 years ago, when everybody but \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  did it. And we all remember what happened to \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in the aftermath.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What is your take on this trio, right? \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2 , right? We have all seen guys like \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  have to go before Congress in these hearings and get raked over the coals, right? Specifically, a lot of Democratic lawmakers hate his guts.\par \par \uc2 ADAMI: Yeah. But the leading bank or the most important bank, or I would say on the planet at the most important bank, maybe in the world has to take that leadership role. Obviously, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , we understand her role and know us as well. So, make sense that triumvirate. Now we can argue whether or not that`s a great triumvirate or some unholy triumvirate, but they did what they needed to do.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:08]\par \par \uc2 I will tell you in the aftermath of that news, though, \uc2 First Republic\uc2 , the stock went down about 17 or so percent. So, the volatility in the underlying equities continues.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, nobody here wants to hear you talk about volatility on the underlying equities. What they want to know is, how scared should we be, right? We are clearly not in 2008. But it`s not like we`re in an awesome situation.\par \par \uc2 ADAMI: No.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, we had a long period of free money, we raised interest rates, the government pumped money into the system.\par \par \uc2 ADAMI: Right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And during all these years, we got the crypto craze, we got the spat craze. The system is fragile.\par \par \uc2 ADAMI: Extraordinarily fragile. And the system is fragile for a number of different reasons that have transpired over the last 20 or so years. But remember --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Should you be scared?\par \par \uc2 ADAMI: Should you be scared? If you have money in the bank, you should not be scared. What basically has happened is they have told you the Federal Reserve, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , himself has told you your money is money good in these banks. So, if you`re worried about your deposits going away, don`t worry about that. What we should be worried about, though, is the health of the system. And that`s probably a much broader conversation.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Neal, let`s talk about this reporting on Donald Trump`s plan that is straight out of the same playbook he has had since birth, that he`s going to attack the Manhattan D.A. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yeah, so first of all, you`ve got this race almost now. I don`t think either of them intended it. But a race between the Manhattan District Attorney Bragg and the District Attorney in Georgia, \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 , to see who`s going to be the first person in American history to indict a former sitting President. Right now, it looks like both of those, Stephanie, are barreling toward an indictment. And perhaps in New York as early as tomorrow, that`s what at least some people are saying. And I think the reasons for that are that \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  has gone before the grand jury. It`s like it`s 20 of time before prosecutors. Stormy Daniels has gone before the prosecutors this week, there was a request to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to testify before the grand jury. And we know that his attorneys have gone in to try and convince the District Attorney not to indict.\par \par \uc2 And so, the story today says basically, Trump is going to use as you say, his old playbook, attack, attack call this political. And I don`t think it`s going to work. I mean, Trump can whine all he wants about the imagined weaponization, but it`s not going to change the evidence against him in court. And yes, it`s worked in the past to try and get prosecutors to flinch. There`s no doubt they`ve melted under Trump`s withering attacks. But I think we`re in a different phase now. If these indictments happen, as I suspect they will, then it becomes a legal question. And these kinds of attempts to try and say it`s all politics. And you know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is behind these indictments. That`s preposterous.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s a reminder to our audience this weaponization of government committee that was put together led by Jim Jordan has resulted in nothing so far. But Neal, it`s crazy. So, you`re saying \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  is right behind \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  in the Stormy Daniels situation, how far behind is Jack Smith and Mar-a-Lago? Could this end up being everything everywhere all at once for Trump?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Nice, very well could. I mean my guess is the Jack Smith investigation, it has two phases. One is the Mar-a-Lago. The documents that dozens of documents \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  stole and brought to his golf club, including very highly sensitive documents. The other is Trump`s role in January 6th. I think that latter investigation is quite a bit behind. And I don`t anticipate that happening soon. But Mar-a-Lago has never been that complicated. I suspect the only complication arose because there were some, you know, incidents been discovered of President Biden and former Vice President Pence having some documents themselves.\par \par \uc2 I think the case is very different for Trump because it`s mostly about him lying and being asked to return these documents and him certifying through his lawyer that he meant that he returned everything and so they had to execute a search warrant when they go on by found. You know, dozens more highly sensitive classified documents. So, I think it`s a lying obstruction case, and I wouldn`t be shocked if Jack Smith brings it, you know, pretty soon.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Kyle, we spent much of last week talking about video from January 6th and knocking down the nonsense posturing that it was a tourist day. We all know it wasn`t. But in the new video that you`ve been reporting on today, the rioters got a lot closer to the senators, including 80 plus year old Republican \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2 , a lot closer than we knew. Why is this coming out now?\par \par \uc2 KYLE CHENEY, POLITICO SR. LEGAL AFFAIRS REPORTER: So, this actually remarkably came out kind of as a result of the release of these videos with \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  by Speaker McCarthy. As a result of that, you know, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  aired some footage of Jacob Chansley, the QAnon Shaman, and tried to portray him as sort of this non-peaceful and compliant and almost tourist there -- the invitation of the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [23:10:15]\par \par \uc2 Well, we asked for some of the videos related to his case after that went came back in the news and the government released some that they had actually been supposed to release a long time ago. One of them was this video that included \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  mid evacuation from -- during the riot. And what we learned for the first time was members of the mob were staring at him leaving the building with barely any guard in between, including Dominic Pezzola, the Proud Boy who`s currently in a seditious conspiracy trial, just staring watching him leave, probably have no idea who it is. But it`s remarkable that two years after this attack, we`re just learning that \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2 , the third in line to the presidency, by the way on that day, within the sight line of the mob.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Has \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  or his office made any comment on this? I mean, he`s got colleagues more on the House side than the Senate who continue to act like it was no big deal. Just another day in the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: You know, one of my colleagues spoke to him and he talked about that moment. He just said basically, it was just go -- he was just in go mode that he didn`t even have an awareness, situational awareness at that time, what was going on around him. So, I don`t think he was even aware that members of the mob, whether they even were there at that moment, well, that`s one of the saw him. And what`s actually amazing is to in that moment, Eugene Goodman, in the famous footage we`ve seen of him facing down the mob and luring them away from the Senate. He`s exactly opposite of where \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2 . He`s looking across, it can actually see that evacuation in progress, potentially. And he actually draws that mob away from Grassley, in a way that I think is -- just another notch in his belt of the things he did that day that hasn`t gotten any notice until now.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Neal, Tish James, she is urging a judge not to give Trump what he wants, which is the other page in his playbook to delay the civil case against him. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Yeah, I mean, I think that`s a pretty good. It`s not surprising that Trump`s going to try and delay and delay and delay. And I think Tish James is absolutely right to force this issue. So, you know, I don`t think that there`s much more to the story than that. I suspect that the judge will not by Trump`s attempt to delay things once more. And I think we`re going to see a lot of action in New York.\par \par \uc2 In addition to these investigations that we`ve mentioned, there`s also new reporting that some federal prosecutors in New York are looking into Trump`s social and Trump`s Media -- media outfit, and weather they took $8 million from people with suspected Russian ties, including possible money laundering. And so, that`s yet another investigation by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And the people who have won over the last six years, every single election or lawyers. Guy, let`s go back to the economy. Let`s go back to the banking. I`m not going to call it a crisis but the situation that we`re in. People are scratching their heads and they`re pointing fingers. What do we need to do? Right? It`s more regulation. It`s less regulation. It`s got to be smart regulation. What`s the right path?\par \par \uc2 ADAMI: We had a conversation earlier. You know, and I don`t agree with Elizabeth Warren about a lot of things. But when she came out in 2018, when they were trying to rollback a lot of Dodd-Frank, by the way, I think 17 Democrats voted to do that and it went through. That rollback was probably somewhat misguided under the Trump administration. But what`s fascinating is \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , which obviously we know what happened to that stock and that bank, they would have passed the stress test, given the parameters that they wanted to. Think about that for a second. So, people will say, how can that be? Because the stress test itself was flawed. The regulator`s set up the wrong tests. They were looking backwards at something that we`ve gotten through, and they should have been looking at other things.\par \par \uc2 For example, interest rate risk, which is citizens, we all deal with. The fact that the regulator`s didn`t make the banks look at that is flabbergasting in a word. I`m not going to say criminal, but it`s -- it makes people scream at the top of their lungs. And I understand that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So, everyone`s trying to get their heads around? What happens with the Fed? And we certainly don`t have Fed watchers watching, but we have people that are concerned about inflation and about rates.\par \par \uc2 ADAMI: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What do you think \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  is going to do next week? And what do you want him to do?\par \par \uc2 ADAMI: Well, it`s a longer answer than I have time for. But I`ll say this, the problems that we`re facing, many of those problems have been created by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  that was begging for inflation years ago. They got the inflation that they want through hubris, they thought they could control it. Now, when trying to control it, all the problems you`re seeing play out right in front of you is on their back. So, they created a problem. They`re trying to fix the problem, and in trying to fix the problem, they`re creating more problems. What is \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  going to do? Well, the \uc2 ECB\uc2 , the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2  they raise rates by 50 basis points today. They have an inflation problem, too. We have an inflation problem here. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  needs to stay the course, Steph.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:11]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Last question before we go. What is your take on this? The Biden administration, potentially banning the use of TikTok in this country, unless the Chinese ownership agrees to step out and sell their state?\par \par \uc2 ADAMI: Yeah, well, that part`s not going to happen. I think we all understand. So, the question is how far they want to go down this road? Now, I`m not going to make many friends with the 13- to 28-year-old, but it should be banned in this deal. It`s funny. We got apoplectic about a Chinese spy balloon flying over the country, which I understand. Meanwhile, in every citizen`s hand, are spy devices on the back of TikTok. It`s mind boggling, so they shouldn`t do it. But I`ll say this. If in fact it`s gets banned, what are the ramifications? What are the Chinese do in retaliation? Is \uc2 Apple\uc2  in the crosshairs, for example? So, there`s so many chapters left in this story, Steph.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: This called TikTok thing, Chinese spying thing. It`s as though we are living in a horror movie. If I said to you, I saw a movie last night where kids were addicted to an app that was spying on this country though that can`t be real. No, it`s real. And our kids are doing it in their beds right now.\par \par \uc2 Kyle Cheney, \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , Guy Adami, welcome to the 11th Hour.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Colorado Senator Michael Bennet is here. We`re going to talk about what the Texas decision on abortion pills could mean for his state and a whole lot more.\par \par \uc2 And later, political divides on full display just about everywhere you look from books to how ridiculous is it, now banks? Will break down what is going on with \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  and Tim Miller. The 11th Hour just getting underway on a busy Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:21:25]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: As fallout from the \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  collapse continues, some moderate Senate Democrats are resisting new rules that would impose tougher regulations on banks. Democratic Senator Michael Bennet of Colorado joins me now. He`s a member of the Senate Finance Committee and one of those senators who stands by their 2018 vote to ease scrutiny on certain banks.\par \par \uc2 Senator, let`s start with that because there`s a lot of people pointing fingers right now saying this would not have happened if those rules had not -- had been rolled back? Well, first of all, and Guy on me said it in our last segment. Even if the full force of the original Dodd-Frank was in place, \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  would have passed. So, help us understand. Take us back to 2018. Why did you go to ease those rules? And why do you stand by it today?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNET, (D) COLORADO: Yeah, let me say Guy -- Stephanie, it`s great to see you again. Guy said it, the Treasury Secretary today said it. And the vote in 2018, for me was actually fairly straightforward. I`ve been working for 10 years, with Colorado`s local banks, with rural banks throughout the Rocky Mountain West, who you know, as a result of the downturn in the regulation that we`ve put in place, after the big downturn in `09 and `08, you know, we`ve seen massive consolidation in the banking industry. And we`ve seen the small banks getting, you know, having to comply with regulations that were very appropriate for the largest banks in America to have to deal with. But the small banks were really struggling to be able to pay the overhead to meet those regulations. And that was what the bill in 2018 was an attempt to do, at least for my part.\par \par \uc2 And obviously, there were provisions in there for the Fed to exercise its discretion on banks the size of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , which by the way, is eight times or more the size of the banks that we`re talking about in Colorado, and in rural America, which has only gotten more consolidated more gobbled up, as the years have gone by. And now I think the people that know the situation best can see it for what it is, which is a huge number of mistakes that they made -- the bank made here, I mean, ridiculous mistakes that you never would have made. You know, they quadrupled their assets over like four years. So, they should have known if it`s going to go up that fast, it could collapse. They were incredibly heavily weighted toward tech, which they -- I mean, it looked great during COVID. But when it came out, as you know, that sector moves together all the time, and they were very concentrated there.\par \par \uc2 And then for reasons that I could certainly not, you know, I don`t understand, they started to buy, you know, they went long paper that had really low interest rates when \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  was telling everybody on planet earth that he was going to raise rates for the reason that Guy said. So, where was the audit committee? Where was the Board of the bank? Where was the regulator? Even forget Dodd-Frank, you know, like, what was the basic blocking and tackling that Americans should be able to expect out of their banks, whether they`re tiny banks are huge banks, and out of the regulator, and I think --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But here`s the problem, Senator. All the questions you`re asking, are such important ones, right? Where was the audit committee? Where -- you know, where was the CEO of this? How did this happen? So, a full autopsy is going to happen. All the questions will be asked, but at the end of the day, if nothing they did was illegal. If it was just outrageously stupid, then there`ll be no real consequences and are you worried we`re going to face backlash from the American people like we did after the financial crisis?\par \par \uc2 [23:25:09]\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Well, I`ve -- you know, actually that`s such an interesting question, Stephanie. I mean, I was in the private sector, when we had the telecom boom in this country. You will remember you are working in the private sector, you probably did, as well. And I can remember those anchors, not you, those anchors on some other networks, pumping up the stock day after day after day, that`s when my mom first bought \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  stock as she called it back then. And, you know, sometimes things that are stupid, aren`t illegal. And often, like 80% of the stuff that gets us into trouble is actually stupid, not illegal. But that`s not a reason we shouldn`t look and we should make sure that we make the best decisions we can based on the facts, as we understand them, not a bunch of sound bites on with all respect. They`re all other programs, cable television.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: New topic, a very, very serious one we have been covering on this network. The medication abortion case in Texas. Talk to us about how your state, Colorado, would be affected if abortion pills are banned.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: This is part of a national ban that the Republican Party is trying to put on in this country. My state was the first state to legalize abortion in America, five years before Roe vs. Wade was even decided. We were the first state to codify a woman`s right to choose after the Dobbs decision stripped women and Americans of a constitutional fundamental freedom for the first time in American history since reconstruction, and now we`re seeing the aftermath. And if this judge -- who by the way, today, Stephanie was literally asking about 19th Century morality laws about carrying immoral products from one state to another as if that is the case with a chemical that was approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  20 years ago and has been used by 5 million American women. You know how much trouble we`re in and we`ve got to fight to make sure that we codify Roe vs. Wade is law of the land. That`s what the people of Colorado want. I think --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: 19th Century morality laws. We have no more time, but I have to ask you this one question. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , in the past, you have been very clear about what a impossible person he is that you have viewed him as public enemy number one in the Republican Party. Has that view changed? Because in the last six months, it appears that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is more willing to work with Democrats. Well, Kevin McCarthy`s vote was being held up and he was being held hostage by the likes of \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 . It was \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  at a bridge in I think, Ohio with \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BENNET: I think it might have been in Kentucky, actually we`re --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Kentucky, you`re right. Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: He, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , had been fighting for that. That`s his home state or home Commonwealth. And Stephanie, I`m not going to say one negative word for once about Mitch because I know he`s convalescing because he fell and he hurt himself. So, I hope -- I just wish him all the best. But I will say this, when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  became president, and I was just running for reelection last November, I was able to check off bipartisan infrastructure bill, bipartisan veterans` law, bipartisan postal reform law, bipartisan laws finally the first time since Reagan was president and shipped everything overseas to Southeast Asia.\par \par \uc2 We had a law that said, you know what, we`re going to bring it back to the United States. So, McConnell had a role to play in all that, and I`m grateful that he did, because the American people saw that, and they liked it. And they said, we don`t have to go back to the freaking chaos of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We can expect something better than that, from our exercise and self-government. So, the one bill the Republicans wouldn`t vote for that was important at the end was a bill to cap drug crisis for seniors, the $2,000 required Medicare to negotiate on behalf of the American people and kept insulin at 35 bucks. They wouldn`t even -- and they stripped the provisions of that bill that would have kept insulin for all Americans at $35. And that`s why we have to keep fighting, but I`m very glad that we were able to get the bipartisan stuff done that we did get done.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Senator Bennet bringing the heat tonight. Thank you for joining us. Very good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BENNET: Thanks for having me. See you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Coming up, conservatives blaming \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  -- I can`t even say this with a straight face -- blaming Silicon Valley Banks failure on a $73 million donation to \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 . Here`s the problem with that, it never happened. When the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:34:38]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , brace yourself, spent more than $73 million on donations to BLM and related organizations.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They donated more than $73 million to \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 . They pledged the summer of 2020 to increase their commitment to DEI. This is their focus.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PETER JAMES DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Roughly $73 million went to \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  related causes that is according to a tracker from the Claremont Institute.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:06]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: BLM. That`s a bunch of BS. As we like to say on this show the truth matters, but only if you hear it. And it turns out, absolutely none of that was true. Our friend Judd Legum looked into that Claremont Institute data that Fox News has been touting all day long. And are you ready for the actual amount that \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  gave to Black Lives Matter? Zero. Zero dollars and zero cents.\par \par \uc2 Back with us tonight, Pulitzer Prize Winning Journalist \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , Associate Editor of The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  and of course, the anchor of The Saturday and The Sunday show on MSNBC, and Tim Miller is here, contributor to The Bulwark and former Communications Director for Jeb Bush.\par \par \uc2 Tim, what do we do with this? All day long, $73 million to \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  and it`s a flat out lie.\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, THE BULWARK CONTRIBUTOR: Well, they do a lot of lie and over there. And they admitted to it in their -- in their private text messages. And I guess this -- the pattern just keeps on going forward, right? If they haven`t suffered any punishment from this in the past, why wouldn`t they keep doing it? Like these guys -- the frustrating part about all of this, if we`re just like not being glib for a second, is that you could come up with a legitimate conservative critique about what led to the \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  collapse. You know, it might not be right -- you know, it might not be exactly right, we could debate it. But you might say, hey, I like Biden juice the economy, too much. Interest rates went up too high. These guys made a bad bet on interest rates. You know, this -- the bank CEO and the Biden administration both have a little bit of accountability here. You could make that argument. None of them are making that argument though because it`s a clown show over there now.\par \par \uc2 And everything we have to say is woke, woke, woke. They have this mind virus where they`re addicted to talking about woke and they`re addicted to making these little racist dog whistles and a lot of cases in the case of the Wall Street Journal that blamed it on the one black on the board. That`s a direct quote from The Wall Street Journal that there was one black on the board that was to blame. It wasn`t so much of a dog whistle. But that`s what these guys in the post Trump era have resorted to. They can`t even make a substantive economic argument anymore.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And quick, quick, historical reminder, one black on the board. Do you remember who was running the entire banking complex during the Great Depression and the financial crisis? White dudes wearing fleece vests.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, Tim makes an unbelievable point. You could make a lot of critiques right now about the Fed, about the Treasury, about a lot of things. They`re choosing woke banks. When are we going to decide this is simply too stupid?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , THE WASHINGTON POST ASSOCIATE EDITOR: Well, the fact that we`re talking about it in this manner, should let everyone know that this is just too stupid. And it`s not a dog whistle anymore. Tim, it`s a full-on bark, is what they`re doing. Woke is the new CRT, is the new you`re playing the race card. And what they`re doing is just by slapping woke on everything. It`s like an accessory on the GOP`s grab bag of grievance. Anything that bugs them, anything that they think will rile up their base against Democrats, against the President, against this administration about anything. They`ll steer the conversation to raise. It is absolutely ridiculous that, well, given what we know now, as Jim said, we shouldn`t be surprised that they`re out there peddling provable lies, saying that a bank gave money to \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 , and it didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 And there should be accountability for Fox News and for those folks for peddling in lies. But I do think it is incumbent upon us in the media. I think it`s incumbent upon right thinking Republicans to stand up and say that argument that they`re making over there is wrong. To Tim`s point, here is the Republican or/conservative argument against what \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  did, and they`re not doing that. They won`t do it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That joke of woke. All right, I want to talk about another really serious issue. The New York -- it`s about education. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  is reporting that there is a publisher that created multiple different versions of their social studies material and some softened or even eliminated references whatsoever to race. They even took the story of Rosa Parks out and they did it so they could gain approval in Florida. Jonathan, Rosa Parks, I mean, my daughter had Rosa Parks in her coloring books in the first grade during women`s history or Black History Month. What in the world is going on here?\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Florida seems becoming unintelligent, a dystopian hellscape for people who want to know the truth about our country and about our country`s history. And quite honestly, Stephanie, I am so tired of Florida and Florida officials and publishers and all these other folks who are also worried about the tender feelings of students. And let`s be clear, they`re worried about the tender feelings of white students.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:11]\par \par \uc2 No one`s talking about the black and brown students or the non-white students in those same classrooms, who have to sit there and learn -- a sanitized version of American history, while at the same time seeing that their own role, their own ancestor`s role in the making and the creation and the maintenance of this country gets completely erased. If you are erasing the heroic deeds of Rosa Parks, that publisher should be -- and those education officials in Florida should really be thinking about what why are we doing that? And they should be ashamed.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, I want to make a correction. They didn`t eliminate Rosa Parks` name. What they did was soften and adjust what they considered some of the harsher parts of the story which harsher parts would translate to the truth and the whole story. \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , Tim Miller, great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 That`s heavy.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Thanks, Steph.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Coming up, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  warned for years that a Louisiana rubber plant is emitting a toxic chemical that likely causes cancer. And you know what`s right next door? An elementary school and children are still attending that school today. They got out of class a few hours ago. Cynthia McFadden investigates why on earth this is happening, and she`s here when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:46:17]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Turn up the volume. We have an important story for you tonight from Reserve, Louisiana. It is part of an 85-mile industrial corridor in a state known -- in a state and it is known as Cancer Valley. The \uc2 EPA\uc2  says a rubber plant near a school has been spewing a chemical into the air that likely causes cancer and residents are asking why this plant is still open. My dear friend, a hero of mine at NBC Cynthia McFadden is investigating this really important story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA MCFADDEN, NBC NEWS SR. LEGAL & INVESTIGATIVE CORRESPONDENT (voice- over): Four-year-old George says he loves going to school at Fifth Ward Elementary in Reserve, Louisiana. But according to the federal government, going to school here could be shortening George`s life, as well as the lives of his 400 or so primarily black classmates.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: One of your neighbors says it`s like sending the kids to a gas chamber.\par \par \uc2 MARY HAMPTON, GEORGE`S GREAT GRANDMOTHER: Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: 83-year-old Mary Hampton is George`s great grandmother. She lives a few blocks from the school. Her father bought the land once worked by slaves giving parcels to his nine children.\par \par \uc2 HAMPTON: He thought of leaving us a legacy. It`s left for his descendants. They don`t care about us. It`s racial, they don`t care y`all just sit-down inhale and die.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Hampton and her lifelong friend Robert Taylor had been fighting to have the kids moved from the school. And the air quality improved ever since the \uc2 EPA\uc2  said seven years ago that an unsafe level of a likely carcinogen called chloroprene was being admitted from the Denka plant right next door to the school. Denka makes a rubber used in wetsuits and other products, the only plant of its kind in the country.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT TAYLOR, HAMPTON`S FRIEND: We`ve been designated sacrifice zone. I don`t want to stand out they will be allowed to sacrifice the lives of us for anybody`s profit. We had the highest cancer risk. \uc2 EPA\uc2  informed us that in 2017.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN (on camera): The highest cancer risk in the country.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: In the country.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: What was your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: I was floored.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN (voice-over): Though Denka says they`ve reduced emissions by 85%, air monitors near the school often register at levels far exceeding the recommended 0.2 level. As we saw when we visited with environmental scientists, Wilma Subra.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): What`s the result?\par \par \uc2 WILMA SUBRA, ENVIRONMENTAL SCIENTIST: Higher than 0.2 chloroprene concentration.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: How much higher? I mean, sometimes I know it varies depending on when and how much the plan is manufacturing. But I mean, what`s the extreme?\par \par \uc2 SUBRA: I`ve calculated it here. 10 times, 20 times, 50 times.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: The acceptable level acceptable?\par \par \uc2 SUBRA: Acceptable level, yes.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Nevertheless, now that the state of Louisiana or the \uc2 EPA\uc2  has forced the plant to comply with the 0.2 recommendation or order the children moved nor have members of the local school board. Patrick Sanders, the recently retired Head of the School Board, who`s a local mortician grew up right next to the plant. Many of his neighbors have died of cancer, including his father and sister. He`s fighting cancer for the second time.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): There are going to be a lot of people sitting at home going hold on a second, why wouldn`t you at least move them as far away from that plant as you could?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK SANDERS, RETIRED HEAD OF THE SCHOOL BOARD: I would agree with those people. For the safety of those kids.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: But you didn`t do it?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: No, I didn`t.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Why?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I didn`t -- I not -- I didn`t. I was one member of 11. It takes the majority of the school board to make this decision.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: But did you stand up and say this is wrong. We`ve got to fix it.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: No, I didn`t.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Why not?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: There`s not a reason as to why not that I can definitively give you did I stand up and beat on the table? No.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: You wish you had?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Looking back, yes.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN (voice-over): But a glimmer of hope just as we`re packing up to leave, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  sent us an email they decided to file a lawsuit in federal court to require Denka --\par \par \uc2 (On camera): -- to significantly reduce hazardous chloroprene emissions from its neoprene manufacturing facility.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:18]\par \par \uc2 HAMPTON: Yes, we want. This is what we`re fighting for. Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: But Robert Taylor is not so sure this will change anything.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): What emergency order, you`re worried this isn`t going to do anything?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: No, not while they`re still operating.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: You`re saying shut him down?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Shut him dig. It should have been done years ago.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Shut them down for George and all the other people here who just want to breathe clean air.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We got more questions. Cynthia McFadden`s here. What the heck is going on here?\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Well, it`s a good question. I mean, we went down to try to answer a single question. Why? Why those kids still going to school at this elementary school that abuts the plan? And, you know, I guess the answer in fairness is it`s complicated. But really, when you talk to these two incredible 80 something year olds who are leading the charge, Mary and Bob, they say essentially, the people that we are supposed to be able to rely on that we`re supposed to be able to trust, they`ve just let us down. And you know, it`s really true the federal, the state and the local level.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What is this company say? The company that`s making this rubber, that`s emitting these toxins?\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Well, the company says, listen, it doesn`t increase cancer risk, one. Two, they say that they are abiding by all the laws and that they`re not in violation of anything. And three, they say, as you heard in the piece, that they`ve lowered emissions by 85%. And, you know, they`re -- they intend to continue until someone stops them.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Why wasn`t anything done to move the kids though?\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Well, you know, I think in some ways, the community has been so beaten down, that they kind of didn`t rise up as you heard in the interview with the man who was on the school board for 20 years head of it, you know, he`s sort of sighs. It`s very interesting that we`ve spoken to the \uc2 EPA\uc2 , continuously since last fall. And they told us today to expect some action in the near future.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Possibly because of your report?\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Well, I don`t know. I -- you know, if we could have pushed a little bit, I hope so.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then, let me just ask this. You know, it`s easy for us to sit in these chairs and we say to you, why don`t these people just move?\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Oh, well --\par \par \uc2 Could they -- I mean, who`s going to buy their house? Could they really move?\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: They can`t sell the house and most people have everything they own tied up in these homes. I mean, you heard, you know, Mary Hampton talk about her father buying this land being so proud that he was giving his children a future. Nine siblings all got little plots of land. Mary was one of the first women to work in the petrochemical companies. And she saved every penny, she told me to build that house. She can`t move.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s easy for us to say move. We have never walked in their footsteps. Cynthia, this -- I`m so grateful that you did this story. I`m grateful for everything you do. But this story is so important. Thank you for bringing it to us.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Thank you for letting us tell the story.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I`m glad you did.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, conservatives. They love to toss around the word. We even tried to find a definition during last week`s nightcap, but it turns out that people who use it most, they don`t actually know what it means either. We`re going to show you when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:55:24]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BETHANY MANDEL, AUTHOR, "STOLEN YOUTH": We hope that you know parents of all political stripes will pick up the book. This is sort of a woke reimagining that is very, very, very far left. Only 7% of Americans consider themselves very liberal and probably fewer of them consider themselves to be woke and so, you know, when we talk about traditional --\par \par \uc2 BRIAHNA JOY GRAY, HOST, "RISING": What does that mean to you? Would you mind defining woke because it`s come up a couple of times that I just want to make sure we`re on the same page?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, going for woke. That was Bethany Mandel, author of a new book about so called woke indoctrination, being asked to simply define the term of the book that she wrote everything about. The folks over at The Daily Show saw this interview and they decided to have a little fun with it. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK, OK, OK. I got into the bonds; the bonds may access pin. It says the password to this on the bond is the definition of the word woke. OK. Give me on the phone with one of those conservatives that uses the word all the time. Patcher it. OK, hey, listen, I need you to define the term woke. Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 MANDEL: So, I mean, woke is sort of the idea that --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Come on just give me the definition so I can put it in, anything? Woah, what do I need to do?\par \par \uc2 MANDEL This is going to be one of those moments that goes viral. I mean, woke is something that`s very hard to define. And we`ve spent an entire chapter defining it. Is sort of the understanding that we need to totally reimagine and reduce society in order to create hierarchies of oppression.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Reimagining and redo. Didn`t work. Give me again.\par \par \uc2 MANDEL: Sorry. I -- It`s hard to explain in a 15 second soundbite.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You know, I`m going to just do it myself. It does take - - woke is stopping hierarchies of oppression.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, that certainly blew up in their faces. Perhaps our friend Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  can enlighten us on the subject. And maybe that author too.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON, MSNBC HOST, POLITICS NATION: Different Americans have different views with woke.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Absolutely, yeah.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And what I always say to people when they start talking about woke, what`s the opposite? Sleep? I mean -- and I think you change the argument. Do you want to be woke or do you want to be sleep?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [00:00:10]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 Wise\uc2  words from the Rev. to take us off the air tonight. And on that note, I wish you all a very good night. It is time to sleep now. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late. I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020230317ej3g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for March 16, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Courtney Kube, Steph Twitty, Stephanie Ruhle,  Vaughn Hillyard, Ashley Parker, Lisa Rubin, Mark Murray, Dasha Burns, Lisa  Rubin, Marissa Parra, John Torres \par \uc2 7,749 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing. Live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Russia`s \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  seems increasingly desperate to change the dynamic of the brutal and bloody war in Ukraine. Is that what was behind this week`s confrontation with a U.S. drone? And what does it say that Putin is now asking his fellow billionaires to save Russia`s economy?\par \par \uc2 Plus, the roller coaster ride on Wall Street shows no signs of stopping with concerns about multiple banks, both of the U.S. and in Europe, but the S&P 500 is actually up. What it all means for finances of everyday Americans?\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies ramping up the pressure on \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Do they think that the Florida Governor can be intimidated out of running?\par \par \uc2 Lots to talk about, but we start with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  under extraordinary and growing pressure both at home and abroad, mired in a war that`s gone on far longer and cost more money and lives than nearly anyone had predicted. Today, Putin addressed Russia`s business elite in person, the first time he`s done that since the war started. He called on them to invest in Russian society to put patriotism above profit. The latest sign of how badly Russia`s economy is suffering.\par \par \uc2 And it comes on the same day the Pentagon released this video showing the confrontation between Russian fighter jets and an American unmanned drone. NBC News has learned that the pilot was on orders from officials inside the Kremlin to harass the drone before ultimately colliding with it and forcing it to crash in the Black Sea, roughly 50 miles from Russian-controlled Crimea.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Courtney Kube who has been ahead of the pack and breaking all of these stories, and retired Army Lieutenant General Steph Twitty, a former deputy commander of U.S. European Command and an MSNBC military analyst. So, Courtney, what do we know today about whether this drone was brought down intentionally or accidentally? What are your sources telling you?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: So, U.S. officials -- defense officials are saying very openly that it was brought down unintentionally. So, you can see in that video that we just got today that when one of those Russian jets comes in at a very high rate of speed, you can`t -- you can`t quite see the actual impact. But you see the result of the impact there where the camera kind of goes pixelated for a moment there. And then when it comes back and we see it again -- again, this is the camera off of that MQ-Nine Reaper drone, you can see that the propeller is clearly damaged by the -- by what was an impact.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Russians, of course, saying there was no collision that occurred here. The -- but this -- the U.S. putting out this video to prove their -- at least this part of their version of the narrative that not only were the Russian pilots dumping fuel, not only were they were flying very erratically and dangerously, but that there was actually a collision between these two aircraft. Now, of course, when it lost that propulsion, when the propeller was damaged, the drone became very difficult to operate and dangerous, so the U.S. made the decision to do two things.\par \par \uc2 Number one, wipe the software. That is take all the information that would be potentially classified that could be exploited by an enemy and clear it off that drone. Number two, they were able to glide it down closer to the Black Sea and then bring it down in a very intentional manner. The goal in most of these cases is to break it apart.\par \par \uc2 We know according to defense officials that there was a relatively wide debris field. General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2 , the Chairman of the Joint Chiefs said yesterday that it was -- it went down in an area that was four to 5000 feet in-depth, a pretty deep part of the Black Sea. And not only that but much of it broke apart. There were small pieces that would have been left behind in the Black Sea there, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, General, we have been looking at this video obviously since they released it. But you see probably more than I do. I know Courtney sees more than I do. And you pointed to that same sort of portion of the video she just talked about, the jet sprays fuel on the drone, and then what is apparently a collision. What do you see when you look at this? What does it tell you?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. STEPH TWITTY, MSNBC MILITARY ANALYST: Well, it`s a couple of things. Obviously, when you take a look at the video -- the video, the pilot of the Su-27 acted irresponsibly, reckless, and unsafe. And as you can see in the video right there where you`re showing, he pulled up the nose in order to spray the fuel on the propeller. He was trying to blind the sensors and disables the sensors with the fuel.\par \par \uc2 And as he pulls up, the second pass here, he does it again. You can see the fuel spraying. On the second pass, if you look at that propeller there, it is damaged. And so, the damage of the propeller occurred on the second pass, and that eventually caused the Reaper to become disabled and the pilots had to probably bring it down. Although as they brought it down, it probably just spun out of control into the Black Sea.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to play for you part of what the Russian ambassador to the U.S. said right after this happened. Take a Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMBASSADOR ANATOLY ANTONOV, RUSSIAN AMBASSADOR TO U.S.: This drone can carry 1700 kilos of explosives. This drone can carry a few bombs. You`ll see that what will be the reaction of United States if you see such Russian drone very close, for example, to San Francisco or New York? What will be the reaction of the United States? For me, it`s clear. For you as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Look. Clearly, the Russians are not happy with the U.S. drones in that area. Talk about the -- why the Black Sea is so important militarily. Why it`s become such a flashpoint?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Yes, for a couple of reasons. Number one is we have three \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies that bordered the Black Sea. You have Romania, you have Bulgaria, and you have Turkey. And we have what`s called NATO Baltic police in there and enhanced air police in there as well. To ensure the safety of the skies of our \uc2 NATO\uc2  partners, we typically -- when I say we, \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies, typically patrol the areas of the skies there.\par \par \uc2 The second thing, we must understand that the Black Sea, although the Russians claim that the Black Sea is theirs, it is in fact, international waters. And so, we have a responsibility to ensure that we are controlling our freedom of navigation and our freedom of air and that area. And so, that`s why you see \uc2 NATO\uc2  and the U.S. typically conduct operations in the Black Sea, although not many naval operations in the Black Sea.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Courtney, let`s go back to the drone itself because as you just said, whatever might have been useful in terms of intelligence on it was disabled very quickly. We did hear yesterday at that Pentagon briefing, there may not be frankly much left of it and what is there, is very deep. Having said that, what is the latest on the search for this drone for whatever might be left of it?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: It doesn`t seem like there`s really going to be any effort by the U.S. military to go and get it. Of course, it`s always a priority to get any of their equipment that goes down. But you need to take a step back and look at where this went.\par \par \uc2 Number one, it`s a very deep area. As General Twitty was just explaining this is a militarized area as well. The Russians believe that they control the Black Sea. So -- and the reality -- the only way to get U.S. ships in there, any military ships salvage ships that they would if they even wanted to, would be through the Bosphorus Strait. So, you can kind of see it there on this map on the lower left-hand side.\par \par \uc2 The Turkish government controls that. They have been very hesitant to allow warships to transit the Bosphorus Strait in and out of the Black Sea in recent months. So, that in and of itself would be a difficult -- a difficult endeavor. But not just that, it would be potentially dangerous for any U.S. military ship to move in there because of the Russian military presence there.\par \par \uc2 So, the depth, the danger, it doesn`t seem likely that there`s going to be an effort here. Plus, as I said earlier, the debris field is pretty wide, so it`d be a difficult recovery effort, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We learned yesterday, General, that defense secretary Austin talked to his counterpart the -- from the Ministry of Defense. We also know that General Milley was trying to do the same. So, what happens in these conversations? Is it essentially the same kind of messaging we saw at that Pentagon briefing, we`re going to do what we can do, as under international law if we`re going to fly over international waters, we`re going to do it you`re not going to stop us, or is there some sort of dance that goes on in these kinds of conversations?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: No, there`s no dance. It`s pretty direct. And I know Secretary Austin pretty well. He used to be my boss. And I really know General Milley extremely well. He used to be my boss. And believe me, they make sure that U.S. equities are clear and loud when they talk to their counterparts here.\par \par \uc2 And so, I don`t see any dance. And I`m quite sure they`ve made it clear that the U.S. will continue to fly in the Black Sea. And so, you can continue to expect that things like this will continue to happen. And in my view, every single day that this war continues, we stand the chance of a mishap like this, and that`s why it`s critical to keep these lines of communication open between the secretary and his counterpart, and General Miller and his counterpart.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Lieutenant General Steph Twitty, always great to have you on the program. Courtney Kube, amazing reporting over the last couple of days. We really appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, we`re also watching the markets this hour as sell-offs at two more major banks, one in the U.S., one abroad, stoking fears of a growing financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 California-based \uc2 First Republic Bank\uc2 , their stocks plummeted today as investors try to identify what regional banks might be at risk after the swift collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  last Friday. Overseas, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 , long-considered a weak link among European banks was forced to borrow billions from the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  just to stay in business. Those twin shocks setting off a turbulent day on Wall Street and on Capitol Hill where the Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  tried to reassure lawmakers and the nation that we`re not headed toward an economic meltdown.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , U.S. TREASURY SECRETARY: I can reassure the members of the committee that our banking system is sound and that Americans can feel confident that their deposits will be there when they need them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC News Senior Business Analyst Stephanie Ruhle joins us now with what you need to know. I mean, look. A lot of people hear banking failures, and they immediately think of 2008 those of us old enough to remember it. How concerned should we all be right now?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC SENIOR BUSINESS ANALYST: OK. 