our future looks like?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, listen. When you hear about a big European bank like \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  having these troubles, that makes people panic. People need to understand. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  has been in trouble for years and years.\par \par \uc2 They`ve had many, many management changes. They basically spun off or shut down lots of core businesses. And their main business, which is private wealth management, managing the money of super-wealthy people based out of Switzerland has been losing clients steadily over the last few years. And I`m not exaggerating when I say every possible scandal, a company could be in, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  has experienced over the last few years, in addition to huge management changes.\par \par \uc2 Now, they just got this huge injection of cash from the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 , which is good. However, how long is it going to last them? Their big problem is that rich people have taken their money to other wealth managers. And it sounds like they`re going to suddenly flood back in. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  is not out of the water.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, Stephanie Ruhle, always great to have you on the program.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, of course, you can see Stephanie on the "11th Hour" right here on MSNBC and it actually airs in the 11th hour on the East Coast. Thanks, Steph.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tries to rattle \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  with potential indictments on the way for the former president in multiple high-profile cases. An abortion battle heating up in Amarillo with a judge`s high-stakes decision set to come down potentially any minute now or any day now. The latest from inside that courtroom, ahead.\par \par \uc2 Plus, steering you in the wrong direction. Why doctors are now warning against using a tool that many of us depend on, on a daily basis? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING Reports only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Anticipation is through the roof over whether former President Trump will be indicted in two high-profile criminal investigations, decisions that could come any day now and have the potential to dramatically shape the unfolding 2024 presidential campaign. For Trump, it`s kind of a split screen. On one side, you`ve got Stormy Daniels who`s now offering to testify in the hush money case, as well as the Georgia election interference probe, new reporting of a phone call from Trump to then-State House Speaker \uc2 David Ralston\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the other side, you`ve got his campaign, once again, being fueled by what the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  calls dark apocalyptic things. And even beyond those verbal bombs, Trump`s Make America Great Again super PAC is doling out legal pressure filing a 15-page complaint against \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  with the \uc2 Florida Ethics Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard is outside Mar-a-Lago in West Palm Beach, Florida. Also with us, Ashley Parker, Washington Post`s senior national political correspondent, and an MSNBC political analyst. MSNBC legal analyst, Lisa Rubin is with me here on set. And Tara Setmayer is Senior Advisor for the Lincoln Project, as well as a former Republican communications director. Great to have all of you here. A lot to cover.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn, let me start with your insights into this kind of split-screen moment for the former president and his campaign diving into 2024. Yet frankly, at the same time facing the unknown from New York and Atlanta investigations.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Right. And let`s be frank about this, Chris. There could be an indictment issued from either the Manhattan District Attorney`s Office or Fulton County District Attorney`s office at any minute, any hour, any day, maybe that indictment never actually comes. And ultimately, grand jury deems that there is not sufficient evidence to bring charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That is what we are waiting for here outside of Mar-a-Lago as we speak.\par \par \uc2 But when you`re looking at this from Donald Trump`s point of view. Right now, he is a top GOP polling across the fields here. And he is facing not just one investigation, two investigations, he`s facing also, you know, a lawsuit about claims of rape.\par \par \uc2 He is facing -- his company is facing a civil lawsuit from the New York Attorney General`s office as we speak in the Trump organization in which their claims of the -- of an inflation of the valuation of the assets of his family company. There is a steady stream of difficult cases that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is staring at here in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 And just here in the last couple of minutes, he put on his social media account, you know, calling attention to one particular prosecutor on this Manhattan District Attorney`s team, who had previously worked as a number three in the Department of Justice under the Biden administration, who also before going to the DOJ had worked in the New York Attorney General`s case working on that civil probe of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . Of course, there is no evidence that has been presented by the former presidents of wrongdoing. Prosecutors are able to go between agencies as they will. But this for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to be an effort to turn these cases -- potential cases on their head and make these investigations into him, an effort to call attention to the Democratic efforts to try to undermine his political ambitions in what he says is the MAGA movement.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Nothing in his messaging and the politics of this surprises anybody, Lisa. But we keep hearing -- well, it could be imminent -- an indictment could be an imminent. In fact, a decision is more accurate, right, a decision. But could it be imminent in either of these cases?\par \par \uc2 LISA RUBIN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: I think it could be. Obviously, yesterday, hearing that Stormy Daniels went in to meet with Manhattan prosecutors, and I should clarify she didn`t go anywhere. Our reporting indicates that she was in Florida and spoke to Manhattan DA office prosecutors by zoom. That adds an additional wrinkle because folks generally understood \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  to be the ultimate witness in this case. And what needs to happen next is perhaps there will be a final meeting with Trump`s team. And then they will ask the grand jury to vote a bill of indictment.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The fact that they brought in Stormy Daniels, does that suggest to you that they needed her, they didn`t have enough, or what does it suggest to you?\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: It suggests to me that maybe there are some questions that needed answering, maybe a discrepancy between two witnesses that they wanted to resolve. But Stormy Daniels didn`t communicate directly with either \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about the settlement. All of those communications were handled through her lawyer at the time, \uc2 Keith Davidson\uc2 . We understand Mr. Davidson has been a grand jury witness. And so, it`s not clear to me that they need her at all to establish the case that many of us understand they`re looking to make.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you about the Atlanta case because the AJC, Atlanta Journal-Constitution talk with five grand jurors which is fascinating. They say they heard a recording of a phone call between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Georgia House Speaker -- the late Georgia House Speaker \uc2 David Ralston\uc2  in which the president asked him to convene a special session of the legislature to overturn those election results -- 2020 election results in Georgia. How significant could that be on tape?\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: I think it`s incredibly significant because many people understood that the Fulton County investigation really focused on the fake electors. We understand that all of the Georgia Trump electors received target letters, for example, at one point.\par \par \uc2 But it goes beyond the fake electors. The scheme to overturn the election had many facets. One of them was to try and send these fake electors to Congress and have them substitute. But then when that didn`t succeed, the next step was to try and get \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to not accept the electors and then to send it back to the state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 But that would have required cooperation from people like \uc2 David Ralston\uc2 , right, to convene a special session of the Georgia legislature and vote again and then send those electors back to Congress. So, I think it shows just the pure obstinacy of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  trying every which way, like a mouse caught in a building, right, trying to find any escape hatch that he could.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And so, we`re going to wait and see either of those cases. But in the meantime, Ashley, you`ve been writing about his legal woes generally. And how you say, this is a perilous moment for him. In what way?\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PARKER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: So, it`s perilous, both legally and politically, right? You did a great job already in this segment outlining the various potential charges and legal issues he may be facing. Politically, you have \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who is really gaining a lot of traction. Still not necessarily the front-runner in a Republican primary, but really worrying former President Trump and his team.\par \par \uc2 But there is a big but there. Despite all of sort of it can feel like and you can lay out the case that the walls are closing in on him, when you talk to people both on his team and outside Republican strategists, some who like him, some who don`t, they say that, especially on the legal front, these sorts of attacks coming at him are the types of things that in a Republican primary, can actually help him. Because when voters feel that he is being attacked and especially if they believe it is an unfair attack, a political attack, a witch hunt, as he himself has desperately tried to message it, it makes them more likely to support him.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, this is problematic in a general election because this is sort of the noise, the cast, the controversy that exhausts more moderate Republicans, independent voters, suburban voters, swing voters. But in a primary situation, counterintuitively, it`s something that can be politically helpful.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. We`ve talked a lot on this program, Tara, about kind of the exhaustion factor, and especially for those moderate voters. I`ve talked to tons of suburban women voters who, in 2020, just told me they were just too tired that Trump had essentially worn them out, even though they might have voted for him in 2016. But if the mood of the country is gloomy and he`s negative, could that work? I mean, he obviously is not changing his messaging. And I want to obviously talk to you a little bit more about this, Ashley, because you wrote about it, but an awful lot of the Republican Party is on that bandwagon.\par \par \uc2 TARA SETMAYER, SENIOR ADVISOR, LINCOLN PROJECT: Right. It`s obvious. As much as the establishment Republicans would like to move on and think that somehow miraculously, indictments will make \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  go away, they`re clearly deluding themselves. Trump is continuing to lead in the polls, as you just showed. He has been laying the predicate for the victimization defense for years now with his base, which still makes up an overwhelming majority of Republican primary voters. And you cannot get to a general unless you go through the primary.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who they seem to think is going to be their savior and step in and replace Trump if for some reason he`s no longer able to run if he`s convicted because indictments matter. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has demonstrated that he`s not up to the task either, making a huge mistake with his territorial dispute comment about Ukraine, riling up the establishment going, oh my gosh, what are we doing here? This guy is not ready for prime time.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  here is still in the driver`s seat. He is the dominant force. He is the front-runner. And any of these legal issues that are facing him will not make a dent in his support for with primary voters going into the election in 2024. They -- it`s the -- I can -- I can shoot someone on Fifth Avenue crowd that still fully embraces him.\par \par \uc2 And are people exhausted? Sure, they are. At the Lincoln Project, we call those folks the baton line voters. The four to seven or eight to ten percent now of Republicans that are looking for a permission structure to walk away from the party because of that exhaustion. But you know, they are not going to make a difference in the primary. That only matters in the general.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`ve only got a minute left, Ashley, but I do want to ask you about your reporting today about how much of the 2024 Republican field has been focusing on these dark, apocalyptic themes following Trump`s lead. I guess the big question is, whatever happened to the optimism of \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , the hope of Clinton and Obama, is it all dead?\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Well, that`s a -- that`s a pointed number of Republicans made to me that initially in the Reagan years, things did not look great, but it was that hope, that optimism, that vision for a future that really helped pull the nation forward and defined Republican politics for decades. But you look at someone like Trump who says you know I am your retribution, if you look down the field, you have people like Nikki Haley and Tim Scott, generally optimistic Republican politicians following suit, and it does seem to be dead, at least for now in this moment.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Vaughn Hillyard, Ashley Parker, Tara Setmayer, thank you. Lisa Rubin, you`re going to stay with me.\par \par \uc2 But I want to show you these live pictures from Paris. These are protesters. Thousands of them taking to the streets over the decision to raise France`s retirement age.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 Wait until you hear the response from some lawmakers there. We`ve got it coming up.\par \par \uc2 And from chief of staff to on the lam. A federal manhunt underway for former Maryland Governor Larry Hogan`s ex-chief of staff. The laundry list of charges he`s facing and where is he. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Big protests have erupted in France over President Macron`s controversial move today to increase the country`s retirement age. Look at this crowd, look at the police barricade. A session of parliament had literally erupted into chaos. Lawmakers booing the prime minister. They sang the national anthem in protest over Macron`s last-minute decision to avoid a vote, instead forcing through his unpopular proposal to raise the retirement age from 62 to 64.\par \par \uc2 This plan has sparked major strikes all across the country since January and is expected to trigger a no-confidence moment in Macron`s government.\par \par \uc2 We`re keeping our eye on what`s happening in the streets of Paris.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, we`ve got a wild story out of Maryland, where a one-time aide to former Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  is now the target of an interstate manhunt.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 Officials put out a wanted poster, here it is, for Roy McGrath after he didn`t show up in federal court on Monday. He`s facing a number of fraud and embezzlement charges, and his lawyer told the judge he doesn`t even know where McGrath is.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get right to NBC News senior political editor \uc2 Mark Murray\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So lay out these charges against McGrath for us, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MURRAY\uc2 , NBC NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL EDITOR: Yes, Chris. McGrath was actually indicted two years ago. And the main allegation against him was that he ended up asking for a severance payment that he said was from -- a permission from governor -- then Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  when McGrath was leaving the Maryland Environmental Services, his job as executive director, to become Hogan`s chief of staff.\par \par \uc2 Now Hogan and the Justice Department have actually said that that was false. He ended up having Hogan`s permission. And that is the central charge against him, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There`s new reporting I`ve seen that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raided his home in Florida. I assume that`s related to the manhunt. I also know he`s married. Do we know if officials have talked to his wife. What else can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Yes, Chris, our reporters and producers who cover the Justice Department confirmed that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  did raid his Florida home yesterday. And "The Baltimore Sun" ended up reporting that the government also seizing the phone of McGrath`s wife. And so the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has been looking all over this now for the past several days. What still remains striking is that no one has yet to find Mr. McGrath.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. We`re going to keep our eyes and ears on that investigation and the manhunt.\par \par \uc2 Mark Murray\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 The high-intensity wait out on the Amarillo courthouse. What will the Texas judge rule in that big abortion case. That story next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:59]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now in Amarillo a decision may be imminent in that big Texas abortion case that has major implications for abortion access from coast to coast. In a consequential hearing that lasted four hours, a conservative Trump-appointed judge appeared sympathetic to antiabortion arguments. And it comes as states continue to impose their own limits. Last night Utah`s governor signed a law that would in effect ban abortion clinics.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Dasha Burns has been covering the case for us in Amarillo, Texas. Lisa Rubin is back with me.\par \par \uc2 Dasha, you were in the courtroom for much of the time that those arguments were being made. Do we have any clues first of all as to when the judge might announce his decision? Do we have any, I don`t know, history that tells us how long he usually takes?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, unfortunately, I don`t have a satisfying answer for you on that one. There have been a lot of unexpected twist and turns in this case already, so we don`t know exactly what to expect. He could rule by the end of the week. It could take him a few weeks to make a decision on this.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you in that courtroom yesterday, the subject of the hearing was the preliminary injunction requested by the plaintiffs in this case. So at the heart of this case is the drug mifepristone. It is the first drug in a two-drug regimen for medication abortion which, by the way, is the most common abortion method in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The plaintiffs, which is a group of antiabortion medical organizations and doctors, claim that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn`t adequately evaluate the safety of the drug when they approved it back in 2000. They also argue that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  endangered women further by loosening regulations around the drug in 2016 and 2021, particularly in `21 when they allowed providers to prescribe medication abortion via telemedicine.\par \par \uc2 Now the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and the government lawyers said in court yesterday that if the judge sides with the plaintiff it would amount to unprecedented action. It would cause significant public harm. They emphasized that this a drug that`s been on the market for 20-plus years, and according to the \uc2 FDA\uc2  data, more than five million women have taken this drug with only 28 deaths associated with it since its approval.\par \par \uc2 Of course folks on both sides of this issue are watching this case very closely because it could be so consequential nationally. Take a listen to some of the conversations we had outside the court yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O`LEARY CARMONA, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, WOMEN`S MARCH: The impact for what happened here today will be felt by all Americans no matter what your feelings are on abortion. It`s a very dangerous attack on our public health system as a country and everybody needs to be concerned.\par \par \uc2 DR. INGRID SKOP, DIRECTOR OF MEDICAL AFFAIRS, CHARLOTTE LOZIER INSTITUTE: I think this is an opportunity for the American people to see the evidence on chemical abortion. The politicized process has been such that over time the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has taken away restrictions and made this much more dangerous for women.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: So two things could happen with the judge`s decision here whenever he does rule. One, he could fully side with the plaintiffs on that preliminary injunction and compel the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to pull this drug off of the market nationwide. Again, while the ruling would be here in Texas from one judge, it would impact even states that have robust abortion protections in place.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that could happen is he could issue a more narrow ruling potentially addressing those changes that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  made like the telehealth option, restricting the drug a little bit further. But again, whatever he does, if he does rule in favor of the plaintiffs, it would have an impact far beyond just Texas, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And if that is what happens, Lisa, then what`s next?\par \par \uc2 Appeals. Let`s start there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Long appeals, a long road ahead?\par \par \uc2 LISA RUBIN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, I think it depends on whether the courts to which this -- I`m sorry this rule is appealed expedite those appeals, right? And I think that anybody who wants to side with Judge Kacsmaryk is of course not going to want to expedite those appeals, not going to want to stay his ruling, if in fact he orders the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to withdraw or suspend mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Are there bigger implications here? Not to in anyway, you know, minimize what the impact would be on access on abortion, but if a judge can overrule the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , if a judge can say, I`m going to substitute my judgment for the science that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  bases its own rulings on, could they do it for any kind of medication?\par \par \uc2 RUBIN: Absolutely. And I think that`s one of the biggest concerns here. This is a drug that has been safely on the market for 23 years. And if they can do it here, as you said, Chris, they can do it and a number of other instances. Vice President Harris earlier in the week actually talked about this, and she said look inside your medicine cabinet.\par \par \uc2 In that medicine cabinet inevitably is prescription medication or over-the- counter medication approved by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 . If they don`t have the ultimate say over what is safe, effective, and legal we are all in danger.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In Utah, let me ask about the state law. The governor there signed a law last night that will ban clinics from providing abortion clinics. The A.P. reports that it adds, quote, "a layer of uncertainty on top of fear that if clinics closed patients may not be able to access care at hospitals because of the variety of staffing and cost concerns."\par \par \uc2 And in fact, when some hospitals, Planned Parenthood were asked what they were going to do, they are not ready right now. They could not say what the plan was. So -- not just in Utah, but in red states where they`re passing these kinds of laws. Nobody seems to know how to keep themselves protected legally or even necessarily what the details of the law mean for them. Is that where 