s been following the story for us.\par \par \uc2 Marissa, give us the little bit of the background. What do we know about this case?\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, you can see, as they walked up for the first time just within the last couple of minutes, how emotional they are. And one thing that we were told is there is video that exists of the last and final moments of Otieno. And our understanding is that the family watched the surveillance video before walking up here so, clearly a very emotional family standing up there right now.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll bring you a summary once we have a chance to listen but to give you a little bit of background here on how this all unfolded, the timeline is important here, March 3rd, police were called about an alleged burglary. And this involved Otieno. He was placed under an emergency custody order, allegedly assaulted Henrico police officers at a different hospital, was taken to a local jail.\par \par \uc2 And then it was during this transfer between a different hospital to the jail and then from that jail to a different hospital, that was when his final moments occurred. And so that video that we were talking about, this was a focal point during a court hearing yesterday. The commonwealth`s attorney made a point to talk at great length about this video, something that we have not seen for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 We do hope that that will be released to the public soon. So all of this coming from the commonwealth`s attorney here who says that the video shows Otieno, face down, handcuffed on the ground with a knee from one of the deputies on his body at one point. She says they smothered him for 12 minutes causing him to suffocate, adding that the preliminary cause of death was asphyxiation, says the video is, quote, "clear and alarming," and also claims that after he died you can see the deputies took over three hours to call Virginia state police.\par \par \uc2 So as we mentioned, this is not 