tition, she has only a map and compass, no GPS allowed.\par \par \uc2 BANISTER: OK. I can see the flag.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: This is orienteering, and for 73-year-old Kathy the stakes are much higher than who wins the race.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Are you doing this for fun? Are you doing this for exercise?\par \par \uc2 BANISTER: I love it.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Are you doing this to keep your brain healthy? All the above?\par \par \uc2 BANISTER: All of the above. I do it for exercise. I do it because I love the puzzle, solving the puzzle.\par \par \uc2 TORRES (voice-over): New research from \uc2 McMaster University\uc2  in Canada shows solving that puzzle is part of keeping her brain healthy. Scientists have found that using GPS does too much brain work for us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They`re extremely convenient, but they take the thinking out of it. They take the brain effort out of it. And it may be doing us a disservice in the long run.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Losing navigational skills can lead to cognitive decline, even dementia, but reading a map stimulates the hippocampus. The part of the brain responsible for memory, navigation and mental mapping.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: By turning off the GPS and using a map instead to navigate through unfamiliar routes, we`re training that part of the brain and it`s less likely to decline.\par \par \uc2 TORRES (on-camera): People who participate in orienteering report better spatial navigation and memory, suggesting that by turning off your GPS and instead relying on a map could be beneficial to your aging brain.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230317ej3g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for March 15, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,216 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JANAI NELSON, PRESIDENT AND DIRECTOR-COUNSEL, NAACP LEGAL DEFENSE FUND: But I do think that there is enough constitutional doctrine to force the court to do the right thing. And the Moore versus Harper case should be determined. And the Merrill versus Milligan case should be determined the right way by these members of the court.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Let`s see if that happens. Evan Milligan and Janai Nelson, thank you both very much. I appreciate you. And that is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN. The Trump- appointed judge in Texas now deciding abortion pill access for the entire country. We`ll have the latest from today`s hearing and Amarillo. Then, Congresswoman \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  on the failure of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , and new fears of a global banking crisis. Plus --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP FIXER: At the end of the day, I complied with everything, and now it`s in the hands of our district attorney Alvin Gregg and his competent team.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The possible indictment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the Stormy Daniels investigation. And the arrest of Chinese billionaire who bankrolled \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2 , TRUMP ADVISER: Looking so great, so great. Thank you, Miles.\par \par \uc2 GUO WENGUI, CHINESE BUSINESSMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BANNON: Love you.\par \par \uc2 WENGUI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Today, the fate of millions of American women`s reproductive health is once again in the hands of a far-right judge. This morning, Federal Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk of the Northern District of Texas provided over hearing in the small city of Amarillo. A coalition of anti-abortion groups brought this case seeking to overturn the Food and Drug Administration`s approval of a medication commonly used in abortions.\par \par \uc2 This drug has been on the market for more than 20 years. It has been proven to be safe and effective. In fact, millions of women take it every year. It is taken along with a second medication to end pregnancy in the first trimester, which is the method used by more than half of all women who have abortions in this country.\par \par \uc2 Of course, this comes in the wake of the anti-abortion movement success in overturning the right to an abortion enshrined for decades in Roe vs. Wade. So, abortion is now illegal under a near complete ban in 13 states, seriously limited in five more. And medication abortion is even more important in those areas with restrictions or limited resources because it is easier to access.\par \par \uc2 But now, it is possible that this one judge could pull this drug off the shelves, not just in those states where they`ve banned abortion, across the country, everywhere. With a stroke of his pen, he could conceivably cut off access to every woman and pregnant person in a nation of 300 million people. Now, you may be wondering, how is that possibly the case? And also, why is all this happening in Amarillo, Texas of all places? And those are good questions.\par \par \uc2 Because the first thing you have to understand is the judge in question here is a product and what Senate Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and Donald Trump`s most successful projects during Trump`s four years in office. Together, they rammed through more than 200 new judges on the federal bench, many of whom are just obviously an avowedly right-wing. Judge Kacsmaryk is no exception.\par \par \uc2 At 45 years old, Kacsmaryk already has a long history of right-wing conservative and anti-abortion beliefs. He led the College Republicans the very conservative \uc2 Abilene Christian University\uc2 , where he wrote in the school newspaper, "The Democratic Party`s ability to condone the federally sanctioned eradication of innocent human life is indicative of the moral ambivalence undergirding this party." That`s the guy that heard the arguments today.\par \par \uc2 During law school, University of Texas, Kacsmaryk focus his attention on the legal foundations for abortion rights and attended meetings of the conservative \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2 . In 2014, he went to work for a conservative legal group called The First Liberty Institute, defending clients like the Christian owners of a bakery who refuse to bake a wedding cake for a same-sex couple.\par \par \uc2 The following year, he became a trustee on the board of an anti-abortion organization that offers housing and adoption services to women facing unexpected pregnancies. Judge Kacsmaryk`s sister told \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , his beliefs are strongly held and there`s no reason to doubt that. "He`s very passionate about the fact that you can`t preach pro-life and do nothing." Again, that`s his sister who shares his views.\par \par \uc2 Then, President Donald Trump`s first nominated Kacsmaryk to the federal bench in September of 2017. Democrats understandably staunchly opposed his confirmation and managed to hold it off for nearly two years. They argued that he was unfit for the role based on his anti-LGBTQ and anti-abortion beliefs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 PATTY MURRAY\uc2  (D-WA): Not only are his views on non-discrimination based on sexual orientation and gender identity concerning, I am also very alarmed by his record as a fervent crusader against women`s reproductive rights, and for the far-right position that someone else`s ideology is more important than a woman`s ability to make her own personal medical decisions.\par \par \uc2 If his arguments had won the day in court, a woman trying to get contraceptive care could face barriers thrown up at her by her employer or even by her pharmacy, all because someone else thinks that their beliefs matter more than a woman`s own personal decisions about her own healthcare.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:05:29]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 , who you saw there and other opponents of Kacsmaryk`s nomination also pointed to his writings in which he has described being transgender as a "mental disorder," called homosexuality "disordered," and said that sexual revolutionaries had made the unborn child and marriage secondary to erotic desires of liberated adults.\par \par \uc2 So, just take a second. There`s no like pretending here, right? We`re well past umpire balls and strikes. We know exactly who this guy is and what he believes. Everyone does, across both sides, right? That`s why they push to get him on the bench and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  persisted. He renominated Kacsmaryk two more times and Senate Republicans, again, led by \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the whole Republican Party, this is not my MAGA thing, finally succeeded and confirming him in June of 2019.\par \par \uc2 OK, so he`s a federal district judge and he takes up his lifetime post in Amarillo. It`s up in the Texas Panhandle. And there`s hundreds of federal district judges, so you know, in the grand scheme of things, doesn`t seem like the biggest deal in the world except, except, except that Northern District, he is the only federal district judge there in Amarillo, OK. And that brings us to why this case today is in Amarillo at all.\par \par \uc2 It turns out that if you`re a party in a lawsuit who wants a hard right- wing outcome, particularly on these kinds of issues, all you got to do is find some plausible reason to bring it to Judge Kacsmaryk in the Northern District of Texas. Find some way to file it in Amarillo. In fact, the Republican attorney general of Texas, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , has done this several times.\par \par \uc2 Ken Paxton`s office is right there. It`s in downtown Austin, so close to the Austin Federal Courthouse that he could stroll over there in less than 20 minutes to file motions. But when \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  really needs the right outcome, he doesn`t go there. Instead, he goes all the way up to Amarillo, a nearly eight-hour drive northwest of Austin to file his suit, specifically in Judge Kacsmaryk`s district where he knows Judge Kacsmaryk will hear it.\par \par \uc2 It`s essentially a legal loophole. Conservatives have basically hacked the judicial system to create this tiny MAGA jurisdiction with a judge who they know will rule on what they file and will favorably be relied upon to do what they want. And so far, this loophole is working out quite well for them.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , Judge, Kacsmaryk has delivered several wins for the right including one that struck down new Biden administration protections for transgender people. Another that forced thousands of asylum seekers to return to Mexico while they waited processing. That one was particularly rich. I mean, that was him declaring from Amarillo, Texas federal immigration policy for the whole nation, the whole border. That asylum ruling, by the way, was overturned by the Supreme Court eventually.\par \par \uc2 Late last year Judge Kacsmaryk also sided with a Christian father who did not want his daughters to access birth control without his permission, challenging a federal program that provides low-cost or free contraception, including to teens without parental consent.\par \par \uc2 That`s the guy who`s ruling on this today. That`s the guy in whose hands the fate of medication abortion in this country currently rests. So, I think there`s a few lessons here. One is that there is still a lot of work to be done to undo all the damage that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , and the entire Republican Party pulled off over the course of four years.\par \par \uc2 It`s also clear criminalizing all abortion, going after contraception everywhere for every woman, and every pregnant person is going to remain a major priority for the conservative movement in the Republican Party. And the thing this situation shows us is how they must be held accountable politically because Judge Kacsmaryk holds his seat for life. He doesn`t have to care about politics. That`s the whole point of getting him in there. That`s why McConnell and Trump expanded the political capital of putting him there. Republican politicians, however, should have to own every single decision that comes from him.\par \par \uc2 I can guarantee you \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has enough political savvy and instincts to want nothing to do with this case. But the outcome, like the reversal of Roe itself and sustained assault on reproductive freedoms nationwide is on him, Trump, and the Republican Party all the way, no question.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now outside the courthouse in Amarillo by NPR National Correspondent Sarah McCammon. She`s among the small few to witness this crucial hearing inside the courthouse today. Sarah, thank you for making time for us today. First, just tell us what the arguments inside that court -- that courthouse was like -- well, like and how the judge, Judge Kacsmaryk seemed to be receptive to either side.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:09]\par \par \uc2 SARAH MCCAMMON, NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT, NPR: Well, first of all, it was witnessed by a pretty small group. Only a few dozen people can fit inside the courtroom here. So, that meant limited access for both press -- the press and the public. And I should mention, you know, this courtroom does not allow recording, the judge kept that rule in place. So, there was no recording, no cameras, and also the judge denied a request from a government watchdog group to livestream the proceedings to the public.\par \par \uc2 So, it was really witnessed by a small group of people. And what I saw in there was basically the plaintiff, which is a group of anti-abortion medical practitioners and organizations who have filed a lawsuit here in Amarillo, challenging the FDA`s approval of Mifepristone. As you know, that drug has been on the market for more than 20 years. The group laid out their arguments against it, and they boiled down to criticisms of the approval process dating back to when Mifepristone was first put on the market in the year 2000.\par \par \uc2 On the other side, we heard from lawyers representing the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , the Department of Justice lawyers on their behalf, who argued that this drug has been in widespread use for two decades plus now. It has a very strong safety record, according to major medical organizations.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There was you mentioned this before, and I think it`s worth highlighting this. In very small number of people, you`re one of the few who got -- who got to listen. We know that this was intentional because there was a scoop by \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  that Judge Kacsmaryk took the unusual step of intentionally delaying public notice of the hearing planned for Wednesday morning in Amarillo. That would later come out.\par \par \uc2 But the idea he said was he told people was going to be -- he didn`t want to getting out. He didn`t want a lot of people. He expressed security or safety concerns. But in the end, the sort of close held in this was intentional on the part of Judge Kacsmaryk. How would you describe his demeanor, his questions, in the course of the hearing today?\par \par \uc2 MCCAMMON: Yes, Chris, he asked a lot of questions. That was one thing I noticed. And he asked questions of both sides. I did notice he seemed to sort of recap the positions, the arguments of particularly of the plaintiffs, just seeming to try to understand what they were saying. And a lot of his questions had to do with sort of what he should do next, or what he could do next.\par \par \uc2 He asked both lawyers for both sides, you know, essentially, if I were to rule in favor of the plaintiffs of these, you know, anti-abortion advocates in some way, what should that look like? Should that look like a wholesale removal of the drugs from the market or perhaps some other half-measure?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Sarah McCammon who is in that courthouse, one of the few today, joining us live from Amarillo, Texas tonight. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn now to Michele Goodwin, who is a professor of law at the \uc2 University of California in Irvine\uc2 , author of Policing The Womb: Invisible Women And The Criminalization Of Motherhood. She works specifically on abortion rights, reproductive freedom, and health care law as well. It`s great to have you on.\par \par \uc2 First, I want to just start with to me this sort of -- take a step back before we get to the law, the sort of manifest and preposterous pretextual nature of this whole thing. I mean, the plaintiffs didn`t suddenly become very concerned with administrative law procedure as it regards drug approval. It`s that this drug is now more important than ever in a poster landscape and they want to get rid of it because they don`t want women exercising reproductive choice because they hate abortion, right? That`s what this boils down to.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE GOODWIN, PROFESSOR OF LAW, \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA IRVINE\uc2 : Well, it`s death of abortion by 1000 strikes post-Dobbs. So, we see Dobbs June 24, 2022, overturning Roe v. Wade in Planned Parenthood v. Casey. And despite the fact that Justice Alito in the majority opinion and Justice Kavanaugh in the concurring opinion said, well, there are guardrails in place. Other areas of privacy won`t be affected. This is an issue that is just about the states. What we see in this litigation is that it`s not just about the states, because what we have are litigants that are seeking this particular judge to issue a nationwide ban on the distribution of this particular drug.\par \par \uc2 And as you`ve already noted, this is a very safe drug. And what they`re using is unfounded rhetoric, for example, that this was a rushed approval process through the \uc2 FDA\uc2  when in fact it took over 54 months of \uc2 FDA\uc2  review. When by comparison the year that this drug came to the market, other drugs are under review for about 15 months. So, there`s a lot of sophistry here and a lot of strategy.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. So -- right, so it`s technically about the -- what they say is the rush process. I think there`s good reason to think that`s pretextual. There`s also, of course, the forum shopping issue. And we should say, this is something that "both sides do," that people seek out judges and jurisdictions they think are going to be more likely to be favorable.\par \par \uc2 But I`ll quote from a piece in Slate by Dennis Aftergut and Laurence Tribe, they say, what`s happening here is not mere judge shopping, as many have called it. That term is traditionally used to describe an individual`s search for nearby court inclined to rule favorably. By contrast, here, there`s a coordinated national strategy enabled by a district court federal bench to bring right-wing legal causes into a single courtroom where a favorable result is a sure thing and where fair-minded appellate review has also been hijacked. Do you think that`s too harsh or is that fair?\par \par \uc2 [20:15:34]\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN\uc2 : Oh, it`s not too harsh. And I think that it is absolutely right as you say that the left does this and the right does this. During the Trump administration, there were those who didn`t want kids trapped in cages that didn`t think there should be a Muslim ban, who also sought judges to issue nationwide bans against the Trump administration doing things that were unconstitutional, and most often they won, even before Republican-appointed judges.\par \par \uc2 But what we see here is something that is incredibly dangerous, because with trying to rip up the rug of abortion rights, what we see is that it makes getting the kind of proper care that women need, and people with the capacity for pregnancy, very difficult. And what we already see in Texas is five women who filed a lawsuit because of near-death experiences where they weren`t able to get the kind of care that they needed. And that`s an atrocity itself. And it`s worth spending some time to talk about that if we get to it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Please continue. I mean, this is a place where those tangible and effects are pronounced and apparent already.\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN\uc2 : Yes. Well, so let`s talk about what it looks like post-Dobbs and what that decision opens for this court and for others. We see that there are women who are being forced to gestate fetuses that have no skulls, even when it means that these women, their life is at risk, such as in the litigation in Texas. We see in South Carolina where members of that legislature are proposing the death penalty for women who are seeking abortion.\par \par \uc2 And behind all of this, Chris, as you`ve talked about previously on your show, the United States is a deadly place to be pregnant. The Supreme Court conceded in 2016 that a woman is 14 times more likely to die by carrying a pregnancy to term than by having an abortion. We know there are racial implications with Black women being four times more likely than their White counterparts to suffer death during pregnancy, and high rates of maternal morbidity. That`s the factual backdrop, while there are those still seeking to infringe on the ability to be able to terminate a pregnancy despite very high maternal mortality and morbidity rates in the United States.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And on that note, and I`ll just read this in closing, because I think it`s a particularly perverse aspect of the plaintiffs argument when arguing for standing of the plaintiffs who some of whom are OBGYNs. One of the arguments they make is that they have -- they have injury, they have standing an injury, because when their patients have chemical abortions, doctors lose the opportunity to provide professional services and care for women and child through pregnancy. It causes harm to providers who can no longer care for their patients and bring about successful delivery of a new life. It also means they cannot build for the costs of medical services. This is an actual argument actually found in the brief by the plaintiffs about why they should be able to get standing in that court.\par \par \uc2 Michele Goodwin, it`s always good to hear from you. Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, the indictment watch is on as Donald Trump`s old lawyer spends another day in front of a New York grand jury. What happens now? Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Adult film actress Stormy Daniels spoke to prosecutors from the Manhattan District Attorney`s office today as the potential for an imminent indictment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  looms large. Daniels, you may remember, received more than $100,000 in hush money back in 2016 when she 