g, look, you know, this was just a nuisance payment. Trump is a famous guy who didn`t want to embarrass Melania. But the problem with that argument is, even if there`s a mixed motive, he`s still liable for the campaign side.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One of the things that`s interesting to me about that, that nugget, and I think they`ve made similar arguments they did on Ari`s show, is that for all the bravado, right -- so, Trump says, if you indict me, it`s only going to make me stronger and it`s ridiculous. They do not want to be indicted. I mean, it`s very clear that like, they`re working very hard to try to waive the D.A. office and to avoid an indictment.\par \par \uc2 LOWELL: Yes, they are 100 percent freaked out about the chance of this happening. Like, I cannot tell you the anxiety that has been going through the Trump legal team, you know, senior advisors, to Trump especially on the campaign. And on the -- on the kind of the legal side as well. They really don`t want to see Trump indicted. And as you say, there`s a lot of bravado, right? They think it will help the campaign, it will boost donations, whatnot. But at the end of the day, they really fear an indictment.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And part of that, I think, has to be the fact that they understand that they`ll now playing in a whole set of different rules, and they`re used to playing it, right? They`re used to civil law. They`re used to lawsuits. They`re used to settlements. They`re used to kicking the can. They used to the court of public opinion. A criminal indictment is just a completely different thing.\par \par \uc2 And no one -- I mean, Trump has never gotten his whole life, right, walking on the edge of law and somehow never experienced this.\par \par \uc2 LOWELL: Right. And now, Trump is having to brace for the first time for the possibility of an actual criminal indictment. And his lawyers were bracing this week. And I think when you get to Sunday, and you know, next Monday is the next time that this grand jury convenes, Trump is then going to be worrying, you know, am I going to be on the chopping block here. And this is something that he`s never faced before. And this is something that gives him real anxiety.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just finally on the timing. I mean, there`s some question about whether maybe Stormy Daniels comes in for the grand jury. We don`t know. But it was -- she was meeting with the D.A. today. Her lawyer putting out a statement saying she was cooperative. What is your -- from your reporting, your reporting closely on this, your expectation of what to look for timing-wise?\par \par \uc2 LOWELL: That`s a good question. I think, you know, we don`t really have a big picture of how this is going to proceed. You know, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  today testify for two hours. He came in before the 2:00 p.m. time that the grand jury convened. He spoke for two hours. He came out at 4:00. That was ostensibly some time for the grand jury to take, you know, potentially a vote on indictment. But it doesn`t sound like that happened today, which means the next time that could happen is Monday. It`s very much an indictment watch for everyone.\par \par \uc2 But you know, Cohen came out today and said, every single one of the jurors ask him a question, and that`s a sign of a really engaged jury. And that`s the kind of thing that I think the DA`s officer is going to look at. Because if you can have a really engaged jury at this stage, if it goes to trial, you have a really compelling witness.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Hugo Lowell, thank you so much for coming in.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, stocks tumble again amid concerns about another bank stability. This is a new one. Congresswoman \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  on what went wrong and what can be fixed next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The family of a protester who was shot and killed by police in Atlanta, Georgia is now suing the city in search for answers about their loved one`s death. The protester`s name is Manuel Esteban Paez Teran. He also went by the name Tortuguita. They`re a part of a group called the Forest Defenders. You may have heard about them. There`s been a group of folks that have been occupying a forest south of Atlanta. It`s actually in Atlanta, protesting the construction of an 85 acre police training facility in the South River Forest in Atlanta. Opponents have named at Cop City.\par \par \uc2 Police say they need to take over this area and erect a new facility for adequate training. Protesters argued the facility would damage the environment and lead to an increasingly militarized police force. One of the things they would do is sort of do SWAT teams sort of trainings in this place.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:03]\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 Georgia Bureau of Investigation\uc2  which has been probing the shooting says that during a protest, and there have been sustained protests, Teran open fire on police shooting and wounding an officer. The police returned fire killing Teran. The agency has said there`s no body cameras footage to corroborate this. They also say the evidence shows the trooper was shot with a bullet from a gun to Teran legally purchased.\par \par \uc2 Now, the claim that Teran shot a police officer has been hard to believe for a bunch of folks who knew him well, including a writer who covered him named Dave Peisner who profiled Tehran. Peisner says that while he doesn`t know what happened that night, Teran was an earnest, joyful person who once told him "The right kind of resistance is peaceful, because that`s where we win. We`re not going to beat them with violence. They`re very, very good at violence. We`re not. We win through non-violence."\par \par \uc2 So, that was the sort of story in the beginning. Then last month, a new development. The \uc2 Atlanta Police Department\uc2  released body camera footage of an officer who wasn`t involved in the shooting where you can hear him su