e implications of contagion and what could happen aside from what we experienced in here in the United States. We did a very good job of making sure that we shut down of Silicon Valley and Signature Bank. But what is the relationship, if any, I think we`ve got work to do to find out.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to ask you a question about the decision making process for the Fed and Treasury to take extraordinary actions to shore up and backstop some of the uninsured depositors in \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 . Now, I think, on the merits, there`s arguments for it, but the process that produced -- and I want to read you this reporting from York Times. There was a lot of lobbying behind the scenes and a lot of high up people, including many prominent Democrats.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:06]\par \par \uc2 Here`s an example. \uc2 Peter Orszag\uc2 , former President Barack Obama`s first budget director, now chief executive of financial advisory at the bank, \uc2 Lazard\uc2 , hosted a previously scheduled dinner at the bank`s offices in New York City`s Rockefeller Center. Among those in attendance were a pair of influential senators, \uc2 Mike Crapo\uc2 , Republican of Idaho, Mark Warner, Democratic of Virginia. Both were sponsors of the 2018 law that rolled back regulations on smaller banks. \uc2 Blair Effron\uc2 , a large Democratic donor whose firm had just been hired by \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  to advise it on its liquidity crunch, was also there.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to people who say they got special treatment because they were wired and connected to upper-echelon Democratic Party members?\par \par \uc2 WATERS: You know, I`ve heard that there are some allegations such as what you just described, but one of the things we have to think about, we have to think about the entire system and whether or not we`re at a point or at a time where we could basically cause what happened in 2008 when we discovered that not only had \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  failed, but it was contagious.\par \par \uc2 And so, the first thing we have to think about is who`s involved and who`s getting harmed. Those depositors deserve to be helped. And while we have rules that, you know, the \uc2 FDIC\uc2  only protects those at about 250,000 or less, we had this bank with about 90 percent that was uninsured. These were startup companies for the most part. And so, we could either say, well, we`re not worried about the jobs that have been lost, we don`t care about the payrolls that are not going to be met, but we didn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 As a matter of fact, I think you are correct when you say that maybe they deserved to be helped, but maybe some who are thinking that they got special treatment have some arguments too. Well, the fact of the matter is, I basically agree that we help out the families, we have to help the startups, we help out the workers, and we got those payrolls going, but that doesn`t say that we`re not going to go deep into the investigation just about how this bank operated.\par \par \uc2 We know it was a go-to bank for startups. They could not get loans in the traditional bank, and so he basically concentrated on this group of startups that were coming to him from all over the country to this bank. But we need to know what we`re going to do in the future about those two are not insured. Are we going to act in the same way? Was this a one-time thing?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: We`ve got to understand why -- what role that these uninsured play and the downfall of the bank. And we`ve got to understand why he didn`t understand their own balance sheet and the fact that they didn`t have the kind of assets to take care of those depositors. In fact, that was a failure. And so, we`ve got a lot to learn. But I think he did the right thing for now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That seems like a fair -- a fair assessment overall. But I agree with you, there`s a lot of questions that we`re going to have to get to bottom to. Congresswoman \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2 , thank you so much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: You`re so welcome. Keep on this story.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We will.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Because there`s a lot to learn.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We will.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, the Chinese billionaire behind \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and a ton of other right wing media gets -- well, you guessed it, indicted on a lengthy list of fraud charges. The full details next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The first time I can remember hearing about \uc2 Guo Wengui\uc2  was back in August of 2020. That`s when \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  -- do you remember this? He was arrested on federal fraud charges. He was accused of using money donated to an organization he was part of called Build the Wall, at the Southern Border to line his own pockets.\par \par \uc2 And the people that arrested Bannon were \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  inspectors on the yacht of a Chinese billionaire off the coast of Connecticut. It struck me as kind of odd at the time. Well, the Chinese billionaire who own the yacht is known as \uc2 Guo Wengui\uc2 . He made his fortune in Chinese real estate but fled China in 2014 when he was accused of bribery and embezzlement. He then became a huge patron of \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , the entire American right-wing, even sponsoring last month`s CPAC.\par \par \uc2 Well, today it was Guo`s turn to be arrested by federal agents on fraud allegations. Federal prosecutors in New York alleged like Guo and a co- defendant conspired to defraud thousands of victims in more than approximately $1 billion through fictitious businesses and other online schemes.\par \par \uc2 Political Investigations Reporter Jose Pagliery has been covering this story for The Daily Beast. And he joins me now. Jose, first give us a little bit of context about -- I really had not been that aware of Guo before the yacht incident with Bannon. But he`s been a very vocal anti-CCP voice. He`s very opposed to the ruling Chinese party, and a real patron for conservatives in the U.S. What --who is he, what`s his deal?\par \par \uc2 JOSE PAGLIERY, POLITICAL INVESTIGATIONS REPORTER, THE DAILY BEAST: So, for those who`ve never heard of him, this is going to sound pretty unbelievable, but in a minute or two, everyone will understand exactly what`s going on here. This guy has a huge voice in the Chinese dissident community. And it`s because right now China, as we all know, is cracking down on these Chinese oligarchs, especially those who threaten \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and his authority there.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is one of those billionaires who fled to the United States. He came here in late 2014, sometime in 2015. And he made New York sort of the home base for this big fight against the CCP. Now, in doing so, he befriended all these far-right wingers. And like you said, he became really close to \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 . Anyone who watches the War Room show that \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  has is familiar with Guo because he`s a sort of like amateur rap artist who has a song calling out the CCP and how bad they are, right?\par \par \uc2 But what people don`t know about him is that he has been sued by everyone, left and right in recent years, particularly in local court in New York, because what he tends to do is he tends to befriend dissidents, bring them in close, and then fool them. He defrauds them. And if they go against him --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Allegedly.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:50]\par \par \uc2 PAGLIERY: Well, right, except that in all of these lawsuits, he has to detail time and time again, exactly what it is that he does. And what he does is he brings people in close and then anytime they start questioning why they`re not getting paid by him, he seeks his mob. He goes on social media and in Mandarin speaks to people and tells them to attack, go out against these people who he has now defamed.\par \par \uc2 Now, what we know about him in recent years is that as he`s been raising all this money from the dissident community, and launching what has now gotten him into trouble with the SEC, he at the same time in court cases has been telling people who fought against him that he`s penniless. And so, this is a really interesting story where we`ve got a guy who lives just three blocks away from Trump Tower in one of the best spots in Manhattan, a beautiful, huge condo that overlooks Central Park, while he`s telling a lot of people that he`s broke, while also raising more than a billion dollars from people that he is telling should be taking part in this effort against the CCP.\par \par \uc2 But what we`ve learned today from this indictment is that what he allegedly has been doing with this money is actually just enriching himself. He`s just pocketing it much like ding, ding, ding, some of the right-wing friends that he`s made over the years.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. I mean, the allegation \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is facing, current federal charges, because he was pardoned in federal charges by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but then he was indicted by the District Attorney in Manhattan for essentially telling people he`s raising money to build a wall using it to line his own pockets. There`s sort of a similar structure to the alleged scheme here, although it`s very sprawling and it seems like he`s got his hands on -- I mean, there`s like a -- there was a stable coin, Himalaya coin scam.\par \par \uc2 In one instance, Guo and his co-defendant built investors out of more than $260 million via purported stable coin called the Himalaya dollar, and a trading coin called the Himalaya coin, or H coin, according to prosecutors. He allegedly spent the cash from other sources he had on a 50,000 square foot New Jersey mansion, a $3.5 million Ferrari, Chinese and Persian rugs worth nearly a million, and a $4.4 million Bugatti.\par \par \uc2 So, am I right that there`s like -- the Feds alleging -- this is not a small thing. This is an enormous, sprawling, complicated kind of octopus of fraud that they allege he is at the head of.\par \par \uc2 PAGLIERY: That`s right. And I think -- so the main -- the key point here is that he launched this media company that would sort of mimic what right wingers here in the U.S. have done, this media company that`s going to call out the big nasty government on the other side of the world. But what the SEC claims he did is that he actually raised funds illegally.\par \par \uc2 Now, the SEC exists to protect investors, everyday investors like you and me, they make sure that companies that go public, right, whose shares you can buy, that these companies are held accountable. They have to make certain reports. They have to disclose certain things. And this company of his is accused of not doing that. And actually, so in a few years ago, he actually settled with the SEC and agreed to pay back some half a billion dollars. But what we`re learning from this indictment is that while he was doing that, he was allegedly doing all -- a whole other rounds of fundraising throughout -- through these really shady means.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, having written a book on Bitcoin almost a decade ago, I can tell you that at this point, anything that touches cryptocurrency makes me roll my eyes. I mean stable Himalaya coin, people should know that that thing is not legitimate. But the thing about that list, by the way, that includes this mega mansion in New Jersey, and these luxury cars and this yacht that`s currently harbored in Connecticut -- that`s parked in Connecticut, all these things he`s accused of having, we have to view that through the context of someone who fled China in 2014 and was accused of corruption there, claimed years later in court -- he was he was deposed. He supposed to be under oath and telling the truth that he had not a penny, while a few months later has raised nearly a billion dollars. I mean, this this stuff doesn`t make sense.\par \par \uc2 And pretty soon we`re going to get a close look at exactly who he is and what he`s doing because now he`s not in a position of power anymore. I mean, up until today, he would surround himself with armed guards, and he just doesn`t have that anymore. The Feds are on top of him and he can`t escape.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, we now have -- you know, we`re going to go through the normal American court process. He is of course innocent until proven guilty. Those are the charges against him. Jose Pagliery, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PAGLIERY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Wednesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230316ej3f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for March 15, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle \par \uc2 8,851 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  gets tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:10]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, nearing the end of the Manhattan DA`s investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  wraps up another day of grand jury testimony of Stormy Daniels meets with prosecutors. So what happens now?\par \par \uc2 Then the high stakes Texas lawsuit over abortion medication, the judge`s ruling could come at any time and the consequences it could have across the country and fallout and concerns over \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 `s downfalls spreading to Europe. Are there still risks for other banks? And will Congress do anything about it as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Wednesday night?\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. We may be one step closer to a first ever indictment of a former president in a criminal case. The Manhattan District Attorney`s investigation into the Stormy Daniels hush money case appears to be entering its final stages. This afternoon, former Trump loyalists \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  finished his second day of testifying before a grand jury investigating the case.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP PERSONAL ATTORNEY: Do I think that it`s wrapping up it appears to be so. I`m probably the last witness that they need. I did not receive any additional subpoenas or documents requesting me to come back. So yes, I`m pretty safe to say that. I`m done.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Cohen testified for about two hours. He said he was questioned directly by several of the grand jurors. And he added this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COHEN: This isn`t a question of vindication. It`s not a question as I stated before about revenge. This is a my position is that at the end of the day, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  needs to be held accountable for his dirty deeds, if in fact, that`s the way that facts play out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Manhattan prosecutors also held a Zoom meeting today with Stormy Daniels herself. Her lawyer tweeted that Stormy responded to questions and has agreed to make herself available as a witness if needed.\par \par \uc2 We`re also following, I`m telling you I certainly am, the latest turmoil involving banks and financial markets. Today, shares of the Swiss Bank Credit Suisse plummeted, setting off fears across the globe all the way to Wall Street. It came after Signature Bank and \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  failed here in the US. The Swiss Central Bank stepped in to say it was ready to support \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . And tonight in the last couple of hours, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  announced it will be borrowing up to $54 billion from the National Bank.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel tonight. Luke Broadwater is here, Pulitzer Prize winning congressional reporter for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2 , a veteran federal prosecutor and former U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Michigan, and my friend Peter Spiegel is here at 30Rockm U.S. Managing Editor for The Financial Times.\par \par \uc2 Peter, let`s start with \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . This comes days after Silicon Valley Bank Signature Bank. We know other regional banks here \uc2 First Republic\uc2  are in trouble. Lots of viewers are going \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  don`t know it never touched it, but it`s a huge bank, should they be worried?\par \par \uc2 PETER SPIEGEL, U.S. MANAGING EDITOR, FINANCIAL TIMES: Look, I think they`re two very different situations. What they have in common is that the banking system is stressed. And when the banking system is stressed banks that have already had bad governance issues or have been running -- run badly start to fall apart.\par \par \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  has been a long running over two years, basically, almost every major scandal that has blown up on Wall Street has involved \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . This has been a badly run bank for a long time.\par \par \uc2 And what triggered the latest thing was yes, \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  has stressed the system. But the Saudi backers who have been giving the money said you know what? We`re not going to give you any more money. And that happened this morning, the bank stock dropped. And as you said, the Swiss Central Bank has stepped in today. And it basically rescue them.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Audience be patient with me, we won`t get too deep in the weeds. But what is $54 billion going to actually do for them because here`s the case, in \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , they had the issue that all these depositors were pulling, you know, we`re pulling out maybe they`ll come back.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 , OK, all they`ve got basically is the wealth management business. Their investment banking business here is now getting spun off yet controlled by the CEO \uc2 Michael Klein\uc2 , who lo and behold, sat on the board of the company and advise this transaction like they`re -- they`ve sold off another part of their business to \uc2 Apollo\uc2 , were they going to make up this 54 bill?\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Well, so look, this this is the part of the restriction they`re trying to do is to consolidate down into the bits of the bank that actually made money which is their Swiss bank, they manage rich people`s money, they want to get back to the poor --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Except lots of rich people have left them.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Hold the money out. So that`s the risk. The risk is the reasons why central bank kind of stepped in was people were pulling their money out so they had to be able to have money to take out of the bank because it`s not bad money sitting in the bank right now.\par \par \uc2 Now, one of two things can happen, right? It`s the middle of night in Switzerland right now, the analysis a couple of days ago --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How about this, this rescue deal gets announced at basically 2:00 a.m. Swiss time what does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 [23:05:00]\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: Well, yes, it did one of two things. One is they stepped in before the markets open in Asia, people don`t fight the Fed, you don`t fight the central bank that can print money will step back and say fine, or they look and say, Gosh, why did \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  need to do this at 2:00 in the morning in Zurich? That`s troubling to me, and they start continuing to pull money out.\par \par \uc2 The risk, as you know, is what`s called counterparty risk, right? So many other banks just don`t want to touch \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  money. And that`s why they need to get the central bank money. So the risk is here on Wall Street, the big American banks a call out there guys on the trading floor and say, You know what, let`s not do business, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 . We have no idea. They`re going to be existing in 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So you going to get your 54 back anytime soon. OK, my last question on this topic. I know that the \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  situation is different, but bigger than that. This is different from 2008. OK. There is not that much leverage in the system. We don`t have a housing bubble under us.\par \par \uc2 However, we are coming off a decade of free money, zero interest rates pile in there, this back craze, the crypto craze, NFTs, inflationary pressures, the government pumping trillions of COVID dollars into the system, and then interest rates getting high, tight, tight over the last year. Is it not fair to say the system is kind of fragile?\par \par \uc2 SPEIGEL: So this is why I think there`s been the sell off, right? There`s that famous phrase, which I use on your show before with \uc2 Warren Buffett\uc2  always says no one knows who`s swimming naked into the water goes out. Certainly the water`s coming out who has been taking that free money and doing bad things about it.\par \par \uc2 The problem is the financial press, even the regulators have a hard time looking into those banks and finding out where people have been over leveraged. We`d found out a little bit too late that was \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 . Same thing happened in Britain, if you remember when they had this gilts crisis where there was a strange debt structures, everyone got freaked out about.\par \par \uc2 What people are worried about is exactly what you said, were people doing bad things with this money? And if so, where? Because as we say, for 10 years, if you`re getting free money, there`s someone around the system`s been doing bad things with it, the nervousness from the financial press as well, because we should be watching this stuff is I have no idea where it is, the regulators don`t know where it is, and the markets don`t know where it is. That`s why there`s been this massive sell off.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, here`s what I can tell you. And I know a lot of bankers. I don`t want to see any of them naked. Luke, what is happening on Capitol Hill. Are lawmakers worried about how fragile things are?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Yes, absolutely. There`s a lot of hand wringing right now in the halls of the Senate, about what to do about this. A lot of senators, especially on the Democratic side of the aisle, are talking about the feelings of regret basically about allowing some of the Dodd Frank regulations to be rolled back under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And there`s talk now about whether those can be reinstated again. They`re going to have a hard time with the Republicans on that. The House is controlled by the Republicans, obviously and you need, you know, 10 Republicans in the Senate to go along with anything.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said the day he`s beginning negotiations with the Republicans to see if some sort of bipartisan legislation could happen to try to reform the banking industry. \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , the number two Republican said, however, what they wanted to do first was more investigation. They want to have hearings, about \uc2 SVB\uc2  find out exactly what went wrong there, get to the bottom of it, and then talk about legislation. So talk about a longer process and more investigation first.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, even if they added regulation tomorrow, that`s not going to do anything to change the fragility of the system. What could is the Fed meeting next week, we had been expecting that they were going to continue to raise rates. I know you don`t have a crystal ball, Peter. But how much is \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  looking at this and saying, I might need to take a pause here.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: So two points on this, the markets are now thinking that he`s going to slow down for two reasons. One, if the banking system is now going to freeze up a bit, which means they`re not going to be lending to you and me --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Which it`s fair to say they are going to freeze up a bit, especially for small and mid-sized banks, this lending Bonanza that`s been going on for all these years. They can`t be doing that in the near future.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: So that is slowing down the economy, will slow down inflation without them needing to raise rates. The other thing is more macro prudential as they call it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What word did you say/\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: macro prudential. I`m sorry for you --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Did you make that word up?\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: (INAUDIBLE) like company, basically the --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I just need to shut up.\par \par \uc2 SPIEGEL: \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , there are banks that have bad bonds on their balance sheet. This is how they`re supposed to be safe. And as the bank`s and the Fed, the Fed has been raising rates, those assets have become less. So the banking system itself is becoming weaker as the Fed raise rates so they have to worry about how their rates affects the real economy but also how it affects the banking system and Wall Street is now betting that they may just take a pause we`re thinking about maybe 25 basis points. We saw even early last week when they would love the inflation numbers look bad maybe 50 basis points.\par \par \uc2 Now people thinking maybe he takes a pause, it`s a tough one Right now in their silent period ahead of the meeting so even had an fed people be able to talk to us, but that`s going to be the thing to watch as you said Congress closing the barn door after the horse escapes that`s going to not going to matter. It`s going to matter what \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  does next week.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Here`s a novel idea for those banks hedge your risk. Barbara, new topic, we heard \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  finish up his testimony today.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:02]\par \par \uc2 Is the Manhattan DA in the late stages of this investigation, I`m guessing yes, because I`m going on vacation next week.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Yes, I`d say so. And the timing, you know, you must you must wash your car just before it starts to rain --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Correct, every time.\par \par \uc2 MCQAUDE: -- thinking indictment, probably feeling like next week might be the time. We know from \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  that he was before the grand jury today. We know that the prosecutors have interviewed Stormy Daniels. We know they`ve had Trump`s lawyers in. They invited Trump himself in to testify, though he declined that. Those all sound like the kinds of things you would want to do at the end.\par \par \uc2 After you have informed yourself as best you can by talking to all of the other witnesses and reading all of the documents, they`ve had 19 or 20 meetings with \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 . And they finally found that he was ready that they could corroborate what he had to say that they believed him, and that they believe they could sponsor that testimony, and that a jury would believe him.\par \par \uc2 So, it seems likely that there`s still one more step remaining, which is to do a summation for the grand jury, and then to present a draft indictment, and then to allow them to deliberate. I would think that could be done in a day or maybe to so early next week and sounded like the time, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How big of a deal as a farm that they talk to Stormy Daniels?\par \par \uc2 MCQAUDE: You know what, I think Stormy Daniels, it`s interesting that they didn`t put her in the grand jury, it may be that they don`t plan to call her as a witness, but that they wanted to know what she had to say to sort of neutralizer as a defense witness. And just make sure there aren`t any surprises out there.\par \par \uc2 You know, before you indict a case, you want to make sure there isn`t going to be some dramatic moment in the trial when the courtroom doors burst open, and someone else comes in and confesses to the crime or says you`ve got it all wrong. That`s not how it happened at all.\par \par \uc2 So, it may be that they don`t need her testimony or even wants her testimony. But they just want to make sure they can lock her into his story so that she can`t, you know, be bought off and fabricate a story later, or that there isn`t some important detail that they have -- had previously been aware of that she could provide to them today.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Luke, clearly, lawmakers on both sides of the aisle are focusing on what`s happening in this Manhattan DA case. For Republicans, I don`t want to even say do they love the case -- do they love the fact that it`s Stormy Daniels, but seeing that the Stormy Daniels situation was so many years ago was the most salacious. Are they really going to push that this is political, political, political, like if they were to choose any investigation to cause indictment would this be the one Republicans want the most?\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Yes, I don`t know. I don`t think any indictment of a former president --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Good point. None of it. Yes, but they politicize anything.\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Yes -- and given that the Georgia cases is also progressing quite quickly, and they`re both -- they could reach Pinnacle at around the same time. So it could be actually multiple grand juries coming forward with decisions about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You know, it always cuts both ways with Trump, right? Like when he`s on the hot seat does the base rally around him? Yes. At the same time, this party is at this critical point right now where they`re deciding whether or not to stick with this guy. And, you know, right, just today, Chip Roy proactively endorsed \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who isn`t even declared yet. And that may not mean much to most of your viewers. But he`s somebody who`s quite influential on the ideological right in among the House Republicans, and so he`s out there already encouraging \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to run.\par \par \uc2 And so if more and more bad news comes out about Trump, do more and more people start to go to DeSantis. You know, it could rally them around him. But I could also see after six years of this turmoil, that there could be a breaking point and so -- at least some segment of the party is looking for someone else.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`ve been saying that for a long time, Luke. Barb, give us the technicals. Let`s say there is a Trump indictment, what happens? He is a former president. Does he appear in court with Secret Service?\par \par \uc2 MCQAUDE: Yes, it`s a fascinating question. I do think he would be allowed to self-surrender. You know, if you have any concerns that a person might flee or might be dangerous, you would arrest them. But I think in the case of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , where everybody knows this is coming. His lawyers would get a call say he`s been indicted. When can we come in in the next couple of days. And he`d be in but he does have to get books, which means he would have to go and be processed, fingerprinted and mugshot taken. That has to be done.\par \par \uc2 So I imagine it could be done with Secret Service President law enforcement is usually very courteous to each --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Holy cow.\par \par \uc2 MCQAUDE: -- (INAUDIBLE) imagine that --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I think I need you to say this to us one more time. You know what I mean? It`s like we become so desensitized with like all these investigations. If you were indicted Barb one more time, what would happen?\par \par \uc2 [23:15:00]\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: So he would be required to appear for an arraignment in court and when he appears he would be booked so he would be fingerprinted and he would be mugshot it before he appears in court.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That is extraordinary just to think about a former president of the United States. Bark, Luke was just mentioning it the case in Georgia is getting closer. It`s getting bigger. It`s getting hotter. There`s a new report out from the Atlanta Journal-Constitution about the grand jury and one of the jurors said this quote, a lot is going to come out. It`s going to be massive. What does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 MCQAUDE: I don`t know. You know, that was an interesting article in the Atlanta Journal-Constitution. I think some of the other things that they said there were, that I thought were really interesting is, if all of America knew what we now know, there`ll be a lot less division and polarization in the country.\par \par \uc2 So I`m really interested in hearing what it is they heard, but, you know, we know from the foreperson who spoke a couple of weeks ago, that they have recommended indictments against more than a dozen people. And so that may be what she -- this grand jury is referring to, when they say massive.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: 