use the power of social media to shout fire in a in a movie theater.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL: For sure one the first one. The fact that you could do wires from your home with dual effect, dual authentication, you get a text and a million dollars goes out the door. That`s going to happen fast. And that`s --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That`s never been before.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL: That`s never been happened before. On the second thing, this whole notion that tax or calcannabis (ph) on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  had feel this. It`s just not. I don`t feel it`s accurate. They didn`t start tweeting until Saturday, and this thing was gone by Friday. Right? That`s FTSE was in the offices on Friday, and they were in Signature Bank on the weekend. So I don`t think it`s a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  thing, but I do think the speed of information, the speed of money movement all that is the new reality that is what we`re living in now.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:04]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then in terms of what needs to be looked at, in the autopsy of all of this, what do we need to focus on because whether it was malfeasance or mistakes, we got to review it.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Versus more regulation. I mean, in 2018, they basically deregulated \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 . I mean, \uc2 Greg Becker\uc2  was lobbying me and others saying we don`t need these regulations. We don`t need a liquidity test. We don`t need a stress test. Guess what, if they had done a stress test or a liquidity test, they would have figured out you don`t hold long term bonds, while interest rates are going up.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But that`s not a license for bad behavior.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Of course it`s not.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Like I get that it`s like one political party wants to blame this guy and other blame this guy, however, and I set it here last night, like, if I don`t lock up all the vodka in my house, it doesn`t mean that my 16- year-old is clear to drink at all and go get behind the wheel.\par \par \uc2 So just because every letter of the law isn`t, we don`t cross every T and dot every I doesn`t mean bankers need to treat this like the wild, wild west, shouldn`t they take some accountability?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Absolutely. I`ve called for a clawback of their stock sales, of their bonuses through executive pay, I think there has to be an investigation into the executives and management team. I have no sympathy for them, and the shareholders and bondholders should go to zero.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is we regulate the four top banks in a different way than we do the state banks and regional banks. And I think the deal has got to be if we`re going to protect depositors in these regional banks, they have to submit to greater scrutiny and greater regulation.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But here`s the problem. What`s happening to the four biggest banks this week, they are having more money come in than, I mean, it`s like --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL: It`s raining.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Winning Powerball -- it is raining money inside the halls of \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  this week. So the effect of this has been people flooding to those big banks, what do you do about it? Because I know that`s not what we want universally. We need our community banks.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL: I think we have to figure out an insurance scheme that is accountable.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s not call it a scheme.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL: Sorry, an insurance plan, insurance model that is relative to the risk that these banks are taking. So if you`re going to take a risk on some of these longer and treasures, pay more insurance and let your depositors know that this is a risky, you`re taking a risk. You may be getting 4 percent, but you`re taking risk --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I like that, the more risk you take the more insurance you get.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL: That`s on you and that`s -- and the more insurance you`re paying, you have to have a risk management structure that can calculate that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Are you asking, where`s the San Francisco fed? And all this?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I`m asking where are the California regulators because the California regulators were supposed to regulate a California bank. But one other thing we could do is we could say, look, the banks have to pay more premium. You made good points earlier that will let go to ordinary depositors. Well, you can mandate that higher accounts pay a larger fee for that premium into the insurance fund so that the person who`s has less than 250,000 isn`t paying the fee. But if you`ve got $3 million $5 million, you are paying part of that bank fee.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we look at this should whether it`s the Fed or the \uc2 FDIC\uc2 , or different regulators start to look at not just how many deposits banks have, but who the depositors are, and the concentration. It was clearly unique that it was a correlation of one. Everybody with their money at \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  was in the same business.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Silicon Valley has, Emil I think would attest to it`s a bit of a herd mentality. You know, when I got my first supporter, every other person wanted to support me. And I think when you have these VCs with large accounts when you pull out, it`s a problem. So they were not diverse for two reasons. They weren`t diverse in industry, and they didn`t have diverse accounts.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s hard to have a run and \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 . You got a lot of people with a lot of accounts when you have big accounts that is more susceptible to a bank run.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I`m out of time. But I have to ask, how worried are you about how fragile the markets are? This is different than a wait. But rates are high. They got high quickly. They were zero forever. We had crypto, we had stacks (ph). We`ve had a lot of crazy things happen.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I`m worried. I think the capital markets are frozen right now. I think we`re going to wait till the next Fed meeting to see how the Fed is going to react to this. Whatever they were going to do my guess is they`re going to do less of it. But who knows? And when who knows means volatility, means fear, means freezing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do you know I bring on the experts because I want to hear who knows. Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , Emile Michael, great to see both.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a GOP state lawmaker is eating his words after voting against a free meal program for hungry students. His reason is never met a hungry person from Minnesota. We`re going to take him to school on the facts when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:43]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. HEATHER GUSTAFSON (D-MN): Besides the healthy meals are giving money back to families feeding kids at school is the right thing to do. Being hungry makes learning almost impossible. There is no worksheet or assignment test or project that will matter to a student who hasn`t had anything to eat. We can vote yesterday and make sure next school year every student gets breakfast and lunch at school. Let`s do that. Let`s feed the kids.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, hunger is a relative term. That right there was Minnesota State Senator Heather Gustafson making the case for her bill proposing free school lunches, but not everyone was on board. Please watch this argument from Republican state senator Steve Drazkowski.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. STEVE DRAZKOWSKI (R-MN): Mr. President, I have yet to meet a person in Minnesota that is hungry. Yet today, I have yet to meet a person in Minnesota that says they don`t have access to enough food to eat. Now, I should say that hunger is a relative term, Mr. President. You know, I had cereal bar for breakfast. I guess I`m hungry now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, for fact sake, Senator, I have never met lots of people but I know they exist. I know lots of them are suffering, like the one in six children in Minnesota who experienced food insecurity, according to the anti-hunger groups.\par \par \uc2 [00:00:09]\par \par \uc2 And they estimated 8 percent of the children in one of the counties you represent, sir, who lived in poverty in 2021. That is up from 7 percent the previous year. Luckily, your colleagues were not persuaded by your argument then they voted to pass the legislation 3826.\par \par \uc2 Once the bill is signed, Minnesota will join states like California and Colorado in providing free meals to kids. So chew on that, Senator.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks and NBC News, thanks for staying up late. I`ll see you at the end of today.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020230316ej3f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for March 15 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Tim Miller, Ali Vitali, Jonathan Lemire, Dasha  Burns, Brian Cheung, Ken Dilanian, Lindsey Reiser, Antonia Hylton, Sam  Brock \par \uc2 8,404 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Could a political battle that splitting the Republican Party also influence the outcome of an actual war? Some Republican lawmakers obviously think so, slamming \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  for arguing the U.S. should stop helping Ukraine. What it could mean for the Congress, the country, and the Kremlin?\par \par \uc2 Plus, the hugely consequential hearing on abortion happening right now in Texas. How the opinion of a single judge could impact the lives and health of millions of women across the country?\par \par \uc2 And Wall Street under pressure again with the Dow falling from the opening bell, now down about 700 points, at least close to it. The new bank and investors are scrutinizing at what it means for your money. Coming up.\par \par \uc2 But we start with the clash over America`s role in Ukraine shaping up to be an early defining moment in the Republican race for president. It pits \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who has now aligned himself with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by calling the war a territorial dispute that the U.S. should stay out of, against a growing number of Republican critics in Congress who say that position is weak and potentially dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2 , (R-LA): I haven`t seen the governor`s comments in the full context. I know how -- I believe -- I just believe that the aid to Ukraine is not charity. I think it`s an act of self-preservation.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROGER WICKER, (R-MS): I`m very disturbed that he feels that way.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): Saying this misunderstanding of the situation. This is not a territorial conflict. This is a war of aggression.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 KEVIN CRAMER\uc2 , (R-ND): The last thing we need is a -- you know is a bully with nuclear weapons, feeling like he`s emboldened and that there`s nobody keeping him in check.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MIKE ROUNDS, (R-SD): Any one of the individuals who has an interest in working as the next president of the United States really needs to get a full briefing before they decide to make up their minds on this particular issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC`s Ali Vitali on Capitol Hill. Jonathan Lemire is Politico`s White House bureau chief and host of MSNBC`s "WAY TOO EARLY." And Tim Miller is a writer at large for the Bulwark. Both Jonathan and Tim are MSNBC political analysts.\par \par \uc2 Yes. Tim, it wasn`t just that a bunch of these senators disagreed with DeSantis on policy, several seem to suggest he simply didn`t know what he was talking about. I mean, is the message maybe you`re not ready for primetime yet?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Some of these guys found their backbone, which was really enjoyable for me. So, kudos to those Republican senators who hadn`t been speaking out in the past when they knew the last president was making statements far more reckless than what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  just made about Ukraine. But I think what`s happening here is they know DeSantis is about ready to get into this race, and they`re trying to flex a little bit of their muscle. I think most of these senators` light would prefer \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  -- almost all of the senators would prefer \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is -- you know, I think that they want to give him a long leash to want to appeal to MAGA voters on a lot of issues, maybe ones that aren`t as important to them. But this is an important issue for the ones who still come from the old, traditional, neocon strong on defense wing of the Republican Party, and they want -- and they`re sending a message to DeSantis as this is over a red line for us, right? And they want him to know that if he`s going to get into this race you know, he has to you know to be a little bit stronger in defense of Ukraine you know, maybe you know that he can offer some certain you know, critiques of the Biden policy.\par \par \uc2 But to call this a territorial dispute to basically seem like you`re siding with the -- what an argument would have come out of the Kremlin in a statement to \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 , you know that is something that they disagree with. And I think that before he gets in this race, they`re trying to use this opportunity to shape you know what kind of foreign policy he would have in a campaign.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali, clear there are -- clearly there are people where you are on the Hill who don`t disagree with him. And I understand you have new reporting that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is getting a congressional endorsement even though he hasn`t even said he`s running yet.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, right. I love when my two beats actually collide and allow me to do them from the same place, which is exactly what`s happening today. On the one hand, you have all of these reactions to DeSantis`s downplaying of what`s happening in Ukraine, and it`s important to overall Democratic -- small D, Democratic values. But then on the other hand too, you have him starting to notch some Congressional endorsements, specifically from Texas Congressman Chip Roy, who just in the last few minutes sent out an e-mail to supporters on his campaign side account, saying that he thinks that DeSantis is the future of the party for many policy reasons, but also because of a proven electability factor.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 The fact that he was able to not just win by 19 points in Florida, but then also basically reshaped the entire makeup of the state and play into the demographic shifts that we`ve seen over the course of the last ten or so years in the Sunshine State. The fact that he was able to capitalize on that is really the larger electability argument that Congressman Roy is leaning on as he endorses someone who, you`re right, isn`t even in the race yet.\par \par \uc2 But I think what`s important about this Ukraine news cycle, as I`ve been talking to senators here on the Hill, the thing that strikes me is that DeSantis -- this is perhaps the most Trumpian thing that DeSantis has been able to do so far. In that, it`s less about the policy and more about the way that he`s been able to shape an entire news cycle, whether people are agreeing with him or disagreeing with him.\par \par \uc2 And I know that Tim will viscerally remember this from 2016. When you`re the person who can set the parameters of the debate, when you`re the person who can drive the news cycle, that`s what Trump did in 2016. It puts everyone else on defense sort of reacting to what you`re saying. It makes you a leader within the party.\par \par \uc2 Again, whether people are agreeing or disagreeing with you is sort of a moot point at this juncture because DeSantis is clearly someone who everyone in the party from here on Capitol Hill out to the campaign trail, have to pay attention to. This has been true for the last few weeks. But I think this news cycle really crystallized it for me that this is someone who, again, disagree or agree, is shaping the party.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I don`t think there`s any doubt about that, Jonathan. I also don`t think there`s any doubt that for a lot of voters out there and given the fact that you know, we acknowledge concerns over China have risen, where you stand on Moscow, where you stand, and how you view \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  matters to a lot of voters. And I wonder how you think maybe these remarks are being viewed in Moscow. Could it give Putin a reason to think he doesn`t need to drag this war out indefinitely, maybe just wait until November 2024?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I think these words were very well received in the Kremlin for exactly that point. If you`re \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  right now, and we know he pays attention to American politics and frankly, has meddled in American politics before, on at least one occasion, he sees now that right at this moment, the two leading contenders for the Republican nomination for President, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and the poll suggests they`re far ahead everyone else are both questioning the United States commitment to Kyiv. And suggesting that the U.S. should not be so involved in this conflict.\par \par \uc2 Territorial dispute is what -- you know that obviously diminishes what this is. This was an invasion of a sovereign nation. Russia invaded Ukraine. And DeSantis has now downplayed it. And we certainly know Trump has repeatedly praised Putin.\par \par \uc2 So, if you are Putin, that`s exactly it. You -- maybe you could feel like I can continue this war of attrition until November 2024. And then I`ve got at least a decent chance of the next occupant of the White House being someone who might frankly cut U.S. support dramatically to Ukraine and President Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 Even in the short term, though, obviously, we heard now some Republican senators really denounced what DeSantis said. This might give life to some of the voices on the Hill, particularly in the House, who already think that U.S. funding to Ukraine should be curtailed at the -- at this moment, even this year -- later this year. So, yes, this is very well received there in the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 As far as voters at home, certainly, your foreign policy is often not a defining issue for an American election. But we certainly know polls reflect that the U.S. is large -- U.S. voters largely supportive of Kyiv, but faded a little bit in recent months as they question the amount of money we`re sending.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, let`s talk about that. The way that it`s moved. I mean, Tim, your old boss, \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  put out a statement. I think it was just about a week ago saying it is in America`s interest to support Ukraine. If Russia were able to conquer, brutalize, and subjugate Ukraine with impunity, expect it to wage war again and expect those wars to damage our economy and threaten our security.\par \par \uc2 Having said that, to Jonathan`s point, look at the polls. In the spring of last year, 17 percent of Republicans said the U.S. was doing too much to help Ukraine. Then it went up to nearly 32 percent, nearly doubling. As of January, it`s at 40 percent. Is DeSantis simply reflecting the movement in the party?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: What a great statement from \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , by the way. It`s refreshing. And I could -- we could use more of that clear-eyed assessment of what`s happening in Ukraine. But yes, look. I think that the -- that what the key numbers there is the group of people that thought we weren`t doing enough to help Ukraine at 17 percent, right?\par \par \uc2 And, so that is basically the pool of the party that you have your Nikki Haley`s and Mike Pompeo`s and Mike -- and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is all fighting over. That`s obviously not enough to win a primary. So, what the key question is, is that middle group, right, the group that it`s about right? A lot of these people to Jonathan`s point, foreign policy isn`t their top issue, right?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 They`re malleable based on what they`re getting from their immediate environment. And so, I do think that if we were still a place where \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  controlled the party, and where, you know, the Fox News primetime hosts were pushing -- were saying that we need to be stronger and that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a little bit too weak on Ukraine, I think you could see that middle group move more into that camp. But that`s not what they`re getting.\par \par \uc2 The leaders of the party, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  are molding the party in their image. So, I think that in one sense, DeSantis is following the party but I think in another sense, these voters are being led by the most influential people into the party -- in the party into a more isolationist stance than maybe they would have otherwise.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That -- does that -- if that`s all true, Jonathan, does that not leave an opening for someone else? I don`t know what maybe it`s a moderate like Sununu, but maybe it`s also somebody like Glenn Younkin. We`ve been hearing that he`s heading to Dallas. He`s going to be meeting with a billionaire. Obviously, it`s helpful to have friends in high places, money- wise, if you`re thinking about running for president, which he may be. But is there an opening for a moderate of any stripe?\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: I mean, I`m simply not sure I see it. There have been Republicans who have spoken out against Trump and then faced voters who didn`t fare very well. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , of course, being the best example, sitting on the January 6 Committee, and then being trounced by 30, 40 points in her primary in Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, there are people and -- which you just played the montage of senators who have a more mainstream view, a more -- what we consider a traditional Republican view of foreign policy. And those Republicans still exist, no doubt. But they`re outnumbered. And I also think that even if there are some Republican voters who might share those views, they still largely agree with Trump or DeSantis on everything else.\par \par \uc2 And it does seem, at least at the moment, like this next election on the right anyway, it`s going to be fought in the culture wars. It`s going to be fought in primetime on Fox News. It`s going to be fought in schools and societal issues, and far less -- far, far less on foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, this is an issue and a significant one but I`m not sure it`s one that`s going to determine an election. And I don`t think it`s enough of one to give life to a more moderate Republican candidate for president. That person would be a significant underdog to the likes of Trump and DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. The big question is, are there are lanes that can be filled or is there really only one lane and it`s already filled? Ali Vitali, Jonathan Lemire, Tim Miller, great having all of you on. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got some breaking news right now from Texas. That high-stakes court hearing with potential national implications as a conservative judge considers whether to order an abortion pill off the market. To have one judge overrule the science-based decisions of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  would be unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Dasha Burns just stepped out of the courthouse in Amarillo, Texas. This is the first we`re hearing from anything that`s happened there. What do we know at this hour, Dasha?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chris. Well, that word, unprecedented, was just used several times by the defense in this case, which is the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , which is the government. Let me just walk you through what I just witnessed inside. We have just heard from the plaintiffs. They were allotted two hours to speak. They spoke for an hour and a half saving that last half hour for a rebuttal at the very end, then the court went to break.\par \par \uc2 Those inside of the court were not allowed to leave the building during the break. Otherwise, I promise you, I would have been out here talking to you. If we had left, we would not be allowed to go back inside.\par \par \uc2 So, after the break, we had the defense come out and address the court. They had the lawyers for the Department of Justice, for the \uc2 FDA\uc2  speak. I stepped out about halfway through the presentation from the government.\par \par \uc2 And again, in their arguments, they said that if the judge did rule in favor of the plaintiffs in this case and issued a preliminary injunction, which as you said, Chris, would take mifepristone, one of the pills in the two-drug regimen for medication abortion, if they took that off the market, that it would be "extraordinary relief," that it appends the status quo, and arguing that it would cause significant public harm. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has approved this drug over 20 years ago. So, the argument on behalf of the government is that this is just would be an unprecedented and extraordinary measure to take a drug off of the market that has been used by people for over two decades.\par \par \uc2 Now, the plaintiffs in the case argue that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  should never have approved the drug in the first place. They say that they did not adequately evaluate its safety. And there`s another piece of this argument. So, just bear with me for a second because this is going to have significant impacts depending on how the judge rules here.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 One is the question of whether or not the \uc2 FDA\uc2  should have approved this drug. The other is regarding some of the changes that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  made in terms of regulating medication abortion over the course of the last several years. They did sort of loosen restrictions around who can prescribe the drug. And recently in 2021 during the pandemic, allowed the drug to be prescribed via telehealth, which has been an option a lot of folks have been using.\par \par \uc2 And this especially would impact those in rural areas. Those -- the folks that are part of minority groups. The people that are often impacted by these sorts of decisions.\par \par \uc2 So, we don`t know when the judge will rule. The arguments are expected to finish up in about an hour or so here. But again, just to emphasize, this hearing today is about the preliminary injunction request on behalf of the plaintiffs in this case, which is asking the judge to compel the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to pull mifepristone off of the market as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know if he will rule today at the end of this hearing or he could roll tomorrow or in the coming weeks. So, we`re going to keep an eye on all of this and give you the updates as we get them here, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, we know that you know this is all playing out under a very watchful America. This has gotten a tremendous amount of attention. Tell us a little bit about this judge and why this has many people who are pro- choice so nervous.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Well, the critics of the case overall, the folks that are in favor of abortion rights have accused the plaintiffs in this case of doing what`s called forum shopping, which is essentially intentionally filing the case in this district knowing that it would get -- go before this judge who is a conservative judge. He was appointed by former President Trump, so that makes abortion rights advocates nervous because of his history of conservative-leaning decisions.\par \par \uc2 The other factor at play here, Chris. We didn`t find out about this hearing until just a couple of days ago. And in fact, this judge intended for the media and for the public to not find out about this hearing until just last night. He held a status conference on Friday with lawyers from both sides of the case, told them that this hearing was going to be scheduled for Wednesday. But he did not put it on the docket, which is where the public can see what`s scheduled to go before the court here.\par \par \uc2 He told lawyers in the case to not publicize that this hearing was happening. His argument was around safety concerns, around threats that he says the division has received. But critics have said -- accused him of secrecy, of unconstitutional action in terms of hiding with -- what they say is hiding this hearing from the public. And it was only after that, that pressure from media and from the public that we actually learned this was happening today. And we were able to be here, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And if I can ask you really briefly, take us inside the mood inside that courtroom given the stakes. And was the judge asking a lot of questions? Was he interrupting the presentation?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tell us what it was like to be in there.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes. It was a very solemn, serious mood. All parties seem to really understand the stakes in this case. It was interesting the judge did get involved quite a bit. He was asking questions of both the plaintiffs and the defendants.\par \par \uc2 When the defendants -- the plaintiffs were presenting, he seems to sort of -- the best way to put it I would say would be to put a finer point on some of their arguments, sort of clarify what the plaintiffs` arguments are. When the defense was presenting from what I saw over the last hour or so, he did press them to sort of fortified -- to calcify their arguments to make clear how the plaintiffs` arguments do not undercut their case in terms of the safety and effectiveness.\par \par \uc2 One thing he really focused in on, Chris, was what he called intervening events, meaning the changes that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  made in 2016 and in 2022 to again, change some of the rules and regulations around the prescribing of this drug, which makes me think that might be a key area when we do eventually get a ruling. That will -- we will get some kind of decision on that question around prescribing this drug via telemedicine around who could prescribe that drug because those are some of the issues that the plaintiffs in this case, they want to roll back some of those changes that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  made, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Again, no devices allowed in that courtroom. This is the first we`re hearing about these critical arguments that are still underway. Dasha Burns, we appreciate you coming out and talking to us just now.\par \par \uc2 Banking fears spread. We`ll check on the markets as we get new details about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  team leading the investigation into \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 Severe weather pummeling the U.S. from east to west with more rain on the way for flood-ravaged California. We have the latest forecast.\par \par \uc2 From movies to golf courses to bowling lanes, the new approach to pricing that just might leave you with sticker shock. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: More turmoil on Wall Street today. Bank fears are now spreading across global markets because of new concerns about the financial health of a Swiss-owned \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 , which is more than three times the size of the failed \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 . Take a look at the numbers right now. The Dow down 530 deep in the red. Although off, their lows really just half an hour ago or less. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  has a major presence here in the U.S. And Meantime, a senior law enforcement official tells NBC that an investigation into the collapse of \uc2 SVB\uc2  is being led by \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents who specialize in white-collar crime.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in NBC News business and data reporter Brian Cheung, and justice and intelligence correspondent Ken Dilanian. What is going on, Brian, with \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  and how worried should all of us be?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHEUNG, MSNBC BUSINESS & DATA REPORTER: Yes. Well, as you mentioned, this is a Swiss bank that has a substantial presence here in the United States. But this is a bank that has had some liquidity issues as of late. There`s been a lot of deposits falling away from the bank in the last quarter or so. And by the way, these are some concerns that have kind of predated the issues at \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 This is a bank that as of late has had another scandal, which is admitting themselves that they have some material weaknesses in the way that they`ve been reporting their financials. So, this is a firm that has had issues as historically. But again, the stock down 21 percent just today, so you see investors are really concerned about that particular firm.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Speaking of stock, Ken, you`re reporting that part of the investigation into the \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  is going to be about stock and stock that was sold, in particular by executives ahead of the closing of that bank.\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC JUSTICE & INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Our friends at CNBC pointed out that over the past two years, executives have sold $84 million worth of stock. CEO \uc2 Greg Becker\uc2  cashed in more than three million in the week before the failure, almost 30 million in the past two years. So, what are your sources telling you about this overall investigation?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: They`re saying that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the SEC has to take a look at whether any inside information was used in those stock sales. That would be illegal. Now, the CEO sold his shares pursuant to a plan that was executed in January.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, I mean, that stock is worthless now. It was $1.3 hundred a share. And so, the government needs to look and make sure that nobody traded on improper inside information. And if they did that, they broke the law. That would be a big problem.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: How complicated are those types of cases?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: You know the data -- the SEC has the data.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: It`s easy for them to understand exactly what was sold and when. The question of motive, that is complicated. Insider trading cases often hinge on, for example, wiretaps, which probably don`t exist in this case. So, proving someone`s motive is a problem.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What did they know, when did they know it, getting inside their brain? So, how do these -- so, you start with \uc2 SVB\uc2  but now you`re looking at global concerns. How do they complicate the job of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , which everyone`s been waiting to see what they`re going to do about interest rates again?\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Yes. Well, it certainly doesn`t make it easier, right? It`s going to make it harder if anything. And prior to all of this, prior to last weekend --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What is that mean to make it harder? A harder decision nobody cares whether or not it`s a hard decision for the Fed. What we want to know is how is it likely to impact them.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Yes. Well, I mean because again, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has been in this effort to try to raise interest rates and take care of inflation. But one of the dominoes to fall that led to \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 `s collapse was higher interest rates. So, if they end up backing off a little bit, which is the talking point on Wall Street right now in terms of raising interest rates, then maybe they don`t get the job done on inflation that fast. So, that would be something that the American people would continue to feel at the price -- at the store as they continue to see high prices. If the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  can`t fully hit the brakes on high inflation because of what`s happening in the financial services sector, high prices might be something that Americans will have to continue to deal with.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A little bit of pressure, maybe.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: A little bit.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ken Dilanian and Brian Chung, good to have both of you here. Thank you. I have a feeling we`re going to be talking about this more in the days ahead.\par \par \uc2 After a series of dangerous disruptions and close calls, how safe is it to fly? There`s an air safety summit today and a former \uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator will join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:47]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The \uc2 FAA\uc2  is holding an urgent air safety summit today after a string of dangerous close calls in the sky and on the ground. More than 600 incidents in fiscal year 2023, including a passenger plane going off a taxiway in Syracuse this week. And a near collision at Reagan National Airport in Washington last week that we just learned about today, when a regional jet rolled in front of a departing United \uc2 Airbus\uc2  jet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: United 2003, cancel takeoff clearance.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Aborting takeoff, aborting takeoff, United 2003.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In an exclusive interview with Lester Holt, the acting \uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator insists that flying is safe but warns of complacency.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILLY NOLEN, ACTING FAA ADMINISTRATOR: We`ve had these events over the past few weeks that gives us a moment to say, let`s stop, let`s reflect, let`s ask ourselves the question, are we missing anything? Is there anything that we should be doing different and remind ourselves always, always that we can never become complacent and never take this incredible safety record for granted?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joining me now MSNBC anchor and correspondent, Lindsey Reiser, who has been following the \uc2 FAA\uc2  summit for us today. And former \uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator, Randy Babbitt. Lindsey, let`s start with the summit itself.\par \par \uc2 Look, it`s not just close calls and planes. We saw that incredible turbulence, somebody died. We saw another passenger on another flight who was threatening to kill people. What was the focus today? And what is the \uc2 FAA\uc2  hoping to accomplish with this summit?\par \par \uc2 LINDSEY REISER, MSNBC ANCHOR AND CORRESPONDENT: They`re calling this call for vigilance a wake-up call here. And we can see why. When we look at these recent incidents, I mean, we`ve had six near misses, Chris, so far this year, including the one that you just mentioned. These planes coming really close to each other on runways, one not having clearance to be on, as another`s landing, for example.\par \par \uc2 And then we have what you mentioned, we had a really -- we had a business jet. They had really bad turbulence, somebody actually die. We saw the \uc2 Lufthansa\uc2  flight where several people were injured there because of turbulence. And then as you mentioned, we saw that cell phone video from the flight in which that man used it created a makeshift blade and threatened flight attendants.\par \par \uc2 Now today`s focus is on these near misses. And experts are pointing out that the system is strapped. Because of COVID, a lot of airlines, they cut workers, a lot of people took early retirement packages. And so not only do they have staffing issues, but they have a really new workforce and they`re calling for more training.\par \par \uc2 They`re also seeing this incredible rebound of the travel industry that they weren`t necessarily expecting so quickly. Here`s what the Airline Pilots Association President said today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JASON AMBROSI, PRESIDENT, AIR LINE PILOTS ASSOCIATION: It`s not just new pilots, it`s new everybody, controllers, flight attendants, ground people. So it`s just in this post-COVID rapid recovery, there`s just so much going on. And it`s so -- I`m very refreshed to hear all the emphasis on training because I couldn`t agree more.\par \par \uc2 In this -- in this environment, when you have so many people fast upgrades, you`ve got, you know, new people flying with each other.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REISER: So, Chris, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator, the acting administrator, said they`re on track right now this year to hire 1,500 new air traffic controllers, 1,800 Next year. We have a lot of people --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New.\par \par \uc2 REISER: New, exactly. And so they`re calling for training and mentorship to try and alleviate that. But we also heard calls for more funding from Congress for the \uc2 FAA\uc2 . In fact, some said the funding right now is piecemeal. They want to see long-term funding packages to help.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 Chris, I also spoke with flight attendants who are more concerned about what`s happening on board with these unruly passengers. They would like to see a national no-fly list. It`s pending in Congress right now. They`d like to see it passed so that these violent offenders aren`t just banned on one airline, they`re banned on all.\par \par \uc2 They say they`d also like to see required on the job self-defense training. Right now, it`s voluntary. They have to do it in their own time.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Randy, Lester, when he talked to the \uc2 FAA\uc2  administrator about these incidents, and whether they were happening more frequently, he replied this as a cluster about two a month. He insists flying is safe. Do you think that people have reason to be nervous?\par \par \uc2 RANDY BABBITT, FORMER FAA ADMINISTRATOR: Well, flying is definitely safe. You know, we`ve had an incredible record of safety over the years. And it`s come from safety management systems. People paying attention, but whenever you see a rash of incidents like this, this is -- I call them yellow blinking lights. This is -- caution lights are on. We`ve -- we need to revisit.\par \par \uc2 And it was spoken while earlier, we wound this industry down with COVID, down to 20 percent of its size, furloughed had excess employees. Now we restored them. and I think it was put and noted very well. We`ve got a lot of people that are either they were off for a while or they`re new hires, whether they`re controllers, pilots, flight attendants throughout the industry.\par \par \uc2 And I think the \uc2 FAA\uc2  was wise to call, you know, the, quote, call to action. Let`s take a look at this. Is there commonality here? What is going on? Some of them are just simple encroachments into a defined safety boundary area where you don`t want two airplanes closer than, but that`s a warning sign too. When you see too many of those, what are we doing? We tried to move too much traffic, you know, without the proper dialogue. You know, this will -- this will come out with more investigation.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, there was an op-ed today in The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  by the - - a former president of the \uc2 Air Line Pilots Association\uc2 , he says that airline safety is actually at a breaking point, quote, "The number nature and potential tragedy of the results involved are unacceptable and concerning. We cannot wait for our aging and understaffed aviation infrastructure to break and to tragedy to occur to demand action. We cannot rely on luck and quick thinking to keep our skies safe."\par \par \uc2 So whether you think it`s that much of a wake-up call that`s needed, what can and should be done quickly?\par \par \uc2 BABBITT: Well, I think the call to action is a great first step. And I think, you know, we -- all of these processes have safety management systems. And those systems are designed with a coalition of the right people, the industry and people themselves, the employees, the unions, and the \uc2 FAA\uc2 . They set guidelines. It`s a time to revisit those, reemphasize those.\par \par \uc2 Safety is not a pattern (ph). Safety is something you do every day. It has to become part of your operational existence. And sometimes you need to refresh people`s memories of that. And, you know, just revisit.\par \par \uc2 The other point and Captain (inaudible) made a very good point, I read that editorial. And his point was well made in the infrastructure side. We have had too many cases where aging equipment, you know, the ground detection equipment radar, for example, as they call it, Airport Surface Detection Equipment.\par \par \uc2 We only have (technical difficulty) airports. That should be at every airport, it`s radar, it warns the controllers, it warns the pilots, but it requires money. And there`s a lot of areas where we simply have aging technology, aging equipment, and the training has to keep up with it. All of those things have to go together in a package.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Randy Babbitt, Lindsey Reiser, thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 President Biden calling for common sense to combat gun violence. I`ll speak with someone who was with the president for that announcement, California`s Attorney General, about what needs to be done, and how schools can be protecting themselves, that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:17]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: On the same day the country saw the 112 mass shooting this year, President Biden announced his latest effort to stretch gun laws as far as his presidential powers will let him, even while acknowledging he can only do so much without Congress` help.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDE, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In the meantime, my executive order directs my Attorney General will take every lawful action possible, possible to move us as close as we can to universal background checks without new legislation. I just -- it`s just common sense to check whether someone is a felon, a domestic abuser, before they buy a gun.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: California Attorney General, \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 , was at that event with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He joins me now. It`s good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 Look, I`m curious about what you think that this could accomplish, this increase in background checks. And I`m asking that in particular, because we`ve had a number of mass shootings recently where we were told that the gun was legally purchased. So how far could that go in helping keep the guns out of people who use them for really dangerous purposes.\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA, ATTORNEY GENERAL OF CALIFORNIA: This is massive what the president has proposed here and what he`s -- actually what he`s done through his executive order, because it provides a nationwide floor of gun safety policies that will apply everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we have various states with different approaches. And the executive order is full of evidence-based, data-based approaches that we know work. Background checks work and he`s moving as far as he can towards universal background checks as possible. Red flag laws, they work. They get guns out of the hands of people before they can hurt somebody. Safe storage works. We shouldn`t be marketing to kids. We should make sure that we`re -- have appropriate oversight of gun dealers who violate the law.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:08]\par \par \uc2 So this is a pretty broad and robust and far reaching set of data proven, data tested, evidence-based approaches that`ll keep people safe. So you got to follow the evidence when you`re making gun policy. And the evidence says that this executive order will save lives.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Your state has the tightest gun laws in the country. It`s one of only eight, for example, that ban assault weapons, something the president keeps pushing for. What do you say to those who might argue none of that has stopped mass shootings in your state? You`ve had 12 so far this year. So, therefore, their argument goes, those laws can`t be very effective.\par \par \uc2 BONTA: I say that the data, the evidence does not lie, that California has the strongest gun laws, gun safety laws in the nation, and one of the lowest firearm mortality rates in the nation, 58 percent lower child firearm mortality rate than the national average.\par \par \uc2 One is because of the other. Our gun laws have made us safer. Are they perfect? Are we immune to America`s disease of gun violence? No. But they have saved countless lives. And if more states and the federal government did what California has done, followed our blueprint, universal background checks.\par \par \uc2 Assault weapons ban, large capacity magazine ban, robust use of red flag laws. More lives would be saved throughout this nation. So we need less kneeling at the altar of the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , where we care more about the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , and money and guns than we do about children and children`s lives. We need more common sense and more courage. And this is not a mystery. This is not hard. Follow California`s lead and we`ll all be safer. It`s as simple as that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Even among parents who agree with your point of view, where gun laws should go, there`s a concern, right? They understand that there is a House that is run by Republicans, that the fight that has been had over and over and over again, and has not been as successful as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or the democratic presidents before him would like it to have been.\par \par \uc2 And so they`re wondering, how do I keep my kids safe, right? We found a story out of Alabama where a school spent $120,000 on two whiteboards. And when they weren`t being used as whiteboards, they turned into bullet proof shelters. Is that what needs to happen between now and when some of these national laws maybe get passed?\par \par \uc2 BONTA: Let me -- let me answer that in a couple of different ways. First, we cannot give up on pushing in Congress. Congress is not absolved of their responsibility to act. If they have courage and common sense, they will act. They did last year with the Bipartisan Safer Communities Act, that was important. A first time -- one of the first time they`ve acted in decades, but important that they did and I think possible to do more, and they should continue to push to do more at the congressional level.\par \par \uc2 You know, when you`re -- when you`re talking about things like whiteboards that turn into a gun safety shelters, or arming teachers with guns, or all of the active shooter drills that we have, that increase the fear and anxiety of our children. I think you need to ask yourself, where are your priorities?\par \par \uc2 We know what can stop the gun violence, what can stop the mass shootings. Do all of those places where they`re using those shelters and proposing arming of teachers, do they have universal background checks? Do they have red flag laws? Do they have assault weapons bans? Do they have large capacity magazine bans? That`s what they should be focusing on. That`s what works. That`s what he data and the evidence says will save children`s lives. So we got to have our priorities straight. We have to have the courage of our convictions and the courage to act here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: California Attorney General, \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 , who was with the president today, thank you so much. Appreciate you taking the time.\par \par \uc2 Power outages across the Northeast, California communities underwater. Is this the new normal?\par \par \uc2 And former Trump fixer, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , resuming his testimony and the hush money probe. So what comes next once he wraps up? That`s all ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:48]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Pilots and drivers are facing brutal whiteout conditions across the Northeast, as the season`s first official nor`easter dumped feet of snow across the region. Right now, more than 170,000 homes from New York to Maine are without power.\par \par \uc2 And take a look at this, that plane struggling to land amid snow flurries early this morning in Rochester, New York. And forecasters warned the high winds and snow are expected to continue through the day today.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is NBC`s Antonia Hylton on the ground in Worcester, Massachusetts. So, Antonia, Worcester has been blanketed by what I understand is two feet of snow. Doesn`t look that high where you are. What`s it like on the ground?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, Worcester actually spans a fair amount of space here, the county, the city. So where I am is about 13 inches, but in Worcester Hills, they have about 30 inches, so well over two feet.\par \par \uc2 And the challenge right now isn`t so much the snow, there`s no more coming down now. It`s that the wind combined with yesterday`s heavy snow has piled up on top of things like lampposts and power lines, and can continue to cause some issues here.\par \par \uc2 The city has thankfully had about 325 trucks going all around the city, clearing as many streets as they can, even as it was continually snowing. They just didn`t want that sticky build-up, making it unsafe for people.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, as a matter of caution, things are still a bit delayed this morning, school got started two hours late. People are still teleworking in many cases for the residents that I`ve talked to. But now things are starting to get back to normal. Yesterday, here in Massachusetts, about 62,000 people without power. Now, we`re down to almost zero. But across New England, as you mentioned, there are still tens of thousands of people waiting to get back to normal.\par \par \uc2 You know, the one good thing about this region of the country is these are people who are used to snowstorms. This was the first big nor`easter of this season. But usually people who`ve lived here, they have things like backup generators. They`re ready. They keep food and extra supplies and batteries and flashlights during the winter time.\par \par \uc2 And so many of the people who I spoke to say that today they`re getting back, they`re getting themselves to work, to school and they feel like by tomorrow morning, things are going to be, you know, business as usual here, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Knock on wood. Antonia Hylton, thanks for braving though the storm for us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 How`s this for sticker shock? Two bowling lanes for two hours, $418.90. Call it dynamic pricing, surge pricing, where it`s happening and why, and the difference it`s making for all of our wallets, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:33]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Fair warning, it may be coming your way, sometimes shocking spike in prices for things like movie tickets, bowling, golf. Here`s an NBC`s Sam Brock.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): From movie theaters to the links, and even bowling alleys. The cost of a family outing, at certain times, is surging. It`s a practice industry insiders called dynamic pricing, where some businesses charge customers at least 20 to 35 percent more based on demand, location, even time of day.\par \par \uc2 Similar to what caused the Swift rifts between \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  and Taylor Swift Fans.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, my gosh, we spent $899 per ticket. Dynamic pricing is the worst.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: But those fluctuating prices are now going beyond concert tickets and theme parks, flooding everyday activities as reported by the Wall Street Journal with some bowling alleys striking at people`s pocketbooks.\par \par \uc2 Jolene Theory (ph) wanted to treat her family, but the price of one lane for her party of eight, 500 bucks left her jaw in a 7-10 split.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: At the end of the day, bowling is not an activity that will keep on our agenda anymore.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: At Miami Lakes Golf Club, a prime weekend tee time of $140 could be twice the price of teeing off on a weekday afternoon. Great for someone like David Plunkett (ph).\par \par \uc2 How does it work to your benefit?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Fortunately, I`m not a guy who has to golf at a certain time. I work in a business where I`m flexible.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The Miami Lakes golf director says they borrowed the strategy from their hotel business next door.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s our revenue from golf rounds alone increased by over 47 percent.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Meanwhile, movie theater chain, AMC, is double dipping on dynamic pricing, charging more for the evening shows than the (inaudible) which are 30 percent cheaper. And in some cities, they also charge extra for seats with a preferred sight line.\par \par \uc2 For Marco and Ariana Garcia (ph), they`ve done father-daughter movie dates since she was a kid.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I prefer coming at night instead, but I guess like the practicality of it. It`s better (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Experts say more businesses are tracking consumer spending habits to determine when or even what to charge more for.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you know that consumers demand a product or a service during certain times, it will allow you to calculate the willingness to pay more.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: To avoid the sticker shock of dynamic pricing, check online for promo codes and discounts that can offset peak pricing. Look for loyalty programs that`ll will allow you to lock in standard rates. And if you want to dodge sticker shock, book that waterpark or tee time as far out as possible to make the most of your buck without paying a premium.\par \par \uc2 Sam Brock, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Wow. OK. We`ve got a lot to cover in the second hour of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. Let`s get right to it.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230316ej3f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for March 14, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Neal Katyal \par \uc2 6,879 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March