ent defense of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lawyer, arguing Donald Trump`s defense as all Trump lawyers do, this time, against the testimony to a grand jury incriminating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that was delivered this week and will be more delivered tomorrow, by a former Trump lawyer.\par \par \uc2 So, a Trump lawyer comes on MSNBC tonight to argue against a former Trump lawyer who has described the way \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  paid off Stormy Daniels to cover up their very brie sexual encounter, according to Stormy Daniels, that`s where we are tonight. This being Trump world, patience is already always rewarded.\par \par \uc2 And so tonight, we can wonder, exactly how much patience will it take to wait for the day when the Trump lawyer who` defending \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on MSNBC tonight will be the Trump lawyer who is accusing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of something criminal in the future, thereby, subjecting tonight`s Trump lawyer to being attacked by the next Trump lawyer, and will the cycle go on for the rest of Donald Trump`s life -- the cycle of Trump lawyer defends Trump vehemently on TV followed by Trump loyal turn against Trump, followed by that Trump lawyer is attacked by new Trump lawyer, who then eventually turns against Trump and so on and so on and so on.\par \par \uc2 The astonishing thing about the Trump lawyer tonight on MSNBC attacking the former Trump lawyer, is that now, both of those lawyers have publicly defended \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the Stormy Daniels case. Here is \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , vehemently defending \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  against the accusation that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had a very brief sexual encounter with Stormy Daniels, which then became the subject of $130,000 in hush money payment from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to Stormy Daniels.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 ` statement about that: Neither the Trump organization nor the Trump campaign was a party to the transaction with Ms. Clifford, and neither reimbursed me for that payment either directly or indirectly. The payment to Ms. Clifford was lawful, and was not campaign contribution or campaign expenditure by anyone.\par \par \uc2 That was \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  back then, now, he saying, exactly the opposite of all that. Here is tonight`s \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , whose name is actually Joseph Tacopina, telling Ari Melber, the only relevant legal point about the Stormy Daniels case, that the lawyer made in tonight`s interview.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE TACOPINA, ATTORNEY FOR \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : First of all, the crucial distinction between separating campaign funds for persona funds, right? On personal fund usage, here`s the bright line, in any case regards to Stormy Daniels, if the spending were the filming of a commitment, or the expenditure, would exist irrespective of the campaign, it`s not a campaign law violation, end of story. This would exist irrespective of the campaign.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, that`s a little preview of Donald Trump`s defense, when and if he`s indicted by a Manhattan grand jury in the Stormy Daniel case, which could happen this week. But it anticipates a crime that the district attorney might not include in the charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . If the Manhattan district attorney brings charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , it will certainly involve the misdemeanor charge o falsifying business records to cover up the Stormy Daniel hush money payment.\par \par \uc2 What the Trump lawyer is anticipating is that the district attorney will be able to enhance the charges to a felony by saying that the crime of falsifying business records was committed in conjunction with another crime of violating campaign finance laws of the state of New York, the theory of that charge would be, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  paid off Stormy Daniels to keep her quiet, and the last week`s presidential campaign, and because Stormy Daniels silence had value to Donald Trump`s campaign, $130,000 payment was, in fact, an illegal campaign expenditure. That`s the essence of the federal case brought successfully against \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  for being the conduit for paying off that $130,000.\par \par \uc2 If that charges filed, we now know, the defense \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will mount, which is I would`ve paid off Stormy Daniels even I were not presidential candidate, therefore, it can`t be a campaign finance violation. But it can be a tax violation in the state of New York because paying off Storm Daniels was not an actual business expense, so, any deduction that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or his company used for that $130,000 would be in violation of New York tax law. That potential violation was not mentioned by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ` lawyers tonight.\par \par \uc2 Whenever you watch a lawyer defending \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on TV you always have to wonder, how long -- how long before the lawyer turned against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? How long before the lawyer is convicted of crimes involving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? How long before the lawyer has a law license suspended because of the work done for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Because all of those things have happened to Trump lawyers and those things are still happening to Trump lawyers.\par \par \uc2 A group of lawyers led by former Massachusetts Attorney General Scott Harshbarger has filed a 22-page complaint against Stefan Passantino, who they alleged committed several crimes, including subordination of perjury, obstruction of justice, witness tampering and bribery, while he was representing January 6th committee witness, Cassidy Hutchinson, while really working for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The 22-page complaint to the office of disciplinary counsel in the Washington, D.C. says, It is a testament to Ms. Hutchinson`s character that she overcame Mr. Passantino` pervasive conduct and testify truthfully, perhaps becoming perhaps the key witness before the committee. Her service to the interest of American democracy and the rule of law cannot be overstated.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Mr. Passantino`s efforts to obstruct and interfere with he contribution to the legislative and historical record cannot be rationalized, dismissed or ignored. If our professional code of ethics is to serve its critical purposes, in her testimony, after changing lawyers, Cassidy Hutchinson testified that Stefan Passantino repeatedly encouraged her to forget things in the course of her interviews with the committee, saying quote, Stefan never told me to lie. He specifically told me, I don`t want you to perjure yourself, but I don`t recall isn`t perjury. They don`t know what you can and can`t recall.\par \par \uc2 In another instance, Cassidy Hutchinson asked him, quote, but if I do recall something, but not every little detail, Stefan, can I still say I don`t recall and he said, yes and what the lawyers in the complaint deem as evidence of bribery, Stefan Passantino reiterated to Cassidy Hutchinson, she would be taken care of by Trump world, offering her employment in exchange for favorable testimony.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to get you a really good job in Trump world. You don`t need to apply other places. We`re going to get you taken care of. We want to keep you in the family.\par \par \uc2 Stefan Passantino refused to disclose to Cassidy Hutchinson, his client, who was paying for her legal fees when she asked him that. Would you mind letting me know where the funding for this is coming from? I want to thank them. I want to thank whoever it is because under kind of figure things out.\par \par \uc2 And he said, if you want to know at the end, we`ll let you know. But we`re not telling people we`re funding is coming from right now.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:01]\par \par \uc2 Don`t worry, we`re taking care of you like, you`re never going to get a bill for this. So, if that`s where you`re worried about.\par \par \uc2 Cassidy Hutchinson did not want a lawyer from Trump world. Explain to her mother this way, quote, I was like, no, I`m completely indebted to these people and I was like, and they will ruin my life, mom, if I do anything that they don`t wan me to do. And how scared she was -- she told her mother, how scared she was to be indebted to the Trump people when her family could not help her out financially.\par \par \uc2 And it wasn`t just that I had Stefan sitting next to me. It was almost like I felt like I had Trump looking over my shoulder because I knew, in some fashion, it would get back to him if said anything that he would find disloyal. And the prospect of that genuinely scared me. You know, I`ve seen this world ruined people`s lives, or try t ruin people`s careers. I`ve seen how vicious they can be.\par \par \uc2 A complaint describes the many ways that Stefan Passantino obstructed justice when it came to this client. Mr. Passantino did not just try to get Ms. Hutchinson to make false statements and misleading omissions. He also tried to get her to resist a third subpoena or to state that she would be an unwilling witness, and discouraged her from preparing for her testimony by reviewing her calendar or other documents.\par \par \uc2 Cassidy Hutchinson told the committee that her breaking point with Stefan Passantino was when he advised her to stop speaking to the committee and risk being in contempt of Congress. Quote, contempt is a small risk, but running to the right is better for you. I took that as Fox News, Trump world, Republicans, I`m going to use air quotes around Republicans. Are going to defend you, they`re going to blast the committee for holding you in contempt if they do that he said, that`s enough of reason for the committee to no hold you in contempt because they`re going to get such bad press over it, and I kept reiterating to him, but, Stefan, if they do prosecute me, I theoretically could go to prison, right? And he said, Cassidy, DOJ will not prosecute for being held I contempt when you`ve already given the committee so much. You need to trust me on this. This is the best option for you.\par \par \uc2 And I said, this is still on the phone, Stefan, I really don`t want to gamble with this. He said, well, just keep giving it some thought. I`ll talk with some people too. But we really think this is what`s best for you, Cass, like, this needs to end at some point, and I think it just needs to end now. In my mind, I thought, this does need to end now. This being our attorney-client relationship\par \par \uc2 The lawyer`s complaint says the office of disciplinary counsel should promptly initiate an investigation of Mr. Passantino`s conduct and if the facts described above are confirmed, seek his disbarment.\par \par \uc2 Leading off our discussion tonight is Stephen Gillers, professor at NYU law school and an expert on legal ethics. Also with us, \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , former acting U.S. solicitor general and an MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 And, Neal, let me just begin with you and what we -- a little sliver of Trump`s legal defense that we learned in Ari Melber`s interview tonight, with the Trump lawyer, where his -- he`s saying that the can -- they can be the wrap, as I were, on any notion of campaign finance violations even though that`s what \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  was exactly what he was found guilty of in federal court.\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yeah, that`s a lawyer versus lawyer dispute, Cohen versus this new lawyer. If there is an indictment, which as I suspect there will be, by the New York district attorney, that`s going to be reviewed in court. And the prosecution is going to have to prove beyond, reasonable doubt, that \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  is saying is right.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s not that difficult to lift, quite honestly, these payments were made right before the 2016 election, and you have to be an idiot to think that it wasn`t going to benefit your campaign, when you`re paying it at that particular moment in that particular rush. And, Lawrence, you`re absolutely right to say, there`s an number of other problems wit the payments, like the tax consequences, because last I checked, paying $130,000 to the porn star you slept with is not a legal expense.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, Professor Gillers, when you listen to that the accusations laid out about Stefan Passantino and his representation of Cassidy Hutchinson, this looks like something that was more than just his thinking.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:04]\par \par \uc2 It certainly reads as something that is not this lawyer making his own decisions for this client. It sounds like there`s a wider conspiracy in that kind of representation.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN GILLERS, NYU LAW SCHOOL PROFESSOR: He abdicated his responsibilities to Hutchinson. He wasn`t really working for Hutchinson he was working for Trump world she was led to believe that he was her lawyer. If the allegations in the complaint are true, he managed to cross more red lines, criminal and ethical in a brief period of time, than is imaginable. And what I say he abdicated his responsibility, I don`t mean by incompetence or negligence or recklessness, all of those also, the fact that he was prepared to put her in criminal jeopardy, with several crimes, in order to protect Trump world from what she might say if she were allowed to appear and speak truthfully before the committee.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , we`re seeing this in different jurisdiction around the country, where these different Trump lawyers have worked and where they are. Where they have the right to practice it`s now a mounting list o Trump lawyers who are facing disciplinary proceedings. \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  was the most prominent of the first round of people who had their license to practice law suspended and it seems like we`ll have more of this\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Yeah. So, Lawrence, generally, do have some hesitations about attorney liability here. I do really want advocacy, I`m worried about reprisals on lawyers, and I do think sometimes the public confuses the sins of a client for the sins of the lawyer. So, I`m inclined, generally, t give people the benefit of the doubt and wait for the facts. But Steven`s -- Professor Gillers is absolutely right with respect to Passantino, if this is true, forget about the duty of candor, there`s a duty to your own client, and it look like he betrayed that with having a secret client in the name of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It`s straight out of like the mafia playbook, not the Oval Office playbook.\par \par \uc2 And then you broaden that lens out, not just a Passantino but \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2 , \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , John Eastman and so on, it`s a rogue`s list. I think there are 17 different Trump attorneys at this point who`ve been officially sanctioned. So, at this point, any lawyer representing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , is essentially playing Russia roulette with their legal license.\par \par \uc2 And if I could just isolate on person, for example, Jenna Ellis, who last week, admitted in the Colorado ethics board that she misrepresented things and not in minor ways. She literally said, we have over 500,000 votes in Arizono that are cast illegally. She admitted, in a fil document, that was a misrepresentation. She went out and tweeted, well, I didn`t lie, I didn`t essentially make a false statement.\par \par \uc2 I mean, give me a break. I mean, she said she acted recklessly, she signed that document she said her actions undermine the American confidence in the presidential election, she said, she admitted she acted with selfish motives 500,000 votes in Arizona that`s preposterous. That`s one out of every seven votes in Arizona, she was saying was cast illegally.\par \par \uc2 And these lies have serious consequences, Lawrence, at this point, today, many Americans still think the election was stolen, and a majority or at least a good chunk of the Republican Party, it`s because of people like Jenna Ellis and her law license does not give her the license to lie to the American people like that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Go ahead, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GILLERS: I think it`s important to distinguish, between Eastman, Giuliani, Powell, and Ellis on one hand, and Passantino on the other. Because what they did, they did publicly. We saw their sins. We could respond. They could be challenged.\par \par \uc2 Passantino was working quietly. All he had to do was rely on Hutchinson`s compliance with his instruction, except for her bravery, her courage, her recognition that something was wrong here and her willingness to speak up which is remarkable for such a young person in a major position like this.\par \par \uc2 We might never know about this. He was working quietly secretly, working his illegal and unethical conduct against back against Hutchinson, and against the committee, and against the administration of justice, if the allegations are true.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, that is such an important distinction.\par \par \uc2 Professor Steven Gillers and \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , thank you both very much for starting off our discussions tonight.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Thank you\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GILLERS: Thank you\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: South Carolina Republican are now trying to enact a death penalty for pregnant women who do not want to be pregnant. Will they call it the pro-life death penalty? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The term pro-life is the single most effective example of what former Harvard professor turned senator Daniel Patrick Moynihan used to call semantic infiltration. Semantic infiltration is the art of forcing your opponents to use your language.\par \par \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  did that when he created the TV channel he calls Fox News when it has and continues to have absolutely nothing to do with news.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:07]\par \par \uc2 But, still, everyone in the real news business commonly falls into this semantic infiltration trap of referring to Rupert Murdoch`s propaganda channel as Fox News.\par \par \uc2 Many of the same people fall into the semantic infiltration trap of referring to abortion opponents by the term they created for themselves, pro-life. The anti-abortion movement has never been, and will never be, pro-life, and they have proven that once again, this time in South Carolina, where the antiabortion movement is now pushing legislation that would subject pregnant women who don`t want to be pregnant to the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 The so-called pro-life movement has never been opposed to the death penalty. Now, the falsely self-proclaimed pro-life movement wants to actually create a new death penalty for pregnant women, the bill in the South Carolina House of Representatives has 16 Republicans cosponsors, for bill that would, quote, ensure that an unborn child who is victim of homicide is afforded equal protection under the homicide laws of the state that bill states that any act that ends of pregnancy is a, quote, willful prenatal homicide. And, of course, in South Carolina that crime carries the death penalty.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Michelle Goodwin. She is the chancellor professor of law at the \uc2 University of California-Irvine\uc2 , and the author of "Policing the Womb: Invisible Women and the Criminalization of Motherhood".\par \par \uc2 Also with us, Mary Ziegler, legal historian and law professor. She is the author of "Dollars for Life: The Antiabortion Movement and the Fall of the Republican Establishment".\par \par \uc2 Professor Goodwin, let me begin with you. Of course, the pope is famously consistent on what could be termed a pro-life position. In that, he does oppose all use of the death penalty, he opposes abortion, but does not think that it is the number on issue that should be approached in a harsh enforcement style. Boy, these people do.\par \par \uc2 But most people involved in the American pro-life movement are not just fully in favor of the death penalty, many of them were discovering now in South Carolina, specifically want to use the death penalty on women who may want to end their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE GOODWIN, LAW PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA, IRVINE: Well, your point, Lawrence, about nomenclature, lexicon mattering, is important. This is an anti-abortion movement. This is not a pro-life movement. It`s not only in South Carolina, certainly, they`re saying the quiet part out loud, with the number of Republican lawmakers that have signed on to this legislation that would lead to the death penalty against women who want to terminate their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 We`ve also seen in Arkansas, in Kentucky and Louisiana. There are lawmakers that are proposing the same in fact, some years ago, and I wrote about this in Georgia, the lawmaker who pushed forward the bill for embryo adoption after it wasn`t acted said, no, what this allows for is for embryos to have the same constitutional rights as pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 And so, what we see with this disregard for the life, the health, the safety, the personhood of women writ large in the states.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Professor Ziegler, we see to be entering a new stage o more and more extreme, definitely entering a new stag of more and more extreme laws being proposed by this group after they have succeeded in getting the Supreme Court to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 MARY ZIEGLER, LEGAL HISTORIAN: Yeah, there`s a rising group of self- proclaim they call themselves abolitionists within the antiabortion movement who argue, the only way to guarantee equal rights for this fetal person is to punish pregnant women and other pregnant people as harshly as you would punish anyone else for murder. This is a descendant faction within the movement.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a threat that you have to take seriously, both on its face, but also because I shifts the overt window, I makes it seem more normal Republicans to embrace the idea of something like executing women for abortion to adopt even more sweeping punishments themselves one way or another, we`re moving toward extremes, in what appears to be normal for the Republican Party as it`s changing pretty rapidly.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Professor Goodwin, this when you talk about policing the womb, as you have in your book, where does this fit in the history of it?\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: Well, Lawrence, there is a very long history within and let me just say, building off of what Professor Ziegler has mentioned, Roe v. Wade was a 7 to 2 opinion, five of those seven justices were Republican appointed. So this this just shows the extreme to which this new faction will go. But this policing, Lawrence, goes way back to American colonialism, to American slavery, the policing of black women`s reproduction in horrific kinds of ways, black women specifically being used for breeding, you can find thousands of advertisements that advertise either for the return of the breeding wench that escaped, or to sell them.\par \par \uc2 But we also see this kind of turn at the turn of the 20th century with American eugenics where poor white people were targeted for coercive sterilization. That too is a part of a policing.\par \par \uc2 Who are the people that are deemed worthy enough to be able to reproduce in the United States and then who are the people that should be forced into reproduction. And we`ve seen this twist about throughout the history of the United States now. And now in these glaring and horrific and cruel ways, less than a year after the Supreme Court issued its ruling in Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Professor Ziegler, you`ve traced how the Republican Party, as Professor Goodman just said back at the Roe versus Wade stage. Was not in this rabid anti abortion position that they`re in now and certainly, the establishment of the Republican Party wasn`t. \\ We`re seeing something interesting in South Carolina with this bill, which is the number of co-sponsors went up over 20, and then it started dropping. They started having second thoughts. Then they started withdrawing their names as co-sponsors after they spent a day cosponsoring the death penalty for pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re seeing this bill, as it sits there, in South Carolina, test each individual Republican legislator about exactly how they feel about the death penalty for women. But of course, all of them -- all of them remain completely united in their opposition to any use of abortion services.\par \par \uc2 ZIEGLER: Yes, I mean it`s just a testament I think to the conflict facing both the anti-abortion movement in the Republican Party. There`s a divide right now in the anti-abortion movement about how much to punish women. There`s a divide within the Republican Party as we know, after the 2022 midterms which was sort of disastrous for the GOP on abortion, about how much to embrace these extreme positions on abortion when they poll badly especially among Gen-Z voters but just generally. And I think you see that with these kinds of bills.\par \par \uc2 But I think the important point is whether or not this bill passes soon which I don`t think it will. We`re going to see more and more of this in the years to come. And it`s going to be more and more normalized to calls for the punishment of women.\par \par \uc2 And so I think now is the time to sound the alarm. Now is the time to take this seriously. Before these bills are actually passing rather than when it`s too late\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Professor Mary Ziegler and Professor Michele Goodwin, thank you both very much for joining our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 ZIEGLER: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, the American military and the Russian military have their first actual contact today since Vladimir Putin`s war in Ukraine began. It happened in the sky over the Black Sea. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:33:50]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today, for the first time since the beginning of Vladimir Putin`s war in Ukraine, American military equipment came into direct physical contact with Russian military equipment in the sky over the Black Sea.\par \par \uc2 The United States blamed Russia saying, quote, "The incident demonstrates a lack of competence in addition to being unsafe and unprofessional."\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the United States` European command said, "At approximately 7:03 a.m., one of the Russian SU27 aircraft struck the propeller of the MQ- 9 drone, causing U.S. Forces to have to bring the MQ-9 down in international waters. Several times before the collision, the SU-27s dumped fuel on and flew in front of the MQ-9 in a reckless, environmentally unsound and unprofessional manner.\par \par \uc2 One U.S. military official described the incident to NBC News as a demonstration of the Russian pilots` complete ineptitude. That was the phrase -- complete ineptitude.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the Russian ministry of defense claimed the drone had been flying with its transponders off, and denied coming into contact with it.\par \par \uc2 The Russian ambassador in Washington was summoned to the State Department today to hear the United States, quote "strong objections".\par \par \uc2 [22:39:57]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Timothy Snyder, professor of history at \uc2 Yale University\uc2 . He is the author of "The Road to Unfreedom: Russia, Europe, America".\par \par \uc2 Professor Snyder, thank you very much for joining us tonight. This incident, I hope, does not sound like the kind of thing that could create any significant increase in tensions. I would assume that at the State Department today there was some stressing or possibly by both sides, that this was something that should not have happened?\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHY SNYDER, HISTORY PROFESSOR, YALE UNIVERSITY: That`s my read on it as well, Lawrence. I mean this is the kind of thing which is not really uncommon. It`s not uncommon for there to be this sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 You said this was the first example of this. It`s the first example of this that`s been reported on. I would put this in the category of deconfliction, right. This is the kind of thing that people generally do want to avoid, and the Russian ambassador`s statement about this has been much less provocative than the Russian ambassador`s statements about other subjects.\par \par \uc2 And I mean, I`m not an expert on this, but what the military folks seem to be saying is that this is really kind of an example of bad flying on the Russian side.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In terms of the politics of where we`re headed in this country on this issue, you now have both of the leading Republican candidates for president, Trump and DeSantis, coming out with anti-Ukraine positions, pro- Russian positions in effect, DeSantis saying -- telling \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 , he doesn`t see what American interest is involved at all in supporting Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And so the message from both of them, is if you get either one of them as the next president of the United States, the message to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is all you have to do is hang on and wait for them.\par \par \uc2 SNYDER: Yes, and that is itself strategically quite dangerous. I mean I expect Mr. DeSantis is calculating that Biden is going to win this thing. You know, the Ukrainians are going to win and so DeSantis doesn`t want this to seem like a feather in Biden`s cap.\par \par \uc2 The problem with that is that when you take that narrow, political approach, you end up creating much more difficult, much dangerous world because you`re incentivizing, by your words -- if you`re DeSantis, you`re incentivizing the Russians to stay in the war.\par \par \uc2 And you know, thereby, what you think of as a narrow political calculation actually becomes a geopolitical disaster. And I think, you know, at some point, Mr. DeSantis is going to have to understand that if you`re going to run for president, you have to think about foreign policy seriously. You can`t just treat it as a kind of (INAUDIBLE) or a kind of side issue in today`s weekly political calculations.\par \par \uc2 You can`t just think about domestic opinion. You have to actually develop some kind of foreign policy. And you can`t do foreign policy, you know, without engaging the people who are interested in harming the United States of America. You can`t -- you form policy by preemptively conceding everything to the people who want to harm the United States of America which is where he is right now unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, I have to say, we use to think that about running for president, especially when it came to governors who didn`t have the advantages that senators have of serving on foreign relations committees and things like that. That the governors were going to have to learn to take foreign policy seriously.\par \par \uc2 The last two Republican nominees for president, both \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , never took foreign policy seriously. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016 never said a half serious word about it. He never said a serious word about it as president. And he never said a serious word about it in his reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 So we have one party that is perfectly happy to tolerate and support a nominee who never says anything serious about foreign policy and therefore, ends up being very encouraging to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SNYDER: I mean, that`s the story of four years of Trump. Four years of Trump that encouraged, not just \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  but the dictators around the world because he made it very clear that in so far as he had an interest in matters foreign, it was in buddying up to dictators.\par \par \uc2 And the thing is, you know, we are -- whether we like it or not, we are in this world. And whether we like it or not, Russia invaded Ukraine and the Russian invasion with Ukraine matters for the international legal order, which allows America to be prosperous.\par \par \uc2 It matters in that if we let the Ukrainians lose, it will be encouraging nuclear proliferation. It matters because the Ukrainians defeating Russia is key to preventing war in Europe down the line.\par \par \uc2 It matters because Ukrainian resistance is what is making Beijing think twice before starting an adventure over Taiwan. It matters because of food supplies around the world. We can`t just close our eyes to that because if you do, those things will come back and they will make a great deal of difference to average Americans.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainians are doing a hug amount for American national security right now, basically for nothing, I mean for a penny on our tax dollar they`re doing a hug amount for American national security.\par \par \uc2 And it would be sad to see major politicians of any party not recognize that.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:59]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yale Professor Timothy Snyder, thank you very much for joining us tonight\par \par \uc2 SNYDER: Glad to be with you\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden announced a new executive order on background checks for gun purchases and he made the announcement at the location of one of this country`s recent mass murders.\par \par \uc2 David Hogg, a survivor of the massacre at \uc2 Marjory Stoneman Douglas High School\uc2  in Parkland, Florida, will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:26]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We live in a country where it is impossible for the head of state to visit the location of every mass murder. But today, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  spoke in Monterey Park, California, where a mass murderer killed 11 people in January.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: 11 souls taken, nine injured. Private mourning made public and sense of safety shattered. Survivors will always carry the physical and emotional scars, families left behind will never be the same.\par \par \uc2 One of the worst mass shooting in California history, a tragedy that has pierced the soul of this nation here in Monterey Park and San Gabriel Valley, the heart of the Asian American community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The president announced a new executive order today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My executive order direct my attorney general to take every lawful action possible to move us as close as we can to universal background check without new legislation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: As chairman of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  in 1993, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  passed the country`s first ban of assault weapons which remained in effect for ten years. He wants to do it again.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I am determined, once again to ban assault weapons and high capacity magazine. I lead that fight to ban in 1994. In the ten years, that law was in place, mass shootings were down. My Republican friends let it expire ten years later and mass shootings tripled since then - tripled.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s finish the job ban assault weapons ban them again, do it now. Enough. Do something do something. Do something big.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is David Hogg, cofounder of March for Our Lives. He`s a survivor of the school massacre at \uc2 Marjory Stoneman Douglas High School\uc2  in Florida.\par \par \uc2 David, thank you very much for joining us tonight. What was your reaction to the presidents announcement of that executive order today?\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOGG, CO-FOUNDER, MARCH FOR OUR LIVES: It is good, Lawrence. This is progress. This shows the power of young people helping to turn out an vote. President Biden knows that young people were a critical part of who helped elect him I 2020. It is only in large part because of young people -- of all ages, of course -- but I want to especially highlight young people, turning out and voting for gun safety candidates that this change is possible.\par \par \uc2 Is this as much as I would like to see? No, but this is a lot more than any previous president has done on this issue especially with, you know, a divided congress.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, I think that this shows that we can make progress here. Just as similar, you made bipartisan action on this.\par \par \uc2 I think as Americans we need to realize everyone to achieve freedom from gang violence. And ensure that she and ensure that all of our communities and all of our families are safe.\par \par \uc2 We need to change the cycle that we are in right now -- an endless inaction, endless debate and turn it into a cycle of dialogue and action, even -- if small after these shootings.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What is the next action for you?\par \par \uc2 HOGG: Well, right now we have been calling, as we have for the past year plus, for President Biden to put a national emergency on gun violence, to marshal all of the resources available by federal government to help fight against scourge but not just mass shootings. But the daily mass shootings that happen in the form of individual shootings across many communities that are affected by daily acts of gun violence. To address the two thirds of gun deaths that are suicides -- that also need to be addressed as well.\par \par \uc2 You know, when Republicans talk about wanting to fund mental health care more, I do see some of their point. But I think it`s important to acknowledge that we need to fund mental health care to address the two thirds of gun deaths that are suicides but let`s not use mental health care as a scapegoat to avoid talking about the white supremacy that was the motivating factor behind shootings like the one in Buffalo and often the same white supremacy that is espoused by people like the shooter at my high school.\par \par \uc2 We need Americans to continue turning out and showing up on this issue because we have shown, that this is a winning issue. Not a single senator or member of Congress in the House lost their election specifically because they voted for the bipartisan Safer Communities Act, the first piece of federal gun legislation in 30 years. Not a single one.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:58]\par \par \uc2 HOGG: This is a winning issue. And it is the same case with the Republicans that voted to increase gun laws after Parkland in Florida, which now they are trying to weaken and lower the age to buy an AR-15 once again because we raise it to 21.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: David Hogg, I`m sorry, as always, for what you endured and survived at your high school and very much appreciate you joining us tonight\par \par \uc2 HOGG: Thank you. And for anybody who would like to support us, they can go to marchourforlives.com. Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, David. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In tonight`s "Never Forget" segment, here are the words that Kevin McCarthy now pretends he never said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: The president bears responsibility for Wednesday`s attack on Congress by mob rioters. He should have immediately denounced the mob when he saw what was unfolding.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:56]\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: These facts require immediate action by President Trump. Accept his share of responsibility, quell the brewing unrest, and ensure President-Elect Biden is able to successfully begin his term.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230315ej3e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for March 13, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Stephanie Ruhle, Jill Wine-Banks \par \uc2 8,247 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: The stories women directors get to direct matters. Opportunity matters. And yes, diversity, equity, and inclusion in this very diverse country matters. And it also happens to be good for business. It`s not a favor to diverse talent. It`s a favor from them to the culture.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AYMAN MOHYELDIN, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Because of the actions that our regulators already taken, every American should feel confident that their deposits will be there if and when they need them.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Banks closing, stocks falling, and the government rushing to shore up the system. What happened this weekend and how to stop it from happening again? Congresswoman Katie Porter joins me on that. Then --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP LAWYER: This is all about accountability. He needs to be held accountable for his dirty deeds.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All eyes on the grand jury as \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  testifies against his former boss. Tonight, just how close are prosecutors to indicting the ex-President. Plus, how a handpick conservative judge effectively hit an abortion pill hearing from the public? And as Trump tackles DeSantis head on in Iowa, cue the softball interviews.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): And I`ll tell you, you know, as governor, and if you`re determined executive, you know, you can make things happen and we`ve done that in Florida.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN (on camera): Good evening, everyone from New York. I`m Ayman Mohyeldin in for a Chris Hayes. There is a lot going on tonight at this hour as we watched two big stories developed. Earlier today in Manhattan, Donald Trump`s former personal lawyer and fixer, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , testified for three hours in front of a grand jury investigation into the ex- president. We`re going to have a lot more on that story coming up.\par \par \uc2 But first, to the potential economic crisis that unfolded over the weekend. The federal government is now stepping in. It is taking extensive measures to shore up confidence. All of this in the wake of the largest bank failure since 2008. That is almost 15 years. It all began with the collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , the 16th largest bank in this country, whose customers included a broad range of startups and tech companies.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday night, those customers, those customers of that bank tried to withdraw $42 billion, that is amounting to one of the largest bank runs in American history. Silicon Valley could not meet that demand, and therefore, U.S. banking regulators jumped in and took control. As the \uc2 Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation\uc2  worked through the weekend to try and ensure customers would be able to access their funds by Monday, news came out that a second institution imploded. Signature Bank based here in New York also saw an avalanche of withdrawals from customers concerned about the Silicon Valley collapse.\par \par \uc2 And so on Sunday, federal regulators took that over as well. In a joint statement, the Treasury Department, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and \uc2 FDIC\uc2  announced that depositors of Silicon Valley would have access to all of their money starting Monday, and that all depositors at Signature Bank would be made whole. The \uc2 Federal Reserve Board\uc2  also announced steps to shore up other potentially vulnerable banks, making available additional funding to eligible institutions to help assure banks have the ability to meet the needs of all their depositors.\par \par \uc2 And then this morning, President Biden assuring the American people that in fact, banks in this country are safe, and that he will be taking further action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today, thanks for the quick action to my administration over the past few days, Americans can have confidence that the banking system is safe. Your deposits will be there when you need them. Small businesses across the country that deposit accounts at these banks can breathe easier knowing they`ll be able to pay their workers and pay their bills.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to ask Congress and the banking regulators to strengthen the rules for banks to make it less likely this kind of bank failure would happen again, and to protect American jobs and small businesses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: But here`s the thing, the fallout did continue today with shares of smaller regional banks plummeting as much as 60 percent in the case of \uc2 First Republic Bank\uc2 . And as a lot of Americans hold their breath this evening hoping that the damage will be contained, they`re also looking for who`s responsible and all of this.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Senator Elizabeth Warren of Massachusetts who helped establish the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2  writes in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , "No one should be mistaken about what unfolded over the past few days in the U.S. banking system. These recent bank failures, they are the direct result of leaders in Washington weakening the financial rules.\par \par \uc2 So, here`s the question tonight. How exactly did we get here? How do we keep this situation from getting worse?\par \par \uc2 Dean Baker is a co-founder and senior economist at the Center for Economic and Policy Research, who correctly predicted the 2008 financial crisis at least two years before it happened. Stephanie Ruhle, of course, Senior Business Analyst for NBC News and host of the "11TH HOUR" right here on MSNBC. they both join me now.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, it`s great to see you.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:30]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: I know it has been a long day for you, as it has been for the markets in this country and investors. But my question to you is, how did this happen? Did anyone see this coming?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, well, let`s make something clear. Who is it a direct result of? Not necessarily politicians, it`s a direct result of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 `s bad behavior, right? So , there`s obviously political implications. There`s rollbacks associated with Dodd Frank, but it was \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 . So how did we get here? Listen, it was a bank that specialized in servicing the tech industry startups. It was the coolest one you could do business with. It was not cool to do business with a more traditional bank if you`re in the startup world.\par \par \uc2 And they basically forced the hand of a lot of startups. They had huge concentration to that one type of customer. Remember, COVID hits, and that business, boom, think about all the tech investment, think about all the places that really rose during that time. And so, deposits for that bank ballooned. It went from 40 billion to almost 200. The bank made a horrible risk decision and invested a bunch of that money in treasuries and mortgage-backed securities, which as soon as interest rate grows, the value of those assets plummeted.\par \par \uc2 So, they went out realizing they had a hole, communicated terribly, decided we`re going to go out and raise some money, and we`ll take a loss doing so. And what did that do? Massively spooked the markets which resulted in what you just said, their customers taking out over $40 billion overnight. And that`s when a run on the bank happens, right?\par \par \uc2 And we`re now living in a world of digital banking where you can pull your money within seconds, where you can shout, there`s a fire in a -- in a movie theater on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and panic begets panic. And that`s how we got here.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, I was going to say $42 billion is not like an -- you just withdrawal from an ATM machine. I mean, that was a serious fear that must have spread among some of these industries and depositors.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, all of these huge VCs that are with us \uc2 SVB\uc2  told all their startups, get your money out now. And that`s what happened. And now, that`s what you`re seeing happen with other small regional banks that don`t have the type of exposure that SVD did. But guess what, if you`re just an average American, and you hear all this happening, you`re like, I`m going to play it safe. I`m going to go to one of the big banks. I`m going to go to one of the big four.\par \par \uc2 And remember, that`s not what the government wants. They don`t want the biggest banks that were too big to fail to now be even too bigger to fail. And that`s where we are.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And all of the money in this country concentrated in those four banks.\par \par \uc2 Dean, so talk to us about the historical perspective here. How does this situation compare or differ from 2008? Are their circumstances even similar?\par \par \uc2 DEAN BAKER, CO-FOUNDER AND SENIOR ECONOMIST, CENTER FOR ECONOMIC AND POLICY RESEARCH: It`s a radically different situation. I think it`s really important for people understand. In 2008, the banks really were in trouble. They had invested a huge amount of money in mortgages, mortgage-backed securities that had really lost a very large portion of their value. And that was because the housing bubble collapsed. So, in a lot of cases, you had house prices that 40, 50, 60, 70 percent, he mortgages on those houses or mortgage-backed securities, they were almost worthless. So, you really did have banks that were insolvent.\par \par \uc2 In this case, you had banks, well, \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , in particular, that exercise just horrible judgment. I mean, it`s just kind of mind boggling. Like, these were professionals. These were -- the CEO of Silicon Bank got $9.9 million last year. You go, what were you doing? And, you know, one point that can`t be emphasized enough. Elizabeth Warren, Senator Warren made it in her editorial column that you mentioned, they weaken regulation in a way that exactly allowed this to happen. Oftentimes ambiguous, you know, did this regulation -- no, here it`s unambiguous.\par \par \uc2 If they had not weaken the regulation in 2018, the \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  would have been subject to regular stress tests. One of the things you do on a stress test you go, what would happen to this banks finances if interest rates went up by three, four, or five percentage points? They would have caught this. And I mean, the bank should have caught it themselves, but the regulators would have caught it. And they said, you`re going to have to raise more capital, shift some of your deposits, may be both, you know, and they won`t have been in this situation.\par \par \uc2 So, this is directly due to the Republican weakening of regulation that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pushed in 2018. And that`s why we`re here.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But it`s not all Republicans that wanted to partially pull back some of Dodd-Frank. There is an argument to be made and it`s around something called regulatory capture, right? When there`s a huge amount of regulation in an industry, you know, who can afford that regulation? Big, big banks, right? You can say you need 1000 more compliance officers and \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  can say, check it, I can do that in a millisecond. A small regional bank cannot do that.\par \par \uc2 So, there is an argument to be made. You have to be mindful about the regulation that you put on big banks and small banks. But in this case, they deregulated in a way that it opened the door for risky behavior that shouldn`t have taken place.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:21]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Well, I guess the point would be made is the reason why you need the regulation is because you can`t trust the banks to actually behave in a way where they`re not taking these crazy risks.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Which you could often say about anyone, right? Like, unless I make very, very clear rules for my 16-year-old son, he will behave as badly as humanly possible. So, I don`t know why we suddenly think this whole idea, oh, don`t tread on me, let these banks regulate themselves. Because this is the killer.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You`ve got all of these people that are anti-regulation, don`t tread on me small government. And then as soon as the you know what hits the fan in the last five days, those are the first people in line saying \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , we need a bailout. And so, the argument is, either you`re against regulation, and you want, you know, survival of the fittest, or you`re pro- regulation and you want these protections, but it`s pretty rich to now demand both.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes. And so, Dean, to that point, what do you make of the federal government`s actions here so far to contain this crisis? A, is it enough? And B, should the government be stepping in, and for lack of better word, bailout these banks?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: I think it will likely be enough because again, there is no underlying problem here. So, go back to 2008, there was an underlying problem. They had all these mortgages that lost most of their values. So, there was really an underlying problem in 2008-2009. Here`s there isn`t. It`s just, you know, one bank that had maybe a couple signature, there might well be some smaller ones out there. I`m sure there are. But the whole banking system is quite sad.\par \par \uc2 Now, in terms of should they have done it, I mean, I think they had to do something to stem the panic, but it is a pain. You know, I mean, you see, as Stephanie was saying, we`ve got these guys in Silicon Valley. They`re the wizards of the world and this and that. They have tens of millions, hundreds of millions. They go, oh, we messed up, you got to rescue us. Think about this. We`re having this big debate about student loan debt forgiveness. So, we have 18-year-old kids that took out a student loan that maybe was bad judgment, and we have always people saying, oh, we -- you know, we have to build character, we can`t forgive 10,000 of their debt. These guys are getting tens of millions that they don`t have a claim to.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Not all of them. There`s also small mom and pops and individuals also with \uc2 SVB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: I know. I mean, I don`t --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We should speak to that. It`s not all just --\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: I mean, Dean, I`ll give you a chance to respond to that, but I know -- I don`t think that`s the point that Dean is just simply making about that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I know. But I just want to be sure. I`m sure we have people watching saying, hold on.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It`s a --\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Go ahead, Dean. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It`s a cross-section. But just to be clear, they were never going to be zeroed out. There was some horrible, horrible reporting from places that should have known better that said, they`re going to be wiped up. No way on earth. So, what you would have seen -- the FTC announced this Friday, Saturday when they first had the takeover, they were going to issue an advance payment. I don`t know if it be 50, 60 percent, a certificate for the rest, they might well have collected 80, 90, even 95 percent, possibly 100 percent. So, the idea that they were going to lose everything they had there, that it was inconceivable.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Dean Baker, Stephanie Ruhle, I have a feeling you`re going to be talking about this on your show tonight.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Possibly.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: We`ll wait and see. "11 HOUR." Dean, it`s good to see you. Thank you so much. Stephanie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, some late-breaking news that Donald Trump`s former fixer testifies before a grand jury. Why Michael Cohen`s big day under oath has been extended into another day this week? And what it means for a potential indictment of the former president. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: As we await a decision regarding the possible indictment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this week, NBC News has learned that the ex-president will not testify before the New York grand jury that is investigating him. That wide-ranging investigation is looking into an illegal hush money payment made to adult film actress Stormy Daniels. Daniel says the money was to cover up an affair she had with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  before he was President, something he denies. But the probe is moving along just fine without the ex-president`s cooperation.\par \par \uc2 Today, Trump`s former fixer \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  testified before the grand jury for about three hours and he is returning on Wednesday for even more testimony. In fact, back in 2018, Cohen pleaded guilty to facilitating the hush money payment that is in question. And he has been a crucial witness in this investigation meeting with prosecutors at least 20 times.\par \par \uc2 Today, Cohen`s lawyer told reporters his client is seeking accountability for the ex-President`s misdeeds.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LANNY DAVIS, ATTORNEY FOR \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 : Michael has spent a long and productive afternoon. I`ll speak for him today, answering all questions, all facts and completely responses. We`re not going to make any comments about what he said other than to repeat what Michael has said. This is about accountability and this is about facts and the truth, period.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: I`ve been dealing with the same issues for now, more than five years, so I feel absolutely fine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Harry Litman is a former U.S. attorney and Former Deputy Assistant Attorney General in the Department of Justice. Jill Wine Banks served as an assistant Watergate Special Prosecutor and as General Counsel to the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2 . Both join me now. It`s great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Harry, I`ll start with you. It`s not really much of a surprise that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won`t testify, at least I don`t think so. You tell me if you agree or not. And even his lawyers must know putting him under oath and at the risk of perjury would be a huge mistake and a possibility.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:10]\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: A kamikaze mission. Not in chance. They`ve already decided they`re going to indict him anyway. All he could do is go in and either perjure himself or dig a deeper hole. No surprise whatsoever from the -- from the time they offered it.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Jill, there has been a sense that in the past, prosecutors might have been wary to put somebody like \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  on a witness stand. What has changed, if anything?\par \par \uc2 JILL WINE BANKS, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: I don`t think anything much has changed, except that there are more witnesses. We`ve seen a list of people coming in that may be corroborating him that have important testimony. But I think what everybody needs to know is that when you have a crime of this nature, the witnesses are usually people who were involved in committing the crime.\par \par \uc2 It`s not a bank robbery, where some innocent person happens to see the bank robber, pull the gun out or hand the note to the teller. This is a crime, like all the crimes that I`ve prosecuted, where it is the co-conspirators who testify against each other. And so, I personally would never have had a problem putting \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  on the stand. And you start with the question of, I didn`t pick this person as my witness, the person who picked him is the defendant. He selected him to work with him and to do this action on his behalf.\par \par \uc2 So, you clear the air right away that he admits his crimes, and he becomes a credible person for this particular crime.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Harry, there are some probably watching this and saying why now, why is this happening again, for something that was at least in the in the public debate, adjudicated only in the public? But why is this happening again five years or so since?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: It`s a really important question, and I think we don`t 100 percent know. I want to see the indictment on what it looks like before we answer. Bragg, the DA now, came into office a year ago, and the case was very far advanced, but he decided not to go forward. And the delay in kind of mothballs for about six months. Then after this successful trial of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , he dusted it off and they`ve decided, I think picking up on what Jill says, that whatever problems \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  presented as a witness, and we know that was one of the things that made them stay their hand, they believe they have enough corroborating evidence now to go forward. And that`s a big part of what the previous six or seven witnesses over the last couple of weeks I`ve been presenting to the grand jury.\par \par \uc2 But the case will be made or broken on Cohen`s word, and he`s going to be the star witness. And the cross-examination of him it`s going to be a very dramatic centerpiece of the trial.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Jill, there is in addition to \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , as Harry was mentioning there, there is also \uc2 Jennifer Weisselberg\uc2 . And some may have forgotten who she is, but she`s the former daughter-in-law of imprison \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  CFO \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2 , name that we`ve all become familiar with. And despite telling reporters outside the DA`s office that she was going to testify in the Trump case today, The Daily Beast is reporting that Weisselberg was actually at the DA`s office to testify before a separate grand jury, totally unrelated to Trump concerning a personal matter, according to a person familiar with her situation.\par \par \uc2 The media stunt cast doubt on her reliability as a witness and could prevent investigators from using her to testify in the future about alleged tax dodging at the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  according to this person. Do you think, Jill, a stunt like that damages her credibility to the point that she cannot be relied on to testify?\par \par \uc2 WINE-BANKS: It`s not helpful. It would have been better had she not done it in the same way that \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  has been very vociferous in talking about having been to the DA`s office 20 times. It would be better for him not to have done that. I don`t think it totally eliminates the possibility. It may be putting the pressure on Weisselberg to flip. Maybe that`s, you know, as we look at why now, maybe it`s because Weisselberg sees what it`s like to be at Rikers Island, and is like, OK, maybe I should try to cooperate.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve also seen David Pecker come in, we`ve seen \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2  come in, we`ve seen Kellyanne Conway come in. That`s a heavy load of possible testimony against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So, I think that that`s why now is that they have built up the corroboration they need to make credible and believable all of this testimony of \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , who is as Harry said, absolutely the key to all this.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All eyes will be on the Manhattan District Attorney`s office later this week. Harry Litman, Jill Wine-Banks, thank you to the both of you. I greatly appreciate it as always. All right, still ahead, switching gears. The 2024 primary is heating up as the front runner under criminal investigation goes to Iowa. His former vice president talks tough about his own boss but won`t do it under oath. And Fox News lobs some pretty soft ball questions to Florida \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . We`re going to tell you about that.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:25:00]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So, the pilgrimages to Iowa are already underway. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in Davenport tonight. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in the very same city just last Friday. They`re both scoring about the same favorability rating with Republicans in Iowa, but Trump still leads nationally. And despite the best efforts of Fox who have been really, really, really trying to make DeSantis a thing in this country, rolling out a glowing profile of the Florida governor, and even a softball interview.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:31]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, HOST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL: If not this time to run for President, would you think at some time, it`s safe to say that that will be one of your goals?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I would -- I would only -- I would only do stuff if I thought there was a rationale for me to accomplish things on behalf of the people. So, it`s all substance driven about whether I could serve or not serve in a variety of capacities. But I`ll tell you, you know that as governor, and if you`re a determined executive, you know, you can make things happen. And we`ve done that in Florida.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Look, we expect the big announcement.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: It depends how good we do in this legislative session.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Thanks, Governor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, joining me here at the table to talk about what is going on on the 2024 Republican race for president, Jelani Cobb, the dean of the Columbia University School of Journalism and a staff writer for The New Yorker, Tim O`Brien, the Senior Executive Editor for Bloomberg Opinion and the author of Trump Nation: The Art Of Being The Donald.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thank you both for joining us, Jelani, I`ll start with you here. Trump is now going after \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , big, big surprise to no one in this country who thought this race was going to get at some point have, and even though he`s not officially in there. He`s been calling up Ron DeSanctimonious, accusing him of manufacturing beef with \uc2 Disney\uc2  to score culture war points. How resonant do you think that is going to be? Is that a factor?\par \par \uc2 JELANI COBB, DEAN, COLUMBIA UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF JOURNALISM: Well, I mean, I don`t know how that plays in that particular crowd. I mean, but there`s an air of this being like the magician doing the trick for the second time. You know, in 2016, when you saw that huge field of Republicans who were on stage, there was one thing that they all had in common, which was that they had never been insulted to their faces. They had never been -- I mean, Ted Cruz`s father being implicated in the Kennedy assassination, all sorts of outlandish things that were pure tabloid kind of ridicule, and absurdity. But it worked. It`s a little bit harder to sell that trick the same -- again, the second time around, though.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Especially when you have an actual track record. And people can call you on things you did or didn`t get done.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Tim, so Trump is still the front-runner. Is this tactic likely to have an impact on voters? And is there a way forward for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in this race if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues on this path?\par \par \uc2 TIM O`BRIEN, SENIOR EXECUTIVE EDITO, BLOOMBERG OPINION: Well, I think it`ll always have an impact with his base because they love the theatrics. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a performance artist. He`s not a policy-driven politician to say the least. And his base heats that up. The problem for the Republicans and Trump is that they`ve lost moderate voters, they`ve lost the middle and swing voters. And those voters didn`t know Trump in 2016. They knew him by 2020 and they know him now. And he`s done nothing at all to sort of try to create a bridge from this, you know, 25 to 30 percent of the MAGA wing of the Republican Party to build that into an authentic national coalition.\par \par \uc2 The party I think wants DeSantis. The problem is every other Republican doesn`t have any traction with that 30 percent. So, every time you see Trump or DeSantis talking about this, it`s essentially hostage videos, because they don`t know a way out yet and I think they`re in trouble with that.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Speaking of hostage videos, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  polling very behind both of these guys, and it seems just from the tone, perhaps a slight change that he`s attacking \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which means he`s perhaps going after him saying that might be his only hope in this race.\par \par \uc2 I want to read you a part of this. He hasn`t criticized that much, but this weekend, he said history will hold \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  accountable for January 6. Is that going to score -- besides the fact that it`s completely infuriating to hear him say that but then not testify or cooperate with the investigation into actually holding him accountable, is that going to matter with voters?\par \par \uc2 COBB: Well, I mean, I think history -- enough history had to elapse for him to come to the point that history would hold him accountable. You know, we all knew that on January 7. You know, most people who had a reasonable assessment of what was going on understood where the lines of culpability were. He was threatened to be lynched. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , his life was threatened. His family members lives were threatened. And you have this long interval of relative silence about it. It`s hard to see how that resonates anywhere. And --\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Not exactly a profile in courage or a profile in backbone.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Not exactly that, no. Tim, does -- I don`t know if you saw our previous conversation just before you guys came on. We were talking about the possibility that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is indicted. Would that be a factor in how this rape -- excuse me, this race shapes up?\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Only if he can`t run for president. You know, I think his voters don`t care. Lyndon Larouche ran for president I think four or five times after being convicted for tax evasion. Eugene Debs got jailed for socialism. He still ran. The only thing that prevents you from running for president under the Constitution is if you`re a seditionist, which is why I think the DOJ investigation of all of these, the Georgia investigation, the two investigations in New York, it`s the one that has the most consequences for him existentially to able to run for the -- for the presidency.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Politically.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: I have a hard time believing he`ll wind up in jail as a result of any of this litigation. But I think that, you know, for him, he has spent decades of voiding accountability under the law.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Have we passed the threshold of him being held accountable before he can become president? Because let`s take any of these cases that you`ve talked about where he could potentially face an indictment, potentially face criminal consequences. But the timeline that we`re talking about now, we`re two years out. He`s already running and you have the primaries and then potentially an election. Could you be in a scenario where the result of one of these trials is coming down the pipe with him being a second term --\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Yes, that could happen. And then it`s like George Santos on steroids is what is the --what do the Republicans do when you have someone who`s under an active investigation for breaking the law and he`s your leading candidate for the White House.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: You`ve been speaking to a lot of Republicans, certainly Republican, I guess, donors who are going to be shaping this race. How do they feel about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this second time around? Are they all in or are they not as much?\par \par \uc2 COBB: Well, I mean, it`s hard to kind of say based on kind of anecdotal conversations I have with people about this. But the one thing that I will say is that there is a bit of fatigue for all of the things surrounding Trump. There was a particular trade-off that people were willing to make in 2016, the things that he said that he would promise that he would deliver, that he would give conservative judges a place on the Supreme Court and so on, don`t have that same sort of draw. Now, you can`t make those same sorts of appeals especially after Roe v. Wade has been struck down.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And do you think culture wars are still going to be a big issue the second time around?\par \par \uc2 COBB: You bet. Where`s Michael Buffer? Where`s Michael Buffer? You can get the announcement. It`s going to be a fight.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: It is the cultural war.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, I have a feeling you guys are both right on that one. Tim O`Brien, Jelani Cobb, thank you so much. I greatly appreciate your insights.\par \par \uc2 COBB: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Still become, Congresswoman Katie Porter says the collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  was totally avoidable. She`s going to tell me what went wrong and what needs to be fixed next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Five years ago, when Republicans were last in control of the House of Representatives, they use the opportunity to rollback banking regulations put into place after the 2008 financial crisis. As that bill was making its way through Congress, California professor named Katie Porter tweeted her concerns writing that it "puts consumers and our economy at risk and shows just how much power Wall Street banks powerful special interests and their high price lobbyists have in Washington." The bill passed, but Porter went on to win a seat in Congress and she is now fighting to get that bill overturned.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Katie Porter of California, the ranking member on the House Oversight Subcommittee on Health Care and Financial Services, who is also running for Senate next year joins me now. Congresswoman, it`s great to have you back on the program. It`s good to see you. So, talk to me about the collapse here the \uc2 SVB\uc2 . It ranks as the second largest bank failure in the U.S. right behind \uc2 Washington Mutual\uc2  back in 2008, just ahead of Signature Bank, which also just happened a few days ago. What is going on here?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KATIE PORTER\uc2  (D-CA): Well, this comes from a failure to appropriately regulate big banks. And we had after Dodd Frank, appropriate regulations in place for exactly this size of bank, big. And lo and behold, no more than Dodd Frank passed, then people started coming around to politicians` offices, offering them corporate PAC checks and lobbying them to repeal those regulations. And we had Democrats and Republicans join in 2018 to repeal the very capital-holding requirements that create a cushion for this exact size of bank.\par \par \uc2 So, for me to hear colleagues later, when this was happening this weekend say this was unbelievable and how could this happen, was frustrating to say the least. This is what happens when we have politicians who cater to Wall Street instead of working families.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: This morning, I`m sure you saw President Biden addressing the country, assuring people their deposits are safe, that taxpayers are not on the hook for the banks. How confident are you of that assessment?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, I have no doubt that that is what President Biden hopes happens and that is the intent here. But I think we`ve seen before with these kinds of programs that the devil is really in the details. And there are some concerning aspects of how this is being rolled out. One of the things we did with the Dodd-Frank Act after the financial -- the last financial crisis was we said, look, assets have to be marked to market. They have to be -- you have to have value of what they`re actually worth.\par \par \uc2 And what the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is doing with this lending program is saying we`ll treat your collateral at par value rather than what it`s really worth today. So, I understand that when you get into a bank failure, there are no good solutions. That`s exactly why we can`t let this keep happening. We have to repeal the regulation that Trump and Congress passed in 2018 to get us back to a place where we have cushions. We don`t put ourselves where there are no good choices.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:36]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So, you began to answer the question I was just about to ask you which is do you think repealing the 2018 deregulation law will be sufficient protection against this kind of thing going forward, Or is there something else that needs to be done on top of repealing it?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, there are definitely other questions here. But let me be clear, I`m introducing legislation to repeal that 2018 law. It was bad then. I said it was bad then, and I was not alone. There were other folks, including Elizabeth Warren, who pushed against this, but Wall Street`s lobby in Washington is simply too powerful. And they were able to get not just Republicans, but I think almost 50 Democrats between the House and the Senate to sign on to this.\par \par \uc2 So, I do think repealing the law is a good start, but I don`t think it`s enough. I think there are real questions about how we think about stopping runs on banks in a digital world. We sort of have analog banking law for a digital economy, and I think we`ve saw that this sort of close the bank on a Friday, reopen it on a Monday, which is sort of the very traditional model, simply doesn`t work where people have online banking, and they can - - they can work over the weekend and remove money and organize kind of continual withdrawals throughout the weekend.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and \uc2 FDIC\uc2  need to think about how we`re going to modernize to deal with this sort of digital bank run, which is really what we have here today.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: I`m glad you brought that up, because obviously the fear is spreading. And you saw that over the weekend with \uc2 First Republic\uc2  stock dropping so significantly, more than 60 percent. You`ve got other banking stocks dropping as well. You need Congress in a situation like this. You need the entire government to move quickly to just kind of boost confidence and get confidence back into the system. But the question is, how does Congress or the administration do something immediately to reassure people about their money in those banks?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, I think that`s part of the logic behind this announcement that all the depositors will be fully protected as we`re trying to reassure people and send a signal that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  understands what`s at stake here and what needs -- what the dangers are of allowing this to continue. I do want to say that this gives an opportunity now for the \uc2 FDIC\uc2  to start doing more evaluations of these -- of these large but not ginormous --that`s really what they are. They`re huge, but not ginormous banks -- and to try to make sure that we don`t have this same problem in other financial institutions.\par \par \uc2 Look, let`s be really clear. What happened here was could have been prevented by good regulation. It also could have been prevented by the executives and directors and management of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  not forgetting the basic fundamental of finance, which is that interest rates can go in only two directions up and down. And it`s their failure to think about that because they were so focused on their bottom line that led to this run on the bank.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Should this bank be allowed to fail?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, so, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has already -- I mean, we`ve already answered that question, which is no. That was the decision that was rolled out on Sunday evening, this morning, that we`re going to protect depositors. Whether ultimately, it all gets moved to another bank and this bank goes out of business, I think that`s not the focus. The focus should be on how do we get here? How do we prevent the next bank from getting here? How do we build regulation to have a durable economy?\par \par \uc2 So often, when my colleagues vote for these kinds of deregulation bills, they explain their votes by saying they are pro-business. I want to be clear, there is nothing pro-business about a bank failure. And we have to start chipping away at this because it`s not just this law and this moment with \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 . It`s an entire mindset about what it means to have a strong economy and what those fights are and what side of those fights you should be on that I think is putting our whole economy at risk today and going forward.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Congresswoman Katie Porter, Congresswoman, thank you so much. I greatly appreciate your time and insights into this. And much more on the banking crisis and how we got here coming up tonight on "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" at the top of the hour. Senator Elizabeth Warren joins Rachel live. That is coming up in just a few minutes from now.\par \par \uc2 But first, how one conservative judge in Texas could shut down abortion across the country. That`s coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: We are closely watching a case that would upend abortion pill access across the country including in states where abortion is still legal. The case was brought by a group of anti-abortion organizations. It seeks to overturn the Food and Drug Administration`s approval of a medication commonly used for abortion. And in a highly unusual move, the judge tried to delay notifying the public about the first hearing in the case.\par \par \uc2 As Katie Benner reported for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk issued or at least asked lawyers not to disclose the hearing scheduled for this Wednesday. Now, after that report, the judge initially -- the judge rather finally issued an order today publicly scheduling the date of the hearing.\par \par \uc2 NEW York Times Justice Department reporter Katie Benner joins me now. Katie, it`s good to see you again. So, the plaintiffs here are asking the judge for a preliminary injunction. If that were granted, would that mean the \uc2 FDA\uc2  couldn`t dispense this abortion -- this abortion medication in all 50 states?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:40]\par \par \uc2 KATIE BENNER, JUSTICE DEPARTMENT REPORTER, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Yes, correct. So, when preliminary injunctions are issued, oftentimes it will prevent something from happening all across the country while the hearing proceeds. In this case, it would prevent the sale of a commonly used abortion drug from being used in states across the country whether or not abortion is banned in those places.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: You in Your piece also write the judge said that court staff had faced security issues including death threats, and that the measure was intended to keep the court proceedings safe. Is that a reasonable argument for keeping the hearing concealed? I mean, this case could potentially impose the most far-reaching limit on abortion access since Roe was struck down.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: So, I don`t want to second guess what the judge said on that private call. you know, I cannot be in his head. I can say that, of course, the issue of abortion is extremely contentious in the -- in the United States. It doesn`t surprise me that well, somebody would worry about safety issues or threats. That`s why we have \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  at courthouses.\par \par \uc2 But I will just say that for the federal government, it really put lawyers in a quandary because for them they feel that unless a case is filed under seal, there`s a big national security risk to it. It could harm the United States` standing and around the globe. It could endanger citizens. They really do not want things under seal and they don`t want things hidden.\par \par \uc2 Now, interestingly, the judge did not order this. He said, please, do this as a courtesy. He asked people to keep this under wraps. He didn`t make an unofficial order, however, and now it has been scheduled.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Let me ask you about this judge here for a moment. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  recently wrote Kacsmaryk who ascended to the federal bench from the conservative legal group \uc2 First Liberty Institute\uc2  has defended his ability to be impartial in his work as a judge. Nonetheless, many of his recent decisions have been wins for the right including one that struck down new Biden administration protections for transgender people, another that forced thousands of asylum seekers to return to Mexico while they awaited processing.\par \par \uc2 Can you -- tell us more about what you have learned in your reporting about Judge Kacsmaryk.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: You know, everything you just said about Judge Kacsmaryk is what`s most commonly known about him. He is -- you know, he is a well-known scholar, a legal scholar in the -- in the conservative movement. What`s -- one of -- there are many things that are interesting about this case coming before Judge Kacsmaryk. One, it echoes things happened during the Obama administration. We saw a lot of cases go into states like Texas, because then they would go to the Fifth Circuit, which tended to lean more conservative.\par \par \uc2 When people did not like Obama`s policies, they would -- they would -- they would initiate lawsuits that would go through that circuit. This is sort of similar to what we saw during the Obama era. Judge Kacsmaryk though, he is the -- he is the judge in Amarillo, Texas. And we see that one of the groups that have -- one of the plaintiffs in this case, Alliance, they are headquartered in Amarillo, but they don`t have a big presence there. Most of their work is done outside of that town. However, they are headquartered there, and that is why the case is brought there.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: I think some people who are watching this might be wondering how an \uc2 FDA\uc2 -approved drug that has been used for over 20 years, how is it possible to challenge this safety of a drug that has been widely used for so long now?\par \par \uc2 BENNER: Yes, of course. So, let`s go through the basic, basic arguments for the plaintiffs. They`re saying this drug never should have been approved. There was not enough consideration given to how it would affect younger people. Of course, what the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and other intervenors are saying is, this has been on the market to your point for more than 20 years. If we thought there were significant safety issues, they would have arisen.\par \par \uc2 Now, we don`t know how the judge will decide. You know, there`s a chance that he ultimately agrees with the plaintiffs. But it`s hard to say this will stop medication abortions in the United States of America. One, there`s a second drug that`s not at the heart of this lawsuit that is also used for medical abortions. And two, these are pills that are now more and more commonly being delivered from other countries into the -- into the United States, even into states where abortion is illegal.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: We`ll see how this plays out Wednesday morning. Katie Benner, thank you so much. Great -- I greatly appreciate your insights and your reporting on this.\par \par \uc2 All right, that is ALL IN on this Monday night. You can watch my show "AYMAN" Saturdays at 8:00 Eastern, Sundays at 9:00 a.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Ayman. Nice to see you. And thank you for the big tease, the big -- the big mention that I`ve got Elizabeth Warren here live this hour.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230314ej3d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for March 11, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui, Susan Del Percio, Juanita Tolliver \par \uc2 6,177 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:28]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Turning point.\par \par \uc2 Right now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is huddling with his attorneys in Florida, gaming out his defense for a possible criminal indictment that could come as early as next week according to "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " four people with knowledge of the matter have said.\par \par \uc2 His lawyers say they have no plans to meet with the Manhattan District Attorney`s office after prosecutors invited Trump to testify before a grand jury next week about the hush money payments made during his 2016 presidential campaign to actor, Stormy Daniels, years after an alleged sexual affair, which Trump has denied.\par \par \uc2 Former Trump fixer \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , who served time in prison for charges related to those hush money payments, met with prosecutors for the 20th and final time yesterday ahead of his grand jury appearance next week.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, current Trump attorney, Joe Tacopina told NBC News this weekend, his client is an extortion victim and that neither of them believe charges will actually be brought. As for the response from Trump himself, the ex- President lashed out on his social media platform, Truth Social, insisting he is the target of yet another witch hunt.\par \par \uc2 Manhattan District Attorney, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  joins me in a moment to tell us well, what he can tell us, as well as we`ll talk about the politics of crime here in New York.\par \par \uc2 And also three years after the death of Breonna Taylor, a new report from the Justice Department finds a pattern of anti-Black policing within the City of Louisville, Kentucky; and in Tennessee, the DOJ is prepping an investigation of the Memphis Police Department two months after the death of Tyre Nichols, a Judge blocking nearly 20 hours of additional footage related to the fatal police beating that led to Nichols` death two months ago.\par \par \uc2 My thoughts on both later.\par \par \uc2 And you don`t want to miss today`s Gotcha, the Woke Edition, because this week, Republican lawmakers blamed everything from industrial accidents to remote work, on antiracism, and the push for inclusion and I will tell you why their anti-woke logic is putting me right to sleep.\par \par \uc2 Big show tonight here on POLITICSNATION. Let`s get into it.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Manhattan District Attorney, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 District Attorney Bragg, thanks for joining us tonight ahead of what appears to be a busy week in your office`s investigation of Donald Trump`s conduct related to those hush money payments that were paid out to Stormy Daniels in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump fixer, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  who has emerged as a central figure in your investigation slated to appear before a grand jury maybe as early as Monday, Trump attorneys told NBC that they have no plans to meet with your office, which has invited Trump to appear before a grand jury related to this matter, a strong signal to many that indictments at least being considered for the former President.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Bragg, I know you can`t say things that will compromise the investigation, but what can you tell us and our audience about the investigation of anything?\par \par \uc2 ALVIN BRAGG\uc2  (D), MANHATTAN DISTRICT ATTORNEY: Reverend Sharpton, always good to see you and great to talk.\par \par \uc2 As you indicate, I can`t talk about anything beyond to say what I`ve said which is that we have an active investigation. Many observed last year the trial of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  and so what I would say is the same team is working with the same type of professionalism and rigor, the same type of team on our active investigation.\par \par \uc2 I`m constrained from saying anything more than that because I don`t want to prejudice any investigation.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Last year when you came into office and you only, in two months, people in your office wanted to push forward for whatever reasons you didn`t.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:04]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You took a lot of heat and I`ve said publicly and I`ve said to you privately, because you come by sometime at National Action Network Saturday rallies talking about gun violence and crime, and how you just seemed unperturbed by all of the controversy.\par \par \uc2 But what does weigh in to you making a decision when some of your prosecutors come on whether you are moving forward in any case, this case and that, what are the things that mean something to you that leads you to a final decision?\par \par \uc2 BRAGG: I appreciate the question.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`re looking at the facts in the law and the facts as they develop. We review documents, we talk to witnesses. And so yes, we live in this world, we may hear what this pundit says, and we may hear a lot of commentary but our focus is on the evidence and the law.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, I`ve been doing this for 20-plus years, so I was new to being a District Attorney and to some of the attention that came with that, but the doing of the job, the investigations, I`ve been doing this for 20 plus years. It is the same thing, the facts and the law.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So whatever happens, whether there is an indictment or not, you`re going to look at the basis of what comes out in terms of how you judge it to the law, it will not be politics, whether it`s one crowd or another, you`re going to go straight down the middle.\par \par \uc2 BRAGG: That`s how I`ve been doing it for 20-plus years, and I was a public corruption prosecutor, Federal and State and whether it was Democrat, Republican, Independent, a former District Attorney, we prosecuted them. An \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent, Mayor, a Deputy Mayor --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So you have no fear of going after people with big titles, even if they were President, and you have no fear of not going if --\par \par \uc2 BRAGG: We follow the facts. It doesn`t matter what party you are, it doesn`t matter what your background is. What did you do and what does the law say?\par \par \uc2 Trump, of course has lashed out at your office from his social media platform Truth Social complaining that these developments constitute a politically motivated witch hunt to derail his presidential campaign orchestrated by you, of course, a radical left prosecutor who has let violent crime run amok here in New York.\par \par \uc2 The best part for me was when he said that because Democrats couldn`t win, they turned to "a tool that has never been used in this way before, weaponized law enforcement." I won`t waste time rehashing what so many in the Civil Rights Movement faced in terms of weaponized law enforcement, but I want to get your reactions to what Trump has said about you.\par \par \uc2 BRAGG: So I don`t follow whatever they may have posted on, focusing on the work. But one thing he said that I want to focus on, which is -- and you mentioned me going to the rallies, and I always start with gun crime.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve been focused like a laser on violent crime here in New York City. And as the Mayor has said, homicides and shootings are down citywide. They`re down even further in Manhattan. And where you and I spent a lot of time, Uptown Manhattan, they are down even further than that.\par \par \uc2 So we have more work to do, but we are focused like a laser on violent crime.\par \par \uc2 And look, one of my predecessors, Mr. Morgenthau used to say we fight crime at the Manhattan District Attorney`s Office, from the streets to the suites, so we can do white collar crime and violent crime at the same time.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I want to get into that, but one issue I understand you would like to talk about is the conviction of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, it was fined $1.6 million, the maximum possible penalty for running a decade long tax fraud scheme. Why was that a symbolic moment for you?\par \par \uc2 BRAGG: It was I thought, very good. I love trials, I think it is a core part of our democracy, where we have just like we talk about voting, I always encourage people to -- people in our community to serve on juries, so you`ve got to see in a public courtroom great public servants making a case and laying bare a decades` long fraud and the inner workings of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So that`s the public moment. That`s when we are allowed to speak in the Courtroom and lay it all bare. So I thought that was consequential the first time we`ve had that kind of a criminal conviction involving the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  and it speaks to the rigor and the professionalism of the career prosecutors in my office.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Okay, let`s get into crime. You`re in your second year as Manhattan DA. According to some \uc2 NYPD\uc2  numbers we got from your office, murders in Manhattan dropped 16 percent in your first year, shootings dropped 19 percent.\par \par \uc2 That trend has extended into your second year, murders down 33 percent in Manhattan, with shootings down 12 percent. Still, that decline has come as other major crimes like burglaries, robberies, and grand larceny surged in New York by 22 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 To an observer looking at that discrepancy What do you say?\par \par \uc2 [17:10:04]\par \par \uc2 BRAGG: So first, we started with what we believe is the most important in terms of our resources, and that is the homicides and the shootings, sitting literally around a table every single day with our law enforcement partners, focusing on the small percentage of people who drive gun violence and we started to bend that curve. The percentage you just showed illustrate that.\par \par \uc2 The beginning of this year showed encouraging data on some of the other crimes you mentioned. So we are seeing our consistent efforts really starting to bear fruit, and so we`re going to continue to do what we do focusing on the small percentage of people who are driving violent crime, working day in and day out with our law enforcement partners who are continuing to bend that curve.\par \par \uc2 We like, and we think it`s encouraging, but we know we have more work to do.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: In fact, when we had a Summit of Blacks that are now holding high office with you were a part of, you had COVID, but you Zoomed in and everybody else was there at National Action Network. We talked about how we`ve got to fight crime and deal with criminal justice reform.\par \par \uc2 You`ve represented Gwen Carr, the mother of Eric Garner, as well as you`ve dealt with the whole question of policing. So tell me how you balance that you live in the community, you live a few blocks from National Action Network, in fact. You teach Sunday school in Harlem. How do you balance that?\par \par \uc2 BRAGG: First, I want to thank you for that convening, even though I had COVID, I was Zoomed in, I felt the energy. To have the Lieutenant Governor, Attorney General, the Mayor, the leaders of both houses, really sitting around talking, that was inspiring, I thought it was a great moment. And you convened us to talk about as you said, both.\par \par \uc2 And we have to have both, right? We have to have both safety and fairness and I`ve been talking about safety through fairness because if we don`t have the trust of the community, witnesses don`t come forward, victims won`t come forward. So we have to have that trust and we get that through emphasizing fairness.\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, I was in a Courtroom watching an Assistant District Attorney do a closing argument in a case involving action by a law enforcement officer that we argue led to the death of someone in custody. So that work is important. That`s public safety work, too. We are going to do that and we will do the gun work, we do it side by side.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I know you made combating human trafficking a priority of your office. What specific steps are you taking to combat this issue here in Manhattan?\par \par \uc2 BRAGG: This is deeply disturbing work and we`ve seen people arriving, young boys and girls arriving through, let`s say, \uc2 Port Authority\uc2  or other ways of coming into our city and people at the standby waiting to prey -- it is deplorable conduct.\par \par \uc2 And so what we`ve been doing is using some of the same mechanisms and techniques we used in gun trafficking -- wiretap investigation, undercover investigations to really root out people who are doing this, preying on our young children who come to this city often leaving very challenging conditions at home, looking for a new start. So, that`s a significant priority for my office. We`ve brought some disturbing but very impactful cases.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you for coming in studio and talking tonight and whatever decision is made, you know, I`m going to be calling you to come back and let`s talk about it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Manhattan District Attorney, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 . You`re always welcome on this show.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Wasserman Schultz\uc2 , Democrat of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, a few topics I want to discuss with you. But first, you heard my interview with Manhattan DA Bragg just now and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in your Home State of Florida right now strategizing his response to possible charges against him related to the Stormy Daniels hush money payments. What`s your takeaway?\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE WASSERMAN SCHULTZ (D-FL): Well, I think it`s important -- and thank you for having me, Reverend Al, as always. I think it`s important to note that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is facing a number of very serious investigations. But we are not talking about some two-bit has-been politician. We`re talking about the person who is the head of the Republican Party in this country and the frontrunner to be their nominee for President of the United States once again.\par \par \uc2 And so it is important to look at the agenda that they`re pursuing now, bullying gay kids, restricting women`s rights, banning education for African-Americans, like my Governor here in Florida is, really banning protests and books.\par \par \uc2 This is the MAGA extremist agenda and the individual who is under investigation and possible indictment is the king and the head of the Republican Party that is spearheading that agenda.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Staying in Florida. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is expected to formally announce that he will seek the 2024 Republican nomination for President soon. He was in Iowa yesterday making his first trip to the first in the nation caucus state and nearly crossing paths with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who will be there Monday.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: DeSantis taking shots at both Trump and President Biden in Iowa over the administration`s responses to the COVID-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 So you`ve got the two presumed frontrunners for the GOP nomination in your backyard, Congresswoman. What sense do you get from Florida voters as to who has the momentum this time and who will Democrats be facing next year in your judgment?\par \par \uc2 SCHULTZ: Well, Reverend Al, I think it`s important to note, I just came from a press conference with my dear friend, former Congresswoman \uc2 Gabby Giffords\uc2 , where we were coming together with Parkland parents who lost their children in the Marjory Stoneman Douglas shooting, to vehemently oppose the permit-less carry gun legislation that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is trying to jam through the legislature this session and is about to reach the floor.\par \par \uc2 And 62 percent of Republicans in Florida overwhelmingly oppose that legislation, but yet they`re going to ram it through doing the bidding of the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and that is the same type of opposition that the rest of his agenda faces.\par \par \uc2 Republicans and Floridians overwhelmingly oppose the near total abortion ban he has proposed, the bullying of gay kids, going after -- book bans and speech -- prohibiting speech and trying to silence the ability for African- American education in the State and now, he wants to take that national. I think he`s going to be in for a rude awakening.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman, the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2 `s ongoing investigation of securities at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  came to something of a head this week. In the House Judiciary Subcommittee hearing that some Republicans accused the FTC and Democrats of harassing \uc2 Twitter\uc2  owner, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , over his embrace of conservative viewpoints on the platform and his decision to reinstate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On the other side, Democrats countered that Republicans are embracing Musk because he parrots their views accusing two journalist, Matt Taibbi Michael Shellenberger of endangering \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees for their own gain and when they released the so-called \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Files in concert with Musk.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHULTZ: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  spoon fed you his cherry picked information, which you must have suspected promotes a slanted viewpoint, or at the very least generates another right-wing conspiracy theory. You violated your own standard, and you appear to have benefited from it.\par \par \uc2 Is it true that you have profited since you are the recipient of the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Files? You`ve made money? Yes or no? Very simple question.\par \par \uc2 MATT TAIBBI\uc2 , AUTHOR, "GRIFTOPIA": It`s probably awash, honestly.\par \par \uc2 SCHULTZ: No, you have made money that you did not have before. Correct?\par \par \uc2 TAIBBI: But I`ve also spent money that I didn`t have before.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Care to follow up on that, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 SCHULTZ: Well, what`s very clear is that yes, the content moderation personnel were nearly completely gutted at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  when \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  took over. He picked journalists -- so-called journalists, his own handpicked ones, the oxymoron, that I used in Committee to spoon feed them, curated information essentially to feed his own narratives that the government was somehow waging a war against conservatives.\par \par \uc2 Not only is that not true, but we were able to expose these two so-called journalists in Committee where they were using that handpicked information to selectively make it seem like conservatives were the victims. And of course, the fact that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  repeatedly asked \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to take down things he found objectionable during his tenure.\par \par \uc2 Those seemingly unbelievably didn`t come up and the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Files, Reverend Al. Isn`t that amazing? So it is a total farce that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is engaged in, and he is jeopardizing their former employees, one of whom even had to sell his home and move because he was being persecuted by people on the right who don`t like the right-wingers have been -- were having their own posts that were endangering people moderated as they should because we need to keep people`s participation online safe and not dangerous.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This week, 44 House Democrats led by Florida Congresswoman \uc2 Lois Frankel\uc2  marked International Woman`s Day with a resolution to adopt a feminist approach to US foreign policy with California Congresswoman Barbara Lee who introduced the resolution along with Frankel saying in an interview that the resolution arose out of women not being centered or present in foreign policy decisions here in the US and abroad.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:18]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As a supporter of the resolution, what should a feminist approach to our current foreign policy look like?\par \par \uc2 SCHULTZ: What a feminist approach to our foreign policy looks like is making sure that the decisions that are made on behalf of the billions of women that live around the world are made with women at the table in those decision making roles.\par \par \uc2 When you have an imbalance in perspective in decision making, when women are only 30 percent of the Ambassadors that represent our country, across the globe, then you end up having an imbalance when it comes to the priorities in decision making and policies that result from the makeup of the decision makers.\par \par \uc2 So ensuring that we have that balance, that we have a varied perspective and that women are represented and have seats that they can pull up to the table and be part of the policymaking worldwide, that is what a fair and feminine feminist foreign policy looks like and it is something that we should lean in to as a country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Wasserman Schultz\uc2 , thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the Republican war on woke is really an assault on your freedom. I will explain in this week`s Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 But first my colleague Richard Lui, with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey, thanks a lot, Rev.\par \par \uc2 Good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Heavy rains continue to soak Central and Northern California where millions of residents are under flood alerts there. Many roads were washed away by excessive rain. More damage is expected throughout the weekend, and even some snow in certain areas.\par \par \uc2 Britain`s \uc2 Defense Intelligence Agency\uc2  saying Russian forces took control of most of Bakhmut in Ukraine. This Eastern area has seen some of the longest and bloodiest fighting of the war so far. Ukraine`s forces say Russians lost more than 500 troops in one day in that conflict.\par \par \uc2 And Indonesia`s Mount Merapi erupted today. It spewed gas clouds and lava. It forced authorities to halt tourism and mining activities as well. No casualties reported.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:26:33]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It is rare to hear a Republican explain exactly what woke means, but many will tell you they know it when they see it, and lately, it seems as if they are finding wokeness just about everywhere they look.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to Georgia Republican Congressman Mike Collins this week talking about the Ohio train derailment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE COLLINS (R-GA): This administration`s focus on DEI is forcing private companies to rethink their goals and one has to wonder, was \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 `s DEI policies directing resources away from the important things like greasing wheel bearings?\par \par \uc2 Now, this insanity must stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman Collins claims don`t add up. \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  union workers haven`t been complaining about diversity causes, they say the company has been ignoring its own safety rules and while management has defended their practices, they aren`t blaming inclusion programs either.\par \par \uc2 But none of this matters to Republican lawmakers who claim to value free enterprise, but now, want to bully businesses into supporting their culture agenda and their culture war agenda at that.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is set to issue the first veto of his administration to block a Republican led bill that would discourage retirement fund managers from considering environmental and social issues in their investments.\par \par \uc2 For the radical right wing, it seems free markets no longer matter and it doesn`t stop there. This week, Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  complained the Oversight Committee was being overwhelmed by hearings about the supposed wokeness of seemingly noncontroversial government policies like remote work for Federal workers and paid internship programs.\par \par \uc2 While at the State level, in my capacity as President of National Action Network, I have marched myself in Florida against Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` effort to brand conversation of race and sexuality in schools as woke and forbidden, a tactic many Republican Governors around the country are now considering.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party that once claimed to champion small government and personal liberty is now dominated by politicians pushing their cultural values on all Americans where they live, where they learn, and even where they work.\par \par \uc2 Not only can they define the word "woke," they also seem to be struggling with the concept of freedom as well.\par \par \uc2 I Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:33:35]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Let`s now bring in my political panel, two of MSNBC`s contributors and political strategists, Susan Del Percio, a Republican and Juanita Tolliver, a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Next week, former President Trump is invited to testify before a New York grand jury as part of the Manhattan DA investigation into the hush money payment allegedly paid to the adult film star, Stormy Daniels. Trump calls the New York case, "a political witch hunt trying to take down the leading candidate by far in the Republican Party."\par \par \uc2 Susan, what`s your reaction to this case? You heard Manhattan DA Bragg here just a few moments ago. What`s your reaction to all this?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, yeah, I believe all the really good lawyers that are on the network that it means that they are close to an indictment.\par \par \uc2 I do have a couple of questions. I would -- you know, I know I`ll never find out, but did Bragg coordinate this at all with Georgia or with the Feds so they knew what was coming because we keep hearing other cases are also close.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is, is that while people are saying it won`t hurt \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the primary, I think that should he be indicted, the numbers -- his numbers are going to tank for the General, and if they`re bad enough, Rev, as I said on the REVIEs, I don`t think he`ll make it to the ballot come 2024.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, Trump said just last weekend he`ll stay in the race, even if he is indicted. Many Democrats would like to see him held accountable, but could criminal charges in this case just another way to rally up his base?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: It could be, right? His most devout supporters aren`t going to leave his side. They`ve shown that over the past, what -- six years, seven years. But the reality is what Susan mentioned, there are a lot of Republicans who see him in the headlines in the most negative ways and are turned off by that. That makes them more open to the alternatives that are running in the Republican primary like DeSantis, right?\par \par \uc2 Like he is creating a runway for these other candidates with all the crimes he has allegedly committed, but the reality still remains that his tone has changed from that statement last week at CPAC where he said he`d be essentially undeterred if indicted, and now with this statement, this angry statement attacking DA Bragg is showing that he clearly is concerned. He is huddling with his attorneys.\par \par \uc2 But I appreciate while DA Bragg didn`t say much about the specifics of this case, what he made explicitly clear is that they are not afraid to take on Trump. They already took on the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  and now they`re going to see this grand jury through and that instills a bit of confidence.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes. I picked that up too in our conversation.\par \par \uc2 Susan, switching gears to Iowa where Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  made his first trip yesterday to the Hawkeye State as part of his book tour. While DeSantis is fueling speculation of a 2024 run, although he has not made an official announcement just yet. Meanwhile, DeSantis new book sold 94,000 copies in his first week, outpacing political memoirs sales by Trump, Pence, Clinton and Obama during the same period.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of DeSantis rollout so far?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: So far, as a political analyst, I have to say he`s doing it pretty well. He`s going and making -- he is making his time count, whether it is for fundraisers or for appearances in Iowa and selling his book. It`s all very carefully orchestrated.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is, DeSantis is unlike all the other names that we`ve heard running for President with the exception of Trump, because he has $71 million in a PAC already. So money is not going to be his problem. He is going to use his legislative session, which started last week and goes until the end of June to just keep pushing his agenda forward.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, let`s turn to California where Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is saying the State will no longer do business with \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  after the drugstore chain said it will not be selling abortion pills in 21 Republican-led States. What do you make of this situation?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: I think it`s sickening and it makes no sense for \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  to essentially take half of the country and say we`re not distributing mifepristone which is a safe \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved legal form of access to abortion and they are doing this in States even where abortion is still legal.\par \par \uc2 So, it is really questionable from \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , but I appreciate the action from \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  because it shows that these Democratic statewide leaders can stand up and hold these massive companies and pharmacies accountable.\par \par \uc2 I also appreciate the language that we saw from Democratic senators who are calling on \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  to specify where are they looking to be certified? What States are they doing this? What will it take to change their minds on this? Because we deserve answers. This is yet another effort by Republican State leaders to try to infringe on the basic right to access basic healthcare across the country and it is harmful, Rev. it is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, thank you very much. And thank you, Susan. And I took note, Susan, that you mentioned the REVIEs as early as March this year. I won`t forget that.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Washington, DC has been dealt another blow when it comes to self-governance. DC Congresswoman \uc2 Eleanor Holmes Norton\uc2  joins us next to talk about.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:43:01]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 This week, the Senate passed a measure overruling the Washington, DC City Council`s rewrite of the Criminal Code for the nation`s capital. The new Code had been in the works for over a decade and would have been the first overall of the district`s criminal statutes since 1901.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is DC Congresswoman, \uc2 Eleanor Holmes Norton\uc2 . Thank you for joining me, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ELEANOR HOLMES NORTON\uc2  (D-WA): My pleasure, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman, the decision to intervene in Washington DC`s criminal justice reform efforts were backed by most Republicans and split Democrats. President Biden said earlier this month that he will not veto the measure, which means it will become law.\par \par \uc2 You are the sole Federal representatives from our nation`s capital. What`s your reaction to this?\par \par \uc2 NORTON: I was very disappointed because the President at first said he would veto the revised the measure and then he did an about face.\par \par \uc2 We also lost some Democrats in the Senate and in the House. Now, some of this may be due to the rise in crime in the country. But this bill had the support of prosecutors here in the District of Columbia as well.\par \par \uc2 So first time we`ve had a revision of our Criminal Code since 1901, it was long past due, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now 17 DC statehood advocates were arrested near the Capitol on Wednesday after protesting the Senate vote to block the reform bill. You introduced the bill that would grant statehood for Washington, DC to give the nearly 700,000 residents voting representation in Congress.\par \par \uc2 How does the fight for statehood play into the Federal government stepping into DC`s ability to self-rule? As long as I can remember, you`ve been championing statehood and now self-rule. And now we have this interference. How does this play into that?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:23]\par \par \uc2 NORTON: Yes, Rev, and the only way to get rid of this interference is to afford statehood to the District of Columbia. Now, we`re getting someplace on statehood.\par \par \uc2 We now have 54 percent of the American people supporting statehood and when you add the notion of taxation without representation, it goes up to 58 percent. So we`re on our way to statehood, all we need is to get back the House of Representatives, which we just lost.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The relationship between Washington, DC and the Federal government is unique, but it is not the only city to have its criminal justice policies second guessed.\par \par \uc2 We recently spoke to the Mayor of St. Louis, where the Missouri State House recently passed a bill to end local control of the St. Louis Police Department. And tomorrow, we`ll be joined on this show by the Mayor of Jackson, Mississippi where the State lawmakers are trying to expand the scope of state-run \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  and create a special judiciary district for the widest part of the city. These are largely Black communities having their decisions overruled by largely White State Legislatures.\par \par \uc2 What`s your take on what`s happening?\par \par \uc2 NORTON: Well, we have that happening at the Federal level, and of course, this city is actually 50/50 Black and White and the racial implications cannot be avoided.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I must ask you the next question. More than two dozen Republicans sitting on the House Oversight and Accountability Committee refused to join their Democratic counterparts in signing a letter denouncing White supremacy and racist conspiracy theories after ranking Democrat \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  urged signing a draft pledge.\par \par \uc2 What do you think is driving this refusal from some of your colleagues?\par \par \uc2 NORTON: It`s certainly disgraceful. I`m not sure what`s driving it. You would think that anybody in the Congress today would be clear on opposition to White supremacy, but what all the Republicans voted against this resolution, now, of course, we`re having people coming across the border that might have influenced it, but it is disgraceful that you can`t even get a resolution opposing White supremacy in the United States today, and of course, that is the Oversight Committee on which I serve.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, in Women`s History Month, it is an honor to have one of the women I`ve admired a long time, Congresswoman \uc2 Eleanor Holmes Norton\uc2 , thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 NORTON: Thank you, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Before we continue, take a look at a brand new monument of Harriet Tubman that was unveiled this week in downtown Newark, New Jersey. It is 25-foot tall, titled "Shadow of a face."\par \par \uc2 Tubman was a legendary abolitionist and women`s rights advocate. She died 110 years ago and present at the ceremony were her great, great, great grandnieces.\par \par \uc2 If you`re in the area, go check it out.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:53:32]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Before we finish our show tonight, two other stories that caught my attention this week, first out of Kentucky where Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  announced this week, the Justice Department found reasonable cause to believe the Louisville Metro Police Department and city government engaged in a pattern of conduct that violated citizens` constitutional and civil rights.\par \par \uc2 Investigation came following the 2020 death of 26-year-old Breonna Taylor; then in Tennessee, where a Judge blocked the release of 20 additional hours of footage and audio related to the fatal police beating of Tyre Nichols in January.\par \par \uc2 The footage was set to be released Wednesday, but an attorney for one of the five Memphis Police officers charged with murdering the 29-year-old Nichols filed the last minute request to block its release. The Judge`s decision came just hours after the DOJ said it would conduct a review of the Memphis Police Department.\par \par \uc2 In February, as President of National Action Network, I delivered the eulogy at Nichols` funeral where Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  also delivered remarks.\par \par \uc2 I do believe that delaying the release of this footage is delaying justice for the Tyre Nichols family and for Tyre Nichols.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:05]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The first round of body camera footage was disturbing on its own and should be enough to convict all officers charged with this murder.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have much more on these two stories with Civil Rights attorney, \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2  tomorrow right here on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez starts at the top of the hour.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:00]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020230312ej3b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for March 9, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ryan Nobles, Jesse Kirsch, Erin McLaughlin, Carol  Lee, Jacob Ward, Bill Karins, Barbara McQuade, Josh Lederman, Keir Simmons \par \uc2 7,641 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Yet another \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  train derails this morning, just as the CEO faces members of Congress who have a lot of tough questions but don`t always get clear answers. A full month after the train crash that got many residents sick, will the railroad keep its word to be in East Palestine, Ohio into the future? And what are lawmakers willing to do to make sure he keeps his word?\par \par \uc2 Plus, President Biden about to lay down a critical marker ahead of the 2024 presidential race, unveiling his budget just about 90 minutes from now. How it will frame the critical economic and political fight with Republicans going forward?\par \par \uc2 And more misery in California as a series of unrelenting storms escalates the danger and the disaster. At least a dozen people have been killed. Now, a new storm could dump another eight inches of rain risking flooding in areas that are already buried under 16 feet of snow. We`ve got a live report on that coming up.\par \par \uc2 But we start with an extraordinary case of bad timing for \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 . Another train derailment reported this one, in eastern Alabama, just hours before CEO \uc2 Alan Shaw\uc2  got an earful from senators accusing him of prioritizing financial gain over public safety, a calculation, critics say, paved the way for last month`s toxic train derailment in East Palestine.\par \par \uc2 This latest train derailment didn`t involve any toxic chemicals or injuries. But it is a fourth derailment of a \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  train in just over a month. On Capitol Hill, Shaw, who has ducked multiple town halls back in Ohio, was challenged by Senator \uc2 Ed Markey\uc2  about his vow to "make things right," something he said repeatedly for the people of East Palestine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ED MARKEY, (D-MA): When you say do the right thing, will you again compensate these families for their diminished lost property value for homes and small businesses?\par \par \uc2 ALAN SHAW\uc2 , \uc2 NORFOLK SOUTHERN\uc2  CEO: Senator, we`ve already committed $21 million. And that`s a down payment.\par \par \uc2 MARKEY: Yes, it is a down payment. Will you commit to ensuring that these families -- these innocent families do not lose their life savings in their homes and small businesses? The right thing to do is to say, yes, we will.\par \par \uc2 SHAW: Senator, I`m committed to doing what`s right for the community. And we`re going to be there as --\par \par \uc2 MARKEY: No. What`s right for the community will then be balanced, which is what we can see from your stock buybacks by what`s right for \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 . We`re not hearing the right things today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC`s Ryan Nobles, who`s from watching all of this unfold on Capitol Hill. Also with me, Heather McTeer Toney, former \uc2 EPA\uc2  Administrator for the southeast region. She currently works with the Environmental Defense Fund and Moms Clean Air Force.\par \par \uc2 All right, Ryan. So, that was not a commitment to compensate people who may never be able to sell their homes. That`s just one of many examples that lawmakers push them on, that lawmakers were not shy. But what is the overall takeaway from this hearing? What is it that they want Shaw to do?\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: I think it`s pretty clear here, Chris, from the questioning that these senators would like to hold Shaw on the record. They`re trying to get him to commit to investments in the community that will try and get it back to what it was like before that train derailment basically turned this community upside down. And that includes cleaning up the soil, cleaning up the air and water, fixing the railway itself, and all of the ancillary pieces of infrastructure that were impacted by it.\par \par \uc2 But it also means an investment specifically in the human beings that live there in the homes that they own that are now not worth nearly as much, and a commitment to pay for their health care in the wake of all -- everything that they`ve been impacted by as it relates to hazardous chemicals. Listen to what Bernie Sanders, the senator from Vermont, pressed \uc2 Alan Shaw\uc2  about just a few minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS, (I-VT): You talked about covering the needs of the people of East Palestine, does that include paying for the health care needs? All of their health care needs.\par \par \uc2 SHAW: Senator, we`re going to do what`s right for the citizens of East --\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: What`s right is to cover their health care needs. Will you do that?\par \par \uc2 SHAW: Everything is on the table, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: And you could tell, Chris, that \uc2 Alan Shaw\uc2  didn`t really want to box himself in on any level and make a commitment that his company would not be able to then fulfill. And that`s why he went back to that same line over and over again, we`re going to do what`s right by the citizens of Ohio. That`s not clearly what the senators wanted to hear but that was him trying to find a balance here, as we are still at the early stages of this recovery in Ohio, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Heather, if the answer is and it was repeatedly, we`re going to do what`s right, who gets to decide what`s right, you know? And so, I guess I`m curious. Your thoughts about what you heard and can they be essentially have their feet held to the fire by whether it`s by Congress, by the \uc2 EPA\uc2 , by any of the multitudes of federal organizations to do what`s right?\par \par \uc2 HEATHER MCTEER TONEY, CLIMATE JUSTICE LIAISON, \uc2 ENVIRONMENTAL DEFENSE FUND\uc2 : Well, it certainly sound like -- sounded like he watched one of my favorite films, the 1980s classic, Do The Right Thing by Spike Lee because that was certainly what he`d said repeatedly. But it came to the question of whether or not that meets the expectations of the communities around East Palestine and quite frankly, communities all across this country that have dealt with train derailments, particularly in environmental justice communities. So, the things that we`re really trying to figure out here are, were there new information that we were learning, and it -- what is the risk currently today, and you`re right, who`s responsible for that risk? I think there`s still more explaining to do on that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Politico did an analysis, Heather, of train accidents in the United States. And it found that \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 `s accident rate rose nearly three times as fast as the industry average over the past three years. It also said, "the increased accident rate comes as the chemical industry predicts a rise in the amount of chemicals that will be shipped by rail trucks and other forms of transportation." This is something your group calls train bombs. Explain a little more what we`re talking about. And what do we do about it so other folks don`t become an East Palestine?\par \par \uc2 TONEY: Well, we can look to South Texas and to Cancer Alley in Louisiana to get an idea of what the impacts could be. Because we have communities in this country that have been suffering for this -- from this for years. And so, the build-out of petrochemical facilities in the Ohio and Pennsylvania area, again, is giving us an indication that this is something we must pay attention to right now.\par \par \uc2 And I think there was some really good points that were made throughout the hearing that connected the number of workers to safety. And that`s a key component for people in the community. How safe am I and is actions that the railroad companies are taking in reducing the number of working -- workers impacting that safe -- impacting that safety? That`s another key component of this that should be thought through and we should ask him some more questions about.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me bring in Jesse Kirsch, who`s in Cleveland, which is the site of another train accident. But you spent a lot of time in East Palestine, Jesse. I was reading a report this morning about residents are still complaining that they`re getting sick, that there are bad smells in their house, that their lips and tongues are tingling. Pledging millions of dollars is obviously great. It`s a step. But I wonder if that -- they`re going to think that`s enough or what they heard today was enough.\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well -- and, Chris, I can tell you that people are thinking about not just today, they`re thinking about years from now, both about their health and about their financial well-being. And those are the two of the things that we`ve repeatedly heard the railroad be asked about at town halls and again on Capitol Hill now. The CEO being asked about this. And I just want to flag two things from the hearing that stood out to me.\par \par \uc2 Alan Shaw\uc2 , the CEO was pressed on, if he would commit to paying for people`s medical bills for covering, people`s health costs down the road as people are worried about that. He said that is something that is on the table. He said everything is on the table.\par \par \uc2 But he was also asked if he would commit at this point to reimbursing people for property value losses because we have heard from people who think their homes are total losses now in terms of their value in the market based on what has happened there. And even if there is no outline air or water issue, there`s a stigma around the community. And that is something that people are worried about. The CEO would not commit to that.\par \par \uc2 So, those are two notable things I think people in this community, we`ve heard them asking similar questions. And I think those are things that are going to stick out to people as they move forward trying to get a sense of what exactly doing right by this community will mean. That is the vow we continue to hear from \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 . They talked about the downpayment they`ve made, but we don`t get many specifics as they talked about the long-term future of that community.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, let me get back to the big picture for a minute, Heather, because another former \uc2 EPA\uc2  Administrator, Judith Enck, wrote an op-ed in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . And I want to read part of it for you. It says, in lieu of a comprehensive plan, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  appears to be playing a game of crisis whack a mole, waiting and then responding to the news cycle.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 For example, she says, in a situation like this, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  should immediately conduct authoritative tests and come up with a plan to address any dangers and communicate all of it loudly and clearly to the affected communities. Instead, it waited a full month and then asked \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  to test for dioxins and come up with a plan. As you step back, now, we`ve got a month behind us. Did the \uc2 EPA\uc2  drop the ball here, Heather?\par \par \uc2 TONEY: Well, I think that when we look at the questions that are being asked now, of course, hindsight is always 2020. And part of the discussion today was to look at the communication gaps. And I think the panel did a great job of walking through each one of those agencies and identifying those gaps. But one of the things my good friend and colleague, Judith, did talk about in that piece that she wrote was that \uc2 EPA\uc2  has been drastically underfunded and defunded over the past few years, especially under the previous administration. And so, trying to refill and then meet those standards, we`re all -- this is where we all have to pull in together here.\par \par \uc2 And one more thing, Chris, you know, I wish we could have panned around a bit to that room because I know there were mothers that were sitting in that room. I was thinking some of those same questions, are there -- were there things we could have done better? Are there places where we could have been and made the community more aware?\par \par \uc2 And certainly, I think that the answer to that is yes. And that it is very incumbent upon not only the federal government but also the state leads and especially Norfolk, who`s responsible and has tried to be -- they attempting to say that they`re going to clean up. We`re still waiting on that. Important for us to hold them accountable for all of that communication.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ryan, before we go, I do want to ask you about something else on Capitol Hill. And that is a status check on Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who was hospitalized overnight. what happened?\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: He was at a dinner off for the Senate Leadership Fund, which is a PAC that supports the Republican majority in the Senate. It was after that event that he tripped and fell and that led to a hospital stay. Last check, he was still in the hospital recovering from that fall. We don`t know how serious the injuries were that he sustained as a result of that fall. We are expected to get some sort of an update later this afternoon as to exactly what this means for McConnell going forward.\par \par \uc2 There`s obviously two answers to this. If it`s a short-term problem, likely won`t be that big of a deal. The Senate will continue on business as usual. If it`s a long-term problem, that could be a challenge for Republicans. McConnell, of course, the most powerful member of the Republican caucus on the Senate side.\par \par \uc2 Of course, President Biden releasing his budget today. We have a debt ceiling fight in the offing. McConnell, usually a key figure in those talks. So, we`ll just have to wait and see exactly just what his prognosis is here before we draw any conclusions as to how this could impact the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  going forward, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I should mention that the president who`s on his way to Philadelphia, there was just a gaggle on board with the Deputy Press Secretary. I think the White House is going to put out a statement but they say the president and first lady wish \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  a swift recovery. Thank you so much, Ryan Nobles, Jesse Kirsch, Heather McTeer Toney. We appreciate all of you being here.\par \par \uc2 Russia has launched its biggest aerial attack on Ukraine in weeks, a barrage of 81 missiles overnight in what Russia says is retaliation for an incursion last week. You can see the scope of the attacks on this map. No region out of reach. People living far from the frontlines were awakened by air raid sirens. At least 11 people were killed.\par \par \uc2 Those missiles specifically though, targeting energy infrastructure, knocking out power across the country. In Kyiv, nearly half the city was without heat, and temperatures were down in the 40s.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Erin McLaughlin is on the ground in Dnipro. And, Erin, tell us if you saw and heard anything, and how are people reacting on the ground.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, this is being seen by Ukrainian officials as another play by the Russians at propaganda, another sort of appeal to a domestic audience there in Russia because militarily they accomplished very little today. What was significant was those hypersonic missiles. Six of the missiles that were launched toward Ukraine were hypersonic missiles. They`re very expensive. They go far distances. They`re very fast and they evade Ukraine`s air defense systems.\par \par \uc2 One of those hypersonic missiles was suspected of having attacked Kyiv landed in a power plant, compromising 40 percent of the city`s ability to provide heat. We actually heard that missile strike when we woke up this morning -- the early hours of the morning, we heard a big boom that echoed throughout the city. That being said, the other sort of major concerning thing that happened today was the attack on the \uc2 Zaporizhzhia Nuclear Power Plant\uc2 , a missile struck according to Ukrainian officials, one of the key power lines connecting that power plant to the Ukrainian power grid. It`s the sixth time during the war that that has happened, forcing Europe`s largest nuclear power plant onto backup generators, concerning the Director General of the \uc2 IAEA\uc2 . Take a listen to the warning he issued about the situation there today.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL MARIANO GROSSI\uc2 , IAEA DIRECTOR GENERAL: This cannot go on. I am astonished by the complacency. Yes, the complacency. What are we doing to prevent this from happening? Each time we are rolling a dice. And if we allow these to continue time after time, then one day, our luck will run out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: You can sense there his outrage at the fact that this has happened again for the sixth time and his warning. That being said, there is no clear answer to his question. It is not clear what is being done to prevent that from happening again, given that Russia currently occupies that nuclear plant, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Erin McLaughlin, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 And moments ago, President Biden landed in Philadelphia. He`s about to unveil his new budget, setting up a battle royale with political power, bragging rights, and your tax dollars at stake. Also, the relentless wild weather in California is far from over, more dangerous conditions on the way and the rising new death toll was well. Plus, a stark new warning from US intelligence about China`s growing threat to national security, the latest from Hong Kong ahead. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: At this hour, President Biden just landing in Philadelphia to formally unveiled his budget in a strategic location battleground Pennsylvania, which tells you a lot about this whole budget exercise. Yes, it is about outlining the president`s 2024 priorities but also, as the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  frames it, about picking a fight with a Republican opposition that`s demanding severe spending cuts. So, Biden has really two major goals with this speech. One, he has to lay out the numbers, right? How much do we spend, who gets what, but the other is pure political messaging?\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Carol Lee is traveling with the president. And Stuart Stevens is the former chief strategist for Mitt Romney`s 2012 campaign and a senior adviser to the Lincoln Project. Good to have both of you. So, Carol, it`s hard to have a debate with much higher stakes than this, deciding how something that`s what six -- seven trillion dollars worth of money being spent. How much do we know right now about what`s in this budget proposal?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Chris, a lot of what`s in the president`s budget proposal will sound familiar to many viewers because there are proposals that he has put forward in the past, many of them, he was unable to get passed through Congress when Democrats had control of the House and the Senate. Now, it`s going to be even more difficult for him to get these things passed with Republicans having control of the House. But some of the provisions that the president is putting forward are things like expanding access to preschool, things like capping insulin cost for $35 a month for everyone, not just for seniors, as the president was able to achieve last year in Congress, but million -- minimum tax for billionaires, for instance, and increasing taxes on corporations and individuals making $400,000 a year or more. The president also proposing to cut the deficit by three trillion dollars over ten years and just shore up the Medicare trust fund.\par \par \uc2 But, Chris, the way he pays for things like that are by raising taxes on wealthier Americans and corporations. That`s something that Republicans don`t support, and so they`ll have to be negotiations on exactly what funding the government looks like later this year. Republicans are supposed to put forward their budget next month at some point, and there are things, Chris, that the White House is hoping that there can be compromised on things like crime prevention, for instance. I spoke with OMB Director Shalonda Young about this. And she`s described the president`s budget as the beginning of a dialogue, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, Stuart, there are a few things more guaranteed to cause political trouble than talking about entitlements, right? Medicare, Social Security, those kinds of things. We also know that they`re running out of money. Where do you see that part of the fight going?\par \par \uc2 STUART STEVENS, SENIOR ADVISER, THE LINCOLN PROJECT: Well, look. I think this puts the Democrats in a place where they want to be. This is they say a fight that they`re picking. You know, there`s been this big food fight inside the Republican Party because the Senate Republican Senatorial Campaign chair \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  put out this plan last year that called for sunsetting every government program every five years. So, that means every five years, you`d look at social security and look at Medicare.\par \par \uc2 And he put this out. It was a written document. They printed hundreds of thousands of copies of it. And \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  acted like he`d never seen this. And there`s this inner party fight. You know if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out there saying that he`ll play, it`s not the cut Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 You know, all those things that were listed in the president`s budget, if you tested those, they would all test weighted the good. I mean, the people who don`t want to raise taxes on people that make $40,000 or more, it`s pretty small. People to think that corporations should pay more is almost universal. This is a good fight that the Democrats are going to have. And you don`t have the Republicans really standing for anything.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, to that point, I mean, isn`t the Republican challenge than what do you offer as an alternative, right? The Biden White House, I think, in many -- in many cases, is looking to say, OK, you think you`ve got a better plan? Tell us exactly what it is, and tell us how you`re going to get it through.\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: Yes. I don`t want to be on the side arguing that we shouldn`t reduce insulin prices to $35 for Americans, you know. I don`t want to be on the side, the side that says that people that make over $400,000 a year should pay the same taxes to people that make $70,000 a year. You know, there`s been this complete collapse of any sort of Republican policy proposals. And you saw that in 2022 when the election played out and no one could really say what the Republicans were for.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 I mean, look. You know, I worked in a party for decades. And there used to be this thing that we like to say, and we sort of believed that we are the party of big ideas. And now you -- look who`s running for president. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is running on this kind of bathrooms and bedrooms plan.\par \par \uc2 This is just small stuff. And I think Republicans are really lost here. They don`t really have any center of gravity that relates to traditionally what a center-right party would be. And, look. I mean, officially, the platform of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  is still to being for whatever \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wants. That`s the -- that`s the platform that they passed in 2020, which just shows a complete lack of any substance or new ideas.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I don`t know how many times I`ve said this on the air. But to "James Carville," it`s the economy stupid. But when you look ahead to 2024, how much of it is about the way, the party`s -- the way a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or a -- or a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  present what they consider to be the priorities for how money should be spent, and how much of it is, frankly, where the economy goes and whether people are still feeling like they`re paying too much for eggs, and whether they feel like they can afford that ticket to go on summer vacation?\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: Well, you know, I think it`s an interesting test of that in 2022, when -- you know, the president went out, and it was something like the Lincoln Project, we had talked about a lot that he should do, and he put democracy on the ballot. And a lot of people say, well, you can`t do that, because it`s really going to be about gas prices, it`s really going to be about you know, grocery prices.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s important if -- for campaigns to invest in what they want to raise to be about. Pick the fight you want to be. So, usually in American politics to bigger candidate wins. Not always, I would say that didn`t happen in 2016. But I think that the task for Republicans here is to come up with some alternative plan that is going to be seen as somehow good for most Americans, somehow decent, somehow compassionate. And that`s a complete struggle the party is having without any success.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Stuart Stevens, Carol Lee, thanks to both of you. I just want to get you caught up because we have a new update on what happened to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the minority leader. The statement from his team now. It reads as follows. Leader McConnell tripped at a dinner event Wednesday evening and has been admitted to the hospital. This is the first we`re hearing of this.\par \par \uc2 He`s being treated for a concussion. He is expected to remain in the hospital for a few days of observation and treatment. The leader is grateful to the medical professionals for their care, and to his colleagues for their warm wishes. Among those as we told you just a short time ago, the president and first lady sending first best wishes to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . And we`ll take you, by the way, live to the president`s remarks when they begin in Philadelphia coming up about one hour from now.\par \par \uc2 While still reeling from what`s already happened, millions of Californians are now bracing for even more severe weather. Why the snow that is already there, combined with these new storms could exacerbate already nightmarish conditions? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:09]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: At least 12 people have now died from the California snowstorms that trapped people in their homes and knocked out power to thousands. Now another monster system is threatening to slam into the West Coast starting tonight.\par \par \uc2 Sixteen million people are under flood watches. The rain expected to last into the weekend, while some in Southern California are digging themselves out from that record-setting snow, and others have had to be rescued, including by intrepid volunteers. The situation is so desperate for some ranchers that local officials have been dropping bales of hay from helicopters to save starving cattle.\par \par \uc2 We have meteorologist Bill Karins tracking where that storm is heading, but we`re going start with Jacob Ward who is live in Santa Cruz, California.\par \par \uc2 Jacob, how are people preparing for the next storm when you`ve still got massive amounts of snow on the ground?\par \par \uc2 JACOB WARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, the rain is just beginning to fall here on the northern California coast, and where I`m standing in Santa Cruz, California, is really a place that gets it two ways. Behind me you can see the river mouth where the rest of California basically drains into the ocean like a bathtub. All the northern California coastal cities like Santa Cruz are that drain point, and that is where you get incredible flood waters coming out of the mountains.\par \par \uc2 So this is a morning really where people are, you know, slowly, I think, taking their last walk with a dog, hitting the surf one last time. But I want to show you here why it`s such a big deal when the flooding hits. These huge pieces of timber you see on the beach in front of me, you know, some weighing a thousand pounds or more. Those don`t come from the ocean. They come from inland.\par \par \uc2 They are swept out by the incredible violence in the waters when they hit, and the expectation is that as the rain that I`m beginning to feel on me now intensifies over the next 72 hours, it`s not only going to bring all of this sort of debris out into the bay like it has in the past, but of course as you mentioned, there`s the problem of the people at the higher elevations. If you trace these water ways up into the mountains, that is where snow is going to be hit by that rain, made even heavier.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen structures collapse already, and the damage is going to go on and on. That is what officials are worried about at this time. That is why the flood watch that so many millions are under is such a concern, not just here but especially at the higher elevations, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Bill, we`ve got all these concerns, what can we expect from this atmospheric river storm?\par \par \uc2 BILL KARINS, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: The biggest concern, Chris, is going to be tonight and tomorrow morning. We`re going to wonder how bad the flooding is going to be. So we had this tropical pool of moisture coming off the Pacific. Two huge storms just driving all of this rain towards the California coast as Jake was just mentioning. It has begun. He`s right here in Santa Cruz and that`s where some of the heavier rain is starting.\par \par \uc2 We may even get thunderstorms out of this. This is not like the last couple of storms we`ve seen. And rainfall totals in the mountains, remember, there`s already feet of snow. Some of these areas will absorb it, will be just fine. Just like a sponge, no problems.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 But some of the lower elevations, some of the snow melt will combine with the rivers and the heavy rain, and that`s when we`re really going to worry. And we`re also very concerned with some of the coastal areas, like the San Lorenzo River, that`s where we could have what we call major flooding. Almost like we have with the storm about a month ago in California.\par \par \uc2 So 16 million people are under flood watches throughout the region, and we are going to get snow out of this. Some of the highest of elevations will get up to six feet, but it`s really above 8,000. It`s even above some of the ski areas like Tahoe and Mammoth. A lot of those areas are going to go snow, it`ll rain, back to snow. So this won`t be about the snow story.\par \par \uc2 And we will get power outages. This isn`t the huge blizzard night we had last weekend, but we will see some wind gusts in the 40 to 50-mile-per hour range. That`s going to cause a lot of issues.\par \par \uc2 So, Chris, my biggest concerns are how bad is the flash flooding going to be tonight into tomorrow morning, will we get damage, will we have homes in trouble, and what`s going to happen to the areas that have snow on their roofs, and then we`re going to add this added weight and will we have roof collapses. So that`s what we`ll find out in the next 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We certainly have seen enough of those already.\par \par \uc2 Bill Karins, Jake Ward, thank you, guys, really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Well, if you can believe it, there`s been another violent incident in the air. It happened on a \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  flight in Dallas. Take a look at this recording because you can see a man in a tan suit who appears to begin pummeling another passenger earlier this week. A witness tells NBC News that the victim had accidentally bumped into the wife of the attacker just minutes earlier in the gate area.\par \par \uc2 And then in that other incident you probably have heard about, the 33-year- old man accused of trying to stab a flight attendant with a broken spoon, he`s expected in federal court any moment now for a detention hearing. Officials say he could face up to life in prison.\par \par \uc2 The biggest state in the country takes on one of the biggest pharmacy chains in the country with abortion access in the crosshairs and maybe the loyalty of customers at stake.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:02]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today it`s California versus \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , not a lawsuit, but a huge political and public relations fight over abortion access. Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  pulling out of a $54 million deal with \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  that was set to renew on May 1st after the pharmacy chain said they would not dispense an abortion pill in 21 states that threatened legal action.\par \par \uc2 Newsome releasing a blistering statement writing, "California will not stand by as corporations cave to extremists and cut off critical access to reproductive care and freedom." But a spokesperson for \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  tells NBC News that the company, quote, "is deeply disappointed by the decision by the state of California not to renew our long-standing contract due to false and misleading information."\par \par \uc2 Now $54 million is a small fraction of \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  nearly $133 billion in sales in the last fiscal year, but what`s still to be seen is how customers will react. Activists and critics are calling for a boycott.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in former U.S. attorney and MSNBC legal analyst Barbara McQuade.\par \par \uc2 So, Barbara, Newsome is accusing \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  of giving in after the legal threats from the 21 attorneys general. Other chains, CVS, \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  have not followed suit. But legally how much of a threat could AG`s pose to the company?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Well, there is certainly some threat here. You know, it is disappointing I suppose for those who care about reproductive rights to see a big company like \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  kind of cave into intimidation tactics. But there is some threat here.\par \par \uc2 There are laws in some of these states that say that these abortion pills cannot be distributed in their states. And until otherwise ruled in some ways, those laws are on the books. And so there is some legal risk for \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think in the long term those laws are likely to be struck down as in violation of the Supremacy Clause or even the Commerce Clause of the Constitution because federal law typically trumps state law, but for now, for the moment, there is some legal uncertainty, and for that reason, you know, corporations like to ask that in a risk averse kind of way and perhaps they see this as the risk averse move.\par \par \uc2 But if they boycott but large numbers of groups, it could be riskier to cave into the demands of these states.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As you know, it`s worth reminding people, though, medical abortions like the ones we`re talking about with these pills account for more than half of all abortions in the country. So given those stakes and the fact that as you indicated this may be just the start, how big and how long of a fight legally could we be looking at here?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: I think it really depends, Chris. You know, there are already some lawsuits pending across the country, and the resolution of those lawsuits could give us some clarity. For example, in West Virginia, a large manufacturer of these pills has sued the state of West Virginia, which has banned the pill and has made some of these arguments that their law is unconstitutional and asked the law to be struck down.\par \par \uc2 If they were to prevail there, then it would have a ripple effect elsewhere. We`ve seen challenges either some going one way to stop bans, some going the other way to challenge the FDA`s certification ruling, and so I would expect that, you know, it may take some months for these things to shake out, but perhaps in a year or so we`ll have some clarity on the law on this issue.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to ask you about another big case. This is Arkansas Governor \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2  signing a measure that rolls back child labor protections. Legally, what does that mean for the fight against underage workers?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yes, it`s an interesting parallel, Chris, to the other story, which is I`m sure why you`re asking about them both at the same time. You know, when you`ve got sort of a state versus federal faceoff in terms of what the laws are.\par \par \uc2 Ordinarily, states have higher levels of child protection laws than the federal government does. Both of them have laws about how many hours a child can work, the -- how late they can work, how early in the morning they can work. Whether they can work under certain kinds of dangerous situations.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 What they`ve done in Arkansas is roll back a state law that previously said that you had to have permission from your parents before you could have a job. That`s not a federal law. And so they can roll back their laws if they want to all the way back to federal laws. But to the extent they go beyond federal laws, that`s when, again, we would see this issue of the Supremacy Clause intervene and say states can`t go beyond those protections that the federal government has imposed.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Barbara McQuade, always great to have you on the program. Thanks so much. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 And right now the Supreme Court is asking for more funding to beef up its security in the wake of Roe v. Wade. They`ve put out a new budget request, and the court is asking for almost $6 million for expanded security activities conducted by court police, another 6.5 million for physical upgrades to the courthouse itself.\par \par \uc2 They`re also requesting more funding for enhanced protection against cybersecurity threats that they may face. The budget request notes that threat assessments have shown, quote, "evolving risks" that require continuous protection of those nine justices.\par \par \uc2 And today new concerns over China`s growing threat to U.S. national security. What \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  is now saying about his military. We`ll take you to Hong Kong next.\par \par \uc2 And as another \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  train derails, what will Congress do to hold \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  accountable? I`ll ask Senator \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2  who was in today`s hearing ahead in our next hour.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:19]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`re hearing some concerning new rhetoric from China`s leader \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  today who`s calling for more quickly elevating his country`s armed forces so they can, quote, "win wars." It comes as top U.S. intel chiefs are back on Capitol Hill for a second day of hearings. Singling out China as our most serious and consequential threat.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Josh Lederman is following all those developments from Hong Kong. And you know this, Xi made those remarks to China`s parliament ahead of a vote to confirm his unprecedented third term as leader. Talk to us about this aggressive posture he`s been taking toward the U.S. and where this strained relationship may be going from here.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, this all takes place, Chris, in the context of this big annual legislative meeting where President Xi is set to start his historic third term, and so his big focus on this day of the section was on the need, in his view, to really upgrade and enhance China`s military to increase its capabilities and things like emergency fields, in bringing its industrial supply chain up to modern standards, in science and technology.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of this fits into what President Xi has tried to emphasize that he calls made in China 2025, which is essentially to bring the civilian and military parts of China`s economy under the same umbrella, make sure that they are all rowing in the same direction. That is something the Chinese government feels is necessary for it to be able to take on and have deterrence against the threats that it perceives from the U.S. and other countries just as U.S. intelligence officials, as you mentioned, like Avril Haynes, the head of the National Intelligence director is saying that China remains the number one threat and concern that they are focused about.\par \par \uc2 So even as China is working to improve its own military and bring it up to a really modern-day standard, the U.S. doing the same as well with President Biden announcing in that budget release just today that he also wants more for the U.S. Military. About $850 billion he wants to include which according to Defense Secretary Austin is about $100 billion more than what was included for our military in the 2022 fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 So you can see both of these countries really trying to step up both their spending and their modernization of the militaries as they prepare for any potential conflict that could lie ahead as China`s Foreign minister just this week warns that he believes the U.S. is putting these two countries on an inevitable path to conflict, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Josh Lederman, thank you for that. Live from Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 The plane that just disappeared, a highly anticipated docuseries is testing out new theories about how MH370 vanished without a trace nearly a decade ago. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:57:18]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s one of those enduring mysteries. Nine years ago, a plane vanished from the sky without a trace. Now a \uc2 Netflix\uc2  docuseries is taking a closer look at the mysterious disappearance of Flight MH370.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Keir Simmons has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Among the greatest aviation mysteries of all time, MH370 disappeared nine years ago. The \uc2 Malaysia Airlines\uc2  passenger plane and all 239 people on board never seen again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Planes go up, planes go down.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Now, a new three-part \uc2 Netflix\uc2  docuseries explores the many theories surrounding what happened ranging from the rational to the unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What planes don`t do is just vanish off the face of the earth.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : A \uc2 Boeing\uc2  777, MH370 was a red eye from Kuala Lumpur to Beijing. It took off for 12:41 a.m. Its last cryptic communication just 38 minutes later. Then at 2:22 a.m., the plane suddenly and inexplicably went dark.\par \par \uc2 In 2014, I reported on the desperate and ultimately doomed search for survivors.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): We`re in the cockpit of a P3 Orion flying at about 500 feet, looking for Flight 370. And it is an extraordinary expanse of ocean out there.\par \par \uc2 (Voice-over): Nine years on, a widely accepted account is that the plane banked left, then flew south across the Indian Ocean for six hours before disappearing. The extensive 2 and a half year search that followed, never finding any trace of it. Since then, pieces of the plane have washed up on beaches.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Netflix\uc2  docuseries features devastated victims` families. It also portrays how social media with its then expanding influence helped fuel conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We received so many reports from the social media. One claimed that the airplane was hijacked.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : One theory is that the pilot deliberately diverted the plane, having practiced on a home simulator but the series says there is little evidence of that.\par \par \uc2 The fascination with MH370 continues. A passenger jet on a routine flight. There on the radar one minute, gone the next.\par \par \uc2 Keir Simmons, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We have a lot to cover in our second hour of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. Let`s get right to it.\par \par \uc2 At this hour, senator silenced.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230310ej3900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for March 8, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Antonia Hylton \par \uc2 8,038 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [23:01:22]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Sorry to Stephanie Ruhle, THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, a Republican rehabs, House lawmakers launch a new investigation into the January 6 committee and its leader, the GOP Congressman himself accused of giving tours before the riot.\par \par \uc2 Then the looming budget battle, Republicans focused on kitchen table cuts. But how`s that going to play with voters?\par \par \uc2 And my one on one with Oscar nominated actress Michelle Yeoh. She opens up about her journey to Hollywood and why this award does matter to more than just her, as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this very special Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. House Republicans are putting the January 6 committee under scrutiny again. A GOP controlled House committee has launched an investigation into the panel. A staff member said it will focus on analyzing how its inquiry was conducted. This new investigation will be led by Congressman \uc2 Barry Loudermilk\uc2 . Remember him? He is the one that the January 6 committee said let a tour of the Capitol a day before the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN, (D) CALIFORNIA JANUARY 6TH COMMITTEE MEMBER: The Committee did ask him to explain why he was giving a tour to somebody who showed up on the fifth of January, who showed up the next day at the Capitol threatening the life of Speaker Pelosi. We didn`t accuse Loudermilk evading, we just wanted him to explain how did this happen? And he refused to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Jan. 6 Committee Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  also weighed in today saying this, "If the House GOP wants new January 6 hearings, bring it on. Let`s replay every witness and all the evidence from last year." Reminder almost every witness was Republican.\par \par \uc2 This comes as the White House is calling out \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  by name for portraying the Capitol attack as largely peaceful. Today in a statement a White House spokesperson said, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  is not credible.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden tweeted about Carlson saying this, "More than 140 officers were injured on June 6. I`ve said it before. How dare anyone diminish or deny the hell they went through?"\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there`s new reporting that House Republicans \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and James Comer are working on a trip to visit jailed January 6 defendants. Politico says, Greene and Comer expect to send out a letter this week inviting other lawmakers to join them on the trip.\par \par \uc2 With that let`s get smarter with help our leadoff panel which I will say is a superb one. Katie Benner, Senior Pulitzer Prize Winning Justice Department Reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  , former U.S. Attorney Joyce Vance, who spent 25 years as a federal prosecutor and former Missouri Senator and MSNBC Political Analyst Claire McCaskill.\par \par \uc2 Claire, help us out, Republicans want to re litigate January 6. They don`t have the truth on their side, and their voters want to move on. So what`s the strategy?\par \par \uc2 FORMER SEN. CLAIRE MCCASKILL, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Listen, here`s what they`re -- I don`t get it. It gives me a headache. I think what they`re doing honestly is their plane to such a small group of very loud people. I mean, everyone is all upset about \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  . Remember, Stephanie, his audience, what? 3 million people. There were 160 million people who voted in the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: The vast majority of Americans are not wanting to go back and be reminded of what happened that day because everyone saw what happened, no matter what \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  says or \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  all of a sudden, first of all, they turn on the police and now they`re going, oh let`s go give comfort to these people who have been found guilty or pled guilty of crimes in our nation`s Capitol. It`s crazy town.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:16]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Did his viewers even know this, right? Because we could all be saying this is crazy. But you`re from Missouri. There`s a lot of Republican voters and Fox viewers, what kind of alternate reality are they living in?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Oh, they are. They`re in an alternate reality, no question about it. I mean, I`m from a state where the Republicans just voted to let minors openly carry weapons without adult supervision, literally.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Why?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Because they are in this little bubble where they think everybody thinks the way they do, and they`re going too far. And that`s why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won. That`s why the midterms were not good for them. So I say if they want to stay on January 6, let him do it. Because if they -- the alternative is cutting the budget, in a way that hurts middle class families, I think 2024 will be a very good year for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Jan. 6 is not a kitchen table topic.\par \par \uc2 Katie, Kevin McCarthy also made those tapes available to January 6 defendants. The Justice Department is pushing back on how these tapes can be used. What`s the -- what`s the Department of Justice has argument here?\par \par \uc2 KATIE BENNER, THE NEW YORK TIMES JUSTICE DEPARTMENT REPORTER: The Justice Department is saying that the defendants had access to the evidence that they needed that that`s part of the process, when you`re going to prosecute somebody, you give them -- the prosecutor say this is what we have, they give it to the defense so they can mount the strongest defense is allowed by our criminal justice system.\par \par \uc2 The Department is saying that these additional tapes do not fall under this sort of agreement. And that what it is essentially is a PR stunt by \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  . You know, for the Department, they`re going to follow the facts and the law, they`re going to do their jobs. These things should not really impact their investigations. And yet, we`re starting to see the world of Fox News encroach even on the Justice Department`s own work.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: A PR stunt. He got 44,000 hours of video, he aired less than four minutes. Big stunt.\par \par \uc2 Joyce, this footage though, could it impact the prosecution`s efforts in these in these cases?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Well, DOJ is arguing that trial shouldn`t be delayed, that this wasn`t the type of evidence that they`re obligated to turn over to defendants under Rule 16. And that seems pretty clear in this situation. DOJ only has to turn over evidence that`s in its possession, and that`s uniquely available to it. These defendants I suppose could have reached out earlier if they wanted this information. But the bottom line is that it`s not relevant to their cases. It`s not exculpatory for them. It doesn`t tend to give them a defense. And what this is essentially is a stunt designed to throw a monkey wrench in these already cumbersome proceedings. Hopefully, the judges will see through the stunt and order that the cases go on expeditiously.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Speaking of stunts, why Claire, would \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and James Comer, want to visit people convicted from January 6 who are currently sitting in jail -- excuse me currently, defendants?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, it really if you think about it, they spent a lot of time talking about putting people in prison, being tough on crime and supporting the police. In one fell swoop, they`ve wiped all that away. Because yesterday, they basically were saying that the police really, there was no problem at the Capitol. They weren`t assaulted, they were fighting for their lives. And believe me, every police officer in the country related to those police officers and what happened.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, they`re now saying, if you have been convicted under the laws of this country, and you are in prison, you deserve our comfort and our aid. And by the way, Trump has promised to pardon them all. So it is really something for the party that likes to say that they are all about the rule of law in supporting employees.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But Katie, couldn`t Trump have done something before he left office if he really wanted to help them or it was too late?\par \par \uc2 BENNER: Of course, I mean, if he -- when he was president, he could have done anything he wanted, including on pardons, excuse me, including preemptively parting pardoning this group of people.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But he didn`t.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: He didn`t do. And I think that what we`re seeing now is that he wants to use even these prosecutions as part of his campaign. And we`re going to watch him exploit these people again and again, because for him, it`s really -- it`s all game he gets, he does not lose anything by using this group of people.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, but with this group of people they actually know better. Can`t they say, excuse me, sir, you had ample time to help me out, and you didn`t.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: So that`s what`s so interesting, because what we`re seeing is a real fight again, which we`ve been fighting about for years now. A fight over the truth. And this doesn`t really impact the rule of law, these investigations, but what it does is it trickles out into the public and the public perception of what happened again and again and again. The only time we`re going to see, they literally bump up against what the Justice Department is doing is if there are any indictments of people including Trump around Trump what happens to the juries what happens to the people in this country who then have to sit down and decide who is telling the truth?\par \par \uc2 [23:10:14]\par \par \uc2 So whoever has the most compelling narrative right now, it does matter.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joyce, what message is being sent when we see some lawmakers appearing to support people who are allegedly involved in the attack on our capitol?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, it`s the same message that they`ve been sending the country really ever since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took office, right? The message is that this is all about political theater. Because everyone knows that these folks have been convicted, many of them have pleaded guilty. There is not a question at issue about improper prosecutions here.\par \par \uc2 To the extent that they`re going to visit the prisons because they`re concerned about the conditions that these prisoners are in. Perhaps they could spend some of their time more productively visiting some of the state prison systems where DOJ has had to file lawsuits, challenging the conditions of confinement is cruel and unusual. Alabama is one of those states, Georgia, there are others.\par \par \uc2 Those sorts of allegations don`t exist here. So this is political pandering. It`s very disruptive for the prison system. This notion of having to bring in a lot of people like this who are essentially very high risk in a prison setting. At one point I accompanied then Attorney General \uc2 Loretta Lynch\uc2  to visit FCI Talladega, the federal prison in Talladega, Alabama. And that was very disruptive. There had to be a very high level of security. She wanted to interact with prisoners that required other layers of security, what you don`t want to do is disrupt the functioning of the prison system. A trip like this, though, seems designed to disrupt the functioning. That almost seems to be the goal here. And we should all be disturbed by that, especially from the party that says that they`re the party of law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then Claire, take me to all of those folks, the Republican folks who have this equivalency with let`s talk about culture issues, let`s talk about anti-woke issues. I get it that that`s a priority. But how does that priority equate with this kind of stuff with a tax on our democracy with rewriting our history?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Yeah, it doesn`t. It makes no sense. And, you know, they have events brewing here, Steph, is a real war within the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tell more.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: You saw the reaction, the different reactions to what \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  did this week. You saw the Marjorie Taylor Greene`s and the crazy caucus say oh, yeah, you know, it`s just tourists and let`s go take them cookies in prison. And then you saw others like \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  saying that`s BS. And you say BS. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  , \uc2 John Thune\uc2  , all of them saying we should not whitewash what happened.\par \par \uc2 My favorite was the interview of \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  , where he said, oh, yeah, I think it`s great. \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  did that. And I wanted the reporter right then to say, well, then how come you ran so fast? If it was just a bunch of sweet tourists taking selfies? It really is interesting, because you do have two camps in the Republican Party. And we`re going to see them square off over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But how does that work? Because especially for the last few years, it`s that right-wing media complex that has been so crucially important to the Republican Party. Is \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  saying no more, I don`t need Tucker.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Hey, listen, crazy always gets more attention than normal. So those folks are going to be the noisiest. They`re going to get the most eyeballs. They`re going to get the most clicks, they`re going to get the most attention. And by the way, I don`t think that`s good for the Republican Party. Because right now, the face the Republican Party, is Santos and \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  . I`m not sure that`s a winning ticket for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s talk about a different face, Katie. Jack Smith, there`s so much talk all the time, you know, more progress is being made. Things are imminent, I removed the word imminent from my vocabulary. What do you think`s happening over there?\par \par \uc2 BENNER: Yeah, so I also do not ever use the word imminent, I think what`s happening is the Justice Department is running down every lead, many of the leads that we`ve saw publicly uncovered by the January 6 committee for the department, they need to make sure that they understand everything that happened, they need to interview all of these witnesses. Because for them, they`re -- they have a different project. The January 6 committee project was to say to the American public, here`s our narrative of what happened for the Justice Department, they need to battle test every potential witness and then need to battle test every potential charge against somebody who they think might have committed a crime. That means interviewing them, subpoenaing them, getting evidence and saying to themselves, will this hold up in front of a jury? Is there something the defense has in their pocket that the J. 6 committee did not air on TV that could undercut our argument. Takes a long time.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: But why did they wait to start to do that after the J. 6 committee finished? Why did they wait so long to begin this process? That`s the question that remains unanswered and I still can`t figure it out.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:04]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But I would sure like to know it. Claire McCaskill, Katie Benner, Joyce Vance, thank you all so much. We`re also tracking important developments on policing and the Justice Department, a Department of Justice review, found that Louisville police engaged in a pattern of discriminatory and unconstitutional behavior toward black people. They also launched another review this time of the Memphis Police after the death of Tyre Nichols.\par \par \uc2 With us for more NBC`s Antonia Hylton. Antonia, talk to us about this new DOJ review of the Memphis Police. What do we know?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Stephanie, the analysis review is going to look into culture procedures, practices within the department. But it`s actually going to be more expansive than that. And what we also know is that there`ll be looking at the culture of units, like the SCORPION Unit. It was a reminder for folks who`ve been following this for weeks now, the five officers at the center of this case were members of a specialized team called the SCORPION Unit. And we see these units across the country in major cities like Baltimore, and Chicago as well. And the DOJ is going to be looking at the purpose and the culture of these teams, too.\par \par \uc2 And ultimately, they`re going to come out with a report of their findings, and also with some recommendations for the city. And the city actually requested the DOJ take this action as part of their own efforts to be transparent right now, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How`s the community reacting to this review? What do they want to see happen?\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: You know, people are grateful that the DOJ is taking this action. They`ve been on the scene since the very beginning, looking into this as a civil rights investigation initially, as well. But there`s also a lot of local sustained effort there. You know, you don`t see images of protests coming out of Memphis every single day. But what I can tell you is that we didn`t members, friends of the family have been in city council meetings, they have been calling the mayor relentlessly. And just last night, the city face to that work that the family pushed for their very clear and consistent demands, they passed five new ordinances that are actually going to have tangible impact.\par \par \uc2 Now, it covers a range of things, but a number of them touch on ways in which individual or independent groups are going to be able to get initial oversight over the Police Department, there`s going to be a strengthened civilian review board, there`s going to have to be more data transparency from the Police Department. And then one that stood out to me was the end of the use of unmarked cars for traffic stops. That`s something I heard time and time and again when I was in the city. And that is, of course, how this all began an accusation of a reckless driving, stop and carry seeming confused about who was pulling him over. Why not even hearing, what the reason was for the stop. So they`re going to be looking into all that as part of this review. And it looks like already locally action is now happening, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, it is a start. Antonia, always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  is here and we got a lot to talk about. Top of the list to fight to protect abortion rights. And you do not want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 And later, she`s dominating the award circuit right now. But she says she`s not done yet. My one on one was superstar actress Michelle Yeoh, and her very important message. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a very important Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:22:23]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Nine months after the Supreme Court overturned Roe vs. Wade, the fight for abortion rights continues in this country. Five women are suing Texas over its near total abortion ban. They say the law prevented them from getting proper medical care and put their lives in danger.\par \par \uc2 And Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  of California said his state will cut ties with \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  over its decision to drop abortion pills in several states where abortion is still legal.\par \par \uc2 Back with me tonight, Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  of California who was recently announced she will be running for Senate next year. Congresswoman, you have said that the right to abortion care is essential in the financial well-being of American families, remind our audience why that is.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KATIE PORTER\uc2 , (D) CALIFORNIA: The decision is a huge one. It affects every aspect of someone`s life, including the cost of childcare, the potential interruptions to the workplace, long-term financial planning, financial security. So big decision and that`s exactly why, given that it`s such a big decision that it should be up to each and every person to make for themselves.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Has California seen an influx of women from other states seeking abortion care since the laws have changed?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Definitely, we`ve had some people choosing to come to California, having to come to California because they`re unable to get health care near them. And to California`s credit, we`ve worked hard to make sure people who need health care can get it. And California has tried to do its part. But I think it`s a terrible shame that people have to leave their communities, leave their families, leave their trusted health care providers in order to travel across state lines to get basic health care.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I got to ask you about \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  , according to your governor, California will no longer be doing business with the company anymore. I`m guessing you deport -- you support this decision?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Absolutely. California does not cower to extremists. We call them out. If \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  wants to make this decision, then California will make its decision which is not to do business with companies that don`t share our values and that restrict people`s ability to get healthcare. I absolutely support the Governor here and as a consumer and by the way, most of women do most of the shopping for everyday items like grocery stores and families. As a woman I`m going to be going in a different store. I`m going to be picking up a card somewhere else other than \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  going forward.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right new topic, let`s talk household economics. I know this is important to you. The White House is urging states to join their push against junk fees at the local level. We`re talking about things like credit card late fees, extra airline fees, how important is this fight for American families?\par \par \uc2 [23:25:04]\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Wall Street makes millions, billions in fact of dollars off these fees. I think the term junk fees sometimes suggest to people that it`s just a few bucks here and a few bucks there. But it`s not, we`re actually talking about most of these fees, you know, Stephanie, $10, $20, $50 $60.\par \par \uc2 With most of these fees, let`s take resort fees and hotels, just put it in the price, just put it in the price. So people could make their choice. Nobody shops for a credit card on the late fee. Instead, it surprises them. And there`s no relationship between these fees and the amount that`s being charged, and what it`s actually costing the business. So I strongly support the Biden administration on this. And let me tell you, I have never met a voter, an American, Republican, Democrat, Independent, that likes to feel cheated and ripped off. And we`ve all had that experience of looking at our bill, looking at the final receipt, and feeling that sense that we got cheated, and we don`t like it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I do not know anyone who goes on vacation and puts their bathing suit on and goes down to the hotel pool and says, yeah, I`m ready to pay a fee to go swim in the pool. So why is it that lawmakers on both sides of the aisle wouldn`t jump to get behind this thing? I mean, it is the most common-sense thing that most Americans are like, yeah, I don`t want to pay for that.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: We have too many elected officials, frankly, on both sides of the aisle that are beholden to big corporations that do Wall Street`s bidding. They take these elected officials take corporate contributions, corporate PAC contributions to get an office, they listen to those corporations when they come walking around, you know, knocking on doors, in the halls of Congress. And then they do their bidding by quietly and consistently refusing to take action on some of the most unpopular, most harmful kinds of consumer practices, including these kinds of junk fees.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: If a consumer business is only successful, because they`ve got these nonsense, junk fees embedded in their costs, the news flash, it ain`t that good of a business. I do want to talk to you. Before you go, though, about misinformation. I know there are a lot of Republican voters in your district you just won in the last midterm. So you are knocking on doors every single day. We talk all the time about the dangers of misinformation. But this week with the Dominion lawsuit with Fox, we are seeing it up close and personal. Do you think any of these revelations are going to impact voters in your district?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, to be honest, one of the problems we face is that the viewers who watch Fox aren`t watching the stations that are talking about these revelations. We see much more segmentation people watch, "their channel," they don`t get information from the same sources. And so we literally when we`re knocking doors, and we`re having conversations. I spend a lot of my time trying to get on the same page with people about facts. So I think this is a real consequence of the loss of journalism, the loss of local newspapers, the loss of local TV stations. And what it means is, I don`t think the Fox viewers are tuning in to hear you and others explain exactly what`s going on. So I think it`s going to make the left feel more frustrated. And I think the right is going to continue to do what they`ve been doing, which sadly, is not getting told the truth.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, the truth matters, but only if you hear it. So we`re going to have to tell it every single night. And those viewers, they`re welcome to come over any night of the week.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thank you for joining tonight. I always appreciate seeing you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Republicans promised not to touch Medicare and Social Security. But they didn`t say anything about cuts to other crucial kitchen table issues, programs that hardworking Americans rely on. We`re going to look at what they are looking to slash, when the 11 Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:33:36]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE, (R) LOUISIANA HOUSE MAJORITY LEADER: We need a strong national defense. We want to strengthen Social Security and Medicare and yet those programs are less stable today than they`ve ever been before. And so as we talk about the debt ceiling and how to get control over spending, I think it`s important to engage the country in just how serious this problem is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I`m not going to subject you to my broken record again. As we`ve talked about many times the debt ceiling is about spending that already happened. And while Speaker McCarthy has said cuts to Medicare and Social Security are off the table and debt ceiling talks. Other cuts are in play.\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reporting hard right House Republicans are readying a plan to gut the nation`s foreign aid budget and make deep cuts to health care, food assistance and housing programs for poor Americans and their drive to balance the federal budget.\par \par \uc2 So let`s discuss with Leigh Ann Caldwell, an anchor for \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  live and co-author of its morning newsletter the early 202. And former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Christine Todd Whitman\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Governor we often hear about kitchen table issues. These programs some of these programs we are talking about that they want to cut are at the essence of programs that hard-working Americans are relying on, is this the Republicans should be targeting.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE TODD WHITMAN\uc2  , (R) FORMER NEW JERSEY GOVERNOR: I don`t think so. I mean when you talk about eliminating Section 8 which helps with renters, low-income, low-income renters pay their bills, when you talk about cutting the housing programs 42%, you know, those are not -- those are not the where we shouldn`t be making the cuts. And we especially should not be making cuts to foreign aid right now.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:13]\par \par \uc2 We need to be strong and show Putin and Jinping and everyone, our enemies that we are strong, and we will stay with our allies. And we will support our allies, and then to start to do away with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  `s counter intelligence budget at a time when we know that we have these problems with China, and we`re all worried about them. And yet they start to cut that budget, decimate it, it just makes no sense. It -- there`s no -- there`s no rationale to it. And that`s what happens when you have a party that doesn`t have a principals central core anymore. This just whatever Trump says they`ll do. And so they don`t have a compass. They`ve lost it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: There`s a lot of Republican lawmakers that agree with every single thing the governor just said. So Leigh Ann, how split is McCarthy`s caucus? It`s not just Republicans versus Democrats. A lot of them are saying right on watching the former governor?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, EARLY 2020 CO-AUTHOR, THE WASHINGTON POST: Yeah, so it`s really interesting, this battle that`s happening within the Republican Party. So they`re supposed to have a budget that comes out, of course, the President`s budget that his, you know, priorities are coming out tomorrow, the Republicans were going to have their budget come out mid-April. And now they`re saying it`s going to be delayed a couple of weeks. The reason, well, because it`s proving a lot harder to get the Republican Party behind a single budget. This is going to be a monumental task for House Republicans to agree on programs to cut, they`re all over the place, especially with the funding cuts that they say that they want.\par \par \uc2 For example, some say they want a balanced budget, some say they want to come go back to pre-COVID spending levels. Well, all of these things they say they want to do, what about not touching Social Security and Medicare for the time being in this round anyway. But they`re going to do it with around the edges. And there`s no way that they can accomplish these goals just by cutting around the edges, by cutting the food stamp program or cutting housing assistance. It`s just not possible. And so that`s the dilemma that House Republicans have reality versus their wish list and trying to sell this to the public.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Governor, let`s talk about the flip side, because the President isn`t just saying, well, let`s just keep spending, he`s looking to raise or cut somewhere. And in part of his proposal, he`s saying he wants to increase taxes on wealthy Americans. That`s how he`s going to potentially extend Medicare funding.\par \par \uc2 Now, that makes sense in a lot of states. But one of the issues is the definition of wealthy in one state means something very different in another state. We have heard from lawmakers, Democratic lawmakers and states like yours, New Jersey, who say if we start raising taxes on people, depending on how much money they make, it can be really bad in some states. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 TODD WHITMAN: Well, it could be but there are ways around that. And there are ways to set the formula so that it is just that a formula isn`t a blanket cut, automatic 10%, let`s say or something like that. That is something that`s based on income levels. And that mean, that`s what we did when in New Jersey when I became governor and I said I`d cut the income tax by 30%. What we did is I actually took, I think it was 130,000 people off the tax rolls entirely because I didn`t think they earned enough in New Jersey to have to pay the income tax that was just going to put an extra burden on them. So we eliminated them, we just didn`t lower.\par \par \uc2 We cut the tax for middle income people but not for the higher -- high end people. They ended up percentage wise paying a bigger portion of the tax -- of the taxes. They didn`t pay more, but they paid a greater portion. Because we cut took all these other people off the rolls. So you can do it that way. There is a way to do it. And we should close loopholes. So the corporation`s don`t get away with not paying anything, their fair share. There`s a lot that needs to be done. But of course, you`ve got to fund the \uc2 IRS\uc2  . And that`s another thing that Republicans are going after. They want to cut the \uc2 IRS\uc2  and the one hand you want to say you want to have taxes fairly administered. But then you want to cut the very people who`ve already been caught under the Trump administration. They stop their ability to do their job.\par \par \uc2 Again, there`s no consistency here. There`s no -- there`s no principled approach. And it`s very frustrating to watch what`s happening. It`s going to have a real -- if we -- if we default on the program that you don`t want to talk about anymore, because you talk about it all the time on the deficit. You know, paying bills that we`ve already incurred, that could set the world into a financial crisis. So I mean this is not something you joke about but I think we have people there in Washington who are new and just don`t understand.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:08]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Wall Street who are saying, oh, no big deal. It`s not going to happen. Just wait.\par \par \uc2 Leigh Ann, my colleague, Carol Lee had an exclusive interview with OMB Director \uc2 Shalanda Young\uc2  . I want to share what she said about the President`s budget.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHALANDA YOUNG\uc2  , OMB DIRECTOR: If you want to talk about being fiscally responsible, he`s done it. This budget shows how he would continue. Yes, this is the beginning of a dialogue. Absolutely. But we`ve seen proposals that go the opposite way. And we believe that Republicans need to put their plan on the table. This President has done that. And we welcome how they`re going to bring down the deficit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Leigh Ann, he does have a plan. But let`s be honest, there is zero chance that Republicans are going to agree to his budget. So is this thing basically just a political proposal?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: It is, but all budgets are. So that`s nothing that`s different. But what this is, Steph, it`s an opening salvo in these negotiations in this conversation about the debt and the deficit. You`re going to have the President`s budget that comes out tomorrow, and you know, how they are -- how the President plans to reduce the deficit, a lot by raising taxes on people who make over $400,000, or millionaires and billionaires, so they say.\par \par \uc2 And they say that there`s a real way to reduce the debt, the deficit by -- without having to cut a lot of government programs. Now, Republicans on the other hand, when if they released their budget, which is also going to be a political document, it`s going to be the extreme opposite where there`s going to be no tax increases. They want to cut spend -- cut the deficit completely by cutting government funding. And so that`s when these negotiations happen and it`s going to roll right into this conversation with the debt limit. And look for the last week of July, Steph, because we think that that is going to be witness big showdown is really going to take place, no vacation.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All eyes will be appealed. Leigh Ann Caldwell, Governor \uc2 Christine Todd Whitman\uc2  , thank you both for being here tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Academy Award winner, it`s pretty amazing ring to it. And actress Michelle Yeoh hopes to gain that title at this weekend`s Oscars. Why she says a win for her is a win for so many others, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:01]\par \par \uc2 (MOVIE CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: If you have not seen everything everywhere all at once yet, do yourself a favor and check it out before Sunday`s Academy Awards. The film has 11 nominations more than any other one this year. The Stellar cast is led by Michelle Yeoh, a Hollywood legend, who you might know from Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon, or the James Bond film Tomorrow Never Dies. She is nominated for Best Actress, the first Asian woman to be nominated in more than 85 years.\par \par \uc2 Well, I had the great honor of sitting down with Michelle to talk about her brilliant career, and the message of this truly unique film, and what an Oscar this weekend would need to her and a whole lot of other people.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE YEOH, OSCAR-NOMINATED ACTRESS: It is completely overwhelming because it`s -- this is the first time I`ve ever been nominated. And I`ve been in the business now. I mean, in Hollywood for more than 20 something, or 30 years. And first time I get a Golden Globe nomination. I mean sitting there and praying that they`re going to say your name is one thing. And when they do you are just -- those emotions just wash over you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Here you are today. And you are one of the most recognizable Asian actors in the world. Did you dream to be in this place?\par \par \uc2 YEOH: No. I don`t think I ever started, as a little girl thinking one day, I`m going to be an actress, let alone a famous actress.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Did you know what when you read the script, were you like, yeah, this is a winner.\par \par \uc2 YEOH: When I read the script, I was like, yes, finally.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Really?\par \par \uc2 YEOH: I read scripts a lot. But generally all these roles are mainly written for men. As an aging woman, you know, those kinds of roles where you as an ordinary woman gets a chance to be --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You are not ordinary.\par \par \uc2 YEOH: -- gets a chance to be extraordinary. It`s -- that is such a precious, precious gem. And when I read it, it was like, you know, it`s all the years that I have trained as an actor. I`m given a chance in this one movie to show you what I am capable of.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: People who saw what have they told you? I mean, for me, what hit me is the relationship with our aging parents, right our impatience around them, and not necessarily appreciating what it`s like for them trapped in their own body.\par \par \uc2 YEOH: Correct. Yes. See, that`s what I loved about the film. It appealed to every person and in a different way. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:03]\par \par \uc2 Sometimes life gets the better of you and so this -- I think this film reminds us that we are human. And it`s OK. And it`s OK and we have to learn to breathe, live with it and move forward and try not to do it again.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You talked about how messages in this film maybe help heal people. Do you feel like it did that for you in your own life?\par \par \uc2 YEOH: Yes, I think it opened my eyes to a lot of things, it opened my eyes that even now at this age, I am still able to be top of my -- what I`m doing, that I am so happy. I never sort of took a step back and say, you know, this is as good as it gets.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That`s exactly what I wanted to ask you. Was there any point in your career where you said, this is enough? I`ve gone far enough, I`m out. I don`t want to do this anymore.\par \par \uc2 YEOH: I didn`t think of it that way. I was very disappointed when the roles got smaller. I have been a very hard working. I still worked constantly. But I didn`t get the way. You know, in my younger days where you were the number one or you were the why is it that the guys get to be action heroes, even when they`re old, right? And why is it women gets relegated to the mom that sits at home worrying about whether the father or the hero rescued her children? It didn`t make sense to me. But the problem was, and also the other thing was like, why would there not more roles that we -- so that we can have a seat at the table?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do you really think things are changing? I mean, for example, it`s rare that you see an Asian LED TV show.\par \par \uc2 YEOH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Or film, breakthrough or get a chance. Do you think that`s changing in a significant way?\par \par \uc2 YEOH: It has changed. Why, I`m saying that, I am seeing there are more stories being told things that I tried to pitch 10 years ago, 15 years ago, would have been met with like, oh, yeah, very interesting. You know, but maybe the time is not right. So we have not for the lack of trying. But it was not being received and minds were not that open yet.\par \par \uc2 Because at that point, it was just talk, just the beginning of what is diversity, inclusivity, you know, don`t always show these people in a very stereotyped way, that you -- they deserve a life. They deserve to have their dreams and their aspirations, their hopes, what are they that`s what we need to understand. So I see, I see the change, I have seen, I`ve been watching the change. And I think there`s a whole group of us that are pushing for that change. And we can take our foot off the pedal in that way.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: With success that you`re having right now, some might say, all right, you just achieved it. Now let`s time to sit back, or do you feel like no, I`ve got the momentum? Let`s rev this engine up?\par \par \uc2 YEOH: Oh, yeah, this momentum has to keep going forwards, we need to say, but this is America as well. Like everything everywhere, all at once. This is in America. This is an Asian fat immigrant family in America. And we have to hold up the mirror to ourselves and to our society and say, we are all here. And we`re all here together.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: People say, oh, awards don`t matter. But the Oscars are coming.\par \par \uc2 YEOH: Yes, it matters.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It matters.\par \par \uc2 YEOH: How, it matters a lot. And it doesn`t -- it`s not just mattering to me, is shining the light on a part of the world, of people that looks like me. Who`s never been included. So it matters. And yes, do I want it? Yes. I want to not just, yes, for me -- but not just for me, but just to say we all can sit at the same table, work together and tell beautiful stories together, so.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I want it for you, too. Oh my god.\par \par \uc2 YEOH: Oh my God, I meet another sister.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Oh my gosh, you are an incredible woman. Thank you for this opportunity. Thank you for sitting down with me.\par \par \uc2 YEOH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Thank you. Gosh, I`m so sorry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And I`m sorry to the other nominees. Well, I don`t have the opportunity to vote. If I did. She`d be getting mine. What an amazing woman. You can watch our full interview on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  . Just go to the MSNBC Channel.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, do you know what today is? I think you do. We`re going to take a look at the state of women`s rights across the globe. Why tonight because, this is a holiday. When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:50]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, International Women`s Day. You may not have noticed, but every single one of our stellar guests tonight were, in fact, women. They were not here to talk about gender issues. They were here because they are the best in their field, talented, smart, extraordinary in every way. And they just happened to be female.\par \par \uc2 Well, every international women`s day since 2012, since I started in TV, I have been doing this 12 years and all, all female broadcasts on this very day. My favorite day of the year. But unfortunately, when it comes to women`s rights across the world, U.N . Secretary General says we are 300 years away from gender equality, warning that the progress that has been made is vanishing before our eyes. Whether it`s right here in the U.S. where abortion access is disappearing in many places to recent reports that Iranian students, the vast majority girls may have been poisoned, or the risks women in Ukraine are facing as Russia`s invasion goes into a second year. And today the U.N . said Afghanistan is the most repressive country for women after the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  `s takeover erase their rights.\par \par \uc2 [00:00:03]\par \par \uc2 Women there may be down but they are not out. The Afghan all girls robotics team has not given up on a more equal future. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AFSANA AHMADI: I can be the voice of my friends and I can do something from here that can help them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And they know they`re the lucky ones safely out of the country, safe enough to dream big.\par \par \uc2 FLORENCE POUYA: Our friends are still in there. And they will be the future scientists, the future leaders, the future engineers, the one who can bring a solution for the cancer.\par \par \uc2 AHMADI: We are also trying hard to serve our country from afar to tell the world and show the world that Afghan girls can be amazing if they are given the opportunity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: A reminder that when some suffer, we all suffer. And the only way we can truly win is together. Because together we rise.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks at NBC News, thanks for staying up late. I`ll see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020230309ej3800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for March 7, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Jen Psaki \par \uc2 8,158 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: That`s tonight`s "REIDOUT". Thanks for watching. ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN, explosive new revelations about what was actually happening behind the scenes at Fox while the pedaled election lies on air. Tonight, we`re combing through hundreds of pages of brand-new filings. Then --\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): I think what happened on January shit six was despicable.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was an attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 KEVIN CRAMER\uc2  (R-NC): I didn`t listen to \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  , I generally don`t. I think it`s (BLEEP).\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Condemnation from all sides as Fox tries to launder the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  is a propagandist publicly pretending to be a newsman.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: With the explicit help of Republican House leadership.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: Speaker McCarthy knows damn well what kind of customer Mr. Carlson is.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And as abortion bans threatened countless women in Texas, one of the five women now suing the state will join me tonight.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA ZURAWSKI, PLAINTIFF IN TEXAS LAWSUIT: It sounds like a pretty sick and twisted plot to a dystopian novel, but it`s not. It`s exactly what happened to me while pregnant in Texas.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Good evening from New York, I`m Chris Hayes. In the wake of Tucker Carlson`s attempt to deceive his viewers about what really happened on January 6, the stuff that we all saw with our own eyes live in real-time, we also now have a trove of new documents related to Fox`s other massive and destructive deception, that would be Donald Trump`s big lie. These are some, not all, the newly released exhibits in Dominion voting systems defamation lawsuit against Fox News.\par \par \uc2 Now, Dominion has been through its legal filings in this case, revealing all sorts of evidence showing that all kinds of Fox and folks inside Fox the hosts, the management knew they were broadcasting lies to their audience in the wake of the 2020 election, but they did it anyway and continue to do it to keep their viewers and their shareholders happy.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, patient zero of the big lie that Fox spread far and wide was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . But to the extent there was some initial actual source, some plausible origin of the it was stolen, it was rigged lie, it turns out it was just one apparently disturbed person. We now have a copy of the email. This is the email that \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  , that`s the Trump lawyer who is largely responsible for spreading election conspiracy theories, forwarded to Fox hosts \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 This was written by a person who goes by the name Mars and it`s long, I won`t read all of it to you, but this person claims that they can "time travel in a semi-conscious state when I`m awake. I see what others don`t see and hear what others don`t hear. The wind, capitalized, tells me I`m a ghost, but I don`t believe it.\par \par \uc2 Now, Mars goes on to make outlandish claims about Dominion voting machines in particular. I`ll quote a little bit here. A piece of code was inserted such that once ballots were fed into the database for tabulation up to three percent of votes for Mr. Trump would automatically switch to Mr. Biden. This was capped at three percent because it was supposedly determined that anything higher than that would raise suspicions. That three percent would be enough to tip a tight race.\par \par \uc2 They also throw in a deranged story about Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  saying that, and again, I quote here, "He was purposefully killed at the annual Bohemian Grove camp, a club for members of the Mega-Group during a week-long human hunting expedition."\par \par \uc2 I got to say, in journalism, you get stuff like this all the time, and people will track you down. They`ll send you letters or emails with this kind of thing, and you learn to basically disregard it. And that brings me to one of the many thoughts I have on this whole thing. Now, it is hard to decide which kind of reaction is worse. Now, there are people, and we see from the evidence in the suit within Fox News with enough functioning baseline cognitive and critical faculties to be able to tell the person who talks to the wind and can time travel may not be the most credible source for such a wild accusation, but still decided to go along with it anyway because that is what their audience wanted to hear.\par \par \uc2 That`s one category. That includes people like \uc2 Fox\uc2  host \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and News Corp Chairman Rob Rupert Murdoch. And then there are those who apparently bought it like, earnestly. \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2  , host of a morning show on Fox Business and Fox News seems to be in that latter category. She was one of Fox`s biggest promoters of the big lie and I have to say, credit where due I guess, based on this new evidence, she wasn`t lying. It wasn`t a bad faith. Apparently, she truly believed it.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:00]\par \par \uc2 In a text conversation with former Trump advisor and podcast host \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  on November 10, 2020, Bartiromo wrote, "OMG, I`m so depressed. I can`t take it. I want to see massive fraud exposed. Will he be able to turn this around? I told my team we are not allowed to say pres elect at all.\par \par \uc2 Bannon replies, "71 million voters will never accept Biden. This process is to destroy his presidency before it starts if it even starts." That`s an interesting tale, isn`t it? Bartiromo writes, "but I`m scared and sad." Well, sometimes we all are. And Bannon replies, "You are our fighter. Enough with the sad. We need you."\par \par \uc2 The real key to this whole story, to all of it -- and again, we`re just going through the hundreds of pages of documents right now in real time, I think is revealed by this exchange. It`s Maria Bartiromo`s producer and it`s a text exchange on November 29. The producer writes to Bartiromo, "To be honest, our audience doesn`t want to hear about a peaceful transition." Aha.\par \par \uc2 This is basically the Rosetta Stone that translates why \uc2 Fox\uc2  did what it did because that producer who wrote that is 100 percent correct. The viewers did not want Fox to tell them the truth that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost the election. \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2  understood that, as did \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  , Fox CEO Suzanne Scott, all the other hosts from \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  , to \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  , Laura Ingraham, their audience did not want to hear about, and I quote again, "a peaceful transition of power to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  " and so \uc2 Fox\uc2  made sure they didn`t hear about it. And guess what? We didn`t get a peaceful transition to the first time since fort Sumpter.\par \par \uc2 David Folkenflik is an NPR News Media Correspondent, author of Murdoch`s World: The Last Of The Old Media Empires. Katie Fallow is a senior attorney at Columbia University`s Knight First Amendment Institute, also successfully sued \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for blocking his critics on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  . And both join me now.\par \par \uc2 David, let me start with you. I mean, we are processing this in real-time. I`m wondering if there`s anything here that`s jumping out to you?\par \par \uc2 DAVID FOLKENFLIK, MEDIA CORRESPONDENT, NPR NEWS: Well, I think on a couple levels. I think that you know, one of the ways in which Fox has made its name over the past 27 years, say, since its founding in 1996, is by wearing its contempt for the mainstream media on its sleeve and saying, we`re with you. They`re against us. They`re against you. We`re in this together.\par \par \uc2 But if you read these private exchanges between Laura Ingraham and \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  and \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  , they look as the journalist at Fox News -- the Fox News Channel as almost though it`s a completely different enterprise. You know, Laura Ingraham talks about how she is angry, how she is -- that she can`t begin to convey her anger at the news side for calling Arizona for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on election night, thus, seemingly perhaps putting the election out of then-President Trump`s reach, and also for their reporting to continue to undermine the claims being made by the President`s surrogates and advocates and champions even on their own network and sometimes on their own shows.\par \par \uc2 You know, they see this as a divide. The height of arrogance aren`t in Michigan, the head of the news decision desk, but also Leland Vittert at a later point, Chris Wallace. They`re attacking their own journalists. It is a civil war occurring behind the scenes at Fox News. That was interesting.\par \par \uc2 I was struck too by \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2  . Fox News made a big deal in legal briefs filed earlier today, saying look, we have this new things that it took a new technical review of Maria`s private personal emails and computers to find these exchanges, but with a Tony Fratto at that time, a spokesman for Dominion handling the crisis that was emerging as Fox helped to amplify these conspiracy theories and lies about Dominion, participating in some sort of election fraud. She said, you know, do you want your CEO to come aboard?\par \par \uc2 And what you learned if you read the full exchange is that she said that amid saying, oh my goodness, it`s really terrible that you think \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  isn`t telling the truth, my goodness. But behind the scenes, what she`s saying to \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  just a few days before is her despair about the idea that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has lost. And so, you`re not seeing anyone stepping up, whether it`s those who seem to believe honestly, as you pointed out, that there was fraud committed \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2  , or those who cynically understand very well that there was not in their primetime lineup.\par \par \uc2 Nobody`s stepping up to say, you know what, our viewers may not want to hear this -\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But we have to tell them.\par \par \uc2 FOLKENFLIK: But we have presented to them all the same.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Katie, there`s a legal theory here that`s interesting to me and it`s -- and it`s rare in these cases, right? So, someone says something about you you don`t like, you sue them for defamation, right? The standard is what is called actual malice. We can get into why it`s that and what that is. But here it`s -- a lot of times it`s like, well, the person really thinks you`re this nasty name they called you, or they made a mistake and they said something about you that was an error in fact which they just got wrong, right?\par \par \uc2 Here it`s the evidence of knowing behind the scenes, all of this stuff about how nuts \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  is. I mean, this to me really -- this really stands out to me. This is an exchange between \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and Ron Mitchell about that -- the famous -- one of the many famous press conferences, right, about the theory. This press conference has gotten bananas. I keep hearing that so bad, I really want to ignore it. \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  and Rudy are effing liars, entirely possible, it`s redacted. And then by my count so far, the election was stolen by a coalition of Soros, Nicolas Maduro, various software companies, and small-time gangsters.\par \par \uc2 They`re having the same reaction internally to the lies that anyone with functioning critical faculty is having.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:35]\par \par \uc2 KATIE FALLOW, SENIOR ATTORNEY, KNIGHT FIRST AMENDMENT INSTITUTE: Yes, I think that`s exactly right. In this latest batch of documents, some of which have been covered before, you see further evidence of this behind- the-scenes discussion among Fox News hosts and executives and producers, saying that they didn`t believe the claims of \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  and \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  , and yet they continue to air them for weeks, and to give them an essentially uncritical platform.\par \par \uc2 And dominion has really assembled and impressively an unusually strong amount of evidence demonstrating that at the time that \uc2 Fox\uc2  was airing the claims of \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2  and others, it knew that those statements were false, and thus had actual malice, as you mentioned, under the Supreme Court`s decision in New York Times versus Sullivan.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that`s an important decision. And it plays a very critical role in our democracy by ensuring that the press and others can speak critically of the government and report on newsworthy events, including the President`s big lie. But there is a limit to it. And I think that`s what this evidence shows.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s also you see, David, the contempt for Trump. They they`re all furious at him. They hate him. He`s -- you know, he`s a destroyer. You see Tucker saying, we`re all pretending we`ve got a lot to show for it because admitting what a disaster it has been is too tough to digest. But come on, there isn`t really an upside to Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, this is -- they would never say this on air, of course. But they`re all just -- and I think it comes down to what is their attitude towards their viewer, which they talk about respecting, but instead what comes across here is just this like, unbearable two faceness that if you caught anyone else doing this that you worked with, in a relationship, who said one thing in your face and complete opposite constantly behind the scenes, you would -- you would really doubt whether they`re telling you the truth.\par \par \uc2 FOLKENFLIK: Well, so there`s this one moment in the deposition or the excerpts of the deposition that lot has been redacted, withheld, of \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  in which basically, he makes clear, he doesn`t think there should have been an apology to Dominion. And there`s a legal reason for that. You know, you can see that in the -- in the filings that his lawyers are saying, we didn`t do anything wrong. We were reporting something and this is important for viewers to know, reporting something that`s inherently newsworthy claims about a national election by the most important citizen of the country, the sitting President of the United States and his surrogates. That`s there.\par \par \uc2 But there`s another reason why an apology would be really hard for \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  and Fox News to do, because they`d have to tell their viewers hey, we told you something that wasn`t true. More than that, we knew it wasn`t true. And more than that, we knew we shouldn`t have been telling it to you or endorsing it at several of his host did, or amplifying it uncritically as so many of his shows did. And that`s a real bind for Fox News right now because you know what, right now people inside Fox, and I`ve talked to a lot of them, know that they showed a kind of disdain and contempt and at best -- at best patronizing their audience by saying they`re going through the stages of grief, so we can`t tell them the truth. And that`s what we saw loud and clear in a lot of these documents.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: One way of showing respect to people is telling them the truth, not lying to them. That`s just my own, perhaps naive view of the ethics of all this. But Katie, there`s what -- here`s the -- here`s the question for me as this all comes out, right? And I think relates a little to David, and I think he`s onto something here. Why not settle this? I mean, this is just humiliating, mortifying stuff, cringy stuff, desperate stuff, two-faced, all of it. Why not settle?\par \par \uc2 FALLOW: Yes, and I will also add, it really provides a strong narrative for why Fox -- from Dominion`s perspective of why \uc2 Fox\uc2  continued to air these false claims because it was worried about losing its audience. And I think that may be the very reason why this case hasn`t settled. I also think it might just be a procedural issue of the both sides have asked the judge to rule in their favor, and they`re kind of assessing where they are. But I think the relationship with the viewer is and the political stance may be more powerful than the bottom line here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I think they`re locked in the same dynamic that David illuminated earlier that it`s too -- it`s too bad to apologize for because it means admitting what you did. David Folkenflik and Katie Fallow, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, we`ve barely scratched the surface of his latest Fox News document dump. \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  is going to join me in the ramification of what we`re learning, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In September 2020, just six weeks out for the presidential election, \uc2 Fox Corporation\uc2  Chairman \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  was sending Donald Trump`s son-in- law and adviser \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  confidential information about a Biden campaign ad. That`s according to the bombshell evidence made public today as part of Dominion voting systems defamation lawsuit against Fox News.\par \par \uc2 At 7:26 a.m. on September 24, 2020, Murdoch wrote, "Jared, I know you`re spending less on TV than Biden. However, my people say his advertisements are a lot better creatively than yours. Just passing by it on. Rupert." Kushner replied, the next day, "Should have some new creative out this week. I did a review and like what I`m seeing. I will now be reviewing this every week until the end as the real money is starting to be spent on TV and digital to move voters universes and turn out the base voters." Murdoch responded, "Your advertisement at 1:00 p.m. this Sunday an improvement but Biden insane football is extremely good, or I think so. We`ll send it."\par \par \uc2 [20:20:34]\par \par \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  served as the White House Press Secretary for President Biden. Her new show "INSIDE WITH \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  " will debut on Sunday, March 19, on MSNBC. And she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Jen, I imagine that this is the kind of thing you can imagine that \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  does, but seeing it in black and white that he`s literally in appearance, we don`t know for sure, sharing common financial ads with the other campaign really does kind of put a fine point on it.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  , MSNBC HOST: It does. I mean, it`s like a scene out of succession or something, Chris, but it is the intertwined aspect that we all suspected that we`ve all seen hints of, but that`s an actual example. I mean, these ads are telling the campaign strategy. There`s a lot of them. The Biden campaign ran a lot of them. So, it`s not clear how much content was shared, how frequently, but still, I mean, that`s information that obviously shouldn`t be shared by a news organization to state the complete obvious.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to -- I want to share this. I think this is a really interesting aspect here that I think gets it`s something. And I think, you know, you were up at the podium, you worked on campaigns, you`re now in a position similar to myself here in front of the camera. And one of the things that comes through in demeaning is they`re terrified, right? They`re losing their audience. They know the reason they`re losing audience is because their audience believes a lie. And so, they have to find a way to feed them something that`s comforting.\par \par \uc2 So, what Tucker comes up with is dead people voting is how he`s going to get the audience back on his side. And so, he has a special undead people voting on one of his shows. This is a screen grab from Exchange on November 11, 2020 that features one of the greatest lines I`ve ever read. Do we have enough dead people for tonight? Unknown. I think we have six or seven names right now. Miller told me they might drop some more names. Good. But obviously they need to do whatever they can to help us. I mean, seriously, let me know if I should call. Miller, maybe Stephen Miller.\par \par \uc2 I should say subsequently, I think he featured four dead voters who voted, three of whom were alive. But this is like -- the creative problem solving happening there is like we need to tell them what they want to hear. How can we do it?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: That is the core thing here, Chris, right? They are afraid to tell people watching them the truth, the facts. And to their credit, they accurately got that Arizona was won by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  first, right, and they reported it. So, when they looked back at that and they did their next couple of day meetings, they didn`t say look at us, well, we got this first. They -- their whole discussion and text chains and emails as we have now seen is, wow, that really pissed off some of our viewers, and we shouldn`t be feeding them so much information that makes them angry.\par \par \uc2 So, not truth telling, but fear of their viewers and fear of their Trump viewers, frankly, and how they would respond to accurate information.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You obviously were -- you served as press secretary, you were up there at the podium, you had a lot of exchanges with \uc2 Fox\uc2  reporters, particularly one -- in particular is the White House Correspondent.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Peter Doocy?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, Peter Doocy. Doocy, son of Doocy. And I think that it was -- I guess my question is, does this change anything about the way that you think about that institution or organization having been on the receiving end of -- which again, you know, at first amendment, free country, you go up there, you ask skeptical questions. That`s how we roll here in America. It`s fine with me. But I`m wondering if it changes the way you think about the place?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: A bit, honestly. I mean, Chris, I think when I was in the White House, there were people like Peter, as much as he would ask questions that were kind of slanted sometimes and a little bit one-sided and lacking context, but people like Bret Baier, Martha McCallum, these are all people that we thought of as being different from \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and Laura Ingraham and \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  , and in many ways they are. But also, in these emails and texts that we saw was some of their questions and acknowledgments that maybe there should be a range of factors that were considered beyond just the actual data of the outcome of the election and the results in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 That`s pretty disheartening. I mean, there are stronger words to use than that. Because these are people and individuals who Fox touts as their mainstream reporters. They`re people who were fair and balanced, who may be a little slanted, but we did always think about them separately. And if you`re sitting in a White House right now, looking at those texts, and a Democratic White House I will note, looking at those texts, looking at those emails, it kind of flies in the face of that.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:08]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yeah, that`s a great point. And I think that`s it`s interesting and complicated, right, because their whole shtick is -- their whole shtick is we`ve got the straight news people, and then we`ve got the opinion programming, and it`s clear that like, obviously, the whole work is run by the same enterprise, right?\par \par \uc2 But then on the other side, the opinion people are ticked off at the straight news, people who were part of the crew that were saying things generally like the election wasn`t stolen or like fact-checking a tweet. You got Hannity and Doocy, news destroyed us every day. You don`t piss off the base every day. They don`t care. Nope. And then Doocy, I think mockingly, they are journalists, all caps. Hannity, I`ve worn them for years. Doocy, we might as well tell people to stop watching at 9:00 a.m. and turn the TV back on at 9:00 p.m. although that`s a little mean to Peter Doocy, who`s on during the day.\par \par \uc2 But there clearly is a battle there about how far is too far and how important the truth actually is, or whether it`s just the base.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Right. And I think one of the big questions is, what the heck do they do now, right? I mean, tonight, Bret Baier aired some of the footage of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  criticizing \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  , right? \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  is on later tonight. So, that feels very awkward in the internal coffee meetings, or whatever they do over there. But what do they do from now? Is that just now because they`re under fire or are they going to double down on being more fair and balanced, or did they can they not do that because they can`t survive in a network that is so worried about losing their viewers by telling them the truth?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: So that`s a big question that I don`t think either of us may know the answer to right now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We don`t. And it matters a lot the state of American democracy as we saw in the after the election and January 6 as well. \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You can watch "INSIDE WITH \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  " premiering Sunday March 19, at 12:00 p.m. Eastern on MSNBC and streaming on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, the footage does not show an insurrection or a riot in progress That`s what \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  tells his viewers to forget what we all saw on January 6. And Republican politicians put them in his place next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s been three weeks since the Republican Speaker of the House Kevin McCarthy gave \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  exclusive access to tens of -- tens of thousands of hours of security footage from January 6. Now, you can bet that Tucker`s crack team of producers has been hard at work in the interim combing through the best moments to cherry-pick out of context. It`s part of their larger effort to launder the reputations of the violent mob that tried to end American democracy functionally.\par \par \uc2 And last night, we got our first preview of what they came up with. I got to say, even by the rock-bottom standards of Fox propaganda, it was laughably bad. Tucker spends a comical amount of time pushing his same disinformation doing is just asking questions shtick to suggested federal agents inside of the riot as part of a false flag operation, trying to paint the armed violent mob as mere tourists on a sightseeing trip to our nation`s capitol when in fact, we have all seen them acting violently.\par \par \uc2 He spends a lot of time on one man that he seems to be obsessed with, Jacob Chansley who`s known as the Q Shaman. Chanlsey was recently sentenced to 41 months in prison, which is a long term I have to say, after pleading guilty to obstructing Congress last year. Tucker decided to push the line that he was actually set up. Going so far as to question how exactly translate even got in the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2  , HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Chansley was sentenced to nearly four years in prison, far more time than many violent criminals now receive. What did Jacob Chansley do to receive this punishment? To this day, there is dispute over how Chansley got into the Capitol building.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Really? Really? Is there a dispute? Because the thing is that Tucker himself aired the footage of the shaman entering the Capitol last night. There he is. That`s him. He`s not hard to miss. He is the shirtless guy wearing horns. So, I don`t think there`s much dispute about how he got in the Capitol. I just saw him get into the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Tucker`s ballyhoo dud also centered on Republican Senator Josh Holley of Missouri in particular. This widely circulated much meme video of Hawley fleeing the Capitol during the insurrection first released by the January 6 Committee, a video that Tucker in a moment of unintentional brilliance has coined the coward tape, which I think is pretty good. That`s what it is.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to Carlson, that video is somehow unfair because other senators were also escorted out of the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: The actual videotape shows that Hawley was one of many lawmakers being ushered out of the building by Capitol Hill Police officers. And in fact, Hawley was at the back of the pack. The coward tape was a lie, one of many from the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Except it wasn`t a lie. Yes, of course, they were all ushered out. They all fled. No kidding, Sherlock. We didn`t need you to tell us that. I mean, the other senators were also escorted out of the Capitol. Way back during Trump`s second impeachment trial, in fact, we actually saw a video of Senator \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  of Utah being escorted out running out as well, right? Romney is running. Romney was not widely mocked like Hawley was because Romney didn`t begin his day by raising a fist in solidarity with the same violent mob just hours Before they stormed the Capitol and tried to lynch the Vice President.\par \par \uc2 And then you think you`re watching this footage, you think well, OK, so Hawley was running, but everyone was running. Then you think well, why was everyone running? Why did those senators have to leave in the first place? Because the violent mob was ransacking the building while members of Congress were sheltering in place and given gas masks.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:24]\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot more where this came from, whatever. Let`s face it. The Kevin McCarthy-Tucker Carlson disinformation hour was an embarrassing flop. And Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  took no time and lambasted the whole pathetic affair.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: It`s a little bit more shameful because Speaker McCarthy knows damn well what kind of customer Mr. Carlson is. McCarthy is not at all surprised about this outcome. And he probably even feels no remorse, even more shameful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, you`re probably thinking well, of course, a Democrat would take issue with Tucker spin, except here`s the thing about today on Capitol Hill, it was not just Democrats. You see Tucker might be more than willing to try and paint the deadly insurrection as a simple tourist visit, but a lot of Republican politicians, almost all of them were there, and they remember the attack, and so they`re not so willing to dismiss it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: Well, I didn`t listen to \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  . I generally don`t. But my recollection of the day is that it was not just some rowdy, peaceful protest of Boy Scouts.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: I think -- I think what happened on January 6 was despicable.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I`m not interested in trying to whitewash January 6.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITT ROMNEY (R-UT): You can`t hide the truth. by selectively picking a few minutes out of tapes and saying this is what went on. It`s so absurd. It`s nonsense.\par \par \uc2 SEN. THOM TILLIS (R-NC):I think it`s bull (BLEEP). If you were just a tourist, you should have probably lined up at the visitor center and came in on an orderly basis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Even Republican Senate Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  thought the whole thing was an irresponsible mess, completely at odds with what the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  say happened on January 6.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): With regard to the presentation on Fox News last night, I want to associate myself entirely with the opinion of the chief of the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  about what happened on January 6. It was a mistake in my view for Fox News to depict this in a way that`s completely at variance with what our chief law enforcement official here at Capitol thinks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It sure seems like even \uc2 Fox\uc2  itself understands the whole thing was dud. I mean, this is a network that`s never shied away from plugging its high-profile content throughout the day over and over and over. But even Fox his own anchors have almost completely ignored McCarthy and Tucker`s big special. That said, Tucker has got about 40,000 hours of video to work with so there sure to be much more attempted laundering of the insurrection even as the Justice Department just announced the arrest of more than 1000 people who stormed the Capitol. Because any credible observer, whatever their political ideology, can tell what happened that day was a tragedy and a crime.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TILLIS: I think it`s bull (BLEEP). If you were just a tourist, you should have probably lined up at the visitor center.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was an attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 CRAMER: To somehow put that in the same category as, you know, permitted peaceful protest is just a lie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Even Republican senators were unwilling to go along with the laundering of January 6 by \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  . They called the riot exactly what it was, an attack on the Capitol. We all saw it. For more than a year, of course, the bipartisan January 6 Committee painstakingly laid out the evidence of that attack and the sprawling effort to bring down American democracy as we know it.\par \par \uc2 Now, new House Speaker Kevin McCarthy has outsourced the MAGA rebuttal of the committee to a cable news host who we know from legal filings is preoccupied with the Fox News stock price.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Zoe Lofgren served on the January 6 Select Committee, and she joins me now. First, Congresswoman, your reaction to the reaction today on Capitol Hill from so many Republicans particularly in the Senate evincing barely disguised disgust at the efforts to essentially launder what happened that day.\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN (D-CA): Well, I was glad to see those comments. Obviously, what Carlson is doing to click -- to quote Senator Tillis is B.S. You know, it`s a complete lie. Why is he doing this? That`s something I think that needs to be discussed. But it`s just garbage to try and say that there wasn`t a riot, that people weren`t harmed, that was somehow some, you know, tourists visit it`s just -- they must think their viewers are stupid, really. How disrespectful of their viewers.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Of course, this all flows from a decision by Kevin McCarthy, the Speaker, to give this footage which is, you know, the public`s footage exclusively to one outlet, if you can call it that. I want to just play him defending it today with sort of pleading ignorant and then get your response to this decision. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you have any concerns about anything that aired?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Look, I didn`t see what was aired. What I want to do exactly is give the transparency to everybody and everybody can make up their own conclusions.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:03]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What do you think about the defense?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Weak, pathetic. You know, we were very cautious in the committee to work with the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  . And we didn`t air anything that they hadn`t cleared. And I happen to know that that was not the case with Mr. Carlson`s video. They gave an outline, but they didn`t clear everything that was taken. So, that`s problematic.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I mean, it`s the public security, but it`s security for a reason to keep bad guys from breaking in and overturning the government. And we`re focusing on January 6 and the MAGA mob, but I mean, ISIS, you know, there`s a lot of bad guys in the world that would like to know how to get in and how to do harm. So, I don`t think it was responsible for the speaker to do this. And also, it`s not responsible, it really is a violation of the First Amendment to pick. If you`re going to release something, you can`t just pick a favorite, you have to release it to everyone, which I would not be comfortable with for the security reasons that I`ve mentioned.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is part of a larger project it seems to me of sort of rehabilitating the reputation of January 6, to sort of wash it, to say it was either not that big a deal, or in fact kind of heroic that the people who participated in and who have faced consequences for that participation are in fact being unfairly picked on, that they`re political prisoners. We know that Kevin McCarthy`s staff member met with the mother of Ashli Babbitt, of course, the mother of the woman who very tragically lost her life when she vaulted through a window into that chamber where members still were and still were evacuating.\par \par \uc2 We also know that \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  has sort of taken up this cause as a cause celeb. Do you think this all fits together? And how much of the Republican caucus is on board with this vision of how to think about January 6 as a kind of noble cause?\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Well, we know that \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  is because she`s said so. I don`t really know how many of my Republican colleagues agree with this, but I do notice that none of them are speaking up. And that`s problematic. We were all there. We were all evacuated. We saw the mob. We know what happened. And to be silent in the face of lies is not responsible.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I don`t know what the -- what the motive is for Mr. Carlson. I mean, obviously, it`s financial, the more viewers he has, and we`re all talking about it now aren`t we? The more viewers he has, the more money \uc2 Fox\uc2  makes, and he likes that. But is there another plan to kind of pave the way for another effort to overthrow the Constitution? I don`t know. But it sure kind of looks like that, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congresswoman Zoe Lofgren, thank you very much for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 LOFGREN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Up next, as Republicans expand their attack against women`s health care across the country, there are five women now suing the state of Texas for the abortion ban that is putting their lives in danger.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAUREN HALL, PLAINTIFF IN TEXAS LAWSUIT: I love Texas. And it kills me that my own state does not seem to care if I live or die.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In the wake of the Supreme Court`s overturning of Roe v Wade, Republican politicians have been dealing with the fact that their extreme position on abortion is wildly unpopular. Nearly two-thirds of Americans saying abortion should be legal in all or most cases, a majority opinion that also holds in 43 states including Florida, for instance. But that isn`t stopping Florida Republicans from proposing to tighten the state`s current 15-week abortion ban to six weeks.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republican lawmakers will be quick to tell you that there are exceptions such as for saving the life of the mother or when a fetus has a fatal abnormality. But the reality on the ground is those exceptions are meaningless essentially. As we heard today from women in Texas who are suing the state for denying them abortions despite grave health risks to themselves and their fetuses.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNA ZARGARIAN, PLAINTIFF IN TEXAS LAWSUIT: My heart broke into a million pieces. I didn`t even know a thing like that could exist until that moment.\par \par \uc2 HALL: I love Texas. And it kills me that my own state does not seem to care if I live or die.\par \par \uc2 ZURAWSKI: It sounds like a pretty sick and twisted plot to a dystopian novel, but it`s not. It`s exactly what happened to me while pregnant in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Those are three of the five women now suing the state of Texas in what appears to be the first lawsuit by women who were themselves pregnant against one of the state abortion bans that have proliferated since the Supreme Court`s overruling of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Zurawski is one of the plaintiffs in this lawsuit. Molly Duane is a senior attorney at the Center for Reproductive Rights and the lead counsel on the case. And both join me now. Thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 Amanda, I thought I`d start with you with just before getting into your story, the decision to come forward and talk about this and how difficult it must be and how you made the decision to do it nonetheless.\par \par \uc2 ZURAWSKI: Hey, Chris! Thanks for having me. Yes, to answer your question, it`s been fun incredibly difficult. I think people might think that talking about it over and over again somehow makes it easier, but it certainly doesn`t. What I do know is that there are countless women who have been through something very similar to what I went through. And it`s for all of those women that I continue to speak out, because a lot of people don`t want to, or they`re afraid to, or they don`t have the opportunity to. But these stories need to be told, because it`s continuing to happen. And we know that people need to hear these stories because this is happening all across the state of Texas and all across the country.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:04]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I have to say, as a journalist, just I want to extend my gratitude for the choice to do this, which I know is extremely difficult precisely for that reason that we know this is happening and yet it`s -- it is hard - -it`s a hard story to tell because understandably, most of what is happening with this is happening in silence and quiet and away from the public eye. Can you -- can you tell us -- I think you`re 18 weeks pregnant when you got the news that you got about what happened to you?\par \par \uc2 ZURAWSKI: Yes, I was just one day shy of 18 weeks pregnant. So, my husband and I had been trying to get pregnant for about a year and a half. And it took a lot of procedures and tests and surgeries and misdiagnoses and a lot of fertility treatment. And last spring, we finally conceived and we`re just over the moon excited.\par \par \uc2 And my first trimester and that first bit of my second trimester was pretty smooth sailing. And then right around the 18-week mark, I had some concerning symptoms. So, I contacted my doctor and she had me come in right away. And very quickly, I was diagnosed with a condition called cervical insufficiency. And essentially what that meant was my body was dilating way too early. And I was, inevitably, we were told by multiple doctors, going to lose our daughter.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, my water broke. I lost all of my amniotic fluid. My membranes ruptured and it was very clear that there was nothing the doctors could do at that point to save our daughter. So, what should have happened at that point was they should have been able to intervene to provide an abortion so that we could ensure the respectful passing of our daughter, Willow, and prevent me from getting sick. But because of the ban in Texas that had just recently been passed and gone into effect, they couldn`t do anything. Because Willows heart was still beating, and I wasn`t yet sick. So, my life wasn`t considered at risk or in danger. So, we just had to wait for one of those things to happen for her heart to stop moving or for me to get sick.\par \par \uc2 So, eventually, it took three days for one of those things to happen. And in my case, I became septic and spent three days in the ICU and then another three days at the hospital being treated for sepsis, which had and is continuing to have long-term effects on my health and my future fertility.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I am so, so sorry that happened to you. And it just sounds just unconscionably awful ordeal. Molly, I wonder if you could tell us what the legal theory is. Obviously, that`s anyone I think listening to that is horrified by that. What is -- what is your suit saying on legal terrain about why Texas shouldn`t have to stop doing this or be held to account for it?\par \par \uc2 MOLLY DUANE, SENIOR ATTORNEY, CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS: Well, thanks for having us, Chris. And thank you for letting Amanda tell her story. It is so important for the public to hear these real stories of real people who are denied abortion care.\par \par \uc2 What our lawsuit is doing is really quite simple. We`re asking the State of Texas to acknowledge that people like Amanda should have received abortion care, which in their cases, was emergent necessary care to save their lives, to save their fertility, and to save the future children that they hope to have. And what we need the Texas courts to do is clarify what this law means.\par \par \uc2 And specifically, what we need the State of Texas to do is to say, if any of the women in this lawsuit they think weren`t sick enough to get an abortion, then they need to tell us which ones and why.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This seems to be the issue here, this ambiguity, intentional, I think largely, Amanda, it means that women like yourself are in this position. What do you want to see come out of, Amanda, your decision to join this lawsuit and to speak out about what happened here?\par \par \uc2 ZURAWSKI: I think first and foremost for me, it`s about education. Abortion is health care, plain and simple. And a lot of times it`s necessary the life-saving health care, sometimes for the life of the mother, in my case, sometimes for the life of the fetus. And one of my fellow plaintiffs cases, she was pregnant with twins and she needed the abortion of one to protect the life of the other.\par \par \uc2 I think there`s a stigma about what someone who needs and seeks an abortion looks like. And I want people to understand that it`s simply a standard medical procedure and oftentimes it`s needed to save lives.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Amanda Zurawski, thank you again for joining us tonight. And Molly Duane, I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DUANE: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Tuesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230308ej3700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for March 7, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Gabe Gutierrez, Sam Brock \par \uc2 7,492 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Tonight we`ve got the admission from \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  that he hates \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  passionately. That is the headline of the night. That is tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:38]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, a look behind the scenes at Fox News during the 2020 election, with texts and emails released as part of Dominions $1.6 billion lawsuit, hosts continued to push Trump`s debunked big lie with \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  questioning if they went too far.\par \par \uc2 Then, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  cherry picking and flat out misrepresenting the events of January 6. The condemnation we`re hearing from some Republican lawmakers and the brutal kidnapping in Mexico to Americans dead to survivors back on American soil. The warnings to Americans traveling to that area as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. We are still digging into a mountain of new evidence tonight. A brand new court filing in the $1.6 billion defamation suit from Dominion voting systems against Fox News dropped just a few hours ago and the latest trove of documents includes text messages, emails, and depositions that reveal even more about what was going on inside the network, as it regularly pushed Donald Trump`s lies that the 2020 election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 These new documents show Fox News Chairman \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  himself was asked directly about that during his deposition in January of this year, he was asked quote, have you ever believed that Dominion was engaged in a massive and coordinated effort to steal the 2020 presidential election? Murdock replied, No. He was also asked, Have you ever believed that Dominion committed election fraud by rigging the 2020 presidential election? Murdock responded, No, I have never seen any evidence.\par \par \uc2 The filing also included this email from January of 2021 from Murdoch to Fox News CEO Suzanne Scott, where he writes still getting mud thrown at us. Maybe Sean and Laura went too far. Of course referring to primetime anchors Laura Ingraham and \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel tonight. Geoff Bennett is here the CO anchor of PBS NewsHour, and PBS`s Chief Washington correspondent and an MSNBC political contributor. Jeremy Peters joins us political reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  . They`ve rest in his here a national political reporter at \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and former federal prosecutor Renato Mariotti. He is also a legal affairs columnist for POLITICO.\par \par \uc2 Jeremy, I wanted you to be here because you go inside Fox News. Every day you are reporting what sticks out to you the most in this filing?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY PETERS, THE NEW YORK TIMES POLITICAL REPORTER: I think what you see first and foremost is almost a kind of contempt for President Trump coming from some unlikely sources, namely, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and his producers. And the reason this is so ironic, of course, is few people on Fox News have been as big cheerleaders for President Trump as \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 So what he`s saying privately things like he thinks what Trump is doing by refusing to concede the election is disgusting. He says at one point about Trump, I hate him passionately. And these are the kinds of things that you knew certain people in Trump`s orbit, said privately, but they never really leaked out into the public.\par \par \uc2 Well, this leaked out in a pretty big way. And it`s all the more striking because somebody like \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  is now trying to rewrite the history of the 2020 presidential election say that it was corrupt and dishonest which he said last night on the show, while we have texts from November 2020 when he says that all of this voter fraud stuff sounds completely insane to him. And it`s crazy and that the President needs to stop doing it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Crazy, crazy. Renato, does this new dump -- does all this information make Dominions case even stronger?\par \par \uc2 RENATO MARIOTTI, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Yes, it`s really hard for me to see, frankly, how Dominion hasn`t extracted a massive settlement from Fox News already. I really think the only explanation is that Dominion thinks they have such a strong hand that they are not, you know, they`re holding all of this over Fox`s headed they`re demanding a massive settlement. You know, frankly, you know, this is what proof of actual malice looks like.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:00]\par \par \uc2 You know, I use those words actual malice. You know, Stephanie, that is the very high standard that there isn`t a First Amendment lawsuit. You have to prove that the other side actually knew they were lying or they had reckless disregard for the truth. Usually a non-starter, I mean, those that`s the sort of thing you can never actually threw up almost impossible to do. And here that`s literally what the Fox executives are admitting people like \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  , unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, but then this is what doesn`t make sense about that argument. Because on its face, I totally agree with you on like, yes, oh, my gosh, Dominion has the best hand ever, and \uc2 Fox\uc2  is screwed.\par \par \uc2 But does Fox believe that they`re not? Because given all the evidence that`s been out there? Wouldn`t you think that they would be telling their anchors, dial it back pipe down, when in fact, turn on Fox News Tonight, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  has doubled triple quadrupling down.\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: Yes, I think that Dominion is an unusual case because there you actually have a plaintiff that suffering monetarily. In other words, this isn`t a public figure that is essentially, you know, OK, you know, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  is making up lies about an officer and how he was injured. For example, on January 6, you know, here you actually have a company that is willing to go to the matches, it`s in the lawsuit and put preservation notices out there, so that they actually had all these emails and communications.\par \par \uc2 I think this was during the pandemic when \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  was communicating more in writing that he was usually. I really think that they have evidence here that my guess is they don`t, you know, actually have in writing in other cases.\par \par \uc2 So I suspect \uc2 Fox\uc2  is being more careful behind the scenes. Now, they`re being more careful about what they actually know as opposed to saying privately that they are, you know, know that this is false. And so I just think that`s what they`re doing, because this is their business model.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I don`t know anybody who knows anything about January 6 would beg to differ given what \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  has been saying for the last 48 hours. Geoff, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 GOEFF BENNETT, CO-ANCHOR PBS NEWSHOUSE: I think that the what has been revealed in these documents really serve as the most visceral and tangible proof of what has been the strongest criticism of Fox News for decades, that it is really a political operation, a propaganda machine, and a business enterprise that uses the veneer of news and wraps itself in the 