him because of that?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, we`ll soon find out. Things are about to get wild. Imagine, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the primary. People want to watch that. Juanita Tolliver, Christopher Shays, thank you for being here tonight.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the two survivors of a kidnapping in Mexico back in the US. You`ve heard tidbits of this story all day. We`re going to break down what`s actually going on, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:46:20]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: By now, you`ve probably heard about that brutal kidnapping of four Americans in Mexico. Well, U.S. officials confirmed today that two of the four Americans who were shot and taken by gunmen are back on U.S. soil, but two others are dead and their families are desperate for answers. NBC`s Gabe Gutierrez has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, the two Americans who survived the brazen kidnapping are back on U.S. soil after a harrowing few days. Latavia Washington McGee seen here today in an ambulance is among the survivors. Her mother who spoke with her directly tells NBC News.\par \par \uc2 The violent abduction caught on camera. The victims dragged onto this white pickup truck. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says gunman had opened fire on the group shortly after they`d crossed the Texas border into Matamoros, Mexico. Today, authorities confirmed two of the Americans had been killed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We extend our deepest condolences to the family and loved ones of the deceased.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: McGee was joined by her cousin Shaeed Woodard and friends Zindell Brown and Eric James Williams on the days long trip from their homes in South Carolina to Mexico, or what a law enforcement official familiar with the matter says was a cosmetic medical procedure.\par \par \uc2 Mexican authorities say Williams also survived but was shot in the leg. We spoke with his wife Michelle today by phone.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE WILLIAMS, WIFE OF ERIC WILLIAMS: I didn`t know anything until Sunday morning when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  came. Everything just seemed so surreal to me.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: She said she spoke with her husband for a few minutes today before he went in to surgery.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I am very happy that he`s alive but I`m also heartbroken for the other families who can`t say the same.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: She says the four were childhood friends taking the trip together to share driving responsibilities. So called Medical Tourism is on the rise. Pre-pandemic estimated 1.2 million Americans traveled to Mexico each year for elective medical treatment, mostly cosmetic procedures and complex dentistry.\par \par \uc2 But Matamoros is in the Mexican state of Tamaulipas, which the State Department classifies as level four do not travel. Violence there has intensified among warring factions of the notorious Gulf Cartel.\par \par \uc2 Today, the governor of Tamaulipas said one person who was guarding the kidnapping victims was arrested. They were found in a wooden house in Matamoros and over the past three days investigators say they`ve been moved to several locations, including a medical clinic to create confusion.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: The cartels are responsible for the deaths of Americans and we are fighting as hard as possible. The DEA and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are doing everything possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: With me now is retired four star Army General Barry McCaffrey. He is normally here talking to us about the war in Ukraine. But tonight he`s tapping into his time as the former director of the \uc2 Office of National Drug Control Policy\uc2  under President Clinton.\par \par \uc2 General, two people are dead. Two people were shot at. What in the world happened here.\par \par \uc2 GEN. BARRY MCCAFFREY, RETIRED U.S. ARMY: A minor data point and a violent ongoing struggle for the domination of Mexico`s territory maybe 20, 30 percent of Mexico`s under the control the cartels. There may be as many as 200 armed groups who may not be involved in the drug trade but are extorting for murdering Mexican citizens.\par \par \uc2 So it`s a real nightmare. Those cartels are capable of putting together battalion sized units of seven, 800 Narcos with heavy machine guns, armored vehicles, and successfully confronting the Mexican army. The place is out of control.\par \par \uc2 But President AMLO has a goofy policy hugs not bullets and what`s essentially happening Stephanie is the cartels are the principal criminal Big hugs, not bullets.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:03]\par \par \uc2 And what`s essentially happening, Stephanie, is the cartels are the principal criminal enterprise inside the United States. So it`s a very, very dire situation.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And how dangerous is Mexico right now? Are there specific parts of the country people should avoid? I mean, spring break is here.\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: OK, I get asked that all the time. Half of the Mexican state are under a travel advisory from the \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  , all the border states are avoid travel. Five of the Mexican states have the same level for danger classification that the State Department uses for Afghanistan, Syria and Iraq.\par \par \uc2 There is no justice inside Mexico. Their law enforcement tend to be corrupt, and incredibly incompetent and dominated by the cartels. The Mexican Marine will fight for the state. The Army also, that`s the only tools left to really provide security inside Mexico.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So is there anything we can do to make people safer or it`s another country, not our jurisdiction?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Well, we got to pay attention to it. You know, it`s an astonishing situation that people contract with Mexico are absolutely superb. There`s no question that Mexico is a family oriented, humble, hard- working nation of incredible economic importance.\par \par \uc2 As you know, it`s the number one trading partner by some counts, Mexico and Canada, the only two vital nations on the face of the earth. So we got to do more.\par \par \uc2 And right now all we`ve got is fairly low level contract between U.S. law enforcement prosecution and Mexican authorities who we think we can trust. But the state of affairs in Mexico is dire. You know, you`re probably going to be safe if you`re an 18-year-old college boy or girl headed to Cabo for spring break, probably. But there are no guarantees and met Americans get murdered down there and get abducted and extorted every day of the week. So it`s a very tough situation.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It is a very tough situation and these four people who went down there is a tragedy that too lost their lives. General Barry McCaffrey, thank you for joining us tonight. When we come back, you have got to see this. Do not go to bed. Do not turn the TV off, because this matters, a bluegrass prodigy. And I`m not talking about David Gura. We`re going to introduce you to a man who did not let anything keep him from picking up the fiddle. We`re certainly glad it didn`t. When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:00]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Hope you`re ready for some sweet dreams tonight boys and girls because we present to you the unlikely Fiddler. Michael Cleveland was not dealt an easy hand in life. He was born blind and mostly deaf. But he was also born with a love a passion for music. And despite all the odds he used that love to become one of the most accomplished fiddle players of his generation. My colleagues Sam Brock has his very special story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): To hear Michael Cleveland play the fiddle is to experience bluegrass packed with passion and fury the melodies of a lifelong love affair.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (on camera): It feels like bluegrass is in your bones.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL CLEVELAND, AMERCIAN FIDDLER: It`s all I`ve ever really wanted to do. And something that I was born into. My grandparents were big fans of Bluegrass music.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voiceover): Michael was born blind and doesn`t have hearing in 80 percent of his left ear and a quarter of his right. Despite his challenges, Michaels grandparents took them to shows all over his hometown of Henryville, Indiana, starting at six months old and by three or four, he was hooked to their eight track player.\par \par \uc2 CLEVELAND: I would take one of those speakers and laying flat on the ground and lay my right ear on that speaker and go sleep listening to Bluegrass.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Behind the brilliance of his prodigy of fearlessness to learn.\par \par \uc2 CLEVELAND: Find a good jam session that I`m getting into.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Now we placed a 400 person Krauts from Phoenix to Fort Myers.\par \par \uc2 CLEVELAND: Oh man, it`s just a pure adrenaline rush.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Boosted by a family who only wanted to share his dreams.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (on camera): Do you think if your grandparents today saw this?\par \par \uc2 CLEVELAND: I don`t think they would be surprised.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: They`d be darn proud.\par \par \uc2 CLEVELAND: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Sam Brock, NBC News, Fort Myers, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And all American inspiration. Michael Cleveland and his famous fiddle are taking us off the air tonight. But before I let you go, I want to share we have a very special guest tomorrow. Actress Michelle Yeoh joins me one on one to talk about why an Oscar for her would mean everything everywhere all at once. Here`s just a little sneak peek.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: People said, Oh, awards don`t matter. But the Oscars are coming.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE YEOH, MALAYSIAN ACTRESS: Yes, it matters.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It matters.\par \par \uc2 YEOH: How it matters a lot and it doesn`t -- it`s not just mattering to me is shining the light on a part of the world of people that looks like me, who`s ever been included.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [00:00:05]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Again you can catch my one on one with Michelle Yeoh tomorrow night right here on THE 11TH HOUR. And I can tell you, it`s going to be a special show.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late. I will see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020230308ej3700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE for March 06, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alicia Menendez, Jonathan Capehart, Maria Teresa Kumar \par \uc2 8,410 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [23:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2  , MSNBC HOST, THE SUNDAY SHOW: Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 Reminder, I`ll see you, on Saturday, at 9 AM, for "THE SATURDAY SHOW," and again, on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 THE 11TH HOUR starts right now.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST, THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE: Tonight, the fight for electability. Donald Trump`s new pledge, to stay in the race, even if he is criminally charged; \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  appears ready, to take his Florida policies, nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Then, the battle for abortion rights, after \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  says it will not sell abortion pills, in 21 states, and a new lawsuit, just filed in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, a key moment, in the war, in Ukraine, the battle for Bakhmut, the Ukrainian forces under intense pressure, as Russia`s ammunition supply runs low.\par \par \uc2 As THE 11TH HOUR gets underway, on this Monday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I am Alicia Menendez, in for Stephanie Ruhle.\par \par \uc2 A showdown over who will be the Republican presidential nominee appears to be taking shape, between the twice-impeached ex-President, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , and his once-loyal backer, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  . In the coming days, both men are expected to head to Iowa, home of the first GOP caucus.\par \par \uc2 And even though DeSantis hasn`t officially entered the race, this weekend, we saw a preview of what a potential faceoff will look at.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, Trump made his pitch, to supporters, for nearly two hours, CPAC, where he took a thinly-veiled swipe, at the Florida governor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are going to finish what we started. We started something that was a miracle.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE CHEERS & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : We`re going to complete the mission.\par \par \uc2 We are never going back to the party of \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  , Karl Rove and Jeb Bush.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE CHEERS & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : We`re not going back to people that want to destroy our great Social Security system, even some in our own party, I wonder who that might be.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, I declared, I am your voice. Today, I add, I am your warrior. I am your justice.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE CHEERS & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : And for those, who have been wronged and betrayed, I am your retribution. I am your retribution.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE CHEERS & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: DeSantis followed, on Sunday, promoting his record, during a speech, at the Ronald Reagan Library.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): When the world went mad, when commonsense suddenly became an uncommon virtue, Florida stood as a refuge of sanity, a citadel of freedom, for people, throughout the United States and indeed, throughout the world.\par \par \uc2 And for years, you didn`t see our administration leaking like a sieve; you didn`t see a lot of drama or palace intrigue. What you saw was surgical- precision execution day after day after day. And because we did that we beat the Left, day after day after day.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: We`re also following new revelations, from \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  , about the panic, Fox News, after the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Fox, as you`ll recall, was the first network to call Arizona, for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and was, quote, "Besieged with angry protests not only from President Donald J. Trump`s camp from its own viewers. Never mind that the call was correct; \uc2 Fox\uc2  executives worried that they would lose viewers to hard-right competitors like \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  ."\par \par \uc2 The Times reports top Fox executives held a Zoom meeting, on November 16th, 2020, on, quote, "How to keep from angering the network`s conservative audience again by calling an election for a Democrat before the competition."\par \par \uc2 Fox\uc2  responded to The Times with this statement. Quote, "Fox News stood by the Arizona call despite intense scrutiny. Given the extremely narrow 0.3 percent margin and a new projection mechanism that no other network had, of course, there would be a wide-ranging post-mortem surrounding the call and how it was executed no matter the candidates."\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s bring in our lead-off panel. Luke Broadwater, Pulitzer Prize-winning Congressional Reporter, for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  ; Tara Palmeri, Senior Political Correspondent and Partner at Puck News; and Tim Miller, a Contributor to The Bulwark, and the former Communications Director for Jeb Bush.\par \par \uc2 It is good to see you all.\par \par \uc2 Tim, you were at CPAC. You live to tell about it.\par \par \uc2 Here is some of what McKay Coppins of The Atlantic wrote. Quote, "The apparent decline in interest isn`t just about CPAC. It speaks to a serious problem for Trump`s 2024 campaign: His shtick has gotten stale."\par \par \uc2 Where are Republicans now on Trump? Does this all come down to electability? What are we not seeing, not having been on the ground, at CPAC?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, THE BULWARK CONTRIBUTOR, FORMER COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR TO JEB BUSH: Yes, that was - that`s maybe too declarative a statement, from McKay, from what I saw.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And CPAC definitely was pretty sad, and was smaller than it`s been in past years. But the crazy was burning as hot as ever, when you were there. And the people there wanted Trump, and only Trump. He won the straw poll, going away, right?\par \par \uc2 And so, the question is for those that didn`t show up, are they ready to turn the page? Are they ready to go to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  ? Might it just be an off-year? The actual election isn`t until next year. Might the real reality just be that the whole party is CPAC now?\par \par \uc2 I went to a Turning Point USA conference, called AmericaFest, in December that was raucous, and packed, and had a ton of Trump fans, there, and also some DeSantis fans.\par \par \uc2 And so, I hope that it`s true that the sad state of CPAC is a sign that the party is ready to move on. But I fear it might just be more that folks that want to have their MAGA fix, the MAGA itch-scratch, have a lot of other options, right now. And it`s no longer just this one once-a-year gathering.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tim, talk to me about that list that Trump rattled off. "We`re not the party of \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  , or Karl Rove, or Jeb Bush."\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Who was that a signal to?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, I think it was kind of a sub-tweet at \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , and a preview of what`s coming, is that, right now he feels like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has taken some of his MAGA mojo.\par \par \uc2 And I think Trump is going to try to position DeSantis as a phony MAGA, somebody that`s from the pre-Trump GOP, who is more in line with \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and the Bush family. And so, there`s still - there`s some Republicans that want that, right? But not a majority. The majority of the party preferred Trump to Bush. Crazy as that might seem!\par \par \uc2 And so, Trump is trying to speak to those people, who may be like Trump and DeSantis, like the Trump Party better than the Bush Party, and he`s going to try to use that as a wedge to try to get those people, in his camp, rather than move on to the Florida governor.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tara, on Sunday, you also had DeSantis seeming to present arguments, for his candidacy. I think if you have watched his speeches before, this is sort of his greatest hits, talking about how his State responded to COVID.\par \par \uc2 I wonder, though, if the things he`s saying will both move those GOP voters, who don`t think Trump can win, and woo some Trump loyalists, because he`s got to do both?\par \par \uc2 TARA PALMERI, PUCK NEWS PARTNER: Exactly. And it`s a really difficult task, because he can`t alienate Trump`s voters. So, he really can`t attack Trump head-on. They have to come to the conclusion themselves, that Trump is not electable. So, this really hinges on the idea of electability.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , is also pushing the idea that he`s more effective, at executing, the MAGA movement, although the truth is that so much of the MAGA movement isn`t exactly about ideology. It`s about a feeling. And even just listening to the two of them, it`s a very different presentation.\par \par \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , he comes off angry, like Trump, and combative. But he sounds more like politicians, from the past. And that`s why, I think Trump see that as a weakness that he can tie him to. And that`s why you keep hearing him say, "DeSantis is a RINO."\par \par \uc2 People may not know that much about DeSantis. They know a lot about Trump. They saw him on The Apprentice, right? They just know of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  . And so, I think, Trump is trying to shape the narrative, right now, before he really has to get, into the ring, with DeSantis. And that`s why he`s saying these things, even though DeSantis is arguably more conservative than Trump.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: He`s certainly--\par \par \uc2 PALMERI: So, it`s really interesting.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Whether or not he is more conservative, because I think we sort of all agree that we have lost the definition of that term, at this point, in American politics. He`s certainly as Trumpy, if not Trumpier than Trump, right? Putting migrants on planes, to Martha`s Vineyard?\par \par \uc2 PALMERI: Right.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: In terms of the stunts, in terms of the cruelty?\par \par \uc2 Luke, my colleagues, Sahil Kapur, and Scott Wong, they had a good piece, recently, where they talked about that classic quote, "Every U.S. Senator looks in the mirror, and sees the president." But, at this moment in history, that is not true, because none of them want to be running against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 I wonder then, when you are working your sources, on Capitol Hill, what it is that GOP members, of the House, of the Senate, are telling you, about a potential DeSantis-Trump showdown?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Right. Well, there`s really a split, between the two chambers, as it stands, right now.\par \par \uc2 You don`t see a ton of support for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the Senate. He hasn`t - he and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  haven`t spoken, since January 6, 2021. There`s still a lot of bad blood with some, in the Senate leadership.\par \par \uc2 And I think about only five Republican senators have endorsed Trump, to date. A lot of them are looking at Tim Scott, as a potential option. One of the colleagues that they really respect that`s going to be running, and or many believe might run, in the primary.\par \par \uc2 On the House side, it`s quite different. \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  , who`s in leadership, has already endorsed Trump. There`s about 30 members, who already are lining up, to support Trump. Some of the investigations, they`re running, out of the House, are essentially to frame up the race, for Trump, and to create a conflict, between him, and Biden, where he looks better than Biden.\par \par \uc2 So, the House - and I would expect more and more endorsements, to come from the House. So, he`s going to have a lot of support in the House.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:00]\par \par \uc2 But we have not seen a lot of people, running to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , in Congress. You`re not seeing a flood of endorsements for DeSantis yet. Of course, he hasn`t actually gotten in the race, and that might change when that happens.\par \par \uc2 But they`re casting about, many people in Congress, are casting about, for someone else, besides Trump, to support. And, right now, you`re not seeing a ton of it.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yes. The way you articulate it, is almost as if they`re standing in the halls, someone, anyone, like "Please, let`s find a name to put on the ballot here!"\par \par \uc2 Tara, one of those names, Nikki Haley, she`s gotten into the 2024 race. She was at CPAC.\par \par \uc2 PALMERI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I want you to take a listen to what she heard up on stage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (VIDEO - GOP PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE NIKKI HALEY HECKLED AT CPAC)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Trump! Trump! Trump! Trump! Trump! Trump! Trump! Trump! Trump! Trump! Trump!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nikki! Nikki! Nikki! Nikki!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Trump! Trump! Trump!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: OK. So, taking selfies, not up on stage, my bad.\par \par \uc2 Still, the message the same, they want Trump. I mean, what is she facing, in this quest, to win over these voters?\par \par \uc2 PALMERI: It`s interesting that in the CPAC straw poll that she came in second, for the vice presidential pick. So, I think that it just shows that Nikki Haley lack the kind of grassroots support, in the Republican Party. She`s a made by Trump candidate, who`s trying to say that he`s too old, and needs to move on. But she clearly just doesn`t have the support.\par \par \uc2 I would even say showing up at 3 percent is pretty impressive, considering that this has become the Trump show, CPAC. She was not in friendly territory. And she`s - hasn`t exactly come out swinging against him. But she`s certainly trying to - she`s intimating that she`s ready to, and she might eventually swing at him.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s just, demurring, which I find to be interesting. It`s like you`re hearing them all sort of like take whacks at each other. Pompeo and Haley are going after each other. They`re laughing at DeSantis. But they`re all very reluctant to actually attack Trump. And I think if you`re going to continue with that posture, I don`t know how you really beat him.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yes, I mean--\par \par \uc2 PALMERI: So, I mean, she might be just staying like this, for a while, until she absolutely has to.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tim, Tara referenced that VP straw poll. So, let`s talk about Kari Lake, who I understand you spent some time with, the Republican nominee for governor, Arizona, who lost, refused to concede.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE, FORMER GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, ARIZONA, FORMER GOP CANDIDATE FOR ARIZONA GOVERNOR: We lost elections. We`re having corrupt stolen elections, OK? We`re having corrupt stolen elections. President Trump won that election.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Don`t you think you really lost the election because you didn`t reach out to the McCain voters quite but as you--\par \par \uc2 LAKE: I did reach out to them.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: --you told McCain voters to get out of the room.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: No, I didn`t. You know what?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: And you campaigned hard-MAGA.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: That`s fake news!\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I went to your last event. It was \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  , it was all these guys. It was hard-MAGA. You didn`t pull into moderate people (ph).\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Well let`s talk - let`s--\par \par \uc2 MILLER: You didn`t go (ph) to moderate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Let`s--\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Maybe you would have won, if you would have done that.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: I brought the Establishment together. Let me finish. I brought the Establishment together. Unfortunately, a few of them didn`t come over. And they rigged the election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tim, hard pivot, from election denialism, to talk of electability, and who she didn`t reach out to.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: What did you take away from that exchange with her?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes, right before that, she`s - it`s very relevant to this conversation, was I prefaced that by saying Nikki Haley was on stage, saying, "We`ve lost, seven of the eight - last eight popular votes. We`ve lost three straight elections, if you look at midterms, and 2020, and that means that we need a little bit of a new path, from this ultra-MAGA path."\par \par \uc2 And I posed that to Kari, whether she thought that was true. Obviously, she rejects that, in part because ideologically she rejects it, but also because she`s pretending like she`s still the governor, and she`s pretending like she won.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s interesting that that path that Kari`s offering, which is not to change at all, to continue down the full, ultra-MAGA path, and to not even acknowledge that that`s been a failed path, in three straight elections, is what is appealing more, to the Republican base, not every single person in the Republican base, but she won that VP straw poll. She had bigger crowds, than Nikki Haley and \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 So, can \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  bridge those two groups? Maybe. It`s a pretty wide bridge, when you`ve got one side saying, "We didn`t even really lose," and denying reality, and "We should be as MAGA as possible," and the other side is saying, "Well, hey, let`s live on the real world."\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: In the real world.\par \par \uc2 Luke, Politico points out, "January 6 was once an albatross for Trump," as I would argue, it should be. It is apparently no longer disqualifying for his candidacy.\par \par \uc2 Does this mirror what we`ve seen happen with the House GOP, in terms of how they now see January 6, and the language that we heard from Trump, on that stage, about "I will be your warrior. I will be your retribution."\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you go to Town Halls, around the country, for a Republican primary, January 6 is not something to be distanced from or condemned.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s now being framed as "You need to fight for the people, who were arrested, for breaking into the Capitol, and you need to be on their side."\par \par \uc2 And you saw that that is what Trump did with releasing this video, where, I guess, there`s a - some of the prisoners are singing, and he`s saying the national anthem, or something, during it.\par \par \uc2 So, for the normal people, who were at the Capitol, who saw what happened that day, who saw cops get beat with hockey sticks, and a mob storm and smash up the building, and everyone, including Republicans and Democrats, alike, run in fear? It`s just a completely different reality.\par \par \uc2 And so, the Republican primary that Trump is running, and may, well, this may well be successful, in that primary. But that strategy is going to turn off a ton of swing voters.\par \par \uc2 And I think anybody, who analyzes any of the polling, or the electorate, is going to realize that if you run in lockstep, with the rioters, there`s no way you`re going to appeal, to the swing voter, and the moderates, in the general. So, it`s a very, it`s a risky strategy, but it may well win, in a primary.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: It turns out American voters really do care about democracy, as we saw, during these midterms.\par \par \uc2 Luke Broadwater, Tara Palmeri, Tim Miller, thank you all so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , promising to stay, in the presidential race, even if he is indicted. We`re going to talk to \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2  , about how that might play out.\par \par \uc2 And later, exclusive reporting, on a new legal challenge, to the near-total abortion ban, in Texas. Maria Teresa Kumar is here on that, and the ongoing battle, over access to abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a Monday night.\par \par \uc2 [23:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can you take this moment to assure your donors and your supporters that you`re in this race to stay no matter what happens with those investigations, if you are indicted one or more times?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : Sure. Oh, absolutely. I wouldn`t even think about leaving. These are fake stories. These are horrible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , at CPAC, over the weekend, saying that he will stay in the 2024 race, even if he`s facing criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Trump is facing several current investigations, including a DOJ Special Counsel probe, and a special Grand Jury probe, in Fulton County, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Back with me tonight, \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2  , a veteran federal prosecutor, and former U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Barb, it is good to see you.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us, from a legal perspective, about Trump, staying in this race, even if he`s facing criminal charges, even if he is indicted?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE\uc2  , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY, UNIVERSITY OF MICHIGAN LAW SCHOOL: Well, Alicia, the really unusual thing, I think, about this scenario is there`s nothing, in the Constitution that would prevent him, from staying in the race.\par \par \uc2 The requirements for president are only that a person have attained the age of 35, and be a natural-born citizen. So, there`s nothing constitutionally that would preclude someone, from running for president, even while they`re under indictment. I imagine that the Framers of the Constitution just simply did not contemplate such a scenario.\par \par \uc2 And so, he could continue to run, while under indictment. But it would certainly complicate things.\par \par \uc2 Someone who is indicted often has their travel restricted, for example, if they are released on bond. I imagine that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be released on bond, would not be detained, pending trial. He`s not a danger to the community, or a risk of flight, under traditional measures, of those things. So, he`d be allowed to be out.\par \par \uc2 But, a candidate has to travel, all around the country. And so, it would require special permission, for that.\par \par \uc2 There are court dates that a person has to make. Imagine that that rallies have to be scheduled, around court appearances?\par \par \uc2 And then of course, there`s a trial, which could take place, if President Trump were to - former President Trump were to be indicted, this year, a trial would likely take place sometime next year, during campaign season. Imagine that? It`d be a very strange scenario with, people outside the courthouse, selling Trump campaign posters, and stickers, and other paraphernalia.\par \par \uc2 So, a really unusual scenario, if this were to come to pass.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Barbara, I was also struck, watching a bunch of the sound from CPAC. I always say, I watched, so that our viewers don`t have to.\par \par \uc2 That you have Trump, in the face of these investigations, onstage, just repeating the Big Lie, in front of an audience, in front of media. And I wonder, again, from a legal perspective, I get that prosecutors, at this point, have like a cornucopia of evidence, they don`t need more.\par \par \uc2 But I am curious, if you are his legal team, how you feel about the fact that he`s up, on stage, saying it over and over again?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: It`s really complicated to represent someone, who is a public figure.\par \par \uc2 Because the typical traditional advice to a client is to "Keep your mouth shut. Do not say anything, because we`re not sure how this is going to shake out at trial. And we want to keep all of our options open. If you say things now, those statements can be used against you, and you`re locking yourself into a story." And so, that`s always the advice, for most clients.\par \par \uc2 But when someone is a public figure, as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, he needs to be able to answer to his voting base, and his constituents. And so, staying silent about what happened on January 6 really isn`t an option for him.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:00]\par \par \uc2 So instead, he has chosen to embrace the whole thing, and double-down, even as you talked about, in your prior segment, recording a song with some of the January 6 prisoners, who have been convicted, and are now serving prison sentences, for their conduct, on January 6, fully embracing them, talking about how he would pardon them, if elected.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think that there`s no real middle ground here. Either, you have to say that it was a terrible crime, or you have to embrace them. And that is the choice that he`s made. But later, at a trial, certainly, all of these statements can be used, against him, to demonstrate a case that he was perpetuating a fraud, against the American people.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: We talked about Special Counsel. We talked about Fulton County.\par \par \uc2 I want to make sure that we`re talking about the Manhattan D.A.`s office, because former Trump aide, \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2  , met with them today. This investigation is on Trump`s involvement, in paying hush money, to Stormy Daniels, as a reminder, because there are a lot of investigations we`re keeping track of here.\par \par \uc2 Your take, on where this particular one is headed?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Well, it really seems to be accelerating, in recent weeks. We had \uc2 Hope Hicks\uc2  , this week. Kellyanne Conway was in there, last week, according to reports. They`ve had executives from the National Enquirer, in there. It really seems to be getting to the stages of putting in the key witnesses.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors generally start with the concentric circles at the farthest reaches from the target, and then getting closer and closer to that person, as they approach a decision. And that`s because you want to question those witnesses, when you are as informed as you can possibly be.\par \par \uc2 And so, it says to me that they`re getting very close, to making a charging decision. Unclear what that charging decision will be. But they wouldn`t be putting these people, in the grand jury, if they weren`t very serious, about considering criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Speaking of timelines, Barbara, of Trump`s legal strategy, we all know, at this point. Slow investigations down, as much as possible.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Obfuscate at every turn.\par \par \uc2 There is a political ticking clock here, with this upcoming election. If you are Jack Smith, how do you square all that?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Well, he seems to have been moving very rapidly, since he became the Special Counsel, in this case. And we do see him taking these kinds of last steps, of subpoenaing people, like \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  , again, someone who`s such a key witness, you would want to put that person last. So, it does seem that he too, is getting close to the end.\par \par \uc2 But you`re right. If we get to the point where, say worst-case scenario, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is elected president, again, and takes office, in January of 2025? Remember, the old rule about you can`t indict a sitting president. And the point of that is because it would be too distracting, from his duties, as President, to have to deal with, being under indictment, being charged, in defending oneself.\par \par \uc2 And so, unless this case, were all completely over by then, you need to have a problem. And so, I think to make sure a trial is finished, by the time, we get to January of 2025, he`s got to be thinking about an indictment, before the midpoint of this year.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: What a way to say that time is of the essence!\par \par \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2  , as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a near-total abortion ban left one woman, with a near-death experience. Now, she and others are challenging the Texas ban, in a new lawsuit, fighting to protect the lives, of other mothers, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Texas has become ground-zero, in the fight, over abortion rights. The Lone Star State has some of the strictest abortion laws, in the country, some of the harshest penalties.\par \par \uc2 Yamiche (ph) Alcindor has the details on a new lawsuit that could challenge the State of Texas and save women`s lives.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): When Amanda and Josh Zurawski found that they were expecting a baby girl, they were overjoyed. The name they picked for her, "Willow."\par \par \uc2 AMANDA ZURAWSKI (ph), WIFE OF JOSH ZURAWSKI (ph): I mean, it was magical. The first 17 weeks were very easy, very smooth sailing.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (voice-over): But just before week 18, something felt wrong. Amanda`s body was preparing for labor too early. The news from her doctor was devastating.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI (ph): And she said pretty quickly, all in one breath, "You`re going to lose the baby. She`s not going to survive."\par \par \uc2 I thought what they would do is go in and intervene immediately. But she told me that they couldn`t, because the baby`s heart was still beating, and I wasn`t sick.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (voice-over): Laws, in Texas, ban doctors, from performing abortions, except when a medical emergency endangers a person`s life, or major bodily function. Those who violate the law could face up to life in prison.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (on camera): Take me into what it was like to wait.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI (ph): I just existed, in sorrow and fear. I was really, really scared.\par \par \uc2 JOSH ZURAWSKI (ph), HUSBAND OF AMANDA ZURAWSKI (ph): This just isn`t something that you do. With any other kind of medical issue is you have to ask, "Am I sick enough to get treatment?" It was just it was infuriating.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (voice-over): Three days after the diagnosis, Amanda`s health deteriorated. Doctors performed an abortion. But serious infections led to sepsis. And Amanda spent three days, in the ICU.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI (ph): Because of the law, I very nearly died. Nothing about this is pro-life.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (voice-over): Now, Amanda is part of a group of seven plaintiffs, represented by the Center for Reproductive Rights, who plan to file a lawsuit, against the State of Texas, tonight.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI (ph): Until there is a legal fight, nothing`s going to change. And something has to change.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:00]\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (voice-over): But John Seago, President of Texas Right to Life, who helped craft the Texas Heartbeat Act, says the law is clear, as written, and that doctors, like Amanda`s, are misinterpreting it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO, PRESIDENT, TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE: If they are saying they have to delay, and wait, the days, like you`re talking about? That is medical malpractice.\par \par \uc2 But if it is uncertain that this child may survive, or may not, Texas is saying, "We don`t jump to conclusions."\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (on camera): The doctors are saying, "The law is not clear. And, as a result, I`m going to act on the side of being cautious. And that means that I might have to wait for a woman to get sicker and sicker, before I can perform an abortion that I know she needs."\par \par \uc2 SEAGO: That`s the wrong conclusion.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (voice-over): \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2  is the President of the Center for Reproductive Rights.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2  , PRESIDENT & CEO, CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS: The issue here is not that the doctors are being too cautious. The reality is, without clarification, of this Texas law, without a change, in the circumstances that are happening, today, it is going to come that women are going to die.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (voice-over): Seago disagrees.\par \par \uc2 SEAGO: The law is not putting lives in danger. The law is black and white, about what a doctor can do, what he cannot do.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (on camera): Does it ever give you pause that there are situations that have happened after this law, where women`s lives have been put at risk?\par \par \uc2 SEAGO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Our mission is to save lives. So, the fact that some women`s lives have been jeopardized is something that we take very seriously.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI (ph): Little outfit.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (voice-over): Amanda and Josh are still grieving their loss.\par \par \uc2 J. ZURAWSKI (ph): I know.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI (ph): Damn onesie gets me every time!\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (voice-over): They hope something changes.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI (ph): I want nothing more than to have kids. But I am terrified to be pregnant again.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR (voice-over): Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News, Austin, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: With us for more, Maria Teresa Kumar, the President and CEO of Voto Latino. She joins me now. She is also an MSNBC Contributor, and a former board member, for Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 Maria, I mean, absolutely heartbreaking. I wonder what you make of this lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, VOTO LATINO PRESIDENT & CEO, PLANNED PARENTHOOD FORMER BOARD MEMBER, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: It a long time coming. And it`s so sad that so many women have had to basically get on the brink, of almost dying, and not only having to grieve their loss of a failed pregnancy, but having to actually hold these individuals, to account.\par \par \uc2 Alicia, let`s be clear. This is not about science. This is not about making sure that a woman is OK, nor the fetus. This is all about control. And what we are seeing, right now, is not only at odds with what we believe in separation of Church and State, but this is basically government at its worst, basically bending, to religious ideology that not every bit every single American adheres to.\par \par \uc2 And sadly, the women will die. And it`s going to be disproportionately poor women, women who do not have access, women who don`t understand the law, women who do not know how to access alternatives, in order to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, let`s talk about that. Because there was a South Carolina woman, who has been arrested, for allegedly using medication, to induce an abortion, back in 2021. This is what we have been warned about. How worried are you that what we`re seeing happen, in South Carolina, becomes the new normal?\par \par \uc2 TERESA KUMAR: Incredibly worried, because one of the things that the Texas law does, very similar to the South Carolina law, is that it basically provides any individual, to be a vigilante, and say, "This person assisted someone to go to a clinic."\par \par \uc2 So for example, if you call an Uber, and the Uber drives you, to an abortion clinic, and they deem that that is a type of assistance, in terminating a pregnancy. It has so many repercussions.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why one of the things that we`re seeing is other states getting into the fight. We`re talking specifically, in this case, I`m speaking about \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  . He declared today a war on \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  , because \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  decided that it was not going to sell one of two widely-available, FDA- approved drugs that allows women, to receive this kind of medication, so that they could self-administer abortion care. And so, the fact that we`re seeing Democratic governors coming in and doing it, that`s where the fight is.\par \par \uc2 And let`s not - let`s not kid ourselves, the fact that California, the fourth largest economy, in the world, is now going toe-to-toe with Texas, saying "This is how we`re going to crack down on it," because what we`re seeing not just in Texas, but potentially in the Supreme Court, if it goes to this, what we`re going to see is not going to be just a Blue state-Red State, who has access to the abortion care, but it`s going to impact every single American women, regardless of where they live.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right. That is why this abortion case, in Texas that we`re all watching, under Judge Kacsmaryk, keep reminding our viewers, it will not just be people, in Texas, who will not have access to those abortion pills. It will be nationwide.\par \par \uc2 And here`s the thing though, about Texas. In Texas, you`ve had, before Roe versus Wade was overturned, S.B.8, turned regular citizens, into abortion bounty hunters. You remember that.\par \par \uc2 We`re waiting to see about this Texas judge, if he`s going to ban abortion pills, nationwide.\par \par \uc2 And you have a Texas Republican, introducing a bill that would charge credit card companies, with a felony, for processing payments, related to abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:00]\par \par \uc2 I mean, again, we keep being reminded that they were not going to stop at Roe that Roe was the beginning. It was not the end. What is going on down there?\par \par \uc2 TERESA KUMAR: What`s going on is that you have a Republican governor run by a Texas GOP that is incredibly extreme. They again, are imposing their religiosity, on the American people, in Texas. And they`re not going to stop there.\par \par \uc2 And that is why it`s imperative that every single Democratic governor does exactly what \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  did, basically, take it to them, because otherwise, we are in a fight for our lives. This is not ideological. This is about a woman`s health, and ability, to basically decide for herself what is good for her family, but also make sure that she is keeping herself safe.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: It also comes on the heels of a midterm, where voters were very clear about the fact that they didn`t like this kind of extremism, that even if they don`t consider themselves pro-choice, necessarily, a lot of them saw this as government overreach.\par \par \uc2 And I wonder, as they continue, in Texas, for example, but in other states too, to push the line, if there then are going to be electoral consequences, for these laws?\par \par \uc2 TERESA KUMAR: We`ll see, as we`ll see electoral consequences, at the top of the ticket. But a lot of these states have highly-gerrymandered districts, in congressional races.\par \par \uc2 And that is where the challenge is. And that`s why it`s going to be really important that we see, again, a consistency of state legislators, in Democratic - in Democratic governors mansions, doing the exact same thing that \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 Because until then, if people do not feel that the fight is coming to them, you`re going to see a lot of Republicans, who are very well-organized, very well-orchestrated, when it comes to knocking down the State Houses, making sure they`re passing very similar legislation.\par \par \uc2 This is part of a concerted effort, with the Republican Party, where they are going into Texas, as test cases. And if it succeeds there, then they will go to other states.\par \par \uc2 And that`s one of the reasons why it`s important, again, that the American people continue to be very vigilant that they continue protesting, that they keep voting, but just as importantly, it`s important that governors that are of Democratic states, make sure that they`re doing the exact same thing.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Maria Teresa Kumar, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Russia running out of ammo, now, soldiers are left to fight with shovels. How the shortage could give Ukraine a leg-up, in the battle for Bakhmut, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 [23:45:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: We`re tracking important developments, in the brutal fight, for the Ukrainian city of Bakhmut. After months of bombardment, by Russian troops, laid waste to the mining city, Ukraine`s top generals say, they want to bolster their forces there, despite the city being nearly encircled, by Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 With us, for more, are Sabrina Siddiqui, White House Reporter, for The Wall Street Journal, who has been covering the war, in Ukraine; Jeremy Bash, former Chief of Staff at the CIA, and at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 Jeremy, I`m going to start with you.\par \par \uc2 What do we need to know about the battle for Bakhmut? How important is this city?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY BASH, FORMER CIA CHIEF OF STAFF, FORMER PENTAGON CHIEF OF STAFF: Well, in the last couple of minutes, the Ukrainian General Staff posted a statement, on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , this via First Alert that they have repelled more than 140 different attacks, over the course of the last 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 I think what the Ukrainian military is trying to say is that Bakhmut, although it may not be strategically important, it`s symbolically important, because they do not want to see the Russian flag, flying over any more territory, in Ukraine. And the Russians currently control the approaches to the city, from the north, from the south, from the east.\par \par \uc2 But the Ukrainians have been fighting block to block, in some cases, hand to hand, in a display of bravery that the world has seen.\par \par \uc2 And the world is rallying behind Ukraine. I think it`s just motivating Ukraine`s allies, in the West, to provide them with greater sophisticated weaponry, more support, more funding, keep the aid flowing. And so, this has really galvanized support for Ukraine, as Russia has made a very bloody advance.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I promise, we`ll get back to that question, of additional resources, because it is important.\par \par \uc2 I want to take a look at this though, from the Russian side, Jeremy, which is the head of the private military group that`s leading Russia`s Eastern advance, warning that the entire front could collapse, if they retreat, from Bakhmut.\par \par \uc2 But according to them, the Kremlin still has not provided ammunition, some Russian soldiers, now fighting with shovels. What does that suggest to you?\par \par \uc2 BASH: Well, this is a hybrid force, made up of regular Russian troops, but also the Wagner Group`s so-called recruits that they`ve taken from prisons, people without uniforms, without weapons.\par \par \uc2 When they make regular runs at the different trench lines that the Ukrainians have fought, the Ukrainians have said, in some cases, it`s hard to fight them because they`re so erratic, so ill-trained that they`d rather face off against the normal regularized Russian infantry. And so, this is an effort, by the Russians, to throw anything against the Ukrainian military, to see what sticks.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Sabrina, I promised I`d come back to this question of resources.\par \par \uc2 And NBC News has learned that two Ukrainian pilots are in the U.S., undergoing assessment, to see how long it would take them, to train on attack aircrafts.\par \par \uc2 The White House previously said it would not send fighter jets to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Is this reporting a sign that that position has changed?\par \par \uc2 SABRINA SIDDIQUI, THE WALL STREET JOURNAL WHITE HOUSE REPORTER: Well, I think it could be a sign that the U.S. has not entirely closed the door, on providing F-16s, to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:00]\par \par \uc2 But U.S. officials are still exercising caution. They say that this activity is more routine, and part of the U.S. Military dialog, with Ukraine, and that the current position on providing F-16s, hasn`t changed.\par \par \uc2 And the thinking, within the Biden administration, is still what it also is among other Western allies, which is that providing F-16s, to Ukraine, is impractical, for a number of reasons.\par \par \uc2 One that, it could require considerable training of Ukrainian pilots, that the Russians actually have extensive anti-aircraft systems that could easily shoot those jets down. And really, the U.S. and other allies are still very mindful of taking any action that could be seen as an escalation, between \uc2 NATO\uc2  and Russia.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, the Biden administration, the U.S. officials are maintaining no change in position, on F-16s. But the Ukrainians are still extensively lobbying, for those jets. And so, the door has not entirely been closed.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So, and Jeremy, when the door has not entirely been closed, that means that there are still conversations happening, behind closed doors, about what this would look like. How crucial would these jets be for Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 BASH: Yes, I think, airpower, in general, is going to be decisive and incredibly important, in the coming several months. Whether those are unmanned aerial vehicles, drones, or whether they are manned fighter aircraft, I think it`s to be very important for the Ukrainian Air Force to be able to hit targets against Russian-advancing troops. If they`re going to have any hope of ejecting Russians, from territory that they currently occupy, you need air power to do it.\par \par \uc2 And I think this report that we`re training and potentially going to train or evaluate the training of F-16 pilots, is a signal that either we`re going to be sending F-16s directly, or we`re going to green-light the provision of F-16s, from other \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies, to Ukraine. I think that`s a smart move.\par \par \uc2 I think the Biden administration has learned one important lesson, over the last year. It`s "Don`t telegraph what we`re not going to do. Just keep the door open to different possibilities." Sometimes our red lines have conferred to pink dotted lines, to clear lines. We`ve provided tanks. We`ve provided drones. We`ve provided a number of different capabilities that we - in the past that we wouldn`t provide.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Sabrina, who has reported on those morphing lines nodded knowingly.\par \par \uc2 It is striking to me, Sabrina, that as we`re having this conversation, Republicans, at CPAC, were having a very different one.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Why were these politicians more interested in dealing and helping Ukraine than helping their own citizens?\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2  , FORMER TRUMP WHITE HOUSE CHIEF STRATEGIST: This is how you get $32 trillion in debt, going to $52 trillion in debt. This is how you get your kid to die, on foreign battlefields, in Iraq and Afghanistan, and now in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): No money to Ukraine. And that country needs to find peace, not war.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE CHEERS & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR GREENE: I will look at a camera, and directly tell Zelenskyy, you better leave your hands off of our sons and daughters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Sabrina, I`ve got about 30 seconds left.\par \par \uc2 But I am very struck, by the politics, and the consequences, of that messaging.\par \par \uc2 SIDDIQUI: Yes. You`re seeing the lingering impact of former President Trump`s isolationist rhetoric, within the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 And Republican leaders, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  , and Kevin McCarthy, are saying, "These are just voices, on the fringes of the party. We very much stand behind Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 But I think what you`re seeing at CPAC is that this worldview is actually becoming a lot more mainstream, within the GOP.\par \par \uc2 And it could have significant consequences, because there are going to be other votes that come before Congress, on aid for Ukraine, economic, military and humanitarian assistance. And that`s going to have to pass muster, in this new Republican House majority.\par \par \uc2 And so, there are significant implications, for whether or not Kevin McCarthy, would be able to actually corral the votes that he needs. And it`s just very fascinating that this is happening in a conflict that is widely viewed, around the world, as a battle, between democracy and autocracy, but seemingly not so, within the majority of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Those are exactly the stakes.\par \par \uc2 Sabrina Siddiqui, Jeremy Bash, thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, it was a dark but important turning point, in the Civil Rights Movement. And tonight, a reminder that the march for voting rights, is far from over, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 [23:55:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2  , CIVIL RIGHTS LEADER: We`re marching today to dramatize to the nation, and dramatize to the world, the hundreds and thousands of Negro citizens of Alabama, but particularly here in the Black Belt area, denied the right to vote. And we intend to march to Montgomery to present said grievances to Governor George C. Wallace.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The last thing, before we go tonight, "The March Continues."\par \par \uc2 That was the late civil rights leader, and Georgia congressman, \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  , in 1965, when he was just 25-years-old. He`s about to set out on the march for voting rights, from Selma to Montgomery.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow marks the 58th anniversary of the day, now known as Bloody Sunday. The brutal attacks, carried out by police officers, on Lewis, and others, became a turning point, in the Civil Rights Movement.\par \par \uc2 President Biden was in Selma, yesterday, marching alongside civil rights leaders, over the Edmund Pettus Bridge, to commemorate the solemn anniversary. A speech, at the event, he spoke about his memories, of the day, and explained how voting rights are still under attack, today.\par \par \uc2 Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT, UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: They forced the country to confront the hard truths and to act to keep the promise of America alive.\par \par \uc2 I was a student up north in the Civil Rights Movement. I remember feeling how guilty I was. I wasn`t here. How could we all be up there and you going through what you went through, looking at those - I can still picture - can still picture the troopers with their batons and wands and whips.\par \par \uc2 The right to vote, to have your vote counted is the threshold of democracy and liberty.\par \par \uc2 With it, anything is possible. Without it - without that right, nothing is possible.\par \par \uc2 And this fundamental right remains under assault.\par \par \uc2 The conservative Supreme Court has gutted the Voting Rights Act over the years.\par \par \uc2 Since the 2020 election, a wave of states and dozens - dozens of anti- voting laws fueled by the Big Lie, and the election deniers.\par \par \uc2 We know that we must get the votes in Congress to pass the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Advancement Act.\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE CHEERS & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And the Freedom to Vote Act.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: 58 years later, \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  and America`s March for Equality continues.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you a good night. I`m Alicia Menendez, in for Stephanie Ruhle. And catch me, every weekend, on "AMERICAN VOICES," from all of our colleagues, across the networks, of NBC News. Thank you, for staying up late.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020230307ej3600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for March 6, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Vaughn Hillyard, Matthew Dowd, Jesse Kirsch, Dana  Griffin, Bill Karins, Ali Vitali, Courtney Kube, Matt Bradley, Joyce Vance,  Tom Costello \par \uc2 8,229 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Brutal and bitter nomination fights in modern history. After taking veiled shots at each other from across the country, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  are both gearing up for their debuts in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Plus, take a look at these extraordinary images. Another \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  train flying off the tracks less than 200 miles from that derailment in East Palestine a month ago. With the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  set to arrive, there are today, there are no reports of any toxic chemical spills. But lots of questions about how in the world this happened again.\par \par \uc2 And what started out as a wild weather story has quickly turned into a life-threatening disaster. Scores of California residents hemmed in by literal walls of snow, more than six feet high. They`re trapped and they`re running out of food. We`ll have a live report on the rescue operations that are underway right now.\par \par \uc2 Today, the battle lines for the 2024 Republican nomination are being drawn more clearly than we`ve seen to date, setting up what may be a long and nasty fight for the Republican nomination. We heard it in Donald Trump`s borderline apocalyptic address to a less than robust MAGA crowd at CPAC, while the still undeclared \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  amped up his attacks on liberals in his speech to a packed house at the Reagan Library, portraying himself as the antidote to Trump-style chaos.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R-FL): In for years, you didn`t see our administration leaking like a sieve. You didn`t see a lot of drama or palace intrigue. What you saw was surgical precision execution day after day after day. And because we did that, we beat the left day after day after day.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I know battle. They know it. I know it. You know it. Everybody knows that. This is it. Either they win or we win. And if they win, we no longer have a country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Now both Trump and DeSantis are headed to Iowa in the coming days as the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports that the former president is digging in for an ugly and protracted battle. NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard spent the weekend at CPAC. He`s back now and joins me on set. Susan Page is the Washington bureau chief for USA Today. And Matthew Dowd is MSNBC`s senior political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Oh, Vaughn, what fun you had this weekend at CPAC. Look, Trump is reportedly gearing up, right? He hasn`t been doing much now. He`s reportedly not just going to go out and do rallies, but apparently, if reporting is right, policy speeches as well. What is this campaign look like going forward? Do we know?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Right. The campaign advisors I`ve talked to, they intend for this should be a very deliberate campaign from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  making the case that he over his four years, five-four conservative policies. If we go back to 2015 -- 2016, remember the questions we were having? It was, is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  actually, right a pro- life? Is he actually a fiscal conservative, right? This is --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Is he serious about running? Because this is what I recall.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: He`s serious about running. Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Do they -- what type of a Republican would he actually be in the White House? That`s not a question this time around. We all lived through those four years. And so, for now, for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , he`s going and making the case to conservatives around the country, stick with me that after those four years that we could do this again.\par \par \uc2 Next week, a week from now he`s going to Iowa. And then focus there is this education proposal that he put forward in which one of the proposals is to slash federal funding for "any school or program pushing critical race theory, gender ideology or other inappropriate racial, sexual or political content onto our children." And another proposal --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But when you`re at CPAC, are people talking about policies when they talk about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Chris, let`s be very straightforward about this. for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , a lot of this is about grievance politics, right?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: He is an aggrieved individual. He says I am going to essentially do what I can to take down the deep state, the Democratic Party. At the same time there are conservative -- or policies today -- modern-day conservative policies that were being discussed at CPAC.\par \par \uc2 One of those being banning gender-affirming care for minors. We heard that over and over from speakers on stage. The border, really -- you know, fentanyl. We -- there are very much issues that propel and compel Republicans to politics. Let`s be very clear. A lot of these are so-called --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: That frankly, he used to get elected the first time and to keep the troops together since then.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Correct. And what did you also hear from him this weekend? The Social Security.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: No.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: He said there`s other Republicans out there taking a shot at \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: There`s other -- there are others that want to reform entitlement. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said I am here to protect them. And so, for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , he is intending to try to make a -- there`s a lot of folks that are going to take the question with the actual policies that he is presenting and putting forward but they are issues that he intends to actually run on.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A lot of people are talking, Matt, about the differences between these two men. Let me talk to you, at least on the surface, they`re both running on the same sort of platform, which is look, what I`ve done.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , it`s look. What I did as president. Now we`re seeing with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , look what I`ve accomplished as governor, that`s going to tell you what kind of president I`m going to be. But do you see differences? What differentiates them or might in reality in the eyes of a Republican voter?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, MSNBC SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, to me, the big difference is one is Trump and one`s not Trump. And that`s the big -- that`s the big difference that there -- he`s going to try to push in this. And I think he`ll try to highlight one is a loser, which is what DeSantis campaign will try to brand \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as in, you can`t win with this guy. And I`m a winner. I just won in a landslide, the largest victory for a Republican in Florida in modern -- in modern times.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s that`s more of it. It`s not going to be an issue. I don`t think they`ll ever be an issue debate with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . You never get into an issue debate. He doesn`t care about issues.\par \par \uc2 I think the interesting things are these are probably the two biggest practitioners of grievance politics that we`ve seen in our times in politics. And so that strive for the top of the ticket will actually be a search for the bottom is who can go and who can be the cruelest, who can be the meanest, who can take on what their view is enemies of the America they want and that`s what those strive for. But one will try to do it as I`m not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . You know me. I won. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost a series of elections. You don`t want to go with him again.\par \par \uc2 That, to me, will be the decisional point. And the vote -- and the -- and the -- and the pivot upon which the voters in the Republican primary, they liked both of these men. In fact, they liked \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  more now than they did at the beginning of the process in 2015. So, they liked both of these folks. They liked them both for the hateful politics they push. But one wants to be say I`m a winner, the other one he wants to say he`s a loser.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, I think there`s another similarity, Susan, I`ll posit this that they both are in many ways backward-looking not just in running on their record because that`s always to some extent, right looking back, but it tells you what they`re going to do going forward. But look at DeSantis. He`s pitching Florida as a model for the country. But in doing so, for example, he reignited or tried to reignite the debate over COVID. Take a listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: When the world went mad when common sense suddenly became an uncommon virtue, Florida stood as a refuge of sanity, a citadel of freedom for people throughout the United States, and indeed, throughout the world. We refused to let our state descend into some type of Fauci and dystopia where people`s rights were curtailed and their livelihoods were destroyed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But to Matt`s point of grievance politics, Susan, he`s also pushing conservative policies like banning diversity programs at public universities, expanding gun rights, restricting abortion rights, how does that help him beat Trump or does it?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2  : Well, I think it helps him beat Trump in the Republican primary. You know, one of the interesting things we`re seeing is it`s possible we are not going to see a replay of the Republican primaries from 2016 when he had a big field and that helped \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  win the nomination. I mean, at the moment, it`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  with no one else really getting a toehold so far. That`s a different landscape for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to face.\par \par \uc2 And in \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , he faces someone with whom he has few ideological differences. The differences are stylistic. And this, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has a veto-proof majority in the Florida Legislature. So, we should expect them in the next few months to pass his agenda, which will enable him to say look, look at what I can get done and contrast that also with for President Trump.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There`s one thing we haven`t said yet, Vaughn, and I don`t think you saw this at CPAC. But there is the exhaustion factor. It`s hard to be exhausted by \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  because frankly, a lot of the country is just getting to know who he is. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has a completely different story.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: To your point of we all know where we`ve been. We all know what he did for the four years he was president -- the four years since. But is your sense that the exhaustion factor is significant enough to help \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: I think that a conversation actually had with two operatives just this morning centered on the question of how many Republicans who used to be actively engaged in Republican primaries had become disenchanted with the party, maybe even changed your party registration to independent or Democrat so they could vote another party`s primary. You know, how many of those folks that are maybe more tuned with the likes of a \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  , a \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  , a \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  , and \uc2 Martha McSally\uc2  ? How many Republican voters are out there? We`re talking 15 percent, 20 percent, 25 percent that maybe -- I`m not meeting out on the road as much here. They`re a little quieter over the last eight years.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 But folks that want to be engaged are frustrated by what -- when I say conservatism, what that means today. It doesn`t mean the same thing as 12 years ago, right? And so how many of those folks are going to reengage in this Republican primary presidential contest? They couldn`t do that four years ago, but how many of them are going to be in Iowa and New Hampshire, that maybe I`m not running into on the side of the road or at CPAC or at, you know, the \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  event in Cedar Rapids that I was at two weeks ago?\par \par \uc2 But folks that were quietly re-engaged within the Republican Party and tried to take hold that event, that is frankly, an answer that I don`t have, but I`m interested to see. And that may not be in Ron DeSantis`s vote, it may not even be a Nikki Haley vote, right, but it may be for somebody like a \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  or a \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. So, we`re going to talk about that a little later on in our next hour. Right? There -- a moderate could possibly win the Republican nomination. But, Matt, there`s another interesting development from CPAC, I`m curious to hear your take on. Kari Lake won the vice presidential poll of people like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Nikki Haley. Is that a name-recognition thing or does it tell us something about the MAGA wing of the party?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: I think it definitely tells us something about the MAGA wing. But I wouldn`t call it the MAGA wing. I would call it the base of the Republican Party in this. You know what`s fascinating is I watched you show that clip of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , which he was at Simi Valley, which is obviously the home of the Reagan Library is that if \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  showed up at this CPAC convention, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  , their hero of past -- of the past would be booed because of where he stood on bridging divides where he stood on various issues. He would be booed today at that CPAC convention in the course of the stat shows you --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Would \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  himself, boo? Right -- he`s not -- he wasn`t the kind of person who would do that. But would he has -- his son`s suggestion -- suggested, I think Michael Reagan suggested this, be unhappy with what he heard in his library from \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 DOWD: I think if \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  had shown up and saw Kari Lake, he would have thought he was in one of the movies in the 1940s. One of the be movies that he starred in that this is like this can`t be real, right? This is not real in the course of this.\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake, to me, is so typical of where the Republican Party is. She`s loved by the base. She says the craziest things that they cheer her when she says the craziest thing. She`s still denying the election, that she obviously lives in a state now governed by a Democrat there in Arizona. And I said I think the fact that we`re saying that \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  would be booed and would be out of sync and Kari Lake was a hero, tells you everything you want to know about where the Republican Party is today.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Oh, that`s right. Michael Reagan was actually talking about Kari Lake. He wasn`t talking about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  . All right, confusing my weakened speeches. Susan, there`s no modern precedent for a defeated former president competing in a primary like this. So, what are you going to be watching for going forward? We just heard was one of the things that Vaughn is going to be looking for after talking to his sources, but what are you going to be looking for to gauge political strength -- political weaknesses, sort of how this race is going to unfold going forward?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: You know, it`s not only unprecedented in modern times because we have a former president who was defeated for re-election running again, but it`s also impressive because we could have a nominee -- a candidate who has been criminally indicted and is still running for president. Former President Trump said this weekend he would run even if he is indicted, which is clearly a possibility in several of the investigations against him.\par \par \uc2 I think that thing you look for is whether \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  can consolidate the vote that is not Trump and convince some of the Trump voters that it is OK to vote for him. And that`s going to be a harder task than you think because Trump is a great hero to a significant number of Republicans. He is the face of this party, and he is not likely to go quietly away if \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  seems to be gaining ground or even winning the nomination.\par \par \uc2 Would \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  support \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  if he wins the Republican nomination?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: No.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: I`m not sure I know the answer to that question.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I have one more question even though we`re out of time, \uc2 Matt Dowd\uc2  . And that is, again, since he answered a question about it. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were to be indicted, does that hurt him or does it just energize his forces?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  -- I -- it`s not often that we`re able to pick out at something that he says is truthful and probably accurate, which is, as he said in the course of that, that the -- he actually thought an indictment would help him. I actually agree with that because it feeds into they`re all against us. Everyone is against us. I`m fighting for you. And their -- for their taking me on but they`re really taking you on, which is basically what he said.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And so not only do I think he said I would run even if indicted, I think he`s running because he might be indicted. That`s, I think, pined behind his strategy. I actually think among the Republican base before you get to a general election, it would actually help him.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Such interesting stuff. Matthew Dowd, Susan Page, Vaughn Hillyard, who will be on the road for what the next 18 months or so along occasionally with the rest of us, thank you all.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: I`ll bring you along for some of it, yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, you`ve got to see this heart-stopping clip out of Ohio. Take a look. The view from one Springfield Ohio driver, it came -- do we have that? At that exact moment. Another -- there it is, another \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  train ran off the tracks. The driver is actually slowly backing the car away from that dangerous scene.\par \par \uc2 Officials report no injuries or threats to public health. The railway company confirms there were no hazardous material onboard the 28 cards that derailed. But this is \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  `s second derailment in the state in just weeks. The fourth in less than five months in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Jesse Kirsch who was on the ground there for us in Springfield. Jesse, officials from the \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  are now investigating, as I understand at the site where you are, what more can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So, Chris, two things. One, you can see this is still a blocked-off roadway. We`ve got a cleanup ongoing here. But the trains are moving again. We see multiple trains coming by on these tracks, \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  trains.\par \par \uc2 And as you mentioned, this company has now had four derailments in this state in less than five months. Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  , calling that on acceptable making another push for legislative reform, something we know that has bipartisan support in Congress right now. And of course, the CEO of \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  is expected to be testifying on Capitol Hill later this week.\par \par \uc2 And the ramifications of what happened roughly a month ago in East Palestinian are clear, even here, more than 200 miles away. I spoke with a gentleman earlier. And I pointed out to him at the \uc2 Ohio Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  says that nothing hazardous leaked into the soil, into the air, or into the water here. Despite that, he says he is still skeptical of what he`s hearing. And what comes to mind is what happened in East Palestine. And of course, residents there are still concerned about their health, about their financial well-being, about the future of that small community after what happened there about a month ago.\par \par \uc2 Back here, this is obviously a fresh derailment. You mentioned federal investigators are now looking into this incident. We have no official word on cause -- we still don`t have an official word on cause of what happened in East Palestine. And one of the things I asked of Norfolk Southern Railroad yesterday is what they would say to people who question whether this railroad should be allowed to operate right now, considering what is going on. The railroad says that safety is its number one priority.\par \par \uc2 They`ve actually just unveiled today a new safety plan in the aftermath of the East Palestine incident. That safety plan includes a push for improving -- expanding their network of sensors, which tracks the temperature of the wheel bearings. One of the things that \uc2 NTSB\uc2  we know is looking at from that -- from that derailment in East Palestine. So, they continue to emphasize safety as something they`re focused on and say that after each -- and each derailment, they do an investigation. And if there are takeaways to improve safety, they will implement those.\par \par \uc2 So still to be determined what comes out of this scene. But we do know now the government is independently investigating separately from the railroad. However, as we heard from a resident here earlier and what we`ve heard from people in the East Palestine, that doesn`t necessarily take away some of the skepticism in communities based on what`s been going on with the railroad.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, they`ve got a long, long climb to get credibility back. Jesse Kirsch, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 A nightmare situation in California. Unusual snowstorms have left thousands without power, but others are completely trapped. Now, some people are running out of food. We`ve got a live report from the ground next.\par \par \uc2 The strict new anti-abortion measures and how changing the definition of life-threatening is being used by conservative lawmakers. And the stark warning from Iran`s Supreme Leader about the ongoing suspected poisonings of schoolgirls is ahead. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The effects of wild and dangerous weather are being felt from coast to coast. Right now, more than 120,000 households in Kentucky don`t have power after hurricane-force winds killed at least five people there. Another 25,000 without electricity after heavy snow in Michigan. 20,000 also in the dark in California where officials say some areas could be snowed in for a week as historic blizzards ground traveled to a standstill. Already people in the mountains near LA are pleading for more resources. They`re writing to help us in the snow. You see it right there.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Dana Griffin is live from those mountains in Crestline, California. And NBC News Meteorologist Bill Karins is here with the very latest for us. Dana, what is it like on the ground today, and how complicated and dangerous it is to try to get resources to people who are snowed in?\par \par \uc2 DANA GRIFFIN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Chris, first of all, good afternoon. It is very dangerous and very complicated. And the best way to show you that is literally just to kind of illustrate it for you here on the ground.\par \par \uc2 I want you to take a look behind me. This is the only grocery store in this town. If you look through the windows, this is not a reflection of the outside. You`re actually looking inside Goodwin & Sons Market where so much snow and debris has fallen when that roof caved in on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 But the beautiful thing about the story is how much the community has come together. So many items have been donated, including 50 of these shovels from the Rancho Cucamonga Lowes. This is the last one. And they say that they used shovels like this because the plastic ones have been breaking because the snow is just so heavy and dense and compact, really icy.\par \par \uc2 But take a look at this distribution center that has formed outside of this now-shuttered grocery store. There are protein shakes, formula, diapers, food, so many items that people really could use right now. We`re going to walk over to the distribution lines. You can see still this line stretches down to the end of the parking lot.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at some of the people. There are a little kids here. Parents bringing their children. One of the new items that we`ve seen here is DiGiorno Pizza, obviously something that you can freeze and pop into an oven if you know you`re a resident that still has heat. There are still hundreds of people without heat.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And the big concern now is how many people are at those higher elevations that are still stuck inside their homes. One woman who was standing inside this line commented that we`re the lucky ones. We could actually leave our homes. A lot of them we`re having to hike out of feet of snow to try to get down here.\par \par \uc2 But this is just the beauty of this community of how much they have come together to help each other out. They`re even giving out dog food and bird seed because people have animals that they`re also trying to keep alive during this really frustrating time for them. And the concern now is come the end of the week on Friday, when more snow is expected in this community, how much more is that going to affect these community -- this community. So, everyone`s trying to stay optimistic, but they`re really looking for more help, for plows to reach their streets so that more of them can get out, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Dana Griffin, thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 So, Bill, I mean, honestly, when she first -- that -- the camera person, like went into the grocery store, I was having trouble getting oriented that the huge snow pile was actually inside that grocery store. Give us a sense of just how bad it is there.\par \par \uc2 BILL KARINS, MSNBC BROADCAST METEOROLOGIST: And how bad it`s going to get?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARINS: I mean I hate it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`m not sure I want to know.\par \par \uc2 KARINS: So many times, we have these big events and your -- you know biggest question is, oh, what`s next. And a lot of times, it`s not much or it`s something small. I`m fearful of what`s coming Thursday into Friday, and then possibly Tuesday to Wednesday, the week after that. We`re in a very active weather pattern for the West.\par \par \uc2 And what`s going to change is that these next couple storms are going to be what we call atmospheric rivers and they`re going to be warmer. So, all of a sudden, we`re going to get rain on top of these roofs that a lot of snow, it`s just going to soak it all up, and then we`re going to have -- dealing with roof collapses, we`re going to have flooding issues. I mean, California is going to be a mess for at least the next two weeks. And we may not have even seen the worst of it.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve got a couple of baby storms. We`ll call these as little storms off the coast right now. Mostly it`s only cruises problems in the northern half of the Sierra Mountain chain. So, we still have these winter storm warnings from Yosemite up to Kirkwood. Mammoth Lakes is not in this one -- Mount Shasta. So, this is northern half of California so we`re giving southern half of California a break.\par \par \uc2 And as far as the timing goes, it`s just going to be a persistent flow coming off the ocean. So, the orthographic lifts of the mountains when the air rises up the mountains, it produces snow. So, they will get 12, 18 maybe in some cases, two feet of snow over the next couple days.\par \par \uc2 But it`s kind of spread out a little bit. Temperatures are fairly warm, so the snow levels are pretty high. We get a little heavier blast coming in Wednesday. And that`s when we should see additional problems, especially for anyone that`s still trying to dig out in this area.\par \par \uc2 And, Chris, it`s just amazing. You know if you told me six months ago, we`d be talking about too much rain or too much snow in California, I wouldn`t have believed you. I mean, this has been an incredible winter for snow and rain. And it looks like it`s going to continue through March.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Bill Karins, always good to see you, my friend. Thank you. And stay with us. A live update on where our search and rescue efforts stemmed from the Sheriff of Madeira County, California. That`s coming up in our next hour.\par \par \uc2 Police are now interviewing victims, security officials, and attendees searching for a cause of a stampede that killed one person and injured eight more at the end of a concert in Rochester, New York. The mayor promising accountability this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MALIK EVANS, MAYOR OF ROCHESTER, NEW YORK: This is a tragedy of epic proportions. What I saw last night in the aftermath of what happened at this concert breaks my heart and it is totally unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As it had just wrapped up in a downtown venue when the Rochester police chief says the crowd of thousands began to surge toward the exits. Two of the eight surviving victims are said to be in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 New information about the classified documents taken from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  , a top Democrat revealing what the Gang of Eight has been told. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:50]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A rare occurrence on Capitol Hill today. Top lawmakers from both parties agreeing on one thing, the need for intelligence officials to share more information about what`s in those classified documents found on the Biden, Trump and Pence properties. Two members of the Gang of Eight spoke with our Chuck Todd on "MEET THE PRESS."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE TURNER\uc2  (R), OHIO: In the things that we do know, one of the things we know is that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is not being forthcoming.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM HIMES (D-CT): Let`s just say that neither one of us are satisfied that we got enough information. We got a flavor for what was there. And I won`t speak for Mike, but I will tell you having been given a flavor, this is a very serious issue. This wasn`t stuff that we can say clearly does not matter. It matters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Ali Vitali is on Capitol Hill for us.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Ali. I mean, there have been widespread bipartisan calls for more details. So where does this go from here?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, and Chris, think about it. Even just to get to last week`s briefing, you had to have Democrats and Republicans on both sides of Congress asking for months since those documents that were found at Mar-a-Lago became public. That`s the result of months of asking, is a meeting that they had last week with the Gang of Eight that you see on your screen there where they got some information.\par \par \uc2 But even walking into that meeting, people like Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  , the top Republican on the Senate Intel Committee, didn`t have high hopes for a high degree of transparency. Instead, their view walking in was that this was going to be sort of like the opening salvo, the first conversation that they`re having about what could potentially be in these documents.\par \par \uc2 But you listen to the two top -- the Republican and Democrat on the House side of the Intel Committee, and it`s clear what they said on "MEET THE PRESS." They`re not satisfied with what they`ve gotten so far. They`re still not sure that sources and methods weren`t compromised by these classified documents being found out of their usual chain of command, and they want more information.\par \par \uc2 Now, how they get that information is sort of the question of the day up here on Capitol Hill. Clearly we`re going to hear more pressuring from all of these key senators and congresspeople on this issue, but, again, they might continue running into the same problem, which is that the director of National Intelligence and other entities have said that while this risk assessment that they`re doing because Congress asked them to is something that they could get briefed on.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 They are running into the problem of those special counsel investigations and not wanting to compromise those. So a careful balancing act on the part of the administration here. But, again, lawmakers just want more information about what was specifically in these documents.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, I know you`ll keep us posted. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 U.N . members have reached an historic agreement to protect the world`s oceans. It`s a unified treaty and it`s been in the works for nearly 20 years to protect biodiversity on the high seas. Translation, they`re creating a new body which is supposed to manage conservation of ocean life and set up areas to protect