many as 10 more may be joining them in the coming days as part of this larger event.\par \par \uc2 Now during the reporting on this, Chris, our colleague Carol Lee and I spoke with a number of administration officials and Defense officials about this. And they insist that despite this event that`s occurring at a U.S. Military base in Arizona, the administration still is resisting the calls from Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  and other Ukrainian officials to provide attack aircraft like F-16s to the Ukrainians. They continue to say that they have not made that decision.\par \par \uc2 We actually heard some specifics about that for the first time from the Pentagon`s top policy official Dr. Collin Cole last week before the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2  . He said that it would take as much as 18 months to train some of these Ukrainian pilots on aircraft like the F-16. But we spoke with Defense officials after that, and part of this assessment will determine whether that actually is the case.\par \par \uc2 If you have pilots who have experience on other attack aircraft like MIGs that the Ukrainians fly, it`s possible they could cut that training time down to as low as six months. And we have to say, you know, since this war started, since Russia invaded Ukraine just over a year ago now, the Ukrainian military has repeatedly been able to surpass expectations about how quickly they can train on some of these more difficult and advanced systems that the U.S. and others have been providing them, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It is fascinating stuff. Courtney Kube, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s top general told President Zelenskyy today that they don`t want to give up on the key eastern city of Bakhmut even as Russian forces are closing in.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Matt Bradley is following the story for us.\par \par \uc2 So, Matt, I had heard the speculation Ukraine was ready to retreat after all that fierce fighting in Bakhmut. Well, what are officials there saying now?\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Well, now we`re hearing, as you just mentioned, from President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  saying that it looks as though the generals -- and we know that President Zelenskyy doesn`t run the day-to-day of this war, it`s the generals who do so. They say that they want to stay in Bakhmut, that they want to continue defending this city.\par \par \uc2 Now as Courtney Kube just mentioned a moment ago, this has been going on for a year ever since Russia`s invasion happened in February 24th back last year. This battle for Bakhmut has been going on almost as long as that. It is by far the longest battle that Ukraine has endured since the beginning of this fight. And it`s arguably the most lethal and the most damaging.\par \par \uc2 But we`re seeing here two sides pit against each other that seem to be almost entirely equal in terms of strength, even though the Ukrainians seem to be having the upper hand in a lot of different areas throughout the country. In Bakhmut, they seemed very evenly matched. Much of the fighting has been led by the Wagner Group on the Russian side, which is headed by \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  who`s an oligarch, who`s very close to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 He`s been leading the fight, not the Russian regular military, for most of this time. But, you know, the fact that there`s been so much damage on both sides for this entire battle, and that there`s been so much death on both sides this entire battle, it really has baffled Western military analysts who`ve been watching this, who`ve been advising the Ukrainians. They wondered why the Ukrainians don`t withdraw and why the Russians themselves don`t withdraw from this city and focus on other places.\par \par \uc2 Because the fact is that Bakhmut, while it does have some strategic value, is really not worth the amount of material and lives that both sides have been spending. It is, as \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  , the U.S. Defense Department secretary said just today, that it would be a symbolic victory for Russia to take Bakhmut, not really a strategic victory. But really the fight for Bakhmut has also opened up a lot of fissures within the military leadership in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 While you just mentioned how leaders in Ukraine seem to be a bit more on side, they all are together with Zelenskyy offering to defend Bakhmut, to continue their stand against the Russian invasion there, the Russian attack has really been so damaging to the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:12]\par \par \uc2 And that`s because, as I mentioned, this man Prigozhin, who leads the Wagner Group, this is a private military -- a private army. He has been repeatedly accusing the Kremlin, not necessarily Putin himself, but the leaders of the Ministry of Defense there in Russia of not providing them with the weapons they need in order to succeed in Bakhmut. So as this battle rages on the Russian side, despite all the advice, despite all of the conventional military thinking, it really has done a lot of damage to the unity of the Kremlin and the Russian war effort. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Once again, Ukrainians who are often outperforming what a lot of people think that they can.\par \par \uc2 Matt Bradley, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 The moves that threaten reproductive rights across the U.S., the states taking on abortion and the intricate strategies some lawmakers are using.\par \par \uc2 And millions of Americans now left without the means to put food on the table. I`ll ask Congressman \uc2 Jim McGovern\uc2  of Massachusetts if Congress will do anything about it. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now two Republican governors have bills on their desks that would make it harder for people to access abortions in their states. In Utah, there`s a measure that would effectively ban abortion clinics from operating, pushing all abortions medical or surgical in that state to hospitals. In Wyoming, two bills that would ban abortions with narrow exceptions and criminalize the use of medications for abortions.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in former U.S. attorney and professor at the University of Alabama Law School, Joyce Vance.\par \par \uc2 Joyce, let me just start with an example. Abortion was long considered protected by the Wyoming state constitution, which guarantees adults the right to make their own health care decisions. So lawmakers wrote this bill saying abortions are not health care.\par \par \uc2 I mean, is there any limit, essentially, to what states can legislate or I guess in this case define?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: So there really aren`t any limits on states any longer. Before the Supreme Court sort of ripped all of the protections that in previously accompanied abortion off, states had limits on what they could do. But in Dobb`s last term, the Supreme Court said there is no constitutional right to get an abortion. That means states can set up whatever legal regime they want. It can be completely permissive or it can ban abortion outright, and that`s why we`re seeing states like Utah and Wyoming pass these very restrictive measures. There`s no longer any concern that it`s not constitutional.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So does it include this? Another example, Joyce. Exemptions from that law include a pregnancy that would endanger the woman`s life or health, but they specifically say that does not cover being suicidal. They do not consider being suicidal a threat to a woman`s life. Could these rewrites be the basis for a legal challenge or simply, again, because they`re state legislatures and they`re writing laws, they can define anything any way they want to?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So there will be challenges. I feel certain of that. The problem of that is that with the Supreme Court, this very conservative Supreme Court, is the ultimate decider on abortion issues. It`s tough to feel optimistic, and so the example that you identify, Chris, you know, this law that has very limited exceptions for the health of the mother, will doctors still perform those procedures at the risk of falling under criminal suspicion?\par \par \uc2 So you know that that will disincentivize them from performing or providing services in virtually all circumstances, and then as you say, even that bill has no carve out whatsoever for the mother`s emotional health or mental health. So these are not protections for women. These are simply efforts for these bills to survive scrutiny in the courts if they get there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you about a bill sort of on the other end of the spectrum. In Washington state where abortion is legal, state lawmakers are proposing laws to protect women who are getting an abortion. One would block subpoenas for information from other states where it`s illegal. So in other words, a woman who lives in a state, let`s say Texas where there are restrictions, decides she wants to come to Washington state to get an abortion, that would block anyone in that state from sending that information back to Texas or any other state where it might be used to prosecute her, right?\par \par \uc2 I know, for example, there are countries that won`t share information with the U.S. or don`t have extradition treaties with the U.S. Maybe that`s a bad comparison, but would this be a new area of state law, this blocking, any sharing of information?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So I think the extradition agreements are actually a really good example because there are some countries, the United Kingdom is one of them, that says, look, we don`t agree with the United States policy on the death penalty, so we won`t submit folks in our care to your jurisdiction. That`s essentially what`s going to happen with this litigation over whether one state that chooses to outlaw abortion can impose its belief in other states.\par \par \uc2 For instance, when citizens of Texas travel elsewhere to obtain medical services, look, this is the next frontier in litigation. Five years ago, we would have all said there`s no chance that Texas can interfere in Washington, but now I think we have to be honest and say we just don`t know what the courts will rule.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`ll be talking about this continuously.\par \par \uc2 Joyce Vance, it`s always good to see you, my friend. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead, the latest scare in the sky. Why one Florida bound train had to make a rare emergency landing. Wait until you hear those details, next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The plane just shook and then it dropped. It was -- very terrifying because a lot of kids and a lot of women.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:38:35]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today we`re talking about new aviation concerns because there have been more scary incidents in the sky, including a rare emergency landing and severe turbulence that left one passenger dead.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Tom Costello has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): High drama on the runway at Havana`s airport on Sunday. A \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  flight, departing for Fort Lauderdale, forced to make an emergency landing. The airline says an apparent bird strike hit the plane`s engine and its nose shortly after takeoff.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Chaos. Complete chaos. Talking about dropping really quick and the fuse getting filled up, and nobody could breathe, and everybody`s screaming.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Meanwhile, a separate instance of severe turbulence in the air turned deadly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Requesting medical assistance on the runway.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: A private CL-30 jet carrying five people on its way to Virginia from New Hampshire encountered severe turbulence on Friday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is the tower with a medical emergency, landing runway 6.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The pilot diverted the jet from its original route to make an emergency landing in Connecticut. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  and \uc2 NTSB\uc2  confirming the extreme turbulence caused fatal injuries to a passenger on board.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s not rare to have serious injuries, broken bones, people being jostled about, but to have someone actually die means that something traumatic really must have happened.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Meanwhile the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  is investigating and the manufacturer of the jet says it will fully support and provide assistance to all authorities as needed.\par \par \uc2 It comes after yet another midair incident last week.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230307ej3600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for March 4, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 5,945 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:19]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2  , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICS NATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: A stark contrast.\par \par \uc2 Right now, my mind is on Selma, Alabama where residents are preparing to commemorate one of the most pivotal events of the Black freedom struggle.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, I will be joining you live from Selma, where I will be taking part in the 58th Anniversary of Bloody Sunday in my capacity as the head of the National Action Network.\par \par \uc2 President Biden will make his third visit to Selma as Commander-in-Chief and will call for new action to protect the voting rights activists risked their lives for back on March 7, 1965.\par \par \uc2 But as Democrats prepare to mark that solemn memorial and grace and affection, and reflection, Republicans will close out this year`s Conservative Political Action Conference with more of the cartoon conservatism that has been on display for the last four days, with tonight`s keynote speech coming from none other than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , scheduled to take the stage within the hour.\par \par \uc2 The contrast is stark and emblematic of the divide between the parties right now broader than ever, and the same lack of seriousness driving conservative rage at CPAC is reflected by Republicans on Capitol Hill, in the nation`s State Houses and in Governor`s Mansions as they waged a war on social progress, which they demonize politically as woke, while economic lifelines like the enhanced food assistance benefits that expired this week after helping millions of Americans weather the pandemic, those lifelines are now allowed to languish without plans to replace them.\par \par \uc2 What Republicans do appear to have plans for are cuts to safety net programs like food assistance, even as millions of Americans are paying more to eat than they have in decades.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Congressman Steven Hosford, Democrat of Nevada, and chair of the \uc2 Congressional Black Caucus\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Congressman, Mr. Chairman, thank you for joining us tonight. We`ll both be in Selma tomorrow; you as a US congressman and chair of the Black Caucus; myself as head of NAN commemorating those that risked and lost their lives for voting rights on March 7, 1965 at the Edmund Pettus Bridge.\par \par \uc2 Nearly three generations later, voting rights in America specifically access for people of color remain under attack by conservative State Legislators with lawmakers in at least 32 States having pre-filed or introduced 150 restrictive voting bills by the end of January, at least 27 election interference bills introduced in 10 States in that same time, according to the Brennan Center for Justice.\par \par \uc2 You`re in the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  sharing a Black Caucus whose constituents has always been disproportionately affected by voter suppression attempts. How would you characterize the state of voting rights in 2023 ahead of tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE HORSFORD (D-NV): It is critical. Reverend Al, thank you for having me on.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Congressional Black Caucus\uc2  is here on the ground with you. We will see you tomorrow. I`m here in Montgomery, Alabama, with many of my colleagues today. We were over in Selma earlier today, and we`ll be back there tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But let me just be absolutely clear. As you stated, voting rights is among the top of the agenda for the \uc2 Congressional Black Caucus\uc2  , as it has been, because of the fundamental protections that it guarantees, but as your viewers rightfully understand and know, too many States have made it harder for people to vote. They put restrictions and suppression tactics in place and that is why the \uc2 Congressional Black Caucus\uc2  is working hard to make sure that we protect that fundamental right.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:08]\par \par \uc2 HORSFORD: And this is the first time in 40 years that Republicans have not worked with Democrats, in order to reauthorize some of those most fundamental protections.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, this week, the pandemic era boost added benefits under the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program, it came to an end, every SNAP recipient now stands to see about $100.00 less per month for food. For some households, it`ll be $250.00 or more at a time when food insecurity has risen, notably in the United States and disproportionately in Black communities that are already three times more likely to experience hunger, according to Federal data.\par \par \uc2 It also comes out as some leading House Republicans are using their majority to explore broader cuts to Federal food assistance and with that, of course, the Federal budget.\par \par \uc2 But what does the end of these expanded benefits mean for folks in your Las Vegas district?\par \par \uc2 HORSFORD: Well, for children and families that rely on SNAP benefits in my district in Nevada and all across the country, it means food insecurity. It means literally not having the ability to have the basics that they need to take care of themselves and to have food on the table.\par \par \uc2 For children, it means going to school hungry. It means them not having the quality of life that they desperately need. We hear from our constituents all the time how Democrats delivered for them. We put people over politics by passing these benefits, and now Republicans in Congress want to take those resources away or to cut them back, and that is yet another thing.\par \par \uc2 I`m issuing a letter along with the Congressional Hispanic and Asian Caucus with the Black Caucus speaking about the need to protect these benefits, and it is one of the things that we`re here in Montgomery and Selma to showcase.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Terri Sewell\uc2  , who represents this district, has been hard hit just after the horrific hurricane that hit the city. They need help. That`s what Congresswoman Sewell said and that is what the \uc2 Congressional Black Caucus\uc2  will continue to fight for.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, as we await Donald Trump`s speech closing out this year`s Conservative Political Action Conference, and despite all of the talk that CPAC no longer has the influence it once did on the GOP and all the analysis suggesting that Trump`s grip on the GOP has slipped as he runs for the presidency this third time.\par \par \uc2 What do you take from the fact that he still has top billing at what is still one of the most prominent annual gatherings for conservatives?\par \par \uc2 HORSFORD: Well, sadly, what it means is that the Republican Party is controlled by the MAGA wing, the extreme MAGA wing that is represented by the Marjorie Taylor Greene`s and the Matt Gaetz`s that continue to deny the results of the last election, that do not want to protect democracy, that have undermined our ability to protect people`s fundamental right to vote.\par \par \uc2 And now, they want to literally cut things like Social Security, Medicare veterans benefits, hurt families who are on SNAP. Look, we know that the choices you started with, the contrast could not be more clear. It is not about the election in 2024; however, it is about what we are doing every single day right now to deliver for constituents in our districts across this country. That`s what the \uc2 Congressional Black Caucus\uc2  is focused on. That`s what our members are focused on and that is what the American people want us to focus on.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, two developments in the fight over reproductive rights. The pharmacy chain, \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  , announcing that it will no longer supply abortion pills in nearly two dozen States where Republican Attorney Generals have objected.\par \par \uc2 This, along with a looming Federal Court decision in Texas, deciding whether a key drug used in medication abortions will be halted after lawsuit from antiabortion groups and physicians.\par \par \uc2 What can be done federally to maintain access to these abortion drugs before they`re out of reach for millions of women?\par \par \uc2 HORSFORD: We need to codify the protections of Roe through the House Democratic supported Women`s Freedom for Health Care Act, something that Representative Chu and other members have led on and I am a cosponsor for it.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:04]\par \par \uc2 HORSFORD: This would protect women`s fundamental right to make their own healthcare decisions about their body, to make sure that those freedoms continue to be available. I am concerned with companies like \uc2 Walmart\uc2  taking away these products that women need in order to decide for themselves, how to have a family, when to have a family, and in States like Nevada, that protect a woman`s right, we are literally taking away the resources that women need, and this does disproportionately affect Black women who need to have these freedoms and these protections codified in Federal law.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Before we lose you, President Biden took heat from a policing reform situation from police reform advocates, and a number of House Democrats while he signaled that he will not veto, but rather sign the House GOP resolution blocking changes to criminal punishments in the district`s criminal code.\par \par \uc2 The first time in more than three decades, the Congress is expected to overturn a DC local law. Where do you come down on that?\par \par \uc2 HORSFORD: I believe in local control. I believe that DC residents and their government should be able to decide for themselves what they want to pass and especially the District of Columbia that does not have the representation, because they are not approved as a State, which is something that I also support.\par \par \uc2 I hope that my colleagues in the Senate would hear the needs of the residents in DC as they take up these resolutions that were passed by House Republicans. But ultimately, I believe in local control and DC residents and their government making their own decisions.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, Chairman Steven Horsford, thank you, and I`ll see you tomorrow in Selma.\par \par \uc2 All of you at home, make sure you watch POLITICS NATION tomorrow, with the Sunday show will be live from Selma, Alabama at 5:00 PM Eastern following the 58th Anniversary of Bloody Sunday.\par \par \uc2 I will march over the Edmund Pettus Bridge along with President Biden and others. Then on POLITICS NATION, I will ask and host \uc2 Keisha Lance Bottoms\uc2  , senior White House adviser; as well as \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  , III, Congresswoman \uc2 Terri Sewell\uc2  , and the Reverend \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  , all of us from Selma tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, today former President Trump is set to speak just minutes from now at the conservative CPAC conference in Washington. My political panel is standing by to talk about the Republican field for 2024 ahead.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague, Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, a very good Saturday to you.\par \par \uc2 Some stories we`re watching this hour: Authorities in Frederick, Maryland are on the scene of an overturned tanker truck that crashed and caught fire this afternoon damaging nearby homes and vehicles. Crews say the fire is under control and hazardous materials were contained before they reached a nearby creek.\par \par \uc2 The driver of the truck was killed. No other injuries have been reported.\par \par \uc2 At least seven people are dead after a series of severe storms tearing through the Southern United States. Deadly storms and high winds, and even a handful of tornadoes led to widespread power outages from Texas to Tennessee and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 That weather system has taken a turn toward the northeast now where forecasters expect it to dump heavy snows on Northern New England by the end of this week.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, author and spiritual adviser \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2  kicking off her second presidential bid earlier this afternoon. Williamson is the first Democrat to enter the 2024 presidential race officially. She says her candidacy is challenging the system, not incumbent President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 More POLITICS NATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:18:44]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Tomorrow, I will be in Selma, Alabama for the 58th anniversary of Bloody Sunday.\par \par \uc2 It is hard to believe just three years ago, I walked the Edmund Pettus Bridge for the last time with by late friend, Georgia Congressman \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  , who was there at the original March in 1965 at the beginning of a long life of public service. He had already been an organizer and a freedom writer.\par \par \uc2 But back in March of 2020, the pandemic hadn`t even started. George Floyd was still alive and the 2020 election was still months away. A lot has changed since then. But at the same time, we are still fighting some of the same battles that were raging when Congressman Lewis was still alive.\par \par \uc2 President Biden will be on hand tomorrow to call for new protections of voting rights that are under attack in many States across the country. Only now, a proposed bill defending those rights carries John`s name.\par \par \uc2 The Black history that \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  made on that fateful Sunday in 1965, as he and others endured beatings at the hands of police as they fought for civil and voting rights is now under assault by race-baiting politicians who want to erase the African-American experiences from our schools, which is why I travel to Florida this month in my capacity as head of National Action Network to call out Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 [17:20:23]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  won`t be with us physically tomorrow as we cross the Alabama river yet again, but he is still marching alongside us in spirit. As long as we are rising up and starting good trouble, John`s memory will live on.\par \par \uc2 Please join us tomorrow for a special POLITICS NATION. I`ll be joined by \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  , III; Representative \uc2 Terri Sewell\uc2  of Alabama, and the Reverend \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:26]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICS NATION.\par \par \uc2 You`re looking at live pictures from CPAC, the Conservative Political Action Conference wrapping up outside of DC this afternoon. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is expected to take the stage to deliver the keynote address at any moment.\par \par \uc2 But in the last hour, Trump won a straw poll with 62 percent of the votes, his closest challenger was Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who scored 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now to talk about it is my political panel, Maura Gillespie, former aide to former Republican Representative \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  ; and Michael Hardaway, former staffer to Democratic Senator Dick Durbin, and Minority Leader \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 CPAC has traditionally been a showcase for Republican presidential hopefuls. Let`s start off with Trump`s presidential campaign, which was announced in mid-November and has been off to a rocky start as the former President has dealt with self-inflicted scandals and legal investigations.\par \par \uc2 Trump has been doing a bit of reset in recent weeks. Last week, he visited Ohio after the train derailment there and he has proposed some unusual new policies, including a national contest to design futuristic freedom cities with massive skyscrapers and flying cars.\par \par \uc2 This question is for both of you. Is the third Trump campaign looking more serious than it did when he kicked off?\par \par \uc2 Maura, you first, then Michael?\par \par \uc2 MAURA GILLESPIE, FORMER AIDE TO FORMER REPUBLICAN REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  : It`s more serious, I would say no. I think this is about ego. We`ve seen it with a trip in the East Palestine train derailment, showing up there, it was a political move. It was self-serving.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, I think that`s what we`re going to keep getting is an ego-driven campaign by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michael?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HARDAWAY, , MAURA GILLESPIE, FORMER AIDE TO FORMER REPUBLICAN REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 ADAM KINZINGER\uc2  : I agree, he is not a serious candidate and this is about ego in my view.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at his actual policies, there`s no there, there. This just seems to be an exercise in self-promotion, and possibly get back for losing the last election.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now another Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley, gave a speech at CPAC yesterday and was heckled by some Trump supporters while she was taking pitches. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 [VIDEO CLIP PLAYS]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Other potential Republican presidential candidates, including former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  , and Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  decided not to attend CPAC at all. If you`re a Republican with presidential aspirations not named Trump, is it better to show up or skip it? Maura, you first, then Michael.\par \par \uc2 GILLESPIE: CPAC used to be important. It used to be relevant. And now it`s just a side note for people in the DC political bubble to talk about, and that is really where it is now and I think you`re seeing that with the minimal attendance.\par \par \uc2 I thought that Nikki Haley attending was a misstep, because we do need thoughtful leadership and it is great to see a woman running for the highest office in the land. I just don`t see how this was a positive for her to attend and kind of been to the portion of our party that shows up at CPAC. It is a Trump best. And unfortunately, she did. So I was disappointed.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michael?\par \par \uc2 HARDAWAY: No serious individual needs to show up to CPAC. It currently is a circus, and that \uc2 Matt Schlapp\uc2  is PT Barnum.\par \par \uc2 By the way, it is interesting to me that \uc2 Matt Schlapp\uc2  who has been accused of sexual assault is still hosting this event and none of these Republican candidates have been made to answer for their participation, notwithstanding those charges. And so I find that interesting, but I will say that CPAC today is a complete joke and no one who truly is a serious candidate should show up to that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: CPAC grew into a major event for Republicans as the party veered to the right during the Trump years, but now that the right wing appears split between those who still love the former President and those who want to move on, Maura, does CPAC still matter?\par \par \uc2 [17:30:10]\par \par \uc2 GILLESPIE: I think the lack of attendance, and it being mostly a Trump supported event does tell us that maybe and again, I`m being an optimist here, but maybe we`re seeing the light that we have to move on and move beyond \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , and that`s my hope with CPAC this year, but I`m not -- again, like most people at home, they`re probably not paying attention to what`s happening at CPAC and that`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 We need to move forward, and move beyond this fringe of the party.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michael?\par \par \uc2 HARDAWAY: CPAC still matters for the far right. I think no moderate Republican is paying attention to what`s happening this week, but the far right has essentially turned CPAC into one very large Trump rally, and so it matters for them.\par \par \uc2 But in its grand scope of whether CPAC helps decide the Republican candidate, it`s irrelevant.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, I think that clearly, we`re going to see later, former President Trump and what he has to say, and I`m sure to many of us, it will not matter.\par \par \uc2 We`re looking at him live right now taking the stage and clapping for himself, although he`s probably trying to act like he`s clapping for the audience, but he is clapping for himself as he always does.\par \par \uc2 Maura Gillespie and Michael Hardaway, thank you for coming.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a break.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a new push for reparations on the Federal level. I will talk with two faith leaders from different religions, about why they`ve come together to call on President Biden to take action.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:36:38]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICS NATION.\par \par \uc2 This week, a group of more than 200 faith leaders from across the country issued a challenge to the Biden administration, calling on the White House to establish a commission to study reparations before Juneteenth this year.\par \par \uc2 Two of those are joining me now.\par \par \uc2 Bishop Vashti Murphy McKenzie of the National Council of Churches, and Rabbi Jonah Pesner of the Religious Action Center of Reform Judaism. Thank you both for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been hearing more discussion about reparations since the George Floyd protest of 2020, but mostly at the state and local level. Why are you pushing for Federal action now?\par \par \uc2 Let me go first to you Bishop McKenzie, and then you, Rabbi Pesner.\par \par \uc2 BISHOP VASHTI MURPHY MCKENZIE OF THE NATIONAL COUNCIL OF CHURCHES: Well, I would say, why not? Because we don`t have great confidence that this Congress is going to really act on HR 40. That`s a piece of legislation that asked for a Commission to study reparations and make recommendations not only about slavery, but also the heirs of enslavement, the unjust policies and practices that happened in our country, and so that was back in 1989.\par \par \uc2 So why not? Now is the time.\par \par \uc2 And so we need an executive order to push this process ahead, especially while cities like Evanston, Newport News, Detroit, now the State of California have reparations on the agenda and actually have reparations in practice. Now is the time.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And Rabbi, as you get ready to give your response, I can say Bishop that I know Congresswoman Sheila Jackson Lee , who is pushing HR 40 has also asked the President to sign an executive order pushing that.\par \par \uc2 Rabbi Pesner, why are you as well?\par \par \uc2 RABBI JONAH PESNER, DIRECTOR, RELIGIOUS ACTION CENTER OF REFORM JUDAISM: Well, first of all, great to see my dear friend, Bishop Vashti McKenzie and great to be back with you, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 I agree with Bishop McKenzie, what took us so long? Why not yesterday? Why not -- we have 400 years of enslaved African people, we have 400 years of systemic racism, we have 400 years of voter suppression and Jim Crow; if not now, when, as we say in the Jewish tradition.\par \par \uc2 For us as Jews, the children of the enslavement of the ancient Hebrews, it is personal. It says in the Book of Exodus, the text you both know very well, that as the Israelites were coming out of Egypt, they took possessions from the Egyptians with them, gold and silver and refinements, and the Rabbis teach, this was not theft, but rather, this was reparations for the 300 years of their enslavement.\par \par \uc2 Later, in our tradition, the Rabbis debate, Hillel and Shammai, what to do when you discover that a house has been built with a stolen beam and the great Rabbi Shammai says, well, you`ve got to tear the house down and give the beam back. And Rabbi Hillel says, well, you don`t tear it down, but you at least have to compensate the person from whom that beam was stolen.\par \par \uc2 Folks, we have 400 years of reparations to confront, because folks who are the descendants of African slaves had their lives, their bodies, their wealth, their livelihood, all stolen from them, and it was compounded year- over-year by systemic racism and Jim Crow. So, if not now, when, Reverend Al?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I might add that this is not the first time you`ve taken hard positions, Rabbi. You joined me in calling on a Ministers March on Washington about three or four years ago and raised some of these issues.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But reparations proposals have languished in Congress for decades as was said, but we are still -- we are seeing developments in some States and communities as the Bishop mentioned.\par \par \uc2 California`s Reparations Task Force just wrapped up a two-day public hearing. We`ve spoken several times on the show with Chairman Camilla Moore about the serious study of systemic discrimination being carried out by that panel. Recently, the City Council in Boston voted to study how reparations might work in their city.\par \par \uc2 What do you think these communities are taking action now first, Bishop, and then you, Rabbi.\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: I think we need to strike while the iron is hot. Since the death of George Floyd or since the murder of George Floyd, this has become a topic that has moved forward, but one of the things I think, Reverend Al, that we need to understand is that reparation comes in several forms.\par \par \uc2 There is satisfaction, truth seeking, secession of violence, recovery, reburial of remains, and there`s restitution. That`s what we talk about restoring survivors to their original situation. There is also compensation, that is a monetary event for any economic accessible damage. And then there`s rehabilitation, which we don`t talk about a lot -- medical and psychological care and legal and social service.\par \par \uc2 And then there is the guarantee of non-repetition, which lends to policy. That means reform, judicial reform, law enforcement reform. We need to take a look at all of these forms of reparation when we say we need to have it now, we need a Commission to this study, and we need sure and complete remedies for the harm that has been caused.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rabbi?\par \par \uc2 PESNER: I agree with Bishop McKenzie once again. I would also acknowledge the Reform Jewish Movement, which I`m honored to lead, passed a resolution in 2019, calling for the passage of HR 40, which by the way, is just a resolution to call for the study of reparations. It doesn`t even say what reparations look like.\par \par \uc2 If we can`t even start a conversation about how we would compensate the descendants of African slaves, what -- you know, what`s holding us back?\par \par \uc2 So in 2019, we became the first major Jewish denomination organization to pass that call. It was before the lynching of George Floyd. So we`ve been at this conversation a while.\par \par \uc2 And I would also say, as a Rabbi and as a Jew whose family and descendants were the victims of the Holocaust, the Shoah, we understand the role of reparations because there was the movement for the \uc2 German government\uc2  to do reparations for the Jewish community in light of what the Nazis had done to the Jews.\par \par \uc2 The United States has its own precedent because of the reparations that were paid to the families who were in the internment camps in Japanese- Americans during World War Two. So there is certainly precedent for this.\par \par \uc2 The HR 40 just calls for the study of reparations. We`re asking President Biden in the letter that my colleague, Bishop McKenzie, organized of 200 faith leaders, which I was proud to sign, and many of our colleagues were proud to sign just calling on the Biden administration to do this by executive order. Because, I mean, folks, let`s be honest, this HR 40 gets reintroduced every single session of Congress, and for some reason, Reverend Al, it doesn`t get passed. So let`s just get it done.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Bishop McKenzie, your group cited the erasure of Black history as one of the reasons you`re calling for action -- Reparations Action.\par \par \uc2 This is an important topic I recently traveled to Florida in my capacity as head of National Action Network to demonstrate against Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  ` ban of a high school African-American Studies course. Many faith leaders joined .\par \par \uc2 Talk about how these two issues are connected, Bishop?\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Well, you know, Reverend Al, this is really an important subject. When you erase a person`s history, when you try to wipe them off the face of the earth, you are trying to not only erase their contributions, but actually who they are.\par \par \uc2 We have to remember that back in the 1800s -- let`s say the end of enslavement, Emancipation Proclamation, more than one-half of the economic assets in the United States were enslaved persons. There is no other -- there is not one single area in our country, whether it is business, whether it`s commerce, whether it`s inventions, where Africans did not make a wonderful contribution. It is written there.\par \par \uc2 So you can`t erase our history. You can`t say they were just indentured servants who were brought to this country, who had housing and food, that is absolutely not true.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:05]\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: We were brought here against our will. We survived the Middle Passage and have survived every -- absolutely everything that was meant to destroy us and dehumanize us.\par \par \uc2 And so no, we need to teach Black history, which is American history, so that we can understand the contributions that we made. We were just not sitting on the sidelines. We were not just cattle chattel. We were absolutely active participants in building this country and that needs to be taught not only to Black children, but every child of every ethnic group that makes up the American population.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rabbi, on Thursday, a man was charged with using \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to threaten to kill Michigan State government officials who are Jewish. One of those targets being Michigan Attorney General Dana Nessel.\par \par \uc2 The American Defamation League found that antisemitic incidents in the US have reached an all-time high in recent years and overall hate crimes continued to rise in major American cities last year with police data showing that at least six metropolitan areas recorded levels not seen since the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 How can a national dialogue on reparations help address this rise in hate speech and hate violence?\par \par \uc2 PESNER: You know, Reverend Al, you know, I say this all the time, our safety as Jews, is in our solidarity with all the people who experience bigotry and violence and hate, whether it`s Black and brown folks, Asian, Asian-American, the AAPI community, LGBTQIA folks, unfortunately, Muslim community, unfortunately, there has been a real targeting of Jews. And you and I saw this when we were marching together after Charlottesville, when they chanted "Jews will not replace us." Right?\par \par \uc2 We know there`s a direct link between White supremacy, this kind of nostalgia from the Confederacy and to keep systemic racism, lock stock and antisemitism, the hatred of Jews.\par \par \uc2 So I know that our safety is in our solidarity when we bring together people across all kinds of lines and I also believe that our redemption as a nation may yet come through a multiracial, multifaith, and inclusive democracy in which every voice is heard because every vote is actually counted, which is why I`m glad you started this show tonight with voting rights.\par \par \uc2 I know you`re going to be in Selma tomorrow. Let`s not forget that Goodman, Chaney, and Schwerner gave their lives registering voters, let`s not forget them.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Two Jews and a Black -- Goodman, Chaney, and Schwerner -- two Jews and a Black.\par \par \uc2 PESNER: Amen. Amen. Because they understood what we understand now, which is the forces of bigotry, antisemitism, anti-Jewish hatred, anti-Black racism, and all these forms of hate, they are in the kind of unholy alliance, and we have to rise up together, both to keep each other safe, and stand in solidarity and to make this the inclusive, just multiracial democracy, that we know we can be.\par \par \uc2 The unfinished business of reparations is saying, we have to actually atone for our past and be the kind of country that we can be proud of.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Last question, last year, a dozen Civil Rights and religious groups similarly sent a letter to the White House calling on President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to sign an executive order to study reparations by Juneteenth, and he evidently hasn`t taken action.\par \par \uc2 Why do you think this time will be different? First to the Bishop, then to the Rabbi? Let`s be brief, I`m out of time.\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Election, election, election 2024, 2024. Being able to go into the election with some wins, because there were some promises made the last time. Some of the promises have been kept, but there are still promises yet to go.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rabbi?\par \par \uc2 PESNER: Amen. Bishop.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you both Rabbi McKenzie and Rabbi Pesner.\par \par \uc2 PESNER: Mazel Tov, Rabbi McKenzie.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Bishop McKenzie, both of you preaching for us tonight. Thank you both for being on.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 MCKENZIE: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:53:54]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Today is Women`s History Month as we are in our fourth day of the month and I think about a woman named Amelia Boykin who was a resident of Selma and was the reason that the activist and civil rights leaders of their time came to Selma at her behest, after the killing in Marion, Alabama, not far from Selma, of Jimmie Lee Jackson, saying we`ve got to do something about the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  and others heeded her cry and that`s what led to them going to Selma. And it was on that Sunday, March 7th, that headed across that bridge, staff members of \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  , Jr., Jose Williams, \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  , who was then leading the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee were teargassed and beaten and a hundred Blacks walking behind them ran across that bridge for their lives, and Amelia Boykin that Black woman, citizen of Selma, laid on the ground being teargassed.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:07]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we march tomorrow, we are marching for Amelia Boykin, who fought many decades later. And I remember going across that bridge behind her even in a wheelchair, next to Congresswoman \uc2 Terri Sewell\uc2  , dreaming and believing that the bill they got passed in `65 would be upheld and voting rights protected.\par \par \uc2 I remember in 2020, as we were marching across that bridge, we were told that \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  couldn`t make it that year because he was suffering from what would later cause his death, and as we got to the top of the bridge, a car pulled up, and \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  stepped out and I was honored to help hold him up as he spoke to us one last time.\par \par \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  and Amelia Boykin won`t be there tomorrow on a bridge still named after a former member of the \uc2 Ku Klux Klan\uc2  , Edmund Pettus, but I`ll be there, President Biden will be there, others will be there, \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  , \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  , III, and others, because we don`t want to forget that the right to vote is sacred, dipped in the blood of Blacks and Whites and Jews and Gentiles that fought to give us that right.\par \par \uc2 On Women`s History Month. I remember Amelia Boykin, and more important, I`m going to do what she would expect we will continue doing.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow from Selma, Alabama at 5:00 PM Eastern following the 58th Anniversary of Bloody Sunday.\par \par \uc2 I will march over the Edmund Pettus Bridge alongside President Obama, then on POLITICS NATION, I will host \uc2 Keisha Lance Bottoms\uc2  , senior White House adviser, as well as \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  , III; Congresswoman \uc2 Terri Sewell\uc2  , and the Reverend \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  ,\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 [18:00:46]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020230305ej3400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for March 3, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,112 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. MALLORY MCMORROW (D-MI): I have nothing but time and I`m going to use all of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: She says she got time. Jahan -- she won the week. Ja`han Jones -- and De La Soul -- thank you very much. I appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN" with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voice-over): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT GAETZ\uc2  (R-FL): We have been taking whistleblower testimony from brave patriots.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Breaking news on those so-called whistleblowers.\par \par \uc2 REP. DANIEL GOLDMAN (D-NY): I mean, of the dozens of whistleblowers you have already talked to, that came to talk to your staff.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): Yes, what do you want me to turn over there?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, the Republican stunt committee to launder an insurrection goes poof. The ranking Democrat on the Weaponization Committee joins me live.\par \par \uc2 Then, you know Fox News is a problem when they lose the room at CPAC.\par \par \uc2 STEVE BANNON\uc2  , TRUMP ADVISER: They don`t respect you. Read the depositions. The deposition they have a fear, a loathing and contempt for you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And as \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  takes legal abortion drugs off the shelves, how a right-wing judge in Texas could eliminate reproductive rights for every woman in the country.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAVITA PATEL, NBC NEWS MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: We have had safety standards that are now being undermined by complete lies and misinformation.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. So, Republicans have now been in control of the House for about two months. They have what`s beyond a shockingly little to show for it. They barely eked out the, House Majority, following the most disappointing Midterm results for an out of power party in decades. A midterm that was so disappointing, I would argue, many others have, largely because Republicans focus so intently on fringe Fox News culture war issues.\par \par \uc2 And now, their governing agenda is focused almost exclusively on fringe Fox News cultural war issues. For instance, do they have a serious credible plan to tackle inflation? No, of course not. Have they even pretended to have one? Not as far as I can tell. They`re focused on \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and whether or not we should cut off aid to Ukraine. And I have to admit, the Democrats who are now unburdened from the responsibilities of unified government, which are difficult, are having some fun.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  of Georgia to task, who`s the face of the Republican Party, for a spurious claim she made about overdose deaths.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  and a few more, you`re going to have a lot of Republicans running our way. Isn`t she amazing? The reason -- she was -- she was very specific -- I shouldn`t regress probably. I`ve read -- she was very specific recently saying that a mom, a poor mother who lost two kids to fentanyl, that I killed her sons.\par \par \uc2 Well, the interesting thing is that fentanyl they took came during the last administration. Look, folks -- anyway, I don`t want to get started.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, you kind of wanted to get started, didn`t you? Credit where it`s due, it`s probably a smart political strategy for Democrats to highlight and elevate \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  at every possible turn from now until 2024. Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  also went on a very satisfying terror this week after his colleague \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  used a rather worn and obnoxious Republican verbal tick favored by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): The gentle lady was making a grammatical error that I heard some of her colleagues make before. I believe she referred to a Democrat solution. I heard another member talk about a Democrat member and a Democrat plan. I just wanted to educate our distinguished colleagues that Democrat is the noun. When you use it as an adjective, you say the Democratic member or the Democratic solution, or the Democratic plan. And so, I assume it`s a good-faith grammatical error the first few times.\par \par \uc2 But after people were corrected several times and they continue to say it, it seems like it`s an act of incivility as if every time we mentioned the other party, it just came out with a kind of political speech impediment like the Banana Republican party. As if we were to say that every time we mentioned the Banana Republican member or the Banana Republican plan or the Banana Republican conference, but we wouldn`t do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You could kind of tell he`d been holding that one in for a while. There was also that moment a few weeks ago when freshman New York Democrat Dan Goldman, part of the new Republican-led committee on the alleged weaponization of the government, pushed the committee chair of that committee, Jim Jordan, on the dozens, he said, of secret whistleblowers that Jordan claims exist without offering any evidence.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:02]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: You talk about dozens of whistleblowers. When are we going to get that information?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: When they -- when they testify -- when we work with -- I`ll work with the ranking member on that.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: You don`t have any transcriptions of their interviews?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: We have the first one and we have the dozens who come and talk to our office.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: They talk to your office privately?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: They talked to Republican staff, right?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: And they`re not transcribed, no notes, no nothing?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: He first one happened Tuesday --\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: No, no, I`m not talking about the first one. I`m talking about the dozens.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: So, it happens Tuesday. The next one happens tomorrow. The third one happens next Wednesday, and we`ll continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: You just said dozens. Do have notes from those or they`re just talking to your staff?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Chairman.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Are there dozens, Jim? Really, dozens? So that exchange happened last month. And the reason we played it for you, it`s notable, because since then, the committee has had testimony from a few of those so-called whistleblowers behind closed doors. Republicans claim that these men who are all former or suspended \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents have inside information about anti- conservative bias among federal law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 And I got to say, to take a step back for one moment, right, the idea in the abstract that there are corrupt abuses of power among the intelligence or national security law enforcement agencies in the \uc2 United States Federal Government\uc2  seems perfectly plausible to me. In fact, look at the history of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  . There`s been a pretty long record of that in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , OK.\par \par \uc2 So, in the abstract, not a crazy contention. The devil is in the details though. Are these really credible folks? According to a new 300-plus page report from the Democrats on the committee, Jim Jordan star witness don`t exactly seem to have the goods. So, one of them is a retired agent named George Hill. He once tweeted that the January 6 Insurrection was actually a setup, who claimed that the January 6th pipe bomb, remember that person that we have footage of planting those pipe bombs an