de the mayor`s veto, OK. The council elected by the citizens of the Washington, DC. And that should have been that except, except that`s not how it works in D.C. It`s how it works everywhere else. It`s how it works in West Virginia but not in the District of Columbia which is not a state and does not have the full Democratic autonomy that all the other states do.\par \par \uc2 In DC, Congress has the power to just sweep in and overturn the law. And guess what? That`s exactly what the Republican-controlled House voted to do last month. And now, with the help of a handful of Democratic Senators, including \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of West Virginia, the Senate is poised to do the same.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and the majority of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  are going to tell the residents of the District of Columbia who of course are the ones who will experience the effects of this law what is safest for them. They don`t get to tell West Virginia that you probably shouldn`t put a lot of guns on campus. That doesn`t work both in directions.\par \par \uc2 Now most people assumed that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who has been a vocal supporter of home rule and self-determination of the District of Columbia would veto this if it passes. But no, no, no. In a surprising and disappointing twist, yesterday, the President announced that while he supports D.C. statehood and home rule, he does not support some of the changes \uc2 D.C. Council\uc2  put forward. And if the Senate votes to overturn what D.C. council did, I`ll sign it.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:26]\par \par \uc2 Oh really? I guess \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  knows best what would be safest there. Biden`s decision reportedly blindsided members of the Congressional Black Caucus including DC`s non-voting delegate to Congress \uc2 Eleanor Holmes Norton\uc2  . She told reporters "This is news to me and I`m very disappointed." And she has every right to be disappointed and angry along with her 700,000 constituents because in 