cts presented by Dominion at this point, it`s really compelling. So, the bar is high. Dominion has to show that \uc2 Fox\uc2  acted with actual malice, which means that they acted with a reckless disregard for the truth.\par \par \uc2 And what you see, for example, in that correspondence you just cited, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  is basically admitting that it`s really reckless to --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Shockingly reckless.\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: It`s shockingly reckless and they`re saying this is B.S., and they left this paper trail.\par \par \uc2 What I think is really interesting is where was legal in all of this. I mean, even Laura Ingraham I think went to law school, and they`re leaving this whole paper trail, which means that they must have believed that they would never be held accountable to this. But there is such a damning paper trail from the top all the way to the bottom, not just one-row reporter or one-off statement. This is a business model. This is what they were doing for the purpose of making profit. And that`s what I think is compelling.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Not only did Laura Ingraham go to law school, she clerked on the Supreme Court of these United States for \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  . The lawyer who`s the head of Fox legal via den was also a very, very accomplished and decorated lawyer. He was up for a federal judgeship under \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  at one point, but yes, I think they didn`t do a great job.\par \par \uc2 It`s so funny, the shockingly reckless, I have expected to find a text from a Fox host saying, It`s like we`re acting with actual malice.\par \par \uc2 Asha Rangappa and Howard Dean, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, sometimes the story requires more digging to find the truth, have a little bit of just old fashioned shoe leather reporting burst and other right-wing misinformation bubble about accusations made against the Missouri Children`s Hospital.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:43:47]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Amidst a barrage of legislation being introduced and passed in states to restrict or outright ban, gender-affirming care for teens and in some cases, adults.\par \par \uc2 There was a bombshell story published earlier this month, they accused a pediatric gender clinic in Missouri of mistreating minors.\par \par \uc2 Now, the story is published in the \uc2 Free Press\uc2  . It`s a news website founded by a performatively contrarian former \uc2 New York Times\uc2  op-ed writer. The op- ed itself it wasn`t a reported piece, it was first person, it was authored by a woman named Jamie Reed, a self-described queer woman who says she is married to a trans man and who worked for years at Washington University`s Transgender Center at St. Louis Children`s Hospital.\par \par \uc2 Reid said is that the center pushed children into puberty blockers and hormone therapies without proper mental health assessment, without providing patients adequate counseling or information about side effects.\par \par \uc2 Reid also sent a 23-page sworn affidavit fold explosive accusations to Missouri Attorney General Andrew Bailey.\par \par \uc2 Now, the story had an immediate impact. Attorney General Bailey launched a multi-agency investigation into the center, Missouri Republican Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  announced his office would be investigating as well.\par \par \uc2 Now, I read the piece when it was published, two things immediately came to mind. First, that this was not, you know, an anonymous allegation, this person was named there on the record, a sworn affidavit, so that counts for something.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:11]\par \par \uc2 And that it`s entirely possible that there are clinics or doctors in this country who are doing a bad job of administering gender affirming care, they`re not doing it properly.\par \par \uc2 Now, if that`s the case and it likely is somewhere in this country, it`s not an argument itself for banning that care, right? We can all concede there are public school teachers for instance in this country who are not doing great job, even doing a bad job, and we`re not going to outlaw public school.\par \par \uc2 The second thought I had as I read this account, which was unnerving, right, is that if this is true, if what this person is saying is true, then other people know about it, right? It is likely there`ll be several, if not many other people who are going to come forward and corroborate it with some additional reporting. OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, now we have this piece from the St. Louis Post Dispatch. This piece is based on interviews with almost two dozen parents of children who were seen at the clinic, not just one person, who say their experiences were essentially the opposite of what Reed described.\par \par \uc2 Contrary to Reed`s claims that children were rushed into irreversible treatments, "parents reported a well-defined step-by-step approach that could be halted at any time. Slow methodical adjustments began at home long before medications were used, testing out new names using, different pronouns, cutting hair shorter, growing it long."\par \par \uc2 The social transitions ran concurrently with mental health care, sometimes lasting years. Only then the parents said was medication considered.\par \par \uc2 None of them describe the kind of shocking sort of drive by treatment that was painted in Reed`s op-ed of the center.\par \par \uc2 Right now, as Missouri lawmakers are considering a proposed legislation that would ban gender-affirming care for minors, all of it, right? In a debate, which is intensified following Reed`s op-ed. Here`s how one St. Louis teenager Chloe Clark described their experience at the Transgender Center.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHLOE CLARK, RECEIVED TREATMENT AT THE TRANSGENDER CENTER: In middle school, I was presenting male mostly, but I recognized towards the end that I was trans female, or that`s what I thought at the time. Now I recognize that I`m nonbinary.\par \par \uc2 I think that the center is incredibly, like an incredibly good center. All the people who I talked to, they were both incredibly kind and incredibly helpful throughout this process. They gave me support, they talked about all these different options, it was really helpful.\par \par \uc2 I do think that the Missouri state legislature is acting like it`s just the kids making these decisions without any input from the parents. There`s -- I had to negotiate with my parents, I had to talk to them and it took some convincing despite the fact that they`re incredibly accepting people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I would urge everyone, everyone, please, whether it`s this piece or the next one that stokes panic about kids supposedly being pressured into being transgender, put on medication too early. Just take a beat. Consider it carefully before accepting it as gospel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:52:43]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  , second largest pharmacy chain in the country has announced it will not sell abortion pills in 20 states including at least four are where, listen to this, abortion still legal. Alaska, Florida, Iowa and Montana.\par \par \uc2 That decision is going to hurt a lot of people but it potentially could get far worse, with a ruling expected any day on a case that challenges the FDA`s approval of one of the pills used in medical abortion.\par \par \uc2 Now, that decision we made by Judge Kacsmaryk, that`s him this guy. He`s an anti-abortion Trump appointed federal judge in Amarillo, Texas. They filed with him to sort of form shops so that he would have the case. They know what they`re going to get because despite all the conservative talk about things like state rights, anti-abortion activists don`t want anyone in any state to be able to have an abortion. They say it`s murder.\par \par \uc2 They also want to keep you from even finding out about abortion as Jessica Valenti writes in a newsletter Abortion, Every Day, a new Texas bill would force internet providers, seriously, to block access to any website that carries information about abortion medication or tells women how to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 And Jessica Valenti joins me now. It`s great to have you here. I`ve said this before on air and I will say it again, the work that you`re doing under \uc2 Substack\uc2  is the most essential compendium of the post-Dobbs moment.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA VALENTI, WRITER, ABORTION, EVERY DAY NEWSLETTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Anywhere, anywhere. Thank you for doing it.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s talk first about \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  . It does seem like they just got successfully bullied. I don`t think they necessarily want to do this. But they got steamrolled by these Republican A.G.s.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: Yes, it was a complete intimidation that campaign that worked, they caved. First, they came to Kansas, we saw it a week or so ago. And now they`re caving to everyone, right? Even in states as you said, where abortion is legal. They`re saying, you know what, we don`t want to deal with it. We don`t want to deal with the pressure, not just from these A.G.s, but anti-abortion groups also are launching harassment campaigns against CVS, \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  , any pharmacy that is considering carrying abortion medication.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We should be clear that this -- the two drugs that are used for medical abortion are two of the most studied drugs in the entire country, as you would expect, because they have been so controversial.\par \par \uc2 And also, they`re approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  . There`s no question about the safety here, right? It`s been 20 years that we`ve had these.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:02]\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: Yes, they`re completely safe. We`ve been using them for years and years and years. And the other thing that I think people miss, and I want to point out, this is also a medication that women use to complete miscarriages. Right.\par \par \uc2 And when you talk about limiting this medication, you are not just talking about limiting access to abortion, you are talking about making it impossible for women who are having miscarriages to get the care that they need.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, Adam Serwer`s quote, the cruelty is the point, that is what comes up again and again and again when you`re talking about what`s happening with abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And we have seen time and time again, in the post-Dobbs reality, women who have non-viable fetuses being forced to carry them until they`re in dire medical straits. And this is a live reality day in day out, we only hear this kind of like, it`s like the iceberg right above, we only hear a tiny percentage of people that, you know, want to go on the record, want to talk to press about the most intimate, awful thing that`s happened to them and you know --\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: We are willing to deal with the harassment from the right wing. I cannot tell you the number of women I have heard from who they just don`t want to go public, right?\par \par \uc2 They`re having these things happen to them, but they can see what`s happening online to the people who come forward, they can see the way that people are harassed the way that people`s stories are doubted or picked apart. They`re just not interested. And I can`t really blame them.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, there`s now -- so there`s that, right? So, they`ve already got - - and these are in the 20 states. The thing that looms over all this is the possibility of a Trump federal district judge who`s got a lawsuit that again, they sort of form a shop, they go to him, they file in Emerald, Texas.\par \par \uc2 I want to just read a quote of his just to give you -- this is from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  . He once wrote that the sexual revolution brought us a world where and I quote Judge Kacsmaryk here, marriage, sexuality, gender identity, and even unborn child must yield to the erotic desires of liberated adults.\par \par \uc2 So, I feel like you`ve got a sense of where he is on this.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: (INAUDIBLE) for a very specific reason.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, very specific. But the idea is that he can issue a nationwide injunction that like no one would be able to use a pill.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: Yes, people don`t -- I think a lot of people don`t understand that this is not just something that is going to impact anti-choice states. But every single state, it matters to all of us.\par \par \uc2 Now, hopefully, we will still be able to get access to that medication even if that decision comes down the wrong way. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  can still keep it on the market, even if they need to, like reevaluate and prove once more that it`s safe, even though we know it`s safe.\par \par \uc2 And doctors are thinking about, you know, just having a protocol for abortion medication just with that second pill, but it`s more uncomfortable. It`s more painful. It`s not quite as effective. Again, cruelty.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, the other thing that`s crazy here, right, is the legal uncertainty then becomes a kind of excuse or a leverage point for the pharmacies, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, like, yes, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  can keep it on, they can -- even if there`s a nationwide injunction.\par \par \uc2 Also, the legality of all this very unclear, it`s unclear whether it`d be like expedited review, etcetera, etcetera. But like, we`re seeing how \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  is dealing with intimidation by these forces anyway, like it gives them essentially leverage to be like, well, I don`t know if you should stop that.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: And that`s what they said about Kansas as well. They`re like, well, the law is sort of unclear in Kansas. No, it`s not. It`s not unclear, but they`re using any sort of conversation that they can, any sort of controversy that they can just to put their hands back and say, I don`t want to deal with that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And we don`t know when this is going to come down, it can come down any day.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: No, it can come down any day, any minute. We have no idea.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Another story that I think is fascinating, because it sort of -- we`ve seen this happen, right? So, post-Dobbs is sort of like leave it to the states -- leave it to states and then we`ve seen a bunch of states, both relatively conservative and fairly liberal, that have passed pro- abortion rights statewide, either referenda or (INAUDIBLE) laws.\par \par \uc2 I did not know this story until I read your indispensable \uc2 Substack\uc2  . In Mississippi, they`re talking about discussing about changing the ballot laws to do what?\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: They want to bring back ballot measures in Mississippi except for abortion. That`s the one -- that`s the one thing that you can`t change.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It is such a tell. So, they`re talking about bringing back ballot measures. But putting into the law that it doesn`t apply to abortion.\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: It doesn`t apply to abortion, you can`t. And they said specifically, the GOP there said specifically, we want to bring it back but we`re a little bit nervous that they are going to restore abortion rights because they know how popular abortion rights are even in red states. And so, we`re just going to add this provision in to keep that from happening.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They tried something like this in Ohio, where they floated this idea of moving the standard for ballot measures up to 60 percent. Because they wanted to protect the regime there. And they eventually had to back away under public pressure. We`ll see what happens in Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 Although to me, the tell here is that Republicans in Mississippi are worried they would lose a statewide referendum on abortion in Mississippi, says a lot about the politics of this when you give it to people. We`ll see how that continues to play out.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Valenti, who`s \uc2 Substack\uc2  you should look up and subscribe to, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:07]\par \par \uc2 VALENTI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN for the week. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230304ej3300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for March 3, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ellison Barber, Morgan Chesky, Symone Sanders- Townsend, Allie Raffa \par \uc2 7,969 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:00:24]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC HOST: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City. Alex Marquardt in the initial hours now of what will be a lifetime behind bars. The judge piling on during sentencing, calling the double murder, defense and assault on the judicial system. We`ve got a live report coming to you in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 Plus, reports that President Biden has now tapped some of the most successful democratic governors in the country to help lay the groundwork for his reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 And fury erupts again at a town hall in East Palestine. Residents whose patience is clearly being tested to the limit nearly a month after that toxic train crash.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIN BROCKOVICH, ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVIST: This has touched my family, this has touched my friends and it will touch me to the cellular level when I get diagnosed with cancer, ALS or whatever is going to come down the road if I stay in this contaminated, toxic town.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: More on that and the failure of \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  CEO to show up the second time he`s avoided coming face to face with those residents. But we begin in South Carolina with today`s dramatic sentencing of disgraced former lawyer Alex Marquardt. A little more than 15 hours after the jury went into deliberations, Marquardt found out he`ll serve two consecutive life sentences for the horrific murders of his wife and son.\par \par \uc2 That news delivered after Marquardt once again proclaimed his innocence. And the judge told him that many have received a death penalty probably for lesser conduct.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE CLIFTON NEWMAN, SOUTH CAROLINA CIRCUIT COURT: I know you have to see Paul and Maggie during the night times when you`re attempting to go to sleep. I`m sure they come and visit you. I`m sure.\par \par \uc2 ALEX MARQUARDT, CNN SENIOR NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: All day and every night.\par \par \uc2 NEWMAN: Yes. I`m sure. And they will continue to do so and reflect on the last time they looked you in the eyes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: This case was just overflowing with gruesome and horrific details for more than 75 witnesses and 800 pieces of evidence. Any appeal could take years. NBC`s Ellison Barber is outside the courthouse in South Carolina. Also with me, former U.S. attorney in South Carolina, Bill Nettles. And former prosecutor, David Henderson. Good to have all of you here. Ellison, I know you`ve been talking to people on the ground following this case so closely for us throughout emotions have to be high. What sticks out to you in all of this?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I mean, the prosecution very quickly said they feel like justice was served in this case that they agree with the jury. They think the evidence that they presented was overwhelming. And that`s why the verdict of guilty on all four counts was delivered and delivered so quickly. This jury deliberated for two hours and 51 minutes. That is not a very long time at all the defense.\par \par \uc2 They have already said that they plan to file an appeal. They say they feel like there are a number of things that they have grounds for appeal at both the state and federal level and say they will start that process filing. The paperwork for it in the coming days. The judge had a lot to say at this sentencing hearing. He talked about Alex Marquardt logging and he had a back and forth with him.\par \par \uc2 And the judge said to him about the lies after Alex said again that what he meant on the stand when he talked all the web of tails we weed, when he said he meant, I lied and I continued to lie. The judge said the question is, when will you stop?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEWMAN: And it might not have been you. It might have been the monster you become when you take 15, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60 opioid pills. Maybe you become another person. The person standing before me was not the person who committed the crime, those same individual.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: From everything we could see on the live feed of the sentencing hearing from everything that our colleague who was sitting in the courtroom watching closely saw Alex Marquardt did not look back once at his family who was in the courtroom after he received his sentence when he arrived in court today, Chris. It was a very different look than we have seen in the six weeks leading up to this.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 He was no longer in regular clothes, no longer in a button-down shirt, no tie, no coat. He was in a jumpsuit. A prison jumpsuit. You see him getting out there, his hands and feet both shackled. He typically comes in with his hands shackled, the feet, that was something different today. Other reporters as well as myself tried to ask him if he had anything to say, he did not. He walked in quietly. When he left he had nothing to say either.\par \par \uc2 His only remarks today were the ones we heard him repeat twice in court. I am innocent. He said I did not kill -- he said I did not hurt my wife, Maggie. I did not hurt Paul-Paul. The judge pushing back on that saying, essentially, the evidence here shows that you have lied, shows that you`re duplicitous. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, Bill, we`ve talked a lot on this program about the big gamble. And South Carolina Attorney General Alan Wilson said Marquardt taking the stand was probably the nail in the coffin. Do you think he`s right? I mean, one juror said it took only 45 minutes actually to come to a verdict. So obviously, it didn`t help him.\par \par \uc2 BILL NETTLES, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY IN SOUTH CAROLINA: Yes. I mean, I don`t -- I don`t -- in hindsight, obviously was the right thing, right? I mean, he went on the stand and had the jury believed him, we`d be having a different outcome here. So -- I mean, it hindsight, yes. He probably shouldn`t have taken the stand. At the time, you know, I can see the rationale for him taking the stand.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, that`s courthouse where he grew up. And, you know, at one time, I mean, it was almost a barometer of the -- of the sway they had over the community. I got to imagine, you know, five or six years ago, if he`d had gotten on the stand and testified. They would have probably -- they might have believed him. So, it was a gamble. And, you know, obviously, it didn`t pay off.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, David, do you see grounds for appeal?\par \par \uc2 DAVID HENDERSON, FORMER PROSECUTOR: Chris, I do not. And I think the easiest way for me to summarize my view of that, overall, is at one point, his son Buster tried to pass him a John Grisham novel during the trial. And I have to believe part of what he was thinking was, hey, Dad, I don`t know if you`ve read this but they`ve got a better trial strategy in this book than you all do. Maybe it`s about time you flip the script.\par \par \uc2 And I say that because we are going to see a motion for ineffective assistance of counsel as a basis for a new trial. I think he`s got a better chance of winning the lottery than he does of succeeding on that motion because they typically don`t assess it in terms of trial strategy. And the bulk of the evidence that was used to convict him here was introduced by him and his lawyers.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know why you thought it was a good idea to say that your housekeeper chipped and failed and died at your house which setting aside how implausible that is, you approached her children at their funeral and said, hey, let`s hustle the insurance company out of money. And you did it and you never gave them their check. You talked about hiring contract killers, you tried to blame the victim, who was your son.\par \par \uc2 And when you did that, you forgot most people are going to conclude that the apple doesn`t fall far from the tree. And I say all that to say that that motion for ineffective assistance of counsel, he acts it needs to fall against himself because there`s no way his lawyers went along with that. That was his idea. His lawyer said, you know what, he`s a lawyer. It`s his life. He wants to roll the dice. We`ll go with this trial strategy.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, I want to -- I mean, when you do talk about his housekeeper, it leads me to what the chief of sleds said today. That, of course, is the South Carolina Law Enforcement Agency. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK KEEL\uc2  , CHIEF OF SLED: Today is not the end. It`s the next step in a long road to justice for every person who has been victimized by Alex Marquardt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Bill, I think the references obviously to the financial allegations against him, but I also want to look briefly if I can, at the other deaths that have been associated with the Marquardt family. I`ll start with the Mallory beach incident. A young woman died in that boating accident. Paul was at the wheel, there has been a civil suit filed. Is that her family`s last best recourse?\par \par \uc2 NETTLES: Yes, I mean, for recovery, all that. I mean, the question is going to be how much assets families still have? I mean, obviously, there`s some insurers. I believe there`s also a lawsuit against Parker`s Convenience Store for selling them alcohol under age. So yes, I mean, that lawsuit is very much still alive now. And it`ll outlive the death of Paul. Because if you really think about it, Paul didn`t have a whole lot of assets.\par \par \uc2 They really were suing the family, for the family letting them, you know, have that alcohol and have the boat and then they`re going to be suing the convenience store which sold them alcohol underage. So yes, the civil suit about the boat act is still very much alive.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And what about Gloria Satterfield, Bill? Their family as we said, their family housekeeper for a long time who died in a fall at the Marquardt house. Could that case potentially be reopened? It`s gotten a lot of attention because of what was on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  frankly.\par \par \uc2 NETTLES: Yes. I mean, I think the -- any hope of a criminal case, that would be a little tough, right?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:01]\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know it`s cold case and, you know, in terms of like criminal like who actually pushed her down to step or someone actually killed her. I mean, it`s possible but I think that`s a long shot. And, you know, I believe that the civil suit against the Marquardt and the law firm for the children not getting their money has already been resolved.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And then Stephen Smith, a young man who was a classmate of Alex Marquardt`s oldest son, Buster, he was found dead on a road 10 miles from their home. In 2021, it was reported that SLED had reopened and investigation into his death based on information gathered during the course of the double murder investigation of Paul and Maggie. Now, I wonder if that is something that comes to the forefront once again.\par \par \uc2 NETTLES: Look, I mean, I`ve got enormous respect for Chief Keel. And, you know, one of the things that we know, based on the way that this investigation was handled was it was really, really tight investigation. There weren`t a lot of leaks. And the way an investigation is supposed to work is that you`re not supposed to know that the investigation is going on. And you shouldn`t be hearing about an investigation going on.\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, while I have every faith to believe that they are taking a good hard look at it and I`ve been told, you know, it`s been reported that they`re taking a good hard look at it. Yes. I believe that if they can find anything there, they will -- they will go forward with it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, David, back to the original reference, I think, which is that we know Alex Marquardt is accused of at least 99 financial crimes in 19 different indictments. He was pressed on the -- on the stand, he basically said, yes, I did that. Yes, I did that. Yes, I did that. Admitted to stealing millions of dollars. So, do those cases just go away? What happens now that he`s imprisoned for the rest of his life?\par \par \uc2 HENDERSON: Because those cases don`t just go away. But this is a situation where the justice system has to have some common sense. He doesn`t have a reasonable basis for appeal. And once those appeals have shut down, he`s serving consecutive life sentences. There is no universe where he is ever going to see the light of day again. When you`ve got to remember as terrible as all of his crimes are.\par \par \uc2 There are lots of other people who have suffered who also deserve their day in court. And so, I think he`s confessed somewhere north of 100 different crimes at this point that you could potentially charge him with. At some point, the justice system needs to say, you know what, he is never going to see the light of day again. He`s never going to hurt anybody else at some point. We`ve got to make sure we get justice for all the other people who are waiting down here at the courthouse for it.\par \par \uc2 And having been a special crimes prosecutor. There`s a long line of people like that. So, I hope we balance justice with some degree of common sense. Long term as it concerns Mr. Marquardt.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Bill Nettles, thank you so much. It was a pleasure to have you on the program. And David Henderson, we`ll speak with you again in our next hour. And next hour, we`re also going to hear from a juror who served in another high-profile murder case. His take on the verdict and what happens inside a jury room.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, more than 100,000 people in Texas, Mississippi and Louisiana are without power today after tornadoes touched down overnight as part of a severe storm system across the southern U.S. Powerful winds tore the roof off and grocery store, overturned tractor trailers and forced the cancellation of hundreds of flights in the Dallas area. That storm right now continuing its path eastward and is expected to bring severe thunderstorms and heavy winds to the Ohio Valley before dropping snow on the northeast tonight.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Correspondent Morgan Chesky is in Little Elm, Texas. That`s just north of Dallas. What are you seeing where you are? It doesn`t look good behind you.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris. It has been a wild 24 hours for these people. And we saw this line of storms approached yesterday evening. Tornado sirens were going off all over the Dallas Fort Worth area. And what`s behind me are the remnants of a grocery store here. I`ll just step out of the way here. We can kind of push in on what these straight- line winds did.\par \par \uc2 It was a combination of tornadoes and powerful winds that really did the brunt of the damage over the last 24 hours here, Chris. And I can tell you that in Texas, officials are now reporting five confirmed tornadoes, Louisiana also reporting another tornado. But it was what this wind did in between that, as you can see here caused a lot of the damage more than enough to rip the roof right off, covering multiple cars down below.\par \par \uc2 And I have to tell you, Chris, that right now this threat is far from over here. We`re seeing this same storm system make its way to the east. And right now, at last check, we have upwards of 70 million Americans facing high-wind alerts over the next 24 hours or so stretching from here in Dallas to Jacksonville, Florida all the way up north to Pittsburgh coupled with a flood threat as a result of these drenching rains that are coming with this string of thunderstorms.\par \par \uc2 I want to make clear this was not an isolated powerful storm here, Chris. There -- it was essentially a squall line that roared across this area late yesterday evening and into the overnight hours where it caused so much damage.\par \par \uc2 [16:15:04]\par \par \uc2 So, fortunately, here they`re having a chance to pick up some of the pieces now that we have a beautiful day. But that`s going to be changing for a lot of folks to the east of us as the storm continues marching on, and proving to be dangerous there. We know Kentucky also suffered from overnight storms as well. So, we are really seeing weather extremes in the middle of the country, Chris.\par \par \uc2 Not to mention that a storm in California could drop even more feet of snow stored there and cause real issues for the folks that are already buried beneath record amounts of snow in the San Bernardino mountains outside Los Angeles. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The weather this winter has been unbelievable exhausting at the very least. Morgan Chesky, thank you for that. And we`ve got new reporting today that President Biden is looking outside the D.C. bubble to ensure a party unity. Can it move his not yet official campaign to offense?\par \par \uc2 A South Carolina woman arrested for taking an abortion pill more than a year ago. Could this become the new norm in the post Roe era?\par \par \uc2 And a high-stakes meeting between President Biden and German Chancellor \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  with Ukraine topping the agenda. We`ll take you there ahead. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:30]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As Republicans are split between high-profile events this weekend, President Biden is trying to seize the political offensive. That`s from new reporting from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  . It says, Team Biden wants to consolidate Democratic support going outside D.C. to enlist successful mayors and governors in his bid for party unity. According to the post, this National Advisory Board will be asked to travel, attend events, appear on television and perform other duties as high-level surrogates for the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 At first, and then Biden once, he launches his campaign. I want to bring in Symone Sanders-Townsend. Host of Symone on MSNBC. Former campaign adviser for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and former chief spokesperson for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  . Symone, look, this group isn`t just going to send out a bunch of surrogates, the Post says, it also hopes to minimize the chance of dissent at a time when it`s no secret. A lot of Democrats are uncomfortable about Biden`s age. Is this the way to go about -- go about it?\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, MSNBC HOST: Look, I think this is a very smart strategy. This strategy, I believe, builds on a strategy that is very similar that then President Barack Obama employed in his reelection campaign in 2012. Soliciting voices from across the spectrum that can go out there and not only defend the sitting president and his agenda, and frankly, what he is going to rally to, you know, what he`s selling to voters to get them to send him back for another four years.\par \par \uc2 But also, proactively -- to practically talk about things and make folks feel like they are involved in what is happening. These surrogates when I was in campaign life, I used to talk about the barber shops, the beauty shops, and the Bible studies, Chris. And these surrogates are going to be folks that go to those kinds of places to hear directly from the people and meet them where they are.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, for other folks who are not on this advisory board but, you know, have roles to play in any campaign and particularly in a battleground. Is the idea, I don`t know, so, this is the message, let`s all get on the same message, is it -- we`re casting a diverse net because clearly, that`s one of the priorities that they said they have? Is it -- is it, I`m almost ready to announce all of the above?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: It`s a little all of the above, right? OK. So, part of the reason you do a National Advisory Committee like this and if you look at the names that are at least in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  article, and again, a formal announcement hasn`t been made, but I could assume that we will know every -- just about every single name on that list. It will be familiar to folks, these are people that are already regularly booked on television, right?\par \par \uc2 Folks that people want to hear from. People that various constituencies like and so these are not folks that you`ll have to tussle with some of our Bookers if you will, Chris, to get them on television. These are people that folks already want to hear from. So, that`s a positive. The --there are also folks that bring a little star power depending on who they are, when they go into various states.\par \par \uc2 Someone like Governor Wes Moore, for example. I was at the White House and many other folks last week for the White House Black History Month program. Governor Moore and the first lady of Maryland were both there. Let me tell you, they had a photo line going in the corner of the room. People from all walks of life, whether they were members of Congress, or National Black leaders who were invited to the White House that day, they wanted to see the governor and the First Lady and they wanted to shake -- the first lady of Maryland and they wanted to shake their hand.\par \par \uc2 And so, you said you asked Governor Moore to go into a Georgia or a place - - or a place in Michigan, for example. And folks know who he is. Couple that with the trust of leaders on the ground and you`ve got a very interesting draw for your event.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You`ve been inside a couple of presidential campaigns. I know this because I was on the campaign trail with you more than a few days. How much do you lean on governors -- friendly governors in battleground states to give you the lay of the land? Who knows much better about the intricacies of running a campaign not to in any way disparage a presidential campaign because they put their own leaders in every state, but how important can they be in figuring out the lay of the land?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Look, I think governors are important, particularly governors who just recently ran a race. So, if you look at a Governor Pritzker in Illinois, or you looking at Governor Moore in Maryland. Again, recently elected governors. Governor Whitmer in Michigan. Governors are important but I would -- I would argue, Chris, there is nothing more important than the people who are closest to the issues.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:01]\par \par \uc2 And so, it is also always important you got to make your touches with the congressional offices, you don`t want your members of Congress to get upset but you have to go in and make sure you`re speaking to mayors of the town that you`re going to. And their staff that you are talking to, the city council people and their staff. You want to ensure that when you are out on the campaign trail and you are bringing your principal in and in this case, for the Democrats, it will very likely be President Biden and Vice President Harris when they are coming into a community they are coming in and that their engagement is as authentic as possible.\par \par \uc2 And some folks don`t always do that well. And I do think that my former colleagues who currently work in the White House and some folks were at the DNC, it is something they are thinking about.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right, Symone. You`re going to stay with me because we have more to talk about a little later on in the show. But in the meantime, I want to go to the White House because we just saw President Biden award the Medal of Honor to a black Vietnam veteran nearly 60 years after he left the battlefield. NBC`s Allie Raffa is covering that for us. I want to hear about Colonel Paris Davis because I understand that his recommendation for the medal was actually lost and it had to be resubmitted a few times. Tell us about it.\par \par \uc2 ALLIE RAFFA, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris. What a moment that was when we saw President Biden lay that Blue Ribbon holding the Medal of Honor around retired Army Colonel Paris Davis` neck. This was truly a moment that`s a testament to Davis` patience and perseverance. Because he was originally recommended for this prestigious honor nearly six decades ago.\par \par \uc2 That recommendation was then lost, resubmitted, lost again, and then it took a group of volunteers in 2016 to resubmit all of this paperwork a third time before this was taken up again, as some of his supporters have argued that racism played a role in why this didn`t happen sooner. But this is something that Davis has said he himself doesn`t choose to dwell on. So, you can imagine how momentous this was when he finally received this well- deserved honor.\par \par \uc2 He`s one of the country`s first black Special Forces leaders. President Biden talked about his time and leading his unit through this brutal battle at a Vietnamese army camp. President Biden laying out in great detail. The injuries he suffered, he talked about the gunshot wounds he suffered, he was hit with shrapnel from grenades at one point one of his hands was so mangled that he was only able to shoot his rifle with his pinky finger.\par \par \uc2 And he still managed to call for air support and save every single man in his battalion. Even dragging them up hills and putting them into safety and rice paddies until that support came. So, this was quite a moment when we saw this happen today. This well-deserved honor after nearly six decades of waiting for that honor, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What an extraordinary moment. Let`s say that name again. Colonel Paris Davis, hero of the Vietnam War. Thank you so much, Allie. Raffa. Appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 And then our next hour, NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard at CPAC with some real insights after talking to voters and politicians.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are at the 2023 CPAC. Yes,\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: This place, CPAC used to be conservative`s weekend out. It was the annual gathering for the who`s who across the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2  FORMER UNITED STATES PRESIDENT: The conservative movement is alive and well.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD (voice over): George H.W. Bush made the rounds here and \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  . \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and \uc2 John McCain\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Yes. \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  too.\par \par \uc2 PAUL RYAN\uc2  , FORMER SPEAKER OF THE U.S. HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES: I don`t see this great divide in our party. What I see is a vibrant debate.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: But the rise of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has changed these halls. Nikki Haley, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  , Ted Cruz are here. But \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair Ronna McDaniel , \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  , and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  are not.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD (on camera): What does it say though from the Trump team`s perspective about Governor DeSantis not being here?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You know what, I think, you know, the absence speaks volumes. I know who is going to show up because he does every time which is President Trump.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD (voice over): Also not, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD (on camera): What does it say about you being here versus the likes of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  or even a Kevin McCarthy?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, if you keep going down the line, you`re going to name enough names where I`d say, well, I`m not a RINO. And that`s why I`m here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Vaughn Hillyard, thank you. Still to come. A South Carolina woman charged on suspicion of taking abortion pills to enter pregnancy. Is this a sign of what`s to come in the post Roe era? We`ve got that story next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:19]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: An escalating controversy today after a woman in South Carolina was arrested this week for illegally taking medication to have an abortion. According to the affidavit obtained by MSNBC, she was taken to the hospital back in October of 2021 with labor pains and told doctors she had taken an abortion pill. She then delivered a stillborn baby at 25 weeks. But South Carolina law prohibits abortions after 20 weeks gestation and the hospital notified police.\par \par \uc2 This case raises wide-ranging questions about women`s privacy and whether more states will look to prosecute women who use medication to end a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in former federal prosecutor and MSNBC legal analyst \uc2 Carol Lam\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 So, Carol, as you well know, normally a medical provider is charged in a state where abortion is illegal, not the woman, and in fact, I saw a recent poll that showed 14 percent, just 14 percent of Americans think a woman should be prosecuted if she has an abortion. But could this become more common now, especially with Roe overturned?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LAM\uc2  , FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: Yes, Chris, I think what you`re pointing out is very important here. This is a sign, I think, of increasing different use of prosecutorial discretion than we`ve seen before. It`s important to point out here that the abortion here occurred actually in October of 2021. That was before the Supreme Court ruled in the Dobbs case and removed what most women thought was a constitutional protection.\par \par \uc2 But the decision to prosecute was not made until just a few days ago, and so that actually indicates a change in the prosecutor`s thought about what`s the appropriate thing to do here.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 And the reason that that is interesting and maybe a harbinger of things to come is, as you point out, very few Americans are actually comfortable with the idea of prosecuting a woman herself when a pregnancy has been terminated, and in this case the fetus was quite far along, it was 25 weeks, and that was illegal. That was criminal back in 2021. But the prosecutor decided not to bring charges at that time.\par \par \uc2 And so the only thing that appears to have changed is that the Dobbs decision came down in June of 2022 and perhaps now law enforcement is feeling a little bit more comfortable in these more marginal cases where the fetus is farther along bringing the case against the woman herself in a self-managed abortion.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And as you know, there are all kinds of legal cases out there related to abortion, and now \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  says it will not dispense abortion pills in several states even where they remain legal after nearly two dozen Republican attorneys general threatened the company with legal action. I wonder if you see more big companies going to feel like they have to make - - you know do things similar to this if they have to remove the possibility of any legal liability.\par \par \uc2 LAM: Yes, so what kicked this all off was that back in January, the Biden administration and its \uc2 FDA\uc2  decided to change the rules with respect to who can -- who can dispense, who can dispense the drug that`s used in self- medicated abortions, mifepristone, so what they did was they said now pharmacies can actually dispense this drug with a prescription from a certified provider, but that pharmacy actually has to get additional certifications in order to do that.\par \par \uc2 The 20 attorneys general who wrote to these pharmacies and said we are telling you now that we have the authority to enforce our state laws, and we will enforce our state laws if you go ahead and provide these drugs to people who ask for them even with a prescription. That really does put the pharmacies in a difficult position right now because this is now a conflict between federal law and state law because pharmacies do have -- I`m sorry, states do have the ability to regulate pharmacies to some extent within their state borders.\par \par \uc2 And in the case of Kansas, for example, the Kansas state law says that these medications can only be provided if there is a physician in the room providing these drugs. So I do think that there has been an emboldened law enforcement effort here now that the Dobbs decision did come down, and I do think that we are going to see more companies placed in this very difficult position.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Carol Lam\uc2  , thank you so much for coming on the program as always. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Frustration and fury among residents in East Palestine boils over during the most contentious town hall yet. The toxic chemical tests the \uc2 EPA\uc2  is now demanding. We`ve got a live report from Ohio next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:16]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today it seems patience has long run out in East Palestine, Ohio. It`s been nearly a month since that train derailment, and there are new concerns over yet another toxin. Last night`s town hall meeting without a doubt the most emotional and heated we`ve seen local residents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 EPA\uc2  monitors have not detected any volatile organic compounds above levels of health concern in the community. That are attributable to the train derailment. In addition --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Don`t lie. Don`t lie to us.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`re happy to take your questions.\par \par \uc2 DARRELL WILSON, ASSISTANT VICE PRESIDENT OF GOVERNMENT RELATIONS, \uc2 NORFOLK SOUTHERN\uc2  : We are sorry. We`re very sorry for what happened. We feel horrible about it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you care about us, get our grandkids out of here now, get my children out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Jesse Kirsch in is Canfield, Ohio. People couldn`t quite understand it. The last guy said if you care about us, get my grandkids out of here, get my children out. Get us out. Sounds like it was a really rough and emotional time. Where are we with all this right now, Jesse?\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris, to say that there`s still skepticism in this community I think is an understatement. When we were at this town hall last night, pretty early on an official from the \uc2 EPA\uc2  was on stage and was talking about the testing that has come back so far and showing that the air quality is safe, and people just burst out laughing and pushed back on that idea.\par \par \uc2 So there`s this skepticism both in government and in the railroad, which says that it is going to do right by this community and make things right. There was a representative at this event. We went through the better part of an hour before we heard from that representative. He did take some questions from community members as well, but there were also questions from the crowd, people asking where`s Allen, referring to Allen Shaw, the CEO of \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 We know that he is expected to testify on Capitol Hill next week, but he was not at this public event with residents and government officials last night. So that was one of the questions. You talk about people trying to get their children out of here. People are trying to up and move. People have made their lives in that community and are asking for the company to consider paying for their property so they can get out because they`re worried that their homes, what they`ve invested in, are total losses at this point.\par \par \uc2 I want to play for you now part of one of the other exchanges we saw. There were several emotionally charged moments throughout the night. And this is one of the exchanges that also involved the mayor of East Palestine. He was there to be the moderator between the state and federal officials as well as the railroad. Here is part of what went on at the town hall last night.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want you to tell me why everybody in my community is getting sick.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR TRENT CONAWAY, EAST PALESTINE, OHIO: I want the same answers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why am I sitting here -- well, then let`s get them.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONAWAY: They`re here but everybody has to make 15-minute statements. I`m sorry. We`re doing the best we can here. And by the way, just so everybody knows, I`ve tried to keep my cool, and now I`ve lost it. And I apologized. I`m a part-time mayor. I have to feed my family, too.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I know that.\par \par \uc2 CONAWAY: So I`m doing the best I can.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: All of this coming as the \uc2 EPA\uc2  says once it approves a plan from the \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  Railroad, the railroad will be able to start temporarily removing tracks to dig up contaminated soil. And the \uc2 EPA\uc2  says that is something that is expected to begin today.\par \par \uc2 I just want to read one quick line for you, Chris, from a statement the \uc2 EPA\uc2  gave us a short time ago. They said it is possible that people in the area may smell odors from the soil from the derailment site, as this soil from the derailment side has moved. Odors from the chemicals involved in the derailment can be strong at levels much lower than what is considered harmful to health.\par \par \uc2 But officials did say last night that it is possible that there could be vapors put into the air from the aftermath of this as well. So we`re trying to get some more clarity on what the potential added consequences and health risks are to this community with the cleanup effort now going on. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jesse Kirsch, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 A major political predicament for President Biden, the move that`s left some House Democrats feeling totally blindsided.\par \par \uc2 And inside the Murdaugh deliberation room, what are those discussions really like? I`ll ask someone who served on the jury in a very high profile case ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:26]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Some House Democrats apparently pretty unhappy today, and their anger directed at an unexpected target, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . They`re apparently complaining they were blindsided by the president`s announcement that he will not veto a Republican bill that`s aimed at tackling rising crime in the nation`s capital, even though every indication was that a veto would happen. One Democrat telling "The Hill," "It`s amateur hour," and added, "Heads should roll over at the White House over this."\par \par \uc2 With the president`s eye on 2024, it could be an indication of the growing importance of crime as the political issue.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ryan Nobles is on Capitol Hill, Symone Sanders-Townsend is back with us.\par \par \uc2 So, Ryan, "Politico" reports some House Democrats are seething. What happened here?\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, and I can tell you from our own reporting that Democrats are seething, particularly on the House side. \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2  , who`s chair of the Progressive Caucus, just put out a pretty tough statement saying that she strongly disagrees with President Biden`s decision here, that the Washington, D.C. should be able to govern themselves. \uc2 Eleanor Holmes Norton\uc2  , who is the delegate that represents the District of Columbia, said that she found out basically through media reports not from the White House.\par \par \uc2 It`s a two-fold problem here for the White House. First, it`s their relationships with Democrats and those on the left but they also find themselves in an uncomfortable position right now because crime continues to be a bigger and bigger campaign issue, and as the president eyes a 2024 campaign relaunch, it`s clear that Republicans are going to hone in on the issue of crime and particularly crimes in these urban areas that are predominantly governed by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So this is President Biden trying to show that he wants to be tough on crime and especially on an issue, Chris, where this bill was likely going to pass. So he was going to be forced with the decision here. And if he isn`t going to get Democrats to prevent it from going forward, the White House seemed to think that this was something that the president needed to sign into law, if it becomes the vote of course next week.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Symone, this comes on the heels of \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  losing reelection in Chicago, the loss of suburban New York congressional seats in November people say is another indication. Crime will continue to be a big issue in 2024. Did somebody at the White House mess up here? And do Democrats ignore crime at their own peril?\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, MSNBC HOST, "SYMONE": So I would disagree with the notion that anybody in this country is ignoring crime, right. If you go to any local city, every single mayor across the country is dealing with this crime issue. I do think it`s just not good business, it`s bad politics, if you are making decisions and not notifying the people who are closest to the issue.\par \par \uc2 In this case, for me, that would be House Democrats. If I were the folks who had to make the notifications, that would be folks from Washington, D.C. the mayor, the city council, and obviously Congresswoman \uc2 Eleanor Holmes Norton\uc2  , and then moving on to folks in the Congressional Progressive Caucus.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think there`s two things here. And what Ryan said needs to be bold and underlined here, is that this bill was going to pass, why? Because it was bleeding -- they were bleeding support from Democrats in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  . They didn`t have all the Democrats in the Senate on one accord saying they are not going to go along with this, for the very reasons that you laid out, Chris, and that I just noted. Crime is an issue across this country.\par \par \uc2 Now I had an opportunity to talk to some local D.C. folks, and folks from Mayor Bowser`s camp, and Mayor Bowser herself. And I think the local view from their perspective is this, is that this can and it should have been handled locally. They never want congressional interference in what they are doing here in the District of Columbia, but that the conversation really should not be about criminal codes, but the crime conversation should be about crime prevention. And I believe that locally here in Washington, D.C., that`s where the conversation is going to go.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Symone Sanders-Townsend and Ryan Nobles, always great to talk to both of you, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Caring for Boston`s rough sleepers. The powerful and inspiring story of a doctor trying to make a difference among those living on the streets. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:57:41]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For nearly 40 years, Boston doctor Jim O`Connell has spent countless nights treating the most vulnerable, Boston`s homeless population.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Harry Smith spoke to him about why he does it and what he`s learned.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRY SMITH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Two nights a week for 30 years Dr. Jim O`Connell has done exactly this.\par \par \uc2 DR. JIM O`CONNELL, BOSTON HEALTHCARE FOR THE HOMELESS PROGRAM: Remember me from before? How are you, by the way? What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Fulfilling the healer`s oath with Boston`s homeless. His patient this night, Michael. Tells us he spent a good portion of his life in prison. As his regrets spill out, O`Connell tends to the cut hand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: O`Connell urges Michael to come to a clinic. No telling if he`ll show.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Who`s out here? Who`s out on the streets?\par \par \uc2 O`CONNELL: Oh, it`s a real mix of people. For example, you know, about 25 percent of the men that we`ll see out here cannot read or write.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voice-over): He says most have endured significant trauma, physical, psychological, many self-medicate, are addicted.\par \par \uc2 O`CONNELL: And little did I understand how complicated it was, and you can do a lot to ease their suffering and help them out, even though you may not end their homelessness.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: A classroom whiz at Notre Dame, he studied philosophy and theology. Then \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): You know what, people would look at that and say, why the hell is that guy out here?\par \par \uc2 O`CONNELL: You know, I don`t know exactly how to answer that. I was lucky to be thrown into it, and lucky that it hit the right nerves for me.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voice-over): And lucky for the patients of the Boston Healthcare for the Homeless Program he helped built. The envy of many a city.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I love this place. It`s really, really all I have in my life.\par \par \uc2 O`CONNELL: How are you?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: O`Connell seems to have a special instinct for people at risk. His most important skill, listening, learned in his years as a bartender, his life`s work featured in a new book.\par \par \uc2 (On-camera): Should we be more empathetic as a public?\par \par \uc2 O`CONNELL: Yes, I think that`s a tall order for the public. But I would say if the public had a chance to see what we have been watching for years, you couldn`t help but be empathetic.\par \par \uc2 SMITH (voice-over): Harry Smith, NBC News, Boston.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We have a lot to cover in our second hour of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. Let`s get right to it.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230304ej3300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for March 3, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alex Wagner, Jonathan Capehart, Glenn Kirschner \par \uc2 7,078 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 March 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:01]\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: We have even more to bring from my conversations with the students and their parents and their teachers on our show next week.\par \par \uc2 But for tonight, that`s it for us. Now, it is time for THE LAST WORD with \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  , who is in for Lawrence. Good evening, my friend. I haven`t seen you in so long.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2  , MSNBC HOST: I know. This is the only way I get to see you, is Tuesday through Friday night. But we`re going to have to get together the next time I`m in New York. And great reporting on this, Alex. I can`t wait to see what you have coming up next on this.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Thank you, my friend. I can`t wait to see you for the next hour.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: All right. Alex, have a good Friday night.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: You too.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: So, how is that weaponization committee going? You know, the House Republican`s big Jim Jordan, \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  project, the special select subcommittee that was going to expose what exactly? It seems like Jim Jordan doesn`t even know. Now, one Republican is trashing the subcommittees process as, quote, very much amateur hour.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to Rolling Stone on the condition of anonymity, they added that airing this, quote, stuff on live television would make us look like morons. Here is Congressman Dan Goldman, a Democratic member of the subcommittee and an actual former prosecutor who has actually worked on actual investigations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): When they come and testify, you`ll have access to the transcript, like everyone on the committee want.\par \par \uc2 REP. DAN GOLDMAN (D-NY): You mean your staff is not going to turn it over to our staff? We`re just in the dark?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: No. When the transcript is done, you will get the transcript.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: I mean of the dozens of whistleblowers you have already talked to, that came to talk to your staff.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Yes. What you want me to turn over there?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Their names.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Notes? Did anyone take notes?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Well, I`ll be happy to talk with the ranking member on how we handle that information.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: In that clip, Congressman Jim Jordan claimed to have spoken to dozens and dozens of witnesses about the, quote, political nature of the Justice Department. It turns out, according to a report released by Democrats in the House Judiciary Committee, based on interviews conducted by Republican staff, the first three witnesses interviewed sound like absolute kooks who don`t even for the definition of a federal whistleblower.\par \par \uc2 The 316-page report concludes, quote, we are able to draw a number of striking conclusions about the state of the Republican investigation. First, the three individuals we have met are not, in fact, whistleblowers. None of these witnesses has provided evidence of misconduct by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , the Department of Justice or any other public official. Each offered a wide range of personal opinions but to the extent that they testified about matters to which they claim to have firsthand knowledge at all, none showed any evidence of wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 Additionally, there is reason to doubt the credibility of these witnesses. Each endorses an alarming series of conspiracy theories related to the January 6th Capitol attack, the COVID vaccine and the validity of the 2020 election. One has called repeatedly for the dismantling of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  . Another suggested that it would be better for Americans to die than to have any kind of domestic intelligence program. Say what?\par \par \uc2 Not only do Jim Jordan`s witnesses, who, based on the Democrats report, appear to be disgruntled former \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials, not fit the definition of a whistleblower, all were bought and paid for by a small cabal of former Trump officials, including Kash Patel, who sent one of the witnesses $5,000 immediately after the two first connected.\par \par \uc2 The report goes on to say, quote, based on this evidence, committee Democrats conclude that there is a strong likelihood that Kash Patel is encouraging the witnesses to continue pursuing their meritless claims, and, in fact, is using them to propel his vendetta against the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , Justice Department and Biden administration on behalf of himself and President Trump.\par \par \uc2 And there it is. What is this subcommittee trying to do exactly? Because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the only president to have been impeached twice, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the only president to have required two special prosecutors running three separate investigations, which covered his 2016 presidential run, almost his entire time in office and his actions leaving office.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a defendant in multiple civil lawsuits and a subject in criminal investigations in multiple states. He directed rioters to storm the Capitol, which we all watched on television, live as it happened. He has lied repeatedly and relentlessly about his election loss to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , lies we now know not even Fox anchors believed.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:07]\par \par \uc2 Defending \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a dead end. Trump is a loser. This has been proven in the last three elections. And for some reason, House Republicans` number one issue is proving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a victim. We won`t be hearing from those weaponization witnesses at a public hearing anytime soon. As the report says, quote, it is no surprise that House Republicans have so far refused to allow these individuals to testify in public.\par \par \uc2 Next Thursday, the Jordan subcommittee will hold the hearing instead on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and keeping with the theme to try to prove \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a victim of social media. We all remember what happened last time. The House held a hearing on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: You`ve got the government saying your terms of service was don`t have to comply with the First Amendment, but the government saying we don`t think these accounts comply with your terms of service, please take them down. You see a problem there, Mr. Roth?\par \par \uc2 YOEL ROTH, FORMER HEAD OF TRUST AND SAFETY, TWITTER: Mr. Chairman, I`m seeing a flashing red light. I`m happy to answer the question.\par \par \uc2 Do I think that that`s a valuable use of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  `s time? No, but I don`t see in a request for review a problem under the First Amendment, no.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: I sure do. I thank the gentleman. I yield back.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Chair recognizes Mr. Connolly for five minutes.\par \par \uc2 REP. JERRY CONNOLLY (D-VA): Thank you, Mr. Chairman. My, my, my, what happens when you hold a hearing and you can`t prove your point?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Joining us now, Democratic Congressman \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2  of Texas, a member of that select subcommittee. Congressman, are you surprised by the way it`s going for Republicans?\par \par \uc2 REP. COLIN ALLRED (D-TX): No, I`m not. So much of this committee is about projection, it`s about things that either they did during the Trump administration, somebody who ran for office saying, lock her up, somebody who we know the \uc2 IRS\uc2  , seems to be, was weaponizing some cases against some Americans. And so a lot of this is about projections, about what they did or what they wanted to do and what they think is being done now.\par \par \uc2 As somebody who has served in the Obama administration and now in Congress, I think this is the tinfoil hat committee. There is nothing here. They said it`s going to be the response to January 6th committee. They`ve given it apparently a similar budget, and they have nothing because there is no there, there. And it`s also a waste of taxpayer dollars and of our time. Jonathan, this is not what our constituents expect us to be doing to trying to help \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  come back to the White House. They expected us to be trying to make their lives better. The subcommittee is doing nothing to help with that.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And today at CPAC, \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  called for defunding the police, and, quote, abolish the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , the CDC, ATF, DOJ to cheers from the crowd, which brings me to a basic question. What is this committee about?\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: Well, I don`t think they know except that they are trying to do whatever they can to delay the groundwork for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to come back to the White House. These are Trump loyalists. And the only reason, Jonathan, that this subcommittee was formed was so that Kevin McCarthy could become speaker. This is the deal that he made to have Jim Jordan on his side and to have him not run against him and to have him nominate him those many times that we saw that it took for them to get a speaker even after all the deals that he cut and gave away basically everything but the kitchen sink. That`s the only reason the subcommittee was formed.\par \par \uc2 And so now they have some conclusions, which is what they want to prove, and they`re trying to find some way to prove something that has not really happened. And that`s really what I think the American people are saying. It`s why it has been a dud.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Let`s go to what is happening in Texas, as House Republicans are trying to rewrite January 6th. Republicans in states are still pursuing voter suppression. In Texas, Republicans are considering legislation that would prohibit college campuses from being polling places. As a voting rights attorney, what`s your response to that?\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: Yes. We had 9.6 million registered Texans not vote in the last election. You would think that as a state you would try and see what can we do to help the folks who were not able to vote or didn`t vote in the last election get involved. But instead they`re trying to target kids who are on college campuses.\par \par \uc2 I`ve run voter protection programs in Texas on college campuses. I know how critically important those polling locations are for those kids. But I`ll tell you what, Jonathan, the rationale behind this that they`re saying is that it`s not safe to have a polling place on a college campus. But they also forced through, allowing folks to carry firearms on a college campus. So, which one is really about safety? We know it`s not about.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:00]\par \par \uc2 This is about suppressing the vote.\par \par \uc2 And I have to say that we have talked a lot about efforts to overturn elections after the election is over and how undemocratic that is. We have to pay more attention in this country to what they`re doing before the election to try and fix the results or to try and make sure that certain folks don`t have a chance to vote in that election.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And that actually dovetails nicely with a follow-up question I have, and that is, are you worried we are taking our eye off the ball to voter suppression efforts in the states?\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: Well, I think that we are in a really difficult moment because we have a Supreme Court that is hostile to voting. And as a voting rights lawyer, that really hurts me because I can think of an effective lawsuit that you could bring the challenge this blatantly discriminatory law, if it passes.\par \par \uc2 But then you know that the Fifth Circuit will probably be overturned if you get a good ruling and that the Supreme Court, well, they`ll probably not going to be on your side. And that`s why we have to be so aggressive in passing voting rights legislation in the Congress.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why, as you know, and we spoke about this previously during those efforts, it was so important that we try and push through the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Act in the last Congress. That is why the filibuster cannot stand in the way of doing something about this. Because, otherwise, states like Texas are in a race to see how many Texans they can make it difficult to vote for.\par \par \uc2 They are almost running out of things to do, Jonathan. I`m not kidding. They`re almost running out of ways to make it hard for certain communities in our state to vote and they`re trying to have to get even creative now. And that`s the stage that we are in.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Congressman \uc2 Colin Allred\uc2  of Texas, always great to talk to you. Thank you for coming to THE LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 ALLRED: Thanks, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And there are -- there is a bit of movement in one of the criminal investigations involving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  today. Donald Trump`s former personal lawyer, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  , said that he believes he will be called to testify before a grand jury investigating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , quote, very soon.\par \par \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  has met with the Manhattan District Attorney Alvin Bragg`s Office 18 times since their criminal investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  began. This news comes after \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reported that former Trump Adviser Kellyanne Conway met with Manhattan prosecutors investigating Donald Trump`s role in the hush money payment to Stormy Daniels.\par \par \uc2 As for Kellyanne Conway`s role in Trump`s hush money payment, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  report, quote, Mr. Cohen has said that Ms. Conway played a small yet notable role in the payment. She was the person Mr. Cohen alerted after making the payment, he wrote in his 2020 memoir. I called Trump to confirm that the transaction was completed and the documentation all in place, but he didn`t take my call, obviously a very bad sign in hindsight, he wrote. Instead, he wrote, Ms. Conway called and said she`d pass along the good news.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Glenn Kirschner, former federal prosecutor and host of the Justice Matters Podcast. Glenn, great to see. What do you make of this news, and it doesn`t seem the Manhattan investigation is escalating, and if so, to what?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, Jonathan. The Kellyanne Conway plea piece is really interesting and potentially really important as a piece of evidence that provides some additional support to the notion that there was a campaign finance conspiracy going on between \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , because if you`ll recall \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  paid off Stormy Daniels $130, 000 really to bury deeply damaging information about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , hiding that from the American voters.\par \par \uc2 And we now know that when he, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  , called \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to say, in a very real sense, boss, the crime complete, well, apparently, he didn`t get to talk to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  but Kellyanne Conway called him back. And what did she say? Quote, I will pass on the good news. What she was saying was she was going to pass on the news from \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that mission accomplished, Stormy Daniels has been silenced.\par \par \uc2 I think one of the important questions that D.A. Bragg`s prosecutors will have to ask themselves is, was Kellyanne Conway knowingly in the conspiratorial loop or was she unknowingly in the conspiratorial loop? Either way, it`s another important piece of the puzzle on the campaign finance violations that \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  has already testified he committed at the direction of and for the benefit of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, do you expect Manhattan prosecutors will meet with Stormy Daniels?\par \par \uc2 [22:15:00]\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yes, and I believe they`ve already reached out to Stormy Daniels` attorney. So, I suspect if they haven`t already had some communications with Stormy Daniels, they will want to. I mean, she is an integral part of the campaign finance violation story. So, I would suspect, I know if I were investigating this case in the grand jury, I would certainly want to present her testimony.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: So then, Glenn, what are you looking for next in evaluating where this will go?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You mentioned that \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  has met with the prosecutors from the New York District Attorney`s Office 18 times. Jonathan, I handled some really big cases, including RICO cases in the courts of Washington D.C. I`m not sure I ever met with a witness 18 times.\par \par \uc2 Now, I am sure there are lots of financials that they need to pore through with \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  so he can help not only put them in context but help explain what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may have been personally involved in. But, you know, the reporting is that it looks like \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  is very close to testifying before the New York grand jury. That will sort of be the culmination of his work with the New York prosecutors. And I think once they present him, he`s one of the big ticket witnesses, I think we may begin to hear about D.A. Bragg moving toward an indictment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: All right then. Glenn Kirschner, as always, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, elections have consequences. That`s certainly true in Michigan, where Democrats now control the statehouse and where some LGBTQ kids now believe they have a future after Republicans went viral for attacking them during the election. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: In the November midterms, Michigan voters gave Democrats control of all three branches of the state government for the first time in 40 years. And now, Democrats are delivering.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Single members present having voted without objection, the secretary will close the board and announced the vote.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Mr. President, on the final passage of Senate Bill 4, there are 23 aye vote, 15, no votes and zero excused.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Majority of the members elected in serving having voted, the bill is passed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: On Wednesday this week, the Michigan Senate voted to expand the state`s civil rights protections to include sexual orientation and gender identity. The bill passed 23 to 15 with three Republicans joining Democrats in support of the bill.\par \par \uc2 Next, the bill heads to the Michigan House, where it is expected to pass before making its way to Governor Gretchen Whitmer`s desk. Governor Whitmer has already said she intends to sign the bill into law. Activists in Michigan have been trying to codify civil rights protections for LGBTQ folks for years. The Detroit Free Press reports, quote, S.B. 4 is a reintroduction of an identical bill from the previous legislative session. Under the prior Republican-controlled legislature, the bill never received a hearing.\par \par \uc2 You know that saying, elections have consequences, it`s true. Progress is possible when people turn out to vote for it. And now, to quote our next guess, with the passage of S.B. 4, Democrats in Michigan are telling LGBTQ youth that they have a future.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. JEREMY MOSS (D-MI): 18 years ago, when I was 18 years old, I didn`t know who I was or what I was going to be. That year, Michigan voted for one of the harshest marriage bans in the country that had previously mentioned. I thought I had no future. Not just no future here in Michigan, I thought I had no future. What kind of life could I possibly live just by existing my life was unlawful.\par \par \uc2 I`ve come a long way to get here. Our community has come a long way to get here. I know many of you have come a long way and opened up your hearts and minds in the last year since this long journey began. It`s time to write the final chapter. You all have an opportunity now to be a part of it. And when this vote comes on the board, you will tell generations of people yet to come that they have a future too.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Joining us now is Democratic State Senator Jeremy Moss of Michigan. He represents the state`s seventh district, which includes parts of Detroit and its suburbs. And he was the lead sponsor of S.B. 4. Senator Moss, great to see you again.\par \par \uc2 We spoke last summer after you made viral speech calling out Republicans from politicking against LGBTQ people in an election year. What a difference in election makes, huh?\par \par \uc2 MOSS: I feel like we are living out how it started and how it`s going (INAUDIBLE). Yes. Seriously, though, this has been a true, long journey for LGBTQ Michiganders. Last year, the Republican majority in the state senate wouldn`t even passed a symbolic, nonbinding five-month resolution. And here we are advancing on this 50-year journey, truly, here in the state of Michigan, on putting LGBTQ protections in our state`s civil rights law.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:03]\par \par \uc2 So, all of the people who rejected the heat in the bigotry and wanted to see a Democratic majority solve real issues and real problems, our new Democratic majority is delivering for them.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Senator Moss, I want to play another powerful moment from your speech on Wednesday. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOSS: Just this last week, I heard from a former friend of an 80-year-old woman who cut out everyone from her life when she moved into a senior living facility. She said, I am no longer a lesbian. I am just a bridge player. She didn`t want to lose a secure place to live in her remaining years so she could not be both a lesbian and a bridge player.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Senator Moss, tell us what this means to people like the woman we just heard you speak about.\par \par \uc2 MOSS: It means economic security for LGBTQ Michiganders. So, this civil rights law protects people in the act from discrimination in housing, in employment and in receiving publicly available goods and services. But LGBTQ folks are not protected in the law. So, they could have been fired, they could have been evicted just for who they are, how they identify and how they love.\par \par \uc2 This gives them a chance to compete in Michigan`s economy fairly, just like everybody else, that they can`t be put out of housing, they can`t be fired, they can`t be denied services because of who they are. And they can live and authentic life. Think about that senior woman who has to live the remaining years of her life in the closet because she`s afraid she won`t have a secure roof over her head in her remaining years. This tells her and everyone else that she is liberated, to live just as how she is.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right, liberated again in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Governor Whitmer said, quote, bigotry is bad for business and the business community in Detroit has also been supportive of this bill. Talk about the economic aspect of codifying these protections.\par \par \uc2 MOSS: Our largest employers came out in support of the bill. Our Michigan chamber, which leans right, came out in full support of this bill. Because not having these protections places us at an economic disadvantage. This is a talent tool, recruitment, retaining talent here in the state of Michigan, not just LGBTQ talent but people will bypass Michigan if their children are discriminated against, if their neighbors aren`t tolerated in the workplace. So, putting this forward and putting it into law demonstrates that you can get a secured job here in the state of Michigan because of who you are. And it opens the doors for people to want to call Michigan home.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Michigan State Senator Jeremy Moss, great to see you again. Thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 MOSS: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Coming up, \uc2 Dwyane Wade\uc2  and Gabrielle Union`s extraordinary call to arms as parents. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:32:48]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Earlier this week, and \uc2 NBA\uc2  veteran \uc2 Dwyane Wade\uc2  and actress Gabrielle Union were presented with the President`s Award by the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  and used their acceptance speech in an extraordinary way.\par \par \uc2 They spoke directly to their daughter Zaya, who is trans, about how proud they are of her. They also spoke passionately about the common goals when it comes to the struggle for civil rights for black people and LGBTQ people and particularly for people who are members of both those groups.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DWYANE WADE\uc2  , FORMER NBA PLAYER: Zaya, you have made me a better human just simply by being who you were born to be, our baby girl, Zaya Wade.\par \par \uc2 GABRIELLE UNION, ACTRESS: Black trans people are being targeted, terrorized and hunted in this country. We are hopeful that we may witness a real shift in the fight for justice. The moment the movement makes room for everyone. Everyone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: They were speaking as parents who know their child, support their child, their daughter, and want her to be safe.\par \par \uc2 It is undeniable that there is a right-wing effort to make trans people a political target for their own political gain. You only have to look at what the Republican governor in Tennessee did yesterday. He signed into law a total ban on gender affirming health care for transgendered children despite calls for him to veto the bill and threats of incoming litigation.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Bill Lee\uc2  also signed off on a new law to prohibit, quote, "adult- oriented entertainment including, quote, "male and female impersonators from public property and limited to age restricted venues."\par \par \uc2 Republicans are running this same playbook across the country. And it is undeniable that trans people in this country are experiencing real violence and harm.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:51]\par \par \uc2 A UCLA School of Law Study found transgender people are more than -- four times more likely than cisgender people to experience violent victimization, including rape, sexual assault, and aggravated or simple assault. And experts say transgender women of color are more likely to be murdered than their cisgender female peers.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Kelley Robinson, president of the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2  . Kelley great to see you again. Thanks for coming to THE LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 Talk about the importance of what we saw \uc2 Dwyane Wade\uc2  and Gabrielle Union do at the NAACP Awards, particularly -- I watched the speech now three times. When \uc2 Dwyane Wade\uc2  says about Zaya, you know, born into the person you were meant to be, our baby girl, I mean, my heart just swelled hearing that.\par \par \uc2 KELLEY ROBINSON, PRESIDENT HUMAN RIGHTS CAMPAIGN: Absolutely. Because they are not speaking as activists. They are speaking as parents. You know, \uc2 Dwyane Wade\uc2  also said that my job is to facilitate your hopes and your dreams. And isn`t that what we all want as parents? Isn`t that what we all should want as people in our community who are here to support our kids and our youth.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve got to say that \uc2 NAACP\uc2  Image Awards was powerful in so many ways, yes because of their speech but also because of the ways they highlighted the queer community.\par \par \uc2 I mean you saw Queen Latifah hosting it. You saw Britney Griner and her wife come out on stage and be greeted with a standing ovation. That was an important moment for our community. And also a call to action.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Speaking of all those folks you just mentioned, there are some folks who can`t, who still are having a time of it trying to make the connection between the two civil rights. So what are the biggest challenges to connecting the civil rights fights of people of color, of black people, and queer and trans folks?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: This is one fight. Look, if our opposition has taught us anything, it is that our faith and our freedom are all intertwined.\par \par \uc2 I mean whether you look at Florida, where they are attacking AP African American Studies at the same time as Queer Theory, or you look across the country where the same people that are attacking reproductive freedom and racial justice and voting rights are also coming for trans kids.\par \par \uc2 This is a moment where we have to stand together. Our very survival requires it. And as Gabrielle Union said, the question is, are we going to fight for some of us or are we going to fight for all of us?\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And so then, how is the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2  working to connect these two fights for equality?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Look, we are showing up and we`re showing up full force, especially when you look at the state legislative landscape across the country, we are experiencing an all out attack. Of course, we have seen hundreds of anti LGBTQ Plus bills move across the state, nearly 400 in fact, more than we`ve ever seen. But we`re also seeing attacks across all of our communities.\par \par \uc2 So we feel like our job is to tell the story of what`s really happening, to call out the hate for exactly what it is, and to build an intersectional coalition that is fighting back. And that`s what we`re seeing play out on the ground in states across the country.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: So Kelley do think -- is this a generational fight that we are in? Like once we get past this generational fight, will maybe not be in the clear but be in a much better place than we are right now?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: I think this is a turning point moment. I think our opposition has found that their attacks on our communities have fallen flat. They have come for marriage -- same sex marriage year after year, and they have lost year after year. They saw a devastating defeat in so many races in the 2022 election cycle, where their attempts to use attacks on the trans community to draw wedges in the black community fell flat in places like Georgia.\par \par \uc2 So they are grasping straws for anything that will work. And this is the time -- this is the them when we can come together and say that it is not going to happen, not on our watch. And I think that`s the kind of movement energy that you`re seeing rise up.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: So then, what do you make of this law that was just signed by Tennessee Governor \uc2 Bill Lee\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: It`s harsh. It is. Especially when you see these bills that really are attacking our kids. I think the story that we need to tell is the one that Gabrielle Union and \uc2 Dwyane Wade\uc2  did. That these bills are actually resulting in real life violence.\par \par \uc2 You know, one in five of all hate crimes are perpetrated against LGBTQ Plus community. That`s what they`re motivated by. We are seeing the Proud Boys show up at drag queen story hours. There are real life implications.\par \par \uc2 And even though this bill has been signed into law, we have seen hundreds of people rise up in Tennessee to protest against it because it`s not the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:58]\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: And we know that this bill is intentionally vague in the way that it`s written. It`s intention had been to ban drag, but really it`s true intent is to create a culture of fear in Tennessee, to push people back into the closet.\par \par \uc2 So this is a temporary victory. But it`s one that`s motivating people across the country to see the hypocrisy playing out in the state.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: President of the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2  , Kelley Robinson, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Coming up, the fall out from the Dobbs decision is coming for the blue states. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:40:33]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: If you live in a state where abortion is legal and think you`re safe the activist Supreme Court and the radical anti-abortion agenda, think again.\par \par \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  confirmed that it would not sell abortion pills in several states, including states where abortion is legal, after it received letters from 20 Republican attorneys general threatening legal action against the company.\par \par \uc2 So if you live in Alaska, Iowa, Montana, or Kansas, right now abortion is legal, but Republicans still have managed to block access to abortion pills in those states.\par \par \uc2 And let`s not forget, Kansas voters overwhelmingly voted this summer to keep abortion protected in the state constitution. The right-wing group of officials also targeted six other national pharmacies, like CVS, \uc2 Rite Aid\uc2  and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  , after the Biden administration moved to expand abortion access by allowing pharmacies to become certified to sell abortion pills.\par \par \uc2 As Politico notes, the decision is the latest to demonstrate how widely abortion access can vary state to state in a post-Roe America, even in places where there are no bans in effect, as elected officials tussle with the federal government, activists and corporations to block the availability of services.\par \par \uc2 In addition, a lawsuit in Texas being decided right now by a Trump appointed judge threatens to end access to abortion pills nationwide -- blue states that means you too -- by overturning its \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval.\par \par \uc2 The "Texas Tribune" reports that abortion rights advocates have grown increasingly concerned that the case is likely to be decided entirely by conservative judges who might be eager for a chance to restrict abortion access even in Democrat-led states where the procedure has remained legal.\par \par \uc2 This is what the Dobbs decision has wrought. And erosion of women`s rights has a root cause that is not just limited to abortion. In a piece for "The Atlantic" titled "Originalism is going to get women killed", Madiba Dennie writes, quote, "Originalist ideology glorifies an era of blatant oppression along racial, gender and class lines. The consequences of chaining constitutional interpretation to a time when much of the country was much worse off and only a rarefied few held power is as foreseeable as it is deadly.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, attorney and columnist, Madiba Dennie. She is the former counsel for the Democracy Program at the Brennan Center for Justice. Madiba, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 What`s the logic of originalism if our society has evolved so much since the constitution was written?\par \par \uc2 MADIBA DENNIE, ATTORNEY AND COLUMNIST: Well, thank you for having me, Jonathan, first of all. Secondly, if I am to try to put originalism in its best possible light and think about how they were defying its logic, they would say that it`s mutual and consistent and that the meaning of the constitution doesn`t change.\par \par \uc2 You have one fixed, discoverable idea because whatever it might be to people who wrote it, however the public originally understood it at the time it was enacted -- that`s what it means now.\par \par \uc2 So that`s how they were trying to portray it as something that is reasonable, fair, and unbiased, when actually all it means is that the bias is already structurally built-in.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 And you write in your piece, quote, "instead of counseling, if it isn`t broke don`t fix it, originalism instructs, if it still broken you still can`t fix it. A prescription for permanent crisis in America, unsolvable in the present because they were not solved in the past."\par \par \uc2 Gun violence is just one example of a crisis that is getting worse while the court is loosening regulations on guns. How can the courts ignore this?\par \par \uc2 DENNIE: It`s really troubling and problematic. They are basically using this theory of constitutional interpretation, originalism, as an out to just do what they would`ve wanted to do anyway and secure their preferred policy goals rather than actually interpreting the constitution in a way that makes sense and protects all of America`s residents equally.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Right. And you know, this gets to the next question I was going to ask you which is, you know, originalism was Justice Alito`s reasoning for overturning Roe v. Wade. And Justice Thomas alarmingly alluded to going after contraception and gay rights using the same interpretation.\par \par \uc2 So what are the broader implications for other rights if this Supreme Court is more inclined to use originalism?\par \par \uc2 [22:49:57]\par \par \uc2 DENNIE: That`s an excellent question. I think something that has been missed is that Dobbs is just the beginning. If the Supreme Court and the lower courts that try to adhere to their rulings had their way, especially as the judiciary has been stacked by arch conservatives, they`re going to be able to chip away at any number of rights.\par \par \uc2 Now, any women-related rights are especially vulnerable, things like we mentioned contraception but also the case I was writing about in the Atlantic article, was a case really for the Second Amendment where basically, the courts said that, we didn`t used to protect women from gun violence in the past, so we can`t do so now. It said that a law that prohibited domestic violence offenders from having a gun was actually violating the Second Amendment rights because there wasn`t a sufficiently analogous law in history, which of course there wasn`t.\par \par \uc2 Domestic violence was legal for much of that time. So it`s really absurd and it`s clear how the bias is baked in already. They`re acting as if it`s neutral, but really it`s a political choice to ignore all of the progress that law and society has made over centuries.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, the best thing I have learned from what you said is, the bias is baked in already.\par \par \uc2 Madiba Dennie, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 DENNIE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Coming up, Vice President Harris is close to making history again, and this time, she`ll be beating a slavery defender, plus some of my exclusive interview with second gentleman Douglas Emhoff. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:51:32]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: So as vice president, I`m also the president of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  . And in that role I broke John -- this is from historians here -- I broke \uc2 John Adams\uc2  record of casting the most tie breaking votes in a single term.\par \par \uc2 So for context, this kid who was born in Oakland, California and graduated from an HBCU, just broke the record of \uc2 John Adams\uc2  . I think we should all fully appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: That was Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  last September. Now, Vice President Harris is poised to beat another record in the Senate set by George Washington`s vice president.\par \par \uc2 Serving two terms as vice president, \uc2 John Adams\uc2  holds the record for second most total tiebreaking votes in Senate history with 29. And now, Vice President Harris has tied with \uc2 John Adams\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 In her 29th tiebreaking vote this week, Vice President Harris voted to confirm Margaret R. Guzman to be United States district judge for the district of Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 The all-time record holder is a man who could never imagine a vice president like \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  , America`s seventh vice president, pro self- slavery defender John C. Calhoun, who served under \uc2 John Quincy Adams\uc2  . He holds the record for the most tie breaking votes in the senate at 31.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think we should all fully appreciate it. History can take a turn.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: History can take a turn. An old-time slavery defender about to be displaced on the record books by America`s first black female vice president. You love to see it.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the first woman in America`s history to be elected to sit a heartbeat away from the presidency. And this is the first time America has ever seen a man in the spousal role for a winning presidential ticket.\par \par \uc2 Douglas Emhoff is also making history himself as the first Jewish person to be in this very public role.\par \par \uc2 I sat down with him for an exclusive wide-ranging interview, where we discussed all of that and of course, the vice president`s first half of her first term in office.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: There are a lot of stories written about the vice president. Some good, some not so good, some that -- you know, portray her in a way that someone who has known her and covered her, that yes, I know that person. And then others were like that`s not quite right.\par \par \uc2 What is the one thing you would love for the American people to know about Vice President Harris that they might not already know.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS EMHOFF, SECOND GENTLEMAN OF THE UNITED STATES: Who is she? What do I see? What do millions of people see?\par \par \uc2 You saw that woman in Munich a week or so ago talking about crimes against humanity. You`ve got this woman who is just in South Carolina, advocating for increased broadband.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got this woman who has traveled all around the country speaking out against pushing back on fundamental rights, which is this horrible Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got someone who is a public servant, not a politician. She has spent every minute of every day of her professional life serving the people. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s what I see. Somebody who, yes, people work hard, she works hard, but what she accomplishes so much as it`s made me see (INAUDIBLE) people think I`m good at this, it`s good. I get to watch her. She`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:56]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You can watch my full interview with the Second Gentleman tomorrow on "THE SATURDAY SHOW" which starts at 9:00 a.m., right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD. I will see you tomorrow at 9:00 a.m. on "THE SATURDAY SHOW."\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230304ej3300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for February 26, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,182 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:00:26]\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, and welcome to POLITICSNATION. Tonight`s lead, multitasking. Last week, the Biden administration reminded its Republican detractors and the nation why voters overwhelmingly chose President Biden and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, as diplomat in chief, wrapped up his successful East European trip, marking the first anniversary of Russia`s invasion of Ukraine with a surprise visit to Kyiv and a renewed pledge of solidarity as the war drags into its second year.\par \par \uc2 Here at home, top Biden officials were on the ground in East Palestine, Ohio, responding to that town`s train derailment disaster. The \uc2 EPA\uc2  and FEMA working hand in hand with local officials to clean up the mess and hold \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  accountable for their accident.\par \par \uc2 But whether it was Ukraine or Ohio, Republican politicians could only offer photo ops and bad faith criticisms threatening U.S. support for Ukraine as another aid package is unveiled and running to the cameras in East Palestine, Ohio, to lay blame directly at President Biden`s feet, even as local officials were applauding the federal response literally as late as just moments ago.\par \par \uc2 That choice between competence and chicanery played out in Virginia last week, voters in the old dominion choosing competence in a special election to succeed the late \uc2 Donald McEachin\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 In a minute, I`ll talk to the Democrat that won that race, the first black woman U.S. congresswoman ever elected in the state of Virginia in the commonwealth. But first, but we start with Congressman \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2  , Democrat of Washington State and the Ranking Democrat on the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Congressman, we were following another news conference this afternoon from East Palestine, Ohio. A regional administrator from the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  said the agency has identified additional disposal sites for the waste from the derailment. The agency also said railroad service will resume tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Now, tonight I`m interested in your take on Republican lawmakers criticizing the administration`s handling of the disaster. Because, as I laid out at the top of the show, while \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in East Palestine last week, essentially for a photo op as he runs for president a third time, President Biden was taking flak from some of Trump`s allies in Congress for going to Ukraine and not Ohio, even though you had top Biden officials, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  and FEMA all on the ground working with local authorities to clean up the mess.\par \par \uc2 It seems to me that this administration can walk and chew gum at the same time. Why do Republicans suddenly take issue with a president that can delegate?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM SMITH\uc2  (D), WASHINGTON: Well, it`s opportunism, obviously. They`re trying to make political hay out of anything they can. But the overarching theme that we see is exactly what you described, competent government. What basically President Biden promised, as opposed to the chaotic fiasco that was the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 We see this play out certainly in East Palestine, but I`m following closely the events in Ukraine and the President`s leadership on that, pulling the coalition together, keeping \uc2 NATO\uc2  together, has really made an enormous difference. Meanwhile, the Republicans just criticize. It`s all they do.\par \par \uc2 So I think we have strong leadership for President Biden in both instances to meet the challenges that we face. And it`s a good advertisement for the Biden administration, particularly when you contrast it with the chaos that was the Trump administration, of course, culminating in his effort to forcefully stay in office on January 6. So I think the contrast is as clear as you described it.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, also talking about the Trump administration, they conveniently forget some of the deregulation they did on railways and other issues that certainly and clearly are in the picture here with the situation in Ohio. But let me go here, and I thank you for joining us tonight. Because this weekend, China released a proposal for a ceasefire and for peace talks between Ukraine and Russia, claiming to have a neutral stance on the war.\par \par \uc2 However, there`s also accused the west of provoking the conflict by providing Ukraine with defensive arms. Meanwhile, in a news conference, Ukrainian President Zelenskyy downplayed the possibility of near term peace talks, saying that Russian President Putin, quote, is no longer the same person, and quote he once was.\par \par \uc2 This comes after President Biden called on Putin to stop the war during his speech in Poland on Monday and his administration wanting China against sending lethal assistance to Russia. Does this war have an end in sight from where you sit?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I think it has a path towards that. And look, we all want peace in Ukraine. But I think we should all be abundantly aware that in one sense, peace could easily be achieved. Putin could stop attacking and withdraw. And he started this war. Russia invaded, unprovoked, and has been continuing to prosecute the war in brutal, ruthless fashion, you know, violating some of the most basic rules of humanity.\par \par \uc2 So if we want to end this war, if China wants to in this war, they could tell Putin there are some times ally to stop. In fact, if China wanted to stop this war, they could stop it very quickly by imposing the same sort of sanctions on Russia that we have imposed upon them. If China did that, Russia would have nowhere to turn, and they would have no choice but to bring about peace.\par \par \uc2 So I think we all need to keep in mind what started this and what is continuing it. The path to a peaceful resolution starts with stopping Putin. And if he won`t stop on his own, or countries like China won`t use their leverage to stop him, then we need militarily to stop him by aiding Ukraine. And that`s exactly what the Biden administration is doing, providing the arms and the weapons to blunt the invasion and turn it back, as began about six months ago, when Ukraine retook Kharkiv --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: -- and Kherson. They showed that they can do it, and we are now arming them to take back more territory. And the path is push Putin back and force him to the peace table. That`s what we`re trying to do. And that`s the leadership that President Biden in the coalition supporting Ukraine, and of course, the incredibly brave, courageous Ukrainian people. That`s the path we`re forging.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, last month, you said in an interview that you were concerned that our military would not have the infrastructure, or the readiness that it would need to engage with a foreign adversary, 