designs ways to help black families access the loans that they need to buy houses and begin to generate that well. They`re working in six different sort of urban metropolitan areas. And Seattle King County is one of them, to help with homeownership and to help close that wealth gap.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you, Congressman \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2  of Washington State.\par \par \uc2 Now to Virginia, where earlier this week, Jennifer McClellan overwhelmingly won a special election for a vacant Richmond based congressional district to succeed the late Democratic Congressman \uc2 Donald McEachin\uc2  , making her Virginia`s first black woman to ever be elected to Congress. And she joins me right now.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman-elect, I want to start by congratulating you on your historic win. And how does it feel to have broken that barrier making history? I don`t know if she can hear.\par \par \uc2 REP.-ELECT JENNIFER MCCLELLAN (D),VIRGINIA: Well, it blows my mind. We`re still having -- can you hear me?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, I hear you now. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCLELLAN: I think -- can you hear me -- OK.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCLELLAN: It blows my mind (INAUDIBLE) in 2023, but I`m very honored.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, your win also adds to the record number of women and women of color serving in Congress. How do you plan on representing women`s issues and also racial justice issues in your time on Capitol Hill?\par \par \uc2 MCCLELLAN: Well, my experience every issue is a woman`s issue. Every issue, and racial justice issue, and I`ll bring my unique perspective as a black woman, woman every issue that I face, to see how we can address disparities in that issue that still go back to Jim Crow and the legacy of slavery.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, 12 democratic states have sued the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  to challenge certain federal restrictions imposed on the distribution of the abortion pill, a mifepristone. I know, I`m mispronouncing it, but saying that those pills that those -- that limits are not supported by evidence that they`re trying to present.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a separate lawsuit by the anti-abortion activists that seek to end access to the drug is proceeding in Texas with federal judge, a federal judge there could revoke the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of the pill effectively, it would be banning its use nationwide.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Virginia House Republicans rejected a last-ditch effort to force a vote on a proposal that will let Virginia voters decide whether to enshrine abortion rights in the state constitution. A new Public Religion Research Institute poll shows national support for abortion rights is at 64 percent, up more than 10 points since 2010.\par \par \uc2 Where do you see the abortion access fight in Virginia going? And also, how do you plan on working with the GOP in Washington on this issue, considering this majority of the House?\par \par \uc2 MCCLELLAN: Well, first, let me say that constitutional amendment was mine. And that hearing was my last hearing as a state senator, where I said that decisions on health care including abortion care should be between patients and their physicians and that politician (INAUDIBLE) not insert themselves in situation that trained to do.\par \par \uc2 I think the Senate Democrats will remain a blip brick wall against any efforts to roll back the progress we`ve made in Virginia. And I will continue to fight for reproductive freedom at the federal level. I`ve passed the laws in Virginia that made us the first state to expand access to abortion by removing any unnecessary restrictions, I will continue that fight in Congress.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Virginia Republican Governor Glenn Youngkin has reportedly asked his education secretary for review of the Advanced Placement African American studies course dad has made headlines since it was rejected by -- in Florida by Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 According to a spokesperson for Youngkin, the course will be reviewed to ensure it complies with Youngkin`s executive order that would, quote, ban critical race theory. That, of which, he signed in the beginning of last year. What`s your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 [17:15:10]\par \par \uc2 MCCLELLAN: You know, Governor Youngkin talks out of both sides of his mouth. He says that we should teach complete and (INAUDIBLE) and yet he is doing everything he can to put barriers in the way of (INAUDIBLE) for a complete and accurate history when it comes to black American history and the legacy of slavery and Jim Crow. You can`t have it both ways.\par \par \uc2 And if we`re going to heal as a country, we need to talk about the trauma that we suffered as a country through slavery, Jim Crow, and its lasting legacies on communities today. And education and teaching that history is critically important to that healing. It`s critically important to a thriving healthy democracy.\par \par \uc2 And Governor Youngkin seems to be more interested with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  on who can reach the Trump base by finding the accurate teaching of African American History.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, am out of time, but I have to follow that up by asking you this. There`s been those that said Youngkin may be looking at running for president. Clearly, everyone feels that the Florida Governor DeSantis is. Do you think Youngkin is easing toward playing the culture war card to try to set himself up nationally as a more popular figure in the national GOP circles?\par \par \uc2 MCCLELLAN: And he ran for governor of Virginia using that far. He has tried to use it throughout his governorship so far, so absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Representative-elect Jennifer McClellan, thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Later on POLITICSNATION, the popular comic strip Dilbert is done, after recent racist comments from his creator. But this has apparently been a long time coming, I`ll explain. And after the break, we`re rising above the political game to see who`s doing the real cleanup following the toxic train wreck in rural Ohio.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague Richard Lui with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev., a very good Sunday to you. Search and rescue operations are underway at the moment off the coast of Italy. A ship carrying migrants ranted rocks and broke apart shortly after dawn this morning. The death toll is currently at 59 and expected to rise. The shift left Turkey five days before the wreck carrying as many as 200 passengers.\par \par \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  face a debate about minimum wage for drivers in the country`s biggest city. At LaGuardia Airport today, \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers on a 12- hour strike because of that. New York will hold hearings on a minimum wage for drivers Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 And parts of California, they remain under winter weather alerts through Wednesday. The Sierra Nevada areas under a blizzard warning and winter storm dumped massive amounts of precipitation across Southern California this weekend. More than 6.5 feet of snow hit the mountain area just this weekend and more than 5 inches of rain in the Cucamonga Canyon close by.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:22:37]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: On Wednesday, 19 days after a train derailed in East Palestine, Ohio, causing an environmental crisis, former President Trump arrived at the town and behaved as if he had just discovered the place. A spokesman for the presidential hopeful claim Trump`s visit came before any senior White House official had made the trip. Of course, that wasn`t fair or true.\par \par \uc2 President Biden as you may recall, visited a literal war zone this week. So it`s not like he hasn`t been busy looking after the country`s national interest. And also, the president`s people have been on the ground, most significantly, \uc2 EPA\uc2  Administrator \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2  , who has been a familiar face in the region.\par \par \uc2 Now this is Reagan on Tuesday drinking the town`s tap water with Republican Congressman \uc2 Bill Johnson\uc2  and Ohio Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  . When Trump stopped by, he brought bottles of water with his name on them and ordered McDonald`s. Regan might not be the Biden Cabinet member Republicans want to pick a fight with but he happens to be the one of the government officials with the most authority to address the urgent problems right now.\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  announced it was taking control of the waste disposal at the derailment site. The \uc2 EPA\uc2  will also be overseeing \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  `s cleanup and working alongside the CDC to monitor the health impact of the toxic spill for people and wildlife. Most of this work will take place off camera, which is probably why you don`t hear many politicians talking about it.\par \par \uc2 It`s easy to play the blame game when a disaster occurs. And there will be plenty of Republicans who claim this administration doesn`t care about what is happening because it feeds into the same divisive lies they`ve been telling their supporters for year.\par \par \uc2 But from where I`m standing, I see the country`s black, first black male environmental chief working around the clock to help a mostly white rural community in crisis, alongside a team of many competent and dedicated public servants.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:12]\par \par \uc2 That`s exactly how our government should work. And we should rise up and support their efforts rather than try and tear them down. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Joining me now is my political panel. Political Analyst and former \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair Michael Steele and Opinion Writer for The Washington Post Dana Milbank. Dana, let`s start in Ohio. Some Republicans have blasted President Biden for going to Ukraine, while he has not yet visited the state of the toxic train derailment.\par \par \uc2 Although as we noted in the last segment, his administration has been working around the clock responding to the incident. I want to play something a Republican Congressman Michael McCaul of Texas, said earlier today.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:13]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL, (R) TEXAS: I think that`s a false choice. I think the president should have gone to Palestine when we have this major chemical spill, but doesn`t mean we disregard what`s happening, this struggle for the global balance of power that we`re facing right now. We haven`t seen anything like this as my father`s generation World War II.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Ohio`s Republican governor has also said the President has been in touch and offered him whatever help he needs. Are Republicans criticizing the White House for their response to the derailment, just playing politics?\par \par \uc2 DANA MILBANK, THE WASHINGTON POST POLITICAL COLUMNIST: I`m shocked that politics is occurring here, Reverend Al. You know, I think it`s even worse than you were noting, you know, sort of the unfairness of targeting Biden for not going to the derailment in Ohio. It`s really a case of, you know, Republicans have for several years now been working to defund the \uc2 EPA\uc2  , to defund the Transportation Department, to weaken the regulations that would give the government any ability to prevent this sort of thing, and to respond to just this sort of thing. And then something bad happens and what do they do, they turn around and blame the government, they blame the administration. So it`s essentially sabotage the government and then blame the sabotage government for failing to act. So yes, it`s politics