cans sort of rally around him. And, you know, one concerned about let`s go to be able to afford it. So there you are.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The issue here is the normalizing of this.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I mean, people can say things and say, well, I`d misspoke or whatever. But to normalize this and that like it`s OK in the middle of the culture, the cartoon is that is what is very disturbing.\par \par \uc2 But, Michael, staying with you a minute. Let`s talk about Nikki Haley. In an op-ed for the New York Post, the Republican presidential candidate called for cutting foreign aid to countries she says, hate America, such as China or Pakistan. And you`ve been following the campaign of the former South Carolina Governor and Ambassador to the U.N ., since it launched earlier this month. Well, what`s your take on it so far?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: It`s unfortunate, in fact, I tweeted out to Nikki to stop doing this to herself. Stop -- stop, you know, dumbing down your knowledge and influence in this space. She knows damn well, that that -- that is nothing more than playing to a very narrow isolationist based within the GOP. Because the reality of it is, that`s not how it works in foreign policy. You just don`t say oh, we`re just going to, you know, cut off any resources or funding that anybody who hates us.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:01]\par \par \uc2 Well, on any given day of the week that could include our allies, that could include some people who are now enemies, I mean, who are friends who could be, you know, frustrated with us on something else, is a domestic, you know, protest is it you know government level protests, how are you defining hate us? So this just shows the performative BS not just here domestically, but internationally, that Republicans are willing to do in order to cuddle a very, very small, you know, base vote. That actually doesn`t reflect where the party has been historically, or not even where the party is now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Dana, for the last question I want to get really both of you, but I`m going to start with you, Dana. Speaker Kevin McCarthy is under fire for sharing about 44,000 hours of surveillance camera footage from January 6, with Fox News host \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  , other media organizations, including NBC News have demanded access to the tapes as well. The Speaker is more than just a political leader. They also oversee the day-to-day administration of the Capitol, including security. Is there something unprecedented about what McCarthy has done here, Dana?\par \par \uc2 MILBANK: Yeah, I mean, day after day, it seems that he breaks precedent after precedent. This is another one of the promises he made to the holdouts to give him the speakership. According to \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  himself. This is one of the things that Kevin McCarthy promised to release all of this footage, of course, he`s not released -- that he`s released it, specifically, to \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  , who can edit it and doctorate any which way.\par \par \uc2 Look, I understand why the other media are asking for the same thing. The truth is this doesn`t belong in the public domain for anybody. In Kevin McCarthy sort of cravenness to cater to the \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  and a Tucker Carlson`s, he is risking the security of himself and of his colleagues and of the capital itself. So, you know, it was the sort of thing that if this footage is in the hands of any media, as happens with any national security information that`s in the possession of the media, they need to work with the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  and others, so they don`t compromise the security of the Capitol. And you can be pretty sure that`s not what Tucker Carlson is going to be doing with this information.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michael, same question.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Yeah, I couldn`t agree with Dana more. This is reckless. It is irresponsible, incomprehensible. If any democratic president under any circumstances, say I`m