eech and him reading his speech. They say New York is ungovernable.\par \par \uc2 New York is not governable.\par \par \uc2 They say that businesses are leaving all of these, I declare politically incorrect.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just find it funny that people sort of thought of him as America`s mayor. Because that wasn`t real Giuliani, the Giuliani of that 92 riot, that`s \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Joining me now is the Director and Executive Producer of the documentary, Rebecca Gitlitz. So, Rebecca, thank you for being with us. Now, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  has been a household name in American politics for several decades, and a familiar face to New Yorkers such as myself for even longer. Why did you decide to do such a deep dive on his life story now?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA GITLITZ, "WHEN TRUTH ISN`T TRUTH: THE \uc2 RUDY GIULIANI\uc2  STORY" DIRECTOR: Well, thank you so much for having me on. And for being in the documentary. You were so invaluable to us. I think right now Rudy stays in the public eye, right? It`s been 50 years and we`ve seen this new coming of Rudy. And so we really wanted to look back and look at where he`s come from and what he`s done and sort of challenge the conventional beliefs of, you know, this was a mayor that took down prime. This was America`s Mayor. This was, you know, he has this massive fall from grace which I would argue is maybe not true.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:09]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, tonight`s second episode on your documentary looks at Giuliani`s campaign to unseat New York City`s first black Mayor David Dinkins. And his embrace of race baiting politics in a deeply divided city. And people who are not familiar, what was race like then, explain?\par \par \uc2 GITLITZ: Yeah, I mean, I think that we saw two New York`s during the Giuliani era and when more so coming out of the Dinkins mayoral time, you know, we ended that time with the New York City Police riot, which is almost erased from history. And this was a place where \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  stood on a stage in front of hundreds of off duty police, not off duty. But yeah, off duty police --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thousands, it was about 10,000, I believe.\par \par \uc2 GITLITZ: And they were screaming for a sitting mayor, the first New York City is the first time we had a black mayor in New York City. And, you know, as the we had someone who was saying we just wanted to hold the line. And that`s very similar to what we hear Congressman Raskin saying about January 6, two years later. And so, you know, I think we were in a race for it in that time.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yeah. And -- now these were off duty police because I remember that this was the time period. And I think I talked about in the interview you, where we are seeing blacks killed in certain segments of the city. And so it was a big racial divide. We were doing marches and 10,000 off duty policeman marched on his black man, calling him the N word. I mean, you`ve got it in the field. I mean, these are cops. These are people supposed to be protecting us openly calling him the N word.\par \par \uc2 GITLITZ: Yeah, I mean, this was a watershed day as Mayor de Blasio talked about it for \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  . We saw that he was willing to do just about anything to get elected. And, you know --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We spoke at this rally. You cover at Giuliani`s role in the city hall police right in 1992. For those of us active in New York politics at the time, it was no surprise Rudy would go and be a player and what happened 30 years later on January 6, without giving too much away for the remaining three episodes. Can you talk about the similarities between the January 6 and the police right because they storm City Hall screaming the N word, and the keynote speaker of the rally was \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 GITLITZ: It sure was. You know, Kevin Baker says in the film, this night or this day was the birth of Rudy the fascist. And I think that that is really true. We see a rioting, police officers shaking City Hall, flipping over police cars, and he`s standing on a podium riling up the crowd talking about David Dinkins and he blames you and he blames me and there`s -- you know, the N word is being held up and Dinkins is a washroom attendant like you know, if you`re a public servant at this time, you would quiet down the crowd, you would ask for some respect. And that is not what we saw. And, you know, we saw \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  standing on the podium on January 6, saying by any means necessary trial by combat, right? So this was a dress rehearsal for him.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Giuliani is seem to have reduced himself to punch line after 2020 election. And yet this week, he was in Ohio meeting with residents affected by the toxic train spill, and of course boasting and embellishing about his response to 911. If there`s still another chapter to be written in the 78- year-olds, political life, what do you think it will look like?\par \par \uc2 GITLITZ: I think Rudy has this ability to keep pushing forward, you know, regardless of what the naysayers say, he lives and breathes to be on this balcony, right? He will always be in what was one of my favorite lines of the film and us versus them mentality. So, he will find people that will follow him and be the us and he will find people to go against that`d be the them.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rebecca Gitlitz, thank you. And you can watch and should watch an all-new episode of When Truth Isn`t Truth: The \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  Story tonight at 9 p.m. Eastern on MSNBC. And if you missed the first episode, it is streaming now on Peacock. Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:54:17]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This is the last show during Black History Month 2023. And Black History Month is a month put aside to put emphasis on black history. The founders of Black History Month which started as black history week did so because black history was omitted from being taught and discussed and educational facilities as well as other social settings at the turn of the last century coming forward. And the need to make sure that blacks and their contributions and what we`ve done was included is what Black History Week and then Black History Month was about.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:03]\par \par \uc2 Therefore, it is ironic to some and insulting to me that in the month that we celebrate black history, you have governors like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  trying to go back to the reason we had to have Black History Month in the first place. They`re trying to eliminate and distort and in their way, try and bring black history into what is comfortable for them and excusing the truth. So they can have it in the same way it was done before there was ever black history.\par \par \uc2 So my admonition to you as we in two or three days leave this month, is make sure that don`t just have studied black history, make some, fight for black history to be maintained. Don`t just remember black history, fight and make some black history, fight people that are trying to cut it off. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5 p.m. Eastern. "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez starts right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020230227ej2q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for February 25, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui, Susan del Percio, Juanita Tolliver \par \uc2 6,657 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2  , MSNBC HOST, POLITICSNATION: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Facts versus fiction.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the war in Ukraine is entering its second year, an invasion \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  hope would be wrapped up within weeks has become a long and deadly debacle for the Russian President, thanks to Ukrainian bravery and international resolve.\par \par \uc2 President Biden marked the first anniversary of the war this week with a surprise visit to Kyiv followed by a speech in Poland expressing solidarity with the Ukrainian people, as the administration announces another round of aid.\par \par \uc2 The President also appeared alongside Ukraine`s President at a virtual conference calling on allies to remain united against Russia.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Bidens` Republican critics at home are intent on spinning their own divisive and deceptive narratives. Some GOP lawmakers are trying to cut off additional aid to Ukraine, calling the President`s policies a failure. While former President Trump traveled to the site of the toxic train derailment in Ohio, as he runs for President a third time, claiming the disaster is somehow the White House`s fault.\par \par \uc2 As in Ukraine, the situation on the ground in East Palestine doesn`t match the story being told by right-wing media, once again this afternoon, Federal authorities from the \uc2 EPA\uc2  , FEMA, and other Federal organizations are working hand-in-hand with local officials trying to clean up the mess and hold \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  accountable for their accident.\par \par \uc2 A lot to cover, so let`s get started.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Congressman Jimmy Gomez, Democrat of California, and member of the Intel Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, let`s get started with -- let`s follow up a news conference this afternoon from East Palestine, Ohio. A regional administrator from the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  said the agency will be taking over waste disposal at the site from \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Now, this also comes after residents and workers near the site where the train carrying hazardous chemicals derailed earlier this month, have been diagnosed with bronchitis and are suffering from other health conditions that doctors suspect are linked to the chemical exposure, and this is something many of us have argued about that is why you don`t want a lot of these plants and a lot of these facilities near communities that are poor people, and particularly Black and people of color are disproportionately there. Here we see it in another community. What is your reaction?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIMMY GOMEZ (D-CA): You know, this is something that we`ve seen in Los Angeles and Ohio, throughout the country, where you see a lot of these chemical plants that are leaving the rail lines, cutting through areas that are lower income, Black or Latino, and when an accident occurs, as it did in East Palestine, they are the ones that bear the brunt.\par \par \uc2 I believe it is up to the Federal government, as well as the State government to take steps to make sure that these people don`t get sicker, that they`re protected, and at the same time, that the communities are cleaned up.\par \par \uc2 But in the long term, we just have to re-examine our land use policies across the country. The poor and the working class are not the ones who should bear the brunt of chemical pollution and rail accidents and things that we`ve seen, because it`s something that we`ve seen across the country, and it`s something that must come to an end.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And in the irony of former President Trump going when it was under his administration, some of these things around train brakes and other things were relaxed. And now, he is going to go and blame this on this Biden administration without addressing some of the environmental safeguards that was relaxed under the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 But let me go to this, yesterday marked the one year anniversary of Russia`s invasion on Ukraine. The Biden administration has announced a new $2 billion package of aid to Ukraine that includes more drones, ammunition, alongside another round of sanctions against Russia. This comes days after President Biden made an unannounced visit to Kyiv and promised to continue America`s commitment to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 What are your thoughts on this war and this administration`s handling of it one year on?\par \par \uc2 [17:05:02]\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: I think this administration and Congress has taken appropriate steps in order to support an ally who is fighting for freedom, making sure that they are pushing back against the Russian aggression, and doing it in a strategic way that brought along the international community, that gave them the resources and the tools to win this war; and also, I would say, defied the skeptics.\par \par \uc2 Remember, everybody thought that Russia would overrun Ukraine in a matter of weeks, if not months, and that did not happen, and that is because this administration was taking the appropriate steps at the appropriate time to make sure that the Ukrainians can defend their homeland.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, some Republicans in Congress, like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  have become more vocal in their opposition to more aid for Ukraine, with Republicans now holding a slim majority in the House. Are you concerned about an erosion of domestic political support for countering the Russian invasion?\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: I am concerned that there are individuals within the GOP that are spouting Russian talking points, and they are doing this in order to break apart the consensus that we`ve had in this country between Democrats and Republicans that says we`ve got to support Ukrainians because it`s a fight for freedom, it is a fight against tyranny and that is why we`re doing it.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things that \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  are doing is that they`re just using the Russian talking points to sow division, and we can`t let that happen, because if we do, the Russians will think that our resolve is not 100 percent, and they`ll try to take advantage of it and it means that more lives will be lost if we do not maintain the course and push back to this aggression from the Russians.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It is very interesting that there seems to be a split even in the GOP because you have a Minority Leader in the Senate of McConnell, I`m sorry, the Majority Leader in the Senate, standing up saying he supports fighting for freedom with Ukraine, and I can only wish that he would also support some of that for some of our domestic concerns.\par \par \uc2 But while I have you, let me ask you about your home state. Earlier today. Your colleague in the House, fellow California Congresswoman Barbara Lee held a kickoff event in Oakland in her campaign to replace retiring Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  . Some of the Democrats in the State`s congressional delegation are said to be considering a run as well including \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  , Adam Schiff, and \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  . What`s your take on this race?\par \par \uc2 GOMEZ: I think it`s going to be one of the hot races in 2024. This Senate seat doesn`t come open once -- maybe once every 30 years, once every 25 years, and when that happens, everybody jumps in and we have a lot of talent in the pool of candidates. You have Barbara Lee, you have Adam Schiff, you have \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  , you have \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 I think it`s going to make an exciting race and in the end, one of them is going to be the next senator of the Great State of California and would represent it well. It`s going to be sad that some of them are no longer going to be in Congress because we need more fighters like them in the Halls of Congress every single day, but I think it`s going to be an exciting race.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you, Congressman Jimmy Gomez.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congresswoman Sheila Cherfilus McCormick, I hope I`m pronouncing your middle name correctly.\par \par \uc2 She`s a Democrat of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you this, Congresswoman McCormick, first of all, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with your Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and his assault on education. Between his laws curtailing the mention of race, gender, and sexual identity in primary schools and of course, his recent ban on an AP African- American Studies course, which I`ve protested directly.\par \par \uc2 Now a new bill introduced by Republican lawmakers this week would give the governor even more control, giving your State Board of Governors the power to direct universities on removing fields of study, University majors and minors that concern race and gender studies and to withhold spending from programs that include those areas.\par \par \uc2 The State`s public universities are overseen by the Board of Governors, 14 of its 17 members appointed by the Governor. What`s your reaction to this week`s escalation?\par \par \uc2 REP. SHEILA CHERFILUS MCCORMICK (D-FL): Well, it`s infuriating because this is a continuation of his attack on minorities and people of different backgrounds and gender. What we`re seeing him doing with this bill at this point is really removing the funding and putting all of the power into the GOP-appointed people to ensure they are now screening any information that our children and our college students need to know.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:13]\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: And at the end of the day, we see that at the heart of his attack is really removing all the diversity, equality, and inclusion programs that actually help us step towards being a more perfect union.\par \par \uc2 He attacks the educational system, African-American AP studies, so people wouldn`t understand why we even developed these programs. It really is rooted in our history of inequalities.\par \par \uc2 These inequalities still persists today. We`re still seeing inequality and unequal numbers of participation in certain job fields, we are still seeing minority students who are going to State Colleges and actually being accepted not equal to our counterparts and we are still feeling the effects even when it comes to pay, and we have to address our past and that`s what he`s trying to attack.\par \par \uc2 Because once we can erode those standards in that information, it allows him to pass and more people to live in their bigoted extremisms, like he has been doing.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, DeSantis and his Republican allies are also pushing a bill that would weaken protections for journalists against defamation lawsuits, part of DeSantis` ongoing beef with media outlets that he contends are biased against conservatives.\par \par \uc2 The bill has implications beyond Florida, because it has been written in such a way as to challenge decades` old Supreme Court precedent on defamation cases. This is the same tactic Republicans used to chip away at Roe versus Wade, isn`t it, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: It definitely is. And that`s been one of his -- one of his lanes that he`s been trying to do. We saw this kind of behavior when it came even towards during our minority access district. And here he is, again now trying to make sure he censors what the American people know and what they can hear, and these are more an escalation of his intimidation tactics.\par \par \uc2 His hope is not just to get this legislation passed so he can censor journalists, but also to send a resounding message to anybody else, if you talk against him, if you are against him, he`ll come after you, and that is the kind of retaliatory nature that we`ve seen our Governor practicing throughout the entire State.\par \par \uc2 But you know, for the good -- the good part is that we`re starting to see more people come together, more coalitions coming together, saying that we are going to protect our democracy at any cost and I don`t think he anticipated this kind of pushback to everything that he is doing, especially in our Hispanic communities and our Caribbean communities.\par \par \uc2 This looks exactly like the dictatorships and the totalitarian regimes that people left their country to come to the United States to participate in democracy. Even while I was out in the streets, I had people from Venezuela saying, you know what, the Governor is starting to look more and more like a dictator and they are absolutely right, and that is the trajectory right now that he is heading down.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And they are bringing more and more people together. When I was there two weeks ago, as head of National Action Network, we had a March, it was a diverse crowd, hundreds of people came out. We had students walking out this week of all areas, from schools all over the State, diverse. He is really, in my opinion, uniting people. That may not have been his goal, but it might be his circumstance.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, while we have you, tomorrow, we`ll have Congresswoman Jennifer McClellan fresh off her special election victory in Virginia this week. She, of course, is now the first Black woman ever sent to the \uc2 US Congress\uc2  from that State of Virginia You yourself are now in your second year in Congress, after prevailing in a special election last year to succeed the late Congressman \uc2 Alcee Hastings\uc2  , who I knew well.\par \par \uc2 How would you characterize your tenure so far? Your experience as a fresh woman member over the last year and what advice might you give Congresswoman McClellan as she begins her turn?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, I`m extremely excited to welcome her into our fold. I think she`ll be an excellent addition and I also feel like my presence in Congress at this point coming from the State of Florida has been timely.\par \par \uc2 We need more people who can actually articulate the stance of the people without leaning into the extreme MAGA Republicans and stand up for our community. And so that`s really what we`ve been aiming at and what I would advise her to do is to continue to remember the people who sent her, and to make sure that we can all come together and make sure we`re moving towards a more perfect union.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I must ask you this Congresswoman, a Texas Federal Judge will soon decide whether to cut off access to pills used for abortion and miscarriages nationwide. This comes after conservative groups filed a lawsuit to revoke the 22-year-old \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of the drug.\par \par \uc2 In preparation of this ruling, the House is taking steps to ensure access to the drug goes as uninterrupted as possible for people across the country. How concerned are you about the Judge`s upcoming decision that could come any moment?\par \par \uc2 [17:15:05]\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: I`m very concerned. I`m very concerned because it seems like week after week, month after month, we have people who are trying to force their ideologies on all of us -- women, minorities -- just American people and at every confrontation, even though they`re losing, we saw that with the midterm, they are still pushing this agenda forward, and it really does inhibit our ability to make decisions for ourselves, but also our democracy.\par \par \uc2 And as you mentioned, we are coming together and coalescing, and making sure that it doesn`t go forward, but for some reason, the extreme Republicans are not getting the message. They`re not getting the message that we will stand up for our democracy at all cost, and make sure that everyone has the right to make their own decisions.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman McCormick, thank you for joining us this evening.\par \par \uc2 Speaker McCarthy hands over 40,000 hours of January 6 footage to just one media organization, but their cozy exclusive could come at a high political cost for the Republican leader.\par \par \uc2 But first my colleague Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, a very good Saturday to you.\par \par \uc2 Wild winter weather, to tell you about first, across the country. Nearly half a million homes and businesses in Southern Michigan are without power. An ice storm pounded the region leaving one person dead. Warmer temperatures are expected later this weekend.\par \par \uc2 And in California, a blizzard warning was issued in Southern California yesterday for the first time since 1989. Going north overnight in San Francisco, they recorded a record low temperature of 39 degrees.\par \par \uc2 And disgraced attorney, Alex Murdaugh faces an additional misdemeanor charge, reporters learned late last night. That`s according to our NBC affiliate WCBD citing jail records.\par \par \uc2 He spent an emotional second day on the witness stand yesterday in his double murder trial. He is charged with killing his wife and son.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:21:06]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You may have heard me say before that many Republicans in \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  ` Florida appeared to be working overtime to turn the Sunshine State into a right-wing fantasyland, the kind of place many of us would consider a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 The Governor and his allies in the State House are trying to cancel Black History and erase discussion of homosexuality in schools, and they`ve gone to war with \uc2 Disney\uc2  for the crime of being inclusive and diverse.\par \par \uc2 And now, they are scheming to supercharge the death penalty in a State with an extremely poor track record for handing out the ultimate penalty. Some GOP lawmakers outraged by Parkland School shooter, \uc2 Nikolas Cruz\uc2  being sentenced to life without parole by a jury of his peers are pushing for a law making it dramatically easier for others to be executed.\par \par \uc2 The bill would allow Judges to overrule a jury`s life sentence in favor of the death penalty, a practice Florida dropped years ago that is not allowed in any other State, and juries would be allowed to sentence a person to death with a simple majority instead of a unanimous decision.\par \par \uc2 In essence, the proposal doubles down on capital punishment in a system that is already badly broken. Records show that Florida has executed nearly 100 convicted murderers since the 1970s, while 30 death row prisoners have seen their cases overturned in the same period. That`s the highest rate of death rate exonerations in the country.\par \par \uc2 Meaning this new plan would almost certainly lead to the execution of more innocent people. And a disparate number of those wrongfully executed will be Black, as the links between the death penalty and structural racism are deeply rooted, especially in the South.\par \par \uc2 It is outrageous to see this bill embrace by politicians who claim to be prolife on abortion. If an unborn child`s life has value, the life of an innocent person who has been wrongfully convicted should also matter.\par \par \uc2 In fact, I could think of few bigger failures of a State than to put someone to death for a crime they did not commit. It appears to me that Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  isn`t interested in justice, he is focused on generating publicity for a possible presidential run.\par \par \uc2 He may be building support with the GOP base, but he has also gotten the attention of those of us who refuse to stand by and watch the country bullied backwards into reliving many of its most tragic mistakes.\par \par \uc2 Governor DeSantis, I was in Florida as head of National Action Network to protest your efforts to roll back the clock on Black History and I will continue to call out your bigoted and heavy-handed schemes as long as you keep dreaming them up.\par \par \uc2 I gotcha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:28:56]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is my political panel, two MSNBC contributors and political strategists, Republican Susan del Percio and Democrat, Juanita Tolliver.\par \par \uc2 Susan, let me go to you first.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday marked the one year anniversary of the war in Ukraine and President Biden marked the occasion this week with a foreign trip that included a historic stop in Kyiv, as well as a stop in Poland. At the halfway mark of the Biden presidency, he made this historic trip.\par \par \uc2 His response was of course, to the Russian invasion, stands as one of the major foreign policy legacies. How would you grade his handling of this conflict?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Probably an A-minus only because no one handles it perfectly. There is always some issues here and there, but I think what is also so important is not just how he has been handling the foreign policy as it goes to the war, but what he did beforehand.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:06]\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Getting our allies, our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies in line after having to deal with former President Trump, this was a really important thing that President Biden did. He said, "You can trust America, I am here." And his visit to Ukraine wasn`t just a message to Ukrainians, it was a message to the rest of the world, "I am fully in."\par \par \uc2 And it also happens to have a unique side benefit, if you will, of politically putting the Republicans in a somewhat divided place because there are those traditional institutional Republicans like \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  , who says, we must fund Ukraine, we must win this war and we`ll do what it takes, and then there is the extreme wackadoos like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and others who say, oh, no, we shouldn`t spend any money there. We should just come home.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, back to Stateside, Juanita, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  visited East Palestine, Ohio, this week to hand out Trump branded water. He stopped by McDonald`s and takes swipes at the Biden administration handling of the toxic train derailment there.\par \par \uc2 While he was there, he claimed his administration had nothing to do with what happened, even though the Biden White House has suggested Trump`s deregulation of the rail industry helped lay the groundwork for the disaster. Perhaps even more importantly, Trump didn`t offer any detailed ideas for how to deal with the cleanup or prevent future accidents.\par \par \uc2 What was your take on the Trump visit?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: He is not there to help people, Rev, and you just put your hands on it right there, when you said he offered no solution. He was there for a campaign visit where he threw out hats and like you said, Trump bottled water and then went to McDonald`s for a retail campaign stop because this was a campaign visit.\par \par \uc2 Remember, this is a community in Ohio that voted overwhelmingly for Trump in 2020, and so that`s what he went back there for. But, he absolutely has no interest in responding to the needs of the people who are as the days pass, being diagnosed with chemical-related illnesses according to doctors and nurses on the ground. I`m talking respiratory issues like chemical bronchitis and more.\par \par \uc2 And so unless -- until he provides anything of substance, we need to call that visit what it was, a campaign visit, and juxtapose it with what we`ve seen from \uc2 EPA\uc2  Administrator, \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2  , or Secretary Buttigieg who went to the ground, who actually talked to people, who engaged local officials, who are trying to find solutions, not only to the ongoing environmental issues happening on the ground, but accountability for \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  as that deregulation you mentioned before from Trump has, let`s be real, potentially had an impact on this derailment, and potentially as it could be a cause for it, but those investigations are ongoing, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Susan, give me your view on this debacle in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: I think, you know, as we know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  only does things that serve him. So, I think he thought this event would serve him in two ways. One, it would change the story coming out of Georgia and the grand jury, because he didn`t like that and he certainly didn`t like the story about his daughter having to go to the counsel.\par \par \uc2 But also I think he really thought like I want to be treated like a candidate. I want to be candidate Trump because maybe DOJ will be less likely to go after me. So it was all a self-serving. thing. As a matter of fact, a school even had to be shut down for the day because of security concerns of his visit. This was nothing but -- yes, it`s just nothing but trouble for the people there.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me ask you this, Susan. A group of media organizations, including NBC News have asked Speaker Kevin McCarthy to share 44,000 hours of security footage from January 6, after FOX News host, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  said he was given access to the videos. It is not hard to guess why the Speaker might want to give this exclusively to a friendly media outlet, but wouldn`t it perhaps be better for Republicans politically to simply move on from January 6th?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Yes, this is an example of bad government and bad policy, I mean, bad politics, excuse me. And the politics like you highlighted are horrible. Why anyone in the Republican Party wants to relive what happened January 6 is beyond me.\par \par \uc2 But also you know, this type of thing is the first step of having state-run media, when you only provide information to one source that you believe is favorable to you and you start locking out the rest and that to me is extremely dangerous.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:11]\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: And that you know, just the data in itself, the video in itself is going to be used against certain people, that`s for sure. But more importantly, data like that should be an information and video should be given to every media outlet. It is one thing to give someone a sneak peek beforehand, but boy, state sponsored media, that`s democracy going way downhill.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, your take on the tapes going to FOX News.\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: I couldn`t agree with Susan more how dangerous it is. But it is also really dumb, Rev. People in this country just voted against extremism in 2022, to the point that Republicans had no wave. They picked up a few seats in the House and that is why they have the slim majority that they have right now.\par \par \uc2 So clearly, the public does not respond well to the GOP talking about how extremist they are, or how they celebrate January 6th and this insurrection, which we know threatened the lives of every single staffer, worker, Member of Congress, and the former Vice President who were all in that building and the police officers who were defending it.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s really dumb for Kevin McCarthy to do this, but we all know why. He is looking to FOX News to provide him with positive coverage and potential cover when he has to work with his conference and go against them and try to pass the debt limit deal. Right? We know that`s what`s on the horizon here.\par \par \uc2 So this is him trying to get ahead of that knowing the negative coverage that and response he is going to get from extremist Republicans in his conference, and FOX viewers, and so, he is trying to give himself some cover right now because he is beholden to them all.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: My last question to both of you, Juanita, you first. Right now, in States where abortion is still legal, providers could lose the ability to prescribe pills used for abortion and miscarriages. This is due to a lawsuit put forth by conservative groups who argue that the drug was improperly approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  . A Federal Judge out of Texas is expected to make his decision in the case, as soon as today possibly.\par \par \uc2 What is at stake in this case?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Everything, Rev, and I`m genuinely afraid because this is going to potentially be a national ban, which means your State Legislature can`t protect you and ensure your access to this, and we know which demographic groups are going to be disproportionately harmed, the same ones that had limited access to basic healthcare and abortion care during Roe, and now in this post Roe reality, and that`s Black and Brown people, that`s low income people, that`s people living with disabilities, that`s people who are migrants or people living in rural communities.\par \par \uc2 These people stand to lose yet another step or a pathway to accessing abortion healthcare, and I think it is important to emphasize that this would have again, national impact. So there is no cover here through State Legislatures.\par \par \uc2 And so, we all need to consider this as yet another step in taking away a baseline pillar when it comes to reproductive healthcare, and I appreciate Vice President Harris calling it out as the attack on our public health that it is, and this is harmful, and we don`t know what`s going to happen next. We don`t know the other alternatives available to people and that`s why I`m genuinely afraid, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We are out of time. But Susan, your take on this?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: You know, I agree, absolutely, with the issue of accessibility and how this is a dangerous thing to propose to women. But politically, it`s just ridiculous that this lawsuit -- they moved forward with this lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 We saw firsthand in 2022 that voters did not want extremism, even when it came to the issue of choice.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Susan del Percio and Juanita Tolliver, thank you both for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 After the break, one woman takes the fight against hunger to new heights by helping to literally feed American. I will explain just ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:42:55]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rising food prices and inflation are eating away at many poor communities in America, many of which are still recovering from the COVID- 19 pandemic. Black Americans are more than three times as likely to face hunger than White Americans according to the nonprofit, \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the CEO of \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  , Claire Babineaux-Fontenot. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 First of all, "\uc2 Forbes\uc2  " Magazine named \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  the number one charity in the US last year based on the value of private donations, the first time a food charity took the top spot, with \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  taking in $4 billion -- with a B -- to support about 200 regional food banks that helped about one in seven Americans. Talk to me about the growing need and how your organization is rising to meet that challenge.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE BABINEAUX-FONTENOT, CEO, FEEDING AMERICA: Well, first, let me thank you for having me, Reverend Al, especially during Black History Month, when so many Black families are struggling to make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 The reality is that in this country, the richest country in the history of civilization, we have a real problem around hunger and poverty in this country. I`d like to make it clear that at \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  , we care equally about every single person facing hunger, but the reality is not everybody faces it in the same way.\par \par \uc2 Communities of color are in ordinately struggling with food insecurity, and we want to do something about it; so, I hope your audience wants to do something, too.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I want to show some stats provided to us by your organization, citing data from the Department of Agriculture, which found that in 2021, twenty percent of Black individuals lived in food insecure households, 22 percent of Black children lived in food insecure households, and Black Americans were three times more likely to face hunger than White ones.\par \par \uc2 What`s driving that disparity?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:10]\par \par \uc2 BABINEAUX-FONTENOT: Oh, there are a whole plethora of things that are driving that disparity. There are systems in place, wheels, I sometimes call them that simply need to be broken and we are working on making certain that we do exactly that.\par \par \uc2 So we do a really effective job of feeding the line, making certain that people who show up in need of our services receive them. But the reality is, what we really need to also be doing is focusing a lot of energy on making certain that we shorten those lines.\par \par \uc2 So at \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  , we have a bold aspiration for the nation that includes reducing food insecurity rates overall by half by the end of the decade, and reducing disparities by half in that same timeframe. That`s going to happen community by community, learning from the communities themselves about what it is that they need.\par \par \uc2 One of the things I`m particularly proud of, Reverend Al, is that at \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  , we go to the source, we ask people experiencing hunger, what do they want for themselves? What are the solutions that they would like us to invest in? And we`ve been investing in those solutions.\par \par \uc2 And with your indulgence, I`m actually coming to you from my hometown of Opelousas, Louisiana, where we`re making one of those investments. I`d love to talk to you a little bit more about it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now on that note, \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  has teamed with MacKenzie Scott, the former wife of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  who donated $20 million to help food groups serving communities of color. Talk about the impact these funds will have.\par \par \uc2 BABINEAUX-FONTENOT: Absolutely. So when MacKenzie Scott made that investment at the national office after having made some local investments, it was put upon us to really ask ourselves, how do we live into the spirit of that gift? So we came up with what we refer to as the Food Security Equity Impact Fund.\par \par \uc2 And what we`ve done is, 100 percent of the money and the seed money was that $20 million from MacKenzie and we`ve been trying to attract more dollars, and hopefully your audience will participate. We take 100 percent of that money is dedicated to going inside of communities, investing in community solutions.\par \par \uc2 So I alluded before to this town that I`m from Appaloosas, where there`s a multigenerational Black farm, Chenier Farms, \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  invested through the Food Security Equity Impact Fund in that farm. We made certain that along with them and their industry, that they had the resources that they need to grow these really nutritious crops.\par \par \uc2 And then we`ve created with them an ecosystem where we purchase the produce coming out of those crops, and give it back into the community. We`re doing things like that in rural communities, we`re doing it in urban communities. You have great footage of Chenier Farms right now that your audience is able to see.\par \par \uc2 I want the audience to know that what people need in communities is not for us to come in with a Black box and tell them what the solutions need to be. They need us to invest in their ideas, shoulder-to-shoulder and be partners with them in driving for those solutions. We already are seeing success and we`re just getting started.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow. Before you go, Claire, we`re here in the last few days of Black History Month and at the same time, you`re fighting the widespread hunger that you`ve laid out for us here, including in your hometown.\par \par \uc2 You`re blazing a trail as a Black woman CEO of what is now the nation`s top charity. Does that ever factor into you as you`re going about your work literally feeding America?\par \par \uc2 BABINEAUX-FONTENOT: Oh, absolutely. I believe it should matter that I come to this role with experiences. I am a Black woman in America, that means something in this country, and it ought to inform how I approach communities.\par \par \uc2 I`m a person who comes from a community that doesn`t have a lot of investment. That ought to inform how I approach this work. I have known so many people who don`t have a lot of resources, but who are highly resourceful. So that ought to be the way that we approach the work.\par \par \uc2 I am a part of a network that knows that that`s the way to do it. We`re really a local organization when you get down to it. We have food banks across the country that are members, we have affiliated food banks, 6,000 agency partners, over a million volunteers, where if you want to put a number on this Reverend Al, like in 2020, we served 60 million people 6.7 billion meals, and in 2021, fifty three million people still needed to rely upon the charitable food system.\par \par \uc2 I want to be a part of breaking that wheel. I don`t want them to have to stand in that line to be able to feed their babies. I want to make certain that we participate in positive change. I know we can. I hope we`ll have a lot of company after this show.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, I hope so, too. Thank you, Miss Babineaux-Fontenot, the CEO of \uc2 Feeding America\uc2  . Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:04]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Tomorrow on POLITICSNATION, the director of the new MSNBC film, "When Truth Isn`t Truth: The \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  Story" joins me right here on set.\par \par \uc2 I was interviewed as part of the series. It explores Giuliani`s rise to power, fall from grace, and how little he changed through all of it. That`s tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION just ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Tonight, police departments across the country are on alert after online tracking organizations picking up chatter from neo Nazi groups about a so-called national day of hate against Jewish communities.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thankfully, there have been no reports of any major incidents, but we continue to see evidence of a rise in antisemitism in the United States and troubling behavior from those claiming to represent hate groups.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, theater goers in New York City are heckled by protesters identifying as Neo Nazis. The show they were waiting to see is called "Parade," a play about a Jewish factory worker who was convicted of murder and lynched in early 20th Century Atlanta, after a highly publicized trial that was tainted by antisemitic tropes and stereotypes.\par \par \uc2 As head of the National Action Network, I was outraged to hear about such hateful behavior directed at a Broadway dramatization of historical events that shaped the identity of Jewish-Americans, especially those living in the South.\par \par \uc2 So myself and the NAN organization went out and ordered and bought 25 tickets to "Parade" to share with some of NAN`s younger members. I figured any play that got Nazis this upset is probably worth the attack. It might be worth your time too.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020230226ej2p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE 11TH HOUR for February 24, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Richard Engel \par \uc2 8,064 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 February 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:00:21]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, one year of war, memorials throughout Ukraine and across the world, marking the brutality of Russian aggression as the fight for democracy continues.\par \par \uc2 Then, how one lawsuit in Texas could overturn access across the nation to abortion pills. What the consequences could look like.\par \par \uc2 And our Friday nightcap is in the house breaking down the major headlines and biggest winners of the week as the 11th Hour gets underway right now.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. We begin this evening in Ukraine where it is now 6 am on Saturday morning in Kyiv. A deadly and brutal war is now entering its second year officially. February 24th 2022 was day one of Russia`s unprovoked invasion. For the past year, Ukrainians have endured relentless attacks from Russian forces. Thousands of people have been killed and injured. Millions were forced to leave the country while entire communities were destroyed. Today, President Zelenskyy marked one year of war with a ceremony honoring those who died fighting to defend Ukraine. He vowed that 2023 would be the year of victory over Russia. But as NBC`s Richard Engel reports, Ukraine is bracing for a very tough road ahead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDETAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): This war doesn`t appear anywhere close to over. Poland today delivered new German-made leopard tanks just the first four of 14. Ukraine is expected to launch a new counter offensive when more weapons including American tanks arrived.\par \par \uc2 A year ago, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  thought Ukraine would fall quickly that Ukrainians would be happy to join Russia. Instead, they packed off their families to be refugees. 13 million are displaced from their homes. And then Ukrainians went out to fight.\par \par \uc2 Today, the White House announced another $2 billion in military aid for Ukraine and impose new sanctions on Russia. Although sanctions so far have not deterred Putin. Secretary of State Blinken with a blunt message at the \uc2 UN\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  , SECRETARY OF STATE: Members of this council should not fall into the false equivalency of calling on both sides to stop fighting. One man, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  started this war. One man can end it.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: And now after giving Russia a critical economic lifeline, China is offering to play Peacemaker presenting a 12-point peace plan that`s light on specifics. U.S. Ambassador to the U.N ., \uc2 Linda Thomas Greenfield\uc2  speaking to NBC, Andrea Mitchell with a warning if China were to give weapons to Russia.\par \par \uc2 LINDA THOMAS-GREENFIELD\uc2  , U.S. AMBASSADOR TO THE U.N .: It would be a game changer. And it would be it would be something we would have serious concerns about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: President Zelenskyy has been pressing the U.S. for F16 fighter jets. And here`s what President Biden said tonight when he was asked about that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , U.S. PRESIDENT: We`re sending him what our seasoned military thinks he need to know. He needs tanks, it needs artillery. He needs air defense, including another Highmark. There`s things he needs now that we`re sending him to put him in a position to be able to make gains this spring and this summer going into the fall.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You don`t think he needs F-16s now?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, he doesn`t need F-16s now. There is no basis upon which there is a rationale according to our military now to provide F-16s.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But you`re not ruling it out?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I am ruling out for now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: In many cities around the world today, there was clear support for Ukraine. Blue and yellow decorated buildings while crowds took to the streets calling for Russia to end its invasion. And in Washington D.C. tonight there was a candlelit vigil to honor Ukraine`s endurance in the face of this brutal war.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel. Will Taylor joins us, the former U.S. Ambassador to Ukraine. Now the Vice President of the \uc2 U.S. Institute for Peace\uc2  for Russia in Europe, Igor Novikov, a former adviser to Ukrainian President Zelenskyy and my old friend \uc2 Garry Kasparov\uc2  , the Founder and Chairman of the Renew Democracy Initiative, a Russia pro-democracy leader and of course, a former world chess champion.\par \par \uc2 Igor, it is good to see you in Kyiv safe with your family. Tell us how has your life changed over the last year? How are you doing? You have young children?\par \par \uc2 IGOR NOVIKOV, FORMER ADVISOR TO PRESIDENT ZELENSKYY: Well, Stephanie, my children actually away up until the end of March. So, I`m all alone here. In terms of how my life change will completely -- and to be honest I mean I`ve gained some experiences and some new skills that I never thought I would have. For example, I can tell different explosions by their sound. So, it`s a completely different world. And I don`t think I`m going to see my wife anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:17]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But what is it like every day you said your children won`t be back until late March, are Ukrainian children in school? What is what is day-to- day life like there?\par \par \uc2 NOVIKOV: Well, we`ve adopted. It`s completely different from what it used to be. So, we have to take into consideration bombings, you know, energy disruptions, everything, like it`s a completely a different world now for us. But to be honest, I think the ordinary people are coping better than they were, you know, a year ago. And the main reason for that is that the fear is gone. Because, you know, there`s no longer uncertainty, there`s no longer fear of escalating, you know, the worst this happens, and we just have to live through it basically.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ambassador, we have spoken a lot over the last year. This time when the war began, did you think it would still be going on a year later?\par \par \uc2 AMB. WILLIAM TAYLOR, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO UKRAINE: I didn`t, Stephanie. I was surprised as many of us were about the start of that war. We couldn`t imagine Putin actually invading his neighbor. First time in a massive sense, of course, he`d been invaded in the beginning in 2014. But then again, in his massive Sensient, on the 24th of February last year, it was astounding. And I was absolutely sure that the Ukrainians would fight. And indeed, they have. They fought heroically and amazingly and pushing the Russians back. I think, Stephanie -- I think this year, I think in the next six months, Ukrainians with the weapons and with the ammunition with the long-range fire that you`re just talking about with Richard Engel. I think that the Ukrainians can bring this war to an end.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Garry, here we are, one year end, what are you thinking about tonight?\par \par \uc2 GARRY KASPAROV\uc2  , HUMAN RIGHTS FEDERATION CHAIRMAN: I have to agree with Ambassador that it`s actually nine years. It`s one year of full-fledged invasion. But the war started nine years ago with annexation of Crimea. And Putin trying, trying to take a huge chunk of Ukrainian territory but failed and had to be satisfied for a while with a small piece of territory in Eastern Ukraine in Donbas area. But for me, this big attack was always about -- it`s about timing. So, not if but when.\par \par \uc2 And Putin, by the way, give him credit. He was never hiding in the darkness, with his plans, himself and Russian propaganda kept talking 24/7, the last nine years, eight years prior to the February 24th 2022, that Ukraine was not a real estate and Russia had to take the territories that were -- that would you buy historical justice and you name. So, I think that`s this war in the making. And unfortunately, the free world paid no attention to his warning signs. I think many of these tragedies could have been avoided if Ukraine receive weapons that it needed earlier. And even now, I`m not sure why, you know, the free well is so slow and provide the great weapons that he needed to defend herself and to put an end to this war. Though, again, I agree with Ambassador, with this American help, with European nations now lining up and also providing necessary assistance, I think Ukrainians can end this war this year.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, Garry, take us inside Russia. Is there any real opposition to Putin left inside Russia?\par \par \uc2 KASPAROV: Look, talking about opposition to Putin today is as talking about opposition to Hitler in 1944. So, the nation is paralyzed. It`s the brainwash, and the only way to actually change situation in Russia is to demonstrate the Russian public that the war is being lost. So far, those who are watching Russian television, they`re not so sure. Here we`re talking about American weapons coming in and Ukraine is ready to start counter offensive, but the Russians they see a very different picture. And they see that the front line is still, you know, it`s stalled. And Putin kept -- keeps telling them that we need 200,000 more, half a million more of fresh troops and will win the war.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, they don`t fighting Ukraine, they`re fighting \uc2 NATO\uc2  , which explains why the war is so tough, so only you granted victory or just the beginning of Ukrainians` counter-offensive that will penetrate Crimea. That`s the signal that may start changing the mood of Russians. They have to recognize the days of empire are over and we have to start thinking about something else and recognize that, you know, Russia has to crawl back to -- begging for forgiveness, paying reparations, accepting this military defeat, to find its way into the family of civilized nation.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:17]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Putin versus \uc2 NATO\uc2  . Ambassador, I want to show you, my partner Ali Velshi earlier today asked President Zelenskyy about what he thought Putin`s goals were beyond Ukraine. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Last week, the President of Poland had said that if this war is still going on one year from today, there is a real danger that an empowered Russia will invade another state. Given how effectively you have held back a Russian advance with NATO`s help here in Ukraine, is it even conceivable that Russia could invade another state, particularly a \uc2 NATO\uc2  state?\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINE PRESIDENT (through translation): Unfortunately, yes. Unfortunately, I believe that`s possible. And that might happen. Why? I can give you an explanation. President Putin needs to demonstrate successes and victories. So, there is not going to be a success on the battlefield in Ukraine. And he will not succeed with the massive revanche in Ukraine, so he would need to demonstrate success.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Wow, Zelenskyy basically saying Putin couldn`t beat us. He`s going to go after one of you guys or someone else? Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: I do. I do. I think it`s a real danger. This is why Ukraine has to win. It`s one of many reasons Ukraine has to win. Because if Russia is on the border with \uc2 NATO\uc2  , or he could easily take a non-\uc2 NATO\uc2  nation like Moldova, or he could take parts of a \uc2 NATO\uc2  nation like one of the Baltic States. There is a really -- you have to go back. \uc2 Garry Kasparov\uc2  can tell us more about Russian history. But there`s a long history, a sad history of Russian expansion of empire, as Garry just said, of empire, of colonialism, of aggression. And we have to worry about that that is a constant in Russian history.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Igor, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 NOVIKOV: I think most people don`t understand how fragile actually Putin`s position is at the moment. And that`s very surprising. But look at Transnistria, for example, if anything happens there. So, for example, if Moldova reaches out to Ukraine and asks for help, that would be a major defeat for Putin. Secondly, I mean, we`re not getting bombed. It`s like the 25th of February the morning and nothing happened over, you know, the anniversary days. So, I think Putin is now similar to a driver, you know, driving down the highway 200 miles an hour and having lost control.\par \par \uc2 Now, I think there is opposition to Putin in Russia, but that opposition is a warmongering opposition. So, if you look at, you know, some statements from Prigozhin, from Kadyrov and some of the clans within the Kremlin, I mean, Russia is no longer the monolith it used to be so slowly losing control. And that`s why I agree with my President, I think, you know, in this situation, you know, Russia is likely to be chaotic in its decisions and there is likelihood that can invade either Moldova or even the Baltics.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Garry, same question to you?\par \par \uc2 KASPAROV: No, I do not believe that we can seriously talk about warmongering oppositions. Kadyrov by the way is very silent. I actually believe that Kadyrov was already preparing for a day when Putin collapses and he can claim Chechen independence. But that`s a separate story. As for Prigozhin, his troops are being destroyed around Bakhmut. And he`s fighting with Russian generals who do not want him to have any say about how money is being spent on this war.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s talking about war mongering opposition to Putin is like you`re debating who could be worse than Hitler and Stalin. Putin is the spine of this multi-fastest system has been built in Russia. So that`s why Putin`s collapse means that the whole system will go down as a house of cards. But because the success of this war against \uc2 NATO\uc2  and let me emphasize that`s the war they`re fighting according to Russian propaganda. I can force Putin to do something absolutely crazy because he has no other options. I mean, forget about nukes but attacking one of the Baltic States, it`s actually, geographically, is more feasible than Moldova. That could be an option. That`s why we need Ukraine to win the war this year as -- sooner rather than later, to demonstrate success on the battlefield. And Crimea by the way is the key is more important than Donbas. Of course, whole territory must be liberated. But Crimea is the staple of the Putin mythology. At the moment Russian public sees that Crimea is just -- is being overtaken by Ukrainians, you will see the dramatic shift in public opinion in Russia. And then you can see the real opposition to Putin, the beginning of uprising and revolt.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:11]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Igor, there are some Americans that believe the U.S. should rethink its level of support to Ukraine. What`s your message to them? What do you want them to understand?\par \par \uc2 NOVIKOV: Well, there`s a simple rule of thumb there. The less support we get and the slow we get it, the more support the U.S. will have to provide in the end. Because obviously, of the inefficiencies of that process, if the U.S. wants us to win. And if it doesn`t quite provide support at all. So, look, I think we have to realize that this is probably the last year of this war, and what the victory actually looks like depends on how quickly and how efficiently we get all the support that we need.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s certainly hope it is the last year of this war. Thank you all for being here. Igor, I`m sending you and your family safety and warmth in this time. Here we are, year two. Ambassador Will Taylor, Igor Novikov, and \uc2 Garry Kasparov\uc2  , thank you all so much.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the court case that could have a huge impact on access to abortion pills. Dr. \uc2 Kavita Patel\uc2  is here on what`s at stake for women around the country.\par \par \uc2 And later, buckle up, our Friday nightcap is here to break down all of the big news of the week from Kevin McCarthy handing \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  the January 6th video to the East Palestine train disaster. What it really says about our political discourse in this country. The 11th Hour just getting underway on a Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:21:18]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , (D) U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: This is not just an attack on women`s fundamental freedoms. It is an attack on the very foundation of our public health system.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: A Texas lawsuit could cut off access to pills used for abortion and miscarriage care across the nation. After conservative groups filed suit to revoke the 22-year-old \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of the drug. The decision from a Trump- appointed judge could come at any time.\par \par \uc2 Let`s welcome back Dr. \uc2 Kavita Patel\uc2  , clinical physician and former Senior Policy Director during the Obama administration. I`m so glad you`re here. We need to understand what this all means. So, if this judge decides to revoke the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval, what are the consequences for us?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 KAVITA PATEL\uc2  , CLINICAL PHYSICIAN: Yes, Steph, this is unfortunately a reality that many of us, especially in the clinical side are coming to grips with because what it means is that the majority of abortions right now are done with medications. And they`re done safely. As you mentioned, 22 years this drug has been approved, under incredibly strict risk monitoring might I add. So, this isn`t just you know, available, by any means in the previous years. Only in the recent year has the Biden administration made it more accessible but in line with what we`re doing in clinical medicine.\par \par \uc2 If this goes through, this means that mifepristone, which is the first part of a two-drug regimen that we use in medication abortions, would no longer be available, essentially, revoking the FDA`s drug approval that has stood for almost 25 years. That would force all of us and we`re all thinking about the implications of this, to use the second drug, which is generally part two misoprostol, which is something that is very -- it`s a drug that`s used all around the world, Steph, in just all by itself. But when it`s used with mifepristone, it is a much more humane and decent way to give a woman the experience of having to have her uterine contents expelled, the pain and a lot of the side effects get diminished when we use these two drugs together. What this means is that women will be -- people who are pregnant will be scared, clinicians will be even more unlikely to do any of the things that they are legally allowed to do right now. And the confusion that exists from state-to-state, city-to-city will only compound and all for something that quite honestly seems to be plucked out of thin air and put the agency`s credibility, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  in jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then what are the alternatives for care, right? If more than half of U.S. abortions are medication? What`s -- what is going to happen? What is it going to look like?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Right. So, what it will look like is that we will be forced to use that second part to of that two-drug regimen misoprostol in isolation by itself. You might ask, Steph -- well, what would stop people from trying to go after that? You`re absolutely right to think that way. But the law that had the approval that being brought into the court, and what`s at stake right now is really about the first drug. And so, we would have to really use the second drug alone. And what that means for women is that it could be a longer process to experience a medication abortion.\par \par \uc2 And Steph, just to level set right now there are many women who are experiencing and going through very safe medication abortion, but we have all these decades of experience with both of these drugs. So, flip the switch and then start to use something that we just haven`t had that experience with in the United States. We have worldwide, but in the United States, that`s just going to put women at risk for no reason whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Could it end up putting a bigger burden on surgical abortion providers in states where it`s legal because they`re already under an enormous amount of stress?\par \par \uc2 [23:25:02]\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yeah, it`s possible. So, what we don`t know, we do not have enough data because we`ve had the ability to safely administer both the one drug that a two-part drug regimen. And because of that, we don`t have enough data to know that if we just had to switch to misoprostol only would that result in more women having to present as you mentioned, so seek any sort of procedures, or even to seek evaluations. Steph, a lot of women might not have the confidence, especially if they`re continuing experiencing symptoms, cramps, diarrhea, vomiting, nausea, they would rightly be concerned and need to seek care, all for what something that literally was, again, it`s amazing to me how 22 years of safety data doesn`t seem to be enough. And what could this mean for any other drugs that we use in reproductive right? Or for that matter in medicine?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That`s exactly what I want to ask you about. How unusual is it to see a legal challenge to a drug that was approved 20 hard years ago?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Right. Not only is it not so unusual, but I used to work on \uc2 FDA\uc2  policy and work with the agency very closely. Many of us can`t even believe that we are here. We`re actually all talking about the hope that maybe the Supreme Court or somebody could say this ruling, because the implications to your point, Steph, are so broad, and mind blowing that it just undermines everything we`ve done. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  is not just regarded in the United States, as our top premier safety organization. It is regarded that way in the world. So can you imagine this is a drug that is listed by the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  as an essential medicine. So, think about the implications of that, and what that means and sends a message to the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I`m really glad you joined us tonight. It`s not like you joined us with good news but you definitely made us more smarter and prepared for what could be ahead. Dr. \uc2 Kavita Patel\uc2  , always good to see you. And sidenote, I really liked the rainbow behind you. Excellent.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, that toxic train derailment in Ohio a perfect, a perfect symbol of the state of our politics right now. Our Friday night cap is here on that and a whole lot more when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:31:57]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The train wreck in East Palestine, Ohio sent toxic chemicals into the air and water and for residents of this small Ohio community, the cleanup is just the beginning. But the political fallout equally toxic is only getting worse. And it has come to represent all that is wrong with American political discourse today.\par \par \uc2 Well, you`re in luck because our Friday nightcap is here to discuss all of it. Mara S. Campo, Anchor and Managing Editor of Revolts Black News. BD Wong, who I`m a superfan of, Actor and Director of the upcoming show. Yes, I Can Say That, by another nightcap fav Judy Gold, Ron Insana is here, my dear friend, CNBC Senior Analyst, and George M. Johnson, author of All Boys Aren`t Blue and We Are Not Broken. We got a lot to cover.\par \par \uc2 Mara, I turned to you first because I don`t even know what to do with this. We are seeing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Republicans attack, attack President Biden saying he`s not helping East Palestine because it is a rural white community, he would do so much more if it was an urban black community. That`s not even true.\par \par \uc2 MARA S. CAMPO, ANCHOR, REVOLT BLACK NEWS: Yeah. And we`re also having some people point out the fact that Trump had a hand in deregulating a lot of this. So as the investigation proceeds, there are questions about what went wrong, and ultimately, who`s responsible and who`s accountable. So, politics does play a big role in this. We saw \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  being criticized for not showing up earlier. I think that`s a fair criticism. I think that when something like this happens, you do want to show the community, that it`s a priority for you and that you care about them. But when it comes to accountability, which I think is a big part of this conversation right now, as it should be, when we`re investigating what went wrong, and what happened and who`s ultimately accountable. The question when it comes to accountability is, what kind of accountability will count, because in cases like this, where you have these big companies, at the end of the day, they`re going to be hit with a fine or with a civil settlement that is going to be small money to them, it`s a rounding error. This company --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Based on money lobbying, then they will on fire.\par \par \uc2 CAMPO: Absolutely. And for some of them, they build these fines into their budget. It`s a line item. And so, when you`re talking about accountability, what can you implement, that`s going to actually matter? People are asking for criminal charges, potentially, if there are signs of criminal negligence. And so, I think that`s a bigger conversation to be had because regulations are one thing, but they only really matter if they have teeth. And if it`s a, you know, drop in the bucket, then people will just keep doing what they`re doing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Or these fines get covered by insurance companies. Take us back to this, Ron, because again, a wider audience is hearing people like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  say, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  isn`t showing up. He`s not helping the American people. Remind our audience of what the Trump administration did, deregulation wise, that put us in this risky position.\par \par \uc2 RON INSANA, CNBC SENIOR ANALYST: And it`s not just the Trump administration itself. \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  which of course is the rail line in question here lobbied aggressively to make sure that certain restrictions on railroads, particularly freight railroads were rolled back. That is their core business. And \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  did a fabulous job outlining the fact that \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  itself had internal suggestions that would override existing security concerns and allow engineers and conductors to bypass concerns while a train was in motion.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:04]\par \par \uc2 And if you saw any video of this train, with flames coming out from underneath it, they ignored some of the warning signs that exist. So, this is a multi-level problem that goes back to originally deregulation during the Trump-era, when by the way, we had thousands of train issues. No one visited any locations during that period when these things happen. And now blaming people to judge or blame \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is extremely convenient, because it happened while they were in office, but has nothing to do with the root cause. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  also by the way, was in Ukraine, when he was being criticized for not going to East Palestine, Ohio, where there`s a major global conflict going up.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So, what Ron and Mara just did was something novel. They told the truth, they told the truth to every person watching tonight, but there are millions of people who aren`t watching. What do we do with that, George? The fact that I go back to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  telling people well, Biden want to help a poor white community. That`s just not true. But lots of people think it is.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE M. JOHNSON, AUTHOR, "ALL BOYS AREN`T BLUE": Yeah. And I mean, it`s in the Far East. I mean, it`s like