istory of lying directly to his audience as to the other two or three members of their primetime lineup. This is something that we should all have access to and be able to vet on our own.\par \par \uc2 WONG: But to me, it`s not just about \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  . To me, it`s about Kevin McCarthy, and about how he has on his list of privileges, this ability to give this -- give all of this footage to \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  or whoever he gives it to. That kind of blows my mind.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 INSANA: -- and purposes.\par \par \uc2 WONG: Yes, and it`s also about what you`re talking about, which is the playing dirty thing.\par \par \uc2 INSANA: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WONG: They`ll do -- he`ll do but he doesn`t care how it looks or what the ramifications are or how ethical or unethical it seems. He`s just going to do what he wants his outcome to serve his outcome.\par \par \uc2 CAMPO: But it also speaks to how weak he is politically. I mean, this is the weakest Speaker in modern political history. So, I think we`re going to see a lot more actions like this, because he has to do whatever he has to do to appeal to the far right of the party, because it only takes one to call for a vote to remove him from office. I mean, that`s an incredibly weak position to be in.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It is, but right now, he has a very strong hand in just the fact that he is the Speaker of the House. None of us are and he`s handing this footage over to one single individual. And in all of this, I do want to remind our audience what this original story was about, the residents of East Palestine. These are people who just want to know if it`s safe to drink the water that they and their kids have a future. It is what all of us want.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, we`re going to dig into our MVPs of the week during a time of renewed culture wars. We`re going to highlight who won the week. Not all of them were good guys when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:49:04]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It is only February and already state lawmakers across this country have introduced over 300 Anti-LGBTQ bills. They range from bathroom bands to Tennessee leading the way on a bill to ban drag shows on public property. Well, our nightcap is still here. George, you know, I`m turning to you first. Your book "All Boys Aren`t Blue" has been under attack for years?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What do you think about this onslaught of bills? I mean, drag shows who even cares that much?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yeah. I mean, we have to get to the reality of the situation, a census came out, I believe it`s 2019 or 2020 census and it basically said that generation Z was about to be more nonwhite than white and was already identifying as 20% LGBTQ. And so, when we look at what is happening, the book bans, we will look at Roe v. Wade, when we look at Don`t Say Gay Bills, they are all tied together. This one census that is showing that the demographic in this country is changing so rapidly is the reason that all of these anti-LGBTQ bills are coming out. It is a reason why they are trying to force white women in particular to have more children.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:12]\par \par \uc2 It all goes hand in hand, they need one, they want to keep the heteronormative paradigm of this country intact. Two, they want to keep white men in power. And three, all of this, like I said, it just goes back to where this country has always started. This country has always oppressed people who were nonwhite, this country has always oppressed people who were not heterosexual as those dynamics are starting to shift rapidly with Gen Z as they are my favorite generation because they are ready for war at all times. We are watching the pushback primarily B against them. And I just happen to get caught up in the flow because I just happen to write a little book about my story about black and queer.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK. OK, then given where we are in culture wars, I`m guessing your MVP this week has got to be somebody in this mix?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes. My MVP this week is a good friend of mine. Her name is Hope Giselle. She is a black transwoman who is an author. She is an activist. She is a D.I. specialist and she is the first openly black transwoman to speak at \uc2 NASA\uc2  . She is doing the work to create safer spaces for Black LGBTQ people. She has always been about it. She has been someone who has protected B at times and has spoken up on behalf of my book at times. And she`s just a brilliant black woman who I think deserves all of her flowers now and deserves to be booked across this country because she is just amazing, fabulous, and she`s just about it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: She is the moment. BD, how about you?\par \par \uc2 WONG: Oh, well, I`m going to hop on the drag visibility wagon because -- well, actually --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What a wagon.\par \par \uc2 WONG: It`s a fabulous wagon. The -- actually was like -- it`s actually Broadway that I`m really into this week. I`m a proud member of the Broadway Community.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I`m -- Broadway always wins on this show.\par \par \uc2 WONG: Yeah, OK, great, great.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Always wins.\par \par \uc2 WONG: Well, Jinkx Monsoon, a really incredibly talented and very visible drag performer has taken over a role in Chicago and broken all box office records. Chicago has been running for like 20 years, or more than that. And so for someone to come in and not only breathe fresh, artistic life into a show, but to create that visibility, which we absolutely need to counteract all of this transphobia and drag phobia is really important. But also, I wanted to throw a shout out to the cast of Parade, the musical, which is having incredible antisemitic protests, based upon the content of the show, and how the cast and the production and the theater and New York City Center who are producing the show have stepped up and spoken out against it. I think there`s some kind of day of hate that is being organized. Have you ever heard of such a thing?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: No. But it`s being combated with a day of love and support for the community this weekend. Who`s your MVP, Mara?\par \par \uc2 CAMPO: My MVP is Rihanna.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, always.\par \par \uc2 CAMPO: She`s my MVP every week and it`s not because of the Super Bowl performance, though, that could have heard her friend respond well.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WONG: Hundred feet off the ground.\par \par \uc2 CAMPO: Amazing. It`s because of her Vogue cover. So, she was on the cover of British Vogue. And the photo is of her in front of her husband leaving, you know, he`s behind her.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: A step behind her --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAMPO: I`m sorry, not her husband --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAMPO: I`m so excited about a potential wedding, her partner. And she is in front of him and she`s leading him and he`s holding the baby and the baby is smiling and the baby looks so happy. And you know, the cover really triggered a lot of messages. And it`s because she is this very, you know, bold, confident pose. She`s leading the man. She looks like she`s in charge. But I saw something completely different. I saw a family unit that really supports one another. I saw a woman who is running a business and she`s learning how to juggle and manage a family. I saw two partners who are learning how to whose -- everybody can`t lead at the same time.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You`re giving me the chills.\par \par \uc2 CAMPO: It was amazing. But the best thing that I saw was the happy baby. That boy`s smile could light up a room and that tells you everything you need to know about how he`s growing up.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Misogynist, be damned. Ron, take his home.\par \par \uc2 INSANA: I`m going with \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  because one year after the invasion of Ukraine --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: -- to this day\par \par \uc2 INSANA: -- talking about a transformational character in so many different ways a comic actor who becomes the president of the country after having played the president of a country on TV, and then turning out effectively to be a hero for his nation. It`s just -- it`s such a transformative moment I think for them. It tells us a lot about what can be done. What individuals can do, what one person can do to change and bend the arc of history. I don`t see how he can`t be considered a hero and he`s gotten a lot of great support from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and from those in Europe but I think he stands head and shoulders above many other leaders right now with respect to what he`s been able to do in the last year.\par \par \uc2 [23:55:08]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Those are heroes across the board. I`m going to wrap up with an MVP that is not a hero, but certainly had an extraordinary week. We talked about it in our last segment, and it is \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  . It cannot go unnoticed that Kevin McCarthy, the Speaker of the House, is now working hand in hand with a known propagandist.\par \par \uc2 Now, thanks to Kevin McCarthy, \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  has 44,000 hours of sensitive government security camera video footage from January 6, what he does with it and the effect it could have, we do not know. But congrats \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  , you won the week, wake up America. This is happening on our watch. Mara Campo, BD Wong, thank you so much for being here. Ron Insana and George Johnson. Thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late with us. I will see you at the end of Monday.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020230225ej2o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for February 21, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris JansIng, Kristen Welker, Ben Rhodes, Erin McLaughlin, George  Solis, Elise Jordan, Kimberly Atkins Stohr, Shaquille Brewster, Shan Wu,  Tom Costello \par \uc2 10,590 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I`m Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 In a soaring speech before a huge crowd in Warsaw, President Biden frames the war in Ukraine as a battle between hope and fear, between freedom and dictatorship. How that compares to Vladimir Putin`s war of aggression and new threats against the West, and what all of it means for the prospects for peace? We`ll have live reports from Warsaw, Kyiv, and Moscow coming up.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  may not be going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , but he`s definitely going after his voters. How the Florida governor`s bid to win over police unions and donors could be a testing ground for his 2024 campaign?\par \par \uc2 And George Santos telling us something we already knew that he`s a terrible liar. Then he follows that up with more lies. The latest on the embattled Republican who now claims he wants to be the most transparent lawmaker in Congress.\par \par \uc2 But we start with a defining day for the U.S.-Russia relationship as well as for safety and security around the globe. Today, President Biden and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  gave dueling speeches, laying out dueling visions, both of the war in Ukraine and what the world order should be. Both say huge stakes, democracy and Ukrainian freedom on the U.S. side, while in Putin`s mind, it`s his country`s very existence. And neither man giving any indication they`re willing to back down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Over the past year, the United States has come together with our allies and partners in an extraordinary coalition to stand against Russian aggression. But the work in front of us is not just what we`re against. It`s about what we`re for. What kind of world do we want to build?\par \par \uc2 There is no sweeter word than freedom. There is no nobler goal than freedom. There`s no higher aspiration than freedom. Americans know that and you know it. And all that we do now must be done so our children and grandchildren will know it as well.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA: (Speaking in a foreign language)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The elites of the West are not hiding their goals. They -- as they say, trying to inflict a strategic defeat on Russia. What does that mean to us? That means they want to end with Russia. They want to turn the local conflict into a phase of global conflict.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker is in Warsaw with the president and Ben Rhodes served as deputy national security adviser in the Obama administration and is an MSNBC political contributor. So, Kristen, President Biden`s speech today was a powerful indictment of Russian tactics, both globally and specifically on the battlefield. But what did we learn about the U.S. position, and frankly, the U.S. strategy to find a way out of this conflict?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, MSNBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, we didn`t learn a whole lot about the strategy to find a way out of the conflict but what we did hear, as you say, was this incredibly strong condemnation of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his tactics. The president again, accusing him of crimes against humanity. That was a charge that was first leveled by the vice president over the weekend. The president building on that allegation, accusing him of brutality, Russians of rape, of other horrific crimes, and saying that there should be accountability.\par \par \uc2 I also thought it was striking, Chris, President Biden made the decision to take on part of what President Putin said today head-on where Putin said that he was forced into this war because Russia was potentially going to come under attack by the West. The president called that out as patently false, saying that that was just never going to happen. And then notably, he said that here we are, as we are just days away from marking the one- year anniversary of Russia`s war in Ukraine, Putin has failed at every turn.\par \par \uc2 The president is saying Kyiv stands strong, Kyiv stands proud, Kyiv stands free, and making the case that democracies have gotten stronger, \uc2 NATO\uc2  has gotten stronger, and really a call to action, Chris. So, if you think about strategy, that continues to be the strategy of the United States to keep \uc2 NATO\uc2  unified, to keep its global allies unified in this fight, to isolate Russia to end this war in Ukraine. It`s a challenge abroad. It`s a challenge at home because of course, with a sharply divided Congress, you do have some Republicans who are saying they don`t want to write a blank check for Ukraine. So, that is going to be the challenge for President Biden moving forward to actually execute on that call to action to keep allies and members of Congress at home unified behind this fight.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kristen Welker traveling with the president, thank you for that. So, Ben, listening to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  , he is not going anywhere. He seems intent on escalating this fight, in part by suspending the last nuclear agreement we had with Russia, the New START Treaty. So, I want to play part of what Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  said in response to that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATES: The announcement by Russia that it says suspending participation in New START is deeply unfortunate and irresponsible. We`ll be watching carefully to see what Russia actually does. We will, of course, make sure that in any event, we are postured appropriately for the security of our country and that of our allies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What do you make of what we heard Putin say today, Ben, is it gamesmanship or does it actually increase the threat to U.S. national security and International Security?\par \par \uc2 BEN RHODES, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it certainly makes things more dangerous, Chris. I mean, what you lose without a New START Treaty is any verification regime, any transparency, or insight into Russia`s strategic nuclear forces. And so, obviously, we would benefit from having that kind of arms control in place that`s why the United States and Russia and before that the Soviet Union have had arrangements like this for decades.\par \par \uc2 I think what it shows you is the degree to which \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is just -- you know, he`s just cutting the cord on any way back to a relationship with the United States and the West. Obviously, the invasion of Ukraine was the most extreme manifestation of that. The sanctions had been put in place have further driven, obviously Russia away from the West. And now, here he is tearing up kind of the last piece of the arms control regime between the United States and Russia.\par \par \uc2 And I think its symbol -- it signals the era that we`re in, which is an era of direct competition and confrontation between Russia and the West. And Putin -- you know, this is the only place left for him to go. But it`s a place that he`s relatively comfortable.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The White House really didn`t want this to be a split screen but it`s almost impossible not to make those comparisons because of the diametrically opposed views of what we heard from the two men, views of reality, but also forward-looking. And that`s what I want to ask you about, Ben, sort of this big picture that I think was maybe summarized by what the president said when he said what kind of world do we want to build and he frame that, you know, five years from now, your children, your grandchildren. Is that what`s at stake here?\par \par \uc2 RHODES: Yes. And I think it was smart of them. And look, the split screen, you can`t avoid it. An anniversary is not a time where people are laying out new detailed plans. It`s a time when you`re reminding people what this is all about. What are we fighting for? What is at stake here? And both Putin and Biden have their own ways of doing that.\par \par \uc2 Now, I think they`re also very smart to go to Kyiv yesterday because a year ago, I think very few people would have bet that a U.S. President, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , would go to keep before \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  . And that`s the ultimate symbol of his failure. He does not control Ukraine, he does not control Kyiv, he`s been humiliated, and his military has.\par \par \uc2 However, to your point, they`re still there. Russia is a big country. Putin shows no signs of going anywhere. There`s not uprisings against him. He`s entrenched in power. He`s finding some workarounds to the sanctions. So, the positive story is what Ukraine has been able to achieve I think if there`s some area of concern, it`s that Putin is just as entrenched as ever, and just as dug in as ever, and there are a lot of people dying.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think what President Biden is trying to do is signal a message of endurance. We`re in this for as long as it takes. And you know, it may not get better before it gets worse. You know, there`s a lot of violence. There`s a lot of suffering that`s taking place.\par \par \uc2 He wants to create that horizon. This is about big things like whether nations can make their own decisions for themselves. This is about the survival of freedom in this world. And that if we stick to this now, through some difficult days, the payoff five years, 10 years from now in Ukraine and around the world is going to be more freedom, more democracy, and a message that people do what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  did in Ukraine ultimately fail and are punished for it. And I think an anniversary is the right time to kind of pull back the camera and show people that picture of what we`re doing and why we`re doing it and where this is all going.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Which leads me perfectly to Ukraine, Ben, if you can stay with us. I want to go to NBC`s Erin McLaughlin, who is in Kyiv. So, this does bring us back to the question, Erin, of what Ukraine is willing to do, right? And do they have a plan? Is there a vision to end this war? We heard Kyiv`s security chiefs, they just this week that Ukraine would end the war by parking its tanks in Moscow`s Red Square. What should Americans know about Ukraine`s willingness to fight for months, even years if necessary? We have seen their resilience in this year one.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Chris. Well, earlier today, I sat down with a top Zelenskyy advisor, \uc2 Mykhailo Podolyak\uc2  , and he told me and was quite clear that they have no intention of sending Ukrainian tanks into Russian territory.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 They have no intention of seizing Russian territory. What they want is the Ukrainian territory that is currently being occupied by Russian forces. And in his view, that includes Crimea. That is the definition of victory in the eyes of Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 I asked him what the next six months will look like from his vantage point. Take a listen to what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MYKHAILO PODOLYAK, ADVISOR TO PRESIDENT ZELENSKYY: (Speaking in a foreign language)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If get the necessary types and quantities of weapons, primarily shells, and missiles because this is a very intensive artillery and reacts to artillery warfare, I think in six months, we will be approaching the finalization of this war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: I also asked him about those fighter jets that Ukrainian leaders have been repeatedly asking for. He told me that they are currently in talks with the Biden administration for those fighter jets. And they are confident, confident that the Biden administration will eventually sign off on fighter jets for Ukraine. Again, what Ukrainians see as absolutely essential for pushing Russian forces out of their country. A quite optimistic view here in Kyiv tonight, especially on the heels of the president`s visit yesterday that is being seen as a big boost of confidence for the entire country. Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Erin McLaughlin, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 So, Ben, the administration has tried to be judicious about the decisions it makes. There have been some delays, and critics have said too long delays and deciding to do things like yes, let`s get Germany to get some tanks in there. But the F-16s really are something much bigger. Do you see that happening? Do you -- I don`t know if sharing the optimism is the right word, but believe that that`s likely to happen, and what needs to happen if even six months is going to be realistic?\par \par \uc2 RHODES: I think that F-16s are you know very difficult for the Biden administration because that`s a weapon that could be used to directly attack Russian targets inside of Russia. I think it`s prudent to be talking to Ukrainians about it because one thing that you have to bear in mind is that Ukrainian pilots have to be trained to fly those planes. And so, you know, this could take six months or a year just even from a decision to have a capacity for Ukrainian pilots to fly those planes. So, I think you may want to be having those conversations now in case you want to make the move to that kind of capability.\par \par \uc2 I think, Chris, the bottom line is that they are being judicious in that you don`t want to start a war between the United States and Russia. That has been one of President Biden`s principles from the very beginning of this policy. And so, they`re going to be calibrating and deliberating when they provide certain weapons systems and how they provide them. I think they`re somewhat cautious about Crimea as well because they think that -- I think probably rightly \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  , you know, in his own wrong and warped worldview sees Crimea as a part of Russia, not just in terms of the annexation that he passed, but in some kind of deeper way. And so, they`ve been careful about that.\par \par \uc2 I think what`s going to happen now is you have this kind of grinded-out artillery battle that`s taking place. As the weather improves, you`re going to see probably offensives by both sides. We`ll see if the additional tanks and weaponry that have been provided to Ukraine makes a difference, or whether the Russian manpower advantage from some of the call-ups being done makes a difference.\par \par \uc2 And then there`ll be constantly reassessing. Where does this war look in three months? Where`s it look in six months? Are the Ukrainians making rapid advances that suggest they can push Russia out of all their territory, or is there some stalemate settling in and we have to consider what kind of diplomacy we want to bring to bear to help the Ukrainians?\par \par \uc2 I think right now, we`re going to go through the next chapter of this war, which is going to be the offensives that take place through the spring. And they`ll continue to be making decisions about heavier weapons based on how things look on the ground, what the needs are from the Ukrainians, and what the momentum is on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ben Rhodes, always good to have you on the program. Thanks for coming in. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And in our next hour, I`ll speak with someone who has negotiated face-to- face with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  , former U.S. ambassador to Russia, Michael McFaul. And at this hour, we`re getting a live look at the show of force in Ohio. You see there on the left of your screen. We just saw the governor. The \uc2 EPA\uc2  Administrator and governors of both Ohio and Pennsylvania, all in East Palestine to continue to address the toxic train derailment there. With anxiety increasing and residents complaining of rashes and headaches, nausea, and other symptoms, a new health clinic has opened its doors while just about an hour ago.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s George Solis from Ohio. So, George set the scene for us both in terms of that show of force and the response to the health concerns.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE SOLIS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: That`s right, Chris. Well, the head of the \uc2 EPA\uc2  , \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2  announcing that \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  was now going to be held accountable for all cleanup associated with the derailment site.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Everything from the soil contamination, the water contamination to any costs incurred by the \uc2 EPA\uc2  if they go into people`s homes, and address any of the concerns -- health concerns related to that. They`re also saying that the \uc2 EPA\uc2  will charge \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  three times whatever cost they incur if they find that this company is not doing what they said they`re going to do. They`re also asking the company to go to any public meetings at the EPA`s request. So, they`re really putting them in the hot seat so to speak.\par \par \uc2 As far as that health clinic goes work, a few residents are a little upset with how this is starting to play out because they were understanding that this might be a clinic where they might be able to see a doctor, get some tests done. Turns out this is really more of a place where they can go to get some information, maybe sign up for insurance, and maybe find out which doctor they can go to including their primary care doctor, to maybe further answer some of those health concerns that they`ve had here in light of this derailment. I want to play you a little bit of what the C -- excuse me, a little bit of what the head of the \uc2 EPA\uc2  said a little moments ago. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL REGAN\uc2  , EPA ADMINISTRATOR: We`re not going to leave this community behind. We`re going to -- we`re not going to leave this community to manage this aftermath alone. We are with you. But let me also be crystal clear. \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  will pay for cleaning up the mess that they created, and the trauma that they inflicted on this community.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: Yes, Chris. That was part of this tour, the head of the \uc2 EPA\uc2  and the other officials are going to some residents` homes, showing them that they are testing some of the air and water and that those concerns -- there`s concerns about their health are still being listened to, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: George, can I just go back to what you said about the health clinic because I read a number of local news reports in Ohio and they indicated that there were going to be doctors, nurses, and other professionals like folks who understand about toxic chemicals. None of that is available there?\par \par \uc2 SOLIS: Yes. So, the last information we saw from the Ohio Health -- Department of Health is they have nurses there and a toxicology expert, but no mention of doctors there. They`re saying they`re not doing any blood analysis there. They`re not prescribing any kind of medication or anything to that effect that this was really not about physical exams. Today is really more just about assessing and really recommending where people should go.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Got it. OK, George, thank you so much for that. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And in the wake of the derailment, Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  wants the nation`s freight railroads to improve safety immediately today. And we just heard George allude to some of this. Buttigieg announced a new package of reforms. His department will begin tracking the inspections of routes used by trains carrying hazardous materials. He`s also proposed electronically controlled brakes on certain trains carrying hazardous materials and for Congress to increase fines that the government can levy against rail companies that violate those rules.\par \par \uc2 And near Cleveland, an explosion at a metal plant killed one person and injured at least a dozen others. The force of that explosion was so strong on Monday. It left a crater behind, threw debris across the street, and sent a smoke plume into the sky that could be seen for miles. Investigators are still trying to figure out exactly what caused that explosion.\par \par \uc2 New Signs that may suggest a shift away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  within the GOP. How will the former president respond to the pressure? And polls now open in battleground Wisconsin. Why a Supreme Court race is being called the most consequential election of the year? And embattled Congressman George Santos calling himself a terrible liar as he digs himself even deeper into a hole. Does his party`s leadership care? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New signs of a Republican shift away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with donors and key Trump supporters taking a good look now at a potential 2024 challenger \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  . DeSantis is targeting Trump territory just off a one-day three-stop visit with police unions in New York, suburban Philadelphia, and suburban Chicago. He`s got a pro-police message to a group that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has owned since his first run for president in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s campaign cash. DeSantis traveled south from Tallahassee to Trump`s own backyard this week. They`ll both be in Palm Beach to meet with Republican donors. Those events just four miles and one day apart.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Kimberly Atkins Stohr, columnist for The Boston Globe and MSNBC The political analyst. Also with me, Elise Jordan, a former aide in the George W. Bush White House and State Department. She is also an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Kim, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is definitely getting Donald Trump`s attention. The former president took aim at him at that event last night. He`s been going after him on Truth social. He talks about having a bigger crowd than DeSantis. He`s been calling him a rhino, and then he went after him on Fox News. What are you hearing, though, about the Trump campaign overall? How worried are they about DeSantis?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, clearly, I mean, just from the evidence that we`re seeing, there is some worry there because DeSantis of all of the 2024 hopefuls so far seems to be the one going most directly at Trump`s base. He`s been the one that`s been fomenting the same sort of like culture war issues that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is known for, whether it`s over education and his attempt to stop the effort -- African Studies, AP classes, or shipping immigrants who cross the border to blue states, and now going to these cities and having this very pro -- you know, anti-crime pro-police message.\par \par \uc2 He`s really trying to aim straight for the same playbook as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a bid to be the heir apparent. So, you would see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , someone who in the past claimed credit for DeSantis being elected as governor in Florida now feeling pretty steam that he seems to be trying to be his biggest competitor in the primary now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Elise, what do you make of the DeSantis strategy, at least so far, right? He`s not going on the attack against Trump, but he is, as we said, going after the money, going after some of his base like the police unions.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 ELISE JORDAN, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Chris, he`s trying to lay the groundwork for his presidential bid clearly. His book is going to come out on February 28. And, you know, while he can try to shore up the loan order fraud, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  pretty clearly dominates with that. It`s been his thing since 2016 and he has really hammered home the crime message. So, kind of tough to make inroads there.\par \par \uc2 But I would argue that DeSantis -- \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is doing really well with the Republican donor class. I don`t necessarily see Trump supporters moving away. I think that there`s going to be, you know, 30 to 40 percent of Republican voters who are going to vote for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  no matter what. And of that percentage, how many of those would be willing to vote for anyone else, or will \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  convince them that any other nominee would be illegitimate?\par \par \uc2 I think there`s a strong possibility that even if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost, he`s going to pull so many voters from whoever the Republican nominee would be. And I -- and I don`t actually think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would lose the Republican nomination to DeSantis at this stage in the game.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, Democrats, Kim, seem to be taking DeSantis seriously, at least to the extent that Illinois Governor \uc2 J. B. Pritzker\uc2  slammed him. The DNC slammed him. They said actions speak louder than words. And it was President Biden and Democrats who pass the American Rescue Plan that funded first responders and law enforcement departments across the country. If \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  had his way, Florida would have had the resources to deliver for the law enforcement community.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got the mayors of New York City, Chicago, also going after DeSantis. I mean does this suggest in spite of a lot of them saying well, he hasn`t been tested on the national stage, that they are worried he could be a strong opponent to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 STOHR: Well, I think they are doing what they can at this juncture to go after who seems like they may be the front-runner. And DeSantis has -- I agree with Elise, I think it will be very hard to pull Trump`s most core base away. But we have seen some polling that there is not just among the establishment -- the establishment Republicans, but among voters, there may be some Trump fatigue. And we don`t know the extent that is yet.\par \par \uc2 So, Democrats at this point, are trying to go after whoever may be the front runner for now or for the future as best that we -- as best as they can. And we`ve seen someone like Pritzker going sort of, you know, point by point countering what DeSantis is talking about. I think we`ll probably see more of that. And that will shift as you know, more people get into this race and the race kind of shakes out a little bit more, but I think Democrats aren`t taking anything for granted now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s talk about the few -- the other people who might get into the race. And I wonder, Elise, if you think there is room for a more, I guess moderate Republican, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  today profiled New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  who says you can be as conservative and not be an extremist. Can that message resonate with Republicans who vote in primaries?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Chris, it`s a message that I love. But no, I don`t think that it will resonate in a Republican primary. I think we`re really seeing a repeat of 2016 shaping up and possibly \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  isn`t even going to be challenged as much as he was in 2016 when other candidates would at least criticize them. You see how most candidates really just shy away from attacking Trump directly because they know that the counter punch can really wound them. And so, they`re still walking on eggshells a bit. And so, this year, he hasn`t now -- if he stays out of legal trouble, he`s still in a pretty strong position.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Elise Jordan, Kimberly Atkins Stohr, thank you both so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Right now, polls are open in the most consequential race a lot of people probably haven`t even heard of. We will dig into what`s really at stake in that critical race in Wisconsin. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:01]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right now, Wisconsin voters are casting their ballots in a high stakes race with national implications. The primary battle is for an open seat on the state Supreme Court. And already it`s attracting a lot of eyes and millions of dollars. The runoff in April will decide the political makeup of the currently conservative court that will hear cases that range from abortion rights to election access.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Shaq Brewster from Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 So, the "Milwaukee Journal Sentinel" puts it this way. They say the race is, quote, "unmatched in its consequence to policy." Talk a little more about what`s at stake here on both sides.\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, you hear that description because this conservative court has been at the center of some really controversial rulings over the past couple of years. We`re talking about rulings that struck down COVID restrictions, forced an election just weeks into the pandemic, made drop boxes illegal, and also was at the center of redistricting, siding with Republican maps, legislative maps that favored Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So Democrats see this as a rare opportunity to flip the makeup of this court, while Republicans see this as an opportunity to secure some of that power. Look here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): In America`s perpetual battleground state is what could be the most consequential election of 2023. Wisconsin`s fight for control of the state Supreme Court making national headlines and prompting historic spending.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Madison liberals are trying to take over the Wisconsin Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They think your healthcare providers should go to prison for providing abortion care.\par \par \uc2 PROF. BARRY BUDEN, DIRECTOR, ELECTION RESEARCH CENTER, UNIVERSITY OF WISCONSIN: The person elected this spring will create a majority that will rule on everything that`s important in public policy in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Just like the midterms, a focus on abortion access, the procedure effectively outlawed across the state by an 1849 ban that was revived after the overturning of Roe. This court will determine its fate.\par \par \uc2 GRACIE SKOGMAN, LEGISLATIVE DIRECTOR, WISCONSIN RIGHT TO LIFE: This is from a pro-life perspective the most impactful election that we have had in decades.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:00]\par \par \uc2 TANYA ATKINSON, PRESIDENT AND CEO, PLANNED PARENTHOOD ADVOCATES OF WISCONSIN: The future of reproductive freedom hangs in the balance of one person`s election.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: Both sides, Wisconsin Right to Life and Planned Parenthood, running massive voter engagement operations.\par \par \uc2 ATKINSON: Planned Parenthood has been involved in Supreme Court races in the past. The goal is to be much more involved in the Supreme Court race.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: From in-person events and door knocking to text messaging and digital outreach, groups going beyond November`s efforts.\par \par \uc2 SKOGMAN: To my knowledge we`ve never done a program registering folks to vote in the way that we`re doing now. We`ve never had such a large grassroots effort.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: The race nonpartisan in name only, two conservatives and two liberals competing in February`s primary. The top two overall finishers will advance to the general election in April.\par \par \uc2 BUDEN: The race will affect certainly policy immediately in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: The winner will set the ideological balance of the currently conservative court through at least 2024. A potential flip would allow the court to revisit recent conservative wins that block the use of ballot drop boxes and projected gerrymandered legislative maps.\par \par \uc2 BUDEN: Abortion policies, election policies, the rights of unions, the power of the governor, educational issues, all of those things are ending up before the Supreme Court in recent years and are going to come up very quickly again. So just in terms of what policies are on the ground, this court will have an immediate effect.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER: And Chris, we`ve seen a steady flow of people going into the polling location behind me. It`s important to note here that it will be the top two overall candidates who will advance to the general election. So while it`s not likely, it is possible that two of the conservative candidates or two of the liberal candidates will win tonight, meaning we may know the ideological makeup of this court for years to come by this evening. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. You don`t usually see a lot of people going out to vote in February, especially in a primary. But given the amount of money spent on this and the stakes, I think this might be an exception.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Shaq. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And right now, 15 million people from California to Minnesota are under alerts, bracing for a massive winter storm. It`s expected to be at its worst tonight in the Pacific northwest and then make its way to the Minneapolis area by tomorrow. Minnesota could see blizzard conditions, including roughly a foot of snow for most. About a foot and a half in some isolated areas. And wind gusts of up to 45 miles per hour. New England is expecting snow and ice later in the week as that storm moves east. Buckle up.\par \par \uc2 Inside the Fulton County grand jury, the fascinating behind-the-scenes details we`re just getting from a juror who heard the testimony of key witnesses. We`ve got that for you next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:45]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For the very first time we`re getting some fascinating details from inside that Georgia grand jury that recommended perjury indictments in the Trump election meddling investigation. The Associated Press spoke to a former jury member who said she knew it was a major case when they were led down a staircase into a garage beneath a downtown Atlanta courthouse where officers with big guns were waiting. From there, they were ushered into vans with heavily tinted window and driven to their cars under police escort.\par \par \uc2 We also got some colorful details about the case`s biggest witnesses, from \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  to \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Shan Wu, a former federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with this, the juror can`t talk details of testimony, but I was struck by a revelation about an unnamed witness. Here`s what they said. At least one person who resisted answering questions became much more cooperative when prosecutors offered him immunity in front of the jurors. How often does that happen?\par \par \uc2 SHAN WU, FORMER FEDERAL PROSECUTOR: It`s very unusual, Chris, to have immunity offered on the spot like that. Normally that would be arranged ahead of time. So it`s possible that maybe it appeared that it was spontaneous to the grand jurors, but perhaps there had been discussion ahead of time. But if they just sort of like dropped it on that witness at that moment, that would be unusual because it`s a pretty heavy lift to decide to grant someone immunity.\par \par \uc2 And I have no doubt that the offering of immunity would tend to open the lips of some recalcitrant witnesses because that`s exactly the protection you`re hoping to have, and of course if they give you immunity, you no longer would have a Fifth Amendment privilege against self-incrimination. The immunity would destroy the ability to use that privilege.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We don`t know any of the specifics, but obviously it was enough that this grand juror noted it. Right. So obviously it was a dramatic moment. What`s usually the bar, especially in a case like this, for offering someone immunity?\par \par \uc2 WU: It`s a pretty hefty high set bar. Usually as a prosecutor, you want to have what`s called a proffer with a defense counsel where they`re going to lay out for you what the likely testimony would be and to show how valuable it is so that the prosecutors are comfortable giving that witness immunity. The worry, of course, for the prosecutors, is you give somebody immunity and it turns out that they really have done a lot of wrongdoing that you`re not aware of.\par \par \uc2 And now you`re kind of stuck with this immunized deal, which is not only unjust, but can greatly hurt the case because other people who may be charged may say, look, that person was the real bad actor, not me, and you gave them immunity. So it`s a very careful decision they have to weigh before they do that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So the juror also shared some thoughts on key witnesses. Again, she couldn`t talk about what they said, but she said \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  was funny and invoked privilege to avoid answering many questions. She said she was surprised when Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  politely answered questions and even joked with the jurors after fighting so hard to avoid testifying. And then she described Georgia`s Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  as, quote, "a really geeky kind of funny."\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 How much are jurors in your experience influenced by either famous faces or just the personalities of people who are on the witness stand?\par \par \uc2 WU: I think they are influenced by that a great deal. Sometimes a grand jury might even choose to decline to indict somebody if that person, the target has appeared and made a good impression. That`s rarely done because it`s a big gamble obviously to expose that witness to that sort of sworn testimony because they could create further problems for themselves.\par \par \uc2 With these particular folks, I thought what was really interesting was, you know, Graham being a good politician is of course charming and folksy with them even after putting up a big legal fight. The sheer amount of privilege issues this grand jury had to deal with is pretty unusual. But it does reflect the normal process to assert a privilege where you go question by question, rather than using some broad shield where I don`t even have to show up. You`ve got to show up, take it question by question.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Shan Wu, always good to see you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WU: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Embattled Congressman George Santos calls himself a terrible liar. But does that even matter to GOP leadership?\par \par \uc2 And the son of a prominent South Carolina attorney accused of killing two members of his family takes the stand to testify. What he`s telling juror about his family and the night of the murders. That`s coming up in the next hour of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:25]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Congressman George Santos is making a big admission that he`s a terrible liar. In another head-scratching TV interview, this time with Piers Morgan, the freshman Republican claims the lies about his life and resume weren`t about tricking anybody, and also revealing why he didn`t think he`d get caught.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, HOST: I`m just struck, not necessarily that a politician would lie, but that you would think no one would find out.\par \par \uc2 REP. GEORGE SANTOS (R-NY): Well, I`ll humor you this. I ran in 2020 for the same exact seat for Congress and I got away with it then, and I guess --\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Right. Well, that`s honest.\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: Stupid.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: So you thought actually they`re not going to find out.\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: No, I didn`t think so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let`s talk about it with Elise Jordan, former aide to \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and White House and State Department, and now an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 So thought he`d got away with it, Elise, and I guess you could make the argument that at least with Kevin McCarthy, he seems to be correct. Does none of this matter to the GOP leadership?\par \par \uc2 ELISE JORDAN, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Chris, Kevin McCarthy wants to keep his five votes and he can`t afford to lose one of those five votes. George Santos helps get him to 218. Then that`s what it is at the end of the day. He has to watch his Republican votes and with such a thin margin, he is going to look away from George Santos and let justice roll its course.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean --\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: He`s under investigation by so many different entities, though, right now, Chris. Like, this isn`t tenable for the long term.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But would you think, or maybe it`s happened and he`s just not listening. Would you think that at least Kevin McCarthy or someone in Republican leadership might say to him, look, we`re not getting rid of you, but you`ve got to stop talking about this all 