 NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It`s a travel hassle familiar to any family traveling with kids. Either shell out the extra cash for seat selections upfront or try to wing it at the gate.\par \par \uc2 Now \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  is rolling out a new seating policy to make the skies a bit friendlier. Allowing accompanying parents and adults to sit next to children younger than 12 without paying extra. That`s a big deal for parents like Nathan Herrig and his family of four.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HERRIG, PARENT: It takes away one of the most stressful parts of flying, which is, you know, what am I going to do with my kids on the flight.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Along with the ticketing policy, United says it`s also unveiling new technology that will open up more seats on its flights to help automatically keep younger children next to an adult in their party. Giving access to regular economy seats and preferred seats if needed. No extra fee.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s not uncommon to see seat selection as much as $50, $60, or $70 per person. And so if you`re talking about a family of four, that can run well over $200 just to reserve your specific seat.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The new feature will be available to families purchasing either regular tickets or basic economy tickets, which typically have more restrictions. The move comes as regulators, lawmakers, and the White House have taken sharp aim at so-called junk fees that airlines charge.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`ll prohibit airlines from charging $50 round trip for a family just to be able to sit together. Baggage fees are bad enough. Airlines can`t treat your child like a piece of baggage.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The airline industry says carriers try to seat families together often at the gate, but families sometimes buy seats together that cost more. Experts say United`s new boarding tool should remove some of the boarding stress for families.\par \par \uc2