 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:45]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: For the very first time we`re getting some fascinating details from inside that Georgia grand jury that recommended perjury indictments in the Trump election meddling investigation. The Associated Press spoke to a former jury member who said she knew it was a major case when they were led down a staircase into a garage beneath a downtown Atlanta courthouse where officers with big guns were waiting. From there, they were ushered into vans with heavily tinted window and driven to their cars under police escort.\par \par \uc2 We also got some colorful details about the case`s biggest witnesses, from \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  to \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Shan Wu, a former federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with this, the juror can`t talk details of testimony, but I was struck by a revelation about an unnamed witness. Here`s what they said. At least one person who resisted answering questions became much more cooperative when prosecutors offered him immunity in front of the jurors. How often does that happen?\par \par \uc2 WU: It`s very unusual, Chris, to have immunity offered on the spot like that. Normally that would be arranged ahead of time. So it`s possible that maybe it appeared that it was spontaneous to the grand jurors, but perhaps there had been discussion ahead of time. But if they just sort of like dropped it on that witness at that moment, that would be unusual because it`s a pretty heavy lift to decide to grant someone immunity.\par \par \uc2 And I have no doubt that the offering of immunity would tend to open the lips of some recalcitrant witnesses because that`s exactly the protection you`re hoping to have, and of course if they give you immunity, you no longer would have a Fifth Amendment privilege against self-incrimination. The immunity would destroy the ability to use that privilege.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We don`t know any of the specifics, but obviously it was enough that this grand juror noted it. Right. So obviously it was a dramatic moment. What`s usually the bar, especially in a case like this, for offering someone immunity?\par \par \uc2 WU: It`s a pretty hefty high set bar. Usually as a prosecutor, you want to have what`s called a proffer with a defense counsel where they`re going to lay out for you what the likely testimony would be and to show how valuable it is so that the prosecutors are comfortable giving that witness immunity. The worry, of course, for the prosecutors, is you give somebody immunity and it turns out that they really have done a lot of wrongdoing that you`re not aware of.\par \par \uc2 And now you`re kind of stuck with this immunized deal, which is not only unjust, but can greatly hurt the case because other people who may be charged may say, look, that person was the real bad actor, not me, and you gave them immunity. So it`s a very careful decision they have to weigh before they do that.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So the juror also shared some thoughts on key witnesses. Again, she couldn`t talk about what they said, but she said \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  was funny and invoked privilege to avoid answering many questions. She said she was surprised when Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  politely answered questions and even joked with the jurors after fighting so hard to avoid testifying. And then she described Georgia`s Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  as, quote, "a really geeky kind of funny."\par \par \uc2 [13:45:03]\par \par \uc2 How much are jurors in your experience influenced by either famous faces or just the personalities of people who are on the witness stand?\par \par \uc2 WU: I think they are influenced by that a great deal. Sometimes a grand jury might even choose to decline to indict somebody if that person, the target has appeared and made a good impression. That`s rarely done because it`s a big gamble obviously to expose that witness to that sort of sworn testimony because they could create further problems for themselves.\par \par \uc2 With these particular folks, I thought what was really interesting was, you know, Graham being a good politician is of course charming and folksy with them even after putting up a big legal fight. The sheer amount of privilege issues this grand jury had to deal with is pretty unusual. But it does reflect the normal process to assert a privilege where you go question by question, rather than using some broad shield where I don`t even have to show up. You`ve got to show up, take it question by question.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Shan Wu, always good to see you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WU: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Embattled Congressman George Santos calls himself a terrible liar. But does that even matter to GOP leadership?\par \par \uc2 And the son of a prominent South Carolina attorney accused of killing two members of his family takes the stand to testify. What he`s telling juror about his family and the night of the murders. That`s coming up in the next hour of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:25]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Congressman George Santos is making a big admission that he`s a terrible liar. In another head-scratching TV interview, this time with Piers Morgan, the freshman Republican claims the lies about his life and resume weren`t about tricking anybody, and also revealing why he didn`t think he`d get caught.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, HOST: I`m just struck, not necessarily that a politician would lie, but that you would think no one would find out.\par \par \uc2 REP. GEORGE SANTOS (R-NY): Well, I`ll humor you this. I ran in 2020 for the same exact seat for Congress and I got away with it then, and I guess --\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Right. Well, that`s honest.\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: Stupid.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: So you thought actually they`re not going to find out.\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: No, I didn`t think so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let`s talk about it with Elise Jordan, former aide to \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and White House and State Department, and now an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 So thought he`d got away with it, Elise, and I guess you could make the argument that at least with Kevin McCarthy, he seems to be correct. Does none of this matter to the GOP leadership?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Chris, Kevin McCarthy wants to keep his five votes and he can`t afford to lose one of those five votes. George Santos helps get him to 218. Then that`s what it is at the end of the day. He has to watch his Republican votes and with such a thin margin, he is going to look away from George Santos and let justice roll its course.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean --\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: He`s under investigation by so many different entities, though, right now, Chris. Like, this isn`t tenable for the long term.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But would you think, or maybe it`s happened and he`s just not listening. Would you think that at least Kevin McCarthy or someone in Republican leadership might say to him, look, we`re not getting rid of you, but you`ve got to stop talking about this all the time? Keep your head down. Do the work.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Do you think that someone who has lied the way that George Santos has lied is actually going to be willing to not draw attention to himself? It`s almost as if he somewhat enjoys the scandal that surrounds him. Going and doing an interview like that and putting himself up for interrogation when he knows that he has told so many lies. It really is just incredible and he needs to get help if not in prison.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: He says he doesn`t like the attention. But he also said that if he had to do it again, he wouldn`t run again but he hasn`t made a decision about whether he actually will run again. It does get, Elise, a little hard to follow.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Well, Chris, for now he`s in a good position. He`s making six figures in a legal way. He has all of the benefits of having a seat in Congress. He has a large staff. He has some nice perks. Why wouldn`t he just wait it out? He probably is less exposed even almost when he`s in office. But these investigations, you know, his \uc2 FEC\uc2  filing, it`s just a disaster. He had so many expenditures that were just under $200, which you don`t have to provide receipts for. There`s just so much there-there you wonder when is it finally going to be too much.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We shall see. Elise Jordan, always good to have you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest U.S. airlines just made it a whole lot easier and cheaper for you and your family to sit together on your next trip. What you need to know about United`s new seating policy, next. And it`s right here on CHRIS JANSING REPORTS.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:57:49]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A new family seating policy at one of the biggest airlines United just as spring break travel season is ready to take off. We get the money saving details from NBC News correspondent Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It`s a travel hassle familiar to any family traveling with kids. Either shell out the extra cash for seat selections upfront or try to wing it at the gate.\par \par \uc2 Now \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  is rolling out a new seating policy to make the skies a bit friendlier. Allowing accompanying parents and adults to sit next to children younger than 12 without paying extra. That`s a big deal for parents like Nathan Herrig and his family of four.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN HERRIG, PARENT: It takes away one of the most stressful parts of flying, which is, you know, what am I going to do with my kids on the flight.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Along with the ticketing policy, United says it`s also unveiling new technology that will open up more seats on its flights to help automatically keep younger children next to an adult in their party. Giving access to regular economy seats and preferred seats if needed. No extra fee.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s not uncommon to see seat selection as much as $50, $60, or $70 per person. And so if you`re talking about a family of four, that can run well over $200 just to reserve your specific seat.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The new feature will be available to families purchasing either regular tickets or basic economy tickets, which typically have more restrictions. The move comes as regulators, lawmakers, and the White House have taken sharp aim at so-called junk fees that airlines charge.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`ll prohibit airlines from charging $50 round trip for a family just to be able to sit together. Baggage fees are bad enough. Airlines can`t treat your child like a piece of baggage.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The airline industry says carriers try to seat families together often at the gate, but families sometimes buy seats together that cost more. Experts say United`s new boarding tool should remove some of the boarding stress for families.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s going to be better for airline gate agents who don`t have to try to play musical chairs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Tom Costello, thank you for that report.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got a lot to cover in our second hour of CHRIS JANSING REPORTS. Let`s get right to it.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230222ej2l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for February 19, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,464 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 February 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:00:46]\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON, MSNBC HOST: On this Presidents` Day weekend, two of our commanders in chief, one current, one former, are facing perhaps their greatest challenges in politics and in life. Tomorrow, President Biden will travel to Poland, where he`ll mark the one year anniversary of Russia`s invasion and war in Ukraine, with calls for Ukraine`s allies to stay the course.\par \par \uc2 As the U.S. accuses Russia of crimes against humanity in Ukraine and the Republican majority in the \uc2 U.S. House\uc2  threatens to curtail the billions of dollars in aid that has gone to Ukraine now going into a second year. Here at home, the earliest electoral challenges to President Biden are materializing.\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side, former Republican South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley has joined \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in pursuit of the GOP nomination in 2024. And 2020 Democratic contender \uc2 Marianne Williamson\uc2  is expected to make her own announcement that she`s running for the White House again. She`d be the first Democrat to challenge President Biden thus far.\par \par \uc2 And the country is wrestling with the news that our 39th President, \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  , has decided to receive hospice care at home this weekend at the age of 98. President Biden has been briefed on Carter`s health. Biden having been one of the Senate`s earliest supporters of Carter`s campaign for president.\par \par \uc2 I was just a young activist when President Carter took office. I`ve always appreciated his grace and our interactions over the years. My thoughts and prayers are with his family.\par \par \uc2 We start this evening with Senator Ben Cardin, Democrat of Maryland. Senator, first of all, thank you for joining us this evening. It`s perfect time to have a member of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee with us. So ahead, being that this is right ahead of President Biden`s trip to Poland tomorrow, he will speak to the world and Ukraine`s \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies from the same location he did last year when he told the world that Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  could not remain in power.\par \par \uc2 But as the war enters its second year, he must now contend with a Republican majority in the House that has threatened to end unequivocal aid to Ukraine as this war drought drags on. Your thoughts on that and the upcoming anniversary of this war, Senator?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN CARDIN (D), MARYLAND, FOREIGN AFFAIRS COMMITTEE: Reverend Sharpton, first, it`s good to be with you. President Biden has shown tremendous leadership globally in helping the Ukrainians defend themselves. I don`t think anyone thought that we would have this type of unity that is continuing now as we enter the second year of this unprovoked attack by Russia on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We saw President Biden provide the necessary help so the Ukrainians could defend it itself. I must tell you, at the Munich Security Conference this week, we saw unity among both the Democrats and Republicans that were there saying that we do need to continue to support Ukraine in these efforts,\par \par \uc2 But I would acknowledge we have challenges in the House of Representatives on the Republican side. This is not a time for us to weaken in our defense of Ukraine. They`re the front line of defending Democratic states.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me ask you another foreign relations question, Senator. Last week, the U.S. has called off its search for several aerial objects shot down earlier this month. These unidentified objects dominated the news for a few days when they were first spotted. President Biden played down any link between these objects and a Chinese surveillance balloon shot down off the South Carolina coast earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Still, that incident added to tensions between the U.S. and China. What can you tell us about the conversations you`ve had in the Senate on the Foreign Relations Committee regarding the spy balloon or any of these unidentified objects? Should people be concerned about the security of our airspace?\par \par \uc2 [17:05:15]\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: I think the fact that we were able to discover the balloon and take down the spy balloon indicates that we are ahead of the curve in defending our national security. There`s no question that China violated our airspace. They were using it for spying purposes. We were able to track it and shoot it down and make sure it could not compromise our national security.\par \par \uc2 I think this demonstrated that the United States can defend its borders, and I think the President handled it right. The other flying objects, we don`t know enough about it yet it might have been just innocent use of space where they shouldn`t have been. It could have jeopardized the safety of commercial flights. So the President did the right thing to make sure that public safety was prevailed.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You announced this weekend that Maryland has received $5.1 million from the Safer Communities Act, the bipartisan gun control package that passed in the Senate over the summer. That money will go toward gun violence prevention in your state.\par \par \uc2 And Senate Democrats this week reintroduced a bill that would expand federal background checks for all gun purchases in the aftermath of the shooting at \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  last week. Can we expect to see this Senate, where Democrats have a very narrow majority, pass more gun control legislation in this next two years?\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, first, let`s applaud the passage of the Safer Communities Act. It`s the first time in over a decade you`re able to pass significant gun safety legislation, and it will have an impact. As we see right now, money coming to Baltimore and Maryland to help in regards to violent crime.\par \par \uc2 We also providing help to our schools to deal with behavioral health issues. So that was an important first step, but it`s a first step. We need to do a lot more. I agree with Senator Murphy`s bill in regards to background checks. I also support abolishing these assault weapons. There`s a lot of things that we can do to make it safer for our community.\par \par \uc2 We have too many guns on the streets. We need to continue to pursue sensible gun safety legislation. I know that we`re going to try to do that, but I`ll be perfectly frank about this. I don`t have a lot of confidence that the House of Representatives will advance a gun safety legislation.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator, we thank you for being with us. But before I let you go, I want to bring in my next guest, the Governor of Maryland Wes Moore. And I know you both want to tell us why you think it`s very important that the new \uc2 FBI\uc2  headquarters be built in your state of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 You and the state of Virginia have just over a couple of months to make your final pictures to the \uc2 General Services Administration\uc2  . So very quickly, maybe 30 seconds each, tell us why it should go to Maryland. Senator, you first, then Governor Moore.\par \par \uc2 CARDIN: Well, first, I`m so excited that Governor Moore being on with Governor Moore. He`s providing great leadership in Maryland. Maryland -- Prince George`s County is the right place. It`s the best value for the taxpayers, and it`s the right location so the \uc2 FBI\uc2  can carry out its mission. But it`s also a matter of basic fairness.\par \par \uc2 Prince George`s County has been denied the types of federal facilities that the other areas around Washington had. This is the right decision for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  . It`s the right decision for the taxpayers, and it`s the right decision in regards to social justice.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Governor?\par \par \uc2 GOV. WES MOORE (D), MARYLAND: The Senator is absolutely right, and I`m thankful for his leadership on this. This is a legacy defining decision for this White House and for a White House that has been so focused on ensuring that there is fairness and equity in the way these decisions are made.\par \par \uc2 The clear answer really is Prince George`s County. Also, when you think about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , their current mission and increasingly their future mission is going to be around cyber threats. And if you think about the state of Maryland, the state of Maryland is the home of the NSA. The state of Maryland is the home of cyber -- U.S. Cyber Command.\par \par \uc2 The University of Maryland, it is home of four of the best HBCUs, historically black colleges and universities in this country. So there`s a workforce pipeline. When you include that with the transportation assets that Prince George`s County has, when you conclude it for the fact that we will beat any other jurisdiction on cost, and you include it and you include that with the administration`s commitment to equity, the true decision is going to be Prince George`s County.\par \par \uc2 So we feel very good that the decision for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  building to be in the state of Maryland aligns deeply with this legacy defining decision that the federal government is about to make.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:01]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, let me thank you both on that. Thank you, Senator Cardin. Let`s stay with Governor Moore. Governor on Wednesday, President Biden was in your state touting how his economic plans has helped to create 12 million new jobs, more than any president has created in four years.\par \par \uc2 Maryland`s unemployment rate decreased of 4 percent in December. How are the Biden administration`s policies impacting your state specifically?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, the fact that the President Biden just in the past two weeks has been to the state of Maryland twice has been huge. The first time was when he came in Baltimore and announced a joint venture, a joint investment that we`re making in the Frederick Douglass Tunnel, which is going to be responsible for 30,000 jobs, 30,000 jobs in the state of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 And then being down here in Prince George`s County in Lanham, where we were talking with our union, with -- at a union hall, talking about the importance of making sure that these are strong union jobs. If you look at Maryland`s economy, the truth is, over the past eight years, the U.S. economy has grown by 23 percent, GDP has grown by 23 percent.\par \par \uc2 During that same period of time, Maryland`s has grown by 11. Our economy is not keeping pace with where the national economy is moving. That`s why these partnerships that we have with the federal government, that`s why the strong relationships that we have with the White House is so important to Maryland. And for this to be Maryland`s decade, it means we are and we will continue to move in partnership with the Biden administration in order to make that happen.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let`s turn now to a former governor who has entered the 2024 presidential race, Nikki Haley. Nikki Haley has become the first major challengers to -- challenger to her former boss \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the Republican side. What qualities do governors present that would make them a good president? And I know you`re focused on being governor now, and you and I have talked for a long time, but running for President in the cards for you someday, Governor Wes?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, I tell you, I love being the Governor of Maryland because governors actually have an ability to get real things done very quickly. I mean, I think about in this first -- in the first three and a half weeks that I`ve been the governor of Maryland. We have been able to, on day one, release $69 million that was focused exclusively on things like making sure there`s an equitable rollout for the cannabis industry.\par \par \uc2 Making sure that we had $3.5 million of previously unreleased funds that went towards supporting abortion providers. And when I say that Maryland will be a safe haven for abortion rights, we`re putting an actual budget behind that shows what that looks like.\par \par \uc2 That in our first executive order that we were actually able to create a department of service and civic innovation where we`re now creating a pathway for every Maryland high school senior to be able to have a year of service after they finish high school. And by putting together an executive order that`s actually ordering all of our agencies and departments to be able to report on their minority business enterprise, women business enterprise, veteran business enterprise goals.\par \par \uc2 So that when we have a 29 percent goal in the State of Maryland, we actually plan on hitting those goals. Our ability to be able to create economic pathways for work, wages and wealth, we`re able to show in the first weeks what that looks like and actually have a balanced budget to be able to reinforce it.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that`s what people are looking for in -- from their governors. And I think here in the State of Maryland, we`re able to deliver on that and deliver on that quickly.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You answered why governors may have a distinct way of running for president. You totally avoided me asking whether it was in the cards for you. But I just wanted to stipulate that. We`ll go on -- because I want to go to switch gears to the executive order you signed this week requiring state agencies to report data concerning Maryland`s Minority Business Enterprise program in an effort to increase participation for minority and women on firms and state government contracts.\par \par \uc2 In 2013, the state set a 29 percent MBE goal, but it has never reached that level. How do you plan to attack this goal differently than what`s been tried in the past?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: By actually being intentional. You know, the challenge we`ve had is that we`ve had a 29 percent goal in the State of Maryland. But if you have a goal that you come nowhere near hitting without any real forms of accountability, it`s not a goal anymore, it`s a suggestion.\par \par \uc2 And the State of Maryland has had a 29 percent suggestion when it comes to MBE participation. And what we are doing by laying this out and by instructing all my Cabinet secretaries and all of our agency heads and saying, this is the goal, tell us where you are. Tell us the challenges you`ve had in getting there and what you plan on doing in order to fix it.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:02]\par \par \uc2 They now know that we have a chief executive who is going to take this seriously. And our ability to be able to create pathways for work, wages and wealth. Our ability to be able to address the fact that Maryland right now has an eight to one racial wealth gap that is hindering and harming all of us.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the racial wealth gap has cost this country $16 trillion in GDP and gross domestic product over the past two decades. And that`s not of a group, that is up in the entire country. And so by making sure that we are deliberate and intentional about being able to have participatory economics so that all of these groups that have historically been left out can now be included in this larger Maryland growth story is something that they know that the governor takes very seriously. And we will have all of our agency heads and Cabinet secretaries who are moving towards accomplishing that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And it`s not just good for those communities and the companies, as you say, it`s good for the country. It brings revenues back into the country.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you very much, Governor Wes Moore, for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, fighting for some turf. 2024 presidential hopeful \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is coming to New York to rally in one of Donald Trump`s most loyal strongholds. But first, my colleague Richard Lui with today`s top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev., very good Sunday to you. The White House says that President Biden is in touch with former President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  . Carter`s receiving hospice care at his home in Georgia. The President tweeted this afternoon, quote, "Jill and I are with you in prayer and send you our love."\par \par \uc2 Carter, 98, announced yesterday that after a series of hospital stays, he wants to spend his remaining days at home with his family.\par \par \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  resumes classes tomorrow, that`s one week after a gunman killed three people and injured five others on campus. Hundreds celebrated the lives of victims Brian Fraser and Alexandria Verner at two separate funeral services yesterday morning. The mode of still being investigated. The 43-year-old suspect was found dead hours after by suicide.\par \par \uc2 And most search and rescue efforts in Turkey were halted today after that 7.8 magnitude earthquake that struck February 6. The death toll in Turkey and Syria now standing more than 44,000 and is expected to still climb further.\par \par \uc2 More politics nation with Reverend Sharpton right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:21:40]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to PoliticsNation. Let`s turn to my political panel for their insights on today`s big stories. Joining me now is former Republican Representative Joe Walsh of Illinois and Democratic Strategist Kurt Bardella.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  will be spending Presidents` Day by meeting with police unions in large blue cities such as New York City and in Chicago, ahead of its Merrill race. Joe, what do you make of DeSantis aligning himself with these police unions?\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. JOE WALSH (R), ILLINOIS: Game on, Rev. Game on. Look, let`s be clear. The Republican Party, my former political party, is clearly still the party of Trump/trumpism. You know, I know we`re a weekend in to Nikki Haley announcing her run for the Republican nomination. She has no chance.\par \par \uc2 Republican voters want a son of a bitch. They want a jerk. They want an authoritarian. DeSantis is trying to steal that role from Trump, who has done very well with it. So this is DeSantis going right at Trump, trying to see if he can come across as every bit the culture warrior that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is. That`s what Republican voters want.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Kurt, Florida`s governor has definitely gotten into the head of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . Trump has been trying to come up with a nickname for his potential presidential rival, but it won`t be meatball Ron. Trump said yesterday it would be inappropriate to use that nickname.\par \par \uc2 Imagine Trump talking about what`s appropriate, but it almost feels as if Trump is struggling to steal the spotlight back from DeSantis in recent months. What`s your take on this?\par \par \uc2 KURT BARDELLA, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, I can`t imagine \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanting to use a nickname like that, given the fact that this is the same person who has a penchant for eating, you know, whoppers and Big Macs all the time. So that would probably boomerang back on him.\par \par \uc2 It just tells you how desperate Donald is to try to even get attention this way. I mean, think about it, we`re talking about the highest office in the land here. And the conversation on the Republican side is who can come up with the best nickname for something like that? I mean, if they really get that desperate, I guess they can tune in Monday night to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.mondaynightraw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.mondaynightraw}}}\uc2  if they need some really good nickname ideas because this is about the dumbest conversation you could possibly have when it comes to politics, the presidency.\par \par \uc2 But we should expect nothing less than a Republican nomination. The Republican contest, it`s a race to the bottom. And if it really comes down to who has the best or worst nickname, I don`t think that`s going to bode too well for Republicans in 2024, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Tomorrow, President Biden will be heading to Poland as he prepares to mark the one year anniversary of Russia invading Ukraine. The U.S. has formally determined that Russia is guilty of crimes against humanity. A recent poll shows that supports for sending weapons to Ukraine is waning in the U.S. down from 60 percent and made to 48 percent today.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:02]\par \par \uc2 Joe, what challenge will Biden have on the world stage and here at home to motivate people to continue his efforts in Ukraine`s fight against Russia?\par \par \uc2 WALSH: Hey, Rev., it needs to be said right at the outset that the President has done a masterful job up to this point of rallying the world and rallying this country in defense of Ukraine. This makes sense, right? Americans, rightly, don`t like prolonged wars.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be a challenge to the President to continue to make this case why this is so doggone important to keep defending Ukraine, especially now, in the face of what could be just an awesome and terrible Russian onslaught.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine needs help more now than they did even seven or eight months ago. President Biden, who`s done really well up to this point, Rev., he`s got to like kick it into overdrive and make the case to the American people as to why this is so important to keep continuing.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Kurt, the United States is concerned that China could soon provide Russia with lethal aid in its war against Ukraine. Earlier today, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  stressed to NBC News how important it is to maintain a good relationship with China.\par \par \uc2 Now this comes as frustrations remain 15 days after the U.S. military shot down a Chinese spy balloon that flew over the country. How well is the Biden administration handling these mounting tensions with China?\par \par \uc2 BARDELLA: Well, I think that they`re doing a very good job, Rev., and that they`re making very clear the unequivocal position of the United States and have taken appropriate action, like shooting down that Chinese spy balloon. I can`t think of anything more harmful to the global world order than if China decides to try to supply Russia with dangerous weapons. They`ll be used against the people of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And if you want to talk about something that would immediately mobilize the American people behind this war to increase support, to increase the call for supporting with weapons and financial resources, China getting involved that way would do that. You would see those poll numbers that you just referenced that the former congressman was talking about, you would see those numbers skyrocket and favor sporting Ukraine if China decides to get involved that way.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you, Joe Walsh and Kurt Bardella.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, does Nikki Haley have a Trump problem? I`ll explain in a moment. And later, will the city of St. Louis award reparations to his black residents? Much more PoliticsNation in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:31:59]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to Politics Nation. Former South Carolina Governor and U.N . Ambassador Nikki Haley has announced her candidacy for president on Tuesday, making her the fifth woman and the first woman of color to run for the Republican nomination. This morning, she appeared on Fox News Sunday, and was asked why she`s running for President. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.N. AMBASSADOR: I don`t want to wait for someone else to fix this. I want to make sure we get in there and fix this. I`m not a lawyer. I`m an accountant. I`ve never worked in D.C. and I think it`s time that we start putting a fire under what`s happening in Congress. I think we need term limits. I think we need mental competency tests for people over the age of 75. And I think we need far more transparency than we`re seeing today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Joining me now is Lauren Leader, co-founder and CEO of All In Together, a nonpartisan, nonprofit focused on engaging women to participate in American politics.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining me today. Let me go here, first Ms. Leader to start. You recently wrote an op-ed for political highlighting that Nikki Haley has a, "woman problem," saying she may not have a fair shot at running a campaign as a female candidate among the GOP. Why do you believe that Haley may be at a disadvantage running for president in today`s Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN LEADER, ALL IN TOGETHER CO-FOUNDER AND CEO: Well, I think you have to start with the fact that every woman that runs for the office of the presidency is that an automatic disadvantage because it`s no one has -- no female has ever won before. And any every woman that it runs is breaking ground in their own way.\par \par \uc2 You know, in 2016, obviously, there was just a tremendous amount of well documented sexism, that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  had to contend with it every step of the way. And in 2020, we saw that again, when there are less than six women were running for president on the Democratic side. And that`s in a party that mostly embraces diversity, embraces gender equality.\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side, the record is less good. And it feels that already wasn`t great. When we -- when Carly Fiorina ran in 2016, she dealt with just a barrage of overt sexism primarily from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . And the party that Haley is running in today has gone further right. And some of the groups that underpin Republican politics, including the Proud Boys and other sort of extremist white supremacist groups that have gotten a larger and larger share of Republican voters. And frankly, just mindshare, in the last few years, are a valid anti-feminist.\par \par \uc2 So -- and then you add the media to that. And we already saw this week, my colleague on CNN, Don Lemon, who you know, just hit a nerve in terms of the sort of blatant everyday common sexism that women in the public sphere have to contend with. So it`s not just the environment which is already very difficult but it`s also the politics of Republican party today.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:07]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, abortion became a major issue in the midterms after the Supreme Court overturned Roe vs. Wade particularly, it became a concern for voters that are female voters. Nikki Haley has supported restrictions on abortions throughout her career, although she would not say this week whether she supports a federal ban. Do you think that position will hurt Haley with many female voters she`s hoping to appeal to?\par \par \uc2 LEADER: Yeah, and I think that`s a real question. And I think she`s staying clear of it for reason. I`m actually not sure that they figured out exactly the lane they want to go in. And, I mean, she has been staunchly anti- abortion her entire career. She`s supported legislation that excluded any exemptions for rape and incest. But she`s running at a time when abortion politics has totally changed, not just on the left, but on the right. 69% of Americans polled in a recent poll with Gallup this week say that including Republicans say that they do not agree with the restrictions. How far is restriction on abortion, it`s gone. And even one of her own protegees Nancy Mace in the State of South Carolina has come out to say the Republican Party has gone too far.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s going to be -- it`s problematic for every Republican running because voters showed us in 2022, just how angry they are about the end of Dobbs. So the question is going to be for a woman Republican running today, how does she walked that line, someone who`s always been anti- abortion at a time that women voters, especially the moderate women voters that she is saying she wants to win over are really uncomfortable with how far abortion restrictions have gone.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Women of color in politics like Haley, who`s the daughter of Indian immigrants are twice as likely than other candidates to be targeted with misinformation and disinformation, and are most likely to be targeted with sexist and racist abuse, according to research from the \uc2 Center for Democracy and Technology\uc2  . And of course, Hayley is challenging former President Trump, who has a long history of making bigoted attacks against his rivals. How do you see this playing out on the campaign trail?\par \par \uc2 LEADER: Yeah, well, that`s exactly right. And there`s an avalanche of reporting over the last few years that has shown exactly what that report showed, which is that women in the public sphere, women when they run for office are absolutely subject to just an avalanche of disinformation and smears online. I don`t think she`s going to be an exception to that.\par \par \uc2 I am bracing myself for what is going to be very likely, a very ugly, Republican primary and presidential contest. And you know, I give her a lot of credit for running and being unintimidated by that. So far, she`s shown our propensity to hitting back. All the research actually shows us the right answer that the only way to combat online disinformation is to take it on. And a lot of other candidates when they run for office around the country have struggled with this, just like what do you do when there`s an, you know, just a barrage of disinformation coming at you.\par \par \uc2 And again, whether you`re Republican or Democrat, like this is about democracy. Everybody that runs should be running with a fair and level playing field. Women of color, regardless of party should not be smeared for their race or their gender. And I really think we need to keep talking about this rug. And then the next, you know, year and a half, like we have to hold the media, hold each other accountable, hold the political world accountable, because at the very least, we need to ensure that people who step up to run are protected, you know, from the worst of what the internet can throw at them.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I`m out of time, but I have to follow up on that because --\par \par \uc2 LEADER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: -- it is sadly, not surprising that Nikki Haley has faced a racist backlash from the right. However, some Democrats have also made some questionable attacks in particular, some critics have accused her of quoting white washing her pants by going more American sounding middle name. Should Democrats be more thoughtful in the way they criticize Haley? Brief briefly, please.\par \par \uc2 LEADER: 100%, it`s not OK, regardless of political party. If Democrats want to create a world where diversity and politics is the norm, then we are absolutely as responsible as anyone on the right and the standard needs to be high and we need to hold everyone to it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Lauren Leader, thank you for being with us. Coming up, more mayors across the country are exploring options for reparations in their cities. One of them joining me next. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:43:01]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to Politics Nation. The city of St. Louis finds itself in the middle of both our nation`s reengaged conversation around policing reform and the evolving push for several cities and even some states to explore reparations. As the city`s progressive, mayor fights on dual fronts to prevent state control of her police department and to repair decades of systemic harm to the black residents of her city.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now St. Louis Mayor \uc2 Tishaura Jones\uc2  , a Democrat. Mayor Jones, thank you for being with us tonight. In December, you signed an executive order establishing a nine member volunteer commission, all St. Louis residents of diverse backgrounds to recommend opportunities for reparations in the City of St. Louis, it`s my understanding that you`ve been working with other mayors and exploring options for reparative justice in this city as part of a coalition of mayors looking at the issue, as a number of cities and state, the state of California being one either exploring reparations or implementing them. What is it about St. Louis specifically and its history that prompted you to take this step?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR TISHAURA JONES, (D) ST. LOUIS: Well, thank you for having me, Reverend Al. It`s honored to be with you this evening. St. Louis is no different than any other urban city that is hyper segregated, and has experienced deep restrictions and other intentional policies that have widened the racial wealth gap. We`re overdue to at least start the conversation about reparations and what that would look like. And reparations is more than just a payment. It`s also looking at the policies that created the environment where black family is worth 10 times less than a white family.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, St Louis only got local control of its police force 11 years ago through a statewide referendum. I understand that Republican state legislators in Missouri have introduced legislation that we return control of the St. Louis metropolitan police department to the state. With the governor appointing four commissioners to serve alongside the president of the St. Louis Board of Aldermen. That board would take control of St. Louis PD at the end of August. What in your view is behind this push to return control of your police department to the state?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, Reverend Al, this is a slap in the face to Missouri voters. They voted overwhelmingly 11 years ago to return the police department to local control and every police department across the country except Kansas City, Missouri has local control of its police department and Kansas City under Mayor \uc2 Quinton Lucas\uc2  is actually suing its state or suing the State of Missouri for local control of its police department. And this is also happening in Jackson, Mississippi under Mayor Chokwe Lumumba. You know, there is a -- I think that this is a concerted attack to try to take away leadership from black leaders around this country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Before we run out of time, Mayor I want to get your reaction to the now overturned conviction of former Missouri prisoner, Lamar Johnson who served nearly 30 years for murder that he always maintained he did not commit. His 1994 conviction built largely on eyewitness and informant testimony that has since been called into question. And on Tuesday, a St. Louis Circuit Court Judge exonerated Johnson for that murder after some three decades of incarceration. What`s your take away Mayor Jones?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, I couldn`t be happier for the family of Lamar Johnson. He served 28 years for a crime that he didn`t commit and also want to commend the circuit attorney and the other lawyers for not giving up. The one good thing the Missouri legislature did in 2021 was passed the law to set, to allow for circuit attorneys and other DAs to start processes like this, to free people who had been unjustly detained and unjustly incarcerated for decades.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Mayor Jones, thank you for being with us. More Politics Nation just ahead. And tonight I want to recommend a new documentary that I`m interviewed in MSNBC Films presents, When Truth Isn`t Truth: The \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  Story. It is a four part -- a new four-part series which explores the former prosecutor and man`s rise to power his fall from grace and how little he changed in between. Catch episode one tonight on MSNBC at 10 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:52:57]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: On this past Wednesday, hundreds joined us as we marched in Tallahassee, Florida against Governor DeSantis and the College Board trying to eliminate and censor aspects of black history. And everywhere, I`ve gone since then people have been outraged. I`m in Louisville, Kentucky tonight, where this morning I preached for Reverend Walter Malone and his 40th Pastoral Anniversary. And from there I toured the amazing Muhammad Ali Center, which I will talk about in details in the future.\par \par \uc2 I even went on late night, The Late Show With Stephen Colbert and argued about this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: He`s trying to use the race wedge so Baby Trump that you call DeSantis is trying to use black history month to go and run for president and some of us are saying that our history is too important to us to let that happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We cannot allow the history of blacks or others because others will be threatened if it`s not stopped here to become some political prop for ambitious politicians that want to play the race card to advance their appealing to a base that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has helped to build and embellish. If it is gotten away with in Florida it is a threat here in Kentucky and Mississippi and all over this country. We cannot see blacks, we cannot see Latinos, we cannot see those in the communities that have been under attack by DeSantis be treated in a way less than fair and just it is something that we must stay on. We`re going to continue to fight in Florida. He calls this a national concern. It`s a concern of the LGBTQ community, black community, Latin community and many whites that stood with us. History must not be censored.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:06]\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thank you for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5 p.m. Eastern. American Voices with guest host, Michael Steele, starts at the top of the hour.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020230220ej2j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for February 16, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ayman Mohyeldin, Michael Steele, Claire McCaskill \par \uc2 7,802 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 February 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Check out the latest episode of the podcast, Roy Wood Jr., also hilarious, wherever you get your podcasts. Leslie`s current tour, Leslie Jones live is also happening right now until June with Lenny as her opening act. Oh, I will definitely be seeing that. That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". We love them.\par \par \uc2 ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AYMAN MOHYELDIN, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN. The partial Fulton County grand jury report is out as the Proud Boys move to subpoena Trump in their seditious conspiracy trial. Tonight, Congressman Daniel Goldman on the big moving pieces in the Trump investigations. Plus --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We don`t yet know exactly what these three objects were.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: The president shares what he knows about the UFO`s America is shooting down. Then --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: What I am saying is I don`t kick sideways. I`m kicking forward.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: As Trump`s first challenger sidesteps Trump, Claire McCaskill and Michael Steele on the policy issues Republican candidates cannot avoid.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: A federal ban on abortion, would you sign that?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: We need consensus on this. Our goal --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Consensus on abortion?\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And what we know about John Fetterman`s health as the senator announces he is being treated for clinical depression when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN (on camera): Good evening, everyone from New York. I`m Ayman Mohyeldin in for Chris Hayes. There is a subpoena out for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  this evening. Lawyers representing leaders of the right-wing group known as the Proud Boys who are currently on trial for seditious conspiracy for their role in the January 6 attack are demanding the ex-president`s testimony as part of their defense.\par \par \uc2 Trump, of course, infamously delivered this message to the Proud Boys during a presidential debate with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WALLACE, FORMER ANCHOR, FOX NEWS CHANNEL: Are you willing tonight to condemn white supremacists and militia groups and to say that they need to stand down and not add to the violence in a number of these cities as we saw in Kenosha and as we`ve seen in Portland?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Give me a name. Give me a name. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: White supremacist and right-wing militia.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : Who do you like me to condemn?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Proud Boys, stand back and stand by.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Now, these are not the circumstances under which many people would have predicted Trump would be subpoenaed because quite honestly, the ex-President is embroiled in so many various investigations. There`s also the special grand jury investigation into Trump`s election interference down in Georgia following his 2020 loss. We all remember that. We are awaiting what Fulton County District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  has called imminent decisions on indictments in that case.\par \par \uc2 Today, her office released a heavily redacted preview of the special grand jury`s final report. And while it offers little in terms of specifics, the preview does note this. "A majority of the grand jury believes that perjury may have been committed by one or more witnesses testifying before it. The grand jury recommends that the district attorney seek appropriate indictments for such crimes where the evidence is compelling."\par \par \uc2 Now, we don`t know which witness or witnesses the special grand jury thinks lied under oath in that testimony. The wide-ranging inquiry spoke to dozens of folks with knowledge of the case including a number of key Trump allies like his former National Security Adviser \uc2 Mike Flynn\uc2  , two of his lawyers \uc2 John Eastman\uc2  and of course \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  , and one of his top advocates in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  , none other than \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  who like Trump, called Georgia Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  about altering the results of the election. For his part though, Graham says he is confident in his own testimony.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I have no idea what their plans are, who they - - who they`re looking at.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What about this recommendation for the indictments, sir?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: No one has ever talked to me about anything. I went -- honest testimony as I saw it so, you know, I don`t know what they`re going to do.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you think -- would they be in touch with your lawyers? Do you have any idea if they have any concerns about --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I have no idea. And Stan, don`t ask me. No one`s ever said a word to me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you see this as a closed chapter from your end?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I hope so. Yes. Let`s put it this way. I did what I was asked to do. I testified as honestly as I could with the privileges I had. They were very professional.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Now, we really don`t know what next steps the Fulton County investigation will take, but one Atlanta attorney who represented the Georgia State Assembly in the special grand jury inquiry says that he thinks a Trump indictment is likely. "There is no chance that Willis goes with no indictment of Trump."\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, we`re going to wait like everyone else and see if that does in fact pan out, but Trump is also facing potential trouble from special counsel Jack Smith`s federal investigation into both January 6 and of course his mishandling of classified documents down in Mar-a-Lago. Smith has subpoenaed former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  for his testimony related to the January 6 event. Pence is probably the most pivotal witness outside the ex-president himself but he is vowing to fight that subpoena using somewhat novel legal logic involving the Vice President`s role as both the leader of the Senate and at the same time a member of the executive branch.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:24]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I`m going to fight the Biden DOJ subpoena to appear before the grand jury because I think it`s unprecedented and it`s unconstitutional. My fight is on the separation of powers. My fight against the DOJ subpoena very simply is on defending of the prerogatives that I had as President of the Senate to preside over the joint session of Congress on January 6.\par \par \uc2 We`re prepared to take this fight into the court and if needs to be taken to the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Now, CNN and the Wall Street Journal are now reporting that Trump`s coup-supporting Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  has also received a subpoena from the special counsel`s office although NBC News has not yet confirmed that report. Along with Pence, Meadows of course you may recall was one of the most pivotal witnesses to Trump`s attempted coup. He was the point person. He was the one fielding messages from the various accomplices to that big lie. And his involvement, his involvement in all of this, in this single case, signals that Smith`s office is looking very closely at the ex-president`s inner circle, his trusted confidence, which means we could still see a subpoena for Trump himself in this case as it moves forward.\par \par \uc2 But for now, as of tonight, the only subpoena for Trump is again, from that Proud Boys trial. Lawyers for the militia group are likely trying to pin the blame for their client`s behavior on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . And while it is true that Trump bears much of the responsibility for in citing the deadly riot on January the 6th, he did not work alone.\par \par \uc2 Jacob Glick researched the Proud Boys and other January 6 agitators extensively when he served as investigative counsel during the second impeachment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for inciting the attack. And he was -- he has continued, rather, that research to this day in a new piece for Lawfare titled "The Long Descent To Insurrection." He explores the various ways that the proud boys and other right-wing gangs both self-radicalized against democracy and towards political violence. All of this in the months leading up to January the 6th.\par \par \uc2 Jacob Glick joins me now. Jacob, it`s great to have you with us this evening. So, I think a lot of people this evening would want to know what role does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  play as something of a messianic figure among these militia groups? Do they just simply follow him and how much do they hang on every word he said?\par \par \uc2 JACOB GLICK, FORMER INVESTIGATIVE COUNSEL, JANUARY 6 COMMITTEE: It`s great to be with you. Well, I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was an inspiration and an opportunity for these extremist groups like the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers. For people like Stewart Rhodes and \uc2 Enrique Tarrio\uc2  they really saw Trump as a moment, an opportunity for them to exploit. His rhetoric exploit uh his willingness to flirt with political violence to bring themselves into the public square and to see themselves, style themselves as this kind of paramilitary guard that would impose their own vision of law and order onto the country.\par \par \uc2 And we saw that occur as early as -- you know, earlier in 2020 when Stewart Rhodes testified to the select committee in his deposition that he wanted Trump to invoke the Insurrection Act over the summer for The Oath Keepers to go out into the streets during BLM protests and detain protesters. So, that`s something that the Oath Keepers and the Proud Boys leadership really hope \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would do and act aggressively. And for rank-and-file members of the Oath Keepers in the Proud Boys and of course, thousands of other Americans who were involved in far-right extremism leading up to January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s rhetoric, his actions to vilify \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  in Michigan for her COVID restrictions and to say stand back and stand by to the Proud Boys -- we received testimony in the Select Committee from a proud boy Jeremy Bertino for example that that comment at the debate, that tripled the membership in his view of the Proud Boys in his chapter.\par \par \uc2 So, we saw time and time again that Trump`s words and actions provided an opportunity for these groups to mainstream themselves and legitimize themselves in the months leading up to January 6th. And in that way, he was extremely important.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, so talk to me a little bit about this because on one hand, you had the demagoguery of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , the disinformation of his big lie. But as you have reported, these groups also self-radicalized against democracy independently from that demagoguery and the big lie.\par \par \uc2 GLICK: Well, so, again, this is coming off of my work on the January 6 Select Committee looking at the witnesses that we deposed over and over again telling a very similar story about radicalization into an anti- democratic coalition that coalesced at the Capitol but didn`t start at the Capitol. And I think Trump is a central figure in that Journey.\par \par \uc2 You have Trump coalescing a lot of folks against COVID-19 safety measures. In March and April, you had \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  villainizing Black Lives Matter protests over the summer and blaming them all on Antifa. And that`s the exact story that a lot of these witnesses told the select committee as they moved closer and closer to the moment that would take them to the Capitol. And so, I think there`s a much more enduring anti-democratic movement that the evidence the Select Committee uncovered than a single bad day. And that`s our work now is to try to confront what that means.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:29]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: In that recent article that I just cited a moment ago, you write, our evidence shows that the violent energy that burst forth on January the 6th had been cultivated during the tumultuous months prior including in the most fascistic fantasies of Oath Keepers leader Stewart Rhodes and chief Proud Boy Enrique Tarrio. Can you speak to us for a moment about what exactly happened in those months leading up to the insurrection?\par \par \uc2 GLICK: Sure. So, I think the most important development to think about is really the Black Lives Matter protests over the summer of 2020 which of course were overwhelmingly peaceful and the historic racial justice movement across the country. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and many of his allies and including these extremist groups chose to paint these events as largely set up operations by Antifa to destroy our society as they saw it.\par \par \uc2 And so, that became a blueprint for these groups especially the Oath Keepers and the Proud Boys to take it upon themselves to go out and police protests with weapons to try to complement what law enforcement was doing and take it upon themselves to take vigilante action against what they saw as Antifa and oftentimes take action against violence -- sorry, against peaceful protesters too.\par \par \uc2 And so, what we saw in those summer months was a permission structure for these groups to go out and take the law into their own hands and take weapons into their own hands and say we`re the force of law order, we are - - we are the people who are able to do justice. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did nothing to stop this tumble into the abyss of authoritarian and anti- democratic thinking. In fact, he stoked that violence with the stand back and standby comment a few months later.\par \par \uc2 So, that was really the key moment where in our depositions we saw. These individuals think that political violence was a real path forward for them.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, Jacob Glick, thank you so much. It`s been a pleasure. I greatly appreciate your reporting on this and much more. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GLICK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Congressman Daniel Goldman represents New York`s 10th Congressional District. He previously served as Lead Majority Council during the first impeachment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . Congressman, it is great to see you again. First, what do you make of this subpoena for the ex- president? I mean, does it surprise you at all?\par \par \uc2 REP. DANIEL GOLDMAN (D-NY): No, it doesn`t surprise me. Many of these defendants have tried to blame their conduct on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . It`s a losing legal strategy but it does bring to the fore how central the former president was to everything that happened in the lead up and including January 6th. And certainly, you know, if you want to talk about weaponization of the federal government, then we ought to look at what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not only during his entire presidency, but certainly, in November, December, and January while he was still president.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Let me switch to another subpoena and that is the former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and him defying this current subpoena from the special counsel. I want to play for you more of his excuse as to why he won`t comply with it. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I would say in this very moment, it`s also wrong to establish a precedent where a legislative official could be called into a court by an executive branch. That runs against literally hundreds of years of not only American law but common law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: In Mike Pence`s version of the American Constitution, the Vice President of the United States cannot be subpoenaed by anyone. He can`t be subpoenaed by Congress, he can`t be subpoenaed by the Department of Justice. As a member of the legislative branch yourself, what do you make of his interpretation of when a vice president can be subpoenaed?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Look, it`s grossly overplayed and it`s quite clear that the former Vice President is trying to stall for time figuring that the special counsel will have to wrap up his investigation, you know, and make a decision about whether or not to indict at some point this year. And if he can come up with a colorable claim that can go to the Supreme Court, then that`s the Trump way of losing -- of winning by losing so to speak which is delay, delay, delay.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t make any sense from a legal perspective, but it is truly shocking to me that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  would not want to do the right thing, provide his testimony to the Department of Justice to explain to them the conversations that he had with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , with \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  , with others, with you know, \uc2 John Eastman\uc2  . The list goes on and on that would not fall under his role as the President of the Senate. So, it is disappointing but not surprising.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:21]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Let me ask you about what this means, you know, about the potential subpoena for fellow members of Congress, Jim Jordan, Scott Perry, for example. People with legitimate claims to the legislative branch, would that be appropriate if they were subpoenaed?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Yes. Look at what happened with Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  who fought his subpoena in the Fulton County District Attorney`s investigation and he lost and he had to go testify. You`re not above the law if you`re a Member of Congress. You don`t have a blank slate to just do whatever you want under the Speech and Debate Clause. Obviously, there are things we do that fall squarely under the Speech and Debate Clause, but it is not absolute immunity from any subpoena.\par \par \uc2 And what, you know, private conversations that Scott Perry had with Jeffrey Clark at the DOJ or otherwise has nothing to do with the Speech and Debate Clause. So, that would be a loser of an argument. But again, we`re running out of time right now. The Special Counsel is running out of time. And so, you know, we know that these members of Congress avoided testifying. They defied their Congressional subpoenas, lawful Congressional subpoenas from the January 6 Committee. They are now on a committee in Congress that has jurisdiction to investigate ongoing investigations into themselves.\par \par \uc2 So, we are dealing with people who do not abide by the rule of law and we got -- we have to make sure that we are enforcing that for everyone.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Speaking of your colleagues, your Republican colleagues there in Congress, they seem eager to talk about anything and everything but these specific cases against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . What is your assessment of their short tenure in power so far?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Look, I think what is very clear is they have reached a conclusion and now they`re trying to fill in the evidence. But as I`ve tried to do in some of the hearings we had last week and many of my other terrific Democratic colleagues are doing, we`re just bringing the facts. We are pointing out what actually has happened, what the truth and the evidence actually shows. And what we`re seeing is that those facts, that truth, that evidence does not support any of the overreaching partisan allegations that they have made from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 And so, I expect that we will continue to bring the American people the facts and the evidence and that that will undermine the conclusory allegations that the Republicans have made from start to this point in this Congress.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Congressman Goldman, really quickly before I let you go, do you have any thoughts on the developments today in Fulton County with the special grand jury redacted report that was released and where you think that case might go next?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: You know, it`s very interesting that they released that -- those small excerpts of the larger report which was clearly I think when you read the excerpts, a very thorough report. And I look forward to reading the rest of it or learning about it if there is an indictment. But it is noteworthy of course that they pointed out that someone did perjure him or herself in front of them. We have known uh for a long time that president - - Former President Trump and those around him have a very unsettling relationship with the truth. And so, I`m very interested to see who uh would be in the crossfire of a perjury charge.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: As we all are waiting to hear from that eminent decision from the uh Fulton County District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  . Congressman Daniel Goldman, always a pleasure, sir. Thank you so much for your time. Coming up --\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Thanks Ayman. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Likewise. Coming up, a highly unusual announcement from Washington, DC as the President tells the nation what he knows about the recent rash of UFOs. That`s coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Today, we saw a surreal scene at the White House as the President of the United States held a press conference to speak about unidentified flying abject -- objects, excuse me. President Biden explained that the three UFOs that he ordered downed in recent days did not appear to have nefarious purposes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We don`t yet know exactly what these three objects were, but nothing right now suggests they were related to China`s spy balloon program or that there were surveillance vehicles from other -- any other country. The Intelligence Community`s current assessment is that these three objects were most likely balloons tied to private companies, recreation, or research institutions.\par \par \uc2 I want to be clear, we don`t have any evidence that there has been a sudden increase in the number of objects in the sky. We`re now just seeing more of them partially because the steps we`ve taken to increase our radars. I`ve directed my team to come back to me with sharper rules for how we will deal with these unidentified objects moving forward, distinguishing between those that are likely to pose safety and security risk that necessitate action and those that do not.\par \par \uc2 but make no mistake, if any object presents a threat to the safety and security of American people, I will take it down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:25:14]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Now, Biden also updated the American people on the status of that Chinese spy balloon shot down earlier this month and his strategy in dealing with Chinese leadership.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Our exports have lifted components from the Chinese balloons payload off the ocean floor. We`re analyzing them as I speak. And what we learn will strengthen our capabilities. Now, we`re also continue to engage with China as we have throughout the past two weeks. As I`ve said since the beginning of my administration, we seek competition not conflict with China. We`re not looking for a new Cold War.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, meanwhile, back on the ground here in the U.S., frustration and fear is growing in and around the community of East Palestine, Ohio where a train carrying hazardous materials derailed and caught fire two weeks ago. Last night, East Palestine residents gathered at a town hall meeting and questioned officials and the official guidance that their air and water is safe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We find it very hard to believe that there is now particular matter in the air that could cause us harm after practically an atom bomb was released over our small community.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We don`t know where the water`s coming down to. We don`t know how far it`s going down. I have a baby and I don`t know if I can use my tap water to fill her formula.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How should us kids feel safe with the smell on our street?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Can we wash our clothes and dry them outside?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I can`t return to my home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And representatives from the rail company responsible for that derailment, \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  , well, they did not show up to that town hall as expected. They cited fears for their personnel safety as the reason why. And today, the administrator of the \uc2 U.S. Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  visited East Palestine trying to reassure the residents there that the federal government will uncover the truth.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL REGAN\uc2  , ADMINISTRATOR, \uc2 EPA\uc2  : This incident has understandably shaken this community to its core, forcing families to temporarily leave their homes, worry about their health and safety of their children, and even question the information that they are receiving from all of us.\par \par \uc2 The community has questions and they deserve answers. I want the community to know that we hear you, we see you, and that we will get to the bottom of this. We are absolutely going to hold \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  accountable and I can promise you that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Believe it or not all of this comes amid another train derailment just this morning in Van Buren Township, Michigan. That is west of Detroit. One car on that train contained hazardous materials but it remained on the tracks and it was removed from the site according to Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2  of Michigan. But that train was owned by the same company that operated the train that derailed in Ohio. And they now have a lot of questions to answer.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Tonight, Pennsylvania Democratic Senator \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is at Walter Reed National Military Medical Center where he checked himself in for treatment for clinical depression. His office says that he is receiving treatment on a voluntary basis and that while he has experienced depression on and off in his life, it has become more severe in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 In a tweet his wife, Gisele Barreto Fetterman, wrote "after what he`s been through this past year, there`s probably no one who wanted to talk about his own health less than John. I am so proud of him for asking for help and getting the care he needs."\par \par \uc2 In addition to winning one of the most competitive Senate seats in the country, Fetterman has spent the last year recovering from a near-fatal stroke that he sustained on the campaign trail. Last week, Fetterman was hospitalized the day after the State of the Union Address after reporting feeling lightheaded. Tests ruled out another stroke but the recovery has been slow and difficult for the freshman senator.\par \par \uc2 As New York Times Congressional Correspondent Annie Karni writes, the possibility that he may have missed out on a crucial recovery period has become a source of pain and frustration for Mr. Fetterman and people close to him who fear that he may suffer long-term and potentially permanent repercussions. His schedule as a freshman senator has meant that he has continued to push himself in ways that people close to him worry are detrimental.\par \par \uc2 And Annie Karni joins me now. It`s great to have you with us on this. What do we know about the Senator`s condition this evening?\par \par \uc2 ANNIE KARNI, CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT, NEW YORK TIMES: Well, we know that it wasn`t another stroke or and he didn`t have any seizures. He checked out of the hospital last week with a clean bill of health physically. But what I reported last week when I was working on a bigger piece was that, you know, the auditory processing issues and the physical aspects that he`s been adjusting to are only part of it. There`s -- you know, he`s been he`s been struggling mentally and they announced today that he`s checking in himself for what they said was depression that has become severe.\par \par \uc2 And you know, post-stroke depression is incredibly common. I talked to a doctor today who said it`s incredibly common, very serious, but also very treatable. So, I think that there`s a lot of relief among people close to him that he`s getting the care he needs and hope that, you know, in a matter of days or weeks he`ll be back to the Fetterman that, you know, wooed voters on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes. And speaking of your piece from last week about Senator Fetterman adjusting to Senate life, you wrote that aids and confidants describe his introduction to the Senate as a difficult period filled with unfamiliar duties that are taxing for someone still in recovery, meetings with constituents, attending caucuses and committee meetings, appearing in public at the White House and at the State of the Union Address as well as making appearances back home in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 It is physically taxing for anybody, certainly for someone recovering still from a near fatal stroke. How have these new responsibilities affected his first two months in office?\par \par \uc2 KARNI: Well, they`ve clearly taken a toll. And I think that a few things are going on here. First of all, you know, this time -- it wasn`t his first time running for Senate. He`s wanted to be in the Senate for seven years. He really wants this job and he really wants to prove to himself and to everyone that he can do it. And he`s been showing up at everything.\par \par \uc2 I talked to a lot of his Democratic colleagues who say he`s showing up for every committee hearing, every caucus lunch. He`s doing the job but he`s been pushing himself and not -- you know, he`s living alone in Washington during the week. His wife and three children still live in Braddock, Pennsylvania. And you know, what I heard from people that he`s not taking care of himself and there`s also a little discomfort about, you know, adjusting to his new reality which does have some -- you know, he can`t -- he has auditory processing issues. He relies on a tablet to communicate for the most part. And he attracts a lot of attention. You know, he`s this big celebrity. Even his colleagues stop him for selfies. And he`s kind of befuddled by why there`s so much interest in him and really wants less of it.\par \par \uc2 But you know, he campaigned on being a solid 51st Democratic vote in the Senate for President Biden`s agenda. He was described to me as someone who`s sort of the anti-\uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  . He doesn`t want to be in the middle of every negotiation. They`re viewing this as a six-year term. He wants to keep a low profile for now and they view a lot of -- they have a lot of time to settle in. But it has been a more difficult transition than I was expecting to hear about when I started reporting that story a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes. And part of the appeal of Senator Fetterman was that he was a man of the people and he was very accessible and made himself available to a lot. And that`s what appealed to a lot of the voters in Pennsylvania. There`s been a tremendous outpouring of support and empathy from Fetterman`s Senate colleagues.\par \par \uc2 As you mentioned, people wanting to take selfies with him before. And certainly today, you noted in your piece how not too long ago this kind of political grace for mental health would have been unthinkable in our current political climate. Talk to us a little bit about how that has changed with the spotlight being on him today.\par \par \uc2 KARNI: It`s changed a lot. You know, I got tons of emails of just mostly expressing compassion for him and feeling for him. And that`s one thing that, you know, his campaign noticed during the campaign that, you know, there were a lot of questions about his health during the campaign, and voters felt for him. But this has really changed. I mean, a senator -- I mean, it`s still -- we`re going to see what the reaction to this is but a senator talking publicly about checking himself in for clinical depression, not calling it exhaustion or using some euphemism is a kind of an incredible moment that I think a lot of people are -- will be thankful for.\par \par \uc2 And just you know, the last one that really prominent -- this is decades ago but Eagleton was chosen as George McGovern`s running mate in the 1970s and had to drop off the ticket after 18 days when it was discovered that he had been treated for depression and hospitalized three times for depression. Later in 2006, McGovern said he regretted that decision and just know no one understood mental health in those days.\par \par \uc2 I think we`re in a really different place now where so far, you know, you`re going to see a lot of good wishes for him to recover and understanding especially in this post-pandemic moment where there`s a lot more understanding of mental health and what people are going through. But it`s going to be interesting to watch him navigate kind of becoming a really prominent spokesman for this. I don`t know if that`s a role he had planned to play.\par \par \uc2 We saw Giselle Fetterman`s tweet saying, you know, there`s nothing he would like to talk about less than his own health. He really wants to you know, talk about the issues, talk about the people of Pennsylvania, but this has been thrust on him. What he does with it and with this platform and being someone who could potentially help other people through his own experience, you know, if there`s an opportunity there and we`ll just see you know, how this plays out moving forward.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:03]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes. An important conversation must be had around this. Annie Karni, thank you so much. And we certainly wish our best to the senator in his recovery. Annie, thank you so much for your reporting on this important story. Coming up, it is a real quandary for Republican Presidential hopefuls.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2  , HOST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL: If you had to delineate where say, you and President Trump differ on issues, where would you start?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, first, let`s say in reference to Whoopi.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: The struggles of trying to take on Trump in 2024 without taking on Trump himself. That is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, so as the Republican field for the 2024 Presidential Nomination continues to evolve, the candidate who may present the greatest challenge to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has yet to announce his intentions. But if you are in Florida, you might have noticed your governor has been giving almost daily press conferences positioning himself in front of prominently displayed American flags, all kinds of flags with placard spelling out his anti-woke, anti-book agenda, canceling African-American studies, courses for high school students, all while performing a sort of minor league Trump routine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE CONTRERAS, REPORTER, THE GUARDIAN: Joe Contreras, I`m with The Guardian Newspaper and the Washington Monthly magazine. Among the areas you identified --\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): That`s like left of the left, right?\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: Can I ask my question?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: No, I know. I mean, you can but I just -- I`ve seen. It`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Wow. So, while \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is apparently practicing to appeal to the party`s base of Trump loyalists, Nikki Haley has now become only the second declared candidate and she appeared on Sean Hannity`s show last night where she had a lot of trouble answering what I would say is a very basic question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: If you had to delineate where say, you and President Trump differ on issues, where would you start?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, first, let`s say in reference to Whoopi, you know, the thought of me getting into the race makes the Liberals heads explode. And so, I welcome it because it shows we`re doing something right. You know, when it comes to what we`re looking at -- now, listen to the first 20 minutes of your show. Look, at everything that`s wrong in this country and tell me we don`t need new leadership. But the difference is we need new generational leadership.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Where do you see, if you see, policy differences beyond what you mentioned which are generational differences was? What specific policy areas would you -- would you say part with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: What I am saying is I don`t kick sideways, I`m kicking forward. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the president. He`s the one I`m running against. And what I`m saying is you don`t have to be 80 years old to be president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Look, you may have noticed that Nikki Haley did not name a single difference between her and her former boss \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  except that she`s younger. But while Haley says that she`s not going to "kick sideways, there are real policy differences among some of the declared and expected Republican candidates which they are not going to be able to avoid.\par \par \uc2 Claire McCaskill and Michael Steele join me next to explain what kicking sideways actually means.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, so throughout her political career, Nikki Haley touted her anti-abortion stance, calling herself very pro-life and passing a 20-week abortion ban when she was the Governor of South Carolina. But here`s the thing, when she announced her candidacy for president this week, there wasn`t a single mention of abortion or her pro-life position and there is good reason why.\par \par \uc2 The overturning of Roe versus Wade is wildly unpopular in this country. In fact, nearly two-thirds of Americans disapproved of the Supreme Court decision. This according to a \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2  poll taken last summer. And now that Republicans have achieved that anti-abortion goal, they don`t want to touch it. They don`t have anything to do with it.\par \par \uc2 And so, when Nikki Haley went on the "TODAY SHOW" and Craig Melvin asked her for her position on a National Abortion ban, you can see how much she did not want to answer that question.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Let`s talk about abortion for a moment. Senior senator from South Carolina \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has proposed a federal ban on abortion. Would you sign that?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI: We need consensus on this. Our goal --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Consensus on abortion? Would you support a federal ban?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI: I think we -- not a full-out federal ban because I don`t think that`s been put on the table. I think what \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has put on the table is 15 weeks. And I think if we`re looking at 15 weeks, what we need to understand is we are not OK with abortion up until the time of birth. And so, we should at least decide when is it OK.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Claire McCaskill is a former two-term Democratic senator from Missouri and Michael Steele is the former chair of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  . They both joined me now. It`s great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Michael, the abortion issue backfired big time on Republicans last year and it`s an issue where they are in the minority of in this country. 72 percent of Independents disapprove of Roe`s overturning. If she can`t answer this question and have a clear-cut position on it now, how is she and other Republicans going to contend with this in a general election?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: They`re not because it has not been thought through. It is --the ramifications of any level of ban on abortion after 50 years of this right of being embedded in not just the culture but the health care of our country, you don`t account for that. There`s no accounting for that, let alone, OK, answer the question what happens when the baby is born and now you have, you know, women in poverty being forced to have children etcetera.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, the side skirt on that question from Craig was oh, well, you know, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is, you know, talking about you know, ban right up to the birth of the baby. Well, what about a federal ban at 15 weeks? What about a federal ban at 26 weeks? What about a federal ban because that`s the -- that`s the piece here that the Republicans for 50 years said they did not want to go to because they believe the states should rightly make that decision, right?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:40]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, she dodged the question. I think to your point, she misunderstood it. It`s not about a timeline, it`s about the idea of a federal ban.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: No, it wasn`t misunderstood, it was deliberately avoided.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: So, the question -- so that that question among many questions are going to be put in front of these candidates and then they have to top it all off with the cherry above \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes. Claire, what do Democrats do here? Because there was a little criticism that the issue of abortion which was such an animating force for people in this country in the midterms did not take up but a small portion of the president`s State of the Union Address. Do you think in hindsight that was a mistake? How forceful should he be heading into the election on this issue?\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Oh, I think voters are going to know very clearly by the time November of 2024 rolls around which party is going to protect women who have been raped and women who have been assaulted, victims of incest, it`s going to be very clear. The Republicans have laid down a marker and they cannot abandon that.\par \par \uc2 And you know what I was thinking when I watched this rollout? You know, she said I`m not a politician and I`m not from Washington and I`m going to clean out the swamp or whatever she said posing as a crocodile. You know, it was so political. It was so conventional. Every phrase she uses was poll-tested. You know how you can tell a politician? One that won`t answer the question.\par \par \uc2 She wouldn`t answer \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  about any policy differences she had with Trump. She wouldn`t answer Craig Melvin about whether or not she supported a federal ban on abortion. Every phrase she uses has been poll-tested. This is a conventional politician running for president in a conventional way and I got news for you. I don`t think that works in today`s Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, some have said it`s the Republican Party of 2016 at the very least that she`s running for, not 2024. And Michael, according to the AP, you`ve got former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who`s called for parents to elect and fire school principals. You got Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  banning instruction on sexual orientation and gender identity in kindergarten through third grade. Nikki Haley, the former Ambassador who`s now announced she is running, wants to take aim at critical race theory.\par \par \uc2 It seems to me they -- she and others, they want to run on the Glenn Youngkin playbook, the cultural war playbook not the issues, because to Claire`s point, she couldn`t even answer a single policy difference between her and the former president.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: So, that becomes the big distraction. The seriousness of abortion, the seriousness of health care, the economy, even our engagements overseas specifically with Ukraine begin to get blindsided and bumped to the side. You know, that`s sort of kicking to the side, you know.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Is that what you meant by side-kicking?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Yes. Talking about -- talking about these cultural war issues. The deconstruction of the educational system in the country, OK, fine, let`s have that conversation but then what`s your plan when parents are firing their principals and school board members and all this other stuff is going on? You have to be prepared for the consequences that devolve from that because it`s not an evolution, it`s a devolution of the process. So, that`s not thought through either just like abortion. There is the immediate push for the emotion and that`s the play.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Claire, the first week of a candidacy usually should be the easiest one. You`ve got the platform to yourself. You`re just going out there. You`re supposed to be rolling out some crystal-clear ideas. It`s not off to a good start for Nikki Haley this week.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: No, I would say it`s not. And you know what she should really be worried about? Here`s what she should really be worried about. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not afraid of her. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  just quietly said good luck and faded into the background. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is afraid of somebody, he comes after them with everything he`s got.\par \par \uc2 He is not afraid of Nikki Haley and it`s obvious. And I don`t know how she distinguishes herself in today`s Republican Party just by spouting pole- tested phrases that really don`t mean anything. I think she`s in trouble.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, it seems like it is -- it is off to a very rocky start. Claire McCaskill, Michael Steele, always a pleasure. Thank you so much for your time this evening.\par \par \uc2 And that is ALL IN on this Thursday night. I`m Ayman Mohyeldin in for Chris Hayes. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230217ej2g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for February 07, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Rachel Maddow, Joy Reid, Alex Wagner, Chris  Hayes, Jen Psaki, Nicolle Wallace, Garrett Haake \par \uc2 9,731 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 February 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Something good.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And all of us...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 All of us...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Folks, it`s difficult, but it`s simple. All of us in this chamber, we need to rise to this moment. We can`t turn away. Let`s do what we know in our hearts that we need to do. Let`s come together to finish the job on police reform. Do something. Do something. That was the plea of parents who lost their children in Uvalde. I met with every one of them. Do something about gun violence.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Thank God. Thank God we did, passing the most sweeping gun safety law in three decades. That includes things like that the majority of responsible gun owners already support: enhanced background checks for 18 to 21-year- olds, red flag laws, keeping guns out of the hands of people who are a danger to themselves and others.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 But we know our work is not done. Joining us tonight is Brandon Sae (ph), a 26-year-old hero. Brandon (ph) put his college dreams on hold to be at his mom`s side, his mom`s side when she was dying from cancer.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And Brandon (ph)...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Brandon (ph) now works at the dance studio started by his grandparents, and two weeks ago during the Lunar New Year celebration he heard the studio door close and he saw a man standing there pointing a semi-automatic pistol at him. He thought he was going to die, and he thought about the people inside. In that instant he found the courage to act and wrestle the semi- automatic people away from the gunman who had already killed 11 people in another dance studio. 11. He saved lives. It`s time we do the same. Ban assault weapons now!\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Ban them now! Once and for all.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 I lead the fight to do that in 1994.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And in 10 years that ban was law and mass shootings went down. After we let it expire in the Republican administration, mass shootings tripled. Let`s finish the job and ban these assault weapons, and let`s also come together on immigration.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Make it a bipartisan issue once again.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 We know we now have a record number of personnel working to secure the border, arresting 8,000 human smugglers, seizing over 23,000 pounds of fentanyl in just the last several months.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 We`ve launched a new border plan last month on lawful migration on Cuba, Haiti, Nicaragua and Venezuela has come down 97 percent as a consequence of that. But American border problems won`t be fixed until Congress acts. If we don`t pass my comprehensive immigration reform, at least pass my plan to provide the equipment and officers to secure the border.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And a pathway to citizenship for dreamers, those on temporary status, farm workers, essential workers. Here in the people`s house, it`s our duty to protect all the people`s rights and freedoms. Congress must restore the right and the...\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): (inaudible)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Congress must restore the right that was taken away in Roe v. Wade and protect Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Remember (inaudible). The Vice President and I are doing everything to protect access to reproductive healthcare and safeguard patient safety, but already more than a dozen states are enforcing extreme abortion bans. Make no mistake about it. If Congress passes a national ban, I will veto it.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 But let`s also pass...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Let`s also pass the bipartisan Equality Act to ensure LGBTQ Americans, especially transgender young people, can live with safety and dignity.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 [22:05:07]\par \par \uc2 Our strength is not just the example of our power, but the power of our example. Let`s remember the world`s watching.\par \par \uc2 I spoke in this chamber one year ago, just days after \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  unleashed his brutal attack against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 A murderous assault, evoking images of the death and destruction Europe suffered in World War II.\par \par \uc2 Putin`s invasion has been a test for the ages. A test for America. A test for the world.\par \par \uc2 Would we stand for the most basic of principles?\par \par \uc2 Would we stand for sovereignty?\par \par \uc2 Would we stand for the right of people to live free of tyranny?\par \par \uc2 Would we stand for the defense of democracy?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 For such a defense matters to us because it keeps peace and prevents open season on would-be aggressors that threaten our prosperity.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 One year later, we know the answer.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we would and we did. We did.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And together, we did what America always does at our best. We led. We united \uc2 NATO\uc2  . We built a global coalition. We stood against Putin`s aggression. We stood with the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, we`re once again joined by Ukraine`s Ambassador to the United States. She represents not just her nation, but the courage of her people.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador -- our Ambassador is here, united in our -- we`re -- and united in support of your country. Would you stand so we can all take a look at you.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Because we`re going to stand with you as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Our nation is working for more freedom, more dignity, and more -- more peace, not just in Europe, but everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Before I came to office, the story was about how the People`s Republic of China was increasing its power and America was falling in the world. Not anymore.\par \par \uc2 We made clear and I made clear in my personal conversation, which there have been many, with President Xi that we seek competition, not conflict.\par \par \uc2 But I will make no apologies that we`re investing to make America stronger. Investing in American innovation, in industries that will define the future that China intends to be dominating.\par \par \uc2 Investing in our alliances and working with our allies to protect advanced technologies so they will not be used against us. Modernizing our military to safeguard stability and deter aggression.\par \par \uc2 Today, we`re in the strongest position in decades to compete with China or anyone else in the world. Anyone else in the world.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And I`m committed --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 I`m committed to work with China where we can advance American interests and benefit the world. But make no mistake about, as we made clear last week, if China`s threatens our sovereignty, we will act to protect our country. And we did.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Look, let`s be clear, winning the competition should unite all of us. We face serious challenges across the world.\par \par \uc2 But in the past two years, democracies have become stronger, not weaker. Autocracies have grown weaker, not stronger. Name me a world leader who`d change places with \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  . Name me one. Name me one.\par \par \uc2 America is rallying the world to meet those challenges from climate to global health, to food insecurity, to terrorism, to territorial aggression. Allies are stepping up, spending more and doing more.\par \par \uc2 Look, the bridges we`re forming between partners in the Pacific and those in the Atlantic. And those who bet against America are learning how wrong they are.\par \par \uc2 It`s never, ever been a good bet to bet against America. Never\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 When I came to office, most assured the bipartisanship assumed was impossible. But never believed it. That`s why a year ago I offered a Unity Agenda for the nation as I stood here.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:00]\par \par \uc2 We`ve made real progress together.\par \par \uc2 We passed a law making it easier for doctors to prescribe effective treatments for opioid addiction.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 We passed the gun safety law, making historic investments in mental health. We launched the ARPA-H drive for breakthroughs in the fights against cancer, Alzheimer`s and Diabetes and so much more.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 We passed the Heath Robinson Pact Act, named after the late Iraq War veteran whose story about exposure to toxic burn kits I shared here last year.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 I understand something about those burn pits, but there`s so much more to do. We can do it together. Joining us tonight is a father named Doug, from Newton, New Hampshire. He wrote Jill, my wife, a letter and me as well about his courageous daughter Courtney, a contagious laugh, his sister`s best friend - her sister`s best friend. He shared a story all too familiar to millions of Americans and many of you in the audience.\par \par \uc2 Courtney discovered pills in high school, it spiraled into addiction, and eventually dead from a fentanyl overdose. She was just 20 years old. Describing the last eight years with her, Doug said, there`s no worse pain. Yet their family has turned pain to purpose, working to end the stigma an change laws. He told us he wants to start a journey toward American recovery. Doug, we`re with you. Fentanyl is killing more than 70,000 Americans a year.\par \par \uc2 (YELLING)\par \par \uc2 Big -- you got it.\par \par \uc2 (OFF-MIC)\par \par \uc2 So, let`s launch a major surge to stop fentanyl production and the sale and trafficking with more drug detection machines, inspection cargo, stop pills and powder at the border.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Working with couriers like \uc2 FedEx\uc2  to inspect more packages for drugs. Strong penalties to crack down on fentanyl trafficking. Second, let`s do more on mental health, especially for our children. But millions of young people are struggling with bullying, violence, trauma. We owe them greater access to mental healthcare at their schools.\par \par \uc2 (OFF-MIC)\par \par \uc2 We must finally hold social media companies accountable for experimenting or doing running children for profit. It`s time to pass bipartisan legislation to stop big tech from collecting personal data on our kids and teenagers online.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Ban targeted advertising to children. And impose stricter limits on the personal data that companies collect on all of us.\par \par \uc2 Third, let`s do more to keep this nation`s one fully sacred obligation, to equip those we send into harm`s way and care for them and their families when they come home. Job training, job placement for veterans and their spouses as they come to - return to civilian life. Helping veterans afford their rent because no one should be homeless in America, especially someone who served the country.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Denis McDonough\uc2  , he`s here, of the V.A. We had our first real discussion when I asked him to take the job. I`m glad he did. We were losing up to 25 veterans a day to suicide. Now we`re losing 17 a day to the silent scourge of suicide, 17 veterans a day are committing suicide. More than all the people being killed in the wars.\par \par \uc2 Folks, V.A.`s doing everything it can, including expanding mental health screening, truant programs that recruits veterans to help other veterans understand what they`re going through, get them the help they need. We got to do more.\par \par \uc2 And fourth, last year Jill and I reignited the Cancer Moonshot that I was able to start with President Obama who asked me to lead our administration on this issue. Our goal is to cut the cancer death rates at least by 50 percent in the next 25 years. Turn more cancers from death sentences to treatable diseases. Provide more support for patients and their families. It`s personal to so many of us. So many of us in this audience.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Joining us are Maurice and Kandice, an Irishman and a daughter of immigrants from Panama. They met and fell in love in New York City and got married in the same chapel as Jill and I got married in New York City. Kindred spirits. He wrote us a letter about his little daughter Ava. And I saw her just before I came over. She was just a year old when she was diagnosed with a rare kidney disease, cancer. After 26 blood transfusions, 11 rounds of radiation, eight rounds of chemo, one kidney removed, given a 5 percent survival rate.\par \par \uc2 He wrote how in the darkest moments he thought, if she goes, I can`t stay. Many of you have been through that as well. Jill and I understand that like so many of you. If you`ve read Jill`s book describing our family`s cancer journey and how we tried to steal moments of joy where we could with Beau, for them, that glimmer of joy was the half-smile of their baby girl. It meant everything to them. They never gave up hope. Little Ava never gave up hope. She turns four next month.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 They just found out Ava is beating the odds, is on her way to being cured of cancer. And she`s watching from the White House tonight, if she`s not asleep already.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 For the lives we can save...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 For the lives we can save and the lives we have lost, let this be a truly American moment that rallies the country and the world together and prove that we can still do big things. Twenty years ago, under the leadership of President Bush and countless advocates and champions, he undertook a bipartisan effort through PEPFAR to transform the global fight against HIV/AIDS. It`s been a huge success.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 He thought big, he thought large, he moved. I believe we can do the same thing with cancer. Let`s end cancer as we know it. Cure some cancers once and for all.\par \par \uc2 Folks, there`s one reason why we have been able to do all of these things, our democracy itself. It`s the most fundamental thing of all. With democracy, everything is possible. Without it, nothing is. For the last few years our democracy has been threatened and attacked, put at risk. Put to the test in this very room, on January the 6th.\par \par \uc2 And then just a few months ago, an unhinged big lie assailant unleashed political violence in the home of the then-speaker of this house of representatives. Using the very same language the insurrectionists used as they stalked these halls and chanted on January 6th. Here tonight in this chamber is the man who bears the scars of that brutal attack. But is as tough and strong and as resilient as they get. My friend, \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2  . Paul, stand up.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 But such a heinous act should have never happened. We must all speak out. There is no place for political violence in America. We have to protect the right to vote, not suppress that fundamental right. Honor the results of our elections, not subvert the will of the people. We have to uphold the rule of the law and restore trust in our institutions of democracy. And we must give hate and extremism in any form no safe harbor.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Democracy must not be a partisan issue. It`s an American issue! Every generation of Americans has faced a moment where they have been called to protect our democracy, defend it, stand up for it. And this is our moment.\par \par \uc2 My fellow Americans, we meet tonight at an inflection point. One of those moments that only a few generations ever face where the direction we now take is going to decide the course of this nation for decades to come. We are not bystanders to history. We are not powerless before the forces that confront us. It is within our power, of, we, the people. We are facing the test of our time. We have to be the nation we have always been at our best, optimistic, hopeful, forward-looking, a nation that embraces light over dark, hope over fear, unity over division, stability over chaos.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:09]\par \par \uc2 We have to see each other not as enemies, but as fellow Americans. We`re good people.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 The only nation in the world built on an idea and only one.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Other nations are defined by geography, ethnicity, but we`re the only nation based on the idea that all of us, every one of us is created equal in the image of God, a nation that stands as a beacon to the world, a nation in a new age of possibilities.\par \par \uc2 So I`ve come to fulfill my constitutional obligation to report in the state of the union, and here`s my report. Because the soul of this nation is strong, because the backbone of this nation is strong, because the people of this nation are strong, the state of the union is strong.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 I`m not new to this place. I stand here tonight having served as long as about any one of you have ever served here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 But I`ve never been more optimistic about our future, about the future of America. We just have to remember who we are. We`re the United States of America, and there`s nothing -- nothing beyond our capacity if we do it together! God bless you all and may God protect our troops. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Wow. We had heard rumors ahead of this State of the Union Address from President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that this was a lengthy speech. We were settling in.\par \par \uc2 This speech was long and word count. But it was delivered with a lot of energy and a lot of pace. And a combativeness and sort of liveliness that I`m not sure that I`ve ever seen in this particular type of presidential address.\par \par \uc2 We had a number of instances in which it appeared to be -- our sense was that there were members of Congress on the Republican side who were shouting at him, heckling, at least responding to him. Rather than him shutting that down or ignoring that or have it be sort of a crisis moment, he instead took those opportunities to have the argument, to challenge them back, to bring the pace even further, to the fore.\par \par \uc2 I think probably one of the most remarkable of those moments, -- halfway through in discussing first the debt and the deficit, and they need to raise the debt ceiling, and then ultimately whether or not social security and Medicare will be threatened by the Republican Congress. He appeared to turn the heckling back on the Republicans and sort of trick them into agreeing with him publicly that they would not do that.\par \par \uc2 As you were remarking on that, it was a moment that reminded you a little bit not so much of a previous State of the Union but of that moment during the Obama presidency, when he went into the Republican Caucus meeting and had a back and forth with them.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST, "ALL IN": This was at the peak of the ACA debate. You don`t want to debate someone who is onstage, generally. You want to stay out of debates with the person that has the bully pulpit.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And has the only microphone in the room.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And the only microphone. And I thought that was a truly -- a remarkable moment for a bunch of reasons. One, it was kind of him rhetorically at his best.\par \par \uc2 Two, I think you got a sense of what he would be like in the room in negotiations on the budget. Like he was kind of cutting, but you ocular at the same time. He was sort of joking about also getting consensus.\par \par \uc2 It also showed -- and this was, I think, throughout the first two thirds of the speech, he -- the agenda, on domestic policy that he has proposed and passed and he`s proposing now, is broadly popular stuff. And he really just led with that and stayed with that and wanted to keep putting their face. And when he, said some of you have proposed sunsetting Medicare and Social Security, they got angry, because the truth of the matter is, some of them have propose that, including Rick Scott, who ran the Democratic Senate -- the Republican Senate campaign -- much to the chagrin of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  , as well as Ron Johnson.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:12]\par \par \uc2 So, he`s squarely on the facts there. And I think you saw in that moment, like he understands that he has 60/40 on his side in a bunch of issues particularly on this brass tacks kitchen table economic stuff that he dominated the speech with and it`s more than happy to put Republicans on the defense --\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST, "THE REIDOUT": He said stand --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And it drove them crazy in a way that he --\par \par \uc2 REID: Stand up and show them we will not cut Social Security. Then they start howling -- so, stand up then. Stand up.\par \par \uc2 It`s brilliance speech craft, honestly because it uses \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  sort of natural, pugnacious, sort of lighthearted pugnaciousness to his advantage and their extreme disadvantage. The things they`re howling at are not arguably.\par \par \uc2 Rick Scott not only propose sunsetting Social Security. He wrote it down and put it forward as their plan for America to Mitch McConnell`s outrage. He was like, don`t write that down and publish it. He`s like, no, I`m going to write go on Fox News and defended.\par \par \uc2 So, he`s right on the facts. He literally said, fine. Offer your plan. He starts with riffing with them.\par \par \uc2 And as you 