at they started getting rowdy, there was the immediate paragraph before that when he said, the last two years, my administration is cut the deficit by more than $1.7 trillion. Under the previous administration, America`s deficit went up four years in a row. We start getting grumbling from Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Because of those record deficits, no president added more to the debt in any four years than my predecessor. More and more angry. At which point he says, look it up. Look it up. Look it up.\par \par \uc2 And they are getting matter and lateral matter. It culminates, on the same page of that script in the next paragraph, the next point that he makes, in him getting them against their will to stand up and cheer for Joe Biden`s assertion that no one is going to cut Social Security or Medicare ever.\par \par \uc2 And they`re going to do it. It starts off with him saying, I`ll veto it, and they`re saying, you won`t have to, we won`t do that, and he`s like, okay.\par \par \uc2 We all had a unifying moment. I have never seen anything like that in the State of the Union or in any presidential address.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST, "THE LAST WORD": That was single most confrontational state of the union address that we have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And anyone who picked \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  for that is quite a good guesser. I mean, I never would have predicted that this guy, who goes in there, deliberately determined to do the opposite --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes --\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: -- ends up delivering the most confrontational State of the Union Address ever and the confrontation was directly and mostly one-on-one with the person who is in a very strong competition for stupidest person in the room, and that`s \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  . And the camera found her, jumping up, individually, yelling at him when he was reciting Rick Scott`s Medicare, Social Security proposal, which is, it expires every five years, and we think about it again.\par \par \uc2 She`s jumping up, yelling "liar" at him when he says that. And he engages with her and with that group of over there. And does something we have never seen before -- he negotiates the budget during the State of the Union, on live TV. And he wins.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Wins.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And gets their, in effect, unanimous consent.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:40]\par \par \uc2 I mean, this was the worst possible night for Kevin McCarthy. The negotiation ended in that room with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  yelling at a president who took her on, handled it right in front of McCarthy. And McCarthy, by the way -- there is an isolate camera of McCarthy that`s going to show him saying, no, no, no every single time they did that.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: And trying to shush --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: He was trying to silence -- trying to shush every one of them every time.\par \par \uc2 This is over for McCarthy. The year is over. He lost it right there in that moment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I can`t read -- Maybe I shouldn`t know this -- it`s a bit heavy. But she gets up and starts yelling in like the strangest fur coat. I mean the people were comparing it to the wife of a deposed Russian oligarch or something. And she gets up and just starts pointing and making a spectacle. It`s the opposite of what Kevin McCarthy needed tonight. He needed his members to have --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Maybe it was cold. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I mean look it -- I mean Biden understands the truth, which is McCarthy has no power in that caucus. He`s along for the ride. And as much as he`s going to say, not all of you, the reality is the mad men are running the asylum.\par \par \uc2 At the very end of the speech, Biden is making a sort of -- in some ways, platitudes towards, you know, what democracy means. And there is no place for political violence in America. In America, we must protect the right to vote, not suppress that fundamental right. We honor the results of our elections, not subvert the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 There is a season in which this is stating the obvious. And yet half of this room did not get up. McCarthy, the best Kevin McCarthy could do was applaud from his seat because as you were saying, you were talking about, as it was happening, his Republican Party does not want to recognize how a democracy works, does not want to take subverting the will of the people off the table.\par \par \uc2 That`s the reality. And that`s why, in the most high stakes negotiation of the moment, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is talking to \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I will say, in terms of logistically, what`s happened here, we are watching the president in the room, continuing to have some quite detailed conversations with lots of people who were there to see the speech.\par \par \uc2 We expect that the Republican response to the president`s State of the Union will happen five minutes after President Biden leaves the chamber. But any bets on how long it is going to take him to leave the chamber? He`s more -- he`s taking an even sweeter time of it than he did on the way in.\par \par \uc2 We see Kevin McCarthy back at the dais, hanging out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What happened?\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I mean, he`ll have to gavel out the session, presumably, at the end of it. I will say that the speech structurally. And Lawrence, I take your point, absolutely, on how confrontational this was.\par \par \uc2 But he starts the speech with a congratulations to this Congress and to Kevin McCarthy, the new speaker of the House. And he, you know, also congratulates \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  but goes out of his way to do Kevin McCarthy first, to congratulate Senator McConnell and his longevity as a senate leader.\par \par \uc2 He ends with a shutout for George W. Bush`s global aids program. And that`s why the lead singer from U2, Bono was there, to celebrate the success of PEPFAR, which is a very controversial Republican administration great if lasting legacy.\par \par \uc2 And he throughout, is willing to not only talk about the ideal of bipartisanship, but praise every bipartisan aspect of everything that he has been able to do as president.\par \par \uc2 And while giving the Republicans that much love, he also absolutely pins them down like a butterfly under glass on the substantive thing he needs them to agree to so they won`t crash the economy by holding that particular --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes, and this is a standard play for this that when the president is up there, saying the single thing that embarrasses your party the most, which is usually something that has been said by one or two or five members of your party, you just sit there silently. It`s going 0to go by really fast.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You just sit there silently. Ok. This party, with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  leading them decides we want to really emphasize what you just said about one of our ideas and we want to emphasize it by denying it. Every media outlet except the propagandists will tomorrow be saying, yes, this is a Republican idea, Biden was right.\par \par \uc2 And then Biden engages in it brilliantly in that situation. And that is where experience matters. That took all the experience in the world. And his comfort in that room and the fact that he is not out of that room because he loves that room so much.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:59]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And it`s the slowest exit of any president ever because he loves being there. And that grip that he had, that poise and control that he had in that moment just changed what is going to happen this year.\par \par \uc2 NICOLLE WALLACE, MSNBC HOST: And also builds. So that`s the first time he engages -- he`s in the fentanyl section that culminates in the border. And -- they didn`t stop after that engagement. -And so he is ready for it.\par \par \uc2 He`s turning around and engaging her while Kevin McCarthy is craning his neck to look at God knows what.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: So he builds on that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: He`s trying to get them to stop -- which McCarthy is kind of the referee, right. He`s the guy here who is telling you and he`s showing you on his face this is how harmful this is for us. What she`s doing out there to this president this is how harmful it is to us.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: -- the argument that went into this is there is a lot of anxiety in the Democratic sort of political world about Biden`s age. He shattered that tonight. He`s obviously -- his experience -- it`s similar to what \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is able to do.\par \par \uc2 There`s certain things that an experienced politician can do, to your point, Lawrence, that an experienced politician, even a Kevin McCarthy, who`s been around a long time, but is obviously not as ready for primetime, as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , who had done these negotiations at a high level as a United States Senator, and was, you know, a committee chairman, just knows everyone, have those people in the room, he knew how to please \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  apart from \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and cleave in the audience.\par \par \uc2 He kept saying, I get it. He kept talking to the audience, bringing the audience in, when he is essentially saying, if you are a Republican who thinks more like a \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  or a \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  , you can leave those cuckoos behind. Let them go -- the lady in the fur is not your people. I`m your people.\par \par \uc2 I`m your people -- if you are at all moderate, if you are at all normal and you have a normal political will he would add things like -- fees for airlines, fees for hotels. He`s like, we are going to stop all that.\par \par \uc2 The American people, he said, are tired of getting suckered. That sounds like what Trump said when he ran. He`s saying, we are not going to let them sucker you anymore. Everyone who has a normal desire to see politics protect you from corporations who steal from you, from people who are chucking drug therapies you can`t afford for your kid, you are with us.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie and them who are screaming? They are over there. He set them aside and set them apart. I thought it was brilliant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You also saw it in just the sheer structure of the speech, two thirds of the speech was on this stuff. It was on brass tacks, their agenda -- the bipartisan bill, the Chips Act, job creation, junk fees, all this sort of stuff.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: $130,000 jobs for which you do not need a four-year college degree.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 All the other stuff, and I will say, this is something that I was shocked by honestly. Abortion got three lines. Roe v. Wade got overturned last year.\par \par \uc2 Now if you are \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , you think -- well there`s not (INAUDIBLE) we`re going to do and I will say that I`m going to, you know, I`m going to veto a national abortion ban. But it was, you know, it`s a zero sum game, right. You`re making choices about what you emphasize and what you don`t.\par \par \uc2 And what you saw was, that was the lead of the speech was that the kind of middle class political, economy, economic agenda stuff. Everything else, which is in the back half of the speech, half the pivot, including Ukraine, including LGBTQ rights, including abortion, including immigration -- that just got very little.\par \par \uc2 And that`s a choice they are making about where they are strongest with this audience and with this Congress.\par \par \uc2 REID: Well, he did spend a lot of time, though, I think with Tyre Nichols family, which I thought was another really strong point in the speech.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: It was subtle. It was subtle.\par \par \uc2 And his interaction --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And that got a lot more time than --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Got a lot more time, right? And he sort of spent time with it and thought what he said was really smart. If you are someone who is frustrated that there hasn`t been more action on police reform, he did not get up there and say the normal Democratic talking points. We need to fund the police more, give them more money.\par \par \uc2 He did say the training line which was a bit tired and it is sort of what is standard in the discussion of it. But he said, we need to have police earn the trust of the community. He recounted the talk that black folk, all of us, gave our kids because those were sort of terrified --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: With the emotion.\par \par \uc2 REID: With emotion --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: He actually choked up a little bit when he was talking that part of it.\par \par \uc2 REID: Absolutely and he understood it and he sort of -- the empathy was there for the victims of police violence. And he did not say, we are going to fund the police more. He said, we are going to fund communities. We are going to fund community programs. We`re going to make sure there`s less crime in these neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 He talked about funding the people. And I think that will resonate with folks who have been very skeptical that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , the working class cop- friend Democrat will actually have passion for police reform.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It was also interesting too -- I will say that I -- well I love the State of the Union as much as the next door, I have found that over the years, the use of people -- regular people as props to make points in the speech, I find to be cynical and it bothers me.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:57]\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: When he got to this part of the speech with Tyre Nichols` mother and father and he singled them out and he just said, welcome. And there was this quiet moment where they were acknowledged in the room.\par \par \uc2 But then he stayed with that story of their son. And he stayed with the topic. And he went back to them again and again. And he kept looking back at them. And he stayed with that story and he talked about -- he quoted her. He said that we should respond to her, to his mother. It was the opposite of the person being there.\par \par \uc2 REID: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: And that, actually, I think, was maybe structurally unique in terms of the way presidents -- I find it very moving.\par \par \uc2 But to spend -- to have somebody there -- yes, there`s going to be a bunch of people. And you tag them in order to make a point. I got it. It`s ok to do that with Bono. It`s not ok to do that with a mother who has just lost their son.\par \par \uc2 REID: And you know that Biden -- the difference between Biden and a lot of these -- you know that he spent an hour and a half with that woman. You can feel that he has spent actual time with the people he invited, that he made a personal connection. Because literally, that`s what Biden-ing is, right.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There`s also this -- I mean there`s this that he is trying to do. And you see it in the agenda. And Joy, you mentioned this before.\par \par \uc2 To the extent there was something in the Trump critique of the post \uc2 NAFTA\uc2  neoliberal order, right. The hollowed out towns, the fact that manufacturing went away, the fact that you used to be able to get a job without a college degree. And there was something to that part of the critique.\par \par \uc2 The whole thing is to make real policies that actually address that, and to lead with the messaging on it. So like if you are an iron worker, and you`re going to build a bridge. We`re going to have these Intel jobs that are not going to require a college degree like the whole way of trying to make the core of the Trump critique about the kind of (INAUDIBLE) parts of America in the post 1990s economy which is true.\par \par \uc2 Like that`s a true thing about the country. It is so striking how on message they were on that and how focused they are on that median voter. That voter they imagine.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE:: But I think he thinks that`s the central problem with f American democracy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: The fraying at the edges to restore the soul of the nation, you`ve got to bring back --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Those thoughts, yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: -- parts of the Republican Party that have defected to Maga-land.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Still? Is he still --\par \par \uc2 No, President Biden has just left the room. And I can tell you last year after the State of the Union, it took him 17 minutes to get out of the room after he consumed his speech which was the all-time record. Tonight he beat it. Tonight he puts in even longer than 17. It was longer than 17, I think.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: It was 20 or 22 minutes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: So that was the longest time --\par \par \uc2 And that starts the clock ticking -- I`m just -- 5 minutes until we expect the Republican response from newly minted Arkansas Governor \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Before we get to that Republican response, I want to see if quickly we can get just a word from Garrett Haake who`s been in the chamber for this. Garrett, we have spent a lot of time talking about what we perceived to be the back and forth between the president and what were effectively hecklers on the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 We were sort of chained to the pool camera in terms of what we could see. From inside the room, did you have any further clarity on who was yelling at the president and what that back and forth was like in the few instances in the speech where that happened?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Rachel, certainly the most interactive State of the Union I`ve ever covered. Some of it was clearly intentional on the part of the president, when he kind of did the rope-a- dope on protecting Social Security and Medicare. I mean that was clearly designed to elicit the reaction that it got.\par \par \uc2 And you saw the president kind of give as good as he got in that back and forth. What happened after that was mostly \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and a handful of other Republicans in the back of the room trying to engage the president further on issues around the border and on fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 You heard \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  called the president a liar. You heard some shouting about China spying on us, secure the border. And another member who I could not identify who is sitting just below me, kind of away from the rest of the most sort of pro Trump Republicans who stood in the back center of the chamber, who said during the portion of fentanyl deaths that it`s your fault, which kind of drew the most gasps and kind of the most negative reaction from fellow Republicans. That was clearly just taking things a little bit too far.\par \par \uc2 So a very, very animated room. I think you can probably argue in all those instances, played to President Biden`s advantage a little bit obviously the intentional kind of rope-a-dope on Medicare and Social Security.\par \par \uc2 And then Republicans chasing him and trying to respond to him in a way that Kevin McCarthy, warned them this morning in their closed-door conference meeting not to do.\par \par \uc2 That the lights and the cameras and the microphones would be on them and in an effort to look like sort of serious governing partners, that wouldn`t do. And there they were.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And Garrett, in the moment, it looked to us like Kevin McCarthy -- we are trying to read his body language a little bit which we all had disagreements as to what his body language meant. But some of us felt like he was saying shush, shush, trying to make eye contact with members were yelling at the president. Telling them to hush. Is that fair? Is that what it seems like?\par \par \uc2 [22:44:56]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I would have a similar read here too. I mean first of all, we know that that`s not the way that he wanted to present his conference, in terms of getting into a shouting back and forth with the president.\par \par \uc2 He`s gone to great lengths over last week to try to present himself and Republicans as sort of worthy partners on something like negotiating the debt ceiling. That they belong in the room with the president. They belong at the White House, sitting down at the table like adults.\par \par \uc2 And other Republican members, even Byron Donalds tweeted, today about the adults being back in charge in Washington. It was not the image that Kevin McCarthy wanted to have his members yelling at the president.\par \par \uc2 But he knows as well as anybody where those cameras are in the chamber and when you`re the speaker, one of them is always on you. And so his ability to communicate that to his conference at least in any way that would be effective, was pretty limited as well.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: One last question for you, Garrett just about in terms of like sightlines and the kind of thing that you could see, when there were Republican members, and you singled out \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  as one of them, we just played some clips where you can see her repeatedly screaming "liar" and kind sneering angrily at the president while she was doing it.\par \par \uc2 When the president was responding to some of those heckling moments was he looking at the individual Republicans who were screaming, and going back at them. That`s what it looked like when we had the camera on him at the podium. But was it -- is that the direction in which he was looking and directing his own rebuttal.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I got the sense that President Biden was prepared for some of this, right. On the Medicare and Social Security he clearly was prepared to respond directly. He used that other line that he likes to use about, you know, his old football coach used to say good luck in your senior year. That was clearly directed back at a member.\par \par \uc2 But I think as the heckling went on, the desire by the president to engaged directly kind of went away. I mean he would sort of pause and look in the general direction, but you know from my perspective, it`s a little bit difficult to tell over the clapping and the noise as it happens in real time.\par \par \uc2 But it didn`t seem like he was interested in engaging with those interruptions, when it got beyond kind of the substantive policy things he wanted to engage with them on.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Garrett Haake, invaluable for us to have you in the room and able to report on some of that.\par \par \uc2 We`re now expecting Republican response to the State of the Union from \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (LIVE EVENT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS (R-AR): I`m \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2  . Being a mom to three young children taught me not to believe every story I hear. So forgive me for not believing much of anything I heard tonight from President Biden.\par \par \uc2 From out of control inflation and violent crime to the dangerous border crisis and threat from China, Biden and the Democrats have failed you. They know it and you know it. And it`s time for a change.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, let us reaffirm our commitment to a timeless American idea that government exists not rule the people but to serve the people.\par \par \uc2 Democrats want to rule us with more government control. But that`s not who we are. America is the greatest country the world has ever known because we`re the freest country the world has ever known. With a people who are strong and resilient.\par \par \uc2 Five months ago, I was diagnosed with thyroid cancer. It was a hard time for our family, particularly our kids -- Scarlet, Huck and George. But we kept our faith and persevered. Thanks to exceptional doctors here in Arkansas, a successful surgery and the grace of God, I`m cancer-free.\par \par \uc2 Through it all, I couldn`t help but think about my mom. She was 20 years old, and into her first year of marriage when she was diagnosed with spinal cancer. The doctors told her she might not live and if she did live, they said she would never walk again. And if she did walk, she`d definitely never have children.\par \par \uc2 The daughter she was told she`d never have was just sworn in as the new governor of Arkansas, and is speaking to you tonight.\par \par \uc2 Adversity and fear of the unknown can paralyze us. But faith propels us to charge boldly ahead. We can`t stand still in the face of great challenges. You and I were put on this earth for such a time as this to charge boldly ahead.\par \par \uc2 I`ll be the first to admit President Biden and I don`t have a lot in common. I`m for freedom, he`s for government control. At 40, I`m the youngest governor in the country. And at 80 he`s the oldest president in American history.\par \par \uc2 I`m the first woman to lead my state, and he`s the first man to surrender his presidency to a woke mob that can`t even tell you what a woman is.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:56]\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE SANDERS: In a radical left America, Washington taxes you and lights your hard earned money on fire. But you get crushed with high gas prices, empty grocery shelves, and our children are taught to hate one another on account of their race. But not to love one another or our great country.\par \par \uc2 Whether \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  believes this madness or is simply too weak to resist it his administration has been completely hijacked by the radical left. The dividing line in America is no longer between right or left. The choice is between normal or crazy.\par \par \uc2 It`s time for a new generation of Republican leadership. Upon taking office just a few weeks ago, I signed executive orders to ban CRT, racism, and indoctrination in our schools, eliminate the use of derogatory term Latinx in our government, repealed COVID orders, and said never again to authoritarian mandates and shutdowns.\par \par \uc2 Americans want common sense from their leaders. But in Washington, the Biden administration, is doubling down on crazy. President Biden inherited the fastest economic recovery on record, the most secure border in history, cheap, abundant, homegrown energy, fast rising wages, a rebuilt military, and a world that was stable and at peace. But over the last two years, Democrats destroyed it all.\par \par \uc2 Despite Democrats trillions in reckless spending and mountains of debt, we now have the worst border crisis in American history. As a mom, my heart breaks for every parent who has lost a son or daughter to addiction. 100,000 Americans a year are now killed from drug overdoses, largely from fentanyl pouring across our southern border.\par \par \uc2 Yet the Biden administration refuses to secure the border and save American lives. And after years of Democrat attacks on law enforcement and calls to defund the police, violent criminals roam free while law abiding families live in fear.\par \par \uc2 Beyond our border from Afghanistan to Ukraine from North Korea to Iran, President Biden`s weakness puts our nation and the world at risk. And the presidents refusal to stand up to China, our most formidable adversary, is dangerous and unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is unwilling to defend our border, defend our skies and defend our people. He is simply unfit to serve as commander-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 And while you reap the consequences of their failures, the Biden administration seems more interested in woke fantasies than the hard reality Americans face every day. Most American simply want to live their lives in freedom and peace. But we are under attack in a left-wing culture war we didn`t start and never wanted to fight.\par \par \uc2 Every day we are told we must partake in their rituals, salute their flags, and worship their false idols -- all while big government colludes with big tech to strip away the most American thing there is -- your freedom of speech.\par \par \uc2 That`s not normal. It`s crazy and it`s wrong.\par \par \uc2 Make no mistake Republicans will not surrender this fight. We will lead with courage and do what`s right, not what`s politically correct or convenient Republicans believe in an America where strong families thrive in safe communities, where jobs are abundant and paychecks are rising, where the freedom are veterans shed their blood to defend is the birthright of every man, woman and child.\par \par \uc2 These are the principles Republican governors are fighting for and in Washington, under the leadership of senate Republicans and Speaker Kevin McCarthy, we will hold the Biden administration accountable.\par \par \uc2 Down the street from where I sit is my alma mater -- Little Rock Central High. As a student there I will never forget watching my dad Governor \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2  and President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  hold the doors open to the Little Rock Nine, doors that 40 years earlier had been closed to them because they were black.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:53]\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE-SANDERS: Today, those children once barred from the school house, are now heroes, memorialized in bronze at our state house.\par \par \uc2 I`m proud of the progress our country has made and I believe giving every child access to quality education, regardless of their race or income is the civil rights issue of our day.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, I will unveil an education package will be the most far-reaching, bold, conservative education reform in the country.\par \par \uc2 My plan empowers parent with real choices, improves literacy, and career readiness, and helps put a good teacher in every classroom by increasing their starting salary, from one of the lowest to one of the highest in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Here in Arkansas and across America Republicans are working to end the policy of trapping kids in failing schools and sentencing them to a lifetime of poverty. We will educate, not indoctrinate, our kids and put students on a path to success.\par \par \uc2 It`s time for a new generation to lead. This is our moment. This is our opportunity. A new generation born in the waning decades of the last century, shaped by economic boons and stock market busts, forged by the triumph of the cold war and the tragedy of 9/11, a generation brimming with passion and new ideas to solve age-old problems. A generation moored to our deepest values and oldest traditions yet unafraid to challenge the present order and find a better way forward.\par \par \uc2 If we seize this moment together, America can once again be the land of the free and the home of the brave.\par \par \uc2 During my two and a half years at the White House, I traveled on every foreign trip with the president. A trip I will never forget, was on December 25th, 2018. My husband Brian and I had just cleaned up wrapping paper that was shoved into every corner of our house, thanks to our three kids. When I had to walk out on my own. Family`s Christmas unable to tell them where I was going that night, because the place I`d be traveling was so dangerous they didn`t want anybody to know that the president was going to be on the ground even for a few hours.\par \par \uc2 We boarded Air Force One in complete and total darkness. There were no lights on the plane, no lights on the runway. Our phones and computers shut down and turned in. We were going completely off the grid.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 12 hours later in the pitch black of night, we landed in the war torn part of western Iraq. It was again a similar scene. No lights on the plane, no lights on the runway, the only thing you could see was coming from about a mile away in a dining hall where hundreds of troops, who were in the fight against ISI had gathered, expecting to celebrate Christmas with senior military leadership from around the region.\par \par \uc2 They had absolutely no idea, that the president and first lady were about to walk into that room. And when they did, it was a sight and a scene and a sound I hope I never forget.\par \par \uc2 The room erupted, men and women from every race, religion, and region, every political party, every demographic you can imagine started chanting in perfect unison over and over and over again. USA, USA, USA.\par \par \uc2 It was an absolutely perfect picture of what makes our country great. One of the young soldiers yelled from the back, Mr. President, I reenlisted in the military because of you. And the president said, and son, I`m here because of you.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after that young soldier came up to me, he said Sarah, you have a tough job. I told him what I do is nothing, you take bombs and bullets -- that`s a tough job.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:51]\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE-SANDERS: And in a moment that I know I`ll cherish for the rest of my life, that soldier reached up and he pulled the brave rifle`s patch he wore on his shoulder and he placed it into my hand -- a sign of ultimate respect.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020230208ej270002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE STATE OF THE UNION for February 7, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow, Nicolle Wallace, Chris Hayes, Joy Reid, Alex  Wagner, Ari Melber, Steve Kornacki, Jen Psaki, Garrett Haake \par \uc2 10,442 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 February 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: It comes but once a year. For politics junkies, it is like Christmas and New Years and Arbor Day, and in obscure states, Pacific holiday. You can`t really remember the reason for all rolled into one. It is State of the Union night and we are so happy you are here for it with us.\par \par \uc2 This is the live look at the U.S. Capitol tonight. Members of both chambers of Congress, their guests, Supreme Court Justices, cabinet secretaries, generals and admirals, ambassadors, they`re all gathering to hear this big, heavy on the pump and circumstance annual address from the President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 On the other end of Pennsylvania Avenue, at the White House, we are watching for President Biden to depart from the White House for the Capitol. That is expected pretty shortly.\par \par \uc2 I`m Rachel Maddow here at MSNBC headquarters in New York City joined by my colleagues Chris Hayes and \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , and Nicolle Wallace, and Alex Wagner, and Ari Melber. Great to be here with you guys. I also do really mean it when I say thank you to you at home for being with us for this event tonight. We know you have every choice in the world where to watch this thing.\par \par \uc2 Last year, 38 million people watched President Biden give this speech. It was carried live on something like 19 different networks so we know you could be watching anywhere and that makes us particularly grateful to have you here.\par \par \uc2 President Biden tonight will be speaking to a very different Congress than he spoke to in his last two State of the Unions. We`ll have a stark reminder of that tonight when instead of the familiar face of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  over the President`s shoulder where she has been for the past four years, we instead will have a new guy. Republicans won a slim majority in the House in the last election and that means at tonight`s State of the Union, instead of seeing \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  there over the president`s left shoulder, we will see the new Republican Speaker of the House Kevin McCarthy. He will be up there sitting next to Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another prominent new look at tonight`s State of the Union will be the expected presence of the newest member of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 . She is expected to be there tonight and expected to be joined by several of her Supreme Court colleagues. But if passed as any prologue, probably not all of them. That`s always a fun parlor game to play. We shall see.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, we`re told to expect that among the guests in the chamber will be a lot, more than a dozen family members parents and siblings of African Americans who have been killed by police. These invitations were extended by the Congressional Black Caucus which today issued a call for more extensive use of body cameras for a national database of police misconduct, for a mandatory de-escalation training for all police. The parents of Tyree Nichols who was killed in Memphis last month, they are expected tonight in the House Chamber. They`re expected to be sitting in the viewing box with first lady Jill Biden.\par \par \uc2 For the third year in a row, tall black metal fencing has been erected around the Capitol. That`s part of the security measures that now attend every presidential address to Congress since the January 6th attack. It is something that is likely to consider into the indefinite future.\par \par \uc2 And of course, tonight`s address comes as a global scale devastating natural disaster continues to unfold in Turkey and in Syria. The death toll from the massive earthquake there now has passed 7,000 people. It is expected to continue to rise. Two big American search and rescue teams are on route right now to join that international response.\par \par \uc2 So, coming up this hour, we will have the great Steve Kornacki here to talk about where the President stands at this point in his first term. He`ll go through some of those numbers with us. We`re also going to be joining this hour by White House Senior Advisor Anita Dunn. She has worked closely with President Biden on tonight`s address and we are hoping she will give us something of a sneak preview, hoping it`s not the same as expecting. She`s pretty disciplined. I don`t know what we`re going to be looking out of her but we`ll try.\par \par \uc2 Nicolle, let me ask you. You`ve worked in the White House. Why don`t you get us started here tonight. I do feel like this is a bit of a civic holiday for civic storks including all of us here and everybody watching right now. How is it different inside the White House? What is a day like there?\par \par \uc2 NICOLLE WALLACE, MSNBC HOST: So it`s both -- I mean, it`s also a night where you get to try it out all your favorite White House movie references. So, I asked President Biden Senior Advisor Jenna O`Malley Dillon this afternoon. I said, after the balloon was shot down, was there an American president moment where -- you know, where he says, ladies, I need a new speech. And you rip it up and write a new China section. She basically said no.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:14]\par \par \uc2 But I think that to the degree that there was ever any let Biden be Biden debate in this White House, I don`t think there was. I think that`s been had. And President Biden will be what we`ve seen him be. He is reflexively drawn to the idea of bipartisanship even if Washington doesn`t really have an appetite for being bipartisan. But I think that with the White House would push back on, if they were here, is to say listen we passed a big infrastructure bill, it`s what we`re going to be running for when we take this president`s record to the country. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  thinks it`s good enough to have appeared with us while Kevin McCarthy was on vote 347 to become Speaker.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that to the degree that any debate takes place inside a White House about what the state of the union will be about, there`s an inside and an outside debate. And I think this White House has had that debate and they`ve settled on an outside message. They don`t really play an inside Washington game. They take a message to the country and they probably view this night as you noted with 38 million people watching is a great opportunity to speak outside the Washington noise to the whole country.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Right. Even on the bipartisanship issue, it`s like there isn`t an appetite from it particularly, I think, from the Republican side in Washington. I think that everybody`s sort of expecting that`s the real politic. But a message of seeking bipartisan goals and seeking bipartisan compromise is something that everybody likes to hear and sells to the public even if nobody is doing it.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: And with Biden -- and with Biden, he believes it. It`s real. And so, to the degree that you can only sell what is real, that is a real thing about Biden that he would like for there to be bipartisan accomplished.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: They also have -- I mean I think they have a record really to run on. And I think particularly when you think about the economy which the entire story going into the Midterms was people don`t like the economy because inflation is high and inflation was high and we hadn`t seen inflation like this in 30 years, they have the lowest unemployment rate since 1969. They`ve created more jobs than any American president ever. They`ve created more manufacturing jobs than since the 1950s, the biggest manufacturing base recovery since the 1950s. They have money being deployed from battery factories and manufacturing in red areas. Inflation Reduction Act spending is getting those places. So, there`s like an actual story to tell about what the kind of Biden economy looked like.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t think they have to -- there`s always a sense in the last year of like looking over their shoulder to not seem out of touch because inflation was high and it`s true it`s still high and the price of eggs are high.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Eggs came down.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Eggs came down. But I honestly think -- I don`t think they have to look over their shoulders as much on them anymore. I think they could be very forthright about the economic record.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: You know, there`s a way in which the previous president was a nostalgia president, right, a Nostalgia for like the 1950s when you know, sort of women were incarcerated in the kitchen and you know the Blacks had no civil rights, right? But Biden is also a nostalgia president. To your point he`s not faking that. He really does have in a lot of ways kind of a wistful nostalgia for the era in which he and Strom Thurman could make things, like -- and sometimes in the worst way, right?\par \par \uc2 He actually -- and I think the other piece of it is you have to remember who Biden is. And it`s one of the reasons he`s been able to get these wins. Biden is a White working-class guy. He`s a little money now but he actually 