ng that was creepy about it though is that it`s like latex glove white, like it almost looks like a medical device.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: I think is -- you ask how I know this. She --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I`m getting different type of analysis of the type of white balloon.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:01]\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Yes, it`s like a latex --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It`s like, what type of white?\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Let`s be clear. It`s not a flat white. It`s flat latex white.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Ask me more. I got more.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: You know, I mean, as much as he wants to be the statesman for the entire country, he also is acutely aware of the fact that Democrat`s success in part hinges on drawing a line between the two parties.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: But I don`t know that we`re going to see that Biden tonight.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: No.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I think -- I think we`re going to see bipartisan happy --\par \par \uc2 REID: Bipartisan Biden.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: -- we can all get along Biden tonight. I don`t think we`re going to hear him --\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Yes, but think it`s important to note that the strategy publicly can be one thing in this moment, but it also is -- you know, he`s going to be fanning out across the country, you`re -- there`s going to be a lot of White House messaging between now and an official announcement to run for the presidency again in 2024. And you will not not hear words about the extreme MAGA wing of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 ARI MELBER, MSNBC HOST: I don`t have any -- I don`t have any balloon reporting.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MELBER: I have nothing on that. But this is the moment for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to, in that positive way, say he cares about governing and he knows how to do it and they don`t. And to what Nicolle was saying about going sort of over the filter and Chris` points about the jobs, I mean, in January 21, when they came into office, there was 50,000 new jobs that month. Now, this January, 500,000. Every president since Reagan has never broken 300,000 monthly jobs as an average. This president with the calls they made including on the economy and things that were fought at the time is averaging 500,000 jobs a month.\par \par \uc2 So, as the -- as the old saying goes, yesterday`s price is not today`s price. And I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can sell a new price that they are actually a higher value presidency that two years in they have results to sell tonight and tell the American public, to Chris` point, I think you`re absolutely right, there was always this conversation about oh, but things aren`t perfect and inflation. And no, things aren`t perfect, have you been around in the last six years?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, exactly.\par \par \uc2 MELBER: People know that but are things getting better? This is a night to go over the media filter, over the Internet filter, over the conspiracy theories and tell the public whatever your pain is, it`s getting better because this stuff is working. And I think he can actually like bring that home with numbers.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And there`s -- yes, there`s short punchy ways to do it, more jobs created than in any other two-year president -- two-year period under any president. We`ve got the lowest unemployment rate since 1969. We`ve got wages rising higher than prices. We`ve got inflation dropping. We`ve got fewer uninsured people than at any time in the history of the United States.\par \par \uc2 I mean, all of those things are easy. All of those are short sentences. And this -- that`s the sort of thing that a president lives for to be able to brag on that kind of stuff.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: And eggs kidnapped.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And eggs. Also, that balloon`s weird lady.\par \par \uc2 Steve Kornacki, we will not ask you for any balloon reporting but we did want to check in with you, Steve, about whether there is a story to tell about how President Biden is doing at this point in his presidency, whether there`s -- in particular, a numbers based story.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I think it`s really interesting to look at Biden and to look at the broader political climate right now. And I think one thing that jumps out when you look through the numbers is I`m not sure there is a precedent in modern times for quite where we are in our politics right now.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take you through some of the numbers because there are some numbers here with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that politically for him aren`t just not good. They`re flat out bad. I mean his approval rating still on average is sitting at 44 percent. He`s been in the low to mid 40s really for the first -- for the bulk of his presidency so far. He`s sitting at 44 on average right now.\par \par \uc2 Here in our latest NBC News poll just a week ago, we were asking folks about the cost of living. Is your income keeping up with the cost of living? And you can see here our own NBC poll, almost two-thirds of voters saying they think they are falling behind, that their income is going up slower than the cost of living is, that air of economic pessimism, it is still very much there.\par \par \uc2 And then you ask about some specific attributes, some leadership, and character, and personality attributes. Again, from our poll here about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he does pretty well in this question of is he easy going or likable, but he`s underwater when it comes to honest and trustworthy, competent and effective, having the necessary mental and physical health. These are some very alarming numbers politically for Biden and for his party. And yet when I say the broader political climate is a little bit unprecedented, here`s what I mean by that.\par \par \uc2 Take that Biden approval rating we just showed you coming into this year three Midterm -- State of the Union, 44 percent. These are the other three presidents in the last generations, who like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , saw their party lose control of the House in their first Midterm. Trump in 18, Obama in 10, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  back in 94. This is where they were in their approval rating basically at this same point as they delivered their State to the Union Addresses. Basically, Biden is right smack in the middle of that territory.\par \par \uc2 But look at this. This is how the Midterms went for those three other Presidents. Trump, Obama, Clinton, they got slaughtered. Biden and the Democrats lost nine seats in the House. That`s historically a pretty good performance for a White House Party. And in fact, you could have seen a scenario, if you looked at New York state where Democrats could have actually held the House with a couple breaks going their way.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:08]\par \par \uc2 So, Biden has all of these negative numbers that historically have added up to horrible midterm news, and he`s not coming off a bad midterm at all. So, I think what it does is it sets up the political climate. And you can see by the way the reason for this is that Biden is unpopular, his party is unpopular, but the Republican Party and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who`s been unusually prominent for a former president, even less popular.\par \par \uc2 So, it sets up a question here for our political climate for the coming year heading into 2024, what we`re going to find out in 2023 among Democrats? There`s not a lot of enthusiasm for running Biden for a second term but because Democrats had such an unexpectedly positive Midterm, you`re not seeing Democrats lining up to challenge him. It looks like he is going to run again. No indications yet of a major challenge to Biden. And so, it sets up the big question on the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 This is from an ABC Washington Post poll the other day. Do the Republicans want Trump or do they want someone else to be their candidate in 2024, and they are torn down the middle on that. And for the Democrats standpoint, they were able to defy political gravity in 2022 because of the lingering presence of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Are Republicans going to decide in 2023 that they are still the party of Trump or that they are a different party. It`s a key question I think just in terms of looking to the climate for these next two years of Biden`s presidency.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: That`s so interesting. Steve Kornacki, thank you very much for that. I mean, I think the one thing that we could probably all agree on is that the Democrats would love for the Republican nominee to be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right? The Democracy in Washington would love that just because they feel like Trump against Biden again, now they know how it went the first time and they`re pretty sure they know how it goes the second time.\par \par \uc2 I think Democrats aren`t -- despite those poll numbers, don`t see some way to scramble things politically that works in their favor.\par \par \uc2 REID: And even if they did -- you know, there`s one don`t think President Biden understands having been in Washington since he was 29 years old. He understands how to use power. The shift in the calendar of the Democratic primaries all but assures that there will be no challenger. I mean, we`re talking about leaning into South Carolina where Black voters handed him the nomination and essentially wiping Iowa which is the one state where somebody like a \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  or someone else good could make some noise. That calendar is a Biden calendar, so that is off the table.\par \par \uc2 But I think also, you have to remember, the climate that Democrats are in is not just about Trump, it`s about Roe v. Wade. It`s about the Supreme Court that is as unpopular as it has ever been, as untrusted as it has ever been and which snatched away a right women had had for 50 years. And so, now, you have a climate where your choice is Biden or the people who want to make abortion illegally nationally, Biden, or the people who are saying the abortion pill they`re ready to take that away. So, I think that as long as Republicans behave like Republicans and they`re MAGA whether it`s Trump or DeSantis or whoever, they`re all acting the same way and having the same extreme policies, Biden`s in good shape.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And polling numbers aren`t voting numbers, right? I mean, heading into the midterms, we saw those same numbers for President Biden. Heading into November, his numbers, his approval ratings were the same as they are now, as were the numbers in terms of general feelings about the two parties. But we saw what voters did, a vast over performance by Democrats and underperformance by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And quickly, the other thing I would say is that macroeconomic conditions tend to be more predictive of presidential re-elections than they do Midterms. We don`t know what it`s going to look like in two years, but it`s possible if inflation comes down and we avoid a recession, that he`s also sitting on those economic, you know, right track, wrong track, is my family getting ahead. Those numbers aren`t necessarily the same numbers we`ll have in two years.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: President Biden is expected to be leaving the White House soon tonight for the short ride over to the U.S. Capitol to give his State of the Union Address.\par \par \uc2 Joining us here next live from the White House is one of the president`s closest advisors who helped him write tonight`s speech. That`s next. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: The reason we are fixated on this otherwise boring-looking door is because we are keeping an eye out for President Biden to depart the White House which we expect any moment to head over to the U.S. Capitol. Tonight will be the first time President Biden addresses a joint session of Congress since Republicans narrowly took over the House in last year`s midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, as we`ve been talking about, vastly outperformed expectations in those midterms. Republicans vastly underperformed expectations. But still Republicans in one chamber in the House, they were able to eke out a tiny majority which then earned them the right to spend a whole week in more than a dozen votes trying to agree that Kevin McCarthy would ultimately be their leader.\par \par \uc2 That of course was a remarkable spectacle and time none of us will ever get back. But tonight, for the first time, we will hear President Biden formally state what he hopes to get done during the next two years of what will be divided government. The Wall Street Journal reports that ahead of today`s speech, Democrats from the House met with President Biden`s senior White House advisor Anita Dunn who told them that they should expect the message in the President`s speech tonight to be upbeat and optimistic.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:03]\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from the White House is Anita Dunn, senior advisor to President Biden. Ms. Dunn, thank you so much for joining us tonight. It`s a real honor to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 Oh, listen, I am thrilled to be with you. Thank you for having me on. We know that you are a pro and that you are disciplined and you are not going to let any cats out of the bag. However, we do know that you talked today to House Democrats about what they should expect. What can you share with us and with our viewers about what sort of mood President Biden has heading into this and what he`s hoping to convey.\par \par \uc2 ANITA DUNN, SENIOR ADVISOR TO PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : So, you know, the State of the Union speech is, you know, it`s a special speech. It`s the biggest audience that the President will probably speak to this year. And it`s a night when he addresses the nation, you know, as president of the United States. So, he said he was running to be a president for all Americans, and tonight, he is a president for all Americans.\par \par \uc2 What you should expect to hear is a story about progress and a story about what the President sees as the possibilities of the United States moving forward. He will talk about the accomplishments of the last two years, how much we have gotten done in this country, and the resilience of the American people coming out of the coveted pandemic. He`ll talk about the 12 million jobs that have been created, the 3.4 percent unemployment rate the lowest since 1969 which for a lot of your viewers is probably just a date in the history book, right?\par \par \uc2 So, it`s been a long time since unemployment was this low. He`ll talk about the 800,000 manufacturing jobs that have been created in this country since he became president, and the idea that America can once again lead the world in manufacturing. So, he`ll talk about the progress we`ve made and then he`ll talk about the possibilities ahead of us as a nation.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s an optimistic speech and it is one that makes up the point that all of this is possible because of democracy. It is possible because of the Democracy we live in and that democracy is fragile and that we can`t take it for granted, and that we all have to work at it. It`s not a partisan issue. It is -- it is an American issue. So, I think, you know, that is my cliff note version. There`s a lot I left out.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Anita, it`s Chris Hayes. There was a Times column today by -- well, full disclosure, my wife, who --\par \par \uc2 DUNN: I actually saw that, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I think if you might have worked for your husband when he was a White House counsel. But talking about what the President might or might not say about the Supreme Court. Obviously, one of the biggest things to have happened in the intervening year between the last State of the Union is the court overruling Roe. And I`m curious what we can expect.\par \par \uc2 The President is obviously an institutionalist. He has not spent a lot of rhetorical firepower on the court in the past, but should we anticipate that he will talk about the Dobbs decision and what it means?\par \par \uc2 DUNN: I think you can certainly anticipate that the president will talk about the freedoms and rights that are so important to us as Americans and so important to us as we move forward in this country, and his determination to make sure that women in this country have the full right to determine their own health care and their own reproductive health care. So, I think you can anticipate that, Chris, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Anita, it`s Rachel again. In terms of the divided government that he is facing and the fact that it won`t be \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  over his shoulder, it`ll be Kevin McCarthy, you said that he was going to talk tonight about the progress that he`s made thus far but also the possibilities about what else can be done. Is the President planning on presenting not just optimism but realism as to what House Republicans might allow or might concede to participate in terms of legislation and governing?\par \par \uc2 Well Rachel if you recall last year the president in his State of the Union speech presented the nation with what he called a unity agenda, issues that he feels Democrats and Republicans should be able to work on together, helping our veterans. And out of that agenda came to Pact Act to help veterans who had been exposed to toxic substances in Iraq and also their families.\par \par \uc2 He talked about opioid and fighting the war on opioids as part of that Unity agenda. He talked about cancer and the cancer moonshot and reducing the number of deaths in this country from cancer as part of a unity agenda where everybody should be able to work together. And he talked about mental health as an issue where people should be able to work together.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, I think you can anticipate that he will talk about issues that where the parties should be able to put aside their differences and work