s for 50 years, so he has a process. There`s three stages of the process I think I can say from experience. One is what is this all about, right? And I think Nicolle can relate. This is any president. It`s like what am I trying to say here? What am I trying to say? The second for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is deep in the rabbit hole of policies and who is the expert on cyber and who`s the expert on oligarchs. And the last one is when he starts to finally make little tweaks into the words.\par \par \uc2 That`s today. That`s the process that`s happening today as well as the cutting because these speeches are always longer. It`s like 50 pounds of policies and ideas and contrast that has a one little one pound and one hour bag that you have to jam it all into. But the one thing about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is it`s not final until it comes out of his mouth. So, he could be making changes in his mind, he could be making change -- final changes as he`s speaking.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Do you mean he`s an ad liber or do you mean that he`s a scribbler, like a last moment on the --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: He`s a scribbler and an ad liber, Rachel. I would say he`s definitely an ad liber. He feels the room. He can feel the emotion in the room. I think they`re going to be some very emotional moments tonight when he talks about Tyre Nichols` parents who are sitting in the box. That is an emotional moment.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will feel the room in that moment. He`ll feel the emotions in the moment. And I think when he calls out \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2  -- I`ve heard he`s also going to call out \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  for being the longest serving member in the Senate, or the longest leader in the Senate. So, there are moments that are vintage who he is, but he knows how to live in the moment. But he also scribbles.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I`m also -- this might be the most boring place to take this but I`m going to do it anyway. I`m fascinated --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Go for it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I`m fascinated by the lobbying process inside the government to get into the speech, right? So, you`ve got, you know, over a dozen different agencies, everybody`s got their priorities, people want -- are lobbying hard. There`s this incredible like, informational funnel right, from all the people and all the programs. And people want a shout out in the speech and all the different constituencies and stakeholders down into that. And like, how those decisions get made and how involved the president is in those decisions.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: This is very nerdy but we`re in a safe place here. There`s several binders of policies. Nicolle remembers this process that it starts with of all of the ideas that could possibly be in the speech. Those are narrowed down simultaneous with as the speech is being written, or this is the process that I`ve always been a part of. The lobbying part, and this is when it gets really crazy, is the last couple of days where cabinet members --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, phone calls.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: -- people who care about an issue, they start calling people. I did a little round table with some speechwriters the other day. Cody Keenan told me someone waited outside of the bathroom for him once. So, he came out of the bathroom and they said, I need this in the speech. And he said, it`s in three hours. That happens. He was left a bottle of bourbon by the Secretary of Education \uc2 Arne Duncan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REID: Smart.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Very smart. That is the last couple of days. It is -- it is when people just are desperately want their thing in that speech.\par \par \uc2 REID: Can I ask you, Jen? You know, Biden strikes me as somebody who is very natural except he doesn`t seem to enjoy the part of politics where he has to attack the other side.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REID: How much is their lobbying to get him to do some red meat in a speech where it feels like the natural Biden tendency, is to praise \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: He doesn`t feel lobbyable on that even. Like, you know what I`m --\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I`m curious as to the answer to that.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes, it doesn`t -- it doesn`t -- it`s not him. It doesn`t feel authentic to him. He`s never going to go into a room including the House chamber and go punch a bunch of people in the face. I think what that you convince him of if you`re sitting in a White House in a moment like this or when I was just a year ago is that there are key moments where he needs to draw the contrast with the other side. It`s not for the totality of an hour-long, hour and 15 long, or however long the speech is, it is key moments.\par \par \uc2 I expect and I`ve heard from my former colleagues he will do that on moments like the debt limit, on abortion, obviously police reform .There are moments -- Social Security. And some of those moments, and this is very interesting for all of us to watch are who stands up and who sits down, right? Because there are things that I know Nicolle is making this point earlier, 80 percent, 75 percent, 85 percent of the public supports, right, a range of them, police reform, banning assault weapons, protecting Social Security. He`s going to -- I bet and I`ve heard this from my former colleagues, take moments to watch people sit during those moments.\par \par \uc2 If you`re not for protecting Social Security, you`re going to sit on your hands during that. OK, well that`s an interesting thing for the public to see.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: So, there`s a way to implicitly do it by essentially putting people on the spot by calling the question.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Joining us now from inside the House Chamber is NBC News Senior Capitol Hill Correspondent Garrett Haake. We`ve got a pretty good view about what`s going on on the floor there and who`s talking to who and what kind of kibitzing is happening. But Garrett, 