he President comes. And I don`t -- having covered \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  for a long time, I don`t get the sense that he was in a big hurry to talk to George Santos. But given his presence on the aisle there, he can sort of inject himself into the conversation when anyone arrives.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It is amazing. I mean, lots of members of Congress have been problematic in lots of ways including those who have gone to prison, right? We have seen -- it`s not, you know, the first scoundrel to ever -- to ever be in Congress. But to have somebody who`s I think universally viewed as a con man who is --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: -- on his way out, to be there on a night like this, it is a -- it`s a distraction and an unusual thing. I think actually we actually got tape of this interaction between him and Senator Romney. Let`s watch.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: We were rocking here with that --\par \par \uc2 REID: The last word was party.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Well, a lot of Santos and another thing.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: And it`s about the party, I think. Amateur lip reader on set here.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: God, the fact that we have to care what he says --\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: I know.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: -- is a remarkable -- is a remarkable thing.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He got that isle seat though.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Well, he doesn`t have anything else to do. So, he could have been there at 8:00, right, getting a seat.\par \par \uc2 REID: But he`s got -- he`s interesting because he doesn`t seem embarrassed at all. Like, he doesn`t seem -- the least bit embarrassed.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Yes, there`s no--\par \par \uc2 REID: At all.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No, he`s chilling with Matt and Chip Roy. He`s with the homies.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: He`s like, I got mugged in the middle of Fifth Avenue. They took my shoe. I`ll see you in prison.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Chief Justice and the Associate Justices of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Here -- let`s see the \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  member is --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Members of the Supreme Court. Lot of cheers.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: That must be coming from the Republican side of the isle.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: This is interesting. Behind the newest justice, Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , those are two retired Supreme Court Justices, Judge Kennedy and \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 . We don`t usually see retired justices of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Rounded out the missing judges. Seat filling. I`m kidding.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:07]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: That is an interesting thing. We are not seeing obviously Justice Gorsuch. We did not see him nor Justice Thomas, nor Justice Alito. Justice Alito, I believe, correct me if I`m wrong, has not attended a Supreme Court since he was caught on camera sneering at and sort of shouting at President Obama during one of his State of the Union Addresses. That was his last one.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think that`s correct. Yes, that was the President Obama attacking these \uc2 Citizens United\uc2  ruling and Alito saying, no, exaggeratedly. Which, again, accepts a little bit of the precedent context for what President Biden might say tonight about the court of writ and large.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes, perhaps though.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I mean, they have -- we were talking about the court before. I mean, the other thing that`s going on with the court, it`s unpopular decisions but it`s also some of this public dialogue that`s happened between Justice Kagan and Justice Roberts there at competing events in which they`ve talked about those perceived legitimacy of the court.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And then the very, very difficult public reporting about ethics issues both for Justice Thomas in terms of not recusing from cases in which his wife is playing a material role, but also recently about Justice Roberts. His wife having a job in which effectively -- the First Lady here Jill Biden entering for her to take her seat in the visiting box that she`s reserved.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REID: And if you think about --\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: And Justice Alito`s remarks. I mean, the Chief Justice Roberts revelations recently I think are particularly troubling essentially his wife`s job affords law firms an opportunity to put money in his pocket when they`ve got cases before the court. All those ethics issues surrounding the court right now just can`t be ignored forever. But if President Biden alludes to any of them tonight, you could not (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 REID: But it is sort of -- they are not facing the public tonight, and in general, they do not seem to feel any need to stand before the public and justify six of them deciding the rights of hundreds of millions of people. They seem to have no sense, much like George Santos, no shame or embarrassment about the idea that they are seen as having shaky ethics and no accountability.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. Speaker, the President`s Cabinet.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: But support for them has dropped. The support for the institution of the Supreme Court has dropped. Desire for expansion has increased.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: Right, I would also be knocked over with a feather or something later if he mentioned it. He`s a protector of the association of the court.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No way.\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: No way he touches that I don`t think.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes, all the questions about changes to the court or term limits for the court or reforms to the court. President Biden dispatched very easily with the time-honored tradition of giving it to a blue ribbon panel to produce a document that was then put on a shelf --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: -- and never revisited, but that`s where the politics of that stand right now. And I think that`ll be probably -- I mean, anything could happen. We may be surprised. This is the President`s cabinet arriving today and they`re a little bit of interesting news today. There`s been so little turnover at the highest levels of President Biden`s Administration for these first two years and very stark contrast to what we saw under the last president.\par \par \uc2 Today, in fact, we learned that a member of the cabinet, a Cabinet Secretary is leaving and that is Labor Secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  who is leaving to go do the most \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  thing imaginable.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: He is going to become -- he`s going to be joining the National Hockey League Players Association and leaving his role in the cabinet. We`ve also just learned that it is Secretary Walsh who will be the designated Survivor tonight. So, his -- the news of his resignation from the cabinet will be as small a distraction as possible given that he`s the only cabinet official I believe to have left.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: And stepping into the NHL at time when it`s been the subject of a little bit of controversy for the lack of diversity in its management and to some degree has been cowed by Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . So, it`ll be an interesting time for \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2 , and probably a lot of hockey as well.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: But Jen, let me ask you about that, the lack of turnover. I mean, we`re seeing -- what we`re seeing here is these cabinet officials come down the aisle is all the people who President Biden named to these jobs when he first became president. And there hasn`t been turnover and it has been a stable workplace.\par \par \uc2 Now, I say this with some irony because you`re not there anymore but these folks are. There haven`t been very many departures. That has been sort of a calm steady place to work.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: It has been. And I would say what`s remarkable about it is not