par \uc2 [22:00:11]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 Everyone so I like see you doing some stories. I think about your father and I think, wow, Carl Wagner, if he was still with us, he really would have enjoyed that, it added to our understanding to it. I have one later in the show tonight that does involve my father. If he could see what we are doing later in this program, he would just think it`s the best thing we have ever done. So --\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": Listen, I have a sense of what that -- guess who that guest is just from your \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed, and I am very excited to see your interview, maybe you are tears, and I know my dad would be thrilled just at his hand over that we do every single night.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: He sure would. He sure would. Well, you know what I -- how I feel about your father.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: And I know how he feels about you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thanks, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Have a great show.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, Andrew Weissmann will join us in our first segment tonight to discuss the latest discovery of classified documents involving former vice president not named \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . So far, no Republicans, exactly zero Republicans, have called for a special prosecutor to investigate Mike Pence`s possession of classified documents, discovered two years after he left office.\par \par \uc2 But we begin tonight with future defendants. Those were the key words spoken today in a Georgia courtroom by Fulton County District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  . She was in the courtroom today to protect the rights of future defendants, who are the focus of a report written by the special grand jury that \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  empaneled to hear evidence of a possible election crimes committed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and others in Georgia in an attempt to overturn the last presidential election.\par \par \uc2 The grand jury issuing the grand jury -- it was a special grand jury that does not have the power to indict, but only to submit a report. That report has been submitted to District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  and to the judge in the case, Robert McBurney. The grand jury requested that the report be made public. A First Amendment attorney appeared in court today, representing news organizations, and ask that the report be released in full immediately.\par \par \uc2 District Attorney Willis was the first to speak in today`s session, when she said this --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FANI WILLIS\uc2  , DISTRICT ATTORNEY, FULTON COUNTY, GA: In this case, the state understands the medias inquiry and the world`s interest. But we have to be mindful of protecting future defendant`s rights. And so, what the state does not want to see happen and don`t think that there is any way that the court would be able to guarantee, is that, if that report was released, there somehow could be arguments made that it impacts the right for later individuals -- multiple -- to get a fair trial, to have a fear fair hearing, to be able to be tried in this jurisdiction. The list can go on and on.\par \par \uc2 So, representative states of Georgia, and these citizens -- I know we have this common interest -- we want to make sure that everyone is treated fairly. And we think, for future defendants to be treated fairly, it`s not appropriate at this time to have this report released. At this time, in the interests of justice, and the rights of -- not the state, but others -- we are asking that the report not be released. Because, you haven`t seen that report, decisions are imminent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Decisions are imminent. A district attorney and the judge are the only people who are currently allowed to see the report. When district attorney said decisions are imminent, she heavily implied that the judge knows that decisions are imminent simply by reading the report. The district attorney made it clear that she would not oppose the release of the report at some point after those imminent decisions are made, made by her.\par \par \uc2 The decisions about those future defendants, with the district attorney agreeing to eventually release the report, the judge turned to the attorney representing the news organizations and said this --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE ROBERT MCBURNEY, FULTON COUNTY SUPERIOR COURT: So, Mr. Clyde, your clients are going to get the report eventually. Can we go home?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS CLYDE, ATTORNEY FOR MEDIA COALITION: No, your honor. Obviously, we believe the report should be released now and in its entirety. And that approach is consistent with the way the American judicial system operates. In other words, it is not unusual for a district attorney or a prosecuting authority to be generally uncomfortable with having to release -- progress of the case.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:05]\par \par \uc2 That occurs all the time. But the judicial system time and time again has said, when matters are brought to the court system, we are going to be requiring them to be made public, because the fate of the public -- in the court system is much improved by operating in a public way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The judge said that his decision may turn on some of the highly unusual aspects of the Georgia laws that define the scope of secrecy in Georgia grand juries.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCBURNEY: I am trying to understand the basis for the request for nondisclosure. And I am approaching it from a number of angles. One is the fairly limited scope of secrecy of grand jury work in Georgia -- and with that I am particularly influenced by the Olson case, where the Supreme Court made plain that their view of the statutory framework for grand juries is that, really only deliberations are secret. Secret is not the only touchstone here. But that is what is in the oath, that deliberations are kept secret.\par \par \uc2 You -- had you`ve been present for anything that happened in front of a grand jury -- are not bound by any statute or oath to maintain secrecy about anything that happened. None of the witnesses who appeared are bound by any oath. Their oath is simply to provide truthful testimony, not to disclose the testimony to the media, their uncle, or anything like that.\par \par \uc2 Grand jurors are bound by their oath only not to discuss deliberations. So, unless we -- and I believe it is a stretch -- unless we somehow stretch to say their final report is their deliberations, then I think we are already outside the statutory realm of what a secret. That does not mean that something should be disclosed just because it is not secret, as part of the grand jury. But going into this, my thinking was, everything with a grand jury`s secret and -- have to be an exception. And in Georgia, it seems like it is almost the reverse.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading off our discussion tonight, Georgia State Representative Tanya Miller. She is a former Fulton County assistant district attorney and a former federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Also with us, Andrew Weissmann, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  general counsel and former chief of the criminal division in the eastern district of New York. He is a professor of practice at \uc2 NYU\uc2  law school and an MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 And, Tanya Miller, let me begin with you. I want to big get to the details of Georgia law about grand juries, where I learned a lot today that surprised me. But what is your reading of what happened in that courtroom today?\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. TANYA MILLER (D), GEORGIA: Well, Lawrence, I think what we saw is what we often see with prosecutors who want to control the narrative of their investigation. Madam District Attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  is obviously concerned about the details in this report, this lengthy report, we can assume. The special purpose grand jury meant for more than seven months and heard 75 witnesses. We heard Madam D.A. today said in court.\par \par \uc2 That will give everyone a sneak preview into all of the evidence in the case, all the testimony in the case, before she is prepared to formally indict it. So, we heard her when she said she is concerned about future defendants and whether or not there would be pretrial publicity that could prejudice the case, that would allow them to ask for all kinds of things like the venue to be changed, the case to be removed from Fulton County.\par \par \uc2 I`m sure those things are real. But I suspect what she wants to do here -- which is not uncommon for prosecutors -- is keep the evidence as close to the chance that she possibly can until she absolutely has to release it. Once these future defendants are indicted, we know that, statutorily, they are entitled to discovery. The cat will be out of the bag. And so, the need for secrecy is less.\par \par \uc2 So, that is what I am reading, sort of, between the lines of the presentation that the state made today.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Because you know her and you know the way she works, I am really intrigued by that phrase, future defendants. She could have said possible future defendants. She did not.\par \par \uc2 She appears to be someone who is very careful in her choice of words. She also emphasized, as you just said, the possible impacts that this could have on future defendants and what they might try to use this for in an appeal, should there be convictions in this case. And so, she very clearly seemed to be standing up there saying, as clearly as she could, in this particular session, at this level, that there will be future defendants, and I am worried about what they will do in appealing convictions using this decision.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:11]\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I think you are absolutely right. There are a number of ways, if she was trying to sort of hedger bets here, if she was unsure about whether or not she was going to present this to a regular grand jury, that she could have said it, but she specifically said that there will be future defendants. And her concern is that these feature defendants, these future individuals who are formally charged in this indictment -- we don`t want to give them any ammunition on appeal.\par \par \uc2 So, she is not only forecasting that there will be an indictment. I think that is a fair guess to make, although none of us know foreshore. But that she intends to pursue this vigorously -- and she intends to defend this all the way up to and through conviction and to appeal. And she wants to keep her cases as airtight as possible.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann, what did you take away from this hearing tonight?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMANN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, as you mentioned, Georgia law is quite unusual. You have these two separate grand juries. And there was a lot of complicated legal argument. When I step back, what I was thinking is that this may end up being mooted out.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  said they there are going to be imminent charges, even if the judge was to rule against her and say that this report is going to be made public, it does not appear that he is going to rule quickly. There was a lot of discussion about his giving notice to both sides. And both sides may appeal that and get to stay.\par \par \uc2 So, one thing that the D.A. can do here is basically act with lightning speed to go forward with the charges and then, as she said, repeatedly, she was not taking the position that the court should never be released. And then, at that point, it could be released and the public would be able to see that.\par \par \uc2 There would be some of the downside that you and Tanya referred to. But there would be less at that point. And so, I think that is something to keep your eye on. And I think the word imminent was, I think -- I think everyone took notice of that.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tanya Miller, I was stunned when I heard the judge describe the laws governing secrecy in Georgia grand juries. I mean, I understand that in grand juries elsewhere, a witness to a grand jury can immediately walk out of the courthouse and tell every single thing, publicly, that they said in the grand jury room.\par \par \uc2 But he was saying today, a prosecutor who is in the grand jury room, can go out and discuss what happened in the grand jury room. He was saying that grand jurors, apparently in Georgia, have the right to discuss it publicly. They simply cannot discuss the deliberations, that the secrecy only applies to the grand jurors themselves, not anyone else who is in the room.\par \par \uc2 And that it only applies to their deliberations -- that was a very surprising, narrow definition of grand jury secrecy to meet.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yeah. And the reason why it probably is even more surprising to folks is that, in practice, in Fulton County and in state grand jury proceedings, secrecy is respected. It is sort of the custom. We don`t go out and talk about what happens in the grand jury. You don`t see grand jurors going out forecasting or giving a recap of testimony in of evidence that they heard.\par \par \uc2 Most of the proceedings in state court are not transcribed. So, there is no record of what happens. So, in practice we just sort of become accustomed to this notion that grand jury proceedings are secret. The problem is that we are faced with this very unique situation. And while these are the laws on the books, these are not statutes that we used often.\par \par \uc2 The court kept talking about two prior special presentment`s that happened in the state that he was looking to for guidance. One in Gwinnett County, one in DeKalb County, but that`s it. There`s really a dearth of case law on it. And when you read the statute and when you take that in conjunction with the Olson decision, secrecy is defined fairly narrowly.\par \par \uc2 So, Judge McBurney was sort of doing what he does, and that, is set the framework legally. He does not typically come into court with an answer. He hears people out. But he is going to hear these issues pretty thoroughly before he takes the bench especially in a case like this. And I think you saw him flexing that, that he looked at the law, he understood what it said, and he kind of had to really focus in. Okay, we are not talking about a secrecy issue that is supported by case law. So, tell me why I should keep this secret.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:02]\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Tanya Miller, thank you very much for your Georgia state law expertise. I believe we are going to need more of it going forward apparently for future defendants in this case. Thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Imminently.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: My pleasure. Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: And now, it`s Mike Pence`s turn. Lawyers for \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  revealed today that they discovered what they call a small number of classified documents in a search of Mike Pence`s home in Indiana. The lawyers say they made the discovery a week ago and alerted the National Archives in a process you know by now. The National Archives immediately notify the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to the National Archives, Attorney Greg Jacob, a former White House counsel the Vice President Pence, who is now the designated representative for dealing with Pence`s White House records, wrote -- the additional records appear to be a small number of documents that aren`t classified markings that we`re in a inadvertently boxed and transported to the personal home of the former vice president at the end of the last administration.\par \par \uc2 Vice President Pence was unaware of the existence of sensitive or classified documents at his personal residence. Vice President Pence understands the high importance of protecting sensitive and classified information and stands ready to and willing to cooperate fully with the National Archives and any appropriate inquiry.\par \par \uc2 Just four days before, Pence`s lawyers say they discovered classified documents at his home, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  criticized President Biden for having documents marked classified at his home after his service as vice president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT: I welcome the decision by attorney general garland to appoint a special counsel. If we have a special counsel reviewing classified materials that were found at Mar-a-Lago, we need to have a special counsel in this case. And that`s progress.\par \par \uc2 The handling of classified materials and the nation secret is a very serious matter. And as a former vice president of the United States, I can speak from personal experience about the attention that ought to be paid to those materials when you are in office and after you leave office. And clearly, that did not take place in this case.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann, but by the \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  standard, he would want us to have a special prosecutor in his case by now.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: Well, I think there are a number of ways to look at this. First, there is an issue where the government really has to do an assessment of how it is dealing with classified information. When I was at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , when there was top secret SCI material, that is not something that you had in your office and you could make a mistake and bring it home. It was something that someone gave you. You looked at it and you gave it back and it was signed out. And you (AUDIO GAP) SCIF, sensitive compartmentalized (AUDIO GAP) facility.\par \par \uc2 And so, there clearly is a problem and one of the things that we don`t know is how extensive it is. I suspect that we are going to hear more, because people are going to start looking through boxes of material. And Pence could be the tip of the iceberg. So, that is sort of one.\par \par \uc2 The other is, from a criminal perspective, although it shouldn`t be, this really is a gift to both former President Trump and to President Biden. It shouldn`t be because there is such a huge difference between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Biden and Pence. There is zero evidence of obstruction of justice, whereas \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appears to be like an inept Harvey Keitel in "Pulp Fiction". I mean, it`s not just apples and oranges, to quote \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  , it`s apples and orangutans.\par \par \uc2 But as a practical matter, it is going to be very important if there is going to be a criminal case, for the first time ever against a former president, that those differences are clearly articulated and resonate with the American public. And unfortunately, I think that as we see the sort of drip and drip of classified information, it really, in many ways, is a gift to both the current president and the former president as to whether it is going to undermine the backbone and will of the Department of Justice to bring at this.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Andrew Weissmann, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMANN: You are welcome.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Coming up, Republicans are threatening the freedom to travel within the United States. Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez-Masto\uc2  is leading the fight in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  against this bit of Republican fanaticism.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:15]\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CATHERINE CORTEZ-MASTO (D-NV): We see it every single day in the state of Nevada. Women coming to our state for their health care needs -- and now we have our extreme Republicans trying to block women from traveling to my state and trying to criminalize those women and those doctors who, in my state, have supported the right of women to choose.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I have introduced the freedom to travel for women for health care for people in this country, that also protects our doctors in providing essential healthcare to women in need.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The freedom to travel? In the 21st century, the freedom to travel within the United States is being threatened by fanatical Republicans.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:03]\par \par \uc2 The freedom to travel is one of the defining characteristics that makes a country a country. The Republican member of the House of Representatives, who is now a candidate for the United States and, it from Indiana, now advocates imposing a federal law that will ban any and all travel across state lines in the United States for the purposes of obtaining abortion services.\par \par \uc2 Here is Congressman Jim Banks of Indiana, in a radio interview last week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM BANKS (R-IN): We have some very blue states that did not do what Indiana did. Indiana was the first state in the country, after the Dobbs decision, to pass a pro-life bill.\par \par \uc2 So there is much more that we must do, that we need to do, that I`m going to fight for the House, and when I get to the Senate, I`m going to fight for it there to, figure out a way as well. I am for federal legislation, I`m for strong laws of the state level, whatever we can do.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez-Masto\uc2  of Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 While he is not a senator yet, but he could be. Senator Banks -- you have just been reelected to a six-year term. You could be facing him on the Senate floor, where he will be advocating a national ban on interstate travel for the first time.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CATHERINE CORTEZ-MASTO (D-NV): Well, hello, Lawrence. It`s great to join you and thank you for bringing this to everybody`s attention. This is the very reason why I`m back in the Senate. This is such an important issue in the state of Nevada. Why? Because, in 1990, the people in the state of Nevada voted to codify Roe versus Wade, because Nevadans believe in the right of women to choose.\par \par \uc2 And at that time, it was about two thirds of the voters who voted to support it. And they still do. This crosses party lines in Nevada. This is Republicans, Democrats, independents -- this is men and women. They support a women`s right to choose. And this is one of the reasons why I was reelected to the Senate, to continue to fight for the right of women`s freedoms. In this instance, the personal freedom when it comes to the right to make these decisions about their health care.\par \par \uc2 And as -- look, let me just say, Lawrence, as we can see, the far-right extreme Republicans, who not only cheered the repeal of Roe versus Wade, want to take it even further. Forget about states rights, which is really what they were supporting at the time when they repealed Roe versus Wade, forget about states rights, they would even impede my state from providing essential health care to women, and criminalized them and doctors.\par \par \uc2 And so, that`s why this is such an important issue for us to continue to fight to protect women, to protect doctors, to protect, really, a majority of Americans in this country who believe in the right of women to choose.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You, in the drama of the last election, yours was very dramatic -- your reelection marked the 50th Democratic senator, followed by \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  , giving the margin of 51 for Democrats in the Senate. So, without you, they would be back down to that 50 spot again, where they were before.\par \par \uc2 So, as you were running that race, how important was this issue in that election? And how important will it be in future elections?\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ-MASTO: Well, it was so important. That`s why I`m here. By being the 50th, holding the Senate, we can stop any federal abortion bans that these right-wing extremists want to bring in Congress. That`s the very reason why I am here.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you, being home when I was home campaigning and talking to Nevadans over these last two years, the repeal of Roe versus Wade and what`s happening, the erosion of women`s rights in this country, when it comes with their health care, was a number one issue for so many people.\par \par \uc2 I stood and had press conferences, not with Democrats, but also Republican men and women on this very issue, who were concerned that the women`s rights to choose was being taken away. And the next step that they were seeing, these federal abortion bans and then trying to criminalize doctors and women -- who wanted to come to our state to seek their health care rights.\par \par \uc2 So, as you just pointed out with Congressman Banks, this isn`t over. There is a minority of extreme individuals in this country who don`t support what a majority of Americans want in this country, when it comes to women`s rights to choose. And we have to continue this fight. Because they are going to take it as far as they can take it. And we have to be the backstop.\par \par \uc2 My winning in Nevada and becoming the 50th seat here for the Senate Democrats to control in the Senate is a backstop from any extreme Republicans trying to pass a federal abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So are there ten or nine is really -- might be all you need -- Republicans in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  who are willing to join with you to create the 60 vote threshold that you would need in order to proceed to your bill?\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ-MASTO: Well, I`m counting on it. I`m working on it because I think there should be. If it is not just about a woman`s right to choose and the right to make that decision, it is about states rights. This also is an issue of state`s rights. This also is an issue of state`s rights. My state has decided to respect a woman`s right to reproductive freedom and to make these decisions.\par \par \uc2 And so we should not criminalize any woman who wants to come to the state of Nevada to seek health care. So if you believe in states rights, then yes, you should be joining me on this legislation.\par \par \uc2 So we will see where Republicans stand. But I tell you, at the end of the day, I know there are some of my Republican colleagues who do believe the same way I do. And I`m hopeful we have enough that there is bipartisan support to move forward to protect these women and these doctors who are providing essential health care.\par \par \uc2 Because in my state, this isn`t a partisan issue. This is about personal freedom for women and the right for them to choose. And I am hopeful that some of my colleagues in the Senate on the Republican side believe the same way.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Do you think there is any way -- well, you know, I find myself asking the question and then I think about who I am asking it about. The \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  , the current \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  , that they would actually restrict the right to travel -- they would support restricting the right to travel in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Prior to this court, I would say there is no chance, that that would be ruled unconstitutional in a second. But with this court, who knows.\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ-MASTO: Well, and you are absolutely right. I mean I sat through some of those hearings. I listened to the justices, the nominees coming forward and say, don`t worry, Roe versus Wade is protected. It`s precedent, you don`t have to worry. Nothing`s going to happen to it.\par \par \uc2 And look where we are today. So really, what I have seen with the right- wing and the previous administration, putting individuals on the bench that are far extreme to the right. I don`t trust anything, quite honestly, that`s coming out of this court right now that respects what a majority of Americans in this country support in those personal freedoms of women in this country to make these decisions is essential.\par \par \uc2 And the court clearly does not think that`s appropriate. And so listen, I`m with you. I do have concerns about the future, not only the erosion of women`s reproductive freedom rights. But we saw the decision that came out. And \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  ` opinion, wanting to take it even further.\par \par \uc2 So, we know what is at stake here. And it`s really appropriate for all of us to stay on guard and continue to fight.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez-Masto\uc2  , thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 CORTEZ-MASTO: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, this next segment is personal -- deeply personal. 20 years before she was born, my father fought all the way to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  for the father of our next guest. I only wish that her father and my father were still here because they would think that our next segment is the most important thing that this program has ever done.\par \par \uc2 And maybe they would be right because just about everything else we do is a variation of something you could have learned elsewhere. This one is a story that I think could only be told here.\par \par \uc2 Andrea Joy Campbell is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:33:34]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Our next guest is nothing less than a miracle to me. And when you hear her speak he might feel the same way. My father met her father when her father was a young man in big trouble, facing trial in Boston on bank robbery charges. By that time my father, who went to college and law school night while he was a Boston police officer had become the go-to defense lawyer for bank robbery in Boston after being one of the defense lawyers in what was then the biggest bank robbery in American history -- the Brinks robbery in Boston, which was the first time bank robbers stole over a million dollars.\par \par \uc2 The Brinks robbers were all convicted and sent to prison. And in my father`s next big bank robbery case, our next guest`s father, Alvin Campbell, was also convicted and sentenced to 25 years in prison. But my father took the Campbell case all the way to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  and got the conviction overturned. And Alvin Campbell did not spend the next 25 years of his life in prison.\par \par \uc2 But by the time Alvin Campbell`s daughter was born, 20 years later, Alvin Campbell was back in prison on other charges. It is possible that without that window of freedom that my father won for Alvin Campbell, Andrea Joy Campbell might not be with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I can only wish that both of our fathers were still with us so that they could see what you are about to see. Andrea Campbell`s career reached a new high last week on the first day of her new job.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:50]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: On that day, she thanked a long list of people, who helped her get where she is today and be who she is today, including her husband Matthew and her two young sons, Alexander and Aiden.\par \par \uc2 And then she said this --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA JOY CAMPBELL, MASSACHUSETTES ATTORNEY GENERAL: These are the shoulders I stand on and the people I represent, who will always sustain my faith, my hope and my courage to forge ahead.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 That, of course, includes my father. You see, my father was born and raised in Boston in 1933. He was considered, quote unquote, "a criminal".\par \par \uc2 But my father was incredibly intelligent, having graduated from Boston Tech in 1951. He was accepted to \uc2 Princeton University\uc2  . And instead of attending Princeton he chose a different path, one he hoped as the eldest of seven would allow him to provide for his family.\par \par \uc2 My father, like so many parents, grandparents, guardians, caregivers, including those with unaddressed trauma who make poor choices and struggle with addiction, sought for his children to have more and to do better.\par \par \uc2 My story is built on the foundation of my father`s life and his story. My childhood was filled with incredible instability. When I was eight months old, my mom literally died in a car accident going to visit my father, who was incarcerated at the time.\par \par \uc2 I did not meet my father until I was eight because he was incarcerated. And sadly, he too would die unexpectedly when I was 19 years old, a sophomore in college. I talked to him one morning, he died that same evening.\par \par \uc2 My brothers and I bounced around, we lived with relatives who looked out for us and sometimes in the foster care system. We grew up poor and in public housing.\par \par \uc2 But through my public school education and the five public schools I attended, including Boston Latin School, I was blessed -- I was blessed to have a support system that provided me with safe spaces to cope with this trauma and to learn and to grow. Blessed to have loving relatives and those who would fill the void.\par \par \uc2 Because of that education and so many caring individuals who influenced my life in a moment, a day or a season. I was blessed to become the first in my family to go to college to \uc2 Princeton University\uc2  , and the first of my family to go to law school, to UCLA Law School --\par \par \uc2 Yet that trauma would continue. My brothers, sadly, would cycle in and out of the prison system. And 11 years ago, my twin brother Andre would die while in the custody of the Department of Correction, as a pretrial detainee when he was 29 years old, as a result of receiving inadequate health care.\par \par \uc2 So yes, I deeply understand the magnitude of this historic day -- and that it is more than a personal triumph. An impossible story made possible by the power and grace of God that a girl from a poor family who grew up in public housing in Roxbury, then in Boston could become the 45th attorney general of Massachusetts and the first woman --\par \par \uc2 -- and the first woman of color to be elected to Massachusetts statewide office.\par \par \uc2 There are some who want us to feel shame in our stories, or even try to weaponize them against us. My response is always twofold. No weapon formed against me will prosper.\par \par \uc2 And because of our family history, struggle, encounters with the law and the legal system, our experiences, our realizations -- it begs the question, who better to do the job? To take on the challenge and to do this critical work?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Who better to do the job. The 45th attorney general of Massachusetts, Andrea Joy Campbell, will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:36]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: Thank you to Senator Edward William Brooke III and his legacy. 60 years ago this month, he was inaugurated as the 35th attorney general of Massachusetts, becoming the first black attorney general not only in Massachusetts but in the country.\par \par \uc2 Continuing Massachusetts legacy of being pioneers, and demonstrating that we do indeed live in the best state in the nation.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:48]\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: My hope is that every day women and young people who look like me and see the incredible work of this office will feel less invisible, despairing and lonely because there continues to be a shining example of what is possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now Massachusetts 45th attorney general, Andrea Joy Campbell.\par \par \uc2 You know, when I was a kid in high school, I was getting on the elevator in the court house in Boston with my father, and the attorney general of the state Frank Bellotti came by and my father said, "Hello, General", whereupon I learned that the proper title for you now is general, which I`m sure a lot of the audience doesn`t know, but I will be calling you General Campbell for now on.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to the last point that you made there and what we just showed, about your hope for what you can be, in effect, delivering as an image for little girls and women out there.\par \par \uc2 When you were growing up, there was no \uc2 Andrea Campbell\uc2  to look up to. There was no model when you were 8 years old, when you were working your way through Boston Latin School, the best high school in America, going on to college.\par \par \uc2 What did -- what were you looking at, what was your dream aimed at? Was there a model?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: There may not have been a specific model obviously, in Massachusetts. I am the first black woman and woman of color to be elected statewide, but there were many who came before me, who did a lot to establish that foundation, for the victory to be even possible.\par \par \uc2 And I name that the day of, of course former Senator Edward Brooke as well as governor \uc2 Deval Patrick\uc2  who`s more recent, Congresswoman Ayanna Pressley`s who also is more recent.\par \par \uc2 And so there are folks from back in the day and of course more present, who did establish a foundation for this to be possible.\par \par \uc2 And most importantly, the victory belong to the people. Folks who want folks in office that are representative of their stories, and who represent the struggles that they go through every single day. And will take those stories and with a sense of compassion, a sense of urgency, deliver real results for them.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to take a look at a photograph that a friend of mine took -- a friend of ours took at your inauguration. That`s you with one of the girls from Codman Academy, which is a public charter school in Codman Square, not too far from where you live now, not too far from where I used to live.\par \par \uc2 And one of those girls was overheard saying, while listening to you, I am going to tell my grandchildren about this day. So that message that you are trying to get through to them, we can tell you it got through to them.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: Young people right now are in such despair, as we look at the violence and cycle of violence, and the mass shootings that we`re seeing across this country, and of course, in California. The mental health epidemic that exists in Massachusetts and across this country.\par \par \uc2 Folks want inspiration, they want hope, and that`s exactly what we`re going to deliver. And the best thing about going to work every single day in the attorney general office of Massachusetts, it is indeed the best public law firm in the country, and it has significant tools not only to address violence and gun violence, but to really deal with really tough issues, but to deliver a whole host of solutions to solve for them.\par \par \uc2 And young people in particular, near and dear to me, because I know that not only the next generation, but I have two young boys, and I connect to some of the struggle that parents may be feeling around the future for their children. And so I`m excited about the possibilities ahead.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We just heard from the Senator from Nevada, \uc2 Catherine Cortez- Masto\uc2  , that they`re actually now fighting in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and in Congress over the right to travel. A right that we literally didn`t even think about for the last few years in this country. So implicit in everything about living in this country.\par \par \uc2 And Massachusetts is one of the states that Republicans want to prevent women from traveling to use abortion services there. What can the attorney general of Massachusetts do about that?\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: Quite a bit and the work -- the office has been doing the work in many different ways. And I`ve proposed establishing what I call a Reproductive Justice Unit to make sure that work is intersectional. To not only protect providers but also patients.\par \par \uc2 Right now in Massachusetts we were ahead of the game in the sense of thinking ahead in terms of the possibility of Roe being overturned. So you have the legal right here in Massachusetts to access and have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 But there`s still significant work to do. we have deserts here that we need to close we also need to make sure that not only folks are protected but that they can get a high quality care.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:57]\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: In addition to that Massachusetts is at the forefront when it comes to training providers in this work. So it`s going to require working with \uc2 AGs\uc2  all across the country to make sure that every woman has a right to do whatever they want with their body, and the government doesn`t get in a way with that.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of the day this is about power and freedom so there`s significant work to do and I`m excited for Massachusetts to continue to take the lead.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know when \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  was a district attorney of San Francisco, I had never heard of her and a friend of mine in Los Angeles at the time said you have to come to this event to see her. She is on her way to the White House. And when I saw her, I thought, she`s on her way to the White House. She went from that office to attorney general, the office you have now, so you are now on the \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  track.\par \par \uc2 I will make no predictions about it. I will not ask you about any future plans but the audience should keep an eye on the attorney general from Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 CAMBELL: Thank you, I appreciate that. I will Kamala was my keynote speaker when I graduated from UCLA Law School. So she is near and dear. I`m excited for the work ahead.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Massachusetts Attorney General Andrea Joy Campbell. It is a joy to have you here. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CAMPBELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O5l: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:56:16]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Attorney general \uc2 Andrea Campbell\uc2  gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230125ej1o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for January 23, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 8,021 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Sinema hasn`t officially said that she`s running for reelection next year, but she set herself up to play spoiler in a state where registration by party stands at 35 percent Republican, 34 percent independent, and 31 Democratic. But keeping Arizona blue depends on the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Well, today, Democratic Representative Ruben Diego became the first major candidate to jump into the race with a new launch video. And he will join me here tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 And that is "THE REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): We can see there`s a major difference in the leadership with Kevin McCarthy as Speaker of the House and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  as speaker.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: How Kevin McCarthy`s ironclad bond with MAGA extremists was born. Tonight, new reporting on how the speaker became BFFs with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and what it means is the rest of us. Then --\par \par \uc2 SEN. KYRSTEN SINEMA (I-AZ): As folks know, I`ve declared -- formally declared my independence from what I consider to be a deeply broken two- party system.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What Ruben Gallego`s campaign announcement means for the independent senator from Arizona. Plus, two years after a failed coup in Washington, the unbelievable story of a so-called palace coup in a small Kansas town.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m motion that we removed Mayor Zimmerman as mayor and effective immediately we have a new mayor election.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And on the 50th anniversary of the Roe v. Wade decision they just overturned, new reporting on our right-wing Supreme Court that is coming apart at the scene when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Today, four more members of the far-right extremist group the Oath Keepers were convicted of seditious conspiracy for their role in Donald Trump`s coup. On January 6, 2021, three of the men entered the Capitol building wearing tactical gear, while the fourth headed up a so-called quick reaction force at a Northern Virginia Hotel, standing ready to transport a cache of weapons into Washington D.C.\par \par \uc2 These men join the leader of the Oath Keepers, Elmer Stewart Rhodes III and a top deputy were both convicted of the same very rare charge last year. Remember, as we`ve noted on their show, seditious conspiracy refers to conspiring to overthrow or "destroy by force the government of the United States." And obtaining convictions for it can be quite difficult.\par \par \uc2 Also, today, a jury delivered a guilty verdict against one of the most memorable insurrectionists, the Arkansas man who infamously put his feet up on then-Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has desk, left her a crude note, and stolen envelope from her office. 62-year-old Richard Barnett was convicted of eight federal crimes, including four felonies.\par \par \uc2 Two years on, the Capitol certainly looks a lot different than it did that day. And the 118 Congress is now being run by well, allies of the ex- president, the man who directed that mob just stormed the Capitol. We have noticed an interesting pairing among them. The new speaker has McCarthy and Georgia Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  . This duo has been spending a lot of time together since the very first day of this Congress when we saw Congresswoman Greene acting is one of McCarthy`s lieutenants, mostly unsuccessfully whipping votes.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  has been trying to rebrand herself after being known as a QAnon supporter who regularly spread conspiracy theories. She has suggested the Parkland School shooting was a false flag, even harassing a teenager who survived the massacre, stalking him in sidewalk. Greene has made racist, antisemitic, anti-Muslim remarks. She spoke at a conference led by that white supremacist, the one that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Kanye had dinner with. She has used violent rhetoric, repeatedly making comments about executing Democratic politicians, including \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR-GREENE: She`s a traitor to our country. She`s guilty of treason. She took an oath to protect American citizens and uphold our laws, and she gives aid and comfort to our enemies who illegally invade our land. That`s what treason is. And by our law, representatives and senators can be kicked out and no longer serve in our government. And it`s a -- it`s a crime punishable by death is what treason is. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is guilty of treason and we want her out of our government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s a crime punishable by death. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is guilty of treason. \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  no longer wants to be known as the person who did and said all that, rather as a member of good standing of the new Republican House Majority. And guess what? Kevin McCarthy is helping her with that. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  has extensive new reporting about the ironclad bond McCarthy`s develop of green, an unlikely ally in his battle for political survival.\par \par \uc2 They describe it as a relationship boring and political expediency but fueled by genuine camaraderie. That`s sweet. In an interview, McCarthy described what he likes so much about his colleague, saying, "If you`re going to be in a fight, you want Marjorie in your foxhole. When she picks a fight, she`s going to fight until the fight is over. She reminds me and my friends from high school that we`re going to stick together all the way through." It`s interesting \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  boasted about the fact that had she planned January 6, they would have been armed, it would have been successful.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy has also told allies that he was impressed the congresswoman quickly became known by her initials. "She knows what she`s doing. You`ve got AOC and MTG." For her part, MTG has taken on an outside role as a policy advisor to McCarthy. That phrase really left me scratching my head I got to say. But The Times reports adds that McCarthy has little in the way of a fixed ideology of his own, has come to regard that the Georgia Congresswoman as a vital proxy for the desires and demands of the right- wing base that increasingly drives his party.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to this reporting, McCarthy decided to form the allowance -- the alliance with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  , after looking at the last few Republican speakers, \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  . McCarthy believes they made a mistake by not reaching out to the far-right members of their caucus. This makes a certain kind of sense, I have to say. If you are Kevin McCarthy, and your goal is to survive at least two years as speaker, the recent history does suggest that if you do not at least appease the most radical members of your party, your tenure will be short-lived. In fact, even if you do appease them, it still might be short-lived. It was true for Boehner and it was true for Ryan.\par \par \uc2 So, McCarthy is trying to save himself. He has thrown in with the far- right, basically handing him the keys to the whole powers during that week of failed votes for speaker. And now, people like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  essentially run the House Republican caucus. As the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporting made clear, as Kevin McCarthy`s own on the record the interview does. That includes people like \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  who posted an animated video depicting him killing a fellow member of Congress, \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio Cortez\uc2  . And Andy Biggs, who helps \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tried to overturn the 2020 election, and \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  , who brought a Holocaust denier to the State of the Union Address.\par \par \uc2 These are not popular political figures outside of their own deep red districts and echo chambers, sometimes even within them to be honest. Allowing these folks to take over your party`s agenda is risky, could be substantively and politically disastrous. There`s a reason why \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  didn`t quite do it. They tried to keep them a little bit in check.\par \par \uc2 It`s not an abstract idea, right. We saw what happened when the extreme far-right tried to take over in last year`s midterm elections. It was a debacle for the Republican Party. It`s why McCarthy has a narrow majority. In fact, we know that some new evidence of just how much damage the Trump- backed MAGA right did. There`s a study out of Maricopa County, Arizona. That of course home of the capital of Phoenix, the most important electoral county in that state. It analyzes how many Republican voters did not vote for certain Republican candidates, including Kari Lake for Governor, \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  for Senate, and Mark Finchem for Secretary of State.\par \par \uc2 Now, all three were full MAGA election deniers endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . All three of them lost. The study found Lake lost five percent of voters who most -- otherwise mostly voted Republican. Masters lost more than six percent, Fincham about eight percent. That is an enormous, enormous electoral penalty, a sort of MAGA tax on these candidates. In a closely divided state, it`s not a recipe for political success.\par \par \uc2 And of course, this applies in the House too. Sure, the people in deep red districts like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  , Andy Biggs, \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  , they don`t have to worry. But there are 18 Republicans representing districts that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won in 2020. In fact, that includes George Santos in New York`s Third District. Good luck on that one. But in all 18 of these districts, associating the totality the Republican brand with well, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  , the one who is calling for treason charges and stalking David Hogg, you know, it`s probably not going to be a big hit.\par \par \uc2 We`re already seeing the actual substantive policy concessions that Kevin McCarthy made to the far right. Among the very first votes this new House majority brought up to anti-abortion messaging votes. Just to remind everyone that this is a party that successfully attacked women`s bodily autonomy and is looking to criminalize abortion across the country. They`ve already had to table plans to bring up an extremely harsh border security bill after some moderate members balked at the possibility of outright banning migrants from seeking asylum in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And now queued up and it appears to be part of the deal, is one of the many deals Kevin McCarthy made to get the speakership. It`s a bill to come to the floor that would abolish the \uc2 IRS\uc2  and income tax and replace it with a 30 percent national sales tax. That`s the conservative Wall Street Journal opinion page saying please don`t do this. And they`re right to say that on the politics.\par \par \uc2 Think about that for a second. Republicans take power. They get to control the House. And one of their first big votes is holding a vote on instituting an across-the-board 30 percent national sales tax on all goods and services. Like, the price you pay would immediately percent higher, Broccoli, Big Macs, furniture, a wildly terrible idea. Just ludicrously terrible politics. But honestly, I`m not sure Kevin McCarthy cares that much. That dude is living day to day right now politically speaking. He`s made it for three weeks as Speaker. I mean, there`s a lot of people have the under on that. So, I guess that`s a success. It`s not seem to have a particularly good long term strategy, I got to say.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:28]\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  is a Democrat of California who serves on the Oversight Committee. Tim Miller, writer-at-large for The Bulwark and a former communications director for Jeb Bush. And both join me now.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Khanna let me start with you on the substance of some of this, particularly this proposal. \uc2 Buddy Carter\uc2  is the Georgia politician if I`m not mistaken who`s wanted this for a while. He wants to completely defund and abolish the \uc2 IRS\uc2  , give everyone a 30 percent national sales tax. There apparently was an agreement between Kevin McCarthy and him that he gets a floor vote on this. Are Democrats shaking in their boots at fear of having to vote on this bill?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): Chris, I can`t believe what they`re doing. After running the midterms, I`d say that the price of eggs are too much, that the price of baking is too much, the price of gas is too much. The first thing they want to do is increase those prices by 30 percent. They want to have a tax raise for the working class. And we know that working-class and middle- class Americans are the ones who buy things. The rich, they`re in the stocks and bonds. And so, the Democrats are saying let`s tax wealth. That`s President Biden. And they`re saying no, let`s tax the working and middle class.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, Tim, it`s interesting, I`ve been watching parts of the sort of I guess what`s left of the intelligence of the American right try to -- try to throw the brakes on this idea. Like the Wall Street Journal opinion page and the National Review people being like, guys, I don`t think there`s a good idea. But it speaks to the fact that you made -- like, Kevin McCarthy made these bargains and these deals to get these people to get himself speaker and he`s going to -- he`s going to have to pay up.\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, WRITER-AT-LARGE, THE BULWARK: He definitely is. And look, this Kevin McCarthy-\uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  marriage is the most prime example of what I`ve called the new MAGA establishment, right? Like, there`s -- we spent all this time talking about oh, there`s these internal fractures within the party and the fights between the Tea Party craze -- you know the MAGA crazies and the moderate governing wing to the extent that exists.\par \par \uc2 But that isn`t real