nd sometimes he`s voting completely by himself. Roy and Norman were part of the, you know, kind of opposition to Kevin McCarthy. How significant is to have these people serving on that committee?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Chris, here`s why this matters, and it`s not some insider baseball thing. These three people basically now have veto power on any bill that comes to the floor of the House. So, you know how extreme things are when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is worried that these far-right folks may cut Social Security. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is begging them, please don`t cut Social Security, please don`t ban abortion in all cases. But these folks are further to the right than him. And any deal that McCarthy does on debt ceiling or anything else, they`re going to have a veto over. And really, they`re running the House.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I also got to wonder. I mean, I did think the news about them pulling this plan for a border bill was a little interesting, Tim, because I mean, again, leverage is there if you exercise it. If you`re the 18 members in those Biden districts, it`s a narrow majority. If you could show that you can ban -- I mean, this is organizing 101, right? Like, you`ve got the numbers. If you can organize, you can have some leverage to that doesn`t have, you know, McCarthy and \uc2 Marjorie Taylor-Greene\uc2  like hammering together the barrel to send you over the waterfall in.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:11]\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes, for sure. Just one thing about this Rules Committee members. Ralph Norman was the guy that was texting \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  calling for martial law, right? You know, so this isn`t even just that he`s extreme on policy. You know, I mean, this was -- I mean, literally was advocating for a coup.\par \par \uc2 So, on the immigration element to it, I do think that these so-called moderates are going to flex muscle behind the scene when they can -- behind the scenes when they can, right? Like, there are enough of them -- like if 