there was one lesson in the midterms, it was that the American people don`t want crazy. They don`t want election deniers. They don`t want instructions. And they are basically handing the keys to the people most responsible for the election denial and insurrection.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  and Tim Miller, thank you, gentlemen, both. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, up Independent Arizona Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  learned she will officially have a challenger from the left. A look at their rewards, and risks. Congressman Ruben Gallego`s Senate bid, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. RUBEN GALLEGO (D-AZ): You are the first group of people that are hearing this besides my family. I will be challenging \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  , and I need all of your support.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: After teasing a potential run for months, Democratic Congressman \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  of Arizona, formally announced his campaign for Senate today. So far, Gallego is the only Democrat for the 2024 race, but it is widely expected that Incumbent Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  , a former Democrat who recently became an Independent, will also run for reelection next year.\par \par \uc2 This puts the Democratic Party in a difficult position because on the one hand, Senator Sinema caucuses with the party, was crucial in securing its Senate majority. On the other hand, she has spent her tenure in the Senate, particularly recently gleefully stymying many of her former party`s biggest priorities. Sinema, along with Democrat \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of West Virginia, ended any hope of filibuster reform, and prevented Democrats from pursuing much of President Biden`s most ambitious agenda, by requiring the support of ten Republicans for most legislation.\par \par \uc2 Sinema also dramatically weakened the initial Biden proposal which was basically shared by all Democrats to allow Medicare to negotiate prescription drug prices, measure that was extraordinarily popular with just about everyone except rich pharmaceutical executives. She killed another effort to close the carried interest loophole which essentially serves a way for wealthy hedge fund managers to avoid paying their fair share of taxes.\par \par \uc2 The most infamous of her actions are probably this moment when she delivered a theatrical thumbs down on the Senate floor against the proposal for a $15.00 minimum wage. Now, at first glance, Senator Sinema`s approach is a big befuddling. I mean, it`s one thing for instance, if \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  supports burning coal and you know, extending help to coal companies. I think it`s terrible policy destroying our planet, but it makes some sense politically. I mean, he represents a very conservative state that is dominated by coal interests for years.\par \par \uc2 But she put prescription drugs, taxing wall street, raising the minimum wage. Those are all very popular proposals, even in a swing state like Arizona. And Democrats can be pretty popular in that state, too. They just won all the statewide election to say one. The junior senator from Arizona, Mark Kelly, he`s been a party-line Democrat. He just won reelection by five points back in November.\par \par \uc2 So, there doesn`t seem to be an editorial pressure behind Senator Sinema`s voting record. I think, honestly, just genuinely reflects her political beliefs. Despite starting her political career as a lefty, a green party anti-war activist, Sinema now seems truly genuinely ideologically committed to a theory of politics that fetishize centrism and protects corporate interests at all costs.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:02]\par \par \uc2 And perhaps it is her sincere belief in such a deeply unpopular political project that keeps her from ever really engaging her with her constituents in any meaningful way. Sinema rarely holds town halls in Arizona. She refuses most requests for media interviews. That`s frankly pretty unusual for a sitting senator.\par \par \uc2 Here`s another example of someone who does it differently. Republican Senator \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  of Iowa currently serving as eighth term, the 89- year-old notorious for frequently meeting with and taking questions from constituents, though he admittedly does usually skip over the major Democratic areas of his state. Or take Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  , Democrat of Connecticut, who walks across his entire state speaking with voters along the way every single year. And voters know where to find Georgia Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  . He presides over most Sunday services at Atlanta`s Ebenezer Baptist Church where he remains senior pastor, same role once hell by \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Senator Sinema by contrast rarely speak for the Arizona voters. She is happy to meet with her corporate donors however. Just last week she appeared in a panel with other lawmakers of the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  in Davos Switzerland, the annual meeting for the global elite where the world`s richest oligarch spend a week patting themselves in the back.\par \par \uc2 Sinema even shared a high five with \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  over their refusal to support filibuster reform. Then she justified her support for the filibuster by bragging it stopped Democrats from passing legislation to end voter suppression.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SINEMA: In the resulting two years, the Democratic Party shared a narrative that said we would not have any more free and fair elections in this country if the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  didn`t eliminate the filibuster and pass a massive voting rights package. As you -- as we all know, the filibuster was not eliminated. Joe and I were not interested in sacrificing that important guardrail for the institution. That massive Voting Rights bill was not passed through Congress, and then we had a free and fair election all across the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s notable how comfortable she seems in Davos, how much she enjoys doing this. This is who Sinema is. Like, we stopped the Voting Rights package. Didn`t it all work out fine? I mean, well, I wouldn`t go that far. Sinema`s record now presents an opportunity for \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2  and he`s running for her seat because he`s got to walk a very delicate tightrope in Arizona. He presumably will be in a three-way race between himself, Sinema and a Republican nominee in a state that is about as evenly divided as they come.\par \par \uc2 And running against Sinema from the left is not necessarily going to work. Instead, he`s framing his fight as a kind of populist one, OK, who plans to cross the state painting Sinema is out of touch and himself as a fighter for the working people of Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLEGO: The rich and the powerful, they don`t need more advocates. It`s the people that are still trying to decide between groceries and utilities that needs a fighter for them. There is no lobbyist for working families. We could argue different ways about how to do it, but at the core if you`re more likely to be meeting with the powerful than the powerless, you`re doing this job incorrectly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congressman Gallego will have more to say when he joins Lawrence O`Donnell tonight at 10:00 p.m. And it`s frankly too soon to tell how this will all shake out. But looking at centers like Mark Kelly, Rafael Warnock were able to win in 50-50 states by supporting Democratic proposals, it`s clear Sinema`s voting record is not a prerequisite for political success. She votes for the wealthy and big corporate interests at the expense of regular working people because that`s what she appears to believe in. Well, now, she`s going to have to answer for those