ust like a random like, weeknight council meeting?\par \par \uc2 ROHRLICH: That`s right. That`s right. They were talking about giving a permit for amplified sound at a local Lions Club car show. There was some discussion of local signage regulations. And Larkin then brought up this motion to get rid of Larry Zimmerman who was the mayor that replaced him and which they did.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There was also an official that they got rid of that had been part of the -- one of the people questioning his ethics, right?\par \par \uc2 ROHRLICH: That`s right. The town, or sorry, the city manager was similarly fired that night.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And the city manager in places like this tends to be the person administratively actually running the municipality.\par \par \uc2 ROHRLICH: Exactly. The mayor from what my sources have told me which include a former member of the city council, the mayor is pretty much a figurehead in Goddard. So --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And this city manager had like concerns about Larkin?\par \par \uc2 ROHRLICH: Correct. Correct. The city manager is an Iraq veteran. He`s an upstanding guy as far as everything that I`ve heard. And for some reason, there was some tension between Larkin and this fellow and he got rid of him. And the next day, the assistant city manager or city administrators is the exact title. The next day, the assistant quit.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, one of the concerns now is that they`re not going to be able to get somebody else to come in and fill that position. This used to be sort of a plum assignment. This is a small city outside of Wichita. About, you know, 45 minutes outside of Wichita. And now, I spoke with Mike Proctor whose seat was the one being filled last week and he`s concerned that nobody is going to want this job now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They`re -- I mean, people don`t seem to be taking lying this down -- taking this lying down. I read some local coverage in in the Wichita Eagle and I read your reporting as 