inuing the violence, the suspect drove to a nearby dance studio where another New Year celebration was underway. And thankfully, a heroic employee managed to wrestle the gun away from the suspect, saving lives. You can see the struggle here on surveillance footage obtained exclusively by NBC News.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:09]\par \par \uc2 That young man there who did that, Brandon Tsay, the one wearing a black coat and white hoodie told "NIGHTLY NEWS" Anchor Lester Holt about the experience.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Did he say anything to you?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON TSAY, DISARMED THE MONTEREY PARK GUNMAN: No. That was the scary part. When he came in, he said nothing. His face was very stoic. The expressions were mostly in his eyes looking around finding -- trying to find people, trying to scout the area for other people.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, how did you decide what to do?\par \par \uc2 TSAY: Well, there was a moment I actually froze up because I was -- I had to believe that I was going to die. Like, my life was ending here at that very moment. But something amazing happened, a miracle actually. He started to try to prep his weapons so he could shoot everybody. But then it came down on me that this was the moment to disarm him. I could do something here that could protect everybody and potentially save myself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The suspect got away fleeing the scene, but was eventually found dead of a self-inflicted gunshot wound about 30 miles away. Now, public identification of the victims is beginning as families are notified. The deceased are men and women ranging in age from 57 into their 70s. The family of one woman killed said she spent years going to Monterey Park dance hall on weekends.\par \par \uc2 These folks were out in their community to celebrate at a time when so many of us are savoring that feeling following years avoiding large gatherings due to the pandemic. Police haven`t named the model of gun that was used, but they describe a semi-automatic pistol with an extended magazine, a kind the sheriff described as likely illegal in the state.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are certain patterns in the distinctly American cancer of mass shooting and one is the profile of the shooter. Most -- all of them are men as in this case, but then there are also deviations. They`re usually younger. This suspect was 72, a rarity. To give you an idea of the scope of this, each image you are seeing here is from a different mass shooting over just the past five and a half years.\par \par \uc2 This was the 33rd mass shooting this year. There have been at least four more since then. In fact this, afternoon two students were fatally shot and an adult was injured at a charter school in Des Moines, Iowa. Just hours ago, at least seven more people were reportedly shot and killed in Half Moon Bay, California. That`s just south of San Francisco. According to local official, the victims are Chinese farm workers. A suspect is now in custody. Police have identified him as 67-year-old Zhao Chunli.\par \par \uc2 A lot of factors go into American mass shootings, a problem really we only have. From hateful ideology, to interpersonal violence, but the one constant, the one constant, that is always there, it`s the gun and easy access to it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This weekend marked the 50th anniversary of the landmark Roe v. Wade decision. There were marches and events in cities across the country protesting the right-wing Supreme Court`s decision gutting Roe and abortion rights last summer. Overturning Roe is one of the most seismic and unpopular decisions the court has made in decades. I think it`s both a symptom and a cause of a court that is declining rapidly in public standing, losing its legitimacy, which now appears to be tearing itself apart from the inside.\par \par \uc2 New York Times\uc2  report on the investigation to who leaked the draft decision overturning Roe says, "Some employees said the leak an investigation further tainted the atmosphere inside a court that had already grown tense with disagreement. The leak spurred finger-pointing, they said, with many conservatives convinced that a liberal had engineered the breach and vice versa. Voices are more hush now, the employee said, and doors that used to be open are closed."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, award-winning \uc2 New York Times\uc2  investigative reporter who wrote that story, Jodi Kantor. Jodi, it`s a great piece of reporting. First, I guess, what is your reporting indicate about the way that leak landed inside that institution, what it meant for the court when it came out?\par \par \uc2 JODI KANTOR, INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER, THE NEW YORK TIMES: It`s great to be with you. So, listen, this was already public. We know that this was a very painful leak for the court. They guard their business very closely. And especially the deliberative process, meaning the process of writing draft opinions, talking them out is supposed to be entirely secret.\par \par \uc2 So, the interesting uh question that I was trying to answer is what happened to the leak investigation because we know that a day or two after the week, the Chief Justice announced this investigation. He sounded like he was taking it very seriously. And then we heard very, very little after that. And so, that`s what I started looking into. And the story we published is less about the Dobbs decision itself to overture the right to abortion, and more about the question, can the Supreme Court investigate itself.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This -- and I think this is relevant from a policy perspective because the court answers only to itself, right? It`s -- it is an independent branch of the government. You describe some of the details investigation which I`d love for you to talk about. I want to read part of the piece here. Lower-level employees have been formally interrogated, recorded, pressed to sign affidavits. In contrast, conversations with the justices have been a two-way iterative process in which they asked as well as answered questions, the marshal, Gail A. Curley, wrote. She had seen no need for them to sign affidavit, she said."\par \par \uc2 Tell us about this sort of two-tiered investigation if you will.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:17]\par \par \uc2 KANTOR: Well, so, part of the reason why some people who have worked inside the court are very upset is because they said, you know, how can you perform an investigation that doesn`t examine the nine figures who had prime access to the draft? And how can you not examine the nine most powerful people involved?\par \par \uc2 So, basically employees had never I think experienced anything like this. They were questioned very, very hard. They had to sign these sworn affidavits. They were told that lying even about something small could be a criminal offense. Some of them were told that this was a condition of their employment that they had to answer these questions. And then, you know, they kind of had -- some of them had the sinking feeling that the justices were not being questioned the same way.\par \par \uc2 The justices are treated with such reverence inside the Supreme Court that one of the fascinating details I learned that I hadn`t known is that, you know, when they get into those black robes, there are actually junior assistants who are standing at the ready to put the robes on if the justices don`t want to put on the roads themselves. And that`s kind of the way they were treated in this investigation. They were treated as above everybody else.\par \par \uc2 The Marshal has said publicly that she did speak to the justices during the investigation but she didn`t question them in the same way the Marshal questioned the employees and they didn`t have to sign those affidavits. And frankly, it`s still -- it`s still really unclear what those conversations with the justices were even like.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s funny that you note that Gail Curley who`s the marshal, there was some back and forth in the initial report saying well, it doesn`t look like she integrated -- she interrogated or questioned the justices. Then she put out a statement that talked about an iterative process in which they said things and asked questions, I said things, which was just a fancy way of saying a conversation. It`s very clear. Like, that statement seemed incredibly intentionally misdirectional to me insofar as what she described was having conversations with the justices. It clearly wasn`t the same thing as what was being done to employees.\par \par \uc2 KANTOR: Well, I also want to point out that the complaints that I heard from sources did not just come from the left and did not just come from people who were upset about the outcome of the Dobbs decision. It was bigger than that. It was this question of this place is supposed to stand for fairness and process and integrit, and what am I supposed to think of this investigation if these nine people weren`t questioned in the same rigorous way as everybody else. Listen, as you know, a basic principle of the law is the idea that we are all equal under it.\par \par \uc2 In fact, that`s inscribed on the Supreme Court itself, the building right there, equal justice under law. Steven Mazie who covers the court had a piece of the Atlantic about some of the publicly observable tensions in the way that members of the court, the justices have been speaking to each other in public rural arguments and also in their written dissent sell- public knowledge. But it seems to point to a very high degree of tension. Then you`ve got the leak on top of this. You`ve got the investigation after it. You`ve got the polling which, you know, they all read the newspaper.\par \par \uc2 What -- how would you characterize the atmosphere inside that institution at this juncture?\par \par \uc2 KANTOR: So, what people said is that it`s just really tense and that -- I mean, remember that people close to the decision-making process found out about the Dobbs vote last -- in December of 2021, and it was this giant secret that they had to keep. And listen, the Supreme Court has dealt with plenty of controversy before. If you think of the Bush v Gore year, that was obviously, you know, a really, really tough episode for people inside. But that played out quickly and that played out publicly.\par \par \uc2 This was a secret that hung and then this leak came you know really as a shock to many people inside the institution. And so, then you know, there was the question of the investigation and the process. And so, listen, there have been a number of controversies and scandals in recent years at the court as you know. I mean, I`m sure everybody watching tonight is familiar with them. This one is a little different I think because it`s really about all nine justices.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Jodi Kantor of the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  , fascinating reporting. I was uh happy you got to come on and talk about it tonight.\par \par \uc2 KANTOR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230124ej1n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 PoliticsNation with \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  for January 22, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Josh Lederman \par \uc2 6,575 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2  , MSNBC HOST: Welcome to Politics Nation. We continue our breaking news coverage out of California tonight. 10 people are dead at this hour after last night`s mass shooting in Monterey Park outside of Los Angeles. At least 10 have been hospitalized. A SWAT team has been mobilized in the neighboring \uc2 city of Torrance\uc2  , where a van believed to be linked to the shooting suspect has been surrounded. An unidentified person reported to have been found inside that van, but no information confirming the identity has been released.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has been briefed throughout the day by Homeland Security officials. Vice President Harris offering the White House condolences earlier today as the Monterey Park community struggles to understand what happened. And of course, why. We`ll bring you the latest up-to-date information throughout the hour. Plus, the rest of the day`s political news.\par \par \uc2 But first, let`s go live to Monterey Park, California. NBC`s Josh Lederman is standing-by Josh, what is new at this hour in this terrible story.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Rev, a mass shooting turned into a daylong manhunt, which then led to a barricaded suspect situation over in Torrance, California. About a half an hour from here in Monterey Park where this shooting took place last night. And at this hour, we are waiting for an update from law enforcement about whether they have identified, whether the man that they believe was in the white van that you`re seeing on your screen right now, is there a suspect, whether or not he is still alive or in custody.\par \par \uc2 We know that this situation in Torrance played out hours after police said that they were looking for this white van and they identified the Asian male that they said had been associated with this van and also was in the belief of law enforcement responsible for the shooting last night that killed those 10 people, sent another 10 to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 We then saw police surround this white van for hours. They had all kinds of tactical vehicles and SWAT vehicles surrounding it. And at this point in time, we learned just in the last few minutes from the LA Sheriff`s Department that the SWAT operation is over. And they are no longer trying to apprehend an armed suspect in Torrance. They say the neighborhood is safe.\par \par \uc2 Now of course that still leaves open the question of is this the suspect from Monterey Park? Is he dead? Is he in custody of police? We don`t have that information now. But the immediate threat in Torrance where police have been really standing around that van trying to make sure it was safe for hours, that immediate situation seems to be resolved. And the big question now is, is there still a suspect on the loose responsible for this shooting as this community of Monterey Park, its neighboring communities and really the entire region. I wonder whether there is still an active threat. How this happened? Why this happened with police saying they still don`t know the motive for what took place tragically last night in Monterey Park. Reverend.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Alright. Thank you, NBC`s Josh Lederman. Joining me now is Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  , Democrat of California. He sits on the House Oversight and Reform Committee. Congressman, I have to begin with condolences to your state and to the loved ones of those 10 people confirmed dead after last night`s mass shooting in Monterey Park, which is represented by your Democratic House colleague Judy Chu.\par \par \uc2 I wonder if and what you`ve heard from her about how her constituents are holding up after this tragedy. And how is your state holding up tonight after one of its worst mass shootings in recent memory?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): Reverend Sharpton, it is heartbreaking. I have reached out to Representative Chu. It`s heartbreaking that this has happened in a Lunar New Year, celebration with the Asian American community. But it is also heartbreaking that we`ve had 33 mass shootings just this year in the new year. I mean, something is deeply wrong in America. And we have to - as a nation come together to stop this madness.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Mass shootings, 33 in 2023, just so people are clear. Take a listen, congressman, to what Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said earlier today about this attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (D), VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: A time of a cultural celebration. And yet another community has been torn apart by senseless gun violence. So, Doug and I join the President and Dr. Biden, and I know everyone here in mourning for those who were killed, as we pray for those who are injured and as we grieve for those many people whose lives are forever changed. All of us in this room and in our country understand as violence must stop. And President Biden and I and our administration will continue to provide full support to the local authorities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Your thoughts, congressman, on the vice president`s statements?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I think she spoke for all of us in California, I mean, obviously, she`s from California. This I`m sure is deeply personal to her and to the second gentleman, and the first priority is to capture the suspect, so that further communities aren`t a threat. And I appreciate the support the administration is giving to local law enforcement. Of course, I really salute the local law enforcement for risking their own lives to bring people to safety.\par \par \uc2 But beyond that, we need to have a conversation in this country about mass shootings, not just the use of guns, obviously, that`s a part of it, but broader conversation - it`s tearing us apart.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now switching back to politics for a moment, just after we went off the air last night, we learned that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigators found another set of classified materials, six items consisting of documents with classification markings and surrounding materials discovered during a consensual search of President Biden`s Wilmington, Delaware home, Friday.\par \par \uc2 The White House says it is cooperating with the Justice Department`s special counsel investigation. Of course, your Republican colleagues in the House have been calling on the DOJ to turn over information from its investigation of the matter. The DOJ refusing the request, citing the ongoing investigation, but now two Democratic Senators \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and Tim Kaine have signaled this weekend that they`re open to investigating the president.\par \par \uc2 Well, what`s your response to this latest discovery? And what do you make of Democrats now open to investigating President Biden?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: There is an investigation. Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has appointed a special counsel and President Biden is 100 percent cooperating with that special counsel. They have notified the Justice Department as soon as they found these classified documents. They have invited the Justice Department in to the president`s personal residence to find any other documents that may be classified. That shows to me that the president believes that the rule of law is fully cooperating and he is acting in the way you would want anyone to act in these types of circumstances.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, earlier this afternoon, reproductive rights supporters mobilized coast-to-coast for the 50th anniversary of Roe vs. Wade today. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  marked the anniversary with remarks this afternoon, acknowledging the impact of Roe`s repeal seven months ago and announcing a forthcoming White House effort to protect access to abortion medication.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, some fear that new House Republican majority will press for a national ban on abortion. You sit on the House pro-choice caucus. Do you see that happening?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I do because the speaker has made a deal with the most extreme elements of the Republican caucus and they have been for a ban. Now whether they can get it through the Senate is a different question. But reverend what I have said is we need term limits for these Supreme Court justices who are following a partisan ideological agenda, they`re not following a partisan ideological agenda.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:00]\par \par \uc2 They`re not following the rule of law. This is something the president`s own commission has recommended to the president. And I hope we can finally have reform of this Supreme Court that is totally disconnected, totally disconnected from ordinary Americans lives.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, before we lose you, I must ask you this, California Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  has yet to announce her retirement from the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  , saying she intends to serve the first of her current term ending in 2024. But Congressman - Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  launched her campaign Feinstein seat last week, Congressman - Congresswoman Barbara Lee has also signaled her intention to run. And Congressman Adam Schiff expected - is expected to make his announcement soon.\par \par \uc2 You`ve said you`re waiting to run (ph) for Feinstein`s seat as well. Have you made a decision yet, congressman?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Reverend Sharpton (inaudible) especially with \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  supporters have asked me to run, as you know, I co-chair of Bernie Sanders`s campaign, and \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  won the state of California. But I`ve also said it will weigh heavily on my decision what Representative Barbara Lee does, she has been a hero of mine. She is the first war (inaudible) endless Afghanistan war, I`ve called her profile of courage, and we need an African American woman in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So, if she decides to run, that would be something I`d look at very closely and making my own decision just given the respect and admiration I have for her.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Alright, thank you for being with us, California Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  . Let`s bring in my political panel, Rick Wilson, co-founder of the Lincoln Project, and Michelle Goldberg, MSNBC Political Analyst, and an op ed columnist for the New York Times .\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with the new reports about additional classified items found that President Biden`s Delaware home. We`re starting to hear more Democratic criticism of the president`s handling of the matter, including West Virginia Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  , this morning on Meet the Press. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WV): Hard to believe that in the United States of America, we have a former president and the current president, that basically in the same situation. How does this happen? OK. The only thing I can tell you, Chuck is when I go into the skiff with a secured documents, they always ask are you clean when you walk out? They want to make sure you`re not carrying anything out. And it might be a mistake, you might just put in your other papers, which you double check right there.\par \par \uc2 So that to be held accountable and responsible is what we all are. And to put those in unsecured spaces is irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rick, a new poll taken before yesterday`s revelations shows most Americans think both President Biden and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  inappropriately handled classified documents. But in weighing the severity, Americans believes Trump`s actions were more serious. President Biden has been cooperating with investigators while former President Trump did not fully comply with subpoenas and as openly challenged the DOJ and criticized the special prosecutor in his case. How do you see this playing out politically?\par \par \uc2 RICK WILSON, THE LINCOLN PROJECT CO-FOUNDER: You know, Rev, I think you`re going to end up with a Republican circus on this, even though from what we can tell so far, the handling by the Trump team of this versus the Biden team, the Biden team has cooperated from the beginning. They`ve invited the DOJ to come in and make sure that they button this whole thing up, that they address it properly. It is not great. Nobody loves it. It`s not a great thing. They`re not proud of it.\par \par \uc2 However, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  believed that it was his right to take these documents, store them and for whatever use he was going to put them to when it was in fact wrong. And he fought it legally and every attorney continues to fight it legally at every turn. Whereas the president is engaging right now, I think in a pretty good faith effort to address a problem. We don`t know the content of these documents yet, but we certainly have been made aware that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took some of the most highly classified documents, an SCI level documents with him.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know that these things are in any way comparable at this point. But we can certainly see that Biden doesn`t have the act and effect of a guilty man, whereas Trump seems to be covering this entire thing up in a huge frenzy.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michelle, how do you view these events?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, THE NEW YORK TIMES COLUMNIST: I think it is so frustrating to watch the kind of political media spin up into but her emails mode again. I mean, I agree with Rick that it seems like irresponsible what Biden did. But when Trump - when the raid first happened at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:00]\par \par \uc2 So, everybody talked a lot about how one of the dispositive things in whether or not Trump would be prosecuted or could be prosecuted was intent and it`s really obvious or should be obvious to everyone that the intent is very different here that Biden has bent over backwards, to cooperate with authorities rather than saying that he has the right to declassify these documents in his mind, and that they are his and not the governments.\par \par \uc2 And so instead, what you have is there`s this kind of appearance of symmetry. And I think too many of us in the media rather than trying to explain why they`re different are simply saying, well, it`s going to look one way or it creates the impression, or it feeds the narrative without kind of taking into account that we`re kind of - we`re creating that narrative as we comment on it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right. Michelle, let me stay with you a minute. Today, The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is reporting by Jeff Zients will become President Biden`s new Chief of Staff. This follows the report that \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  is stepping down from the role in coming weeks. Now, Zients helped oversee the White House`s Coronavirus Response. What do you foresee will be his top priorities in his new position?\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Oh, I can`t say I mean, I think that \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  certainly was someone who was really masterful at holding the Democratic coalition together at making, you know, making progressives feel heard and included and kind of integrating some of their priorities into what was often a more centrist agenda. Whether that`s going - whether - I don`t think we know yet whether the new chief of staff represents a change in direction by the administration or just a change in personnel.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rick, as we do the show tonight, we`re focused on two stories. Tragic mass shooting in California and the 50th anniversary of Roe vs. Wade. And I can`t help but think about the fact that on both issues, there`s modern day Republican Party is out of step with most Americans on both issues. polling suggests a majority of Americans support at least some form of legal abortion, and also some form of gun restrictions. You`ve tried with The Lincoln Project to change attitudes within the GOP. Do you see any chance they will soften their stances on either topic anytime soon?\par \par \uc2 WILSON: Well, at The Lincoln Project, we`ve always been good at addressing a narrow slice of Republicans who are range between 7 percent and 11 percent of the electorate. Look about 25 percent of Republican, female voters are pro-choice. About 15 percent of male Republican voters are pro- choice. The gun control stuff is a lot messier with the polling. But I will say this, the way in which the GOP is approaching the issue of abortion in particular, with all these very punitive punishments, all these very excessive things like the Greg Abbott snitch law in Texas, where people were incentivized to report women for having abortions.\par \par \uc2 They have clearly gone well outside the political and even the former ideological mainstream. I mean, George Herbert Walker Bush, whom I worked for back in the day, was - the Bush rule was abortion should always include rape, incest, or the life of the mother, even the people with a strong pro- life view in the past adopted that. Now they are saying nothing. Now they want no exceptions, no exemptions, including things like ectopic pregnancies. And I think it is really taking it from a pro-life movement to a political attack on individual liberty at a level, whether you`re pro- choice or pro-life, the way in which they`re addressing it is so excessive now that it`s causing a backlash.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michelle, on the same subject, do you see any movement at all from the Republican side on either gun control on pro-life or pro-choice?\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Well, yes, I mean, there`s movement, the movement is further towards the Right. You know, it`s kind of a complete refusal to consider any reasonable regulation of gun purchases. And when it comes to abortion, what you see is pressure from some of the activists who want to see this House pass a national abortion ban, even though it has no chance of becoming law.\par \par \uc2 In a Biden Administration, you`re seeing states generally, the pressure is to narrow the exceptions, to be more punitive with women, for example, who are getting pills through the mail to you know, in the past, it was the line of the anti-abortion movement that they didn`t want to bet that women were victims of abortion and so they didn`t want to arrest or prosecute women who ended their pregnancies. That`s really gone out the window and you`re seeing a lot of prosecutors and Republican officials really getting ready to start going after the women who will continue to end their pregnancies, just people under their pregnancies before Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Alright, Rick Wilson and Michelle Goldberg, thank you both for joining me. Much more Politics Nation after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to Politics Nation. Let`s turn to an expert as we follow the latest developments in a Saturday night shooting in Los Angeles County, leaving 10 people dead. Joining me now is Rob D`Amico, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  supervisor and founder of Sierra 1 Consulting. Rob, the Los Angeles Sheriff`s Department released an image of the alleged suspect and officials mentioned they were looking for a white van.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Earlier, we saw images of a SWAT team surrounding a similar vehicle. Why wasn`t the suspected shooter`s name released? And what`s the process in these types of manhunts?\par \par \uc2 ROB D`AMICO, FORMER FBI SUPERVISOR: So, the name release, I think at the time with - when they`re working investigation, if they - they thought if they released the name, it could possibly harm investigation some way, I`m not exactly sure the exact details on it. But something - the release of the picture was again for public safety said, OK, they`re weighing the information that they release, what do we need to get out to help the public help us to put them in a safer place. But what do we want to hold back, and if they released the name, it could have been a whole bunch of press go over to his house, they might not know his house was secure. They might not know if he`d gone to a cousin`s house or something.\par \par \uc2 And then as the public gets that, and they start putting themselves in those positions, it could then lead to something a little bit more difficult or challenging in a tactical environment until they put them down. So, I think the name was they just waited and said, I don`t think anything good is going to come up with this. If someone sees his picture and knows him, they`re going to call in. And they`re going to talk to us about possibly his location or something like that.\par \par \uc2 The white van was being worked at the time, they did not know exactly if he was in there or not. And I still haven`t heard if they confirmed it positively.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right.\par \par \uc2 D`AMICO: But they knew it was connected. And then from what I heard was they when they got up on it and surrounded, they heard something that sounded like a gunshot. So, at that point, they now still have to try to make communications with them. I`ve been in situations where someone was barricaded in a house, they shot the gun, everyone kind of thought they did a suicide. But in reality, he was laying down faking it. And he was waiting for tactical team to come in. But instead, we breach the walls and we put a drone in. And we saw that he wasn`t in fact there.\par \par \uc2 So, the tactical lead up to that was they`re trying to communicate with them, they probably had some type of camera in there, looking for any signs of life. And then they finally got to the point where you actually have to go hands on. They had to open the doors. And then I`m not sure exactly what happened. But most likely, he did, the person in that van committed suicide, they secured the scene and now they`re processing it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now what might be some of the tools that law enforcement would use to discern whether the vehicle that they are looking for is in fact the one the suspect use given that white vans are so common.\par \par \uc2 D`AMICO: Yes, so exactly. Because we looked for white vans during DC site, you probably remember back then it wasn`t the vehicle they`re using. I would say if they had an eyewitness putting him escaping in that van, if they got a partial license plate, if they started like, immediately looking at that closed caption television that someone may have had from a store around where he escaped from to be able to put a tag to that van. I`m not exactly sure how they got onto it in Torrance because that is a bit away, but somehow, they did.\par \par \uc2 And then just going off caution if you can see that the vans parked really odd in that parking lot. So, it was a police engagement that started following it. And then he pulled in and then decided like OK, gigs up type of thing. He might have been listening to news saying oh, they know I have a white van my pictures coming out, something along those lines.\par \par \uc2 But something caused that van to go into a parking lot, not parking the space, it didn`t look like it was intentional. He just stopped. Police surrounded when he first went to the scene, there was a ton of cops. So, the cops were coming up on it, they got it into the safe tactical place with the two armored vehicles, which is great about armor. Now you can do that, pin it in, so he can`t get out and then slowly start and try to bring the situation to a peaceful resolution.\par \par \uc2 And then that`s when I heard, they heard the gunshot. But you still can`t just go in assuming that he ended his life.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now when does law enforcement know when it`s appropriate to confront the target when it`s a standoff like we`ve seen today?\par \par \uc2 D`AMICO: In the old days, a lot of the police like used to do dynamic entries. And if there`s not a hostage involved, I mean, I was on the hostage rescue team. So, you bypass when there`s someone else`s life on the line, you bypass a lot of danger areas to bring that peacefully resolved and that`s why you use explosives, you do all these things to surprise them because you`re going into harm`s way.\par \par \uc2 But the Bureau has always learned, like, if there`s no one else`s life involved, then you can just sit back. You take your time. You make it safe all around and you start talking to them. We have negotiators that do that all the time, and a lot of times they can peacefully talk someone out, giving themselves up or coming to a peaceful resolution.\par \par \uc2 So, time at that point is on your side. You got him contained. He can`t go anywhere. That van can`t move. You`ve basically taken all the civilians out of gunfire range by evacuating storefronts and other vehicles. And now you just can sit.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve been on hostage strongholds for hours and hours where we`ve been rotate a new tactical team for emergency assault just in case something happens. But you can sit there barricaded subjects days if necessary. At one point, he`s going to need something in that van, and then you`re going to try to come to a way to get him food or whatever. And again, that`s where the negotiators come in, working with the tactical teams. But we`ve found that you don`t have to go in, and you shouldn`t. You`re putting long law enforcement lives in jeopardy and the subjects if he`s mentally ill. And you need to resolve it through crisis, you know, management.\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2  , MSNBC HOST: All right. Thank you. Rob D`Amico. More Politics Nation after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:35:44]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to this special coverage here on Politics Nation. Let`s go live to Monterey Park, California. NBC`s Josh Lederman is standing by. Josh, give us the latest.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Reverend Al, we had been expecting a news conference about 20 minutes from now in Torrance, California, which is where police for hours now have been surrounding that white cargo van that they first said earlier today, they were looking for in connection with these incidents.\par \par \uc2 We`re starting to hear that, that news conference where we expected to learn what exactly has happened with that van may no longer take place. So, we`re trying to get to the bottom of how we`re going to get our next update from law enforcement. But clearly there has been a whole lot of activity just in the last couple of hours in Torrance, California, which is about a half an hour from here in Monterey Park, where the mass shooting took place with police. Initially saying that they had a barricaded suspect situation involving that white cargo van, that they thought there was somebody inside the van that might be the suspect from the shooting.\par \par \uc2 And then, later the county sheriff`s office saying that there is no longer an operation in pursuit of an armed suspect in that particular community of Torrance and that the neighborhood is safe. So, clearly, whatever a situation was happening with that van has been resolved. Whether that has been resolved because they determined that it was not their person or because they discovered that who was the person that was in there was dead or some other end to that situation, we don`t know yet. But that -- we do expect that will be the next update that we will get from law enforcement and hopefully some answers about why any of this happened for a community that right now is not only grieving, but struggling with the fact that as we approach, you know, the second evening after this took place last night, there are still so many questions about why. Apparently, this man walked into a dance hall here in Monterey Park, and, according to police, opened fire, killing those ten individuals and injuring many more, Reverend.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Josh Lederman, thank you for that reporting. More coverage on the Monterey mass shooting after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:41:37]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to Politics Nation. Joining me now is Connie Chung Joe, CEO of \uc2 Asian Americans Advancing Justice\uc2  in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Joe, thank you for joining us this evening. While a motive has yet to be established and the shooting has not been classified as a hate crime, this shooting did target a Chinese Lunar New Year celebration in Monterey Park, which has one of the largest Asian populations in the United States. And the suspect in this shooting has been identified by the Los Angeles County Sheriff`s Department as an Asian male. What are you hearing from the Asian community in the L.A. area tonight?\par \par \uc2 CONNIE CHUNG JOE, ASIAN AMERICANS ADVANCING JUSTICE CEO: Right. So, thank you, Reverend Sharpton. First, we do know it`s an Asian shooter suspect against the Asian community. That does not rule out that it could potentially still be a hate crime. We certainly saw that in Laguna Woods, where it was an ethnically motivated hate crime between an Asian against an Asian community.\par \par \uc2 However, we`re hearing that it could also be intimate partner violence or a domestic dispute. What we know in the Asian Pacific Islander community is, regardless of what the intent is, the impact on our community is that we feel targeted and we`re scared and we`re traumatized. As you mentioned, this happened at a Lunar New Year festival. Lunar New Year for many of our API communities is as large as Christmas is here in the United States. And imagine if you had a shooter gun down a Christmas celebration or festival, that`s what it feels like to our community.\par \par \uc2 And in Monterey Park, which is over 60% Asian in the San Gabriel Valley, which is one of the largest Asian neighborhoods in this country. Certainly, our community is feeling scared, and we don`t know if it`s a hate crime or not, but it doesn`t matter, because right now the reaction and the fear and the emotions in our community is that we are feeling targeted again. When you think about the context of this pandemic, where we`ve seen the rise in anti-Asian hate and our community has been scapegoated for that and we`ve seen so many attacks and killings, we are still feeling that fear, regardless of what the actual intent of the shooter ends up being.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yeah. Are you concerned that the shooting and the shooter`s identity, if he is indeed found to be of Asian descent, may provoke more violence against AAPI communities that have already been dealing with the rising hate for the past few years?\par \par \uc2 JOE: We are certainly worried about that. We are worried about, you know, copycat shooters, as sometimes mass shootings do lead to a ripple effect of more shootings. And what we`ve seen is, as the news comes out, if you look at different live feeds and chats, that even when we don`t know what the intent of the shooter was, there is a tremendous amount of hate motivated speech happening in reaction. People are saying, you know, good riddance for Asians and speaking in really violent and racist language against Asians in response to the news feed.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:13]\par \par \uc2 So, we certainly don`t rule out that there will be a ripple effect and that our Asian community could be targeted. And so, we`re being very careful as we talk to local officials and other Asian nonprofits in the community to see what we can do to make sure our community feels safe and make sure we have opportunities for them to heal and really to promote the social services and support that`s available to our community. Making sure people know what`s available, especially that`s in language, in language like Cantonese and Mandarin that a lot of the community members speak and that they`re tied to culturally appropriate resources.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I`m glad you said being careful, because I remember when we had the White House anti-Hate Summit last year, AAPI leaders, Latino leaders, Jewish leaders, myself from NAN. The intensity of what you have to deal with, people really don`t understand in all of the areas that we face, the potential of hate crimes or in fact, hate crimes themselves. No word has been released by the L.A. County Sheriff`s Department on what kind of weapon to suspect he was in the massacre, though the county sheriff said in today`s press conference that it was not a high powered assault rifle. And California does have some of the strictest gun laws in the country. Still, in spite of that, this happened. What more can be done, in your opinion?\par \par \uc2 JOE: We need so much more comprehensive and robust gun control laws in this country. Yes, California as a state is better than maybe the rest of the nation. I know we have protections against not allowing guns for those who have a domestic violence record, for example, but it`s not enough. We need comprehensive gun control in this country.\par \par \uc2 Uvalde, we`ve had it. We had Buffalo, we`ve had Laguna Woods. We`ve had this. We`ve had Atlanta. I don`t know how many more massacres we need to see, whether in our communities of color or just in our schools or in general, for this country to finally say we need to have a control and get the reins on making sure guns are not so freely available to anyone. Because when guns are floating around so easily, these things can happen. And it`s too easy for mass shootings like this, and it`s too frequent that mass shootings like this are happening.\par \par \uc2 And one message I will have for our Asian Pacific Islander community is that the solution to this problem for us is not more guns. And I hear that with a lot of our leaders and within communities of color. I think that`s an important message that we continue to share, that as we are being targeted or if we get victimized or we get hurt, that allowing more guns out on the streets is not going to be the answer.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, briefly, we`re out of time. But how does the AAPI community in Monterey Park heal and repair after this?\par \par \uc2 JOE: So, first, I will say that we are urging community members to reach out and get support. In L.A. County, we have a system called 211, which if you call that number, they will connect you to a network of Asian serving social service providers who have come forward during this rise in anti- Asian hate to provide legal services like our organization does, mental health, victims of crime benefits, so many others. So, whether you`re a direct victim or a vicarious victim, meaning somebody who still feels traumatized by what you`re seeing, we encourage you to reach out. 211 is 24/7.\par \par \uc2 We will also be releasing soon within the API local community a list of resources that you`ll be able to find on our website, as well as others of who are local service providers available to provide support in language for victims and other community members. And then, to the rest of our API community and those in Monterey Park, we want you to know that we are here in support of solidarity of you.\par \par \uc2 I live ten minutes away from Monterey Park. I had staff at that Lunar New Year Festival last night, and I was supposed to be there today with my staff as well as my family. So, I do understand how traumatic this experiences for our community. And I just want to say we are all here looking for healing and support for one another.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:08]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you, Connie Chung Joe, CEO of \uc2 Asian Americans Advancing Justice\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Before we finish the show tonight, I want to share something that is heavy on my mind. This week, the \uc2 Florida Department of Education\uc2  rejected an advanced placement high school course on African American studies, claiming that the curriculum lacked value and promoted a political agenda.\par \par \uc2 Florida`s Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has been waging war on what he calls the wokeness in the state schools and institutions. But many teachers say they are caught in the middle of a political fight.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:02]\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, National Action Network people Reverend Divest Tunes (ph) spoke with Florida State Senator Shevrin Jones, and were told that he will announce with some of his partners, which include attorney General of Black America \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2  and Bishop Rudy McKissick and Reverend HP Homes (ph). They plan to mobilize statewide against this decision, and I`ve committed to come within the next several days with them when they designate the time for a statewide rally and speak at that rally to erase African American studies.\par \par \uc2 When we are trying to get people to understand each other and to appreciate the worth of each other in and of itself is, in my opinion, promoting bigotry and intolerance because we`re not teaching young people how to appreciate each other`s history. DeSantis we are not going to let you deal with this without our confronting this issue.\par \par \uc2 Let me also ask you to pray for those in Monterey Park and let us all remember those families. On last weekend, we remembered the holiday for Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  . You`re judged not by how you only sympathize and empathize with your own. You`re judged by how you deal when it may be other than you and show the same concern and prayers.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:16]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 05:00 p.m. Eastern. American Voices with Alicia Menendez starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020230123ej1m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for January 21, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Jessica Layton, Brendan Buck \par \uc2 6,938 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:35]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2  , MSNBC HOST: Goody evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Half and Half.\par \par \uc2 This weekend, the Biden administration is celebrating two years on the job. The President addressed America`s Black Mayors and all Mayors really in Washington this week, touting policy wins on infrastructure and the economy at the midway mark of his presidential term.\par \par \uc2 Looming ahead in the next two years are new challenges: Republicans in the House are clamoring to use their new majority to investigate the President`s handling of classified documents and to put his Department of Justice under the microscope.\par \par \uc2 And just two days ago, the nation hit its debt limit setting up a high- stakes confrontation over government spending. It is a battle President Biden will have to face without his veteran Chief-of-Staff, \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  . NBC has learned Klain will leave his post in the coming weeks, according to two sources familiar with the decision.\par \par \uc2 All of this, as we mark the 50th anniversary of Roe vs. Wade this weekend, seven months after the landmark ruling protecting abortion rights at the Federal level was overturned by a conservative dominated Supreme Court. It`s another hot button issue that will no doubt demand the President`s attention in the next two years.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Congresswoman Sara Jacobs Democrat of California. She is the House Democratic Caucus leadership representative.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Jacobs, first of all, thank you for joining us. And before we get into the legacy of Roe vs. Wade, I want to get your take on the news that White House Chief-of-Staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  is stepping down. Klain celebrated his second anniversary just yesterday in a position where the average tenure is about 18 months. What`s your reaction?\par \par \uc2 REP. SARA JACOBS (D-CA): Look, I think Ron is going to be very missed in that position, although he is allowed his much deserved rest. I think by virtue of all the things we`ve been able to accomplish in these two years, it is very clear he had been one of the most successful White House Chief- of-Staffs ever.\par \par \uc2 I actually worked with him before I was in Congress, and before he was White House Chief-of-Staff, and I know what a thoughtful and amazing person he is, and he has been such an asset to the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, I can say honestly, in my capacity as head of National Action Network, he was always accessible and has always been a straight shooter.\par \par \uc2 But let me move on, yesterday, the President marked the halfway point of his first term, promoting his administration`s successes over this past two years. That two-year span marks your own tenure in Congress in which you have become the youngest member of the House Democratic leadership as the Caucus now finds itself in the minority.\par \par \uc2 Given all of that, this divided Congress, you must navigate to get things passed. Where do you hope the President throws his weight between now and 2024?\par \par \uc2 JACOBS: By any measure what President Biden has already accomplished in his first term has been historic. We`ve passed the biggest investment in climate change ever. We passed the first gun violence prevention legislation since I was in elementary school.\par \par \uc2 We passed a bipartisan infrastructure bill that Presidents from both parties have been talking about for decades and never been able to get done. We`ve had -- we`ve created 11 million jobs. We pulled millions of children out of poverty.\par \par \uc2 Even if nothing gets done over the next few years, which I don`t think will happen, it has already been a historic term and we`ve been able to do that with the slimmest of majorities in the Congress.\par \par \uc2 So now as we go into the minority, I still think there will be a lot of room to be able to get things done and deliver for the American people. I`ll be focusing on trying to address the high cost of living for families, especially around housing, and childcare, which for my constituents in San Diego are huge, huge burdens and barriers, and I think that there will be some room to do bipartisan compromise on those issues.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:22]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, the Biden White House has maintained its stance that it will not negotiate with House Republicans over our national debt ceiling, which the US hit Thursday. Republicans want spending cuts from the administration, in exchange for raising the limit.\par \par \uc2 Failure to raise the limit by June may result in the country defaulting on its debt for the first time in history, possibly leading to a market turmoil and complicating the government`s ability to make social security payments. Some economists warn recession could ensue.\par \par \uc2 Of course, this is not the first time you`ve had a standoff over the debt ceiling, but now you have the expanded influence of the right wing of this House GOP driving the deadlock, what`s going to happen?\par \par \uc2 JACOBS: Look, I think it`s very simple. Democrats want to protect Social Security and Medicare and the extreme Republicans want to hold the full faith and credit of the United States hostage to be able to cut Medicare and Social Security.\par \par \uc2 And so I agree with President Biden, we are not going to negotiate with hostage takers. Frankly, I think it doesn`t make sense to have us vote on the debt ceiling at all. We`ve already voted to appropriate this funding. We should allow the executive branch to spend the money to execute on what we`ve already authorized and appropriated and voted on in Congress. So, I actually support getting rid of a debt limit. We are only one of two countries that has one.\par \par \uc2 But in the meantime, I think it`s important that we continue standing up for Medicare, for Social Security, for all of the things that we know Americans want and need from their government, and that Congress has already voted to approve.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman, as we discuss priorities, you sit on the Congressional Prochoice Caucus. Opponents of reproductive rights were out in force for yesterday`s March for Life in Washington, and many in the House GOP are supporting a national ban on abortion, seven months after the overturning of Roe after 50 years.\par \par \uc2 I read this week about your plans to reintroduce reproductive rights legislation that stalled in the House last year, specifically your bill to limit the collection of reproductive health data on websites and phone apps.\par \par \uc2 What`s your strategy in getting this bipartisan support on this particular issue in this Congress?\par \par \uc2 JACOBS: Look, I think the fact that the March for Life ended at Congress shows very clearly that this was never about State rights. We know the extreme Republicans want a national abortion ban, and they`ve already introduced it, and it is clear that the majority of Americans don`t want that and they showed that very clearly in this Midterm Election.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to keep standing up for abortion access, for abortion rights, and we`re going to make sure while we`re doing that, we are working to protect people in States that are already criminalizing abortion.\par \par \uc2 And one of the ways we really need to do that is to protect reproductive and sexual health data in the way that my bill would do.\par \par \uc2 Now, there was a poll done in October that showed that the majority of Americans, including the majority of Republicans want Congress to act to protect reproductive and sexual health data from being sold or shared without your consent.\par \par \uc2 And so there is bipartisan consensus among the American people and I`m working now with privacy minded Republicans to try and see if there is a version of this bill, we can craft that we`ll be able to pass in a bipartisan way.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Lastly, Congresswoman as of this week, we now have some GOP House members that have denied the 2020 election results and expressed sympathies with the January 6th insurrectionists sitting on committees tasked with defending our institutions and investigating threats to them.\par \par \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  , \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  , Lauren of Boebert, and Scott Perry, now all sit on the House Oversight Committee. Greene is also on the Homeland Security Committee.\par \par \uc2 You`re on House Democrats Steering and Policy Committee, which makes those Committee assignments for the other side of the aisle. What`s your takeaway on all this?\par \par \uc2 JACOBS: Look, I think that these Committee assignments show very clearly that Kevin McCarthy mortgaged his speakership and the Republican majority to the far right, that he will be the weakest Speaker in history and that he has already given up all the power to the extreme parts of his party.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s a really sad day for America when someone who defrauded small businesses sits on the Small Business Committee, when someone who is being investigated by the Justice Department is going to be sitting on the Committee investigating those investigations, when someone who doesn`t believe in the safety and security of our country sits on the Homeland Security Committee.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:15]\par \par \uc2 JACOBS: So I think this was a moment where Republicans could have shown leadership and could have actually shown the American people that they were willing to fight for what they said they were going to do in the election and address inflation, and instead, they are so focused on kowtowing to the far-right extreme and fighting with each other than doing the right thing for the American people.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, we have to leave it there. Thank you very much for being with us, Congresswoman Sara Jacobs.\par \par \uc2 Now as we just mentioned, tomorrow is the 50th anniversary of Roe vs. Wade, and as some States across the country moved to restrict abortion access after the repeal of Roe last summer, some Democratic Attorney Generals are fighting to protect reproductive rights. One of them is joining me now. Michigan`s Attorney General Dana Nessel.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the Biden administration changed \uc2 FDA\uc2  rules to make abortion pills more readily available in pharmacies; at the same time, the new Republican House Majority has made restricting abortion access at the Federal level a priority, introducing a flurry of legislation including a bill that could subject doctors who perform abortions to criminal penalty.\par \par \uc2 As an Attorney General, how do you navigate the push and pull on abortion going on at the Federal level?\par \par \uc2 DANA NESSEL (D), MICHIGAN ATTORNEY GENERAL: Well, first of all, you know, one of the things that I ran on initially in 2018 was the understanding that we were likely going to see the overturning of Roe, quite honestly after \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  was appointed to the Supreme Court. We knew that it was coming.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of us in States around the country said as Attorneys General that we were not going to enforce these abortion bans that would criminalize what would have been a legal medical procedure for nearly 50 years in the United States.\par \par \uc2 And now, as State Attorneys General in an environment where obviously Roe is now technically no longer the law of the land, and you have some States where it is legal like it is in Michigan now since the voters took back our right and we codified reproductive rights into our Constitution, we have made it a priority to make sure that not only are we protecting people who live in our States, but we will also be protecting people who come to our States in order to access critical healthcare, which is what abortion care is.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Attorney General, you have refused and forced Michigan`s 1931 abortion law, which means performing the procedure a felony.\par \par \uc2 In September, a State Court declared the law unconstitutional. However, abortion opponents in your State are still looking for ways to restrict access in your State, as it is restricted in some neighboring states such as Wisconsin, which has an abortion ban with no exceptions for rape and incest. How confident are you that your State can protect abortion rights?\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Well, unless we have some interference by the Federal government at this point, there is very little that anybody can do because we as the voters put this on the ballot in November, and it passed by 13.5 points, almost 600,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 So it is codified into our Constitution, and of course, not just the right to an abortion, but also of birth control. The ability to manage a miscarriage, even fertility treatments now are codified into the Michigan Constitution.\par \par \uc2 So the only thing that could change that at this point is the Federal government, and if we have Congress passing a law that outlaws abortion and a President who will sign on to it, and that`s why what I have to say to people is this, at this point without protections by the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  , every election is an election where abortion is on the ballot, whether you`re voting for somebody in the State Legislature, or whether it`s a Member of Congress and certainly whoever is President of the United States matters in regard to abortion now until we have another Supreme Court that decides to protect abortion rights again.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let`s switch to voting rights: Democrats in Michigan are trying to expand voting access after their successes in the Midterm Elections, specifically after voters approved a constitutional amendment that creates a nine-day early voting period and requires the State to fund absentee ballot drop boxes.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Are you also looking into automatic voter registration? And have you discussed criminalizing election misinformation?\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Yes, I think that everything is on the table with our legislature, and obviously, we have -- first time in 40 years that we have a Democratic Legislature and a Democratic Governor, and so I think they are looking to ensure that we enshrine voting rights into our State laws to the very best of their ability, and also to protect election workers, which is a significant issue in our State and in States all around the country.\par \par \uc2 So I know that this Legislature will try as hard as they can to do that, but we are only in this place to begin with because the people took back are rights and when we had a Congress that would not protect voting rights and a \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  that will not protect voting rights, and frankly, a State Legislature that wouldn`t do that either when it was controlled by Republicans, it was these People`s Initiatives, both in 2018, when we made access to voting absentee, no reason, and whether it was abolishing gerrymandering in our State, which we also did via a ballot proposal.\par \par \uc2 And then as you indicated, this last time, ensuring that we have as many protections for voting rights as possible. We`re going to continue to do that in the State of Michigan as it seems to get harder and harder to do on a Federal level.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Lastly, I must ask you before I let you go about gun control. Democratic leaders in Michigan, who now control both the House and its Senate have made it clear they want to pass gun laws, specifically universal background checks, new gun storage requirements, and so-called Red Flag laws meant to keep firearms out of the hands of people who pose an immediate threat to others.\par \par \uc2 Even Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  , has said that these are high priorities for her, too. I also know personally that religious leaders in Michigan are joining forces to push the State Legislature to enact sensible gun laws that will keep churchgoers safe.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us about these efforts?\par \par \uc2 NESSEL: Well, that they are underway. And that, you know, there`s a lot of excitement about finally, at long last, having the opportunity to pass these commonsense gun laws that will help protect the residents of our State.\par \par \uc2 These are enormously popular proposed legislation. So many people in our State, both Democrats and Republicans and Independents, of course, as well want to see these laws enacted. So what I can tell you is this, the Legislature is actively working on those laws as we speak, and I think we`re going to see the introduction of them shortly.\par \par \uc2 And I believe that they`re going to pass because here`s why, we know that they work. They are impactful. They are effective. They are going to bring down gun deaths and gun violence in our States, and that is why all of us won our elections, who believed in putting these kinds of laws on the books because we want to protect people the best that we can, and Democrats in our State are determined to do just that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michigan Attorney General Dana Nessel, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Later on POLITICSNATION, House Republicans, and the White House gear up for a clash over the debt ceiling. And Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  trades in her space lasers for a seat on the Homeland Security Committee.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague Jessica Layton, with today`s top news stories -- Jessica,\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LAYTON, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Reverend Al. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Stories were watching at this hour: Five Memphis Police officers had been fired after the Chief there says they violated department policies during a traffic stop that ended up leading to the death of Tyre Nichols, a 29-year- old Black man.\par \par \uc2 The incident is being reviewed by the \uc2 Tennessee Bureau of Investigation\uc2  and the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the DOJ is also investigating baby formula maker, \uc2 Abbott Laboratories\uc2  about a year after the company shut down a plant in Michigan. The closing came after babies who drank that formula got sick and you probably remember the closure then contributed to a nationwide shortage of that infant formula.\par \par \uc2 And another giant leap for retired astronaut Buzz Aldrin, who married his longtime girlfriend yesterday on his 93rd birthday. Aldrin was the second man to set foot on the moon back in 1969 -- never too late for love.\par \par \uc2 I`m Jessica Layton. More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:24:06]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Kevin McCarthy paid a heavy price to get himself elected House Speaker on the 15th ballot earlier this month. This week, he started making good on his debt to his far-right colleagues, many of them are getting plum Committee assignments, including Arizona Congressman \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  , denounced by his own family as unfit for office and banned by Democrats from Committees for posting threatening memes about other members online.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Gosar will now sit on Oversight, a panel charged with promoting accountability and good governance.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  also got a spot on the Oversight Committee, but even more outrageous is our appointment to the Homeland Security Committee.\par \par \uc2 [17:24:01]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman Greene rose to prominence as an enthusiastic supporter of the QAnon conspiracy theory, flagged by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  as a potential terror threat as far back as 2019. Greene has said she now regrets her belief in QAnon, though she has never fully denounced all of their teachings. She voted to overturn the 2020 election and embraced the Stop the Steal Movement that led to the January 6th insurrection.\par \par \uc2 And just last month, I suggested perhaps the protesters that day didn`t go far enough. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): Then January 6th happens and the next you know, I organized the whole thing along with \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  here.\par \par \uc2 [LAUGHTER]\par \par \uc2 GREENE: And I`ve got to tell you something -- if \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  and I had organized that, we would have won.\par \par \uc2 [CHEERING AND APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 GREENE: Not to mention, it would have been armed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And that`s not all, while former President Trump was rightly criticized for just having dinner with Nick Fuentes, just months ago, Congresswoman Greene spoke at a conference organized by the White supremacists.\par \par \uc2 Greene has claimed she didn`t know who Fuentes was, but if she had listened to his speech at the same event, she would have heard him speak approvingly of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and even Adolf Hitler.\par \par \uc2 In an era when law enforcement organizations have identified domestic hate groups as a primary threat to the nation, placing Congresswoman Greene on Homeland Security Committee is like putting the fox in charge of guarding the hen house.\par \par \uc2 The fact McCarthy and his supporters have no qualms about her appointment is a clear sign, they are more concerned with personal power than the safety of our nation.\par \par \uc2 I gotcha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:31:09]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Let`s further dive into today`s big topics with my political panel.\par \par \uc2 Brendan Buck political analyst and former adviser to speak with \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  and Michael Hardaway, founder of Hardaway Wire and former staffer to now Minority Leader, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  and Senator Dick Durbin.\par \par \uc2 Michael, the Biden administration passed the midway mark during their first term, and today NBC News has confirmed that White House Chief-of-Staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  is expected to step down in the coming weeks. It is not unusual to see turnover in the White House at this point in the presidency, but Klain has been an experienced and steady hand. What`s your reaction?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HARDAWAY, FOUNDER, HARDAWAY WIRE: I think he has done an incredibly excellent job of delivering on the President`s agenda. All these historic firsts that we`ve had, under this present President had been executed by \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 As Chief-of-Staff to the President, you`re really the quarterback who is responsible for implementing, executing, and orchestrating the President`s agenda, and I think if you look at things like historic infrastructure that has been passed, and all these other things that have been delivered by this White House, \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  is the person responsible for making sure those trains run on time.\par \par \uc2 And so I think for him, there is a personal toll as well, that comes along with being a White House staffer, much less the Chief-of-Staff. And so it`s completely understandable that he would want to leave at this particular moment, after he has delivered all these very important things.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And he stayed longer than the average.\par \par \uc2 Brendan, Klain`s exit comes as the administration transitions from working with a Democratically controlled Congress to a House run by Republicans. How does divided government changed the way the White House is run?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, this is a completely different job at this point. And like the time for legislating is over. I mean, there`s no more big bipartisan bills, there`s no more big progressive bills that are going to happen. This is pure politics for the next two years.\par \par \uc2 Congressional investigations are coming. Obviously, they need to ramp up for reelection, or at least we expect that`s what they`re going to be doing. So whoever they pick next, I think it needs to be someone who is very familiar with the investigations and oversight role of Congress, someone who knows how to fight down in the mud, because that`s what`s coming at them, but frankly, somebody who is prepared to draw contrast.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think, handedly, the White House has a huge opportunity with Republicans coming into the House. I mean, we saw it with Barack Obama , we saw with \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  , contrasting with a House Republican Congress that is probably going to be very aggressive. And if the White House is able to take advantage of any overreach that could be what provides the boost for the President as he seeks to get reelected.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michael, one of the first issues this new Congress will have to deal with is the debt ceiling. Many House Republicans say they won`t increase the limit unless there are spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  " writes, "For many Democrats, a debt limit standoff could bring political benefits because they believe the public will not side with Republican attempts to extract concessions at the risk of default. Is that a sound conclusion? Many economists are warning a default could be catastrophic for the economy.\par \par \uc2 HARDAWAY: This is an embarrassment. We`re the greatest nation in the world and we continually operate in this way, where every few years if you have a Democrat in the White House and Republicans controlling the House, all of a sudden we have to go through this exercise about money that is spent, and in this particular situation, debt limit allocations have already been put forth and that money has already been spent.\par \par \uc2 It is kind of like at home, if you wait until you get the disconnection notice to pay your bills, it is incredibly irresponsible and I think that most Americans who are in the middle on both sides of the aisle, can look at Washington, particularly with the House Republican conference that is really leading this horrific effort, and I think I will say, I can`t put these people back in power in `24 if this is how they`re going to govern.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:26]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I`ll look at Kevin McCarthy, I think he has got a real opportunity to lead, I just don`t know that he can truly represent the far- right wing of his caucus when he meets with the President on this particular issue, and I think that will be something to watch.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Brendan, for Speaker McCarthy, the debt limit debate could pit him against not only Democrats, but against hardliners in his own party.\par \par \uc2 As you`ve seen firsthand, debates over government spending played a role in the unfolding of the past two Republican Speakers of the House. How do you see this playing out?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes, this is what I`m really worried about for Kevin McCarthy, and there has been a lot of focus on some of the promises he made as it relates to the procedure on the floor and how easy it is to try to kick him out, Committee assignments -- this is what really is most important, he made a bunch of promises to the far right of the conference, that they would be able to cut spending very significantly, and that they wouldn`t negotiate with Democrats on funding the government or on a debt limit increase unless they agreed to do so.\par \par \uc2 And I just think that he was making some promises that he can`t really deliver. So, I`m very concerned about this both for Kevin McCarthy`s political future, but also just how the heck we increase the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 I think, I`ve lived through some pretty dramatic debt limit standoffs on my time on the Hill, I think is a really dangerous tool, and we should just get rid of it, but it is very clear to me that the Republicans don`t actually have a plan for increasing the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 I`m not so sure the White House has a plan for how they are going to increase the debt limit. So I think at some point, they`re going to have to come together. I understand not wanting to negotiate. One person`s negotiation is another person`s fig leaf. There may be some small thing they can give Republicans.\par \par \uc2 But I think people need to realize this is a pretty dire situation, and people need to be thinking a little more seriously about how we`re actually going to execute this.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I`m out of time, but I have to ask both of you this to answer quickly for me.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow is the 50th anniversary of Roe vs. Wade. Is abortion rights going to be the political issue or one of the top political issues for the next two years, Brendan?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Well, Republicans seemed very interested in continuing to have this conversation. The first week of the House, the first theme of the week was voting on bills about abortion. So obviously, it was a political loser for the Republicans in the last election. I`m hoping they learned that lesson, but it`s something that`s really important to them, so they`re not going to drop it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michael?\par \par \uc2 HARDAWAY: I think if Republicans are smart, they`ll drop it, but I don`t think they will. I think Democrats if they are smart, will take it and win with it, because to Brendan`s point, that was incredibly effective for them as it relates to last year`s election and they should really drill down on the idea of personal freedom because that`s what this is about.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Brendan Buck and Michael Hardaway, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: Denver is hundreds of miles from the Mexican border, but the city`s Mayor says it has become a destination for asylum seekers and he is asking President Biden for help.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Michael Hancock joins us to talk about it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:42:09]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You affect people`s quality of life, more than any other group of people in the world, and Mayors know, the measure of success isn`t in how many partisan points you score, it is how many problems you fix.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That was President Biden yesterday addressing the bipartisan US Conference of Mayors.\par \par \uc2 Many city leaders use the audience with the President to ask for additional resources to handle migrants and asylum seekers in their communities.\par \par \uc2 According to Customs and Border Patrol. There were more than 250,000 border crossings last month. That tops the previous record set last May by about 10,000.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the Mayor of Denver, Michael Hancock.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mayor, more than 4,000 migrants who have crossed the Southern border last month wound up in Denver. How is your city handling this influx? And what is it exactly that you hope the Federal government will do to help?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR MICHAEL B. HANCOCK (D), DENVER, COLORADO: Well, first of all, let me say, Reverend Sharpton, it is good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 I`m awfully proud of the people Denver, my city employees who responded to make sure that none of the migrants that came to our city were left out in the cold. We had some tremendous cold trends in Denver during that time. We got as low as 24 below zero during the 30 days that forty-four, forty-five hundred migrants were coming in to our city.\par \par \uc2 And so we`ve spent now about $4.5 million or $4.8 million to care for them, to shelter them, two to three rec centers offline, but I`ve got to tell you, the third sector, the nonprofits and faith communities stepped up and really helped us begin to care for them and we are beginning to see smaller numbers of migrants coming to Denver.\par \par \uc2 But, we are still not out of the woods, we have about 900 migrants still in our care. What we need the Federal government do now, one is to look short, medium, and long range. Short range, we need some shelter, and we need some resources -- money -- as well as maybe some waivers to some of the laws that we have in place or move quickly to change the laws.\par \par \uc2 These migrants want to work. They have showed up at construction sites, they have showed up at hospitality sites asking how they can work. Many of them are here, of course undocumented, but no contact with the Federal government so they can`t work.\par \par \uc2 Long range, simply, the Federal government, in particular Congress needs to stop playing partisan politics and develop an immigration policy that provides a pathway to citizenship.\par \par \uc2 What Biden and the administration -- his administration did was helpful. It gave us some relief, but it doesn`t give us long range relief we are going to need to help these migrants as they seek opportunity in the United States.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  visited the border last weekend as President Biden did two weeks ago. These visits get a lot of attention but do you think they are helpful in understanding the situation in a city like yours, which is roughly 650 miles north of the border?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:17]\par \par \uc2 HANCOCK: Well, I`ll tell you what it does do. We didn`t understand how Denver was the destination for these migrants. To your point, we`re not on the border. We`re not naturally a destination for migrants, and I would imagine that neither is New York City. And Mayor Adams, who is a good friend of mine, well, we have talked about this.\par \par \uc2 I think what going down there does is to help us understand who is sending these migrants to our cities? How are they being coordinated? And help us to hopefully stop the pipeline or giving them better information, so that they understand, whether it`s the smugglers or the NGOs that are sending them our way.\par \par \uc2 Now, when you send them our way, there is a possibility, they might be out on our streets, because we simply don`t have the ability to care for them.\par \par \uc2 So we can bring some truth to those individuals who are coordinating the efforts to transport them to our cities, maybe that`s a good benefit and value, I think it would be.\par \par \uc2 So certainly I don`t chide Mayor Adams or the President going down there, because under the new Biden strategy as well, they have to be on the other side of the border before they can apply for asylum into the US under the new policy that he put forward.\par \par \uc2 So, it makes sense to go down and begin to spread this information because they are spreading information by word of mouth to each other.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Colorado Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  , is calling on your State Legislature to pass a new gun measure including an expanded Red Flag Law at the same time lawmakers in Colorado established an office of gun violence protection 18 months ago with a $3 million budget, but the office has not distributed any grant money to communities to stop gun violence.\par \par \uc2 How important is that money? What would you like to see State lawmakers do to help crack down on gun violence in your city?\par \par \uc2 HANCOCK: Well, I think they are making some good movement. I certainly think Governor Polis is well aware of a lot of the challenges and the root causes of the challenges, and that is why he is looking at not only the gun laws, but also the Red Flag Laws that we have here in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to certainly deal with ghost guns. Many of these guns are being created in the garages of these individuals. We`ve certainly got a limit capacity of the size of the magazines of these guns, because those guns that are being used, these automatics are good for nothing other than killing. And so we limit those capacities within the magazines.\par \par \uc2 The other thing that we need to do, and I think Denver moved to do this, I know we moved to do this, and that is to partner with the US Attorney, Cole Finegan here in Colorado. What we did was to partner with him and to hire four special attorneys to be embedded in his office to be a kind of our liaison.\par \par \uc2 About a third of the crime, not more of these gun violence, the acts of gun violence being committed in our cities around the State of Colorado are being done by people who are previous offenders, and we know that they should not be carrying weapons.\par \par \uc2 And so, instead of using and indicting them on the State laws, we`re going to go straight in and indict them on the Federal laws, we want to send a very strong message. But if you are a previous offender in Colorado, in Denver, Colorado, you`re going to be -- we`re going to do everything we can to make sure we limit your time free on our streets, to continue to further hurt people.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got to think differently about these laws, and we`ve got to continue to work with the State to make sure that we are intensifying the penalties of these laws, so that people understand we mean business when it comes to carrying weapons and using weapons to harm folks in our city.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Mayor, no more time, but I have to ask you very briefly about homelessness. In Denver, it has jumped by 24 percent last year, according to recent statistics, what is your plan to address this?\par \par \uc2 HANCOCK: Well, we`re going to continue to do a lot of the efforts that we have undertaken, Reverend Sharpton. One is to do everything we can to make sure that we have not only immediate, but transitional shelter for individuals, and so we`re pouring almost $300 million in that effort in 2023.\par \par \uc2 But we also try to mitigate the impacts that are being brought to the homeless community by drugs, particularly opiates, and fentanyl, which is devastating not only in Denver, but cities across this country. And then of course, stepping up our game with solution centers, our emerging solution centers around Denver to help those who are suffering from mental health and continue to bring non-police enforcement response to those who are in crises in our community, by putting mental health professionals on the street.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to continue to do everything we can to chip away at it, but we have to make sure that we are attacking the mitigating causes of many people being unhoused in our city.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Denver Mayor Michael Hancock, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:54:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Monday past was the \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  holiday and people commemorated him in many ways.\par \par \uc2 As head of National Action Network, I hosted the Annual National Action Network, Washington, DC breakfast where President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was our Keynote Speaker and we honored \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  who had done such outstanding work on Civil Rights in her time as Speaker.\par \par \uc2 We had \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  , III, co-chair of the breakfast with me and we honored \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  . We also honored Arndrea Waters King who is not only Martin`s wife and mother of one of our great orators, Yolanda King, she is the president of Drum Major Institute and we honored her as we also flew on into New York where we had \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  , the Majority Leader of the Senate and the Minority Leader of the House, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  and the Governor and the Mayor, and many others.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:11]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Why? Because keeping Martin Luther King`s dream is to impact legislation and policy. Dr. King wasn`t just a great orator. He made change happen, put laws on the book and changed policies, and that is what we must do to keep the dream alive.\par \par \uc2 That`s why it was so important to us that the President outlined what he has done in the last two years for Black America. Has he kept his promise that he`d have our back? And he cited specifically things he did from the Inflation Reduction Act and how it impacted disproportionately Blacks and benefited us and how it benefited us with the infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 And he made one particular point to me, that I felt was very important in front of the whole audience. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I made a commitment to you, Al, I said, I`m going to appoint the first Black woman to the Supreme Court, and she is about the smartest one of all, if I might add.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, he did put a Black woman on the Supreme Court, among other things, but we have an uphill fight with a right-wing majority, slight, but there in the House.\par \par \uc2 You can`t just celebrate, Dr. King, we have to keep fighting to keep the dream alive and to make sure they don`t take back some of the things that have been achieved.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with Alicia Menendez starts right now.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:24]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020230122ej1l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for January 20, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alex Wagner, Ali Velshi, Charles Blow, Jill Wine-Banks \par \uc2 8,064 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: We wanted to quickly correct the record of proper use for hydroxychloroquine.\par \par \uc2 [22:00:03]\par \par \uc2 It is primarily treating malaria, also rheumatoid arthritis and lupus. It is still not a COVID cure, though. And Ivermectin, which Fox News also pushed for those sick with COVID, is a medication that is typically used on horses. It too is still not a legitimate COVID cure. Apologies to all, especially the horses.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us tonight. I`ll see you here on Tuesday. Now, it is time for the LAST WORD with Ali Velshi who is in for Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: That was excellent. Because, as you said, and I thought to myself, wait, is that not the horse medicine? Have I`ve been getting it wrong? And then you said, Ivermectin. It`s all in the category of stuff that was not supposed to be talked about during COVID.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Yes. It`s all stuff that has nothing to do with the curing you if you have COVID. It`s for other things, and it`s in that box in my mind. I do not want any horses to be given the wrong medication. As you know, we take equine health care very seriously on this program --\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We certainly do.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: -- as we should, and also the truth. So, that`s the correction.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Thank you for that. I appreciate it. Have yourself a great weekend, we`ll see you next week.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: You too, Ali.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has spent six years stress testing our democracy, our laws, our norms, our sense of decency, and today, two years after he left the White House and disgrace after his failed attempt to overturn Joe Biden`s presidential victory, he`s likely to test us again.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is out of power and facing legal and financial peril from which he believes running for public office can shield him, hence his hasty presidential announcement in November, three days before \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  announced a special prosecutor to investigate him.\par \par \uc2 And so all of this pressure Trump is facing is likely to make him more desperate to recapture his position and our attention with the same flood the zone playbook, lies, hateful rhetoric, undercutting democracy in word, and deed, as he`s been doing it at a breakneck pace online over the last few days.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Florida called \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out yesterday for doing just that with the courts, writing, quote, a continuing pattern of misuse of the courts by Mr. Trump and his lawyers undermines the rule of law portrays judges as partisans and diverts resources from those who have suffered actual legal harm.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump is a prolific and sophisticated litigant who is repeatedly using the courts to seek revenge on political adversaries. He is the mastermind of strategic abuse of the judicial process and he cannot be seen as a litigant blindly following the advice of a lawyer, end quote.\par \par \uc2 That judge, Donald Middlebrooks, hit Trump and his lawyers with a $1 million sanction for the frivolous lawsuit. And today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  quickly withdrew his lawsuit against New York Attorney General Letitia James, who he accused of using her position to pursue a vendetta against him, that happened to be before the same judge.\par \par \uc2 Now, that suit was already expect to fail, as both federal and state courts have already dismissed similar suit filed by Trump against the New York attorney general, but this is just the latest in the string of lawsuits for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . According to New York Times Column is Charles Blow, who will join us in a moment, quote, watching voters reject many of the candidates he endorsed during the midterms and the speakership battle, which ended in Kevin McCarthy ceding so much power that he is essentially a speaker-in- name-only, was another loss for Trump, because it exposed the fact that his devotees no longer mindlessly follow his directions.\par \par \uc2 Republican Party officials certainly aren`t following him in 2024. After Trump`s presidential announcement, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports, quote, the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  has asked several major T.V. networks, including CNN, a Republican boogeyman, to consider sponsoring debates. That`s an early sign that the party is making plans for a contested presidential primary.\par \par \uc2 Now, some Republican supporters have already rallied other candidates. Wyoming Senator \uc2 Cynthia Lummis\uc2  has declared Florida`s \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  the leader of the party. \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2 , \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  , all of whom served in Donald Trump`s administration, are publicly courting speculation about 2024 runs. Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  this week called the 2024 presidential election a wide open race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY) (voice over): Look, let me just say this about the `24 election. It`s going to be wide open. There is going to be a lot of competition, and I hope we nominate someone who can win in `24. And so the voters in the primaries are going to make that decision without any assistance from me. They don`t need any advice from me on how to vote. But I will say it`s going to be a wide open race.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, some Republicans at least are reading the room on Trump because, according to a recent Quinnipiac poll, Donald Trump`s favorability among voters has drop to its lowest level since March of 2015, or are some Republican simply looking at the multitude of legal challenges, criminal investigation by the Department of Justice, by the Fulton County district attorney`s office in Georgia, civil lawsuits from the January 6th attack on the Capitol, a civil lawsuit from E. Jean Carroll who`s accused him of rape and doubting he can escape from all of these things.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:06]\par \par \uc2 But make no mistake, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , though diminished, is still dangerous. He`s still has outsized influence on the Republican Party. Because as Trump has proven, Republican Party officials do not control Republican voters and certainly not the faction of Republican chaos voters over which he has the greatest influence.\par \par \uc2 He`s still willing to wield that influence against whomever he proceeds as a threat or an opponent. And the more he feels backed into a corner, the more he seeks to capture the attention of an electorate slowly moving away from him, the more extreme his rhetoric will get.\par \par \uc2 This week alone, Trump has taken to his social media platform to derive the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , the Department of Justice, Special Counsel Jack Smith as, quote, Trump-hating Marxist thugs. He`s begun referring to American intelligence officials as the Gestapo.\par \par \uc2 Think about that. He claims he wants to be the commander-in-chief to lead our national security and he`s calling some of the people he`d be leading Nazis.\par \par \uc2 Today, he released a video saying that Democrats are, quote, dropping migrants coming in from prison into the country and that mass releases of illegal aliens are depleting our social safety net. He tells his followers almost daily that America`s top institutions are corrupt and unfairly targeting him. He constantly repeats his lie that the 2020 election was stolen from him, although he spell it S-T-O-L-L-E-M when he posted it yesterday.\par \par \uc2 He is still doing it. He is still lying about the election. He is still stoking racism and fear about immigrants. He is still casting Democrats and investigators and the media and anyone who challenges him or tries to hold him to account as existential enemies, even though we have seen that it only takes one person, one chaos after to take his word seriously and lead to violence.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, Trump stoked similar hysteria when he falsely claimed a migrant caravan of, quote, criminals and gang members was descending on the United States posing a national security risk. As Adam Serwer of The Atlantic highlighted at the time, some took the claims of the presidents and his allies seriously. On Saturday, October 27th, 2018, a gunman walked into the Tree of Life Synagogue in Pittsburgh and killed 11 people. The massacre capped off a week of terrorism in which one man mailed bombs to nearly a dozen Trump critics, another killed two black people at a grocery store after failing to force his way into a black church. The Tree of Life shooter to criticized Trump for not being racist or anti-Semitic enough. But with the respect to the caravan, the shooter merely followed the logic of the president and his allies. He was willing to do whatever was necessary to prevent an invasion of Latinos planned by perfidious Jews, a treasonous attempt to seek the destruction of American society and culture, end quote.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, just this Monday, Solomon Pena, a Republican candidate for New Mexico state office, who lost this race during the midterms but has refused to concede and has expressed support for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , was arrested for being behind a plot to shoot at the homes of two county commissioners and two state legislators. One of those Democratic lawmakers, State Senator Linda Lopez, said Pena`s actions represent a bigger issue in our country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STATE SEN. LINDA LOPEZ (D-NM): What I think Mr. Pena represents is a bigger issue that`s in our country. With the insurrection that happened on January 6th, it was on his twitter feed, exactly showing off that he was there, there is so much more of threats that have been made to our secretary of state here in New Mexico and other places across the country. With Governor Whitmer, what has happened with her kidnapping, I think, it`s something that`s much larger than just here in the state of New Mexico. The rhetoric has to stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It has to stop. But what happens if it does not stop?\par \par \uc2 Starting us off tonight, Charles Blow, Columnist for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  , Author and an MSNBC Political Analyst, Jill Wine-Banks, who served as an assistant Watergate prosecutor and is also MSNBC Legal Analyst, Kurt Bardella is a Democratic strategist, he`s contributor to the Los Angeles Times , he`s the former spokesperson of the House GOP Oversight Committee. Welcome to all three of you, friends. It`s great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 Charles, I`m going to start with you. I quoted you in the introduction here. I think we all come to the conclusion that Linda Lopez came to that this has to stop, and I think we`re not under any impression that this is going to stop, this means that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  saying the inflammatory things that he says.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BLOW, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Right. Trump won`t stop doing what he`s doing, but what is happening is something that you said in your intro, which is that the Republican Party is, to some degree, moving away from him. And he can see the power receding from him, and as you point out, that makes him more desperate.\par \par \uc2 Part of what Trump`s appeal is that he`s a folk hero. The folk hero lives on getting away with it, right, whether it`s (INAUDIBLE) getting away with it, whether it`s Bonnie and Clyde getting away with it, whether it`s Dillinger, whoever it is, the folk hero lives on the mystique that they are overcoming a system that they get away with it and defy authority.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:06]\par \par \uc2 The moment that Trump began to be a loser both electorally and on the legal front, the mystique of being a folk hero starts to diminish. He`s just a guy. He`s a guy in a lot of trouble. And there are other people who can demonstrate that they are winners on some level, like a DeSantis who won, not a landslide but with a very healthy margin, and that starts to look more attractive to the voters.\par \par \uc2 Trump sees that as well. He understands he is incredible legal jeopardy. And he looks at the White House as one of the only ways that he can really shield himself from that jeopardy. That makes him desperate. Desperation makes people dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Jill Wine-Banks, let`s talk about the legal side of this thing. Donald has finally started to face some of the music for both his legal shenanigans in terms of the lawsuits that he puts forward and some fears that some real things, real consequences might come for him. How much of this do you think has an impact on the folks who are deserting him?\par \par \uc2 JILL WINE-BANKS, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: I think it has a big impact on him and on them, but mostly on him. I mean, as you pointed out, he`s not dropped a frivolous lawsuit against Attorney General Letitia James as a result of having real consequences imposed on him for a frivolous lawsuit against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 The list of his civil lawsuits takes about a full page in my notebook. The potential criminal cases against him are numerous but not quite as long as the actual, already existing civil cases, but they have very serious consequences. Of course, the most consequential would be anything that has to do with the insurrection, which would prevent him from running for office again, which I think many people, including me, think is the most important outcome is to make sure that he doesn`t darken the door of the White House again and bring this country down with him.\par \par \uc2 And I think that people are starting -- his own party, are starting to see that he is not the leader, that they are starting to see the facts that he is a bad businessman and he was a bad president, and that he could cause serious danger to our democracy that`s been undone helpfully so by President Biden. He`s been able to bring back America as a world leader, and we can`t afford to lose that in two years.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Except all of this being valid, Kurt, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still has sway over, I don`t what you want to call them, the chaos Republicans, that 30 percent who don`t think he`s done anything wrong, really like what he`s got to say, and he`s not given that to anyone. So, while it doesn`t prevent anyone from running against him, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still remains a remarkable force for bad in this country.\par \par \uc2 KURT BARDELLA, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, I think you just look at the effect that he had and the primary process in the last midterm cycle, where even though they all lost in the general election, so many of the candidates that he got behind wonder primaries and that really outlines the overall problem the Republican Party has, that with or without Trump, the bases moved so far to the extreme right and he`s become so legitimize by Republican Party leadership that they still end up with all these mini Trumps popping up who are just fundamentally unelectable in a general election.\par \par \uc2 And you look at what the Republicans of the House just did by appointing so many of these Trumpists, two key high-profile visible committees, like the House Oversight Committee, like the judiciary committee, we saw in the oversight committee, they put 14 election deniers on that committee, people like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  , \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2  , people like Scott Perry, Andy Biggs.\par \par \uc2 When you put those types of voices on those types of committees and with the amount of attention they are going to be getting through the witch hunts that they`re going to pursue against President Biden, that plays right into the messaging of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . This committee, the oversight committee, they might as well just be the unofficial committee to elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as the Republican nominee for president, because everything that they are going to do plays right off of the rhetoric and the style that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to impose on the rest of the country.\par \par \uc2 And there is zeal to try to move forward and move on to different characters and figures in the party, the agenda that they are pursuing right now plays right into the wheelhouse of getting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be the standard bearer of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Charles, to the extent that others are going to be in that race, including \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , whom you mentioned, and \uc2 Nikki Haley\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  , who knows whom else, are they going -- does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  being in the race and staying in the race cause everyone to do what Kurt just said, try and be a mini Trump or try and appeal to that crowd that is going to be very hard to shake away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  but is going to be very valuable if you can achieve that.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:04]\par \par \uc2 Some of the things \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has done just in the last week would suggest that he might be out trying to out-trump Trump in Florida.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Yes. But I think we should make a slight distinction. I have seen Republicans in my lifetime take the policies, the (INAUDIBLE) people they were once behind and leave a person in a desk and let the policies continue to live. For instance, I was in college when \uc2 David Duke\uc2  -- I was in Louisiana when \uc2 David Duke\uc2  ran for governor of Louisiana. He almost won. He won the Republican vote, right? He won a majority of the white vote in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 When he lost eventually because business interest in black people came together to stop him from winning, they kind of backed away. But if you look at the Republican Party in Louisiana 10 years, 20 years after that, they basically adopted every single idea that \uc2 David Duke\uc2  had espoused when he was running.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party is very good at evolution and taking the best or the worst of what a person gives and even abandoning that person. So, I am not sure that Trump has to live because Trumpism lives. I think that Trumpism can live without him. I think DeSantis can step into those shoes. I think that any of these other kind of chaos people that you were talking about can step into that role and are trying to step into it. And they would not, for a second, if they felt like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was weak, they would cut him loose in a heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Jill Wine-Banks, let`s talk about what Kurt was talking about, the investigations that are going to take place. In fact, most of the Republican agenda in the House that Kevin McCarthy has outlined, very little of it has to do with policy, anything, it`s all investigation. What happens here? What real liability is there with the House sort of indicating that they`d like to use the levers of government for what seems like revenge as opposed to real investigation?\par \par \uc2 WINE-BANKS: I think that they will be seen for what it is. You are correct, Ali. This is revenge and it is a diversion from governing. They have the majority and they are doing nothing to set ay policies in place that will help America. When they talk about investigations, most of them are not even remotely possible. This idea of getting evidence from the Department of Justice about ongoing cases, that is just absurd. No court in this world would allow that to happen. People have rights of privacy. There is grand jury secrecy. You cannot get that material. It just won`t happen. And that`s one of the most threatening.\par \par \uc2 The others are things that have nothing to do with the government. I believe in oversight. Obviously, the Republicans don`t, or they would have allowed people to respond to oversight subpoenas. It was not just criminal subpoenas that they did not respond to. It was for just regular, ordinary governmental oversight, things like immigration.\par \par \uc2 And so I think we have to keep focusing on this is what we need to get back to, is governing America and setting in place policies that will help America in the world and Americans in America. So, I don`t think it is going to work at all. I think it will be a diversion and it will end up hurting them in 2024.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Kurt, what is your best sense about -- does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  stay in this race? If it does, do any of the people we have been talking get in? I think a lot of people are betting on the fact that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is going to get in, but \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is up against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , it`s not clear what happens there.\par \par \uc2 BARDELLA: Yes. I think Trump`s day end this because this is really his legal defense mechanism right here. Running for president in Donald Trump`s mind is a legal shield. All the money that he is going to raise might as well just be for a legal defense fund to fend off all the lawsuits and investigations that he is the center of. That`s not how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is really viewing this.\par \par \uc2 And you watch how the first months of his so-called presidential campaign have unfolded, he`s not really acting like a candidate running for president in any way. He`s just sitting there using the specter of that label, presidential candidate, to shield himself from responsibility and accountability. It`s a legal tactic for him. I don`t think that will dissuade other Republicans for wanting to try to put themselves in a position so that if and when the other shoe drops on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , they`re positioned to try to step right in and to take his place. That`s what you`re seeing now, a lot of maneuvering from the Nikki Haleys, from the Mike Pences, is from the Ron DeSantises. They are trying to put themselves in a position where when Trump goes away, they can just step right back in.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Kurt, thanks, as always, for being here, Kurt Bardella, Charles Blow and Jill Wine-Banks, we appreciate you getting us started tonight.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, we`ve got reporting on just who did and did not get interviewed in the Supreme Court`s investigation into the leak of draft opinion overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:04]\par \par \uc2 That is next.\par \par \uc2 And later, did you have to deny you stole money from a veteran trying to save his dying dog this week? Well, if not, then you`re probably not George Santos, if that`s what his real name is. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We have an update to Thursday`s breaking news from the Supreme Court. The marshal of the Supreme Court who conducted the investigation that failed to find out who leaked the draft ruling that overturned Roe v. Wade is now clarifying that she did interview the justices, saying, quote, during the course of the investigation, I spoke with each of the justices, several multiple occasions.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:14]\par \par \uc2 The justices actively cooperated in this iterative process, asking questions and answering mine. I followed up on all credible leads, none of which implicated the justices or their spouses. On this basis, I did not believe that it was necessary to ask the justices to sign sworn affidavits. As NBC News points out, quote, the lack of side affidavits suggest that the judges were not formally interviewed in the same way as court staff, end quote.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republicans are not stopping at the reversal of Roe v. Wade, which would have marked its 50th anniversary on Sunday. There is a push to ban abortion nationwide, to criminalize travel across state lines to get procedures, to get abortion pills illegal and to try to ban the use contraceptives in some places. \uc2 Axios\uc2  is reporting that, quote, some lawmakers are embracing the idea that those who get an abortion when it is illegal should be punished or penalized.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is the Democratic senator, \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2  , of Minnesota. She previously served as executive vice president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  in Minnesota, North Dakota and South Dakota. Senator, good evening, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TINA SMITH (D-MN): Thanks, Ali, it`s good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You said in June when Roe was overturned that you won`t stop until safe reliable access to abortion care is a guaranteed right again, and in some of these referenda that we`ve seen and ballot measures, we`ve seen that Americans seem to -- when given the choice, will choose to codify abortion rights, and yet we`re going farther down the other road. So, if you thought that this was going to stop, it was going to be binary and Roe would be gone and then life would go on, we`re seeing people, like I said, attempting to restrict medication abortion and contraceptives. This is not where some people thought this was going to go.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: No, that is exactly right. Here we are on the verge of the 58th anniversary of Roe, which is no more, and I think a lot of people thought that when roe was overturned, stripping away 50 years of rights for people to be able to control their own bodies and make their own decisions about their health care, that that would be the end of the road, but it really is the beginning of the road.\par \par \uc2 And we can see that today and the marches that were happening in Washington D.C., where people were talking about the next step being to have a national ban on abortion. And always in this conversation, we have to root ourselves in what this means for women who have lost and so many parts of the country the ability to control their own futures because they can`t control their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 And that, I think, is just uppermost on my mind, having worked at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , I remember clearly working and volunteering at \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  on the big Roe protest days and watching the protesters out there with their ugly signs, trying to stop women from going into our clinic, to get the health care that they needed. And those protests are still happening and women are not able to get a health care that they need, and that is wrong.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We saw pictures this week of the protests at pharmacies now because -- for medicated abortion, we saw that happen.\par \par \uc2 Axios\uc2  has a report that puts some numbers to this that make it stark, women in states with abortion bans are nearly two times more likely to die during pregnancy, childbirth or soon after giving birth, according to a report from the Gender Equity Policy Institute. Native American women`s maternal mortality rates were four times higher than those of white women and black women`s rates and they were 2.6 times the rate of white women. The report also found that infants born in states with abortion bans were at 30 percent more likely to die than those in support of states.\par \par \uc2 Now, in a vacuum, one wouldn`t know why that is the case, why are these women more likely to die in states that have abortion bans. You haven`t operated in a vacuum. You were involved in Planned Parenthood, so you know exactly what the relationship is between women not getting reproductive health care and dying.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, absolutely. I mean, that`s because reproductive health care is health care. And if you do not have access to it, then your health is in jeopardy. And we know that in this country, access to any kind of health care is not equal. Black and brown women, indigenous women don`t have nearly the same access. And you see that play out when it comes to reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 Ali, just a couple of days ago, I was hearing a story about a woman who was in an emergency situation in South Dakota, had a need for an emergency, access to abortion. She was very sick. And what happens? Rather than the hospital in South Dakota taking care of her emergency medical need, she is put in an ambulance and driven to Minnesota so that she can get the health care that is potentially saving her life.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:02]\par \par \uc2 Now for that woman, this is not a political issue, this is a health issue. This is a life or death issue. And that is exactly how this impacts people`s health care.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I`ve heard anecdotal fears --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: And what happens when we have Republicans and lawyers interfering in people`s health care.\par \par \uc2 VANIER: I heard anecdotal fears from abortion providers in Alabama where the attorney general has sort of said he`s going to make an example out of somebody. Women who are delaying treatment, somethings goes wrong in the pregnancy, and rather than getting that treatment as quickly as they can, they worry that they don`t want to be the one. They don`t want to be the one that the attorney general comes for. This is somebody trying to sort of illegally get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Yes, yes. It also has of course this deep impact on the providers that she needs, know what to do, but then they have to consult a lawyer to find out whether they`re legally able to provide that health care.\par \par \uc2 That is not the way we should be delivering health care in this country, and that is what has happened because of this rollback of 50 years of protections that is having an impact on people every single day in this country. And people don`t like it, and they are mad about it.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Senator, good to see you. Thank you for joining us. Senator \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Coming up at this hour one week ago, we talked to a reporter who had interviewed some of George Santos` old roommates. They accused him of stealing clothes, checks, even a fancy \uc2 Burberry\uc2  scarf.\par \par \uc2 One week later, George Santos is still in Congress after denying that he ripped off a veteran who was trying to save his dying dog.\par \par \uc2 The weirdest story in Washington just keeps getting weirder. That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:31:32]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: As the curious case of George Santos -- name unconfirmed -- continues to unfold there are so many questions that still need answering. How does he fund his lavish lifestyle? Where did he get the $700,000 that he used to fund his campaign? What crimes, if any, did he commit while he was living in Brazil?\par \par \uc2 But one of the biggest questions still to be answered is how did he get elected in the first place? How did the American political system fail so badly that a political novice whose back story was filled with holes made it onto the ballot and then into the House of Representatives and now on to two committees.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what we know so far thanks to reporting from NBC News, the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2  " and other outlets. George Santos was almost a complete 0unknown when he first came forward to challenge the Democratic incumbent representative \uc2 Tom Suozzi\uc2  in 2020.\par \par \uc2 "The Times" reports, "In normal circumstances, Mr. Santos would`ve been shooed away. Republicans in Nassau County, which comprises the bulk of New York`s third congressional district, have long been famous for exercising tight control over who runs, grooming and rewarding a stable of candidates like an old school political machine.\par \par \uc2 But with a the in lockdown in the early days of the coronavirus pandemic and the district expected to remain under Democratic control, no one else put their hand up to run," end quote.\par \par \uc2 Now, despite the many questions about \uc2 Santos\uc2  and his background that had started swirling in New York`s political circles, after losing in 2020, Santos came forward again. Raised his hand, Republicans again through their weight behind this guy.\par \par \uc2 From the "Times", again. "When Mr. Santos chose to run again two years later, local Republicans again gave him their support. They expected that flipping the district would once again be a stretch. And in any case, the Nassau County Republican Party chairman`s priority was winning state and local offices which control thousands of local jobs and major tax and spending decisions. Efforts to recruit a more formidable candidate like state senator \uc2 Jack Martins\uc2  did not pan out," end quote.\par \par \uc2 Throughout the campaign, the red flags continued to pile up. In late 2021, Santos` campaign reportedly hired a firm to conduct a, quote, "vulnerability study" that turned up many of the issues that the mainstream media did not uncover until after he had been elected.\par \par \uc2 His apparent lack of a college degree, eviction records and other serious issues for a political candidate at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 Here`s how the "Times" described what happened next. "The people working for Mr. Santos convened an emergency conference call to discuss the results on December 1st, 2021. They presented him with a choice, bow out of the race with dignity or stay in and risk letting the Democrats turn up the same information and use it to destroy his political and personal futures.\par \par \uc2 After promising to produce diplomas that would prove his degrees -- he ultimately did not -- Mr. Santos said he would think it over. When he came back a few days later, he said he had spoken with other advisers and was convinced that the findings were not as bad as they were being portrayed. He was staying in the race. Most of his team quit," end quote.\par \par \uc2 Who were those advisors who saw all this stuff and said, don`t worry about it, stick it out? Why did they write off these glaring issues? Who else knew about all of this.\par \par \uc2 NBC News reports that Representative \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  has attempted to position herself as a king maker in New York GOP politics. And she threw her weight behind a number of Republican candidates running for Congress including, dot, dot, dot, George Santos.\par \par \uc2 Here`s another question that needs answering. Can George Santos, who reportedly has ties to the cousin of a sanctioned Russian oligarch, really be trusted as a member of the House Committee on Science, Space and Technology? The guy is a serial liar.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:49]\par \par \uc2 The man at the center of that picture, that particular Santos scandal, is \uc2 Andrew Intrater\uc2  a private equity investor and the cousin of \uc2 Viktor Vekselberg\uc2  (ph). He reportedly invested $625,000 with the investment firm \uc2 Santos\uc2  is working at, specifically because he trusted and admired George Santos.\par \par \uc2 Now, after investing over $200,000 into Santos` various political PACs, Intrater`s had a change of heart, telling the "Times" quote, "I don`t want Republicans having a bum representing Republicans. And I don`t want to have a guy that committed crimes walking free."\par \par \uc2 Santos for his part has admitted to making mistakes but denies having done anything illegal or unethical.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now Pete Strzok. He`s a former deputy of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  `s counter intelligence division and an adjunct professor at Georgetown university School of Foreign Service.\par \par \uc2 Also with us is Steve Israel, who importantly is the former congressman for Santos` district, former chair of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee and a current, I`m sorry for this sir, a current constituent of Congressman George Santos. He is the director of the Cornell Institute of Politics and Global Affairs.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Steve, let me start with you. You wrote a great article that I read a few weeks ago in which you outlined how this essentially happened. That this is not a case where the information did not exist, it largely all existed, some people had it, including some Democrats and some Republicans. What happened?\par \par \uc2 STEVE ISRAEL, FORMER DEMOCRATIC CONGRESSMAN: Well, three things happened, there were three fundamental failures in this process. The first was the Republican Party that nominated Mr. Santos based on one thing and one thing only -- his ideological extremism, his fidelity to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , without even vetting his resume. So that was failure number one.\par \par \uc2 Failure number two I have to be honest, was the Democratic campaign opposing Mr. Santos made a decision that they would not make the investment of funds in the kind of opposition research that would have revealed what we now know -- what we now know too late.\par \par \uc2 And the third and final problem was a sense of complacency. When the Democratic campaign saw these red flags and did try and get attention from various media outlets, they ignored the Democratic campaign. There was a sense that this is a Democratic district, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would have won this district by five, six points. This guy Santos isn`t going to win, so we don`t have to pay much attention to him.\par \par \uc2 You put those three things together and you now have Congressman George Santos, which is not only the weirdest story in Washington, as you rightly pointed out Ali, but also one of the most tragic, at least for what I used to represent.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well, that`s exactly right, Peter. It`s funny to talk about these things that he does, except it`s not funny. He`s a member of Congress. He`s on actual committees. The Science and Technology Committee actually worries me a little bit.\par \par \uc2 This is a guy who`s known to lie, you know, congressmen get information. They get access to information sometimes that the public doesn`t have.\par \par \uc2 So Peter, who is supposed to solve this problem? Information exists, it`s a political process. It`s about the people voting, his party does not want to do anything about it. The Democrats didn`t do enough with this. The media didn`t do enough with this because they figured he wasn`t going to win. So who prevents a guy from George Santos from being elected to the House of Representatives?\par \par \uc2 PETER STRZOK, FORMER FBI COUNTERINTELLIGENCE DEPUTY: That`s a good question. I mean look, if you`re looking for a political solution that`s going to have to come from the political arena. But it`s certainly true that in the context of congress, countries overseas, certainly folks like China and Russia, every day are looking for ways to influence U.S. policy. Every day they`re looking for ways to gain access to congressmen and influence their actions.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, everything we are seeing in the political realm is interesting and certainly it`s the subject of a lot of conversation. But when you look at Santos, the things that are concerning from a national security perspective are this massive volume of funding that he provided himself, that he had others contributing, that up to this point appeared to be unexplained.\par \par \uc2 Certainly you have state, federal and local officials looking criminally at whether laws might have been broken. But there is also a national security question of were foreign powers behind any of this money?\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Right.\par \par \uc2 STRZOK: Are they trying to influence him? Right. He claimed that he`s traveled to Moscow many times. Anything that he says I think needs to obviously be taken with a grain of salt. But there are a number of things from a national security perspective that bear some looking at.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You make an interesting point. The $700,000 might be a campaign finance technicality and that`s how people think of these things, but we actually need to know where did the money come from. Is it from anything that will compromise him.\par \par \uc2 Steve, one of the things you talked about was that your constituency are a lot of moderates, but it becomes hard to get engaged in these things. And that may be why George Santos got passed everybody.\par \par \uc2 Right now, there are a lot of people in your constituency, the one you formerly represented, who are really angry. They feel deceived by who George Santos was. Many of them voted for him.\par \par \uc2 But people don`t get engaged. This is a national problem, it`s not just your constituency Long Island issue. People didn`t show up to meetings that you held. It`s just the way it goes.\par \par \uc2 ISRAEL: This is, you know, there are two pathologies here. One is the pathology of George Santos, this web of deceit that he has weaved for much of his life.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:56]\par \par \uc2 But the other pathology is the political climate that we`re in. That we are so polarized, so tribalized that people don`t -- aren`t all that interested in the truth. They`re interested in opinions.\par \par \uc2 And in my former district, it was a fairly disengaged district. I talked in that piece about having town hall meetings and, you know, we basically had to serve bagels and cream cheese in order to get people to come in my district.\par \par \uc2 Not hyper politics, very moderate, very comfortable, and that created the kind of climate that somebody like Santos could exploit.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It`s a complicated issue. And it just keeps unfolding. Thanks to both of you for making some time for us tonight. Pete Strzok and Steve Israel. Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is showing us what really, really big government looks like, telling Florida students what high school class they can and cannot take. We`ll explain, up next.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:52]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: "Inexplicably contrary to Florida law and significantly lacks educational value." That`s how the administration of Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  describes a new college level course for high school students on African American studies.\par \par \uc2 It`s already being taught in 60 high schools nationwide as a first test. The curriculum was in development for a decade. Endorsing the class, the chair of the \uc2 Howard University\uc2  `s history department, said the course presents quote, "a solid understanding of how African Americans have shaped America, its history, laws, institutions, culture and arts, and even the current practice of American democracy, which sharpens all knowledge about our nation".\par \par \uc2 But according to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in the State Department of Education, of course, cultivated for ten years by scholars and experts is neither lawful nor historically accurate.\par \par \uc2 So you might ask use yourself what specifically is wrong with the AP African American studies class, according to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and his administration, which would be a fair question.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press reports, quote, "Florida education officials did not specify exactly what content the state found objectionable.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Reginald Ellis, an associate professor of history and African American studies at \uc2 Florida A&M University\uc2  , which one of the state`s Historically Black Colleges and Universities. Professor Ellis, thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 REGINALD ELLIS, FLORIDA A&M UNIVERSITY: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 You`ve had a chance to look into this a little bit. Can you glean what the problem is? What the illegal part of this course is? And I mean we can get into it in a second about educational value, but incompatible with the state`s whatever rules or methodology. What are they talking about?\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: You know, when it first came out, I`ve spoke with some my colleagues, and we were very surprised and unfortunately we were disappointed to hear that language was coming from the \uc2 Florida Department of Education\uc2  via the governor.\par \par \uc2 We are very excited two years ago when the college board took on this great task of creating African American studies AP curriculum. I have a five- year-old daughter and I was excited that 12 years from now, she would have the opportunity to take the African American studies AP course as credit for college.\par \par \uc2 So no, we are very disappointed and shocked that it would not -- it would, at this point be blocked.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And just to be clear, because there are all sorts of people in this country very excited about CRT, Critical Race Theory and how it`s being taught to little children. That is not true. This is an actually fully- developed course about African-American history designed for the college level but to be taught to high school students who are high achieving students, right.\par \par \uc2 This is not a random indoctrination course. This was a very deliberate high level course that students would use as part of their applications to colleges.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: For sure, it`s the same as you might have AP U.S. History, AP World History, AP Chemistry and Psychology. And so if you think about it, for the most part, many students don`t have the opportunity to take African American History and until they go to college.\par \par \uc2 And wo when you think about a kid like myself from a rural south west Georgia town in Whigham (ph), Georgia, I was not really exposed to African American history until my first year at \uc2 Florida A&M University\uc2  . I came to \uc2 Florida A&M University\uc2  as an occupational therapy major, and my first introduction to African American history class. I changed my major that night because I fell in love with history. And I fell in love with myself. And I understood for the first time in my life the role that black people, diasporacally (ph)-speaking played in the development of the world but also played in the development of this American experience, this American democracy.\par \par \uc2 So imagine how impactful this African American history AP course will be for hundreds of millions of individuals, not just black people, not just black kids but Americans throughout and how that will impact the development -- the further development of America.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: It`s a remarkable story, your own story about how you changed from occupational therapy to history. Is there any likelihood that public opinion can come down to bear on this decision or is this done in Florida?\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: I am not sure about the politics, but I can tell you that historians throughout the nation were excited about this course, and are still excited about this course.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:52]\par \par \uc2 And I hope that, you know, the governor and the individuals around the governor will reconsider the great value and the great history.\par \par \uc2 I will say that this work has been going on for two years and as professional historians, we know that everything we do goes through a peer review process. And I can confidently say that this work is solid and it is sound -- indeed sound history.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Professor Ellis, good to talk to you. Thank you and a pleasure to meet you, Professor Reginald Ellis of \uc2 Florida A&M\uc2  . He`s a professor of history and African-American Studies.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s LAST WORD is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The U.S. and Germany have failed to reach an agreement on sending tanks to Ukraine. Sources tell NBC News that top Pentagon officials are opposed to sending Abram tanks to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow morning on "VELSHI", I will sit down with Yuriy Sak, he`s the adviser to the minister of defense of Ukraine, about how crucial these thanks would be in Ukraine`s fight against Russia.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:55]\par \par \uc2 You can catch "VELSHI" every Saturday and Sunday here on MSNBC from 8:00 to 10:00 a.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR" starts right now with Alicia Menendez.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230121ej1k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for January 20, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Mike Memoli, Jonathan Lemire, Matthew Dowd, Jake  Sherman, Steve Kornacki, Ken Dilanian, Ali Vitali, Danny Cevallos, Vaughn  Hillyard, Raf Sanchez, Marissa Parra \par \uc2 8,228 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:01]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC HOST: Good day. I`m Chris Jansing live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City. Right now, the White House is mapping, of course they hope can deliver a second term for President Biden. But to get there, he will have to navigate the political minefield of Republican-led investigations by hard-right conservatives. So, why is one White House official calling it a, quote, political gift?\par \par \uc2 Plus, NBC News confirms the director of the CIA traveled to Kyiv to meet with President Zelenskyy last week. That meeting comes at a critical juncture for the war amid new questions about the quantity and quality of U.S. support. And exactly what specifically makes the New Mexico D.A. so sure about the charges against Alex Baldwin -- Alec Baldwin, that could land him in prison for years.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY CARMACK-ALTWIES, NEW MEXICO FIRST JUDICIAL DISTRICT ATTORNEY: It wasn`t that we were trying to charge him with a felony. It`s that what his actions did match the case law for involuntary manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But let`s start exactly two years out from inauguration day. Seeing the early contours of the presidential strategy that the White hopes -- White House hopes will get \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  back to the Oval Office for a second term. The goal, to spotlight President Biden`s legislative achievements of the past 18 months. And contrast that with what a White House spokesman predicted would be Republicans "divorced from reality political stunts."\par \par \uc2 To that end, Politico reports the administration is giddy that some of the GOP is biggest bomb throwers like \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  and \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  are now on the Oversight Committee, believing they are ideal figureheads for a White House, eager to derive the opposition party as unhinged. But for the plan to work the administration needs to maintain its own credibility and effort that`s threatened by its handling of classified documents, even if the President himself doesn`t think so.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`re fully cooperating looking forward to getting this resolved quickly. I think you`re going to find there`s nothing there. I have no regrets. I`m following what the lawyers have told me they want me to do. It`s exactly what we`re doing. There`s no there there. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Mike Memoli is NBC`s s White House correspondent. Jake Sherman is cofounder of Punchbowl News and an MSNBC contributor. Jonathan Lemire is White House bureau chief for Politico and MSNBC political analyst and host of "WAY TOO EARLY" here. And Matthew Dowd is MSNBC`s senior political analyst. So, Mike, I kind of want to parse what the President said. He said, you`re going to find there`s nothing to this, but he also said he has no regrets about the way this has unfolded which are two different things.\par \par \uc2 Does the administration acknowledged they maybe could have an arguably should have dealt with this differently from the start?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, White House officials continued to insist that they had to handle things the way they handled them. They did not want to be seen in any way as potentially interfering with the ongoing Justice Department review. But it`s clear that they had hoped that review would be all but wrapped up in the early stages, as they insist that this was largely just a bit of carelessness on the part of some former staff for packing up the then vice president`s office in 2017.\par \par \uc2 But clearly now the White House feels as that they`re ending the week in a different place than where they began this week, as it`s been put, there was a sort of full boil of a crisis on Tuesday, as we still were dealing with a drip-drip of new revelations about additional documents, additional pages being found. Now they`re trying to -- and you see this in the President`s comments yesterday, try to pivot away from this back to what they had hoped to spend this month doing.\par \par \uc2 Which is to try to highlight those contrasts with the new Republican congressional majority as they look to the next two years and potentially to a reelection campaign. We`re going to see, of course, as the President spoke yesterday, after having dodged reporter`s questions for eight previous instances, when they were put to him, the White House press secretary will be tested again that she`s about to brief reporters this hour.\par \par \uc2 Once again, though, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  is bringing a special guest with her to the podium today. It`s the spokesperson for the National Security Council. We`re going to see the white house trying to talk about both on the foreign policy basis and domestic policy, what they believe are their accomplishments two years into this administration, especially as they look on a foreign policy level coming up to the anniversary of the invasion of Ukraine and the ways in which President Biden had - in their view has united our allies in deterrence of Russia`s invasion there.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s an interesting ongoing effort here on part of the White House to get back on their front foot. But we`re still going to be seeing additional questions raised on a daily basis as more revelations likely are coming in.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Jonathan, you co-wrote the Politico article I quoted from just a minute ago. And I want to read a little more. Where a Biden administration official told you "We are clear eyed about the kind of scorched earth tactics and mud fighting they want to engage in.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 We are going to follow the law and the rules of the game and we won`t shy away from calling him out for flagrantly assaulting norms, order and facts themselves. So, they want to argue the facts of the investigation but still kind of stay above the fray. How complex is it to try to do both of those things?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN LEMIRE, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes. It`s going to be a delicate balancing act here for this White House. And to Mike`s point, they have -- this has been their hope this entire years, to project themselves as the adults in the room. Serious responsible, doing the business of the people, staying away from some of the incendiary headlines and controversies. And well, they give her obviously delta blow to that effort with the discovery of these classified documents.\par \par \uc2 They`ve been a little more assertive about how they`re talking about it recently, and trying to still draw a stark contrast between what was found in these, you know, President Biden`s home and the Penn Center here in D.C. versus, of course, the case with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . Hundreds of documents at Mar-a-Lago and seemingly willful attempts to fight the National Archives efforts to retain them.\par \par \uc2 Some questions about obstruction of justice for the former president. But in terms of these members of the House Oversight Committee, they do think this is a real opportunity. And yes, of course, you don`t want to celebrate too soon. But they feel like these members, the Marjorie Taylor Greenes, Lauren Boeberts, the Paul Gosars of the world, all election deniers, and all the White House says threats to democracy.\par \par \uc2 But they also think they`re potentially a political gift that this sort of radicals or fringe Republicans, the White House says, are going to be unable to help themselves. They`re going to play to the cameras, they`re going to grandstand, they`re going to play for their deep red districts and their national fundraising audiences and just appear out of step with the rest of the American people that sort of act and speak in a way that will turn off a lot of voters, the White House believes.\par \par \uc2 And they think that though, of course, no investigation is fun. No oversight committee is fun for a White House, they think there`s actually going to be some political opportunity here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And Matthew, they understand that the Republicans, the Conservatives are going to do exactly what they said they were going to do, investigate, investigate, and then investigate some more. A lot of this is going to feel personal to this president, whether it`s an attack on his integrity or personal to his family, like the Hunter Biden investigation. Look, you`re never going to get a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  not to think like a dad but you run political campaigns.\par \par \uc2 So, you want your candidate to speak and act sometimes in a different way. What would be your advice in a situation like that when he`s got all this incoming?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, MSNBC SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think to be the reason to adult in the room and I understand the White House`s and the staff`s celebration of the chaos in the House and how it could have political benefit. I actually that -- don`t think that`s what they should be doing. Though I understand that they should be focusing on one, we`re the adults and two, is this is the damage that what`s going on the House can do to you, the American citizen.\par \par \uc2 They`ve put in power. And this is what I would be saying, if I were them. This is a dangerous situation where they put in power, people that do not support our constitutional democracy, who do not want to act in any reasoned common good way. And that`s detrimental to the American public and what we need to get done on behalf of the American public. I get the celebration part of politics.\par \par \uc2 But I actually think the best politics is to be serious, reasoned and calm and show what having this going on in the House is going to be a detriment to the American public.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There`s another part to this, though, Jake. I think it`s important for the White House to be able to successfully contrast themselves with House Republicans or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , yes. But to do that they need to maintain their own credibility.\par \par \uc2 And I want to read from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  op-ed because it says, "The optical equivalence between Mr. Trump and Mr. Biden is phony, of course. But many voters with better things to do with their time, then parse the nuances of presidential record keeping may casually conclude that both men are careless line politicians."\par \par \uc2 And it goes on to argue that the story has already cost Biden maybe the benefit of the doubt going forward. But if the White House is dead set against talking about this case, how do they change that perception?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SHERMAN, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: They wait until the investigation is over and hope it comes out in their -- in their back? I mean, I think this does raise questions. I mean, to the extent anyone`s paying attention, I`m not sure that many -- that everyone is just is paying attention to this document back and forth. Maybe they are, maybe they`re not. But I think -- I think that that is right, is that both people will think that that these are politicians and that if one is doing it, and the other is doing it, and I agree with Matthew here that the argument that worked for Obama in 2012 was that the House is so concerned with tangential issues, you`re hording where are they on these issues?\par \par \uc2 We have these bills. They`re not acting on it. Instead, they`re investigating issue X, Y and Z.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:01]\par \par \uc2 Now that is not to say that the House Oversight Judiciary, all these committees are not going to come up with things that are embarrassing to the president, they probably are. But that they`re also going to overstep and there`s also opportunity for this White House, but you can`t be too -- you can`t overshoot it, because they`re -- the -- these investigations are overdone. They are overwrought. They do overreach.\par \par \uc2 But they do occasionally find an embarrassing tidbit or two about the target. So, I think those two dynamics, they`re not paying attention. They`re investigating, that -- then they`re not --they`re not doing what the American people want. I think -- I think that is what the president`s going to focus on, given his own experience in the Obama administration.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Matthew, as for Republicans, maybe in the 2024 race, we heard Nikki Haley suggest, once again, she very well may be one of them. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NIKI HALEY (R-SC), FORMER GOVERNOR: When you`re looking at a run for president, you look at two things you first look at, does the current situation push for new leadership? The second question is, am I that person that could be that new leader? Yes, we need to go in a new direction. And can I be that leader? Yes. I think I can be that leader.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What`s your thought right now? I mean, do you think they`re going to be a lot of challengers for the nomination on the Republican side? And if so, what are they waiting for?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: Well, I mean, as everybody knows, on this panel, it takes a bit of delusion anyway to run for president of the United States in this country. So, it takes a sense of dumb delusion, even the best of people. She has a serious amount, I think, in my view, of delusion in this. She also went, as you know, Chris, on in that interview to say, I`ve never lost a race before, so I`m prepared.\par \par \uc2 And I would remind her that the presidential political field is littered with people that have never lost a race before, got into president and that I could remind her of Scott Walker in 2016, Ted Cruz in 2016, \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  in 2016, Rick Perry in 2016. All of which had never lost a race before. Got involved in the race --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And by the way, not interrupt you but I`m interrupting you, Matthew, the guy who won the office and is in it right now lost several times. And he`s the one who`s president now.\par \par \uc2 DOWD: Well, that`s, you know, that`s the other part I was going to say. Think about the last 40- or 50 years. \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  lost before he ran and won. The both Bush`s ran and lost before they ran in one. \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  ran and lost a race before he won. And Barack Obama ran and lost a race before he won. So, the reality is, it`s sometimes it`s better to have run and loss and learn what you did or did not do right or wrong.\par \par \uc2 But I think Nikki Haley -- Nikki Haley, today`s polling, exactly where \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is polling in the Republican primary, and I don`t see a lot of people giving \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  a chance to win the Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Matthew Dowd, Mike Memoli, Jake Sherman, thank you all. Jonathan Lemire, you`re going to come back in just a little bit. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, apart from the ever-present politics, candidate Biden in 2024 will have to answer the question of his age. Biden set the mark for the oldest president in U.S. history in 2021 at 78. That is older than \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was when he left office after two terms. Remember this?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I will not make age an issue of this campaign. I am not going to exploit for political purposes My opponent`s youth and inexperience.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Steve Kornacki Is MSNBC national political correspondent. One of the most famous arguing, most effective quips in American political modern history. But the age comparison I think with Biden is really interesting, as well as an economic comparison when you look at him against \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it`s interesting. We`ve been looking today at different modern presidents at the halfway point. What came in the next two years politically for them? So, let`s zoom in on \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  . And here you go. This is January `83. This is the start of the second half of his first term there. He`s delivering his State of the Union address.\par \par \uc2 Reagan`s approval rating, a lot of people forget this. It was very low early in his presidency. The country was in a recession, unemployment was over 10 percent. 39.8 percent with Reagan`s approval rating. Halfway through his presidency. Biden`s right now on average is 42.8 percent. So not much difference. And Reagan before Biden, the last president where there were serious questions that were asked really, throughout those first couple years of his presidency about would he run again, what he run for reelection or would he be too old?\par \par \uc2 Would he have to be a one-term president? It really dominated the political conversation. This is from early 1983. This is an NBC News report. A little grainy there you can see on the screen, but I think we have a clip here. This is what the political conversation was like at the start of the second half of Reagan`s first term.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEFMALE: Will the president run for another term? The pressure is on him to decide and to say what he will do.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:01]\par \par \uc2 Several Democrats are getting set to begin their campaigning. Some Republicans are standing by not knowing if they ought to begin raising money. Will he run? Ken Bode has been asking that question.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am trained to say yes, of course. Yes, of course he`ll run.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One democratic official has bet $1,000 that Mr. Reagan won`t run again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Having said the fact that I assume he will run, I can`t imagine why he would run again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s a big question in Washington these days, will he or won`t he? Why wouldn`t he run? Well, he`d be 78 years old at the end of the second term, Mr. Reagan`s down in the polls and he couldn`t lose the next election. Also, the Republicans could very well lose the Senate in 1984, making him a much weaker chief executive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: So those were the doubts, Chris in early `83. Of course, Reagan did end up running for reelection. The Democrats nominated \uc2 Walter Mondale\uc2  , the former vice president. And the big thing that changed between that clip from early `83 and election night `84 was the economy completely turned around. Unemployment was falling, growth was surging. Reagan ended up winning a landslide reelection over Mondale.\par \par \uc2 Now, we obviously -- we live in a much more polarized country than we did back in 1984. But I do think there are some interesting potential parallels there. When you look at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , the economy has dragged him down a bit the last two years. If it can turn around. Hey, Reagan proved that 70 -- going to the age of 78 as president ended up not being too old in the voters` minds about four decades ago.\par \par \uc2 Could Biden push that number into the 80s? He may test it in a way Reagan did all those decades ago.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. 22 months in political years, still a very long time between now and Election Day. Steve Kornacki, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: All right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New denials from scandal-ridden Congressman George Santos. The new criticism added to the laundry list of allegations against him.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the Rust shooting investigation. Why prosecutors say they knew early that they would file charges.\par \par \uc2 And anti-abortion protesters take over streets in D.C. as we approach the 50th anniversary of Roe v. Wade and the one-year mark since its demise. Where does this fight head next? You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:21:18]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Some developing news link to the January 6 insurrection. Three active-duty Marines now face charges for breaking into the Capitol. One of them posting afterwards. He was glad to be a part of it and that he was waiting for a second Civil War. Ken Dilanian, covers justice and intelligence for NBC News. So, Ken, who exactly are these guys and what did they do?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC JUSTICE AND INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT: So