roversial Don`t Say Gay bill, he`s aligned himself with some of the GOP`s most hard-right lawmakers who have seen the drag community as an object of danger, of scorn.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he is married to a man but he`s making friends with anti-LGBTQ lawmakers. Politically, what`s his play here?\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: That`s just an attempt to stay in power it would seem and cozy up to interest on the right. He`s feeling where the political wind is blowing. And you`re right. There are a number of Republicans who have embraced this anti-LGBTQ legislation who have turned drag queens, you know, into an object of scorn, as you say, in the Times, you`ve been a target for violence. So, for \uc2 Santos\uc2  , it`s the latest bit of hypocrisy.\par \par \uc2 And yes, of course, another lie which we can add to the list, rather lengthy list that involves everything from his -- how his mother died, claiming that she --- it was during September 11th when she was actually a continent away, or to lie about something seemingly innocuous like being on a championship volleyball team when he didn`t play volleyball or even attend the school.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s just yet another moment though where he`s in the headlines. And yet to this point, though, there are some of his fellow Republicans, including some from New York State, where he`s from have called for him to resign leadership is sticking by him and that`s because Kevin McCarthy hasn`t had a vote to spare.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Making claims that are easy to check like where you went to college, right? So, I -- the House has been in recess since last Thursday and picking up on what Jonathan just said.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:05]\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you all of the Santos developments that have come out just while they were gone. He said he will resign if 142,000 people who voted for him asked him to. We learned his mother was not in the U.S. on 9/11. That accused him of cheating him out of funds for his dying dog, by the way, a disabled vet. Now the claims that he performed as a drag queen, we`ve talked all week about whether there`s some kind of cumulative effect to the lies and accusations or maybe a numbness.\par \par \uc2 But what will you be looking forward? Congress comes back next week.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Taking the temperature really, Chris, because as Congress was going out of session before this past week, most of the freshmen New York delegation had said that they wanted Santos to resign. These are all the folks who were elected in the same 2022 cycle from the same state as George Santos. But leadership, as John mentioned, has been mum on this.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy is playing a numbers game, they don`t want to have to push this to a special election where they could likely lose, especially when on the ground, all of our correspondents who have gone there, it shows an anti- Santos sentiment because they feel they`ve been lied to and duped. And, of course they do for just the reasons that you put on the screen. And that`s nothing to say of all of the lies that we know about from before this past week.\par \par \uc2 And so, I do think that at a certain point, it becomes -- you can`t just continue going like this, it becomes unsustainable as Republicans get back in town, I think that`s the question that we have to ask is that as they try to move forward, pushing an agenda, they`re going to have to start in the early phases of the debt ceiling negotiations, they`re going to want to start moving on Oversight and Investigations.\par \par \uc2 That`s what Republicans are going to want the message to be coming out of the first weeks of a Republican majority. They`re not going to want it to be the latest in the George Santos saga, which is, frankly, what it has been for the last several weeks. And so, that might be unsustainable. Whether or not it actually pushes the majority of the conference to say that he should resign. I doubt it.\par \par \uc2 But what could is if there`s actual legal charges, if he were to get indicted on campaign finance, that`s the line that most of my sources have drawn because then it becomes nearly impossible to continue to say he should be a member of Congress. The rest of it, it might be inconvenient, but it might not be enough.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. I get that legal charge part of it, Jonathan. But I do also wonder the first thing that Ali talked about, which is that if he starts to overshadow what they want the message to be, or maybe even just the attention they want to have. When does that reach a breaking point or in the, you know, in the House behind you, does it give them more fodder to talk about the chaos? What they see is the lack of ethics on the other side.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m curious what your take is or what you`ve been hearing from White House folks.\par \par \uc2 LEMIRE: Yes. George Sanders is a pretty popular guy, the building behind me. He`s a pretty easy punch line. And they can point to just this -- the sort of how the Republican Party has become this natural evolution and extension of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , another political character, of course, who lied all the time, incessantly, some harmlessly, many not. And in fact, it was his lies about the election, of course, that led to the violence on January 6.\par \par \uc2 And inspired election deniers from coast to coast. So, \uc2 Santos\uc2  is someone they can point to and say, look, yes, he`s one of them. For Republicans, if they were able to -- if there would ever be a day that would go by when another Santos lie wasn`t emerged, and he could fade from the headlines. Well, we could test the theory that he wouldn`t be a distraction, that he becomes just another backbencher with a cloud over his head.\par \par \uc2 But it`s the fact he`s continues to actively make news is the problem. And if that overshadows the messaging they want, certainly we know that Biden investigations are going to ramp up, there`s going to be hardline negotiating on the debt ceiling. The list is long of things the Republicans want to do with their slim majority.\par \par \uc2 If Santos gets in the way of that, that will be the issue. But to Ali`s smart point, if eventually he does step aside in the wake of a criminal charge or not and a special election is called, it`d be very difficult to see Republicans winning that right now. And their margin is so slim as it is, McCarthy may be very reluctant to force him out.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jonathan Lemire, Ali Vitali, thank you both so much. Have a good weekend. The U.S. rolls out another massive aid package for Ukraine amid a heated debate over getting European tanks to Kyiv. Why that could be a game changer during this critical juncture in the war, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:46]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New questions are emerging about exactly what the assistant director from the set of "Rust" must know. Dave Halls made a deal with prosecutors in their case against both actor Alec Baldwin and the film`s armorer on charges of involuntary manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY CARMACK-ALTWIES, SANTA FE DISTRICT ATTORNEY: David Halls wanted to take responsibility for his actions.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA REEB, SPECIAL PROSECUTOR: He`s going to describe, you know, exactly what happened that day from his perspective. And he will talk about the scene and the movie set and different things that were happening and weren`t happening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joining me now on set MSNBC legal analyst Danny Cevallos.\par \par \uc2 You know, a lot of people didn`t, Danny, that Alec Baldwin was going to get charged here. So when you see a plea deal with the assistant director, who was there, what questions do you have for him, and how much do you think that deal played into the absolute confidence we heard from the prosecutor yesterday?\par \par \uc2 DANNY CEVALLOS, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, I have been critical of the prosecution and keep in mind, I`m a defense attorney so you have to take this with a grain of salt. But it was very telling to me that they cooperated one of these potential codefendants. Why did they do that? Why did they go to the guy that nobody has ever heard of to cooperate him against another defendant?\par \par \uc2 [13:35:02]\par \par \uc2 Is Alec Baldwin a kingpin? That`s normally what you do to kingpins. No, I think he`s just a very high profile celebrity target. And what solidified that for me were the interviews that the district attorneys gave mostly yesterday saying something to the effect, and I`m paraphrasing, nobody is above the law. You hifalutin Hollywood types, you know, you`re too big for your britches. You`re just like everybody else.\par \par \uc2 Now they didn`t say those words exactly, but the message, I thought, was clear. I think from the beginning they had their eye on Alec Baldwin, even though there were many other people involved in this chain of custody, and now they`ve charged the person that I think they always planned to charge. That`s why they cooperated a witness in the form of someone who would have been a codefendant.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, and the assistant director is very interesting in that the lawyers said yesterday, and by the way Vaughn Hillyard, who`s been covering this, is here as well, said something to the effect of there`s like no ax to grind here. He`s just going to tell the truth. The question is how much did he see, how much did he know, what`s the focus on of his testimony? A lot of people have speculated that maybe it`s about overall safety issues on the set.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Right. And, you know, I just got back from New Mexico yesterday.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New Mexico, yes.\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Well, the charges were handed down, and there was some expectation that Alec Baldwin was not going to be charged. And that was what was so stunning to see this involuntary manslaughter charges up against him here. And when you`re talking about what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  `s forensic office, their report, and looking at the actual firearm that was used by Alec Baldwin here, it goes to the heart of the question of what responsibility does one bear when they pick up a firearm. Whether it`s something to be a prop on a movie set or not.\par \par \uc2 Because that report that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  put out, they explicitly said that the firearm could not have discharged a bullet without pulling the trigger there. But then at the same point, in that same report, it says it may not be possible to recreate or duplicate all of the circumstances which led to the discharge of a firearm without a pull of the trigger. So there`s some questions about the basic facts here that are at play that are going to be used by the prosecution against Alec Baldwin.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let me play for you exactly what both sides said. The special prosecutor involved when, and then, Danny, I`ll get your reaction on the other side. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARMACK-ALTWIES: This was something that was more than mere negligence. It was people acting recklessly, people not doing their jobs, people not following safety protocols, not following safety standards, and violating all of the standards that we all have to follow if we have a gun in our hands.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So then I think we also have -- do we have the response from Alec Baldwin, what he had to say about that earlier?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEC BALDWIN, ACTOR: No, no, no, no. I would never point a gun at anyone and pull a trigger at them. Never. No, that was the training that I had. You don`t point a gun at somebody and pull a trigger.\par \par \uc2 CARMACK-ALTWIES: That`s not true. And we know that from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  lab report. That gun would not have fired without the trigger having been pulled.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So that`s what they`re going to have to navigate in front of a jury.\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: This is why people like me tell clients never ever, ever sit down and neither talk to law enforcement and don`t give interviews, and no matter how many times people like me say it, people like Alec Baldwin in this example go and do it. That is a very damaging statement because the reality is, had he just said I never pulled the trigger, you could work with that. But the whole idea of I`m trained to never point a gun at anyone, that hurts his defense.\par \par \uc2 Because had he just said, I never pulled the trigger, I still think this is a defensible case because the argument then becomes, OK, let`s say the jury doesn`t believe that he didn`t pull the trigger, let`s say the jury believes he pulled the trigger. He didn`t believe he was handling a firearm in the sense that he didn`t believe he was handling something that could send a projectile down range. He thought he was handling a block of plastic for all intents and purposes.\par \par \uc2 But I got to admit that interview where he adds on, I would never, beware of anyone who say I would never. That`s kind of character evidence that does not do well because now you bring in all kinds of other evidence. Well, you know, you`re telling us the standard of care you would never point a gun at someone and shoot it? That`s not very helpful. I expect at trial you`re going to see that video of that interview.\par \par \uc2 But I also have to say about the prosecutor. Listen to her interview where she says this was basically -- and I`m paraphrasing -- people all along the chain who made mistakes and didn`t do their job. Well, my argument response if I`m closing is going to be, the prosecution themselves say that this was negligence at many different steps. If that`s the case, and these are not co-conspirators, then Alec Baldwin is, what, 20 percent responsible? That is not beyond a reasonable doubt, ladies and gentlemen. How did I do? Now what do you think?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And then they`re bringing in the emotional angle to this. It`s Matt Hutchens, who after that interview that Alec Baldwin sat with -- is when he became he infuriated with him, angry with him because he took no responsibility for his actions there. And they had had dinner after her passing previously here. So suddenly that`s where you`re getting at, you`re going to be likely hearing from these family members here over the course of the trial here. And that is where when it comes to the responsibility and the negligence involved here, it`s not hard not to listen to the husband of the one individual who did pass away in the tragedy.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Vaughn Hillyard, fresh off another plane, this one from New Mexico. Danny Cevallos, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Our next story might astonish you. The lights at this sprawling Massachusetts high school have been on around the clock for nearly a year and a half because nobody can figure out how to turn them off. A lighting system originally intended to save money and energy has utterly failed at both thanks to a software glitch affecting 7,000 lights. Replacement parts are stuck in China. And as the district waits, taxpayers are shelling out a small fortune to keep the lights on to the tune of thousands of dollars each month. Can you say supply chain issues?\par \par \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  might be giving \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  a run for her money. The reluctant royal memoir "Spare" is likely to be one of the best-selling memoirs of all time after more than three million copies were sold in the first week alone. It`s drawing comparisons to former First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  , whose book "Becoming" has sold more than 17 million copies since it hit the shelves in 2018.\par \par \uc2 And some news from the White House press briefing. The Russian mercenary group the U.S. is targeting and the secret meeting the CIA director just held in Ukraine. That`s all next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:27]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Moments ago, National Security Council spokesman John Kirby with a major move by the U.S. against the Wagner Group. That`s a Russian mercenary group that makes up roughly 10 percent of Russian forces fighting in Ukraine. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, DEFENSE DEPARTMENT SPOKESMAN: We will also impose additional sanctions next week against Wagner and its support network across multiple continents. These actions recognize the transcontinental threat that Wagner poses including through its ongoing pattern of serious criminal activity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The move comes amid three strong indications that right now the war in Ukraine is at a critically important juncture. Western Defense officials are meeting to debate sending European tanks to Kyiv. President Zelenskyy is making his ask clear saying, quote, "Hundreds of thank yous are not hundreds of tanks."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. also just announced its second largest security package for Ukraine. A whopping $2.5 billion, and we`re learning about a secret meeting that the CIA director held with \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC`s Raf Sanchez from Kyiv. And Raf, we`re approaching one year, hard to believe, since Russia invaded Ukraine. What are you hearing on the ground about these European tanks, the importance of them, and could they potentially have a major impact for the Ukrainians who are in that fight?\par \par \uc2 RAF SANCHEZ, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Ukrainian officials definitely think so, Chris. We met today with the secretary of Ukraine`s National Security Council and in his office, he has a big computer where he has a live map of the street by street fighting going on in Bakhmut, that city in the east where so much of the fighting is going on, where those Wagner mercenaries are involved.\par \par \uc2 And he jabbed at that screen which showed every air strike, every artillery strike, and he said this is why we need tanks. And the Ukrainians really feel the tanks could make a big difference on the eastern front right now, especially as Russia is regrouping for what may be a fresh offensive in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the situation on tanks. Germany makes a tank called the Leopard, which is very widely used among the \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies. But nobody can give it to Ukraine without Germany`s permission and that is a step right now that the Germans are not prepared to take. The German chancellor \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  has repeatedly expressed his concerns about escalating the conflict.\par \par \uc2 Chris, as we`ve talked about, Germany very reliant on Russia for its energy supplies. So potentially vulnerability to retaliation from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  . There have been suggestions Germany won`t send tanks unless the U.S. also agrees to do so. The American position is the M-1 Abrams tank used by the U.S. military is not practical for Ukraine. It requires all sorts of very specialized maintenance. It is an absolute gas guzzler. It can use a gallon of gas in one minute at sometimes.\par \par \uc2 So the U.S. would like to see these Leopard tanks, which are in Europe already with spare parts in Europe already, sent to Ukraine and sent to the frontline. Now we heard from three U.S. senators here, including \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  , in Kyiv, he described this whole dynamic about clowns -- about tanks, excuse me, as a clown show, but he didn`t use the word clown. He used an expletive that I can`t say on air. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Raf Sanchez, thank you so much. We appreciate you being there.\par \par \uc2 Right now, just blocks from the Supreme Court, anti-abortion protesters are taking to the streets in their first March for Life rally since the fall of Roe v. Wade. So where does this fight head next? Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:58]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A massive investigation into a hugely consequential leak, in fact, one that made jaws drop across the country just finished with no answer. We`re talking about the leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion that ultimately overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what we can tell you. 82 employees had access to that draft opinion. None admits leaking it. And even with forensic evidence, say, looking for fingerprints, they simply have not been able to figure out who did it.\par \par \uc2 And just a few blocks away from the Supreme Court, anti-abortion advocates are marching in the first March for Life rally since the fall of Roe. This Sunday will mark 50 years since the original 1973 decision and both advocates and critics are still wondering the same thing, what happens next?\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Marissa Parra is live from the National Mall. I see the crowds are there. Talk a little bit, Marissa, about the mood and what the organizers you`re talking to are focusing on now.\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. I mean, in every single will way the conversation around abortion has changed since the last March for Life. Obviously, we are now living in this post-Roe world. Something that`s been reflected all day since we`ve been out here. We have seen and heard rallying cries of rejoicing, of applause when people were talking, taking to the stage.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to move over here to the side because we hear emergency sirens and crews passing through. But we heard resounding applause as people took to the stage saying that we are now in a post-Roe world. We saw lots of signs saying we are part of the post-Roe generation. Obviously, we will look into whatever is happening here behind us. A lot of people here. And one thing we have noticed is that they`re changing the route as well to reflect this new March for Life.\par \par \uc2 Again, you know, March for Life was started as a result of Roe v. Wade. It started the year after. And so what they`re doing now is instead of taking to the Supreme Court alone, they`re stopping at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 And what they`ll be doing is they`ll be marching past the Capitol and then stopping in between the Capitol and Supreme Court to really reflect the key part that both the judiciary and legislation play in what comes next.\par \par \uc2 So let`s talk about that. After Roe v. Wade was overturned, we saw roughly two dozen states either ban or severely restrict abortion access. There are still three states where there`s ongoing legal battles. And that`s just with abortion in particular. We talk about what`s next, abortion access by way of pills is a big part of the conversation. And that is something that I know is going to be a big part of legislative sessions as that happens. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Marissa Parra, we`ll be checking in with you through the day. Thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 And before we go, we want to take a moment to honor someone whose music and lyrics galvanized protesters. Legendary singer-songwriter David Crosby, best known as a member of the Byrds and Crosby, Stills and Nash, and occasionally Young, and for his impassioned hits like "Almost Cut My Hair" and "Wooden Chips." He`s died at 81 after suffering a long illness.\par \par \uc2 The two-time Rock and Roll Hall of Famer`s former band mate Graham Nash saying, in part, quote, "David was fearless in life and in music." Here`s Crosby seven years ago on MSNBC`s "MORNING JOE" explaining where he gets his inspiration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID CROSBY, SINGER AND COUNTERCULTURE ICON: It`s usually love. We usually write about love. I do. Sometimes it crosses your path, you know, and you said, gee, that`s not right. And then the sort of town crier part of our gig kicks into gear. And we say, you know, OK, wait a minute, America is shooting its own children and we better write Ohio.\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, HOST, MSNBC`S "MORNING JOE": Right.\par \par \uc2 CROSBY: It can`t do a steady dive off that. Our job is to make people boogie or take them on a little emotional voyage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And that`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 A note, President Biden will speak at the White House about his track record at the midway point of his four-year term in just a few moments. Joe Fryer will pick up our coverage, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230121ej1k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for January 20, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Emilie Ikeda \par \uc2 7,878 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: THE 11TH HOUR starts right now with Alicia Menendez.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the new fight for abortion rights ahead of the 50-year anniversary of the Roe decision as V.P. Harris takes the movement right to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  ` doorstep.\par \par \uc2 Then, Team Trump facing the consequences for frivolous lawsuits, escaping review from the judge on Trump`s actions of political manifesto against his rivals.\par \par \uc2 And reflecting on President Biden`s two years in office and the uphill battle he faces in this split Congress. One of our favorite historians is here as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Alicia Menendez, in for Stephanie Ruhle. It has been seven months since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. But today, antiabortion activists came to Washington for the annual March for Life, the first since Roe was struck down, and just two days before the 50th anniversary of that original decision. While the antiabortion rights movement has achieved one of its main goals, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  points out, there`s a ". internal debate about how to make an abortion ban nationwide. March leaders emphasized Friday that Roe`s overthrow was just the start, that they will be launching dozens of local marches."\par \par \uc2 Most abortions have been banned in more than a dozen states, and support for a national ban was a major theme at today`s march.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY CARMEN KRAUSE, STUDENTS FOR LIFE: While the march began as a response to Roe, we don`t end as a response to Roe being overturned. Why? Because we`re not yet done.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Everything that happened before Roe, that was just the preseason. We always envisioned this day when it would happen, not if Roe would be reversed, but when? So, to think that, oh, our work is done, well, our work just begun.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: There were a few high-profile Republican politicians at today`s event. House Majority Leader Steve Scalise was the most prominent GOP leader there. Speaker Kevin McCarthy showed his support with a tweet noting that the House recently, "passed legislation providing medical care for babies born alive during abortions." Well, just a fact check here. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  points out that federal law already requires that a baby who survives an attempted abortion receive emergency medical care. Abortion rights supporters were also out in D.C. to protest today`s march and state level restrictions. Abortion opponents now focused on curbing access to abortion pills, which is raising new alarms about the legal battles ahead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CECILE RICHARDS, PLANNED PARENTHOOD FORMER PRESIDENT: It was so clear out of the midterm elections that voters do not want politicians deciding pregnancy. Access to medication, abortion, which is a very safe method of ending pregnancy early on, is so important. We really have to focus on these legislatures and governors who are trying to criminalize doctors, women, anyone who`s seeking medical care.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: On Sunday, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will mark the 50th anniversary of the Roe decision. She`ll be in Florida to give a speech on abortion rights. Last April, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed a bill banning abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 There`s also news tonight about the investigation into the leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion overturning Roe versus Wade. The court says the justices were interviewed on multiple occasions as part of that inquiry. Yesterday, the court said it was unable to identify whoever leaked the document.\par \par \uc2 And with that, let`s bring in our lead off panel, Amna Nawaz, the new co- anchor of PBS NewsHour and an MSNBC Contributor, Irin Carmon, Senior Correspondent for New York Magazine. She`s also the co-author of Notorious RBG, The Life and Times of \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  . Michele Goodwin, Law Professor at the \uc2 University of California, Irvine\uc2  . It is great to see you all.\par \par \uc2 Amna, I`m going to start with, you know, for years, the antiabortion movement, they were in lockstep. They knew what they were working towards. But with this march, there seems to be less unity about how they go forward. Your sense of how overturning Roe has actually changed their movement?\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Alicia, I think the antiabortion rights movement has always been consistent and always been clear. Their goal has always been to end all abortion access in America. Anyone I`ve ever talked to over the last several years has made that very clear. Since the overturning of Roe, I think the real confusion lies with the members of the Republican Party, in which many of the antiabortion rights activists have found their space of support there in terms of legislation and policy making.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party is not speaking with one voice when it comes to what the future of any abortion access in America will look like. And the problem is this, in the post Roe reality, this is where the rubber meets the road. And now we have this mosaic, this hodgepodge of policies and bands across the country. Your rights as a woman to make decisions about your body and your pregnancy really depend on where you live, on which state you live in.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:10]\par \par \uc2 And I think if you look at where the March for Life ended up today, it is indicative of where this battle now is. In previous years, they have marched a Supreme Court to make their point. Today, they marched closer to the Capitol building because they know this is now a legislative fight. And not necessarily a national legislative fight, because let`s be clear, even when Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  came forward with a proposal for a national 15- week ban that took off like a lead balloon. You even have \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  coming out and saying, we don`t think that there`s a national appetite for this right now. There aren`t 60 votes to support a national ban. This is now a battle being fought in state legislatures across the country. That`s where abortion rates will be determined.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right. Irin, let`s talk about that battle. Here`s what The Atlantic wrote this week about abortion pills specifically. Even as abortion opponents celebrate, they`re growing more frustrated about the increased reliance on the drugs which are now used in 54% of U.S. abortions. Your sense of how determined abortion opponents are when it comes to trying to ban the use of these pills?\par \par \uc2 IRIN CARMON, NEW YORK MAGAZINE SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: You know, Alicia, Roe v. Wade -- this decision that we are commemorating in its absence was based on the rights of a doctor to practice their profession. Doctors are no longer needed to end a pregnancy, and that`s revolutionary. And they understand, the antiabortion group, understand that they can shut down clinics. They can now pass laws statewide banning abortion. But it is really, really hard for them to prevent this powerful tool from landing in the hands of seekers. And that drives them crazy. I mean, I remember when I first wrote about this a few years ago, an activist`s, a pro-choice activist telling me they know that they can`t ransack the medicine cabinets of every pregnant woman in America, but damned if they`re not going to try, right?\par \par \uc2 So, there has already been -- there`s a nationwide lawsuit that`s been brought in Texas that attempts to get an injunction against the \uc2 FDA\uc2  for approving the abortion pill. So, that`s one avenue in which they are trying to restrict access by cutting off this pill that`s been available for literal decades through a single judge in Texas. We`ll see in the next few weeks whether they`re willing to go that far. This is a Trump judge who`s a Christian right activist before he was appointed.\par \par \uc2 Then, they`re also trying to figure out ways to criminalize the male transmission. Right now, with the Biden administration in place, they`re very supportive of access to the abortion pill, but that could certainly change with the administration changing, should they change in 2024. And, there`s also been attempts to crack down on providers who are doing television. You know, there`s a lot of things that are happening kind of in gray areas of the law as people figure out the legality of, for example, a doctor in New York. What happens if a doctor in New York decides that they`re going to stretch the boundaries of the law and prescribe to somebody in a state? What kind of immunity is being offered? This legislation that`s already been passed some blue states and is being discussed in New York about protecting these doctors?\par \par \uc2 So, we`re very much -- come a long way from the sort of genteel white doctors that grow being allowed to practice their profession. This is a total uncharted territory. And I think it drives the anti-abortion crazy that it`s so hard to regulate. But that`s precisely what makes it such a powerful tool, even though the people who use that tool are at risk of criminalization.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right. And Michele, all of this happening against a backdrop of misinformation being used as antiabortion tactic. What are you seeing out there?\par \par \uc2 MICHELE GOODWIN, LAW PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA, IRVINE: Well, historically there`s been anti-information or truthful information campaign that`s been undertaken the claims that an abortion would cause someone mental health trauma, that an abortion causes breast cancer, that abortions cause ovarian cancer. This misinformation campaign has been continued through crisis pregnancy centers. Crisis pregnancy centers have sprung up. They`ve been so amplified with so many now across the country. And they use a very interesting tactic. That is, when individuals are searching for abortion online, they`re more than likely to actually see a crisis pregnancy center pop up than an actual clinic that could provide them those services.\par \par \uc2 And while at those clinics, there`s tremendous misinformation that is spread to people who are looking for abortion and reproductive health care. But even more, there are other First Amendment concerns that are springing up now in the wake of the overturn of Roe v. Wade. And that`s something we should be critically concerned about other areas of the Constitution and constitutional protections that are now severely at risk because of Roe`s overturn.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:02]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Amna, I want to circle back to this question of the interplay between the movement and Republicans. Here`s what some members of the GOP have been saying about abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE, (R) LOIUSIANA MAJORITY LEADER: You are showing us and giving us hope that the young people of America support life, defend life, are here to march for life.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOV. \uc2 LARRY HOGAN\uc2  (R) MARYLAND: I don`t think this is the right issue for the Republican Party to be making, and I don`t think it`s really smart politics.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Every life deserves to be protected in law, and the people of Texas agree.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE, (R-SC): With I believe that it`s tone deaf at this point because. It`s never going to pass the Senate. It`s never going to get to the President`s desk design law. We`re only paying the service for the pro- life movement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So, Amna, they`re not singing from the same song book there. What`s behind the division? And why is it that some folks looked at what happened in November and said this is not a winning strategy for us, and others said, hey, let`s continue to lean in here?\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: -- them is that, the pressure they feel from the anti-abortion rights movement, which is, we need to end all abortion in America, will continue. But the reality that we live in is that in this country, the majority of Americans believe that abortion should be legal in most, if not all cases. And that is just a fact. And, Republicans know that. Depending on where you are, the message carries different weight. For example, you look back to the lessons we pulled from the midterms. Prior to the midterms, I was on the ground in Florida in the gubernatorial race there between \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  . There was no single issue that the Democrat candidate there, \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  , was talking about more than abortion access and about how this was about women`s rights and women`s decisions about their bodies. It wasn`t enough to close the gap in the end. But then, you look at other politically mixed places like Michigan or Pennsylvania or Minnesota, where abortion rights made all the difference on the ballot, it absolutely energized the electorate. And that was a lesson for both Republicans and for Democrats coming out of that.\par \par \uc2 So, that is the challenge ahead. Are you going to be the candidate who comes forward and says, I`m going to further roll back abortion access in a key swing state like Michigan or Pennsylvania? We know what the outcome of that will be.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right. Irin, you have \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  saying that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is hiding behind his 15-week abortion ban, already hearing some of what we`re going to hear in the lead up to 2024. There was one opponent of abortion rights who told the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  about Republicans, the pro-life movement is not going to be happy or thanking a candidate simply for saying they are pro-life. Clearly, the bar has shifted here. What are they going to have to do to keep that support? And if you look at it through the lens of 2024, is that potentially then a gift to Democrats?\par \par \uc2 CARMON: Of course, they need to get through a primary. The question is, what does the support of the folks at the March for Life mean at this point, right? Because they -- if the idea is to get the hearts and minds of the majority of Americans, as Amna mentioned, that is not with the abortion bans. The Republican Party has been able to sort of dangle judges and abortion restrictions in an incremental fashion. But I don`t think most people expected that they would get everything that they wanted so fast. And that`s one of the reasons that you`re seeing this kind of boggling. How can you galvanize the average person truthfully? But maybe you can galvanize them with misinformation. But, how can you galvanize them when you can already point to the reality in the states where abortion is illegal?\par \par \uc2 Now, previously, the Republican Party kind of split the difference by saying that it was abortion rights that were actually creating extreme situations on the ground. You remember Trump in his debate with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  talking about babies being ripped from the wolf. But the true extreme reality right now is the state in which abortion has been banned and children who have been raised are moving across state lines to get abortion. Women who are having miscarriages are being forced to bleed out. People are being forced to take matters into their own hands, at great risk to themselves. This is an extreme reality that a new headline every day or every week has confronted Americans.\par \par \uc2 The old Republican talking point that says, you know, but it`s --that`s extreme, which if you listen to the groups, they`re saying, OK, just keep saying that. I think it`s going to be harder to sell. And yeah, they`ll always be people who show up and they want a national ban, but is that enough to galvanize enough people, really, to deliver enough people when they`ve already got their majority on the Supreme Court. They`ve already got Roe. The dogs off the car. And the reality is that millions of people are being impacted today in a way that is probably much more extreme than most Americans thought when they all heard this, talk about speech right.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right. Michele, you have more than a dozen states that have some sort of ban on abortion right now. It`s unlikely Congress can pass a law similar to Roe. I wonder how you see the legal battles progressing in the states. Given, as Amna said at the beginning, that really so much of the attention has been on the legislative front.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:12]\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN\uc2  : So, let`s talk about that. And let`s also set the ground a little bit. Because I think it`s really important to build on what Irin was just mentioning. So, when we think about misinformation, for example, as you would ask before, right, the Supreme Court has said as recently as 2016 in a case called Whole Woman`s Health v. Hellerstedt, that a woman is 14 times more likely to die by carrying a pregnancy to term than by having an abortion. The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  compares the safety to abortion, to that of a penicillin shot. We know that the United States ranks 55th in the world in terms of maternal mortality. It`s a deadly place to actually carry a pregnancy to term in the United States. And that level setting is really important when we then begin to think about the legal aftermath and repercussions from Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 Because as Irin was saying, what we see across the country in states that have banned abortion is that there are women who are needing to bleed out before their doctors feel that they can legally come to the aid of those patients. We have seen a girl have to escape Ohio to get to Indiana in order to terminate a pregnancy. There`s a child serially raped at the age of 10. These horrors are playing out in States, but at the same time what we see are referenda that are pushing forward, that are instantiating reproductive freedom in the state`s constitutions. And in other states, they are rejecting efforts to deny reproductive health care in their state`s constitutions.\par \par \uc2 And that is causing a bit of an uproar within the Republican Party. And, in fact, efforts to try to suppress even the opportunity to put these things, these measures on the ballot. Because what they see quite clearly is that Americans, including Republican women, do not support the agenda that`s been pushed to deny abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 And let`s be clear here that prior to November, when people were worried about whether there would be the Republican tsunami, I kept thinking what mother sits across the table looking at her 10-year-old daughter and thinks, I`m going to allow a state legislator that I don`t even know demand that my 10-year-old, if she`s raped, carry a pregnancy to term and become a mother at the age of 10 or 11? I think that they have truly miscalculated here.\par \par \uc2 And one last point too that I think is really important is that this has not historically been the Republican agenda. Roe v. Wade was a seven to two opinion. Five of those seven justices were Republican appointed. The justice that wrote the opinion in Roe v. Wade, Justice Blackmun was put on the court by \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  . And I think that level setting is also critically important. It really is. And we shouldn`t forget that in what is really a level of insanity that we see that has taken grip of the Republican Party in the United States today on this particular question. And I think it`s really quite dangerous, unfortunately, that these issues now look Democrat or Republican, given how for so long Republicans were on the side of reproductive liberty. One wouldn`t necessarily see that now reflected in the current Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: As always, so grateful for that level setting, Amna Nawaz, Irin Carmon, Michele Goodwin, thank you all so much for getting us started.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, what we are learning from week two of the Proud Boys seditious conspiracy trial, \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2  and a reporter who has been in the courtroom, they are here to break it all down.\par \par \uc2 And later, two years in office for President Biden. Our friend Michael Beschloss is here. What we`ve seen so far and what could be next. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a busy Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:23:45]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , (R) FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Proud Boys, stand back and stand by. But I`ll tell you what? I`ll tell you what? Somebody`s got to do something about Antifa and the left, because this is not a right-wing problem. This is a left-wing.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: His own \uc2 FBI\uc2  director said --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : This is a left-wing problem.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOCLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: A judge ruled that video of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  during a 2020 debate could be used as evidence in the Proud Boys trial that is now underway. Several members of the group, including their leader \uc2 Enrique Tarrio\uc2  , are charged with seditious conspiracy over the January 6 riot. \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports prosecutors revealed new details this week about a meeting between the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers on January 5, 2021.\par \par \uc2 With us for more is Zach Montague, reporter for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  . He has been in the courtroom for the trial, and \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2  , a veteran federal prosecutor and former U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Michigan. She worked with the DOJ during the Biden transition, was a professor at the University of Michigan`s Law School.\par \par \uc2 It is good to see you both. Zach, these new details, they come from a video. What can you tell us about it?\par \par \uc2 ZACH MONTAGUE, THE NEW YORK TIMES REPORTER: Well, I should note that these new details are not ones that have been shown to the jury yet, and they won`t be. But they were interesting to us because, you know, this sort of, sheds more light on a meeting between two of the heads of the most prominent far right groups in the country one day before January 6.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:15]\par \par \uc2 And we knew that this meeting had happened. And what we hear now is that caught by a film crew that was with the Proud Boys, sort of standing far back from them was a male voice saying, this is happening. This is inevitable. Let`s do this fast and together. And we don`t know who said what to who. And the jury will not hear this either. But certainly interesting.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, let`s talk about why the jury won`t hear it. Barb, the Proud Boys defense attorney challenged the admissibility of that video clip showing this meeting, saying, it would prejudice the jury against their clients. They didn`t end up showing it to the jury. Can you talk me through how likely that is, then, to impact the trial?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE\uc2  , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Yeah. so, it`s interesting how often defense attorneys say, I object to that evidence on the grounds that it will prejudice my client. All evidence by the prosecution is intended to prejudice the client. That`s the point, trying to prove them guilty. But there is a rule of evidence that says, if evidence is substantially more prejudicial than probative, a judge should keep it out of evidence because it might simply inflame the passions of the jury as opposed to having them make a decision based on the facts.\par \par \uc2 Sounds like the prosecution, at least for now, held this exhibit out. It may be that it comes in eventually, and sometimes prosecutors are just being really cautious not to commit an evidentiary error that could result in a conviction at trial and then a reversal on appeal. So that could be going on. But I think the reason the prosecutors probably wanted to get this into evidence is just more evidence of a conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 What they have to prove for seditious conspiracy is that this was a preplanned attack to thwart the lawful transfer of presidential power and the use of force against the authority of the United States. And the more evidence they have of planning that occurs before January 6 itself, that can help build that case, as opposed to an alternative explanation, which is, you know, we just showed up. We got excited, it got a little out of hand. And people got violent. That`s different. What they have to prove that this was a preplanned attack, a conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I mean, Zach, you`re talking about multiple defendants who have different representation among their representation, different theories of how they`re going to argue the case. For you, sitting in the courtroom, what has been the feel of the past two weeks?\par \par \uc2 MONTAGUE: Well, it`s been contentious, for sure. Early a day has gone by where we haven`t heard a motion for a mistrial or severance. And a lot of this is, I think, because of some of the evidence issues that Barbara was talking about. I mean, there`s just -- there`s so much to go over in this case. This is a national organization. There`s a whole constellation of players across the country speaking to each other. The government seized phones. They have potentially thousands of texts and videos with content that may be extremely offensive in some cases or extremely violent.\par \par \uc2 This is a group of people that defense attorneys have routinely described as a drinking club or a rugby club. And these are things that a jury could certainly be swayed by. There`s just -- every day, it seems there`s a new dispute over what evidence is going to be relevant in this case, what`s going to be relevant to this conspiracy, and not just what is showing up in these group chats.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yeah, Barb. There`s so much we could talk about with this specifically, but I do want to pivot and make sure that I get you in on this other story that we`ve been following, because you have a Florida judge finding \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his lawyer nearly a million dollars for, "using the courts to seek revenge on political adversaries." Give me a sense, how big of a deal is this?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: I think it`s a very significant matter, Alicia, because judges are really quite reluctant to sanction lawyers. They want to give people the benefit of the doubt if they think that they`ve got a basis for a lawsuit, even if they ultimately fail. The court is the forum for resolving disputes. And so, sanctions are really quite rare. In this instance, to see sanctions in the amount of almost a million dollars is really quite extraordinary. And in fact, the first line of the judge`s opinion in this case are worse. The effect of this lawsuit never should have been filed.\par \par \uc2 And so, when there are sanctions like this, it is punishing a person for abusing the judicial system to serve their own purposes. And it also serves as, you know, kind of a shot across the bow, a deterrent to both this person and anybody else out there who might be considering using the court for this abusive purpose.\par \par \uc2 Number one, it clogs the courts. And so, for that reason, it`s an abuse of the system. But it also, is sometimes used in -- I believe it is in the case of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to try to give credibility to a claim to say, you know, look, in this case, defamation against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  . Not only am I saying it, I am backing that up with a lawsuit. Lawsuit has been filed on this. Well, I think the court here is saying don`t use us to carry your water. There was never any basis for a lawsuit here. Not only are we dismissing your suit, we are going to punish you for filing here and abusing the system.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Fascinating stuff. Zach Montague, your first time with us. I hope you`ll come back. \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2  , as always, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, it seems like there is a new accusation about George Santos around every corner. So, what`s it going to take for Kevin McCarthy`s party to shut him out? Our political panel digs in, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:27]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK MCKINNON, FORMER ADVISER TO \uc2 JOHN MCCAIN\uc2  & \uc2 GEORGE W. BUSH\uc2  : The thing with George Santos is that the story just gets worse and worse and it just gets more oxygen. And the bigger question now is anything that George Santos ever said true, if George Santos walks like a duck, he talks like a duck. Ladies and gentlemen, George Santos is duck.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: There are so many new allegations around congressman George Santos that NBC News has had to put together a timeline to, you know, keep it all straight. The list goes back years, so I cannot read everything. But just this week, Santos responded to allegations he raised money to save a veteran`s dying therapy dog in 2016 and then kept the money for himself. He called the accusations, "shocking and insane." Santos has apologized for lying about his background and said he has done, "nothing unethical."\par \par \uc2 Back with us tonight, Donna Edwards, former Democratic Member of Congress, now a \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  Columnist, and former Florida Republican Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Carlos, several Republicans, they have called for Santos to step down. The Wall Street journal published an op-ed called George Santos has got to go. How long can this go on?\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO\uc2  , FORMER FLORIDA CONGRESSMAN: Well, I think it`ll go on as long as George Santos has the gall to remain in Congress, because it doesn`t seem like he will be expelled unless there is some event that precipitates that and that could be some indictment. And sometimes that doesn`t even happen, sometimes politicians get indicted and they remain until there is a definitive conviction or not. So, it seems like for the time being, House Republicans are going to continue having to answer questions about George Santos, and they`re going to continue feeling the embarrassment of having to serve alongside someone who is clearly a serial liar, perhaps the biggest liar ever elected in the history of the Congress and that`s saying a lot.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Donna, I think it`s an important story, the Santos story, but I think it`s also obscuring other important things that are happening on Capitol Hill, like the House oversight Committee where Hayes Brown is calling it MAGA`s hottest club as its extreme right-wing members gear up to investigate the Biden administration. This in service of right-wing media headlines, not Americans. I just wonder, do you think voters are going to be paying attention?\par \par \uc2 DONNA EDWARDS, (D) MARYLAND, FORMER U.S. CONGRESSMAN: Well, what I do know is that Republicans will not get in the way of getting in the way. They`ve decided that they`re going to keep George Santos. They know that he`s a distraction. But you look at what`s coming up on the House Oversight Committee, and I think what worries me, what concerns me is that it`s going to -- there will be one set of questions and investigation after another. And the truth is that I think Republicans run the danger of getting in their own way on this. And of being of overreaching so much that the American public won`t be able tolerate it. And we`ll get, frankly, quite confused. And then, as long as George Santos is part of the story, Republicans will look like they`ll continue to defend a liar.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: You know, Carlos, the three of us are old enough to remember when the House Ways and Means Committee was the hottest ticket in town. It is wild to me that Oversight, which has been described by others as a place like used to send people that you didn`t have much use for, is now where everybody wants to because it`s the opportunity to create the biggest media moments for their ecosystem. I mean, I just wonder if you have the most radical members of Oversight, causing it to lose sight of the Committee`s actual purpose, what the ramifications are of that, and what it tells you that people want to be on this committee, not necessarily in order to get things done, but in order to make a big show. Instead of being on the committees where they get to decide things like how to spend money and resources?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: That`s right, Alicia, I think that you`ve made a great observation, and it`s evidence of how performative politics have become. People don`t want to actually hold the executive branch accountable, which is the purpose of the Oversight Committee. They want to get on television. They want clicks. They want more followers on social media. That`s why they want to get on the Oversight Committee. When I was elected to Congress in 2014, that isn`t the committee people wanted. You`re right, people wanted to be on Ways and Means. I did. I finally got on my second term. People wanted to be on appropriations. People wanted to be on Energy and Commerce. Now, it`s oversight. Why? Because you can get on television. Because you can get more social media followers. Because you can raise more money, because you can make people more angry and more paranoid for your own good.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:14]\par \par \uc2 And it`s a shame because oversight is important. And, look, no matter whether you`re Republican, Democrat, or somewhere in between, I think we should all agree that Congress has an important oversight rule, no matter who`s in the White House. And certainly, this administration should be subject to scrutiny just like any other. But when you diminish this committee, when you have people on the committee who are just trying to draw attention to themselves, it`s very difficult to do that work.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Maybe we just need to teach all these people that they could just do TikTok dances if what they really wanted were more followers that they could do away with focusing so much on the demise of our democracy. Donna, I want to ask you today to your mark of Biden`s presidency, what do you think of his performance so far?\par \par \uc2 EDWARDS: Well, I think President Biden has a lot to celebrate. I mean, the first two years is probably about the most productive years that we`ve had in, you know, maybe four or five decades, four decades. A lot of productivity, a lot of legislation path. I think that the administration is coming up on a point where because of the divided Congress, they`re going to focus on implementation, on rulemaking, on really putting into play some of the things that they`ve accomplished over the last two years. And you can do the, you know, run down, the Inflation Reduction Act, CHIPS, making sure that we`re really investing in our infrastructure. Gun reform legislation that`s into law that hasn`t been passed in 30 years. These are really amazing accomplishments in an environment where the administration, where the President took over in the midst of COVID when were at our darkest hour and has seen a recovery in the economy.\par \par \uc2 Still some challenges ahead for the President, but, you know, he has a lot to be proud of over these last few years. And we`ll just see whether Republicans who control the House are going to want to get some things done for the next two years or they`re going to spend their time investigating.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: That is, of course, among the big questions. Donna Edwards, \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2  , thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, what is Biden in store for the rest of his presidency? Michael Beschloss is here when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:19]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: This is our historic moment of crisis and challenge and unity is the path forward. The pandemic was raging. Our economy was reeling. But we acted together. Now, two years in is clearer than ever that our plans work, and we`re building the economy from the bottom up and the middle out, not just the top down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: President Biden marked two years in office today by celebrating his accomplishments and reflecting on the crisis our nation has faced during that time. In the second half of his term, the President will face a new set of challenges, but this time with a Republican controlled House.\par \par \uc2 With me to discuss, celebrated author and presidential historian Michael Beschloss, his latest work in a bookshelf full of works is Presidents of War. Michael, it is always so good to see you. So much has changed --\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BESCHLOSS, NBC NEWS PRESIDENTIAL HISTORIAN: Thank you, Alicia. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Much has changed since President Biden took office, just short two years. What stands out to you about that period of time?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: Well, he`s gotten a lot of very important laws passed, maybe more important than any president in half a century, or at least back to Lyndon Johnson, even more than that. But I think the main thing I would say, Alicia, is and forgive me, this is what historians do, you know, let`s go back to two years ago last night, October 19, 2021, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was still president, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has yet to come in. Remember what that was like. That was 13 days after the insurrection and attack on Congress that we now know could have led to martial law and confiscation of voting machines, violence, assassinations of leaders of Congress. That`s what we had two years ago.\par \par \uc2 We also had a terrible pandemic with a president who was making governors in the United States beg for help and who was talking on television about injecting bleach as a way of overcoming it. And at the same time, presidential leadership that was erratic and egotistical, autocratic, a president who went before the United Nations, as you might recall, and was laughed at, and also our rule of law was in jeopardy. That was two years ago.\par \par \uc2 And what I would say tonight is something that Franklin Roosevelt said to Americans on the radio in 1934 and \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  did in 1980 on television asked Americans, so if I might paraphrase, if I could ask all of our friends who are watching, you know, are you better off than you were two years ago? My answer would be this has not been a perfect presidency. And I`ve studied almost all of them. I`ve never seen a perfect presidency.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:00]\par \par \uc2 But let`s look at the main things. Presidential leadership under Biden has been more steady. Policies, much more mainstream, much more shared by a large number of Americans than they were two years before. Rule of Science, this is a president who believes in science, not things like injecting bleach. We are safer from disease and most important, our rule of law has been restored. Our democracy is safer. As we look at it tonight, our elections are less in danger and the future of democracy is a lot brighter than it was two years ago. That`s not all of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing, but it has made a very big difference that he has been there.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: It strikes me, Michael, that we in the press corps are constantly talking about 2024 and when the President will make a decision and pushing forward two years into the future, when today is an opportunity for people like us, for the President himself to reflect back on the two years of his presidency. I know that you have advised him in the past, your sense of how he is thinking, how he is reflecting on this chapter?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: Well, I have talked to him in the past and I always preface it by saying I`m not here to give advice, which would be pathetic. You know, adapt problems, sitting in my room writing books, so all I can do is look at parallels in the past. But I think one thing he`s got to deal with, just as you`ve been talking about much of this hour and I`ve really enjoyed the conversations that I`ve been watching between you and the others and that is he`s going to deal with a Republican House that by most indications is going to be a free show. And I`m not just talking about George Santos. We`ve never seen a situation in all of American history where a speaker is in a situation that`s almost like the Gong Show. One Republican member of Congress can say I don`t like the speaker, let`s have a vote.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`re going to have something that is much more like parliaments in other countries where we may see leaders changing all the time, very unstable, something we`re not accustomed to. And I think if that is something that makes us nervous, I would say two things. Number one, if the Republicans had gotten a 48-vote majority in the House as someone who -- some were predicting this would be something where we would be going down a roller coaster that would be, you know, very treacherous and very unsteady. But got a Democratic Senate, President of the United States with 50 years. He has had an experience in democracy back to when he was a young senator. So, the result is going to be I think that we may see a year that looks much more like 90, 95 when the Republicans took Congress by a huge majority or perhaps 1947 when what \uc2 Harry Truman\uc2  called the good for nothing 80th Congress came into office.\par \par \uc2 And I think it is at least possible that people will look at the distinction between the chaos in the House versus whatever a Democratic Senate does and a Democratic president, whoever is the Democratic nominee in 2024 may be like \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in 1996 in that people will say, you know, we saw what Congress has been doing under Republicans. We didn`t like it. We need a president to make things steady.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: And those are the challenges we can anticipate, to say nothing of crises that could be on the horizon that we cannot. Michael Beschloss, as always, thank you so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Buffalo strong. The City of Good Neighbors rallying behind Damar Hamlin and his beloved Bills, ahead of a rematch with the Bengals, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:59]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN POYER, \uc2 BUFFALO BILLS\uc2  : Dee is back in the building now, you know, and it`s awesome to see his face. Awesome to see him smiling again.\par \par \uc2 MITCH MORSE, \uc2 BUFFALO BILLS\uc2  : The guy is in great spirits, and that also helps with the recovery process for all this as well.\par \par \uc2 GABE DAVIS, \uc2 BUFFALO BILLS\uc2  : To see him come back from that is a blessing from God.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The last thing before we go tonight, rematch and recovery. \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  players were overjoyed to see their injured teammate Damar Hamlin at their practice facility this week. This as they prepare for a significant rematch of sorts this weekend. NBC`s Emilie Ikeda has that story for us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It was a moment that brought the world of pro football to its knees. Star safety Damar Hamlin is suffering an on-field cardiac arrest, ending the Bill`s Bengals game in early January.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s something like we`ve never seen before.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: This weekend, those teams will meet again now in the divisional playoffs, facing off for the first time since that daunting day.\par \par \uc2 TREMAINE EDMUNDS, \uc2 BUFFALO BILLS\uc2  : It was definitely a scary time for a lot of us, but just to see him back, man, and seeing them back on a positive note, and it`s definitely a good thing to see.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: An upbeat and positive Hamlin now making regular visits to the Bills facility. Stunning signs of progress, inspiring the sideline safety`s team.\par \par \uc2 SEAN MCDERMOTT, HEAD COACH \uc2 BUFFALO BILLS\uc2  : We`re just trying to be there for him and walk at his pace, so to speak. It`s been good having him around.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Number three faces a lengthy road to recovery, according to a source close to Hamlin`s family, revealing he still requires oxygen and is having his heart monitored regularly. What that means for the possibility of a Hamlin appearance on Sunday, to be determined.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I will probably come to tears if that happens. It`s just an emotional, amazing story.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: And while the weekend`s high-stake showdowns have eight elite teams going head-to-head, Hamlin is one player everyone can get behind.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): What does number three stand for?\par \par \uc2 BYRON BROWN, MAYOR OF BUFFALO, NY: Number three stands for strength, courage, resilience. Hopefully, with the game coming up against Cincinnati, number three stands for a win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The people of Buffalo have suffered tremendous losses over the past year, from the horror mass shooting at a supermarket to a once in a lifetime deadly winter storm. So, after all that, many across the country are rallying behind Buffalo city of good neighbors this weekend.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you a good night. I`m Alicia Menendez. You can catch me every Saturday, Sunday on American Voices, starting 8:00 -- 6:00 p.m. Eastern. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC news, thank you for staying up late.\pa