honey.\par \par \uc2 REID: Sounds good. Eureka O`Hara, thank you so much. I appreciate and love you. And -- thank you.\par \par \uc2 And that is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 What the Republican electorate wants is a strong executive who utilizes and wields power over his enemies, and then destroys his enemies and makes them grovel.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: From staged arrest for phony voter fraud investigations, to blocking the study of advanced African-American history in high school. Tonight, the sinister campaign to defeat Trump with Trumpism coming straight out of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Then, the disgraced ex-president actually gets punished for actual wrongdoing. Why a major court ruling is already forcing change behavior.\par \par \uc2 Plus, new reporting on the curious crossroad of dark money. The conservative court and a massive payday for Kellyanne Conway. And why the George Santos Saga actually allows the right an opportunity to end a sick bigoted obsession.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2  , HOST, FOX NEWS: It`s a new show called drag queen story hour.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Drag queen story hours.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Police believe it was an attempt to stop a drag queen story hour.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Two months after his low-energy campaign announcement speech, it`s quite clear indisputably that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , the twice impeached disgrace ex-president is the politically weakest he`s ever been since he first declared. And he has utterly failed to clear the Republican field for 2024.\par \par \uc2 The shadow primary to take on Trump is already in full swing. You`ve got would-be candidates like former Trump U.N . Ambassador Nikki Haley, former Trump Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  , both barely hiding their obvious presidential ambitions. Then, of course, even Trump`s own vice president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  , seems like he is almost certainly gearing up to challenge his former boss next year.\par \par \uc2 Frankly, there are not many political observers who think any of these candidates have much of a shot and I`m basically in that camp. Although to be fair, two years from an election, a lot could happen. I mean, two years before the election, people didn`t even know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was going to be in the running back in 2014. You could ask all the early prognosticators who thought Jeb Bush or Rick Perry or Bobby Jindal or Scott Walker were on track to win the 2016 nomination two years out from that election.\par \par \uc2 But as it stands from this vantage point, I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is still at this moment probably the favorite to win the Republican nomination. And if it`s not Trump, the consensus pick very clearly right now at least among influential conservatives and right-wing media and donors crucially is Florida Governor Republican \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Firstly because Republicans think his massive re-election win in Florida two months ago will translate to a national victory, a premise I have to say I find dubious at best but who knows. But also because DeSantis is said to embody what many pundits have deemed either critically or provingly Trumpism without Trump. Trumpism without Trump. What does that mean exactly? Well, I guess first, we need to discuss what exactly Trumpism is.\par \par \uc2 Here`s how I would articulate it succinctly. Trumpism is a political tendency catering to the deep visceral sense that we used to run things until they came along and took that control away. That`s it. And now they run things and we need someone strong, powerful, potent, who will take the country back so we can run it and make it great again.\par \par \uc2 Now, the We`s and the They`s in this formulation are pretty clear. They slide around a little bit. The We`s on average in the main tend to be white working-class folks, particularly those rural Christian identified, although there are no means the only people who fall into this group. In fact, there are people of all races and creeds who share this feeling about this country and support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and MAGA.\par \par \uc2 And as for the They`s in contrast to the We`s, well, the list just keeps getting longer and longer. It`s actually sort of a remarkable aspect of this ideology. It`s Black folks and non-White folks in general. It`s LGBT folks and businesses that host drag brunches. It`s teachers and librarians and academics. Of course, the media, public health officials, woke billionaire -- billion dollar corporations, the so-called deep state, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , just to name a few entities of an ever-growing internally inconsistent list of enemies that Trumpism says have power and took it away from you.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:04]\par \par \uc2 And the fundamental thing that Trump promised basically from the moment he came down the golden escalator back in 2015 is I will punish those enemies on your behalf and take power away from them. Here`s the thing. He largely failed at that goal. Easy to say Trump didn`t make life miserable for lots and lots of people. He succeeded into that. For instance, just one example, the children his administration kidnapped or the countless needless deaths and illnesses that resulted from his egregiously negligent handling of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But ultimately, in the end, I mean, even the stories that Trump tells about himself, right, that he was brought down by this conspiracy, the deep state, speaks to his weakness. He was too ineffectual to properly wield power and deliver on the promise of Trumpism. He ended up leaving two years ago just yesterday. He didn`t get to stay. His coup failed. That`s the appeal of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to the Republican Party. They see someone who is delivering on that promise.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Will Republicans use power? This is my question. Will they wield power because if you -- you have a single takeaway as the result stands right now. It is that what the Republican electorate wants is a strong executive who utilizes and wields power over his enemies and then destroys his enemies and makes them grovel, makes molten salty tears flow from their faces as \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  did with \uc2 Disney\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, big feelings there, buddy. Take a breath. Now, he`s right. The quasi-fascist creed is what the Republican base wants. This is what Trumpism is all about. And yes, let`s count the ways DeSantis is turning that theory of Trumpism into reality starting with that example you just heard, his feud with \uc2 Disney\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 You see, since the 1960s, \uc2 Disney\uc2  has enjoyed special privileges in Florida, home of Disney World, which essentially gives the company full control over the part of the state that contains the theme park Walt Disney World. DeSantis is now working to remove that special status for \uc2 Disney\uc2  , a move that experts say could cost Florida taxpayers up to $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 Now, to be clear on the merits here, I mean, I don`t think it makes sense to cut \uc2 Disney\uc2  a special deal. Certainly, a discussion we had about whether or not a multi-billion-dollar company should be given that much power by a state government to begin with. But that is not why DeSantis is revoking its special status. There`s no principle here. No, he`s just mad because he thinks \uc2 Disney\uc2  , the company that makes kids movies, is too "woke" because it criticized his bigoted don`t say gay law last year that bans discussions of any LGBT issues in some Florida classrooms.\par \par \uc2 And so, he`s wielding the authority of the state government explicitly tit for tat to punish \uc2 Disney\uc2  in retaliation for the views they express contrary to him. The pure distillation of Trumpism, using his power to hurt his and your perceived enemies. And this is just the beginning, right? There`s also the disgusting stunt we`ve covered before in the show where DeSantis is desperate to push the lie the 2020 election was rife with fraud, had more than a dozen mostly black Florida residents arrested on voter fraud charges by police because DeSantis argued they voted with felony convictions on the record, even though a bipartisan coalition of Florida voters approved a resolution back in 2018 restoring voting rights to felons winning over 60 of the vote. And even though Florida election officials had allowed these individuals to register seemingly giving them the green light to vote.\par \par \uc2 But State Republicans made the rules as confusing and opaque and byzantine as possible which resulted in some people who again believed they were eligible to vote, voting and then subsequently being accused of fraud because the state determined they weren`t eligible after all.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): The State of Florida has charged and is in the process of arresting 20 individuals across the state for voter fraud. They did not get their rights restored and yet they went ahead and voted anyways. That is against the law and now they`re going to pay the price for it. This is a third degree felony in the state of Florida. They could face a $5000 fine and up to five years in prison for illegally voting in our elections.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, there he is up there. He`s got the police behind him, right? Contrast DeSantis` pathetic want to be tough guy act there with this little staged photo op, the actual people who had their lives upended for the crime of believing they were allowed to vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s two felony charges for voter fraud but they`ve reduced it to 500 bonds. So, it`s $1000 to --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, my God, man. What the --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, sir. Yes, sir. So, unfortunately, right now, we`re going to have to take you to jail.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, man. Why are they doing this to me. I didn`t do nothing to nobody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, you have a warrant. It`s for voter fraud, OK. So, I know your caught off guard but unfortunately that`s how this stuff works, OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I voted but I -- I voted but I did not commit no fraud.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, so, that`s the thing. I don`t know exactly what happened with it but you do have a warrant. That`s what it`s for.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:10:22]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, you or I may look at that and say that`s odious and despicable what he`s done. I suspect there`s a lot of people at the base to look at that as like a campaign advertisement for DeSantis. Of course, this stunt eventually fell apart or it`s in the process of falling apart because multiple charges have already been dropped including those against the man you saw being arrested a moment ago. But again, clear-cut example of Trumpism without Trump, right? DeSantis using the power of the state sending cops out, right, to arrest people, to hurt folks his base dislikes.\par \par \uc2 Much like he did earlier this week when he banned the teaching of, get this, advanced placement African-American history in Florida schools. Oh, really? Mask off moment for the entire trumped-up debate over so-called Critical Race Theory. And DeSantis understands that this is what is making him so popular among right-wing media and donors in the base, so he`s going to keep doing it.\par \par \uc2 Just today, a Florida judge ruled in favor of DeSantis saying the law prevents the Governor from being forced to reinstate a reformed prosecutor he suspended last year after the man Andrew Warren, a Democrat, said he would not prosecute Florida women trying to seek an abortion. Now, the judge in this case seemed to agree it was a blatant case of political retaliation against a public official solely because he did not share DeSantis` politics but conceded his hands were tied by the existing state law.\par \par \uc2 And honestly, we could actually probably spend the entire hour just listing all the culture war fights DeSantis has picked in order to convince his supporters he`s hurting the right people. That`s what it`s about. It`s the purest form of Trumpism. There`s no conceivable policy rationale for any of it. It doesn`t make anyone`s lies materially better which crucially you think governing would be about. It`s about us making things as bad as possible for them. And it`s why if anyone has a shot of achieving Trumpism without Trump, it is \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Last August, as I mentioned, DeSantis suspended prosecutor Andrew Warren over his pledge not to bring criminal charges against people seeking or providing abortions. Today, a judge ruled that DeSantis violated the Florida State Constitution and the First Amendment upheld the governor`s power to fire Warren to climb the order of his reinstatement.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by former Hillsborough County State Attorney Andrew Warren. Mr. Warren, it`s good to have you here. I wonder if for people that haven`t followed your story, how did you come to office and then how were you removed by the governor, and for what reason?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WARREN, FORMER STATE ATTORNEY HILLSBOROUGH COUNTY: Well, Chris thanks for having me on tonight. I was a former Federal Prosecutor and then I decided to run for State Attorney which is the local district attorney here in the 10th area to give back to my community, to be able to make a difference where my kids are growing up.\par \par \uc2 I was elected in 2016. I defeated a long-term incumbent. I was re-elected in 2020. And we had been doing exactly what the voters elected us to do. And then out of the blue, on August 4 of last year, I was forcibly removed from office by armed deputies, after the governor suspended me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And to be clear, I just want to stress this, you were elected by local folks. I mean, these District Attorney races, I mean, most of them throughout the country, people are familiar this probably with prosecutor in their own county or city, they`re local elections. People run and people who are most affected elect them, and you are the person that the people chose to elect twice. You then after the deposition and came down made a pledge about how you would wield prosecutorial discretion vis-a-vis abortion rights. Tell me what you said.\par \par \uc2 WARREN: Well, I signed on to the idea of a statement that prosecutors across the country had joined in saying where we oppose this mass rush to rollback these abortion protections at the state level. But what I also said is, we will always follow the law. We will evaluate every case on its merits, and we`ll do what`s right to promote public safety and fairness and justice in our community. And the governor interpreted that and some other things I`d say as a violation of a law, that I was somehow neglecting my duty.\par \par \uc2 But the judge very clearly said that that`s not true. He said there wasn`t a hint of misconduct and anything that I had done, and the only thing that I had done wrong is -- and it wasn`t even rocking doing -- I`ve done exactly what I said I was going to do for the voters of Hillsborough County for the people who elected me. And the reason that I was suspended was because my view of criminal justice and my mission as State Attorney didn`t fit with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  his political agenda.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I`ll read from the judge`s ruling here. The record includes no a hint of misconduct by Mr. Warren. So far as his record reflects, he was diligently and competently performing the job he was elected to perform, very much in the way he told voters he would perform it. The assertion that Mr. Warren neglected his duty or was incompetent is incorrect. This factual issue is not close.\par \par \uc2 Now, it turns out basically the role of the governor -- the governor does have this power apparently, and there isn`t a constitutional remedy as found by this judge. Is this power of the governor to suspend or remove local prosecutors, has this been used a lot before or is this a novel application?\par \par \uc2 [20:15:30]\par \par \uc2 WARREN: Well, this is a novel application in this type of instance. And let`s be clear, the judge found that the governor did not have the power to suspend me. He found that the governor violated both federal law and state law. He violated my First Amendment rights. He violated the Florida constitution. What the judge said is that the judge lacks the ability, the power to reinstate me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 WARREN: But he called on the governor to do it himself. So, now, this is the question. What type of man is \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  ? Is he a man that`s true to his word, a man that believes in the rule of law, or is he just totally full of it? He`s -- you know, he`s all talk and no action, a coward that is unwilling to do the right thing even after called out by a federal judge for having broken the law.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Do you see a larger pattern here with the way that DeSantis has wielded the tools of the state towards this? And obviously, you`re one of the individuals he`s targeted here. There seem to be a lot. And again, all of this within I guess his abilities, although the judge saying it was this was frankly not but within his actual authority. But it does seem like a troubling pattern of essentially abusing the authority that has been entrusted with.\par \par \uc2 WARREN: It absolutely is. I mean, Governor DeSantis has shown that he`s more concerned with pulling political stunts than he is with solving problems for people. And that -- it`s really dangerous. Look, people in positions of power without a moral compass are dangerous. People -- politicians who are willing to do anything to promote their own political ambitions to get a vote or applause are dangerous.\par \par \uc2 And here we have someone who is now, you know, rumored to be running for president, who wants to represent the American people. That job is about solving problems. That job is about making our lives better. That job is about helping people achieve the American dream. This governor has shown very little willingness to get his hands dirty to do that difficult work to help people achieve the American Dream. Instead, he likes to call political stunts. That`s his entire M.O.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What is the status of abortion rights in the state of Florida right now? There`s our a ban that the governor signed into law. I forget the week cut off for pregnancy is there in terms of accessing the right but there`s talk of pushing for one even more restrictive.\par \par \uc2 WARREN: Well, the Dobs decision that you alluded to before overturn the federal protections for abortion. However, states can enact their own limitations. And so, Florida law has -- the Florida Constitution protects a woman`s right to choose. Now, the governor championed a 15-week abortion limit that was passed in the last session and that law has been challenged.\par \par \uc2 The first judge who looked at it said it violated the Florida Constitution. That case is still winding its way through the Florida courts. And ultimately, they`re going to decide whether that 15-week ban is in violation of Florida law. The question is if it is, what`s going to happen? I mean, this governor has had several laws that he championed and signed into law have been held unconstitutional, and he still continues to, you know, claim victory around them and just ignore the fact that he`s been called out for breaking the law, for taking away people`s fundamental freedoms, for infringing on our rights as a people.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the governor likes to talk about the Free State of Florida, but I`ll tell you, to a lot of us, it doesn`t feel very free.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Andrew Warner in Hillsborough County, thank you very much for your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 WARREN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, the Republican war on drag performers gets more complicated. How the most famous freshman congressman has put his party in yet another bind, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, like well, every day, we have new evidence of lies in the case of Republican Congressman George Santos. Now, Santos has vehemently denied reports as well as videos and photos that two sources told NBC News show him participating in drag shows. Santos blasts these claims as quote outrageous and categorically false. But now there`s an old Wikipedia page that Politico found. Now, anyone can edit a public Wikipedia page but this stuff was all written in 2011 and the entries are from "a user named Anthony Devolder," a name Santos has used in the past.\par \par \uc2 He wrote that he "started his stage life at age 17 as a gay nightclub drag queen, bragged about winning several gay beauty pageants. In addition to the many spelling errors, the pages full of some pretty wild exaggerations about his success claiming for instance he had a role on a popular \uc2 Disney Channel\uc2  program, Hannah Montana.\par \par \uc2 Now, to be clear if this is a lie, that is to say his denial of being in drag shows it`s completely immaterial. It does not matter at all whether George Santos or Anthony Devolder, whatever he wants to call himself, ever did drag or not. In fact, I would say, maybe the only thing we`ve learned about Santos that makes him seem cool.\par \par \uc2 The situation however does shed some really interesting light on the trajectory of the modern Republican Party, a really troubling trajectory. Because you see, starting back in 2016, even before then, the story the party has been trying to sell voters in recent years is that they are moderating on LGBTQ rights. The Republican Party has said is not the anti- gay party anymore and that rebranding was basically a political necessity in the wake of the Supreme Court`s 2015 decision legalizing same-sex marriage and more importantly the huge swing in public opinion on that issue, a 71-28 issue, right?\par \par \uc2 [20:25:34]\par \par \uc2 Just over the last 20 years, support for same-sex marriage exploded from 40 to over 70 percent. And you can see exactly when that changeover really happened right around 10 years ago. Now, the Republican Party tried for a time to try to make their peace with this in their own way. For instance, in 2016, they invited the openly gay billionaire right-wing co-founder of \uc2 PayPal\uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  to speak at their convention. He was the first gay speaker to ever discuss his sexuality at the \uc2 RNC\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER THIEL\uc2  , CO-FOUNDER, PAYPAL: Of course, every American has a unique identity. I am proud to be gay. I am proud to be a Republican. But most of all, I am proud to be an American.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But of course, that acceptance and inclusivity, if you can call it that, hasn`t lasted. Now the party has regressed, really regressed and dug so far back into their hateful views that one of their most prominent influencers in the American right, Gregory, says that same-sex couples shouldn`t be able to adopt. And the right has also chosen a weird new target for their ire this time. Of all things they have settled off, of all the challenges we face, they have settled on drag shows as the focus of their latest crusade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From coast to coast, public libraries are hosting events advertised for children.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I believe it`s called drag queen story hours. It`s a PBS situation. It`s spreading all across the country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: These drag queens are telling us that they are grooming our children to be drag queens and into the homosexual life.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, HOST, FOX NEWS: The Los Angeles Zoo hosted a drag show last night for all ages.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: They`re bringing drag stars and Cindy Lauper to the White House.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER WHITE HOUSE ADVISER: They`re inviting drag queens to the people`s house as a representation of what -- of what their values are.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s all backwards, right? They`re focused on drag and all this crazy social ideology, drag brunch.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, HOST, FOX NEWS: Usually I`m dragged out of brunch after too many mimosas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Boy, that`s a funny guy. It`s not just right-wing media. Republicans and at least seven state legislators have proposed bills that would legally restrict drag performances which seem constitutionally problematic to say the least. Which brings us back to George Santos, the freshman congressman from New York was at some level supposed to be the face of a new more tolerant, multi-ethnic Republican Party. He`s young, Brazilian-American, and out gay man. Of course, the problem is it turns out he is just a just incomprehensively compulsive liar.\par \par \uc2 And while his apparent lie about performing in drag is literally the least consequential of his many lies, it makes sense he feels he has to fight that one. It is one of the only lies he`s actually cognitive on and gone to bat trying to knock down because drag has become this strange, disgusting, bigoted obsession on the right which is really sad honestly and also quite revealing about the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: For years, before she ever worked for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , Kellyanne Conway ran a polling business. I`m not sure how many employees she had, but Conway regularly appeared on cable T.V. as a Republican pollster and pundit.\par \par \uc2 After she successfully ran Trump`s campaign, became one of his senior advisors in the White House, she had to sell her polling and political consulting firm, and she made bank reportedly getting between $1-5 million for the sale. That was back in 2017.\par \par \uc2 But now, new reporting suggests the company that bought Conway`s polling business had ties to \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  . And of course the man behind the curtain who brought you our current Supreme Court`s sixth member right-wing supermajority. He helped \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  get through his contentious confirmation hearings back in 1991. And has helped pick the Supreme Court justices for every Republican president ever since including the three that Trump got on the bench.\par \par \uc2 Leo met with Trump and his advisers after the election in 2016. It was no secret he was helping them with their judges too, some would say even just running their entitle judge vetting process.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to reporting from Heidi Przybyla at Politico, it also seems that "\uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  appears to have helped facilitate the sale of Conway`s polling company, as she was playing a key role in advocating for Leo`s hand pick list of Supreme Court candidates."\par \par \uc2 Three days after Politico asked about the apparent connection between the sale and one of Leo`s many dark money funds with millions of dollars from undisclosed donors. That particular fund was dissolved, just like that, proof.\par \par \uc2 Heidi Przybyla, the National Investigative Correspondent of Politico who broke the story joins me now. Heidi, great to have you on, it was a great piece of reporting.\par \par \uc2 First, just walk us through the sale itself. Why Kellyanne Conway had to sell and who the buyer was.\par \par \uc2 HEIDI PRZYBYLA, NATIONAL INVESTIGATIVE CORRESPONDENT, POLITICO: Yes, Chris, White House officials are not allowed by law to accept gifts of any kind, even small gifts.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:02]\par \par \uc2 What we`re having here is a two-month investigation by Politico that found that the man \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  , who really is responsible, as you said in your intro for helping Trump fill the Supreme Court appears to have facilitated a pretty big gift at a very important time, at a time when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  new president really didn`t know who he was going to pick on this court from this list that Leo had devised.\par \par \uc2 It was helping the person Kellyanne Conway to sell her company, the person who was helping to advance those picks within the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 Chris, she needed to sell this company because she was starting to get heat from ethics officials on the Hill from the Oversight Committee asking questions, why are you still owning a private company at a time when you`re working as a federal servant in the Oval Office as the person in Trump`s ear advising on Supreme Court picks?\par \par \uc2 Now, Chris, it appears this company no longer even exists. I couldn`t find any documentation that it continues to exist. And the individuals who are involved in facilitating the sale the BH Fund, which is one of Leonard Leo`s many dark money groups, they never disputed my reporting.\par \par \uc2 And that had me go back and look at this latest revelation, Chris, which I made. When I went back and looked at my records and found that within three days of my first approach to all of the individuals who are involved in this transaction, that fund was dissolved, they filed paperwork to dissolve it.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is a fund that was used for years, Chris since 2016, to move hundreds of millions of dollars around to advance a number of Trump`s Supreme Courts. So, that raised a lot of questions as to why instead of answering my questions, disputing my report, they went and dissolved that fund, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, these -- I mean, the campaign finance jurisprudence of the Supreme Court which again, I`ll helpfully put there by \uc2 Leonard Leo\uc2  , it`s a neat little circle here.\par \par \uc2 In citizens united in cases after has created these huge pools of dark money that are very inaccessible, and they don`t have to like tell us a lot.\par \par \uc2 When you say facilitated, like she sold -- did she sell the polling firm to one of the funds Leo controls? Was he just helping her find a buyer? Like, how did it work?\par \par \uc2 PRZYBYLA: And Chris, that is what is so difficult about dark money is that it`s so hard to track down. So, in this case, BH Fund filed a lien simultaneously almost within five minutes of a company called CRC Advisors. CRC Advisors is publicly the company that bought Kellyanne`s company, but because those liens were filed by the same lawyer in the same bank within five minutes of each other in the state of Virginia, it kind of raised my suspicions a bit and so that`s when I went to everyone who was involved in that transaction.\par \par \uc2 No one would dispute that that is exactly what happened, which is that the BH Fund had put up collateral. It looked like to facilitate the multimillion dollar purchase of this polling company, which Kellyanne was arguably the intelligence behind that company. She was the asset, the main asset of that company.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s not only questions about receiving the gift, but whether that company was worth multimillion dollar payout, given that its principal was no longer working for that company.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. So, you start a company that`s basically Kellyanne Conway Inc., then you Kellyanne Conway go work the federal government for the White House, you can`t own the company anymore. But you`re the talent in the firm, you then have a huge seven-figure cash out because someone wants to buy the vestigial husk of your polling firm, which doesn`t include you the person who`s the value at the center of the enterprise. And the collateral post of the transaction is the dude who`s running all these dark money operations trying to lobby to get a Supreme Court pick to you Kellyanne Conway who`s in the president`s ear.\par \par \uc2 PRZYBYLA: And this is all a little confusing, I`ll admit, because I spent two months hunting it down, but at its essence is really about this person who had an outsized influence over the court paying a huge favor to the person who really helped shape the decisions that the president was making at that time.\par \par \uc2 Now, you may ask yourself now, Chris. Well, of course, he was going to choose a conservative, what difference does this make but that wasn`t necessarily true. If you remember, when Trump came in, it was the impression that he really had no fixed beliefs. And that seat had been held open by \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  . And there were also some individuals including \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  , who went to the Oval Office and said, look, you need to heal the nation here by putting \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  back in the position that he was supposed to be at now, that might be long, you know, far-fetched that he was actually going to do that.\par \par \uc2 But the point is, she had a lot of influence over him.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Heidi Przybyla, some great digging, it is very hard to track this stuff down because of the byzantine nature. So, thank you for coming on tonight.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:07]\par \par \uc2 PRZYBYLA: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, what made the federal judge called \uc2 Don Trump\uc2  senior "the mastermind of strategic abuse of the judicial process"? The latest legal embarrassment for the disgraced ex-president next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A rare thing happened today, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  faced accountability in a court of law.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:01]\par \par \uc2 Last March you see, Trump and his lawyers filed this like truly ludicrous lawsuit against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  , \uc2 James Comey\uc2  , a slew of other names made famous in the MAGA verse after the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 There was some coverage of it at the time the suit claimed that Clinton, Comey and others had orchestrated and "malicious conspiracy the Trump campaign colluded with Russia".\par \par \uc2 A U.S. District Court judge dismissed that lawsuit back in September, writing that Trump was "seeking to flaunt a 200 page political manifesto outlining his grievances against those that have opposed him. And this court is not the appropriate forum."\par \par \uc2 The judge went on to slam the ridiculous legal theories and "glaring structural deficiencies of Trump`s argument".\par \par \uc2 In other words, it had no business in court whatsoever. The next day, the very next day, Trump lawyer Alina Habba decided to take the case to the MAGA court of last resort, \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALINA HABBA, DONALD TRUMP LAWYER: When you have a Clinton judges, we did hear Judge Middlebrooks who I had asked to recuse himself but insisted that he didn`t need to, he was going to be impartial.\par \par \uc2 And then, he proceeds to write a 65-page scathing order where he basically ignored every factual basis, which was backed up by indictments, by investigations, the Mueller report, etcetera, etcetera, etcetera, not to mention Durham, and all the testimony we heard there, we get dismissed.\par \par \uc2 Not only do we get dismissed, he says that this is not the proper place for recourse for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . He has no legal ramifications. Well, what is the proper place for him?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, that interview did not