ial of three members and one associate of the right wing extremist group, the Oath Keepers that the second seditious conspiracy trial for Oath Keepers. Also, a separate D.C. jury also heard evidence today in the sixth day of the seditious conspiracy trial of the leaders of another right wing extremist group, the Proud Boys and yet, another jury today heard directly from this man Richard Bigo Barnett, the guy who was infamously photographed with his feet on then Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s desk. He took the stand in his own defense and told jurors he had been looking for a bathroom when he was swept into Pelosi`s office by other rioters and that when he put his feet on Pelosi`s desk, he was "just kind of going with the flow at this point."\par \par \uc2 According to the DOJ, over 480 people have pleaded guilty to a variety of federal charges, at least 40 people have been convicted at trial. And as District Court Federal Judge Amy Berman Jackson said while sentencing one Capitol rioter, "The judiciary has to make it clear it is not patriotism. It is not standing up for America to stand up for one man who knows full well that he lost instead of the Constitution he was trying to subvert".\par \par \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2  served as a U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Michigan, \uc2 Maya Wiley\uc2  is a civil rights attorney and former Assistant U.S. Attorney. And they both join me now.\par \par \uc2 Barbara, let me start with you as someone who ran U.S. Attorney`s Office, this has been described as the most the largest case the Department of Justice has ever pursued with, you know, a thousand different defendants.\par \par \uc2 It`s striking to me both the scale of it also that it`s two years in now, and there still seems like there`s a lot more to go.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE\uc2  , MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: There certainly does. You know, I think one of the things that public doesn`t always appreciate is how prosecutors need to juggle and keep all the balls in the air.\par \par \uc2 A case like this is really enormous from its -- from a case management perspective. And also, while you`re prosecuting one type of case, it means you`re not prosecuting other types of cases.\par \par \uc2 So, this is no doubt taking on an awful lot of resources. But now that the Special Counsel is the one looking at the people who are not present at the Capitol on January 6th, which is how I think they have separated those cases out, people at the Capitol are being handled out of the U.S. Attorney`s Office in the District of Columbia. That`s a big project, as you said, almost a thousand people there. And the special counsel is focusing on people who are not at the capitol that day.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:13]\par \par \uc2 So, that`s really where I think the action is at this point in terms of remaining charges, whether it`s President Trump or others close to him. And that`s in the hands of a group that can focus singularly on that issue.\par \par \uc2 And so, one would hope that that can help move things along more quickly.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Some of the details that emerge in these trials Maya are pretty interesting. And just sort of snapshots of where these people were at and what their beliefs are. This individual Bigo Barnett who was the one who was on the feet on the desk, these are his own words on January 6th, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How did you get it?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD BIGO BARNETT, DEFENDANT IN CAPITOL RIOT CASE: I didn`t steal it. I bled on it, because they were -- macing me and I couldn`t -- see. And so, I figure, well, I`m in her office, I got blood in her office, I put a quarter on her desk even though she (INAUDIBLE) and I left her a note on her desk that says Nancy, Bigo is here you --.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He`s got a pretty tough hill to climb in terms of defense. And he sounds much more apologetic on the stand in his trial understandably, given what`s facing him, but I think it`s likely he`s going to face some real consequences.\par \par \uc2 MAYA WILEY\uc2  , NBC NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, it`s very hard to listen to his testimony and story and match it up with his behavior and think that a jury is going to say, oh, yes, you`re fine. You just got swept up in the moment.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he sounds and talks like someone who was quite intentional, knew why he was there and was happy to storm that office.\par \par \uc2 And as we know from that day, you said it earlier, Chris, we`re talking about people who felt called and instructed to be there, including storming the Capitol, because \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  wasn`t doing what he was supposed to do, because people like \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  were deemed the enemy, thanks to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a ton of evidence we know even just from the January 6 committee, in addition to what we`ve heard in these trials, so I do think that it`s extremely important that we see people like this actually having to face the music for what they`ve done. And I do think it`s important to deterrence to others.\par \par \uc2 And as you say, it`s amazing how people start singing a different tone when they`re looking at a possible jail cell.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and, you know, this gets to a deeper question here about what punishment is for, I`d love to hear you both speak about this.\par \par \uc2 Barbara, I`ll start with you. I mean, it was interesting to see, so Trump gets sanction, right? For this ludicrous lawsuit, a judge says like, no, we`re not -- we`re not doing this. Pay a million dollars, he drops his other ludicrous lawsuit the next day.\par \par \uc2 Now, the U.S. is the most punitive democracy on Earth, we have more people in prison who do more longer sentences than anywhere else. We`re a complete outlier, right?\par \par \uc2 One of the points of punishment is to deter, right? To change the behavior of other people that might say try to form a coup. And it just seems like that`s still out there hanging out, right? Like, the guy is going to run for president, the key deterrent effect remain. Barbara?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yes, you`re absolutely right, Chris, you know, the theories of punishment. I`ve been teaching this in my criminal law class --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We might have lost the shot entirely.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Are you able to hear me?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, now I can hear you. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Oh, I`m sorry. Chris, as I was just saying, I`ve been teaching this in my criminal law class just this week about why we punish people. Sometimes it is to punish them for retribution. Sometimes it is to protect the public from someone who`s dangerous. Sometimes it`s for rehabilitation.\par \par \uc2 But a really important reason is deterrence, to deter this person from engaging in that kind of again, but even more importantly, it`s from deterring everybody else who`s watching and paying attention.\par \par \uc2 Is this OK if I do this thing, I can get away with this thing? Maybe I`ll try again. And so, when you hold people accountable, it sends a message to that person, and to everybody else that this is not tolerable in society, that is not just simply a civil penalty, but it brings with it the moral condemnation of society that you have committed a serious affront to the rest of us who work together under a certain set of rules.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And Maya, that`s really what I`m looking for as a deterrence above all else, right. The other stuff, whatever. But making sure this doesn`t happen, again to me is the key priority.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: Oh, it`s a huge priority. It`s also a priority for saying and sending a message that our political leaders will be held accountable if they`re inciting violence and others because they have a tremendous power and platform to actually make all of us a lot less safe in addition to our democracy.\par \par \uc2 But I just want to go back to one point, Chris, about the system, because it is historic and traditional our system that if you`re low income, you`re a poor person, if you`re black or Latino, you`re likely to face jail, even for nonviolent offenses.\par \par \uc2 If you`re wealthy, white collar crime, you don`t. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is wealthy, he`s privileged. This is something we face for a long time in our system, and we must change and we must change it now.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:14]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s the ultimate example of that on display right now. \uc2 Barbara McQuade\uc2  , \uc2 Maya Wiley\uc2  , thank you both.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN this week, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230121ej1k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for January 13, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Jonathan Allen, Rehema Ellis \par \uc2 8,424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 YOLANDA RENEE KING, CIVIL RIGHTS LEADER \uc2 MARTIN LUTHER KING\uc2  JR.`S GRANDDAUGHTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Yolanda Renee King gets tonight`s "LAST WORD", I`ll see you tomorrow morning on Velshi starting at 8 a.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. THE 11TH HOUR with my great friend Stephanie Ruhle begins right now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the budget battle brewing in Washington after House Republicans use their first week to score political points and not govern.\par \par \uc2 Then, the Trump Org. hit with a maximum $1.6 million fine for a tax scheme days after its CFO was sentenced to jail at Rikers Island. So, what now?\par \par \uc2 And from a wild week in Congress, including multiple calls for a Congressman to resign to the return of the Golden Globes. Our Friday nightcap is here on all of it as the THE 11TH HOUR gets underway right now.\par \par \uc2 Good Friday evening, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. New signs tonight that Republicans running the House maybe setting the stage for the first major showdown in the new Congress. One that could have a huge impact on our economy. Today, Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  sounded the alarm telling Congress, the United States is set to reach its debt limit next Thursday. Yellen said she would have to start taking extraordinary measures to keep paying the nation`s bills if lawmakers do not raise that limit. She added if nothing gets done, the United States could go into default by early June.\par \par \uc2 The Washington Post reporting House Republicans are preparing for an emergency plan for breaching the limit. The Post also reports the proposal would direct Treasury to prioritize certain payments if the U.S. hits the ceiling. The plan is still under discussion and private.\par \par \uc2 Today, the White House said this about negotiating over the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We will not be doing any negotiation over the debt ceiling. There`s been a bipartisan cooperation when it comes to lifting the debt ceiling. And that`s how it should be. This is not political gamesmanship, and we are -- this should be done without conditions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Meanwhile, the House Judiciary Committee is now investigating classified documents that were found in Biden`s former office and his home. Today, Chairman Jim Jordan demanded information from the Justice Department, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the White House. Biden has hired former Obama White House Counsel Bob Bauer to represent him in this documents case.\par \par \uc2 And as all of this is going on, the President is also planning to make his State of the Union Address before a GOP led House. He will be giving that speech on February 7.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel. It`s one of my favs, \uc2 Jon Allen\uc2  , Senior National Politics Reporter for NBC News, Professor Melissa Murray of NYU Law School, she was a law clerk for \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  on the federal bench before her nomination to the Supreme Court. And Brendan Buck is here, former Chief Communications Adviser for both Speaker \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  . And he is now an MSNBC Political Analyst.\par \par \uc2 Brendon, we have had you working overtime over the last two weeks. So of course, at the end of those two weeks, you`re going to have to give Kevin McCarthy a bit of a report card. How did this week go?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, you know, he got he got there eventually. So, you know, that`s -- he got to be feel good about that. Look, the first week of Congress, you`re not expected to do a whole lot. And so, I`m not particularly concerned about a lack of productivity. I guess what concerns me about the house this week was that they brought up the bill about abortion, and I got to look back at this election and just kind of wonder what the heck they`re thinking. Because clearly, the issue of abortion was the thing that kept the House, you know, from having a big night. And instead of coming out and talking about the economy or gas prices, the first thing they did was talk about abortion. And so, not learning lessons very well, doesn`t bode very well. But it shows you that this House is going to be driven by playing to the base, by playing to the far right. But that`s kind of be to be expected. But the big the big fights are to come, the debt limit that you mentioned, I think Kevin McCarthy right now is really just trying to build up some political capital with his own members for when he has to eventually cut a deal because he`s going to need it later.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, they could talk about gas prices, but of course, they`re lower than they were a year ago. And they could also talk about inflation. But according to the last CPI report, while things are still expensive. Inflation is starting to slow.\par \par \uc2 Jon, let`s talk about this debt ceiling. This showdown, we keep hearing, we`re going to head for and it`s going to be a disaster. We have seen this dance before. Is this time different? How worried should we be?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN ALLEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICS REPORTER: I was going to say I feel like I was talking to Brendan Buck about this exact issue a decade ago, you know, just replace some of the players, move on up from Vice President to President move some of the members of Congress up a rung or two.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:09]\par \par \uc2 Look, the hostage shaking that`s going on as far as Kevin McCarthy`s speakership that we saw over the course last couple of weeks, we`re going to see that again on the debt limit. The difference is Kevin McCarthy, if he really wants to can get 218 votes in the House using Democrats were willing to raise the debt ceiling. The problem for him is, if he does that, he runs the risk of the fringe in his party, vacating the chair in the House. And so, he`s going to have to navigate a tightrope.\par \par \uc2 For now, it doesn`t hurt him to have the House pass, essentially, what the messaging bill, to prioritize how the debt would be dealt with by the Treasury Department, the Senate is not going to pass that. President`s not going to sign it to Brendan`s point I think that helps them build capital, probably. But it also escalates the fight.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Brendan, what do you think Kevin McCarthy`s strategy is here?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Well, McCarthy has to show his members that he`s willing to fight for this. I mean, there will be a bunch of things that they do out throughout the year, but only a couple of them really matter. And one of them is funding the government and the other is the debt limit. And that`s coming soon.\par \par \uc2 And but to get the speakership, McCarthy had to make a bunch of promises, particularly on fiscal issues. And these are some of the ones that we didn`t really -- you didn`t see there was this, you know, this sort of handshake deal. And he effectively promised a bunch of the members that they he`s going to go to the mat, to cut spending. And he`s going to insist that the Senate come along and do the same, and that`s just not realistic. And so, he set expectations that they`re going to be able to get some huge budget deal for raising the debt limit.\par \par \uc2 Just not realistic, there`s no way that the Senate is going to negotiate. The White House has already made very clear, but he has to show them that he`s willing to fight. Because as Jon said, if he doesn`t show that he`s willing to fight, they`re probably going to kick him out pretty quickly. So, I don`t even know that they have a plan. I think Kevin McCarthy`s primary plan right now is to show that he`s committed to this fight, eventually, some of these moderates are probably going to have to step in and basically save him, work with Democrats and go around him. But he can`t get to that point until we allow that really to happen. Until he`s basically shown he`s ready to go to war for it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You`re going to have to tell me who those moderates are next week, because I haven`t seen them lately, Brendan. Does the White House, Jon, have a plan? When and if lawmakers can`t agree to raise the debt limit? I know they`re saying they don`t want to negotiate. But they can`t just be sitting here not sweating this thing out?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Well, for most of the last decade, Democrats have been thinking about how to avert an absolute economic crisis if in fact, Congress plunges the nation over the cliff. We used to call the fiscal cliff, when the government shutdown and debt limit all came in at the same time.\par \par \uc2 I think the White House wave is going to be to not move and not move and not move. And then if it doesn`t happen, blame the Republicans and then figure out which executive orders or executive actions could possibly be taken. There are a number of theories out there that pretty much untested at this point. And look, if the nation is unable to pay its debts, everybody`s going to get a real rude awakening as to how the public feels about that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Melissa, let`s turn to Biden and the classified documents situation. We`re seeing a lot of headlines and hearing a lot from Republicans, cut through the noise for us, what actually matters in this case right now?\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, NYU LAW PROFESSOR: Well, obviously, this is political catnip for the Republicans. Biden excoriated former President Trump for the mishandling of documents at Mar-a-Lago, and now it seems the shoes on the other foot, although the two situations are markedly different in a number of ways. First, the volume of documents doesn`t seem to be as vast as was the case in the Trump document situation. And more importantly, the Biden administration upon discovering those documents immediately reported it to the National Archives. They did not inform the Department of Justice. Apparently, they relied on the National Archives, who informed the Department of Justice themselves. But again, that was a gap that perhaps will open them up to some criticism going forward. But we know that Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has appointed a special counsel here to investigate this. This is largely because this is an incredibly sensitive situation, and it would present a conflict of interest for the Attorney General to investigate the president who appointed him.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Jon, what are you hearing from Democrats about this, how they believe President Biden should handle this politically?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: So, number one, if a Democrat tells you they`re not worried about the politics of this, they`re lying. Everyone I`ve spoken to is concerned at the very least about the politics, they`ll sit there and tell you it`s not the same as Trump, but they understand that what this does is takes away that cordial that Biden had to beat Trump with. And that maybe primary opponents to Trump could have beaten Trump with in terms of the classified documents issue. The details don`t always matter to voters that pay that much attention.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:01]\par \par \uc2 The other thing our colleagues Peter Nicholas, Carol Lee, and Mike Memoli just reporting I think a few minutes ago that some of the discomfort the Democrats are starting to express, whether it`s folks who are involved in the public relations game, members of Congress and others who spoke to them. I mean, this is something that Biden White House is going to hear about. And if you watch \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  at the podium, there are a lot of questions you either can`t or won`t answer, and that is politically difficult for any White House.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Brendan, what`s your take care? I mean, Republicans are going to go to town with this. But let`s remember, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his documents situation, he took them, he lied about it, he wouldn`t return them. He then said they were his. He didn`t have to return them. And then he said, he mentally communicated that he declassified them all, that is markedly different than what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done here. How can Republicans turn a blind eye to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and just go gunning for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: I don`t disagree with that at all. But I also don`t think what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did should be a good standard of whether or not you did things the right way. And look, the White House -- and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  brought a lot of this on themselves. Let`s remember this timeline, they learned about this in November before the election. And we`re now just learning about it two months later. And when we first learned about the first batch of classified documents, and they acknowledged it, there was a second batch of classified documents that they already knew about as well, but didn`t disclose that at the time. And so, it just makes you wonder, like, why are they not being forthcoming? And if you`re a Republican, you see that there was a raid on - - and I understand why, but there was a raid on Mar-a-Lago and not very clear disclosure of what`s going on with this president. There are a lot of questions, but I completely agree there`s differences on how this is handled.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, but hold on, hold on, hold on a minutes.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: There`s also a lot of questions we need to answer before we exonerate him.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Before the raid or the legal search happened, former President Trump and his lawyers were asked repeatedly, please, return these documents. Will you sign off and say everything is above board and there`s nothing missing? President Biden has cooperated from the get go. So how can you say, well, there was a raid at Trump`s and there wasn`t with President Biden, President Biden turned everything back in. Trump saying, I got nothing here.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: I don`t dispute that at all. Obviously, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did not handle this correctly. I`m saying that`s not a good standard. And they`re the rush to clear \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in this and to say that he didn`t, you know, this is no big deal. We don`t know enough yet about what happened and how this happened and why they weren`t disclosing and why they weren`t coming forward with it. They are not acting as though people that are that are doing things the right way. So, we have an obligation to ask some of these questions until we get those answers.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And the special counsel in the Biden documents investigation, assuming will do that. Melissa, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I just want, again, I appreciate you pushing back, Steph, on the idea that there was a raid on Mar-a-Lago. There was a lawfully executed search warrant at Mar-a-Lago. And again, no one is defending President Biden`s conduct in the mishandling of these documents, but there is something markedly different about refusing to return them to the agency to which whom they belong. There will be a lot of questions. There`s been a special counsel appointed. This is not going to be the end of it. There certainly has been no exoneration. There`s already been talk of congressional investigations. The judiciary committee is going to be acting at the House, there`s likely to be activity from oversight. So, this President is not getting off scot-free. There will be political ramifications. But these are not the same situations.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Jon, what is the White House saying right now sort of about anything and everything as we close out the first week of Republicans controlling the House?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: I mean, there`s such a wide spread here. I mean, most of the questions that they`re getting right now are about, oh, these classified docs. And then obviously, they are trying as much as they can to point to the disorder and the disarray in the Republican House, which has been on display now since the opening of the Congress January 3, several days before there was actually a speaker.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, we`ve got a lot more weeks ahead. And this showdown is only going to get nastier in the last week. The debt ceiling should matter to every single American out there. Thank you all for starting us off, \uc2 Jon Allen\uc2  , Melissa Murray and Brendan Buck.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the Trump organization gets hit with the maximum find possible for tax fraud. Here`s the problem. It`s not that much money, and the Manhattan DA agrees. So, what can be next?\par \par \uc2 And later, from show votes in the House to the terrible week for George Santos to the Golden Globes return, our Friday nightcap is here on all of it. THE 11TH HOUR getting underway on a busy Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:19:24]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 The Trump Organization\uc2  will pay a $1.6 million fine for its multi- year tax fraud scheme. The penalty is the maximum allowed under New York law. Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  said that his investigation of Trump and his businesses is not over. So, let`s break all of it down with Pulitzer Prize Winning Investigative Reporter David Cay Johnston. He wrote The Big Cheat: How \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  fleeced America and Enriched Himself and His Family. David, I got to get your reaction to this penalty because when I heard 1.6 million, I don`t know give me a break. I would guess Trump`s legal bills are higher than that.\par \par \uc2 [23:20:02]\par \par \uc2 DAVID CAY JOHNSTON, PULITZER PRIZE-WINNING INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER: Very likely they are. The penalty is 100% of the tax basically. And so, Trump is also going to have to pay the tax that was not paid. And remember these, this is a state case, this isn`t the federal tax that was avoided. It was the state level tax, which is roughly a third to a quarter, a quarter to a third of the federal rate.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So, does that mean he`s going to be paying more for federal tax fraud? I`m just saying 1.6 million seems like no biggie. Trump is not in trouble. Trump is not in jail. He`s still a very wealthy man, and could blow his nose with 1.6. And he`s not in jail. Weisselberg is.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSTON: Right. I agree with you about that, totally. Weisselberg was charged by prosecutors who were hoping to get him to turn on Donald, and it didn`t work. He`s now going to the joint that had -- not for very long, maybe 100 days. But it didn`t work. And what matters is prosecuting Trump personally. You can pay fines, and that`s just a ding in this case, a tiny ding in your wallet. But it`s going to prison personally, that is the key to affecting people`s behavior and keeping the system as it should equal of equal justice under law.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And do you have any reason to believe justice will be served to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ? You recently wrote that convicting him of tax fraud could be a slam dunk for Bragg. That`s assuming he even gets in the game.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSTON: Right. Well, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  , the Manhattan D.A. says he`s investigating Trump again. Personally. What I did was write a piece laying out for \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  , a simple roadmap. Donald submitted during the years he was a candidate and President, 26 Business filings that showed zero revenue, but he took hundreds of 1000s of dollars in expenses. While he did this in 1984, and judges for the city of New York in the State of New York, after trials that Trump demanded said that this was civil tax fraud. In addition, his longtime lawyer and accountant Jack Mitnick essentially testified that Donald was a forger. But he wasn`t referred them for criminal prosecution. Well, Donald is on notice now from those judges back them and from a conversation he had with me in 2016, that submitting fictitious business filings is fraud.\par \par \uc2 And Alan Bragg if he just brings a case on those matters could probably very easily win a conviction. It`s not a lot of money. I don`t want to be mislead people about that. But it`s such clear-cut fraud and ordinary jurors will understand that as opposed to tax fraud trials I`ve been to where -- Stephanie, the prosecutors will go, well, when you connect sections to that, to that, to that, to that, to that, to this, to that, to that, to that, and then, and the jurors are like, what? This is plain and simple. You made up a business, you took deductions, it`s a crime, you knew it was a crime. Simple case to present might result in minimal punishment, but it will be a criminal conviction. And then Donald is clearly facing \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  in Atlanta, the Fulton County District Attorney and the case that Jack Smith, the prosecutor brought in by \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to pursue over the human intelligence and other highly sensitive documents that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not only took, but lied and denied about and refused to return as you were discussing in the previous segment.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, let`s actually take a look at what Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  said earlier today about the fines that the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  will have to pay.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALVIN BRAGG\uc2  , MANHATTAN DISTRICT ATTORNEY: We`ve been talking with our partners in our state legislature in Albany about having stiffer penalties, so that the companies that have a lot of resources can`t just price this in, is the cost of doing business. So, we`ve been pushing I said earlier today right after the sentencing, that this financial penalty is not sufficient, and that we`re working to stiffen the penalties.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But here`s the thing, if they`re working to stiffen the penalties, those penalties wouldn`t be retroactive. That`ll be for the next criminal who does this, right?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSTON: Yes, we do not have ex post facto laws in our Constitution. But Alan Bragg is exactly right about this, many of the major wrongs that have been committed, the big bank frauds that we have seen take place among the biggest banks in America. They`re only civil actions and so long as it`s only a civil action, even if it`s applied to individual executives, the company can take care of those executives. It is putting criminals in jail that alters behavior.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:05]\par \par \uc2 And in America, we have a very efficient system for Blue Collar Crime, street crime. What we don`t have is an effective system for white collar crime, which we make almost no effort to produce. In the most recent \uc2 IRS\uc2  report of the people who make over $10 million a year, and that`s about 25,000 households, the \uc2 IRS\uc2  only completed 66 audits, 66 that`s nothing. The federal government convicts fewer than 600 people a year of federal tax crimes, and primarily those are drug dealers, politicians who took bribes or business people who paid bribes. They are not looking for the kind of tax fraud \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does, where he just makes things up. And the \uc2 IRS\uc2  doesn`t have the resources to audit his books. And in Trump`s case, they had to fight -- we had to have a fight by the District Attorney in Manhattan all the way to the Supreme Court just to get access to his business records.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Only 66 audits of the mega millionaires in this country.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSTON: Completely.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Think about it.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSTON: Completely.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Completed. David Cay Johnston, always good to have you here. You make us smarter and slightly more depressed every time we see you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, House Republicans may see week one in power as a success maybe. But it wasn`t for the American voters who put them there. Our Friday night cap is here on that and what voters really want, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:31:12]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: This week, House Republicans finally got down to business after Kevin McCarthy`s chaotic speaker election, cutting funding for the \uc2 IRS\uc2  , voting on anti-abortion bills and setting up a committee to investigate the so called weaponization of the federal government. That`s what they focused on. What didn`t they focus on? What didn`t we hear about inflation, immigration, crime, health, education that things voters actually care about, the things we care about.\par \par \uc2 So, our Friday night cap is here to discuss them all, my friend and colleague NBC News Correspondent \uc2 Rehema Ellis\uc2  , New York Times Best-Selling Author Kurt Andersen. He wrote Evil Geniuses: The Unmaking of America a Recent History. Tim O`Brien, Senior Columnist for Bloomberg Opinion and MSNBC Political Analyst. And Toure, host at TheGrio. His new book is a must read, The Ivy League Counterfeiter, it is out right now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to you all. Tim, I turned to you first, week one of the GOP controlling the House. How do you think it went?\par \par \uc2 TIM O`BRIEN, BLOOMBERG OPINION SENIOR COLUMNIST: It`s a bonfire, and it`s not going to get any better. But I also don`t think, of course they`re not. And of course, they have bad intentions, but they`re doing exactly what they want to do, which is to defenestrate, the federal government, to remove it as a constructive force in American life. And this has been going on for a decade. They`ll follow that path anyway, they can go either to fund the government, they`ll throw sand into the wheels of the normal functioning of the federal government. And they`re getting there. And they`re incredibly inept. They have sensationalized racism and bigotry. They have worn this sort of anti-institutionalism on their sleeves.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not just because they`re juvenile delinquents, even though they are juvenile delinquents. It`s because what they want to do is delegitimize the government. And there are so many things that the federal government does that is just process and structure in our daily lives that we all rely on it for. And I think what we`re going to have to watch unfold now is this sort of savaging of the foundations of normative and important and rational processes that every American relies on.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So, that`s what we`re going to get for the next two years, Rehema, this destructive show?\par \par \uc2 REHEMA ELLIS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: There are a lot of people who hope not, but the reality is, reality bites, here we are. And there are so many people who I know who are in the education wheelhouse and they`re embarrassed, and they`re disappointed. And they`re sad, because there`s a lot to talk about. We ranked 22 out of 27 developed countries in education. And yet we think we`re at the top. We`re down near the bottom. Why is that happening? We just had a Nation`s Report Card come out. That said our kids are not doing well. And the pandemic devastated kids, we lost something 20 to 30 years` worth of education progress in just two years. We`re about to next year lose $124 billion that the federal government had appropriated for recovery from the pandemic, it`s going to be gone.\par \par \uc2 But experts say we`re going to need billions more in order -- and more years to bring our kids back up to where they need to be. And where are they? So just this one thing, we have in one city, one great city in America, children lost 50% in the fourth grade of math proficiency. And what does that mean? Only 3% of fourth graders in the great city of Detroit are proficient in math. And what was it before the pandemic? It was 6%. How are we going to be a great nation going forward, if I`m one of the major cities of this country, the children are so ill prepared. But here`s what people are talking about. Who lives in Detroit and the city of Detroit who`s going to those public schools? Mostly black and brown children, who have been underserved for decades, didn`t just happen. And why isn`t anybody talking about what`s happening to them? Should they be continued to be left behind?\par \par \uc2 [23:35:16]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And here we are, we`ve got the Treasury Secretary today starting to prepare us, they may have to take extraordinary measures to address this debt limit. We -- they may end up cutting social services, that`s what we`re going to end up?\par \par \uc2 KURT ANDERSEN, AUTHOR, "EVIL GENIUSES": Well, the debt ceiling thing which has become, instead of a rare way to gum up things has become their only option of this today`s Republican Party, and they`re going to play it however they play it when it comes up in November. And presumably, presumably, we`ll get through again, as we have in the last time. But it`s the only thing they have. And I think we have to -- I mean, the hopeful news is the good news is that we have a Senate that the Republicans don`t control, they have executive branch, the Republicans don`t control.\par \par \uc2 So, the House and -- is the limited, they can shout, they can investigate the government, they can try to -- they can impeach the housing, \uc2 HHS\uc2  Secretary, none of that is going to go anywhere. And meanwhile, the Senate can keep passing more confirming more judges than has been confirmed in any two-year period during the last two years, for the next two years, and get that business of government done. So, there is I mean -- as grotesque a circus, as the Republican House is going to make Congress, the damage, the actual damage beyond the debt ceiling, that they can do is limited and I don`t think will politically work for them. Yes, their small base will be, oh, yeah, go look at Jim Jordan. But as they for instance, did this week commit to massive budget cuts, which of course can only mean massive social security and Medicare cuts? There`s -- they`re on the road to further implosion of the Republican Party politically.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: See, that`s exactly what I don`t get, right? From a political strategy perspective, how does it work for them to make moves to cut Social Security, to hurt Medicare? Last I checked do senior citizens whose votes they love and Florida, they don`t want their Social Security touch.\par \par \uc2 TOURE, HOST, THEGRIO: Of course, these Republicans that we`re really talking about are living in Ruby Red Districts, right? So, they`re worried about a challenge from the right, but this is part of the second part of the insurrection, right? Now, we`re taking it into the House. They are anti-government, they want to shut it down. I would imagine if you said -- if they get said over under three shutdowns in the next few years, I`m going to bet over, right? That`s what we`re looking at. And it`s all these posturing bills that you talked about. None of these things are going to get through the Senate. We`re going to attack the \uc2 IRS\uc2  , we`re going to tack the federal government, we`re going to attack immigrants. None of these things are going to get through the Senate. So, it`s just posturing. It just stopped. They can go on Fox News, they can go back to their Ruby Red districts and say, look what we did, look, we stuck it to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , we stuck it to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  . This isn`t actually governing. This is just posturing, looking like governing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then, who are going to be the lawmakers that stand up for the things that we need. Education is the perfect example. It doesn`t always have the biggest, strongest, wealthiest lobby out there, but it needs it?\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: But you know what, it`s interesting thing, I wouldn`t have looked at one of what were some of the questions that were on the polls in the midterm election. I didn`t hear about it, but education was in the top 10. And what it