ataclysm. They`ve had to back away from it and that I think is as Kurt has pointed out, you know, once their clown rodeo starts really gunning up, they`re going to have to deal with the consequences of that.\par \par \uc2 You know, the first --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: There`s no adult in the room to pull them in, and they won`t.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, it will have to be their voters. I mean, that was the last time this happened twice when Obama was president. And each time it got up to the point where voters had to scream loudly enough. The Republicans worried about what was happening back in their home districts. It`ll have to get to that point. The first time it happened was during Eisenhower administration. And it was this reaction against Eisenhower wanting to build the National Highway System, which was a massively important infrastructure program that was good for the economy. And somehow that idea now that public spending is a public good, has gotten chewed up in Republican fanaticism.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Kurt, Republicans are very excited about the idea of investigating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on a number of things, but most specifically, this classified documents issue, how will they make the argument? Let`s investigate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , but not make the argument. Let`s investigate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSEN: Well, that`ll be hard. What they`ll say is the Department of Justice is already doing the work of investigating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . And we`re just trying to get even up the scales. Of course, what -- I mean, people have spent the day criticizing \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  for appointing a special prosecutor to look into the Biden things, but he`s trying to in his way, his earnest way, I think, trying to prevent the Republicans from going to town on just exactly that.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I always had my doubts, frankly, about the political and public desire to prosecute \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over the documents is obviously criminal as it was. And so, this will in a kind of optic political way, I think, make it even harder, if not at all, make it harder in a legal way.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what they`ll do and -- but all these investigations, Steph, I mean, what -- you know, it will have no consequence, right, there will have no consequence on politics, will have no consequence on policy. And you can say what you will about the January 3 committee, but it moved needles here and there and provided evidence to the Department of Justice as well. So, this will do nothing but rile up their base, it will not expand their margin or their electorate at all.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And a quick reminder to our audience, so one of the big differences is Donald Trump`s documents. He said he was allowed to take them. They belong to him. And then he said that he mentally declassified them without telling anyone. Those are two very clear differences.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: 13 boxes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes. Do not go anywhere. Our Friday nightcap panel is sticking around. When we come back, you know, we`re talking about him, George Santos, still a member of the House of Representatives, even if Long Island isn`t standing with him, and then return to the Golden Globes, the winds and the misses. When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:44]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Personal space, please.\par \par \uc2 REP. GEORGE SANTOS, (D) NEW YORK: A little personal space. Thank you very much guys.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why won`t you answer our questions, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Congressman, you seem to be dodging questions.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: I`ll be addressing the media soon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wait, when is soon?\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: On my time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Your time?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Good morning. Do you have any comment, sir?\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: No comment for you today, sir.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The New York Republicans are calling you a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: Pardon me, I`ve worked my entire life I`ve lived an honest life. I`ve never been accused sort of any bad doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Real-life Congressman or character from the TV show Veep. Real-life Congressman is the answer. Just days into his new job, New York Representative George Santos is facing calls for his resignation. And he`s also facing multiple investigations.\par \par \uc2 Our Friday night capsule with us, Toure, this is his second week and how can he possibly even do a job?\par \par \uc2 TOURE: I don`t know. I mean, the Republicans are willing to accept and \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  , they`re saying lying isn`t a crime. McCarthy says he`ll put them on a committee. But this to me seems to be an extension of Trump and a notion that guys you can behave like Trump, you can lie. You can be brash, you can run away from media, and the Republican caucus will still support you. Republican voters, in many cases will still support you. So, this sort of Trump virus in American politics will continue on even after 2024 if, you know, God forbid he wins, he`ll lose, right? But like that will continue in the way that other people are taking on the mantle of Trump ism. Just that way of being in politics. It`s kind of gross and scary.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Takes his performance.\par \par \uc2 TOURE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: As opposed to policy.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSEN: He`s a bad road show version of the Broadway of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in terms of trying to be the little mini-me Trump impersonator. It is but I don`t want to be the guy here who always is, but it could be worse. Republicans on Long Island, amazingly, are all getting together and standing up and saying, this guy should resign. His fellow Republican congressman, the County Republican Party out there that they all feel like no, we got to get rid of this guy. He`s bad for us or whatever they feel.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Well, and Kevin McCarthy is a narrow majority and needs every vote he can get --\par \par \uc2 ANDERSEN: He got four.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Everyone but \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  , the Republicans actually has the most power besides Republicans though, Rehema, what message does this send to the country, right, if anyone in private business or in school, did any of these things we`d be fired or expelled?\par \par \uc2 [23:50:12]\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: Educators are beside themselves because of this. Because what do we tell students, if you cheat, if you lie, you will be expelled, you are going to be kicked out of school, you`re going to be ostracized. And yet someone who is now in Congress is cheating and lying and refusing to own up to what he has done in the sense of I will leave. Because he got voted in on a lie. And he says, I`m not going to let these politicians run me out. It`s the constituents I listen to them. Well, he`s going back. He`s coming back to New York next week. He says he`s having no meetings with constituents.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: While he`s welcome to come here for an interview next week.\par \par \uc2 TOURE: As a New Yorker, to say that your parent died in 9/11 and not be true is such a deep insult to what we all went through on 9/11. It`s -- I mean, like it`s not the little like politicians often tell lies. It`s not the little, these are big, huge personality lies. It`s hard.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK. But that is one of many reasons why James Carville says leave him in office. He`s a gift to Democrats, he will be treated like a political pinata over the next couple of years.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSEN: Totally agree. Totally agree with that. He is a gift. He`s the poster boy, for every caricature of corrupt, lying, worthless, useless.\par \par \uc2 TOURE: But you`re then