eat film to black stars, but isn`t interestingly really about race. Like, what why -- what happened there? It`s at the very least ironic that this year where they`re coming back after having been canceled for a year for being too white. The snub this guy.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: They overlooked the most significant film of the year made by a person of color.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSEN: Well, and again happened to be made by a person of color, which is --\par \par \uc2 TOURE: Well, hold on they did nominate probably the best film of the year, everything everywhere all at once didn`t go for that there is no universe, right? Many parallel universes we have where banshees of its sheer it is better than everything everywhere all at once.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: It`s not a comedy.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSEN: I mean, I don`t if you`ve seen benches of insurance, yeah, there`s some dark moments, funny moments, but it was in the comedy category where it was odd.\par \par \uc2 TOURE: I think there`s 200 people who vote for this. The academy is a much larger body. I think the academy is around four or 5000 people maybe 6000. So, there`s a much larger group of people who are deciding.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: And it`s the academy members. It`s not a little group of outside.\par \par \uc2 TOURE: Yeah. What the attention that the Hollywood foreign press is getting is far outside what they deserve.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: Yeah, and the only thing I can say a shout out to the women over 50. There were four women over 50 years old, 53, 50 something 62, 64, glamorous, gorgeous, beautiful women and diversity of these women, right? I watched it and I felt way to go. I can`t say bad ass on television cam.\par \par \uc2 TOURE: You just did. You just did, it`s out there.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes, you can.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: Every single one of them and I sat and I watched clips, and I was just proud.\par \par \uc2 TOURE: You know, one of the things that I see is that there`s a lot of films out there right now and television shows where the ethos is, let`s attack the rich, right? We see this in Glass Onion and White Lotus, in The Menu and other pieces.\par \par \uc2 [23:55:03]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: In this season of White Lotus, not the last one.\par \par \uc2 TOURE: Well, we could have a longer conversation about that. But like -- you know, I was a Bernie guy, right, for a while, right? I understand that vibe. And I think that energy is in the country. But in terms of doing quality fiction, this is a cheap villain that you get, that the audience is like, yes, let`s string them up. But I`m like, have you really earned the right to say this person is a villain within your narrative construct? I think quite often no. And we can`t have villains who are people of color, right? We don`t want women to be villains unless we really earn. But this - - it`s not enough. I`m bored by because this person`s wealthy, we`re going to attack them. I need more -- in the real world, yes, let`s change the taxation and make it more fair. But in a film, I`m like, I need more than that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Toure is bored. That`s our take, right. All right, while the Golden Globes are still navigating how to be better and smarter, I had one clear, clear win, if Jennifer Coolidge gets to be on stage for any possible reason, that`s a big positive for me. Thank you all for being here, Rehema, Kurt, Tim, Toure, thank you all, so, so much.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late. I will see you at the end of Monday.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020230114ej1d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for January 11, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Marisa Kabas, Joy Reid \par \uc2 9,099 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Everything is truly fine. Not a climate dumpster fire at all. And we`re talking California, a state that has been taking action to address the climate crisis. But even that is clearly not enough. And Congress and the rest of the country needs to start taking it seriously or it`s just going to get worse the more you know.\par \par \uc2 And that`s "THE REIDOUT." ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The House will be in order.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The toxic agenda. The new MAGA House plows ahead with the same abortion agenda that was ejected even by Republican voters.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): The majority of people, men and women across my district, and I would gather probably across the state of South Carolina, didn`t agree with overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Nikema Williams\uc2  on the GOP priorities. Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  on getting kicked off committees for Kevin McCarthy. And Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  on what to expect next. Then --\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH CAIRO, CHAIRMAN, NASSAU COUNTY, NEW YORK GOP: He`s not welcome here at Republican headquarters.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER DESENA, SUPERVISOR, NORTH HEMPSTEAD, NASSAU COUNTY: Mr. Santos, haven`t you done enough harm?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He`s a national joke. He`s an international joke. But this joke has got to go.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The entire local Republican party now calling on George Santos to step down.\par \par \uc2 REP. GEORGE SANTOS (R-NY): I will not.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Will you resign now?\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: I will not. Guys, you got to give us space.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And don`t be afraid, little buddy. The woke mob is not coming for your stove.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS, CONTRIBUTOR, FOX NEWS: It`s very hard to judge when it`s just a red -- bright red skillet burning underneath to adjust it.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2  , HOST, FOX NEWS: I would counsel mass disobedience in the face of tyranny in this case.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, HOST, FOX NEWS: Nobody`s going to tell me I can`t cook with gas.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Well, the results are in from the very first election of the year 2023. You may not have seen this. The race flew under the radar a bit, but it was a real test case, a barometer of where the country is just two months after those Midterms. This was a special state senate election in Virginia`s swing Seventh District. It encompasses much of Virginia Beach and parts of Norfolk.\par \par \uc2 This was very much a winnable seat for Republicans who held the district for decades. In fact, the seventh was only up for grabs because its most recent Republican State Senator Jen Kiggans was elected to Congress this past November. So, she went to serve in Congress, vacated the seat.\par \par \uc2 And I remember -- I remember this is a state that gave Republicans their first big electoral success post-\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , right? 2021, Glenn Youngkin won the race for governor. It was the first Republican Statewide win in Virginia in more than a decade. Republicans did well down ballot too. They flipped seven seats in the State House winning back the majority. And after that election with, you know, some reason, the conventional wisdom was that the Republican party was back on track and crucially that attacking \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  , the Democratic nominee had done, was a dead end for Democrats, OK.\par \par \uc2 So, last night, this special election wasn`t just a test case for the Republican Party in Virginia, it`s also kind of a rerun of the themes and the strategies of each party in the most recent midterm elections. On the Republican side, Retired Navy Lieutenant Commander Kevin Adams ran on a very familiar fear of crime agenda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Kevin Adams is the strong leader Hampton Roads can trust.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ADAMS (R-VA), STATE SENATOR CANDIDATE: I`m Kevin Adams and I proudly served our country in the Navy and now I`m running to serve you in the State Senate. In Richmond, I will work with Governor Youngkin to get tough on violent crime.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Meanwhile, the Democrat, Virginia Beach city councilmember and former \uc2 NFL\uc2  player Aaron Rouse centered his campaign on protecting reproductive rights and Republican extremism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AARON ROUSE (D-VA), STATE SENATOR CANDIDATE: When I was in the \uc2 NFL\uc2  , my job was to be the last line of defense. Right now, that`s what we need in Richmond. Extreme Republicans are trying to take away the right to choose in Virginia, rolling back women`s freedom to make their own personal medical decisions. I`m Aaron Rouse. Women`s rights are on the line but I`ll never back down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And all this comes in the context of the Republican Governor Glenn Youngkin`s proposal for a ban on abortion after 15 weeks just introduced by Republican lawmakers today. The governor said he would go even further than that, telling a Christian lobbying group last year he would sign any anti- abortion bill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): I just want to be clear I am a pro-life governor. And as Steve said, any bill that comes to my desk, I will sign happily and gleefully in order to protect life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, most Virginia voters are not on the governor`s side on this issue. A recent poll found that 67 percent of registered voters want abortion to be legal in most or all cases though a narrow 51 majority support a 15-week ban with exceptions. We`ll see if that lasts once the bill comes up and if it does.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s the setting for last night, right? Very familiar, crime on one side, abortion on the other, a swing district recently held by a Republican. And so, last night in the Seventh District, we saw the same result. The same result we saw over and over in the midterm elections. The Democrat Aaron Rouse appears to have won that state senate seat narrowly contested with 50.4 percent of the vote in the unofficial results.\par \par \uc2 And once again, this seems like a pretty clear message to the Republican Party that their extremism, their anti-democratic, pro-MAGA, and particularly anti-abortion politics are a toxic mix for swing voters and in swing districts. And yet, just look what they`re up to in Kevin McCarthy`s MAGA Republican House today. They passed two abortion measures that were essentially anti-abortion messaging votes, basically the exact opposite of what anyone with any political or tactical sense would suggest they do. And it has definitely irked some Republican members of the House from competitive districts who understand this basic reality.\par \par \uc2 For instance, Nancy Mace of South Carolina who in fact voiced her frustrations right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:59]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACE: The majority of people, men and women, across my district, and I would gather probably across the state of South Carolina, didn`t agree with overturning Roe v. Wade. And when I talked to people, Republicans, Independents, and Democrats, this is an issue that is top of mind for folks. And you know, we`re talking about these messaging bills this week -- I`m pro-life but I have many exceptions. This is an issue that we should -- that I want to see us take seriously. And if we`re going to be serious about balancing the rights of women and protecting the right to life, I don`t think the two are mutually exclusive. This is probably not the way to start off the week.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Pennsylvania Congressman \uc2 Brian Fitzpatrick\uc2  who also represents a district that went for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  by six points agreed with Nancy Mace, again, on this network.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BRIAN FITZPATRICK\uc2  (R-PA): Nancy is a friend of mine and I agree with her. She`s right about that. If you look at the overwhelming majority of people in this country, Andrea, they believe in legality on the front of the continuum, and they believe in reasonable limits on the back end. Our policies ought to reflect the will of the people in America. And I don`t think it`s wise to be going down this path.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s not wise but it`s what you`re going to get, Mr. Fitzpatrick. Today, we got yet another indication of where public opinion stands at the onset of the new extremist MAGA Republican House after the longest and most humiliating speaker voted in over 100 years. For the first time in 18 months, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  received a net positive approval rating from The Economist-\uc2 YouGov\uc2  weekly tracking poll. 50 of Americans approve of the way the President has handled his job compared to 40 percent who disapprove. And you have to imagine those numbers have more than a little to do with the spectacle unfolding in the people`s House.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Nikema Williams\uc2  is a Democrat of Georgia. She serves as the chair of the Georgia Democratic Party. She is a member of the House Pro- Choice Caucus. And she joins me now. Congresswoman, what is your reaction to them coming out of the gate, there was defund the \uc2 IRS\uc2  , and there was a Rules package with these two anti-abortion messaging votes today?\par \par \uc2 REP. NIKEMA WILLIAMS (D-GA): I mean, Chris, you would think after wasting an entire week of Congress trying to get them to elect a speaker, that they would be ready to govern and lead our country. But in their very first week in Congress, this extreme MAGA Republican Party has shown us that they are just going to continue to attack women`s reproductive freedom. And I mean, this is a part of their commitment to ban abortion nationwide with zero exceptions. They told us this is what they wanted to do. Voters rejected them at the ballot box, but yet here they are continuing to force their extreme agenda on Americans.\par \par \uc2 And so, we as Democrats are here ready to govern, ready to make sure that we are addressing the needs of the American people, and yet they are still continuing to push these same extreme political stunts.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The -- you`re from the state of Georgia, of course, and that`s a state that saw, you know, arguably the most high-profile race of the midterm which of course was Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in a tight state. Warnock wins that twice to become Senator. And there, there was no like ambiguity, right? \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  , very staunchly anti- abortion. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  a staunch defender of abortion rights. No question about where they stood.\par \par \uc2 There does seem to be a political message that even in the state of Georgia that is you could be on the winning side of majority with that issue. What are the implications for that for the politics of this issue not just today but throughout the rest of this Congress?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I mean, we saw in Georgia, in the Deep South, we had a pro-choice pastor running in my Senator Reverend \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and he came out victorious not once but twice. Well, I guess, if you count all races, five times because he was on the ballot a lot, Chris. But this just shows us that people want to make their own personal private medical decisions. They don`t want politicians involved in these decisions.\par \par \uc2 And as Republicans continue to show us that they`re out of touch with the American people, I think we`re going to continue to see results like we saw in Virginia at the ballot box. These are races that people are going to continue to show up and reject this extremism that the current Republican Party is showing us. And so, we remind our colleagues all the time that they are temporarily in the majority in the House and elections are every two years.\par \par \uc2 As a matter of fact, Chris, we`re already in the next election cycle and we will be back in the majority governing for the people.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:27]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, I want to be precise here because these were sort of -- they were messaging bills, ones was condemnation of attacks on pro-life centers the others was about essentially a fictional procedure.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Crisis pregnancy centers, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: These are -- I wouldn`t necessarily say pro-life centers. These are thinners who literally trick women into coming in thinking they are going to get health care and they`re going to get the care that they need when they`re facing a pregnancy. But these are centers that they come in and most of them don`t even have medical professionals ready to provide care for women who are seeking care at that time.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, this was a resolution of condemnation against -- there had been some vandalism directed at some of them. They were they were condemning that. There was also this procedure that doesn`t really exist where they say you can`t kill children after they`re born which again, you`re shaking your head appropriately. But I raised this just to say this.\par \par \uc2 In the last Congress, if I`m not mistaken, one of their first bills and it might have been HR1 I think in 2017 was nationwide abortion ban at -- I forget what it was. Maybe 10 weeks, maybe 15, or six weeks. It is striking to me they didn`t go with that, right? They are hedging a little bit. Like, they -- and I -- but I don`t think that`s going to last. I mean, if you look at \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in Florida and if you look at Glenn Youngkin, they are going to face enormous pressure to move on a national abortion ban. But you`re closer to it and I wonder if you think we will see that.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Chris, we`re only in the first week and look what we`ve already gotten. So, the Congress is young with the Republicans at the helm. And these tragic decisions that they`re making, that literally impact people`s everyday lives. And we stand by that politicians should not be involved in this decision-making. Doctors they`re seeking to criminalize. In my own state of Georgia, we had a six-week abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 We just saw in South Carolina a Republican Supreme Court reject the six- week abortion ban that they had because of privacy issue. So, this is not something that is just in what they would like to consider blue states. One in four women in their life will seek abortion care. That`s someone that they know, someone they love, someone at their church, someone that they care about in their family. So, that is why democrats stand by women making personal private medical decisions and keeping politicians out of the decision of making these -- making these choices for women.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it was -- I`m sorry, it was a 20-week ban that they voted on and approved. I think one of the first bills back in 2017. It does seem that pressure will continue. It also seems that they`re going to have to at some point start to deliver tangible things. There`s all this talk about the debt ceiling, and we talked last night with a few sort of congressional observers. I guess the question is do you see them even being able to successfully move the appropriations, budgeting, and must-pass bills they have to build -- pass before we get to some debt ceiling standoff.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I mean there`s a reason that Speaker McCarthy and I use that because I don`t know who`s in charge in Congress these days after all of the concessions that he had to make to get there. But there`s a reason that there`s been this tempering of what it might look like for a government shutdown. But let`s be clear, Republicans have zero regard for the American people at home that has to suffer through their lack of governance.\par \par \uc2 Democrats stand ready to govern and lead for the American people and Republicans are playing political games and they`re paying off political debts as they work their way through this very slim non-governing majority.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congresswoman \uc2 Nikema Williams\uc2  of Georgia, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, Republicans seek revenge against the people who had been investigating them. Democratic Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  joins me on the official Republican plan to obstruct justice next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS (R-FL): Look, what we told Democrat leadership when they went down this pathway of removing members from committee is basically saying, you should not do that because if it`s good for the goose, it`s good for the gander.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As soon as House Republicans finally performed the basic task of electing a speaker, they started enacting their revenge against House Democrats, and it was long planned. After the House removed extremist Republicans \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  , \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  from their committee assignments in 2021, Kevin McCarthy promised to get even. Now, he tells Punchbowl News that, as Speaker, "he would block California Democrats Adam Schiff and \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  from spots in the House Intelligence Committee, and moved to take Minnesota Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  off the Foreign Affairs Committee.\par \par \uc2 And just to be clear about what this is actually all about, McCarthy has already fundraising off his promise to "fire the Democratic members in their committees with one reporter posting this screenshot of not one, not two, but three fundraising emails sent out in less than an hour on this topic.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of those members Kevin McCarthy plans to fire, Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  , who in the last Congress, served on the Judiciary, Homeland Security, and Intelligence Committees. Congressman, first, I guess, would you feel better or worse if he was raising a lot of money off of these solicitations?\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL (D-CA): Chris, the only person who supports this is my wife who`s like, great, you`re going to be home to change more diapers. I love it. But the truth is, Chris, it`s purely vengeance. And I just want to walk your viewers through this because this happened uh nearly 10 years ago. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  told me that someone who had been helping our campaign in a district with 35 Asian-Americans may be working with the Chinese government. I did what I hope every one of my colleagues would do which was to help the \uc2 FBI\uc2  get this person out of the country.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:24]\par \par \uc2 Now, fast forward to, Speaker Ryan learns about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  `s investigation into this individual, keeps me on the Intel Committee. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  becomes president and I become, you know, one of the leaders of the impeachment, he has access to this and releases nothing about my cooperation. It`s only now that a Trump Intel official had leaked my cooperation to suggest that there was some wrongdoing on my part, that this is even an issue.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, Chris, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  did something they`d never do. On multiple occasions, they put out a statement that I was nothing but helpful, cooperative, and never under the suspicion of wrongdoing. So, it`s purely only about vengeance. And in that fundraising email, he says, aren`t you tired of Swalwell, Schiff, and Omar`s antics? Let`s fire them. And that`s not good for the Congress. That`s not good for the committee. And that`s not good for a Republican who in two years, if you`re in the, you know, minority, if you want to set this precedent, that`s just not how this place should work.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, to your point here, right? So, there`s this espionage investigation you were notified of. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says you were cooperative with it. This happened 10 years ago. This was -- just to be clear, this is sort of the causes belly. The reason that you brought it up is that the peg that they`re hanging this on is this story. But it seems clear that -- I mean even when you listen to them, when he says it`s their antics, when Congressman Donald says what`s good for the gooses is good for the gander is that what happened was \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  did things that were really outside the bounds and norms of the normal comedy of the house.\par \par \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  had a video in which like, a version of him was murdering AOC. And there was, you know, repercussions for that. And immediately, they were looking for a kind of tit-for-tat scenario and came in looking for that. They don`t seem to be hiding that really so much.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: They`re not. And it reminds me, Chris, of when you know, back in you know, the Boehner era when McCarthy was passed up, you know, the first time he said the quiet part out loud. He said, you know, what have I done for the party? Well, we created this Benghazi Committee, and now Hillary Clinton`s poll numbers are tanking. That`s exactly what he`s doing here with the fundraising email is he`s admitting that this is only about vengeance and there`s no substantive reason, you know, to remove us.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, as Adam Schiff, myself, and Miss Omar lose our committee assignments, who will receive committee assignments? \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  , \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  , and George Santos, if that`s even his name.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Here`s the thing about this. It`s -- I think that this is entirely a sort of tit-for-tat bad faith undertaking. But to put the question back on you that you said, right? Like, I guess, the question is, is there a precedent that was set with Gosar and \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  that was a bad precedent? Or is there a way of policing this or does this become sheer will to power that this now becomes a cycle of each new majority?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: Yes, Chris, if there is a colleague on the Democratic side who advocates, for violence for physical violence to enact your political will, I will be the first person to introduce a resolution to remove them from their committee. That`s not what has happened here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s a good -- that`s a good principle.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: That`s why Gosar and \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  -- you know, I think, advocating for violence and -- you know, advocating for violence instead of voting for political will is a pretty good standard. And no one on our side meets that standard.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You are -- you served on the Intelligence Committee. I don`t know what will be happening in this Congress. So, I want to ask you about the news today about the Biden Administration that aides found a second batch of classified documents in a new location. We don`t know where that location is. Of course, the previous day there were a number of documents that have been found in the Penn Biden Center in Washington, DC apparently immediately reported to the National Archives and turned over. The case referred to a U.S. attorney in Illinois by \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  . Today, new documents. Are you concerned about this?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: Well, Chris, we have the benefit of the Trump case that there were court filings. And so, we know exactly what the documents were. You know, at least not the details, but we know the classifications. We know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was asked over and over and refused to -- you know, to cooperate. And even still today maybe refusing to cooperate.\par \par \uc2 Here, we`re relying on media reports and I`ll take them at their word, but I would like to learn a little bit more. I will say these seem like apples and oranges, but I would also like maybe in the future, for Republicans and Democrats to agree, that maybe we should find, you know, a better way to inventory, you know, classified documents so that, you know, something like this never happens again.\par \par \uc2 But if the Biden team is cooperating, that is in stark contrast to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who is litigating his way and believes that he has the right to possess, you know, these classified documents.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:07]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, in fact, I believe he`s called them my documents. Final question for you. As a member of California delegation, there is a lot of uh news about the Senate seat, the seat held by \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  . I think it`s broadly anticipated at the age of 91, she won`t run for reelection. Representative \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  has announced a bid for that seat. Your colleague Adam Schiff wildly expected to throw his hat in the ring, possibly Barbara Lee. Do you have a horse you`re backing in this race?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: Well, you know, first, I`ll just say, I think the focus of the delegation right now is on the storms at home. And I, like, Adam Schiff noted yesterday, have been on the phone, you know, all week with folks affected by that. So, maybe when we come out of these storms, I`ll consider that. I`ll just say, I -- you know, I like all my colleagues. Adam Schiff has been our chairman and I think, you know, we saw up close and personal, you know, the strength, you know, the fearlessness, and the ability to defend the Constitution when it was on the line in the first impeachment. But yes, I`m going to let these storms settle and then come back to you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think I have good odds now after that answer on where you`ll -- where you`ll end up. Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  , thank you very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: My pleasure. Yes, thanks Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, guess which freshman congressman says he was a star volleyball player at a school he never attended. Another incredible day for George Santos as New York Republicans hold an unbelievable press conference calling on him to step down. That story next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When you build something on lies and no ethics and no responsibility, there`s accountability. George Santos has no business serving in Congress. On behalf of all the board members, and frankly the 750 000 residents living in the town have said it`s time to go. It`s time to get out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, an astonishing site in Nassau County, New York in Long Island as dozens of local Republican officials and party leaders called on their fellow Republican, freshman Congressman George Santos, who was sworn in just four days ago, to resign immediately.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIRO: George Santos`s campaign last year was a campaign of deceit, lies, and fabrication.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He is a stain on the House of Representatives. He is a stain on the third congressional district.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He`s a national joke. He`s an international joke. But this joke has got to go.\par \par \uc2 DESENA: Mr. Santos, haven`t you done enough harm?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s time for you to resign, George.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They are not alone, OK. After that, at least four fellow freshmen, all Republicans from New York, are calling on George Santos to step down. You see them there. Some of them representing districts that are competitive, that Biden won, and worried about their own re-election. But in spite of all that, the man who actually does some authority over Santos, Republican House Speaker Kevin McCarthy says he will still be seating the congressman on committees, and Santos himself says he will not step aside.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: I will not.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Will you resign now?\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: I will not. Guys, you got to give us space.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Guys, give us a little space here.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: New York Republicans are calling you a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: Pardon me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What would embattled members of Congress do without the members-only elevator? While Santos keeps trying to escape reporters in the halls of Congress, the stack of investigations into -- and this is the most crucial thing -- his very strange campaign finances keep piling up. He`s already under local and federal investigation. Today, Talking Points Memo reported the \uc2 FEC\uc2  sent the Santos campaign over 20 letters notifying it of issues with its campaign filings. Also noting, his filings include line items for what appear to be personal expenses, including meals, clothing, and rent, multiple meals took place at a D.C. area casino.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a staggering amount of fantastical tales George Santos has told through the years, saying he caught a severe early case of COVID even though he was on TV four days later, or claiming to be a "proud American Jew," his parents fled Hitler, even though he`s actually Catholic and has no Jewish ancestors as far as we can tell from the records. He said he owned 13 different properties when he`s never been a landlord. Or claiming in a resume he gave to local Republican leaders back in 2020 that he worked at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  and \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  -- he did not -- and he went to \uc2 NYU\uc2  and \uc2 Baruch College\uc2  . He doesn`t have a college degree.\par \par \uc2 And here`s the thing. We found out today. It`s my favorite so far. He didn`t just claim he went to Baruch, he had a whole made-up story about his glory days in his non-existent time there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAIRO: He told me, I remember specifically, I`m into sports a little bit. That he was a star on the Baruch volleyball team and that they won the league championship. What can I tell you?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I`m joined Now by Marisa Kabas, an MSNBC Columnist, Long Island native, who has been covering her Saga. And her newsletter featured the Daily Santos available at her \uc2 Substack\uc2  . It`s great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 MARISA KABAS, MSNBC COLUMNIST: It`s great to be here, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, that was quite a spectacle this morning.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: It was something. I mean, it really made Long Island proud.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It was very Long Island. For those who don`t know Long Island, it was like all the accents, all the characters there. What do you -- what do you think prompted dislike? I mean, he was really forceful. It was not equivocating. What do you think prompted them coming out the way they did?\par \par \uc2 KABAS: I think they`re just so embarrassed. I mean, the way this whole thing has unfolded it`s just one bomb after another. And they realized they had to cut their losses and they were finally just like, we got to get rid of this guy. He is dead weight.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, because from their perspective, there`s no -- this is only bad for them, right? Like, he`s not involved in local politics. He`s in -- he`s in D.C. They look like jokers. And I do think there were some palpable con -- subtext here which is that they never fielded a particularly strong candidate in that District.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:12]\par \par \uc2 KABAS: No.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And this, what they view is a con man swept in, to win it and they did do anything.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: No, they didn`t do their due diligence at all because I don`t think they had -- they didn`t think they had a chance.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: And then --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is embarrassing for them.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: Right. And then when the redistricting dropped pretty close to the primary, they were like, oh, my goodness, we might actually have a chance in this district. And so, it now included more of Queens and Long Island. And they called on the Queen`s GOP to be like oh, do you have a guy? And they said, yes, sure, here`s this guy Santos. And that`s pretty much how it happened.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And in fact -- I mean, the sort of microgeographical story here is like, there`s kind of a chip on the shoulder, Long Island, Nassau County, generally about the city. And there was like all this shade they were throwing at the Queens.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The Queen`s Republican Party. Like, it was the fault of the Queens folks that they didn`t do it.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: Yes. I mean, there is a bit of tension and we are kind of two new districts being melded together and there`s sort of high society, you know, we`re better than that in Nassau County. It`s a very wealthy area. It`s you know, East Egg and West Egg in The Great Gatsby but that`s the district.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, you -- so, this is all high comedy. And I should note, I want to throw no shade at the proud Baruch College Bearcats who have a fine volleyball program, in fact, won the 2022 CUNYAC title over Hunter. I looked that up today. So, shout out to them, but he did not play for them. The big problem here though, of course -- and I think the Nassau folks know this is where`d the money come from, right? We still don`t know -- like This is a guy who`s making 40-50 000 a year, suddenly makes $1 million a year, loans his campaign $700,000., And weeks into this whole story, we still don`t know where that money came from.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: Yes. And that`s what initially got me interested in the first place. I mean, it`s hilarious and it`s entertaining, but I wanted to know how he got so well-funded. And so, I`ve been digging into that. And I think something that we`re going to keep an eye on is that his treasurer, Nancy Marx, is the same Treasurer as \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  and many other local campaigns as well as many other PACs and LLCs. She has about 60 under her name registered to her address. And so, she`s moving a lot of money around in between these. And it`s hard to say if that`s the source of the money, but it`s definitely something that I`m keeping my eye on.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s interesting. In fact, Nancy Marx comes up in some other reporting we saw from Talking Points Memo that people on the campaign would say like, oh, you got to talk to Nancy. Like, she was the one keeping the books and she is a known entity in this world. Presumably people are going to want to talk to her.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: Exactly, yes. I spoke to a candidate in 2020 that had her as a treasurer. And I asked him, how did you even get connected? And he said, oh, someone just said oh you want a treasurer, go to Nancy, she`ll take care of you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, now you`ve got -- I mean, you`ve got investigators now. The Nassau folks -- the other thing that`s fascinating is the -- is the House freshman Republican class, right? So, D`Esposito who`s an adjacent district, right, also Long Island District, he`s looking around. He won a Biden District that was like, plus-15 or something, right? Like, he was like one of the big upsets of the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He understands the incentives here are not -- he doesn`t want to run in 2024 as like Tweedledee and Tweedledum with George Santos in the district next door.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: Exactly. Yes, he wants to differentiate himself. And I think that being a New York Republican is a weird thing, right, because they`re not used to having any sort of power in this area. And as the district has reddened over the Trump years, they realized we should probably you know figure out how to wield our power properly. And they obviously completely stepped on it this time.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, you`ve got the New York GOP today calling for him -- the chair of the New York GOP, four freshman members, including the guy in the district next to him in the New York delegation, every Nassau County Republican. But Kevin McCarthy, I mean, I think what`s clear is he`s got a narrow majority, he can`t afford to lose the vote. He doesn`t want him to resign. And there`s no mechanism to force him to.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: Right. And some people are saying well, he got his vote for the speakership, and he got his use out of George Santos, and now he can kind of let him go. But I don`t see that happening that easily. I think he`s going to have to dragged out of there by his hair.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I don`t think he wants to go. It`s also not clear -- there`s not a good mechanism for expelling him frankly. We talked about expulsion before. But the other thing that`s very clear in the subjects of this is everyday there`s a George Santos story is not good for George Santos, not good for the New York Republican Party, not good for the Nassau County GOP, probably not good for Kevin McCarthy. But McCarthy needs that vote so badly that there`s nothing to do but hold on for the ride. That`s not true of the folks back home who want -- who do not want to face wake up tomorrow to another George Santos story.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: Exactly. And you have these two kinds of competing priorities on people.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: And McCarthy is kind of freaking out because he can`t lose that vote. But back home, they want nothing to do with this. He`s such an embarrassment. I mean, in the -- in the press conference today, they talked about how he pretended to be descendants of a Holocaust Survivor, and how that was a particularly egregious thing being from this district. I grew up there.\par \par \uc2 To explain to you, you know, people get bar and bat mitzvah when they`re 12 and 13. Every weekend in seventh and eighth grade, I had a bar and bat mitzvah to go to in growing up in Jericho, New York.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: And there were three the weekend of mine. It was like a drama. Who`s going to go to who`s? And so, that`s where George Santos is representing.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:09]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and trying to tell people I`m Jewish and sort of appropriating that, it does not work very well. Marisa Kabas, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KABAS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, Republicans are heated about their latest perceived threat to their kitchens. The return of the far-right challenge to come and take it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It is a day ending in why, so there`s another freakout going on over at Fox News. They are very, very agitated, but I`m sure this time there`s a legitimate concern or maybe not. It turns out Fox News and Republicans are up in arms because they say the government is coming to take your stoves.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, HOST, FOX NEWS: Meanwhile, in the kitchen, your gas stove may be bye-bye.\par \par \uc2 MARIA BARTIROMO\uc2  , HOST, FOX NEWS: Joe Biden`s climate change agenda now targeting your kitchen.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Biden is coming for your gas stove.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A ban on your gas stove.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, HOST, FOX NEWS: Ban gas-powered stoves.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2  , HOST, FOX NEWS: The White House is now attempting to ban all gas ovens.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Safety, safety?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And your family dinner is about to get a lot less tasty.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, HOST, FOX NEWS: America is not going to stand for this. There`s not --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A stove like this one could be banned soon.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Nobody`s going to tell me I can`t cook with gas.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, HOST, FOX NEWS: If you like to cook with gas, many people do, I do, flavor of the food if you can do it. It`s reported considering --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Texas Congressman \uc2 Ronny Jackson\uc2  writing in part, "I`ll never give up my gas stove. If maniacs and the White House come for my stove, they could pry it from my cold dead hands."\par \par \uc2 REP. RONNY JACKSON (R-TX): If you want to help me stop this, go to -- go to savethestoves.com. I got a petition up and running that we`re going to send to the Biden Administration and tell them what nonsense this is and have them stuff.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All right, there`s a website up already.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, now as a rule of thumb, anytime you see them going crazy about something on Fox News, you`ve got about an 80 percent chance it`s just outright nonsense. So before you sign on to Dr. Ronny`s stove petition, let`s take a look at what this is about.\par \par \uc2 It all started Monday when a member of the \uc2 U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  was quoted by Bloomberg News saying the following. That gas stoves are "a hidden hazard. Any options on the table, products that can`t be made safe can be banned." Now, that quote is true. He said that. And the first part of that statement is absolutely true. This is something we`ve been learning over the past few years. More and more studies and more and more data is accruing that shows that gas stoves really can be dangerous, that they`re contributing to a variety of health problems including crucially asthma in kids.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the very next line in that same article says, "natural gas stoves which are used in about 40 of homes in the U.S. emit air pollutants such as nitrogen dioxide, carbon monoxide, and fine particulate matter at levels the \uc2 EPA\uc2  and \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  have set are unsafe and linked to respiratory illness, cardiovascular problems, cancer, and other health conditions.\par \par \uc2 Now, I don`t like this news. I too like cooking with gas stoves, but that`s what the data say increasingly, dangerous emissions in your kitchen, which really does not seem like the kind of thing you would want to just leave alone. You probably want to do something about it. Here`s the thing, of course. No one is coming for your gas stove. They are offering cash incentives if you buy a non-gas unit like one of the awesome new induction stoves.\par \par \uc2 The chair of the \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  clarifying today, "I am not looking to ban gas stoves and the \uc2 CPSC\uc2  has no proceeding to do so. Another \uc2 CPSC\uc2  Commissioner clarified, "To be clear, \uc2 CPSC\uc2  isn`t coming for anyone`s gas stoves. Regulations apply to new products for Americans who choose to switch from gas to electric. There is support available. Congress passed the Inflation Reduction Act which includes an $840 rebate."\par \par \uc2 OK, so no Jack-booted thugs showing up with a SWAT team to break into Ronny Jackson`s house to fry the gas stove from his cold dead hands. Instead, thanks to President Biden the Democrats, if you`d like to switch to a healthier, many would argue actually better stove, the government will chip in $840 bucks to help you out. So, deep breaths, everybody, unless you`re anywhere near a gas stove.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m not a conspiracy theorist but when the government takes control of your ability to cook, heat, travel, and eat, the alarm bells really start to ring.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today in the life of the new House Republican majority, they passed two bills underlying their anti-abortion stance, an issue where they are on the opposite side of public opinion. They also got hopping mad about people upgrading their kitchen appliances with safer alternatives. There are some who saw this kind of behavior coming as we watch the caucus meltdown the very first day of Congress over electing Kevin McCarthy as Speaker.\par \par \uc2 Connecticut Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  tweeted, "The problem is this isn`t just today. This is going to be every day in the House Republican majority. It`s not just they won`t be able to govern, it`s that they`re going to be an embarrassing public train wreck while they refuse to govern."\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  who just returned from a trip with a bipartisan group of senators to the Southern Border. You folks in the Senate are already snobs about the House. It`s really intense, and you were in the House before in the Senate, but it`s -- I can only imagine what it`s like in the Senate now at this House.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): Yes, it`s a very low bar, right? The Senate in normal times looks pretty dysfunctional but we look just absolutely glistening these days. And you know, the reality actually is that there`s a really thin silver lining to the, you know, complete dumpster fire that has become the House of Representatives. It`s that there are a handful of Senate Republicans that don`t like what`s going on, don`t like the fact that their party is being branded as this badly dysfunctional.\par \par \uc2 And in the Senate I think you`ll see, you know, just as much bipartisan cooperation between Republicans and Democrats as you saw last Congress because you`ve had, you know, a bit of a reaction from some of the more mainstream Republicans in the Senate to the dysfunction in the House.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, let`s talk about bipartisanship. You`re part of a bipartisan delegation to the border. Now, there`s a whole bunch of developments on the immigration front that I won`t run through. There are several court cases which go in opposite directions over the Title 42 Public Health Emergency that has been declared by the Trump Administration.\par \par \uc2 But there were some new announcements by the Biden Administration on limits to the procedures for seeking asylum from certain countries, caps on the number of asylum seekers, and requirements of moving to third-party countries, right, not doing it at the U.S. border to apply, similar to some of the things we saw in the Trump Administration. And many Advocates and Democratic lawmakers said this is Trumpism under a different banner. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 [20:55:19]\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, that`s not true. And I don`t think it`s fair to what President Biden is trying to do here. All of us are trying to unpack these new announcements but the reality is this. There is a greater diversity of nationality at the Southern Border than at any time in our history. One of our stops this week was in Yuma. And in Yuma, Arizona, there are more Russians presenting themselves at the border than Mexicans. That`s absolutely extraordinary.\par \par \uc2 The problem is when you have people coming from Russia or China or India or Haiti or Cuba, you can`t turn them back around to Mexico. Mexico won`t take them. We have no agreement with the country to send them back. And so, what President Biden is saying here is that we are actually going to now accept a certain number of those migrants, in exchange, Mexico has agreed to take a certain number back as well.\par \par \uc2 So, what we`re trying to do is sort of push people to apply for Asylum earlier in their home country. We`re trying to get Mexico to agree to take some of these migrants back just so that we don`t have these overwhelming numbers at the border, especially from countries where we can`t easily repatriate them.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, it seems clear there is a real capacity issue, right? This is not an invention of Fox News or right-wing propaganda that the number of people presenting for asylum exceeds in many cases both what the current immigration system can handle and then what social service agencies are on the border can handle. And even as they`ve departed to places across the country from Denver to New York City or wherever have been struggling to handle.\par \par \uc2 I just feel like I don`t hear anyone articulating what the actual solution here is. Like, asylums are right for a reason. And to your point, if you`re showing up in Yuma fleeing Russia, I don`t know, man, you probably got a pretty good asylum case. Like, if you -- if you went halfway across the world --\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: No, that`s -- no, that`s right. I mean, you know, so we ran into two Chinese migrants when we were out with border patrol on Monday night. And from what I understand, you know, if you show up from China, you are likely going to get your asylum case approved given the repressive nature of the Chinese regime.\par \par \uc2 So, here`s where I think we need to get to. We should encourage people to apply for asylum in this -- in the United States, but it does not make sense the way that it happens now where you literally have millions of people crossing the border, giving themselves up to Border Patrol and overwhelming the system. We should encourage people to apply for asylum in their home country.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are maybe emergent circumstances where people can`t wait, they need to come here immediately. But we should be having many more people doing this in a much more orderly fashion. And I think that is something the Biden Administration is trying to do and something that is very consistent with our values as a nation of immigrants.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, to go back to this, right? In 2014, when we first saw the first big surge of folks who are showing up the Southern Border mostly coming from Central America, there were -- one of the things the Obama Administration tried to do, right, was to set up a sort of remote asylum option, right? Asylum usually is at the border, refugees apply from a long way away, but refugees is a very, very long process, so this sort of in between, right? That was the option there.\par \par \uc2 But I don`t know. Like, I think Trump got rid of that, right? I`m pretty sure he got rid of that. And it`s not clear to me that like, can you expand that enough to actually appreciably make the numbers go down and also give people the humanitarian relief that they I think deserve.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, and here`s the question. The question is can we accept an unlimited number of asylum seekers? The answer is no. And so, well, there are many in my party who don`t want to admit that, we are going to have to make a decision as to how many asylees we can take, and then encourage those to be processed in their home country, and then frankly, turn those who don`t go through that process back around.\par \par \uc2 Now, that is a very difficult thing to do because many of these people are in desperate straits, but we do just have to admit there is a limit to how many asylees we can take over the course of a given year.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I will say this just to push back on that a little bit, and we may have different views on this. I mean, I remember reading about the one million folks that were in just the city of Damascus who`d come from Iraq during the peak of the Iraq War. There were about a million Syrians who were in Istanbul. Now, I`m sure if you asked the folks in Damascus in Istanbul if that was -- like, it really burdened the systems. But the amount of asylum seekers that other countries have taken other under periods is way, way beyond anything that we`re doing, just to be clear.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: So, no, that`s exactly right. And so, all I`m saying is that you`ve got to allocate the resources that are consistent with the number. Listen, as a nation we have all sorts of reasons to grow, right? We have labor shortages. There`s no way to keep up with China if we don`t -- if we don`t grow the number of people in this country. But what we need to do is actually match the number of people who are processing asylum claims with the number that we`re bringing in. I would argue to hire more people to process those claims.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  , that was very productive conversation. Thank you for joining me. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Thank you. That is ALL IN on this Wednesday night. The "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" show starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS 