y on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. Tonight, we are getting a much clearer picture of how Republicans are planning to leave the House in the 118th Congress. The GOP may have won control by campaigning on kitchen table issues like inflation and crime. But tonight, they use the power of their majority to pass two abortion related bills. One condemns attacks on antiabortion facilities. The other, a so-called born alive bill, punishes doctors for denying care to babies who survive an abortion attempt. No House Republicans voted against either of these bills.\par \par \uc2 And this week, House Majority Leader Steve Scalise made this absolutely false case for that second bill with deeply misleading remarks like these.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE, (R) LOUISIANA: The baby is born alive outside the womb. In some states, they actually are allowing that baby to be killed and calling it abortion. It`s murder, and yet it`s legal in some states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Nope, for facts sake, that is not true. There is no state that allows any doctor to kill a newborn baby. What you just heard is a lie, used to promote laws that curb abortion. Earlier today, South Carolina Republican Nancy Mace criticized her own GOP colleagues for bringing these two bills to the floor. But here`s the thing. When it came down to it, she voted yes for both of them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE, (R) SOUTH CAROLINA: So, this is probably not the way to start off the week. We should be looking at measures, for example, making sure every woman has access to birth control. If you really want to get serious about this issue and reduce unwanted pregnancies, well, I`ve got entire counties in South Carolina that don`t have a single OBGYN doctor. Those are the issues that we really need to be talking about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I`m going to say this one more time for you. That Congresswoman right there who said it was not a good idea, voted for both of those bills. Again, put on a great big show. That`s it.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the House GOP has also opened investigations into President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . The Oversight Committee asked the Treasury Department for the Biden family`s financial records. Oversight is also investigating those classified documents that were found in an office Biden used after he was Vice President. Today, we learned Biden`s aides discovered at least one additional set of classified documents in a different location.\par \par \uc2 It comes as the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  is reporting special counsel Jack Smith is ramping up his investigations into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . The paper says some members of Smith`s team expect that charging decisions involving top secret records found at Mar-a-Lago could come as early as this summer. In The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports, Trump campaign officials received a DOJ subpoena asking new questions about January 6.\par \par \uc2 With that, we have a lot to get to. Let`s get smarter with the help of our lead off panel. Peter Baker is here, Chief White House Correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  , Shelby Talcott, a Reporter for Semafor who covers Trump and the GOP. And former U.S. Attorney Joyce Vance, who spent 25 years as a federal prosecutor. And she is now a Law Professor at the University of Alabama.\par \par \uc2 Shelby, it is your first date here on THE 11TH HOUR, so we start with you. Republicans, they absolutely know these abortion bills have no chance of becoming law. So, what are they doing?\par \par \uc2 SHELBY TALCOTT, REPORTER, SEMAFOR: That`s a really good question. But I think the big thing that they`re doing is essentially what we saw after the midterms, is that the Republican Party had an identity crisis when it came to the issue of abortion. And that`s something that they`re trying to rectify. And I spoke yesterday with a bunch of anti-abortion activists, and they are pushing 2024 Republican candidates and pretty much any lawmakers to pass legislation like this and to go even further and enact some national ban.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think one of the big things is that they`re trying to get on track. They`re trying to come to a consensus because this is an issue that hurt them in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And I think the other thing is antiabortion activists are trying to make the case that in order to win in 2024, Republicans need to make the difference and make the argument as to why Democratic views on abortion are over the top. And so, one of the things they`re likely trying to do is they`re going to pass this. It`s not going to get past. It`s not going to end up on Biden`s death risk. And so that is an opportunity for them to then say, well, look, this group is, are the extremists. And so, I think that`s overall.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But they`re not even accurately or honestly articulating their views. Take Nancy Mace, for example. I saw her today on TV speaking out against these bills, and I`m thinking, whoa, look at her. She`s the only one speaking out against the GOP. That`s brave. And then, lo and behold, when she sits down to vote, she votes for them both.\par \par \uc2 TALCOTT: Yeah, I think it goes to show that the Republican Party is still really split on the issue of abortion, and it`s going to continue to be that way. And maybe Nancy Mace, push comes to shove, didn`t agree with this and still voted for this. And it`s going to be really interesting to see what other measures she disagrees with or people like her disagree with and still end up voting. And so, it`s going to be kind of a litmus test for House Republicans in the next two years.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I don`t know, Peter, do you buy that? Because from where I sit, it`s unclear what her view actually is. She puts on a show for all of us to see saying, oh, I don`t stand with this, but then she votes for it. It`s not like she`s an identity crisis. It`s like she`s pulling one over on us. She doesn`t think all of her viewers see the same thing.\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, look, it`s not the first time a lawmaker, of course, went along with the way that a party has decided to send out bills, even if they don`t think it`s the best or wisest way to go forward, right? It may not mean that she doesn`t agree with the substance of the bill. What she`s saying is this is what we ought not to be spending our time on. We have more important issues to be focused on.\par \par \uc2 Look, this is -- Congress, this is how it works. This is what lawmakers do. Last year, the House, under Democratic control, passed a bill codifying Roe v. Wade, even though that had no chance of passing because they knew the Senate was never going to pass it. That`s it, you know, from the point of view of the party that passes bills like this, they`re called messaging bills and meant to, A, be a statement of principle, B, be a stop to the base of the party that really wants them to fight on these. And C, to be a way of, you know, gaining advantage in the next election by in some way or another putting the other party and if they believe it in a position of weakness by opposing something they think their constituents might be for.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes, and of course, we hear all these things that they`re looking to defund this and defund that. You can`t defund anything without the Senate`s approval, and that`s not going to happen, at least not in the next two years. Joyce, these two abortion bills, how could they curb abortion rights?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, the tension here is, with a Republican Party that on a national level, advocates to let states do what each state wishes to implement. That was the entire rationale in bringing Texas and Mississippi cases in front of the Supreme Court that resulted in reversing Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 But now, we`re seeing what I think there was reasonable suspicion that we would see before the election, that if there was a Republican majority in the House, they would try to ban abortion at a national level. Would that stand up in the Supreme Court? I think that`s a very interesting question. We now have a six three ultra-conservative majority, and the time might be right, but for the fact that these bills will not have any success in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Peter, let`s change topics. Your colleagues at \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  are reporting on special counsel Jack Smith`s investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . We now have this new GOP House majority, and now we`ve got the Biden classified document situation. How could these two things make Jack Smith`s job a lot harder?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yeah, look, you know, first of all, there`s a politics of it, right? Even though there`s substantial differences between the two cases, substantial difference between what President Trump -- former President Trump did and what we`re looking at, what we know so far anyway, in terms of what happened with President Biden. For one thing, President Trump resisted efforts, repeated efforts, including a subpoena, to return these documents. That`s not the case, as far as we know, of anything involving President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that there is classified documents involved with both presidents is going to give President Trump, former President Trump the opportunity to make the case. See, everybody does it. And they`re just prosecuting me. It`s just a witch hunt. And whether that`s true or not, obviously it`s a political weapon, a way of discrediting any prosecution that goes forward and a way of forcing the prosecutors, if they do choose to prosecute.\par \par \uc2 Joyce can talk about as better than I can to try to justify why this deserves prosecution and some other case might not at a different point in time. Now, most classified documents cases, things where people have classified documents they shouldn`t have, don`t necessarily involve a criminal prosecution. What makes the Trump case so different is there seem to be an active effort to obstruct. That`s, at least the way prosecutors are looking at, if you look at the documents they have filed. Again, does not seem to be the case that we know of in the Biden case at this point.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:02]\par \par \uc2 But the fact that they`re close enough makes it just murky enough to create, you know, a fog that former President Trump will use to his advantage.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joyce, educate us?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, I think Peter gets it just right here. These cases are completely different. One involves a willful effort to retain materials, to fail to return them at the request of a government issue, which is a crime under the statute. And then, there`s this long course of obstructive behavior by Trump.\par \par \uc2 In fact, had Trump done what Biden`s team did here? He wouldn`t be in any legal trouble at all. What the Biden team did was they called up the National Archives and they said, listen, we`ve got some stuff that we`ve found. We need to return it to you. The National Archives doesn`t appear to have even been aware it was missing. They didn`t make a request for it. So just from the outset, there`s no similarity in the two situations. But as Peter is, I think, correct to point out, truth doesn`t matter in the highly politicized climate that we exist in today.\par \par \uc2 And although the Special Counsel doesn`t need to consider anything beyond the facts and the law of Trump`s case, it stands on its own. It should be charged or not based on its own merit. I think that there will just pragmatically have to be some consideration of the world we live in, because DOJ is an institution that cares about the public`s perception that it`s acting in a fair and an honest way. And we may well see \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  appoint a Special Counsel for the Biden matter here. But the real issue before you appoint a special counsel is you don`t do that unless there`s a crime to investigate. And based on the facts that we`re publicly aware of today, DOJ likely knows a good bit more. There is no reason here to believe that a crime has been committed by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or anyone on his team.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: If we`re talking about public perception, one of the biggest issues, though, is the public is being filled with misinformation and distractions. Shelby, we know there are going to be investigations on top of investigations into President Biden, and we`re not even sure what all of them are about yet. How ugly is this going to get?\par \par \uc2 TALCOTT: I think it`s certainly going to get very ugly. We`ve already seen the ugliness start, and particularly with a House controlled -- Republican controlled House, it`s not going to get any prettier. We`ve already seen them launch investigations into the weaponization of government, which is going to focus on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the DOJ. And part of that is that they`re going to be allowed to probe ongoing criminal investigations. And so that`s going to be really interesting.\par \par \uc2 Now, I don`t think the Department of justice is going to be very willing to just hand over documents, but again, that`s part of -- that`s a prime example of just how ugly this is going to get.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Peter how`s the White House bracing for all this?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Look, they knew what was going to happen if they lost the Midterms, if they lost the House controlled. They knew this is going to be a period of, you know, subpoena wars and constant investigations. And they have been bracing for it. They`ve been hiring people in the White House counsel`s office who are prepared and specialized in this kind of, you know, partisan investigations. And they recognize that they are going to have to make a decision.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not to follow the precedent that President Trump did when he was in office. This is a really interesting choice for this White House because it says it wants to get back to normal. It wants to get back to respect for the prerogatives of the congressional branch of government, which does enjoy the power of subpoena and does enjoy and have the responsibility for oversight and scrutiny. And yet, obviously, you know, when that`s partisan, when you`re President of the United States, you say, why should I have to go along with this?\par \par \uc2 President Trump basically stiff armed. The Democratic Congress and said, I`m not going to do it. I`m not going to respond to your subpoena. I`m not going to give you the documents that you`re asking for. I`m not going to give you the witnesses you`re asking for. And what the Biden administration now has to do is, if they do that, do they create, therefore, a bipartisan precedent in which Congress is neutered in any legitimate, not just illegitimate, forms of scrutiny. And how do you make a decision between what you think is legitimate form of scrutiny and illegitimate partisan warfare? And that`s a real interesting conundrum that this White House is going to have to answer.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And we wonder why the American people don`t think government gets much done. Joyce, tomorrow, our opening arguments in the Proud Boys seditious conspiracy trial. And today, the judge said this statement can be used as evidence. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , (R) FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Stand back and stand by, but I`ll tell you what, I`ll tell you what, somebody`s got to do something about Antifa and the left, because this is not a right-wing problem. This is a left wing.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: His own \uc2 FBI\uc2  director said left wing --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [23:15:02]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Wow. Wow. We have all seen that quote over and over the last two years. But what is important about it as it relates to this specific trial.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So, the judge ruled today that prosecutors can offer that statement to show that it motivated the Proud Boys as they prepared for January 6. Their leadership is charged with sedition conspiracy. And in this case, the evidence that the government has put together, I think, will be even stronger than in the earlier case involving the Oath Keepers. It will show more of a concerted effort in advance of January 6 to align resources and to prepare for some violence akin to what we saw on that date.\par \par \uc2 You know, the open-ended question, Steph, with the Proud Boys case is whether it will lead DOJ further. Will any of the information here lead them perhaps into the war rooms at the Willard? Will there perhaps be a defendant who decides to cooperate following a conviction? There are a lot of unknowns going into this trial, but it`s clear that the evidence that this jury will hear may not implicate the former President, but it will involve him very directly in the work that the Proud Boys undertook on January 6.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Shelby, for the last five years, we have been hearing over and over how we need tighter, stronger, stricter ethics rules around Congress. And today, the House GOP voted for changes to the Office of Congressional Ethics, but they weren`t making anything tighter or stronger. Tell us what they really did.\par \par \uc2 TALCOTT: Yeah, so they essentially gutted as that headline notes, the House Ethics Office. And this was met by, you know, a lot of praise, I believe George Santos was one of the ones who praised this decision, which was interesting. And so, it kind of leaves them open to being able to do more of whatever they want, if that makes sense.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Sure, it does.\par \par \uc2 TALCOTT: And it`s going to be interesting. It`s going to be interesting that combined with these investigations, combined with these committees, it`s going to be a wild two years, I think.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: No rules for Congress and the party will continue. Peter Baker, Shelby Talcott, welcome to THE 11TH HOUR, and Joyce Vance.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the one and only Captain Sully Sullenberger is here on today`s \uc2 FAA\uc2  meltdown. How could a computer outage ground flights across the country, something that has not happened since 9/11.\par \par \uc2 And later, New York Republicans want George Santos to guess, what? Resign, calling him a pathological liar and a stain. But guess who says not so fast? Kevin McCarthy. And he`s the one in charge. Michael Steele and Robert Gibbs are here on all of it. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:22:31]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Hallelujah. Flights have resumed after the \uc2 FAA\uc2  suffered a massive computer outage that grounded flights across the country. More than 9000 flights were delayed and at least 1000 more were canceled. It is the second major air travel disruption in less than a month. NBC`s Tom Costello has all the details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Government sources tell NBC News, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  system issues began to surface yesterday around 3:30 in the afternoon. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  switched to a backup system, then returned to the main system just before midnight. At 04:45 a.m., the \uc2 FAA\uc2  rebooted the system, but it took time for the critical data to reload.\par \par \uc2 NBC News has now learnt today`s problems were caused by a corrupted file. It was just last week that air traffic in Florida ground to a halt after a different \uc2 FAA\uc2  computer glitch. Over the holidays, \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  suffered a major meltdown, affecting roughly a million passengers. Now, the U.S. travel industry says the country`s aviation infrastructure is in immediate need of an upgrade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joining us now, a true American hero, Captain Sully Sullenberger, who as you recall, safely landed a \uc2 U.S. Airways\uc2  flight on the Hudson River 14 years ago this weekend. I will never forget watching it live next to my two-year-old son thinking I was part of a movie. But no, it was real life. And that real life hero is with us.\par \par \uc2 Captain, I am so glad to see you again. When anything happens in the air, you are the person I want to talk to on the ground. So, please explain to us, what exactly was the system that failed today? Why is it so important? And how in the world does it work?\par \par \uc2 CAPTAIN C.B. "SULLY" SULLENBERGER, MIRACLE ON THE HUDSON PILOT: Stephanie, it`s good to be with you and thanks for calling on me. It`s an important system because it`s the system that provides NOTEM information to pilots. And that`s something about, you know, conditions at airports, runways, lighting and navigation aids. Anything that should be working. That`s not - - anything that`s not normal, pilots need to know about on their flight. And by federal aviation regulation, pilots may not depart if they haven`t had a chance to review all that information. That`s what made this a big deal. It was a big failure.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But the \uc2 FAA\uc2  says it was a corrupted file. That`s what was to blame for the system collapse. I don`t know, I think that it`s really worrisome that just one damaged file could cause this amount of massive chaos.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:04]\par \par \uc2 SULLENBERGER: You know, that`s just an indication of how many of our critical systems that we depend on every day in some ways are brittle and fragile. And that`s why I`ve been saying for a long time after our experience on the Hudson River in 2009 that we need to make our systems, our technology as reliable, redundant and resilient as we can. But in the end, it`s human beings who can really be resilient and solve problems they`ve never seen before. And so, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  , I`m sure, began to work very hard to recover from this.\par \par \uc2 When I say that these systems need to be reliable, that means they don`t fail often. What I mean that they are redundant is that they have effective backups that can be applied immediately. And when I say resilient, it means that they can recover quickly to normal operation. And those qualities were obviously not effective in this case. It`s also important that we not have single points of failure on any of our systems or common points of failure that seems to have happened in this case. In other words, the primary system and the backup system both apparently had the same corrupted file.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, it shouldn`t work that way. How old is this technology? Clearly it sounds like at the very least it`s time to update or upgrade it?\par \par \uc2 SULLENBERGER: Yeah, I think it`s an indication of how many systems at every major organization, every government agency is in desperate need of upgrades. We need to give the resources necessary to do those upgrades. You know, I`ve been saying for a long time there`s a strong business case for making those kinds of quality and safety investments. It`s always better and ultimately cheaper to do the upfront investment to avoid the negative outcome rather than trying to fix the damage later. And when lives are lost, there`s no way to repair that damage. The right thing to do is to make these investments upfront. But it`s hard to do, Steph.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Have we not made these investments because nobody`s lobbying for it, right? When there`s an issue on the business side, we know every major airline CEO is in Washington saying, we need money, we need help. But when it`s the system, when it`s the infrastructure that breaks down, there`s not really anyone banging down the door of the White House. And that`s what we actually need most.\par \par \uc2 SULLENBERGER: Well, we have to have two things exist simultaneously in any democracy to solve complex problems. The public awareness of it and the political will to act. And those two things must happen simultaneously. Those are perishable commodities that only last for a period before we move on to the next strange object. So, I think that`s just one of many reasons why it`s so hard to get the leadership, the Congress to appropriate the funds for \uc2 FAA\uc2  to make the upgrades that they need to make.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Public awareness and political will. You say it so clearly, it sounds very simple. But, of course, we both know it is far, far from that. Captain, I`m so glad you joined us tonight. It is always a pleasure to see you. Thank you for everything you do. Captain Sullenberger.\par \par \uc2 SULLENBERGER: You`re very welcome. Hopefully we can move this needle forward a little bit.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Sure, hope so.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, more calls for George Santos to resign even from within his own party. So, could his shiny new name plaque be coming down anytime soon? Now, Kevin McCarthy has his way. We`ll explain when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:33:28]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH CAIRO, CHAIR NASSAU COUNTY GOP: George Santos` campaign last year was a campaign of deceit, lies and fabrication. His lies were not mere fibs. He disgraced the House of Representatives. He has no place in the Nassau County Republican Committee, nor should he serve in public service, nor as an elected official. He`s not welcome here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Not welcome in Strong Island. The calls for Republican George Santos to resign are now coming from inside the House and his own party. The Nassau County Republican Party and several New York Republican Congressmen have now called on Santos to officially step down. And here`s how George himself reacted when reporters asked him about it today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will you step down?\par \par \uc2 REP. GEORGE SANTOS, (D) NEW YORK: Guys, I will not. Guys, you got to give us a little space here this way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: New York Republicans are calling you a disgrace. You will not resign?\par \par \uc2 SANTOS: Pardon me. Excuse me guys.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Congressman is trying to get in the elevator. Excuse us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When I thought I had a tough day at work. Back with us tonight to discuss, Robert Gibbs, former Obama Campaign Senior Advisor and White House Press Secretary under President Obama . And Michael Steele, former Chairman of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  and former Lieutenant Governor of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Michael, take us inside the Republican Party because Kevin McCarthy, who just got towards Santos` vote, is standing by his boy. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R) CALIFORNIA SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: I try to stick by the Constitution. The voters elected him to serve. If there is a concern and it has to go through the ethics, and let him move through that. But right now, the voters have a voice in the decisions, not where people pick and choose based upon what somebody`s pressing. So, he will continue to serve.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:09]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you going to take any action against him at this point or any of these allegations acceptable to you?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: What are the charges against him?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, he`s --\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Is there a charge against them? You know, in America today, you`re innocent until proven guilty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Except, of course, the person or persona who they voted for is not who George Santos turns out to be. What`s McCarthy`s strategy here?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER RNC CHAIRMAN: To avoid having to deal with the issue as much as possible. He doesn`t want to go against those in the caucus who need that vote. He needs that vote. He only has a four-vote window, and throwing that into a special election in that district could be a problem, could give the Democrats a potential pickup of a seat. Then he`s down to three votes.\par \par \uc2 So, he is less interested in the quality of this candidate, the character or the content of anything having to do with him short of does it help me here in the House? And that`s his main focus. What you saw the Nassau County party was exemplary. To take the steps that they took to say what they said publicly, collectively, then to have the state party chairman reiterate that and reinforce it is a very strong signal from the New York party at least, that there is no place for him in our House.\par \par \uc2 Now, Kevin McCarthy`s House is a different place altogether, and he`s not going to kick him out. I just don`t see it because for Kevin`s calculation, there`s no political upside in doing so.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Oh, well, it`s a long walk to California. Robert, yes, like people are laughing about the absurdity of his lies, but at what point does Santos become a vulnerable blackmail target foreign adversaries?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, FORMER OBAMA WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, that`s a good question, Stephanie. I think what you`re seeing is just generally unsustainable, even as McCarthy and I agree with Michael, tries to save one more vote in his majority and not have to deal with an even slimmer majority. But I don`t see that this is even remotely sustainable in the medium term, let alone the long term.\par \par \uc2 I thought it was interesting when Kevin McCarthy said that he would follow the Constitution, obviously and the Constitution there is the ability to expel a member of Congress that goes through a House Ethics Committee investigation. So, I think that`s the path that we`re heading down now. Look, he`s got a lot of problems. He`s a serial liar. He might also be a criminal. None of those things, I think, Republicans are eventually going to want to have serving next to them in this House.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s talk about what else Republicans are doing, Robert, because despite what they promised on the campaign trail, focus on crime, immigration, inflation, one of the first big moves they have made is to pass abortion bills that have no chance of becoming law. What do you think they`re doing, Robert? This is not what the majority of their voters were looking for.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yeah, I mean, obviously, anybody that watched the results, the 2022 election knows that abortion was an issue that Republicans failed to have answer for, and it penalized them at the ballot box. I think there`s obviously tremendous pressure from the base to do base things right now. And it reminds me, quite frankly, of times, particularly after the 1994 elections, Republicans come into power, you know, President Clinton is struggling, and they really pushed through an agenda, quite frankly, that was hugely unpopular with the American people. Clinton regained his political footing by pushing back on what was going on in the House of Representatives. And I think there`s a real danger for House Republicans right now that they`re going to pursue an agenda that is summarily unpopular with the American people and give a real opening for President Biden to label all Republicans, House Republicans.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Michael, are Republicans just not doing any polling or they are polling and they`re ignoring them. I mean, last I checked, the state of Florida is filled with senior citizens. You think they`re going to be up for it when their Social Security gets cut? I`m going to guess no.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: No, they`re not going to be up for it. But that`s not the kind of polling they`re getting right now. They`re getting clearly targeted polling that is designed to figure out what are the buttons that we need to push to generate two things, a level of excitement among the base when we need it, and a lot of cash to come into the door on the back end. You look at what happened last week during those five days and 15 rounds. After every vote, there was another email that went out from some member of the GOP, starting with \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  asking for money.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:04]\par \par \uc2 So, this is not about serious legislative work. This is not about governing the country in opposition to a president with whom you may have differences on policy. This is about how the grift and the grad continue to animate and move the party at one level and how you consolidate and gain power by exciting a base on another.\par \par \uc2 And so, the reality for whether it`s, you know, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  or \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  or even the Speaker himself, is how do you keep this thing going for as long as it can go? Because at some point, yeah, there`s going to be an accounting, but we`ll just muddy the waters as much as we can and create enough confusion that we`re able to slip up. That`s what they think happened in 2022 with some of the stuff that they did. They didn`t, you know, and it resulted in, OK, we still got the majority, not as big a majority, but we still got the majority. And that`s enough.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We are a country with serious problems, with the Republican Party without a serious agenda. Robert, talk about this. Biden aids, we know, have now found this second batch of classified documents. We know this is going to play out politically in a way the Republicans are going to go nuts about this. How should the White House play it?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Well, I think they should play it just very straight. I mean, these are documents that were found mistakenly in possession and returned. I think the big difference here and there is a difference between what you see, what you saw with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , and that is the intent. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wasn`t intending to take these documents. We know it appears as if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did indeed intend to do those, to take those purposely. When the Vice President`s -- former Vice President, current president`s office found the documents, they were quickly returned to the National Archives. We know that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had to have the \uc2 FBI\uc2  come into Mar-a-Lago to get the documents and repeatedly deceived that he had them. So, I think there`s a big -- they`re going to have to point out the distinctions here and make sure that Republicans don`t try to, what about ism, the idea that these are somehow analogous?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The truth matters, but only if you hear it. They got to keep telling it loud and clear. Robert Gibbs, Michael Steele and his casual cool tonight, and we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Coming up, Russia replaces the man behind the attacks on Ukraine`s infrastructure. What is behind this military shake up as Vladimir Putin`s forces are surrounding a key city in Ukraine? When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:27]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Russian forces appear to be closing in on a breakthrough on the eastern front lines in Ukraine. Fighting continues to rage around the town of Soledar, which has seen some of the fiercest battles of the war so far. My colleague, NBC`s Matt Bradley has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT Tonight, what could be Russia`s first battlefield breakthrough in months, coming at a devastating cost to the city of Soledar in eastern Ukraine. Russian oligarch, \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  releasing this picture, claiming he was in the salt mines under the city and that his private army had taken control.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine tonight disputing his claim. But if he`s right, it would mark a rare Russian victory after months of defeat. You can see the brutality of this fight in the faces at this Ukrainian field hospital in the nearby city of Bakhmut. This Ukrainian soldier didn`t survive.\par \par \uc2 It hurts when it`s not possible to save them, said this surgeon. Civilians here have endured fierce fighting for the last six months.\par \par \uc2 The buildings are destroyed, this woman said. People are left without apartments.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy tonight slamming Russia for trying to claim Soledar`s destruction as some kind of achievement.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, civilians are still in the firing line as this war lurches into its second year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Civilians in the firing line, and the bitter, bitter cold. For more, I want to bring in William Taylor, former U.S. Ambassador to Ukraine. He is also the \uc2 United States Institute of Peace\uc2  , Vice President for Russia and Europe. Ambassador, thank you for being here. I want to start with this fight for Soledar. Why is it so important?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM TAYLOR, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO UKRAINE: So, Stephanie, both sides actually have made it more important than it actually is. There`s nothing really strategic about Soledar. The Russians have made it strategic, made it important for them, as Matt Bradley said, to try to register a win. They`ve not had a win. They`ve not had a win in this war. So, they are looking for one. And then, you get the big story coming out of the Russians Prigozhin, as Matt described, says, they`ve taken Soledar. Whereas, the \uc2 Russian Ministry of Defense\uc2  says, well, maybe not, not entirely clear. And as Matt again said, the Ukrainians are disputing it as well. So, it`s more of the Russians trying to register a win.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:06]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Putin also replaced his war commander after just three months three months. What does that say about their military right now?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: It`s further of what I just said that is they`re in disarray. They`re backbiting. Prigozhin is saying that the military -- that the Defense Ministry is not doing well. Defense Ministry is saying, well, Prigozhin hasn`t really taken it. Putin has gotten a third that put in place, a third commander of all the activity in Ukraine. So, it`s in disarray. Putin is in trouble. He doesn`t have the people, he doesn`t have the leadership, he doesn`t have the equipment. He doesn`t have the momentum. He doesn`t have morale. Putin knows he`s in trouble, so he`s changing things to try to get himself out.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He`s in trouble, but he`s still hurting a lot of innocent people. Ukrainian troops are going to be traveling to Oklahoma and they`re going to learn to operate the Patriot missile system. Does a move like that, could that get viewed as an escalation by Russia?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: We don`t have to worry about escalation from Russia, it seems to me, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Why?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: The Russians have escalated. The Russians have invaded. The Russians are bombing civilian targets. I mean, so what the Ukrainians are doing, Ukrainians are fighting back. The Ukrainians are using these new weapons that the Americans are finally giving. These Patriots are the top end, are the top end of our air defense.\par \par \uc2 And, Steph, you remember for a long time we wouldn`t provide even Stingers. And then we provided Stingers. Now, we`re all the way up to Patriot. Same thing on the ground. For a long time, we wouldn`t provide Javelins to go against the tanks, and now we`re providing HIMARS. So, it`s a movement in the direction of where they should be, of where these weapons should be. The high end is what we need to provide to the Ukrainians, so they can push the Russians out of their country.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Are these specific systems, though, will they make a big difference in the war? Help us understand their capabilities?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: So, the Patriots will make a difference in shooting down cruise missiles and aircraft and ballistic missiles that the Russians are using to attack civilian targets. So, yes, that will be helpful. They don`t provide the kind of weapons that the Ukrainians need to push the Russians out of their country. For that, they need the tanks. And we started giving those.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the Defense -- our Defense Department said, we send 50 Bradley Fighting Vehicles. These are not quite tanks, but they`re close. They`re tracked vehicles, they`re armored vehicles. They allow the military, the infantry to move around and protected vehicles to be able to move quickly and indeed, to push the Russians back. That`s what they need. But they need more than 50. They need hundreds of these tanks. And it turns out that the French are giving tanks. The Germans are giving equivalents. The Brits are about to announce we understand, tanks. So, this is all going in the right direction. It just needs to come quicker.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right then, Ambassador William Taylor, thank you so much for joining us tonight. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Thanks, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When we come back, I cannot even believe we`re talking about this. They have lit the match. Now the right is running wild. What they claim the government is going to raid your house for? And the ruthless reality check we`re going to hit you with. When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:22]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The consumer product safety Commission is deciding on whether to ban gas stoves totally because of safety. Safety. We`ve had these stoves for over 100 years. It`s totally fine to give fentanyl to addicts, but a gas stove is a threat to your life, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, naturally, they decided gas powered stoves, racist, and have to go.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: First Lady, Jill Biden getting roasted online for this photo of her cooking on the very thing that the administration was looking to fix.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You can make odd.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: No, you can make stuff up, and that`s what they`re doing there all day long. So, the last thing before we go tonight, all about gas lighting. If you watched Fox News in the last 24 hours, you might think that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is coming for your gas stove. That is a new and absolutely ridiculous one. For fact`s sake, let`s get to the bottom of it.\par \par \uc2 This all started with a \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  interview where a \uc2 U.S. Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  member, \uc2 Richard Trumka Jr\uc2 . said this about the appliance. "This is a hidden hazard. Any option is on the table. Products that can`t be made safe can be banned." The agency, generally speaking, is concerned about studies that link gas stoves to internal air pollution and childhood asthma rates, and it should be. The entire purpose of the agency is to review the safety of household products.\par \par \uc2 Trump later clarified things and said, they`re not coming for anybody`s gas stove, and any regulations would apply to new products only. But that did nothing to lower the temperature online and over at Fox News. Representative Ronnie Jackson tweeted several times about it, including this treasure. "If the maniacs in the White House come for my stove, they can pry it from my cold, dead hands. Come and take it.\par \par \uc2 [00:00:09]\par \par \uc2 And Senator Tim Scott added this, "You can always count on Biden to gaslight the American people."\par \par \uc2 While some parties are gaslighting on this one, I can safely report it is not the White House. The Press Secretary said this today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The President does not support banning gas stoves, and the \uc2 Consumer Product Safety Commission\uc2  , which is independent, is not banning gas stoves. I just want to be very clear on that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So, there you have it. Lights out. Story over. Fox News, hope you`re watching.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you all a very, very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late. I`ll see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020230112ej1b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for January 11, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Tom Costello, Blayne Alexander, Jeff Guzzetti,  Carol Lee, Ali Vitali, Ashley Parker, Antonia Hylton, Ken Dilanian, Marissa  Parra \par \uc2 8,039 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good day, I'm Chris Jansing, live at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 If you're watching this from an airport gate, you already know the headline. More than 8500 flights delayed or canceled after a key system went down.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Your flight was supposed to leave at 9:30 this morning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What time are you finally going to get to leave now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, 5:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, what are you going to do?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Just wait.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: How much more do passengers have to take before something's done to fix the airline system? Plus, House Republicans are staging the first battles of what looks to be an all-out political world in the next two years, votes on abortion rights, immigration, and the \uc2 IRS\uc2  are all expected soon. But isn't it just for show? And somehow, the catastrophic situation in California continues to get even worse. Relentless rainstorms have already led to the deaths of 17 people. In Santa Cruz, hundreds of homes have been damaged by floodwaters, some ripped right off of their foundations. I'll speak live to the local sheriff about how they're coping, even as they brace for yet another full week of rain.\par \par \uc2 But we start with this tidal wave of flight delays and cancellations that swept across the country early today. The latest in a series of widespread meltdowns that have repeatedly in recent months left thousands of passengers stranded. At one point this morning, all commercial domestic departures were suspended after a breakdown in what's known as the Notice To Air Missions System. Although flights began taking off roughly four hours ago, the ripple effect delayed more than 7500 flights and forced the cancellation of more than 1100 others.\par \par \uc2 Now, the exact cause of the breakdown is still unknown, but the effect, major headaches, and disruptions for the flying public are now all too familiar. And it's been bad for a while. MSNBC has obtained data from FlightAware showing that the number of delayed flights in the U.S. was up 5 percent last year from the year before. The number of canceled flights nearly doubled from 121,000 to more than 210,000.\par \par \uc2 We've got a lot of folks to talk about this with Tom Costello, who covers aviation for NBC News. NBC's Carol Lee is at the White House for us. Blayne Alexander is at Hartsfield Jackson, Atlanta International. And Jeff Guzzetti is a former \uc2 FAA\uc2  and \uc2 NTSB\uc2  investigator, president of Guzzetti aviation risk discovery, and MSNBC aviation analyst. Great to have you all here.\par \par \uc2 Tom, what's the situation right now?\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, MSNBC AVIATION CORRESPONDENT: Well, the system is now back up and operational again. They brought it up at about nine o'clock. It was down for two-plus hours. This first started, we think around 5:00 a.m. or so, it was clearly in the early morning hours, and then it started to snowball.\par \par \uc2 This Notice To Air Missions Computer System, which provides critical information about airport runway closures, for example, or maybe there's construction equipment on the ramp, or maybe Fire Department activity, that computer system went down. And you need that computer system because pilots need to know what to expect at the airport that they're going to. And they really should not be leaving their origination airport until they have that data in front of them. Not only can they print it out with reams of paper. They can also have it on their iPad right there in front of them in the cockpit with real-time data. Well, that went down.\par \par \uc2 And as a result, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  very slowly started delaying the departures, and then it became obvious they need to put a pause on all departures -- commercial departures. So, that happened at 7:00 a.m. And then at 9:00 a.m., the system was back up and running. They validated the system. And so, it's been up ever since but as you mentioned, we've had significant delays.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we're at 75 -- 7700 delays right now in the United States, and 1100 cancellations. And there, that map really illustrates the problems. The big hubs, as you would expect, New York, DC, Atlanta, Charlotte, Chicago, Dallas, Denver, San Francisco, LAX. And of course, the West Coast, also dealing with all of the weather out there. But now it does -- it's going to take a while to work through the ripple effect from all of this.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is suggesting they don't, at the moment, see signs of a cyber- attack. However, the question is, well, what happened to this critical computer? We believe it is siloed, siloed from other parts of air traffic control. But why did this go down? We simply don't have the answer yet.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Jeff, in kind of layman's terms, if you can, is there what you see as the most likely possibility for what happened here?\par \par \uc2 JEFF GUZZETTI, MSNBC AVIATION ANALYST: It's still too early to tell. It could be something as simple as a repair that was done to the computer software that didn't take properly or a power interrupt problem in which they couldn't get the backup system operating. It could be a user error, operator error, or maybe a -- maybe a hack, an unintentional hack. We just don't know at this time.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: How vulnerable though, are we to these kinds of problems from your point of view?\par \par \uc2 GUZZETTI: Well, all systems in the \uc2 United States government\uc2  are vulnerable to cyber-attacks. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  has been working hard to take care of that. The inspector general's office constantly audits them and shows them deficiencies and how they can strengthen their cybersecurity. So, they're doing the best they can. We live in a world with a lot of airplane flights, and a lot of sophistication. And it's a constant challenge for all agencies to keep up with the cyber threat.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Blayne, as we've been saying, this is nothing new, sadly, to flyers. Christmas was a disaster for a lot of people. I mean, Southwest had the biggest problems, but there was weather and other problems that went on, I think. And Tom will correct me if I'm wrong, but I think there was something close to a million passengers that were stranded over the entire Christmas period. What are passengers telling you now today?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: You know, Chris, you talk about that travel nightmare from a few weeks ago, I was covering it almost in this exact same spot. And you saw people who were stranded for days. What we're talking about here, while there are a number of inconveniences, certainly does not appear to compare to what we saw back then when people were stranded for days on end.\par \par \uc2 But certainly, when we're talking about the world's busiest airport here in Atlanta, it's called that for a reason, right? So, when we look at the number of delays, for instance, about 700 or so delays, a few more than 700 flights delayed right now. And when you look at the flight information board, I just took a quick glance a few minutes ago, you can certainly see that it is having an impact here, you go down and you see a number of delays. But when we talk to passengers, it seems as though they are being inconvenienced for a matter of hours, but still able to get on flights today. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN GENTLE, DELAYED TRAVELER: Going to Fort Lauderdale on 11:20 flight. TV came up this morning and said \uc2 FAA\uc2  said there was a problem. So, we started going crazy, going on the phone --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And then we said happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 GENTLE: It's her birthday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My birthday today.\par \par \uc2 GENTLE: We called Southwest, 40 minutes wait, we're on the way to the airport with my brother-in-law, what do we do, we get here. And amazingly, there's only one person in line ahead of us. So, we went up there and they said we can get you on 6:55 tonight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: So, I think they just underscored to you exactly how these ripple effects are happening. We're talking about a number of missed flights. So, even though flights have been back in the air for quite some time, when you talk about the world's busiest airport, yes, there are planes that didn't get to where they were supposed to on time earlier this morning. That means people miss connections, that means missed flights, and so that means kind of seeing them increase as the day progresses.\par \par \uc2 Now, I do think one thing that's important to talk about here when we talk to passengers today, as well as a couple of weeks ago with the Southwest meltdown, is this idea of just having confidence in the travel system in general, confidence with air travel. And that's something that with this incident, even though the delays are minor compared, it certainly takes a hit.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Tom, I do think there's a lot of anxiety for people flying these days than the government does keep track of customer complaints. And I think they're up what, nearly 400 percent between October of 2019 and October of last year. I mean, we keep hearing conversation, should there be more severe consequences when these kinds of -- kind of things happens, more about some kind of passenger Bill of Rights but is that all it is, at this point? Talk any sense that something might happen, especially given this series of problems we've seen, Tom, what are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Yes. Well, let's keep in mind, this is not the airline's fault. And so, the airlines are not going to be held responsible for this. This is the \uc2 FAA\uc2  . This is the government.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Right.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: And perhaps archaic and archaic computer system. And so, if you, a passenger, are hoping to get your money back for your lunch today or hotel, that's not going to happen. This is going to be going down as an act of God and you can't really sue the government asking for your money back. If this were an airline, that's different.\par \par \uc2 I would also say, you know, every time we put up these numbers about record numbers of cancellations or record numbers of complaints, yes, but there are so many caveats, especially if you compare it to the pandemic when there wasn't much going on at all. Keep in mind also, we had a meltdown in the summer travel season between May and July 1. They corrected that pretty quickly. And then, of course, the problems we've had over the Christmas holiday that was pretty much all \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  and their own internal problems, so the other airlines did just fine over the holidays.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 You know, it's easy to gloss over it and say the whole system is bad and we got to blow it all up. In fact, if you start looking at a piece by piece, we have individual issues that need to be addressed. And right now, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  has got a big one. It's got to get to the bottom of it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Jeff, I do think there is the sense -- and I fly a lot and I have noticed changes without a doubt. I think on Christmas when I was trying to come back here to New York, I was told there was a 600-minute delay before an agent could get to me. 600 minutes was -- and I've pretty good status on the airlines. So, I wonder, to pick up on what Tom said, when you hear people say, obviously, the entire system needs to be reworked, we have too many people flying, we don't have the infrastructure, we don't have the staffing, we don't have the planes, is there some truth to that or how do you see it?\par \par \uc2 GUZZETTI: Chris, yes, and no. I completely concur with Tom that there's a variety of reasons why all this has happened. And unfortunately, this NOTAM issue came right on the heels of the \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2  dispatch computer system problem. Sure, aviation is inherently complex, more and more growth every year. The air traffic control system in this country has prevented two airplanes from coming together for decades now.\par \par \uc2 Is the system safe? Yes. Does the system need to be repaired and upgraded? Yes. Is the system completely broken and needs to start over? Absolutely, not. It needs to evolve with the technology and with the social demands of aviation.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, to the idea, Carol, that this today was not about the airlines, what are we hearing from the administration?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: OK. So, what we're hearing from administration officials is that while they don't yet know the specific cause of this disruption, they are not seeing as of now any signs of nefarious activities such as a cyber-attack or something like that and they are promising a full investigation to get to the bottom of this. We heard from transportation secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  just a little while ago on MSNBC where he had talked about that and talked about that decision to bring much of air traffic to a halt. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, SECRETARY, DEPARTMENT OF TRANSPORTATION: What I would say is there is no direct indication of any kind of external or nefarious activity. But we are not yet prepared to rule that out.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC ANCHOR: Is there a critical vulnerability here where the entire country had to be stopped because of something that probably, you know, involve one sector or another?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, you know part of what you're seeing here is an abundance of caution and making absolutely certain that operations were safe. But this is one of the reasons why we need to get these answers and have this review of exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEE: Now, as for President Biden, Chris, we know that he was briefed on the matter earlier this morning by Secretary Buttigieg and said -- and told the secretary that he was to report directly back to him once there's been a determination on what caused all of this. And the president also said that once that determination is made and they know more, then the administration will have some sort of response.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Carol Lee, Blayne Alexander, Tom Costello, Jeff Guzzetti, hopefully, we'll not be talking anytime soon about anything like this but I appreciate you all being with us today. We've got a lot of other news today we want to get you caught up on.\par \par \uc2 After months of Battlefield setbacks, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is putting a new general in charges of his invasion of Ukraine. The previous general only lasted three months. This change comes as Ukraine warns that Moscow was planning a major new offensive, and the Pentagon is bringing Ukrainian troops to the U.S. to train on the Patriot missile defense system as early as next week. The Biden administration announced it would send the weapon system to Ukraine weeks ago. The new training marks a new level of U.S. involvement with its partnership with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 One human rights activist is calling it a massacre as the death toll in Peru rises to 47 after the deadliest day of clashes on Monday, deepening the political crisis there. 17 civilians and one police officer were killed in the south during demonstrations with protesters and human rights activists accusing the country's security forces of using lethal force. Peru's attorney general is now launching a preliminary investigation against the country's new president, his prime minister, and several other officials on charges of genocide and homicide.\par \par \uc2 Students at the University of Idaho are still on edge after they begin their spring semester just two weeks after an arrest was made in the grisly quadruple stabbings near campus. As the suspect prepares to appear in court again this week, students say they want a conviction but also a sense of security.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm a little worried about the safety here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think that would go a long way for bringing peace back to the community.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We find out later that he's literally a student on campus and who's walking around with us. It just -- it felt like they were trying to keep it from us or something.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Many students say they're now arming themselves with flashlights and mace. Others say they plan to take self-defense classes.\par \par \uc2 Well, they said they were going to do it and now they're doing it. How the Republican-led House just took on abortion, impeachment, and basically the entire federal government in less than 24 hours? Plus, the ongoing destruction in California as massive storms and flooding kill at least 17 people. We'll speak with the Sheriff of hard-hit Santa Cruz County ahead. You're watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: House Republicans wasting no time trying to make good on campaign trail promises notably, forming a new House subcommittee designed to probe what they call the weaponization of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 Also turning their attention today to two abortion-related measures and filing articles of impeachment against DHS Secretary Mayorkas over problems at the border. I want to bring in NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali, \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  senior national political correspondent Ashley Parker, also with us, former House Oversight Committee spokesperson for Republicans and current Democratic strategist, Kurt Bardella. Thanks to all of you.\par \par \uc2 Give us some more specifics, Ali, what are the GOP moves right now?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, this is really the start of setting the tone for what the next two years are going to look like. We know that oversight and the use of that subpoena power were going to be critical hallmarks of the way that Republicans leverage this period of divided government. We now see what that's going to look like in part by the actions of some of these standing committees, but then also with the creation of things like this, what they're calling weaponization of government committee.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is likely to be chaired by Jim Jordan. It means that he's going to have a lot of power on key committees, including the Judiciary Committee, but he's going to be one of the main figures in this Republican push for oversight against the Biden administration, as well as multiple agencies throughout. I think the important thing when you think about this weaponization of government select committee is that they're still figuring out right now the ratios, how many Republicans to Democrats are going to be on it. But the lesson that Democrats learned from the way Republicans dealt with the last Select Committee, you'll remember, the January 6 committee is they're still planning to seat members on this committee.\par \par \uc2 Jeffries will make that decision in the coming weeks, of course, but it's important because it will allow them to do what Republicans frankly couldn't during the January 6 committee appearances, which is offer fact- checks in real time and disrupt the narrative the Republicans will try to build as they move forward with their investigations here. So, again, the investigation piece is important and then we're also seeing Republicans today move on two anti-abortion bills. What they're targeting here is born alive legislation. Infanticide remains illegal in this country, of course, but what we're talking about here is effectively late-term abortions, which represent around 1 percent of all abortions nationally in the U.S., Chris.\par \par \uc2 But not full agreement within the Republican conference that this is one of the earliest moves that they should be taking. Some Republicans including Nancy Mace and other more moderates in the conference saying this is an example of Republicans not learning the lessons of 2022 and the midterm elections where so many of these tight races hinged on reproductive access.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, exactly, Kurt. I mean, these might be the hot issues for the Republican base but abortion is -- wasn't a big winner in midterm elections. So, is it the right thing for Republicans to be focusing their time and energy on right now?\par \par \uc2 KURT BARDELLA, FORMER HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE SPOKESPERSON FOR REPUBLICANS: Well, you know, governing is about prioritizing, and for some reason, Republicans in Congress have made the calculation that they'd rather spend time chasing phantom investigations against the Biden family that they'd rather double down on the agenda that was clearly rejected by the majority of Americans in the last midterm election. And so instead of talking about the kitchen table issues, the inflation, the economy, of things that they actually ran on in the midterm elections, they're doing something completely different.\par \par \uc2 And I just don't think that's going to end up being a political winner. I think that most people in this country, they're not looking to Washington for investigations, they're looking for Washington for common sense solutions to the problems that they face every day. I don't think most people in this country really care about \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  . I don't think they care about these witch hunts that Republicans are going to be chasing and these, you know, grievances that they're going to air through this new committee that they formed. I think that they want Republicans and Democrats both to do what they said they would do in the midterm elections and that's do common sense governing.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I mean, Ashley, he's right, number one, but also -- I mean, this is messaging. These have no chance of passing in the Democrat-controlled Senate. That's not something unique to this Congress or this party. But what they are doing, you know, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  -- whether it's \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  or threatening impeachment over what are essentially policy differences, is that a different level than we've seen in the past largely?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PARKER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Right -- that's exactly right. These messaging bills are things that both parties do that signaled to their base, their priorities, and what they care about. The challenge, of course, on the messaging side briefly for Republicans, is while some of these issues may animate the base, what we've seen is that the base has often produced extremist far-right candidates who can win a primary and then can't win a general election and that's why Republicans have been so frustrated in 2018, 2020, and 2022.\par \par \uc2 Now, in terms of what they're actually doing, you're also right, that is a sort of second level, especially this committee to investigate the weaponization of government. And one thing that is particularly ironic about this is President Biden's predecessor, former President Trump, very clearly tried to weaponize the government.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 I guess you could argue on the one hand, it didn't need to be investigated because he just made it clear he would come out and say publicly or tweet that he wanted his attorney general to act as his own personal lawyer and fixer, that he wanted his enemies to be investigated. But it is an interesting question. I would be curious to know since it sounds like there will be Democrats on this committee if they will try to expand this to stuff that preceded President Biden.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. I'm curious, Kurt, how you see this playing out. Let me read you the way Politico put it saying the committee will cause "mutually obscure -- assured obstruction, as the House GOP aims this weaponization panel at the DOJ." Where's this all going?\par \par \uc2 BARDELLA: Well, I think that you're going to see Republicans push to try to investigate things that frankly, the Justice Department is not going to talk about. They're not going to undermine existing cases that they're currently investigating to comply with this. You know, really, it's a PR stunt more than anything. This is just a publicity stunt through the congressional committee.\par \par \uc2 I think Democrats are smart and learning from the lessons of Kevin McCarthy not seating members on January 6. You cannot give the opposition just an open field to do whatever they want. You have to have opposition and resistance. And in our case, contextual setting to actually tell the facts about what's going on, point out the inaccuracies, point out the hypocrisies.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that it'll be one of the most important decisions that \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  makes in his first you know couple of months as a leader is who is he going to appoint to serve as the ranking member to go up against Jim Jordan? I'm looking at members like a David Cicilline, like a \uc2 Ted Lieu\uc2  , like a Madeline Dean, members who are very publicly conscientious, who are good on social media, who are good at messaging, understand the politics, and have played very outsized large roles in some of the previous investigations into the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And in the middle of all this, Ali, there's still the ongoing George Santos controversy.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The Nassau County GOP signaled they're going to call for him to resign. I think there was another Republican group that said the same. Speaker McCarthy just said he should not be seated on any top committees. So, where does that leave him?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Any top committees, Chris, I think that's an important distinction here because as we've been talking to Republican leaders here in Congress, they have been reticent to weigh in in any kind of concrete way about George Santos's future here. Though, there are probably conversations that are happening right now as they're making up these committee assignments of what comes next for George Santos.\par \par \uc2 The thing that works in his favor is that the margins are so slim here that Republicans after just going through that bruising speakership battle, probably do not want to have to deal with a special election in a place that is going to have millions of dollars poured into it as Democrats try to not just seat that they badly need and Republicans try to keep a seat that they badly want to keep in their column. But on the other side of this, it does make it difficult for Santos to continue on in this fashion, especially when the very people that elected him, his own state party, his own county party, are now coming out and saying that he's disgraced the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  , and they think that he should resign.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, Ashley Parker, Kurt Bardella, thank you all so much.\par \par \uc2 And right now, let's take a live check from Los Angeles airport, LAX, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  outage causing travel disruptions all across the country. There's always a domino effect, right? We're going to get an update with one of the busiest in the nation's airports right after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We've been planning this trip for two months, three months, so -- and we have Airbnbs booked, rental cars booked, you know, and that's going to be a nightmare trying to get a refund from everything.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:34]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Air travel continues in disarray across the country. Airports with the most cancellations right now, Denver, Dallas-Fort Worth and Atlanta. That's according to FlightAware. In today's predawn hours, the frustration about it all started pouring out in real time from Providence to Milwaukee, Rome to Madrid, with one recurring word. Stranded.\par \par \uc2 Complaints about air travel have quadrupled since before the pandemic. And today's nationwide ground stop is simply the latest in a string of headaches, inconveniences, well, just plain bad service by airlines. But the flying public really doesn't have a lot of options.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No information from the airline, no e-mails, no text messages, nothing. What I'm hoping is that we're not part of the people that's grounded. Fingers crossed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We did try calling the airline this morning and there wasn't -- we weren't able to get through. Probably because everybody was trying to call.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What makes all of it even worse is that it comes of course as demand is sky high. People trying to escape from all of the rest of life's problems. Be it COVID, bad weather, the economy or never-ending fights in Washington.\par \par \uc2 NBC's Antonia Hylton is at Newark Liberty International Airport in New Jersey. So, look, if you'll allow me a kind of bad pun, Antonia, can we look at this from 30,000 feet? I think people face so many compounding problems right now, whether it's the pandemic, or inflation, or any number of things. So they want to get away, but then they face something like what we saw this morning. How much stress are people you're talking to right now feeling? And is there a kind of cumulative effect?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Chris, you know, on the faces that I have seen throughout today, I would say the overriding emotion is more resignation, sort of exhaustion more so than outrage which we saw a lot of during the holiday season. I think you're right to connect all of this to recent events because that's on the forefront of, you know, travelers', on customer's minds, too. Not only is this not the first delay of cancellation that they've dealt with, it sort of feels cumulative, but there are also the cost issues.\par \par \uc2 You know, one traveler, you actually just played a clip of my conversation with him a moment ago, he was talking to me about how this could have this sort of snowball effect of causing him to lose out on a reservation on the \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  , caused him to lose out on plans and things that he booked for this afternoon with friends on a trip that he has had in the works for months.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 And while that doesn't sound like the end of the world, you know, all of us are feeling the squeeze from inflation, and those cost issues were on the front of the minds of several of the travelers that I talked to today, and because this is, you know, chalked up as sort of an act of God, it's not the airlines' fault themselves, that means that in terms of the reimbursements, the kind of waivers people are going to get back, they are going to need to be in touch with those airlines to see case by case because it's not guaranteed.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to some of the conversations we've had with travelers today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAUREN GARTLAND, NEWARK TRAVELER: I think we all anticipate coming to the airport and everything going smoothly. And today that didn't happen. You can only control the controllable, and this is out of our control. So I can either be all pissed off and angry and let it ruin my day, or I can look at what can I do to do something that's going to make my heart feel bigger and better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: I appreciated the advice that she gave me there. I actually took it to heart for myself. It's a helpful reminder right now. Travelers, customers feel powerless. They are waiting to find out, you know, what was the root cause of this. What kinds of steps might the federal government, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  need to take to really modernize our transportation system? I think this sort of adds to the piling questions after the travel chaos that we all dealt with just a couple of weeks ago. And consumers feel like they deserve to know more about what the conditions are going to be, especially as they are paying top dollar to travel right now, Chris.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, while there's this sense of resignation and exhaustion, people are sitting around waiting to make sure they can get on their flights out today, fingers crossed, but there's sort of these long-term questions about people's relationship with these airlines, their relationship with travel and the kind of demands and desires they might want to see on the federal level in terms of change.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Antonia Hylton, always good to see you, my friend, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We've got new details about those classified documents found in President Biden's former office. So what will the DOJ do about it? And what's it mean for potential charges against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ? That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:03]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We're learning new information about what is in those classified documents found in an office used by President Biden when he left office as vice president. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  " reports that the documents include briefing materials on foreign countries dating from his time as VP. NBC News has not independently confirmed that reporting.\par \par \uc2 The big questions out there right now are, what is the DOJ doing right now, and does that complicate Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  's decision on whether to charge \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in NBC's Ken Dilanian and Ashley Parker is back with us.\par \par \uc2 So, Ken, based on your reporting, what, if any, legal concerns should the president or the people around him have?\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS JUSTICE DEPARTMENT CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, it's a crime to remove classified documents and take them to an unauthorized location knowingly. That's the keyword. There has to be criminal intent. In most cases where classified documents end up in the wrong place in the government are not crimes, they're mistakes. They may be subject to civil penalties. You can lose your security clearance.\par \par \uc2 So it's pretty unlikely, I would say, based on the information that we have right now, that anybody, the president or anyone around him, has criminal jeopardy. Again, unless there's a big thing that we don't know about this situation. Really the questions are more political because -- and especially in the context of the Biden administration Justice Department investigating President Biden's political opponent \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over a very different, but somewhat parallel case of the mishandling of classified documents.\par \par \uc2 And so it's a question of, you know, how did those documents get to the wrong place? Did President Biden, then Vice President Biden, play any role in requesting them or causing them to go to that office where they shouldn't have been? And then why did the White House sit on this information and not disclose this when they first learned about it six days before the midterm elections, Chris?\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the Justice Department, they're conducting a preliminary review under the special counsel stature. Our understanding is \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has already been briefed on many of the results of this review so they know a lot of answers to these questions and now \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has to decide whether to appoint a special counsel to conduct a criminal investigation or whether no criminal investigation is warranted in this case, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Ashley, the reporting is they did the right thing, right? They found these and they immediately notified the archives that they existed. The political question is being discovered six days before the midterm elections, why they didn't say anything then. What are you hearing about that?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PARKER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, there's two political questions. One is that, which so far including even among some Democrats, feels like a fair question. And then there's the -- I guess it's more of a political issue as Ken said, but that right now is the biggest challenge facing President Biden. That Republicans are already up in arms about this investigation into former President Trump's mishandling of classified documents. And this gives them the -- and while the two things are not at all the same based on what we know currently, right, former President Trump defied subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department had reason to believe that he was lying to them and deliberately not turning over classified documents and other documents that belong to the National Archives, this gives Republicans the perfect political cudgel, they call it whataboutism, call it false equivalency, you know, but to basically say it's a witch hunt against former President Trump and why isn't President Biden being treated the exact same way?\par \par \uc2 And even though there's a lot of holes in that argument, they are already making it and you can expect this to continue for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. Speaker McCarthy is calling the handling of this case compared with the investigation into former President Trump's handling of classified documents a double standard. Here's what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Why does this Department of Justice treat people differently? Every time we find something that comes up before the election dealing with the Biden family, it's pushed under the rug.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let's be clear. Two very different situations here.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 Fewer than 12 documents versus more than 300. Biden's team turned them over within a day rather than the \uc2 FBI\uc2  having to come in and seize them. But based on conversations and what you're hearing are they going to make this a short-term thing or is this going to be used by Republicans on messaging on Trump going forward?\par \par \uc2 PARKER: This is going to be used by Republicans as messaging going forward. And again, while it bears repeating, not the same at all based on what we know right now, is a political gift that allows them to claim hypocrisy, to claim a double standard, and to try to put the blame back on Biden. And this is something that former President Trump is masterful at. Sort of shamelessness is one of his political super powers. So when he is accused of something, he will often say, well, that guy did it, too. And that's what we're going to see right now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ashley Parker, Ken Dilanian, good to have both of you on the program. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Millions of folks in California remain under flood warnings right now as a string of massive storms leaves more than a dozen people dead. We'll speak with the sheriff of hard-hit Santa Cruz County, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:05]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: In California today millions are under a flood watch, at least 17 people have been killed by been killed by multiple days of devastating storms.\par \par \uc2 Check out this video of a railroad bridge destroyed by flooding along the Santa Clara River. And then in Los Angeles, look at this, a sinkhole swallowed this car.\par \par \uc2 The rain is continuing to fall in Northern California. And the state's governor warns the situation will only get worse.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D), CALIFORNIA: We're not out of the woods. We expect these storms to continue at least through the 18th of this month. We expect a minimum three more of these atmospheric rivers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The 18th of this month. Let's bring in NBC'S Marissa Parra in Guerneville, California, which is about an hour north of San Francisco, and Jim Hart is the sheriff of Santa Cruz County, which is about an hour south of the city on the bay.\par \par \uc2 So, Marissa, what's the latest where you are? How are people coping with what looks like utter devastation there?\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, so we're along around the Russian River and for people who live around here they know that in Sonoma County, this is a place that has seen flooding with the Russian River before but this has been stressful for people who live around here. We actually just spoke with someone who lives in a trailer just a few feet away from where we're standing really.\par \par \uc2 And what she was telling us was that they were forced to evacuate from here because they were concerned that this river would flood. And at the end of the day, whether it does or does not, this is a very stressful time for everyone around here and obviously, this is a storm, a storm system, over the last 10 days, that has caused so much damage. You've seen so much of it just plastered across your screens there.\par \par \uc2 Sinkholes opening up. We've seen mudslides, landslides. Of course the flooding around here. I mean, this has been costly in every way. At least 17 lives lost. A 5-year-old boy washed away in the flood waters in Paso Robles. We understand that they've been spending the last couple of days trying to search for him but the weather, which has been relentless, has made it very difficult to do so.\par \par \uc2 We understand that they did resume the search this morning. They're taking to the skies with a helicopter and drone when they're able to, but the problem is, we've seen so much rain that they haven't been able to do that as much as they would like to, but they're taking to the waters to try to find him and so far the only thing they have found is his shoe. This is a heartbreaking situation.\par \par \uc2 And then of course just all of the damage that this has caused. So, you know, we're only a couple of miles away from where that toddler was killed last week, so this has been costly in every single way.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Marissa, thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 So, Sheriff, I'm familiar with Santa Cruz County. It's one of the most beautiful places in the country but wow, have you got challenges. What's the situation on the ground there in Santa Cruz right now? What are the biggest problems you're facing?\par \par \uc2 SHERIFF JIM HART, SANTA CRUZ COUNTY, CALIFORNIA: Yes, well, we're still in the middle of it, Chris. It's raining right now. We have more storms coming in, but just the devastating series of storms where our low-lying coastal areas are covered in debris. There's been about 130 homes damaged about, about eight homes destroyed, school closures, we have about 19,000 people without power, 65 roads that need to be repaired, and just a lot of damage from one end of the county to another.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Do you feel like right now you have the resources you need to help all those folks?\par \par \uc2 HART: Yes. We're working with our fire partners and the police departments. We've evacuated about 25,000 people at one point during this series of storms. There's a small town here in this county called Soquel. It hadn't flooded since 1982. It's flooded twice in the last 10 days and so constantly evacuating and then allowing people to come back in and get cleaned up but then another storm comes in and causes more debris flow and flooding.\par \par \uc2 So it's just -- yes, it's a mess. The county, I've been here for 35 years, experienced fires, floods, earthquakes, a couple of tsunamis, and this is about as bad of a natural disaster as I've seen in this county.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And we just heard from Governor Newsom, he was in New York County touring the damage yesterday, warning things are going to get worse in the coming days. What you are hearing right now about what the forecast is, and are you prepared for that?\par \par \uc2 HART: Yes, we were on the phone with the National Weather Service this morning, and it sounds like Friday, Saturday, Sunday, we're looking at about another three to six inches of rain, and we just don't know where the next debris flow or mudslide is going to happen. But we have people in position, in different communities, to help out. And so we're ready, and we'll take on whatever comes our way, but we've had about 23 inches of rain in the last 10 days and we've experienced extreme drought prior to this series of storms.\par \par \uc2 So the soil is saturated. Our rivers are flowing high, so we're concerned about some levee breaches, and we're working hard to keep our community safe.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:07]\par \par \uc2 The good news is, in this county we had a tree fall on a man in a park and suffered a fatality in that situation but so far with almost two straight weeks of nonstop rain and atmospheric rivers, we've experienced one death. So the key is keeping our community members safe, keeping them out of the low-lying areas, and then we'll start worrying about recovery and repopulation once the series of storm leaves our area.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, we'll be thinking about you and wishing you well, as well as we can, under these circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Sheriff Jim Hart, very kind of you to take the time to talk to us today. We much appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HART: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Now to some good news, just days ahead of the \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  playoff game, Bills safety, number three, Damar Hamlin, was just discharged from the hospital and is cleared to go home. The team says he will be rehabilitating from home and with the \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  . Of course, after suffering that cardiac arrest on the field, as millions watching. We also have learned that Damar Hamlin is applying to trademark "3 is Back" and yes, indeed, he is.\par \par \uc2 You know, I got a little seed planted in my head from one of my producers. What if he actually showed up there at Bills Stadium on Sunday? Or even on the jumbotron, I mean, I don't know anything, and maybe it won't happen, but anyway, number three is back.\par \par \uc2 And that's going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday, 1:00 Eastern Time, right here on MSNBC. "KATY TUR REPORTS" will be here after this very short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020230112ej1b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for January 10, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Tim O`Brien \par \uc2 7,105 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Lawrence. I know you have a big guest tonight.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: I do. I have \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  . She has a big announcement she made today. She`s going to join us with an exclusive interview about that.\par \par \uc2 But, Alex, I`m kind of -- I`m kind of thrown because of the profundity of that last thought, that for some it will always be 2020.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WAGNER, MSNBC HOST, "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT": I`m thinking of specific people, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, I need -- I need a minute with that.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: One person in particular.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, I think -- I think if he could only just hold all of the aging process is to 2020, that would be pretty great for all of us, not just him. I`d like him to do it for all of us.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Science, science, science.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Alex.\par \par \uc2 WAGNER: Have a great show.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, after never reaching a unanimous agreement on who should be their speaker of the House, House Republicans are now unanimous about two things. First, House Republicans unanimously voted for a bill to defund the police. That`s right. The very first thing House Republicans voted on was defunding the police. The police they want to defund are the federal tax police.\par \par \uc2 House Republicans have voted unanimously to defund the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  in a way that would specifically target the capacity of the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  to investigate the tax returns of the very richest people in America. Republicans voted unanimously to make it easier for America`s richest people to get away with tax crimes.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans have now voted unanimously to create what our first guest tonight calls the Republican committee to obstruct justice. Republicans are calling the committee which is structured as a subcommittee of the House Judiciary Committee the, quote, Select Subcommittee on the Weaponization of the Federal Government, and that is a perfect example of what former Harvard professor and senator, Daniel Patrick Moynihan, called semantic infiltration.\par \par \uc2 The concept of semantic infiltration is to infiltrate your opposition with your language that forces them to tell the lie that your language tells. A perfect example is Fox News. Every time someone says the words Fox News, they are lying about what Fox News does.\par \par \uc2 That is a deliberate lie created by \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  when he created the Fox Republican propaganda channel and infiltrated the opposition to Republican propaganda by forcing the opposition to call his propaganda channel Fox News. The propaganda channel created by \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  is not now and never has been in the news business, which is why I don`t use the name that \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  gave to his propaganda channel.\par \par \uc2 Every time you use the name that \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  gave his propaganda channel, you have successfully allowed \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  to infiltrate your own vocabulary, your own semantics. That is what Daniel Patrick Moynihan meant by semantic infiltration.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are masters of semantic infiltration. They came up with the phrase pro-life to mean anti-abortion. Everyone is pro-life, but Republicans seized possession of that phrase so that the news media would call only Republicans pro-life, which most of the news media does because the news media is highly susceptible to semantic infiltration. The news media is the target of semantic infiltration.\par \par \uc2 Most of the news media will make no effort at all to try to overcome the semantic infiltration that the Republicans are using in the name of their new subcommittee which as I said they are calling the select subcommittee on the weaponization of the federal government. It is a subcommittee to investigate federal investigators. So, today`s the day that Republicans made congressional history by creating a new investigative committee to launch investigations where Congress has never launched investigations before including ongoing criminal investigations by the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 That`s right. For the first time in the history of the House, a committee has been created to investigate ongoing criminal investigations by the Justice Department, including criminal investigations by the Justice Department of Republican members of Congress, some of whom want to be members of the new subcommittee to investigate the investigators who are investigating them.\par \par \uc2 Here is who the Republicans will not be investigating.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Have you made any messages about how you`re going to handle George Santos in this time?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: No, I`ll tell you when we do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, no investigation of a pathological liar who lied about every single aspect of his personal history and lied about his income and delivered a mysterious seven hundred thousand dollar contribution to his campaign, no investigation of any of that. No investigation of George Santos.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Given all that we know now about what Congressman Santos lied about his resume, the various inquiries into him at the federal local level, do you think that he should be a member of Congress?\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): Well, you saw him seated last week. There were no challenges to that. This is something that`s being handled internally. Obviously, there were concerns about what we had heard and so we`re going to have to sit down and talk to him about it. And that`s something that we`re going to deal with just like there`s a lot of other things we`re going to deal with.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So we`re going to have to sit down and talk to him about it.\par \par \uc2 George Santos is under criminal investigation by the U.S. attorney for the eastern district of New York. George Santos is under criminal investigation by the attorney general of the state of New York. George Santos is under criminal investigation by the Republican district attorney on Long Island.\par \par \uc2 But George Santos is not going to be under investigation at any time by the Republican House of Representatives. We`re going to have to sit down and talk to him about it. That is a lie.\par \par \uc2 Steve Scalise is never going to sit down and talk to George Santos about all of George Santos`s lies and possible criminal conduct, including criminal financial conduct. But Steve Scalise and Speaker Kevin McCarthy and the Judiciary Committee Chairman Jim Jordan are all eager to have their special subcommittee go to work, investigating any federal government investigators who are currently investigating Republicans like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Jim Jordan and Pennsylvania Congressman Scott Perry who according to Cassie Hutchinson`s testimony to the January 6 committee asked for a pardon from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for his possible criminal conduct in trying to overturn the 2020 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 The January 6th committee has evidence that some Republican members of the House of Representatives asked \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for pardons for their possible criminal conduct in trying to overturn the 2020 presidential election. Today, after a Republican on the floor of the House asked the Democrats to, as he put it, populate this committee with serious Democrats. That is to say send serious Democrats to this new subcommittee to investigate the investigators.\par \par \uc2 After that request for serious Democrats to be appointed to that committee, \uc2 Jim McGovern\uc2  , the top Democratic on the \uc2 House Rules Committee\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM MCGOVERN (D-MA): I would just say in response to the gentleman who said that he hopes that we populate this select committee with serious Democrats, that he populates uh the committee with Republicans who did not ask for a pardon, who did not have their phone seized by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The Republicans will have nine members of the committee, the Democrats will have six members of the committee. The only guarantee we have about members of the committee not having asked for pardons is that none of the Democratic members of the committee will have ever asked for a pardon from anyone.\par \par \uc2 Today in his first speech on the floor of the House, our first guest tonight said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DANIEL GOLDMAN (D-NY): The primary purpose of this special subcommittee is to interfere with the special counsel`s ongoing investigation into a conspiracy to overturn the 2020 election. This is a shocking abuse of power. But it`s not just the usual efforts by members on the other side of the aisle to once again do Donald Trump`s dirty work, this time they`re trying to protect themselves.\par \par \uc2 One of them, a member from Pennsylvania, had his cell phone sees pursuant to a court order finding probable cause that he committed a crime.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Yet he has indicated that he wants to be on this subcommittee so that he can undermine a criminal investigation into himself.\par \par \uc2 My Republican counterparts can dress up the subcommittee with a menacing name, but let`s call it what it really is, the Republican committee to obstruct justice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Before Daniel Goldman went to the floor of the House to deliver that speech, he went to the office of George Santos this morning to hand deliver a copy of a six-page complaint against George Santos that Congressman Dan Goldman and Congressman Ritchie Torres delivered to the House Ethics Committee this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VDIEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: We haven`t seen a single movement on the part of Republican leadership. They have not commented on this publicly. They have not condemned George Santos and all of his lies they have adopted him as one of their own.\par \par \uc2 REP. RITCHIE TORRES (D-NY): Best outcome is accountability. I mean we have to send a message that if you defraud the voters, you`re going to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Leading off our discussion tonight is Democratic Congressman Daniel Goldman of New York. He served as a House majority counsel for the first impeachment trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . He is a former assistant U.S. attorney for the Southern District of New York.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight. An important day, your first speech.\par \par \uc2 But let`s begin with that New York congressional teamwork that we just saw. There you are, all members of the New York congressional delegation, including George Santos now, and two of you, you and Ritchie Torres, are going to Congressman Santos`s office to do him the courtesy of giving him a copy of what you have filed with the House ethics committee about your fellow member of the New York congressional delegation. What reaction did you get at Congressman Santos`s office?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Well, he actually came out of his personal office as I went into deliver the deliver the complaint which is required by the rules and I just said to him, I have a complaint for you and he asked me to give it to his staff which I did. But it is really a spectacle that George Santos has a congressional office. This guy has deceived his voters, he`s defrauded his district in New York, which is right near mine and he`s in Queens, in the northern parts of Long Island.\par \par \uc2 And the Republican Party is doing nothing about it. They have not condemned him they have not rebuked him they are embracing him as one of their own and it`s no surprise because they don`t believe in free and fair elections themselves it appears. So why would they condemn George Santos who undermines our free and fair elections?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: All right. I`m going to ask the control room to do a replay on that opening of the door shut where I think in the background, we actually see George Santos which I didn`t notice until Congressman Goldman just mentioned it. Let`s just watch.\par \par \uc2 Is that Congressman Santos walking across the back of the shot there in the background right there? Is that who we`re seeing back there?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Yes, that is.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So that -- that was your moment of truth. Is that the only time you`ve gotten a chance to have a word with George Santos directly?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Yes, it is, although I have spoken to a number of other Republican members of the New York delegation, one of whom, Nick LaLota, out in the first district in New York, further out on Long Island, has also called for an ethics investigation into George Santos. So it is clear there are -- there are many people on the other side of the aisle who are horrified at the fact that we have a serial fraudster in our ranks in Congress and that`s why Congressman Torres and I filed this complaint today because the House needs to hold its own accountable. We cannot go out asking Americans to follow the law if we don`t follow our own laws.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I have one more request for the control room and I don`t want to go all \uc2 NFL\uc2  here, but if we could freeze frame George Santos in the background as he`s walking across the background of the shot, that would be great and we`re happy to talk -- oh there it is. There`s the shot. There`s the still version of the shot.\par \par \uc2 That`s the moment of Congressman Goldman presenting Congressman Santos with the truth and as we just told on that shot for a moment, Congressman Goldman, it seems to me based on your experience in congressional investigations which you`ve participated in the past, also as a U.S. attorney that the mystery seven hundred thousand dollars that that man in the background of that shot claims to have personally lent to his campaign is the central mystery of how that campaign operated.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:01]\par \par \uc2 And it seems to me possibly the most important legal question and legal jeopardy that George Santos is facing.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: I think that`s probably right. I will -- I`m certain that the prosecutors in the eastern district of New York are looking into that seven hundred thousand dollars, the Devolder Organization, which is an entity that he created after he announced his candidacy in 2021, and that miraculously he disclosed to have over more than one million dollars in assets and that he had received a $750,000 salary for most of nearly all of which he siphoned into his campaign.\par \par \uc2 There are a number of red flags that come with that fact pattern, including the fact that this organization was a sham organization that was really just designed to allow Republican donors to far exceed campaign finance limits and they used it as a pass-through via George Santos to get into his campaign. And I would expect that the federal prosecutors are looking at that it and that`s what the House ethics committee should also be focused on and if George Santos truly did nothing wrong as he said he did today, then he should be happy to turn over all of the documents underlying the Devolder Organization and any checks that he received and the campaign finances for his recent campaign.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: To your first speech on the House floor today in which you changed the name to a more accurate name of this new committee that the Republicans have created, calling it the Republican committee to obstruct justice. A couple of things, I think by now, with our audience understanding that the Democrats get to have six members of that committee, many of them are hoping that Congressman Daniel Goldman will volunteer for service on the Democratic side of that committee.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Well, that`s certainly up to the leadership I`d be happy to serve the caucus in whatever way they think would be valuable. But I have plenty of experience taking on Jim Jordan during the impeachment investigation. And I think what is clear about this committee to obstruct justice is it is designed to stick a cog, stick a fork in the wheels of justice and to prevent the Department of Justice, the special counsel`s office from properly executing their investigations to undermining the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in whatever way they can, to undermining executive branch agencies.\par \par \uc2 I saw this when I was a staff member on the House intelligence committee there is an increasing attack on our law enforcement agencies on our intelligence community from the Republican Party and this committee with its incredibly broad powers is now the vehicle that the Republicans are going to use to try to attack those people who protect us at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman Dan Goldman, a freshman congressman in his first week in as a member of the House representative, is already one of the most experienced congressional investigators in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Dan Goldman, thank you very much for leading off our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: Thanks for having me, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  will join us for an exclusive interview on a very big announcement that she made today about her candidacy for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in the state of California. \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:23:14]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The first time \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  went to work in a House committee hearing in 2019, we saw right away that \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  knew how to use props and knew how to play to the cameras recording the hearing. \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  whiteboard instantly became her trademark in hearings.\par \par \uc2 She joined us on this program after that first hearing and has been a steadily rising star in the House of Representatives since then. On Friday night, even though she had no opportunity to speak on the House floor, \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  knew the power of the prop.\par \par \uc2 As you see in this photograph where she is so plainly reading or pretending to read a book titled "The Subtle Art of Not Giving a F", \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  is joining us tonight for an exclusive interview about the big announcement that she made today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KATIE PORTER\uc2  (D-CA): The threat from so-called leaders like \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has too often made United States a place where rights get revoked. Special interests get rewarded. And our democracy gets rid. Especially in times like these, California needs a warrior in Washington.\par \par \uc2 That`s exactly why I`m announcing my candidacy for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in 2024. I don`t do Congress the way others often do. I use whatever power I have to speak our truth to the powers that be. To not just challenge the status quo but call it out. Name names and demand justice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNEL: Joining us now is Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  of California, who is now a candidate for \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Porter, the first question what has to be what America has been wondering since Friday night. Were you really reading that book or just pretending to read that book for the photograph?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: I was absolutely reading that book.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:01]\par \par \uc2 It was my fourth book of the week. I read a book a day during those interminable alphabetical votes in which Republicans struggle to find leadership. And so that book I picked up into little free library on my walk for my basement studio here in Washington to the Capitol building.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Okay, so you really were reading it.\par \par \uc2 So, Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  , that`s the seat you would be running for. She is 89 years old, she would be 91 years old during the reelection campaign if she joins the reelection campaign, 92 years old when elected, that would take her term up to 98, six-year term. She issued a statement today in reaction to your announcement, saying everyone is, of course, welcome to throw their hat in the ring, and I will make an announcement concerning my plans for 2024 at the appropriate time. Right now, I`m focused on ensuring California has all the resources it needs to cope with the devastating storm, slamming the state and leaving more than a dozen dead.\par \par \uc2 What is your reaction to that statement by Senator Feinstein?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, I would echo what Senator Feinstein said. I think he`s exactly right. This should be a race in which there are different people stepping up and Californians get to decide who represents them in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  is a trail blazer. The path she has created for women in politics is one that I was able to follow right through the House of Representatives, and she has accomplished a lot, and I look forward to following in her footsteps to represent California in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 I also want to echo what Senator Feinstein said about the terrible storms ravaging California right now. But those storms really illustrate what is at stake. We cannot continue to have a Washington that ignores the threats of climate change, that is willing to cozy up to special interests like big oil rather than stand up to them.\par \par \uc2 So, my kids are in California, I hope they`re staying safe and dry, and my thoughts are with the responders and all of the people who are suffering and having to hunker down and be safe. But this is what`s at stake in this race. Are we going to have a Senate that stands up to problems that climate stain change, that understands our future is under attack?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You are already being criticized by some Democrats in California, California Democrats who could be rivals to you in this campaign, for announcing in the middle of this storm, saying that that`s an inappropriate time, when California is being battered this way, to announce a campaign like this.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, as I just said, those storms, which are going to keep coming in the next few days, in the next few months, in the next years, a result of us ignoring problems for too long in Washington, decade after decade of not being willing to fight for our environment, of not being willing to understand that climate change is real.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s important that we understand storms illustrate we do not live in typical times. In my three terms in the House, I was sworn in the first time, it was a longer shut down government history, the second time, during an insurrection, and the third time 15 votes to try to find a leader of the House.\par \par \uc2 Washington is broken and we are seeing the facts in California right now of that broken system. So my heart goes out to everyone in California and I`m watching in supporting our local officials, our transit workers, our first responders, everybody who is helping right now, and we need to send people from Washington who are going to solve problems like this. That`s the best thing we can do to ensure Californian the country are safe going forward.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, it`s very common for sitting senators who are choosing not to run in the next election cycle to announce immediately after the election that we just had. I know the senator I work for, New York Senator Daniel Patrick Moynihan, he announced he would not run for election on November 7th of 1998, meaning he would not run in 2000. He announced the full two years ahead of time, immediately after the previous election. So that any people, any candidates thinking of running for it could use the full time they need in an extremely expensive strength like that to mount a campaign. The campaign was eventually mounted by \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  , who won that seat.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to voters, though, who don`t think about, you know, these challenges of running in the biggest state of the union, with the most television markets of any state of the union, most expensive place to run anywhere in America, what do you say to voters who are exhausted by the politics of the last election, that, here you are, as far as, in their sense of it, immediately announcing the campaign for another election?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: I think Californians and Americans understand that Washington isn`t delivering for them. And they see election after election that doesn`t change. Corporations continue to get their way in Washington while families get left behind.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:00]\par \par \uc2 And what they want to see is leaders step up and run to represent them, to put their interest first, not to cave to special interests.\par \par \uc2 And so I will tell you, in talking to voters across Orange County in my own very competitive races, they want more from Washington, not less. They want more people to listen to them, to engage with them, to visit their communities, to hear their problems.\par \par \uc2 So announcing early gives me the time to do the work of understanding California and being ready to fight for it when I`m sworn into the Senate.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Quickly before you go, let me ask you a current House business question then I just asked Congressman Goldman. Would you be willing and volunteer to serve as one of the Democrats on this new select investigative committee that the Republicans are creating?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, I`m always willing to step up and serve when asked. But I will tell you, I`m very, very excited about the work that we`re going to have to do on the Oversight Committee. That will be a full-time assignment, I think, as well.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to continue my work on the House Natural Resources Committee. But of course I`m always willing to step up and help my colleagues, help Democrats, help Americans get the government that they deserve.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  , hoping to become Senator \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  . Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, former Trump company CEO \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2  was sentenced to five months in prison for his role in a criminal tax fraud conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest David Cay Johnston, calls Weisselberg`s imprisonment the roadmap to convict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:31:33]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2  , the 75-year-old chief financial officer of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  is currently incarcerated in the west facility of Rikers Prison in New York City. He was sentenced earlier today to five months in jail and five years of probation for devising and operating a 13- year-scheme to defraud New York authorities and evading tax payment on more than $1 million of income.\par \par \uc2 Here is what Allen Weisselberg`s lawyer had to say about the sentence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS GRAVANTE, JR., ALLEN WEISSELBERG`S ATTORNEY: Today is obviously a difficult day for him but it is a day that he has been preparing for for many months since he entered his plea last august. Mr. Weisselberg came to court today ready to begin his sentence and he is grateful that it has now begun.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2  reached a plea deal with Manhattan District Attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  , where \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2  pleaded guilty to all of the counts in -- the deal was all 15 counts against him in exchange for testimony against two subsidiaries of the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 As a result, factoring in good behavior, \uc2 Allen Weisselberg\uc2  is only expected to serve about a hundred days of his sentence. The Trump company faces penalties of up to $1.6 million. That will be decided in a sentencing hearing on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Al Capone have a few things in common. Al Capone was a famous 1920s organized crime boss who ordered the infamous so-called Valentine`s Day Massacre in Chicago in which seven members of an opposing gang were lined up and executed firing squad style.\par \par \uc2 All of that earned Al Capone the title of Public Enemy Number One in Chicago newspapers. But none of that, none of it earned him a day in prison.\par \par \uc2 Al Capone and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  both grew up in New York City. Al Capone got kicked out of school at age 14 after punching a teacher. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  claims he did the same thing when he was in second grade.\par \par \uc2 In his ghost-written book "The Art of The Deal", \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  proudly proclaimed, "Even in elementary school I was a very assertive, aggressive kid. In second grade I actually gave a teacher a black eye. I punched my music teacher because I didn`t think he knew anything about music and I almost get expelled.\par \par \uc2 This being \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , of course, even that has been called a lie by the music teacher in question, who says that it never happened.\par \par \uc2 Al Capone was eventually sent to prison but not for the worst things that he did. Not for the murders. They got Al Capone for tax evasion. He was charged with 22 counts of tax evasion in 1931. He was convicted of five of them, which got him an 11 year sentence in federal prison, and that could be Donald Trump`s next commonality with Al Capone.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is David Cay Johnston. He`s a Pulitzer prize-winning investigative reporter and the author of "The Big Cheat: How \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  fleeced America and enriched himself and his family".\par \par \uc2 Also with us Tim O`Brien. He`s senior executive editor for Bloomberg Opinion and author of "Trump Nation". He is an MSNBC political analyst and the host of "The Bloomberg Podcast Crash Course" which launched today.\par \par \uc2 David, let`s begin with the criminal liability that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could be facing. Could he end up sharing that resume item with Al Capone of tax criminal?\par \par \uc2 DAVID CAY JOHNSTON, INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER: Absolutely. If \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  , the Manhattan district attorney, goes after Trump personally and he`s got pretty close to a slam dunk criminal fraud case because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 1984, filed a tax return with something called a Schedule C in which he had no revenue and took over $600,000 in expenses.\par \par \uc2 [22:40:01]\par \par \uc2 JOHNSTON: The state of New York and the city of New York auditors disallowed that. Trump demanded a trial. In both cases he lost.\par \par \uc2 The judges said that`s civil fraud, not criminal, but civil. And his own lawyer testified against him and essentially accused him of being a forger.\par \par \uc2 Well, once judges have said that, you are on notice. And yet as candidate and president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  filed 26 more of these Schedule Cs that had zero revenue and expenses. That shows criminal intent.\par \par \uc2 He also had a couple of Schedule Cs where the revenue and the expenses matched to the dollar and a number of retired \uc2 IRS\uc2  officers tell me they have never seen that in their entire careers whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 And I think there`s a very easy case Jurors would understand that \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  could bring, personally, against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , based on these Schedule Cs, which also apply to his state tax returns.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tim O`Brien, now that we have seen the Trump tax returns, thanks to House Ways and Means chairman \uc2 Richard Neal\uc2  who was the Democratic chairman until the Republicans just took over.\par \par \uc2 Those tax returns reveal that I pay more taxes than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . Those tax returns also reveal that if you`re a high school kid with a part-time job, you pay more taxes than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a typical year where he pays $750 or he pays zero. What else did you see in the Trump tax returns?\par \par \uc2 TIM O`BRIEN, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I think, you know, the issues of whether or not he`s going to be prosecuted are obviously germane. He took, I think, at least $300 million in questionable tax deductions over that period of time that I think have to be examined.\par \par \uc2 I think no one really cares about Donald Trump`s wealth or his income per se. In as much as it reflects on his malfeasance and the lies and I think the exaggerations he has told over the years about how much money he has.\par \par \uc2 But I think one of the really salient and important things to focus on is how much money he drew in from overseas. In just two of that six-year period that was disclosed, he had about $100 million in income from overseas jurisdictions while he was president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 That makes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a national security threat. If he`s beholden to foreign private entities or former governments for income, it`s reasonable to conclude that they might have influence over the office of the president and over policy decisions. And that`s not going to go away.\par \par \uc2 He has declared that he is planning to run again in 2024. And we simply, even with this disclosure, Lawrence, we don`t have enough.\par \par \uc2 There are years of tax returns prior to this limited set of six that we received that have to come into the public realm because the issue of Donald Trump`s financial conflict of interest and whether foreign or domestic entities with financial interests of their own have leverage over him is still an unanswered question.\par \par \uc2 The tax returns have come in to the public realm but they`re still limited. They`re not extensive. I`d like to see more from the K-1s ones about his business partnerships. We need more disclosure from those. We need more information about foreign bank accounts. We know from this limited disclosure that he had bank accounts in China, Ireland, the U.K., and Saint Martin. But I think he has others in other jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 And this still has to be scrutinized. And we still have to have full disclosure about the financial forces that have come to bear on this man, because it`s a problem for voters, it`s a problem for taxpayers and it`s a problem for policy makers.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: David, when we hear you discussing the possibility of a New York tax prosecution of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , everyone when they file their tax returns, they basically file a mirror tax return with the federal government, which is using the identical information that they used with their state tax return.\par \par \uc2 So I know everyone out there is wondering, what does this mean in terms of a possible federal tax prosecution?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSTON: Well, there`s plenty of grounds to pursue \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on this including as Tim mentioned, he may have undisclosed foreign bank accounts. And we should be very worried about all these foreign sources of income that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had. And his tax returns, of course, show he`s never been the kind of wealth that he has constantly proclaimed.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:53]\par \par \uc2 JOHNSTON: The federal government can bring a case. The reason they have a state case is that a future \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or sympathetic president to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cannot protect him with a pardon.\par \par \uc2 And what I have outlined, and there are some prosecutors -- I`m not a prosecutor, who have endorsed my approach, is very simple. Jurors will understand it. It`s not a great deal of money. But it is felony crimes. And I think it`s important to bring those.\par \par \uc2 I would be very surprised if the federal government brings a tax charge against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . They have a much more important case to bring about the human intelligence documents were at Mar-a-Lago, especially if we find out that any of our undercover agents or our foreign assets, people who work in other governments like the Kremlin who were feeding information to us were put in jeopardy because of this.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: David Cay Johnston and Tim O`Brien, thank you both very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Thanks Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSTON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Coming up, Damar Hamlin is now raising money for nurses. And in New York City, nurses are on strike for better working conditions.\par \par \uc2 That is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:46:06]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: When you need a nurse, nurses are the most important people in the world. And when you personally don`t need a nurse, nurses are still the most important people in the world along with doctors, who are always there. Always ready when we don`t have any idea that we might suddenly need their healing powers desperately. Damar Hamlin understands that now.\par \par \uc2 When he took the field with his \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  teammates last week, his teammates were the most important people in the world to him, outside of his family. But nine minutes into the game the most important people in Damar Hamlin`s life were the people who could save his life == the doctors, nurses, emergency medical technicians who surrounded him on the field.\par \par \uc2 Damar Hamlin has announced that he is now raising money through the sale of these shirts for medical first responders.\par \par \uc2 And in New York City today, thousands of medical first responders are on strike for, among other things, better working conditions that are more supportive to patients.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIANCA RUSSO, NEONATAL ICU NURSE, \uc2 MOUNT SINAI HOSPITAL\uc2  : I`m in charge of 56 babies at a time. And I also have to be responsible for my staff that`s on the unit. So you could imagine trying to be in charge of 20 nurses and 56 babies is extremely stressful.\par \par \uc2 However, at the same time, all of my nurses have 3 to 4 babies and all needing something differently. So it just, you know, poses huge challenges for us because we can`t give the right amount, the care that we want, to our babies.\par \par \uc2 So the whole thing, the whole issue comes down to staffing. We can`t be stretched this thin anymore. We cannot continue to work three babies, four babies to one nurse anymore. We have to do better. \uc2 Mount Sinai\uc2  has to do better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Lorena Vivas, she`s an ICU nurse and a member of New York State Nurses Association for Mount Sinai Hospital.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight and thank you for what we are learning in the strike about the needs of nurses. What we just heard about a nurse managing 50 to 60 babies and 20 nurses is something everyone needs to know about. And I`m sure everyone wants to improve.\par \par \uc2 What is the stumbling block now on getting what you need?\par \par \uc2 LORENA VIVAS, MEMBER, \uc2 NEW YORK STATE NURSES ASSOCIATION\uc2  MOUNT SINAI HOSPITAL: Thank you, Lawrence, for giving us nurses the opportunity to give the viewers our point of view from the front lines.\par \par \uc2 Simply put, we demand that our hospital, \uc2 Mount Sinai Hospital\uc2  , hire the required amount of nurses for us to be able to practice safely. This deliberate short staffing by a non-profit hospital like ours in an attempt to reduce expenses and widen profit margins is wrong on so many levels.\par \par \uc2 COVID is still very much with us, now this time affecting children. Same with RSV, same with the flu. Now is not time for austerity (ph) measures. We want to provide the safest possible care that we are trained to provide but the hospital is taking that away from us.\par \par \uc2 And we are out there in the streets to let the public know we are not having this anymore.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, I`ve never known nurses to ask for applause. But nurses and doctors got applause in the city you work in every night during the worst of COVID. This city understood your contributions and how you are saving lives.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel as though that feeling of support is still there for you?\par \par \uc2 VIVAS: Absolutely. You have seen hospital leaders come to the media and, you know, disparage us, you know, call the strike reckless. But I want to point out, I want the viewers to be the judge, ok.\par \par \uc2 For the past 5 to 6 years we have been complaining to management that we have been stretched thin.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:54]\par \par \uc2 VIVAS: Our neonatal ICU nurses see legally -- the legal prescribed staffing is one to two sick babies. One nurse to two sick babies. On the regular they handle 3 to 4.\par \par \uc2 I`m a neurosurgical ICU nurse. They`re very, very sick patients. A lot of the times some (INAUDIBLE) three or four patients. I come to work knowing I will not have a break. And that has been the norm for the past several years.\par \par \uc2 And you go to the E.R., you hardly see a nurse. (INAUDIBLE) get mad them because you know what, they have 19 to 20 other nurses.\par \par \uc2 We have been calling management. This has to change and our pleas have fallen on deaf ears.\par \par \uc2 We now refuse to be complicit to this. This is a very powerful statement. You see nurses going out there in the cold. We`re sacrificing not having wages. But those are little sacrifices compared to the risk that the patients are being exposed on a daily basis. Not receiving the proper care that they should be receiving.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Nurse Lorena Vivas thank you very much for joining us tonight. You work for a great hospital and a great hospital should be able to support the great nursing staff there and continue in that greatness. Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 VIVAS: You are very welcome. Thank you for the opportunity.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 [22:56:19]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That`s tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020230111ej1a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for January 2, 2023, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Ryan Reilly \par \uc2 8,256 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 January 2023\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 IRIN CARMON, SENIOR CORRESPONDENT, NEW YORK MAGAZINE: For them to say, we`re not going to keep letting this happen.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Yes. And most of these states will not surprise you or the states where there was formerly slavery that literally went to war against the United States to preserve. There were slavery all over the country, but these are a lot of the states that decided to go to war against us in order to keep it. Not surprising at all.\par \par \uc2 Irin Carmon, thank you very much. And that is, indeed, tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts right now.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Ali Velshi in for Chris Hayes. And a Happy New Year to you. We`re just hours away from Republicans taking control of the House of Representatives. And tonight, Kevin McCarthy might just be the most nervous man in Washington. You see, McCarthy really wants to be Speaker of the House. It has been his singular focus in Washington for years. And that drive is the reason behind his deal with the devil that he made with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 McCarthy has spent years placating Trump and enabling his worst behavior in exchange for Trump`s support and the support of his hard-line allies in Congress for his eventual speakership. It`s why McCarthy went from saying this in the days after January 6th.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The President bears responsibility for Wednesday`s attack on Congress by mob rioters. He should have immediately denounced the mob when he saw what was unfolding. These facts require immediate action by President Trump, accept his share of responsibility, quell the brewing unrest, and ensure President-elect Biden is able to successfully begin his term.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: He went from saying that to going down to Mar-a-Lago two weeks later to kiss Trump`s ring and to beg his forgiveness. So, now, McCarthy`s time is finally here. Tomorrow the House convenes and the speakership vote takes place. And it looks like some of Trump`s most fervent supporters in Congress might now throw Kevin McCarthy under the bus anyway.\par \par \uc2 You see, because of the Republicans surprisingly thin majority in the House, McCarthy can only afford four Republican defectors during tomorrow`s vote. And reporting indicates there may be anywhere from five to ten so- called never-Kevin MAGA congressmen who are not currently supporting McCarthy including \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  of Florida and Andy Biggs of Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Now, if McCarthy does not get the support he needs during the first ballot of the vote, it will be the first time in a hundred years that the vote goes to a second ballot. Even if McCarthy ultimately does clinch the job, he could be starting from a historically weak position which may be why some Republican partisans are loudly pushing for McCarthy in right-wing media.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH\uc2  , FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER: I think the Republican Party right now is in the greatest danger of meltdown that it`s been since 1964. I thought at least the House was going to be a sign of stability and these five guys decide to go out as kamikazes and see if they can sink the whole Republican Party. That`s what they`re doing. This is not about Kevin McCarthy. This is about the right of any five members to basically throw away the entire rest of the conference.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And the would-be speaker himself is making an enormous number of concessions to shore up support for his bid including reinstating the Georgia Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  to her committee assignments which she previously lost as punishment for past statements advocating violence against members of Congress and spreading offensive conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy has also reportedly pledged to support Taylor -- Marjorie Taylor Greene`s bonkers proposal to hold investigations into whether January 6th insurrectionists are being held as -- I hope you`re sitting down. I`m not - - political prisoners. But it doesn`t end there. McCarthy has also promised to bring back an old Congressional rule called Motion to Vacate the Chair. As McCarthy`s proposed it, the rule would allow any group of five disgruntled Republicans at any time to force a vote on whether or not to fire him as Speaker. It`s the same process by the way that was essentially used to push the former Republican Speaker \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  out of the job back in 2015. Bringing it back would effectively kneecap McCarthy`s speakership from the get-go.\par \par \uc2 But in a recent letter, nine current and incoming House Republicans told McCarthy that even those concessions don`t go far enough, which means we could see even more groveling and appeasement before the day is done tomorrow. For his part, McCarthy is projecting confidence.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you have the votes for speaker locked in tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I think we`re going to have a good day tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What are your conversations like with members --\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you prepared to make more concessions in exchange for more support?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: I hope you all have a very nice New Year`s.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:05:05]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: A staffer, by the way, for McCarthy was even seen wheeling McCarthy`s belongings into the speaker`s office which could make for an awfully embarrassing scene should he ultimately lose. But even if he wins, it certainly seems like his deal with the Devil with Trump and Trumpism will end like it always does in further embarrassment and betrayal.\par \par \uc2 Annie Karni is a congressional correspondent at the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  where she reported on Kevin McCarthy`s struggles to keep his conference in line. Tim Miller is a former spokesman for the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  who`s currently a writer at large at the Bulwark they both join me now. Good evening to both of you and happy New Year.\par \par \uc2 Annie, nice to see you again. What`s the likelihood of Kevin McCarthy being speaker by the end of tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 ANNIE KARNI, CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Even his allies are saying they don`t quite see a path to 218, the magic number that he needs to win the vote. But you know, there`s still hours to go. There`s deals to do. It`s difficult to see how it works out. As you said, there`s five hard no`s. Perhaps even more concerning than that is these nine or ten Republicans that signed this letter on Sunday who are kind of we don`t know where they are. They`re indecisive.\par \par \uc2 It`s harder for McCarthy to count votes when he doesn`t quite know where they stand. That included a few incoming freshmen lawmakers. So, the numbers aren`t there for him right now. Something could happen between now and tomorrow morning. One thing that Pro McCarthy people are saying is the biggest thing he has going in his favor is that there`s not a clear alternative. We can talk about that a little bit later but there`s not -- like, their main argument has been OK, you don`t like Kevin McCarthy but who is -- so what? So, what`s your plan?\par \par \uc2 There`s not a -- there`s not like a \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  standing in the wings right now. They think they had that going for them. We`ll see. I`m not going to make prediction right now. This is very volatile situation.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You are wise. You`ve been in this business for a long time. You`re wise enough to do that -- not to do that. Tim, let`s address what Annie was saying. We could -- she said we`ll talk about it later, let`s talk about it right now. There`s -- there isn`t a clear alternative. There`s Andy Biggs who some people voted for. About 15 percent of the conference suggested they might take him. I don`t know whether that was just a protest.\par \par \uc2 But what happens? If Kevin McCarthy doesn`t have the 218 he needs, do they just keep voting until they decide on Kevin McCarthy? Because look, I guess anything can happen what are -- what are some of the possibilities?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, WRITER-AT-LARGE, THE BULWARK: Conceivably they keep voting until they end up with Kevin McCarthy. And I think the five "never Kevin`s" get their -- get a get their scalp, get more concessions, maybe move that motion to vacate down from five members to just one member. And so, I think that there is a potential alternative.\par \par \uc2 And Steve Scalise, he`s not running for it. You know, he comes from the more conservative part of the caucus traditionally than McCarthy has, hasn`t made as many enemies in MAGA world as McCarthy has despite all his efforts to suck up to MAGA world. But again, Scalise isn`t trying. So, I think that would be a kind of situation where he might emerge should you get to ballot four, five, six, and it becomes clear that these never McCarthy`s really aren`t going to budge.\par \par \uc2 I think the reality of this situation is that McCarthy is proud -- is more likely than not to end up getting it. You get at least one of the five to give in and then he has the votes. But he`s not speaker in any meaningful sense if these folks are running the show, right? If they`re in charge and he might get the bust -- you know, he might get to sit behind Biden at the State of the Union, but who`s actually the leader of the party, you know, if he has -- if he has to make concession after concession to the Matt Gaetz`s of the world.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: So, this is an interesting question, Annie. What does it do to weaken him? If he gets the votes -- I mean, even if he makes this concession where whether it`s five members of the House can call for a vote on his speakership or one, in the end, they`d have to convince half the House to get rid of him as Speaker. How much does this weaken him? Obviously, he`s in the shadow of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  who always counted votes and didn`t put votes forward that she couldn`t win. So, he comes after a very strong Speaker of the House. What is the potential weakening of Kevin McCarthy if he limps his way over the finish line here?\par \par \uc2 KARNI: Well, he`s going to be in debt to \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  for one who has been whipping votes for him, who has made a political calculation that she`s very vocally pro-McCarthy and just the reality of a razor-thin majority in the House means that the more extreme members of the conference, the chaos caucus so to speak, has more power. So, he is -- you know part of the concessions he made over the weekend was more ideological diversity on it important committees.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to see some of these more extremists on Appropriations, on Ways and Means, on committees that really matter to running the government. We`re going to see -- you know a lot of these people have been really pushing for the impeachments of if not Biden, then Garland, and Mayorkas, and we`re going to see -- you know, one --another concession he made over the weekend was this subcommittee to investigate -- I forget what exactly it`s called -- about the weaponization of the federal government.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s really a concession for the extreme members to go after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the DOJ agencies they think Biden has used to go after his political enemies. So, we`re already seeing it now. It`s just going to be giving these people a bigger platform, a bigger microphone, more say and powerful committees. And it`s -- you know, it`s going to be led around by the tail, basically. Leadership led around by the tail.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:40]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Tim, it used to be, even when the Tea Party started, there were policy orientations around it. There were -- there were small government policy orientations, but Annie makes a good point. McCarthy`s entire strategy right now even if he did become speaker is I think seven investigations or eight investigations that he`s going to hold. These Freedom Caucus folks who are holding out want yet more investigations. None of which is policy. None of which -- I don`t really understand how this helps them win the next election in 2024.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Look, I think it`s important to understand, the insurgent part of the party now is all about owning the left. It`s about attacking \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  . It`s about extremism and dealing in conspiracism, right? Look, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  , to Annie`s point, and \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  was like a moderate Republican, are both on McCarthy`s team, right? The fissures are not policy fissures. The debate that they`re having is not over oh, can we -- what can we do to better help the American people in the next year. You know, it`s over what tactics can we use to be more aggressive uh in investigating Biden. That`s exactly what the entire fissure is here.\par \par \uc2 There was one uh Republican member who was anonymous. All the brave -- Republican members get brave when they`re anonymous. You know, who said -- I think it was to Jake Tapper today that these guys are all doing it because they`re narcissists. And I think that speaks to the situation. It is their narcissism. It is their desire to get on conservative media that is driving this not actually public service, not trying to help the American people.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Annie, I mean, at some point, McCarthy could say it`s not very leader-like