was really just a breakdown in communications especially with the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  . You know, it`s interesting reading a lot of these raw materials because remember, of course, the committee originally prepared a full chapter on a lot of this or at least the committee staff did. And subsequently because \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and others and the members of the committee sort of decided to focus on Trump, they ended up moving that to the appendix and made it sort of like a sideshow of -- and took out a lot of the good material that they had in there because they didn`t want to just distract from the messaging about Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, what ended up happening is now you have all this just raw good information in these -- in these transcripts that the committee has been releasing. That really do tell us a lot about what happened that day and had these a really well-conducted interviews conducted by members of the staff who, you know, were well informed in their investigation and were able to ask, you know, the right questions of a lot of these individuals. I think that that really is the key thing is the breakdown in Communications because in the -- a lot of these interviews, you have these former -- these former officials talking about it was just sort of obvious, right? Like, that this was a threat.\par \par \uc2 Although there was never a -- there was never a product that was actually produced that would have been, you know, a large threat warning, a memo uh between DHS and DOJ that would have been sent out universally. But they sort of just saw, yes, this is a big threat but it didn`t seem like that message got all the way through particularly to the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  . But the \uc2 FBI\uc2  at least had put in some mechanisms. I think that, you know, basically, this is the sort of stuff we tried to figure out after uh after 911. And it seems like we still have a lot of these same silos and problems today.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: How much of these failures were a product of the system, you know, bad communication -- bad communication channels? And how much of it, as some people are implying, may have been deliberate or may have been caused by interference from somebody who didn`t want the response to be as robust as it should have been?\par \par \uc2 REILLY: You know, I don`t know if I`ll go quite that far. I mean, I think definitely in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  `s response to this afterwards, there are definitely people within the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for just political reasons who don`t want these cases to move forward. We see that within some number of these so-called whistleblowers that have been talking about January 6 cases in the public. In fact, one of them, you know, quit because he didn`t want to work on January 6 cases. And that`s not only one agent or one special agent who`s along those lines.\par \par \uc2 But I did think that like, there`s a tendency within the bureau and this was articulated by an individual named \uc2 Michael Sherwin\uc2  who was the former U.S. attorney during this key period. And he was talking about how the bureau does tend to have this tendency to sort of look down at open source, right? So, he says -- I don`t want to say arrogance but I think there`s a type of institutional arrogance that we don`t -- I don`t get your need to rely upon this guy`s \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed for intel. We have our sources we know what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 I think that that`s an ongoing problem essentially for the bureau because, you know, a lot of these cases now are being solved and handed to them on a silver platter by these online sleuths. So, it just use all these open- source information. And it`s -- they`re playing a really big role in this investigation. And I think that that`s something that the bureau is really going to have to contend with because, you know, as a -- like, a legacy of the organization, a lot of these special agents are really big on sources and, you know, having these connections and having these inside looks.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, we`re in the digital age now. A lot of this is happening out in the open. And you know, intelligence isn`t something that is within the bureau thought to be the career path that you necessarily go on, sometimes they look down at analysts. It`s not as high of a rank obviously as uh as a Special Agent. And I think that that relationship, as well as a relationship with the DHS -- with DHS and the analysts over there, it definitely needs to improve because, I mean, frankly they`re just giving them these cases based all upon open source research.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:31]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Ryan, the report itself, 850 pages roughly, it`s a lot of material. And what the committee is doing is releasing these transcripts and these documents because to put that into the -- into the report would have made it a several thousand-page document. That means that regular people are not going through them typically. People like you are, journalists are. What -- is there anything that`s coming out of the raw material, these texts, these documents, these emails these transcripts of communications that is not, in your opinion, captured by the report?\par \par \uc2 In other words, if you read the whole report cover to cover, if our viewers read the report cover to cover, would they fundamentally have the gist of everything that was going on?\par \par \uc2 REILLY: I think they would in terms of the Trump story for sure. I think that there`s a lot more to be told. And I just -- I think that also the recommendations that you would normally see it come out of a committee like this in terms of what we can actually fix to make sure this doesn`t happen again aren`t really present in that -- in that full report because there`s just this pivot. You know, there`s essentially a way to try to take away any potential blame for intelligence failures or law enforcement failures by saying that no one could have anticipated what exactly