 we are approaching the two-year anniversary of the January 6th attack on the Capitol this Friday. And with the ongoing release of the House Select Committee`s final report, the criminal referrals and the reams of information we continue to get. Anticipation is growing over whether potential indictments are coming and when and for whom.\par \par \uc2 Last week on this program, this is how Committee Member and Democratic Congressman Jamie Raskin described the potential prosecution of the ex- president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): He knew the crowd was armed and dangerous. He said take down the mags, these were his people, they weren`t going to hurt him. He unleashed the crowd to go and fight like hell and said that when fraud is involved, there are very different rules that are allowed and so on. I mean, it`s just -- it`s just an open and shut case from my perspective. And I hear from public defenders all the time saying they have had hundreds of clients go to jail on far less evidence that exist against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Is he right? And will the special prosecutor and the Attorney General agree with Jamie Raskin? To help answer that question, I`m joined now by the former New York Deputy attorney general and former Federal prosecutor Dania Perry who co-authored a report for Brookings last year that outlined the evidence presented in the January 6th hearings against Trump and his associates and the criminal laws applicable to their actions.\par \par \uc2 Dania, good to see you. Thank you for being with us. Put it straight up. Do you think that either the Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  or the Special Prosecutor Jack Smith or the two of them together are going to take up a case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ?\par \par \uc2 DANYA PERRY, FORMER DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL. NEW YORK: Well, they`re certainly actively investigating it. There`s no question about that. And I think Representative Raskin is absolutely right. From his perspective as well as from mine and I think from anybody who paid close attention to the hearings and read the materials, it is an open and shut case certainly for indictment. And I think there was a mountain of evidence that was presented that would support, but most -- I would say most of the violations that the committee is referring to the Department of Justice, I think the Insurrection um referral is maybe a little aggressive although highly symbolic, but I think there`s more than enough that was presented.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:04]\par \par \uc2 I think it`s anybody`s guess then what Special Counsel Jack Smith does with it and ultimately, of course, the Attorney General. But I think this certainly has put a lot of pressure on them to take it very seriously. Of course, the events themselves should have put that pressure on them. But here we are now as you said coming up on two years and the clock is ticking. And the evidence is in not just before the Select Committee, of course, but we know that there are at least two grand juries working overtime to hear their own evidence. So, I think decisions will be made early in 2023 and we`ll see from there. Then we`ve got -- if indictments do follow, then you know, we`re in an unprecedented and uncharted waters and trial schedule and motion practice and all hell probably breaking loose.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Yes, and indictments are indictments. And then there`s, as we know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his lawyer`s ability to slow walk everything. It`s a long process. Do you have any sense of -- I mean, obviously you don`t have inside information on this, but any sense of what the timing on such a process would look like because indictments would be literally the very first step. In most -- in most cases, that`s -- it`s the thing that immediately follows an investigation.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: It`s highly variable. In my experience, and I`ve been both with the Department of Justice and against it for most of my career, decades now, and it`s all over the map. We just saw with \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  . He was indicted within approximately a month of the collapse of his company. Other times you see complex large-scale investigations that can take years.\par \par \uc2 So, here what we do know is that this is an active and aggressive investigation and the special prosecutor has made very clear that he is moving forward, you know, all speed um ahead. So, it should be particularly given the upcoming election for which Trump has already declared his candidacy, I would imagine it would be within the first half of 2023 if there are to be charges, But that is a guess and yours is as good as mine on that.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Well you say -- yes, it`s highly variable. It could take year. You in June, in your report from Brookings, you and your colleagues laid out sort of in real-time what the evidence that was being presented or the -- what the stuff that we were hearing in hearings because obviously, Jack Smith won`t count that as evidence. He`d have to -- he`d have to do it differently. But what was being said and how it applied to laws. And I think the point that you and your colleagues were trying to make is that you are hearing -- I`m not a lawyer so I`m going to use the word evidence. You`re hearing evidence of crimes that were committed and these are the crimes. These are the laws that they have -- that would have been broken. Is that enough for Jack Smith or does he have to redo all of it?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: He will certainly have to redo all of it. He already has had some of the key pieces of evidence. We know. That he`s already had many witnesses who`ve testified in the grand jury. He now has the full body of information that the committee has marshaled. So -- but he`s going to look at it for sure independently and from his own lens. The select committee, you know, as representative Raskin said, they have a perspective but it is a bit of a one-sided perspective by design. I mean, in this case, the Republicans chose not to put those on the committee who might have had different points of view, who might have pushed witnesses or cross-examined them more. So, that is what the special counsel is going to do. He is going to kick the tires and test the evidence. He`s going to look at stuff that maybe is not as helpful to the case as well as on the stuff that is.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Danya, thanks very much for your report. Thanks very much for that report that you put out that sort of gave us so much information in real- time about what was going on in front of us. Dania Perry.\par \par \uc2 All right, still to come, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  succeeds. Where Democrats had failed for more than a decade and that was by helping a Democrat get elected governor of Arizona. Donald Trump`s lasting impact on America`s purple states is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: This was the scene at the state capitol in Lansing, Michigan yesterday as the governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  , was sworn in for her second term. It was a culmination of a big win for Democrats in last year`s midterm elections, one that was certainly not a given as Whitmer herself explained in an interview.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): There was so much chaos politically and in the environment. I don`t know if I would, you know, get an opportunity to serve for four more years. I never imagined I`d win by almost 11 points and come in with a whole new legislature.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: In November, Governor Whitmer resoundingly beat her Trump-backed Republican challenger Tudor Dixon 55 to 44 percent. And Democrats also won the majority in the state legislature giving Whitmer`s party control over the Governor`s Mansion and the capitol for the first time in nearly 40 years.\par \par \uc2 Now, one of the main priorities that Governor Whitmer plans to get done with that majority is repealing a state law from 1931 banning abortion. That would follow the victory for abortion rights won in November when Michigan voters approved a constitutional amendment creating the right to reproductive freedom. And of course, the reason that abortion was such a driving issue in the midterms in Michigan and across the country is because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Republican Party`s successful efforts to overturn Roe v. Wade made it so.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:28]\par \par \uc2 Now, even the ex-president admits as he wrote in a fake tweet over the weekend, "It was the abortion issue poorly handled by many Republicans that lost large numbers of voters." But abortion was not the only issue that contributed to Trump and his party giving away the midterm elections, there was also election denialism which helped propel Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  to victory in Arizona over Trump-endorsed Big Lie proponent Kari Lake. And this morning, Hobbs was officially sworn in as Arizona`s fifth female governor, fifth female governor.\par \par \uc2 Just one week after an Arizona judge rejected Lake`s last bid effort to overturn her loss, he ruled there was no evidence of misconduct that would have changed the outcome of the election. So, in Arizona, Democrats will hold three key offices, Governor, Secretary of State, and now, on the right side of your screen, Attorney General after a recount just completed last week confirmed that Kris Mayes won that race by fewer than 300 votes. All three of these people defeated Republican candidates who denied the results of the 2020 election and still refused to concede their losses in 2022.\par \par \uc2 As the new year begins with these new Democratic administrations beginning in Michigan, in Arizona, and around the country, the lesson of the Midterms is clear Donald Trump`s messages failed and he will have to come up with something else if he`s going to get his fumbling third bid of the Presidency off the ground.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right, the data is in from 2022. It paints a stark picture of gun violence in this country. There were 648 mass shootings last year. That`s four or more victims. That`s the second-highest number on record coming in just behind 2021. And so, it`s no wonder that many Americans live in a sort of a state of high alert fearing that they may be caught up in the next horrific event.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the context for this story highlighted in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  today. Last March, a 22-year-old man was spotted coming out of a bathroom of an Atlanta supermarket "wearing body armor and carrying six loaded weapons" including an AR-15 style rifle. Panic and terror ensued along with the evacuation of the store. Thankfully, as the young man`s lawyer notes, he did not make "any threats or fire any shots and he was arrested without incident."\par \par \uc2 But as the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  puts it, his arrest kicked off a long and as yet unresolved legal odyssey in which the criminal justice system waffled over what it could charge him with and whether to set him free. Clearly, visiting the grocery store with a trove of guns had frightened people but was it illegal?\par \par \uc2 Here to help us dive into that question is \uc2 Ryan Busse\uc2  , a former firearms executive. He`s the author of the book Gunfight: My Battle Against the Industry That Radicalized America. And \uc2 Kris Brown\uc2  , president of the Brady Organization that works to end gun violence. Welcome to both of you. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Kris, there`s a lot to the story, this Atlanta story. And in fact, the man in question is back in jail but it still remains unclear and unresolved. Did he commit a crime? His lawyer says no.\par \par \uc2 KRIS BROWN\uc2  , PRESIDENT, BRADY: Right. I know. I read that article this morning. And I actually tweeted out about it as soon as I read it because ultimately what we see here is a long-standing push by the \uc2 NRA\uc2  to make it so we have guns everywhere. It`s not a surprise only in America would we find someone who had not one, but two, but multiple guns including an AR and body armor in a \uc2 Publix\uc2  , and it`s absolutely terrifying.\par \par \uc2 What`s worse is that in many states that have removed permitting systems both concealed carry and open carry, the issue for prosecutors is what is the norm for behavior without permitting systems that are robust around where and when you carry guns in public. You have a difficult time as a prosecutor making the case that what was happening was grossly irresponsible and reckless which is the kind of standard you need to bring these kinds of charges.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Ryan, you come from an industry and I`m an avid consumer of the publications that come out from the industry because they`re so interesting to understand and how the psyche comes around, that has built this psyche over years. That has caused demand for guns that in most cases people don`t need. This is not a Second Amendment thing. This is people owning a lot of guns and the -- and the paraphernalia that goes along with it, because it does attempt to normalize that sort of behavior.\par \par \uc2 RYAN BUSSE\uc2  , AUTHOR, GUNFIGHT: Yes. Thanks for having me, Ali. I think as with a lot of what we face in America, sometimes the tragedy isn`t what`s illegal, the tragedy here is what`s legal. And as you note, this guy didn`t need six loaded guns including an AR and a tactical shotgun to go grocery shopping, right, but yet Georgia is one of the states that Kris mentioned that has recently rolled back concealed carry permitting.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:10]\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think Americans need to think about it this way. We have about 270 million registered vehicles in the United States, and you don`t have to travel very many of our roads to know it seems pretty crowded. We have a lot of traffic laws, right? We`ve got lines on the road, we`ve got bumpers, we`ve got speed limits. We`ve got -- we`ve got police that are patrolling everything. We`ve got 415 million guns in the United States and we have the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and the NSSF in an irresponsible slice of the American Gun consumers of which I`m not one of those.\par \par \uc2 I am a proud gun owner. I hunted yesterday with my boys. But I`m not okay living in a country with 415 million guns where we`re reducing the amount of controls on ourselves, right? That`s just insane. We wouldn`t -- we wouldn`t roll back with the laws on all the on 270 million cars, right? You have more cars, you need more laws to control them so that -- so that, you know, people can stay safe. And that`s what we`re facing right now.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Kris, you`ve worked hard to sort of clarify that message. Again, generally, it actually works. Most Americans are in favor of certain forms of regulation around guns whether it`s red flag laws or background checks. And we`ve made incremental progress -- very incremental progress on a federal level. We`ve made better than incremental progress on a state level with respect to guns. But how do we get folks to understand this? That when people say it`s about the Second Amendment and the right to self-defense, people in war don`t carry the weapons that the average American has.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, my partner is actually retired Navy. He was a fighter pilot. And before I came on today, Ari, I talked to him about what his requirements were flying over Bosnia for example. He was issued a gun every time he left the carrier and had to check it back in every time he got back. That`s the standard on most military bases in this country. You`re not allowed to carry a gun unless you have a specific reason on that duty on that day, and then it goes back in the armory.\par \par \uc2 I think that part of our history and heritage is actually quite important. We hear references to things like the Wild West, Ali, and in the end, in most of those Western towns, you had to check your gun at the border. You couldn`t bring it in. And I think what you see in the polls which is rising across the country, Americans saying enough, background checks, I want those, extreme risk laws or red flag laws, and assault weapons bans. It`s because of things like this.\par \par \uc2 We don`t believe that guns everywhere philosophy as Americans is consistent with our heritage and certainly isn`t consistent with our public safety. So, we need to see the states incubate much more of a focus around public safety. And we need these permitting systems to be put back in place.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Hey, Ryan, you know, you talked about cars and the rules that surround cars. And most people are not going to tell you that those rules shouldn`t exist. In San Jose California, gun owners now have to show proof of insurance covering accidents involving their guns, including recklessness or negligence. It`s an interesting proposal. I had a friend of mine suggest this to me a few years back and I thought I don`t -- it sounds like an interesting idea. Do things like that help?\par \par \uc2 BUSSE: Well, I think it highlights the fact that gun owners are responsible for their guns, right? We have to be responsible for the bad things that happen and that`s why we need to adhere to laws and we need reasonable restrictions. I think on top of that San Jose case, we`ve got the Michigan parents from the Oxford, Michigan shooting that are going to be tried for being, you know, essentially enabling their son being irresponsible in the way that they bought him and provided him guns.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think the country is reckoning with the realities of this many guns, and this much irresponsible rolling back of legislation. I mean, we`ve got now this slice of Second Amendment absolutists that literally believe, and I`m not making this up, that there shall be no infringement, as there can be zero gun laws. I mean, you know, you want to Howitzer, you want an A-10 Warthog, like, they believe that we should have this stuff. And it`s just not -- a civil society, complex democracy cannot function that way.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Kris, I`ve got 30 seconds left but do you think we`re making progress? There have been a number of state laws that have been brought in the last year. Does it look like progress to you?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: It really does, Ali, look like progress. And the biggest note for that obviously is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  last year signing our first major Federal law that we had in 30 years, the bipartisan Safer Communities Act. We need much more, but the momentum is with us. And people are fed up and fired up, especially younger voters around this issue.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Thanks to both of you for joining me this evening, and a very happy 2023 to both of you. \uc2 Ryan Busse\uc2  and \uc2 Kris Brown\uc2  . And that is ALL IN on this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020230103ej1200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for December 29, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alicia Menendez, Glenn Kirschner, Cynthia Alksne \par \uc2 7,225 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 December 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:01:02]\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Hi, Nicolle. This is like the Christmas present I forgot to ask for. So fun to get this handoff.\par \par \uc2 NICOLLE WALLACE, MSNBC HOST, "DEADLINE: WHITE HOUSE: I get to see you. Happy New Year, my friend. I feel like you come over. We could`ve been in the basement together.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Like twins. Have the best night, Nicolle.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WALLACE: You too.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: House committee investigating the January 6 attack on the Capitol set to dissolve next week with the start of a new Congress, but not before releasing even more transcripts. A new batch of interviews released today offers new insight into the weeks leading up to the Capitol insurrection, and some of the people closest to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , including \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., and his fiancee, Kimberly Guilfoyle.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to be joined by Glenn Kirschner and Cynthia Alksne in a moment to analyze their testimony.\par \par \uc2 But first, let`s take a moment to note the impact of the January 6 committee. Its works shape how the public`s going to remember the attack on our democracy, something millions of Americans watch unfold in real time on their TV screens, with shock and confusion, and horror.\par \par \uc2 Over 18 months, the January 6 committee interviewed more than 1,000 witnesses, reviewed millions of pages of documents and held 10 public hearings.\par \par \uc2 What the January 6 committee accomplished in those public hearings has redefined what a congressional hearing can do. The committee led by Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  gave clarity to the chaos. The American public had seen the images, but the committee gave us the story, understanding of how we arrived, that unthinkable breach.\par \par \uc2 They connected the dots. Each hearing was episodic with guest stars, plot twists, new details, all pointing to an ultimate question, will the Justice Department charge \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for his role?\par \par \uc2 And now, a new documentary is adding more to our understanding of the events of January 6. We have so often seen the rioters, far less often do we see the victims, the people who were there and were terrorized that day.\par \par \uc2 "January 6th", co-directed by our next guest Jules Naudet, is told from the perspective of the first responders and the survivors of the attack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 STEVIN, \uc2 CAPITOL POLICE\uc2  OFFICER: I arrive, I get in the line. Directly in front of me, I just see anger, anger, lots of angry, angry people. And they appear to think that I uh am part of the problem they`re saying a lot of very vulgar things.\par \par \uc2 MUHAMMED, CAPITOL POLICE OFFICER: Some of our (EXPLETIVE DELETED) you, you know (EXPLETIVE DELETED) police. They looked at you and they said, pick a side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Pick a side. Pennsylvania Congressman Susan Wild was sitting in the House gallery, listening to objections about Arizona`s electoral count when \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  alerted members that the Capitol had been breached.\par \par \uc2 The violent mob invaded the building, the image of Congressman Jason Crow comforting Congresswoman Susan Wild symbolized to many the terror those in the Capitol were facing. She`s one of the survivors of the attack interviewed for January 6.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. SUSAN WILD (D-PA): As I`m hearing everybody else talking to their loved ones, I`m actually going through a mental struggle about, do I call my children so they can hear my voice, so I can hear their voices, or do I not call them because I don`t want to worry them.\par \par \uc2 And I decide that, no, I need to talk to my kids. My goal was not to upset them, more than -- more than anything else. I just didn`t want them to be upset. And so I say, I`m okay, I`m going to be okay, and in response, my son says, what do you mean you`re going to be okay, we can hear gunshots and we can hear glass breaking?\par \par \uc2 [22:05:07]\par \par \uc2 And that`s the first that I realized that they could perceive what was happening and right after that, I thought I was -- immediately after I hung up, I thought I was having a heart attack.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Joining us now, documentary filmmaker Jules Naudet. His latest film "January 6th" will premiere on Discovery Plus on January 5th.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, what I am struck by is we have seen so much visual evidence, but you have compiled even more. I wonder what you think this film, what these interviews will add to our understanding of the case against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies.\par \par \uc2 JULES NAUDET, "JANUARY 5TH" DIRECTOR: Well, what we really wanted to do -- my brother and I -- was to show that on that terrible day, there was also what we saw, you know, kind of the worst of what we can see, there are also these moments of humanity, these moments of courage, some that you just talked about. A moment where, Congresswoman Wild is -- I think she`s going to have a heart attack. And she feels a hand on her hand saying we`re going to be okay, and it`s Congressman Crow.\par \par \uc2 And just these little moments of humanity, these moments where you think that, you know, you cannot take any more and all this, this moment of hope with what we wanted to show on that terrible day. What the people there and we have a tendency to forget, but yes, they were congressmen and women senators staffers journalists, and police officers.\par \par \uc2 But under that uniform, under that title, there is a mother, a father, a husband, a wife, and we wanted it that people remembered also not just the political element to all this, but the human toll that it took.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right. There`s someone on the other side of that cell phone call.\par \par \uc2 You know, as you`re working on this film, the January 6th committee, the hearings are happening, did the hearings have any impact on the final cut of the documentary?\par \par \uc2 NAUDET: Not really because, again, you know, I`m -- my brother and I are not journalists. We don`t do investigative reporting. For us, it`s really telling the story of the of the human beings in the middle of that.\par \par \uc2 So, of course, we`re watching um the committee work, but I think that was more the investigation into itself and since we were doing something kind of different, I think where it really -- it helped in a way was finding more images. You know, we we`re fortunate to have an amazing team of researchers. But that`s where we discovered some new images which we thought could illustrate even better the points we were what people were trying to say.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: To this point about the human element of the story, your film ends with the funeral of Brian Sicknick and I wonder why the decision to end the documentary with those images?\par \par \uc2 NAUDET: Yeah. I think it was important to remember the toll of that day. You know, I think it`s as some in the documentary say, it`s true that you know a lot of people do not really either understand or know what happened or have refused to really look at it too closely. And here to see the toll that it took, of course, on Officer Sicknick who died from it, but also the four other police officers who took their lives in the following months, I think we wanted to highlight the cost, the real, true human cost of that, not even talking about the lingering trauma for all the survivors.\par \par \uc2 But we wanted to honor the people who stood in the line that day, then unfortunately didn`t make it back.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Jules Naudet, his new documentary "January 6th" will premiere on Discovery Plus on January 5th. Thanks so much for being with us, Jules.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk now about accountability for the former president and his allies, joining us, Glenn Kirschner, former federal prosecutor, and Cynthia Alksne, former federal prosecutor. Both MSNBC legal analysts.\par \par \uc2 It is good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there`s just now a treasure trove of transcripts here, Glenn. They remind me of the year I spent as a paralegal. What stood out to you in today`s transcripts?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, you know, Alicia, at least you there`s so many important incriminating nuggets, things like \uc2 Stephanie Grisham\uc2  saying that she was talking with people in the West Wing on January 6th, and they were telling her that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is loving the fact that his supporters are fighting for him at the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Now, we all know that`s hearsay, right? But what, you know, the investigative stock and trade of prosecutors, particularly when they`re in the grand jury with witnesses is to follow that sort of hearsay trail back to the -- you know, the horse`s mouth, so to speak, and figure out who are the people that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was saying this to because that`s not hearsay. Those are admissions out of the mouth of a party opponent and they`re admissible in court.\par \par \uc2 So there were lots of nuggets like that that I think are going to give the federal prosecutors so much to work with and frankly, so much work to do in the grand jury.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:01]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Cynthia, to Glenn`s point, I want to read you some more of what \uc2 Stephanie Grisham\uc2 , who again was one of Melania Trump`s aides, said about Trump on January 6th. Quote, Grisham: I heard from several people in the West Wing, he was just watching it all unfold and some of his comments were that these people looked very trashy, but also look at what fighters they were. He did feel they looked trashy but he loved how they were fighting for him.\par \par \uc2 Cynthia what does that tell you about Trump`s state of mind?\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA ALKSNE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, here`s the thing, when you try cases, Glenn and I have tried a lot of cases, one of the things that you do is come up with a framework. And my framework was always, you did it, he liked it, and he`s not sorry. And I tried to find all the evidence that I could do to put in those three categories and what \uc2 Stephanie Grisham\uc2  has done is help us with he`s not sorry.\par \par \uc2 And you add what she says, what we know about him watching and enjoying it, what we know about him saying now even if he got back in office, he would pardon all these people, we know from his conversations to continue to insist that he won the election, when he`s been told by his lawyers, by his campaign manager, by his children, by other staff members, his chief of staff that he lost the election, that that everything it`s going to be easy to prove that he did it, he liked it, and he`s not sorry.\par \par \uc2 And it is time to move forward on the proving of the charges.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Let me pull out another piece of this, Glenn. Trump lawyer Christina Bobb recounts a conversation in which \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  says in a meeting with \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , quote, just give me five dead voters; give me, you know, an example of the illegals voting. Just give me a very small snapshot that I can take and champion.\par \par \uc2 I mean, could this be useful to prosecutors, especially when you talk about the Georgia investigation?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: You know, absolutely. I think Christina Bobb is going to be spending some quality time in the grand jury with the federal prosecutors and what that exposed is there what you just read, Alicia, is that \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  was pretty enthusiastic about joining Trump`s efforts to overturn the elections results.\par \par \uc2 And when you hear things from Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , like hey, just find me five dead people who voted, isn`t that reminiscent of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  telling \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , secretary of state down in Georgia, well, just find me 11,780 votes. And I`ve always maintained, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has some real problems in connection with that Georgia grand jury investigation because \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  himself, a Republican, said these calls that I was taking from \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , I was interpreting what he was saying as you know him asking me to toss out lawfully past ballots.\par \par \uc2 So it really feels more and more like \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  was is an enthusiastic participant in Donald Trump`s scheme.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Cynthia, I want you to take a listen to what Representative Adam Schiff said tonight about what he thinks should happen to Trump`s enablers like \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , \uc2 John Eastman\uc2 , \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 . Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): Those that broke the law should be prosecuted and I hope that the Justice Department will track down all the leads that we have provided and where they feel there`s evidence to prove beyond a reasonable doubt, they won`t hesitate to indict some of these enablers of this crime against our democracy, others for which there may not be sufficient evidence to prosecute, you know, I hope that they will be tarred by their association with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , by their participation in his scheming.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So they would talk a lot about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  I think for a very good reason when it comes to this conversation about accountability. But I wonder how you think the DOJ is approaching these other key figures, these enablers as Congressman Schiff calls them.\par \par \uc2 ALKSNE: Well, the question is who`s involved in the conspiracy, right? It`s a conspiracy to defraud the United States, and a conspiracy usually includes more than one person. So who are those people going to be? Well, it`s probably going to be Meadows, right? Because he was actively involved in it. It`s probably going to be Giuliani who was actively involved.\par \par \uc2 And then it`ll trickle down. The question to me is not who`s going to be involved, but who`s going to flip, because somebody is going to decide they don`t want to be indicted, and they are going to go to Jack Smith at some point, and ask to get a deal. I think this is the beauty really of the Cassidy Hutchison testimony is, as a general policy, I don`t like testimony to come out. I don`t think that helps a prosecution.\par \par \uc2 But in this case, there may be a little silver lining and that is all these people that are witnesses have a chance to sit down and read the transcript of what happened to Cassidy Hutchison. How she was pressured by the Trump lawyer, the Trump Org lawyers, how she struggled with that and to look herself in the mirror, and ultimately decided she wanted to do the right thing, which required breaking away. And that`s what`s going to have to happen with some of these more lower level people who don`t want to be charged in a 731 conspiracy and don`t want to go down with the Trump, Meadows, Giuliani, Eastman ship.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:07]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Glenn, I wonder if you agree with that analysis, that this is about getting folks to flip.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yeah, I do and let me follow up on Cynthia`s point. When you hear other witnesses like Don Jr. and Guilfoyle playing the "I don`t remember" card, well, all we need to do is look at what that lawyer White House ethics lawyer, I think that qualifies as irony, who was representing Cassidy Hutchinson was telling her to do. It doesn`t matter if you recall something, just say you don`t, and you`re going to get away with it.\par \par \uc2 Well, that kind of exposes the M.O. that was being run out of at least Stefan Passantino`s office. And so, I -- and people will say, well, it`s really hard to prove somebody recalls something if they say they don`t, can I tell you, if I don`t recall was a perjury-proof answer, I would have successfully prosecuted very few people over the years. It`s not a perjury- proof answer. You investigate all of the people around the witness who claims not to recall, at least he didn`t know how many times that kind of an investigation produces a witness who says yeah that guy said he was going to go in the grand jury and say he didn`t recall because he didn`t want to give up his friend, his family, his criminal associate, not a perjury proof answer.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Cynthia, before I let you go, just talk to me about the interplay between this investigation and the documents investigation and some of the players that you see connecting the two.\par \par \uc2 ALKSNE: Well, I would hope the document investigation would move forward because it`s a much easier investigation. I mean, and I think it`s not only easier because the proof is out, but there aren`t -- no one is just, you know, thrown down transcripts on the desk of the prosecutors that have to be evaluated and followed up on, and the decision-making is easier because really, the document case -- I mean, come on, the document case is not that hard.\par \par \uc2 You`re not supposed to have the documents. We ask you for the documents. You have the documents. We ask you again. We gave you another chance.\par \par \uc2 We asked you again, we gave you to another chance, and we found all the documents in your possession and you`ve lied about it. I mean, it`s just not a hard case. I`m sorry, it`s just not. Get him indicated, that`s all I can say.\par \par \uc2 This other case is slightly more difficult because of the number of witnesses, the number of transcripts and the decision making that`s required on delineating who to charge and who not to charge and who to flip.\par \par \uc2 But for me, I would get one done, one and done, and get it done right, and then get the second one done. But it is time to get moving.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So I appreciate that you walked us through your more complex framework for the first days and in this case, it`s just like it`s just not that difficult, get it done.\par \par \uc2 Cynthia Alksne, thank you as always for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Kirchner, you are sticking with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Donald Trump`s very bad year about to get worse because the House Ways and Means Committee is expected to make public tomorrow six years of his tax returns, including the year when he started running for president. The day \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  never wanted to come is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:22:57]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The House Ways and Means Committee is set to publicly release six years of former President Donald Trump`s tax returns tomorrow, with a culmination of a years-long legal battle waged by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to keep those returns out of public view, to keep you from seeing them, a battle that ended in November when the Supreme Court cleared the way for the House Ways and Means Committee to access the former president`s tax returns. Members of the committee have defended the public relief of the tax returns as proof that the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2 `s mandatory presidential audit process has not been working.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. STACEY PLASKETT (D-USVI): I, for one, as some of my colleagues have been bemused about, was not initially in favor of releasing the president`s tax returns. As a prosecutor, a former prosecutor, as a lawyer, I had great reserve with the notion of doing that. However, in seeing the report, seeing the dysfunction within the mandatory audit process, as well as seeing the things that should have been looked at, the things that were pointed out by the Outstanding Joint Committee on Taxation from the tax staff as well, that can only be understood with the release of the underlying evidence as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Ahead of the release of Donald Trump`s tax returns tomorrow, our next guest says there`s already evidence that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed tax crimes.\par \par \uc2 Pulitzer Prize-winning reporter David Cay Johnston says a 39-page report released earlier this month by the Joint Committee on Taxation already demonstrates, quote, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  knowingly committed brazen tax fraud during the six years he ran for office and was president. Johnson writes: among the crimes was a tax scheme that, quote, helped the former president convert a federal tax bill that could have been as high as $46 million into a $2 million profit from the federal tax system.\par \par \uc2 He adds: what Trump did again and again and again, taking expenses for businesses with no revenue is so simple, a juror should have no trouble understanding the issues or Trump to be indicted by a federal or New York state grand jury.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:08]\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, David Cay Johnston. He is a Pulitzer Prize winning investigative reporter and the author of "The Big Cheat: How \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Fleeced America and Enriched Himself and His Family". Glenn Kirschner, is also back with us.\par \par \uc2 All right. David, you said that some of these crimes are simple enough for a jury to understand. Break down some of the brazen tax cheating you`ve identified in this analysis.\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOHNSTON, JOURNALIST: Well, in 1984, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was audited by the state of New York and the city, and found to have a, quote-unquote, consulting business, which had no revenue but he took over six hundred thousand dollars of expenses. Auditors disallowed this. Trump demanded a trial.\par \par \uc2 He not only was found by both judges, state and city, to have engaged in civil tax fraud, but this put him on notice that what he was doing, creating a fictitious business that has no revenue and taking big deductions was not allowed. Yet, he did it 26 times over the six years he was a candidate or he was president. That`s powerful and persuasive evidence of criminal intent. And particularly, New York state, through district attorney \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  in Manhattan can use these trials that were held in 1992 to show Trump`s lawlessness.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I`m obsessed with what happened in `84, Glenn, and the fact that he`s basically recycling some of his old tactics. I mean, if there is evidence that he used the same tax strategy that courts have already said were fraudulent, does that then demonstrate criminal intent?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHER: That`s admissible and powerful evidence of criminal intent. I`m so glad that I`m on with David, because I will take his opinion and expertise to the bank all day every day.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, Alicia, you wonder why the American people are so disheartened, disillusioned, demoralized -- these tax returns that are finally being made public have been in the possession of the federal government for years. We now know that there have been \uc2 IRS\uc2  failures, whether it was through negligence or intentional misconduct by the Trump installed \uc2 IRS\uc2  commissioner.\par \par \uc2 The mandatory presidential audits that have been in place since 1977 were not performed by the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . Couple that with the fact that the Trump organization was criminally convicted for basically a tax fraud scheme that ran more than a decade as was Donald Trump`s chief financial officer Allen Weisselberg.\par \par \uc2 And I can`t emphasize this enough, the assistant district attorney in Manhattan argued to the jury in closing arguments that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  explicitly approved that fraud and yet \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not charged in that case. How much evidence of crime, of financial crimes, of tax fraud does it take to begin to hold one man accountable? It is mind-boggling.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: David, we have spent the past week, all of us poring over these transcripts as they`re released tomorrow, we`re going to be poring over a different kind of document these tax returns. What is it that you are going to be looking for?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSTON: Well, lots of people are going to examine where there should be an audit and audit means you go get the business records. But do the final end-of-year balances for his companies match up with the starting year balance for the new company. That`s a common way of tax cheating and manipulating those numbers.\par \par \uc2 Does -- do any of the returns show foreign revenue and if so, from where? Now, they likely will not, but if they in some way put us on to foreign revenue, we want to know about that revenue.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s these charitable gifts where Donald for example gave away land that is earthquake active in Palos Verdes, California, and cannot be developed and took a huge tax deduction for it. There are other examples of his giving away worthless property or undevelopable property, including in Westchester County, and we`ll get more details about that.\par \par \uc2 My concern is that if a prosecution is brought, stick to the clear-cut issues, not the esoteric tax issues that confuse juries. Take what you`ve got that`s clear-cut, stick to those that jurors will easily understand. Frankly, I think a case just on the 26 items could be presented in about three days, opening arguments, bringing the testimony into evidence, and I don`t see any affirmative defenses Trump will have in such a prosecution.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Glenn, I`ve got about a minute left but it looks like you agree.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:53]\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: I do agree. You know, you never want to over-try your case. Under-trying a case is better. Don`t confuse the jury, don`t give them more information than they need, because that can just sow doubt and give jurors too much to debate about jury deliberations.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, the evidence is here. The evidence is clear. There is a pattern that has been established by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that proves not only consciousness of guilt, it proves actual guilt.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: David Cay Johnston, Glenn Kirschner -- thank you both so much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, in a year defined by radical conservative justices stripping away reproductive rights President Biden and \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  are confirming federal judges at a record pace. Law professors Michele Goodwin and Danielle Holley join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:44]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I believe we need judges who understand the experience of the people where they come from. There will be lawyers and judges who will be in charge, who understand -- understand real people and the needs of people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  delivering on that promise by redefining the courts at a historic pace. 97 lifetime federal judges have been confirmed under President Biden, more than any other president at this point in their presidency.\par \par \uc2 With Democrats winning control of the Senate, President Biden`s judicial nominees should continue to be voted on swiftly. But it`s not just that historic pace at which the Biden administration is confirming judges, but the diversity of those judges.\par \par \uc2 The 97 judges include Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , the first black woman to become a member of the court, as well as 28 circuit court judges and 68 district court judges. Three out of every four Biden- appointed judges are women, two-thirds are people of color.\par \par \uc2 President Biden appointed 11 black women and 11 former public defenders to federal appeals courts, more than all previous presidents combined. That means that there are more federal judges who`ve been on the other side of the cases, representing defendants with the least power, the least resources in the justice system, federal benches, closely representing America tonight.\par \par \uc2 And of course, it has the secondary benefit of increasing the pool of experienced, diverse candidates for future Supreme Court shortlists. And some comfort, in a year that will be defined by the radical conservatives on the current court, stripping away the constitutional right to an abortion. A move which has outlawed abortion in more than a dozen states, most of them states governed by radical Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in some states are not only targeting access to abortion, but ways to punish people for providing information on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In an essay to the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " our next guest, Professor Michele Goodwin writes, quote, "There are already two Americas when it comes to the right to abortion. And if foes of the procedure get their way, the same will be true of the ability to exercise the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now Danielle Holley, dean and professor of law at Howard University School of Law; also with us Michele Goodwin, chancellor professor of law at the \uc2 University of California Irvine\uc2 . She`s the author of "Policing The Womb: Invisible Women and the Criminalization of Motherhood." It is great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Professor Holley, I want to start with you. The president`s record, this pace of federal judges, put it into context for us. Why is it such a big deal?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE HOLLEY, DEAN HOWARD UNIVERSITY SCHOOL OF LAW: I think it`s an incredibly big deal, because obviously the federal courts are something that survive a president`s term. And the federal courts can really make a difference, as you were just talking about in Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 So for example, President Biden has appointed, as you noted, 11 black women to the federal appellate courts. In 230 plus years of federal courts we only had 8 black woman to ever serve on the appellate court. So it`s really a historic record of confirmations and nominations that the Biden presidency has really put forth.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: In an interview with the Associated Press, White House chief of staff \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  said, quote, having a more diverse federal bench in every single respect shows more respect for the American people.\par \par \uc2 And Professor Goodwin, there is the gender piece of this, there is the race and ethnicity piece of this. And there`s also the career diversity, right. The fact that you have more public defenders in the mix. That changes the courts themselves.\par \par \uc2 MICHELE GOODWIN, LAW PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA-IRVINE: Well, It absolutely does. And thank you so much for having us both on with you, Alicia. The United States incarcerates more people than any other country in the world, more men and in fact more women than any other country in the world.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that we have had a federal bench and also a Supreme Court that fails to represent the wholeness of what America is all about, including the representation of people who have been criminally charged, is itself something that has been a fault in how people have been nominated to our courts, not just the Supreme Court but also the lower federal courts.\par \par \uc2 And so this is a big corrective and it`s worth noting that we have a constitution where the first ten amendments, many of them are about protecting people in the criminal justice process, and yet we have not had the federal bench represent that.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Staying with you, Professor Goodwin, because I do want to talk about the article that you wrote. We`ve talked extensively, you and I, about the fallout from Dobbs. I wonder what you are watching for next, particularly in states where abortion is now outlawed.\par \par \uc2 [22:40:02]\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN\uc2 : Well, it`s worth noting with an article that I wrote a few years ago with one of my former students who is now on track to becoming a professor herself, Mariah Lindsey, and we wrote about the representation of women on the bench and what that means.\par \par \uc2 And so we were thinking about this. Critical mass makes a huge difference. And that is what we see with the Biden administration and the volume of women that are being nominated to the court. It`s not enough to just do what would be considered tokenism, one woman here or there, but you really need a mass movement in order to make their ways of thinking, methodology, their clarity, come to life.\par \par \uc2 And what we found in a study that we did is that even Republican appointed women to the federal bench are inclined to support reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 And so you don`t have to just look at the Supreme Court with \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  or Justice O`Connor, but if you look at federal courts, prior to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , consistently Republican appointed federal judges that are women support reproductive freedom. So I think the impact is clear, whether Democrat-appointed or Republican-appointed.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Professor, I`m interested by what we are watching right now, sort of under this presidency. I also wonder if something has shifted for Democrats, right if this importance of the court now looms larger, not only because of Dobbs but because of how important the courts were for accountability in the Trump era. I mean has this created a seismic and permanent shift?\par \par \uc2 HOLLEY: You know, I don`t think we`re quite there yet. I think one of the major kind of missing pieces to this is the federal trial courts. So we look at the federal trial courts, the district courts, there are a huge number of vacancies, especially in the south, in the southern district courts.\par \par \uc2 So take a judge like Michelle Childs, who is on the district court for South Carolina, she`s now on the appellate court in D.C. Her space is vacant.\par \par \uc2 So there are two Republican senators in South Carolina and through the blue slip process, they can hold up nominations for the district court. And we are seeing an extensive number of vacancies.\par \par \uc2 So I think before we begin to say that there`s been wholesale change and really seismic shift, we really need to see the pace of confirmations and nominations to the district courts, which make a huge, just huge exception for many cases happen or decided at the district court level.\par \par \uc2 Until we see that pace of nominations really pick up in the southern district courts, I don`t think we can declare it a seismic shift yet. But that`s the direction that it`s moving in.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Professor Goodwin, before I let you go, I want you to talk to me about the way in which this conversation about reproductive rights has started to become about the First Amendment. Understanding the roots of that conversation had been there for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN\uc2 : That`s right. One of the matters concerning pre-Dobbs and now certainly a glaring problem post-Dobbs is the language, the First Amendment, particularly religious protections has been used and weaponized before the Supreme Court and sending signals to state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 That is to say that now we must be concerned about the way in which the protection of religious freedoms will impact people`s access to contraception, or at least the claims, right the claim that one has a religious objection to providing and dispensing contraception.\par \par \uc2 The ways in which the First Amendment has been used as a sword to tell librarians that they may not talk about abortion, that they may not help people search the term "abortion", the way in which there becomes a kind of weaponization in using the First Amendment to protect the rights of those who are anti-reproductive freedom to the harm of people who are pro-choice, who would say that they have a First Amendment right as well.\par \par \uc2 And so the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " piece goes through the myriad ways in which we should be really concerned about the constitution showing (ph) upside down including the First Amendment, protections being limited for those who would seek to protect reproductive freedom and weaponize for those who are antiabortion.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: It lands on the very scary possibility of living in two Americas where the First Amendment is treated very differently in this regard\par \par \uc2 Michele Goodwin, Danielle Holley, thank you both as always.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, if you follow politics as we do, you`ve heard a lot of reporting about the ignorance of some members of Congress, ignorance about who won the 2020 election, (INAUDIBLE) during COVID, the female reproductive system.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:50]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: But there is another side. Members of congress have a huge range of issues to know about -- national security, the economy, Medicare, Medicaid, Ukraine, environmental law -- all aspects of American life and governance.\par \par \uc2 We turn to experts to guide their understanding but some issues are so new and evolving so fast you just have to go back to college.\par \par \uc2 So that`s what one lawmaker on the Artificial Intelligence Caucus is doing. You`re going to meet him next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:49:54]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Our next guest is usually doing homework about now, but like college students across the country, he`s on his winter break. He also has a day job, like a lot of college students, but what makes his story a little different is where he works -- the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And he`s not an intern or an office aide. He is Congressman \uc2 Don Beyer\uc2 , a Democrat representing Virginia`s 8th congressional district, who was just reelected to Congress for a fifth term.\par \par \uc2 He`s been studying things like multivariable calculus at \uc2 George Mason University\uc2  in northern Virginia, working towards a masters degree in machine learning. His goal: to one day apply his knowledge of artificial intelligence to his work in Congress, especially as technology continues to evolve on a rapid rate.\par \par \uc2 Beyer is a member of the House Science, Space and Technology Committee. It`s four more undergraduate math and computer science courses before starting his graduate degree in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Congressman \uc2 Don Beyer\uc2 , who`s also a member of the newly formed Congressional Artificial Intelligence Caucus. Congressman, thank you so much for being with us. Tell me what got you into AI to begin with?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 DON BEYER\uc2  (D-VA): Alicia, I have always been sort of a science nerd my whole life. When I joined the Congress eight years ago and asked to go on the science committee they laughed and said I was the first person ever to ask to be on it.\par \par \uc2 But it has been a wonderful experience. You know, we`re dealing with all the fascinating things that are going on in space, in medicine. I`m particularly impressed by the possibilities of machine learning, artificial intelligence, to see patterns in the immense amount of data that we create but that we are not -- we can`t see just with our human eyes.\par \par \uc2 And you know, all science is cause and effect and discerning those patterns. And I tried to take an online course on AI through \uc2 Coursera\uc2  but the math quickly overwhelmed me. I just didn`t have enough math background.\par \par \uc2 So when I found out that they would take a 72-year-old in the math class there I thought let`s try it. And it`s been really fun.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I love that it was the math, that trip you up. And I also love this description from one of your colleagues about what people think AI is. Quote, "Some people weren`t familiar with AI, think that the biggest drawback of AI is evil robots with red laser eyes. There`s actually drawbacks that are even more substantial than that but more subtle."\par \par \uc2 Help us understand that. What do we need to know about AI technology moving forward?\par \par \uc2 BEYER: Well, it is -- there is a yin and a yang. There`s enormous potential for good. That article mentioned can we to use artificial intelligence to discern a little more predictive capabilities of people who may take their own lives, die by suicide -- we have 47,000 a year.\par \par \uc2 And I am convinced that the deeper we go the more we may be able to prevent a thousand or 5,000 of them by seeing the patterns that evolve.\par \par \uc2 On the downside, of course, we know that like my father used to say, garbage in and garbage out. If you only have a certain set of data going in you`re only going to be able to get a certain kind of conclusion out.\par \par \uc2 The most famous thing is, if you`re looking for CEOs who are going to get a lot of old white man and that maybe what AI predicts. So you want to make sure that the inputs into artificial intelligence are valid across our whole population.\par \par \uc2 The issues of -- not of equity -- it`s really important. Equal AI is what they call it..\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yes. No, the equity question is absolutely huge when it comes to AI.\par \par \uc2 You know, part of the reason I love your story, Congressman, aside from the sort of like congressman goes back to college piece of it is it does feel like we`re living in a moment where the anti-science movement is so strong that to have someone inside the House of Representatives who`s saying science is so critical that I want to spend more time studying it seems like a true value statement.\par \par \uc2 BEYER: And Alicia, when you put that side by side with what you struggle with every day, the fact that we seem to be in America with two different narratives, two different senses of our own history, our own facts. Science should be a place where we all agree. You know, it`s always evolving but there are really hard facts of what we know now.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Congressman \uc2 Don Beyer\uc2 , is this the end of the educational road? You`re going to get a Masters, anything else?\par \par \uc2 BEYER: Well, you know, what I tell my kids is, I hope to have my PhD by the time I`m 80, then I`m going to go get a really good job.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Congressman \uc2 Don Beyer\uc2 , thank you so much for joining us tonight. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BEYER: Thank you, Alicia.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:36]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:59:59]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: That is tonight`s LAST WORD. I`m Alicia Menendez. Thank you so much for watching.\par \par \uc2 "ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES" is up next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221230eict00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for December 26, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Michelle Goldberg, Ben Collins, Andrew Weissman \par \uc2 9,139 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 December 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (on camera): Good evening from New York I`m Chris Hayes happy Holidays and Merry Christmas to those of you who celebrated. It`s been an extraordinary 12 months in American life and politics. The twice-impeached disgraced ex-President is facing multiple criminal investigations, civil inquiries across multiple jurisdictions. His party has full-on embrace an anti-democratic, antisemitic in some cases, platform while the conservative Supreme Court took away the 50-year-old constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 We`ll get to all of that and more tonight but we begin at the end of this year and the historic results of the Midterm Election where Democrats gained a seat in the Senate and Republicans managed to snatch defeat from the jaws of victory. Historically, Midterm Elections of course have been outright disastrous for a first-term president and the party in power.\par \par \uc2 As MSNBC Political Contributor Steve Benen noted, "Since World War II, Democrat Presidents in their first midterms has seen their party lose an average of 40 House Seats and five Senate Seats. Since Watergate, the results have looked even worse for the party. Democratic presidents in their first midterms have seen their party lose an average of 44 House Seats and six Senate Seats."\par \par \uc2 Many political observers on both sides of the aisle were expecting a political bloodbath on the level of say 1994`s Republican Revolution for instance when Democrats lost 52 House Seats and eight Senate seats during Bill Clinton`s first term or the 2010 Tea Party wave when Democrats lost a whopping 63 House Seats and six Senate seats during Barack Obama`s first term. And for the right-wing media, especially Fox News, a red wave was a sure thing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Be an analyst for a second. Does it feel like a red wave?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It feels like a red wave, Brian.\par \par \uc2 STEVE DOOCY, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: You know, your predictions of a red wave are accurate.\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, CONTRIBUTOR, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: So, I met a surfboard and said the red wave is coming.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Red wave rising.\par \par \uc2 SARAH PALIN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: That red wave that I`m convinced is coming.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LAUREN BOEBERT\uc2  (R-CO): The reports I`m seeing show a big red wave coming.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sleepy Joe just guarantee the red wave in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There`s a lot of energy on the ground. You probably hear the rally in the background right now. We think we`re going to have a big red wave in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, ANCHOR, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Democrats are bracing for the worst-case scenario, a red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are officially on a red tsunami watch.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Sean, we`re going to see a red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 CLAY TRAVIS, FOUNDER, OUTKICK: Red tsunami grows.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That means red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: We`re not just going to see a red wave, we`re going to see a red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Poverty, joblessness, critical race theory, crazy gender ideology in our schools. We are going to see a red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And lastly your prediction for tonight?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think we`re going to have a red wave. I think it`s going to be maybe bigger than anyone thought.\par \par \uc2 BOEBERT: On Tuesday, we will be part of a big red wave that says enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 JOEY JONES, CONTRIBUTOR, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Up next, Elon Musk ready to ride the big red wave.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Elon Musk tweeted massive red wave.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Massive Red wave.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Massive red wave.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Massive red wave.\par \par \uc2 BEN SHAPIRO, CONSERVATIVE COMMENTATOR: You`re about to see a red wave that makes day after tomorrow look like nothing.\par \par \uc2 JOE ROGAN, RADIO HOST: That`s going to be responsible for the red wave. I think the red wave that`s coming is going to be like the elevator doors opening up in The Shining.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: Rogan said that the red wave is going to look like the elevator doors opening and the blood pouring out of the elevator in The Shining.\par \par \uc2 ROGAN: Like the elevator doors opening up in The Shining.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: That is correct except it`s not going to be an elevator, it`s going to look more like deep impact, the tsunami at the end but colored red.\par \par \uc2 DAN BONGINO, CONTRIBUTOR, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Remember that Teo Leone Deep Impact disaster movie? That`s the red wave tsunami that`ll come ashore.\par \par \uc2 SHAPIRO: It`s going to be a brutal week for the Democrats beginning on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS: And frankly, I`ve already DVR`d CNN and MSNBC for election night not because I`m going to watch but just because I want to enjoy the tears post-red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: What happened to the red wave, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, what happened to the red wave? Well, once the votes were counted, Democrats narrowly lost the House Majority, losing only nine seats. They actually flipped a Republican senate seat by electing Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in Pennsylvania. But there was still one chance for Republicans to try to claw back something. The Georgia Senate race between Democratic incumbent Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Trump-backed Republican carpetbagger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  headed to a runoff. And once again, as Media Matters pointed out, Fox News was well, they were very bullish about the Republican`s chances.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  for Senate. I like the ring of that. Will you help me draft \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  to run for Senate down in Georgia because I happen to be very fond of him.\par \par \uc2 Herschel, I`m kind of hoping you might consider running for high office in Georgia. Do we want them to run for governor or for Senator? Do I hear George is on your mind?\par \par \uc2 Now, the crop of 2022 Republican candidates just got a lot stronger tonight as football legend \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  announced that he`s jumping into the Georgia GOP Senate primary.\par \par \uc2 Herschel`s enthusiasm in the state of Georgia seems to be through the roof.\par \par \uc2 People of Georgia is in your hands. Your country needs you.\par \par \uc2 Game time in Georgia. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  are live in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): This man will change the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 Teamherschel.com. Teamhershel.com. Teamherschel.com. Teamherschel.com. Teamherschel.com.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Team Hershel.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Help Herschel.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Team Herschel. Teamherschel.com.\par \par \uc2 Teamherschel.com. Teamhershel.com. Teamherschel.com.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Teamherschel.com. Teamherschel.com. Teamherschel.com.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You`ve never lost an overtime game in your life, have you?\par \par \uc2 Hopefully soon to be Senator-elect.\par \par \uc2 I believe you win hands down. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , congratulations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, just hours after the polls closed, NBC News called it, and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was one of the weakest candidates in modern political history who frankly had no business running for a Senate seat, lost a to Sen. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , securing an astonishing midterm election result for the Democratic Party and President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Maya Wiley\uc2  is a civil rights attorney, former Assistant U.S. Attorney, Michelle Goldberg is an opinion columnist for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . Ben Collins is a senior reporter for NBC News. And they all join me now. I think what ends up happening in the news cycle is there`s tons of focus on the midterms in the run-up, then there was this shock afterwards and we all kind of process it, and then you kind of move on. But I`m still not over it.\par \par \uc2 It was -- and the reason I`m not, and not just because I found it gratifying from the perspective of like, the vibrancy and resiliency of American democracy, it is genuinely one of the stunning, most sort of stunning political surprises, turnabouts in my life, really. I mean, in 20 years of covering politics certainly, but in my life.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, and I think that we`ve forgotten that political surprises are not always apocalyptic, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s also true.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: I mean, you know --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Because the one before that was 2016.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right, and before that, and then there was, you know, the kind of closer than expected polls in 2020, and so there was an assumption I think by a lot of political observers that if the polls are off, they can only be off in one direction. You know, it`s why you saw so many people dismissing their own poll results that were -- that actually showed that Democrats were in an OK position.\par \par \uc2 And also, just the sort of barrage of catastrophes that we`ve endured over the last, you know, five or six years. The idea that something could turn out, you know, from the Democrats point of view much better than anyone hoped I think it`s just a reminder that like his -- you know, just as kind of the Arc of History bending towards justice was an exaggerate -- if not an exaggeration, you know, the kind of things don`t work in that linear way.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: In the same way, we`re not just on a sort of endless decline.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s -- I think that`s a really important point that like, that fundamental point because we`ve had a series of like shocks to the system and surprises -- I mean, the global pandemic, right, the war in Ukraine, that they don`t just come in one flavor. And they don`t -- things don`t always just surprise you for the worse.\par \par \uc2 MAYA WILEY\uc2 , FORMER ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY: Yes, and everything you say is true. But we also can`t ignore the fact that we had Dobbs, that we --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: I mean, it was literally a top two issues for most voters. And in addition to that, the electorate has changed. And it is changed in a profound and important way. And that doesn`t make it the dominant part of the electorate yet, but the kids are OK.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: The kids are -- the kids are OK but they`re also the most diverse. And when they show up which they did in the Midterms, they`ve also demonstrated what the future electorate looks like.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let me -- let me just respond to that Dobbs point because obviously it`s -- you can`t talk about the Midterms without it. And in fact, if you were to say well, what`s the one factor that produced the surprise? I think that`s -- that is the one factor but it also is the case. I think to me as I thought about Dobbs in the first half of the year, right? Like, I knew they were going to argue this and I thought pretty clearly the Supreme Court was going to overturn Roe, and then the opinion leaked, and then the actual opinion came down. And I thought and I hope -- I hope and I pray that this would be the sort of wake-up call shocked to the system political activation, and it was.\par \par \uc2 And then I just worried about the short attention span society. Like, really, just like the way that all things function in the news cycle which is like something in the story and then it`s not. It`s like oh, you know, where do we end up on Ukraine? Where are we at on that? Obviously, your bodily autonomy is different than that, but it wasn`t necessarily to me baked in that it would be there in November but for all the work that people put in and the fact that candidates continued to hammer on it and didn`t go away from it.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: Well, it didn`t go away from it, but also linked it to the threats that were coming up to other liberties.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: Because it was awesome. That`s part of my point about the kids, right?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: They knew it was trans rights. They knew it was whether they get to learn about history in schools. They knew it was about whether it`s going to be about student debt. They knew it was going to be about whether you can love who you love. Because that was also explicit in how they overruled Roe. And the tremendous effort that grassroots organizations, community groups, civic engagement groups put in to making sure people knew how to show up despite the efforts to stop them.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:01]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I think that`s exactly right. Like, it was sort of both and that it was necessary but not sufficient. And so, it`s impressive I think as a political accomplishment for all the people that made it matter, right, in November.\par \par \uc2 Maya just mentioned trans rights and trans issues. And I have to say, one of the enduring, and again one of the most gratifying lessons, right, was I watched the world of the sort of furthest reaches of the right-wing which were very obsessed.\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, NBC NEWS SENIOR REPORTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In a very, I think, unnerving way with trans folks in their bodies and what their pronouns are and how they`re living their lives. And they want to isolate them, they want to ostracize them, and some places I think they want to legislate them out of existence basically. And the degree to which that played as a central theme -- I mean, I`ve definitely watched someone live covering the big Michigan like, day before election rally for the Statewide Republican slate where the big star keynote speaker was like the swimmer who`s annoyed that about trans folks swimming. And I was like, whoa, that`s quite a closing argument. But I also was like, I don`t know. I`m not the target for this. This seems weird to me honestly to be your closing argument.\par \par \uc2 And it was so gratifying, Ben, as someone who covers the world in which this sort of obsession percolates that it didn`t seem to really register.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: No, it really didn`t. In fact, I think the opposite happened. I think Maya made a very good point here. And I didn`t hear this from anybody except for maybe Josh Shapiro towards the end that for a lot of people, this was about rights and liberties, right? The Democrats didn`t really run on this, I don`t think. I don`t think they put -- I don`t think they really put this into this one big omnibus thing.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A few did. Josh Shapiro I think --\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Josh Shapiro did. But like, it sort of seems retroactive now. But that`s what was happening. The right was hammering home, let`s take some books off the shelves at least in Florida, right? At least where the most - - right now, the most popular Republican candidate for 2024. At least that`s what he was running. But you know, the people around him were running on. Let`s ban some books that bring up things about gender ideology and you know, the true history of America, basically. And let`s try as hard as we can to legislate trans people out of existence, or at least try to, you know, make it so there`s a cap on the number of them somehow. That`s really what is going on there.\par \par \uc2 So, the Democrats accidentally ran this campaign of, no, you can be what you want to be. You can love who you want to love and, you know, you have rights as a woman to have bodily autonomy. I don`t think they actually put it together until the end though. Maya just did which is really good, but I think that they wound up in this position of being like, oh wait, we`re the American position here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. And I think actually like, liberty, freedom, and democracy which are like the most cliched things to run on, ends up being a huge part of what Democrats did run on right, which again --\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: And you can vote -- you can vote and your vote matters.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: And in some ways, Florida is a kind of test case because in Florida, these things were somewhat disaggregated because, you know, the one thing that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  ran entirely on both kind of opposition to COVID mitigation to "freedom" --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: -- and in opposition to trans people. But what he didn`t have so much is the anti-abortion piece because they only have a 15-week ban in Florida. And without those three things -- you know, without that part of it, I mean, these things are all overdetermined. But he didn`t see the kind of democratic backlash that you saw in so many other states.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And I should note that 15-week ban, he literally would run away from microphones when asked about it, right, about whether to do anything. And after the big victory, a bunch of Republican state house were like, oh, we`re not going to just leave it at a 15-week ban. Like, we`re definitely going uh for more.\par \par \uc2 I want everyone to stick around because I want to talk about the open extremism the, antisemitism, the anti-democratic sentiment that we`re seeing across the right-wing and infiltrating the Republican Party from the top down. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This was a year in which many prominent Republicans openly embraced their very worst instincts. From the sitting congresswoman warmly greeting a white nationalist at his conference to the grotesque conspiracy theories after a violent attack against the Speaker of the House and her family, to calls from the ex-president to terminate the United States Constitution and overturn his election loss.\par \par \uc2 Still with me here at the table, \uc2 Maya Wiley\uc2 , Michelle Goldberg, and Ben Collins. I mean, the sort of notion of extremism on the GOP has been obviously much covered and much discussed and it`s always hard to sort of track the trajectory of it. Obviously, at the top of that is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And we didn`t get to talk about in the last block his role in that Midterm election which was clearly formidable and also I think for a bunch of reasons. One is that he`s personally just profoundly unpopular, one of the least popular public figures in American life. But also because I think he`s come to represent extremism um in a way that`s actually quite different from 2016 when polls showed that viewers -- voters shot him as the more -- as moderate than Hillary Clinton which is striking.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Stuart Varney\uc2  had a very funny exchange with Ronald McDaniel who`s the head of the \uc2 RNC\uc2  and continued to be about Trump where she just can`t say the obvious thing. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STUART VARNEY\uc2 , HOST, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS: Would you publicly say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  bears any responsibility for the sum of the losses in the Midterm Elections. Would you say that publicly?\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , CHAIR, \uc2 RNC\uc2 : Yes, I don`t like this. I don`t like these parceling out. Here`s the one thing that I think people should be talking about, the amount of ticket-splitting, the amount of Republicans that went out and voted for Republican at the top of the ticket. Look at Arizona. The top vote getter is a Republican. Look at Georgia, we had eight of nine statewide races won by Republicans. But why are Republicans going and voting for one Republican and not the other? They have to work hard to bring those Independence.\par \par \uc2 VARNEY: Trump. Isn`t that the answer to your --\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: But that means turnout is not the issue.\par \par \uc2 VARNEY: You likely posed the question. The answer is Trump, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I`m saying, I`m not into the blame game right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:20]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I love it because it`s like she perfectly sets up --\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: She edges right up to it.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Like, why, why? What could it be? And it`s like, yes, it`s staring in the face. And I do think that like, again, him as actual person but him as symbol of extremism and the median voter doesn`t actually like extremism in the wake of the election losses. Even \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  who doesn`t want to say it is articulating the clear logic that that is the problem.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. And this is -- I mean, what`s -- what I think -- again, to go back to the conversation we`re having, what`s so heartening was that we didn`t have this repudiation of Trump obviously in 2016 as people expected. And then, we didn`t -- it was kind of ambiguous in 2020 because it was so close because Republicans did better than expected. This was finally the moment when kind of everybody said oh, people really hate this, right? People really don`t want to go in this direction. But at the same time, you know, a party as it becomes smaller as the Republican Party has in many ways, doesn`t necessarily become more moderate because --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No. In fact, the opposite.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: The moderates are the ones that get peeled off and the hardcore is left and they gain even more power.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No, it`s like a demi-glace. It`s been on the oven for four hours. Like, all of the moderates have boiled off. It`s like the densest, thickest thing there at the bottom of the pot, you know, the more that you like, chase these people away.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Right. And that`s how you end up with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  being a central figure in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: Yes. I mean, like, I`m -- so, what is so stunning, we at the \uc2 Leadership Conference on Civil and Human Rights\uc2  did a civil rights monitor poll with Cornell Belcher. And one of the things that was striking is, of course, everybody was saying across our poll what we know, is people were desperate to save democracy.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: And understand that hate and bias and the divisions that we are too often hearing from Trumpism in explicit and disturbing ways is a big part of that problem. And we even had conservatives in our poll, right, going this is a problem. And so, I say that because I think part of the hope was this really was -- there is a majority in this country that actually just cares about rights, cares about justice, and thinks we shouldn`t be divided. The explicitness of it as hate driven, antisemitic, anti-Black, anti-trans, anti, anti, anti. Fear-mongering has had a huge impact in a good way because we are the majority.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, and also it also feels to me -- and again, this is even one layer more abstract than the specific rights anti-bigotry story you`re saying just generally in the 20 years I covered politics. Like, you generally want to be the moderate versus the extreme. And sometimes that works to the benefit of conservatives against people on the left whose ideas I like, right, who might be viewed as extreme, right? But generally, what you want to do. And so, there are these layups you can take.\par \par \uc2 For instance, you could just condemn like, outright Nazis. Like, guys that like Nick Fuentes who like do joking things about like were all those people actually killed in the Holocaust, right?\par \par \uc2 WILEY: But then you can`t meet with them.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s my point is that like, the easiest layup in the world. Like, in the -- it`s amazing to me that this unbelievable judgment comes down from American people on precisely what you`re saying. Like, we don`t like this. This is gross and extremist. And then like, Nick Fuentes is white nationalist, and Kanye who is on this insane antisemitic bender show up to have dinner with the guy who`s the leader of the party.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes. Well, I think the worry is -- I really hate to add another cooking analogy to this but I think like, the worry is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  realizes this sort of baked into the cake of his base at this point. And it`s way too -- it`s like years and years too late to just slough it off the top, right, especially when they`re making all the talking points the memes for him that he distills down into, you know, regular human --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. He can`t condemn them because they`re his base.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes, exactly. That`s exactly right. So, for him that`s an issue. And that`s part of the problem too is I think Republicans are trying to figure out is his base our base? Like, can we just get rid of these people? And are -- is somebody just going to run on exactly Trump`s positions with a -- with a healthier looking faces?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But again, the thing to me that`s so bizarre just again, from a tactical standpoint, this is not moral, it`s like those people all voted for \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  I think in Georgia. What else are they going to do? You think they voted for \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 ? I don`t think so. Like, the hardcore bigots in Georgia, they voted for \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . But \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  who he didn`t one thing in his life that`s moderate which is that he refused to have a coup in Georgia --\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- gave him an imprimatur with voters like I`m the moderate one.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: Correct.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s not that hard. The fact that these people can`t see the obvious writing on the wall whether there`s a political self-interest.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: We need you to stop coaching people.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:02]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No, I know that. I know that.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: You definitely need to stop coaching people. But you`re absolutely right. But that would -- these were the norms of politics that got broken in 2016.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right. That`s right. That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: What Trump didn`t say, the -- I`m going to turn the southern strategy on its head. The southern strategy says wink, wink, nod, nod.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: I`m going to get you in my base without being explicit --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: As \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  did in 1980.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, property taxes.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: Property taxes, crime.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: School and crime.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: School, but all the -- all the dog whistles. But what Trump did is said no