rt of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , saying this in 2017.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GINNI THOMAS, WIFE OF JUSTICE \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 : I am happy to be here but my husband told me that the president asked about me twice, so I`m wearing my Trump button. We need to -- hey, it is the time to be behind whoever is our president because there`s a war for our country and the things that we believe in going on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, Ginni Thomas has a policy she has. She`s long been a conservative activist. But she wasn`t joking about a war for our country. The January 6 Committee got hold of text messages she sent to Donald Trump`s chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  in 2020 urging Meadows to do what he could to overturn Trump`s defeat and keep him in power. \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  then refused to recuse himself from multiple cases involving Trump`s very effort to remain in power, including some having to do with discovery that might have implicated those very text.\par \par \uc2 Somehow those stories only scratched the surface of the Republican assault on the rule of law. More on the ongoing threat from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his allies next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:35:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The radical right-wing Supreme Court overturned 50 years of precedent in their decisions striking down the abortion protections in Roe v Wade. It was a decision that immediately stripped away health care for millions of people across the U.S. And the consequences of the decision continue to reverberate around the country. But that monumental decision is really only the tip of the iceberg when it comes to this corrupt court. It is ruled by a class of conservative justices that are history making and intent on making more history in the worst way.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in \uc2 Maya Wiley\uc2 , a former Assistant U.S. Attorney and President and CEO of the Leadership Conference for Civil and Human Rights. Andrew Weissman who served as a lead prosecutor in Mueller`s Special Counsel`s Office and as Chief of the Fraud Section at the Department of Justice. And Charles Coleman a former Senior Assistant District Attorney in Brooklyn. It`s great to have you all here.\par \par \uc2 I think the single biggest political actor of this year was the court with the Dobbs decision that overturned a precedent both for what it meant immediately in a way that I`ve never quite seen a court decision in my lifetime. Like, from Monday to Tuesday, you have these right, now, you don`t have those rights. You can obtain an abortion, you can`t obtain an abortion. And also, for the political ramifications. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 WILEY: Oh, I think you`re absolutely right. And as we were just saying, because it also made clear there were other fundamental rights coming, that it was actually a -- there was a game plan. And then making clear that not only are we going to take from more than half the population, by the way, right, when we`re talking about women, but we`re actually going to signal this as a way in which we`re going to behave around fundamental rights in general.\par \par \uc2 Remember, this is the first time the Supreme Court has taken away a fundamental right that is recognized for decades. And we`ve actually been watching this march forward in other cases since Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:34]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s -- yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES COLEMAN, FORMER PROSECUTOR: You know, Maya, the thing about what you talked about with respect to there being more coming is something that I tied to a larger conversation around the securing of democracy. And I think that it`s very interesting for as much conversation as we had during midterms this year about how important it was to secure democracy.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that we neglected to tie into that conversation was the importance of looking at the Supreme Court and what was going on there because at the end of the day democracy is only as secure as our faith in the institutions that uphold it. And the Supreme Court being an example of that is something that we completely overlooked in that discussion.\par \par \uc2 HAYE: This is the thing because that -- I`m so glad you brought that up because that guardrail has held to a certain extent over the last years, right? So, when he was -- when Trump was flooding the courts with nonsense, right, it basically went nowhere. In fact, just recently, we had another test case, right, with Judge Cannon`s, you know, largely viewed to be lawless order giving essentially Donald Trump`s special treatment taking away criminal evidence from the possession of the government which no criminal defendant anywhere in history has ever gotten for any reason, overruled 3-0 Appellate Courts, not appeal to Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing to your point, Charles. What`s So Scary is that it`s like you just need five brainworm folks on the Supreme Court who like are just ingesting the worst media in the universe to go along with it and then that`s the law, Andrew. Like, as someone who`s devoted his life to the law, how do you get up in the morning -- I`m serious -- and think about the law in those terms?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WEISSMAN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Look, that -- I went through this when Bush v. Gore where the decision was one that was very hard to sort of wake up the next day and say oh, this is -- let`s go to work and be a lawyer in a functioning court. And I know judges who had the same view which is how do I actually do this when the Supreme Court is not a model of the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 I do think that your original point, there is an irony here which is, you know, you have Dobbs came out. You have Justice Thomas to your point signaling that this is step one because he`s basically saying there`s no reason that all of these things shouldn`t be reversed. But you have the political reaction. So, yes, it only takes five as Justice O`Connor used to say what the Supreme Court is about, it`s five. You have to just get to that number. But you know, if they continue down this road, there is a sort of real potential political silver lining which we saw in the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, this is -- I want to just raise this issue, right, because I think the only answer -- because you can get into this place of impotent fret about the court because honestly I don`t think the votes are going to be there to expand the court. I really don`t. I think that might change over time. But when you look at this, when you look at the approval rating of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , it`s record-high disapproval right now. I mean, they really are like lost a lot of people this year. And I don`t know if they`re getting them back.\par \par \uc2 There`s also I think a real change in how liberals view the court which I think because of the Warren court for years they viewed it as fundamentally an entity that was -- that was protecting and vouching rights even when it was not doing that as much anymore. And I think Dobbs really ripped the Band-Aid off. How do you think, Maya, about the world of politics and civil society as checks on the court?\par \par \uc2 WILEY: So, one way is that -- and you`re exactly right, Chris, that for example, conservatives have been focused on elections because they can control the courts --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As a means to that.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: As a means to that. And they see it as power and they -- and they think about the Politics as a ways getting the powers of the court. And that has not been true for the most part for a lot of Democratic voters. I think that has shifted. And that approval rating is one element of it. And it -- and now, even for moderates because I think the clear message is that you can have ideology on a court where the expectation was rule of law. And that is a shift that people -- because they`re seeing it in real time.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, and the other thing about it in announcing what you said before about what it announced in Dobbs --\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s the rights thing and then there`s the sort of meta-issue about precedent. And this to me is the other big thing is that the only thing that makes the Supreme Court different in a functional sense from a legislature of nine is stare decisis. That they`re bound by something other than well, now Kevin McCarthy runs the House so the budget`s going to look like this. Yesterday, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  ran it. That`s how legislators work and that`s fine. That`s democracy. But to the extent that you just completely wholesale throw that out, Charles, like, you can`t pretend anymore you`re doing anything other than essentially legislating.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:13]\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: No, you`re correct. And it`s interesting. One of my old law school professors would likely -- would often say that the Supreme Court wasn`t last because they`re always right. They`re always -- they`re always right because they`re last.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: And I think that that`s the thing that people have to remember when you`re looking at it from any sort of moral or overall judge -- sense of judgment, place of judgment in terms of the decisions that are being made. But you`re absolutely on point when you say without the notion of precedent being adhered to if we`re throwing that out then it takes the rails off the entire thing. I think one of the things -- zooming even further back, this is going to cause us to do when we are talking about the -- as an electorate, where we go from here is to look at our elected officials and say from a policy space, how are you going to protect us.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: How are you going to codify this --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: If the court is this way.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: If the court is going to do what they`ve shown themselves starting to do, how can I count on you to protect me from them?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMAN: So, I do think it is complicated though because there are decisions by the Supreme Court where they say, you know, we made a mistake and they do reverse themselves. And so -- and so, you can`t just say every time they sort of don`t uphold stare decisis and say, you know, we`re just going to adhere to it. Here, it`s just such an -- I think they`re two things. One is such an abrupt shift with the personnel changing without sort of, you know, (INAUDIBLE) which is just like, off you go when you`re going to change or anything, or just leave it. It feels like he`s got his five and he`s going to do what he needs to do. And then it`s the substance of the decisions.\par \par \uc2 I mean, when you overrule Roe v. Wade and people have spent 50 years with that as a fundamental right, you`re going to have a different reaction that it`s not really about sort of storage stare decisis or not. It is really much more reaction to what the substance of the decision is rather than sort of the mechanism.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want everyone to stick around. Next, I want to take a look at all the numerous legal issues facing the disgraced ex-president, where it all stands and what it`s going to look like in 2023. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This year has seen an absolute avalanche of legal issues for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The twice-impeached disgraced ex-president`s namesake business was found guilty in an illegal tax fraud scheme. He faces a civil inquiry by the New York Attorney General to his business practice as well as renewed scrutiny from the Manhattan D.A. of the 2016 hush money payment made to an adult film actress.\par \par \uc2 On the federal level, there`s of course, the new special counsel, Jack Smith, and his dual investigations into separate inquiries, the mis