nced before the next presidential election so that`s one area we have to worry about.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is your advice is like, understandable is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMAN: Federal is going to happen. The real name of the game is the states because there`s no ability to be pardoned federally for a state crime. So, if there`s a Georgia indictment or a New York indictment, that`s where you have to worry, because if you`re indicted in New York, you`re going to go to jail. If in Georgia, you`re probably going to go to jail as well unless they change the rules and allow the governor to pardon you. So, that is where we need to spend our time is convincing -- looking at those cases and figuring out what the defenses are there.\par \par \uc2 And both of those I think there are potential issues. I mean, I think the strongest of all of the things that we`ve seen at least from the outside is the Mar-a-Lago case. That I don`t see --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Strongest in which direction?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMAN: Strongest instance that the government is going to indict and it`s hard to see a valid defense there.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And why is that?\par \par \uc2 WEISSMAN: That is just a simple case. He took the documents. He knew he took the documents. There probably going to be a whole lot of lawyers from the White House who are going to probably be key witnesses to say I told him not to do it. I told him it was illegal. I told them that these are government property. And the fact that it took 18 months of drips and drabs is going to be unbelievably damning evidence.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:17]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Renato Mariotti has used this line. I think others have as well, Charles, that he said it`s kind of like a drug case. Like, if you get searched and you`ve got a pound of cocaine, like you`re in a lot of trouble. You got some explaining to do because like, the thing is there, right? You`re in possession of the illegal thing. You have to come up with some theory that`s going to have to be a pretty good one to get out above that problem.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: You know, for most of the year, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , from a legal perspective, spent the year as the Gingerbread Man. No one could capture him. No one could sort of pin him down until this Mar-a-Lago thing exploded.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it`s true.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: And I agree with Andrew. It`s the most straightforward route as a former prosecutor that I would use or look at in terms of everything else because it`s so cut and dry. The intent is there. The timeline and the notice, all of the things add up. And then you actually get the documents with his personal effects. I mean, it`s really the easiest case to establish. And he really doesn`t have a defense -- a defense with it.\par \par \uc2 I expect that he will likely play to the court of public opinion in as much as trying to minimize the charges because he doesn`t have a leg to stand on in the court of law.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I want to talk about that in a second, but you wanted to say something.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: Well, I`m going to go with -- I agree, by the way, that this is strongest prosecution. I`m just going to say Georgia, Georgia, Georgia. Fulton County jury, he is directly engaged in trying to find an exact number of votes that he needs to win. He`s made multiple calls. He sends his chief of staff down there. He is directly implicated, clearly trying to influence the outcome of that election. It is not going to be an easy -- I`m not saying he can`t mount a defense. Of course, he can mount one. I`m just looking at the juries he`s going to face in Fulton County. And I`m just saying, he`s got a lot --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He`s got a lot of problems. The line that I`ve used before on the air and I continue is that I lived in Chicago where I got some -- I started as a reporter. And there are a lot of corruption stories in Chicago as you might know. And when I heard that phone call, one of my first thoughts was, if there was a recording of a Chicago Alderman who just lost a race calling the Cook County Clerk and saying I just need these votes, that Alderman would be indicted by the Northern District U.S. attorney by the end of the week in a big -- like, we got him like PR thing. It would be a -- it would be a badge of honor.\par \par \uc2 And I saw that sort of thing -- those cases roll up all the time. When Rod Blagojevich is on tape being like, this thing is golden, right? Like --\par \par \uc2 WEISSMAN: I think it`s a little more complicated there. And that you know, the job as a prosecutor or defense lawyer is to make sure you`re looking through everything for whatever possible arguments. You know, it wasn`t -- that wasn`t a phone call just with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on it and just with --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s true.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMAN: So, he`s -- it is so important for the state to know exactly what happened beforehand. He was clearly reading in part from a script. You want to make sure that he does not have a defense of saying, wait a second, this was blessed --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I was told by my lawyer.\par \par \uc2 WEISSMAN: This was blessed. I mean, look, lawyers have great and not great because a lot of times lawyers will bless something that they shouldn`t have. But if you do that, it gives the client a potential defense.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And this -- and this gets to something I think is really important is that these are -- there`s reason -- these are all -- I mean, aside from the Mar-a-Lago case which we can talk about back again, you know, I think you`re right. Like, the there`s a weird thing where the most serious things he did are the hardest cases to make. You know what I`m saying? Like, it`s like --\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I keep coming back to this scene. It`s the most important crime that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed is he tried to end constitutional governance of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: But there`s a reason for that because they`re very complicated things, right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: And so --- and I said from very, very long ago earlier this year what ends up being, you know, the thing that nails the coffin shut for him may not be the most exciting or sensational thing. Like, think about you know, the fact that the Eliot Ness I believe --\par \par \uc2 HAYES; Yes, Capone with the taxes.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: Al Capone -- it was taxes, right? And so, it may not be the most sensational thing --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: -- that brings him down, but there`s a reason for that because this is a guy who has been involved in a lot of very deep and convoluted --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Shady business.\par \par \uc2 COLEMAN: -- shady business.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: But he hasn`t stood trial for them all.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is also true.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: And I just -- I just want to say, I think he is going to be indicted in Georgia. I think what we`re talking about is what does he actually go to prison for. We don`t know. And Mar-a-Lago is the best bet. I completely agree with that. But I`m just saying, a man who`s out here saying he`s going to run for president is facing very serious state charges. And what he is known for is being the mob boss that says, wink, wink, nod, nod.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILEY: He was not winking and nodding in this case.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is very, very good point. \uc2 Maya Wiley\uc2 , Andrew Weissman, Charles Coleman, I learned a lot at the table. Thank you very much. That is it for this special edition of ALL IN. I hope you are having a fantastic holiday. Have a great night.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221227eicq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for December 16, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Sahil Kapur \par \uc2 8,207 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 December 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: -- going to let him win the week as well. All right, my choice -- my choice for who win the week is Dr. Claudine Gay. She`s the new president of Harvard. She is Black. She`s a woman. She`s going to ruin (INAUDIBLE) day.\par \par \uc2 Matt Binder and Victor Shi, thank you. You all won the week for being here. That is tonight`s "REIDOUT". ALL IN with Chris Hayes starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Mr. Chairman, in numerous places, our constitution strongly opposes insurrection and rebellion.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: New reporting the January 6 Committee is preparing to vote on multiple criminal referrals for \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): Just like everyone else in our country, he is responsible for his own actions and his own choices.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, what we know about what those charges are and what it would mean for the special counsel`s investigation. Then, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and a study in absolute power corrupting absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, CEO, TWITTER: You show the link to the real-time information, bad evasion obviously.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And guess which question has Florida`s governor literally running away from reporters?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. When it convenes on Monday, the January 6 Committee could vote to send multiple criminal referrals of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to the Department of Justice. NBC News is now reporting that while no formal decisions have been made, the committee is weighing whether or not to make three criminal referrals for the twice-impeached ex-president on charges of conspiracy, obstruction of an official proceeding and inciting an insurrection. Multiple sources stressing to NBC news that all three options are on the table, but nothing is yet set in stone.\par \par \uc2 Now, the committee is expected to meet multiple times over this weekend before it makes its final decision and convenes on Monday. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  was the first to report the committee was mulling criminal referrals on obstruction conspiracy, but notably did not include that third charge of inciting an insurrection which reflects the evolving nature of this conversation heading into Monday`s vote.\par \par \uc2 As the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reports, "it had been why widely expected the panel would recommend charges against Trump for obstructing an official proceeding of Congress and conspiracy to defraud the United States. The panel`s members had already argued in federal court they believed it was likely he committed those two felonies. But the addition of an accusation of insurrection was a new, if perhaps unsurprising development.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, it`s worth noting that inciting an insurrection was the subject of Donald Trump`s second impeachment last year. You might remember that Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  of Maryland who sits on the January 6 Committee as well as the subcommittee currently exploring criminal referrals was the lead impeachment manager during that second impeachment trial where he made a forceful case that Trump incited the violent mob.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: We are imploring the president to help, to stand up, to help defend the United States Capitol, the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  which is under attack. We are begging essentially and he was nowhere to be found, nowhere to be found. And as I`ve emphasized this morning, that dereliction of duty, that desertion of duty was central to his incitement of insurrection and inextricable from it. Inextricable, bound together. It reveals his state of mind that day, what he was thinking as he provoked the mob to violence and further violence, and powerfully demonstrates that the ex-president knew, of course, that violence was foreseeable, that it was predictable and predicted that day since he was not surprised and not horrified. No, he was delighted.\par \par \uc2 And through his acts of omission and commission that day, he abused his office by siding with the insurrectionists at almost every point rather than with the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2 , rather than with the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, of course making the case in that kind of proceeding, an impeachment proceeding or the court of public opinion, is different than making it in a court of law. And convicting a former president of inciting Insurrection would be an unprecedented challenge for federal prosecutors even if charges were to be brought against Trump. But it`s worth remembering the criminal referrals from the committee and the Justice Department are just that. They`re referrals. They`re basically letters. The ultimate decision whether or not to prosecute the ex-president resides with special counsel Jack Smith at least at the moment.\par \par \uc2 Still, a congressional committee impaneled for the solemn duty of investigating the attack on the U.S. Capitol, then recommending that a former president face criminal charges would be a historic moment regardless of the outcome. The fact that January 6 investigators even considering these referrals is a testament to just how grave the ex- president`s actions were and how seriously the committee takes its responsibility to hold him accountable for them.\par \par \uc2 Luke Broadwater is a Congressional Report at the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  who helped break this story for the paper. Sahil Kapoor, the Senior National Political Reporter for NBC News. And both join me now.\par \par \uc2 Sahil, let me just start with you on some of the kind of technical questions here about what exactly the process looks like. So, my understanding is there`s a subcommittee that`s -- the main committee committing these -- considering these referrals, those will then be passed to the main committee which is going to vote on them in public on Monday? Is -- am I tracking that correctly?\par \par \uc2 [20:05: 38]\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: That does sound like how this is going to play out, Chris. These recommendations, these three recommendations reflect the work of a subcommittee that had been tasked with drawing them up. And it is now up to the full committee to decide whether or not to vote on them. They could vote on one of them, they could vote on uh some of them, they could vote on all of them, or none of them.\par \par \uc2 The three charges again are insurrection, obstruction of an official proceeding of Congress, that would be the one on January 6th, and conspiracy to defraud the United States. That is a huge deal that this committee is actively considering votes on all of these three criminal charges. That`s according to three sources uh who spoke to NBC News` Capitol Hill team.\par \par \uc2 Two things I want to emphasize. The first is that no final decision has been made on whether to vote on any of these charges. And the second, as you point out, Chris, Congress is not a prosecutorial body. This decision lies solely with the Justice Department. And this would simply be a very high-profile recommendation that has been investigating this for the better part of a year.\par \par \uc2 And there has been a debate surrounding this committee as to whether to pursue criminal charges versus \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at all. There`s been one school of thought that suggests what`s the point of it, it`s simply a recommendation, it has no force, you know, and could potentially even backfire politically by creating the impression that the DOJ is under political pressure.\par \par \uc2 And there`s this other school of thought that seems to be gaining more traction which is the idea that if the January 6 Committee`s work is above politics, they should act like it. They should act on conviction. They shouldn`t be worried about the political blowback of doing what they believe is the right thing to do. And that seems to be the context in which this possibility has risen, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I will say, having interviewed all the members of committee in numerous, numerous times over the course of the Committee`s life, I`ve asked questions recently similarly along those lines. I mean the idea that this would be essentially political or would sort of back the DOJ into a corner, and multiple members have said essentially look, we should make the judgment call we think is correct and let the chips fall where they may.\par \par \uc2 Luke, I want to talk about those first two charges that Sahil just mentioned which is obstructing an official proceeding and conspiracy to defraud. And as you note in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporting that we read from, these are two federal crimes in the U.S. code that the committee`s own law lawyers have already argued in federal court. They believe the president committed when they were trying to get John Eastman`s emails subject to the crime-fraud exception. Just explain what that public record already is.\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Right. In some ways, there`s absolutely no surprise about the committee wanting to pursue these charges because they`ve already said it in court, right? And in fact, a judge, a federal judge has agreed with the committee, and in a really blistering opinion some months ago, said that it was more likely than not that both \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 John Eastman\uc2  had committed these crimes.\par \par \uc2 And so, that -- you know, obviously, that was -- that was applying a civil standard, a more likely than not standard not a beyond the reasonable doubt standard, but it was a powerful ruling. And so, those crimes do carry tens of years in jail in prison if they were to be pursued. Some of the actual rioters who broke into the capitol have been charged with obstruction of a official proceeding. So, it`s -- that`s a count that Federal prosecutors have already used.\par \par \uc2 Now, what they haven`t done is they haven`t started to apply it up the chain to the political actors, right? What they -- what they`ve done is they have taken these more serious charges to the organizers of the Oath Keepers who -- and in some ways, that is a step up beyond the rioters because we`re getting to conspiracy and organization. So, we`re starting to see the Feds move in that direction. But the January 6 Committee is hoping they can lay out a really clear case that will be very persuasive to the prosecutors and frankly to the public.\par \par \uc2 What they view one of their main goals is educating the public about what these crimes are and what the facts and the evidence are to support them.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I wonder, Sahil, we may not know so, I`ll just ask. But you know, the referral is one thing. I wonder if our understanding of what -- how that referral will come about. Will they vote on it in public on Monday do we think? Will there be something bigger than the referral itself in terms of, as Luke was saying, a kind of case behind that particular statute if they vote for it.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:11]\par \par \uc2 KAPUR: Well, Chris, they haven`t gotten quite so detailed on it. What we are expecting is a public meeting on Monday at 1:00 p.m. Eastern time in the newly renamed Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  Caucus Room where this committee has been meeting. And they expect to do two things. They expect to roll out and vote on adoption of their final report and also vote on these potential criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 Again, we don`t know if they`re going to do it, but if they do it, the expectation that is they do it side by side on Monday during that meeting.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, there has been largely a tremendous amount of unanimity shown from this committee across partisan lines. There have been some reporting about some back and forth between \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and some staff members on some questions of emphasis regarding \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . There does seem to me, Luke, in your reporting and others, some genuine debate here about this question and some suspense as to what the committee actually is going to fundamentally decide on these criminal referrals.\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Right. So, we reported back in the spring that, you know, the committee`s staff and their investigators, many of whom are U.S. attorneys and really serious prosecutors, had already concluded that they had enough evidence to recommend criminal referrals against Trump. But there was some debate about whether it was the right course of action.\par \par \uc2 I remember \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2 , one of the committee members coming out and saying it`s basically -- a criminal referral is worthless. It`s just a piece of paper. But I think over time what has happened is as the committee has continued their investigation, they`ve gotten ahead of the Justice Department and they revealed so many things at their hearings. They saw the Justice Department started to pick up on their evidence and call in witnesses they had already interviewed based on evidence they had revealed at hearings. And they started to see they could have a real influence in education -- in educating the public and revealing this evidence.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think they`ve come around to the idea that this is their duty that they`ve collected this evidence, they`ve done the work, they`ve analyzed the law, and it`s their job now to put it out as a referral.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Luke Broadwater, Sahil Kapur, thank you both, gentlemen, for your great reporting. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, with reports that the January 6th Committee is considering criminal referrals against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , how would this decision impact the already sprawling DOJ investigation? Well, former Federal Prosecutor Renato Mariotti joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We are following the breaking news the January 6 Committee is actively considering recommending criminal charges against ex-president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , including the crime of insurrection against government of the United States. The possible criminal referrals against the twice-impeached president are obstruction of an official proceeding with Congress and conspiracy. The final decision hasn`t been reached yet but the committee is meeting this weekend before voting on possible referrals on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Renato Mariotti, a former Federal prosecutor, now a Legal Affairs Columnist for Politico Magazine. Renato, I want to start with what would be by far the most dramatic of these referrals. And again, in the context that a referral is essentially a letter from Congress to the Department of Justice, but that is for insurrection. And it`s just interesting, I think, to read the U.S. criminal code on this. It`s not a crime that`s very often charged. Whoever insights sets foot on assists or engage in any rebellion or insurrection against the authority of the United States and the laws thereof or gives aid or comfort there too shall we find under this title or imprisoned not more than 10 years or both and shall be incapable of holding any office under the United States.\par \par \uc2 What do you think about that as an actual plausible thing that you could -- you could possibly charge \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with?\par \par \uc2 RENATO MARIOTTI, LEGAL AFFAIRS COLUMNIST, POLITICO MAGAZINE: I actually think of the charges that are being considered by the committee, this is the one that their evidence is the strongest on. I think it fits the conduct to a tee, right, because it`s an incitement to violence, an incitement to an insurrection here. I think that is really in fact what he was doing.\par \par \uc2 I think the primary challenge here is a legal one, is a First Amendment challenge because usually the President of the United States giving a political speech or a public-minded speech is protected under the First Amendment. And so, you know, I think the testimony of Cassidy Hutchinson is so important. And this is an area where the committee really I think broke ground. Her testimony where Trump said that, you know, he didn`t care the people in the audience had weapons because they weren`t there to attack him, they were going to be marching to the Capitol. I really think that is what would get that particular charge over that person moment (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And the First Amendment, we should be clear, I mean, would exist for anyone, right? I mean, you -- anyone, even not the president, has -- you could have politically protected speech in which you can get up and say, you know, I hate the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . I would like to see the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  overthrown. That`s my view. Like, that`s First Amendment- protected speech.\par \par \uc2 But you bring up a good point about the ultimate prosecutability, right? There`s a difference between being the prosecutor has to bring a case and make sure you don`t lose it and the analysis happening from the committee. How do you think about those two different ways of thinking about crimes?\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: Yes. I have to say, when I was a prosecutor, we were focused first and foremost on whether we had the evidence. And one of the things I liked about this particular charge is that there`s no question, right, that he said those words. There`s no question that people who are in the audience then proceeded to violently attack the capitol. So, a lot of the facts are easy to prove here.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. That`s a good point.\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: Yes, right? And it doesn`t really turn on his mental state which is great too. That makes things easier. The challenge is a legal one. And I do -- and there are whenever there`s a First Amendment issue in a case, it`s a challenge. It`s the same thing when there`s like a threat made to a judge. But I do think, you know, this is one where the committee`s evidence actually will help overcome that hurdle.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In terms of those other two crimes, what`s notable to me there which we said before is the fact that a federal judge has already found it more likely than not that he committed these two. How do you see these referrals should they happen landing in the world of Jack Smith, the special counsel over the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: Well, I actually think the 1512 referral is the one that`s more important to Jack Smith. In other words, there -- we`re talking about obstruction of an official proceeding. That can include, for example, an obstruction of this Congressional committee. So, for example, it could mean -- and my dog is also --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Your dog is obstructing this official proceeding but I -- but I`m fine with it.\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: But in in all seriousness, I do think that that`s also potentially part of what might be under the 1512. But I do think, you know, regarding the obstruction of an official proceeding, I think that, you know, that is -- that is a little bit more challenging because it goes to if it`s -- if they`re talking about the proceeding on January 6th, it goes to Trump`s mental state. So, it`ll be interesting to see exactly what evidence the committee is going to point to there,\par \par \uc2 I think that it`ll be -- to the extent to which they`re talking about their own, for example, potential threats made (INAUDIBLE) otherwise, I think that could be very interesting. And to the 371, I think the question is where is the intent to defraud. And I think that`s got to be that fake electors scheme.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, I guess -- right, the fake lecture scheme as a sort of substance of fraud which seems, you know, again fairly straightforward. But I guess the big question to me is just, does it matter -- I mean, I think it matters to the committee because it fulfills a kind of expressive purpose, it fulfills a kind of public education role which I think they see themselves as having, right? And I think it also does soften the ground should the Department of Justice decide to indict, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, in some ways it says look, in a very public transparent fashion, these are the crimes we think he committed. But does it matter at all, one way or the other, do you think to Jack Smith?\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: I don`t think it matters much to Jack Smith. I actually think to the extent that the committee was ever -- was talking about obstruction as to their own investigation I think it would matter. In other words, if they knew something he didn`t about witness tampering. But I think this is really about educating the public. Yes, I remember years ago after -- you know, early after I graduated from Law School, you know, reading the 911 Commission report. And that was being very meaningful.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s really what this committee is doing. They`re creating a historical record and they`re letting the public know we believe the evidence is there.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Renato Mariotti, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MARIOTTI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  becomes the latest brainwormed billionaire to prove nobody should have absolute power. More on Musk`s latest attempts to stifle critics next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We are living in a new gilded age of extreme inequality and power- mad billionaires calling the shots and dominating business as well as politics. In fact, we are just coming off the first experiment in this country`s long history of a power-mad billionaire, by his own calculation at least, occupying the highest office in the land. It was arguably the most catastrophic failure of a presidency in our history certainly since the Civil War, ending with an attempt to upend the constitutional republic. And now, almost a year after the end of Donald Trump`s term in office, we are witnessing another power-mad billionaire attempt to coerce and capture the American discourse.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , CEO and founder of \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , bought the website that helped launch Donald Trump`s first campaign. And Musk now seems intent on emulating Trump in his new role as head of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Last night, Musk went on a sort of Mad King off with their heads purge that some are already calling the Thursday night Massacre.\par \par \uc2 Musk suspended the accounts of several high-profile journalists who work at organizations including CNN, \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , and \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . Their alleged infraction, and it`s not quite clear they actually did it because we don`t have any transparency, was reporting on and sharing information about an account that Musk permanently banned from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  the previous day called Elon Jet.\par \par \uc2 Now, that account started in 2020 by then high school student and fan of Musk, posted publicly available information about the location of Musk`s private plane. It actually appears to be owned by \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 . This is data that is easily accessible online from basically every plane in the sky. It`s actually really cool to look at. This is what the real-time flight traffic around the world looked like earlier this evening on one of the sites that tracks it.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  requires all planes to send out signals of their real-time location and other flight data. If you think about it for more than 15 seconds you can understand why you wouldn`t want to keep that private. Having that data out there helps to prevent crashes. It keeps people on the ground informed.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:04]\par \par \uc2 But according to \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , sharing that public data on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is equivalent to posting "basically assassination coordinates in obvious direct violation of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  terms of service."\par \par \uc2 That`s a real stretch for a bunch of reasons, not the least of which is the terms of service he retrofitted to match the infraction. He added them prohibiting the sharing of anyone`s current location without their permission after he banned the ElonJet account, see how that works?\par \par \uc2 And I`m pretty sure this new terms of service is just totally incoherent. I mean, Musk would have violated that rule himself with this selfie that he posted last month saying "just leaving Twitter H.Q. code review."\par \par \uc2 In his terms, that was an assassination coordinates for all the engineers in that room. But that obvious ludicrous hypocrisy didn`t stop Musk. He went so far as to publish these cues, the bad journalists of doxxing him, maliciously spreading private information in a Twitter Spaces discussion last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, CEO, \uc2 TWITTER\uc2 : Showing real time information about somebody`s location is inappropriate. And I think everyone on this call would not like that to be done to them. And there`s not going to be any distinction in the future between journalists, simple journalists, and regular people, everyone`s going to be treated the same. They`re not special because you`re a journalist. You`re just -- on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , you`re a citizen. So no special treatment. You dox, you get suspended. End of story.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, if there`s any remaining doubt about Musk`s motivations, the Thursday Night Massacre also just happened to include the official kind of Mastodon, a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  competitor. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  also began blocking many links to Mastodon which is about (INAUDIBLE) as it gets, I`m still not clear why that happened or what the halfhearted excuse is.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  bought \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . He could do what he wants with it. It`s not a violation of First Amendment. The true lesson of the heart of this story is not about \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  or content moderation. It`s about what happens when an individual hopped up on his own narcissism and power and being told he`s brilliant, used to getting his way decides to rule like a king. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 That person has nothing checking him. No board, no advisors, no bureaucracy, no one pushing back. Of course, that is the world that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  inhabited for years, he ran his weird family enterprise inherited from his daddy, which was a private business with no shareholders, no board.\par \par \uc2 The Trump Organization\uc2  was run almost like a medieval fiefdom, a small circle of yes-men, whatever the boss says goes.\par \par \uc2 And then, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tried to transfer that leadership style into the highest office in the land in the most powerful government on Earth.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, the bureaucracy around him, the checks and balances of American government, the courts, Congress stopped him from doing the presidential version of what \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  got up to last night.\par \par \uc2 Courts, Congress, and regular people doing their jobs like Lieutenant Colonel Alexander Vindman, restrained \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from the worst excesses of one-man rule. I don`t like this person, get rid of them, throw them in jail. We`re not making distinctions about journalists. That happens in other countries, we fought a revolution to ensure it would not happen here in fact.\par \par \uc2 Now, luckily, the stakes are much lower with \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . It`s not the nuclear arsenal. In fact, I`s almost loath to bring it up tonight.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a clear lesson, a very clear one, an important one on the same theme. You cannot trust anyone with absolute power in any domain. No matter if it`s the U.S. presidency, a social media enterprise, a cable news show, a condo board association, even a pillow company.\par \par \uc2 Left unchecked, they will drive it into the ground because people need checks. They need bureaucracy. They need institutions. They need pushback in all circumstances. And you certainly cannot trust power-mad billionaires surrounded by yes men and lackeys who think they see further and better than everyone, but they always have the best ideas.\par \par \uc2 In that way lies room, we just learned this lesson for the country. Hopefully, we will learn it in every other domain as well.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, those journalists remain banned from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  as \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  polls users about whether he should reinstate their accounts. I`ll talk to one of Musk`s suspended targets next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:38:57]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Last night, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  CEO and self-proclaimed free speech advocate \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  purged at least eight journalists on the platform. They`re supposed offense were sharing information of another account he banned this week that posted publicly available information about Elon Musk`s private jet.\par \par \uc2 These are journalists who regularly cover Musk and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and say they did not violate any \uc2 Twitter\uc2  policies.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of the targets of Elon Musk`s Twitter purge Aaron Rupar, independent journalist, publisher of the Public Notice Newsletter. We`ve had him on the program before.\par \par \uc2 Aaron, how are you doing?\par \par \uc2 AARON RUPAR, PUBLISHER, PUBLIC NOTICE NEWSLETTER: I`m doing good. Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So what, when, how did you find out that you had been suspended or banned on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 RUPAR: Yes, I was actually playing on the floor with my 7-month-old and I started getting messages around 6:00 p.m. here Central Time yesterday on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and on Gmail, texting, people telling me oh my goodness, your \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account has been suspended.\par \par \uc2 And at first I thought, you know, maybe people were trolling me or joking, or maybe there was some sort of a technical issue. But sure enough, I pulled up my \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account on my phone. And at the top of my page, there was a notice that my account had been permanently suspended.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:06]\par \par \uc2 And when I first saw this, I was completely baffled as to what the rationale for this could have been. I`m actually had a pretty low-key day on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  yesterday, because we had a blizzard here. So my kids were home all day and afternoon I basically logged off.\par \par \uc2 And it took interacting with reporters who were talking to a number of myself and other people who have been banned to piece together hours later that it had to do with posting a link to the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page for the ElonJet account, which you were just talking about.\par \par \uc2 That apparently hours after I posted a tweet just noting that even though the account was banned on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was still alive on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , Elon declared that to be a violation of their terms of service, and then the next day banned me.\par \par \uc2 So, it took multiple hours even after that to hear anything from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I eventually did hear from one of their trust and safety officials confirming that the link to the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page was the cause of me being banned.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been led to believe that the ban is temporary, and that at some point will be reinstated. Of course, there`s a poll that Elon has on his account right now, asking people whether we should be reinstated immediately or in a week.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m hopeful that eventually we`ll get our accounts back. But yes, I was as shocked as anyone to see that. This apparently rose to the level of being suspended from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, it`s funny. For years, there has been this complaint from people on the right about \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , basically that the critique goes like this. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was run by a bunch of liberals who enforced their liberal bias and who got to be on the side and who got banned. It sort of pretended to be a neutral arbiter, it was actually on the on the side of liberals and kept banning conservative accounts they like.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is one of the people that subscribes to precisely that critique, in fact, has gone to great lengths to make that critique.\par \par \uc2 And as far as I can tell, basically, the idea here is to take over \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and do exactly in reverse what the accusation is that the previous owners did, as far as I can tell, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 RUPAR: Yes, and that`s kind of a depressing thing is that, you know, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has been so valuable for myself and probably for you, I`m sure as well, you know, networking with other journalists in, you know, getting perspective from people who you wouldn`t normally speak to.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, in the previous era, there was kind of a sense that it had a rules-based order that, you know, if accounts were suspended, there was a way to appeal. And I`ll note that the link to appeal my suspension is broken on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , which I thought was very on brand, that if you want to appeal, there`s no way to do that, you know, using the proper channels.\par \par \uc2 And so, I actually had to e-mail one of the trust and safety people finding their e-mail address independently of that.\par \par \uc2 But you know, there was a sense that things kind of made sense. And then it was almost kind of a quasi-public institution of assert, even though of course, it`s a private company.\par \par \uc2 And now, I think what this shows yesterday is that, of course, you know, the terms of service are basically whatever Elon wakes up and wants them to be on a given day.\par \par \uc2 And if they want to ban someone like myself, who, you know, I really had no investment in this ElonJet thing, I just thought it was newsworthy when it was banned from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that it was still active on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , because I think that kind of spoke to Elon being, you know, this purported free speech and robust speech advocate. And yet, you know, information that`s publicly available was too sensitive for him to allow on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , even though it was still allowed on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, you know, it`s unfortunate to see what`s happening with \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And I guess we all have to make our individual decisions about when to cut baits, you know, and when it`s no longer worth kind of sticking around on there.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, obviously, I find it very valuable and have for a long time and have learned a lot from a lot of people. The poll, which has been put out to fight about your reinstatement, which has a kind of King Joffrey feel to it, like, you know, in front of the -- in front of the banquet like, whoa, do we let it back?\par \par \uc2 Again, all of this has to me is about this sort of like the kingly wins, but it`s also about, you know, the critique here that the right has of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is just identical to the critique of the, "liberal media" that`s developed under the Nixon years, particularly by \uc2 Roger Ailes\uc2  who developed it and developed it, you know, working for Nixon, and then would go on to found Fox News, right?\par \par \uc2 The idea is, you know, they`re pretending to be unbiased, but really, they`re all libs and the only -- the only thing that`s really unbiased you can really trust is if it`s run by us conservatives.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s basically what`s happening here, right? I mean, and the only question is, how much that matches the world of social media where it actually matters what the user base is and who the users are because you don`t control the programming there the way you do over on Fox News.\par \par \uc2 RUPAR: Yes, And I think the other thing that`s important to note here is that this will very likely have a chilling effect on coverage of Musk.\par \par \uc2 You know, as you mentioned earlier, myself, I`ve done a couple newsletter articles being critical of him and you know, reporters who have been critical of him in the course of their reporting were among those banned.\par \par \uc2 And now we`re going to have to think twice, you know, if our accounts are reinstated, before we post something that`s critical of him, or even just, you know, down the middle of reporting, that could be construed as being critical of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , because apparently, we can be banned for any reason whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:03]\par \par \uc2 The tweet that I posted that led to me being banned, as I mentioned earlier, when I posted it, it did not violate the terms of service only hours later, after Elon changed them, did it violate the terms of service.\par \par \uc2 And so, you can kind of operate in that way. You know, it seems like almost anything that you post could be construed as violating the terms of service retroactively.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, if you could create laws that didn`t exist at the time people broke them, and then accuse people of breaking them, then you don`t really have any sort of legal regime to speak of.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I mean, I think you know, A. J. Liebling, who`s the great New Yorker writer for decades, you know, famously said about the inherent contradictions between free markets and a free press. That freedom of the press belongs to those who own one. And there`s a version of that on display with the Mad Twitter King this evening.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Rupar, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 RUPAR: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  never met an issue he couldn`t turn into a media circus until now. What has the man that some people are calling short Trump running from the cameras? Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:42]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You always know when Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  wants to pick a big cultural war fight that`s going to play well on Fox News. Like this week when he called for a grand jury to criminally investigate the manufacturers of COVID vaccines. When he boasted about his election police squad arresting 24 residents for voter fraud, even though the state told them all they could vote.\par \par \uc2 When he signed his Don`t Say Gay bill, announced Stop Woke Act, slammed \uc2 Disney Corporation\uc2  because they had the gall to oppose his Don`t Say Gay bill.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis is a stunt man who pulls political stunts to feed the content on primetime stunts. Stunts that often fall apart and cause serious harm to real people.\par \par \uc2 Like dropping a group of men, women and children, migrants from Venezuela who are seeking asylum in the U.S. on a random island off the coast of Massachusetts under false pretenses.\par \par \uc2 Those people arrested on fraud. Many have been told by election officials they could legally vote. Now those cases are falling apart and charges are getting dropped.\par \par \uc2 There`s one thing when really significant part of actually governing, like the actual power of this state that has to do with freedom and rights and autonomy and not being told by the government what to do. You might even see it`s about freedom, which DeSantis does not want to talk about. And that`s abortion.\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court`s Dobbs ruling striking down Roe v. Wade in June, Florida banned abortions after 15 weeks with some exceptions. Anti-abortion advocates want more. Basically, they want a total abortion ban at six weeks.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is a savvy enough political operator understand that a total ban, the six-week ban wouldn`t be popular in Florida.\par \par \uc2 So, while he was running for reelection this year, he basically ran away from the question. I mean, that literally.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What would you like to see as an ultimate end to abortion? Is that your thought (PH)?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s weird. He`s such like a swaggering tough guy when he`s got his like set pieces and his big press conferences where he announces what new enemy of the day he`s taking on.\par \par \uc2 Not there, though. You noticed that? Not there.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you think happened after his big win in the election? \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was asked again about a six week ban and now he said he is "Willing to sign great life legislation."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Florida State Representative \uc2 Anna Eskamani\uc2 . She previously served as the public affairs director for Planned Parenthood in South Central Florida. She now leads the Democratic caucus in the state legislature on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Representative, it`s good to have you. First, can you tell me a little bit of what the state of Florida abortion law is now post Dobbs?\par \par \uc2 STATE REP. \uc2 ANNA ESKAMANI\uc2  (D-FL): Well, thanks so much for having me. Right now in Florida we have a 15 week abortion ban signed into law by Governor DeSantis with no exceptions for rape, incest or human trafficking. It is impacting our patients, we are seeing doctors allow women to become more sick before they can access care. And we`re seeing minors be forced to go to other states when they experience incest in order to seek medical care for termination of that pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 So, even though it`s a 15-week ban, it still has been devastating. And we are preparing, as you just reported on Governor DeSantis pushing an even more extreme ban.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, given that you serve in the state legislature and your background is in reproductive rights, my understanding as I was falling this issue in the run up to the election was it was always clear that Florida Republicans weren`t going to stop at 15 weeks. For folks that argue that this is murder, that`s totally insufficient. They don`t want to allow past six weeks or so, or not at all.\par \par \uc2 And so, it was only very clear that Republicans in the State wanted that. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  seem to successfully evade ever definitively answering the question of whether he would support them inevitably, throughout the entire election as far as I can, the campaign.\par \par \uc2 ESKAMANI: Absolutely. I mean, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , is practicing an ideology of convenience, where he preaches freedom, in the case of vaccines and masks, they want to take away bodily autonomy and freedom for more than half the state.\par \par \uc2 And he would run scared, whenever he was asked about abortion, he would pivot to a different subject matter, even when he signed the abortion ban that we have currently into law, he talks about other issues while signing that ban.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:07]\par \par \uc2 So, he`s clearly running scared from this issue. And he should, because majority of Floridians agree that abortion should remain safe and legal. And as we saw around the country, abortion access is a winning issue.\par \par \uc2 We know that in the United States, we know that here in Florida.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, who is -- so there is clearly been pressure from the anti- abortion conservatives and Republicans who want to ban abortion completely, or pass a six-week ban in this case, to get him on board. What`s happened in terms of the movement on that, because it now seems like the fix was kind of in from the beginning, or people that I know who know this space always thought it was going to be the case that if he won, which seemed likely, and got the majorities in the statehouse, Election Day was going to come and go and then boom, presto, now on to the six week ban, is that basically what`s happening?\par \par \uc2 ESKAMANI: Well, here`s the deal, Chris, Governor DeSantis wants to run for president against former President Trump. And Trump can take credit for the entire Dobbs decision as he have done for months now.\par \par \uc2 And so, if you`re going to run against President Trump, you have to be anti-abortion, you have to be very extreme on your anti-abortion positions. But I would argue that running for president as the ticket for the Republican Party trying to appeal to every American, it`s not popular to ban abortion.\par \par \uc2 So, DeSantis finds himself in a really interesting place where he`s feeling pressure from the right to