e air of sort of desperation and decline, as someone who studies this and is talking to Trump voters, is it real?\par \par \uc2 SARAH LONGWELL, PUBLISHER, THE BULWARK: Yes, it`s real. You know, I have been -- I`ve done a number of focus groups with Trump 2016 and Trump 2020 voters since the midterms, and the signals couldn`t be clearer. We are finding very little enthusiasm for a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  2024 campaign. You know, people are -- it`s not that they`re breaking with Trump. And I sort of want to be clear about this.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: They`re not rejecting him. They`re not saying I hate him now. They`re just saying, you know, I think we should look to the future. I think there are better options. When we ask, are you excited? You know, in one group, there was one guy and another group, people were kind of like doing this. And a lot of people just said no, you know, I`m much more interested in \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  or I`m interested in somebody new, you know, \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2 , Tim Scott. They`re throwing out names. But it is clear that, you know, they won`t -- if it`s Trump, they`d vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: But they`re looking right -- they`re shopping. They want something new. They`re looking for something exciting. This stick which was so sort of funny and, I guess, interesting in its own weird way last time, has really lost, you know, its luster, its newness. And now, they`re on for a new show.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, it`s hard. It`s hard to keep a show going year after year after year. The Apprentice ratings went down over time. Eventually, the show was canceled.\par \par \uc2 Molly, let me -- let me ask you. I mean, the thing that jumped out to me was the fact that this indicator, favorability rating, that Trump always had this sort of weirdly high floor and low ceiling, so his numbers would sort of bounce around in this range, right? Like, people had these various set and polarized opinions of him, but you couldn`t break down through the floor no matter what he was doing in the most insane circumstances, you know, much to the sort of consternation and um hair pulling of liberals like myself. It does seem like that might be going away at least in the poll data. How serious should we take that polling data?\par \par \uc2 20:10:43]\par \par \uc2 MOLLY MURPHY, DEMOCRATIC POLLSTER: I think we should take it seriously. I think that he is really at a lower point in his popularity than we have seen in many, many years. But the last time we saw him this low was shortly after his first announcement, and we saw how that went. So, I do, you know, think that if you are \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  right now, you are not happy with how things are going, you are not happy that this is what is being talked about shortly after you`ve announced your campaign, but I think two things can be true. And you could also rather be \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in this Republican primary field than any other candidate. And I happen to think both of those things are true.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think -- I think I`m there with you although I`m moving -- yes, you`re nodding your head. I think I`m there with you. And what you just said, Molly, is like it`s the -- it`s the formative cataclysm of our political life, right? It`s the fact that like that guy, the Trump Steaks guy got himself elected president like --\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, my question to you, Sarah, is from where you sit, what`s different now, right? So, why -- like, what makes it different than 2015? What are the differences in how people are perceiving him or where the running room might be than they were seven years ago?\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: Oh, there`s so many things that are different. I mean, first of all, that act was new. He was saying things. You know, the build the wall, the way he talked about immigrants and immigration. You know, at the time, when he said -- you know, he called for a ban on Muslims, I think we all thought, well, you can`t say that. You know, when Access Hollywood came out, we thought, well, that`s the end of him. But he had this incredible staying power. And so, you know, we began to sort of think nothing could take this guy down.\par \par \uc2 But the one thing that has chipped away at him is sustained electoral defeats. He lost in `18, he lost in `20, and now he has lost again in 2022. And so, he cannot tell people, hey, you`re going to get sick of all the winning because nobody believes that anymore. And the election denialism, the making it all about him -- you know, he had sort of a fresh message, insane as I thought it was, we hadn`t quite heard things like that before. And he was, you know, changing the whole coalition of the Republican Party the way he talked about trade and others issues. He was different from other politicians.\par \par \uc2 But now, there`s tons of Trump imitators. I mean even the way that people talk about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is in the context of Trump. They call him Trump with a brain or Trump without the baggage. Somebody, you know --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Shorter Trump?\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: They just -- they always talk about him --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They say shorter Trump.\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: They don`t -- they -- I haven`t heard -- I haven`t heard shorter Trump. But they`re all like Trump is the context for everything. And so, now, there`s this sort of appetite for the fighting. There`s the -- like, he`s changed the party. He`s changed what people crave, but he is not the only one who can deliver that now. And so, that`s just -- that`s -- yes, things are super different. And the midterms really changed everything, his weakness after the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Now -- but I also -- and I -- sorry to go on, but I just -- I also want to caution. We have seen him fight his way out of corners before.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean --\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: And there are weird --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Look, a cardinal --\par \par \uc2 LONGWELL: Yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: A cardinal principle of this show is that we don`t predict the future and the future is unwritten and I don`t know what will happen in the future. Now -- like, to me, the analysis is right now, he at a political low point power-wise. And I think that`s true. I think it`s borne out by what we`re seeing. But things change, right? Like we had a once-in-a- century pandemic I didn`t see that coming, you know. You could -- anything could happen.\par \par \uc2 The thing that I think contributes to this, Molly, too, is having covered the 2020 primary in the Democratic Party, you know, electability loomed enormously large, right? Like, who is going to win? And that I think ultimately had -- that was what carried the day was the Judgment particularly of South Carolina Democratic primary voters, particularly Black South Carolina primary voters who were like, we know American politics and we think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has got the best shot of winning. And I think they actually made a pretty darn good bet. And I think that is probably going to figure larger, I would imagine, in the minds of the electorate of the Republican Party this time around. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I think that may be true but I think that also requires the Republican candidates who may get in this field, whether it`s DeSantis, you know, whether it is \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  or \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , or whoever other potential anti-Trump candidates are thinking about getting in this primary. The other piece that helped \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is once he did show momentum in some of his early primary wins, a lot of those candidates got out so that they could consolidate this primary. We did not see that happen in 2016 which is a huge part of the reason that Trump was successful. None of those -- you know, if this had been Trump