ssion that DePape made to Sergeant Carla Hurley while he was in the hospital the next day just hours after the attack.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s talk about DePape and that -- and that tape. I mean, one of the things basically that happened, I think, just in the broader context of this was, the news came across, I think many people intuited immediately this probably had some political motivation. This is the house of the Speaker of the House. It then came out that he was looking for see Pelosi, and then there was this desperate effort to sort of spin it away by some that it couldn`t be a political motive or that he wasn`t doing it because he had right-wing views.\par \par \uc2 It seems by his own admission that he was on a kind of personal mission of violence against people that he viewed as political enemies who were liberals.\par \par \uc2 VAINSHTEIN: Yes, absolutely. I mean, in -- you know, he readily admitted to the disgust that he felt toward, you know, an array of, you know, leftist politicians, Democratic politicians in D.C. And in fact, even in the recording, he appeared to be very emotional and in some cases pretty angry when he was describing Pelosi, Trump, and you know, he mentioned other people on the list that he had plans to target in some way.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The list as I see has been reported included \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , of course, Joe Biden`s son, the actor Tom Hanks, the California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 . And this really struck out to me. And this is quite interesting and someone random, a feminist anthropologist named Gayle Rubin. Now, that is not a household name in the way that say Tom Hanks or \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  might be, but that`s a gender theorist who`s written about queer theory. She`s written about, I think, drag shows, and has been identified in a very specific part of the right-wing media, my understanding, is like the theorist behind gender transgression.\par \par \uc2 VAINSHTEIN: Yes. I think that name on the list stood out to me too. I mean, I think it took a lot of the people in the courtroom by surprise. It`s -- you know Gayle Rubin isn`t a name you, you know, would expect to hear in this laundry list of people that DePape mentioned. It`s unclear exactly what -- you know, what issues he had with the professor. But he had at one point told investigators that he was targeting, you know, a radical professor. So, it`s possible that this was the person he was speaking about.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He is in custody, I imagine. What is the timeline that we anticipate for the actual prosecution here?\par \par \uc2 VAINSHTEIN: Yes. He is in custody. He`s being held -- he`s being held without bail. There`s a little bit of a parallel timeline going on with the state`s case against him and the federal case against him. So, his next appearance in court will be on December 28th. That will be for his arraignment for the state charges against him. And those charges include attempted murder, elder abuse, false imprisonment, assault with the deadly weapon, as well as, you know, the specific count that relates to the Pelosi family which was threatening the life of or causing serious bodily harm to a public official.\par \par \uc2 On the federal side, the prosecution has asked for more time, so they`ll probably be back in court in February. And he`s already pleaded not guilty to charges of attempted kidnapping and assault.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:39]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Annie Vainshtein doing some great reporting with the San Francisco Chronicle. And thank you for making some time for us. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 VAINSHTEIN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come. If you can`t beat them, change the rules. The latest Republican attempt to rig the electoral system, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Republicans in Georgia have now lost three \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  races in two consecutive runoffs. First with the election of Democrats \uc2 John Ossoff\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  in 2020, and then with Senator Warnock winning a full six- year term earlier this month. So, how are Georgia Republicans responding to these repeated consecutive losses?\par \par \uc2 Well, by calling to change the state`s elections, of course. Republican Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  is asking state lawmakers to end general election runoffs which Georgia holds when no candidate wins at least 50 percent of the vote. Raffensperger said, "No one wants to be dealing with politics in the middle of their family holiday."\par \par \uc2 Now, the ironic thing is that Georgia started general election runoffs towards the end of the Jim Crow era in 1964 to make it harder for Black candidates to win office. Now, the law is not helping Republicans win, they want to change it. Republicans around the country are also moving to make it harder for voters to pass ballot measures after five states had abortion on the ballot in the midterms, California, Montana, Michigan, Kentucky, and Vermont. It was a clean sweep in favor of Reproductive Rights. Seeing the writing of the wall, Republicans in Ohio race to make it harder for citizen-led initiatives to pass.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The amendment would make it so a citizen-initiated Constitutional Amendment on any ballot would have to pass by 60 percent of the vote rather than the current simple majority.\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2  (R-OH), SECRETARY OF STATE: If you don`t think that your idea is broadly popular enough to muster sixty percent vote of the people, then maybe you should not consider bringing it to the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, let`s be clear. Republicans didn`t admit it publicly at first that this was about abortion, though obviously it was. Ohio had instituted a six-week abortion ban within hours of the Supreme Court`s Dobbs ruling in June overturning Roe v. Wade. and abortion rights advocates planned ballot measures to amend the Ohio State Constitution in order to guarantee abortion access.\par \par \uc2 But a Cleveland Plain Dealer reporter obtained a letter from a Republican sponsor of the change that said, "After decades of Republicans work to make Ohio a pro-life state, the left intends to write abortion on demand into Ohio`s Constitution." So, it was exactly what it looked like. But here`s the good news. The plan failed after protesters showed up en masse at the State House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Our ballot, our vote. No on six! Our ballot, our vote. No on six! Our ballot, our vote. No on six!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They got caught red-handed and then they got called out and it didn`t pass. That is the good news, the very good news. But that`s not the whole story. The bad news is that Ohio lawmakers did just pass a sweeping voter suppression bill. That would require voters to present photo I.D., limit ballot drop boxes, reduce days for early voting. That bill now goes to Republican Governor \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  for signing.\par \par \uc2 Elections lawyer Marc Elias who fights to protect voting rights across the country today tweeted that if DeWine signs the bill, Ohio will be sued. And Marc Elias joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The election might be over but the work to protect free and fair elections is still continuing and absolutely vital. That`s especially considering the fact that so many states are moving to restrict it. And the fight happening over those states and whether the voting franchise will be restricted or grown is happening in courtrooms across the country from Arizona, to Pennsylvania, and Florida. And a lot of it being done by election lawyer Marc Elias, the founder of Democracy Docket. And Mark joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Marc, good to have you. Let`s start on the Ohio law. This seems like a -- it`s almost like, you know, like a seasonal ritual, like the Mariah Carey song, you know, hitting the airwaves that, you know, post-elections Republican state houses move their voter restriction suppression laws. In Ohio, it`s a little odd because Ohio Republicans had a good night. And I thought they already had like voter I.D. and all this stuff. So, what`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 MARC ELIAS, FOUNDER, DEMOCRACY DOCKET: Yes, so, Chris, it is a ritual. It`s exactly what you said. Remember, after 2020, we saw voter restriction laws put in place in Florida and Texas, both of which Republicans had good nights in 2020. In Iowa and Arkansas, both are states that Republicans had good nights in 2020. So, it`s not about fraud, it`s not about, you know, anything real, it is about signaling to their base that they are going to crack down on voting and make it harder.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a -- there`s a part they say out loud which is essentially we`re making it harder to vote because you know, that`s kind of what we do. And then there`s the dog whistle side which is, you know -- and you know who it`s going to impact the most.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The case of Ohio you said you will sue, what -- why and how would you sue would this law to be signed?\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: So, look, I -- you know, I`ve been very transparent about this. The first state to pass an ominous voter suppression law for no good reason after 2020 was Iowa. We sued within 24 hours. Then came Georgia. We sued within two hours. Then came Florida. We sued within six minutes. We sued Arkansas, we sued Kansas, we sued Texas. All the seven states that passed omnibus voter suppression laws, we wrote litigation immediately.\par \par \uc2 Ohio is like a little bit in a time warp. I guess they didn`t -- they didn`t want to be part of the herd two years ago, so they`re kicking off the voter suppression season for 2023. But, you know, if they bring a lawsuit that infringes on fundamental rights which include the fundamental right to vote, and they do it for no good reason as they are prepared to do, then they`ll wind up in a courtroom and they will likely wind up losing.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:47]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, my North Star on this tends to be making it easier or harder to vote, whatever the ideological partisan valence of that, right? Like, I want as many people to vote possible. Sometimes, by the way, I really think -- I keep stressing this. There are going to be races in which that helps Republicans. It really is the case that there will be races where more people turning out will help Republicans, so I don`t care about that.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s some other stuff that doesn`t feel like it has an ideological or even principled valence, like for instance, the Georgia runoffs, which to me seem kind of a bad idea. And if Raffensperger of Georgia wants to get rid of it, it seems like it simplifies the election. Like, why not just, you know, whoever gets the most votes the first time around. Do you think there`s some principle at stake here?\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: I do think there`s a principle at stake in Georgia. And by the way, I agree with you. You know, I`m -- there`s no question that I`m a partisan, but when it comes to voting rights litigation, you know, I`ve sued New York, I`ve sued Michigan, I`ve sued Maine, and I`ve sued Republican states. And you know, I took some heat because I sued Cochise County to make them certify, and people were like, but if they don`t certify, I know the Democrat could win. And the answer is yes, but counties have to certify their elections. I agree with you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right, yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: With respect to Georgia, let`s -- let us be clear. The runoff system in Georgia is the product of a racist system, OK.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: It is a racist system to have these runoff elections. The question that I have raised is why \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  and the Republicans were OK with that racist system when it was benefiting them, and why they were willing to make that racist system harder on election officials and voters by shortening the runoff period in 2022. And now, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  is all of a sudden saying, oh, we should get rid of this because it`s a burden on election officials.\par \par \uc2 Chris, this has been a burn on election officials from the beginning, but that`s been a trade-off that Republicans have been willing to make in order to disenfranchise Black voters. So, get rid of the runoffs, 100 percent, but call them out for hypocrisy a thousand percent.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Good. I`m glad you landed there because -- right, I totally agree. And in some ways, it almost looks like them trying to solve a Rubik`s cube, right? Because it`s like -- they`re like, oh, we lost that one. What if we -- what if we shortened -- what if we shorten the time? That`s like, oh, man, OK. What if we get rid of them altogether? It`s like -- and there is this weird -- I have to say, there is a strange -- you see it even in -- you know, even as they think of Republicans have largely -- and you and I have discussed this the program -- walked away from this like, flagrant big lie kind of thing except for Kari Lake, right? People basically saying we lost. There`s still this like weird obsession about methodology and as the -- or the timing or like, what do the ballots come in the mail. And it`s like, it`s just a question of how many people vote.\par \par \uc2 Like, Republican turnout was good in this election. They lost because they lost winnable races because they didn`t persuade people. They still seem very obsessed with this methodological question I think is a way of dodging that.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: And I have to say -- I think, Chris, the one that is the biggest puzzle to me is the dropboxes, because honestly, I`m not aware of any partisan valence to the dropboxes. I don`t know that it helps Democrats or hurts the Democrats or helps Republicans.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Who cares?\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: It`s a weird obsession though, Chris. It`s just weird that they keep trying to ban secure metal containers.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, right. As if like -- I want to be like, they can`t -- it can`t hurt you. Like, it can`t hurt you. It`s just a box. It`s a box of metal. And people are going to put their votes in there. And -- but I do think what it ends up playing this role -- there`s two -- there`s two things here. Obviously, there`s a long history in this country of voter suppression, right, particularly along racial lines, right? There`s that. But I also think it`s playing a weird kind of ideological role right now which is a means of avoiding the hard truths about the more basic stuff that happens in elections, which is like did you run good candidates and did you have a good message? If you could talk -- if it`s the metal box`s fault, then you never have to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: That`s exactly right. I mean, one of the things that has happened is that rather than confront bad candidates, bad issue set, bad campaigns, they have demonized voting which is an odd thing to do if you`re trying to win elections.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: But they`ve demonized it, and then they bring these ridiculous post- election challenges like we`re seeing in Arizona sort of as part of the further theater of losing --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: -- so that they can further their grievance as to what happened.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Well, I think you should try to win votes and let a lot of people vote, as many people vote as you can, and try to get a majority of them. It`s my hot take.\par \par \uc2 Marc Elias, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221216eicf00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for November 30, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Rachel Maddow, Jen Psaki \par \uc2 8,550 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: These things then start to accumulate and people want to know about you.\par \par \uc2 MARK LEIBOVICH, STAFF WRITER, THE ATLANTIC: Yes. And he`ll be called upon to open his mouth.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: It`s sometimes the kryptonite that candidates like this just, you know, don`t want to do.\par \par \uc2 REID: Yes. Well, thank you. Great piece. Excellent work.\par \par \uc2 LEIBOVICH: Thanks, Joy.\par \par \uc2 REID: We`ll definitely have you back. Mark Leibovich, thank you very much. That is tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN" with Chris Hayes is next with his special guest, the great Rachel Maddow. That starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: As the verdict of this case makes clear, the department will work tirelessly to hold account of those responsible for crimes related to the attack on our democracy on January 6, 2021.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The Attorney General underscores the landmark conviction of Trump`s foot soldiers for conspiring to steal an election. Tonight, Rachel Maddow on this historic decision for the Justice Department and what it means for the ongoing investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then, Marc Elias on what could be criminal charges for the rogue Arizona officials trying to steal votes in Cochise County.\par \par \uc2 ANN ENGLISH, MEMBER, COCHISE COUNTY BOARD OF SUPERVISORS: The county attorney told us early on, when you break the law, I can`t represent you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Plus, checking in on National Republicans in disarray.\par \par \uc2 MIKE LINDELL, CEO, MY PILLOW: I am 100 running for the \uc2 RNC\uc2  chairman against \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And how the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  outmaneuvered a radical Supreme Court to make same-sex and interracial marriage the law of the land. When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. You know, in the aftermath of the January 6 attack on the Capitol in 2021, there was this period when we were all on this show, including us, in shock and also trying to figure out what exactly had just happened? What specifically are the right words to even describe what had transpired? Was it a riot, a mob that got out of hand, an invasion, an attack?\par \par \uc2 And pretty quickly, I think within a matter of few days or weeks, most people, including us, understood it was an insurrection. It was an attempted coup d`etat. Because what happened on that day was fundamentally the most profound betrayal of the country`s Democratic principles. It was an attempt to overthrow the will of the people to throw out your vote, my vote, all our votes, to overthrow the rightful government and to install an illegitimate government of their choice, a ruler using force to veto the people.\par \par \uc2 And after that attempt failed on January 6, in the nearly two years since, a lot of people on the right elected leaders, folks on Fox News and others, they`ve gone to great lengths to try to minimize the events of that day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ANDREW CLYDE\uc2  (R-GA): There was no insurrection. And to call in an insurrection, in my opinion, is a bold-faced lie.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Was January 6 an Insurrection or could it be more accurately described as a mob of misfits?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was Trump supporters who lost their lives that day, not Trump supporters who were taking the lives of others.\par \par \uc2 CLYDE: You know, if you didn`t know the TV footage was a video from January the 6th, you would actually think it was a normal tourist visit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, you wouldn`t really think that if you looked at the footage. Now, though, the truth about the attempted coup about the Insurrection has been established definitively in law because yesterday a federal jury brought the first convictions of seditious conspiracy in a January 6 case against two of the masterminds of the right-wing gang The Oath Keepers. Elmer Stewart Rhodes III and Kelly Meggs worked to further Donald Trump`s aims. His aim is to remain in office against the will of the people and against our constitutional republic.\par \par \uc2 Now, the United States code of laws defines seditious conspiracy as "if two or more persons conspire to overthrow, put down, or destroy by force the government of the United States, or to levy war against them, or to oppose by force the authority thereof, or by force to prevent hinder or delay the execution of any law of the United States, or by force to seize take or possess any property of the United States contrary to the authority thereof."\par \par \uc2 And while that`s sort of dense legal language, in total, it`s a difficult thing to prove beyond a reasonable doubt. Convictions for a seditious conspiracy are rare for that reason. But that is what the jury found Elmer Stewart Rhodes and Kelly Meggs guilty of yesterday. That conviction now establishes as a matter of law. And as a matter of history, this crime was committed on the run of two and the day of January 6th. An attempt on the life of our democracy was committed.\par \par \uc2 We all watched it happened. We already knew it. But now it`s official and indisputable. I think that has broad implications for the ongoing investigation impending cases, and indeed for all of American politics at this moment of democratic peril and promise.\par \par \uc2 There`s members of another far-right group, The Proud Boys, that will face trial on again seditious conspiracy charges in the next few weeks. One of their former leaders Jeremy Bertino has already pleaded guilty. But of course, the chief suspect in all of this is that man, right? It`s the ex- president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And now a man running for president again and the leader of one of the two major parties.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice must decide if that person, the person who commanded, ordered, inspired essentially did everything in his power to wage this assault on the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , the authority thereof, if he will be held accountable for his actions as some of his foot soldiers now have.\par \par \uc2 Today, appearing publicly for the first time since the convictions of The Oath Keepers, attorney general \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  spoke about the DOJ`s commitment to the investigation.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:49]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARLAND: Last evening, a jury of the defendant`s peers found each of them guilty of serious felony offenses. As the verdict of this case makes clear, the department will work tirelessly to hold accountable those responsible for crimes related to the attack on our democracy on January 6, 2021.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, Attorney General Garland has been very careful, very rarely commented on these cases. In fact, it`s DOJ policy not to comment on pending cases. But things have changed now, right? First, those convictions happen so we can talk about a case that`s closed. But he may also be more willing to talk now that he has passed off investigations specifically into the ex-president to the special counsel Jack Smith.\par \par \uc2 Those investigations include both the Mar-A-Lago documents investigation as well as the sprawling January 6th case where the grand jury has been churning through Witnesses including most recently top Trump advisor \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 . And of course, the way this works, is that Jack Smith will be the arbiter deciding whether or not to bring charges against the ex- president. And as that`s the case he has predictably become the target of right-wing attacks. Even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself has shared accusations about this is -- standard stuff -- Smith`s wife and sister-in-law claiming he can`t be neutral because members of his family are liberal. We`ve seen this time and time and time again, right?\par \par \uc2 The reason these kinds of prosecutions of sedition like the one that the Department of Justice pulled off Successfully, the reason they`re so challenging is that the actions that they target themselves are fundamentally political ones, inescapably political ones. And their actions that cross the line from what is allowed and protected rightfully in a constitutional liberal democracy to what is a criminal assault against it.\par \par \uc2 Jack Smith will ultimately have to make a series of very difficult calls about what charges can and cannot be brought if any. Who should be held accountable and how. But now, he will be doing that in the aftermath of this truly historic declaration through our legal system, through us our citizens, our jury system, that there was indeed a seditious conspiracy and assault against the very Republic itself.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is my dear friend, Rachel Maddow. She`s the host "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" on MSNBC and, of course, her new podcast Rachel Maddow Presents Ultra which follows a remarkable that somewhat forgotten seditious conspiracy trial undertaken by the Department of Justice against Nazi sympathizers at the close of the second world war, a trial that did not have the same results as yesterday`s.\par \par \uc2 It`s great to have you on, Rachel. Given that -- we`ll get into that trial, but given that it`s not at all a sure thing, it was not a sure thing when these charges were brought, the significance --- there`s the legal significance but also some broader, I think, symbolic significance of these convictions yesterday.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: I absolutely think that`s true. And I think -- I mean, I don`t know from direct experience but I can surmise that the prosecution team at the Justice Department and the senior Justice Department officials who greenlit, bringing seditious conspiracy charges in these cases we`re very aware of that. I mean we know from history, we know from the papers of the prosecutors who brought cases like this in the past that they were very worried that acquittals or mistrials or anything other than a failure to secure a conviction in a case like this doesn`t just matter to those defendants. It matters to the movement that they represent.\par \par \uc2 And if you -- if you take a swing at this -- swing at them like this and you miss, it can be very damaging. It can make them seem vindicated. It can fuel their organizing and their ability to recruit. It can energize their efforts. And so, it`s a -- it`s a sort of high-risk, high-reward thing game.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: If you bring us a seditious conspiracy charge and it fails, which is more often than not, what happens, you can energize this movement that you`re otherwise acting against. That said, if you bring a seditious conspiracy charge and it succeeds as it has in the case of two of these Oath Keepers, you have as you said, changed history. And you have clarified in an unimpeachable way that what happened in that incident, what happened on January 6 was an effort to overturn the government by force. And that has been determined by the courts.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:07]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Let`s talk about the 1944 trial that makes up a good portion of your incredible podcast which everyone should listen to. and it`s totally compelling and addictive, in which they weren`t able to get that outcome despite, as we learned in your telling, like, pretty good evidence that there were people conspiring with the actual Nazi government actually seeking to aid and abet their aims in this country during World War II.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes. I mean, it was the evidence against the great solution case defendants in 1944 was stunning, um including -- I mean, one of the things that was alleged against them is that they were in a conspiracy with the Hitler government. And some of the evidence that emerged, that emerges sort of late in the podcast that you`re about is evidence from the Nazi side, from the Nazi files, and from after Nazi leaders. They`re like, yes, we were working with those Americans.\par \par \uc2 I mean, to have it confirmed in that way, you just -- it`s mind-blowing. But yet those prosecutions we`re not successful. Four years before that in 1940, there was a seditious conspiracy prosecution brought against 17 members of a paramilitary fascist group called the Christian Front where they had U.S. military heavy machine guns and we`re planning on shooting up armories, they had been stockpiling -- making and stockpiling bombs and stolen U.S. military weapons. They were absolutely planning on doing what the government charged them with doing, but there wasn`t a successful prosecution there either.\par \par \uc2 It`s just really hard to convict people of sedition. We have a First Amendment right to say and believe terrible things and to associate with whomever we choose to. There are strong constitutional protections that are in the way of these types of prosecutions for the good. Our constitution protects us in that way.\par \par \uc2 Sedition is also hard to prove because by definition, we`ve talked about this before, if you`re on trial for section, that means your efforts to overthrow the government didn`t work. And so, it can -- in respect, it looked like it was not that serious or never a real risk of succeeding.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, and that -- and that I think is a really key part of all this and I think a key part of understanding all this because it is notable to me the defense from the right, very rarely has been like they were doing the right thing, right? It was basically these were goofballs, these was misfits, this was a mob, this was an accident. It wasn`t -- it didn`t really amount to much. And it`s striking how much that was the defense in the trials that you talk about that really, is this really that big a thing, basically?\par \par \uc2 And for -- you know, I think the contention of yourself and myself and, you know, our shows is that yes, this was a very big thing. I think the American people have increasingly realized that. But there`s something about the Declaration here that establishes that along with the committee`s report coming that seems to be important in that respect for history if nothing else.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: For history and for how this moment is remembered, right? So, the right as -- and those clips that you just showed in your open of members of Congress saying this was a normal tourist visit and they didn`t do anything wrong and things just got a little bit out of hand, I mean, you can show those alongside to people even further right on the ideological spectrum who were talking about January 6th is one of the greatest moments in U.S. history and very exciting and marvelous, fabulous. And that -- they can say whatever they want, but they ought to have a good fight with history um in terms of the other side of that argument.\par \par \uc2 And the other side of that argument which is that January 6 was an attempt to overthrow the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  by force and that`s bad and it was a serious assault on us as a democracy and that`s bad. And anybody who was associated with that ought to be effectively excluded from any, any reach at political power ever again for the rest of their lives. I mean you`ve got to fight that in history. And having a seditious conspiracy conviction in the Oath Keeper`s case -- and remember, there`s two more seditious conspiracy cases coming up. Proud Boys coming up next week. More Oath Keepers held -- being brought up on the same charges December 19th.\par \par \uc2 These convictions tell you in history what this was. And yes, they`ll try to contest it but it`s going to make it much harder for them to Define this as something that was normal or laudable.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And that of course brings us to the person who is at the center of this conspiracy. And it wasn`t -- I mean, parts of it were conspiracy because it was behind closed doors. Parts of it was just very publicly saying I want to stay in power against the will of the people, which is of course \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who is you know the subject of multiple criminal inquiries by the special counsel. We kind of know that, confirmed by the Attorney General now.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a strange thing, right? There`s this mismatch because at some level it`s like what he did was sedition, what he did was treasonous, what he did was the greatest betrayal of the oath that I`ve probably seen in my -- in my life, certainly. But that doesn`t necessarily square with like the case that you could make to get him -- against him and make a conviction. And that really difficult call is really squarely placed before Smith, the special prosecutor at this point.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:06]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes. And I mean, it`s funny I printed out the cheat sheet on the -- on the verdict for the Oath Keepers defendants, right? And it`s a -- it`s a grid because it`s hard to keep track of how many of these -- how many of these defendants facing how many different charges and which ones were guilty and which ones weren`t. Two of the five defendants were held -- were found guilty of seditious conspiracy. Three of them were found not guilty. All of them were found guilty of obstruction of an official proceeding.\par \par \uc2 I mean there`s a mix of different ways that this can be approached legally. And even this one jury looking at this one bolus of evidence came to a very complex conclusion in terms of what these people are actually guilty of. That`s important in terms of thinking that this is some sort of slam dunk. We`ll see if that sort of matrix-like response from the jury holds for the next seditious conspiracy trials too.\par \par \uc2 But I think the other part of it, Chris, is that if they are going to bring seditious conspiracy or other serious felony charges against the former president for his role as the sort of center of the octopus that results in this assault on the government on January 6, first of all there`s nothing to compare that to. That is a decision that is going to be a decision that is made independent of whatever happens to any paramilitary pro-Trump groups. But also, it will invoke a whole new level of Justice Department discipline and confidence which is can they stand up to the partisan hurricane that they will unleash as Republicans, the president -- former president himself, but also 