ors defied state law Monday, refusing to certify the county`s midterm election results.\par \par \uc2 The state`s former Democratic Attorney General and the former Republican Maricopa County Attorney said in a letter the prosecutor should "consider criminal charges." Adding that they "Take no pleasure in making this prosecution recommendation." But as the former County attorney wrote, "This craziness has to stop."\par \par \uc2 The county is now being sued by Arizona Secretary of State. Ann English, the lone Democrat on the board who voted to certify, was blunt about the fallout.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ENGLISH: Anytime you break the law there should be consequences. And now, we`re in the throes of consequences. We, in Cochise County, being sued, we`ll have to find a lawyer to represent us and we won`t be represented by the County Attorney because the county attorney told us early on, when you break the law, I can`t represent you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Marc Elias is a voting rights litigation expert, founder of Democracy Docket. His law group is also suing the Cochise County Board of Supervisors over the refusal to certify. And he joins me now. Marc, I`m going to get into this specific case, but before we do, you and I sort of always do this sort of half -- glass half empty half full thing. But it is striking to me that after all that we heard and all the fears that we had, this lone County in Arizona really does appear to be the only one doing this kind of flagrant frontal dereliction of their administrative and democratic duty.\par \par \uc2 MARC ELIAS, FOUNDER, DEMOCRACY DOCKET: Yes, I think that`s right, Chris. I mean, there were two counties on Monday that willfully failed to certify, Cochise County in Arizona, Luzerne County in Pennsylvania. We sued both of them. Luzerne today decided they`d certify. And Cochise really is a thing unto itself because they are digging themselves deeper and deeper in and frankly digging themselves a deeper and deeper hole.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s a -- it`s a glass half full in the sense that, you know, we haven`t seen the widespread refusal to certify elections that we feared, but it`s a pretty low bar we`ve set for ourselves since this used to be nothing other than a celebration of democracy and a ministerial act.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, which brings us to this, right, which is that it does seem important that there`s accountability when you so flagrantly violate that. So, what are they doing right now and what are the legal steps being taken in response?\par \par \uc2 [20:25:08]\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Well, so what they`re doing right now is trying to find a lawyer. I mean, Chris, I don`t know if you saw the latest, but you know, they -- as the -- as the Democrat on the board said, the county attorney won`t represent them because they`re breaking the law. So, then they voted yesterday to hire the lawyer who had represented the Cyber Ninjas. Do you remember that whole fiasco?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: They were the one that run that crazy --\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: But they haven`t checked --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Just so folks remember, they were the ones that ran that crazy recount in Maricopa County privately funded. The that -- they`re looking for the bamboo for the Chinese ballots. That was the Cyber Ninjas. Continue.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Right. So, they needed a lawyer who would be willing to represent them. So, they figured he`s our guy. Except they didn`t check with him first. And so, late this afternoon, he basically said, no, no, I`m not representing you. So, right now, what they`re largely doing is trying to figure out what to do about the fact that they have not filed an answer to the lawsuit as they were ordered -- they were ordered to and they are to appear in court tomorrow. So, I suspect what they`re doing is trying to find a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 But in all seriousness, what they`re doing is they`re breaking the law. And they`re 