to me is -- this is a little bit of an elevated question, but I do think there`s genuine ideological exhaustion in the Republican Party. And I think there`s actually some ideological exhaustion in the Democratic Party. But, you know, Trumpism there -- anyone that`s tried to make ideological coherence out of Trumpism, and there`s some gestures there, hasn`t really been able to pull it off.\par \par \uc2 And so, at the end of the day, it`s like well, what is this thing? You know, they didn`t have a platform. What are they going to do this time around? Are they going to cut Social Security? Are they going to vote for the bosses in the railroad strike over the workers? Will they going to rub up for the bosses? It`s like, you got to have something at the core of all this culture where stuff where, you know, it`s hard to get that far.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Right. Look, there are some moral grounding lacking here. And there have been of course historical differences between Democrats and Republicans over decades, right? But there are some viewpoints that used to be stalwarts, and Tim could speak to this, of the Republican Party which is being against antisemitism, right, being for helping countries around the world who are fighting big bullies like Russia. There are big vote -- raising the debt limit so that we don`t -- we`re not on the brink of financial collapse. The question is who are they?\par \par \uc2 And I do think there are some Republicans, maybe more than a few, even \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who does know where they are who are rooted in that but are not brave enough right now to continue to be vocal about that and lead on those fronts. And that`s a problem too.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:29]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , I`ll say here. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is a very transactional politician. He understands whose material interests he represents and whose coalitional interest he represents. And he`s laser- focused on that. Like, he doesn`t -- he`s not sitting there being like are we on the side of the bosses in this labor dispute or the union? Like, he knows he`s on the side the bosses. He knows he`s on the side of the chemical companies. He knows he`s got K -- he`s got to do what K Street wants. He knows he`s got -- you know, judges and folks opposed to abortion.\par \par \uc2 But those are material interests or collisional interests, it`s not an ideology. And that to me is still the sort of big missing thing at the core of all this, although maybe I think --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Right, who are they?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, who are they? Tim Miller, \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , who are you? You guys are great. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, after basically being dared by Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , the Senate went ahead and did it. It finally passed legislation protecting same-sex marriage -- same-sex marriage. Congressman \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2  joins me on the struggle to get to this point and what comes next ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Lately, Republican politicians have been making a bold claim that they`re no longer the party of corporate executives and deregulation and tax cuts for the rich, no. That the party are of the working class now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOSH HAWLEY\uc2  (R-MO): Republicans have got to be the party of working people and working class culture. That`s what we`re fighting for.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): The heart and soul of the Republican Party. We are a working-class party now. We are a blue-collar party. That`s what we should have been and that`s what we are. And the Democratic Party has abandoned the working-class men and women, the millions of people who are out of jobs who are seeing their wages pulled down.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): We have people in charge of government that do not care about working Americans and are completely out of touch with commonsense wisdom and the working class values of millions and millions and millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, there you have it. Republicans sticking up for regular people, Democrats, they say, have become the party of woke software engineers in Silicon Valley and out-of-touch Coastal Elites. And today, the working People`s Party, the Republicans, had an incredible opportunity to make good on their rebrand.\par \par \uc2 You see, the Biden Administration has asked Congress to pass legislation to prevent a rail strike. And what it would do is fairly controversial to a lot of folks in the labor movement because it would force rail workers to accept a contract the majority of those same workers voted down. And the reason they voted down in large part is because it contained no provisions for universal short-term paid sick leave. They basically don`t have any.\par \par \uc2 So, today, the House controlled by Democrats, right, voted across bipartisan lines to force the rail workers to accept that deal anyway. But there was another vote, to try to make the best of a bad situation, a second bill on the House floor today which would just go ahead and provide the rail workers with the sick leave they`re asking for.\par \par \uc2 And you might think, well, this sounds like a perfect opportunity for Republicans to outmaneuver Democrats on their supposed working-class bonafides. But wouldn`t you know it, nearly every House Republican voted against the bill, giving blue-collar rail workers some paid sick days. The vote passed 221 and 207 thanks to party-line support from the Democrats, not a single nay in the caucus, and only three, three Republicans voted yes with them.\par \par \uc2 Not exactly what do you expect from the party of the working class. Now, the bill goes to the Senate where Rubio and Cruz and Hawley will have a chance to vote. Some of them have said they`re going to vote for the sick days. We`ll see. Vermont Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , the leading ally of the rail workers, told his Republican colleagues it`s time to put their money where their mouth is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (I-VT): Look, you know, you have a number of Republicans who claim -- who claim to be supporters of the working class. Well, if you are a supporter of the working class, how are you going to vote against the proposal which provides guaranteed paid sick leave to workers who have none right now? Put up or shut up. If you can`t vote for this to give workers today who really have hard jobs, dangerous jobs, if you can`t give them, you can`t guarantee them paid sick leave, don`t tell anybody that you stand with working families.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As with so many issues in American life, if you could find 10 Republican votes to vote yes on behalf of this, of seven sick days to avoid a filibuster, problem solved. Unfortunately, that`s much easier said than done. No matter how much pampered Princeton podcaster Ted Cruz tries to pretend he`s some kind of man of the people, there in the Senate as he says for the men and women with calluses on their hands, when it comes time to choose between working people and the rich bosses, you know which side Republicans are on. Just watch which way they vote.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The vote yesterday in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  was truly historic for many reasons because it got over the 60-vote threshold because Utah Republican Senator \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  voted for the bill. Because even the Church of Latter-Day Saints endorsed it to name a few.\par \par \uc2 The bill was the Respect for Marriage Act. And when it passed, it codified federal -- when passed, it codified federal protections for same-sex marriages into U.S. law. It was a product of years of tough legislative work and organizing. One of the people we may have most to thank for it is Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  because it was his threat written in a concurring opinion when he and the other right-wing justices on court overruled 50 years of precedent and striking down Roe v. Wade that made it perfectly clear were coming for everything, he basically said in his concurrence, including marriage equality. And that was a spark for this bill.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of court`s disastrous decision on abortion, we`ve seen political mobilization in state houses and Congress to fight to preserve and retain the rights this extreme court has either taken away or appears to be targeting.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is one of the people who is instrumental in the House version of this, New York Democratic Congressman \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2 . Congressman, congratulations on the legislative success here. How are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 REP. MONDAIRE JONES (D-NY): I`m feeling good. You know, the House will vote on this bill next week and it will pass. This is historic legislation. It is not perfect. There will still be work to do to secure marriage equality in every single state in this union. But it is a major step forward and it is unlike anything I imagined growing up closeted until recent years seeing all the progress that we`ve made.\par \par \uc2 But, Chris, as you know, a main impetus for this is the Supreme Court`s foreshadowing that it will not only take away marriage equality overruling its own presidents, but that is also coming for other fundamental rights as though taking away the 50-year-old constitutional right to abortion were not enough.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:33]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: How much -- I mean, I`m testing my own thesis here of the introduction of the segment. How much was that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  concurrence which got a lot of attention understandably where he basically says, look, there`s an entire line of cases due process, right, sort of liberty rights of what you can do in the -- in the sort of -- what you could be protected from the state interfering in vis-a-vis your own familial and personal autonomy. There`s a whole line of cases. It`s all hogwash. We want to come for all of it.\par \par \uc2 How much did that factor in getting to this point where you`ve now got -- this is about to be passed by the House and sign into law?\par \par \uc2 JONES: It was a huge impetus. As you know, a lot of times, you know, folks will kick around legislation in this place, but the only time it will actually get a floor vote is when there`s some urgency around it as perceived by leadership and by the American public. And I will also say that this is a bill that also protects the right to interracial marriage.\par \par \uc2 Isn`t it crazy that in the year 2022, Congress has to respond to a threat emanating from the Republican Party, you know, through the Supreme Court uh with respect to the ability of people of different races to simply get married? I mean, this is stuff that we take for granted in our everyday lives that are now in question because of the Republican Party and its extremism. And so long as we have the gavel, Democrats will fight to protect fundamental rights.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I should note that in that concurrence, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  lists off a bunch of these cases. Griswold being, of course, the birth control case. There`s Roe, there`s Obergefell. I believe Lawrence which is the sodomy case. I think he doesn`t actually mention Loving v. Virginia which is the course of jurisprudence that finally finds it a constitutional right to marriage equality. Many people noting that that was omitted from that line of cases. Of course, there`s other uh other grounding for it constitutionally.\par \par \uc2 What does it mean about the politics of this issue to see 60 votes? I mean, the sort of glass half full, right, is that you still have 36 Republican senators, of not mistaken, voting against this. But to see, you know, Cynthia Loomis of Wyoming and \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  of Utah voting for this legislation, it passing with a strong majority.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You know, it can be interpreted in two different ways. And I`m ever the optimist, and so, I am pleased that in addition to unanimous Democratic support for the simple proposition of marriage equality, folks being able to love who they love and live freely and live as full human beings, that a dozen Republicans in the Senate also join the chorus. But as you mentioned, the majority of Republicans in the Senate did not.\par \par \uc2 And what`s ironic about this is we hear so much about states` rights from Republicans. Well, this is legislation that simply says the states can decide whether there will be marriage equality in those States, and the federal government has to recognize it. And so, now that we are protecting states` rights in this context, Republicans are making up something else. Because of course, it was never about states` rights, it was always about how can we discriminate against marginalized people.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And we should -- we should be clear here, right, what you just said. This doesn`t mandate a national regime of marriage equality in all 50 states which is what the Obergefell decision effectively does. What it does say is your existing marriage and the marriage law of your state is protected and recognized by the federal government, right? So, it doesn`t - - I mean, Alabama could still, if they overturn Obergefell, outlaw marriage equality, but it does secure those fundamental rights for people that had been married, for people who had been married in states that do recognize it.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s exactly right, Chris. And therein lies the outstanding work that we need to do. It`s why Democrats have to be able to, you know, get enough senators, to get rid of the filibuster, so that we don`t have to rely on Republican support, and so that we can expand upon this important work that we`re going to codify after a final passage in the House next week.\par \par \uc2 There will still be about 30 or so states after this legislation was passed where according to state constitutions and statutory law, marriage equality is not going to exist in those states if and when the Supreme Court overrules its own recent precedent in Obergefell, saying that marriage is the constitutional right in the land.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And that`s where, of course, we have seen this happen, right, in Wisconsin, an 1859 law, you know, thawed like a frozen cavemen coming back to life to ban abortion in that state after the Dobbs decision. Of course, the next step for Democratic majority is should they get enough votes, would be to enshrine abortion rights in federal law. We will see if that happens. That`s one to watch for next.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Wednesday night. "ALEX WAGNER TONIGHT" starts right now. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020221201eibu00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for November 27 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,377 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION. Tonight`s lead, unchartered territory.\par \par \uc2 Right now, I can believe America has to actually ask this question. Will a former vice president, having been one of the targets of a violent insurrection, comply with federal prosecutors` request for testimony connected to a criminal investigation of a former president`s role in that insurrection. It is unprecedented but here we are, as \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is reportedly considering the Justice Department`s request for his testimony around January 6th, first reported in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  last week, after repeatedly rebuking the congressional probe and investigating former President Donald Trump`s efforts to overturn the 2020 presidential election. Will Pence go through with it, or how will Trump, his former boss, respond if he does?\par \par \uc2 Also unprecedented, New York Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  seems poised to become the first black leader of the House Democratic Caucus as House Democrats vote for new leadership this week, and Republican Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy pledges retribution for some of Donald Trump`s most strident Democratic critics in the House when the incoming Republican majority takes over next year. I will talk to one of the highest ranking House Democrats in just a moment about what all these shifts mean for the party and for the country.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, the Georgia Senate runoff, less than ten days away, early voting now in its second day. Democrat Reverend Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  both out campaigning this holiday weekend. Warnock about to get in assist from former president \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who campaigned with him earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s get started. Joining me now, House Majority Whip Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , Democrat of South Carolina. Mr. Majority Whip, thank you for joining us tonight. And as I laid out in the intro there, I am sure I am not the only person who finds it surreal that a former vice president is merely considering testifying to the Justice Department in connection with his criminal investigation of Pence`s former boss and former president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and his efforts to overturn the 2020 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Now, former Vice President Pence has resisted testifying before the congressional January 6th probe because he views it as partisan. But he has no such excuse when it comes to the DOJ, which both parties have always recognized as an independent institution. How do you respond to this, Congressman Clyburn?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES CLYBURN (D-SC): Well, first of all, thank you very much for having me. You know, I find it very interesting that all of us in the office swear to uphold the Constitution of the United States. We don`t swear to uphold the limbs of any one person but the Constitution. And I do believe that when we take these offices, we ought to do everything we can not just to uphold the Constitution but to preserve this democracy for our children and grandchildren.\par \par \uc2 And I am a little bit surprised about Mr. Pence. I was there that day. I went out of the same door that he went out of when we were escaping that mob. And he was a target of that mob. And for him to not do what he can to make sure that sort of thing never happens again is beyond me.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:00]\par \par \uc2 I do believe that he has a responsibility to the people of this country to uphold the Constitution and to do what he can to reserve our democracy and to continue our track to a more perfect union.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congressman, as you know, early voting in the Georgia Senate runoff opened up this weekend, and both parties sending out their big names with less than ten days to go. Former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  will return in Atlanta on Thursday to campaign with Democratic incumbent Senator Reverend \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  in his contest against Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What does former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  bring to the Warnock campaign at this time? And what does Warnock`s Senate victory do for the House Democrats next year?\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Well, you know, I think we have to remember that 50/50 does not constitute control. Most issues that get to the Senate floor first come through committees. And if those committees are 50/50, a 50/50 tie means the question will not prevail. If we get this seat, then we will have a majority in the committees and that is where the real work is done.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a very critical election. People need to go out and vote for Warnock. And I want say here, my good friend, G.K. Butterfield, with whom I spoke earlier today, he reminded me that December 6th in 1865, Georgia became the 27th state to ratify the 13th Amendment. So, December 6th is a very, very historic day. And I am calling upon all people of goodwill in Georgia to make December 6th, 2022 another historic day.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That is a great point. And Butterfield would come up with that. But for you that don`t know, the 13th Amendment that both Congressman Clyburn and Butterfield are referring to is what made it unconstitutional to have slavery. We were not freed by the emancipation proclamation. That was a presidential proclamation that lasted from Lincoln. We were freed by the 13th Amendment on June 6th. That is a very important day.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, current House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy is in the mix of a fraught campaign to become speaker. To that end, he has pledged to remove three of your colleagues, \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 , Adam Schiff and \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 , from House committee assignments in January, if he is successful. Republicans will have a very narrow majority next Congress. Do you see him getting the majority votes he needs for these removals if he becomes speaker, that is?\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Well, you have minister, a P.K., a preachers kid, today is Sunday, vengeance is mine says the Lord. I would say it to Mr. McCarthy don`t put yourself in a place of meeting out vengeance. Let`s try to run this country. Let`s do the things that are necessary to make people`s lives better so that they can prepare to pass on to their children and their grandchildren a country better than they found it.\par \par \uc2 Going out, doing this retribution, doing to a member something that he perceived as being unfair to one of your members, when everybody knows that there was just cause for those people being removed from their committee. You cannot do it just because you have got to have just cause. And that is what we had.\par \par \uc2 So, I would say to my friend, please, let`s go about the business of trying to bring this country to gather and let`s do the things that are necessary to move an agenda that will improve the lives of all Americans and stop all of this tit-for-tat.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, let me ask you this quickly because we are out of time, but your colleague, Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , who I have known and worked with for decades now since he was a younger man, he is still a young man, he is poised to become House Democratic after this week`s leadership vote. In addition, Massachusetts Congresswoman Katherine Clark is slated to become Democratic whip. Texas Congressman \uc2 Pete Aguilar\uc2  is slated to become caucus chair.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:02]\par \par \uc2 Now, you are returning to your old post as assistant leader. What can you tell us about House Democrats` direction under this new era of leadership?\par \par \uc2 CLYBURN: Just a little correction there, the House chair of our caucus, \uc2 Pete Aguilar\uc2  of California, will become (INAUDIBLE) if these votes go right. And chairing our message group is going to be \uc2 Joe Neguse\uc2  out of Colorado. So, these are going to be the four people who will be leading our party.\par \par \uc2 I am looking forward to serving with our new leader. I think he is a very highly qualified member of Congress. He represents New York well. I think he will represent our Democratic Party in a way that will make us all proud. So, I am looking forward to serving under him and our new whip and our new chair and our new chair of the message group.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you for being with us, Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is California State Senator and Congresswoman-elect Sydney Kamlager. She is set to represent California`s 37th district in the House. And thank you for joining me on this holiday weekend, Congresswoman-elect.\par \par \uc2 I want to start by congratulating you on your win. And you are succeeding a friend of mine, Los Angeles Mayor-elect \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 . You are coming in her seat at a record breaking number of 124 women will serve in the 118th Congress this January. And this new Congress will include a record number of black, Latina and Hispanic women in the House, 27 black women, 18 Latina and Hispanic. What do you think is driving the rise in the number of women in Congress?\par \par \uc2 REP.-ELECT SYDNEY KAMLAGER (D-CA): Well, thank you for having me. It is great to be on. We need to all raise a glass to Congresswoman \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  for becoming the first female to be the mayor of Los Angeles. Women made great strides thanks to this election cycle, I think, because our bodies were on the ballot like never before. And yet there are still hundreds of thousands of \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  seats that still have always and only been held by men.\par \par \uc2 It is also important to note that the black and brown women getting to Congress in record numbers, and, I like many of my colleagues, represent a district that is not majority African-American. So, my credibility to my constituents does not come because of my privilege. It comes because I, like my female colleagues, worked to get into these positions. And we do that by listening, by translating, by easing fears, by stepping into the gaps and by pushing, pushing, pushing for legislation that sees all of us.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, when you take office, Republicans will hold the House with a slim majority. It is not clear what action the GOP might take on abortion in the next section but some Republican lawmakers have voiced support for a 15-week national ban for putting forward over the summer by Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 . And in the last session, the Life at Conception Act introduced by Virginian Republican Congressman Alex Mooney had 166 co-sponsors. That is more than the amount women currently -- amount of women currently in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 Now, you sat on the board of directors of Planned Parenthood in Los Angeles. How do you plan to defend reproductive rights while in Congress?\par \par \uc2 KAMLAGER: Well, first, I want to say that Republicans are where they are today because they double down on silencing women. We just saw a national mandate happen in these midterm elections that say that when you legislate without thought to the real impact on a woman`s body, you are going to fail. We are not talking in the abstract, Al. We are talking about legislation that will impact our mothers, our sisters, our daughters, our aunties, criminalizing women and our bodies and those that are providing care to us and those that are helping us make the decisions that we need to make.\par \par \uc2 So, my hope is that folks get their heads out of the sand and look at the exit polls from this election and realize that women are going to continue to step up and vote when it comes to the ability for us to have autonomy (INAUDIBLE) this is not in parallel with my values, it is another thing to say this is what the law says you can and cannot to do with your own body when it comes to health care.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:23]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, you have said you would like to carry on Congresswoman \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 ` efforts to pass the George Floyd Justice and Policing Act. Well, what do you think are the prospects for police reform in this divided Congress?\par \par \uc2 KAMLAGER: I am hopeful. I mean, there are police walking around in communities and neighborhoods across this country, whether they are blue communities or red communities. I worked on a number of bills when I was the state senator in the state assembly on equal due process, on probation reform, on the Crisis Act that will fund community based organizations to respond to certain 911 calls so that law enforcement doesn`t have to, on qualified immunity, on having a database where you can take out actors. Those are all important.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, we should be working with law enforcement to build consensus about how we can improve this system because recidivism is a failure. And we should all be focused on how to address the discriminatory and overpunitive nature of a system that is not making people whole and is not successfully rehabilitating people so they can reenter community. And that should be something that everyone is focused on.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, as a California state senator, you advocated for the green new deal and immediate climate action. And you have been a strong champion for environmental justice. What are your plans to act on climate policy and the environmental justice when serving in Congress?\par \par \uc2 KAMLAGER: Well, I am going to continue to lead and I`m going to take my California playbook with me to Washington, D.C. We have a responsibility to lead in the international arena by leading here at home. The reality is that so many countries where black and brown people live are suffering disproportionately because of the actions that we are taking here in this country. We have also seen that gas prices really do affect what all of us put on our tables.\par \par \uc2 So, how do we lead? We have to talk about lowering our dependence on foreign oil, how we diversify our energy portfolio, how we are working with our brothers and sisters in labor to define what just transition really looks like, how we are building green and how we are thinking -- here in my district, I funded a number of urban farming programs. I want to continue to do that how we talk about green canopies rather than concrete canopies, how we support initiatives that allow us to act locally in the climate space because we will see dividends when we are talking about health care, transportation, emissions, et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 There are so many things that we can do here not just in our districts, but in our states, and across this country that will make a difference not only for us but for our partners across the world.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you, Sydney Kamlager, and congratulations again. We look forward to continuing to talk with you as you are now a congressperson.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, I will tell you how the rise up and make a difference this holiday season.\par \par \uc2 And later, Donald Trump`s third presidential campaign is already off the rails. Republicans are looking to distance themselves from the former president after reports that Trump broke bread with a white supremacist in Mar-a-Lago. My political panel will weigh in.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev, a very good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Some of the stories we are following right now, there were mass protests in major Chinese cities overnight. They were rallying against strict zero- COVID lockdown policies. Beijing, Chengdu and Wuhan, each with tens of millions of residents, had atypical marches in the streets. Protesters saying they are confined to their homes to stop the virus, in some cases, confined for months at a time.\par \par \uc2 A New York councilman is urging the LGBTQ-plus community to be, quote, extra vigilant on going out after the two separate -- after, rather, two separate suspicious in that community. Bottcher warned that the suspects may be targeting, drugging and robbing gay men in various neighborhoods in Manhattan. WABC reporting that there are some 40 victims so far with seven dead.\par \par \uc2 And shoppers spending topped $9 billion online for Black Friday, says Adobe Analytics data.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:03]\par \par \uc2 That is after a record Thanksgiving Day of online shopping of $5.3 billion dollars. Most buying, by the way, happened on phones, at over 53 percent of transactions.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: While most of us enjoyed a big Thanksgiving meal with our families, nearly 34 million Americans, including more than 9 million children went hungry, according to \uc2 Feeding America\uc2 . In New York City, specifically, a total of nearly 1.5 million people live below the poverty line. That is why on Thursday I was at National Action Network`s House of Justice in Harlem, alongside elected officials like New York City Mayor Eric Adam, clergy, NAN staffers, volunteers and advocates and community members, to hand out holiday meals to those in need.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:03]\par \par \uc2 This is what true community looks like to those of us raised with the mindset and tradition that services power. And that spirit of generosity is more important than ever, because the times are tougher than ever for those struggling to make ends meet. Inflation is over 7 percent and joblessness rose to nearly 4 percent last month.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration announced in September its plans to end hunger in 2030. It`s an ambitious goal that will take more than just government actions. The initiative includes private sector partnerships with companies, such as public grocery store chain which has pledged over $23 million to fight hunger. Charitable groups will play such a role in an important role as well, as they always have.\par \par \uc2 And that is where you can start to make a difference today. Consider donating to your local food bank, or an organization that does outreach to homeless, asylum seekers and other vulnerable groups. This is the time of year for counting our blessings. And if you find your cup is full, consider rising up to pay that good fortune forward to someone else who is less fortunate. In order to make a better world for all of us, we all have to be involved.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:30:46]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back. House Democrats will pick a new slate of leaders this week, the top of which is likely to be the groundbreaking ascension of New York Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  to House Democratic Leader, making him the first black person to lead the Democratic Caucus as it navigates what will be a slight minority in the next Congress.\par \par \uc2 On the other side, the top Republican in the House, Kevin McCarthy, is navigating his own challenges to become speaker, trying to please both moderates in his caucus and the far right members that he now needs to get what he ultimately wants.\par \par \uc2 Joining me discuss all of this is Congressman \uc2 Greg Meeks\uc2 , Democrat of New York, Chair of the House Foreign Affairs Committee. Congressman Meeks, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get right into it. Your colleague, Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , is poised to succeed \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  as House Democratic Leader after this week`s leadership vote. Jeffries become the first black American to lead a Congressional Caucus. And as a fellow member of the New York state delegation, having worked with \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  in the House for years, and knowing him, I mean, you and I was talking about you and I and former Governor David Paterson came along together when we were young guys in politics and civil rights to \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  and \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  and all. We watched Hakeem come behind us about the same gap and age, about 12, 15 years. What can we expect the next Congress to be under his leadership of the caucus?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 GREGORY MEEKS\uc2  (D-NY): Well, first, thanks for having me, Rev. And I think that \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  is the perfect person to come after \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . And at the perfect age, the way that he can communicate messages to the American people, the way that he has the ability to organize, I`ve watched him, you know, now being one of the senior members of Congress, sometimes you get to sit back and observe.\par \par \uc2 And I watched him on the House floor, for example, talking to members, members coming to him, and him resolving issue that they may have and getting people to work together. I watched him when he was dealing with criminal justice reform and appropriate sentencing for individuals who were incarcerated, to make sure that they were not -- those that were falsely incarcerated and others, I`ve watched him work together even with Republicans to get things done.\par \par \uc2 And I think that you will also see that he is perfectly fit because he`s the individual that can, you know, some will call him, you know, he can relate to the hip-hop generation, which is right below him, as well as an older generation, my generation, your generation and older.\par \par \uc2 So he, again, can bring the Democratic caucus together, and at the same time has the ability to work with others if they truly want to work in a bipartisan way. And the ability to deliver a message. He`s one of the most articulate individuals in Congress, but he`s also an individual that is a thinker, and, and thinks and plans ahead, which I think is tremendously important as a leader.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And then you and I are about the same year, about a year probably, but I do know hip-hop, Mr. Congressman, but we`ll talk about that another night.\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: I know too.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, both of us have kids that keep us up to date so and then -\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And that -- and that little kids, so.\par \par \uc2 But I want to talk foreign aid, aid to Ukraine specifically. Kevin McCarthy needs the support of the overwhelming majority of his caucus to become speaker, that includes Republican moderates, who`ve been supportive by varying degrees of the American aid to Ukraine as a defense against Russia`s invasion. And it also includes some far right members who have called for that aid to be cut once the incoming GOP takes over the House.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been to Ukraine and you`ve met with Zelenskyy along with Speaker Pelosi. The White House has recently asked for $37 billion in aid to Ukraine and its defense against Russia`s ongoing invasion. Many Republicans have yet to publicly take a position on the request ahead of the shift in the House.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:17]\par \par \uc2 Now, as House Foreign Relations chair, which side do you think McCarthy ultimately will stand on, the moderates or the small but noisy for a minority -- the far right minority that opposes but McCarthy still needs to become speaker, he has to deal with them. How is this going to play out?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: It`s going to play out very interestingly, Rev. Look, you know, they -- the person who -- the current ranking member of the Foreign Affairs Committee, who is presumed to be the chair come January 3rd, he`s has to, you know, decide what he`s going to do. He had been with me a strong partner about funding for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But then recently, I`ve also heard him talk about what the Freedom Party had been talking about as far as oversights and hearings in regards to that money. And so whoever McCarthy will put some pressure on him because McCarthy has to make some deals, he doesn`t have the votes yet himself to get to 218, so he has to cut some deals with some of the so-called freedom party. And it`s going to be interesting with a moderate like Mike McCaul as to what pressure he`s going to feel and what he`s going to do, is he going to follow what I think he -- what I know he believes? Or is he going to yield to McCarthy and the Freedom party to go the direction that they are in?\par \par \uc2 Because clearly, we saw the last vote, there`s almost 50 members of the Republican Party that did not vote for the funding for Ukraine. And so the influence that are going to have on McCarthy, as well as McCaul, is going to be something that we`ll have to see, because it`s unknown at this point, just as is unknown at this point whether and how McCarthy is going to get the 218 votes to become speaker.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congressman, while there`s still time left here, we still have the pending runoff in the Georgia Senate race between \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , early voting currently underway ahead of the December 6 runoff. Big name Democrats and Republicans returning to Georgia to campaign for the contenders. That, of course, includes former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , who will be in Atlanta for Senator Warnock on Thursday. But also on the ground for Warnock, grassroots organizers trying to drive black voters to the polls for the second time in two months, hoping that the drawn out Senate race won`t tamp down those voters enthusiasm levels, as Senate Democrats seek a 51-seat majority next year.\par \par \uc2 Watching this race from New York and from the U.S. House, what are the stakes in your view?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: It`s huge, Rev. In fact, as chair of the Congressional Black Caucus as political action committee, we`ve sent and we`ll send even more money down there and the members of Congress will be traveling to Georgia to also be part of that voter turnout effort, because what`s online is huge.\par \par \uc2 We call, number one, that -- there was one or two vote that we always had a problem with in getting the Democratic agenda passed in the last Congress or in this Congress, you know. And so having a Reverend Warnock there means that you don`t have that one vote cushion, just that one vote cushion, you have more than that to get it done, so that the policies and the things that we want to get done, there`s more likelihood to get that.\par \par \uc2 And on top of that, you know, the Senate seat is for six years. And, you know, two years from now, we have another elections coming up. And to have Reverend Warnock there for the next six years, that`s a seat that you don`t have to worry about. You haven`t lost, that`s a plus one already going into the next election, so that`s important.\par \par \uc2 But the overall agenda that the American people want to get done protecting Social Security, which is huge. And, you know, we see where \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has been in that regard, so I hope that is a record turnout on December 6. In fact, people started early voting and started in Georgia already --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: because that is a hugely important election.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Congressman \uc2 Gregory Meeks\uc2 , thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the holiday weekend hasn`t deterred voters in Georgia from heading to the polls and yet another special election for Senate. We have all the latest info on the rematch between Reverend Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:40:28]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Let`s bring in my political panel for their insights on a few big topics. Joining me now is Danielle Moodie, host of the Woke AF Daily podcast, and with Rick Tyler, Republican strategist.\par \par \uc2 So, Danielle, President Biden is spending the weekend in Nantucket where he`s expected to discuss with his family, whether or not he`ll run for reelection. How do you think Democrats stronger than expected showing in the midterms have affected the president`s thinking as he moles this decision over the holidays?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE MOODIE, HOST, WOKE AF DAILY PODCAST: Well, Rev, one, Happy Thanksgiving to you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Same to you.\par \par \uc2 MOODIE: And the reality is that we have underestimated President Biden, President Biden has shown up in every single matchup that we thought that he was going to fail at, particularly in the midterm elections. He showed up for Generation Z and Generation Z showed up for him. So do I think that he has any concerns about whether or not people will back him as the next presidential candidate? No, I don`t, because I think that in every which way that he`s been able to show up with student loan debt relief, with COVID relief, with any which way in which the American people have needed him, he has shown up and so I`m good with this president being our Democratic nominee.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rick, let`s turn it down to Trump who is 12 days into his third bid for the White House. He`s now facing backlash for having dinner Tuesday at Mar-a-Lago with white supremacy Nick Fuentes. And I mean, Kanye West, of course, was then arranged did they say, but Fuentes is the one that he`s getting a lot of pushback on.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:20]\par \par \uc2 Trump claims he did know Fuentes. Republican Congressman James Comer of Kentucky addressed this incident on "MEET THE PRESS" earlier today. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): Well, he certainly needs better judgment and who he dines with. I know that he`s issued a statement said he didn`t know who those people were. I would not take a meeting with that person, no. I wouldn`t take a meeting with Kanye West either. But that`s my opinion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Rick, let me just say what -- and I`ve said this earlier today, I have had meetings with former President Clinton, former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , even Bush on several occasions, and you always -- you must remember, they still have a Secret Service detail. It`s smaller than when they went office. But it`s a detail you always have to submit who`s coming and particularly if it`s a sit-down dinner or something like that for security reasons.\par \par \uc2 How would Trump not know who Kanye was bringing? And how would this person not be screened? And if you run in that lucid off the -- what do you have it, off the book kind of situations, and why are you running for president again? And what do you make of Republicans publicly criticizing Trump over his actions and not coming to his defense? Are the tides finally turning in terms of Republicans coming out or at least holding back from defending him on such egregious acts?\par \par \uc2 RICK TYLER, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Well, first of all, Rev, for the reasons you point out, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and absolutely -- knew absolutely who Nick Fuentes was, and the fact is, it`s a disgrace. He disgraced himself by having dinner with a known anti-Semite, white supremacist.\par \par \uc2 But really, nothing has really changed. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was unfit for office before he had that dinner. And he`s just proven that he`s unfit again to hold that office again. And so there`s not much more to say about that. It`s disgraceful.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Danielle, switching gears now, tomorrow early voting will resume in Georgia Senate runoff between a Democratic incumbent Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and his Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 According to the Secretary of State`s office, at least 70,000 people voted yesterday, during the first day of early voting. That`s nearly as many Georgians who voted at the beginning of the general election. President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is expected to return to the Peach State to campaign for Warnock. And last week, Walker got a boost from Georgia`s top Republican, Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What are you hearing about the mood of the Georgia electorate going into the race?\par \par \uc2 MOODIE: I think that the people of Georgia are ready for this runoff election. I think that they -- that the decision is very clear, Rev. I mean, you have a Reverend Warnock who is out there for the people of Georgia, who has been there, who has been talking about the needs of the people of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And then you have \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who is a puppet for the Republicans, right, loser, a puppet for the Republican right. We don`t know what he stands for. We don`t know what he`s about. But we know that he is a great talking head for those white Republican leaders in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that the decision for Georgia is very clear. And I`m so grateful for the fact that the judiciary in Georgia stood up for the Democrats, stood up for justice and said, yes, the people of Georgia should be able to vote on a Saturday after the Thanksgiving holiday, as opposed to what it is that the Republicans were trying to stand on and say that, oh, no, they shouldn`t be able to. So I think that the people of Georgia will once again show up like they did in 2020 and tell the -- and tell the Republican Party exactly what it is that they need. They need a leader, not a walker.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rick, earlier today, Colorado Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , made the case for stronger national gun policy following the deadly LGBTQ club shooting that happened last week. Here`s what he told Chuck Todd earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS (D-CO): This looks like this would have been a good instance for the use of Colorado`s new Red Flag Law which has been used several 100 times but could have been used even more to prevent these kinds of tragic events. I think all of these things, including the Red Flag Law and expanding its use should really be looked at.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:00]\par \par \uc2 But again, it`s not just about gun policy, yes, that should be on the table to discuss in a thoughtful rational way, but we also need to talk about mental health, and we need to talk about the divisive rhetoric that even some people who aspire to positions of leadership in our country, unfortunately, use almost every day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, President Biden has also expressed an interest in pursuing some type of gun control legislation in the lame duck session. Do you think it will happen, Rick?\par \par \uc2 TYLER: No, because he`s proposing things that we know don`t work. There are things that will work, Red Flag Laws, and I would be curious as to why the Red Flag Law was not used in Colorado. Red Flag Laws should be looked at very carefully. Background checks should be used.\par \par \uc2 There should be no -- I believe there should be no sale of a firearm, including private ownership that doesn`t go through a background check. It`s very important to keep these -- keep weapons out of and I`m a concealed carry holder. I don`t want people who have no business, no training, no stability, or have a record of violence, domestic violence, are being able to get their hands on a gun.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t think it`ll pass, because there`s just not enough -- there`s just not enough people who are going to support it, and he`s aiming for things that really won`t work when he couldn`t be aiming for things that will save lives.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Danielle and Rick, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:55:04]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: On Friday, December 9th, the documentary movie, "Loudmouth" will hit theaters around the country and you can find out which date it will play by going to nationalactionnetwork.net and where in your state it will be showing.\par \par \uc2 It is a documentary movie about my life as an activist from my teenage years all the way up to now. But what is important to me about it, I had nothing to do with the editing of it. It was done by John Legend`s film company, along with Kedar Massenburg and director, Josh Alexander, with no input from me, but they used raw footage interviewed me, but they use footage going back to the `80s and the `90s.\par \par \uc2 And Donald Trump`s New York, where people would chase us and call us the N word. And when I would leave protest marches, they would come out openly and throw watermelons at us. You will see in this documentary, northern racism. You will understand the Donald Trump`s play into that crowd. That`s where he grew up around. Queens is where he was born and raised. Watch Loudmouth. I had to be loud for our issues to be heard in New York, along with many others. Go see Loudmouth and learn about manicured racism in the north. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5:00 PM Eastern. "AMERICAN VOICES WITH ALICIA MENENDEZ" starts right now on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020221128eibr00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for November 24, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Jacqueline Alemany, Ali Vitali, Barbara McQuade,  Julie Tsirkin, Steve Patterson, Marissa Parra, Greg Bluestein, Kavita  Patel, Keir Simmons \par \uc2 7,785 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Thank you for staying with us on this Thanksgiving holiday. I'm Chris Jansing at MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 We begin this hour with a growing debate within the January 6 committee, new reporting from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  on the frustration among current and former committee staff members on outgoing Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 's overwhelming focus on former President Trump for the Committee's final report. Joining me now are NBC Capitol Hill correspondent Ali Vitali, Washington Post Congressional investigations reporter Jackie Alemany, and former U.S. Attorney Barbara McQuade, great to have all of you here. So, Jackie, you and your colleagues spoke with 15 current and former staffers on the committee and you report they're angry and they're disillusioned, what's going on and what might be left on the cutting room floor?\par \par \uc2 JACQUELINE ALEMANY, CONGRESSIONAL INVESTIGATIONS REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: Yes, Chris. Essentially, the frustrations with Vice Chairwoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  have been building for some time now amongst some committee staff over sort of her iron grip over the contents of the investigation and the public hearings. But there was sort of an assumption that the majority of the work despite what had been left out of the public hearings that we saw over the course of the summer that it would make it into the final reports. Remember, there were five different teams -- there are five different teams in the January 6 staff. Each team assigned to a different topic. And the hearings primarily pulled from the work of the gold and the red team whose findings were mostly focused on Trump's role in the January 6 insurrection.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of these tensions boiled over a few weeks ago after a virtual conference call that was actually reported first by the -- your NBC colleague Ken Dilanian that were investigators and counsel on the January 6 committee staff were told that a large majority of the work unrelated to Trump and outside of the red and the gold team were going to be left on the cutting room floor, either put in the appendix or posted online, elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 And the feeling is sort of been amongst a lot of these people that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , her control over the report has been unparalleled, and that she has channeled some of her political ambitions into what this report looks like at the end of the day at the expense of a more comprehensive historic accord. That includes the totality of things beyond Trump, including failures of the \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2 , various intelligence failures, why there wasn't a level of preparedness to stop an attack like this, despite the available evidence, along with the findings of the green team.\par \par \uc2 And that was the team that was true -- that was following the money and put together according to what our sources have told us an excellent and comprehensive look at who funded what in terms of getting these insurrectionists to the Capitol that day, paying for some of the equipment organization, etcetera. And that -- as one senior Democratic aide told us what was a very excellent report that was submitted in terms of the drafting process, but at the end of the day, you know, revealed things that were objectionable but completely legal.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Ali, look, there's a couple of different ways you can look at this report, right?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: One of them is the historical record a very important part of this. The other is the public consumption, the political impact that it will have.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Politically, is there a feeling that this report could impact voters -- Americans' perceptions? Is the sense that what people saw in the hearings has pretty much formed intractable opinions? I mean, I guess it all ends up at the same place, which is, what is the real reason for this report? What did they want it to accomplish?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Well, I think the thing that has been consistent over the course of the last year as all of us have been covering the January 6 committee is that the politics is built into this. You can't extricate it from what they're actually doing, which they would say is above the politics. It's just creating a public record of what happened on January 6, an historic event that everyone on the committee agrees should never happen again. And so, their goal is to lay out the failures that led to it, but you can't talk about that without talking about Trump. And because of the politics that are in it -- in what actually happened on January 6, trying to overturn the results of the 2020 election that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost, all of that is part and parcel.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 But what has been true over the course of the last year as well, is that we've been watching public opinion not necessarily have any radical changes in response to what they're seeing. What's consistently been true in the polls is that people are pretty baked with how they see January 6. That's not to say, though, that the narrative that the committee has not -- has put forward hasn't been compelling, and they have been very targeted. Especially when you have to consider the fact this committee was aware that they are in a media environment that is extremely saturated.\par \par \uc2 And what we saw them try to do repeatedly is they would pick one different prong of what they were trying to focus on in these hearings, whether it was the money, the militia groups, the myths, and disinformation online, and then, of course, the trump of it all, and they would really drill down in each hearing, at least over the course of the summer. Now, what you're getting to when you do the final report is everything just goes in at once. And so it makes sense that they are trying to balance putting all of the information in there that they have, with also the continuation of a narrative that they worked really hard to establish which is repeatedly putting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  at the center of all of this.\par \par \uc2 Every single hearing that we covered, Jackie and I were in the room for all of them. All of them, even as they would meander on little tangents that brought new information, they would always cut back to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as the thesis statement, the thing that they were always reminding viewers and people who were listening, this is what it's all about. And so, it's not exactly surprising that the final report would have such a heavy Trump focus to it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Barbara McQuade, as you well know that if you go into a courtroom and you make a closing argument, you are directing it at 12 people in a jury box, right? The closing argument, if that's how we're going to look at this committee report, the jury is the United States, every voter, every American, arguably even the world as we look at it as a statement of how we feel about democracy and what should happen in a democracy. Looking at it from that perspective, where do you think the focus of this final report should be?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes. I don't have a problem with making the focus of this the misconduct of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Well, all of those things that the committee has been investigating, you know, far-right extremism, lapses, and failures by law enforcement and the Intelligence Committee, all incredibly important. But the thing that is an existential threat to the country is a president who uses his power to undermine democracy. That has got to be by a factor of 1000, the most important part of the story.\par \par \uc2 And so, if you throw in everything, but the kitchen sink, you tend to dilute that part of your message. So, I don't have a problem with that. But I would give it this caveat. It is still critically important that those other matters be included in the historical record somewhere. And there's been talk, you know, as you mentioned, in an appendix, online, somewhere else, it needs to be there because those are the things that still need to be addressed by Congress to make sure that we fix those gaps in the law that allow all of those other dangerous things to fester. So, I think you can have both and but I do agree with the approach that you need to take the biggest priority, the existential threat presented by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and make sure that is front and center of the final argument that the committee makes to the American people.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Jackie, former President Trump is reacting to your reporting attacking \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  through social media posts. A spokesman for Cheney is responding to her critics in writing, including, "\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the first president in American history to attempt to overturn an election and prevent the peaceful transfer of power. So, damn right Liz is prioritizing understanding what he did and how he did it, and ensuring it never happens again. Some staff have submitted subpar material for the report that reflects long-held liberal biases about federal law enforcement, Republicans, and sociological issues outside the scope of the Select Committee's work."\par \par \uc2 There is so much in your story that struck me as above what we normally see as political partisan fighting. There was another quote, in your story from a democratic aide about the parts that might be left out. And it was. It's not a class project. Everyone doesn't get a participation prize. This is brutal stuff. Could it affect how this final report eventually gets done?\par \par \uc2 ALEMANY: I think that there is some hope that potentially the committee in the last month of its existence, at least in its current form, changes course a little bit to incorporate more of the work that some staffers, again current and former, are agitating for. You know, I think, Barb, you make some really excellent points. And that is what we heard a lot of yesterday on -- from sources who were on the -- more on the side of Cheney here that they don't want a Mueller report redo, they want something that's compelling that people are actually going to read.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 But I think this gets at the heart of a schism that has emerged on the committee. And it's actually been an undercurrent, really for the past year and a half, which is, you know, this feeling that -- at least amongst Cheney, that Trump is the issue versus others who believe that, yes, Trump lit the fuse, but now he's irrelevant, and that -- you know, Trump's political future is sort of, besides the point and that the -- he has sort of caused the -- this sort of rot to grow in the Republican Party of extremism, militia group, lone wolf attacks, election deniers, and that at this point, is a more important target. And so, by focusing narrowly and primarily on the former president, we missed sort of the broader point.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's obviously it's not my job to say what I think is the most compelling but the -- a lot of the people that we've spoken with believe that by de-prioritizing these findings and leaving them out of the primary body of work that people are going to go to, you're doing a disservice to the American public to prevent a future attack like this. That -- you know, they agree Cheney has been extremely valuable and important to the success of the committee overall that they've seen so far, but that that sort of her unilateral -- what they view as her unilateral veto power and control over the report writing process has veered their work in the wrong direction at the moment.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, we'll see what happens with that. But, Ali, before we go, I want to mention an update overnight.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: The Democrat winning Alaska's out large House seat, and Republican Senator \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  defeating her opponents after years of criticism from former President Trump. You spent time in Alaska.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A lot of people were interested in it because they wanted to see if \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  could make a comeback. Do these results surprise you at all, and does it tell us something about moderate candidates in general?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: It tells us something about moderate candidates, especially in a ranked-choice voting system. I think that was the thing that was most striking to me when I was there in August. We were there for the results right before the special election when Mary Peltola, the Democrat, won that House race. The first time -- in the weeks between the special election and the general election, we saw many staffers for the former congressman \uc2 Don Young\uc2 , who held that seat for decades, come forward and say that this would be a continuation of Young's legacy by putting Mary Peltola in this seat.\par \par \uc2 So, there was a lot of crossover between Republicans and Democrats alike. And that's the point of the rank-choice voting system is to take some of the hyper-partisanship out of it. It tends to favor more moderate candidates. And I don't mean that in an ideological sense, necessarily. I mean it in a consensus-building sense.\par \par \uc2 So, when you look at the fact that Peltola was able to come out with this, yes, it was a rebuke of Palin and her brand of politics, which many Alaskans told me they felt was more national than Alaska-focused. And this is a state that certainly thinks that their focus needs to be on what Alaskans need, not on the broader national Republican Party and conservative agenda. So, that was striking on the Palin front.\par \par \uc2 But the Murkowski battle was always one that was viewed as a top proxy battle for former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He went to the state and I think said something along the lines of it beat -- she was his number one target. So, the fact that she was able to fend off a Trump-endorsed Challenger in \uc2 Kelly Tshibaka\uc2  is again, an outgrowth of the rank-choice voting system. But also, the fact that Murkowski has such a strong national brand in the state of Alaska, and now she's coming back to Congress.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali Vitali, Jacqueline Alemany, Barbara McQuade, thank you all. Happy Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: You too.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Up next. Two communities missing family members at their Thanksgiving table after mass shootings. We're on the ground in Colorado and Virginia next. Plus, sticker shock. This year, you could be eating one of the most expensive Thanksgiving meals ever. I'll talk to one lawmaker about those high prices and what can be done about it. And we're bracing for a potential triple demic of respiratory illnesses this winter. Ahead, a doctor with what you need to know as parents face the unimaginable.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There hasn't been a single week in 2022 without a mass shooting according to the gun violence archive. Let that sink in. Every day, communities around this country are being ripped apart by this uniquely American problem. We're on the ground in just two of them this Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 In Chesapeake, Virginia, officials are still trying to make sense of a deadly shooting at the \uc2 Walmart\uc2  there. Police say a store manager shot and killed six of his own co-workers before turning the gun on himself. Five of those victims have been named. The sixth is a 16-year-old boy.\par \par \uc2 In Colorado Springs, five families are without their loved ones this Thanksgiving. The suspect accused of opening fire at an LGBTQ nightclub will face formal charges on December 6.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now from Chesapeake Virginia is NBC's Julie Tsirkin, in Colorado Springs, NBC's Steve Patterson. Julie, what else can you tell us about the victims?\par \par \uc2 JULIE TSIRKIN, MSNBC REPORTER: Yes. Look, Chris, today on Thanksgiving, it's all about the victim. Six people, all employees of this \uc2 Walmart\uc2  behind me lost their lives Tuesday night when a gunman, the overnight store manager opened fire killing them, three in the break room, one that was trying to run out in the front of the store, and two more that died in the hospital later on. All these victims lost their lives -- you see them on their screen, for no reason.\par \par \uc2 They were ranging in age from 16 to 70. And all morning behind me at this makeshift memorial, people coming here, members of the community, to lay flowers in remembrance of those lives that were lost. I spoke to one woman who works at a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  an hour away. She's worked there for over 22 years. Take a listen to what she told me and why she came here today.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY JHONE CHATKOVSKY, WALMART CASHIER: And I open my phone and like -- you know use. All of a sudden something's going on. And I see the \uc2 Chesapeake\uc2  shooting going on. And I just don't believe they happen this way, you know. That's going to surprise me.\par \par \uc2 And I thought the next day is Thanksgiving, you know like everybody gets together or the family. That's what we're -- for the first time, we're close for this employees to get spent time the family. That was just the first time, everybody be happy. I'm so sorry to what happened to this family. It could happen to our store, it could happen anywhere, you know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TSIRKIN: It could happen anywhere, Chris. We heard that from multiple people we spoke to this morning. And at this hour, still no motive in the shooting.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You know, when I started covering these attacks back in Columbine, people used to say, I can't believe that happened here. Now, people aren't surprised it can happen anywhere.\par \par \uc2 Steve, Colorado Springs is learning that. President Biden and the First Lady, we just literally got this from the White House pull, just a short time ago called The owners of Club Q to offer condolences. I'm wondering if on this Thanksgiving we've heard of any -- from any of the families.\par \par \uc2 STEVE PATTERSON, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. In the lead-up to this Thanksgiving, we have. But you have to imagine five families in abject pain, five seats at the dinner table that will be empty. This has to be incredibly hard. I haven't managed to speak to any of the families today.\par \par \uc2 I did speak to the family of Raymond Green Vance, the young man 22 years old, who had his whole life ahead of him. He was just getting his life together. Here in Colorado Springs with the new job, a new apartment, the girlfriend -- the girlfriend, Cassie, who is the daughter of the hero in this case, Richard Fierro helped stop the gunman. He lost his life. And I spoke to his mom who still believes in some way that he's right around the corner. I spoke to his grandmother, who is just -- you could tell they were best friends, and such sadness and such horror for her.\par \par \uc2 But they're trying to come together as a family, and I imagine that is going on today, and trying to remember the good things. And one of them is that they were his -- he was their gentle giant. This is a big kid, plays football, really respected in his community. But his grandma is just so sad. I want to play with some sounds that I think might be hard to hear but it's important to hear. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ESTHELA BELL, GRANDMOTHER OF RAYMOND GREEN VANCE: And I said, oh my God, what happened? So, I call him and they told me that everyone was dead. They told me that he was gone. I just worked away. And my managers are following me. It was just so hard for me. And he said I just can't tell you because I don't know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: And you might -- again, hard to hear that. You've heard the condolences, of course, from the first lady and the president to the owners here. Important to note of what a cultural touchstone this was for the LGBTQ community. They had had Thanksgiving dinner here for the past 10 years. Of course, that won't happen this year. A local church is going to pick that up as well as this community tries to heal. Back to you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Steve Patterson, thank you for that very moving interview. Julie Tsirkin, thank you as well.\par \par \uc2 2022 has brought a 20 percent increase in the cost of your Thanksgiving dinner. What we're not thankful for, inflation? I'll talk with Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2  of Michigan about what if anything Congress can do to address the ongoing economic pressures. You're watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Inflation is the annoying guest nobody wants at the Thanksgiving table this year because prices of everything from the star of the show, the turkey, to the stuffing, to pumpkin pie all the way up this year. And if you think cooking it all from scratch might spare you a few pennies, think again. The costs of staples, like eggs, butter, and flour have also skyrocketed. So, how are people fighting this surge? NBC's Marissa Parra is out of the \uc2 Safeway\uc2  grocery store in Washington, DC. When you talk about last-minute shopping, this is very last minute. What are you hearing from folks there today?\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: So, to give you a sense of how many people are out here, so there were thousands that were here just at this store yesterday, and then safely told me that just here alone, they sold over 500 turkeys within the last 48 hours. So, kind of along the lines of what we're seeing with travel even though travel is more expensive, that didn't stop people from taking to the skies or hitting the roads. Yes, food might be more expensive, but that's not stopping us from seeing people shopping for those Thanksgiving ingredients. Maybe they're just doing it a little differently.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PARRA: You got a full cart. Is the price -- is the price what you expect it to be?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Let's not talk about grocery prices. We've noticed the prices. We've noticed the price increases.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: Have you made any adjustments because of it?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: How so? What are you not getting?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's a good question. Um, I'm considering -- reconsidering the store brand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I mean, I put the wings -- the wings back and got wingettes but we've lost so many loved ones within the last two years due to COVID. And if I got to pay an extra $10 for a pack of chicken to see the family that I have, I'm going to spend the money.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PARRA: That is such a wholesome answer. I love that answer. And so, one thing I just want to point out is that one of the reasons that we're seeing this increase in food prices, there's a couple of contributing factors. The war in Ukraine driving up the price of bread, right, the avian flu driving up prices of Turkeys like the ones you see behind me, and then, of course, the fuel prices driving up the cost of shipping that food all across the country. But like you heard like you just saw, and like you're seeing behind me, not stopping people from showing out to get their last-minute necessities ahead of the big dinner if they haven't started already.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Marissa Parra, thank you. I hope you get some turkey sometime today. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Michigan Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Happy Thanksgiving. It's good to see you. There have been some signs of relief on the inflation issue, but overall prices are still a big problem for families across the country. Not everyone can say cost me $20, $30 more, that's fine. Some people don't have it. Do you think enough is being done to fight inflation right now?\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL (D-MI): Well, I think that -- and Happy Thanksgiving before I start saying anything else. I think that we are trying to do a lot of things. The president has been, the Democratic House. We still have the House for another month, and I hope Republicans are going to work with us and that we don't see dysfunctionalism as we go into the new Congress.\par \par \uc2 We're doing a number of things, addressing the Ukraine war, trying to reach agreements that doesn't stop the supply of wheat where (INAUDIBLE) bringing down the cost of energy. We're bringing our supply chain home, which has been a big factor. I think we've got to continue to work on all of these things in a very bipartisan way.\par \par \uc2 The economy is weird right now. We still have some of the highest job numbers that we've seen, as well as unemployment numbers we've seen in several decades. And I think the election sent a message to everybody, which is they want to see us work together to address these problems and keep trying to fix them. Not see partisan dysfunction and not get anything done.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: If there's one thing you think you legitimately might be able to get done between now and the end of this session that might help. What do you think it might be?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Well, I have to think about that. I mean, actually, I hope we don't play games with the budget period. I hope we don't play games with the debt ceiling because that just contributes a very complicated climate in any economic issue. I do think we will get the budget done and I think it's an important social thing the Democrats have done before the end of the year, like you saw what the Supreme Court did addressing the overturn of Roe.\par \par \uc2 I think that there are Republicans and Democrats that want us to address the subject of (INAUDIBLE) or same-sex marriage before this Congress ends so that doesn't become something else. A serious issue at a time when we need to be focused on other serious issues. I do think that's a possibility or something.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me ask you about one very serious issue that's on the minds of a lot of Americans today. President Biden talked earlier today about the rash of mass shootings and the prospects for more gun legislation. Let me play that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm going to try to get rid of assault weapons.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED REPORTER: During the lame duck?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I mean, whenever I -- I got to make that assessment as I get in and I start counting the votes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Is there something you feel has a reasonable chance that could get it through, whether it's now, whether it's later? Do you think that this rash of shootings is having any impact on people who have been reluctant to get on board? Where do you view the possibility of legislation right now?\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: I'm a very honest person when it comes to discussing (INAUDIBLE) on gun legislation. I'm proud that President Biden wants to try to take it on again. I was there. It was my husband. We did not like the idea of taking -- banning those guns away, but actually -- I'll never forget the night long as I live. \uc2 John Dingell\uc2 , who I love, didn't agree with banning guns, who's a board member of the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and slept with a gun every night under his pillow. And yet when we didn't ban the assault weapons, it was one of those ugly night, the screaming matches that occurred, it happened.\par \par \uc2 And people are dying. People keep dying. This isn't something that's just all of a sudden happening. Nobody -- when people want to protect the Second Amendment, but how come -- you're not seeing it anywhere. In school, in a grocery store, in a movie theater, in a shopping center, in a church, a place of worship. We have to do something, and we need to get people that have heart and soul who understand.\par \par \uc2 One of the things \uc2 John Dingell\uc2  said to me before he died was, Deborah, times change. People need to realize how times are changing. We'll get it done in the House. Of course, let the Senate do it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Congressman \uc2 Debbie Dingell\uc2 , on that note, I again wish you and yours a Happy Thanksgiving and thank you for spending some of it -- your day with me. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 DINGELL: Happy Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Up next, what two major Georgia Supreme Court decisions on abortion and voting could mean for the Senate runoff next month. We've got that, coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We're following two big headlines out of the Georgia Supreme Court ruling that early voting in the Senate runoff can start as soon as this Saturday rejecting an appeal from Republicans and giving a boost to Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In another case, the court has reinstated the state's six-week abortion ban. An issue that could drive more voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Greg Bluestein, political reporter for the "Atlanta Journal Constitution" and an MSNBC political contributor.\par \par \uc2 Is it true you ran a half marathon this morning?\par \par \uc2 GREG BLUESTEIN, ATLANTA JOURNAL CONSTITUTION POLITICAL REPORTER: Run would be an overstatement. Jogged. But yes, finished it thankfully.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And here you are in a suit, in a tie. I'm blown away. So impressed. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:01]\par \par \uc2 Let's talk politics, shall we? Give us the lay of the land. Is this Senate race still as close as it seems?\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: Yes, we've only seen very scattered polling but the polling shows it's within the margin of error. It's 51-47 race Warnock up top. But, you know, with the electorate so unpredictable, it's hard to poll these races accurately because we just don't know who's going to show up after Thanksgiving for this December 6th runoff. But I can tell you that Warnock's campaign is fairly optimistic that things are tilting their way.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: OK. So the most recent poll I saw was from AARP, and Warnock is leading among independents by 15 points. And those between the ages of 18 and 49 by 24 points. Walker on the other hand is leading among those over 50 by nine points. So what are we going to see between now and that voting that's going to maybe make the difference?\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: Yes. Well, on one hand, you know, the fact that older voters are backing \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is a cause for concern for Democrats because those older voters tend to vote disproportionately in runoff elections here in Georgia and elsewhere. But what we're going to see, particularly from Senator Warnock's campaign is this sort of a two-step. He's going to be appealing to core base supporters with the helped of former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and other big named Democrats.\par \par \uc2 But he's also going after split-ticket voters. 200,000 voters backed Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , a Republican, but did not back \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in the midterm. Those 200,000 voters might hold the key to this election for Senator Warnock.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And besides the controversy obviously over the accusation that Walker paid for a former girlfriend's abortion, how has the abortion rights issue played in this race and is the reinstatement of the abortion ban in anyway likely to impact the runoffs? Might it, you know, energize some voters?\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: It could. I mean, it focuses attention in Georgia all over again on abortion rights which was one of the top issues that voters said were motivating their votes. And so now Democrats have this Supreme Court ruling to once again go out and energize their voters. And so Democrats are hopeful that this Supreme Court ruling, although they didn't agree with it, that this Supreme Court ruling could energize voters at just the right time for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Greg Bluestein, who not only run a half marathon but already has his Christmas tree up. You are amazing. Thank you so much and Happy Thanksgiving to you and yours. Appreciate you today.\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: You too.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Health officials are on alert today concerned that Thanksgiving gatherings could bring about an unwelcome guest. A surge in what is already the triple threat COVID infections, severe flu and a rising number of cases of the respiratory virus RSV. That tripledemic as it's become known is already pushing many hospitals and their resources to the limit.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Dr. \uc2 Kavita Patel\uc2 , former Obama White House policy director and an MSNBC medical contributor.\par \par \uc2 So good to see you. A lot of folks are gathered in close conditions today. Close quarters. What steps should all of us take in the face of these viral threats?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 KAVITA PATEL\uc2 , MSNBC MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Chris, I'm thankful to be here. Did not run a half marathon but now I'm feeling a little more motivated after that last segment.\par \par \uc2 Number one, just make sure if you're feeling sick and people are around the table you can still have a good time, but I would encourage people to try to increase your ventilation. Take those home tests. A lot of COVID cases are coming in because people are confused and they're not sure if they've got flu or a strep or RSV or any number of viruses. COVID is still out there even though the rates around the country are really pointing to influenza and RSV viruses.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So I read something pretty scary from the president of the Children's Hospital Association. Intensive care units are at or above capacity in every children's hospital in the United States right now. What should parents know?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Yes. So parents need to know two things. Number one, RSV is something that can absolutely just catch up on you. It's very common for children who have RSV. It presents like a common cold and in fact in most people it is, but in little children especially those under 2 it can really attack the small air waves. If your child looks like they're not taking their food in, that they're just really hungry for air, they're using other muscles like their neck or their stomach to breathe, that is a reason to call the pediatrician first if you're uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 Get to an emergency room especially a children's emergency room but be prepared to wait. We know that children's hospital are so overcapacity that it's hard. So be patient. Even though that's the hardest thing to do as a parent concerned about your child.\par \par \uc2 And then number two, older people can get RSV, too, Chris. So it's something that we want to just keep people on alert for. If you've got a child with RSV, wipe down surfaces. The droplets tend to live on surfaces up to hours, same with flu. Keep surfaces. Don't use common utensils and just try to humidify, use over-the-counter medication if your doctor gives you that advice.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And let me ask you finally about the Biden administration's push to get Americans vaccinated. I mean the numbers on booster shots are dismal. You were policy director under former President Obama. What can be done to get those numbers up?\par \par \uc2 [13:45:04]\par \par \uc2 PATEL: I think we need to keep pushing not on messages to just go out and get vaccinated. This is really a local ground game. We need to really make sure that doctors themselves -- I've talked to unfortunately, Chris, too many doctors and nurses who say, you know what, I didn't even get the booster. It didn't really do much. I've already had COVID and the primary series. I don't need the booster. So we just need to educate people that staying up to date is the best thing and making it easy for people. But this really needs to be done. Employers can get in this game, teachers, educators.\par \par \uc2 All we need to do is just make sure that it's not just COVID. Again, to your point. Flu vaccine rates are down. We're seeing measles and other regular childhood vaccination rates that are low. So getting back into the routine of getting into your physician or caretaker's office, talking to them. That's what's going to probably help the rates the most.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Dr. \uc2 Kavita Patel\uc2 , who did not run in the half marathon today but are you cooking?\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Did not. I'm actually pleasantly happy to be here so that I'm not having to cook but I'll probably on the cleanup crew. So that tells you what my household is like. Right?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I'd rather cook, actually. My feeling is if you put enough gravy on everything, it's delicious.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: It's true.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Kavita, it's great to see you. Thank you for coming. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 PATEL: Thank you. Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Up next, far from home. A look at how our brave troops are marking Thanksgiving overseas while serving our country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have a family here and we have a mission here as well, so.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Friends and family. I miss them like crazy and there's no one like them. They can't replace them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:21]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Airports have prepared for a surge in passengers over the Thanksgiving holiday, with a number of travelers expected to hit their highest level in three years, but staffing and aircraft shortages that have plagued the industry so far haven't reared their ugly heads this week, so what's ahead?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, the founder of the Points Guy, Brian Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, Brian. Pretty good news on the travel front this week so far, but as we look to the December holidays, what you are watching for?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KELLY\uc2 , THE POINTS GUY FOUNDER: Well, you know, things have been good this week. There is a weather pattern coming into the East Coast this weekend, so there could be more delays but, you know, the airlines, they had their meltdowns this summer. They seem to have taken the fall to rest up, staff up, and you know, there might be some lines in airports but overall things are looking good.\par \par \uc2 This is an early holiday week. Thanksgiving is almost a week early. So the holiday season will be longer. Will it get strained by Christmas and New Year's? We'll see but so far things are looking good.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And I guess the big question is, will it strain our wallets? I want to look at some of the stats for Christmas travel compared to last year. Domestic flights up 40 percent in price. International flights up 26 percent. 32 percent higher prices for hotels. So the big question is, are there any deals? And I know that you talk about something called the 21 days out rule. What is that?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Yes, so basically book as far in advance as possible, if you're thinking of going somewhere, you know, at the end of the year, book now. Also, you'll see that international flight prices have not increased as much as domestic. And with COVID restrictions being relaxed pretty much everywhere, except a couple of places in Asia, the key here is the U.S. dollar is at record highs, so more and more Americans are going to Europe or going to South America, where the dollar is strong. And you don't see 32 percent hotel increases around the world.\par \par \uc2 So that's where the best deals are. But I also recommend using Google Flights. You know, being flexible by a day or two, using their calendar search feature will help you sniff out the cheapest fare. There are some out there, but you've got to hunt for them.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, let me ask you about this because we're still seeing people trying to catch up on vacations after being home bound during COVID and one of the statistics you sent us really struck me. 92 percent of Americans have travel plans over the next six months. So if you could travel outside of the holidays, what are the best tips for travelers?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Absolutely. So you know the school holidays are just brutal for families, and I know a lot of people don't have flexibility but, you know, people are now seeing, you know, Florida, double or even and triple the air fare prices, so, you know, maybe you do a driving trip over school holidays, and you take a bigger trip in the summer when there's more flexibility. But it can be tough out there.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Brian Kelly, it's always great to see you. It looks like you're home for the holidays so have a great Thanksgiving. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Thanks. You too.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: President Biden and the first lady spent part of their Thanksgiving morning calling American troops, and each branch of the military who are stationed both here in the U.S. as well as abroad. Of course many were mobilized to Europe earlier this year as Russia invaded Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Here's NBC's chief international correspondent Keir Simmons from London. Hey, Keir.\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS CHIEF INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, good day to you. I'm not on the parade route, I'm on a London bus route, not quite as exciting but here to show you what is a haven of Americans in London. This place is called Sunday in Brooklyn. Inside it's already buzzing as they get ready to serve Thanksgiving dinner.\par \par \uc2 We've traveled around Europe to meet America's men and women in uniform celebrating Thanksgiving while serving their country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2  (voice-over): It's not like today's Thanksgiving parade back home. But it is a celebration of a military family that is sacrificing so much this Thanksgiving. More than 1200 American troops are supporting \uc2 NATO\uc2  troops in the Baltics, part of a \uc2 NATO\uc2  show of unity as tensions continue in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 From here in Lithuanian to Lavia, at Camp Adazi, the first infantry division, apart from family these holidays. Ready for combat in icy conditions, 5,000 miles from home, these soldiers take comfort in making the most of a beloved American tradition, even if it isn't quite like Thanksgiving with the family.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The holidays are kind of hard, you know, seeing them at the table, without me, but like I said, I have a family here and we have a mission here as well, so.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Friends and family. I miss them like crazy. And there is no one like them. They can't replace them.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : On constant alert, there is not much downtime.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 The camp kitchen plans a home away from home Thanksgiving feast.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Turkey, macaroni and cheese, collard greens, like everything. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : With apple pie and pumpkin pie. And a Happy Thanksgiving cake.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The meal is going to be great. The meal is definitely going to be great. We got a great team that is putting the meal together, and it's going to be awesome.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : And entertainment from the First Infantry Division's Big Red Band, who traveled all the way from Kansas, sharing souls from home and a special tribute for their service.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We get to play music that they're looking forward to, hopefully bring some remembrance of home to them, even though we're all away from our families but we can still show that we're here for each other.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: The First ID Band, planning a tour for troops in the Baltics this Thanksgiving, bringing their cheer to all serving their country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : God bless our servicemen and women. In a little taste of the Thanksgiving menu here, guys, traditional roast turkey breast, there's cranberry sauce, homemade corn bread, and for dessert, apple crumble. It's lunchtime here. I'm getting a little hungry. Happy Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Happy Thanksgiving to you, Keir Simmons, and our thanks to all the men and women in uniform who serve to keep us safe.\par \par \uc2 That's going to do it for me today. A big thank you to you for watching. Happy Thanksgiving, everybody. I'll see you back here tomorrow from 12:00 to 2:00. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday, 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020221125eibo00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for November 23, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Jonathan Capehart, Glenn Kirschner, Neal Katyal \par \uc2 7,062 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 November 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:46]\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Hey, Alicia, great to see you. Have a happy Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST, "AMERICAN VOICES": You too, friend.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: For the third year in a row, the United States reached a grim milestone. We surpassed 600 mass shootings in America, according to the Gun Violence Archive. That averages to be more than one shooting per day. This year could become the second highest for mass shootings since the group began tracking data in 2014.\par \par \uc2 These horrific numbers put into perspective the tragic loss of life caused by gun violence that we saw just this week. Two mass shootings in three days.\par \par \uc2 Last night, a manager opened fire on fellow employees at a \uc2 Walmart\uc2  in Chesapeake, Virginia, killing six people, and then himself. Just three days earlier, another mass shooting at Club Q in Colorado Springs, the only gay club in that Christian, conservative stronghold, killed five people and injured nearly two dozen more.\par \par \uc2 The suspect will be held without bond after appearing in court today, facing murder and hate crime charges.\par \par \uc2 As "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " put it, mass shootings in the United States this year have come at a pace so fast that one community has barely started mourning the losses before another takes place. Every country in the world has all of the same issues that have been attributed to American mass shootings, economic and social issues, mental illness, personal disputes, political anger, hate, and hateful rhetoric, sudden flares of rage.\par \par \uc2 Only in America are people routinely and repeatedly killed. No civilian can be safe from a weapon of war that shot hundreds of bullets in a single minute -- grocery stores, malls, schools, churches, synagogues, movie theaters, concerts, nightclubs, massage parlors, a Fourth of July parade on a neighborhood street, the list goes on and on. What makes America different are the guns.\par \par \uc2 In a statement today, President Biden promised more action on guns, saying, quote, because of yet another horrific and senseless act of violence, there are now even more tables across the country with empty seats this Thanksgiving. There are now more families that know the worst kind of loss and pain imaginable. This year, I signed the most significant gun reform in a generation, but that`s not nearly enough. We must take greater action.\par \par \uc2 The death tolls are unbelievable, but what about all the other people who survived and are victimized by gun violence? That`s the long shadow cast by the senseless acts of violence. All of the families will be without their loved ones this Thanksgiving for the very first time.\par \par \uc2 That includes families who lost children at Robb Elementary School in May, even though Uvalde has long since been pushed out of the headlights by other mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 The Sandy Hook Memorial has just been unveiled, as we approach the ten year anniversary of that mass murder. Have those families had another normal Thanksgiving? Will they?\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Lucy McBath\uc2  ran for Congress to fight for gun safety after her 17-year-old son was shot to death over loud music at a gas station ten years ago today. So, in an open letter, she wrote: Justice will be the day that no parent ever has to bury their child again. Jordan, thank you for watching out for your mom. Thank you for being on this mission with me, every day, to keep our communities safe.\par \par \uc2 We have witnessed the struggle of people that survived grievous injuries, and former Congresswoman \uc2 Gabby Giffords\uc2  who survived an assassination attempt that killed six others in 2011. She had to learn to walk and talk again. She wrote recently about the injuries that she`s still recovering from 11 years later.\par \par \uc2 Anytime somebody expresses frustration about the slow pace or progress, or a challenge that they faced, I tell them what has become my mantra. Move ahead.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:05]\par \par \uc2 I don`t wallow, and I would rather look ahead than look back.\par \par \uc2 The ones who live are traumatized by what they lived through, like Arnulfo Reyes, a teacher in Uvalde, who survived when 19 of his students and two fellow teachers did not.\par \par \uc2 Richard Fierro is a hero. He stopped the shooter, saved lives, and walked out of Club Q. Does he seem unharmed?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FIERRO, SUBDUED CLUB Q GUNMAN: I tried to save people, and it didn`t work for five. There are five people that aren`t home right now. And I thank God and it`s Thanksgiving, I went through this at Thanksgiving in Iraq, man. We lost dudes.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know what else to do. I really hope people kind of use this and shake someone`s hand, give someone a hug, give them a kiss. These are good people, man. These were all kids.\par \par \uc2 I feel no joy, I`m not happy, I`m not excited, the guy is still alive, and my family is not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Joining us now, John Feinblatt, president of Everytown for Gun Safety.\par \par \uc2 John, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 Polls show clear majorities support more gun safety laws. What will it take to stop the madness of mass murder after mass murder?\par \par \uc2 JOHN FEINBLATT, PRESIDENT, EVERYTOWN FOR GUN SAFETY: Well, thank you for having me, Jonathan. It`s been a very sad week, and in many ways, I wish I wasn`t here with you tonight.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I think that we have to take Gabby Gifford`s mantra, and look forward. The truth is, this year, Congress passed and President Biden signed the first gun safety bill in 26 years. He broke the logjam. It had bipartisan support with every Democrat and 15 Republicans on it. And over the objection of the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , which is particularly significant.\par \par \uc2 But I think what we`ve got to do is make sure that we have a solid, firm, gun sense majority in Congress. And, you know, the first thing that comes to mind for me is making sure that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who is a poster child for the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , is not elected. And that we send Senator Warnock back to Congress.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And so, if Senator Warnock is sent back to Congress, sent back to the Senate, what is the most important policy that can be pushed for -- that can be pushed for on guns right now?\par \par \uc2 FEINBLATT: Look, we have to close the loopholes in the background check system, which still looks a little bit like Swiss cheese. We have to support assault weapon bans, and we have to have a federal red flag law. Just look at Colorado, Colorado Springs, it gives us a blueprint for something that we have to do in the future. They had a red flag law.\par \par \uc2 That red flag law might have stopped the perpetrator, but it`s one thing to pass a law, it`s another thing to implement it, and they did not use the red flag law. They had the tool, they didn`t take it out of the tool box. So, we have to make sure that every state with a red flag law has training, that person knows if you see something, say something. We have to pass a federal one, so every state has a red flag law, which can be lifesaving if we use it.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: John, this is your work, and we have known each other for a long time. How do you avoid feeling hopeless, fighting on this issue?\par \par \uc2 FEINBLATT: Well, you know, when we first met, many, many years ago, gun safety was the third rail of American politics. At election time, people ran away from it. The political calculus has changed so significantly, we`ve got -- as I say, every Democrat is voting on gun safety bills, 15 Republicans. We have people no longer running away from it, but, in fact, at election time, are running towards it.\par \par \uc2 So, I think that we`ve made significant progress, but there`s more to do, there`s no question about it.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: John Feinblatt, thank you for coming to THE LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 FEINBLATT: Thanks, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: The murder of five people at a gay club in Colorado Springs did not happen in a vacuum. NBC News reports that a new study found that reports of hate crimes against LGBTQ people in major cities increased by over 51 percent in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Some Republican lawmakers and right-wing media personalities are relentless -- relentless pushers of anti-LGBTQ rhetoric. The report by \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2  found that in the month after Florida Republicans passed the "don`t say gay" bill, quote, extremist politicians and their allies engineered an unprecedented and dangerous anti-LGBTQ+ misinformation campaign that saw discriminatory and inflammatory grooming content surge by more than 400 percent across social media platforms.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:07]\par \par \uc2 There`s been a concerted effort by right wingers to menace LBGTQ folks out of public spaces. The attack at Club Q now makes folks wonder if they are safe in their own spaces.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA THURMAN, CLUB Q SHOOTING SURVIVOR: Our community is shattered. This is the only LGBTQIA+ space that we have in the city of Colorado Springs. Where are we going to go?\par \par \uc2 How can we do anything knowing that something like this can happen?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Joining us now, Kelley Robinson, the new president of Human Rights Campaigns. Also with us, Kierra Johnson, executive director of the National LGBTQ Task Force.\par \par \uc2 Kelley, I just want to give you the four to start us off, with your reaction?\par \par \uc2 KELLEY ROBINSON, PRESIDENT, \uc2 HUMAN RIGHTS CAMPAIGN\uc2 : I have no words. This is devastating. Look, safety in the United States is an illusion. In the last week, we have seen three mass shootings take place. We are not safe in grocery stores, we`re not safe on school trips, we are not safe in nightclubs and other places that we go to celebrate who we are.\par \par \uc2 This is a moment of crisis, especially for the LGBTQ+ community, especially for black and brown, and people of color in this country. But the thing that is important to know, is that this is not happening by accident. We see a direct line from violent LGBT -- anti-LGBTQ+ plus rhetoric online, from anti-LGBTQ attacks led by political leaders, to the deadly events that transpired this week.\par \par \uc2 So, my message is, look, for every politician attacking your community, we see who you are. We are demanding better.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Kierra, how can we combat that kind of rhetoric?\par \par \uc2 KIERRA JOHNSON, NATIONAL LGBTQ TASK FORCE EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR: I think we have to be diligent, and we have to call it out for what it is. Kelly was just saying it. You don`t get to cede violence and hate, through language, through law, through policy, though cultural norms, and then, you know, thoughts and prayers your way out of it, right?\par \par \uc2 Like we`ve got to be -- we`ve got to be calling these people out for what they are saying, for what they are seething. And hold them responsible for what it`s causing in, you know, in our communities across the country.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, Kelley, what will it take? You are both talking about the rhetoric, the inflammatory rhetoric that Republican politicians and right-wing folks in media have been engaged in. What will it take to make them stop?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: I want to be clear about what this crisis is about. One in five of any type of hate crime is now motivated by anti-LGBTQ+ bias.\par \par \uc2 We cannot look away from the reality of what our community is experiencing. And we`ve got to address it in a multi-pronged way. We need social media platforms to enforce their own nondiscrimination policies, and stop this proliferation of hate speech online. We need the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and local law enforcement agencies articulate a plan, to not only prevent, but to end these violent hate crimes that are occurring.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, we are calling on everyone to take a role in fighting back against the hateful attacks that we`re seeing from politicians at the state level. And to also do everything that we can to address this epidemic of gun violence. But if there`s any hope here, the hope is that we can do this together, people are not standing for this level of extremism.\par \par \uc2 We saw that a few Tuesdays ago in the 2022 midterms. We are seeing a shift right now. Our opportunity here is to turn the energy that people have, the anger that they have, the fear that they have, into meaningful action and policy change.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Kierra, how many to create a more inclusive society so that there is more forceful pushback when right wingers make these hateful statements?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: You know, we`ve got to be engaging people at the state level, and at the federal level, and getting people to continue -- or begin, in some cases, to put pressure on their electives. And when I say electives, I don`t just mean the congressional members, right?\par \par \uc2 Of course, I do mean our electives in Congress, but in the state legislators. I mean, frankly, in our school boards, and city councils. We cannot underestimate our own power, and there`s a lot of power at the local and state level. And we`ve got to be engaged around nondiscrimination policies.\par \par \uc2 We see it on the federal level, far too many policies and bills that have been introduced, that are laying dormant, like the George Floyd Justice and Policing Act.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:08]\par \par \uc2 There`s no reason that that`s not moving. We -- I was in Colorado when Columbine happened. It was surreal to me to be in 2022, watching this happen all over again. And the reality is, it`s the same thing. We are -- we are seeing this violence, the dehumanizing rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 We are seeing, you know, easy access to guns, and it`s -- no matter where we are seeing, it in relationship to black folks getting gunned down in the streets, people gunning down folks in churches, people getting gunned down in clubs and bars. Folks, it is just, at what point are we going to make enough noise, and hold their feet to the fire?\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Kierra Johnson, Kelley Robinson, we just want to point out two of the nation`s largest LGBTQ civil rights organizations run by two powerful Black women. Thank you both very much for coming to the last word.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  made it clear after the insurrection at the capitol that he had no interest in talking to the January 6th Committee. But what happens when it`s the Justice Department who wants questions answered? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:21:09]\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: When it comes to the January 6th Capitol riot, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is more than willing to talk about his experiences that day, so long as he is able to promote his book in the process. If you`ve got television cameras or newspaper pages to fill, even better.\par \par \uc2 The one group that he won`t talk to about Donald Trump`s attempt to overturn the 2020 election, the January 6th Committee. He closed the door on that less than a week ago in a TV interview, of course.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT: I never stood in the way of senior members of my team cooperating with the committee, and testifying. Congress has no right to my testimony.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: You`re closing the door on that?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I`m closing the door on that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: But what about Justice Department prosecutors? That is the question tonight, following a new report from "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " that the DOJ investigators are looking to question the former vice president as part of its January 6th investigation.\par \par \uc2 "The Times", quote, Mr. Pence, according to people familiar with his thinking is open to considering the request, recognizing that the Justice Department criminal investigation is different from the inquiry by the House January 6th Committee, whose overtures he is flatly rejected. The discussions about questioning Mr. Pence are said to be in their early stages. Mr. Pence has not been subpoenaed and the process could take months because Mr. Trump can seek to block, or slow his testimony by trying to invoke executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 Trump trying to invoke executive privilege to stop two of Pence`s top aides from speaking to the Justice Department`s grand jury. It didn`t work. And both reportedly testified to the grand jury over the summer. Is their former boss next? And will Trump try to stand in the way of the testimony if he is indeed willing?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , former acting solicitor general of the United States. Also with us is Glenn Kirschner, a former federal prosecutor, both are MSNBC legal analysts.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 Glenn, I`m starting with you. From a former prosecutor`s standpoint, what`s the benefit of speaking to Pence, especially considering that they`ve already spoken to his two top deputies?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yeah, you want to get -- you want to get it right out of the horse`s mouth. Let`s remember, Pence is not only a witness for Donald Trump`s pressure campaign, trying to get him to violate the law, the Electoral Count Act. He is also a victim.\par \par \uc2 And I have to say, Jonathan, I heard \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  say that he was closing the door and testifying on the January 6th Committee, I found that to be deeply unpatriotic. He puts these revelations in his book, he profits off of revealing information about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  trying to pressure him to overturn the election results.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to testify before a congressional committee, that is trying to enact legislation to prevent another insurrection in the future, he closes the door on that. As I said, I found that to be deeply unpatriotic.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, I see you nodding your head there, Neal, and I want you to respond to what Glenn said. But what do you make of the former vice president refusing to talk to the January 6th committee while on his book tour?\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Exactly. There are two investigations about January 6th. One is done by Congress, and the other is done by the Justice Department. The Justice Department one is a criminal investigation, meaning jail time is possible there. The congressional one is one about uncovering facts.\par \par \uc2 Pence has said in that interview you showed last week that he is refusing to talk to Congress, which is -- I agree with Glenn entirely, it`s unpatriotic. But I also say, it`s frankly disqualifying to say that this guy wants to be president of the United States and is afraid to go and tell the truth before Congress.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:08]\par \par \uc2 And he`s going -- you know, his aides have gone and testify, but he`s been hiding in the shadows, and is continuing to hide thus far. So, I`m glad to see that the Justice Department is finally calling him on this, and saying, look, you`re like the central figure of a lot of this, and all of the key times, I mean, you are kind of the Forrest Gump of January 6th. And so you have got to tell us what happened.\par \par \uc2 And, look, I think that there is a chance that Trump might cooperate -- Pence would cooperate with a subpoena. If you think about, it if an angry mob tried to kill you or me, you`re probably having a few things to say to the investigators. And so, there`s that possibility.\par \par \uc2 But I think that the more likely thing is that Trump will try, as he always does, any legal maneuver to block his testimony for the executive privilege, and as you say, that`s exactly the maneuver that failed before, and it will fail again.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: You know, to Neil`s point, Glenn, you know, he was there, meaning \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was there, and the committee hearings reveal how much danger Pence himself was in on January 6th. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER WHITE HOUSE AIDE: I remember Pat saying something to the effect of, Mark, we have to do something more. They`re literally calling for the vice president to be effing hung. And Mark responded something to the effect of, you heard it, Pat, he thinks Mike deserves it, he doesn`t think they`re doing anything wrong. To which Pat said something, this is effing crazy, we have to do something more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Glenn, what charges have the prosecutors been looking at for Trump or his circle for January 6th?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Oh, goodness. Where to begin?\par \par \uc2 So, inciting an insurrection, potentially still a seditious conspiracy. I think there`s still some evidence that needs to be developed on that front. Obviously, instructing an official proceeding, the electoral college count, the book certification. He was unlawfully pressuring a high government official, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , urging him to violate the law.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , Jonathan, he can close the door on testifying to the January 6th Committee, he could even close the door on a voluntary interview with federal prosecutors. Federal prosecutors cannot compel him to come in for and voluntary interview.\par \par \uc2 You know what he can`t close a door on? A federal grand jury subpoena. Let him try to close that door, and I suspect that special counsel Jack Smith, who kicked that door wide open, perhaps even off of the hinges, and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  will end up inside of a federal grand jury.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Neil, we`ve got one more question for you, but on an almost different subject, it`s about the jury deliberations that are underway in the Oath Keepers seditious conspiracy case. It was an ambitious chart.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that the verdict will impact what the DOJ decides to do about future January 6th charges?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: That one is to Glenn, I think, not to me.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Thank you, Neal, I appreciate that. We`ve been watching that for weeks.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: All good.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you, no case is a sure thing, but I want prosecutors to do -- former colleagues of mine tried really an expert case. As often as we are criticizing decisions with the Department of Justice, we have to give them props because the American people were extremely well-represented in this prosecution. I suspect the things are going to fall, prosecutions are going to begin to see guilty verdicts come out of that jury.\par \par \uc2 It`s still going to take some time. It was a long trial, and there are five defendants on trial. I think if this suspicious conspiracy charges stick, you will see that emboldened the prosecution to continue to move forward. You might even see defendants who are pending trial in seditious conspiracy cases. There are more Oath Keepers to be tried, the Proud Boys will be tried for conspicuous conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 You might see some of those dominoes start to fall, some more guilty pleas entered, even perhaps with cooperation. I think that that will keep the ball rolling in the direction of justice.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Neal, I owe you one, because my faulty memory, I owe you one.\par \par \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , Glenn Kirschner, thank you both very much for coming back to THE LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  had trouble saying the word election on television last night. But with his right off against Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , now just 13 days away, that might be the least of his worries. We`ll explain, after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:34:29]\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: If Senate Republicans want to enter the new Congress in a split Senate, and maintain a power-sharing agreement, giving them equal say on committees, they must get Georgia voters to elect this man.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: First of all, this election is more than \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . This election is about the people. And I said this is we the people, not we the government.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 That was an unfortunate mispronunciation of the word "election". Especially considering Walker`s current controversies. Aside from Herschel Walker`s many gaffes, new reporting suggest the political landscape in Georgia could be shifting less than two weeks from the Senate runoff.\par \par \uc2 Today, the Georgia Supreme Court reinstated a ban on abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, less than a week after a lower court overturned that ban. Mid term exit polling data from NBC News found that among Democratic voters in Georgia, abortion was the top issue that brought them to the polls. Additionally, a majority of Georgia voters say abortions should be legal.\par \par \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , who is also a reverend, made abortion rights a core tenet of his campaign, and earned nearly 38,000 more votes than \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in Georgia`s general election.\par \par \uc2 Organizers in Georgia are hoping that voters will again award \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  more votes to defend reproductive rights in the runoff election.\par \par \uc2 One group representing veterans and military families released this advertisement this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It is a simple choice really. Either you and I get out to vote to protect the freedoms we have fought for, or we get a senator who will vote to ban abortion first chance he gets.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Early voting in Georgia will start this Saturday in at least 22 counties. Georgia`s Republican secretary of state tried to block that because of a Georgia law that bars Saturday voting if there is a holiday within two days before it. But Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Georgia`s Democratic Party sued, arguing that that restriction did not apply to runoffs. The courts agreed.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now LaTosha Brown, cofounder of the Georgia-based organization, the Black Voters Matter Fund. Also with us, Stuart Stevens, a veteran of five Republican presidential campaigns. He is a principal with Resolute Square, a pro-democracy media platform.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 LaTosha, Black Voters Matter was on the ground in Georgia today, distributing food ahead of Thanksgiving and stressing the importance of the runoff election. As you`ve talked to voters throughout the state, what are you hearing? Does enthusiasm feel high?\par \par \uc2 LATOSHA BROWN, COFOUNDER, BLACK VOTERS MATTERS FUND: One, thank you for having me, Jonathan. (INAUDIBLE) you know, we have been on the ground, and what we`ve been hearing from voters is that voters are very determined about what is happening in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 We are looking at Herschel Walker so many people have (AUDIO GAP). They are saying they`re really concerned about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , not only about being (AUDIO GAP) embarrassing. He`s also being (AUDIO GAP) we have to know that he`s really for the community but what we`re hearing on the ground makes (AUDIO GAP).\par \par \uc2 The people are very resolute that they`re going to come out and that they`re going to vote, and I think what we`re going to see is (AUDIO GAP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Resolute -- that is, that is the right where.\par \par \uc2 Stuart, how do you think today`s ruling, reinstating the state`s six-week ban on abortion will impact this race. And conversely, how motivated are conservatives to come out for Walker, given that the Democrats have already won control of the Senate?\par \par \uc2 STUART STEVENTS, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: Well, you know, the whole Walker candidacy was an absurdity. In the general election, he got what 200,000 fewer votes than Kemp did at the top of the ticket. So this was just a bad idea, that it`s sort of playing itself out inevitably.\par \par \uc2 I think that it really -- the only excuse to vote for Walker really, if you`re a Republican was control of the Senate. And that is gone now. So I don`t think this is going to be particularly close. The only danger would be if Georgia voters thought that Warnock had it in the bag.\par \par \uc2 But all the efforts that going on on the ground I think will counter that. I don`t think it`ll be (INAUDIBLE) votes.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And LaTosha, Senator Warnock`s latest ad was about Thanksgiving. Let`s play some of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): Politics these days is often used to divide us. But Thanksgiving offers us an opportunity to consider all the things we share in common. We all want better lives for our children. We all want to live in safe and secure communities. We all want to be treated with dignity and respect.\par \par \uc2 Let us pause this Thanksgiving, and celebrate that these are the values that inspire us all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: LaTosha, that ad made zero mention of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  or the runoff election, and ended with a prayer. How do you feel that ad will play among Georgia voters who local reports suggest have become fatigued and are tired of the nonstop attack ads?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: They`re absolutely tired (INAUDIBLE) at the end of the day, I often say to our community it`s our politics.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:52]\par \par \uc2 BROWN: And so what we`re seeing is I think in that ad, I think that Senator Warnock makes a real human connection. (INAUDIBLE) This is really about (INAUDIBLE) the people of Georgia -- this is not about a political race. This is really about our power, including a person in office meeting an agenda that was (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And before we go, there is new reporting from the "Texas Tribune" that found that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has been claiming a tax exemption in Texas that he can only qualify for if he is a primary resident of Texas.\par \par \uc2 And Walker took that exemption this year, even after launching his Senate bid. MSNBC reached out for a comment, but has received no response. My question to you, Stuart is, do you think at this point in the race, that that development will have any impact, whether legally or politically on Herschel Walker`s candidacy?\par \par \uc2 STEVENS: Well, you know, in this kind of runoff, at the end of the campaign, you want to win every day. And the fact that it comes out that you don`t really live in the state that you`re running for wouldn`t be counted be counted as a plus.\par \par \uc2 I mean you just have to imagine, what was it like in the room where they said, you know, we should get \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  to run for the Senate in Georgia. I mean it`s just a ridiculous idea. I mean no one in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  -- if he did get elected, would ever say you know, I`m going to ask Herschel what he thinks about this issue.\par \par \uc2 And Georgia has had tremendous senators in the past, and they have two great senators now. And I think that that ad by Warnock I thought was terrific. He is a very good advertiser. He`s running a very good campaign.\par \par \uc2 So I think this is one of the things where the better candidate who`s running the better campaign is going to win this thing.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: And we`re going to find out for sure on December 6th. LaTosha Brown, Stuart Stevens, as always, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has long been a trail blazer on the issue of LGBTQ rights. Coming up next, a Navy veteran who saw Pelosi`s leadership firsthand 35 years ago when the Reagan administration was failing the nation, as the AIDS epidemic took hold will join us.\par \par \uc2 [22:42:03]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: It has been with great pride in my 35 years in the house that I have seen this body grow more reflective of our great nation, our beautiful nation.\par \par \uc2 When I came to the Congress in 1987, there were 12 Democratic women. The new members of our Democratic Caucus will be about 75 percent women, people of color, and LGBTQ.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: The second national march on Washington for lesbian and gay rights was held more than 35 years ago on October 11th, 1987 in response to the Reagan administration`s failure to respond to the AIDS epidemic.\par \par \uc2 This is how the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " described the march in 1987. "The rally began at 9:00 a.m. with music and a statement from Dan Bradley, a White House aide in the Carter administration, who suffers from AIDS. Mr. Bradley said he took greatest satisfaction in the fact that after a lifetime of struggle and fear, I had the courage in 1982 to say loud and clear, I am gay and I`m proud. That message was echoed throughout the day-long demonstration as a succession of speakers urged participants to stand up for their rights and to fight against the stigmas and stereotypes often attached to homosexuals."\par \par \uc2 More than 200,000 people marched that day, including the newly-elected congresswoman from San Francisco, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How are you enjoying your first few months in congress?\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Absolutely great. And this is a great day to be at the march.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why did you decide to come out here today? You`re one of the few members of Congress here.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Well, I have a strong contingent of San Franciscans here in Washington for it. I`ve marched in the AIDS walk, the gay freedom day parade. And this is a logical thing for me to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Our next guest was also at the march that day. This is Navy veteran James Brown. He posted this photo online the day Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  announced her retirement as Democratic leader in the House. He wrote this caption for the photo.\par \par \uc2 I marched with the California contingent with my friends from the Bay Area, where I was stationed. And there was \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . She saw what I was doing. She got me. She seemed to know what I was going through and why I was there.\par \par \uc2 And she, a person who is going to go make some laws, made me know that it mattered to her. That we were doing good work that day. And that she was going to continue that progress in the capital that we were all marching toward.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is the Navy veteran from that photo with Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , James Brown.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Brown, thank you for being here. It is great to see you. You posted that photo after \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  announced she was stepping down from Democratic leadership. What was your reaction to hearing her announcement?\par \par \uc2 JAMES BROWN, NAVY VETERAN: Well, I was going about my business. It was the end of the workday, and I didn`t know that it was going to happen. And I heard it on TV. I heard the announcement, I heard the speech that you just showed.\par \par \uc2 And I had to sit down. I did not expect to have that kind of reaction. But what it is was it provided for me, like a book end to a story that started on that day.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:57]\par \par \uc2 You know, I am a lot older now, she is a lot older. And I saw that in that moment. And I felt close to her, because I remember that she was such an important part at the beginning of this journey for me.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: What was your reaction, your first reaction, to see \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  on Pennsylvania Avenue that day?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, it makes me so happy to see that picture. And I`ve never seen the video that you just played of that day. And it was -- just things were so dark then, to be a gay -- a gay person in America with the government`s response to AIDS and being in the military.\par \par \uc2 You know, I did not think it would be such a big deal to be gay in the military. But I had to hide it from my friends. And it just seems so stupid. And all of that was because of government policy.\par \par \uc2 And so, then there is \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  at the march. And you know, I did not know who she was. My friends were political activists, and they said, that`s \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . She`s our new congresswoman. And I thought God, she`s so hot. First of all, let`s go talk to her.\par \par \uc2 I had a crush on Marlo Thomas when I was little. And she looks enough like her.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: She does.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: She really does. And she did then. So, I went over and started talking to her. And she heard my story. And she told me, like this is what I am here for. It`s not going to be like this once I get up there in the capital that we marched to.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Mr. Brown, one more question for you. And, I mean, you look hot too in that uniform. And I`m wondering, what made you wear your uniform to that march?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well that was part of the reason --\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: So that was it, you just wore your uniform. Wear your uniform?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: That wasn`t. That wasn`t. That wasn`t even -- I mean, I would`ve worn my dress clothes if that`s what I was going for. No, I just want to show that gay people were in the military. And I expected there to be a lot more people there in uniform. But there weren`t.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: What would you say to Speaker Pelosi if you had a chance to talk to her, as she winds down her time as House Democratic leader?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, of course, I would say thank you to her for her life of service. Something that I hope that we can do in this moment is reflect on what a life, a passionate and dedicated service can do, to be able to see how life was then for a gay American in the military, scared of AIDS compared to how it is now. And to see that she was there in leadership when all of that was happening shows us what a human being can do when they work really hard.\par \par \uc2 And she is an inspiration to us all. But -- thank you and I know she is not going to probably start arresting anytime soon, but she deserves to, if she chooses to.\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: James Brown, you know, you both are still cute, after all of these years. Thank you, Mr. Brown, very much for coming to the LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s LAST WORD is next.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 [22:53:09]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: President Biden and his family are spending the Thanksgiving holiday on Nantucket. Vice President Harris and her family are spending the holiday in California.\par \par \uc2 And on this Thanksgiving eve, I am taking a point of personal privilege to share once again one of my favorite videos ever. November 29th -- November 24th, 2019, three years ago tomorrow. Then presidential candidate \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  answering my husband Nick`s question about Thanksgiving turkey preparation. This was in South Carolina, while she was getting a mic check before an appearance on Politics Nation. And since I don`t know anything about cooking, I recorded the whole answer on my phone. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If you have time to do a white brine, that`s fine. And do it, like, a pot of water, a couple of baileys, a little sugar, a couple of peppercorns, you can even do it a slice of orange, something like that. Oh, yes, hi. Yes, I`m here.\par \par \uc2 Ok, I`m going to talk about a recipe while you check things, is that ok? Ok, so tell me if I`m annoying.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ok.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Hey, how are you doing? Hi ok, so Nick, if you ok -- Nick, -- but a dry brine is easier. And do a brine through 24 but 48 hours is best if you have the time. Make sure -- you guys getting a fresh turkey?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And so, just -- one minute on, I have one minute.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ok. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Kosher salt, fresh ground pepper, maybe chop up a little thyme. And just, and then, you can mix it -- ok, so, do the salt and pepper all over, like -- just like lather that baby up, right. On the outside, in the cavity.\par \par \uc2 You can also chop -- but not with the thyme, just the salt and pepper. Mix that up also with some thyme. You can even do a little rosemary if you want under the skin with some butter, before you`re going to cook it. So that that butter will just melt in there.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:54]\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And then get a nice big bottle of cheap white wine to base with butter.\par \par \uc2 Yes, hi.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAPEHART: Yes, hi. Happy Thanksgiving. And remember to give thanks to the farm workers who work tirelessly every day to get that food to our tables.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD. I will see you on the "SUNDAY SHOW" starting at 10:00 a.m. eastern, right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s coverage continues right now with "ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020221124eibn00001\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-